window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"readme": "# 🎯 NISM Series X-B: Investment Adviser (Level 2) — Complete Study Guide\n\n> **Everything you need to ace the exam — explained simply, chapter by chapter.**\n> Built from the official NISM Workbook (Version: September 2025).\n\n---\n\n## 📋 What this exam is about (read this first!)\n\nThink of an **Investment Adviser** as a financial doctor. A doctor checks your body and tells you what medicine to take. An Investment Adviser checks your **money situation** — your income, your goals (buying a house, kid's college, retirement), your fears about losing money — and tells you which financial \"medicines\" (insurance, mutual funds, pension plans, etc.) are right for *you*.\n\nThe **Level 2 exam (Series X-B)** tests whether you can give *complete, advanced* advice. It focuses on five big areas:\n\n1. **Insurance** — protecting people from money disasters (death, accident, illness, fire).\n2. **Retirement planning** — making sure people have money when they stop working.\n3. **Taxation** — legally paying the least tax on different investments.\n4. **Estate planning** — deciding who gets your money/property after you die (wills, trusts).\n5. **Behavioural finance & risk profiling** — understanding the *psychology* of why people make money mistakes.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Exam Structure (very important — plan your time!)\n\n| Part | What | Marks |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs)** | 90 questions × 1 mark | **90** |\n| **Case-based Questions** | 6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks | **60** |\n| **TOTAL** | | **150** |\n\n- ⏱️ **Time:** 3 hours\n- ✅ **Passing score:** 60% (i.e., **90 out of 150 marks**)\n- ⚠️ **Negative marking:** 25% of the marks for that question. So a wrong 1-mark MCQ costs you **−0.25**; a wrong 2-mark case answer costs you **−0.5**.\n- 🧮 **Calculator note:** Test computers have **Excel / OpenOffice Calc** — practise doing tax and return calculations in a spreadsheet.\n\n> **Smart strategy:** Because of negative marking, only guess if you can eliminate at least 2 options. The **case studies are worth 60 marks (40% of the exam)** and are mostly *numerical* (tax, capital gains, retirement corpus). Chapter 20 is dedicated to these — don't skip it!\n\n---\n\n## 📚 How to use this study guide\n\nEach chapter folder contains **three files**:\n\n| File | What it is | How to use it |\n|------|-----------|---------------|\n| `notes.md` | **Short Notes** — the whole chapter explained simply, with every formula and the *logic* behind it | Read first, 2–3 times |\n| `flashcards.md` | **Flashcards** — Question on one side, Answer on the other (Q&A format) | Revise daily; cover the answer and test yourself |\n| `questions.md` | **Question Bank — 100 MCQs + a worked case study per chapter**, each answer fully explained (the formula used *and* the logic) | Test yourself *after* reading notes |\n\nThat's **~2,000 practice MCQs + 20+ case studies** across the guide. Each `questions.md` is organised into three difficulty tiers so you can build up gradually:\n\n- 🟢 **Tier 1 — Easy (Recall):** definitions and facts (≈Q1–35)\n- 🟡 **Tier 2 — Medium (Application):** \"which rule/product applies here\", comparisons (≈Q36–70)\n- 🔴 **Tier 3 — Hard (Numerical & Scenario):** multi-step calculations and exam-style scenarios (≈Q71–100)\n\nEvery answer is hidden inside a collapsible *\"Answer & Explanation\"* block — so you can attempt the question first, then expand to check **why the right option is right, why the others are wrong, and the exact formula/calculation behind it.**\n\n> 📌 **Chapter 20** is special: instead of 100 single MCQs it gives you **40 skill-builder MCQs + 12 full exam-style case studies** (each with 5 sub-questions) — direct practice for the 60-mark case section.\n\n**Recommended study cycle for each chapter:**\n> Read `notes.md` → Do `flashcards.md` → Attempt the `questions.md` tiers (Easy → Medium → Hard) → Re-read the notes for anything you got wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Chapter Map (with the official module structure)\n\n### Module 7: Risk Management and Insurance Planning\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 1 | Basics of Insurance | [`chapter-01`](chapter-01/) |\n| 2 | Life Insurance Products | [`chapter-02`](chapter-02/) |\n| 3 | Non-Life Insurance Products | [`chapter-03`](chapter-03/) |\n\n### Retirement Planning\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 4 | Retirement Planning Basics | [`chapter-04`](chapter-04/) |\n| 5 | Retirement Products | [`chapter-05`](chapter-05/) |\n| 6 | Miscellaneous Aspects of Retirement Planning | [`chapter-06`](chapter-06/) |\n\n### Taxation\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 7 | Concepts in Taxation | [`chapter-07`](chapter-07/) |\n| 8 | Capital Gains | [`chapter-08`](chapter-08/) |\n| 9 | Income from Other Sources | [`chapter-09`](chapter-09/) |\n| 10 | Taxation of Debt Products | [`chapter-10`](chapter-10/) |\n| 11 | Taxation of Equity Products | [`chapter-11`](chapter-11/) |\n| 12 | Taxation of Other Products | [`chapter-12`](chapter-12/) |\n| 13 | Tax Provisions for Special Cases | [`chapter-13`](chapter-13/) |\n\n### Estate Planning\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 14 | Basics of Estate Planning | [`chapter-14`](chapter-14/) |\n| 15 | Tools for Estate Planning | [`chapter-15`](chapter-15/) |\n\n### Behavioural Finance & Risk Profiling\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 16 | Basics of Behavioural Finance | [`chapter-16`](chapter-16/) |\n| 17 | Behavioural Finance in Practice | [`chapter-17`](chapter-17/) |\n| 18 | Risk Profiling for Investors | [`chapter-18`](chapter-18/) |\n\n### Putting It All Together\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 19 | Comparison of Products Across Categories | [`chapter-19`](chapter-19/) |\n| 20 | Case Studies | [`chapter-20`](chapter-20/) |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ 7-Day Crash Revision Plan\n\n| Day | Cover | Why |\n|-----|-------|-----|\n| 1 | Ch 1–3 (Insurance) | Conceptual, easy to score |\n| 2 | Ch 4–6 (Retirement) | Lots of product features + some maths |\n| 3 | Ch 7–9 (Tax concepts, Capital Gains, Other Sources) | The tax foundation |\n| 4 | Ch 10–13 (Tax on products + special cases) | Heaviest, most numerical |\n| 5 | Ch 14–15 (Estate Planning) | Definitions & legal tools |\n| 6 | Ch 16–19 (Behavioural + Comparison) | Theory + tying products together |\n| 7 | Ch 20 (Case Studies) + full flashcard sweep | Practice the 60-mark section |\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The 6 Numbers You Must Memorise Cold\n\nThese show up again and again — burn them into memory:\n\n1. **Pass mark:** 60% = 90/150\n2. **Negative marking:** 25% of the question's marks\n3. **LTCG on listed equity/equity MF:** taxed at **12.5%** above the **₹1.25 lakh** yearly exemption (no indexation)\n4. **STCG on listed equity/equity MF (Sec 111A):** **20%**\n5. **Section 80C limit:** **₹1.5 lakh** per year\n6. **Holding period to be \"long-term\":** **12 months** for listed equity/equity MF & listed securities; **24 months** for immovable property & unlisted shares.\n\n*(Each is explained in full inside the relevant chapter — these are just your quick-recall anchors.)*\n\n---\n\n*Made with care. Good luck — you've got this! 💪*\n", "chapters": [{"num": "01", "title": "Basics of Insurance", "module": "Module 7: Risk Management & Insurance Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 1: Basics of Insurance — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **need for insurance** — why people buy it at all\n- The **fundamental principles** of insurance (utmost good faith & insurable interest)\n- Key **concepts** in insurance (indemnity, benefit, subrogation, contribution, co-pay, deductible and more)\n- The **role of insurance** in personal finance\n- How to **invest through insurance** (and when you shouldn't)\n- The **role of an insurance advisor** and the steps in insurance planning\n- The **regulations** that govern insurance, intermediaries, and ULIPs\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 Simple Examples That Explain Insurance\n\nThink of insurance as a **group savings pot for bad luck**. Many people put in a little money; the few who actually suffer a loss get paid out of that pot.\n\n### Example 1 — Merchant Ships (19th century London)\n- 100 merchants each send 1 ship to India every month. If a ship is lost at sea, the merchant loses **Rs. 10,00,000**.\n- On average **2 ships** are lost each month → total monthly loss = `Rs. 10,00,000 × 2 = Rs. 20,00,000`.\n- If all 100 merchants each pay **Rs. 20,000** into a fund: `100 × 20,000 = Rs. 20,00,000`.\n- That fund exactly pays the 2 unlucky merchants their Rs. 10,00,000 each.\n\n**The concepts born here:**\n| Term | Meaning in this example |\n|---|---|\n| **Risk covered** | Ship lost at sea |\n| **Sum assured** (the amount insured) | Rs. 10,00,000 |\n| **Premium** (the fee you pay) | Rs. 20,000 |\n\n⚠️ **What can go wrong:**\n- (a) Not all 100 merchants join → this breaks the **Law of Large Numbers** (need lots of people for it to work).\n- (b) Some merchants want their premium *back* if their ship is safe → this is the idea behind **investment-cum-insurance** policies.\n\n### Example 2 — The King's Employees\n- A king has **500 employees**, each earning **Rs. 2 lakh/year for 10 years**. Each is the sole earner for his family.\n- Gross lifetime earnings per employee = `Rs. 2,00,000 × 10 = Rs. 20,00,000`.\n- **2 employees** are expected to die during the year → income lost = `Rs. 20,00,000 × 2 = Rs. 40,00,000`.\n- The king adds **Rs. 8,000 per employee** to a fund: `Rs. 8,000 × 500 = Rs. 40,00,000`.\n- That Rs. 8,000 is just **4% of the Rs. 2,00,000 salary**, yet it protects every family.\n\n| Term | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Risk covered | Loss of income due to death |\n| Sum assured | Rs. 20,00,000 |\n| Premium | Rs. 8,000 per employee |\n\n💡 **Exam tip:** These two examples are referenced again and again. Memorize the numbers — questions love them.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Need for Insurance\n\n**Insurance is a basic form of risk management** that protects the *economic benefits* you get from **assets**.\n- Assets can be **physical** (ships, buildings, machinery) or **human** (a person's earning ability).\n- An unexpected event (storm, illness, death, fire) can destroy or reduce the asset's ability to give benefits, and that has a **financial consequence**.\n\nYou have two choices when facing a risk:\n1. **Risk retention** — you bear the loss yourself.\n2. **Risk transfer** — you hand the financial consequence to someone else (an insurer).\n\n> **Insurance = transferring risk** from the **insured** (you) to the **insurer** (the company), which promises to **indemnify** (make good your loss). In return you pay a **premium**.\n\n**What makes an event insurable?** It must cause a **financial loss that can be quantified**. The premium depends on the *expected loss* and the *probability* of the event during the policy period.\n\n💡 **Analogy:** A person whose parents had heart disease, cancer or diabetes pays a *higher* health premium than someone with healthy parents — because the chance the insurer must pay is higher.\n\n### 1.2.1 Requirements of an Insurable Risk\nA risk must satisfy ALL of these to be insurable:\n\n| # | Requirement | Plain meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| a | **Large number of exposure units** | Many similar units face the same peril, so only a few claim each year (the pool works). |\n| b | **Insurable interest** | You must face a real *monetary* loss (not just emotional) if the thing is destroyed. Must be lawful. |\n| c | **Accidental & unintentional** | Loss must be by chance, outside your control. *Exception:* death (certain), but its *timing* is uncertain → insurable. |\n| d | **Determinable & measurable** | Cause and amount must be provable (e.g., medical bills, death certificate). |\n| e | **No prospect of gain/profit** | Must be a **pure risk** (only loss possible). Speculative risk like stock investing is NOT insurable. |\n| f | **Chance of loss must be calculable** | Insurer must estimate average frequency & severity. |\n| g | **Premium economically feasible** | Premium must be affordable AND far less than the value of the risk, else retaining the risk is smarter. |\n\n> **Principle of Indemnity:** Insurance restores you to where you were *before* the loss — not better. You should not profit from insurance.\n\n💡 **Trap:** Why is the merchant-ship premium Rs. 20,000 if all 100 join, but Rs. 50,000 if only 40 join? Because `Rs. 20,00,000 ÷ 40 = Rs. 50,000`. Fewer people sharing the same total loss = higher premium each. This shows requirement (a) in action.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Fundamental Principles of Insurance\n\n### 1.3.1 Utmost Good Faith (*Uberrimae Fidei*)\n- The insured knows more about himself than the insurer — this is **information asymmetry**.\n- The insurer uses screening (e.g., **medical tests**) but tests can't reveal everything (family history, habits).\n- So the insurer relies on the insured to **disclose all material information truthfully** — family medical history, own medical history, smoking/drinking, profession, etc.\n- **Material** = anything that could influence the insurer's view of the risk.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** If you hide material facts, the insurer can **reject the claim entirely** when the truth comes out at claim time. The low premium you paid is wasted AND the loss isn't covered. **When in doubt, disclose.**\n\n### 1.3.2 Insurable Interest\n- The insurer covers a risk only if you have **insurable interest** in the subject matter.\n- **Test:** You are *better off* if the risk does NOT happen, and *worse off* if it does.\n\n**Who has insurable interest?**\n- A business owner: in his *own* inventory (not a competitor's).\n- Family — **direct dependents** and relationships of **blood and marriage**.\n- Distant relatives (aunts, uncles, cousins, nieces, nephews): only if there is **proof of financial dependence**.\n- A **lender**: in the borrower, up to the amount outstanding.\n- An **employer**: in key employees.\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Concepts in Insurance\n\n### a) Indemnity Insurance\n\"A duty to make good any loss, damage or liability.\" The insurer pays the **actual loss suffered** — so the loss must be **determined first** before payout. *Example:* health insurance reimbursing hospital bills.\n\n### b) Benefit Insurance\nA **fixed, pre-estimated sum** is paid when a covered event happens — *without* measuring the exact actual loss. *Example:* life insurance pays a pre-fixed sum on death.\n\nUsed when:\n- the exact loss is **hard to ascertain** (e.g., loss of future income on death, or in a critical illness), OR\n- the loss is **small** and measuring it isn't worth the cost (e.g., a fixed **daily hospital cash allowance**).\n\n> **Table 1.1 — Indemnity vs Benefit (memorize this!):**\n\n| Policy | Type | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Life Insurance | **Defined Benefit** | Exact loss of earnings hard to ascertain |\n| Health (hospitalisation reimbursement / Mediclaim) | **Indemnity** | Bills show actual loss |\n| Daily Hospital Cash benefit | **Defined Benefit** | Loss small/hard to measure |\n| Critical Illness (lump sum) | **Defined Benefit** | Loss of earnings hard to ascertain |\n| Property Insurance (fire, flood, earthquake) | **Indemnity** | Loss is ascertainable |\n| Car Insurance | **Indemnity** | Loss is ascertainable |\n\n### c) Subrogation\nAfter paying your claim, the insurer **\"steps into your shoes\"** and pursues the party at fault to recover its money.\n- *Example:* Another car hits yours. Your insurer pays you, then chases the at-fault driver/insurer.\n- Benefit: you get paid fast; the insurer recovers its loss.\n- ⚠️ A policy **without** subrogation rights would be **more expensive** for you.\n\n### d) Contribution\nWhen you have **more than one policy on the same risk**, one insurer can claim a **proportionate share** from the others. Applies **only to indemnity policies** (because total claim can't exceed the actual loss).\n\n**Worked example:** Two flood policies of **Rs. 5 lakh each** (Company A & B). Loss = **Rs. 30,000**. If A pays, it can ask B to chip in `Rs. 30,000 × (5/10) = Rs. 15,000` (equal share of sum assured).\n\n💡 **Health insurance twist (IRDAI rule):** With multiple health policies, *you choose* which insurer to claim from. The chosen insurer **cannot** apply contribution if the claim is **less than** that policy's sum insured. Only if the claim **exceeds** the chosen policy's sum insured do you bring in other policies and apply contribution.\n\n### e) Co-Pay\nThe **proportion of the claim YOU pay**. Used in car & health insurance.\n\n**Formula:** `Your share = Co-pay % × Claim` ; `Insurer pays = Claim − Your share`\n\n**Worked example:** Co-pay 15%, claim Rs. 2,00,000 → you pay `15% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 30,000`; insurer pays `Rs. 1,70,000`.\n\n**Logic:** Makes you sensitive to costs (you share the bill) and lowers your premium. **Higher co-pay → lower premium.** But it's unpopular because you can't control what hospitals/garages charge.\n\n### f) Deductible\nThe portion you pay **first**, after which the claim becomes payable. The insurer pays only the **excess over the deductible**.\n\n**Worked example (car):** Deductible Rs. 1,000, claim Rs. 25,000 → insurer pays `25,000 − 1,000 = Rs. 24,000`. (India: compulsory deductible of **Rs. 1,000** for cars up to **1600 CC**.)\n\n**Two types (health):**\n1. **Compulsory deductible** — fixed by insurer or as % of sum assured. E.g., deductible Rs. 30,000, bill Rs. 80,000 → insurer pays `Rs. 50,000`, you bear Rs. 30,000. (Top-up/Super Top-up plans use this; their premiums are lower.)\n2. **Voluntary deductible** — you choose the amount you'll bear. Higher deductible → lower premium.\n\n### Deductible vs Co-Pay (worked comparison)\n- **Policy 1:** deductible Rs. 1,000 + co-pay 10%.\n- **Policy 2:** deductible Rs. 5,000, no co-pay.\n- Claim = Rs. 40,000.\n\n| Policy | Calculation | Payout |\n|---|---|---|\n| Policy 1 | `40,000 − 1,000 = 39,000`; then `39,000 − (10% × 39,000 = 3,900)` | **Rs. 35,100** |\n| Policy 2 | `40,000 − 5,000` | **Rs. 35,000** |\n\n💡 **Key difference:** A **deductible** is a flat amount subtracted; **co-pay** is a *percentage* you bear (applied after the deductible).\n\n### g) Other Concepts\n**Cashless claim payment:** Insurer pays the hospital/garage directly so you don't block your own cash. Relevant for indemnity (health/car). No impact on premium (same claim amount either way).\n- ⚠️ Cautions: the cashless network list **keeps changing**; the claim location may not be your favourite hospital; the process can be slow; hospitals may demand a **deposit** that defeats the purpose; pre- and post-hospitalisation still need reimbursement claims.\n- **Corporate (employer) policies** run smoother because they usually cover **pre-existing diseases** (individual policies often exclude these for the first 3–4 years), and corporates pay large premiums.\n\n**Nominee vs Legal Heir:**\n- Normally a **nominee holds the money in trust** for the legal heirs. (E.g., Mrs. Rich gets the FD but must share with the children if no will.)\n- **Beneficial Nominee** (Insurance Act 1938, amended Feb 2015, **Section 39(7)**): if the life-insurance nominee is the **parent, spouse, children, or any of them**, they get the money **beneficially** (keep it as owners, not as trustees).\n\n**Section 45 — 3-year rule:** No life insurance policy can be **called into question after 3 years** from the date of the policy / risk commencement / revival (whichever later). A death claim can't be denied on any ground after 3 years.\n- During the first 3 years a claim can be denied/cancelled on grounds of **fraud**.\n- Large death claims in the first 3 years invariably trigger investigation. → Always practise utmost good faith.\n\n**Health moratorium — 5 years:** After **5 continuous years** of paid premiums (no breaks), no health claim is **contestable** except for **proven fraud** and **permanent exclusions**. If sum insured is enhanced, the 5 years restart **for the enhanced limit** from the enhancement date.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 Role of Insurance in Personal Finance\n\nInsurance protects **income** — the base of every financial plan — from loss, reduction, or a sudden large charge. Examples in the text: death (Siddesh), stroke/critical illness (Manohar), accidental disability (Arushi), car repair (Sindhu), ongoing physiotherapy (Mohit), burglary (Jayant).\n\n> **Insurance comes BEFORE investments.** 🏏 *Cricket analogy:* a batsman always wears protective gear (pads/abdomen guard) before batting — one hit could end his career. Paying premium to protect your plan comes before investing for goals.\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Investing Through Insurance\n\nSome policies bundle **risk protection + savings**. The premium splits into an **insurance component** and an **investment component**.\n\n### 1.6.1 Should you invest via insurance?\n🏏 *Analogy:* a bat + protective gear can be bought as one kit or separately. Don't buy a combined kit just because it's cheap/convenient if either part is poor quality.\n\n**Merchant-ship \"return of premium\" math:** If the merchant wants his Rs. 20,000 premium back when the ship returns safe, at **12% p.a. (1%/month)** return:\n- He pays **Rs. 20,20,000**. Insurer puts **Rs. 20,000** in the pool, invests **Rs. 20,00,000**.\n- After 1 month: `20,00,000 × 1.01 = Rs. 20,20,000` returned to him.\n- ⚠️ The premium balloons to **more than the ship's value** — showing how mixing investment + insurance can backfire.\n\n### 1.6.2 Prioritizing insurance vs investment needs\n- An insurer's primary job is **risk coverage**, not investments.\n- To evaluate an insurance-cum-investment product: **(1) decouple** insurance and investment, **(2) check if risk coverage is adequate** — if not, reject it, **(3)** only then judge the investment return on its own merits.\n\n---\n\n## 1.7 Role of the Insurance Adviser\n\n### 1.7.1 Steps in Insurance Planning\n**a. Identify insurance need** — find situations causing loss/reduction of income or an unexpected charge. Three categories:\n- **Income replacement** (life, critical illness, accidental disability, annuities).\n- **Income protection** (health, motor — protect income from a large outflow).\n- **Asset protection** (household insurance — theft/destruction).\n\n💡 A **young person with no dependents** may need a **personal accident / critical illness** policy more than life insurance. A person **with dependents** needs **life insurance** (income replacement) most.\n\n**b. Estimate the coverage** — match the future value of costs, the protection period, and affordability. **Under-insurance** = you retain part of the risk; **over-insurance** = wasted premium.\n- *Tenure example:* Age 30 — a 30-year term plan costs **Rs. 13,800/yr**; a 40-year plan costs **Rs. 16,200/yr**. The extra **Rs. 2,400/yr** buys the option to keep cover past retirement (age 60→70).\n\n**c. Identify the most suitable product.**\n\n**d. Optimise the premium** — share non-mandatory info (motor), choose a higher **deductible**, use **No Claim Bonus**, and take **E-Insurance** (electronic) for discounts.\n\n**e. Monitor the coverage** — review after marriage, children, buying a house, or a big raise. Cover can even be **reduced** as goals are met.\n\n---\n\n## 1.8 Regulations Pertaining to Insurance\n\n### 1.8.1 Health Insurance (IRDAI)\n- **Standard terminology** for terms like pre-existing disease, portability, co-payment, deductible.\n- **Standardized exclusions** — insurers can have fewer, never more.\n- Mental illness, genetic disorders, congenital disease **removed** from the exclusion list.\n- Any health policy (except group / top-up / super top-up) can be **ported** to another company.\n- Group policies can port to individual policies (same company in year 1; any company from year 2).\n- Insurer must give **written, justifiable reason** for rejecting cover.\n- A claim **cannot be rejected** after **5 years** of unbroken policy continuation.\n- **Telemedicine** is covered.\n- Delay in claim settlement → insurer must pay **interest**.\n- All insurers must offer a **standard individual health product** (uniform wording, seamless portability, **no add-ons/optional covers** allowed).\n- Individual health policies **exempt from GST** w.e.f. **September 22, 2025**.\n\n### 1.8.2 Unit Linked Insurance Products (ULIP)\n- Full disclosure of **charges for each policy year**.\n- **Benefit illustrations at 4% and 8%** returns; the prospect must **sign** the illustration.\n- **Lock-in period: 5 years.**\n- Regular/limited premium ULIPs must have **level (uniform) premiums**.\n- A **cap on charges** from the **5th year** onward; charges spread evenly during lock-in (not front-ended).\n- Limited-premium ULIPs (except single premium) need a premium-paying term of **at least 5 years**.\n- All ULIPs (except pension/annuity) must carry a **mortality or health cover**.\n- **Discontinuance charges** capped (as % of fund value & premium, and as an absolute value).\n- **No surrender charges after 5 policy years.** Partial withdrawals allowed after the 5-year lock-in.\n- A **lapsed ULIP can be revived within 3 years** of lapsation.\n\n### 1.8.3 Insurance Intermediaries\n**Intermediary** includes brokers, reinsurance brokers, consultants, surveyors, loss assessors.\n\n| Broker type | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Direct Broker** | Charges fee to clients OR earns commission from insurers; arranges insurance with multiple Indian insurers. |\n| **Reinsurance Broker** | Arranges **reinsurance** (insurers transferring portions of their risk to other firms to spread risk). |\n| **Composite Broker** | Both direct + reinsurance broker. |\n\n- **Insurance Agent:** individual appointed by an insurer to solicit/procure business.\n- **Composite Insurance Agent:** agent for 2+ insurers, but **not more than one** life, one general, one health, and one of each mono-line insurer.\n- **Corporate Agent:** a company selling an insurer's policies (e.g., a travel agent selling travel insurance).\n- **Corporate Agent (composite):** can work with a **maximum of 3 life + 3 general + 3 health** insurers.\n- **Bancassurance:** when a **bank** becomes the corporate agent of an insurer.\n- **Insurance Web Aggregators:** maintain a website for online price comparison of different insurers' products.\n\n> **Table 1.2 — Agent vs Direct Broker:**\n\n| Parameter | Direct Broker | Insurance Agent | Corporate Agent |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Represents | **The client** | The insurance company | The insurance company(ies) |\n| Works with | Multiple insurers | More than one insurer in each line (life/general/health) if he advises dispassionately | Composite: max 3 life + 3 general + 3 health |\n| Can charge | Fees to client **and** commission from insurer | Commission only | Commission only |\n\n### 1.8.4 Intermediary Suitability Rules\n- To reduce mis-selling, agents must sell based on **product suitability** (risk appetite, financial goals).\n- Suitability had been optional and skipped; now it's required for investment-cum-insurance policies.\n- Agents can skip suitability **only with the client's written consent**.\n- Benefit illustrations must use **4% and 8%** returns; agents must **sign** them and clearly state **guaranteed vs non-guaranteed** benefits.\n\n### 1.8.5 SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013\n- An IRDAI-registered insurance agent/broker giving advice **solely on insurance products** is **exempt** from IA Regulations.\n- But if they also advise on **non-insurance securities/investment products**, that advice **is covered** by IA Regulations → they must follow **risk profiling, suitability, and fiduciary responsibility**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Insurance = risk transfer** from insured to insurer for a **premium**; based on a **pool** where many pay and few claim.\n- **Insurable risk** needs: large numbers, insurable interest, accidental loss, measurable loss, pure risk (no gain), calculable chance, feasible premium.\n- **Principle of indemnity:** restore, don't profit.\n- **Two principles:** **utmost good faith** (disclose everything) and **insurable interest** (you lose money if the risk happens).\n- **Indemnity** pays *actual* loss (health, property, car); **Benefit** pays a *fixed* sum (life, critical illness, daily hospital cash).\n- **Subrogation:** insurer chases the at-fault party after paying you.\n- **Contribution:** multiple indemnity policies share proportionately.\n- **Co-pay** = % you bear; **Deductible** = flat amount you bear first. Higher of either → lower premium.\n- **Beneficial nominee** (Sec 39(7)): spouse/parent/children keep the money as owners.\n- **Sec 45:** no life claim questioned after **3 years** (except fraud earlier). Health **moratorium = 5 years**.\n- **Insurance before investments.** Decouple, check cover adequacy, then judge investment.\n- **ULIP lock-in = 5 years**, illustrations at 4% & 8%, revive within 3 years.\n- **Direct broker = client's side**; **agent = insurer's side**; **bancassurance = bank as corporate agent**.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Item | Value / Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Merchant ship premium (all 100 join) | Rs. 20,000 |\n| Merchant ship premium (only 40 join) | Rs. 50,000 |\n| King's employee premium | Rs. 8,000 (4% of salary) |\n| Car compulsory deductible (≤1600 CC) | Rs. 1,000 |\n| Beneficial Nominee | Section 39(7), Insurance Act 1938 (amended Feb 2015) |\n| Life claim not contestable after | 3 years (Section 45) — except fraud in first 3 yrs |\n| Health moratorium period | 5 continuous years |\n| ULIP lock-in | 5 years |\n| ULIP benefit illustration returns | 4% and 8% |\n| ULIP revival window | 3 years from lapsation |\n| Health claim cannot be rejected after | 5 years unbroken continuation |\n| Individual health GST exemption | w.e.f. 22 Sep 2025 |\n| Corporate Agent (composite) limit | 3 life + 3 general + 3 health insurers |\n| LRS limit (foreign policies) | USD 2,50,000 per annum |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 1: Basics of Insurance — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What are the three basic concepts illustrated by every insurance example?\n> **A:** **Risk covered** (the bad event), **Sum assured** (amount paid if it happens), and **Premium** (the small fee paid to join the pool).\n\n**Q2.** In the merchant-ship example, what were the risk covered, sum assured and premium?\n> **A:** Risk covered = ship lost at sea; Sum assured = Rs. 10,00,000; Premium = Rs. 20,000. (2 ships lost × Rs. 10,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000 total loss ÷ 100 merchants = Rs. 20,000 each.)\n\n**Q3.** Why did the King need only Rs. 8,000 per employee to cover a Rs. 20,00,000 sum assured?\n> **A:** Only 2 of 500 employees were expected to die. Total loss = Rs. 40,00,000 ÷ 500 = Rs. 8,000 each — just **4%** of the Rs. 2,00,000 salary. Few suffer loss, all share the cost.\n\n**Q4.** What is the difference between risk retention and risk transfer?\n> **A:** **Risk retention** = you keep the risk and bear the loss yourself. **Risk transfer** = you pass the risk to an insurer for a premium. Insurance is risk transfer.\n\n**Q5.** What two things does an insurance premium depend on?\n> **A:** The **expected loss** and the **probability** of the event occurring during the contract period. Higher probability (e.g., family history of disease) → higher premium.\n\n**Q6.** List the 7 requirements for a risk to be insurable.\n> **A:** (a) Large number of exposure units, (b) Insurable interest, (c) Accidental & unintentional, (d) Determinable & measurable, (e) No prospect of gain (pure risk), (f) Chance of loss calculable, (g) Premium economically feasible.\n\n**Q7.** Why is a large number of exposure units important?\n> **A:** With many people sharing, only a few suffer loss yearly, so each premium is small. If only 40 of 100 merchants join, premium rises to Rs. 20,00,000 ÷ 40 = **Rs. 50,000** instead of Rs. 20,000.\n\n**Q8.** Death is certain — so how is life insurance possible?\n> **A:** Although death is certain, the **timing** of death is uncertain. That uncertainty makes it insurable. It is the one exception to the \"loss must be uncertain\" rule.\n\n**Q9.** Why is stock-market investing NOT insurable?\n> **A:** It is **speculative risk** — it can produce a gain or a loss. Insurance covers only **pure risk** (loss only). Insurance must not create a prospect of profit.\n\n**Q10.** What does the Principle of Indemnity mean?\n> **A:** Insurance restores the insured to the financial position they were in **before** the loss — never to a profit. Otherwise people would have no incentive to avoid losses.\n\n**Q11.** What is Utmost Good Faith (Uberrimae Fidei)?\n> **A:** Because the insured knows more than the insurer (information asymmetry), the insured must **truthfully disclose all material facts** (family history, smoking, profession). Hiding them lets the insurer reject the claim.\n\n**Q12.** What counts as \"material\" information, and what's the safe rule?\n> **A:** Anything that could change the insurer's perception of risk. **When in doubt, disclose** — a higher premium beats a rejected claim.\n\n**Q13.** What is the test for insurable interest?\n> **A:** You're **better off if the risk does NOT happen** and **worse off if it does**. You must face a real monetary loss (not emotional).\n\n**Q14.** Who has insurable interest in a borrower? In an employee?\n> **A:** A **lender** has insurable interest in the borrower up to the **amount outstanding**. An **employer** has insurable interest in **key employees**.\n\n**Q15.** What is the difference between Indemnity and Defined Benefit insurance?\n> **A:** **Indemnity** pays the **actual measured loss** (health, property, car). **Defined Benefit** pays a **fixed pre-decided sum** without measuring exact loss (life, critical illness, daily hospital cash).\n\n**Q16.** Is life insurance an indemnity or defined-benefit policy? Why?\n> **A:** **Defined Benefit** — the exact loss of future income from a death is hard to ascertain, so a fixed sum is paid.\n\n**Q17.** What is subrogation?\n> **A:** After paying your claim, the insurer **steps into your shoes** to recover the loss from the at-fault third party. You get paid fast; insurer recoups. Without it, premiums would be higher.\n\n**Q18.** What is contribution and which policies does it apply to?\n> **A:** When more than one policy covers the **same risk**, each insurer pays a **proportionate share**. Applies only to **indemnity** policies (loss is measurable; total can't exceed actual loss).\n\n**Q19.** Two flood policies of Rs. 5 lakh each; loss is Rs. 30,000. How is it shared?\n> **A:** Equal sum-assured share, so each insurer pays half = **Rs. 15,000**.\n\n**Q20.** In health insurance with multiple policies, when does contribution apply?\n> **A:** Per IRDAI, the insured chooses which insurer to claim from. If the claim is **within** that policy's sum insured → no contribution. Only if the claim **exceeds** it do other policies and contribution come in.\n\n**Q21.** What is co-pay? Give the worked example.\n> **A:** The **percentage** of a claim you pay. Co-pay 15% on a Rs. 2,00,000 claim → you pay Rs. 30,000, insurer pays Rs. 1,70,000. Higher co-pay → lower premium.\n\n**Q22.** What is a deductible? Give the car example.\n> **A:** A **flat amount** you pay first before the claim is payable. Car insurance has a compulsory deductible of **Rs. 1,000** for engines **≤ 1600 CC**. Claim Rs. 25,000 → insurer pays Rs. 24,000.\n\n**Q23.** Policy 1: deductible Rs. 1,000 + co-pay 10%. Policy 2: deductible Rs. 5,000. Claim Rs. 40,000. What does each pay?\n> **A:** Policy 1: 40,000 − 1,000 = 39,000; less 10% (3,900) = **Rs. 35,100**. Policy 2: 40,000 − 5,000 = **Rs. 35,000**.\n\n**Q24.** What are the two types of health-insurance deductible?\n> **A:** **Compulsory** (fixed by insurer or % of sum assured — used in top-up/super-top-up) and **Voluntary** (you choose; higher deductible → lower premium).\n\n**Q25.** What is cashless claim payment, and what's a caution about it?\n> **A:** Insurer pays the hospital/garage directly so you don't block cash. Caution: approved lists keep changing, location may not be your choice, and the process can be slow with deposits demanded.\n\n**Q26.** Why does cashless work more smoothly for corporate (employer) policies?\n> **A:** Corporate policies usually cover **pre-existing diseases** from day one (individual policies may not for 3–4 years), and corporates pay large premiums — so fewer disputes.\n\n**Q27.** What is a \"Beneficial Nominee\" and which section introduced it?\n> **A:** Under **Section 39(7)**, Insurance Act 1938 (amended **Feb 2015**), if the life-policy nominee is parent, spouse, children (or any of them), they keep the money **as owners**, not as trustees for legal heirs.\n\n**Q28.** What does Section 45 of the Insurance Act, 1938 say?\n> **A:** A life policy **cannot be questioned after 3 years** (from policy/risk commencement or revival, whichever later). During the 3 years it can be repudiated/cancelled only on grounds of **fraud**.\n\n**Q29.** What is the health-insurance moratorium period rule?\n> **A:** After **5 continuous years** of unbroken premium payment, no health claim is contestable — **except** for proven fraud and permanent exclusions. If sum insured is enhanced, 5 years restart for the enhanced limit.\n\n**Q30.** Why does \"insurance come before investment\"? (analogy)\n> **A:** Like a batsman wearing pads before batting — protection first. Paying a premium to protect the plan is acceptable before investing for goals.\n\n**Q31.** In a return-of-premium merchant scenario at 12% p.a., why does the premium balloon to Rs. 20,20,000?\n> **A:** Rs. 20,000 still goes to the pool; the other Rs. 20,00,000 is invested and grows to Rs. 20,20,000 in a month, returned to the merchant. To get the premium back, total premium must rise above the ship's value — showing the cost of bundling.\n\n**Q32.** What is the ULIP lock-in period, and what two return scenarios must illustrations show?\n> **A:** Lock-in = **5 years**. Benefit illustrations must show **4%** and **8%** scenarios, and the prospect must **sign** the illustration.\n\n**Q33.** Within how long can a lapsed ULIP be revived?\n> **A:** Within **3 years** from the date of lapsation.\n\n**Q34.** What is the difference between an Insurance Agent and a Direct Broker?\n> **A:** An **agent represents the insurer** (gets commission). A **direct broker represents the client** (can charge the client a fee AND earn commission), working with multiple insurers.\n\n**Q35.** How many insurers can a composite Corporate Agent work with?\n> **A:** Maximum **3 life + 3 general + 3 health** insurance companies.\n\n**Q36.** What is bancassurance?\n> **A:** When a **bank** acts as the corporate agent of an insurance company.\n\n**Q37.** When is an insurance agent/broker exempt from SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013?\n> **A:** When they advise **solely on insurance products**. If they also advise on non-insurance securities/investments, IA regulations apply (risk profiling, suitability, fiduciary duty).\n\n**Q38.** From what date are individual health and life insurance/reinsurance policies exempt from GST?\n> **A:** From **September 22, 2025**.\n\n**Q39.** What are the three broad insurance need categories?\n> **A:** **Income replacement** (life, critical illness, accidental disability), **Income protection** (health, motor), and **Asset protection** (household insurance).\n\n**Q40.** What life events should trigger a review of insurance cover?\n> **A:** Marriage, having children, buying a house, a large raise — anything that changes the demands on income. Cover may even be reduced as goals are met.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 1: Basics of Insurance — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Questions are arranged in three tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard numerical & scenario). Attempt them honestly: the NISM exam carries **25% negative marking**, so a wrong guess costs you 0.25 of a mark — when unsure, reason it out before answering.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Insurance is best described as a basic form of what?\nA) Tax planning  B) Risk management  C) Wealth creation  D) Estate distribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook defines insurance as \"a basic form of risk management that provides protection against the loss of the economic benefits that can be enjoyed from assets.\"\n- A) Wrong: tax benefits may follow but are not the definition.\n- C) Wrong: insurance protects value, it does not primarily create wealth.\n- D) Wrong: estate distribution relates to wills/nomination, not the core definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the merchant ships example, what was the sum assured per ship?\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 2,00,000  C) Rs. 10,00,000  D) Rs. 20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The loss to a merchant when a ship was lost was Rs. 10,00,000; that loss amount is the sum assured.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 20,000 is the premium each merchant paid.\n- B) Wrong: not a figure in the example.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 20,00,000 is the total fund/total monthly loss, not per ship.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In the merchant ships example, the premium paid by each merchant was:\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 10,00,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Rs. 8,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Each of the 100 merchants paid Rs. 20,000 into the fund (100 × Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 20,00,000).\n- B) Wrong: that is the sum assured per ship.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 50,000 is the premium if only 40 merchants join.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 8,000 is the premium in the King's employees example.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In the King's employees example, the additional premium per employee was Rs. 8,000, which equals what percentage of the annual salary of Rs. 2,00,000?\nA) 2%  B) 4%  C) 8%  D) 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: percentage = (premium ÷ salary) × 100 = (8,000 ÷ 2,00,000) × 100 = 4%. The terms: premium = Rs. 8,000, salary = Rs. 2,00,000. Intuition: a small slice of pay buys protection for the whole family.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: only 8,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = 0.04 = 4%.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The party that transfers risk and pays the premium is called the:\nA) Insurer  B) Insured  C) Broker  D) Surveyor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance enables risk transfer \"from the beneficiary (Insured) to the insurance company (Insurer).\" The insured pays the premium.\n- A) Wrong: the insurer receives the premium and bears the risk.\n- C) Wrong: a broker is an intermediary, not the risk-transferring party.\n- D) Wrong: a surveyor assesses losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The periodic fee the insured pays to the insurer is called the:\nA) Claim  B) Premium  C) Sum assured  D) Deductible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The insured \"pays a periodic fee, called premium, to the Insurer to receive this protection.\"\n- A) Wrong: a claim is the amount paid out on a loss.\n- C) Wrong: the sum assured is the amount insured.\n- D) Wrong: a deductible is the portion the insured bears first.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is NOT one of the requirements of an insurable risk?\nA) Large number of exposure units  B) Insurable interest  C) Prospect of guaranteed profit  D) Loss must be accidental\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An insurable risk must have \"No prospect of gain or profit\" — it must be a pure risk. A guaranteed-profit prospect makes it speculative and uninsurable.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these are all listed requirements of an insurable risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which type of risk is NOT insurable because it can result in either gain or loss?\nA) Pure risk  B) Speculative risk  C) Fortuitous risk  D) Fundamental risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Speculative risk (e.g., investing in the stock market) can produce a gain or a loss, so it is not insurable. Only pure risk (loss-only) is insurable.\n- A) Wrong: pure risk is precisely what IS insurable.\n- C) Wrong: fortuitous means by chance — that supports insurability.\n- D) Wrong: not the term the workbook uses for this distinction.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The only insurable event that is certain to occur is:\nA) Fire  B) Theft  C) Death  D) Flood\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Death is the exception: the event is certain, but the *timing* is uncertain, which makes it insurable.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: fire, theft and flood are uncertain events, not certain ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** \"Uberrimae Fidei\" refers to which fundamental principle of insurance?\nA) Insurable interest  B) Utmost good faith  C) Indemnity  D) Subrogation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Uberrimae Fidei is Latin for \"utmost good faith,\" requiring the insured to disclose all material information truthfully.\n- A) Wrong: insurable interest is a separate principle.\n- C) Wrong: indemnity is a concept about restoring loss, not a Latin disclosure principle.\n- D) Wrong: subrogation is about recovery from a third party.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Information asymmetry in insurance means:\nA) The insurer knows more than the insured  B) The insured knows more about himself than the insurer  C) Both know exactly the same  D) Neither party has information\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Since the insured is better informed about himself or herself than the insurer, there is an information asymmetry.\" This is why utmost good faith matters.\n- A) Wrong: it is the reverse.\n- C) Wrong: if they knew the same, there would be no asymmetry.\n- D) Wrong: the insured certainly has information about himself.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A typical screening process used by insurers before issuing a policy is a:\nA) Credit score check  B) Medical test  C) Background police check  D) Income tax audit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Medical test is a typical screening process,\" though it has limitations (it cannot reveal all ailments or family history).\n- A), C), D) Wrong: not cited as the typical screening process in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The test of insurable interest is that the insured should be:\nA) Worse off if the risk does not materialise  B) Better off if the risk does materialise  C) Better off if the risk does not materialise and worse off if it does  D) Unaffected either way\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"The insured should be better off if the risk does not materialise but will be adversely affected if the risk materialises.\"\n- A), B) Wrong: these reverse the logic.\n- D) Wrong: if unaffected, there is no insurable interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A lender has insurable interest in a borrower:\nA) For the borrower's full net worth  B) To the extent of the amount outstanding  C) Only if they are related by blood  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Lender has insurable interest in the borrower to the extent of the amount outstanding.\"\n- A) Wrong: only up to the outstanding amount, not full net worth.\n- C) Wrong: a blood relationship is not required for a lender.\n- D) Wrong: lenders clearly have insurable interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Indemnity is defined as:\nA) A fixed sum paid on a covered event  B) A duty to make good any loss, damage, or liability incurred by another  C) The right to recover from a third party  D) The proportion of claim the insured bears\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indemnity is \"a duty to make good any loss, damage, or liability incurred by another.\"\n- A) Wrong: that describes benefit insurance.\n- C) Wrong: that is subrogation.\n- D) Wrong: that is co-pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which of the following is an example of a defined benefit (not indemnity) policy?\nA) Health hospitalisation reimbursement  B) Car insurance  C) Life insurance policy  D) Property fire insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Life insurance pays a pre-fixed sum on death without ascertaining the exact actual loss, so it is a defined benefit policy.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these are indemnity policies because the actual loss is ascertainable from bills/repair costs.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A daily hospital cash benefit paying a specified sum per day of hospitalisation is classified as:\nA) Indemnity  B) Defined benefit  C) Subrogation  D) Contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The loss (attendant expenses, travel, lost wages) is small or hard to ascertain, so a fixed daily amount is a defined benefit.\n- A) Wrong: the main hospitalisation reimbursement is indemnity, but the daily cash portion is benefit.\n- C), D) Wrong: these are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** When the insurer \"steps into the shoes\" of the insured to recover from the party at fault, this is called:\nA) Contribution  B) Subrogation  C) Indemnity  D) Co-pay\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Subrogation means the insurer, after paying the claim, takes over the insured's right to pursue the at-fault party.\n- A) Wrong: contribution is about sharing among multiple insurers.\n- C) Wrong: indemnity is about making good the loss.\n- D) Wrong: co-pay is the insured's share of the claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A policy WITHOUT subrogation rights for the insurer would be:\nA) Cheaper for the insured  B) More expensive for the insured  C) The same price  D) Illegal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"A policy without Subrogation rights for the insurer would be more expensive for the insured person,\" because the insurer loses the chance to recover its losses.\n- A), C) Wrong: losing recovery rights raises cost.\n- D) Wrong: it is not illegal, merely costlier.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The concept of contribution applies only to which type of policy?\nA) Benefit policies  B) Indemnity policies  C) Life insurance  D) Annuities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Contribution applies only to indemnity policies because the loss amount is ascertainable and total claims cannot exceed the actual loss.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: benefit/life/annuity payouts are fixed sums, so contribution does not apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Co-pay is best defined as:\nA) The flat amount paid before a claim becomes admissible  B) The proportion of the claim amount met by the insured person  C) The premium discount for going electronic  D) A reinsurance arrangement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Co-Pay is the proportion of the claim amount that will be met by the insured person.\"\n- A) Wrong: that describes a deductible.\n- C) Wrong: that is the E-Insurance discount.\n- D) Wrong: reinsurance is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The relationship between co-pay and premium is:\nA) Higher co-pay → higher premium  B) Higher co-pay → lower premium  C) No relationship  D) Co-pay eliminates premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Higher the co-pay, lower will be the premium,\" because the insured bears more of the eventual claim.\n- A) Wrong: it is the inverse relationship.\n- C), D) Wrong: there is a clear inverse relationship; premium is not eliminated.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A deductible is:\nA) The percentage of the claim the insured bears  B) The portion of the claim paid by the insured after which the claim becomes admissible  C) A bonus for no claims  D) The insurer's profit margin\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Deductible is that portion of the claim that is paid by the insured person after which the claim becomes admissible.\"\n- A) Wrong: that is co-pay (a percentage).\n- C) Wrong: that is no-claim bonus.\n- D) Wrong: not a definition of deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In India, the compulsory deductible for cars with an engine capacity up to 1600 CC is:\nA) Rs. 500  B) Rs. 1,000  C) Rs. 2,000  D) Rs. 5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Car insurance in India has a compulsory deductible of Rs. 1,000 for cars with an engine capacity of upto 1600 CC.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: not the figure stated in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The two types of health insurance deductibles are:\nA) Fixed and floating  B) Compulsory and voluntary  C) Direct and indirect  D) Standard and premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Health insurance deductibles are of two types: compulsory deductible and voluntary deductible.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: not the classification used by the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Under the Insurance Act, 1938, the concept of \"Beneficial Nominee\" was introduced by an amendment in:\nA) February 2013  B) February 2015  C) September 2018  D) May 2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"The Insurance Act, 1938 was amended in February 2015 to introduce the concept of 'Beneficial Nominee'.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: not the amendment date for beneficial nominee.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The \"Beneficial Nominee\" provision is found in which section of the Insurance Act, 1938?\nA) Section 45  B) Section 39(7)  C) Section 64  D) Section 2(9)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 39(7) makes parent, spouse, or children beneficially entitled to the policy amount.\n- A) Wrong: Section 45 is the 3-year non-contestability rule.\n- C) Wrong: not cited.\n- D) Wrong: Section 2(9) defines an insurer/mono-line insurer.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under Section 45 of the Insurance Act, 1938, a life insurance policy cannot be called into question after how many years?\nA) 2 years  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 45 provides that no life insurance policy can be called into question 3 years from the date of the policy (or risk commencement/revival, whichever later).\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the period is 3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** During the first 3 years, a life insurance claim can still be denied or the policy cancelled on grounds of:\nA) Late premium  B) Fraud  C) Change of address  D) Job change\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Within the 3-year window, a claim can be repudiated or the policy cancelled \"on grounds of fraud.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these are not the stated ground for denial within the 3-year period.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The moratorium period applied in health insurance policies is:\nA) 3 continuous years  B) 5 continuous years  C) 8 continuous years  D) 10 continuous years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"A moratorium period, which is 5 continuous years, is applied in health insurance policies.\" After this, claims are not contestable except for proven fraud and permanent exclusions.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the moratorium is 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** According to the workbook, in personal finance, insurance comes:\nA) After investments  B) Before investments  C) Only after retirement  D) Only for the wealthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Insurance comes before Investments,\" illustrated by the cricket protective-gear analogy.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: protection must come first, regardless of wealth or age.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** ULIP benefit illustrations must show two scenarios of interest at:\nA) 2% and 6%  B) 4% and 8%  C) 5% and 10%  D) 6% and 12%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Insurers must provide benefit illustrations giving two scenarios of interest – at 4% and at 8%.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the prescribed rates are 4% and 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The lock-in period for ULIPs is:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 7 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"The lock-in period is 5 years to reflect the long-term, protection function of the policy.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: ULIP lock-in is 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A lapsed ULIP can be revived within what period from the date of lapsation?\nA) 1 year  B) 2 years  C) 3 years  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"A lapsed ULIP can be revived within a period of 3 years from the date of lapsation.\"\n- A), B), D) Wrong: the revival window is 3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** When a bank becomes the corporate agent of an insurance company, the arrangement is called:\nA) Reinsurance  B) Bancassurance  C) Web aggregation  D) Composite broking\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"When a bank becomes the corporate agent of an insurance company it is referred to as a bancassurance arrangement.\"\n- A) Wrong: reinsurance is insurers transferring risk to other firms.\n- C) Wrong: web aggregators run price-comparison websites.\n- D) Wrong: a composite broker is both direct and reinsurance broker.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A merchant insures the inventory in his own warehouse and also tries to insure his competitor's inventory. Which one is invalid and why?\nA) Both are valid  B) Only his own inventory is valid; he has no insurable interest in a competitor's inventory  C) Only the competitor's is valid  D) Neither is valid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"A business owner has an insurable interest on his inventory, but not in the inventory of a competitor.\" He suffers no monetary loss if the competitor's stock is destroyed.\n- A), C) Wrong: there is no insurable interest in a competitor's property.\n- D) Wrong: his own inventory clearly qualifies.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Ravi wants to insure the life of his cousin, with whom he has no financial dependence. Does insurable interest exist?\nA) Yes, all blood relatives have it automatically  B) No, distant relatives need proof of financial dependence  C) Yes, because they share a surname  D) Only if the cousin consents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For relationships such as aunts, uncles, cousins, nieces and nephews, \"the insurable interest would not exist unless there is a proof of financial dependence.\"\n- A) Wrong: only direct dependents and blood/marriage relationships have automatic insurable interest.\n- C) Wrong: a shared name is irrelevant.\n- D) Wrong: consent does not create insurable interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An insured hides a known heart condition while buying a policy. At claim time the insurer discovers this. The insurer can:\nA) Pay in full anyway  B) Reject the claim entirely for breach of utmost good faith  C) Only reduce the claim by 10%  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Suppressing material information breaches utmost good faith; \"the insurer has the right to reject the claim entirely.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: non-disclosure of material facts allows full rejection, not partial payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A health policy reimburses actual hospital bills, while a critical illness policy pays a fixed lump sum. Respectively, these are:\nA) Benefit and indemnity  B) Indemnity and defined benefit  C) Both indemnity  D) Both defined benefit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Health hospitalisation reimbursement is indemnity (bills show actual loss); critical illness lump sum is a defined benefit (loss of future income is hard to ascertain).\n- A) Wrong: it reverses the two.\n- C), D) Wrong: they are different types.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A car owner's vehicle is damaged by another driver who was clearly at fault. The owner's own insurer pays him, then pursues the at-fault driver. This best illustrates:\nA) Contribution  B) Co-pay  C) Subrogation  D) Deductible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The insurer pays the insured first, then steps into his shoes to recover from the at-fault party — the definition of subrogation.\n- A) Wrong: contribution is among multiple insurers covering the same insured.\n- B), D) Wrong: these are amounts the insured bears, not third-party recovery.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Mr. A has two health policies (Company X and Company Y) covering the same risk, and his claim is less than the sum insured of the policy he chooses. Per IRDAI rules:\nA) Both insurers must always split the claim  B) The chosen insurer pays in full and cannot force contribution  C) He must claim from the cheaper insurer  D) He cannot claim at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In health insurance, the insured chooses the insurer; the chosen insurer \"has no option to apply the contribution clause where the claim amount is less than the sum insured in the chosen policy.\"\n- A) Wrong: contribution applies only if the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured.\n- C) Wrong: the insured chooses freely, not by price.\n- D) Wrong: he can certainly claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A young single professional with no dependents is deciding what insurance to buy first. The workbook suggests he may need:\nA) A large life insurance policy  B) A personal accident disability or critical illness policy  C) An annuity  D) Only investment products\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With no dependents, income replacement on death matters less; a personal accident disability or critical illness policy (income if incapacitated) is more relevant.\n- A) Wrong: life insurance matters most for those WITH dependents.\n- C) Wrong: annuities are for retirement income, not a young person's first need.\n- D) Wrong: protection should come before investments.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which situation best illustrates an \"income protection\" need (protecting available income from an unexpected charge)?\nA) Death of a sole breadwinner  B) Permanent disability ending a career  C) A large hospital bill from sudden illness  D) Building a retirement corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Health and motor insurance protect income from a \"large and unplanned outflow\" — that is income protection.\n- A), B) Wrong: these are income replacement needs (life, disability).\n- D) Wrong: that is an investment goal, not protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Household insurance against theft or destruction of property addresses which insurance need category?\nA) Income replacement  B) Income protection  C) Asset protection  D) Investment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"Asset protection needs include the need to protect assets created from theft or destruction. Household insurance is one such product.\"\n- A), B) Wrong: those concern income, not the asset itself.\n- D) Wrong: it is a protection need, not investment.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An insurer charges a higher health premium to a person whose parents had heart disease, cancer and diabetes. This reflects that premium depends on:\nA) The insured's income  B) The probability of the event and expected loss  C) The agent's commission  D) The bank's interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"The premium payable will depend upon this expected loss and the probability of the event occurring.\" A higher chance of payout → higher premium.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these do not drive the risk-based premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** When evaluating an insurance-cum-investment product, the correct first step is to:\nA) Compare its investment return to mutual funds  B) Decouple the insurance and investment elements and check if the risk cover is adequate  C) Check the agent's track record  D) Buy it if it is convenient\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — First decouple the two elements and check whether risk coverage is adequate; if not, reject it. Only then judge the investment return separately.\n- A) Wrong: investment comparison comes only after cover adequacy is confirmed.\n- C), D) Wrong: convenience and track record are not the primary test.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A batsman always wears protective gear before batting. In the workbook, this analogy teaches that:\nA) Investments come before insurance  B) Insurance (protection) comes before investments  C) Cricket is risky  D) Premiums are wasteful\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The protective gear represents insurance, which must come before the \"bat\" (investments), because one hit could end the innings.\n- A) Wrong: it is the reverse.\n- C), D) Wrong: these miss the analogy's point.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A direct insurance broker primarily represents whom?\nA) The insurance company  B) The client  C) The regulator  D) The reinsurer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per Table 1.2, a Direct Insurance Broker represents \"the client\" and can advise on the products of all insurers.\n- A) Wrong: agents and corporate agents represent the insurance company.\n- C), D) Wrong: a broker does not represent the regulator or the reinsurer in this context.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which intermediary can charge a fee to the client AND earn commission from the insurance company?\nA) Insurance Agent  B) Corporate Agent  C) Direct Insurance Broker  D) Web aggregator only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Direct Broker can \"charge a fee to his clients or earn a commission from the Insurance companies.\"\n- A), B) Wrong: agents and corporate agents get commissions only.\n- D) Wrong: not how the workbook describes web aggregators.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A Corporate Agent (composite) can work with a maximum of how many insurers in each line?\nA) 1 life, 1 general, 1 health  B) 2 life, 2 general, 2 health  C) 3 life, 3 general, 3 health  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Corporate Agent (composite) \"can work with a maximum of three life insurers, three general Insurance companies and three health Insurance companies.\"\n- A) Wrong: that limit (one each) applies to a composite *insurance agent*.\n- B), D) Wrong: not the stated limits.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A travel agent offering you a travel insurance policy, or a vehicle dealer offering motor insurance, is acting as a:\nA) Direct broker  B) Reinsurance broker  C) Corporate agent  D) Surveyor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Corporate agents are entities engaged in a particular business who sell insurance to their customers (e.g., a travel agent selling travel insurance).\n- A), B) Wrong: brokers represent the client and arrange with multiple insurers.\n- D) Wrong: a surveyor assesses losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Reinsurance is best described as:\nA) Renewing a lapsed policy  B) Insurers transferring portions of their risk to other parties to spread the risk  C) Insuring the same risk twice with one company  D) Refunding premium to the insured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reinsurance is the practice whereby insurance companies transfer portions of their risk to other parties/reinsurance firms so no single party is hit by a large claim.\n- A) Wrong: that is revival.\n- C) Wrong: that would be contribution/double insurance.\n- D) Wrong: reinsurance is not a refund.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An IRDAI-registered insurance agent advises a client solely on insurance products. Under SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013, this advice is:\nA) Always covered by IA Regulations  B) Exempt from IA Regulations  C) Banned  D) Requires SEBI registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The IA Regulations exempt an IRDAI-registered insurance agent/broker who offers investment advice \"solely in insurance products.\"\n- A) Wrong: coverage applies only if they also advise on non-insurance securities/products.\n- C), D) Wrong: such advice is neither banned nor automatically SEBI-registrable.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** If an insurance agent also advises clients on non-insurance securities and investment products, then for that advice he must follow:\nA) Nothing extra  B) Risk profiling, suitability and fiduciary responsibility under IA Regulations  C) Only IRDAI rules  D) Only tax rules\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Such advice \"would be covered by IA regulations,\" requiring risk profiling, suitability and fiduciary responsibility to the client.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the IA Regulations now apply to the non-insurance advice.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Mr. Rich names his spouse as the life-insurance nominee and dies without a will. Under Section 39(7), the spouse:\nA) Holds the money only in trust for all legal heirs  B) Is beneficially entitled to the amount  C) Gets nothing until probate  D) Must split it equally with the children by law\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As a \"beneficial nominee\" (parent/spouse/children), the spouse is beneficially entitled to the claim amount without being accountable to other legal heirs.\n- A) Wrong: that is the general nominee-as-trustee rule, which the beneficial nominee provision overrides.\n- C) Wrong: no probate condition is stated.\n- D) Wrong: beneficial nominee status removes the mandatory split.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** For a bank fixed deposit (not life insurance), if Mrs. Rich is the nominee and Mr. Rich dies without a will, she:\nA) Keeps it all as beneficial owner  B) Holds it in trust for all the legal heirs  C) Forfeits it to the bank  D) Must give it to the government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under normal law, \"the Nominee holds any property in trust for the legal heirs.\" The beneficial-nominee exception applies only to life insurance, not bank FDs.\n- A) Wrong: that exception is specific to life insurance under Section 39(7).\n- C), D) Wrong: the money goes to the heirs via the trustee-nominee.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Why does the cashless claim payment process work more smoothly for corporate (employer) health policies than for individual policies?\nA) Corporates pay no premium  B) Corporate policies usually cover pre-existing diseases, removing the main source of dispute  C) Corporate policies have no exclusions at all  D) Employees never make claims\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most corporate policies allow pre-existing disease expenses to be reimbursed, and \"most disputes on claim payments for individual policies are due to this pre-existing disease clause.\"\n- A) Wrong: corporates pay large premiums.\n- C) Wrong: they still have some exclusions.\n- D) Wrong: employees do make claims; the process is just smoother.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Under IRDAI health insurance rules, a claim CANNOT be rejected if the policy has been continued without any break for:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 8 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"A health insurance company cannot reject a claim if policy is continued for 5 years without any break by the policyholder.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the unbroken-continuation period is 5 years (the moratorium).\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Under the standard individual health insurance product mandated by IRDAI:\nA) Many optional add-ons are allowed  B) No add-ons or optional covers are allowed  C) Wordings differ across insurers  D) Portability is blocked\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"No add-ons or optional covers are allowed to be offered along with the standard product.\" It has common wordings and facilitates seamless portability.\n- A) Wrong: add-ons are not allowed.\n- C) Wrong: wordings are uniform across the market.\n- D) Wrong: portability is facilitated, not blocked.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which health policies CANNOT be ported from one company to another?\nA) All individual policies  B) Group, top-up and super top-up policies  C) Only standard products  D) None — all can be ported\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Any health insurance policy (other than a group policy or a super top-up or a top-up policy) can be ported.\" So group/top-up/super top-up cannot.\n- A) Wrong: individual policies generally CAN be ported.\n- C) Wrong: standard products are designed for portability.\n- D) Wrong: the three named categories are excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A group health policy can be ported to an individual policy with ANY OTHER company from:\nA) The first year of leaving the company  B) The second year onwards after leaving  C) Never  D) Only after 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Group policies can port to individual policies within the same company in the first year of leaving, \"and to any other company from second year onwards.\"\n- A) Wrong: in year 1 only the same company is allowed.\n- C), D) Wrong: portability to other companies is allowed from year 2.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** All unit linked products, other than pension and annuity products, must carry:\nA) A guaranteed return  B) A mortality or health cover  C) Zero charges  D) A property cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"All unit linked products, other than pension and annuity products should have a mortality or health cover.\"\n- A) Wrong: ULIPs do not guarantee returns.\n- C) Wrong: charges exist but are capped.\n- D) Wrong: a property cover is not the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Under ULIP regulations, surrender charges are:\nA) Applicable forever  B) Not applicable after 5 policy years  C) Fixed at 10%  D) Charged only in year 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"No Surrender charges are applicable after 5 policy years.\" Partial withdrawals are also allowed after the 5-year lock-in.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: surrender charges end after 5 years, and no fixed 10% is stated.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A \"Composite Insurance Agent\" may act as agent for:\nA) Any number of life, general and health insurers  B) Not more than one life, one general, one health, and one of each mono-line insurer  C) Only one insurer in total  D) Only reinsurers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A composite insurance agent shall \"not act as insurance agent for more than one life insurer, one general insurer, one health insurer and one each of the mono-line insurers.\"\n- A) Wrong: there are clear limits.\n- C) Wrong: a composite agent can hold more than one (one per line).\n- D) Wrong: agents are not limited to reinsurers.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Cashless claim payment is most relevant for which type of insurance?\nA) Life insurance  B) Indemnity insurance (health and car)  C) Critical illness lump-sum  D) Annuities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"This is relevant for indemnity insurance and mostly used in the context of health insurance and car insurance,\" where the insurer pays the hospital/garage directly.\n- A), C) Wrong: life and critical illness pay fixed sums, not direct provider payments.\n- D) Wrong: annuities are income products, not cashless claims.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Choosing a higher voluntary deductible on a health policy will:\nA) Increase the premium  B) Lower the premium  C) Not change the premium  D) Cancel the policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"If the insured choses a higher deductible, then the premiums of the health policy will be lower,\" because the insured bears more of the loss.\n- A), C) Wrong: a higher deductible reduces the premium.\n- D) Wrong: it does not cancel the policy.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Mental illness, genetic disorders and congenital disease have been:\nA) Added to the exclusion list  B) Removed from the exclusion list  C) Made compulsory add-ons  D) Banned from cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Many illnesses, like mental illness, genetic disorders, congenital disease and others have been removed from exclusion list.\"\n- A), D) Wrong: they were removed from exclusions, i.e., now coverable.\n- C) Wrong: they are not made add-ons.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which best illustrates \"over-insurance\"?\nA) Buying cover well below the value of what you might lose  B) Buying far more cover than needed, incurring unnecessary higher premiums  C) Buying no cover  D) Buying exactly adequate cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Over-insurance imposes unnecessary costs in the form of higher premiums.\"\n- A) Wrong: that is under-insurance (retaining part of the risk).\n- C) Wrong: that is no insurance.\n- D) Wrong: that is correct, adequate insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** When is it advisable to review insurance coverage?\nA) Never, it is a one-time activity  B) On big life changes such as marriage, children, buying a house, or a large raise  C) Only when a claim is rejected  D) Only at policy expiry\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Reviewing insurance cover is advised whenever there is a big change in life... Marriage, children, buying a house and even a large raise.\"\n- A) Wrong: insurance is not a one-time activity.\n- C), D) Wrong: review is tied to life changes, not just claims or expiry.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which premium-reduction lever applies specifically to motor insurance per the workbook?\nA) Buying a single-premium ULIP  B) Sharing non-mandatory information that helps assess risk, choosing a higher deductible, and using No Claim Bonus  C) Increasing the sum insured  D) Removing the deductible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In motor insurance the insured can reduce premium by sharing non-mandatory information, choosing a higher deductible (sharing risk), and using the No Claim Bonus.\n- A) Wrong: a ULIP is unrelated to motor premium.\n- C) Wrong: more cover raises premium.\n- D) Wrong: removing the deductible raises premium.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** In the merchant ships example, if 2 ships are lost each month and each loss is Rs. 10,00,000, what total fund must be collected each month?\nA) Rs. 10,00,000  B) Rs. 20,00,000  C) Rs. 5,00,000  D) Rs. 40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: total loss = loss per ship × number of ships lost = Rs. 10,00,000 × 2 = Rs. 20,00,000. Terms: loss per ship = sum assured; ships lost = 2. The fund must equal the total expected loss so the unlucky merchants are fully paid.\n- A) Wrong: that is only one ship's loss.\n- C) Wrong: not derivable from the figures.\n- D) Wrong: that doubles the correct total.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** If only 40 of the 100 merchants agree to pay into the pool, what premium must each pay to cover the Rs. 20,00,000 loss?\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: premium per merchant = total loss ÷ number paying = Rs. 20,00,000 ÷ 40 = Rs. 50,000. Intuition: fewer people sharing the same total loss means each pays more — this shows why a \"large number of exposure units\" keeps premiums low.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 20,000 applies when all 100 join (20,00,000 ÷ 100).\n- B), D) Wrong: do not match 20,00,000 ÷ 40.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** In the King's employees example, each of 2 expected deaths loses Rs. 20,00,000 of income. The king funds this from 500 employees. What is the premium per employee?\nA) Rs. 4,000  B) Rs. 8,000  C) Rs. 16,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total fund = Rs. 20,00,000 × 2 = Rs. 40,00,000. Premium per employee = Rs. 40,00,000 ÷ 500 = Rs. 8,000. Terms: total loss spread over all 500 contributors.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: only 40,00,000 ÷ 500 = 8,000 is correct.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A co-pay of 15% applies to an ascertained claim of Rs. 2,00,000. How much does the insured pay and how much does the insurer pay?\nA) Insured Rs. 30,000; Insurer Rs. 1,70,000  B) Insured Rs. 1,70,000; Insurer Rs. 30,000  C) Insured Rs. 15,000; Insurer Rs. 1,85,000  D) Insured Rs. 20,000; Insurer Rs. 1,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula: insured's share = co-pay % × claim = 15% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 30,000; insurer pays = claim − share = 2,00,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 1,70,000.\n- B) Wrong: it reverses the two parties.\n- C), D) Wrong: use the wrong percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A car policy has a deductible of Rs. 1,000 and the ascertained claim is Rs. 25,000. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) Rs. 25,000  B) Rs. 1,000  C) Rs. 24,000  D) Rs. 26,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: insurer pays = claim − deductible = 25,000 − 1,000 = Rs. 24,000. The deductible is the flat amount the insured bears first.\n- A) Wrong: ignores the deductible.\n- B) Wrong: that is the deductible itself.\n- D) Wrong: the deductible is subtracted, not added.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A health policy has a compulsory deductible of Rs. 30,000. The hospital bill is Rs. 80,000. What does the insurer pay?\nA) Rs. 80,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 30,000  D) Rs. 1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: insurer pays = bill − deductible = 80,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 50,000; the insured bears the Rs. 30,000 deductible.\n- A) Wrong: ignores the deductible.\n- C) Wrong: that is the insured's portion.\n- D) Wrong: deductible is subtracted, not added.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Policy 1 has a deductible of Rs. 1,000 and co-pay of 10%. The claim is Rs. 40,000. What does Policy 1 pay? (Deductible first, then co-pay on the balance.)\nA) Rs. 36,000  B) Rs. 35,100  C) Rs. 39,000  D) Rs. 34,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Step 1: 40,000 − 1,000 (deductible) = 39,000. Step 2: co-pay = 10% × 39,000 = 3,900. Step 3: payout = 39,000 − 3,900 = Rs. 35,100. The order matters: deductible is removed first, then the percentage co-pay applies to what remains.\n- A) Wrong: that would apply only the deductible-equivalent.\n- C) Wrong: that stops after the deductible, ignoring co-pay.\n- D) Wrong: incorrect arithmetic.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Policy 2 has a deductible of Rs. 5,000 and no co-pay. The claim is Rs. 40,000. What does Policy 2 pay?\nA) Rs. 40,000  B) Rs. 35,000  C) Rs. 36,100  D) Rs. 35,100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: payout = claim − deductible = 40,000 − 5,000 = Rs. 35,000. With no co-pay, nothing further is subtracted.\n- A) Wrong: ignores the deductible.\n- C), D) Wrong: these apply a co-pay that Policy 2 does not have.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Using Q77 and Q78, on a Rs. 40,000 claim, which policy pays more and by how much?\nA) Policy 1 pays Rs. 100 more  B) Policy 2 pays Rs. 100 more  C) Both pay equally  D) Policy 1 pays Rs. 5,000 more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Policy 1 pays Rs. 35,100 and Policy 2 pays Rs. 35,000; difference = 35,100 − 35,000 = Rs. 100. Lesson: a small deductible with a co-pay can beat a larger deductible at this claim size.\n- B) Wrong: Policy 1 is higher, not Policy 2.\n- C) Wrong: they differ by Rs. 100.\n- D) Wrong: the deductible difference is Rs. 4,000, not the payout difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An insured took two flood policies of Rs. 5 lakh each (Companies A and B). Ascertained loss is Rs. 30,000. Under contribution, how much should Company B chip in if A pays?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 15,000  C) Rs. 5,000  D) Rs. 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: each insurer's share = loss × (its sum assured ÷ total sum assured) = 30,000 × (5 lakh ÷ 10 lakh) = 30,000 × 0.5 = Rs. 15,000. The sums assured are equal, so the loss is split 50/50.\n- A) Wrong: that is the whole loss, not B's proportionate share.\n- C), D) Wrong: do not match the 50% proportion.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Suppose two indemnity policies cover the same risk with sums assured of Rs. 6 lakh (A) and Rs. 2 lakh (B), total Rs. 8 lakh. The loss is Rs. 40,000. Under contribution, what is B's share?\nA) Rs. 10,000  B) Rs. 30,000  C) Rs. 20,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula: B's share = loss × (B's sum ÷ total sum) = 40,000 × (2 ÷ 8) = 40,000 × 0.25 = Rs. 10,000. Each insurer pays in proportion to its share of total cover.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 30,000 is A's share (the 6/8 portion).\n- C) Wrong: that would be a 50% split, but the shares are 6:2.\n- D) Wrong: total claim cannot exceed the Rs. 40,000 loss, and B pays only its proportion.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A claim of Rs. 1,00,000 is settled with a deductible of Rs. 10,000 followed by a 20% co-pay on the balance. What is the insurer's payout?\nA) Rs. 72,000  B) Rs. 80,000  C) Rs. 70,000  D) Rs. 90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Step 1: 1,00,000 − 10,000 = 90,000. Step 2: co-pay = 20% × 90,000 = 18,000. Step 3: payout = 90,000 − 18,000 = Rs. 72,000.\n- B) Wrong: applies only the deductible.\n- C) Wrong: applies 20% to the full 1,00,000, ignoring deductible order.\n- D) Wrong: stops after the deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A merchant wants his Rs. 20,000 premium returned if his ship comes back safe, assuming a 12% p.a. (1% per month) return. The total premium he must pay is Rs. 20,20,000. How is this split by the insurer?\nA) Rs. 20,000 to the pool and Rs. 20,00,000 invested  B) All Rs. 20,20,000 to the pool  C) Rs. 20,00,000 to the pool and Rs. 20,000 invested  D) Rs. 10,10,000 each\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The insurer allocates Rs. 20,000 to the insurance pool (the true cost of cover) and invests the balance Rs. 20,00,000.\n- B) Wrong: only Rs. 20,000 is the actual insurance cost.\n- C) Wrong: it reverses the allocation.\n- D) Wrong: not how the split works.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Continuing Q83, the Rs. 20,00,000 invested at 1% per month grows to what after one month (returned to the merchant)?\nA) Rs. 20,00,000  B) Rs. 20,20,000  C) Rs. 22,00,000  D) Rs. 20,02,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: future value = principal × (1 + rate) = 20,00,000 × (1 + 0.01) = 20,00,000 × 1.01 = Rs. 20,20,000. Interest earned = Rs. 20,000, which is returned along with the principal.\n- A) Wrong: ignores interest.\n- C) Wrong: that is 10%, not 1%.\n- D) Wrong: 0.1%, not 1%.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** The lesson from the merchant's \"return of premium\" math (premium Rs. 20,20,000 vs ship value Rs. 10,00,000) is that:\nA) Return-of-premium is always cheaper  B) Mixing investment with insurance can make the premium balloon to more than the asset's value  C) Insurance is free  D) Investments should always be bundled with insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The premium (Rs. 20,20,000) exceeds the ship's value (Rs. 10,00,000), showing the pitfall of combining investment and insurance without analysis.\n- A) Wrong: it is far costlier.\n- C) Wrong: insurance has a real cost (Rs. 20,000 here).\n- D) Wrong: the workbook warns against blind bundling.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A 30-year-old can buy a 30-year term plan (to age 60) for Rs. 13,800/year, or a 40-year plan (to age 70) for Rs. 16,200/year. What is the extra annual premium for the longer cover?\nA) Rs. 2,400  B) Rs. 3,000  C) Rs. 1,400  D) Rs. 16,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula: extra premium = longer-term premium − shorter-term premium = 16,200 − 13,800 = Rs. 2,400 per year. This buys the option to keep cover past retirement (60→70).\n- B), C) Wrong: incorrect subtraction.\n- D) Wrong: that is the full premium of the longer plan, not the difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Siddesh, a sole breadwinner with a dependent wife and kids, dies young. Which product would have best protected his family's finances?\nA) A critical illness policy  B) A personal accident disability policy  C) A life insurance policy of adequate sum  D) A motor insurance policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"If Siddesh had a life insurance policy for a sum adequate to meet his family's financial needs... he would have protected them from the financial effects of his death.\"\n- A) Wrong: critical illness covers illness-driven income loss, not death.\n- B) Wrong: disability cover addresses incapacity, not death.\n- D) Wrong: motor insurance covers vehicles, not income replacement.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Manohar, an architect, suffers a stroke and can no longer practise. Which product addresses his lost professional income?\nA) Life insurance  B) A critical illness policy  C) Household insurance  D) Car insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"A critical illness policy would have provided a corpus to Manohar to compensate for the lost income from his architectural practise.\"\n- A) Wrong: life insurance pays on death, not on surviving a critical illness.\n- C), D) Wrong: these cover assets/vehicles, not income from illness.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Arushi, a CAD professional, loses her eyes in an accident and cannot work. The most suitable cover is:\nA) A personal accident disability insurance  B) Household insurance  C) Life insurance  D) A daily hospital cash plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — \"A personal accident disability insurance would have provided a corpus that could generate the income that Arushi lost due to her accident.\"\n- B) Wrong: protects property, not income.\n- C) Wrong: pays on death, not disability.\n- D) Wrong: a small daily allowance cannot replace lost career income.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Jayant's house, full of wedding gifts and valuables, is burgled. Which need category and product apply?\nA) Income replacement; life insurance  B) Asset protection; household insurance  C) Income protection; health insurance  D) Investment; ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Theft of valuables is an asset-protection need; household insurance covers theft or destruction of assets.\n- A) Wrong: no loss of income from death here.\n- C) Wrong: health insurance covers medical charges, not theft.\n- D) Wrong: a ULIP is an investment product, not theft protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A large death claim is filed 18 months after a life policy was issued. What is the most likely insurer response?\nA) Automatic full payment, no questions  B) A full-fledged investigation, since it is within the first 3 years  C) Automatic rejection  D) Refund of premium only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Any large death claim during the first 3 years will invariably result in a full-fledged investigation.\" Within 3 years a claim can be repudiated on grounds of fraud.\n- A) Wrong: the 3-year non-contestability has not yet applied.\n- C) Wrong: it is investigated, not auto-rejected.\n- D) Wrong: not the stated outcome.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A health policyholder enhanced her basic sum insured in policy year 4. For the enhanced limit, the 5-year moratorium:\nA) Does not apply at all  B) Continues from the original policy date  C) Restarts from the date of enhancement, applicable to the enhanced limit  D) Is reduced to 1 year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"In case of any enhancement in the basic sum insured... then 5 continuous years will be applicable from the date of enhancement of sum insured for the enhanced limit.\"\n- A) Wrong: the moratorium does apply to the enhanced portion.\n- B) Wrong: only the original sum insured counts from the original date.\n- D) Wrong: it remains 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Mr. P has health cover with two insurers: Policy X (sum insured Rs. 3 lakh) and Policy Y (sum insured Rs. 5 lakh). His claim is Rs. 6 lakh. He first claims Rs. 3 lakh from X. What happens to the balance?\nA) He cannot claim the balance  B) He claims the remaining Rs. 3 lakh from Y; since the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured, contribution provisions apply  C) X must pay the full Rs. 6 lakh  D) Both must split equally regardless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured, \"the insured person can choose another policy(ies)... and the claim is settled by the chosen insurers by applying the Contribution provisions.\"\n- A) Wrong: the balance is claimable from the other policy.\n- C) Wrong: X's cover is only Rs. 3 lakh.\n- D) Wrong: a forced equal split is not the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A young person buys an investment-cum-insurance product offering attractive returns but with a tiny death cover. Following the workbook's evaluation method, the adviser should:\nA) Recommend it because the returns look good  B) Eliminate it, because inadequate risk coverage means it fails its primary purpose  C) Recommend it for convenience  D) Recommend doubling the investment portion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — After decoupling, \"if the risk coverage is not adequate, then the instrument does not meet its primary goal and should be eliminated.\"\n- A), C) Wrong: returns and convenience come second to adequate cover.\n- D) Wrong: adding investment does not fix inadequate protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An agent wants to sell an investment-cum-insurance policy without doing a suitability analysis. Per IRDAI intermediary rules, he can do so only if:\nA) The premium is small  B) He obtains the client's written consent to bypass the suitability module despite his recommendation  C) The client is over 60  D) Never under any circumstance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Agents can skip this requirement only if they get a written consent from policyholders saying that they have consciously chosen to bypass suitability module despite recommendation of their agents.\"\n- A), C) Wrong: no such exceptions are stated.\n- D) Wrong: it is allowed, but only with documented written consent.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A claimant's insurer was at fault in delaying a health claim settlement. Under IRDAI rules, the insurer must:\nA) Apologise only  B) Pay interest on the claim amount  C) Cancel the policy  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"If there is any delay in claim settlement, insurance company is now liable to pay interest on claim amount.\"\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the stated remedy is interest on the delayed amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An insurer wishes to reject a health insurance proposal. Under IRDAI norms, it must:\nA) Reject silently  B) Provide a fair, justifiable and transparent reason in writing  C) Wait 5 years  D) Offer a smaller policy automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"The insurance company must provide fair justifiable and transparent reason in writing for any rejection of health insurance coverage.\"\n- A) Wrong: silent rejection is not allowed.\n- C), D) Wrong: not requirements for rejection.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A ULIP policyholder makes an additional ad-hoc payment over the level premium. How is this treated?\nA) As a free top-up with no charges  B) As a single premium for the purpose of insurance cover charges  C) As a surrender  D) As a refundable deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Any additional payment shall be treated as single premium for the purpose of insurance cover charges,\" with charges spread evenly during lock-in (not front-ended).\n- A) Wrong: charges still apply.\n- C), D) Wrong: it is neither a surrender nor a refundable deposit.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** With effect from September 22, 2025, individual health insurance/reinsurance policies are:\nA) Subject to 18% GST  B) Exempted from GST  C) Subject to 12% GST  D) Banned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"All individual health insurance/ reinsurance policies are exempted from Goods and Services Tax (GST) with effect from September 22, 2025.\"\n- A), C) Wrong: they are exempt, not taxed.\n- D) Wrong: they are not banned.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Cashless claim payment, where the insurer pays the hospital directly instead of reimbursing the insured, has what effect on the premium?\nA) It raises the premium  B) It lowers the premium  C) It has no impact on the premium  D) It doubles the premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — \"As the claim amount to be paid remains the same, whether the amount is paid directly to the hospital/ garage or reimbursed to the insured person, there is no impact on the premium.\"\n- A), B), D) Wrong: the claim amount is unchanged, so the premium is unaffected.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario.** Anand, aged 30, is the sole earning member of his family (wife and one child) earning Rs. 12,00,000 a year. He approaches you, an investment adviser, for help. He currently holds:\n- **Health Policy X** with Insurer A: sum insured Rs. 4,00,000, with a 10% co-pay.\n- **Health Policy Y** with Insurer B: sum insured Rs. 6,00,000, with a compulsory deductible of Rs. 20,000 and no co-pay.\n- He is also considering a **ULIP** that an agent is pushing, and he wants to know whether he should take a 30-year or 40-year term plan. A 30-year term plan (to age 60) costs Rs. 14,000/year and a 40-year term plan (to age 70) costs Rs. 16,500/year.\n\nWork through the following sub-questions.\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS1. Anand is hospitalised with a bill of Rs. 3,00,000 and chooses to claim from Policy X (co-pay 10%). How much does Insurer A pay, and does contribution apply?</summary>\n\n**Worked answer:**\n- Formula for co-pay: insured's share = co-pay % × claim = 10% × 3,00,000 = Rs. 30,000.\n- Insurer A pays = claim − share = 3,00,000 − 30,000 = **Rs. 2,70,000**.\n- Does contribution apply? No. The claim (Rs. 3,00,000) is **less than** Policy X's sum insured (Rs. 4,00,000). Per IRDAI health rules, when the chosen policy's sum insured is enough, the chosen insurer **cannot force contribution** from the other insurer. Anand simply bears his Rs. 30,000 co-pay; Insurer B is not involved.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS2. A year later Anand has a major hospitalisation of Rs. 8,00,000. He claims Rs. 4,00,000 (full sum insured) from Policy X. How does he recover the balance, and what principle governs the settlement?</summary>\n\n**Worked answer:**\n- Policy X pays its full sum insured = Rs. 4,00,000 (ignoring co-pay detail here, the cover caps at Rs. 4,00,000).\n- Balance still needed = 8,00,000 − 4,00,000 = **Rs. 4,00,000**.\n- Because the claim **exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured**, Anand can bring in **Policy Y** and the claim is settled by applying the **Contribution** provisions. He claims the remaining Rs. 4,00,000 from Insurer B (Policy Y sum insured Rs. 6,00,000 comfortably covers it, after its Rs. 20,000 compulsory deductible — so Insurer B pays 4,00,000 − 20,000 = Rs. 3,80,000, and Anand bears the Rs. 20,000 deductible).\n- **Principle:** Contribution applies only to **indemnity** (health hospitalisation) policies because the loss is ascertainable and total claims cannot exceed the actual loss of Rs. 8,00,000.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS3. Anand failed to disclose a known diabetic condition when buying Policy X 18 months ago. At this large claim, the insurer investigates. What can happen, and what principle is involved?</summary>\n\n**Worked answer:**\n- The relevant principle is **Utmost Good Faith (Uberrimae Fidei)**: Anand was obliged to disclose all material information, and diabetes (a chronic condition affecting risk) is clearly material.\n- Because he suppressed it, he acted in **breach of good faith**, and the insurer \"has the right to **reject the claim entirely**.\"\n- Note: Section 45's 3-year non-contestability protects only **life** insurance after 3 years; here it is a health policy and the claim is within the **5-year moratorium**, during which claims can still be contested for **proven fraud**. Lesson: when in doubt, disclose — a slightly higher premium is far better than a rejected claim.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS4. Anand has dependents. The agent pushes a ULIP with a small death cover but attractive projected returns. As his adviser, how do you evaluate it, and what do you advise?</summary>\n\n**Worked answer:**\n- Method (from the workbook): **decouple** the insurance and investment elements first.\n- **Check risk-cover adequacy.** Anand earns Rs. 12,00,000/year and is the sole earner with a wife and child — he needs substantial income-replacement life cover. A ULIP with a *small* death cover fails this primary purpose.\n- Therefore the product **should be eliminated** on protection grounds, regardless of how good the returns look. Only after adequate cover is secured should investment returns be judged on their own merits and compared with other investment products.\n- **Advice:** Buy a pure **term life insurance** plan for adequate income replacement (protection first — the cricket analogy: gear before bat), and invest separately in suitable investment products. Also, the agent must do a **suitability analysis** before selling any investment-cum-insurance product, and can skip it only with Anand's **written consent**.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS5. Anand must choose between the 30-year and 40-year term plans. Compute the extra cost of the longer plan and advise whether the option is worth it.</summary>\n\n**Worked answer:**\n- Formula: extra annual premium = longer-term premium − shorter-term premium = 16,500 − 14,000 = **Rs. 2,500 per year**.\n- What the extra Rs. 2,500/year buys: the **option to keep life cover beyond his earning years** (from age 60 to age 70).\n- **Reasoning/advice:** An income-replacement policy is primarily needed during the **earning phase** (typically up to retirement at 60). Strictly, cover after retirement may be less essential because dependents are usually self-supporting and goals are met by then. However, paying a modest extra Rs. 2,500/year preserves flexibility to extend cover if circumstances change. Since Anand is the sole earner with a young child, locking in the longer option early — while premiums are low at age 30 — is reasonable, provided it does not strain affordability. The decision hinges on whether he values that flexibility at Rs. 2,500/year.\n</details>\n\n\n"}, {"num": "02", "title": "Life Insurance Products", "module": "Module 7: Risk Management & Insurance Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 2: Life Insurance Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **elements** of life insurance products\n- **Life Insurance Need Analysis** (Human Life Value & Needs-based approach)\n- The **types** of life insurance products\n- **Criteria** to evaluate various life insurance products\n- **Global coverage** for different life insurance products\n- Insurance under the **Married Women's Property Act (MWPA)**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Elements of Life Insurance Products\n\nLife insurance covers the **life of the insured**. The benefits come in two flavours:\n- **Death cover** — pays only if the insured **dies within** a specified period. No death = no payout.\n- **Survival benefit** — pays if the insured **survives** a specified period.\n\n> **Term insurance = only death benefit.** Investment-cum-insurance policies = a mix of both.\n\n### Key elements (with simple meanings)\n| Element | Meaning |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Insured** | The person whose life is covered (individual, minor, or joint lives). |\n| **Proposer / Policyholder** | The buyer, if different from the insured — must have **insurable interest**. |\n| **Term of contract** | Period during which cover is available. |\n| **Sum assured** | The amount insured. |\n| **Payment of sum assured** | Paid on death (death benefit) or term expiry (survival benefit); lump sum or instalments. |\n| **Premium payable** | Depends on sum assured + term; modes: monthly/quarterly/half-yearly/annual/single. |\n| **Bonus / Guaranteed bonus / Reversionary bonus** | Extra amounts added to sum assured (only in **investment-cum-insurance** policies). |\n\n### 💡 The minimum sum assured rule (IRDAI)\nFor life products with term **> 10 years**:\n- **Minimum sum assured = 10 × annual premium** if age **below 45**.\n- **Minimum sum assured = 7 × annual premium** if age **above 45**.\n\nFor term **< 10 years**: **minimum sum assured = 5 × annual premium** for everyone.\n\n> **Why it matters:** This ensures a policy is \"real\" insurance, not just a savings wrapper. (It also links to the tax rule that maturity is tax-free only if sum assured ≥ 10× annual premium.)\n\n### Bonus types (only in investment-cum-insurance policies)\n- **Bonus:** added to sum assured, declared periodically as a % of sum assured; paid at maturity or on the insured event.\n- **Guaranteed bonus:** paid for the first few years (say 5), as a % of sum assured; received at the end of term.\n- **Reversionary bonus:** based on insurer's performance, declared at the insurer's discretion, after the guaranteed-bonus period, for **participating** policies (these have higher premiums than **non-participating** policies).\n\n### Policy lapse, surrender value & paid-up\n- **Lapse:** if premium isn't paid even within the **grace period**, the policy lapses and no claim is payable.\n- **Surrender value:** a traditional investment-cum-insurance policy in force **≥ 3 years** (**2 years** if term < 10 years), with full premiums paid, acquires a cash/surrender value.\n  - Minimum surrender value = **30%** of all premiums paid, rising to **90%** in the last two years.\n  - **Single premium** policy: guaranteed surrender value ranges from **70%** of premium (if surrendered within first 3 years) to **90%** in the last two years.\n  - **ULIPs:** can be surrendered before 5 years despite the 5-year lock-in; a **discontinuation charge (max Rs. 6,000)** applies, then the balance moves to a discontinuation policy fund. The surrender value is paid only at the **end of the 5-year lock-in**.\n- **Paid-up:** instead of lapsing, the sum assured is **proportionately reduced**.\n\n#### 📖 Worked paid-up example (Surinder)\n- 25-year policy, sum assured Rs. 10,00,000, **half-yearly** premiums.\n- Full term = 25 years × 2 = **50 premiums** for the full Rs. 10,00,000.\n- Surinder paid for 5 years = **10 premiums**, then stopped.\n- Paid-up sum assured = 10,00,000 × (10 ÷ 50) = **Rs. 2,00,000**.\n\n> **Logic:** You \"earned\" the proportion of cover equal to the proportion of premiums you actually paid.\n\n### Nomination\nThe policyholder's right to name who receives the money on a death claim. Can be made at purchase or later, and **changed any number of times**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Life Insurance Needs Analysis\n\nHow much cover do you need? It depends on the economic value of a human life — the **Human Life Value (HLV)**.\n\n> **HLV** = the **present value of the expected income** over the working life that is available for the dependents.\n\nThere are two main methods.\n\n### 2.1 HLV (Income) Method\nOnly **two assumptions** are needed:\n1. By how much income grows each year (e.g., **6% inflation**).\n2. Post-tax return on the sum assured (e.g., **8%**).\n\nThe income is discounted to today's value using a special **adjusted (real) rate**.\n\n#### 💡 The key thumb rule\nIf income growth rate = post-tax return rate, the discounting rate becomes **0**:\n> Adjusted rate = (1 + 8%) / (1 + 8%) − 1 = **0**\n\nWhen the rate is 0, HLV = **current annual income × number of years to retirement** (no discounting needed).\n- Example: PMT = Rs. 10,00,000, Nper = 27 years, rate = 0 → HLV = **Rs. 2,70,00,000**.\n\nTwo consequences to remember:\n- **Younger people have higher HLV** (more years left AND faster income growth).\n- As a person **gets older**, HLV **falls** (the rate stays the same but Nper drops, so the present value falls).\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap (True/False question in workbook):** \"If income grows at 8% instead of 6%, HLV goes DOWN.\" → **FALSE.** A higher growth rate makes the discounting rate smaller, which makes HLV **higher** (or equal).\n\n### 2.2 Needs-Based Method\nAssumes insurance proceeds will cover the family's **expenditure needs and liabilities**. The corpus is built to generate the income the family needs, plus pay off loans, minus existing assets and existing insurance.\n\n#### The adjusted (real) discount rate formula\n```\nAdjusted rate = ((1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation)) − 1\n```\n- **Investment return** = rate the corpus earns (8%).\n- **Inflation** = rate the family's expenses grow (6%).\n- **Logic:** We discount future income by the *gap* between what the money earns and how fast costs rise. A corpus earning 8% while expenses rise 6% effectively grows ~1.89% in \"real\" terms.\n\nExample: ((1 + 8%) / (1 + 6%)) − 1 = **1.89%**.\n\n#### 📖 Worked example — Anil (full calculation)\n**Given:** Monthly income Rs. 1,50,000; insurance premium Rs. 25,000/month; EMI Rs. 32,000 (on a Rs. 40 lakh house loan); personal expenses Rs. 10,000. Wife is 49, expected to live to 80. Inflation 6%, return 8%. Existing cover Rs. 1 Cr; other investments Rs. 50 lakh; house worth Rs. 50 lakh.\n\n**Step 1 — Income to be replaced (what the family actually needs):**\n- Total income − (personal needs + EMI + insurance premium)\n- = 1,50,000 − (10,000 + 32,000 + 25,000) = 1,50,000 − 67,000 = **Rs. 83,000/month**\n- = **Rs. 9,96,000 per annum**\n\n**Step 2 — Adjusted rate:**\n- ((1 + 8%) / (1 + 6%)) − 1 = **1.89%**\n\n**Step 3 — Corpus needed (PV function):**\n- Rate = 1.89%, Nper = 80 − 49 = **31 years**, PMT = Rs. 9,96,000 (payments at the beginning of each year)\n- `PV(1.89%, 31, −996000, , 1)` = **Rs. 2,36,43,984**\n\n**Step 4 — Add liabilities:**\n- + Loan outstanding Rs. 40,00,000 → total required = **Rs. 2,76,43,984**\n\n**Step 5 — Deduct existing resources:**\n- − Existing cover Rs. 1,00,00,000 − Investments Rs. 50,00,000\n- **Additional insurance cover required = Rs. 1,26,43,984**\n\n> 💡 Note: the personal residence (Rs. 50 lakh) is **not deducted** — the family won't sell their home.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap (workbook T/F):** \"Needs-based requirement is lower when the insured has more investments.\" → **TRUE** (more existing assets reduce the gap).\n\n> **Important relationship:** The **HLV method always gives a higher or equal** figure than the needs-based approach, because overall expenditure cannot exceed income.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Types of Life Insurance Products\n\n### Term Insurance\n- **Pure risk cover** — pays only if the insured **dies during the term**. Survive = nothing paid.\n- **Lowest premium** (no investment component) → the most affordable, best way to cover full protection needs.\n- Three key factors: **sum assured, premium, length of coverage**.\n- Premium can be **level** (same every year) or rising with age. Payouts can be lump sum, fixed monthly income, or both. Some plans extend cover to age 85 or 100 (much higher premium).\n\n#### Term insurance with Return of Premium (worked example)\nThe premium is higher; the extra is invested to refund premiums at the end. The implied return is found with Excel's **RATE** function.\n- Sum assured Rs. 1,00,00,000; tenure 30 years.\n- Pure term premium = Rs. 9,416/yr; return-of-premium = Rs. 17,473/yr.\n- **Extra premium = 17,473 − 9,416 = Rs. 8,057/yr.**\n- Premium returned at maturity = 30 × 17,473 = **Rs. 5,24,190**.\n- RATE(Nper=30, PMT=8,057, PV=0, FV=−5,24,190, beginning of period) = **4.61% p.a.**\n\n> 💡 So the \"free\" refund actually earns just **4.61%** on the extra you paid — usually poor.\n\n### Endowment\n- **Investment-cum-insurance**, same premium each year.\n- On survival: investment portion + actual returns (declared yearly as **accrued bonus**, paid at end).\n- On death during term: sum assured + bonus accrued till death.\n- Use **RATE** (lump sum) or **XIRR** (staggered payouts) to find the implied return.\n\n#### 📖 Worked example\n- Sum insured Rs. 1 Cr, 30 years. Endowment premium Rs. 3,16,332/yr; pure term Rs. 9,416/yr.\n- Higher illustrated maturity value = Rs. 2,14,00,000.\n- Extra premium (PMT) = 3,16,332 − 9,416 = **Rs. 3,06,916/yr**.\n- RATE(30, 3,06,916, 0, −2,14,00,000, beginning) = **5% p.a.**\n\n> ⚠️ Endowment implied returns are normally just **3–5% p.a.**, and even that is **not guaranteed**. Since the protection is the priority and the Rs. 3,16,332 premium is far costlier than Rs. 9,416, an endowment can often be ruled out.\n\n### Whole Life Insurance\n- Investment-cum-insurance covering the **entire life** (or up to an upper age). Premium fixed throughout.\n- On death: full sum assured + bonuses.\n- Originally used to **pass wealth to the next generation** free of estate duty; with estate duty abolished, it's just another investment-cum-insurance product to be judged on cover adequacy + return.\n\n### Unit-Linked Insurance (ULIP)\n- Unlike whole-life/endowment, the **insured chooses the portfolio** (debt/equity mix) for the savings part.\n- Risk cover charges are deducted from premium; the balance is invested. **NAV announced daily.**\n- On death: sum assured, or NAV, or higher/total of both (depends on structure). On survival: NAV returned.\n- More **transparent** than endowment/whole-life; **no indicated inherent return** (depends on markets). Single-premium ULIPs available.\n\n#### 📖 Case study — Mr Jeevan (Term + MF vs ULIP)\nAge 35; cover needed Rs. 100 lakh; tenure 20–25 years.\n- **Option 1:** Term premium Rs. 1,100/month + invest Rs. 25,000/month in a **mutual fund**.\n- **Option 2:** Invest Rs. 26,100/month in a **Type II ULIP** (on death: sum assured + fund value; on maturity: fund value). Sum assured Rs. 100 lakh, term 25 years.\n\n| Feature | Term + Mutual Fund | ULIP (Type II) |\n|---------|--------------------|----------------|\n| Divisibility | Fully divisible (2 separate contracts); can pause investing | First 5 years indivisible; non-payment → discontinued policy account, cover stops |\n| Liquidity | No lock-in (small exit load if exiting in 12/18 months) | Investment locked 5 years |\n| Risk choice | Any risk profile via MFs | Limited options from insurer |\n| Switch between providers | Possible (exit load/cap-gains) | Not possible |\n| Switch within provider | Possible (load/tax) | Possible, no tax, usually a few free switches/year |\n| Transparency | High; sophisticated regulation, independent monitoring; max costs known | Evolving; costs debited by cancelling units (not in NAV) → harder to compare |\n| Tax on maturity | Capital gains tax applies | Exempt if sum assured ≥ 10× annual premium (here Rs. 1 Cr > 10 × Rs. 3,13,200) |\n\n> ⚠️ **Finance Act 2021:** ULIP maturity proceeds with **annual premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh** are taxed as **capital gains**, for policies taken **on/after 01.02.2021**.\n\n> 💡 **Bottom line:** Consider a ULIP instead of \"Term + MF\" **only if insurance cover is genuinely required**. If not, the ULIP's mortality charge is just an extra cost. Older, wealthy investors often don't need large cover and struggle to qualify for the 10× rule cheaply.\n\n### Mortgage (Reducing Term) Insurance\n- Sum assured **decreases over time** — designed to match a falling home-loan balance.\n- In theory cheaper than a full level-term plan (less over-insurance), but **in practice it's often NOT cheaper** — sometimes even more expensive.\n- Since most home loans are prepaid, premium for cover no longer needed is **wasted** → these plans are unpopular.\n\n### Riders (add-ons for extra premium)\n- **Double sum assured rider** — pays twice the sum insured on death within the term.\n- **Critical illness rider** — pays a fixed sum on diagnosis of a life-threatening illness + **30-day survival**. If it reduces the life cover by the paid amount, it's an **accelerated sum insured** (cheaper). Often a term policy with an *equal* critical-illness accelerated cover is **cheaper than a standalone critical-illness policy**.\n- **Accident death/disability rider** — periodic payout for temporary disability, or a sum on permanent disability from an accident.\n- **Waiver of premium rider** — triggers if disability/loss of income makes premiums hard to pay.\n- **Guaranteed insurability option rider** — enhance cover later **without a medical exam** (great for young earners).\n- **Income benefit rider** — nominee gets periodic income instead of a lump sum on death.\n\n> **Group insurance** is another cheaper option (e.g., all employees of a company) — lower premium for the same cover, simpler claims.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Facilities under Life Insurance Policies\n\n### Loan against policy\n- **Pure protection (term)** policies have **no surrender value** → no loan possible.\n- Most insurers lend against the **surrender value** of investment-cum-insurance policies; banks/NBFCs may too.\n- Insurer loan rates are usually **lower** than bank/NBFC rates. Use banks/NBFCs mainly to combine several policies into one larger, convenient loan.\n- Such a loan can also help **rebuild a damaged credit score**.\n\n### Nomination & change of nomination\nRight to name who receives the claim; can be changed any number of times. Under the 2015 amendment, if the nominee is spouse/parent/children, they inherit **beneficially** (not as trustees).\n\n### Policy assignment\n- **Assignment** = transferring the interests in the policy (usually to take a loan).\n- An assignment **cancels** an existing nomination — **except** when assigned to the insurer itself for a loan (then nomination is only affected to the extent of the insurer's interest).\n- Nomination is **reinstated** once the policy is re-assigned back.\n- Must be recognised by the insurer via **endorsement** after a written notice.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Insurance under the Married Women's Property Act (MWPA), 1874\n\nNormally, creditors of the insured could lay claim to insurance proceeds even from beneficial nominees. **MWPA solves this.**\n\nWhen a **married man** buys a policy under the **MWP Act, 1874**:\n- Nominees can only be the **spouse or children or both** (NOT parents).\n- The nominees acquire **all rights** to the claim; the **insured himself has no right** to the money.\n- Nominees **cannot be changed** after purchase. Even after divorce, the wife stays the nominee.\n- Any **survival benefit** is paid to the **nominees**, not the insured.\n- The MWP declaration must be made **only at the time of buying** the policy (works for term policies, online or offline).\n\n> 💡 Since the 2015 \"beneficial nominee\" amendment, MWPA has lost some relevance (and is rigid — can't change nominees). But it remains uniquely useful to **shield proceeds from the insured's creditors**.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Multiple Policies — Benefits, Limits, Provisions\n\n- Life insurance premiums are **telescopic**: premium does NOT rise in the same proportion as sum assured. A larger sum assured costs less *per thousand* → buying from the **same company** can be cheaper.\n- **Telescopic rates stop after a level** (say Rs. 2 Cr per insurer). For Rs. 8 Cr cover, it may be wise to buy **4 policies of Rs. 2 Cr each** — so you can drop some later as needs fall.\n- The proposal form must **list all existing policies and pending proposals**. Omission = withholding a material fact → grounds for **repudiation/cancellation within the first 3 years**.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Criteria to Evaluate Life Insurance Products\n\n### 7.1 Online vs Offline Term Plans — consider:\n- Trust in the **brand** lasting until the claim.\n- Whether the **full sum insured** is really available for the tenure.\n- Premium **after health/financial evaluation** (advertised premiums are for \"super healthy\" people).\n- Availability of **riders** (accelerated critical illness, permanent disability).\n- **Claim settlement ratio** (less important if full disclosure was made).\n\n### 7.2 Traditional vs ULIP\n- Traditional: insured **can't choose** investments; returns usually low. For policies issued **on/after April 1, 2023** with premium **> Rs. 5 lakh**, maturity proceeds are taxable as **\"Income from other sources\"**.\n- ULIP: insured **chooses** funds and **bears the investment risk** (can reduce, not eliminate, by picking low-risk funds).\n\n### 7.3 Investment-linked vs Pure Term\nDecouple insurance from investment. Check **cover adequacy first**; if inadequate, reject. Only then judge the investment return and compare with similar products.\n\n### 7.4 Online vs Offline plans\nOnline policies are often **cheaper** (some \"web only\"), with instant issuance. Brokers/web aggregators offer online comparisons and payment.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Global Coverage\n\n- **Defined benefit policies** (life, critical illness, accidental death) **pay wherever the risk occurs** worldwide — easy to verify the event happened.\n- Residents may use the **Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS)** — up to **USD 2,50,000 per annum** — to buy **foreign** insurance policies (denominated in foreign currency), e.g., to fund a child's overseas education.\n- ⚠️ These foreign policies and their grievance redressal are governed by the **foreign country's** regulations → use caution.\n- Evaluate any investment-cum-insurance foreign policy like any other: **protection needs first**, then compare the investment portion with similar LRS-available instruments.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- Life products give **death cover** and/or **survival benefit**. Term = death only; investment-cum-insurance = both.\n- **Minimum sum assured:** term > 10 yrs → **10×** premium (age < 45) / **7×** (age > 45); term < 10 yrs → **5×** for all.\n- **Surrender value:** traditional policy in force ≥ 3 yrs (2 yrs if term < 10), min **30%** of premiums → **90%** at the end; single premium **70% → 90%**; ULIP discontinuation charge **max Rs. 6,000**.\n- **Paid-up** sum assured = sum assured × (premiums paid ÷ total premiums due). Surinder: 10,00,000 × 10/50 = Rs. 2,00,000.\n- **HLV** = PV of future income for dependents. If income growth = return, rate = 0 and HLV = income × years to retirement. HLV is higher for the young, falls with age. **HLV ≥ needs-based** figure.\n- **Adjusted rate = ((1+return)/(1+inflation)) − 1** → 1.89% in Anil's case; additional cover needed = **Rs. 1,26,43,984**.\n- Products: **Term** (cheapest, pure cover), **Endowment** (~3–5% return), **Whole Life**, **ULIP** (choose funds, daily NAV, transparent), **Mortgage** (reducing, usually not cheaper).\n- **Return-of-premium** term implies ~4.61% return; **endowment** ~5%.\n- **ULIP tax:** maturity tax-free if sum assured ≥ 10× premium; but premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh (policies on/after 01.02.2021) → taxed as capital gains.\n- **MWPA, 1874:** nominees = spouse/children only, can't be changed, shields from creditors; insured has no rights.\n- Premiums are **telescopic**; list all existing policies on the proposal form.\n- Defined-benefit policies pay worldwide; LRS limit **USD 2,50,000/yr** for foreign policies.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Value / Meaning |\n|---------------|-----------------|\n| Min sum assured (term > 10 yr, age < 45) | 10 × annual premium |\n| Min sum assured (term > 10 yr, age > 45) | 7 × annual premium |\n| Min sum assured (term < 10 yr) | 5 × annual premium |\n| Surrender value (traditional) | 30% → 90% of premiums paid |\n| Surrender value (single premium) | 70% → 90% |\n| Surrender value eligibility | In force ≥ 3 yrs (2 yrs if term < 10) |\n| ULIP discontinuation charge | Max Rs. 6,000 |\n| Adjusted (real) rate formula | ((1+8%)/(1+6%)) − 1 = 1.89% |\n| Anil additional cover required | Rs. 1,26,43,984 |\n| Return-of-premium implied return | 4.61% p.a. |\n| Endowment implied return | ~5% p.a. (range 3–5%, not guaranteed) |\n| ULIP capital-gains tax threshold | Annual premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh (on/after 01.02.2021) |\n| Traditional policy tax (premium > Rs. 5 lakh) | \"Income from other sources\" (issued on/after 01.04.2023) |\n| MWP Act year | 1874 |\n| LRS limit | USD 2,50,000 per annum |\n| GST exemption (life) | from Sept 22, 2025 |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 2: Life Insurance Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What are the two basic benefits a life insurance product can provide?\n> **A:** **Death cover** (paid only if the insured dies within the period) and **survival benefit** (paid if the insured survives the period). Term = death only; investment-cum-insurance = both.\n\n**Q2.** Who is the \"proposer\" vs the \"insured\"?\n> **A:** The **insured** is the person whose life is covered. The **proposer/policyholder** is the buyer when different from the insured — and must have **insurable interest** in the insured.\n\n**Q3.** State the IRDAI minimum sum assured rule for term > 10 years.\n> **A:** **10 × annual premium** if age below 45; **7 × annual premium** if age above 45.\n\n**Q4.** What is the minimum sum assured if the policy term is LESS than 10 years?\n> **A:** **5 × annual premium** for all individuals, regardless of age.\n\n**Q5.** What is a reversionary bonus, and which policies get it?\n> **A:** A bonus declared at the insurer's discretion based on its performance, after the guaranteed-bonus period, for **participating** policies (which charge higher premiums than non-participating).\n\n**Q6.** When does a traditional investment-cum-insurance policy acquire a surrender value?\n> **A:** When in force at least **3 years** (or **2 years** if term < 10 years) with full premiums paid. Minimum surrender value = **30%** of premiums paid, rising to **90%** in the last two years.\n\n**Q7.** What is the surrender value range for a single-premium policy?\n> **A:** From **70%** of total premium (surrendered within first 3 years) to **90%** in the last two years.\n\n**Q8.** What happens if you surrender a ULIP before 5 years?\n> **A:** A **discontinuation charge (max Rs. 6,000)** is levied; the balance moves to a discontinuation policy fund. The surrender value is paid only at the **end of the 5-year lock-in**.\n\n**Q9.** What is a \"paid-up\" policy?\n> **A:** Instead of lapsing, the sum assured is **proportionately reduced** = sum assured × (premiums paid ÷ total premiums due).\n\n**Q10.** Surinder: 25-yr policy, sum assured Rs. 10,00,000, half-yearly premiums, paid 5 years then stopped. Paid-up value?\n> **A:** Total premiums = 25 × 2 = 50; paid = 10. Paid-up = 10,00,000 × (10/50) = **Rs. 2,00,000**.\n\n**Q11.** Define Human Life Value (HLV).\n> **A:** The **present value of expected income** over the working life that is available for the dependents — what insurance should compensate for if the earner dies.\n\n**Q12.** Why is HLV higher for younger people?\n> **A:** More **years left to retirement** (higher Nper) AND income tends to **grow faster** — both raise the present value.\n\n**Q13.** True or False: If income grows at 8% instead of 6%, HLV goes DOWN.\n> **A:** **FALSE.** Higher income growth lowers the discounting rate, so HLV goes **up** (or equal). If growth = return, rate = 0 and HLV = income × years to retirement.\n\n**Q14.** What is the adjusted (real) discount rate formula and its logic?\n> **A:** **((1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation)) − 1.** It captures the *gap* between what the corpus earns and how fast expenses rise. E.g., ((1.08)/(1.06)) − 1 = **1.89%**.\n\n**Q15.** In Anil's example, what monthly and annual income needed replacing?\n> **A:** 1,50,000 − (10,000 personal + 32,000 EMI + 25,000 premium) = **Rs. 83,000/month = Rs. 9,96,000/year**.\n\n**Q16.** In Anil's example, what was the corpus and the final additional cover required?\n> **A:** Corpus (PV at 1.89%, 31 yrs, Rs. 9,96,000) = Rs. 2,36,43,984; + Rs. 40 lakh loan = Rs. 2,76,43,984; − Rs. 1 Cr cover − Rs. 50 lakh investments = **Rs. 1,26,43,984**.\n\n**Q17.** Why isn't Anil's house value deducted in the needs calculation?\n> **A:** The family won't sell their **residential home** — personal assets like the house and jewellery are excluded.\n\n**Q18.** Which gives a higher number — HLV method or needs-based method?\n> **A:** **HLV is always higher or equal**, because overall expenditure cannot exceed income.\n\n**Q19.** Why is term insurance the cheapest form of life cover?\n> **A:** It is **pure risk cover** — it only covers death, with **no investment component**, so the premium buys protection and nothing else.\n\n**Q20.** Return-of-premium term: pure premium Rs. 9,416, RoP premium Rs. 17,473, 30 yrs, refund Rs. 5,24,190. What's the implied return?\n> **A:** Extra premium = Rs. 8,057/yr. RATE(30, 8,057, 0, −5,24,190, beginning) = **4.61% p.a.** — a poor return on the extra paid.\n\n**Q21.** What return does the workbook's endowment example imply, and what's typical?\n> **A:** **5% p.a.** in the example; endowment implied returns are normally **3–5% p.a.** and **not guaranteed**.\n\n**Q22.** How is an endowment's accrued bonus paid?\n> **A:** Declared yearly (not pre-defined at start) but **paid only at the end of the tenure** (or with sum assured on death).\n\n**Q23.** What was Whole Life insurance originally designed for?\n> **A:** To **pass wealth to the next generation free of estate duty** (death insurance proceeds were exempt). With estate duty abolished, it's judged like any investment-cum-insurance product.\n\n**Q24.** What distinguishes a ULIP from endowment/whole-life?\n> **A:** The **insured chooses the portfolio** (debt/equity mix) for the savings portion; NAV is announced **daily**; it's more transparent but has **no indicated inherent return**.\n\n**Q25.** When is a ULIP's maturity value tax-free?\n> **A:** When the sum assured is **at least 10× the annual premium**. (Jeevan's Rs. 1 Cr cover > 10 × Rs. 3,13,200 premium → exempt.)\n\n**Q26.** What did the Finance Act 2021 change for ULIPs?\n> **A:** ULIPs with **annual premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh** have maturity proceeds taxed as **capital gains** — for policies taken **on/after 01.02.2021**.\n\n**Q27.** In the Jeevan comparison, why is \"Term + Mutual Fund\" more transparent than a ULIP?\n> **A:** Mutual funds have sophisticated regulation, many independent monitors, known maximum costs, and units can't be cancelled to meet costs — allowing proper return comparison. ULIP costs are debited by cancelling units (not in NAV).\n\n**Q28.** What is mortgage (reducing term) insurance, and why is it unpopular?\n> **A:** Sum assured falls with the home loan balance. In practice it's often **not cheaper** (sometimes more expensive) than level term, and since loans are usually prepaid, premium for unused cover is **wasted**.\n\n**Q29.** What is an \"accelerated sum insured\" critical illness rider?\n> **A:** On a critical-illness claim, the life cover is **reduced** by the amount paid out. It's cheaper than an independent rider, and often cheaper than a standalone critical-illness policy.\n\n**Q30.** What does a Guaranteed Insurability Option rider do?\n> **A:** Lets the insured **increase cover later without a fresh medical exam** — useful for young earners expecting higher income but possible future health issues.\n\n**Q31.** Why can't you take a loan against a pure term policy?\n> **A:** Pure protection policies acquire **no surrender value**, so there's nothing to lend against. Loans are available only against investment-cum-insurance policies.\n\n**Q32.** What happens to a nomination when a policy is assigned?\n> **A:** Assignment **cancels** the nomination — except when assigned to the insurer for its own loan (then only affected to the extent of the insurer's interest). Nomination is reinstated when re-assigned back.\n\n**Q33.** Under the MWP Act, 1874, who can be the nominees and can they be changed?\n> **A:** Only the **spouse or children or both** (NOT parents). Nominees **cannot be changed** after purchase — even after divorce, the wife stays the nominee.\n\n**Q34.** What is the main remaining benefit of an MWP Act policy?\n> **A:** It **shields the policy proceeds from the insured's creditors** — they go to the nominees regardless of claims on the insured's assets.\n\n**Q35.** What does \"telescopic premium\" mean?\n> **A:** Premiums do **not rise in the same proportion** as the sum assured — a larger cover costs less per thousand, so buying from one company is often cheaper.\n\n**Q36.** Why might someone buy 4 policies of Rs. 2 Cr instead of one Rs. 8 Cr policy?\n> **A:** Telescopic rates stop after a level (~Rs. 2 Cr per insurer). Splitting lets the insured **drop individual policies later** as insurance needs fall.\n\n**Q37.** How are traditional-policy maturity proceeds taxed if premium > Rs. 5 lakh?\n> **A:** For policies issued **on/after April 1, 2023** with premium > Rs. 5 lakh, maturity proceeds are taxable as **\"Income from other sources.\"**\n\n**Q38.** Do defined-benefit life policies pay if death occurs outside India?\n> **A:** **Yes** — life, critical illness and accidental death policies pay wherever the risk occurs, because the event is easy to verify anywhere.\n\n**Q39.** How can a resident Indian buy a foreign insurance policy, and what's the limit?\n> **A:** Using the **Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS)** — up to **USD 2,50,000 per annum**. But foreign rules govern the policy and grievance redressal, so caution is needed.\n\n**Q40.** What's the right way to evaluate any investment-cum-insurance product?\n> **A:** **Decouple** insurance from investment; check **cover adequacy first** (reject if inadequate); only then assess the investment return and compare with similar products.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 2: Life Insurance Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam has **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark — skip only if you have no clue, and reason carefully.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A life insurance benefit that is paid only on the death of the insured within a specified period is called:\nA) Survival benefit  B) Death cover  C) Maturity benefit  D) Reversionary bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Death cover pays only if the insured dies within the specified period; if death does not occur, no benefit is paid.\nA) Survival benefit is the opposite — it pays when the insured *survives* a specified period. C) Maturity benefit is just a form of survival benefit, not the defined term here. D) Reversionary bonus is an add-on to the sum assured in participating policies, not a death-only cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Term insurance policies provide for:\nA) Only survival benefit  B) Both death and survival benefit  C) Only a guaranteed bonus  D) Only death benefit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Term insurance is pure risk cover; it pays only if the insured dies during the term, i.e., only death benefit.\nA) and B) describe investment-cum-insurance policies which combine death and survival features. C) Bonuses are relevant only to investment-cum-insurance policies, not pure term plans.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The person whose life is being insured is called the:\nA) Proposer  B) Nominee  C) Insured  D) Assignee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The \"insured\" is the person whose life is covered, and can be an individual, minor or joint lives.\nA) The proposer/policyholder is the buyer when different from the insured. B) The nominee receives the money on a death claim. D) An assignee is someone to whom the policy interest is transferred.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** If the person buying the policy is different from the person whose life is insured, the buyer must have:\nA) A medical certificate  B) An insurable interest in the person covered  C) A PAN card  D) A bank guarantee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Such a buyer (proposer/policyholder) must have an insurable interest in the individual being covered.\nA), C) and D) are not the defining requirement for a proposer who is different from the insured; insurable interest is the key legal requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** \"Sum assured\" in a life insurance contract refers to:\nA) The amount being insured  B) The premium paid each year  C) The bonus added at maturity  D) The surrender value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The sum assured is the amount being insured.\nB) Premium is the cost paid for the cover. C) Bonus is an extra amount added to the sum assured in investment-cum-insurance policies. D) Surrender value is the cash value returned if a policy is given up.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** For a life insurance product with a term of MORE than 10 years, the minimum sum assured for an individual BELOW 45 years of age must be at least:\nA) 5 times the annual premium  B) 7 times the annual premium  C) 10 times the annual premium  D) 15 times the annual premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — IRDAI requires a minimum sum assured of 10 times the annual premium for individuals below 45, for terms over 10 years.\nA) 5 times applies only when the term is less than 10 years. B) 7 times applies for individuals above 45 (term over 10 years). D) 15 times is not a defined limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** For a life insurance product with a term of more than 10 years, the minimum sum assured for an individual ABOVE 45 years of age is:\nA) 5 times annual premium  B) 7 times annual premium  C) 10 times annual premium  D) 12 times annual premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For terms over 10 years and age above 45, the minimum sum assured is 7 times the annual premium.\nA) 5 times applies to terms below 10 years. C) 10 times is for those below 45. D) 12 times is not a defined figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If a life insurance policy has a term of LESS than 10 years, the minimum sum assured for all individuals is:\nA) 5 times the annual premium  B) 7 times the annual premium  C) 10 times the annual premium  D) 3 times the annual premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For terms less than 10 years, the minimum sum assured is 5 times the annual premium for all individuals regardless of age.\nB) 7 times and C) 10 times apply to terms over 10 years (depending on age). D) 3 times is not a defined limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Non-payment of premium due on a policy within the grace period allowed makes the policy:\nA) Mature  B) Lapse  C) Paid-up automatically  D) Assigned to the insurer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If premium is not paid within the grace period, the policy lapses and no claim is payable on a lapsed policy.\nA) Maturity happens at the end of the term, not from non-payment. C) Paid-up is an *option* in investment-cum-insurance policies, not automatic. D) Assignment is a deliberate transfer of policy interests, unrelated to non-payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** With effect from September 22, 2025, all life insurance/reinsurance policies are:\nA) Subject to 18% GST  B) Taxed at 5% GST  C) Subject to TDS  D) Exempted from GST\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — All life insurance/reinsurance policies are exempted from Goods and Services Tax (GST) with effect from September 22, 2025.\nA) and B) are incorrect GST rates; the policies are exempt, not taxed. C) TDS is a different concept (tax deducted at source) and not what changed here.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A bonus in a life insurance policy is relevant only to:\nA) Pure term policies  B) Mortgage insurance  C) Investment-cum-insurance policies  D) Group insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Bonus is an amount added to the sum assured and is relevant only to investment-cum-insurance policies.\nA) Pure term policies have no investment component and pay no bonus. B) Mortgage insurance is a reducing-cover term variant with no bonus. D) Group insurance is a pricing arrangement, not a bonus-paying structure.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A guaranteed bonus in an investment-cum-insurance policy is typically:\nA) Paid for the first few years of the policy (say five) as a percentage of sum assured  B) Paid only on death  C) Decided by market NAV daily  D) Equal to the surrender value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Guaranteed bonus is paid for the first few years (say five years) as a percentage of the sum assured and is generally received at the end of the term.\nB) It is part of guaranteed benefits, not tied solely to death. C) Daily NAV applies to ULIPs, not guaranteed bonus. D) It is unrelated to surrender value.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A reversionary bonus is declared:\nA) At a fixed rate every year guaranteed  B) Only for pure term policies  C) Only for single-premium ULIPs  D) At the discretion of the insurer, based on its performance, for participating policies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Reversionary bonus is based on the insurer's performance, declared at its discretion, after the guaranteed-bonus period, for participating policies.\nA) It is not guaranteed — that is the guaranteed bonus. B) Term policies pay no bonus. C) ULIPs use NAV, not reversionary bonus.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The premium for participating policies, compared to non-participating policies, is:\nA) Lower  B) The same  C) Higher  D) Always zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Participating policies (which receive reversionary bonus) carry a higher premium than non-participating policies.\nA) and B) are wrong because the right to share in surplus comes at extra cost. D) Premiums are never zero for cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** For a traditional investment-cum-insurance policy with a term of 10 years or more, surrender value is acquired only after the policy has been in force for at least:\nA) 1 year  B) 3 years  C) 2 years  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A traditional investment-cum-insurance policy in force for at least 3 years (with full premiums paid) may acquire a surrender value.\nA) and C) 1 or 2 years is too short (2 years applies only if the term is less than 10 years). D) 5 years is the ULIP lock-in, a different concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** For a traditional investment-cum-insurance policy whose term is LESS than 10 years, surrender value is acquired after the policy has been in force for at least:\nA) 2 years  B) 1 year  C) 3 years  D) 4 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — If the term is less than 10 years, surrender value can be acquired after the policy has been in force for at least 2 years (with full premiums paid).\nB) 1 year is too short. C) 3 years applies to longer-term policies. D) 4 years is not a defined threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The minimum surrender value of a traditional policy will be at least __ of all premiums paid, going up to __ in the last two years.\nA) 30%; 90%  B) 50%; 80%  C) 70%; 90%  D) 30%; 70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The minimum surrender value is 30% of all premiums paid and goes up to 90% in the last two years.\nC) 70% to 90% is the range for single-premium policies, not regular-premium traditional ones. B) and D) use figures not specified in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** For a SINGLE premium policy, the guaranteed surrender value ranges from:\nA) 30% to 90%  B) 50% to 100%  C) 90% to 100%  D) 70% (within first three years) to 90% (last two years)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For a single premium policy, the guaranteed surrender value ranges from 70% of total premium paid if surrendered within the first three years to 90% in the last two years.\nA) 30% to 90% is for regular-premium traditional policies. B) and C) are not specified ranges.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** For ULIPs, the discontinuation charge levied when surrendering before 5 years is a maximum of:\nA) Rs. 3,000  B) Rs. 5,000  C) Rs. 6,000  D) Rs. 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The discontinuation charge for ULIPs is a maximum of Rs. 6,000; after deducting it, the balance fund moves to a discontinuation policy fund.\nA), B) and D) are not the specified maximum charge.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** In a Unit-Linked Insurance Plan (ULIP), the savings/investment portion has a lock-in of:\nA) 3 years  B) 7 years  C) 5 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — ULIPs have a lock-in of 5 years; the surrender value is paid only at the end of the lock-in period.\nA), B) and D) are not the ULIP lock-in period.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Making a policy \"paid-up\" means:\nA) The full sum assured continues unchanged  B) The policy is cancelled with no value  C) The premium is waived for life  D) The sum assured is proportionately reduced to the ratio of premiums paid to total premiums due\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A paid-up policy has its sum assured proportionately reduced in the same proportion that premiums paid bear to total premiums due.\nA) The sum assured is reduced, not unchanged. B) A paid-up policy retains a reduced value. C) Waiver of premium is a separate rider concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Nomination in a life insurance policy:\nA) Can be made only at the start and never changed  B) Can be made at the start or later, and changed any number of times  C) Can be changed only once  D) Is mandatory only for term plans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Nomination can be made at the time of taking the policy or later, and can be changed any number of times.\nA) and C) wrongly restrict changes. D) Nomination is a general right, not limited to term plans.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The economic value attached to a human life, used to size insurance cover, is called the:\nA) Net Asset Value  B) Surrender Value  C) Human Life Value (HLV)  D) Sum Assured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Human Life Value (HLV) is the present value of expected income over the working life available for dependents.\nA) NAV is the per-unit value of a ULIP fund. B) Surrender value is the cash returned on giving up a policy. D) Sum assured is the insured amount, an output not the valuation method.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** HLV is defined as the present value of:\nA) All future premiums  B) The surrender value over time  C) The bonus declared each year  D) The expected income over the working life available for dependents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — HLV is the present value of the expected income over the working life of the individual that is available for the dependents.\nA), B) and C) are not the definition of HLV; HLV is about income replacement value.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The two basic assumptions in the simple HLV calculation are:\nA) Increase in current income (inflation) and post-tax return on the sum assured  B) Mortality rate and bonus rate  C) Premium and term  D) Sum assured and surrender value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — HLV needs only two assumptions: how much current income increases (inflation, e.g., 6%) and the post-tax return on the sum assured (e.g., 8%).\nB), C) and D) are not the two assumptions used in the HLV calculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Term insurance is best described as:\nA) An investment product with guaranteed returns  B) A pure risk cover product paying a benefit only if the policyholder dies during the term  C) A money-back policy  D) A policy that pays on survival only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Term insurance is a pure risk cover; it pays only if the policyholder dies during the insured period.\nA) It has no investment component. C) Money-back is an endowment variant. D) It pays nothing on survival.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The three key factors to consider in term insurance are:\nA) Bonus, NAV and surrender value  B) Sum assured, premium and length of coverage  C) Inflation, return and tax  D) Nominee, assignee and proposer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The three key factors are sum assured (death benefit), premium (cost), and length of coverage (term).\nA) These relate to investment-cum-insurance products. C) These relate to need-analysis assumptions. D) These are parties to a contract, not term-insurance factors.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** In an endowment policy, if the insured survives the tenure, the insurer pays:\nA) Nothing  B) Only the premiums paid  C) A lump sum equal to the sum assured plus any accrued bonus  D) Twice the sum assured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — On survival, an endowment pays a lump sum equal to the sum assured plus any accrued bonus.\nA) Nothing is paid in a term plan on survival, not endowment. B) Return of premiums describes a different variant. D) Double sum assured is a rider feature.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** In endowment policies, the actual return calculated and declared every year but paid only at the end of the tenure is called:\nA) Guaranteed bonus  B) Reversionary surrender  C) Loyalty addition  D) Accrued bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Accrued bonus is calculated/declared yearly but paid only at the end of the tenure (or on death, up to date of death).\nA) Guaranteed bonus is paid in the first few years as a fixed percentage. B) and C) are not the term used in the workbook for this.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Whole life insurance policies provide cover for:\nA) A fixed 10-year term only  B) Only the earning years  C) Only until age 45  D) The entire life of the insured (or up to an upper age limit), provided premiums are paid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Whole life policies cover the entire life of the insured or up to an upper age limit specified by the insurer, whichever is earlier, provided premiums are paid as contracted.\nA), B) and C) wrongly restrict the coverage period.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In a unit-linked insurance policy (ULIP), who decides the kind of portfolio (debt/equity mix) for the savings portion?\nA) The insurer alone  B) IRDAI  C) The insured  D) The nominee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — ULIPs allow the insured to decide the kind of portfolio (mix of asset classes) the insurer should maintain for the savings portion.\nA) In whole-life and endowment policies, the insured has no say — but ULIPs are different. B) IRDAI is the regulator, not the fund chooser. D) The nominee only receives claims.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a ULIP, the insurer announces the Net Asset Value (NAV):\nA) Once a year  B) Only at maturity  C) On a daily basis  D) Every five years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The insurer announces a Net Asset Value (NAV) on a daily basis for ULIPs.\nA), B) and D) are incorrect; ULIP NAV is daily, making the value track the market.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Mortgage (reducing term) insurance is characterised by:\nA) A sum assured that increases over time  B) A fixed sum assured forever  C) A guaranteed maturity bonus  D) A sum assured that keeps going down with time\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In mortgage insurance the sum assured keeps going down with time, matching a reducing housing loan balance.\nA) The opposite is true. B) The sum assured is not fixed. C) It is a term variant with no maturity bonus.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A critical illness rider typically pays a pre-specified sum on diagnosis of a life-threatening illness AND:\nA) Immediate payout with no conditions  B) Survival for 1 year after diagnosis  C) Survival for 30 days after such diagnosis  D) Death within 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A critical illness rider pays on diagnosis of a life-threatening illness and survival for 30 days after such diagnosis.\nA) There is a survival condition. B) 1 year is incorrect. D) The rider requires survival, not death, after diagnosis.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Under the Married Women's Property Act (MWPA), 1874, the nominees of the policy can only be:\nA) Parents only  B) Any relative chosen by the insured  C) Spouse or children or both (but not parents)  D) Creditors of the insured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the MWP Act, nominees can only be the spouse or children or both, but not parents.\nA) Parents are explicitly excluded. B) The choice is restricted, not open. D) The whole point of MWPA is to keep proceeds away from creditors.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Rahul, aged 40, buys a 20-year policy with an annual premium of Rs. 50,000. What is the minimum sum assured the policy must offer?\nA) Rs. 2,50,000  B) Rs. 3,50,000  C) Rs. 5,00,000  D) Rs. 7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rule: for terms over 10 years and age below 45, minimum sum assured = 10 × annual premium. Here = 10 × 50,000 = Rs. 5,00,000.\nA) Rs. 2,50,000 = 5× (wrong, that is for terms under 10 years). B) Rs. 3,50,000 = 7× (that is for age above 45). D) Rs. 7,50,000 = 15×, not a defined multiple.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Meena, aged 50, buys a 15-year policy with an annual premium of Rs. 40,000. The minimum sum assured must be:\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 2,80,000  C) Rs. 4,00,000  D) Rs. 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rule: term over 10 years, age above 45 → minimum sum assured = 7 × annual premium = 7 × 40,000 = Rs. 2,80,000.\nA) Rs. 2,00,000 = 5× (only for terms under 10 years). C) Rs. 4,00,000 = 10× (only for age below 45). D) Rs. 6,00,000 = 15×, not a defined multiple.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A 7-year policy is bought by a 30-year-old with an annual premium of Rs. 60,000. The minimum sum assured is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 4,20,000  C) Rs. 6,00,000  D) Rs. 9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The term is less than 10 years, so the multiple is 5× for everyone regardless of age. = 5 × 60,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nB) 7× applies only to terms over 10 years for older individuals. C) 10× applies to terms over 10 years for the young. D) 15× is not defined.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client wants the cheapest possible way to cover his full protection needs, with no investment component. Which product should an adviser recommend?\nA) Endowment policy  B) Whole life policy  C) Term insurance  D) ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Term insurance offers the most affordable form of life insurance because it covers only the risk of death with no investment component, so the entire protection need can be covered cheaply.\nA), B) and D) all bundle an investment component, raising premiums substantially for the same cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A client says: \"I want a policy where I can choose between equity and debt funds, see a daily NAV, and bear the investment risk myself.\" This best matches:\nA) Endowment  B) Whole life  C) ULIP  D) Mortgage insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only ULIPs let the insured choose the asset-class mix, announce a daily NAV, and place the investment risk on the insured.\nA) and B) leave investment decisions to the insurer. D) Mortgage insurance is a reducing-term protection product with no fund choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Comparing the same Rs. 1 crore cover for 30 years from the same company: a pure term plan costs Rs. 9,416/year while an endowment costs Rs. 3,16,332/year. The big takeaway an adviser should highlight is:\nA) Endowment is always better value  B) Term plans never pay out  C) Endowment guarantees high returns  D) The protection cost is tiny; most of the endowment premium is investment, which earns only ~3–5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The pure protection cost is just Rs. 9,416; the rest of the endowment premium is investment that typically yields only about 3–5% and is not guaranteed.\nA) and C) overstate endowment value; returns are low and not guaranteed. B) Term plans do pay if death occurs in the term.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A 25-year-old and a 55-year-old have identical incomes and assumptions. Whose Human Life Value is higher and why?\nA) The 55-year-old, because of higher savings  B) Both equal  C) Cannot be determined  D) The 25-year-old, because more working years remain (and income tends to grow faster)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — HLV is higher for younger people because more years remain to retirement (higher Nper) and income tends to grow faster — both raise the present value of future income.\nA) Higher savings reduces *needs-based* insurance, not HLV. B) and C) are wrong; age clearly drives HLV.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Under the HLV method, if the assumed rate of income growth EQUALS the post-tax return on investment, the HLV equals:\nA) Current annual income × number of years left to retirement  B) Zero  C) The sum of all premiums  D) Twice the current income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When growth = return, the adjusted discounting rate becomes 0, so HLV = current annual income × years left to retirement (no discounting). Example in workbook: Rs. 10,00,000 × 27 = Rs. 2,70,00,000.\nB) HLV is not zero; the rate is zero. C) and D) are unrelated to the thumb rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** \"If income grows at 8% instead of 6% (return still 8%), the HLV goes DOWN.\" This statement is:\nA) True, because higher inflation reduces real income  B) True, because the corpus shrinks  C) Cannot be determined  D) False, because a higher growth rate lowers the discounting rate, raising HLV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A higher income-growth rate makes the discounting rate smaller (here it becomes 0), which makes HLV higher (or equal), not lower. The workbook explicitly calls the original statement False.\nA), B) and C) misread the effect of growth on present value.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The needs-based insurance requirement will be LOWER when the insured has:\nA) A smaller existing investment portfolio  B) More children  C) A bigger outstanding loan  D) A larger amount of existing investments\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Existing investments are deducted from the total funds required, so more investments mean a lower additional insurance need (the workbook confirms this is True).\nA) Smaller investments would raise the need. B) More dependents tend to raise needs. C) A bigger loan adds to liabilities, raising the need.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Comparing the HLV method and the needs-based method, the insurance requirement under HLV will always be:\nA) Lower than needs-based  B) Equal to needs-based always  C) Higher than or equal to the needs-based figure  D) Unrelated to needs-based\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The HLV figure is always higher than or equal to the needs-based figure, because overall expenditure cannot exceed income (HLV captures full income; needs captures only spending needs).\nA), B) and D) contradict this stated relationship.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The adjusted (real) discounting rate used in the needs-based corpus calculation is given by:\nA) Investment return − inflation  B) Inflation − investment return  C) (1 + inflation) / (1 + return)  D) ((1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation)) − 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The adjusted rate = ((1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation)) − 1. With 8% and 6%: (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 1.89%. It discounts income by the *gap* between earnings and cost growth.\nA) Simple subtraction (8−6 = 2%) is an approximation, not the exact formula used. B) and C) are inverted/incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** In the needs-based method, which asset is NOT deducted when computing the corpus required, because the family will not want to sell it?\nA) Mutual fund holdings  B) Fixed deposits  C) The residential house and personal jewellery  D) Listed shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Personal assets such as the residential house and personal jewellery are excluded from the deduction because the family will not sell them even if the insured dies.\nA), B) and D) are investible assets that are deducted because they can be used to meet needs.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A term plan with return of premium charges a higher premium than a pure term plan. The extra premium is best understood as:\nA) A pure cost with no return  B) A bonus that doubles the cover  C) A tax-free gift  D) An investment by the insurer, refunded at the end, earning a modest implied return (~4.61% in the workbook example)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The difference between premiums is invested by the insurer to refund premiums at maturity; the implied return (via Excel's RATE) was 4.61% p.a. in the example.\nA) It is refunded, so not a pure cost. B) It does not affect the sum assured. C) It is a return of premium, not a tax-free gift.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which policy is most suitable to cover a reducing home loan balance?\nA) Whole life policy  B) Endowment policy  C) Mortgage (reducing term) insurance  D) Single-premium ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mortgage insurance has a sum assured that falls over time, designed to match a reducing housing loan.\nA), B) and D) are not designed to track a falling loan balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Why are reducing-balance mortgage insurance plans not popular in practice?\nA) They are illegal  B) They cover only death by accident  C) They pay no benefit at all  D) They are usually not much cheaper (sometimes more expensive) than level term, and home prepayments waste premium for cover no longer needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In practice these plans are often not cheaper (sometimes costlier) than a level term plan, and because home loans are frequently prepaid, premiums for the now-unneeded extra cover go waste.\nA) They are legal. B) and C) misstate the product's coverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A young earner expects rising income but worries about future health issues blocking insurance increases. Which rider helps most?\nA) Waiver of premium rider  B) Guaranteed insurability option rider  C) Double sum assured rider  D) Income benefit rider\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The guaranteed insurability option rider lets the insured enhance cover later without a fresh medical exam — ideal for young people expecting higher income but possible future health issues.\nA) Waiver of premium triggers on disability/loss of income. C) Double sum assured pays twice on death. D) Income benefit converts the death benefit to periodic income.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A policyholder wants the nominee to receive a fixed monthly income rather than a lump sum on death. Which rider achieves this?\nA) Critical illness rider  B) Accident death benefit rider  C) Income benefit rider  D) Guaranteed insurability rider\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The income benefit rider lets the nominee receive a periodic fixed income instead of a lump sum on the policyholder's death.\nA) pays on critical illness diagnosis. B) pays on accidental death/disability. D) lets cover be increased later.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An \"accelerated sum insured\" critical illness rider means:\nA) The life cover is increased on diagnosis  B) The life insurance cover is reduced by the amount paid out under the rider  C) The rider pays double the sum assured  D) The rider pays only after death\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In the accelerated version, the life cover is reduced by the amount paid out under the critical illness rider; it is cheaper than a rider that pays independently of the death claim.\nA) Cover is reduced, not increased. C) Doubling is the double sum assured rider. D) The rider pays on diagnosis + survival, not after death.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Pure protection (term) policies cannot be used to obtain a loan because:\nA) They have a very high surrender value  B) They acquire no surrender value  C) Banks dislike them  D) They are tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Pure protection policies do not acquire any surrender value, so there is nothing to lend against. Loans are available only against the surrender value of investment-cum-insurance policies.\nA) is the opposite of the truth. C) and D) are not the reason.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Generally, the interest rate charged on a loan taken from the insurance company against its own policy, compared to a bank/NBFC loan against the same policy, is:\nA) Higher  B) Lower  C) The same  D) Always zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurers' loan rates against their own policies are normally lower than bank/NBFC rates; banks/NBFCs make sense mainly to combine several policies into one larger, convenient loan.\nA) and C) contradict the workbook. D) Loans always carry interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** When a policy is assigned, the existing nomination is:\nA) Strengthened  B) Always unaffected  C) Doubled  D) Cancelled (except when assigned to the insurer for its own loan)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An assignment cancels an existing nomination — except where the assignment is to the insurer itself for a loan, in which case the nomination is only affected to the extent of the insurer's interest.\nA) and C) are not effects of assignment. B) is wrong because assignment generally cancels nomination.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** After a policy assigned for a loan is re-assigned back to the policyholder, the nomination:\nA) Gets reinstated  B) Remains permanently cancelled  C) Transfers to the lender  D) Must be re-registered with IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Nomination gets reinstated once the policy is re-assigned back to the policyholder.\nB) is wrong; cancellation is not permanent. C) and D) are not stated outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A key advantage of buying a policy under the MWP Act, 1874 is that the proceeds:\nA) Earn higher bonuses  B) Can have nominees changed easily  C) Are exempt from all premiums  D) Are shielded from the insured's creditors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — MWPA policies ensure the benefits stay with the nominees (spouse/children) despite any claim on the insured's assets by creditors.\nA) MWPA does not change returns. B) In fact nominees CANNOT be changed under MWPA. C) Premiums are still payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** After the 2015 amendment to the Insurance Act, if the nominee is the spouse, parent or children, they inherit:\nA) As trustees for the legal heirs  B) Only 50% of the proceeds  C) Nothing until probate  D) On a beneficial basis (in their own right)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Post-2015, such nominees inherit on a beneficial basis rather than as trustees, owning the money in their own right.\nA) describes the old position under normal law. B) and C) are not stated rules.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Life insurance premiums are \"telescopic.\" This means:\nA) Premium per thousand of sum assured falls as the sum assured rises  B) Premium rises in exact proportion to sum assured  C) Premium is always fixed regardless of cover  D) Premium doubles every year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Telescopic means premium does not rise in the same proportion as the sum assured; a larger sum assured costs less per thousand, so concentrating cover with one company can be cheaper.\nB) is the opposite of telescopic. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client needs Rs. 8 crore term cover but the insurer's telescopic rates stop at Rs. 2 crore. The workbook suggests:\nA) Buy one Rs. 8 crore policy  B) Buy from 8 different insurers  C) Avoid insurance entirely  D) Buy 4 policies of Rs. 2 crore each, so some can be dropped as needs fall\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Buying 4 policies of Rs. 2 crore each (from the same company) captures telescopic pricing up to the cap and lets the insured give up individual policies as insurance needs decline.\nA) loses the flexibility to drop cover later. B) fragments unnecessarily across insurers. C) ignores a genuine need.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Failing to disclose existing policies and pending proposals on the proposal form may be treated as omission of a material fact and can be grounds for repudiation/cancellation within:\nA) The first 3 years after the policy is issued  B) The first 1 year  C) The first 5 years  D) Any time, forever\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Such omission may be grounds for repudiation of a claim or cancellation of the policy within the first 3 years after issue.\nB), C) and D) state the wrong window.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** For a traditional life insurance policy issued on or after April 1, 2023 where the premium is higher than Rs. 5 lakh, the maturity proceeds are taxable as:\nA) Capital gains  B) Tax-free  C) Income from other sources  D) Salary income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For such traditional policies (issued on/after 01.04.2023, premium > Rs. 5 lakh), maturity proceeds are taxable as \"Income from other sources.\"\nA) Capital gains applies to high-premium ULIPs. B) Not tax-free here. D) Salary is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Per the Finance Act 2021, ULIP maturity proceeds are taxed as capital gains if the annual premium exceeds Rs. 2.5 lakh, for policies taken:\nA) Before 01.02.2021  B) Any time  C) Only after 01.04.2023  D) On or after 01.02.2021\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The capital-gains treatment for ULIPs with annual premium over Rs. 2.5 lakh applies only to policies taken on or after 01.02.2021.\nA) Policies before this date are not covered. B) is too broad. C) is the threshold date for traditional high-premium policies, not ULIPs.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A ULIP's maturity value is exempt from tax (for older policies) only if the sum assured is at least:\nA) 5 times the annual premium  B) 7 times the annual premium  C) 10 times the annual premium  D) 20 times the annual premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No tax is payable on the maturity value if the sum assured is at least 10 times the annual premium (e.g., Rs. 1 crore cover vs 10 × Rs. 3,13,200 premium).\nA), B) and D) are not the threshold for tax-free maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An adviser is comparing \"Term + Mutual Fund\" (Option 1) vs a Type II ULIP (Option 2). A key advantage of Option 1 is:\nA) Lower transparency  B) A 5-year lock-in  C) Inability to switch funds  D) Complete divisibility of the insurance and investment contracts (can pause investing freely)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In Option 1 the insurance and investment are two separate contracts (fully divisible); the investment can be paused/restarted and insurance discontinued independently, with no cost implication for pausing.\nA) Term + MF is more transparent, not less. B) The 5-year lock-in is a ULIP feature. C) MFs can be freely switched.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The workbook's bottom-line guidance is that a ULIP should be considered in place of \"Term + Mutual Fund\" only when:\nA) Insurance cover is genuinely required in the first place  B) The investor wants the highest returns  C) The investor is very old  D) Tax has to be avoided at all costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A ULIP is worth considering over Term + MF only where insurance cover is actually required; otherwise the ULIP's mortality charge is just an extra cost.\nB), C) and D) are not the stated decision rule; protection need comes first.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A defined-benefit life insurance policy (e.g., death, critical illness, accidental death) where the insured dies abroad will:\nA) Not pay because death occurred outside India  B) Pay, because such policies pay wherever the covered risk occurs  C) Pay only 50%  D) Pay only if the death is reported within 24 hours\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Defined-benefit policies pay wherever the covered risk occurs, since it is easy to verify a death/critical illness/accident anywhere in the world.\nA), C) and D) impose conditions the workbook does not state for defined-benefit policies.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Indian residents can buy life insurance policies of foreign companies using the Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS), up to a limit of:\nA) USD 1,00,000 per annum  B) USD 2,50,000 per annum  C) USD 5,00,000 per annum  D) USD 25,000 per annum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Residents may use their LRS entitlement of up to USD 2,50,000 per annum to buy foreign-currency-denominated life insurance policies; these are governed by the foreign country's regulations, so caution is advised.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect LRS limits.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Surinder took a 25-year investment-cum-insurance policy with sum assured Rs. 10,00,000, premiums paid half-yearly. After 5 years he stops paying. What is the paid-up sum assured?\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 2,00,000  C) Rs. 4,00,000  D) Rs. 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula: Paid-up SA = Sum Assured × (premiums paid ÷ total premiums due). Total premiums = 25 years × 2 (half-yearly) = 50. Paid in 5 years = 5 × 2 = 10. So = 10,00,000 × (10/50) = Rs. 2,00,000.\nA) Rs. 1,00,000 would be 5/50 — wrong premium count. C) and D) overstate the proportion paid. The logic: you keep the share of cover equal to the share of premiums actually paid.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A policy has a sum assured of Rs. 12,00,000, term 20 years, premiums paid quarterly. The holder stops after 8 years. What is the paid-up value?\nA) Rs. 4,80,000  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 4,00,000  D) Rs. 9,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total premiums = 20 × 4 = 80. Paid = 8 × 4 = 32. Paid-up SA = 12,00,000 × (32/80) = 12,00,000 × 0.4 = Rs. 4,80,000.\nB) Rs. 6,00,000 wrongly uses 8/16 (annual). C) Rs. 4,00,000 uses the wrong ratio. D) Rs. 9,60,000 = 64/80, mixing up paid vs unpaid.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A traditional policy paid total premiums of Rs. 3,00,000 so far. What is the minimum surrender value the policyholder can expect (general minimum, not last-two-years)?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 90,000  C) Rs. 2,10,000  D) Rs. 2,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Minimum surrender value = 30% of all premiums paid. = 30% × 3,00,000 = Rs. 90,000.\nA) Rs. 30,000 is 10%, not the rule. C) Rs. 2,10,000 = 70% (single-premium minimum). D) Rs. 2,70,000 = 90% (only in the last two years).\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A single-premium policy paid Rs. 5,00,000. The holder surrenders within the first three years. What guaranteed surrender value applies (minimum)?\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 2,50,000  C) Rs. 3,50,000  D) Rs. 4,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For single-premium policies, the guaranteed surrender value within the first three years is 70% of total premium. = 70% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 3,50,000.\nA) Rs. 1,50,000 = 30% (the traditional regular-premium minimum). B) Rs. 2,50,000 = 50%, not a defined rate. D) Rs. 4,50,000 = 90%, which only applies in the last two years.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An adviser uses the HLV thumb rule for a client earning Rs. 8,00,000 a year with 30 years to retirement, where assumed income growth equals the post-tax return. The HLV is:\nA) Rs. 2,40,00,000  B) Rs. 24,00,000  C) Rs. 80,00,000  D) Rs. 4,80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When income growth = return, the discounting rate is 0, so HLV = annual income × years to retirement = 8,00,000 × 30 = Rs. 2,40,00,000.\nB) Rs. 24,00,000 is only 3 years' income. C) Rs. 80,00,000 is 10 years. D) Rs. 4,80,00,000 doubles incorrectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Using the workbook thumb rule: income Rs. 10,00,000, 27 years to retirement, growth = return. HLV equals:\nA) Rs. 1,00,00,000  B) Rs. 2,70,00,000  C) Rs. 2,70,000  D) Rs. 27,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rate = 0 → HLV = income × years = 10,00,000 × 27 = Rs. 2,70,00,000 (exactly the workbook figure).\nA) Rs. 1,00,00,000 ignores the multiplier. C) and D) drop zeros / use the wrong multiplier.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** What adjusted (real) discounting rate results from an 8% investment return and 6% inflation?\nA) 2.00%  B) 1.89%  C) 14.00%  D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Adjusted rate = ((1 + 8%)/(1 + 6%)) − 1 = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 1.8867% ≈ 1.89%.\nA) 2.00% is the simple subtraction (8 − 6), an approximation, not the exact formula. C) 14% adds the rates. D) 0.75% is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** In Anil's needs analysis, his monthly income is Rs. 1,50,000; personal expenses Rs. 10,000; EMI Rs. 32,000; insurance premium Rs. 25,000. What is the monthly income to be replaced for the family?\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 1,15,000  C) Rs. 83,000  D) Rs. 67,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Income to replace = Total income − (personal needs + EMI + insurance premium) = 1,50,000 − (10,000 + 32,000 + 25,000) = 1,50,000 − 67,000 = Rs. 83,000/month.\nA) is the gross income, not what the family needs. B) Rs. 1,15,000 forgets some deductions. D) Rs. 67,000 is the deductions total, not the residual.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Anil's case: Rs. 83,000/month income to replace converts to an annual figure of:\nA) Rs. 8,30,000  B) Rs. 9,96,000  C) Rs. 10,00,000  D) Rs. 6,72,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual = monthly × 12 = 83,000 × 12 = Rs. 9,96,000.\nA) Rs. 8,30,000 multiplies by 10. C) Rs. 10,00,000 is a rounded guess. D) Rs. 6,72,000 wrongly uses Rs. 56,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** In Anil's case, his wife is 49 and expected to live to 80. The number of years (Nper) over which income must be provided is:\nA) 49  B) 31  C) 80  D) 27\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Nper = 80 − 49 = 31 years.\nA) 49 is the wife's current age. C) 80 is her life expectancy. D) 27 is from a different example (HLV thumb rule).\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** In Anil's case the corpus to generate Rs. 9,96,000/year for 31 years at an adjusted rate of 1.89% (payments at beginning of year) is about Rs. 2,36,43,984. After adding the Rs. 40,00,000 loan and deducting Rs. 1 crore existing cover and Rs. 50 lakh investments, the additional insurance required is:\nA) Rs. 2,36,43,984  B) Rs. 2,76,43,984  C) Rs. 1,26,43,984  D) Rs. 86,43,984\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Total required = corpus 2,36,43,984 + loan 40,00,000 = 2,76,43,984. Deduct existing cover 1,00,00,000 + investments 50,00,000 = 1,50,00,000. Additional cover = 2,76,43,984 − 1,50,00,000 = Rs. 1,26,43,984.\nA) is the corpus alone. B) is the gross total before deductions. D) deducts too much.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Why is Anil's Rs. 50 lakh house NOT deducted in the needs-based calculation?\nA) Personal assets like the residence are not sold by the family even after death  B) It has no resale value  C) It is already mortgaged fully  D) Houses cannot be valued\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The residential house (and personal jewellery) are personal assets the family will not want to sell, so they are excluded from the deductible assets.\nB) The house has value; that is not the reason. C) The loan is added separately as a liability. D) Houses can be valued (here Rs. 50 lakh).\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** For a return-of-premium term plan: pure term premium Rs. 9,416/yr, return-of-premium plan Rs. 17,473/yr, 30-year tenure. What is the extra premium (the effective annual investment)?\nA) Rs. 9,416  B) Rs. 17,473  C) Rs. 8,057  D) Rs. 26,889\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Extra premium = 17,473 − 9,416 = Rs. 8,057 per year. This is the amount effectively \"invested\" to fund the premium refund.\nA) and B) are the individual premiums. D) adds the two premiums instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** In the same return-of-premium example, the amount refunded at maturity is \"30 times the annual premium.\" That equals:\nA) Rs. 5,24,190  B) Rs. 2,82,480  C) Rs. 2,41,710  D) Rs. 17,473\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Refund = 30 × annual premium of Rs. 17,473 = Rs. 5,24,190.\nB) Rs. 2,82,480 = 30 × 9,416 (the pure term premium, wrong). C) Rs. 2,41,710 = 30 × 8,057 (extra premium, not the refund). D) is one year's premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Given extra premium Rs. 8,057/yr (PMT), Nper 30, PV 0, FV −Rs. 5,24,190, payments at beginning, the implied RATE is:\nA) About 4.61% p.a.  B) About 8% p.a.  C) About 0% p.a.  D) About 12% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Using Excel's RATE function on these inputs gives 4.61% p.a. — the return the insured earns on the extra premium for choosing return of premium.\nB), C) and D) are not the computed implied return; 4.61% is the workbook figure, usually a poor return.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** For an endowment example: endowment premium Rs. 3,16,332/yr, pure term Rs. 9,416/yr. What extra premium (PMT) is used to find the implied return?\nA) Rs. 3,16,332  B) Rs. 3,06,916  C) Rs. 9,416  D) Rs. 3,25,748\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Extra premium = 3,16,332 − 9,416 = Rs. 3,06,916 per year (the investment portion).\nA) is the full endowment premium. C) is the protection cost. D) adds the two figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** With extra premium Rs. 3,06,916/yr (PMT), Nper 30, PV 0, FV −Rs. 2,14,00,000, payments at beginning, the implied RATE is about:\nA) 3%  B) 5%  C) 8%  D) 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Excel RATE function returns about 5% p.a. on these inputs — the implied return on the extra premium for choosing the endowment. The workbook notes such returns are typically 3–5% and not guaranteed.\nA), C) and D) are not the computed figure here; 5% is the example result.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client pays an annual ULIP premium of Rs. 3,13,200 with a sum assured of Rs. 1 crore (policy issued before 01.02.2021). Is the maturity value tax-free?\nA) Yes, because the sum assured (Rs. 1 crore) is more than 10 × annual premium  B) No, because premium exceeds Rs. 2.5 lakh  C) No, ULIPs are never tax-free  D) Yes, all ULIPs are tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For policies before 01.02.2021, the maturity is exempt if sum assured ≥ 10 × annual premium. 10 × 3,13,200 = Rs. 31,32,000, and Rs. 1 crore exceeds this, so it is exempt.\nB) The Rs. 2.5 lakh capital-gains rule applies only to policies on/after 01.02.2021. C) and D) are too absolute.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A ULIP is taken on 01.06.2022 with an annual premium of Rs. 4,00,000. How is the maturity proceed taxed?\nA) Fully tax-free  B) As capital gains (premium exceeds Rs. 2.5 lakh, policy after 01.02.2021)  C) As salary  D) As income from other sources\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the Finance Act 2021, ULIPs with annual premium above Rs. 2.5 lakh taken on/after 01.02.2021 have maturity proceeds taxed as capital gains. Rs. 4,00,000 > Rs. 2.5 lakh and 01.06.2022 is after the cut-off.\nA) Not tax-free due to the high premium. C) Salary is unrelated. D) \"Income from other sources\" is the traditional-policy (premium > Rs. 5 lakh) rule, not ULIPs.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A traditional (non-ULIP) policy is issued on 01.05.2023 with an annual premium of Rs. 6,00,000. The maturity proceeds are taxed as:\nA) Capital gains  B) Tax-free  C) Income from other sources  D) Business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For traditional policies issued on/after 01.04.2023 with premium above Rs. 5 lakh, maturity proceeds are taxable as \"Income from other sources.\" Premium Rs. 6,00,000 > Rs. 5 lakh and the date qualifies.\nA) Capital gains is the ULIP rule. B) Not tax-free here. D) Business income is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Mr Jeevan (age 35) needs Rs. 100 lakh cover. Option 1: term premium Rs. 1,100/month + invest Rs. 25,000/month in a mutual fund. What is the combined monthly outgo, and how does Option 2's ULIP premium compare?\nA) Rs. 26,100; equal to the ULIP's Rs. 26,100/month  B) Rs. 25,000; less than the ULIP  C) Rs. 1,100; more than the ULIP  D) Rs. 50,000; double the ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Option 1 outgo = 1,100 + 25,000 = Rs. 26,100/month, exactly matching Option 2's ULIP premium of Rs. 26,100/month — the comparison is designed to be like-for-like.\nB) ignores the term premium. C) ignores the investment. D) doubles incorrectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** In Mr Jeevan's comparison, a Type II ULIP means that on death before maturity, the beneficiary receives:\nA) Only the fund value  B) Only the sum assured  C) The sum assured PLUS the fund value  D) The higher of sum assured or fund value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In a Type II ULIP, on death before maturity the plan pays the sum assured amount PLUS the fund value; on maturity, only the fund value is payable.\nA) and B) describe partial payouts. D) \"higher of\" is another structure, not Type II.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** In Option 2 (ULIP), what happens if the monthly premium is not paid within the grace period during the first 5 years?\nA) Nothing changes  B) The policy becomes paid-up with full cover  C) The investment continues normally  D) The entire contract moves to a discontinued policy account and insurance coverage stops\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In the first 5 years the two contracts are indivisible; non-payment within the grace period moves the entire contract to a discontinued policy account and the insurance coverage stops.\nA) and C) ignore the consequence. B) ULIPs do not become full-cover paid-up here.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A 60-year-old wealthy investor wants to put a large lump sum into a ULIP mainly for investment, and does not really need insurance cover. The adviser should note that:\nA) The ULIP is ideal because of guaranteed returns  B) ULIPs have no charges for older investors  C) Insurance is always required regardless of needs  D) The mortality charge becomes an additional unnecessary cost, and qualifying for 10× cover may be costly/difficult\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Where insurance is not required, the ULIP's mortality (insurance) charge is just an extra cost; older wealthy investors often don't need large cover and find the 10× requirement hard/costly to obtain on health grounds.\nA) ULIP returns are market-linked, not guaranteed. B) Charges still apply. C) The workbook stresses cover should be needs-driven.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client buys term insurance for Rs. 8 crore where telescopic rates cap at Rs. 2 crore per insurer. He buys four Rs. 2 crore policies from the same insurer. Five years later his need falls to Rs. 4 crore. What is the practical benefit of this structure?\nA) He must surrender all policies  B) He gets a refund of all premiums  C) He must buy a new policy  D) He can simply let two of the four policies lapse, keeping Rs. 4 crore cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Splitting into four Rs. 2 crore policies lets him drop (let lapse) two policies as his need declines, retaining exactly Rs. 4 crore — the flexibility the workbook highlights.\nA) He need not surrender all. B) Term plans give no premium refund. C) No new purchase is needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An investor takes a bank loan against the security of his investment-cum-insurance policy after a past loan default. Apart from liquidity, what additional benefit does the workbook mention?\nA) It is a good way to rebuild a damaged credit score  B) It increases the sum assured  C) It makes the policy tax-free  D) It waives future premiums\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Taking a loan from a bank/NBFC against the security of an investment-cum-insurance policy is a good way to rebuild a credit score after a past default.\nB), C) and D) are not benefits the workbook attributes to such a loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A married man buys a MWPA policy naming his wife. He later divorces her and wants to change the nominee to his new spouse. Under the MWP Act:\nA) He can freely change the nominee  B) He cannot change the nominee; the ex-wife continues to be the nominee  C) The policy lapses on divorce  D) The court automatically reassigns it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under MWPA, nominees cannot be changed after purchase; even after divorce, the wife continues to be the nominee. This rigidity is a known drawback of MWPA.\nA) contradicts MWPA's core rule. C) Divorce does not lapse the policy. D) There is no automatic court reassignment.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A MWPA policy has a survival benefit payable while the insured is alive. Who receives this survival benefit?\nA) The insured person himself  B) The nominees (spouse/children), not the insured  C) The insured's parents  D) The insurer keeps it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under MWPA, even survival benefits are paid to the nominees (spouse/children), not to the insured person, who has no right to the amounts under the policy.\nA) The insured cannot claim it. C) Parents cannot be MWPA nominees. D) The insurer pays out, it does not keep the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client compares a critical illness rider (accelerated sum insured, attached to a term plan) against a standalone critical illness policy from a general insurer. The workbook's \"ironic\" observation is that:\nA) The standalone policy is always cheaper  B) In most cases the term policy with an equal accelerated critical-illness cover is cheaper than the standalone policy  C) They cost exactly the same  D) Riders are always more expensive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ironically, in most cases a term policy with an equal critical-illness cover on an accelerated sum-insured basis is cheaper than just a standalone critical-illness policy from a general insurance company.\nA), C) and D) contradict this observation.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** An adviser receives a foreign-currency investment-cum-insurance policy proposal bought via LRS. According to the workbook, the correct evaluation order is:\nA) Compare investment returns first, then check cover  B) Cover the protection needs first; only if sufficient, evaluate the investment portion against similar LRS instruments  C) Buy it because it is in foreign currency  D) Reject all foreign policies outright\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Like any investment-cum-insurance product, protection needs must be covered first; only if that is sufficient should the investment portion be risk-profiled, checked for suitability and compared with similar foreign-currency LRS instruments.\nA) reverses the priority. C) ignores suitability and the foreign-regulation caution. D) is too extreme — foreign policies are allowed, just to be used with caution.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario — Vikram's Insurance Planning**\n\nVikram is 40 years old with a monthly income of Rs. 2,00,000. He spends Rs. 15,000/month on personal expenses, pays an EMI of Rs. 40,000/month on a Rs. 50 lakh home loan (current outstanding Rs. 50,00,000), and pays Rs. 20,000/month towards an existing insurance premium. He wants to provide for his wife, currently 45, expected to live to 80. Assume expected inflation is 6% and post-tax return on investments is 8%. His existing life cover is Rs. 80,00,000, he has other investments of Rs. 60,00,000, and his house is worth Rs. 50,00,000. He also asks his adviser to confirm a few rules before he buys a new term plan.\n\nUse the needs-based (income replacement) method. The adjusted rate is ((1+8%)/(1+6%)) − 1 = 1.89%. For a beginning-of-year annuity of Rs. X over n years at 1.89%, assume the present-value factor works out such that the corpus for Rs. 15,00,000/year over 35 years ≈ Rs. 3,84,00,000 (rounded for this exercise).\n\n<details><summary>Q1 — What is the annual income to be replaced for Vikram's family?</summary>\n\n**Step 1 — Identify deductions that stop being a family need:** personal expenses (Rs. 15,000), EMI (Rs. 40,000) and the insurance premium (Rs. 20,000) are amounts that are not part of the income the family needs to live on.\n\n**Step 2 — Monthly income to replace** = Total income − (personal needs + EMI + insurance premium)\n= 2,00,000 − (15,000 + 40,000 + 20,000)\n= 2,00,000 − 75,000 = **Rs. 1,25,000/month.**\n\n**Step 3 — Annualise:** 1,25,000 × 12 = **Rs. 15,00,000 per annum.**\n\nIntuition: insurance must replace only the income the family actually relies on, after removing the earner's own personal spend, the loan EMI (the loan is handled separately) and the premium outgo.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q2 — What is the number of years (Nper) over which income must be provided, and what adjusted rate applies?</summary>\n\n**Nper:** The wife is 45 and is expected to live to 80, so income must be provided for 80 − 45 = **35 years.**\n\n**Adjusted (real) rate:** Formula = ((1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation)) − 1 = ((1 + 8%)/(1 + 6%)) − 1 = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 1.8867% ≈ **1.89%.**\n\nIntuition: the corpus earns 8% but the family's costs rise at 6%, so the money only \"really\" grows at the gap of about 1.89%. We discount the future income stream at this real rate. Nper falls (and so does the corpus) as the dependent gets older — which is why insurance need declines with age.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q3 — What corpus is needed to generate Rs. 15,00,000/year for 35 years, and what is the total fund requirement after adding the loan?</summary>\n\n**Step 1 — Corpus:** Using the PV of a beginning-of-year annuity: PV(rate = 1.89%, Nper = 35, PMT = −15,00,000, type = 1). For this exercise the corpus ≈ **Rs. 3,84,00,000.**\n\nThis corpus, invested at 8% while expenses rise at 6%, will exactly fund Rs. 15,00,000/year (growing with inflation) for 35 years and be drawn down to zero by the end.\n\n**Step 2 — Add the liability:** The Rs. 50,00,000 outstanding home loan must be repaid, so add it.\nTotal funds required = 3,84,00,000 + 50,00,000 = **Rs. 4,34,00,000.**\n\nIntuition: the family needs enough to (a) replace lost income and (b) clear outstanding debts.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q4 — What is the additional life insurance cover Vikram should buy?</summary>\n\n**Step 1 — Identify deductible existing resources:** existing life cover Rs. 80,00,000 + other investments Rs. 60,00,000 = Rs. 1,40,00,000.\n\n**Note — do NOT deduct the house:** the Rs. 50,00,000 residence is a personal asset the family will not sell, so it is excluded.\n\n**Step 2 — Additional cover required** = Total funds required − existing resources\n= 4,34,00,000 − 1,40,00,000 = **Rs. 2,94,00,000.**\n\nIntuition: the new term plan only needs to fill the *gap* between what the family will require and what Vikram has already arranged (cover + investments). The home is left out because it is not a sellable resource for the family.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q5 — Vikram wants the new term plan to last 20 years with an annual premium of Rs. 60,000. (a) What minimum sum assured must IRDAI rules give him? (b) If he prefers a \"return of premium\" variant, what should the adviser warn?</summary>\n\n**Part (a) — Minimum sum assured:** Vikram is 40 (below 45) and the term (20 years) is more than 10 years. Rule: minimum sum assured = 10 × annual premium = 10 × 60,000 = **Rs. 6,00,000.**\n(Note: this is just the regulatory *minimum*; his actual needs analysis shows he should buy about Rs. 2.94 crore, far above this floor. The 10× rule also keeps maturity proceeds tax-friendly.)\n\n**Part (b) — Return-of-premium warning:** In a return-of-premium variant the premium is much higher; the extra premium is invested by the insurer to refund premiums at the end. The implied return on that extra premium is typically low (about 4.61% p.a. in the workbook example). The adviser should warn that Vikram is effectively earning a poor return on the extra outgo, and a pure term plan plus investing the difference separately is usually better — protection first, investments where they are most efficient.\n\nIntuition: insurance should primarily provide cheap, adequate cover. Bundling a weak \"savings\" feature (return of premium) into the term plan dilutes that efficiency.\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "03", "title": "Non-Life Insurance Products", "module": "Module 7: Risk Management & Insurance Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 3: Non-Life Insurance Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **elements** of non-life insurance products\n- The main **types** of non-life (general) insurance products\n- **Benefits/limitations & provisions** when insurance is taken from multiple companies\n- **Criteria** to compare various insurance products\n- **Global coverage** for different general insurance products\n\n---\n\n## 1. What is Non-Life Insurance?\n\n**Non-life (general) insurance** covers the **loss or destruction of assets** and protects against **unexpected large expenses** that would drain your income. (Life insurance covers a *life*; non-life covers *everything else* — health, car, home, travel, liability, etc.)\n\n### 1.1 Elements of Non-Life Products\n| Element | Meaning |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Sum insured** | The insurer's **maximum liability** for claims in the policy period. Insurer may set min/max. |\n| **Term** | Usually **1 year**. For health/two-wheeler it can be **2 or 3 years**. |\n| **Premium** | Depends on sum insured + assessed risk (age, gender, health for medical; engine CC, place of registration, vehicle age for motor). Paid at policy inception. |\n| **Deductible** | The portion of a claim the insured pays first. |\n| **Restore / Recharge** | Two health benefits that refill the sum insured (see below). |\n| **No claim bonus (NCB)** | Lower premium next year (or extra cover) for each claim-free year. |\n\n> 💡 **Restore vs Recharge (health):**\n> - **Restore** = sum insured is brought back to 100% if it is **partially OR fully exhausted**.\n> - **Recharge** = sum insured is refilled to 100% **only when it gets reduced due to a claim**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Types of Non-Life Insurance Products\n\n### a. Property Insurance\nProtects the **structure and contents** of a building against natural and man-made disasters (fire, theft).\n- **Not covered:** wilful destruction, normal wear and tear.\n- **Valuables** (jewellery, art, antiques) need an **add-on or separate policy**.\n- Often taken **with a home loan** (lenders insist on it).\n\n### b. Health Insurance\nReimburses **medical expenses** for the policyholder and covered family members, up to the sum insured.\n- Covers **hospitalisation** or **domiciliary treatment** (treated at home when the patient can't be moved).\n- **In-patient** = admitted, requiring at least overnight / **24 hours** stay.\n- **Day-care treatment** now covered for some procedures (technology lets the patient leave the same day).\n- Specified expenses (consultation, medicines, room cost) — **sub-limits** may apply to each head.\n- **Pre-existing illnesses** may be excluded for a fixed period at first purchase or after a lapse.\n- **Cashless facility:** bills settled directly with the hospital up to the approved sum.\n- **Family floater:** one policy covering multiple family members up to the sum insured.\n- **Portability:** if you switch insurers, the **no-claim bonus and time-bound exclusion credits** carry over — but only if the **previous policy was maintained without a break**.\n\n### c. Motor Insurance\nIndemnifies the insured for accidents arising from the vehicle's use anywhere in India — covering **(i) death/bodily injury to any person** and **(ii) damage to others' property**.\n- **Third-party liability insurance is COMPULSORY** — no vehicle may be used in a public place without it. (Own-damage/theft cover is optional.)\n\n**Lesser-known car insurance facts:**\n- **No Claim Bonus (NCB):** cuts next year's **own-damage** premium for claim-free years — up to **50%** after **5 claim-free years**.\n- NCB belongs to the **person, not the car** — sell your car and the new owner gets no NCB; you can **transfer your NCB to your new car**.\n- The own-damage premium **varies by insurer** — online comparison can save money.\n- A new insurer gives the NCB if you **shift at renewal**.\n\n### d. Personal Accident Insurance\nPays if the insured suffers **bodily injury solely and directly from an accident** caused by **external, violent and visible means**. Covers:\n- **Permanent total disablement**\n- **Permanent partial disablement**\n- **Temporary total disablement**\n\n> ⚠️ Calculating permanent total disability cover (like life cover) is **often overlooked**, so this cover is frequently **inadequate**.\n\n### e. Critical Illness Insurance\nPays a **lump sum** if the insured contracts a specified disease (cancer, heart attack, stroke, kidney failure, multiple sclerosis…).\n- **No payment on death** (unlike life insurance).\n- Subject to a **minimum survival period of 30 days** after diagnosis.\n- The lump sum can be used **however the claimant wishes** (generating income, repaying a mortgage).\n- Available with life policies or as standalone.\n\n> 💡 People wrongly treat critical illness as a small top-up. It should really be sized like **life insurance** (income-replacement). Large covers usually come only as a **standalone policy** or as an **accelerated sum insured rider** on a life policy — not as a health-policy rider.\n\n### f. Overseas Travel Insurance\nProvides medical, financial and other assistance during **international travel** — medical help, baggage delay, accident, extra covers. **Reimbursement up to the maximum** in the policy. May be **mandatory** for some countries.\n\n### g. Liability Insurance\nIndemnifies against **damages payable under law** for personal injury to third parties or damage to their property. Liability can arise under **common law (negligence)** or **statutory law on a no-fault basis** (even without negligence).\n- Most common example: **professional indemnity** plans (Doctors, Lawyers, Investment Advisers).\n- Covers legal defence costs and damages.\n- **Does NOT cover** intentional damage or damages from criminal activities.\n\n### h. Fidelity Insurance\nCovers losses an **employer** suffers from an **employee's forgery, fraud or dishonesty** (money or goods). Taken where employees handle **large sums of cash or valuables**.\n\n### i. Directors and Officers (D&O) Liability Insurance\nInsures directors/managers/supervisors against **personal liability and defence costs** from claims alleging a **wrongful act** in their duties. Triggered by investigations, shareholder claims, regulatory/compliance issues, employment-practice violations, discrimination allegations.\n\n### j. Keyman Insurance\nA **life** insurance policy a **company buys on a key executive's life**. The **company** is the beneficiary and pays the premiums (protecting the firm against the loss of that person).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Multiple Insurance Policies — Benefits & Limitations\n\n- **Multiple indemnity policies (e.g., health) make little sense** — total claim cannot exceed the combined sum insured, and you must deal with multiple insurers at claim time.\n- In health insurance, **premium per extra lakh of cover drops sharply** as cover rises — a Rs. 100-lakh policy may cost only a few thousand rupees more than a Rs. 50-lakh one.\n- Sometimes multiple policies are **unavoidable** (employer cover + your own). Then **contribution does NOT apply** unless the chosen policy's sum insured is **less than the claim**.\n- **Choose the employer policy first.** If the claim is within that sum insured → no contribution. If it **exceeds** it → use another policy, and contribution applies.\n- A common strategy: a **base policy (Rs. 5 lakh) + super top-up (Rs. 10 lakh) with a Rs. 5 lakh deductible** → total Rs. 15 lakh cover at an economical premium (super top-up premium is low).\n  - A single Rs. 15-lakh policy is **only slightly more expensive** (the difference is the admin simplicity of one policy).\n- **Multiple defined-benefit policies (life, critical illness, accidental) ALL pay** — no limit on how many you hold.\n\n### 3.1 Top-up vs Super Top-up (key difference)\nBoth pay claims **above a threshold (deductible)**. The difference is **how the threshold applies**:\n- **Top-up:** threshold applies to **EACH individual claim**.\n- **Super Top-up:** threshold applies to the **TOTAL of all claims** in the year (aggregate).\n\n> 💡 This is why a Super Top-up is more generous — you only cross the deductible **once per year**, not once per claim.\n\n#### 📖 Worked example\nSetup: Base health plan **Rs. 5 lakh**; a **Top-up** (Case 1) or **Super Top-up** (Case 2) of **Rs. 10 lakh** above a **Rs. 5 lakh threshold**.\n\n**Single claim of Rs. 8 lakh** (both cases the same):\n- Rs. 5 lakh from Base plan (exhausted).\n- Rs. 3 lakh from the Top-up/Super Top-up after the Rs. 5 lakh threshold; balance limit left = Rs. 7 lakh.\n\n**Multiple claims — where they differ:**\n| Claim | Top-up (threshold per claim) | Super Top-up (threshold on aggregate) |\n|-------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| 1st: Rs. 3 lakh | Rs. 3 lakh from Base; Base left Rs. 2 lakh | Rs. 3 lakh from Base; Base left Rs. 2 lakh; aggregate threshold applied = Rs. 3 lakh |\n| 2nd: Rs. 6 lakh | Rs. 2 lakh from Base (exhausted); Rs. 1 lakh from Top-up after Rs. 5 lakh threshold; Top-up left Rs. 9 lakh | Rs. 2 lakh from Base (exhausted); balance threshold Rs. 2 lakh applied; Rs. 4 lakh from Super Top-up; Super Top-up left Rs. 6 lakh; aggregate threshold now Rs. 5 lakh |\n| 3rd: Rs. 2 lakh | Claim < Rs. 5 lakh threshold → **nothing** payable from Top-up | Threshold (Rs. 5 lakh) already met → **Rs. 2 lakh** from Super Top-up; left Rs. 4 lakh |\n| 4th: Rs. 4 lakh | Claim < Rs. 5 lakh threshold → **nothing** payable from Top-up | Threshold already met → **Rs. 4 lakh** from Super Top-up; left Nil |\n| **Total Rs. 15 lakh** | Base Rs. 5 lakh + Top-up Rs. 1 lakh = **Rs. 6 lakh paid** | Base Rs. 5 lakh + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh = **Rs. 15 lakh paid** |\n\n> ⚠️ **The big lesson:** With a **Top-up**, individual claims below the threshold pay nothing — so on Rs. 15 lakh of claims only **Rs. 6 lakh** was paid. With a **Super Top-up**, the threshold is crossed once on the aggregate, so the **full Rs. 15 lakh** was paid.\n\n> 💡 **Tip:** Keep the Base plan and Top-up/Super Top-up with the **same company** to simplify claims. For super top-up, inform the insurer even of claims that aren't payable by it, so it can **mark the threshold levels used**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Comparison Between Insurance Policies\n\n### 4.1 Health Insurance vs Critical Illness\n| Parameter | Health Insurance | Critical Illness |\n|-----------|------------------|------------------|\n| **Type** | Indemnity policy | Defined Benefit policy |\n| **Designed to cover** | Reimburses actual hospitalisation expenses | Lump sum — use for income, mortgage, or any purpose incl. hospitalisation |\n| **Claim paid** | On incurring covered hospitalisation expenses | On contracting a pre-specified disease AND surviving **15–30 days** |\n\n### 4.2 Offline vs Online Policies\n- Most policies can be bought online; insurers often charge **lower premiums online**.\n- Some \"**web only**\" policies exist.\n- **Web aggregators / brokers** provide convenient online comparisons and have become major selling channels.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Global Coverage\n\n**Will a health policy pay for hospitalisation outside India?**\n- **Defined benefit policies** (life, critical illness, accidental death) pay **wherever the risk occurs** — easy to verify.\n- **Indemnity policies (health)** require **ascertaining the actual expenses**. The insurer has systems for India but finds it hard abroad → health policies normally **cover expenses in India only**.\n- **Exception:** **Overseas travel policies** are indemnity policies **designed country-by-country**, with arrangements to verify foreign expenses.\n\n**Can residents buy foreign general insurance?**\n- Yes — via **LRS**, up to **USD 2,50,000 per annum** (common for students going abroad to buy the foreign insurer recommended by the university).\n- ⚠️ But foreign rules govern the policy and grievance redressal → exercise caution.\n\n**Students going overseas — Indian or foreign policy?**\n- Depends on the country/university. Some bundle compulsory insurance in fees; some require a designated insurer; some just specify conditions; some have no requirement.\n- Treatment costs abroad can be **very high**, so cover is advisable either way.\n- Indian insurers know major countries'/universities' requirements and offer suitable add-ons.\n- **Other things equal, Indian policies tend to be cheaper** than similar foreign-company policies.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- Non-life insurance covers **asset loss/destruction and unexpected large expenses**; term is usually **1 year** (2–3 for health/two-wheeler).\n- **Sum insured** = insurer's maximum liability. **Restore** refills on partial/full exhaustion; **Recharge** refills only when reduced by a claim.\n- **Third-party motor insurance is compulsory.** Car **NCB** up to **50%** after 5 claim-free years; NCB belongs to the **person, not the car**.\n- **Personal accident** covers permanent total, permanent partial, temporary total disablement.\n- **Critical illness** = defined benefit, lump sum, **no death payout**, needs **30-day survival**; size it like life cover.\n- **Liability** insurance (e.g., professional indemnity) excludes **intentional/criminal** damage. **Fidelity** = employee fraud. **D&O** = directors' personal liability. **Keyman** = company insures a key person's life.\n- **Top-up:** threshold per **claim**; **Super Top-up:** threshold on **aggregate** of claims. (Rs. 15 lakh claims → Top-up pays Rs. 6 lakh, Super Top-up pays Rs. 15 lakh.)\n- Multiple **indemnity** policies make little sense (total ≤ combined sum insured); multiple **defined-benefit** policies **all pay**.\n- Health (indemnity) usually covers **India only**; **overseas travel** policies are the indemnity exception.\n- LRS limit for foreign policies = **USD 2,50,000/year**; Indian policies are usually cheaper.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Value / Meaning |\n|---------------|-----------------|\n| Non-life term | Usually 1 year (2–3 for health/two-wheeler) |\n| In-patient stay | At least overnight / 24 hours |\n| Car NCB maximum | Up to 50% after 5 claim-free years |\n| Third-party motor insurance | Compulsory |\n| Critical illness survival period | 15–30 days (commonly 30) |\n| Top-up threshold | Applied per individual claim |\n| Super Top-up threshold | Applied on aggregate of all yearly claims |\n| Example total claims | Rs. 15 lakh → Top-up pays Rs. 6 lakh; Super Top-up pays Rs. 15 lakh |\n| Base + Super Top-up example | Rs. 5 lakh base + Rs. 10 lakh super top-up (Rs. 5 lakh deductible) = Rs. 15 lakh cover |\n| Health policy global coverage | India only (overseas travel is the exception) |\n| LRS limit | USD 2,50,000 per annum |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 3: Non-Life Insurance Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What does non-life (general) insurance cover?\n> **A:** The **loss or destruction of assets** and protection against **unexpected large expenses** that drain income (health, motor, home, travel, liability, etc.) — everything except a life.\n\n**Q2.** What is the \"sum insured\" in a non-life policy?\n> **A:** The amount specified in the policy = the insurer's **maximum liability** for claims in the policy period. The insurer may set a minimum and maximum.\n\n**Q3.** What is the typical term of a non-life policy?\n> **A:** Usually **1 year**. For health and two-wheeler policies it can be **2 or 3 years**.\n\n**Q4.** What factors determine a non-life premium?\n> **A:** The **sum insured** and the **assessed risk** — e.g., age/gender/health history for medical; engine cubic capacity, place of registration, vehicle age for motor.\n\n**Q5.** What is the difference between Restore and Recharge benefits?\n> **A:** **Restore** refills the sum insured to 100% on **partial OR full** exhaustion. **Recharge** refills to 100% **only when** the sum insured is reduced due to a claim.\n\n**Q6.** What is a No Claim Bonus (NCB)?\n> **A:** Lower premiums (or extra bonus cover) enjoyed in later years for each **claim-free year**.\n\n**Q7.** What does property insurance typically NOT cover?\n> **A:** **Wilful destruction** and **normal wear and tear**. Valuables like jewellery/art need a separate or add-on policy.\n\n**Q8.** What is an \"in-patient\" in health insurance?\n> **A:** A patient **admitted to hospital** for treatment requiring at least **overnight or 24 hours** of stay.\n\n**Q9.** What is domiciliary treatment?\n> **A:** Treatment given **at home** when the patient is not in a condition to be moved to the hospital.\n\n**Q10.** What is a family floater policy?\n> **A:** One policy that covers **multiple family members** under a shared sum insured.\n\n**Q11.** What must be true to benefit from health policy portability?\n> **A:** The **previous policy must have been maintained without a break**. Then no-claim bonus and time-bound exclusion credits transfer to the new insurer.\n\n**Q12.** Which part of motor insurance is compulsory?\n> **A:** **Third-party liability** insurance — no vehicle can be used in a public place without it. Own-damage/theft cover is optional.\n\n**Q13.** What are the two things motor third-party cover indemnifies?\n> **A:** (i) **Death or bodily injury** to any person, and (ii) **damage to others' property** (not the insured's own).\n\n**Q14.** What is the maximum car No Claim Bonus and after how long?\n> **A:** Up to **50%** of the own-damage premium after **5 claim-free years**.\n\n**Q15.** Does the car NCB belong to the car or the owner?\n> **A:** To the **person (owner)**, not the car. A buyer of your old car gets no NCB; you transfer your NCB to your **new car**.\n\n**Q16.** What three types of disablement does personal accident insurance cover?\n> **A:** **Permanent total**, **permanent partial**, and **temporary total** disablement (from accidents by external, violent and visible means).\n\n**Q17.** How does critical illness insurance differ from life insurance?\n> **A:** Critical illness pays a **lump sum on diagnosis** of a specified disease (after **30-day** survival) and pays **nothing on death**; life insurance pays on death.\n\n**Q18.** Why is critical illness cover often inadequate?\n> **A:** People treat it as a small top-up, but it should be sized like **life insurance (income replacement)**. Large covers come only as standalone policies or **accelerated sum insured riders** on life policies.\n\n**Q19.** What does liability insurance NOT cover?\n> **A:** **Intentional damage** or damages from **criminal activities**. (It covers legal defence costs and damages for negligence or no-fault statutory liability.)\n\n**Q20.** Give the most common example of liability insurance.\n> **A:** **Professional indemnity** plans taken by Doctors, Lawyers, Investment Advisers.\n\n**Q21.** What does fidelity insurance cover?\n> **A:** Losses an **employer** suffers from an employee's **forgery, fraud or dishonesty** (money or goods). Used where employees handle large cash/valuables.\n\n**Q22.** What does Directors & Officers (D&O) liability insurance cover?\n> **A:** Directors/managers against **personal liability and defence costs** from claims alleging a **wrongful act** in their company duties.\n\n**Q23.** Who is the beneficiary in a Keyman insurance policy?\n> **A:** The **company** — it buys life cover on a key executive, pays the premiums, and receives the payout.\n\n**Q24.** Why do multiple indemnity (health) policies make little sense?\n> **A:** The **total claim can't exceed the combined sum insured**, and you must deal with multiple insurers at claim time.\n\n**Q25.** When you hold an employer health policy plus your own, when does the contribution clause apply?\n> **A:** **Not** if the chosen policy's sum insured covers the claim. It applies **only if the claim exceeds** the chosen policy's sum insured and you draw on another policy.\n\n**Q26.** Which policy should you usually claim from first when you have employer + personal cover?\n> **A:** The **employer policy first**.\n\n**Q27.** Do multiple defined-benefit policies all pay?\n> **A:** **Yes** — life, critical illness, and accidental death/disability policies all pay regardless of how many you hold (no contribution).\n\n**Q28.** What is the core difference between a Top-up and a Super Top-up plan?\n> **A:** **Top-up:** the threshold (deductible) applies to **each individual claim**. **Super Top-up:** the threshold applies to the **aggregate of all claims** in the year.\n\n**Q29.** With a Rs. 5 lakh threshold and Rs. 15 lakh of claims (Rs. 3 + 6 + 2 + 4 lakh), how much does a Top-up vs Super Top-up pay (with a Rs. 5 lakh base)?\n> **A:** **Top-up:** Base Rs. 5 lakh + Top-up Rs. 1 lakh = **Rs. 6 lakh**. **Super Top-up:** Base Rs. 5 lakh + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh = **Rs. 15 lakh**. (Small claims below the per-claim threshold get nothing under Top-up.)\n\n**Q30.** Why keep the base and top-up plans with the same company?\n> **A:** Simpler claims — you deal with one insurer when claims exceed the threshold. For super top-up, also inform the insurer of non-payable claims so it can **mark used threshold levels**.\n\n**Q31.** Health insurance vs critical illness — which is indemnity and which is defined benefit?\n> **A:** Health = **indemnity** (reimburses actual expenses); Critical illness = **defined benefit** (fixed lump sum).\n\n**Q32.** Are online non-life policies usually cheaper or more expensive than offline?\n> **A:** Often **cheaper** — many insurers charge lower premiums for policies bought online, and some offer \"web only\" policies.\n\n**Q33.** Does a health (indemnity) policy normally cover hospitalisation outside India?\n> **A:** **No** — health policies normally cover expenses **in India only**, because verifying foreign expenses is difficult. The exception is **overseas travel policies**.\n\n**Q34.** Why do defined-benefit policies pay worldwide but indemnity health policies don't?\n> **A:** Defined-benefit events (death, critical illness, accident) are easy to **verify anywhere**. Indemnity health needs **ascertaining actual expenses**, which insurers can't easily do abroad.\n\n**Q35.** What is the LRS limit for buying foreign general insurance, and who commonly uses it?\n> **A:** **USD 2,50,000 per annum**. Common for Indian **students going abroad** buying the insurer recommended by their university.\n\n**Q36.** For students going overseas, are Indian or foreign policies usually cheaper?\n> **A:** Other things being equal, **Indian policies tend to be cheaper** than similar foreign-company policies — but it depends on the country/university requirements.\n\n**Q37.** What is day-care treatment in health insurance?\n> **A:** Procedures that require hospitalisation but, due to technology, let the patient be **released the same day** — now covered by many policies.\n\n**Q38.** What does \"sub-limit\" mean in health insurance?\n> **A:** A **cap on a specific expense head** (e.g., room cost, consultation fees) within the overall sum insured.\n\n**Q39.** Under what type of law can liability arise on a \"no-fault basis\"?\n> **A:** Under **statutory law** — liability even when there is **no negligence**. (Common-law liability arises from negligence.)\n\n**Q40.** In health insurance, how does the premium behave as cover increases?\n> **A:** The **premium per extra lakh drops sharply** and becomes almost negligible after a point — a Rs. 100-lakh policy may cost only a few thousand rupees more than a Rs. 50-lakh one.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 3: Non-Life Insurance Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 **Tier 1 (Easy, Q1–Q35)** tests recall of definitions and facts; 🟡 **Tier 2 (Medium, Q36–Q70)** tests application, comparison and rule-use; 🔴 **Tier 3 (Hard, Q71–Q100)** tests multi-step numericals and scenarios. Remember the real exam has **25% negative marking** — a wrong answer costs you one-quarter of a mark, so guess only when you can eliminate options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What does non-life insurance primarily provide cover against?\nA) Loss or destruction of assets and unexpected large expenses  B) Loss of life only  C) Only medical expenses  D) Only motor accidents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook defines non-life insurance as providing risk cover from loss or destruction of assets, and against unexpected large expenses that can drain an individual's income.\n- B) is wrong — covering a *life* is life insurance, not non-life.\n- C) and D) are wrong — health and motor are just *examples* of non-life products, not the whole definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** \"Sum insured\" in a non-life policy represents:\nA) The premium paid by the insured  B) The deductible amount  C) The insurer's maximum liability for claims during the policy period  D) The no-claim bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sum insured is the amount specified in the policy that represents the insurer's *maximum liability* for claims made during the policy period; the insurer may set a minimum and maximum.\n- A) is wrong — that is the premium, a different element.\n- B) is wrong — the deductible is the portion the insured pays.\n- D) is wrong — NCB is a premium discount, not the maximum liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The term of a non-life insurance policy is typically:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 1 year  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The term is typically 1 year, although for health and two-wheeler policies it may be 2 or 3 years.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — these are longer than the standard 1-year term; 2–3 years is only the exception for health/two-wheeler.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** For which products can the non-life policy term extend to two or three years?\nA) Health and two-wheeler  B) Property and liability  C) Travel and fidelity  D) Keyman and D&O\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook specifically names health and two-wheeler policies as cases where the term may be two or three years.\n- B), C) and D) are wrong — these are not the named exceptions; they follow the typical 1-year term.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The premium payable on a non-life policy is a function of:\nA) Sum insured and the assessed risk  B) Only the sum insured  C) Only the insured's income  D) The deductible alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Premium is a function of the sum insured *and* the assessed risk (e.g. age/gender/health for medical; engine CC, place of registration, vehicle age for motor).\n- B) is wrong — risk also matters, not just the sum insured.\n- C) is wrong — income is not the stated basis.\n- D) is wrong — the deductible reduces claims, it is not the premium basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A \"deductible\" is best defined as:\nA) The bonus for no claims  B) The maximum the insurer pays  C) The premium discount for online purchase  D) The portion of the claim that is met by the insured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A deductible is the portion of the claim that the insured bears themselves.\n- A) is wrong — that is the no-claim bonus.\n- B) is wrong — that is the sum insured.\n- C) is wrong — that is an online-purchase discount, unrelated to deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Under the \"restore\" benefit in health insurance, the sum insured is restored to 100%:\nA) In case of either partial or full exhaustion  B) Only when reduced due to a claim  C) Only at renewal  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In a restoration benefit, the insurer restores the sum insured to 100% in case of *either partial or full* exhaustion.\n- B) is wrong — that describes \"recharge\", which refills only when the sum gets reduced by a claim.\n- C) and D) are wrong — restore happens on exhaustion within the policy period, not only at renewal, and not never.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Under the \"recharge\" benefit, the sum insured is restored to 100%:\nA) On partial or full exhaustion  B) Automatically every month  C) Only if no claim is made  D) Only when it gets reduced due to a claim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Recharge refills the sum insured to 100% *only when it gets reduced due to a claim*.\n- A) is wrong — that is the \"restore\" benefit (partial or full exhaustion).\n- B) is wrong — there is no monthly auto-refill described.\n- C) is wrong — recharge is triggered by a claim reducing the sum, not by the absence of claims.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** \"No claim bonus\" (NCB) refers to:\nA) The benefit of lower premiums in subsequent years for each claim-free year  B) A penalty for claiming  C) The deductible amount  D) The survival period\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — NCB is the benefit of lower premiums enjoyed in subsequent years for each year of no claims; it can also be offered as additional bonus cover.\n- B) is wrong — it is a reward, not a penalty.\n- C) and D) are wrong — those are unrelated terms (deductible and survival period).\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Property insurance generally means insuring:\nA) Only jewellery and antiques  B) Third-party liability only  C) The structure and contents of a building against natural and man-made disasters  D) The owner's life\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Property insurance generally means insuring the structure and the contents of the building against natural and man-made disasters such as fire and theft.\n- A) is wrong — valuables like jewellery and antiques typically need an *add-on or separate* policy.\n- B) is wrong — that is liability/motor cover.\n- D) is wrong — a life is covered under life insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Which of the following is generally NOT covered under property insurance?\nA) Fire  B) Theft  C) Wilful destruction and normal wear and tear  D) Man-made disasters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Wilful destruction of property and loss/damage due to normal wear and tear are generally not covered.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — fire, theft and man-made disasters are exactly what property insurance covers.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Valuables such as jewellery, art and antiques typically require:\nA) No cover at all  B) Only third-party cover  C) An add-on or separate insurance policy  D) A keyman policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — These valuables typically require an add-on or separate insurance policy beyond the standard property cover.\n- A) is wrong — they can be covered, just not by the base policy automatically.\n- B) and D) are wrong — third-party and keyman covers are unrelated to insuring household valuables.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Property insurance is normally taken along with a:\nA) Travel booking  B) Motor policy  C) Critical illness policy  D) Home loan, as the lender insists on it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Such policies are normally taken along with a home loan because the lender insists on the borrower taking it.\n- A), B) and C) are wrong — none of these is the context the workbook gives for property insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Health insurance claims are typically allowed for:\nA) Cosmetic procedures only  B) Routine dental check-ups only  C) Any expense without limit  D) \"In-patient\" and domiciliary treatments per policy terms\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Claims are typically allowed only for \"in-patient\" treatments and domiciliary treatments, according to the terms of the policy (plus day-care for some procedures).\n- A) and B) are wrong — these are not the stated claimable categories.\n- C) is wrong — claims are subject to the sum insured and sub-limits, not unlimited.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** An \"in-patient\" is a patient who:\nA) Is admitted in a hospital requiring at least overnight or 24 hours of stay  B) Is treated only at home  C) Visits the OPD briefly  D) Buys medicines from a pharmacy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An in-patient is one admitted in a hospital for treatment requiring at least overnight or 24 hours of stay.\n- B) is wrong — home treatment is \"domiciliary\".\n- C) and D) are wrong — brief OPD visits and pharmacy purchases are not in-patient hospitalisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** \"Domiciliary treatment\" means:\nA) Treatment at a foreign hospital  B) Day-care surgery  C) Patients treated at home when not in a condition to be moved to hospital  D) Treatment only for accidents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Domiciliary treatment is when patients are treated at home because they are not in a condition to be moved to the hospital.\n- A) is wrong — location abroad is a separate \"global coverage\" issue.\n- B) is wrong — day-care is a distinct category for procedures that need hospitalisation but allow same-day release.\n- D) is wrong — domiciliary is not accident-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Why do many health policies now cover \"day care treatment\"?\nA) Because hospitals charge less  B) Because it is legally compulsory  C) Because advancement of technology lets the insured be released the same day for certain procedures needing hospitalisation  D) Because the term is 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Day-care treatment covers certain procedures which require hospitalisation but, due to advancement of technology, allow the insured to be released the same day.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — none is the reason given in the workbook for day-care cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The \"cashless facility\" in health insurance means:\nA) The insurer pays cash to the insured upfront  B) No premium is charged  C) Claims are paid only after the policy ends  D) Bills are directly settled with the hospital and the insured need not pay upfront, up to the approved sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the cashless facility, bills are settled directly with the hospital and the insured is not required to pay upfront, up to the sum approved for this facility.\n- A) is wrong — the insurer pays the hospital, not cash to the insured.\n- B) and C) are wrong — premiums are still charged and claims are settled during the policy, not after it ends.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A \"family floater\" policy:\nA) Covers multiple family members under the same policy up to the sum insured  B) Covers only the eldest member  C) Is a defined benefit policy  D) Covers only travel risks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A family floater policy covers multiple family members under the same policy up to the sum insured.\n- B) is wrong — it covers multiple members, not just one.\n- C) is wrong — health insurance (including floaters) is an indemnity policy.\n- D) is wrong — it is health cover, not travel cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under health insurance portability, what can be transferred when switching insurers?\nA) The premium already paid  B) The hospital network only  C) Benefits of no-claim, bonus and time-bound exclusions for existing conditions  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Portability lets the benefits of no-claim, bonus and time-bound exclusions for existing conditions be transferred when the insured switches insurer — provided the previous policy was maintained without a break.\n- A) is wrong — paid premium is not transferred.\n- B) is wrong — the network is not what portability transfers.\n- D) is wrong — portability exists precisely so credits are not lost.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which type of motor insurance is COMPULSORY in India?\nA) Own-damage cover  B) Theft cover  C) Third-party liability insurance  D) Zero-depreciation cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Third-party liability insurance is compulsory; no motor vehicle can be used in a public place without it. Insurance against own damage or theft is not compulsory.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — own-damage, theft and zero-depreciation covers are optional.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The car \"No Claim\" bonus can go up to what percentage of the premium?\nA) 10%  B) 25%  C) 50% after 5 claim-free years  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NCB can go up to 50% of the (own-damage) premium where no claims have been made for 5 years.\n- A) and B) are wrong — these understate the maximum.\n- D) is wrong — there is no 100% discount; the cap is 50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The car \"No Claim\" bonus is attached to:\nA) The car  B) The insurer  C) The registration plate  D) The insured person, not the car\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The NCB is available to the insured person and is *not* attached to the car. So a buyer of a used car gets no NCB, while the original owner can transfer the NCB to a new car they buy.\n- A), B) and C) are wrong — the bonus follows the person, not the vehicle, the insurer, or the plate.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Personal accident insurance pays when the insured sustains bodily injury caused by:\nA) Any illness  B) Old age  C) External, violent and visible means, solely and directly from an accident  D) A pre-existing disease\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It pays if the insured sustains bodily injury resulting solely and directly from an accident caused by external, violent and visible means.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — illness, old age and disease are not accidents by external violent visible means.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which disablement type is NOT listed as covered under personal accident insurance?\nA) Permanent total disablement  B) Permanent partial disablement  C) Temporary total disablement  D) Temporary partial disablement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook lists permanent total, permanent partial and temporary total disablement. \"Temporary partial disablement\" is not in the list.\n- A), B) and C) are wrong as answers because they ARE the three listed covered types.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Critical illness insurance pays:\nA) A reimbursement of actual hospital bills  B) A payment on death  C) A monthly pension  D) A lump sum benefit if the insured contracts certain specified diseases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — It provides a lump-sum benefit if the insured contracts specified diseases such as cancer, heart attack, stroke, kidney failure or multiple sclerosis.\n- A) is wrong — that describes indemnity health insurance.\n- B) is wrong — unlike life insurance, there is no payment on death.\n- C) is wrong — it is a lump sum, not a pension.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Critical illness reimbursement is usually subject to a minimum survival period of:\nA) 7 days  B) 15 days  C) 30 days after diagnosis  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Reimbursement is usually subject to a minimum survival period of 30 days after diagnosis of the critical illness.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — 30 days is the figure stated (the comparison table notes a 15–30 day range, with 30 commonly used).\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Overseas travel insurance typically provides cover in the form of:\nA) A lump sum on death only  B) Reimbursement up to the maximum amount mentioned in the policy  C) Unlimited payment  D) A monthly income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Travel insurance provides medical, financial and other assistance during international travel as reimbursement up to the maximum amount mentioned in the policy.\n- A), C) and D) are wrong — it is capped reimbursement, not a death lump sum, unlimited, or income.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Liability insurance does NOT cover:\nA) Damages for negligence under common law  B) Legal defence costs  C) Intentional damage or damages from criminal activities  D) No-fault statutory liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Liability insurance will not cover intentional damage or damages caused due to criminal activities.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — negligence (common law), defence costs, and no-fault statutory liability ARE within the scope of liability cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The most common example of liability insurance is:\nA) Keyman insurance  B) Property insurance  C) Professional indemnity plans taken by doctors, lawyers or investment advisers  D) Travel insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The most common example is professional indemnity plans taken by professionals like Doctors, Lawyers or Investment Advisers.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — these are distinct product categories, not the cited example of liability insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Fidelity insurance covers:\nA) Losses to an employer from an employee's forgery, fraud or dishonesty  B) The employee's medical bills  C) Directors' personal liability  D) Vehicle damage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A fidelity insurance policy covers losses sustained by the employer as a result of an act of forgery, fraud or dishonesty from an employee (loss of money or goods).\n- B) is wrong — that is health insurance for the employee.\n- C) is wrong — that is D&O liability insurance.\n- D) is wrong — that is motor insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Directors & Officers (D&O) liability insurance protects the board/management against:\nA) Loss of company property  B) Employee fraud  C) Personal liability and defence costs from claims alleging a wrongful act in their duties  D) Vehicle theft\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — D&O insurance insures directors, management and supervisory/managerial employees against personal liability and defence costs from claims alleging they committed a wrongful act in the line of their duties.\n- A) is wrong — property loss is property insurance.\n- B) is wrong — employee fraud is fidelity insurance.\n- D) is wrong — vehicle theft is motor insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** In keyman insurance:\nA) The key executive is the beneficiary  B) It is an indemnity health policy  C) The government pays the premium  D) The company buys a life policy on a key executive, is the beneficiary, and pays the premiums\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Keyman insurance is a life insurance policy a company purchases to cover itself against the loss of life of a key executive; the company is the beneficiary and pays the premiums.\n- A) is wrong — the company, not the executive, is the beneficiary.\n- B) is wrong — it is a *life* policy, not indemnity health.\n- C) is wrong — the company pays, not the government.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Critical illness insurance is currently available:\nA) Only as a government scheme  B) Either with life insurance policies or as standalone policies  C) Only as a property add-on  D) Only abroad\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Critical illness cover is currently available either with life insurance policies or as standalone policies.\n- A), C) and D) are wrong — it is not a government scheme, not a property add-on, and not foreign-only.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Liability under liability insurance may arise under:\nA) Only common law (negligence)  B) Only statutory law  C) Common law on the basis of negligence OR statutory law on a no-fault basis  D) Neither; only contracts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Legal liability may arise under common law on the basis of negligence, or under statutory law on a no-fault basis (i.e. even when there is no negligence).\n- A) and B) are wrong — they capture only half the picture.\n- D) is wrong — both common law and statutory bases are explicitly stated.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Ravi holds two indemnity health policies of Rs. 5 lakh each. What is the most his hospital claim can total across both?\nA) Rs. 10 lakh, but the total claim cannot exceed the actual loss  B) Rs. 20 lakh  C) Unlimited  D) Rs. 5 lakh only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Indemnity policies reimburse *actual loss*; the total claim cannot exceed the sum insured of all policies put together (Rs. 10 lakh), and never more than the actual expense incurred. This is why multiple indemnity policies \"make very little sense\".\n- B) is wrong — Rs. 20 lakh exceeds the combined sum insured.\n- C) is wrong — indemnity is capped by actual loss and combined sum insured.\n- D) is wrong — the combined cover is Rs. 10 lakh, not Rs. 5 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Meena has cancer cover under three separate critical illness (defined benefit) policies. On a valid claim:\nA) Only the largest pays  B) They are pro-rated to actual expense  C) All of them will pay irrespective of how many she holds  D) Only the employer's policy pays\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For multiple *defined benefit* policies (life, critical illness, accidental death/disability), all of them pay irrespective of how many the insured holds, because they pay a fixed sum, not actual expenses.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — those describe indemnity-style contribution, which does not apply to defined benefit policies.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An employee has an employer health policy and an independent personal health policy. The workbook advises that when claiming, she should normally:\nA) Claim from her own policy first  B) Claim from the employer policy first  C) Split equally always  D) Not claim at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She should normally choose the employer policy first. The chosen insurer cannot apply the contribution clause if the claim is less than that policy's sum insured.\n- A) is wrong — the employer policy is the suggested first choice.\n- C) is wrong — equal splitting is not the rule; contribution applies only if the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured.\n- D) is wrong — she is entitled to claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** When does the contribution clause become applicable for multiple health policies?\nA) Always, in every claim  B) Never, for health  C) Only for defined benefit policies  D) Only where the claim amount exceeds the sum insured of the chosen policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Contribution is not applicable except where the sum insured of the chosen policy is lower than the claim amount; then the claim is settled across insurers by applying contribution.\n- A) is wrong — it does not apply if the claim is within the chosen policy's sum insured.\n- B) is wrong — it can apply to health when the chosen policy is insufficient.\n- C) is wrong — defined benefit policies all pay separately; contribution is an indemnity concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** The KEY difference between a Top-up and a Super Top-up plan is:\nA) In a Top-up the threshold applies to every individual claim; in a Super Top-up it applies on the total of all yearly claims  B) Top-up is cheaper always  C) Super Top-up has no deductible  D) Top-up covers death\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In a Top-up plan the threshold (deductible) is applied for *every claim*, whereas in a Super Top-up plan the threshold is applied on the *total of all hospitalisation claims for the year* (aggregate).\n- B) is wrong — relative price is not the defining difference.\n- C) is wrong — both have a threshold/deductible.\n- D) is wrong — neither is a death-benefit product.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Why is a Super Top-up generally more generous than a Top-up?\nA) It has a higher premium  B) It covers wear and tear  C) Its threshold is crossed only once per year on the aggregate, so smaller individual claims after that are payable  D) It pays on death\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Because the threshold applies to the aggregate, once the year's total claims cross it, subsequent claims (even small ones) are payable. In a Top-up, each small claim below the per-claim threshold pays nothing.\n- A) is wrong — higher premium is not why it is more generous.\n- B) and D) are wrong — these are not health-cover features.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A base policy of Rs. 5 lakh + super top-up of Rs. 10 lakh with a Rs. 5 lakh deductible gives total coverage of:\nA) Rs. 10 lakh  B) Rs. 5 lakh  C) Rs. 15 lakh  D) Rs. 20 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Base (Rs. 5 lakh) covers up to the Rs. 5 lakh deductible level; the super top-up (Rs. 10 lakh) covers above that — total Rs. 15 lakh of cover at an economical premium.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — Rs. 5 + Rs. 10 = Rs. 15 lakh of combined coverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Why is the super top-up strategy economical compared with a single large policy?\nA) The super top-up premium is quite low, so base + super top-up is only slightly cheaper than one big policy  B) The single policy is always far cheaper  C) Super top-ups are free  D) Base policies have no premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The super top-up premium is quite low. A single Rs. 15 lakh policy costs only slightly more than base + super top-up; the small difference reflects the admin simplicity of one policy.\n- B) is wrong — the single policy is *only slightly* more, not far cheaper.\n- C) and D) are wrong — premiums are charged on both.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** For an employee whose employer provides Rs. 5 lakh cover, the IDEAL way to add cover is:\nA) A super top-up with the deductible set equal to the employer cover  B) A second full indemnity policy of Rs. 5 lakh  C) A keyman policy  D) A fidelity policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Super top-up plans are ideally suitable for employees seeking cover above their employer's; the employee sets the deductible equal to the employer-provided amount, and the super top-up covers claims above it.\n- B) is wrong — duplicating indemnity cover makes little sense.\n- C) and D) are wrong — keyman and fidelity are unrelated to topping up personal health cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Why is it advisable to keep the base plan and the top-up plan with the same insurer?\nA) It is legally required  B) Because premiums must match  C) To avoid dealing with two companies when a claim exceeds the threshold  D) To double the sum insured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If the base and top-up are from different insurers, the insured must deal with both when a claim exceeds the threshold; keeping them with one insurer simplifies claims.\n- A) is wrong — it is advisable, not legally mandated.\n- B) and D) are wrong — neither is the stated reason.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** For a super top-up where two insurers are involved, an additional requirement when a claim is below the threshold is:\nA) Nothing needs to be done  B) The base insurer must refund premium  C) The policy lapses  D) The super top-up insurer must still be informed so it can mark the threshold levels used\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Even when no claim is payable on the super top-up, the company handling it must be informed so it can mark the threshold (used) levels, since the threshold applies to aggregate claims.\n- A) is wrong — informing is required to track the aggregate.\n- B) and C) are wrong — no refund or lapse occurs.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A health insurance policy is classified as which type of policy?\nA) Defined benefit  B) Indemnity  C) Keyman  D) Pure protection\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Health insurance is an indemnity policy — it reimburses actual hospitalisation expenditure incurred.\n- A) is wrong — defined benefit pays a fixed lump sum; that is critical illness.\n- C) and D) are wrong — these are unrelated categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A critical illness policy is classified as which type of policy?\nA) Defined benefit  B) Indemnity  C) Liability  D) Fidelity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Critical illness is a defined benefit policy — it pays a fixed lump sum on contracting a specified disease (and surviving the required period).\n- B) is wrong — indemnity reimburses actual expense (health insurance).\n- C) and D) are wrong — those are different product lines.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Sita is diagnosed with a covered critical illness but passes away on day 20. Why may the claim fail?\nA) The policy covers death  B) The premium was too low  C) She did not meet the minimum survival period (about 30 days) after diagnosis  D) Critical illness is an indemnity policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Critical illness reimbursement is usually subject to a minimum survival period (commonly 30 days, table notes 15–30) after diagnosis; surviving only 20 days may fail this condition.\n- A) is wrong — there is *no* payment on death under critical illness.\n- B) is wrong — premium level does not determine the survival condition.\n- D) is wrong — it is a defined benefit policy.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which policy is best suited to replace lost income if a breadwinner gets cancer and cannot work?\nA) A small critical illness rider  B) Property insurance  C) Fidelity insurance  D) A critical illness cover sized like life insurance (income-replacement)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook stresses critical illness should be sized like life cover for income replacement; large covers usually come as standalone or as an accelerated sum-insured rider on a life policy.\n- A) is wrong — a small rider gives only a token amount, the common mistake.\n- B) and C) are wrong — they do not replace income on illness.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Anil sells his old car (which had 50% NCB) and buys a new one. Regarding the NCB:\nA) The buyer of the old car inherits the 50% NCB  B) Anil can transfer the NCB to his new car; the buyer of the old car gets none  C) The NCB is lost entirely  D) The NCB stays with the old car\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NCB belongs to the *person*, not the car. The original owner can transfer NCB to a new car; the buyer of the used car gets no NCB.\n- A) and D) are wrong — NCB is not attached to the car.\n- C) is wrong — Anil keeps and transfers it.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Priya shifts her car insurance to a new insurer at renewal with a clean claim record. The new insurer:\nA) Cannot give any NCB  B) Only gives NCB after 10 years  C) Doubles her premium  D) Provides the NCB on shifting at renewal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A new insurance company provides the \"no claim\" bonus if a policy is shifted at the time of renewal.\n- A) is wrong — NCB is portable to the new insurer at renewal.\n- B) and C) are wrong — neither is supported by the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A hospital bill is incurred by an Indian resident in London under an ordinary Indian health (indemnity) policy. It will normally:\nA) Be fully paid  B) Be paid at double rate  C) Not be covered, as indemnity health policies normally cover expenses in India only  D) Be paid only if it is a defined benefit policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indemnity health policies normally cover expenses incurred in India only, because verifying losses abroad is difficult.\n- A) and B) are wrong — foreign expenses are generally not covered by an ordinary Indian health policy.\n- D) is misleading — health *is* indemnity, not defined benefit; defined benefit policies are the ones that pay anywhere.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which policy is the EXCEPTION that, although indemnity, can cover expenses incurred abroad?\nA) Ordinary health insurance  B) Property insurance  C) Overseas travel insurance, designed country-by-country  D) Fidelity insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Overseas travel policies are indemnity policies designed specifically for each country, with arrangements to ascertain foreign expenses — the exception to the \"India only\" rule.\n- A) is wrong — ordinary health covers India only.\n- B) and D) are wrong — neither is the cited exception.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A life insurance death claim arising abroad will:\nA) Be paid wherever the covered risk occurs, as it is a defined benefit policy  B) Not be paid since it occurred overseas  C) Be paid only in India  D) Be pro-rated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Defined benefit policies (life, critical illness, accidental death) pay wherever the covered risk occurs, because the event is easy to verify anywhere.\n- B) is wrong — location does not bar payment for defined benefit.\n- C) and D) are wrong — there is no India-only or pro-rata restriction for defined benefit events.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** An Indian resident wants to buy a foreign insurer's policy. They may use the Liberalised Remittance Scheme up to:\nA) USD 25,000 per annum  B) USD 1,00,000 per annum  C) USD 2,50,000 per annum  D) USD 5,00,000 per annum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indian residents can use their LRS entitlement of up to USD 2,50,000 per annum to buy insurance policies of foreign companies.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — the stated LRS limit is USD 2,50,000 per annum.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A caution about buying a foreign insurer's policy under LRS is that:\nA) It is illegal  B) The policy and grievance redressal are governed by the regulations of the country where issued  C) It cannot cover students  D) It is always cheaper than Indian policies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Such policies and the grievance redressal mechanism are governed by the regulations of the country where they are issued, so caution must be exercised.\n- A) is wrong — it is permitted within LRS.\n- C) is wrong — students commonly buy university-recommended foreign policies.\n- D) is wrong — other things equal, *Indian* policies tend to be cheaper.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** For students going overseas, other things being equal:\nA) Indian policies tend to be cheaper than similar foreign-company policies  B) Foreign company policies are cheaper  C) No insurance is ever needed  D) Only defined benefit policies work\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Other things being equal, Indian policies tend to be cheaper than similar policies issued by foreign companies.\n- B) is wrong — the reverse is stated.\n- C) is wrong — cover is advisable as overseas treatment is costly.\n- D) is wrong — this is not a defined-benefit-only matter.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A bank manager wants to protect the bank from a cashier embezzling money. The right policy is:\nA) D&O liability  B) Keyman insurance  C) Fidelity insurance  D) Property insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Fidelity insurance covers employer losses from an employee's forgery, fraud or dishonesty — taken where employees handle large sums of cash or valuables.\n- A) is wrong — D&O covers directors' personal liability, not employee theft.\n- B) is wrong — keyman covers loss of a key person's life.\n- D) is wrong — property insurance covers assets, not employee dishonesty.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A company wants protection against financial loss if its star CEO dies suddenly. The right policy is:\nA) Critical illness  B) Fidelity insurance  C) Personal accident  D) Keyman insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Keyman insurance is a life policy a company buys on a key executive; the company is the beneficiary and pays the premium, protecting against the loss of that person's life.\n- A), B) and C) are wrong — these do not insure the company against the death of its key person with the company as beneficiary.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A doctor wants cover against patient lawsuits alleging professional negligence. The right policy is:\nA) Liability (professional indemnity) insurance  B) Property insurance  C) Travel insurance  D) Fidelity insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Professional indemnity (a form of liability insurance) covers professionals like doctors against legal expenses and damages for which they are found liable.\n- B), C) and D) are wrong — none addresses professional negligence claims.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A company director faces a shareholder claim alleging a wrongful act in his duties. The relevant policy is:\nA) Keyman  B) Fidelity  C) D&O liability insurance  D) Property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — D&O liability insurance covers directors against personal liability and defence costs from claims (e.g. shareholder claims) alleging a wrongful act in their duties.\n- A) is wrong — keyman insures the company on the director's life.\n- B) is wrong — fidelity is employee dishonesty.\n- D) is wrong — property covers assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Comparing Health vs Critical Illness on \"claim paid\": health pays on incurring hospitalisation expenditure, while critical illness pays:\nA) On death  B) Monthly regardless of illness  C) On contracting a pre-specified disease and surviving the required period  D) On retirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Critical illness pays on contracting a pre-specified disease/illness and surviving the required period (15–30 days).\n- A) is wrong — no death payout.\n- B) and D) are wrong — it is not a periodic or retirement benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Why do many insurers charge lower premiums for policies bought online from their websites?\nA) Online policies cover less  B) Lower distribution cost; some even offer 'web only' policies  C) It is mandated by law  D) Online policies have no claims\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Many insurers charge lower premiums for policies bought online directly from their websites, and some offer 'web only' policies.\n- A) is wrong — online does not mean less cover.\n- C) is wrong — it is a commercial practice, not a legal mandate.\n- D) is wrong — online policies can still have claims.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** The role of insurance web aggregators / online brokers is mainly to:\nA) Settle all claims directly  B) Set premium rates for insurers  C) Replace IRDAI  D) Provide convenient online comparison of policies for informed choice, and act as a major selling channel\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Web aggregators / online brokers provide convenient online comparison enabling informed choice and have become major sources of selling policies.\n- A) is wrong — claim settlement is the insurer's job.\n- B) and C) are wrong — they neither set rates nor replace the regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** In health insurance, the premium per additional lakh of coverage as the sum insured rises:\nA) Increases sharply  B) Stays exactly constant  C) Doubles each lakh  D) Drops significantly and becomes almost negligible after a certain amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The premium rate per additional lakh of coverage drops significantly as coverage increases and becomes almost negligible after a certain amount (e.g. a Rs. 100 lakh policy may cost only a few thousand more than Rs. 50 lakh).\n- A), B) and C) are wrong — the marginal premium falls, it does not rise, stay flat, or double.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A pre-existing illness may be excluded from cover for a fixed period:\nA) Always permanently  B) Only for travel policies  C) When insurance is taken for the first time, or if renewed after a lapse  D) Only for defined benefit policies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Pre-existing illnesses may be excluded for a fixed period when insurance is taken for the first time, or if renewed after a lapse.\n- A) is wrong — the exclusion is for a fixed period, not always permanent.\n- B) and D) are wrong — this is a health (indemnity) feature, not travel- or defined-benefit-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** To benefit from health policy portability, the previous policy must have been:\nA) From a foreign insurer  B) Surrendered before switching  C) Maintained without a break  D) A defined benefit policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — To benefit from portability, the previous policy should have been maintained without a break.\n- A), B) and D) are wrong — none is a portability condition; a break in coverage forfeits the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** The own-damage portion of car insurance premium:\nA) Is fixed by law for all insurers  B) Is not fixed and varies from insurer to insurer, so online comparison can save money  C) Is the same as third-party premium  D) Cannot be compared online\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The own-damage portion is not fixed and varies from insurer to insurer; an online comparison from a broker or web aggregator can save substantial amounts.\n- A) is wrong — only third-party rates are regulated/uniform; own-damage varies.\n- C) is wrong — it is a separate component.\n- D) is wrong — it can and should be compared online.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Motor insurance indemnifies the insured for accidents arising from vehicle use:\nA) Anywhere in India, including third-party death/injury and damage to others' property  B) Worldwide  C) Only at the registered address  D) Only on highways\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The insurer indemnifies the insured for accidents arising out of the use of the vehicle anywhere in India, covering (i) death/bodily injury to any person and (ii) damage to others' property.\n- B) is wrong — the cover stated is anywhere in *India*, not worldwide.\n- C) and D) are wrong — it is not limited to the registered address or highways.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n> Unless stated otherwise, the setup is the workbook's standard example: **Base health plan = Rs. 5 lakh**; **Top-up / Super Top-up = Rs. 10 lakh** above a **threshold (deductible) of Rs. 5 lakh**.\n\n**Q71.** Single claim of Rs. 8 lakh under the **Top-up** case. How much is paid in total?\nA) Rs. 5 lakh  B) Rs. 8 lakh  C) Rs. 3 lakh  D) Rs. 10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Logic: the Base plan pays first up to its Rs. 5 lakh limit, then the Top-up pays the excess above the Rs. 5 lakh threshold.\nFormula: Total paid = Base payment + Top-up payment, where Top-up payment = Claim − Threshold (if claim > threshold).\nCalculation: Base pays Rs. 5 lakh (exhausted). Top-up pays Rs. 8 − Rs. 5 = Rs. 3 lakh. Total = 5 + 3 = **Rs. 8 lakh**. (Top-up balance left = 10 − 3 = Rs. 7 lakh.)\n- A) and C) are partial amounts (only one plan).\n- D) is wrong — that is the top-up's full limit, not the amount paid.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Same Rs. 8 lakh single claim under the **Super Top-up** case. Total paid?\nA) Rs. 3 lakh  B) Rs. 5 lakh  C) Rs. 8 lakh  D) Rs. 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a *single* claim, Top-up and Super Top-up behave identically because the threshold is crossed once anyway. Base pays Rs. 5 lakh; Super Top-up pays Rs. 8 − Rs. 5 = Rs. 3 lakh. Total = **Rs. 8 lakh** (Super Top-up balance left = Rs. 7 lakh).\n- A) and B) are single-plan amounts.\n- D) is wrong — the claim exceeds the threshold, so it is payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Multiple claims under the **Top-up** case: 1st claim Rs. 3 lakh. What is paid, and what is left in the Base plan?\nA) Rs. 3 lakh paid from Base; Base left Rs. 2 lakh  B) Rs. 3 lakh from Top-up; Base full  C) Nothing paid  D) Rs. 5 lakh from Base\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The Base plan (Rs. 5 lakh) absorbs the first claim. Calculation: Base pays Rs. 3 lakh; Base limit left = 5 − 3 = **Rs. 2 lakh**. The Top-up's per-claim threshold (Rs. 5 lakh) is not crossed by this Rs. 3 lakh claim, so the Top-up pays nothing yet.\n- B) is wrong — the Base pays first, not the Top-up.\n- C) is wrong — the Base does pay.\n- D) is wrong — only Rs. 3 lakh (the claim) is paid, not Rs. 5 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Continuing the **Top-up** case (Base left Rs. 2 lakh), 2nd claim Rs. 6 lakh. What does the Top-up pay?\nA) Rs. 6 lakh  B) Rs. 1 lakh  C) Rs. 0  D) Rs. 4 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Step 1: Base pays its remaining Rs. 2 lakh (now exhausted). Step 2: For the Top-up, the per-claim threshold of Rs. 5 lakh applies to THIS claim of Rs. 6 lakh → Top-up pays Rs. 6 − Rs. 5 = **Rs. 1 lakh**. (Top-up balance left = 10 − 1 = Rs. 9 lakh.)\n- A) is wrong — it ignores both the Base payment and the threshold.\n- C) is wrong — the claim does exceed the per-claim threshold.\n- D) is wrong — that miscomputes the threshold subtraction.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Continuing the **Top-up** case, 3rd claim Rs. 2 lakh (Base exhausted). What is paid?\nA) Rs. 2 lakh from Top-up  B) Rs. 2 lakh from Base  C) Rs. 5 lakh  D) Nothing — claim is below the Rs. 5 lakh per-claim threshold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Base is exhausted. For the Top-up, each claim must individually exceed the Rs. 5 lakh threshold. Rs. 2 lakh < Rs. 5 lakh, so **nothing** is payable; Top-up limit stays at Rs. 9 lakh.\n- A) is wrong — the per-claim threshold is not crossed.\n- B) is wrong — the Base has no limit left.\n- C) is wrong — no such amount is payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Continuing the **Top-up** case, 4th claim Rs. 4 lakh (Base exhausted). What is paid?\nA) Rs. 4 lakh  B) Nothing — below the Rs. 5 lakh per-claim threshold  C) Rs. 1 lakh  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Again, the Top-up applies its Rs. 5 lakh threshold to each individual claim. Rs. 4 lakh < Rs. 5 lakh → **nothing** payable; Top-up limit stays at Rs. 9 lakh.\n- A) and C) are wrong — no payment as the per-claim threshold is not met.\n- D) is wrong — no such payout.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Across all four Top-up claims (Rs. 3 + 6 + 2 + 4 = Rs. 15 lakh), total amount actually PAID is:\nA) Rs. 15 lakh  B) Rs. 6 lakh  C) Rs. 10 lakh  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sum the payouts: Base Rs. 3 + Rs. 2 = Rs. 5 lakh; Top-up Rs. 1 lakh; claims 3 and 4 paid nothing. Total = 5 + 1 = **Rs. 6 lakh**. This shows how a Top-up's per-claim threshold leaves small claims unpaid.\n- A) is wrong — that is total *claims*, not amount paid.\n- C) and D) are single-component figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Now the **Super Top-up** case, multiple claims: 1st claim Rs. 3 lakh. What happens, and what aggregate threshold has been applied?\nA) Base pays Rs. 3 lakh; Base left Rs. 2 lakh; aggregate threshold applied = Rs. 3 lakh  B) Super Top-up pays Rs. 3 lakh  C) Nothing happens  D) Rs. 5 lakh threshold fully applied\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Base pays the Rs. 3 lakh claim; Base left = 5 − 3 = Rs. 2 lakh. Because Super Top-up counts the threshold on the *aggregate*, the Rs. 3 lakh that fell within the deductible zone counts toward the threshold: aggregate threshold applied so far = **Rs. 3 lakh** (balance threshold to apply = Rs. 5 − Rs. 3 = Rs. 2 lakh).\n- B) is wrong — the Super Top-up pays nothing yet; the Base pays.\n- C) is wrong — the Base does pay and the threshold counter advances.\n- D) is wrong — only Rs. 3 lakh of the Rs. 5 lakh threshold has been used.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** **Super Top-up** case, 2nd claim Rs. 6 lakh (Base had Rs. 2 lakh left; aggregate threshold used = Rs. 3 lakh). How much does the Super Top-up pay?\nA) Rs. 1 lakh  B) Rs. 6 lakh  C) Rs. 4 lakh  D) Rs. 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Step 1: Base pays its last Rs. 2 lakh (exhausted). Step 2: Balance threshold still to apply = Rs. 5 − Rs. 3 (already used) = Rs. 2 lakh; this is applied to the remaining part of the claim. Of the Rs. 6 lakh claim, Rs. 2 lakh went to Base, the next Rs. 2 lakh completes the threshold, leaving Rs. 6 − 2 − 2 = **Rs. 4 lakh** paid by the Super Top-up. (Super Top-up left = 10 − 4 = Rs. 6 lakh; aggregate threshold now Rs. 5 lakh.)\n- A) is the Top-up answer (per-claim threshold), not Super Top-up.\n- B) and D) miscompute the threshold logic.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** **Super Top-up** case, 3rd claim Rs. 2 lakh (aggregate threshold of Rs. 5 lakh already fully applied; Base exhausted). What is paid?\nA) Nothing  B) Rs. 2 lakh from the Super Top-up; balance left Rs. 4 lakh  C) Rs. 2 lakh from Base  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the full Rs. 5 lakh aggregate threshold has already been met, every later claim is payable in full from the Super Top-up. So Rs. 2 lakh is paid; Super Top-up balance = 6 − 2 = **Rs. 4 lakh**.\n- A) is the Top-up outcome (small claim below per-claim threshold) — not Super Top-up.\n- C) is wrong — Base is exhausted.\n- D) is wrong — only the Rs. 2 lakh claim is paid.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** **Super Top-up** case, 4th claim Rs. 4 lakh (threshold already met; Base exhausted; Super Top-up had Rs. 4 lakh left). What is paid?\nA) Rs. 4 lakh from the Super Top-up; balance left Nil  B) Nothing  C) Rs. 4 lakh from Base  D) Rs. 6 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Threshold already applied, so the full Rs. 4 lakh is paid from the Super Top-up; balance left = 4 − 4 = **Nil**.\n- B) is the Top-up outcome, not Super Top-up.\n- C) is wrong — Base is exhausted.\n- D) is wrong — only Rs. 4 lakh is payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Across the same four claims (Rs. 15 lakh total) under the **Super Top-up**, total PAID is:\nA) Rs. 6 lakh  B) Rs. 10 lakh  C) Rs. 5 lakh  D) Rs. 15 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Base Rs. 5 lakh (Rs. 3 + Rs. 2) + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh (Rs. 4 + Rs. 2 + Rs. 4) = **Rs. 15 lakh**. The aggregate threshold was crossed only once, so all claims got paid.\n- A) is the Top-up total — the contrast the workbook highlights.\n- B) and C) are single components.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** On Rs. 15 lakh of claims, the Top-up paid Rs. 6 lakh and the Super Top-up paid Rs. 15 lakh. The extra amount the Super Top-up paid is:\nA) Rs. 6 lakh  B) Rs. 9 lakh  C) Rs. 15 lakh  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Difference = Super Top-up paid − Top-up paid = Rs. 15 lakh − Rs. 6 lakh = **Rs. 9 lakh**. This quantifies why the aggregate-threshold design is more generous.\n- A), C) and D) are the individual totals, not the difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A claim of Rs. 12 lakh hits a Base Rs. 5 lakh + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh (Rs. 5 lakh threshold) in a single event. Total paid?\nA) Rs. 10 lakh  B) Rs. 12 lakh  C) Rs. 5 lakh  D) Rs. 7 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Base pays Rs. 5 lakh; the Super Top-up pays the excess above the Rs. 5 lakh threshold = Rs. 12 − Rs. 5 = Rs. 7 lakh (within its Rs. 10 lakh limit). Total = 5 + 7 = **Rs. 12 lakh** (whole claim covered).\n- A) is the top-up limit, not the payout.\n- C) is just the Base.\n- D) is only the top-up portion.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A claim of Rs. 18 lakh hits the same Base Rs. 5 lakh + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh (Rs. 5 lakh threshold). What is the maximum payable and what does the insured bear?\nA) Pay Rs. 18 lakh; insured bears Rs. 0  B) Pay Rs. 10 lakh; insured bears Rs. 8 lakh  C) Pay Rs. 15 lakh; insured bears Rs. 3 lakh  D) Pay Rs. 13 lakh; insured bears Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Base pays Rs. 5 lakh. Super Top-up pays the excess above the Rs. 5 lakh threshold but is capped at its Rs. 10 lakh limit → pays Rs. 10 lakh. Total cover = 5 + 10 = Rs. 15 lakh. Claim Rs. 18 − Rs. 15 = **Rs. 3 lakh** borne by the insured.\n- A) is wrong — total cover is only Rs. 15 lakh.\n- B) and D) miscompute the Base + Super Top-up combination.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A health policy has a room-rent sub-limit. The bill is Rs. 2 lakh with room rent inside the sub-limit, but the policy applies a 10% co-pay. How much does the insured pay (co-pay only)?\nA) Rs. 10,000  B) Rs. 20,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Rs. 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A co-pay is the percentage of the claim the insured bears. Formula: Co-pay amount = Co-pay % × eligible claim = 10% × Rs. 2,00,000 = **Rs. 20,000**. The insurer pays the remaining Rs. 1,80,000.\n- A) is 5%, not 10%.\n- C) is the whole bill.\n- D) is wrong — a co-pay always leaves a share with the insured.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A policy has a deductible of Rs. 25,000 and a 20% co-pay on the balance. On a Rs. 1,25,000 claim, how much does the insurer pay?\nA) Rs. 80,000  B) Rs. 1,00,000  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Step 1: subtract the deductible (insured pays first): Rs. 1,25,000 − Rs. 25,000 = Rs. 1,00,000 remains. Step 2: apply the 20% co-pay on the balance: insured pays 20% × Rs. 1,00,000 = Rs. 20,000, so insurer pays 80% × Rs. 1,00,000 = **Rs. 80,000**.\n- B) ignores the co-pay.\n- C) confuses the order/percentages.\n- D) ignores both deductible and co-pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A car's own-damage premium is Rs. 12,000. After 5 claim-free years the maximum NCB applies. What is the discounted own-damage premium?\nA) Rs. 6,000  B) Rs. 9,000  C) Rs. 3,000  D) Rs. 12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Maximum NCB = 50% after 5 claim-free years. Formula: Discounted premium = Premium × (1 − NCB%) = Rs. 12,000 × (1 − 0.50) = **Rs. 6,000**. NCB applies only to the own-damage portion.\n- B) is a 25% discount.\n- C) is a 75% discount (above the 50% cap).\n- D) is wrong — it ignores the NCB.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A health premium for Rs. 50 lakh cover is Rs. 22,000; the workbook notes a Rs. 100 lakh policy may cost only a few single-digit thousand rupees more. Which estimated Rs. 100 lakh premium is MOST consistent with this?\nA) Rs. 44,000  B) Rs. 27,000  C) Rs. 70,000  D) Rs. 1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The premium per extra lakh drops sharply; doubling the cover adds only \"a few single-digit thousand rupees\". Rs. 22,000 + about Rs. 5,000 ≈ **Rs. 27,000** fits.\n- A) wrongly assumes premium doubles with cover.\n- C) and D) overstate the increase, contradicting the \"negligible marginal premium\" point.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Three siblings each independently hold a Rs. 50 lakh life insurance policy on their own life. On a valid death claim of one sibling, the total payout from these defined benefit policies is:\nA) Rs. 50 lakh, split three ways  B) Rs. 1.5 crore from all three  C) Nothing, due to contribution  D) Rs. 50 lakh from that sibling's policy (each defined benefit policy pays its full sum)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Each policy is on a *separate* life; only the deceased sibling's policy is triggered, paying its full Rs. 50 lakh. Defined benefit policies pay the agreed sum without contribution.\n- A) is wrong — no splitting for defined benefit.\n- B) is wrong — the other two siblings' policies are not triggered (they are alive).\n- C) is wrong — contribution does not apply to defined benefit policies.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An employee has employer health cover of Rs. 4 lakh and an own policy of Rs. 10 lakh. A hospital claim is Rs. 3 lakh. Following the workbook's guidance, what happens?\nA) Split between both insurers via contribution  B) Claim from employer policy first; since Rs. 3 lakh < Rs. 4 lakh sum insured, no contribution applies  C) Claim only from the own policy  D) Nothing is payable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choose the employer policy first. As the Rs. 3 lakh claim is less than the chosen policy's Rs. 4 lakh sum insured, the insurer has no option to apply the contribution clause.\n- A) is wrong — contribution applies only if the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured.\n- C) is allowed but not the advised first choice.\n- D) is wrong — the claim is payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Same employee (employer Rs. 4 lakh + own Rs. 10 lakh). Now the claim is Rs. 7 lakh. Following the guidance, what is the consequence?\nA) Employer policy alone pays Rs. 7 lakh  B) Nothing is payable  C) Since Rs. 7 lakh exceeds the employer's Rs. 4 lakh sum insured, the insured uses another policy and contribution provisions apply  D) Own policy pays and employer refunds premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the claim (Rs. 7 lakh) exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured (Rs. 4 lakh), the insured can claim under another policy and the claim is settled by applying contribution provisions across the insurers.\n- A) is wrong — the employer policy cannot pay beyond its Rs. 4 lakh sum insured.\n- B) is wrong — the claim is payable, just across policies.\n- D) is wrong — no premium refund mechanism applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An employee gets Rs. 5 lakh employer cover and buys a super top-up of Rs. 7 lakh with a Rs. 5 lakh deductible (matching the employer cover). A single hospitalisation costs Rs. 9 lakh. What does the super top-up pay?\nA) Rs. 9 lakh  B) Rs. 4 lakh  C) Rs. 5 lakh  D) Rs. 7 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The employer cover absorbs the first Rs. 5 lakh (also satisfying the Rs. 5 lakh deductible). The super top-up pays the excess above the Rs. 5 lakh threshold = Rs. 9 − Rs. 5 = **Rs. 4 lakh** (within its Rs. 7 lakh limit).\n- A) is the full claim, ignoring the deductible.\n- C) is the employer portion.\n- D) is the super top-up's limit, not the payout.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Continuing Q93's setup (employer Rs. 5 lakh + super top-up Rs. 7 lakh over Rs. 5 lakh deductible), if total claims for the year reach Rs. 13 lakh, what is the maximum total coverage available?\nA) Rs. 13 lakh  B) Rs. 12 lakh; insured bears Rs. 1 lakh  C) Rs. 7 lakh  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum cover = employer Rs. 5 lakh + super top-up Rs. 7 lakh = Rs. 12 lakh. Of Rs. 13 lakh claims, Rs. 12 lakh is covered and the insured bears Rs. 13 − Rs. 12 = **Rs. 1 lakh**.\n- A) is wrong — total cover caps at Rs. 12 lakh.\n- C) and D) are single components.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A student remits money to buy a foreign insurer's policy for USD 60,000 and later another for USD 2,10,000 in the same year under LRS. Is this permissible within the LRS cap?\nA) No — total USD 2,70,000 exceeds the USD 2,50,000 per annum limit  B) Yes — no limit applies  C) Yes — the limit is USD 5,00,000  D) No — foreign policies are banned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The LRS limit is USD 2,50,000 per annum. Total remitted = 60,000 + 2,10,000 = USD 2,70,000, which **exceeds** the cap.\n- B) is wrong — there is a cap.\n- C) is wrong — the cap is USD 2,50,000, not 5,00,000.\n- D) is wrong — foreign policies are permitted within the LRS limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A Top-up plan (Rs. 5 lakh threshold per claim) receives, during the year, four separate claims of Rs. 4 lakh each (total Rs. 16 lakh), and there is no base plan. How much does the Top-up pay?\nA) Rs. 16 lakh  B) Rs. 11 lakh  C) Rs. 0  D) Rs. 4 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In a Top-up, the threshold applies to each claim. Each Rs. 4 lakh claim is below the Rs. 5 lakh per-claim threshold, so each pays **nothing** → total = **Rs. 0**. This dramatically shows the weakness of a Top-up for several moderate claims.\n- A) and D) ignore the per-claim threshold.\n- B) wrongly treats the threshold as a one-time aggregate (that would be a Super Top-up).\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Same four Rs. 4 lakh claims (Rs. 16 lakh total), but under a **Super Top-up** of Rs. 12 lakh with a Rs. 5 lakh aggregate threshold (no base plan). How much is paid?\nA) Rs. 0  B) Rs. 16 lakh  C) Rs. 11 lakh  D) Rs. 12 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The threshold applies to the *aggregate*. The insured first absorbs Rs. 5 lakh of total claims; the Super Top-up pays the rest up to its limit. Eligible above threshold = Rs. 16 − Rs. 5 = Rs. 11 lakh, which is within the Rs. 12 lakh limit → **Rs. 11 lakh** paid.\n- A) is the Top-up outcome, not Super Top-up.\n- B) ignores the Rs. 5 lakh aggregate deductible.\n- D) is the plan limit, not the amount payable here.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A personal accident policy pays Rs. 50 lakh for permanent total disablement (PTD) and 50% of that for a specified permanent partial disablement (PPD). The insured suffers that PPD. What is the payout?\nA) Rs. 50 lakh  B) Rs. 25 lakh  C) Rs. 0  D) Rs. 75 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PPD pays a defined fraction of the PTD sum. Formula: Payout = fraction × PTD sum = 50% × Rs. 50 lakh = **Rs. 25 lakh**.\n- A) is the full PTD amount (a more severe category).\n- C) is wrong — PPD is a covered category.\n- D) exceeds the sum insured.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Two claims hit a Base Rs. 5 lakh + Super Top-up Rs. 10 lakh (Rs. 5 lakh aggregate threshold): first Rs. 4 lakh, then Rs. 9 lakh. What does the Super Top-up pay on the second claim?\nA) Rs. 9 lakh  B) Rs. 8 lakh  C) Rs. 4 lakh  D) Rs. 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — First claim: Base pays Rs. 4 lakh (Base left Rs. 1 lakh; aggregate threshold used Rs. 4 lakh). Second claim Rs. 9 lakh: Base pays its last Rs. 1 lakh (exhausted); balance threshold = Rs. 5 − Rs. 4 = Rs. 1 lakh applied to the claim; Super Top-up pays Rs. 9 − 1 (to Base) − 1 (threshold) = **Rs. 7 lakh**... let us recheck: of the Rs. 9 lakh, Rs. 1 lakh goes to Base, the next Rs. 1 lakh completes the threshold, leaving Rs. 7 lakh — but the answer key states Rs. 8 lakh, so recompute: aggregate threshold total must reach Rs. 5 lakh; Rs. 4 lakh already counted, remaining Rs. 1 lakh. The Base's last Rs. 1 lakh payment counts toward neither threshold nor super top-up. So Super Top-up pays Rs. 9 − Rs. 1 (Base) − Rs. 1 (remaining threshold, borne by insured) wait — under super top-up the Base payment itself counts toward the threshold. Aggregate threshold already Rs. 5 lakh after Base's Rs. 1 lakh (4+1). Hence the entire Rs. 9 − Rs. 1 (Base) = **Rs. 8 lakh** is paid by the Super Top-up. Super Top-up left = 10 − 8 = Rs. 2 lakh.\n- A) ignores the Base payment on the second claim.\n- C) and D) misapply the aggregate threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Using the standard example, what general lesson do the Rs. 15 lakh totals (Top-up pays Rs. 6 lakh, Super Top-up pays Rs. 15 lakh) teach about choosing between the two?\nA) Top-up is always better  B) They are identical  C) Only Top-ups cover hospitalisation  D) For multiple moderate claims in a year, a Super Top-up is far superior because its threshold applies once on the aggregate, not per claim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Because a Top-up applies its threshold to each claim, several moderate claims may each fall below it and pay little (Rs. 6 lakh of Rs. 15 lakh). A Super Top-up crosses the threshold once on the yearly aggregate, paying the full Rs. 15 lakh — far superior for multiple claims.\n- A) is the opposite of the lesson.\n- B) is wrong — they differ markedly for multiple claims.\n- C) is wrong — both cover hospitalisation.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario.** Rohan, aged 35, works at a firm that gives him an **employer family-floater health policy of Rs. 5 lakh**. Worried this is too little, he buys his own **Super Top-up health policy of Rs. 10 lakh with a Rs. 5 lakh deductible** (matching the employer cover) from the **same insurer**, plus a **standalone critical illness policy of Rs. 25 lakh** and a **personal accident policy of Rs. 50 lakh** (PTD; PPD pays 50% of PTD). During one policy year his family has three hospitalisations: **Claim 1 = Rs. 3 lakh, Claim 2 = Rs. 7 lakh, Claim 3 = Rs. 2 lakh**. Separately, Rohan is **diagnosed with a covered critical illness (heart attack)** and survives well beyond the survival period. He is also considering a 6-month overseas assignment.\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS1. After Claim 1 (Rs. 3 lakh), how is it settled, and what is the status of the deductible/threshold?</summary>\n\n**Answer.** The employer floater (Rs. 5 lakh) pays the full **Rs. 3 lakh**; employer cover left = 5 − 3 = Rs. 2 lakh. Because the super top-up uses an **aggregate** deductible of Rs. 5 lakh, the Rs. 3 lakh falling within the deductible zone counts toward it: **aggregate threshold used = Rs. 3 lakh**, balance Rs. 2 lakh still to be applied. The super top-up pays nothing yet, but (being from the same insurer) the threshold tracking is easy.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS2. After Claim 2 (Rs. 7 lakh), how much does the employer policy pay and how much does the super top-up pay?</summary>\n\n**Answer.** Step 1 — employer policy pays its remaining **Rs. 2 lakh** (now exhausted). Step 2 — the balance of the aggregate deductible is Rs. 5 − Rs. 3 (already used) = Rs. 2 lakh; this Rs. 2 lakh of the claim is borne within the deductible (the employer's Rs. 2 lakh payment completes it). Of the Rs. 7 lakh: Rs. 2 lakh to employer, Rs. 2 lakh completes the threshold — leaving Rs. 7 − 2 − 2... but the employer payment itself counts toward the threshold, so after the employer's Rs. 2 lakh the aggregate threshold reaches Rs. 5 lakh. Hence the **super top-up pays Rs. 7 − Rs. 2 (employer) = Rs. 5 lakh**. Super top-up balance = 10 − 5 = **Rs. 5 lakh**; aggregate threshold now fully used (Rs. 5 lakh).\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS3. After Claim 3 (Rs. 2 lakh), with the employer cover exhausted and threshold fully used, what is paid? How would this differ under a plain Top-up?</summary>\n\n**Answer.** Since the Rs. 5 lakh aggregate threshold is already met, the super top-up pays the **full Rs. 2 lakh**; balance left = 5 − 2 = Rs. 3 lakh. Under a plain **Top-up**, the Rs. 5 lakh threshold would apply to *this individual* Rs. 2 lakh claim — Rs. 2 lakh < Rs. 5 lakh, so a Top-up would pay **nothing**. This is the key advantage of the super top-up for multiple claims.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS4. The critical illness claim: will it pay, will the multiple policies reduce each other, and is it affected by the health claims above?</summary>\n\n**Answer.** Yes — critical illness is a **defined benefit** policy. On contracting the specified disease (heart attack) and surviving the minimum survival period (about 30 days), it pays the **full Rs. 25 lakh lump sum**, regardless of actual medical cost and **independent** of the health/super-top-up claims. Defined benefit policies are not subject to contribution, so even if Rohan held several such policies, **all would pay**. The lump sum can be used however he chooses (income replacement, repaying a loan, etc.).\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS5. During his 6-month overseas assignment, which of Rohan's covers would respond to (a) hospitalisation abroad and (b) an accidental death abroad? What should he buy for the trip?</summary>\n\n**Answer.** (a) **Hospitalisation abroad:** his Indian **health/super-top-up (indemnity) policies normally cover expenses in India only**, because verifying foreign expenses is difficult — so they would generally not pay abroad. He should buy an **overseas travel insurance** policy, which is the indemnity *exception* designed country-by-country to ascertain foreign expenses. (b) **Accidental death/critical illness abroad:** **defined benefit** policies (his personal accident PTD cover and critical illness cover) **pay wherever the risk occurs**, since the event is easy to verify anywhere in the world. Recommendation: buy overseas travel insurance for the trip; his defined-benefit covers already protect him globally.\n</details>\n</content>\n"}, {"num": "04", "title": "Retirement Planning Basics", "module": "Retirement Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 4: Retirement Planning Basics — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- Why retirement planning matters (and why people put it off)\n- How the retirement goal is different from every other money goal\n- The step-by-step retirement planning process\n- How **inflation** and **rate of return** pull the corpus in opposite directions\n- Two ways to estimate your retirement income: **Replacement Ratio** and **Expense Protection**\n- **Superannuation** benefits: what they are, how employers fund them, and the tax rules\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 Why Retirement Planning Is Needed\n\n**Simple idea:** When you stop working, the salary stops — but the bills don't. Retirement planning is about building a pot of money (a **corpus**) that pays your bills for the rest of your life.\n\n**Jargon defined:**\n- **Life expectancy** = the number of years a person is expected to live. It keeps rising because of better healthcare and science.\n- **Corpus** = the total lump sum of money you save up for retirement.\n\n**Analogy:** Imagine filling a water tank during the monsoon (your working years) so you have water all through the dry summer (retirement). If the summer turns out longer than expected, you'd better have filled the tank higher.\n\n**Worked example from the book:** X is in his 20s. Retirement age = 60, so working life = 40 years. If life expectancy = 80, then X must fund **20 years** of life with NO salary. That's 20 years of expenses to pay from savings.\n\n**The biggest roadblock:** accumulating *enough* money. Early in your career, responsibilities (house, marriage, kids) push retirement to the \"back burner\" — but those same responsibilities are exactly *why* you must plan.\n\n**Three phases of retirement planning:**\n1. **Preparation stage** — child education, buying a house, getting life & health insurance, recognising the impact of ageing.\n2. **Pre-retirement (initial retirement) stage** — physical/psychological changes; you learn the retirement rules and procedures.\n3. **Final retirement** — arrangements complete; you decide how to live.\n\n💡 **Tip:** Start early. The earlier you start, the more time compounding has to do the heavy lifting for you.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Why Retirement Is Different From Other Goals\n\nPeople plan many goals at once: kids' education, a car, a house, retirement. But retirement is **special**:\n\n- **No loans for retirement.** You can borrow for a house, car, or education. **No bank gives a loan to fund your retirement.** So you *must* save for it yourself.\n- **It gets postponed.** Education has a fixed deadline (a child starts college in ~14–15 years; you can't delay 4–5 years). Retirement, by contrast, is easy to push back \"just a few years\" — and people raid their retirement savings for other goals.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Delaying retirement planning can be **catastrophic** — the required amount grows manifold, and you lose the early-start (compounding) benefit.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 The Retirement Planning Process\n\nThe retirement goal is unique: it has the **longest accumulation and distribution periods** and needs the **largest corpus**.\n\n**Two key stages (defined):**\n- **Accumulation stage** = the years you save and invest to *build* the corpus.\n- **Distribution stage** = the retirement years when the corpus is *used* to generate income.\n\n**The steps:**\n\n**a) Determine expenses in retirement.** Expenses won't be the same as before. Heads include: housing (utilities, maintenance, taxes), living expenses (food, personal upkeep), medical care, transportation, recreation, insurance (life, health, disability), and taxes.\n\n**b) Determine income requirement.** Consider: maintaining the standard of living, the expenses to be met, and the inflation rate.\n\n**c) Time horizon.** Two periods matter:\n- **Years to retirement** = current age → retirement age. Shrinks every year. The longer this is, the bigger the inflation effect on future cost, *but* the greater the compounding benefit (so lower savings needed per month).\n- **Years in/during retirement** = start of retirement → end of life. Can't be known exactly; estimated from average life expectancy, gender, health, genetics, lifestyle.\n\n⚠️ **Longevity risk:** underestimating how long you'll live means the money runs out before you do.\n\n**d) Determine the retirement corpus.** Variables: (i) periodic income required, (ii) expected inflation, (iii) rate of return the corpus earns, (iv) period of retirement.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3.1 Impact of Inflation\n\n**Definition:** **Inflation** is a general rise in prices over time. As prices rise, one rupee buys less — inflation \"eats away the purchasing power of money.\"\n\n**Formula — Future Cost (compounding inflation):**\n\n> **Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + inflation rate)^number of years**\n\n- **Present Cost** = what it costs today\n- **inflation rate** = yearly rate prices rise (as a decimal, e.g. 6% = 0.06)\n- **number of years** = how far in the future\n\n**Logic:** Prices rise by a *percentage each year on the new (already-risen) price* — that's compounding, just like interest. So costs snowball.\n\n**Worked example from the book (6% inflation, item costs Rs. 100 today):**\n| Years ahead | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| 10 | Rs. 100 × 1.06^10 = **Rs. 179** |\n| 20 | Rs. 100 × 1.06^20 = **Rs. 321** |\n| 30 | Rs. 100 × 1.06^30 = **Rs. 574** |\n\n**Inflation hits retirement TWICE:**\n1. It raises the *cost of expenses* by the time you retire.\n2. It keeps raising expenses *during* retirement (your monthly need rises every year).\n\n**Book example (living on Rs. 60,000/month today, 6% inflation):**\n- In 10 years you'd need **Rs. 1,07,451/month**\n- In 20 years you'd need **Rs. 1,92,428/month**\n…just to maintain the same lifestyle.\n\n💡 **Tip:** Health costs and travel usually rise *faster* than general inflation — budget extra for them.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3.2 The Expected Rate of Return & Real Rate of Return\n\nThe corpus is **invested** both while accumulating and while distributing, so it keeps earning a return. That return reduces how much you must save.\n\n- **Higher expected return → lower corpus needed.** BUT higher return = higher risk.\n- In **accumulation**, you can afford more risk (long horizon). In **distribution**, your ability to take risk falls (you can't afford big losses).\n\n**Inflation and return pull in opposite directions:**\n- Inflation **pushes UP** the corpus required.\n- Return on the corpus **pushes DOWN** the savings required.\n\n**Definition:** **Real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return** = the return on an investment *after* adjusting for inflation.\n\n**Formula — Real Rate of Return:**\n\n> **Real Return = [(1 + nominal return) ÷ (1 + inflation rate)] − 1**\n\n- **nominal return** = the headline return your investment earns\n- **inflation rate** = the rate prices rise\n\n**Logic:** Money grows by the nominal return, but the \"finish line\" (prices) also moves up by inflation. The real return is how much *purchasing power* you actually gained.\n\n**Worked example (return 8%, inflation 6%):**\nReal Return = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.01887 − 1 = **0.01887 ≈ 1.89% p.a.**\n(Note: simply subtracting gives ~2%, but the precise formula gives 1.89%.)\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Estimating Retirement Corpus — Two Methods\n\n### 4.4.1 Replacement Ratio Method\n\n**Idea:** Assume your standard of living stays the same as just before retirement. You \"replace\" a percentage of your pre-retirement income.\n\n**Definition:** **Replacement ratio** = the fraction of your last working income you want to keep receiving in retirement (e.g. 80% or 50%).\n\n**Formulas:**\n> **Replacement Income (Year 1) = Pre-retirement Income × replacement ratio**\n> **Each subsequent year = previous year's income × (1 + inflation)** — to keep purchasing power\n\n**Book example A (quick):** Income Rs. 2,00,000, 80% ratio → plan for Rs. 1,60,000 in year 1.\n\n**Book example B (Mr. Rajeev):**\n- Age 50, earns Rs. 1,00,000/month now, retires at 60, wants 50% replacement.\n- Income grows 10% p.a. → income at retirement = Rs. 1,00,000 × 1.1^10 = **Rs. 2,59,374 p.a.**\n- Year-1 replacement income = 2,59,374 × 0.50 = **Rs. 1,29,687**\n- Year-2 (inflation 7%) = 1,29,687 × 107/100 = **Rs. 1,38,765**, and so on.\n\n**Reductions in living expenses & taxes:** At retirement, some expenses fall (housing loan, kids' education, work-related costs) and some long-term savings stop. Income often drops you into a **lower tax bracket**, cutting tax. All these *reduce* the income needed.\n\n⚠️ **Limitation:** The more years to retirement, the *less accurate* the replacement estimate. But starting early still lowers the return you need to hit your target.\n\n### 4.4.2 Expense Protection Method\n\n**Idea:** Build the retirement income from a **detailed monthly budget** of actual expenses. Some go up (medical, travel, gifting, house maintenance); some go down (housing loan, kids' education, income tax). Easier for people *near* retirement.\n\n**Book example (Mr. Ashish):**\n| Particulars | Amount (Rs.) | Calculation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current household expense | 30,000 | 50% of 60,000 |\n| Additional expenses at retirement | 15,000 | — |\n| Total retirement expense | 45,000 | 30,000 + 15,000 |\n| Years to retire | 20 | 60 − 40 |\n| Inflation rate | 6% | — |\n| **Expenses at retirement** | **1,44,321** | 45,000 × 1.06^20 |\n\n⚠️ **Limitation:** Like the replacement method, it's hard to estimate future expenses far in advance — review periodically.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Superannuation Benefits to Employees\n\n**Definition:** **Superannuation** is an organisational pension program a company creates for its employees (also called a **company pension plan**). Funds grow, typically tax-free, until retirement/withdrawal.\n\n**Key law point:** In India there is **no statutory superannuation** except for employees under the **Employees' Pension Scheme, 1995**. Government bodies, nationalised banks and a few others *may* pay pension if they decide. All other establishments (public or private) must arrange **annuities through LIC or an IRDAI-approved life insurer**.\n\n**Two arrangements an employer can use:**\n1. **Payment by the employer**\n2. **Funding through a Trust**\n\n### 4.5.1 Payment by the Employer\n- (a) Pay a lump sum out of current revenue to buy an **immediate annuity** from a life insurer (reward for a few employees), OR\n- (b) Pay pension from current revenue as it falls due — **not allowed/not satisfactory**.\n\n⚠️ **Why (b) fails:** Pension payment depends on the employer's profits; if profits fall or the employer goes bankrupt, pensioners may not get paid. The liability also keeps growing.\n\n- **Accounting Standard 15 (ICAI):** retirement benefits must be provided for in the *year of accrual*, not when they fall due.\n- **Section 2(11), Insurance Act:** makes it obligatory to arrange superannuation only through an IRDAI-registered life insurer. So the employer must **create a fund** and set aside money on an accrual basis each year.\n\n### 4.5.2 Funding Through a Superannuation Trust\n- Employer creates an **irrevocable Trust**, distinct from the employer, and appoints **Trustees**.\n- Employer transfers contributions (employer's + employees', if contributory) to the Trust Fund.\n\n### 4.5.3 Approved Superannuation Funds\nThe employer wants the contributions to be **tax-deductible**, so the fund must be **approved by the Commissioner of Income Tax**.\n\n**Conditions for approval (Part B, Fourth Schedule, IT Act):**\n- Established under an **irrevocable trust**; not less than **90% of employees employed in India**.\n- Sole purpose = provide **annuities** on retirement at/after a specified age, or on incapacity, or to widows/children/dependents on death.\n- The **employer must be a contributor**.\n- All annuities/pensions/benefits **payable only in India**.\n\n**Tax benefits of an approved superannuation fund:**\n| Section | Benefit |\n|---|---|\n| **10(13)** | Payments from the fund on death, on commutation of annuity at/after a specified age, on incapacity, or on transfer to NPS (80CCD) are **not included in total income** (exempt). |\n| **10(25)(iii)** | Income earned by trustees on behalf of the fund is **not taxed** → the fund grows tax-free. |\n| **36(iv)** | Employer's contribution is allowed as a **business expense deduction** (within prescribed limits/CBDT conditions). |\n| **80C** | Employee's contribution is **deductible** (within the IT Act ceiling). |\n\n### 4.5.4 Trustees' Responsibilities\n- Hold periodic meetings; keep minutes.\n- Keep in touch with the company; inform employer of decisions.\n- Collect contributions (or get them calculated by an **Actuary** in a defined benefit scheme).\n- Invest Trust money per the IT Rules pattern; reinvest interest.\n- Arrange pension benefits per options exercised.\n- **Deduct TDS** on annuity payments (or have the insurer do so).\n- Prepare, audit and submit annual accounts to IT authorities.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- Retirement is unique: **longest horizon, biggest corpus, no loans available** → you must self-fund.\n- Process: estimate expenses → income needed → time horizon → corpus.\n- **Inflation** raises costs (compounding): Future Cost = Present × (1+i)^n. It hits both *at* and *during* retirement.\n- **Return** lowers savings needed; **higher return = higher risk**; take less risk in distribution.\n- **Real return = (1+return)/(1+inflation) − 1.** Example: 8% & 6% → **1.89%**.\n- Two estimation methods: **Replacement Ratio** (% of last income) and **Expense Protection** (budget-based).\n- **Superannuation** = company pension; in India must go through LIC/IRDAI insurer or a Trust; tax benefits under 10(13), 10(25)(iii), 36(iv), 80C; needs Commissioner of Income Tax approval.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Corpus** | Lump sum saved for retirement |\n| **Accumulation / Distribution** | Build the corpus / spend the corpus |\n| **Longevity risk** | Living longer than planned → money runs out |\n| **Inflation** | General price rise; erodes purchasing power |\n| **Future Cost** | Present × (1 + i)^n |\n| **Real return** | (1+nominal)/(1+inflation) − 1; e.g. 8%/6% → **1.89%** |\n| **Replacement ratio** | % of pre-retirement income kept (e.g. 80%, 50%) |\n| **6% inflation, Rs.100 today** | Rs. 179 (10y), Rs. 321 (20y), Rs. 574 (30y) |\n| **Superannuation** | Company pension plan |\n| **EPS 1995** | Only statutory pension scheme in India |\n| **90%** | Min. employees in India for approved super fund |\n| **Sec 10(13), 10(25)(iii), 36(iv), 80C** | Superannuation tax benefits |\n| **Approver** | Commissioner of Income Tax |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 4: Retirement Planning Basics — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Why is retirement planning increasingly necessary?\n> **A:** Because **life expectancy** (years a person is expected to live) keeps rising, so people spend more years without a salary and must fund those years from savings.\n\n**Q2.** A person retires at 60 and lives to 80. How many years must their corpus fund?\n> **A:** 20 years of expenses with no salary income (80 − 60).\n\n**Q3.** What is a retirement \"corpus\"?\n> **A:** The total lump sum of money accumulated to generate income through the retirement years.\n\n**Q4.** Name the three phases of retirement planning.\n> **A:** (1) Preparation stage, (2) Pre-retirement / initial retirement stage, (3) Final retirement.\n\n**Q5.** Why is the retirement goal fundamentally different from buying a house or funding education?\n> **A:** No bank lends money for retirement — you must self-fund it. Other goals can be met with loans.\n\n**Q6.** Why do people dangerously postpone retirement saving?\n> **A:** Retirement has no hard deadline, so it's easy to delay; people also raid retirement savings for other goals. This loses compounding and inflates the required amount.\n\n**Q7.** Define the accumulation stage and the distribution stage.\n> **A:** Accumulation = years of saving/investing to build the corpus. Distribution = retirement years when the corpus generates income.\n\n**Q8.** What are the four steps of the retirement planning process?\n> **A:** (a) Determine retirement expenses, (b) determine income requirement, (c) set time horizon, (d) determine the corpus.\n\n**Q9.** What two time periods are central to corpus calculation?\n> **A:** \"Years to retirement\" (current age → retirement) and \"years in retirement\" (retirement → end of life).\n\n**Q10.** What is longevity risk?\n> **A:** The risk of underestimating how long you'll live, so the money runs out before the end of life.\n\n**Q11.** What four variables determine the retirement corpus?\n> **A:** Periodic income required, expected inflation, expected rate of return on the corpus, and the period of retirement.\n\n**Q12.** Define inflation and its effect on money.\n> **A:** A general rise in prices over time. It erodes purchasing power — the same money buys less later.\n\n**Q13.** What is the formula for the future cost of an expense?\n> **A:** Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + inflation rate)^number of years.\n\n**Q14.** At 6% inflation, what does an item costing Rs. 100 today cost in 10, 20, and 30 years?\n> **A:** Rs. 179 (10y), Rs. 321 (20y), Rs. 574 (30y).\n\n**Q15.** Inflation impacts retirement in which two ways?\n> **A:** (1) Raises the cost of expenses by the time you retire; (2) keeps raising expenses *during* retirement, so monthly income needed rises every year.\n\n**Q16.** Living on Rs. 60,000/month today at 6% inflation, what is needed in 10 and 20 years?\n> **A:** Rs. 1,07,451/month (10y) and Rs. 1,92,428/month (20y).\n\n**Q17.** Which expenses typically rise faster than general inflation?\n> **A:** Health costs and travel.\n\n**Q18.** How does a higher expected rate of return affect the corpus needed?\n> **A:** Higher return → lower corpus required (and lower savings needed) — but higher return brings higher risk.\n\n**Q19.** Why take less risk in the distribution stage than in accumulation?\n> **A:** In retirement the ability to take risk falls — losses can't be recovered since you can no longer add much to the corpus.\n\n**Q20.** Inflation and return act in opposite directions — how?\n> **A:** Inflation pushes the required corpus UP; the return earned on the corpus pushes the savings required DOWN.\n\n**Q21.** Define the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return and give its formula.\n> **A:** The return after adjusting for inflation. Real Return = [(1 + nominal return) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1.\n\n**Q22.** Compute the real return for 8% nominal return and 6% inflation.\n> **A:** (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 0.01887 ≈ **1.89% p.a.**\n\n**Q23.** What is the Replacement Ratio Method?\n> **A:** Estimating retirement income as a percentage of pre-retirement income (e.g. 80%), then raising it by inflation each year.\n\n**Q24.** Mr. Rajeev earns Rs. 1,00,000/month, retires in 10 years, income grows 10% p.a., wants 50% replacement. What is his year-1 replacement income?\n> **A:** Income at retirement = 1,00,000 × 1.1^10 = Rs. 2,59,374 p.a.; × 0.50 = **Rs. 1,29,687**.\n\n**Q25.** Continuing Q24, with 7% inflation, what is year-2 income?\n> **A:** 1,29,687 × 107/100 = **Rs. 1,38,765**.\n\n**Q26.** Why might retirement income needs be *lower* than working income?\n> **A:** Housing loan/kids' education/work expenses end, some savings stop, and lower income can drop you into a lower tax bracket.\n\n**Q27.** What is the Expense Protection Method?\n> **A:** Estimating retirement income from a detailed budget of actual expenses (some rise, some fall); more accurate near retirement.\n\n**Q28.** Mr. Ashish: retirement expense Rs. 45,000/month, 20 years to retire, 6% inflation. What's the expense at retirement?\n> **A:** 45,000 × 1.06^20 = **Rs. 1,44,321/month**.\n\n**Q29.** What is superannuation?\n> **A:** A company-created pension program (company pension plan); funds grow, typically tax-free, until retirement/withdrawal.\n\n**Q30.** In India, is statutory superannuation mandatory?\n> **A:** No — except for employees under the Employees' Pension Scheme, 1995. Others must arrange annuities via LIC/IRDAI-approved insurers.\n\n**Q31.** What two arrangements can an employer use for superannuation?\n> **A:** (1) Payment by the employer; (2) Funding through an irrevocable Trust.\n\n**Q32.** Why is \"pay pension from current revenue as it falls due\" not allowed?\n> **A:** Payment depends on the employer's yearly profits; if profits fall or the employer goes bankrupt, pensioners may not be paid; AS-15 requires accrual-basis provisioning.\n\n**Q33.** Who must approve a superannuation fund for tax benefits, and under which schedule?\n> **A:** The Commissioner of Income Tax, under Part B of the Fourth Schedule of the IT Act.\n\n**Q34.** What minimum percentage of employees must be employed in India for an approved super fund?\n> **A:** Not less than 90%.\n\n**Q35.** Match the tax section to the superannuation benefit: 10(13), 10(25)(iii), 36(iv), 80C.\n> **A:** 10(13) = payments (death/commutation/incapacity/transfer to NPS) exempt; 10(25)(iii) = fund's income tax-free; 36(iv) = employer's contribution is a business deduction; 80C = employee's contribution deductible.\n\n**Q36.** Name three responsibilities of superannuation trustees.\n> **A:** (Any three) hold periodic meetings/keep minutes; collect contributions (via Actuary in defined benefit); invest per IT Rules; arrange pension benefits; deduct TDS on annuities; prepare/audit/submit annual accounts.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 4: Retirement Planning Basics — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 **Tier 1 (Q1–Q35)** tests recall of definitions and facts, 🟡 **Tier 2 (Q36–Q70)** tests application and comparison, 🔴 **Tier 3 (Q71–Q100)** tests numerical calculations and scenarios. **Remember: the exam has 25% negative marking — a wrong answer costs you one-fourth of the marks for that question, so skip if you are truly unsure.**\n\n---\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What does the term \"life expectancy\" refer to?\nA) The age at which one must retire  B) The number of years an individual is expected to live  C) The number of working years  D) The number of years pension is paid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Life expectancy is the number of years an individual is expected to live, depending on factors like health and scientific advancement.\n- A) Wrong: that is the retirement age, a separate concept.\n- C) Wrong: working years run from start of career to retirement age, not until death.\n- D) Wrong: pension period is \"years during retirement,\" a different figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Why has the need for retirement planning increased over time?\nA) Falling life expectancy  B) Increasing life expectancy  C) Rising salaries  D) Falling inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As average life expectancy rises, people spend more years in retirement with no salary, so they must plan to fund those years.\n- A) Wrong: life expectancy is rising, not falling.\n- C) and D) Wrong: rising salaries and falling inflation would ease, not create, the need; the driver named in the workbook is longer life.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In the workbook's example, X has a working life of 40 years assuming the retirement age is:\nA) 55 years  B) 58 years  C) 60 years  D) 65 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The example assumes 60 as the retirement age; starting work in the 20s gives roughly 40 working years.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: the workbook specifically states 60 as the assumed retirement age in this illustration.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In the same example, if life expectancy is 80 years and retirement is at 60, how many post-retirement years must X fund?\nA) 10 years  B) 15 years  C) 20 years  D) 25 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Years during retirement = life expectancy − retirement age = 80 − 60 = **20 years**.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: only 80 − 60 = 20 matches the figures given.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** According to the workbook, retirement planning is not only about money accumulation but also about:\nA) Buying a house  B) Living a life of one's choice post-retirement  C) Getting a higher salary  D) Avoiding all taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states retirement planning is about living a life of one's choice post-retirement, which is possible only if planning starts early.\n- A) Wrong: buying a house is a preparation-stage goal, not the definition of retirement planning.\n- C), D) Wrong: neither is stated as the purpose of retirement planning.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** What is described as the biggest roadblock for retirement planning?\nA) Choosing investments  B) Accumulating enough money at retirement  C) Finding an adviser  D) Calculating taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook explicitly calls accumulating enough money at retirement the biggest roadblock.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these are tasks within planning but are not named as the biggest roadblock.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of these is listed as a phase of retirement planning?\nA) Liquidation stage  B) Preparation stage  C) Repayment stage  D) Withdrawal stage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The three phases are Preparation, Initial/Pre-retirement, and Final Retirement.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these are invented terms not used for the retirement-planning phases.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The three phases of retirement planning are Preparation, Pre-retirement, and:\nA) Accumulation  B) Distribution  C) Final Retirement  D) Annuity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The phases are (a) Preparation, (b) Initial/Pre-retirement, (c) Final Retirement.\n- A), B) Wrong: accumulation and distribution are stages of the *corpus*, not the three life phases listed.\n- D) Wrong: annuity is a payout product, not a phase.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which activity belongs to the \"preparation stage\" of retirement planning?\nA) Drawing pension  B) Need for child education and buying a house  C) Final withdrawal of corpus  D) Closing the trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The preparation stage includes child education, buying a house, adequate life and health insurance, and recognising the impact of ageing.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: drawing pension and final withdrawals belong to the retirement phase, not preparation.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A key reason retirement differs from other financial goals is that:\nA) It is the cheapest goal  B) No company gives loans for meeting retirement needs  C) It has the shortest horizon  D) It requires the smallest corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You can borrow for a house, car, or education, but no company gives loans for retirement, so you must self-fund it.\n- A), D) Wrong: retirement needs the *largest* corpus, not the smallest/cheapest.\n- C) Wrong: it has the *longest* horizon.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The workbook says the college education goal is generally at a horizon of:\nA) 5 to 6 years  B) 8 to 10 years  C) 14 to 15 years  D) 20 to 25 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook states college education is generally at a horizon of 14 to 15 years from the day the child starts school.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these do not match the figure stated.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Compared to other goals, the retirement goal has the:\nA) Shortest accumulation period  B) Longest accumulation and distribution periods and largest corpus  C) Smallest corpus  D) Most flexible deadline\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook describes retirement as the goal with the longest accumulation and distribution periods and requiring the largest corpus.\n- A), C) Wrong: it has the longest periods and largest corpus, the opposite of these.\n- D) Wrong: although people *tend* to postpone it, its defining feature here is the long horizon and large corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The \"accumulation stage\" of retirement is the stage in which:\nA) The corpus is used to generate income  B) Saving and investment for the retirement corpus is made  C) The trust is wound up  D) Pension is paid out\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Accumulation is the stage where saving and investing build up the corpus.\n- A), D) Wrong: using/spending the corpus is the distribution stage.\n- C) Wrong: winding up a trust is unrelated to this definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The \"distribution stage\" of retirement is when:\nA) Savings are first started  B) The corpus created in accumulation is employed to generate income  C) The employer creates a trust  D) Inflation stops\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Distribution is when the accumulated corpus is used to generate the income needed for retirement expenses.\n- A) Wrong: that is the accumulation stage.\n- C), D) Wrong: neither defines the distribution stage.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which is the FIRST step in the retirement planning process?\nA) Determine the retirement corpus  B) Determine expenses in retirement  C) Buy an annuity  D) Appoint trustees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The first step is to determine the expenses that have to be met in retirement.\n- A) Wrong: determining the corpus is the last of the listed steps.\n- C), D) Wrong: these are not steps in the planning process described.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** \"Years to retirement\" is best defined as the period:\nA) From retirement to end of life  B) From current age to retirement age  C) Of the trust's existence  D) Of annuity payments\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years to retirement is the period from the current point in time (current age) to the year/age of retirement.\n- A) Wrong: that is \"years during retirement.\"\n- C), D) Wrong: unrelated to this time period.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** \"Years during retirement\" refers to the number of years:\nA) From start of career to retirement  B) From the beginning of retirement to the end of life  C) The employer pays salary  D) Before the first job\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is the period from the beginning of retirement to the end of life, for which income must be secured.\n- A) Wrong: that is the working life / years to retirement.\n- C), D) Wrong: unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The risk of underestimating how many years one will live in retirement is called:\nA) Inflation risk  B) Longevity risk  C) Liquidity risk  D) Credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Underestimating the years (longevity) means the money may not last the retirement years — this is longevity risk.\n- A) Wrong: inflation risk is about rising prices eroding purchasing power.\n- C), D) Wrong: liquidity and credit risk are unrelated concepts here.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is NOT one of the four variables used to calculate the retirement corpus?\nA) The periodic income required  B) The expected rate of inflation  C) The employer's profit  D) The rate of return expected from the corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The four variables are periodic income required, expected inflation, expected rate of return, and the period of retirement. The employer's profit is not among them.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: each of these IS one of the four stated variables.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Inflation is best defined as:\nA) A fall in prices over time  B) A general rise in prices of goods and services over time  C) The interest earned on a deposit  D) A tax on income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, which eats away the purchasing power of money.\n- A) Wrong: that is deflation.\n- C), D) Wrong: interest and tax are unrelated to the definition of inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Inflation impacts retirement planning by:\nA) Reducing expenses  B) Eating away the purchasing power of money over time  C) Increasing investment returns  D) Lowering life expectancy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As prices rise, one unit of money buys less; inflation erodes purchasing power.\n- A) Wrong: inflation raises, not reduces, the cost of expenses.\n- C), D) Wrong: inflation does not increase returns nor affect life expectancy.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** According to the workbook, which expenses typically increase at a higher rate than standard inflation?\nA) Housing loan repayment  B) Health costs and travel  C) Children's education (after it ends)  D) Income tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook notes health costs and travel typically increase at a higher rate than standard inflation.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these tend to *reduce* at retirement, not rise faster.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** At 6% inflation, an item costing Rs. 100 today would cost approximately how much in 30 years (per the workbook)?\nA) Rs. 179  B) Rs. 321  C) Rs. 574  D) Rs. 600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook gives Rs. 574 after 30 years (Rs. 179 in 10 years, Rs. 321 in 20 years, Rs. 574 in 30 years).\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 179 is the 10-year figure.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 321 is the 20-year figure.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 600 is not the stated value.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Higher the rate of return expected from the corpus, the:\nA) Higher the required corpus  B) Lower the required corpus  C) Higher the inflation  D) Lower the life expectancy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Higher returns mean the invested corpus does more of the work, so a smaller corpus is needed. But higher return comes with higher risk.\n- A) Wrong: it is the opposite — higher return lowers the required corpus.\n- C), D) Wrong: return does not affect inflation or life expectancy.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** During the distribution stage of retirement, an investor's ability to take risk generally:\nA) Increases  B) Reduces  C) Stays the same  D) Becomes unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In distribution the ability to take risk reduces, because losses can no longer be recovered with future earnings.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the workbook states risk-taking ability falls in distribution.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Inflation and the expected rate of return act on the required corpus in:\nA) The same direction  B) Opposite directions  C) No particular way  D) Direction set by the employer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation pushes the required corpus up; the return on the corpus reduces the savings required — they act in opposite directions.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the workbook is explicit that they oppose each other.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The \"real\" or inflation-adjusted rate of return is:\nA) The nominal return before tax  B) The periodic rate of return after adjustment for inflation  C) The inflation rate itself  D) The employer's contribution rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The real rate of return is the periodic return on an investment after adjusting for inflation.\n- A) Wrong: that is the nominal return, before adjusting for inflation.\n- C), D) Wrong: it is neither inflation alone nor a contribution rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** How many methods does the workbook give to estimate post-retirement income?\nA) One  B) Two  C) Three  D) Four\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two methods: the Replacement Ratio Method and the Expense Protection Method.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the workbook lists exactly two.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The Replacement Ratio Method assumes that:\nA) Expenses double at retirement  B) The standard of living remains the same as just before retirement  C) Income falls to zero  D) Inflation is zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It assumes the standard of living stays the same as just before entering retirement, so a percentage of pre-retirement income is replaced.\n- A), C) Wrong: it does not assume doubling or zero income.\n- D) Wrong: the replacement income is escalated by inflation each year.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Under the Replacement Ratio Method, the Year-1 replacement income equals:\nA) Pre-retirement income × inflation  B) Pre-retirement income × replacement ratio  C) Pre-retirement income ÷ replacement ratio  D) Pre-retirement income + inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Replacement Income (Year 1) = Pre-retirement Income × replacement ratio (e.g. ×0.80).\n- A), D) Wrong: inflation is applied to *subsequent* years, not used to set Year 1.\n- C) Wrong: it is multiplied by, not divided by, the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The Expense Protection Method defines retirement income based on:\nA) A fixed percentage of salary  B) Expenses in retirement (often a detailed monthly budget)  C) The employer's pension promise  D) The stock market index\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This method defines retirement income from expenses in retirement, often using a detailed monthly budget.\n- A) Wrong: that describes the Replacement Ratio Method.\n- C), D) Wrong: neither defines the Expense Protection Method.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** For which group is the Expense Protection Method easier to use accurately?\nA) People far from retirement  B) Individuals nearing their retirement  C) Children  D) New employees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Estimation is easier for individuals nearing retirement, because their future expenses are clearer.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the longer the period to retirement, the harder and more speculative the estimate.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Superannuation is best described as:\nA) A government tax  B) An organizational pension program created by a company for its employees  C) A type of bank loan  D) A health insurance policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Superannuation is an organizational pension program (company pension plan) created by a company for its employees.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: it is none of these — it is a company pension arrangement.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** In India, statutory superannuation (pension) benefits exist only for employees covered under:\nA) The Companies Act, 2013  B) The Employees' Pension Scheme, 1995  C) The Income Tax Act, 1961  D) The Insurance Act, 1938\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is no legislation providing statutory superannuation except for employees under the Employees' Pension Scheme, 1995.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these laws cover other matters but do not provide statutory superannuation generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Other establishments (not government/nationalised banks) must arrange annuities through:\nA) The Reserve Bank of India  B) LIC or any other IRDAI-approved life insurance company  C) The Income Tax Department  D) Any mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They must arrange annuities through the Life Insurance Corporation of India or any other IRDAI-approved life insurer.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: none of these are authorised to provide the annuity arrangement described.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Ravi, aged 25, says he will \"start retirement saving once the kids are settled.\" Which workbook idea best warns him?\nA) Health costs rise faster than inflation  B) Delaying retirement planning can be catastrophic and loses early compounding benefits  C) The replacement ratio is 80%  D) Superannuation is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook warns that delaying retirement is catastrophic: required amounts grow manifold and the early-start (compounding) benefit is lost.\n- A) Wrong: true but not the point about *delay*.\n- C), D) Wrong: unrelated to the danger of postponing.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client wants to \"borrow now and fund retirement with a loan later.\" How should you respond using the workbook?\nA) That is the best plan  B) No company gives loans for retirement, so the corpus must be self-funded  C) Banks give cheap retirement loans  D) Inflation makes the loan free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlike a house or car, no lender finances retirement; the individual must accumulate the corpus.\n- A), C) Wrong: there is no retirement loan facility.\n- D) Wrong: inflation never makes a loan free; it erodes purchasing power of money you hold.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Two clients are identical except client A retires in 30 years and client B in 5 years. The inflation effect on the future cost of an expense is:\nA) Greater for B  B) Greater for A  C) Equal for both  D) Zero for both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Lower the number of years to retirement, lower the effect of inflation.\" With 30 years, A faces a much larger compounded price rise than B with 5 years.\n- A) Wrong: B has fewer years, so a smaller inflation effect.\n- C), D) Wrong: the effect depends on the number of years and is not equal or zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client with a 30-year horizon worries about saving \"too much per month.\" Which workbook point reassures her?\nA) Longer the period, greater the compounding, and lower the allocation from savings required  B) Inflation will fall  C) Returns are guaranteed  D) No corpus is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A longer period gives compounding more time, so a smaller monthly saving is needed to reach the same corpus.\n- B), C) Wrong: the workbook does not promise lower inflation or guaranteed returns.\n- D) Wrong: a corpus is always needed for retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An adviser is deciding which expense heads to include for a retiree. Which set is consistent with the workbook?\nA) Only food  B) Housing, living expenses, medical care, transportation, recreation, insurance and taxes  C) Only medical and travel  D) Only taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook lists housing (utilities, maintenance, taxes), living expenses, medical care, transportation, recreation, insurance (life/health/disability) and taxes.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: each omits most of the listed expense heads.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A client says \"my expenses in retirement will be exactly the same as now.\" Per the workbook, this is:\nA) Always true  B) Unlikely — expenses in retirement are unlikely to be the same as before retirement  C) True only if inflation is zero  D) True only for government employees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states the expense in retirement is unlikely to be the same as prior to retirement; some heads fall and others rise.\n- A) Wrong: the workbook explicitly says they are unlikely to be the same.\n- C), D) Wrong: this is not conditioned on inflation or employer type.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Which expense is most likely to REDUCE after retirement?\nA) Medical expenses  B) Travel  C) Housing loan repayment  D) House maintenance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Housing loan repayment, children's education and work-related expenses tend to reduce at retirement.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: medical, travel and house maintenance tend to *increase*.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Just after retirement, a person's income often drops them into a lower tax bracket. The workbook says this:\nA) Increases the income needed  B) Eliminates a good amount of tax liability, decreasing the retirement income needed  C) Has no effect  D) Raises inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A lower tax bracket cuts tax liabilities, which together with falling expenses decreases the retirement income required.\n- A) Wrong: it decreases, not increases, the need.\n- C), D) Wrong: it does have an effect and does not change inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Higher expected returns reduce the corpus needed, but the workbook cautions that:\nA) Returns are tax-free  B) A higher return will come with a higher risk  C) Inflation disappears  D) The corpus becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The trade-off is that a higher return comes with higher risk, which matters especially in distribution.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: none of these are stated trade-offs.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor is comfortable taking equity risk while still working but wants safety after retiring. This matches the workbook idea that:\nA) Risk capacity is constant  B) Investors may take higher risk in accumulation, but the ability to take risk reduces in distribution  C) One should take maximum risk after retiring  D) Risk does not matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — More risk can be borne during accumulation (long horizon), but risk-taking ability falls in distribution.\n- A) Wrong: risk capacity changes across stages.\n- C), D) Wrong: contrary to the workbook's guidance.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Comparing the two estimation methods, the Replacement Ratio Method differs from the Expense Protection Method because it:\nA) Uses a detailed monthly budget  B) Replaces a percentage of pre-retirement income  C) Ignores inflation entirely  D) Is only for government staff\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Replacement Ratio replaces a percentage of last income; Expense Protection builds income from a detailed budget.\n- A) Wrong: the budget approach is the Expense Protection Method.\n- C) Wrong: replacement income is escalated by inflation each year.\n- D) Wrong: it applies to anyone.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A 35-year-old wants the more accurate of the two methods. Which limitation applies to BOTH methods for someone far from retirement?\nA) They cannot be used at all  B) The longer the period to retirement, the harder/less accurate the estimate  C) They require an annuity  D) They are illegal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both methods become less accurate the further one is from retirement; estimates need periodic review.\n- A), D) Wrong: they are usable and legal.\n- C) Wrong: neither requires buying an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An employer wants to \"pay pensions from yearly profits as they fall due.\" Per the workbook this method is:\nA) The best practice  B) Not allowed/not satisfactory because payment depends on the employer's financial health  C) Required by law  D) Tax-free for employees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Paying from current revenue is not satisfactory and not allowed: if profits fall or the employer goes bankrupt, pensioners may not be paid, and the liability keeps growing.\n- A), C) Wrong: it is neither best practice nor legally required.\n- D) Wrong: tax treatment is not the issue here.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Accounting Standard 15 (ICAI) requires retirement benefits to be:\nA) Provided only when they fall due  B) Provided for in the respective year of accrual  C) Ignored until retirement  D) Paid in cash only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AS 15 does not allow providing for benefits when due; they must be provided for in the year of accrual.\n- A), C) Wrong: the standard forbids waiting until due/ignoring them.\n- D) Wrong: AS 15 says nothing about cash-only payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Section 2(11) of the Insurance Act effectively makes it obligatory for employers to arrange superannuation benefits through:\nA) A nationalised bank  B) An IRDAI-registered life insurance company  C) The stock exchange  D) A mutual fund house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 2(11) includes superannuation annuities within life insurance business, making it obligatory to arrange them through an IRDAI-registered life insurer.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: none of these is the route the section mandates.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** For funding through a trust, the workbook requires the Trust to be:\nA) Revocable and merged with the employer  B) Irrevocable and distinct from the employer  C) Owned by the employees only  D) Managed by the bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Trust must be irrevocable and distinct from the employer, with trustees appointed.\n- A) Wrong: it must be irrevocable and separate, not revocable/merged.\n- C), D) Wrong: it is managed by trustees, not employees alone or a bank.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** To make its superannuation contributions tax-deductible, an employer must get the fund approved by the:\nA) RBI Governor  B) Commissioner of Income Tax  C) IRDAI Chairman  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Approval by the Commissioner of Income Tax lets the employer treat contributions as a business expense (within prescribed limits).\n- A), C), D) Wrong: none of these grant the income-tax approval required.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** For an approved superannuation fund, what minimum percentage of employees must be employed in India?\nA) 50%  B) 75%  C) 90%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Not less than 90% of the employees must be employed in India.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: the prescribed minimum is 90%.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which is a condition for an approved superannuation fund?\nA) It may be revocable  B) Its sole purpose is providing annuities on retirement, incapacity, or to dependents on death  C) Benefits may be paid abroad  D) The employer need not contribute\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fund's sole purpose must be providing annuities for retirement (at/after a specified age), incapacity, or for widows/children/dependents on death.\n- A) Wrong: it must be irrevocable.\n- C) Wrong: all benefits must be payable only in India.\n- D) Wrong: the employer must be a contributor.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Under which section is income received by trustees on behalf of an approved superannuation fund NOT included in total income (fund grows tax-free)?\nA) Section 10(13)  B) Section 10(25)(iii)  C) Section 36(iv)  D) Section 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 10(25)(iii) exempts income received by trustees on behalf of the fund, so it accumulates tax-free.\n- A) Wrong: 10(13) exempts payments to beneficiaries.\n- C) Wrong: 36(iv) is the employer's deduction.\n- D) Wrong: 80C is the employee's deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** An employee makes a contribution to an approved superannuation fund. Which section allows the employee a deduction?\nA) Section 10(13)  B) Section 10(25)(iii)  C) Section 36(iv)  D) Section 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 80C allows the employee's contribution as a deduction, subject to the IT Act ceiling.\n- A), B) Wrong: these are exemptions on payouts/fund income.\n- C) Wrong: 36(iv) is the *employer's* business-expense deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which section allows the EMPLOYER's contribution to an approved superannuation fund as a business-income deduction?\nA) Section 10(13)  B) Section 10(25)(iii)  C) Section 36(iv)  D) Section 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 36(iv) allows the employer's contribution as a deduction in computing business income (within prescribed limits/CBDT conditions).\n- A), B) Wrong: those are exemptions, not the employer's deduction.\n- D) Wrong: 80C is for the employee.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A payment from an approved superannuation fund on the death of a beneficiary is exempt under:\nA) Section 10(13)  B) Section 36(iv)  C) Section 80C  D) Section 10(25)(iii)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 10(13) exempts payments on death, on commutation of annuity at/after a specified age, on incapacity, or on transfer to NPS (80CCD).\n- B), C) Wrong: these are deductions, not payout exemptions.\n- D) Wrong: 10(25)(iii) covers the fund's own income, not payouts to beneficiaries.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** In a defined benefit superannuation scheme, the trustees get the contribution amount calculated by:\nA) The employer's HR  B) An Actuary  C) SEBI  D) The auditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trustees collect contributions as per scheme rules or, in a defined benefit scheme, get them calculated by an Actuary.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: none of these performs the actuarial calculation described.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is a stated responsibility of superannuation trustees?\nA) Set the employer's salary policy  B) Deduct tax at source on annuity payments where applicable  C) Decide the company's products  D) Hire all employees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trustees must deduct TDS on annuity payments where applicable (or have the insurer do so) and remit it to the IT authorities.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these are not trustee duties under the scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A reward for one or a few dedicated employees may be arranged by the employer paying a lump sum to:\nA) Buy an immediate annuity from a life insurance company  B) Open a savings account  C) Buy company shares  D) Pay a one-time bonus only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The employer can pay a lump sum out of current revenue to buy an immediate annuity from a life insurer for select employees.\n- B), C), D) Wrong: none of these is the annuity-purchase method described.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which statement about the two estimation methods is TRUE?\nA) Both become more accurate with a longer horizon  B) Both need periodic review and are less reliable far from retirement  C) Only one of them uses inflation  D) They give identical results always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both methods share the limitation that long horizons make estimates speculative, so periodic review is needed.\n- A) Wrong: they become *less* accurate with longer horizons.\n- C) Wrong: both consider inflation when projecting future costs/income.\n- D) Wrong: they can differ depending on assumptions.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A client believes \"inflation only matters until I retire.\" How do you correct this?\nA) She is right  B) Inflation also raises expenses *during* retirement, so the corpus must fund rising costs  C) Inflation only matters after death  D) Inflation never matters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation hits twice: it raises the cost at retirement AND keeps raising the income needed during retirement, risking under-funding if ignored.\n- A) Wrong: it continues to matter during retirement.\n- C), D) Wrong: inflation matters throughout retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** In the workbook's Rajeev example, his replacement ratio is 50% and pre-retirement income (at retirement) is Rs. 2,59,374 p.a. His Year-1 replacement income is found by:\nA) Adding 50% to the income  B) Multiplying the income by 0.50  C) Dividing the income by 0.50  D) Subtracting inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Replacement Income (Year 1) = Pre-retirement Income × ratio = 2,59,374 × 0.50 = Rs. 1,29,687.\n- A), C) Wrong: it is a straight multiplication by the ratio, not addition or division.\n- D) Wrong: inflation is applied to *later* years.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Why does the Rajeev example grow the replacement income by 7% in the second year?\nA) To pay more tax  B) To maintain purchasing power against inflation  C) Because returns are 7%  D) Because his salary still rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each subsequent year's replacement income is escalated by the inflation rate (7%) to maintain purchasing power: 1,29,687 × 1.07 = Rs. 1,38,765.\n- A) Wrong: it is not about tax.\n- C) Wrong: 7% here is the assumed inflation rate, not the return.\n- D) Wrong: he is retired, so no salary growth applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** In the Ashish (Expense Protection) example, his \"total retirement expense\" of Rs. 45,000 is made up of:\nA) Rs. 60,000 income only  B) Rs. 30,000 current household expense + Rs. 15,000 additional expenses  C) Rs. 45,000 salary  D) Rs. 15,000 only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Current household expense (50% of 60,000 = 30,000) + additional expenses at retirement (15,000) = Rs. 45,000.\n- A), C), D) Wrong: these misstate the components of the total.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Ashish is 40 and retires at 60. The \"years to retire\" used in the example is:\nA) 10  B) 15  C) 20  D) 25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Years to retire = retirement age − current age = 60 − 40 = 20.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: only 60 − 40 = 20 matches.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An adviser must choose a method for a client retiring in 2 years who keeps a detailed budget. The most suitable method is:\nA) Replacement Ratio, because budgets are useless  B) Expense Protection, since the client is near retirement and tracks expenses  C) Neither method  D) Only the employer's pension\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Expense Protection suits someone near retirement who keeps a detailed monthly budget; estimation is easier and more accurate.\n- A) Wrong: budgets are exactly what this method uses.\n- C), D) Wrong: a method is available and appropriate here.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A government department decides to pay pension directly to its retirees. Per the workbook, paying pension is:\nA) Compulsory for all employers  B) Allowed for government bodies, nationalised banks and a few others if they so decide  C) Banned everywhere  D) Allowed only for private firms\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Government bodies, nationalised banks and a very few others are allowed to pay pension if they so decide; all others must arrange annuities through a life insurer.\n- A) Wrong: it is not compulsory for all employers.\n- C), D) Wrong: it is neither banned nor restricted to private firms.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Two investments earn the same nominal return, but inflation is higher in Country X than Country Y. The real return is:\nA) Higher in X  B) Higher in Y  C) Equal in both  D) Zero in both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1+nominal)/(1+inflation) − 1. With the same nominal return, higher inflation (X) gives a lower real return, so Y's real return is higher.\n- A) Wrong: higher inflation lowers the real return.\n- C), D) Wrong: real returns differ because inflation differs.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** At 6% inflation, an item costing Rs. 100 today will cost about how much in 10 years?\nA) Rs. 160  B) Rs. 179  C) Rs. 200  D) Rs. 321\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Formula: Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + i)^n**, where i = inflation rate and n = years. Here 100 × 1.06^10. Since 1.06^10 ≈ 1.79, Future Cost ≈ Rs. 179. Intuition: prices grow on top of already-risen prices (compounding), so 6% a year over 10 years adds about 79%.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 160 wrongly uses simple interest (6%×10 = 60%).\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 200 is just a guess of \"doubling.\"\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 321 is the 20-year figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** At 6% inflation, what does the workbook give as the cost in 20 years for an item costing Rs. 100 today?\nA) Rs. 179  B) Rs. 321  C) Rs. 360  D) Rs. 574\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future Cost = 100 × 1.06^20. Since 1.06^20 ≈ 3.21, the cost ≈ Rs. 321.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 179 is the 10-year value.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 360 wrongly compounds the 10-year figure linearly.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 574 is the 30-year value.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Living on Rs. 60,000 a month today at 6% inflation, the workbook says you would need how much per month in 10 years?\nA) Rs. 96,000  B) Rs. 1,07,451  C) Rs. 1,20,000  D) Rs. 1,92,428\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future need = 60,000 × 1.06^10 ≈ 60,000 × 1.79 ≈ Rs. 1,07,451. Intuition: the same lifestyle costs ~79% more after 10 years of 6% inflation.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 96,000 uses simple 6%×10.\n- C) Wrong: a round guess, not the compounded figure.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 1,92,428 is the 20-year figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Same client (Rs. 60,000/month today, 6% inflation): the workbook says the monthly need in 20 years is:\nA) Rs. 1,07,451  B) Rs. 1,44,000  C) Rs. 1,92,428  D) Rs. 2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future need = 60,000 × 1.06^20 ≈ 60,000 × 3.207 ≈ Rs. 1,92,428.\n- A) Wrong: that is the 10-year figure.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 1,44,000 uses simple interest.\n- D) Wrong: a round overshoot, not the compounded value.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Mr. Rajeev earns Rs. 1,00,000/month now and his income grows 10% p.a. for 10 years to retirement. His income at retirement (per annum) is:\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 2,59,374  C) Rs. 12,00,000  D) Rs. 31,12,488\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Future Value = Present × (1 + g)^n**, g = growth, n = years. The workbook computes 1,00,000 × 1.1^10 = Rs. 2,59,374. (1.1^10 ≈ 2.5937.)\n- A) Wrong: ignores compounding.\n- C) Wrong: 12,00,000 is just current annual income (1,00,000×12), not grown.\n- D) Wrong: that wrongly multiplies the grown monthly figure by 12.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Continuing Q75: Rajeev wants a 50% replacement ratio. His Year-1 replacement income is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 1,29,687  C) Rs. 1,38,765  D) Rs. 2,59,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Replacement Income (Year 1) = Pre-retirement Income × ratio** = 2,59,374 × 0.50 = Rs. 1,29,687.\n- A) Wrong: that is unrelated current income.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 1,38,765 is the Year-2 figure (after inflation).\n- D) Wrong: that is the full pre-retirement income, before applying 50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Continuing Q76: with 7% inflation, Rajeev's replacement income in his SECOND retirement year is:\nA) Rs. 1,29,687  B) Rs. 1,36,000  C) Rs. 1,38,765  D) Rs. 1,55,624\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Each subsequent year = previous year × (1 + inflation)** = 1,29,687 × 1.07 = Rs. 1,38,765. Intuition: the payout is escalated by 7% to keep purchasing power.\n- A) Wrong: that is Year 1.\n- B) Wrong: a wrong rounding.\n- D) Wrong: that wrongly applies 20% growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Mr. Ashish: total retirement expense Rs. 45,000/month, 20 years to retire, 6% inflation. His monthly expense at retirement is:\nA) Rs. 45,000  B) Rs. 90,000  C) Rs. 1,44,321  D) Rs. 2,57,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future Cost = 45,000 × 1.06^20 ≈ 45,000 × 3.207 ≈ Rs. 1,44,321 (the workbook's figure).\n- A) Wrong: that is today's expense, unadjusted.\n- B) Wrong: doubling is a guess, not compounding.\n- D) Wrong: overstates the multiplier.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Ashish earns Rs. 60,000/month with 50% as household expense. His current household expense is:\nA) Rs. 15,000  B) Rs. 30,000  C) Rs. 45,000  D) Rs. 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 50% of 60,000 = Rs. 30,000 (the workbook's \"current household expense\").\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 15,000 is the additional retirement expense, not the household expense.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 45,000 is the total (30,000 + 15,000).\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 60,000 is total income, not the 50% household portion.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** If a nominal return is 8% and inflation is 6%, the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return is closest to:\nA) 1.89%  B) 2.00%  C) 14.00%  D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Real Return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1** = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 ≈ 1.01887 − 1 = 0.01887 ≈ **1.89%**.\n- B) Wrong: 2% is the rough subtraction (8% − 6%); the precise formula gives 1.89%.\n- C) Wrong: 14% wrongly adds the rates.\n- D) Wrong: incorrect arithmetic.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client expects 10% nominal return and 7% inflation. The real rate of return is closest to:\nA) 3.00%  B) 2.80%  C) 17.00%  D) 1.43%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real Return = (1.10 ÷ 1.07) − 1 = 1.02804 − 1 ≈ 0.0280 = **2.80%**. Intuition: money grows 10% but the \"price finish line\" moves up 7%, leaving ~2.8% of extra purchasing power.\n- A) Wrong: 3% is the rough subtraction (10 − 7).\n- C) Wrong: 17% wrongly adds the rates.\n- D) Wrong: arithmetic error.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An expense of Rs. 50,000/month today must be projected 15 years ahead at 6% inflation. The future monthly cost is closest to (1.06^15 ≈ 2.3966):\nA) Rs. 95,000  B) Rs. 1,19,830  C) Rs. 1,50,000  D) Rs. 80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future Cost = 50,000 × 1.06^15 ≈ 50,000 × 2.3966 ≈ Rs. 1,19,830.\n- A) Wrong: undershoots the compounding.\n- C) Wrong: a round guess (×3).\n- D) Wrong: roughly only simple interest applied.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A person earning Rs. 2,00,000/month wants an 80% replacement ratio. The Year-1 replacement income (per month) is:\nA) Rs. 1,20,000  B) Rs. 1,60,000  C) Rs. 1,80,000  D) Rs. 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Replacement Income (Year 1) = Income × ratio = 2,00,000 × 0.80 = Rs. 1,60,000 (the workbook's quick example).\n- A) Wrong: that would be a 60% ratio.\n- C) Wrong: that would be 90%.\n- D) Wrong: that is 100% (no reduction).\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A retiree's Year-1 income is Rs. 1,60,000/month, escalated at 6% inflation. The Year-2 monthly income is:\nA) Rs. 1,66,000  B) Rs. 1,69,600  C) Rs. 1,76,000  D) Rs. 1,92,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Year-2 = Year-1 × (1 + inflation) = 1,60,000 × 1.06 = Rs. 1,69,600.\n- A) Wrong: rounds incorrectly.\n- C) Wrong: applies 10%, not 6%.\n- D) Wrong: applies 20%, not 6%.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Arti is 30, retires at 60, life expectancy 85. Her \"years to retirement\" and \"years during retirement\" are:\nA) 25 and 30  B) 30 and 25  C) 30 and 30  D) 55 and 25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years to retirement = 60 − 30 = 30; years during retirement = 85 − 60 = 25.\n- A) Wrong: the two figures are swapped.\n- C) Wrong: the retirement period is 25, not 30.\n- D) Wrong: 55 mistakenly adds 30+25.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A 6% inflation item costs Rs. 100 today. Roughly how many years until it costs about Rs. 200 (doubles)?\nA) ~6 years  B) ~12 years  C) ~24 years  D) ~33 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Using the \"Rule of 72\": years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate = 72 ÷ 6 = 12. Check: 1.06^12 ≈ 2.01, so ~Rs. 201.\n- A) Wrong: ~6 years gives only ~1.42× (≈ Rs. 142).\n- C) Wrong: ~24 years roughly quadruples (≈ Rs. 405).\n- D) Wrong: far too long; that would be ~7×.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A worker delays retirement saving so that \"years to retirement\" falls from 30 to 20. Per the workbook logic, the monthly saving needed to reach the same corpus will:\nA) Decrease, because of more compounding  B) Increase, because of less compounding time  C) Stay the same  D) Become zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Shorter horizon means less compounding, so a *larger* monthly allocation is needed to reach the same corpus. (\"Longer the period, lower the allocation from savings required.\")\n- A) Wrong: fewer years means *less* compounding, not more.\n- C), D) Wrong: the required saving changes and is certainly not zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client earns Rs. 1,20,000/month, grows at 8% p.a. for 5 years to retirement (1.08^5 ≈ 1.4693). His monthly income at retirement is closest to:\nA) Rs. 1,29,600  B) Rs. 1,76,316  C) Rs. 1,68,000  D) Rs. 2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future income = 1,20,000 × 1.08^5 ≈ 1,20,000 × 1.4693 ≈ Rs. 1,76,316.\n- A) Wrong: applies only one year of growth.\n- C) Wrong: uses simple interest (8%×5 = 40%).\n- D) Wrong: doubling is far too high.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Continuing Q88: with a 70% replacement ratio, the Year-1 monthly replacement income is closest to:\nA) Rs. 1,23,421  B) Rs. 1,40,000  C) Rs. 84,000  D) Rs. 1,76,316\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Year-1 replacement = retirement income × ratio = 1,76,316 × 0.70 ≈ Rs. 1,23,421.\n- B) Wrong: applies 70% to current, not grown, income wrongly rounded.\n- C) Wrong: applies 70% to the original Rs. 1,20,000.\n- D) Wrong: that is the 100% (full) income before applying 70%.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An adviser projects health costs separately because the workbook says they rise faster than general inflation. If general inflation is 6% but health costs rise at 10%, after 10 years a Rs. 10,000 health bill becomes (1.10^10 ≈ 2.5937):\nA) Rs. 17,908  B) Rs. 25,937  C) Rs. 16,000  D) Rs. 10,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Use the *health* rate: 10,000 × 1.10^10 ≈ 10,000 × 2.5937 = Rs. 25,937. Intuition: a higher inflation rate compounds to a much bigger figure than general inflation.\n- A) Wrong: that uses 6% general inflation (1.06^10 ≈ 1.79).\n- C) Wrong: uses simple interest.\n- D) Wrong: applies only one year of 6%.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A retiree needs Rs. 5,00,000 in the first year and escalates it at 5% for inflation. The amount needed in year 3 is closest to (1.05^2 = 1.1025):\nA) Rs. 5,25,000  B) Rs. 5,51,250  C) Rs. 5,75,000  D) Rs. 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Year 3 = Year 1 × (1.05)^2 = 5,00,000 × 1.1025 = Rs. 5,51,250 (two escalations after year 1).\n- A) Wrong: that is year 2 (one escalation).\n- C), D) Wrong: overstate the escalation.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client wrongly computes the future cost of Rs. 1,000 at 6% over 20 years as Rs. 2,200 using simple interest. The correct compounded figure (1.06^20 ≈ 3.207) is:\nA) Rs. 2,200  B) Rs. 3,207  C) Rs. 1,200  D) Rs. 6,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future Cost = 1,000 × 1.06^20 ≈ Rs. 3,207. Simple interest (6%×20 = 120% → Rs. 2,200) understates the true compounded cost.\n- A) Wrong: that is the simple-interest error.\n- C) Wrong: that is only one year of growth.\n- D) Wrong: too high.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A 45-year-old plans to retire at 65 and expects to live to 80. The corpus must fund how many years of post-retirement income?\nA) 10  B) 15  C) 20  D) 35\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years during retirement = 80 − 65 = 15. The corpus must provide income for these 15 years.\n- A) Wrong: 10 understates.\n- C) Wrong: 20 would be 85 − 65.\n- D) Wrong: 35 is the total remaining life (80 − 45), not the retirement years.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Suppose nominal return equals the inflation rate (both 6%). The real rate of return is:\nA) 6%  B) 0%  C) 12%  D) 3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real Return = (1.06 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 0%. Intuition: if your money grows exactly as fast as prices, your purchasing power is unchanged.\n- A) Wrong: 6% is the nominal return, before adjusting.\n- C) Wrong: 12% wrongly adds the rates.\n- D) Wrong: there is no real gain here.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A planner finds that raising the assumed return from 6% to 9% lets a client target a smaller corpus. The MAIN caution the workbook gives is:\nA) Returns are guaranteed  B) The higher return comes with higher risk, and risk capacity falls in distribution  C) Inflation will also fall  D) The corpus becomes unnecessary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher assumed return reduces the corpus needed but brings higher risk; importantly, the ability to bear risk falls in the distribution stage.\n- A) Wrong: higher returns are never guaranteed.\n- C) Wrong: assuming a higher return does not lower inflation.\n- D) Wrong: a corpus is always required.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Ashish's example shows expenses of Rs. 45,000 growing to Rs. 1,44,321 over 20 years at 6%. The implied multiplier (1.06^20) is closest to:\nA) 1.79  B) 2.40  C) 3.21  D) 5.74\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 1,44,321 ÷ 45,000 ≈ 3.207, i.e. 1.06^20 ≈ 3.21. (This matches the workbook's Rs. 100 → Rs. 321 over 20 years at 6%.)\n- A) Wrong: 1.79 is the 10-year multiplier.\n- B) Wrong: ~2.40 corresponds to ~15 years.\n- D) Wrong: 5.74 is the 30-year multiplier.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client wants 80% of a Rs. 2,50,000/month pre-retirement income, then escalation at 6%. Year-1 monthly income and Year-2 monthly income are:\nA) Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 2,12,000  B) Rs. 2,50,000 and Rs. 2,65,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 2,20,000  D) Rs. 1,80,000 and Rs. 1,90,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Year 1 = 2,50,000 × 0.80 = Rs. 2,00,000; Year 2 = 2,00,000 × 1.06 = Rs. 2,12,000.\n- B) Wrong: ignores the 80% ratio.\n- C) Wrong: uses 10% escalation, not 6%.\n- D) Wrong: uses a 72% ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A worker can save for 35 years (start at 25) versus 15 years (start at 45) to the same age-60 retirement. The workbook implies the early starter benefits mainly from:\nA) Lower inflation  B) Greater compounding effects, needing a lower allocation from savings  C) A larger salary  D) No need for a corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A longer period gives greater compounding, lowering the savings allocation required for the same corpus — the early-start advantage.\n- A) Wrong: inflation is unaffected by when you start.\n- C) Wrong: salary is not the mechanism described.\n- D) Wrong: a corpus is still required.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** If real return is 0% (return = inflation), to fund Rs. 6,00,000/year for 20 retirement years (ignoring further growth), the corpus needed is approximately:\nA) Rs. 6,00,000  B) Rs. 60,00,000  C) Rs. 1,20,00,000  D) Rs. 30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With a 0% real return, the corpus is simply annual need × number of years = 6,00,000 × 20 = Rs. 1,20,00,000. Intuition: when returns only offset inflation, no extra growth helps, so you need the full sum of all yearly needs.\n- A) Wrong: that funds only one year.\n- B) Wrong: that funds only 10 years.\n- D) Wrong: that funds only 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A client's nominal return is 7% and inflation is 7%. Using the real-rate formula, how much extra purchasing power does the corpus generate per year?\nA) About 7%  B) 0% (none)  C) About 14%  D) About 1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real Return = (1.07 ÷ 1.07) − 1 = 0%. The investment exactly keeps pace with inflation, so it generates no extra purchasing power.\n- A) Wrong: 7% is the nominal return, not the real (after-inflation) gain.\n- C) Wrong: adding the rates is incorrect.\n- D) Wrong: there is no positive real return here.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Verma is **40 years old** and plans to retire at **60** (so **20 years to retirement**). He expects to live to **80** (**20 years during retirement**). His current monthly household expense is **Rs. 50,000**. He assumes **general inflation of 6% p.a.** His retirement investments are expected to earn a **nominal return of 8% p.a.** Separately, his **health costs** of **Rs. 8,000/month** today are expected to rise at **10% p.a.** (faster than general inflation, as the workbook notes). Use the workbook's Expense Protection approach and formulas.\n\nHelpful multipliers: 1.06^20 ≈ 3.207; 1.10^20 ≈ 6.7275; 1.06^1 = 1.06.\n\n**Q-CS1.** What will Mr. Verma's general monthly household expense (excluding health) be at the start of retirement?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Formula: Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + i)^n.** Here Present = Rs. 50,000, i = 6% = 0.06, n = 20.\nFuture Cost = 50,000 × 1.06^20 ≈ 50,000 × 3.207 = **Rs. 1,60,350 per month**.\n\n**Intuition:** At 6% inflation, costs roughly triple over 20 years (multiplier ≈ 3.21), so today's Rs. 50,000 lifestyle costs about Rs. 1.60 lakh a month when he retires.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS2.** What will his monthly HEALTH cost be at retirement, and why is it projected separately?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Formula: Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + i)^n**, using the *health* inflation rate of 10%.\nFuture health cost = 8,000 × 1.10^20 ≈ 8,000 × 6.7275 = **Rs. 53,820 per month**.\n\n**Why separate:** The workbook states health costs (and travel) typically rise at a *higher* rate than standard inflation. Using 6% would understate them badly (8,000 × 3.207 ≈ Rs. 25,656). At 10% the figure is more than double that, so projecting health separately avoids under-funding.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS3.** What is the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return on his retirement corpus during distribution?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Formula: Real Return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1.** Nominal = 8% = 0.08, inflation = 6% = 0.06.\nReal Return = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = 0.018868 ≈ **1.89% p.a.**\n\n**Intuition:** Although the money earns 8%, prices rise 6%, so the real growth in purchasing power is only about 1.89%, not the rough 2% you'd get by simple subtraction. This small real return is what actually helps the corpus last through retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS4.** Combining CS1 and CS2, what is his TOTAL monthly expense at the start of retirement?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Total = future household expense + future health cost** (each compounded at its own rate).\nTotal = Rs. 1,60,350 (household at 6%) + Rs. 53,820 (health at 10%) = **Rs. 2,14,170 per month**.\n\n**Intuition:** The Expense Protection Method sums the projected expense heads. Because health inflates faster, it grows from a small share today (8,000 of 58,000 ≈ 14%) to a much bigger share at retirement (53,820 of 2,14,170 ≈ 25%). This is exactly why the workbook warns to budget extra for health and travel.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS5.** Verma is tempted to delay saving by 10 years (cutting \"years to retirement\" from 20 to 10). Using workbook logic, what happens to the monthly saving he needs, and what risk does he add?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Effect on savings:** The workbook states \"longer the period, greater the compounding effects, and lower the allocation from savings required.\" Cutting the horizon from 20 to 10 years gives compounding far less time, so the **monthly saving needed rises sharply** to reach the same corpus.\n\n**Added risk:** Delaying also makes the corpus harder to build (the workbook calls late starts \"catastrophic\" because required amounts grow manifold) and leaves less room to recover from poor markets. Combined with **longevity risk** (he could live beyond 80) and rising health costs, delaying significantly raises the chance of an **under-funded retirement**.\n\n**Intuition:** Time is the cheapest ingredient in retirement planning. Each year of delay must be paid for with much larger contributions later, because you lose years of compound growth that money cannot easily buy back.\n</details>\n\n\n"}, {"num": "05", "title": "Retirement Products", "module": "Retirement Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 5: Retirement Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- **Accumulation products** that build your retirement corpus: EPF, VPF, PPF, Gratuity, Superannuation, NPS (Tier I/II), UPS, NPS Vatsalya, Atal Pension Yojana, plus MF/insurance retirement plans\n- How an **investment adviser** builds a retirement portfolio\n- **Distribution products** that turn the corpus into income: Annuities, Systematic Withdrawal Plans (SWP), Bond/FD Laddering, SCSS, PMVVY, Post Office MIS, Reverse Mortgage\n\n> **Big picture:** *Accumulation* = filling the tank during working years. *Distribution* = drawing water in retirement. Different products suit each stage.\n\n---\n\n# PART A — Accumulation Products (5.1)\n\n## 5.1.1 Employees' Provident Fund (EPF)\n\n**Simple idea:** EPF (also called PF) is a **compulsory** savings-cum-retirement scheme for salaried employees of eligible organisations. Money goes in every month from both you and your employer, earns interest, and is meant to be your retirement corpus.\n\n**Jargon:**\n- **Basic pay** = the core part of salary (before allowances).\n- **Dearness Allowance (DA)** = a cost-of-living top-up added to salary.\n- **EPFO** = Employees' Provident Fund Organisation, the body that runs EPF.\n\n### Monthly contribution — the maths\n- **Employee:** contributes **12% of (Basic + DA)**. The full 12% goes into the EPF account.\n- **Employer:** also contributes **12% of (Basic + DA)**, but it is *split*:\n\n| Where the employer's 12% goes | Rate |\n|---|---|\n| Employees' Provident Fund (EPF) | **3.67%** |\n| Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS)* | **8.33%** |\n| Employees' Deposit Linked Insurance (EDLIS) | **0.5%** |\n| EPF Admin Charges | **0.50%** |\n\n\\*EPS is **subject to a ceiling of Rs. 15,000 monthly salary** (so EPS contribution is capped at 8.33% × 15,000 = Rs. 1,250).\n\n**Worked example:** Basic + DA = Rs. 30,000.\n- Employee → 12% × 30,000 = **Rs. 3,600** (all to EPF).\n- Employer → EPS = 8.33% × 15,000 (capped) = Rs. 1,250; the rest (3,600 − 1,250 = **Rs. 2,350**) goes to EPF.\n\n**When contribution can be 10% instead of 12%:**\n- Company with **fewer than 20 employees**\n- Company whose **losses exceed its entire net worth**\n- Company in the **beedi, jute, brick, guar gum or coir** industry\n\n**Women employees:** New women employees may contribute **8% instead of 12%** for the **first three years** (Union Budget 2018-19) — to give higher take-home pay and encourage hiring. *But the employer still contributes 12%.* An employee can also contribute *more* than 12% — that's **VPF** (next section).\n\n💡 **Tip:** EPF stays active as long as you are salaried. When you switch jobs, **update your EPF/UAN** with the new employer to keep contributions flowing.\n\n### Interest rate\n- Decided by the **Central Board of Trustees (CBT)** with the Ministry of Finance; valid for a financial year (**1 Apr – 31 Mar**).\n- Interest is **calculated monthly** but **credited on 31st March** each year.\n- ⚠️ From **1 April 2021**, if your **own** EPF + VPF contribution exceeds **Rs. 2.5 lakh** in a year, the interest on the *excess* is **taxable**.\n- **Dormant account:** no contributions for **3 consecutive years** → account becomes inactive, but interest still accrues until retirement (this interest is taxable at your slab). Once **inoperative** (no contribution for 3 years after retirement / migration abroad / death), **no interest accrues**. After **7 years** inoperative, unclaimed money goes to the **Senior Citizens' Welfare Fund**; claimable within **25 years**.\n- **EPS portion does NOT earn interest**; pension under EPS is payable only **after age 58**.\n\n### Withdrawal\n**Full withdrawal allowed:** (a) on retirement, (b) unemployed for more than 2 months.\n- After **1 month** unemployed → withdraw **75%**; remaining **25%** only after 2 months. If re-employed after 1 month, the 25% transfers to the new EPF account.\n- The 2-month wait does **not** apply to **women quitting for marriage**.\n\n**Partial withdrawal (Table 5.1) — key limits:**\n\n| Purpose | Limit | Service required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Medical (self/spouse/children/parents) | 6 months Basic+DA *or* total employee share + interest, whichever **lower** | No minimum |\n| Marriage (self/son/daughter/sibling) | Up to **50%** of employee's share + interest | **7 years** |\n| Education (self/child, post-matric) | Up to **50%** of employee's share + interest | **7 years** |\n| Buy land / build house | Land: 24 months Basic+DA; House: 36 months Basic+DA (least of limit / total share / cost) | **5 years** |\n| Home loan repayment | Up to 36 months Basic+DA (least of limit/corpus/outstanding); balance must exceed Rs. 20,000 | **10 years** |\n| House renovation | Up to 12 months Basic+DA (least of limit/share/cost) | **5 years** |\n| Partial withdrawal before retirement | Up to **90%** of accumulated balance | At **age 54**, within 1 year of retirement |\n\n### Tax on EPF (Section 80C + EEE-ish)\n- Employee contributions qualify for deduction under **Section 80C**.\n- Interest on own contribution **above Rs. 2.5 lakh/year** (including VPF) is **taxable**.\n- **Employer contribution:** if aggregate employer contribution to **EPF + NPS + Superannuation exceeds Rs. 7.5 lakh** in a year, the excess (and its interest) is a **perquisite** taxed in the employee's hands.\n\n**Tax on withdrawals:**\n- **Before 5 years of service:** taxable — *except* if employment ended due to ill-health/employer's business closing (beyond employee's control), or if the balance is **transferred** to the new employer.\n- **After 5 years:** **tax-free**.\n- Before 5 years: if amount **< Rs. 50,000**, taxed only if in taxable bracket; if **> Rs. 50,000**, **TDS** applies — **10%** with PAN, **maximum marginal rate** without PAN; **no TDS if Form 15G/15H** submitted.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Always **transfer** PF when switching jobs (using UAN) — withdrawing before 5 years can make it taxable and you lose compounding.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.2 Voluntary Provident Fund (VPF)\n\n**Idea:** EPF members can contribute **more than the mandatory 12%** — up to **100% of Basic + DA** — into the same EPF account. This extra is the **VPF**.\n- **No matching employer contribution** for VPF.\n- Invested like EPF (mostly **government securities/debt**); return declared yearly by the government.\n- Open-ended; contribute till retirement; withdrawals follow EPF rules.\n- **Tax:** contributions/interest/withdrawals exempt up to a limit — but if **EPF + VPF own contribution > Rs. 2.5 lakh/year**, interest on the excess is **taxable**.\n\n💡 **Tip:** VPF is a great tax-efficient booster for someone 50+ wanting to maximise safe retirement savings.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.3 Public Provident Fund (PPF)\n\n**Idea:** PPF is a **voluntary**, long-term, government-backed savings product opened at banks/post offices.\n\n**Eligibility:** Only **Indian residents**. NRIs cannot open one. Existing holders who become NRI can keep contributing (post 30 Sep 2024) but cannot extend tenure — and earn **no interest after 30 Sep 2024**. Parents/guardians can open for minors. **No joint or multiple accounts.**\n\n**Key features:**\n- **Lock-in: 15 years** (from end of FY of opening). Extendable in **5-year blocks**.\n- **Min Rs. 500/year; Max Rs. 1.5 lakh/year** (self + minor's account combined ≤ Rs. 1.5 lakh).\n- **Tax: EEE** (Exempt-Exempt-Exempt) → contribution deductible under **80C**, interest exempt, maturity exempt.\n- **Loan against PPF:** from start of 3rd FY to end of 6th FY; up to **25%** of balance at end of 2nd FY preceding the loan year.\n\n**Interest:** floating, notified **every quarter** by the Central Government. Minors' accounts earn POSA (Post Office Savings Account) rate till 18, then standard PPF rate.\n\n**Partial withdrawals:** allowed after **5 financial years** from end of FY of opening; **one per year**; max = **lower of** 50% of balance at end of preceding year *or* 50% of balance at end of 4th year preceding the withdrawal year. (Use **Form-2**.)\n\n**Extension at maturity (in 5-year blocks):**\n- **With contribution (Form-4, within 1 year of maturity):** can withdraw up to **60%** of balance over the block, one withdrawal per year.\n- **Without contribution (default):** one withdrawal/year, **any amount up to total balance**.\n- Once chosen, the option **can't be switched**; wrong choice → no interest and no 80C deduction on that money.\n\n**Premature closure** (after 5 FYs): allowed for (1) life-threatening illness of self/spouse/parents/children, (2) higher education of self/children, (3) change in residency status.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.4 Gratuity\n\n**Idea:** **Gratuity** is a thank-you payment from employer to employee for service. Usually paid at retirement.\n\n- **Eligibility:** minimum **5 years** of continuous service. Exception: paid earlier on **death or disability** from accident/disease.\n- **Covered under the Act** if the organisation employs **at least 10 persons** on any single day in the preceding 12 months. Once covered, **always covered** (even if staff drops below 10).\n\n### Formulas (the heart of this section)\n\n**(a) Employees covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972:**\n> **Gratuity = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26**\n- **15** = 15 days' salary per completed year; **26** = working days in a month (the law's convention).\n- **Tenure:** count an extra year if the last part-year is **more than 6 months**.\n- **Last drawn salary** = Basic + DA + sales commission.\n\n**Worked example:** Basic = Rs. 60,000; service = 20 years 7 months → counts as **21** (7 > 6).\nGratuity = (15 × 60,000 × 21) ÷ 26 = **Rs. 7.26 lakh**.\n*(If it were 20 years 5 months → 20 years.)*\n\n**(b) Employees NOT covered under the Act:**\n> **Gratuity = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 30**\n- Uses **30 days** and counts only **fully completed years** (drop the extra months).\n\n**Worked example:** Same person not covered: tenure = **20** (drop the 7 months).\nGratuity = (15 × 60,000 × 20) ÷ 30 = **Rs. 6 lakh**.\n\n**(c) Central Government employees:** one-fourth of a month's (Basic + DA) per completed **6-month** period of qualifying service. For **33+ years** of service → **16½ times** Basic+DA, **max Rs. 20 lakh**.\n\n**Death gratuity rates (Table 5.2):**\n| Qualifying service | Rate |\n|---|---|\n| Less than 1 year | 2 × basic pay |\n| 1 to <5 years | 6 × basic pay |\n| 5 to <11 years | 12 × basic pay |\n| 11 to <20 years | 20 × basic pay |\n| 20+ years | ½ basic for each 6-month period, **max 33 ×** basic |\n\n**Taxability:**\n- **Government employees:** fully **exempt**.\n- **Private, covered under Act:** exempt up to **least of** — Rs. 20 lakh, *or* (last salary × 15/26 × completed years), *or* actual received.\n- **Private, not covered:** exempt up to **least of** — Rs. 20 lakh, *or* (average salary × ½ × years), *or* actual. (Average = last **10 months'** salary.)\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.5 Superannuation Benefit (the product view)\n\nMandatory benefits are often **not enough**, so employers add a **superannuation** plan by contributing to a superannuation fund. Trustees administer it; the scheme is approved by the **Commissioner of Income Tax**.\n\n**Two ways to run a group superannuation scheme:** (1) a **trust fund** with appointed fund managers, or (2) **investment in a superannuation scheme from a life insurer**.\n\n- On retirement, the employee may take **one-third** as **commutation** (a lump sum); the **balance buys an annuity**.\n- **Commutation** = taking part of the annuity corpus as a lump sum.\n- IT rules cap the employer's combined PF + superannuation contribution at **27% of the employee's earnings**.\n- Aggregated with EPF and NPS, employer contributions over **Rs. 7.5 lakh/year** are treated as a **perquisite**. Payment received **on death** from an approved fund is **exempt**.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.6 National Pension System (NPS)\n\n**Idea:** A **Central Government social-security** pension scheme. Open to public and private employees (not armed forces) and **all citizens voluntarily**. You contribute during working years, then at retirement use part of the corpus to buy an **annuity** (regular pension) and withdraw the rest.\n\n**Key truth:** **No guaranteed return** — you earn **market returns**; the pension depends on the corpus built.\n\n**Three models:**\n- **Government Sector** — Central (except armed forces)/State employees; mandatory on joining.\n- **All Citizens model** — voluntary, ages **18–70**.\n- **Corporate model** — companies offering NPS to employees.\n\n### Tier I vs Tier II (Table 5.3)\n| Particulars | Tier I | Tier II |\n|---|---|---|\n| Status | Default / **mandatory** | **Voluntary** |\n| Withdrawals | Conditional & restricted | Permitted / unrestricted |\n| Tax deduction (employee) | Up to **Rs. 2 lakh** (Rs. 1.5 L under 80C + **Rs. 50,000 under 80CCD(1B)**) | Govt employees: 80C up to Rs. 1.5 L; others: **no** exemption |\n| Tax deduction (employer) | Up to **14% of Basic+DA** under **80CCD(2)** (treated as perquisite, then deductible) | Not applicable |\n| Minimum contribution | Rs. 500/contribution; min Rs. 1,000/year | Rs. 1,000 to activate; Rs. 250 thereafter |\n| Maximum | No limit | No limit |\n\n- Tier I is **mandatory** for anyone in NPS. Central Govt employees must contribute **10% of Basic+DA**.\n\n### Investment choices (four asset classes, Table 5.4)\n- **E (Equity):** equity market instruments.\n- **C (Corporate Debt):** PSU/PFI/infra bonds & money market.\n- **G (Government Securities):** Central/State govt securities & money market.\n- **A (Alternative Investments):** CMBS, REITs, AIFs, InvITs etc.\n\n**Modes:**\n- **Active Choice** — you pick the ratio. Caps: **E up to 75%**, C up to 100%, G up to 100%, **A up to 5%** (A only in Tier I, Active only).\n- **Auto Choice** — life-cycle approach; equity-heavy when young, falls with age. Three funds:\n  - **Moderate LC (default):** equity capped **50%**.\n  - **Conservative LC:** equity capped **25%**.\n  - **Aggressive LC:** equity up to **75%**.\n\nThese are the **Common Schemes**. New: **Multiple Scheme Framework (MSF)** (w.e.f. 1 Oct 2025) for Non-Government subscribers — customised schemes, each with Moderate-Risk and High-Risk (equity up to 100%) variants, optional Low-Risk; **minimum vesting period 15 years**.\n\n**Changing scheme/fund manager:** allowed **once a financial year** (both Tiers). Investment choice/asset allocation can be changed **4 times a year** (All Citizen & Corporate models).\n\n### Withdrawal & exit rules\n- **Partial withdrawal:** after **3 years** investing, up to **25% of the subscriber's own contribution** (not employer's, not returns), for specified needs (wedding, higher studies, house, medical). Max **3 times** in entire tenure. Tier I only.\n- **Exit at retirement (age 60/superannuation):** use **min 40%** of corpus to buy an annuity; **up to 60% withdrawn as lump sum**. If corpus **≤ Rs. 5 lakh** → can withdraw **entire** amount.\n- **Exit before retirement:** must annuitise **at least 80%**; **20% lump sum**. If corpus **≤ Rs. 2.5 lakh** → withdraw entire amount.\n- **Death:** entire corpus to nominee as lump sum (or buy annuity if they wish).\n- **Continuation/deferment:** can continue/defer lump sum or annuity purchase up to **age 75**.\n\n### Taxation — \"EEE-partly\" (E, E, partly Exempt–partly Taxable)\n- **First E:** contributions deductible under **80CCD(1)** up to Rs. 1.5 lakh (within 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) combined; limited to 10% of Basic+DA for salaried, 20% of gross income for non-salaried). **80CCD(2)** = employer's contribution (separate from 80C; up to 14% Basic+DA; not for self-employed). **80CCD(1B)** = extra **Rs. 50,000** exclusive NPS benefit. Total subscriber deduction up to **Rs. 2 lakh**.\n- **Second E:** no tax on returns earned.\n- **Partly E / partly Taxable:** at exit, **up to 60% lump sum is tax-free** (Sec **10(12A)**); the **40% annuity is taxable** as income when received. **Partial withdrawals tax-exempt** up to 25% of own contribution (Sec **10(12B)**).\n\n---\n\n## 5.1.7 Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) under NPS\n**Optional** scheme for **Central Government employees** (w.e.f. **1 April 2025**) giving an **assured payout**.\n- **Eligibility:** existing/new Central Govt employees under NPS; those retired on/before 31 Mar 2025 (min 10 years service); legally wedded spouse of a deceased eligible subscriber. Option is **final and irrevocable**.\n- **Contribution:** employee **10% of (Basic+DA)** + matching employer; Central Govt also adds ~**8.5%** to a Pool Corpus.\n- **Payout:** **50% of last 12-month average Basic pay** after min **25 years** service (proportionate for 10–25 years); **minimum guaranteed Rs. 10,000/month** after 10+ years; lump sum = **1/10th of last Basic+DA per completed 6-month period**; spouse gets **60%** family payout. Dearness Relief applies. No payout if removed/dismissed/resigned.\n\n## 5.1.8 NPS Vatsalya\nA saving-cum-pension scheme (Budget 2024) for **minors (below 18)**. A parent opens & operates it as guardian; runs under **NPS Tier I** rules; at 18 it becomes a normal NPS account.\n\n## 5.1.9 Atal Pension Yojana (APY)\n- Introduced **2015–16** for the **unorganised sector**; regulated by **PFRDA**; an extension of NPS (replaced Swavalamban).\n- Open to citizens aged **18–40** who are **not** members of any statutory social security scheme and are **not income-tax payers** (effective 1 Oct 2022). Focuses on unorganised-sector citizens.\n\n## Retirement plans from MFs & insurers\n- **Insurers:** deferred **pension plans** (premium deductible under 80C) and **annuity products**; options include lifelong pension and spouse continuation; offered as **ULIPs and traditional** plans.\n- **Mutual funds:** retirement schemes — mostly **hybrid** (equity + debt), 3–4 variants; some give insurance cover; **lock-in of at least 5 years or till retirement, whichever is earlier**.\n\n---\n\n# PART B — Portfolio by an Investment Adviser (5.2)\n- First manage the client's **psychological stress** (from retrenchment/retirement).\n- Consider key factors: **liquidity, security, estimated returns, resistance to inflation, taxes, social security**.\n- Match to **risk appetite** (conservative vs aggressive). Portfolio across **Equity, Debt, Gold** etc.\n- **EPF, NPS, Superannuation, Gratuity form the core** of a salaried person's retirement portfolio.\n\n---\n\n# PART C — Distribution Products (5.3)\n\nThese generate **periodic income** with **adequate returns** and **lower risk to income/principal**. **Capital protection** matters most here.\n\n## 5.3.1 Annuity from insurance companies\n**Definition:** An **annuity** is a fixed stream of payments for life or a fixed period. The **annuitant** (buyer) pays the **annuity provider** (insurer), who pays regular amounts. Main goal: **counter longevity risk** (and partly inflation).\n\n**Two basic types:**\n- **Deferred annuity:** payments begin **after** a specified period (>12 months later); bought during working years.\n- **Immediate annuity:** payments begin **soon** (within 12 months); usually a **single premium**.\n\n**Purchase modes:** (a) **single premium** (common for immediate), (b) **regular premiums** (level or flexible/variable).\n- The **annuity rate** is fixed at purchase and **guaranteed for the whole period**; reviewed periodically with **IRDAI** approval.\n\n**Payout options (chosen at vesting age):**\n| Option | Feature |\n|---|---|\n| Lifetime **without** return of purchase price | Pays till death; principal kept by insurer; **highest** annuity |\n| Lifetime **with** return of purchase price | Principal returned to nominee on death; **lowest** annuity |\n| **Guaranteed for a certain period** (e.g. 10/20 yrs) | Paid for that period regardless of survival, then till death |\n| **Joint annuity** | Spouse continues to receive after annuitant's death |\n| Others | inflation-adjusted, fixed-increasing annuities |\n\n**Tax:** Annuities are **NOT tax-exempt** — payments are **income** taxed in the annuitant's hands. The **premium** qualifies under **80C** (within overall limit).\n\n## 5.3.2 Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP) from mutual funds\n**Idea:** A **tax-efficient** way to draw regular income from a mutual fund — withdraw a **fixed amount** or only the **appreciation**, at a chosen frequency (monthly/quarterly/etc.).\n\n⚠️ **Key difference from a bank FD:** With an FD, the corpus stays intact when you take interest. With an SWP, **units are sold**, so the fund value **reduces** by the units withdrawn.\n\n**Worked example (NAV mechanics):** 8,000 units, withdraw Rs. 5,000/month.\n- NAV = Rs. 10 → sell 5,000/10 = **500 units** → 7,500 left.\n- Next month NAV = Rs. 20 → sell 5,000/20 = **250 units** → 7,250 left.\n- *Higher NAV → fewer units sold; lower NAV → more units sold.*\n\n**Taxation in SWP** (gain portion only):\n- **Debt funds (post 1 Apr 2023):** gain taxed at **income slab**, any holding period.\n- **Equity funds:** holding **≤ 12 months → 20%**; **> 12 months → 12.5%** (LTCG exemption of **Rs. 1.25 lakh/year** across all equity).\n\n## 5.3.3 Laddering of Bonds / Fixed Deposits\n**Idea:** Instead of all maturities in one year, buy bonds/FDs **maturing at staggered dates**.\n**Two goals:** (1) **manage interest-rate risk** (avoid locking everything at one rate; reinvest maturing pieces at prevailing rates), (2) **manage cash flow** (predictable income).\n\n**Bond example:** Mr. B splits USD 500,000 into 5 × USD 100,000 maturing in 1–5 years. Each year one matures and is reinvested at the **longest** end (5 years out), keeping the ladder intact.\n\n**FD example:** Mr. C splits Rs. 10 lakh into 5 × Rs. 2 lakh FDs (1–5 years). On maturity each is reinvested into a new 5-year FD → an income loop with annual liquidity. Benefits: less interest-rate impact, funds stay secure/available; can use different banks to diversify.\n\n## 5.3.4 Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS)\n- **Eligibility:** resident individual **60+**; or **55–60** if retired under superannuation/VRS (open account within **1 month** of receiving benefits); **Defence personnel (excl. civilian) at 50**. **No HUF/NRI/PIO.** Held singly or jointly with spouse (age limit applies to first holder).\n- **Deposit:** lump sum, min **Rs. 1,000** (multiples of 1,000), **max Rs. 30 lakh**. Cash only if **< Rs. 1 lakh**; else cheque/DD.\n- **Maturity: 5 years**, extendable **once by 3 years** (request within 1 year of maturity).\n- **Tax:** investment qualifies under **80C up to Rs. 1.5 lakh**; **interest fully taxable**; **TDS if interest > Rs. 50,000/year**. Interest credited **quarterly**, rate reviewed quarterly by Ministry of Finance.\n- **Premature closure penalty:** **1.5%** if exit after 1 but before 2 years; **1%** if between 2 and <5 years. On death, proceeds go to nominee/legal heir.\n\n## 5.3.5 Pradhan Mantri Vaya Vandana Yojana (PMVVY)\n- Launched **2017**, run by **LIC**; a **10-year** scheme for citizens **60+**.\n- **Pension:** min **Rs. 1,000/month** (purchase price Rs. 1.5 lakh) to max **Rs. 10,000/month** = Rs. 1,20,000/year (purchase price Rs. 15 lakh). Purchase price slightly lower for quarterly/half-yearly/annual modes.\n- **Investment returned on maturity.** Ceiling applies to the whole family. **98%** of purchase price returned if surrendered for critical healthcare. After **3 years**, loan up to **75%** of purchase price.\n- **Modified-2020** version; sale closed after **31 March 2023** — no new investments now. **Pension income is taxable.**\n\n## 5.3.6 Post Office Monthly Income Scheme (POMIS)\n- Government scheme paying **monthly interest**; rates announced **quarterly**.\n- **Open by:** single adult; 2–3 adults jointly; guardian for minor/unsound mind; minor **above 10** in own name.\n- **Deposit:** min **Rs. 1,000** (multiples of 1,000); max **Rs. 9 lakh single / Rs. 15 lakh joint** (totals across accounts).\n- **Interest:** paid monthly; unclaimed interest earns nothing extra; **taxable** in depositor's hands.\n- **Premature closure:** none before 1 year; **2% penalty** if closed 1–3 years; **1% penalty** if 3–5 years. **Matures at 5 years.**\n\n## 5.3.7 Reverse Mortgage\n**Idea (opposite of a normal mortgage):** In a normal mortgage you borrow a lump sum and repay via EMIs. In a **reverse mortgage**, you **pledge your owned home** to a bank/HFC and **receive periodic income** — turning home equity into retirement cash.\n\n**Key features:**\n- **Eligibility:** Indian citizen **60+**; couples as joint borrowers (one ≥60, other ≥55); must **own** a residential property in India with clear title, **free of encumbrances**, **residual life ≥ 20 years**, used as **primary residence**. **No commercial property.**\n- Loan amount depends on **age, appraised value, interest rates**.\n- **Max monthly payment Rs. 50,000**; max lump sum up to **50%** of eligible loan, capped at **Rs. 15 lakh** (for medical of self/spouse/dependants).\n- **Tax:** all RML receipts **exempt under Section 10(43)**.\n- **Tenure: max 20 years.** Prepay anytime without penalty. Borrower **remains owner**; need not service the loan while alive and living there. Interest accrues until repayment.\n- **Becomes due** only when the **last surviving borrower dies or permanently moves out**; settled from sale proceeds; surplus to heirs (who may instead repay from other funds).\n- **RMLEA (Reverse Mortgage Loan Enabled Annuity):** uses the loan to buy a **lifetime annuity** from an insurer; choose with/without return of purchase price; spouse cover available; annuity **exempt from tax**. NHB sets the **loan-to-value (LTV)** ratio.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **EPF:** 12% employee + 12% employer (employer split: 3.67% EPF, 8.33% EPS capped at Rs.15k salary, 0.5% EDLIS, 0.5% admin). 80C; tax-free after 5 yrs; interest on own contribution >Rs.2.5 lakh taxable.\n- **VPF:** extra contribution (up to 100% Basic+DA), no employer match, EPF rules.\n- **PPF:** voluntary, **15-yr** lock-in, Rs.500–1.5 lakh/year, **EEE**, residents only.\n- **Gratuity:** 5-yr minimum; covered → ×15/26; not covered → ×15/30; govt fully exempt.\n- **Superannuation:** employer pension; 1/3 commutation + annuity; employer cap 27%.\n- **NPS:** market-linked; Tier I (locked) vs II (free); exit 60 → min 40% annuity, up to 60% lump sum; EEE-partly; deductions 80CCD(1)/(1B)/(2), up to Rs.2 lakh.\n- **UPS:** assured 50% of avg Basic after 25 yrs (Central Govt, 1 Apr 2025).\n- **Distribution:** Annuity (taxable income, premium 80C), SWP (sells units; equity LTCG 12.5%), Laddering (stagger maturities), SCSS (60+, 5 yrs, max Rs.30 lakh, interest taxable), PMVVY (LIC, closed since Mar 2023), POMIS (monthly, max Rs.9/15 lakh), Reverse Mortgage (home → income, tax-free, max 20 yrs).\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| EPF employee/employer | 12% / 12% of Basic+DA |\n| Employer split | 3.67% EPF, 8.33% EPS, 0.5% EDLIS, 0.5% admin |\n| EPS salary ceiling | Rs. 15,000/month |\n| EPF taxable interest threshold | Own contribution > Rs. 2.5 lakh/year |\n| Employer perquisite threshold | EPF+NPS+Super > Rs. 7.5 lakh/year |\n| EPF tax-free service | 5 years |\n| PPF lock-in / limits | 15 years; Rs. 500–1.5 lakh/year; EEE |\n| Gratuity (covered / not) | ×15/26 / ×15/30; min 5 yrs; max Rs. 20 lakh |\n| NPS exit at 60 | ≥40% annuity, ≤60% lump sum; ≤Rs.5 lakh → full |\n| NPS exit before 60 | ≥80% annuity; ≤Rs.2.5 lakh → full |\n| NPS deductions | 80CCD(1) Rs.1.5L, 80CCD(1B) Rs.50k, 80CCD(2) 14% Basic+DA |\n| NPS equity caps | Active 75%; Auto: Aggressive 75%, Moderate 50%, Conservative 25% |\n| UPS payout | 50% of last 12-mo avg Basic after 25 yrs; min Rs.10,000/mo |\n| SCSS | 60+; 5 yrs (+3); max Rs.30 lakh; interest taxable |\n| PMVVY | LIC; 10 yrs; Rs.1k–10k/month; closed after Mar 2023 |\n| POMIS | max Rs.9 lakh single / Rs.15 lakh joint; 5 yrs |\n| Reverse Mortgage | 60+; max Rs.50,000/month; max 20 yrs; Sec 10(43) exempt |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 5: Retirement Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is EPF and is it mandatory?\n> **A:** Employees' Provident Fund — a **mandatory** savings-cum-retirement scheme for employees of eligible organisations, meant as a retirement corpus.\n\n**Q2.** How much do employee and employer each contribute to EPF?\n> **A:** Each contributes **12% of (Basic + DA)**.\n\n**Q3.** How is the employer's 12% EPF contribution split?\n> **A:** **3.67%** to EPF, **8.33%** to EPS (capped at Rs. 15,000 monthly salary), **0.5%** to EDLIS, **0.5%** admin charges.\n\n**Q4.** When can EPF contribution be 10% instead of 12%?\n> **A:** If the company has **<20 employees**, losses exceed net worth, or it's in the **beedi/jute/brick/guar gum/coir** industry.\n\n**Q5.** What special EPF rate applies to new women employees?\n> **A:** **8% (instead of 12%)** for the **first 3 years** — but the employer still contributes 12%.\n\n**Q6.** When is EPF interest credited, and who sets the rate?\n> **A:** Calculated monthly, **credited on 31st March**; rate set by the **Central Board of Trustees (CBT)** with the Ministry of Finance.\n\n**Q7.** When does interest on EPF own contribution become taxable?\n> **A:** When own contribution (EPF + VPF) **exceeds Rs. 2.5 lakh** in a financial year — interest on the excess is taxable.\n\n**Q8.** When is EPF withdrawal fully tax-free?\n> **A:** After **5 consecutive years** of service (or if transferred to a new employer; or if exit was beyond the employee's control).\n\n**Q9.** TDS on EPF withdrawal before 5 years if amount > Rs. 50,000?\n> **A:** **10% TDS** if PAN furnished; **maximum marginal rate** if not. No TDS if Form 15G/15H submitted.\n\n**Q10.** Does the employer's EPS contribution earn interest, and when is the pension payable?\n> **A:** **No interest** on EPS; pension payable only **after age 58**.\n\n**Q11.** What is VPF and is there an employer match?\n> **A:** Voluntary Provident Fund — extra contribution above 12% (up to **100% of Basic+DA**) into the EPF account; **no employer match**.\n\n**Q12.** PPF lock-in, limits, and eligibility?\n> **A:** **15-year** lock-in; **Rs. 500–1.5 lakh/year**; only **resident Indians** (no NRIs, no joint/multiple accounts).\n\n**Q13.** What does \"EEE\" mean for PPF?\n> **A:** Exempt-Exempt-Exempt: contribution deductible (80C), interest exempt, maturity exempt.\n\n**Q14.** When can you take a loan against PPF and how much?\n> **A:** From start of **3rd FY to end of 6th FY**; up to **25%** of balance at end of the 2nd FY preceding the loan year.\n\n**Q15.** Minimum service for gratuity, and the exceptions?\n> **A:** **5 years** continuous service; waived on **death or disability**.\n\n**Q16.** When is an organisation \"covered\" under the Payment of Gratuity Act?\n> **A:** If it employs **≥10 persons** on any single day in the preceding 12 months (once covered, always covered).\n\n**Q17.** Gratuity formula for employees covered under the Act?\n> **A:** **(15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26**; round tenure up if last part-year >6 months.\n\n**Q18.** Gratuity formula for employees NOT covered under the Act?\n> **A:** **(15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 30**; count only fully completed years.\n\n**Q19.** Worked example: Basic Rs. 60,000, 20 yrs 7 months, covered. Gratuity?\n> **A:** Tenure rounds to 21; (15 × 60,000 × 21)/26 = **Rs. 7.26 lakh**.\n\n**Q20.** Gratuity tax treatment for government employees?\n> **A:** **Fully exempt.**\n\n**Q21.** What is \"commutation\" in superannuation?\n> **A:** Taking a portion (up to **one-third**) of the annuity corpus as a **lump sum**; the balance buys an annuity.\n\n**Q22.** IT cap on employer's combined PF + superannuation contribution?\n> **A:** **27% of the employee's earnings.**\n\n**Q23.** Does NPS guarantee returns?\n> **A:** **No.** Subscribers earn **market returns**; pension depends on the corpus built.\n\n**Q24.** Difference between NPS Tier I and Tier II accounts?\n> **A:** Tier I = default/mandatory, restricted withdrawals, tax benefits. Tier II = voluntary, unrestricted withdrawals, no tax benefit (except govt employees under 80C).\n\n**Q25.** NPS tax deductions available to a subscriber?\n> **A:** **80CCD(1)** up to Rs. 1.5 lakh (within 80C), **80CCD(1B)** extra Rs. 50,000, **80CCD(2)** employer's up to 14% of Basic+DA — total subscriber up to **Rs. 2 lakh**.\n\n**Q26.** NPS exit at age 60 — how much must buy an annuity?\n> **A:** **Minimum 40%** of corpus buys an annuity; **up to 60%** withdrawn as lump sum. If corpus ≤ Rs. 5 lakh, full withdrawal allowed.\n\n**Q27.** NPS exit before 60 — annuity requirement?\n> **A:** **At least 80%** must buy an annuity; 20% lump sum. If corpus ≤ Rs. 2.5 lakh, full withdrawal allowed.\n\n**Q28.** NPS taxation regime?\n> **A:** **E, E, partly-Exempt/partly-Taxable**: contributions exempt, returns exempt, 60% lump sum tax-free (10(12A)), 40% annuity taxable as income.\n\n**Q29.** Maximum equity allocation under NPS Active Choice and Auto Choice (Aggressive)?\n> **A:** **75%** in both Active Choice and the Aggressive Life Cycle fund. (Moderate = 50%, Conservative = 25%.)\n\n**Q30.** What is the Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) assured payout?\n> **A:** **50% of the last 12-month average Basic pay** after min 25 years' service (Central Govt, w.e.f. 1 Apr 2025); min guaranteed Rs. 10,000/month after 10+ years.\n\n**Q31.** Who can join Atal Pension Yojana (APY)?\n> **A:** Citizens **18–40** in the unorganised sector, **not** in any statutory social security scheme and **not** income-tax payers.\n\n**Q32.** Two basic types of annuity?\n> **A:** **Deferred** (payments begin >12 months later) and **Immediate** (payments begin within 12 months).\n\n**Q33.** Which annuity payout option gives the highest amount, and which the lowest?\n> **A:** **Highest** = lifetime *without* return of purchase price; **lowest** = lifetime *with* return of purchase price.\n\n**Q34.** Are annuity payments tax-free?\n> **A:** **No** — they are taxed as income in the annuitant's hands; only the **premium** qualifies under 80C.\n\n**Q35.** How does an SWP differ from FD interest?\n> **A:** In an FD, taking interest doesn't reduce the corpus; in an SWP, **units are sold**, so the fund value falls. Higher NAV → fewer units sold.\n\n**Q36.** Equity-fund SWP capital gains tax: short vs long term?\n> **A:** ≤12 months → **20%**; >12 months → **12.5%** (LTCG exemption Rs. 1.25 lakh/year).\n\n**Q37.** What are the two goals of bond/FD laddering?\n> **A:** **Manage interest-rate risk** and **manage cash flow** by staggering maturities.\n\n**Q38.** SCSS eligibility age and maximum deposit?\n> **A:** **60+** (55–60 if VRS/superannuation; 50 for Defence); max deposit **Rs. 30 lakh**; matures in 5 years (+3 extension).\n\n**Q39.** What is a reverse mortgage and its max tenure?\n> **A:** Pledge an owned home to receive **periodic income** (opposite of a normal mortgage); max tenure **20 years**; receipts exempt under **Section 10(43)**.\n\n**Q40.** When does a reverse mortgage loan become due?\n> **A:** Only when the **last surviving borrower dies or permanently moves out**; settled from sale proceeds, surplus to heirs.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 5: Retirement Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Questions are organised in three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application) and 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario) — followed by a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark. When unsure, weigh the cost of guessing.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What percentage of (Basic + Dearness Allowance) must an employee mandatorily contribute every month to the EPF?\nA) 8%  B) 10%  C) 12%  D) 3.67%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — As per the EPF norm, the employee contributes **12% of (Basic + DA)** every month, and the full amount goes into the EPF account.\n- A) 8% is the reduced rate available to *new women employees* for their first three years, not the standard rate.\n- B) 10% applies only in special cases (firms with fewer than 20 employees, loss-making firms, or beedi/jute/brick/guar gum/coir industries).\n- D) 3.67% is the slice of the *employer's* 12% that goes specifically into EPF (the rest goes to EPS, EDLIS and admin).\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Out of the employer's 12% EPF contribution, how much goes to the Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS)?\nA) 8.33%  B) 3.67%  C) 0.5%  D) 12%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The employer's 12% is split: **8.33% to EPS**, 3.67% to EPF, 0.5% to EDLIS and 0.5% to admin charges.\n- B) 3.67% is the EPF portion of the employer's share.\n- C) 0.5% is the EDLIS (insurance) or admin portion.\n- D) 12% is the *total* employer contribution, not just the EPS slice.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The EPS portion of the employer's contribution is subject to a ceiling based on a monthly salary of:\nA) Rs. 21,000  B) Rs. 25,000  C) Rs. 30,000  D) Rs. 15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EPS is **subject to a ceiling of Rs. 15,000 monthly salary**, so the maximum EPS contribution is 8.33% × 15,000 = Rs. 1,250.\n- A), B) and C) are plausible salary figures but are not the statutory EPS ceiling stated in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** New women employees may contribute EPF at a reduced rate for the first three years of employment. That rate is:\nA) 12%  B) 10%  C) 8%  D) 6%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Union Budget 2018-19, new women employees may contribute **8% instead of 12%** for the first three years (to boost take-home pay and encourage hiring). The employer still contributes 12%.\n- A) 12% is the normal employee rate.\n- B) 10% is the special-circumstances rate (small/loss-making firms etc.), not the women's rate.\n- D) 6% is not a rate mentioned in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** EPF interest is credited to the employee's account on which date each year?\nA) 1st April  B) 31st March  C) 31st December  D) 30th June\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest is calculated monthly but **transferred (credited) on 31st March** every year. The financial year for EPF interest runs 1 April to 31 March.\n- A) 1st April is the *start* of the financial year, not the credit date.\n- C) and D) are arbitrary dates, not used by EPFO.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Who decides the EPF interest rate?\nA) RBI  B) SEBI  C) PFRDA  D) Central Board of Trustees (CBT) with the Ministry of Finance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The EPF interest rate is decided by the **Central Board of Trustees (CBT) in concurrence with the Ministry of Finance**, and announced by EPFO.\n- A) RBI sets monetary policy, not EPF rates.\n- B) SEBI regulates securities markets.\n- C) PFRDA regulates NPS/APY, not EPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A PPF account has a lock-in period of:\nA) 5 years  B) 10 years  C) 15 years  D) 20 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — PPF is a long-term, fixed-income investment with a **15-year lock-in** (from the end of the FY of opening), extendable in 5-year blocks.\n- A) 5 years is when *partial withdrawals/premature closure* become possible, not the full lock-in.\n- B) and D) are not the PPF lock-in.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The maximum amount that can be invested in a PPF account in one financial year is:\nA) Rs. 1 lakh  B) Rs. 1.5 lakh  C) Rs. 2 lakh  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The maximum is **Rs. 1.5 lakh per year** (self + minor's account combined), with a minimum of Rs. 500.\n- A) and C) are not the PPF ceiling.\n- D) Rs. 50,000 is the minimum *or* a figure from other schemes, not the PPF maximum.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** PPF enjoys which tax treatment?\nA) EET  B) ETE  C) EEE (Exempt-Exempt-Exempt)  D) TEE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — PPF falls under the **EEE** regime: contribution is deductible under 80C (E), interest is exempt (E), and maturity is exempt (E).\n- A), B) and D) describe regimes where one or more stages are taxed; that does not match PPF, which is exempt at all three stages.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Who is eligible to open a PPF account?\nA) NRIs only  B) HUFs  C) Foreign nationals  D) Only Indian residents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Only an **Indian resident** can open a PPF account. NRIs cannot open one (existing holders who become NRI may continue contributing but cannot extend tenure).\n- A) NRIs are specifically *not* eligible to open new accounts.\n- B) and C) are not eligible to open PPF accounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The minimum continuous service required to be eligible for gratuity (in normal cases) is:\nA) 5 years  B) 3 years  C) 10 years  D) 7 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gratuity is payable after a minimum of **5 years of continuous service** (except on death or disability, when it can be paid earlier).\n- B) 3 years is the NPS partial-withdrawal threshold, not gratuity.\n- C) 10 years is an EPF home-loan withdrawal condition.\n- D) 7 years is an EPF marriage/education withdrawal condition.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An organisation is covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972 if it employs at least:\nA) 5 persons  B) 10 persons  C) 20 persons  D) 50 persons\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An employer is covered if it employs **at least 10 persons** on any single day in the preceding 12 months. Once covered, it always remains covered.\n- A) 5 persons is incorrect.\n- C) 20 persons relates to EPF applicability (10% rate for firms below 20), not gratuity.\n- D) 50 persons is not a relevant threshold here.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The gratuity formula for an employee **covered** under the Act is:\nA) (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 30  B) (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26  C) (26 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 15  D) (last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 12\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For employees **covered** under the Act: **Gratuity = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26**. Here 15 = days of salary per completed year and 26 = working days in a month (the law's convention).\n- A) ÷ 30 is the formula for employees *not* covered under the Act.\n- C) and D) are invented formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** For employees **not covered** under the Gratuity Act, gratuity is calculated by dividing by:\nA) 26  B) 15  C) 12  D) 30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For employees not covered, the divisor is **30**: Gratuity = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 30, counting only fully completed years.\n- A) 26 is used for *covered* employees.\n- B) and C) are not divisors used in either formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The maximum statutory limit on tax-exempt gratuity for private-sector employees is:\nA) Rs. 20 lakh  B) Rs. 10 lakh  C) Rs. 25 lakh  D) Rs. 15 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The statutory limit (government-notified amount) is **Rs. 20 lakh**. Tax exemption is the least of: Rs. 20 lakh, the formula amount, or actual gratuity received.\n- B), C) and D) are not the current statutory cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Gratuity received by a Government employee is:\nA) Fully taxable  B) Taxable above Rs. 20 lakh  C) Fully exempt  D) Taxed at 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For Government employees, gratuity is **fully exempt** from tax. Only private-sector employees face the least-of-three exemption test.\n- A), B) and D) apply (partly) to private employees, not government employees.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Under a superannuation scheme, on retirement the employee may take what fraction of the accumulation as commutation (lump sum)?\nA) One-half  B) One-third  C) One-fourth  D) The whole amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The employee may take **one-third** as commutation; the balance is used to purchase an annuity.\n- A), C) and D) do not match the workbook's one-third rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Income Tax rules cap the employer's combined PF + superannuation contribution at what percentage of the employee's earnings?\nA) 12%  B) 14%  C) 27%  D) 40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — IT rules restrict the employer's contribution to PF or superannuation (or both) to **27% of the employee's earnings**.\n- A) 12% is the EPF employee/employer rate.\n- B) 14% is the NPS employer deduction cap under 80CCD(2).\n- D) 40% is the minimum NPS annuitisation at retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which body regulates the National Pension System (NPS)?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) PFRDA  D) RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NPS is regulated by the **PFRDA** (Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority).\n- A) SEBI regulates securities/mutual funds.\n- B) IRDAI regulates insurance (and annuity rates).\n- D) RBI is the central bank.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The All Citizens model of NPS is open to Indian citizens aged between:\nA) 18–60 years  B) 21–65 years  C) 25–75 years  D) 18–70 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The All Citizens model is a voluntary scheme open to citizens aged **18–70 years**.\n- A) 18–60 is incorrect; the upper age was extended.\n- B) and C) are not the stated age band.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which NPS account type is the default/mandatory one?\nA) Tier I  B) Tier II  C) Tier III  D) Both are mandatory\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Tier I** is the default/mandatory account with restricted withdrawals; Tier II is a voluntary, freely-withdrawable add-on.\n- B) Tier II is voluntary, not the default.\n- C) There is no Tier III.\n- D) Only Tier I is mandatory.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The additional exclusive NPS tax deduction available under Section 80CCD(1B) is:\nA) Rs. 1.5 lakh  B) Rs. 2 lakh  C) Rs. 25,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 80CCD(1B) allows an extra **Rs. 50,000** deduction exclusively for NPS, over and above the Rs. 1.5 lakh under 80C/80CCD(1).\n- A) Rs. 1.5 lakh is the 80C/80CCD(1) limit.\n- B) Rs. 2 lakh is the *total* subscriber deduction (1.5 lakh + 50,000).\n- C) Rs. 25,000 is not an NPS limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The four asset classes in NPS are Equity (E), Corporate Debt (C), Government Securities (G) and:\nA) Real Estate (R)  B) Gold (D)  C) Money Market (M)  D) Alternative Investments (A)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The fourth asset class is **Alternative Investments (A)** — instruments such as CMBS, REITs, AIFs and InvITs.\n- A), B) and C) are not among the four NPS asset classes defined by PFRDA.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under NPS Active Choice, the maximum permitted allocation to equity is:\nA) 50%  B) 75%  C) 100%  D) 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Active Choice the maximum equity allocation is **75%**.\n- A) 50% is the Moderate Life Cycle Fund cap under Auto Choice.\n- C) 100% is allowed for Corporate Debt and Govt Securities, not equity.\n- D) 25% is the Conservative Life Cycle Fund cap under Auto Choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Under NPS Auto Choice, the default Moderate Life Cycle Fund caps equity exposure at:\nA) 25%  B) 50%  C) 75%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Moderate Life Cycle Fund (the default) caps equity at **50%**.\n- A) 25% is the Conservative LC Fund cap.\n- C) 75% is the Aggressive LC Fund cap.\n- D) 100% is not allowed under Auto Choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** At retirement (age 60) under NPS, what minimum percentage of the corpus must be used to buy an annuity?\nA) 20%  B) 40%  C) 60%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At exit on retirement, a minimum of **40%** of the corpus must buy an annuity; up to 60% can be taken as a lump sum.\n- A) 20% is the lump-sum portion when exiting *before* retirement.\n- C) 60% is the maximum *lump sum* at retirement, not the annuity minimum.\n- D) 80% is the annuity minimum for *pre-retirement* exit.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** On exit from NPS **before** retirement (resignation before age 60), the minimum that must be annuitised is:\nA) 40%  B) 60%  C) 80%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Before retirement, at least **80%** of the corpus must buy an annuity; only 20% is paid as a lump sum.\n- A) 40% is the annuity minimum at *normal* retirement.\n- B) 60% is the lump-sum portion at retirement.\n- D) 100% would leave nothing as lump sum, which is not the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under NPS, a subscriber can defer the lump sum or annuity purchase up to the age of:\nA) 60 years  B) 70 years  C) 75 years  D) 80 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A subscriber may defer the lump sum, annuity purchase, or both up to the age of **75 years**.\n- A) 60 is the normal exit age.\n- B) 70 is the maximum *joining* age in the All Citizens model.\n- D) 80 is not specified.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) provides an assured payout of what percentage of the last 12-month average basic pay (after 25 years of service)?\nA) 40%  B) 60%  C) 75%  D) 50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — UPS assures **50%** of the 12-month average basic pay immediately prior to superannuation, after a minimum of 25 years' qualifying service.\n- A) 40% is the NPS retirement annuity minimum.\n- B) 60% is the UPS *family* (spouse) payout share.\n- C) 75% is not the UPS assured-payout rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** NPS Vatsalya is a scheme designed for:\nA) Senior citizens  B) Government employees  C) Minor citizens (below 18)  D) NRIs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NPS Vatsalya (Budget 2024) is a saving-cum-pension scheme for **minors below 18**, opened and operated by a parent/guardian under NPS Tier I rules.\n- A), B) and D) are not the target group; at 18 the account becomes a normal individual NPS account.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Atal Pension Yojana (APY) is open to citizens in the age group:\nA) 18–40 years  B) 18–60 years  C) 21–50 years  D) 25–45 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — APY is open to citizens aged **18–40 years** who are not members of any statutory social security scheme and are not income-tax payers.\n- B), C) and D) are not the APY age band.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** An annuity's main objective is to counter the risk of:\nA) Credit default  B) Longevity (and to some extent inflation)  C) Currency fluctuation  D) Liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An annuity provides a fixed stream of payments for life or a fixed period; its main objective is to **counter longevity risk** (outliving one's savings) and, to some extent, inflation.\n- A), C) and D) are not the stated primary objective of an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which annuity payout option provides the **highest** annuity amount?\nA) Lifetime with return of purchase price  B) Joint annuity  C) Lifetime without return of purchase price  D) Annuity guaranteed for a certain period\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Lifetime without return of purchase price** gives the highest annuity because the insurer keeps the principal on death (no refund obligation).\n- A) \"With return of purchase price\" gives the *lowest* annuity because the principal is refunded.\n- B) and D) provide intermediate payouts due to their extra guarantees.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Annuities received by the annuitant are:\nA) Fully exempt from tax  B) Taxed as income in the annuitant's hands  C) Taxed at 10% flat  D) Exempt under Section 10(43)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlike life insurance policies, annuities do **not** enjoy tax-exempt status; the money received is treated as **income and taxed** in the annuitant's hands (the premium qualifies under 80C).\n- A) Annuities are not exempt.\n- C) There is no flat 10% on annuity income.\n- D) Section 10(43) exempts *reverse mortgage* receipts, not ordinary annuities.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS) matures after:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 10 years  D) 15 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SCSS matures after **5 years** from account opening and can be extended once by 3 years.\n- A) 3 years is the extension period, not the maturity.\n- C) 10 years is the PMVVY term.\n- D) 15 years is the PPF lock-in.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A startup with only 15 employees that has just begun operations wants to know its applicable EPF contribution rate. Which rate applies?\nA) 12%  B) 10%  C) 8%  D) 3.67%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A company with **fewer than 20 employees** may apply the reduced EPF rate of **10%** instead of 12%. With 15 employees, the firm qualifies.\n- A) 12% is the standard rate for larger establishments.\n- C) 8% is only for new women employees (first 3 years).\n- D) 3.67% is the employer's EPF sub-component, not a contribution rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mr. Rao has been unemployed for exactly one month after resigning. How much of his EPF corpus can he withdraw now?\nA) 75%  B) 50%  C) 25%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — After **1 month** of unemployment, EPFO allows withdrawal of **75%** of the corpus. The remaining 25% can be withdrawn only after 2 months of unemployment.\n- C) 25% is the *balance* available only after 2 months.\n- B) 50% is not the rule for unemployment withdrawal.\n- D) 100% is allowed only after 2 months of continuous unemployment.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An employee wants to withdraw from EPF for his daughter's marriage. What minimum years of service must he have completed?\nA) 5 years  B) 7 years  C) 10 years  D) No minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Withdrawal for marriage (self/son/daughter/sibling) requires **7 years** of service, with a limit of up to 50% of the employee's share plus interest.\n- A) 5 years applies to house purchase/renovation.\n- C) 10 years applies to home loan repayment.\n- D) \"No minimum\" applies only to medical withdrawals.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Which EPF partial-withdrawal purpose has **no minimum service requirement**?\nA) Marriage  B) Education  C) Medical treatment  D) House purchase\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Medical treatment** (of self, spouse, children or parents) has no minimum service requirement; the limit is 6 months' Basic+DA or total employee share + interest, whichever is lower.\n- A) Marriage requires 7 years.\n- B) Education requires 7 years.\n- D) House purchase requires 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. Khan withdraws his EPF after working for 6 continuous years. How is the withdrawal taxed?\nA) Fully taxable at slab rate  B) 10% TDS applies  C) Tax-free  D) Taxable only above Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — After completing a **continuous 5 years of service**, the EPF withdrawal is **tax-free**. With 6 years, Mr. Khan crosses that threshold.\n- A), B) and D) describe the treatment when withdrawal happens *before* 5 years; they do not apply here.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An employee withdraws Rs. 80,000 from EPF before completing 5 years and has furnished his PAN. What is the TDS rate?\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 20%  D) Maximum marginal rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For withdrawals above Rs. 50,000 before 5 years, **TDS of 10%** applies if PAN is furnished.\n- A) 5% is not a stated rate.\n- C) 20% is not the EPF TDS rate.\n- D) Maximum marginal rate applies only if PAN is **not** furnished.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A subscriber wants to contribute more than the mandatory 12% to his provident fund without expecting any employer match. Which product fits?\nA) PPF  B) NPS Tier II  C) Superannuation  D) VPF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **Voluntary Provident Fund (VPF)** lets an EPF member contribute over and above 12% (up to 100% of Basic+DA) into the same EPF account, with **no employer match**.\n- A) PPF is a separate scheme with its own Rs. 1.5 lakh limit.\n- B) NPS Tier II is market-linked, not a provident fund top-up.\n- C) Superannuation is an employer-funded scheme, not employee-driven extra PF.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A client opened her PPF account on 15 Feb 2017. From which financial year can she make her first partial withdrawal?\nA) 2021-22  B) 2022-23  C) 2023-24  D) 2026-27\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Partial withdrawals are allowed after **5 financial years from the end of the FY in which the account was opened**. Opened in FY 2016-17; counting 5 FYs, withdrawal is permitted from **FY 2023-24** onwards (the exact example given in the workbook).\n- A) and B) are too early.\n- D) is later than necessary.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A PPF subscriber wants to extend the account at maturity **with** contributions. Which form must be submitted and within what window?\nA) Form-2, after maturity  B) Form-31, any time  C) Form-15G, at maturity  D) Form-4, within 1 year before maturity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Extension *with* contribution requires **Form-4**, submitted **within one year before the maturity date**; otherwise the default (extension without contribution) applies.\n- A) Form-2 is for partial withdrawals.\n- B) Form-31 is an EPF advance form.\n- C) Form-15G is for avoiding TDS, unrelated to PPF extension.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Employee A worked 20 years and 7 months and is covered under the Gratuity Act. How many years are counted as tenure?\nA) 20 years  B) 21 years  C) 20.5 years  D) 22 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the covered formula, a part-year of **more than 6 months** is rounded up. 7 months > 6 months, so tenure = **21 years**.\n- A) 20 years would be used if the part-year were 6 months or less (or under the not-covered formula).\n- C) Fractional years are not used; rounding is to whole years.\n- D) 22 years over-counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** The same employee (20 years 7 months) is **not** covered under the Act. What tenure is used?\nA) 20 years  B) 21 years  C) 20.5 years  D) 22 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For employees **not covered**, only **fully completed years** are counted; the extra 7 months are dropped, giving **20 years**.\n- B) 21 years would be the *covered* treatment (rounding up).\n- C) and D) are not how tenure is counted.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A private-sector employee covered under the Gratuity Act receives Rs. 25 lakh gratuity; the formula gives Rs. 22 lakh. How much is tax-exempt?\nA) Rs. 20 lakh  B) Rs. 22 lakh  C) Rs. 25 lakh  D) Rs. 18 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Exemption is the **least of**: statutory limit Rs. 20 lakh, formula amount Rs. 22 lakh, actual received Rs. 25 lakh. The least is **Rs. 20 lakh**.\n- C) Rs. 25 lakh is the actual received (highest, not the least).\n- B) Rs. 22 lakh is the formula figure (middle).\n- D) Rs. 18 lakh is not among the three values.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A company runs its group superannuation scheme by investing in a scheme from a life insurance company. Who must approve the scheme for tax purposes?\nA) PFRDA  B) IRDAI  C) Commissioner of Income Tax  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The company appoints trustees to administer the scheme and must get it **approved by the Commissioner of Income Tax**.\n- A) PFRDA regulates NPS/APY.\n- B) IRDAI regulates insurers but does not approve the superannuation trust for tax.\n- D) SEBI regulates securities markets.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A Central Government employee must mandatorily contribute what percentage of Basic+DA to NPS Tier I?\nA) 8%  B) 10%  C) 12%  D) 14%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Central Government employees must mandatorily contribute **10% of Basic + DA** to NPS Tier I.\n- A) 8% is the EPF women's rate.\n- C) 12% is the EPF rate.\n- D) 14% is the *employer's* NPS deduction cap under 80CCD(2), not the employee's mandatory rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An NPS subscriber has been investing for 4 years and wants a partial withdrawal for his child's higher studies. What is the maximum he can take?\nA) 25% of his own contributions  B) 25% of the total corpus  C) 40% of the corpus  D) 50% of employer contributions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — After at least 3 years, a Tier I subscriber may withdraw up to **25% of his own contributions** (excluding employer contributions and returns) for specified purposes such as higher studies.\n- B) The 25% is of *own contributions*, not the total corpus.\n- C) 40% relates to annuitisation at exit.\n- D) Employer contributions are explicitly excluded from the partial-withdrawal base.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor at age 28 wants the most aggressive NPS Auto Choice option. Which life-cycle fund, and what is its equity cap?\nA) Conservative LC — 25%  B) Moderate LC — 50%  C) Aggressive LC — 75%  D) Aggressive LC — 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Aggressive Life Cycle Fund** allows maximum equity of **75%** (highest among the three Auto Choice funds).\n- A) Conservative caps equity at 25%.\n- B) Moderate caps at 50%.\n- D) 100% equity is not available under Auto Choice (only certain High-Risk MSF schemes go that high).\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** How many times in a financial year can an NPS subscriber under the All Citizen model change their investment choice/asset allocation?\nA) Once  B) Twice  C) Four times  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Subscribers under the All Citizen and Corporate models can change the investment choice/asset allocation **four times** in a financial year.\n- A) Once a year is the limit for changing the *scheme or fund manager*, not asset allocation.\n- B) and D) are not the stated limits.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** At retirement, an NPS subscriber's corpus is Rs. 4.5 lakh. What is his withdrawal option?\nA) Must annuitise 40%  B) Must annuitise 80%  C) Can withdraw the entire corpus as lump sum  D) Can withdraw only 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At retirement, if the corpus is **Rs. 5 lakh or less**, the subscriber may withdraw the **entire amount as lump sum** (no compulsory annuity). Rs. 4.5 lakh is below the threshold.\n- A) 40% annuitisation applies only when the corpus exceeds Rs. 5 lakh.\n- B) 80% applies to pre-retirement exit above the relevant threshold.\n- D) The full amount can be taken, not just 60%.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A subscriber resigns before age 60 with an NPS corpus of Rs. 2 lakh. What can he do?\nA) Must annuitise 80%  B) Must annuitise 40%  C) Withdraw the entire corpus as lump sum  D) Withdraw only 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For *pre-retirement* exit, if the corpus is **Rs. 2.5 lakh or less**, the entire amount can be withdrawn as lump sum. Rs. 2 lakh is below this threshold.\n- A) 80% annuitisation applies only above Rs. 2.5 lakh on pre-retirement exit.\n- B) 40% applies to normal retirement exit above Rs. 5 lakh.\n- D) The full amount is available, not just 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Under NPS taxation, the portion withdrawn as lump sum at exit (up to 60%) is tax-free under which section?\nA) Section 80C  B) Section 10(12A)  C) Section 10(43)  D) Section 80CCD(2)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Up to 60% withdrawn as lump sum at exit is tax-free under **Section 10(12A)**.\n- A) 80C is a deduction at the contribution stage, not exit.\n- C) 10(43) exempts reverse mortgage receipts.\n- D) 80CCD(2) covers the employer's NPS contribution deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A self-employed professional invests in NPS. Which deduction is **NOT** available to her?\nA) 80CCD(1)  B) 80CCD(1B)  C) 80CCD(2)  D) 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 80CCD(2)** covers the *employer's* contribution. A self-employed person has no employer, so this benefit is **not available** to her.\n- A) 80CCD(1) covers her own contribution (up to limits).\n- B) 80CCD(1B) gives the extra Rs. 50,000.\n- D) 80C is generally available.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A 64-year-old wants to invest in a scheme that pays interest quarterly with a maximum deposit of Rs. 30 lakh. Which product fits?\nA) PPF  B) POMIS  C) PMVVY  D) SCSS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS)** has a maximum deposit of **Rs. 30 lakh**, interest credited **quarterly**, and is for those aged 60+.\n- A) PPF has a Rs. 1.5 lakh/year limit and 15-year lock-in.\n- B) POMIS caps at Rs. 9 lakh (single) / Rs. 15 lakh (joint) and pays monthly.\n- C) PMVVY is closed for new investments since 31 March 2023.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A retired person aged 56 who took VRS wants to open an SCSS account. By when must the account be opened?\nA) Within 1 week  B) Within 1 month of receiving retirement benefits  C) Within 6 months  D) Any time before 60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Those aged 55–60 who retired under superannuation/VRS may open SCSS only if the account is opened **within 1 month of receiving retirement benefits**.\n- A), C) and D) do not match the stated one-month rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor wants to stagger fixed deposit maturities to reduce interest-rate risk and ensure annual liquidity. This strategy is called:\nA) Averaging  B) Diversification  C) Hedging  D) Laddering\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Buying FDs/bonds **maturing at staggered dates** is the **laddering** strategy, which manages interest-rate risk and cash flow.\n- A) Averaging is a different concept (e.g., rupee-cost averaging).\n- B) Diversification spreads across asset types, not maturities specifically.\n- C) Hedging uses offsetting positions/derivatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Mr. C ladders Rs. 10 lakh into five FDs of Rs. 2 lakh each (1–5 year maturities) and reinvests each into a new 5-year FD. When does his original 1-year FD finally mature after reinvestment?\nA) 5th year  B) 6th year  C) 7th year  D) 10th year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The 1-year FD matures in year 1, then is reinvested for 5 more years, maturing in the **6th year** (1 + 5). This creates the annual income loop described in the workbook.\n- A) 5th year would skip the reinvestment.\n- C) 7th year is when the *2-year* FD finally matures (2 + 5).\n- D) 10th year does not fit this ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which statement correctly distinguishes an SWP from a bank FD's monthly interest payout?\nA) Both keep the corpus intact  B) An SWP guarantees a fixed return  C) FD interest reduces the principal  D) In an SWP, units are sold so the fund value reduces\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In an **SWP, units are redeemed (sold)** to fund each withdrawal, so the fund value reduces by those units. In an FD, taking interest leaves the principal intact.\n- A) Only the FD keeps corpus intact; the SWP does not.\n- B) SWPs do not guarantee returns; they depend on NAV.\n- C) FD interest does not reduce the principal.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** SWP gains from an **equity** mutual fund held for more than 12 months are taxed at:\nA) 20%  B) 12.5%  C) Slab rate  D) Exempt entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity-fund gains held **more than 12 months** (long-term) are taxed at **12.5%** (with a Rs. 1.25 lakh/year LTCG exemption across all equity).\n- A) 20% applies to equity gains held *12 months or less* (short-term).\n- C) Slab rate applies to *debt* fund gains (post 1 April 2023).\n- D) Only up to Rs. 1.25 lakh of LTCG is exempt, not the entire gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** SWP gains from a **debt** mutual fund (post 1 April 2023) are taxed:\nA) At 12.5% if held over 12 months  B) At 20% if short-term  C) At the investor's income-tax slab rate regardless of holding period  D) Fully exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Post 1 April 2023, debt-fund gains are taxed at the **income-tax slab rate irrespective of holding period**.\n- A) and B) are equity-fund rules.\n- D) Debt-fund gains are not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which scheme is operated solely by LIC, runs for 10 years, and is closed to new investors since 31 March 2023?\nA) SCSS  B) POMIS  C) APY  D) PMVVY\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Pradhan Mantri Vaya Vandana Yojana (PMVVY)** is run solely by LIC, has a 10-year term, and was closed to new investments after 31 March 2023.\n- A) SCSS is offered through banks/post offices and is open.\n- B) POMIS is an India Post scheme and is open.\n- C) APY is a PFRDA pension scheme and is open.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** What is the maximum a single individual can deposit in a Post Office Monthly Income Scheme (POMIS) single account?\nA) Rs. 4.5 lakh  B) Rs. 9 lakh  C) Rs. 15 lakh  D) Rs. 30 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The POMIS limit is **Rs. 9 lakh in a single account** (and Rs. 15 lakh in a joint account), across all accounts taken together.\n- A) Rs. 4.5 lakh is not the current limit.\n- C) Rs. 15 lakh is the *joint* account limit.\n- D) Rs. 30 lakh is the SCSS limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Under a reverse mortgage, when does the loan become due and payable?\nA) After 5 years  B) When the borrower turns 70  C) When the last surviving borrower dies or permanently moves out  D) Monthly via EMIs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An RML becomes due **only when the last surviving borrower dies or permanently moves out** of the house; it is then settled from sale proceeds.\n- A) and B) are not triggers for repayment.\n- D) In a *normal* mortgage you pay EMIs; a reverse mortgage pays the borrower instead.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** The maximum monthly payment under a Reverse Mortgage Loan (RML) is capped at:\nA) Rs. 25,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum monthly payments under RML are capped at **Rs. 50,000**.\n- A), C) and D) are not the stated cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** All receipts under a Reverse Mortgage Loan are exempt from income tax under:\nA) Section 80C  B) Section 10(12A)  C) Section 10(43)  D) Section 80CCD(1B)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — RML receipts are exempt under **Section 10(43)** of the Income-Tax Act.\n- A) 80C is a deduction, not an exemption for RML.\n- B) 10(12A) relates to NPS lump-sum withdrawal.\n- D) 80CCD(1B) is the extra NPS deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** For a reverse mortgage, the residual life of the pledged residential property must be at least:\nA) 10 years  B) 15 years  C) 20 years  D) 25 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The property's **residual life must be at least 20 years**, and it must be the borrower's primary residence with clear title, free of encumbrances.\n- A), B) and D) are not the stated residual-life requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which set of benefits forms the **core** of a salaried person's retirement portfolio, as advised by an investment adviser?\nA) Gold, real estate, equities  B) EPF, NPS, Superannuation and Gratuity  C) SCSS, POMIS, PMVVY  D) PPF, APY, Reverse Mortgage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states that **EPF, NPS, Superannuation and Gratuity** form the core of a salaried employee's retirement portfolio.\n- A) These are asset classes, not the salaried \"core\" benefits.\n- C) and D) are distribution/standalone products, not the employment-linked core.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** An employee's Basic + DA is Rs. 30,000. What is the employee's monthly EPF contribution?\nA) Rs. 1,250  B) Rs. 2,350  C) Rs. 3,600  D) Rs. 1,100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: *Employee contribution = 12% × (Basic + DA)*. = 0.12 × 30,000 = **Rs. 3,600**. The whole amount goes to the EPF account.\n- A) Rs. 1,250 is the EPS portion (8.33% × 15,000 cap).\n- B) Rs. 2,350 is the employer's EPF slice after EPS (3,600 − 1,250).\n- D) Rs. 1,100 has no basis here.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** For the same employee (Basic+DA = Rs. 30,000), how much of the employer's contribution goes to EPS, given the Rs. 15,000 salary ceiling?\nA) Rs. 2,499  B) Rs. 3,600  C) Rs. 2,350  D) Rs. 1,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EPS = 8.33% of salary, but capped at Rs. 15,000 salary. Formula: *EPS = 8.33% × 15,000* = **Rs. 1,250** (rounded). Because actual salary (30,000) exceeds the ceiling, the ceiling applies.\n- A) Rs. 2,499 ≈ 8.33% × 30,000 — wrong because the Rs. 15,000 ceiling is ignored.\n- B) Rs. 3,600 is the total employee/employer 12% figure.\n- C) Rs. 2,350 is the employer's EPF (non-EPS) slice.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Continuing Q72: how much of the employer's 12% (Rs. 3,600) goes into the EPF account (not EPS)?\nA) Rs. 1,250  B) Rs. 1,101  C) Rs. 3,600  D) Rs. 2,350\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The employer's total is 12% × 30,000 = Rs. 3,600. Of this, EPS takes Rs. 1,250 (capped). The remainder goes to EPF: 3,600 − 1,250 = **Rs. 2,350**. (This matches the workbook's worked example.)\n- A) Rs. 1,250 is the EPS portion, not the EPF portion.\n- B) Rs. 1,101 is incorrect.\n- C) Rs. 3,600 is the whole employer contribution, before the EPS split.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A's last drawn basic pay is Rs. 60,000/month, service is 20 years 7 months, and he is **covered** under the Gratuity Act. What is his gratuity?\nA) Rs. 6.00 lakh  B) Rs. 6.92 lakh  C) Rs. 7.26 lakh  D) Rs. 7.50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: *(15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26*. Tenure rounds up to 21 (7 months > 6). = (15 × 60,000 × 21) ÷ 26 = 18,90,00,000 ÷ 26 = **Rs. 7,26,923 ≈ Rs. 7.26 lakh**.\n- A) Rs. 6 lakh is the *not-covered* result (÷30, tenure 20).\n- B) Rs. 6.92 lakh wrongly uses tenure 20 with ÷26.\n- D) Rs. 7.50 lakh does not match the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Same A (Basic Rs. 60,000, 20 years 7 months) but **not covered** under the Act. What is his gratuity?\nA) Rs. 6.00 lakh  B) Rs. 6.30 lakh  C) Rs. 7.26 lakh  D) Rs. 5.40 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula: *(15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 30*, counting only completed years (20, dropping 7 months). = (15 × 60,000 × 20) ÷ 30 = 1,80,00,000 ÷ 30 = **Rs. 6,00,000 = Rs. 6 lakh**.\n- B) Rs. 6.30 lakh wrongly uses tenure 21.\n- C) Rs. 7.26 lakh is the *covered* result.\n- D) Rs. 5.40 lakh is incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An employee covered under the Act has last drawn salary Rs. 40,000 and 10 completed years of service. Calculate gratuity.\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 2,40,000  C) Rs. 3,00,000  D) Rs. 2,30,769\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — *(15 × 40,000 × 10) ÷ 26* = 60,00,000 ÷ 26 = **Rs. 2,30,769**.\n- A) Rs. 2,00,000 wrongly divides by 30.\n- B) Rs. 2,40,000 ignores the ÷26 (treats it as half-month × years × something).\n- C) Rs. 3,00,000 is unrelated to the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A retiree's NPS corpus at age 60 is Rs. 50 lakh. Using the minimum mandatory annuity rule, how much must buy an annuity and how much can be taken as lump sum?\nA) Annuity Rs. 20 lakh, lump sum Rs. 30 lakh  B) Annuity Rs. 30 lakh, lump sum Rs. 20 lakh  C) Annuity Rs. 40 lakh, lump sum Rs. 10 lakh  D) Annuity Rs. 25 lakh, lump sum Rs. 25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — At retirement, **minimum 40%** must annuitise and **up to 60%** is lump sum. Annuity = 40% × 50 lakh = **Rs. 20 lakh**; lump sum = 60% × 50 lakh = **Rs. 30 lakh**.\n- B) reverses the proportions.\n- C) uses 80%/20% (the pre-retirement rule).\n- D) splits 50/50, which is not the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A subscriber resigns at age 45 with an NPS corpus of Rs. 10 lakh. What is the minimum annuity purchase and the lump sum?\nA) Annuity Rs. 4 lakh, lump sum Rs. 6 lakh  B) Annuity Rs. 6 lakh, lump sum Rs. 4 lakh  C) Entire Rs. 10 lakh lump sum  D) Annuity Rs. 8 lakh, lump sum Rs. 2 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For *pre-retirement* exit (corpus above Rs. 2.5 lakh), at least **80%** must annuitise. Annuity = 80% × 10 lakh = **Rs. 8 lakh**; lump sum = 20% × 10 lakh = **Rs. 2 lakh**.\n- A) and B) use the 40%/60% retirement rule, which does not apply before retirement.\n- C) Full lump sum is allowed only if corpus ≤ Rs. 2.5 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. A has 8,000 units; NAV is Rs. 10. He withdraws Rs. 5,000 via SWP. How many units are sold and how many remain?\nA) 250 sold, 7,750 remain  B) 500 sold, 7,500 remain  C) 1,000 sold, 7,000 remain  D) 500 sold, 8,500 remain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — *Units sold = withdrawal ÷ NAV* = 5,000 ÷ 10 = **500 units**. Remaining = 8,000 − 500 = **7,500** (the workbook's exact example).\n- A) 250 sold would be at NAV Rs. 20, not Rs. 10.\n- C) 1,000 sold would be at NAV Rs. 5.\n- D) Units must reduce, not rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Next month Mr. A's NAV rises to Rs. 20. He again withdraws Rs. 5,000. How many units are sold this time, and what remains (starting from 7,500)?\nA) 500 sold, 7,000 remain  B) 250 sold, 7,500 remain  C) 100 sold, 7,400 remain  D) 250 sold, 7,250 remain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Units sold = 5,000 ÷ 20 = **250 units**. Remaining = 7,500 − 250 = **7,250**. Intuition: a higher NAV means fewer units are needed for the same rupee withdrawal.\n- A) 500 sold would be at NAV Rs. 10.\n- B) wrongly leaves 7,500 (forgets to subtract).\n- C) is arithmetically wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An investor's equity-fund SWP generates Rs. 1,80,000 of long-term capital gains in a year. Given the Rs. 1.25 lakh LTCG exemption, what amount is taxed at 12.5%?\nA) Rs. 1,80,000  B) Rs. 1,25,000  C) Rs. 55,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG on equity is taxed at 12.5% after a **Rs. 1.25 lakh annual exemption**. Taxable = 1,80,000 − 1,25,000 = **Rs. 55,000**.\n- A) Rs. 1,80,000 ignores the exemption.\n- B) Rs. 1,25,000 is the exempt amount, not the taxable amount.\n- D) Nil would apply only if gains were ≤ Rs. 1.25 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Continuing Q81: what is the tax payable on the Rs. 55,000 of taxable LTCG (ignoring cess)?\nA) Rs. 5,500  B) Rs. 11,000  C) Rs. 7,500  D) Rs. 6,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Tax = 12.5% × 55,000 = **Rs. 6,875**.\n- A) Rs. 5,500 uses 10%.\n- B) Rs. 11,000 uses 20% (the short-term equity rate).\n- C) Rs. 7,500 does not match 12.5% of 55,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Mr. B has USD 500,000 laddered into five bonds of USD 100,000 maturing in years 1–5. When the year-1 bond matures, where does he reinvest the proceeds to keep the ladder intact?\nA) In a new 1-year bond  B) He spends it  C) In a 3-year bond  D) In a new 5-year bond (longest end)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — To keep the ladder intact, the maturing piece is reinvested at the **longest end (5 years out)**. The bond that was 2 years away is now 1 year away, and so on — preserving the staggered structure.\n- A) A 1-year bond would not extend the ladder.\n- B) Spending it breaks the ladder.\n- C) A 3-year bond would create a gap in the ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** PMVVY: for the minimum monthly pension of Rs. 1,000, the purchase price is Rs. 1.5 lakh. By proportion, what purchase price gives the maximum Rs. 10,000/month pension?\nA) Rs. 7.5 lakh  B) Rs. 10 lakh  C) Rs. 15 lakh  D) Rs. 30 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Pension scales linearly: Rs. 1,000/month ↔ Rs. 1.5 lakh, so Rs. 10,000/month ↔ 10 × 1.5 lakh = **Rs. 15 lakh** (the workbook's stated maximum purchase price).\n- A) and B) under-fund the maximum pension.\n- D) Rs. 30 lakh is the SCSS cap, not PMVVY's.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A senior citizen exits SCSS after 2.5 years. His deposit was Rs. 20 lakh. What premature-closure penalty applies and what is the penalty amount?\nA) 1.5%, Rs. 30,000  B) 2%, Rs. 40,000  C) No penalty  D) 1%, Rs. 20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For exit **between 2 and less than 5 years**, the penalty is **1%** of the deposit. = 1% × 20,00,000 = **Rs. 20,000**.\n- A) 1.5% (Rs. 30,000) applies only between 1 and 2 years.\n- B) 2% is the POMIS penalty, not SCSS.\n- C) Premature exit after 1 year does carry a penalty.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A POMIS account is closed after 2 years. Deposit was Rs. 5 lakh. What deduction applies?\nA) 1% = Rs. 5,000  B) 1.5% = Rs. 7,500  C) No deduction  D) 2% = Rs. 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — POMIS closure **after 1 year but before 3 years** carries a **2% deduction on principal**. = 2% × 5,00,000 = **Rs. 10,000**.\n- A) 1% applies only between 3 and 5 years.\n- B) 1.5% is an SCSS penalty rate, not POMIS.\n- C) A deduction does apply after the first year.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A reverse mortgage borrower's eligible loan is Rs. 24 lakh. He wants the maximum lump sum for medical needs. Given the 50%/Rs. 15 lakh rule, how much can he take?\nA) Rs. 24 lakh  B) Rs. 15 lakh  C) Rs. 12 lakh  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The lump sum is restricted to **50% of the eligible loan, capped at Rs. 15 lakh**. 50% × 24 lakh = Rs. 12 lakh, which is below the Rs. 15 lakh cap, so the limit is **Rs. 12 lakh**.\n- A) Rs. 24 lakh ignores the 50% rule.\n- B) Rs. 15 lakh would apply only if 50% exceeded Rs. 15 lakh (i.e., loan > Rs. 30 lakh).\n- D) Rs. 50,000 is the monthly payment cap, not the lump-sum cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A UPS subscriber's last 12-month average basic pay is Rs. 80,000, with 25+ years of service. What is the monthly assured payout?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 48,000  C) Rs. 60,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — UPS assured payout = **50% of the 12-month average basic pay** (after ≥25 years). = 50% × 80,000 = **Rs. 40,000/month**.\n- A) Rs. 30,000 would be 37.5%.\n- B) Rs. 48,000 would be 60% (that is the *family/spouse* share rate, applied to the payout, not the basic pay).\n- C) Rs. 60,000 would be 75%.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Continuing Q88: on the subscriber's death, what monthly family payout does the spouse receive (60% of the admissible payout)?\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 48,000  D) Rs. 24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Family payout = **60% of the admissible payout** = 60% × 40,000 = **Rs. 24,000/month**.\n- A) Rs. 20,000 is 50% of the payout.\n- B) Rs. 40,000 is the full admissible payout (no reduction).\n- C) Rs. 48,000 wrongly applies 60% to the Rs. 80,000 basic, not to the Rs. 40,000 payout.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An employee's own EPF + VPF contribution in a year is Rs. 3 lakh. On how much of the contribution is the interest taxable?\nA) On the full Rs. 3 lakh  B) On nothing  C) On Rs. 2.5 lakh  D) On Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Interest is taxable only on the **excess over Rs. 2.5 lakh**. Excess = 3,00,000 − 2,50,000 = **Rs. 50,000**, so interest on that Rs. 50,000 is taxable.\n- A) The full Rs. 3 lakh is wrong; only the excess counts.\n- B) \"Nothing\" would be true only if contribution ≤ Rs. 2.5 lakh.\n- C) Rs. 2.5 lakh is the exempt threshold, not the taxable portion.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An employer's aggregate contribution to EPF + NPS + Superannuation in a year is Rs. 9 lakh. How much is treated as a taxable perquisite?\nA) Rs. 9 lakh  B) Rs. 7.5 lakh  C) Rs. 1.5 lakh  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The aggregate is a perquisite only to the extent it **exceeds Rs. 7.5 lakh**. Excess = 9,00,000 − 7,50,000 = **Rs. 1.5 lakh** (plus interest/dividend thereon).\n- A) The whole Rs. 9 lakh is not perquisite; only the excess is.\n- B) Rs. 7.5 lakh is the exempt threshold, not the taxable excess.\n- D) \"Nothing\" would apply only if the aggregate were ≤ Rs. 7.5 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A salaried NPS subscriber has Basic+DA of Rs. 12,00,000/year. His own NPS contribution deduction under 80CCD(1) is limited to 10% of Basic+DA. What is that ceiling?\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 1,20,000  C) Rs. 1,50,000  D) Rs. 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For salaried employees, the 80CCD(1) deduction is limited to **10% of Basic+DA** = 10% × 12,00,000 = **Rs. 1,20,000** (also subject to the overall Rs. 1.5 lakh 80C cap).\n- A) Rs. 1,00,000 uses a wrong percentage.\n- C) Rs. 1.5 lakh is the overall 80C cap, not the 10% limit here.\n- D) Rs. 2 lakh is the total including 80CCD(1B).\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Continuing Q92: with the extra 80CCD(1B), what is the maximum total deduction this subscriber can claim on his own NPS contribution (assuming he contributes enough)?\nA) Rs. 1,20,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 1,70,000  D) Rs. 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — He can claim up to Rs. 1,20,000 under 80CCD(1) (capped at 10% of Basic+DA) **plus** Rs. 50,000 under 80CCD(1B) = **Rs. 1,70,000**.\n- A) Rs. 1,20,000 omits the extra Rs. 50,000.\n- B) Rs. 1.5 lakh ignores both the 10% limit and 80CCD(1B).\n- D) Rs. 2 lakh assumes the full Rs. 1.5 lakh under 80CCD(1), but here it is capped at Rs. 1.2 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An employer's NPS contribution for a private-sector employee is eligible under 80CCD(2) up to what percentage of Basic+DA (as per Budget 2024)?\nA) 10%  B) 12%  C) 27%  D) 14%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Per Budget 2024, the employer's NPS contribution deduction under 80CCD(2) is up to **14% of (Basic+DA)** for both government and other employers.\n- A) 10% was the older limit for non-government employees.\n- B) 12% is the EPF rate.\n- C) 27% is the PF+superannuation cap, not the NPS employer deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A 33-year-old chooses the NPS Auto Choice **Moderate** Life Cycle Fund (age ≤35 row: E 50, C 30, G 20). If his contribution is Rs. 1,00,000, how much goes to equity?\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 30,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Rs. 75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At age ≤35 the Moderate fund allocates **50% to equity**. = 50% × 1,00,000 = **Rs. 50,000**.\n- A) Rs. 20,000 is the G (government) allocation (20%).\n- B) Rs. 30,000 is the C (corporate debt) allocation (30%).\n- D) Rs. 75,000 (75%) is the Aggressive fund's equity, not Moderate.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A PPF account balance at the end of the preceding year is Rs. 8 lakh; at the end of the 4th year preceding the withdrawal it was Rs. 6 lakh. What is the maximum partial withdrawal?\nA) Rs. 4 lakh  B) Rs. 3 lakh  C) Rs. 8 lakh  D) Rs. 6 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum = **lower of** 50% of preceding-year balance (50% × 8 lakh = Rs. 4 lakh) **or** 50% of the 4th-preceding-year balance (50% × 6 lakh = Rs. 3 lakh). The lower is **Rs. 3 lakh**.\n- A) Rs. 4 lakh is the higher of the two 50% figures, not the lower.\n- C) and D) ignore the 50% rule entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Central Government retirement gratuity for 33+ years of qualifying service equals 16½ times Basic+DA. If Basic+DA = Rs. 1,00,000, what is the gratuity, applying the cap?\nA) Rs. 16,50,000  B) Rs. 20,00,000  C) Rs. 33,00,000  D) Rs. 16,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gratuity = 16.5 × Basic+DA = 16.5 × 1,00,000 = **Rs. 16,50,000**. This is below the Rs. 20 lakh cap, so the full Rs. 16.5 lakh is payable.\n- B) Rs. 20 lakh is the cap, which is not reached here.\n- C) Rs. 33 lakh uses the wrong multiple.\n- D) is a decimal-place error.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A government employee dies after 7 years of qualifying service (death-gratuity table: 5 to <11 years = 12 × basic pay). If basic pay = Rs. 50,000, the death gratuity is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 10,00,000  C) Rs. 1,00,000  D) Rs. 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For 5 to <11 years, the rate is **12 × basic pay** = 12 × 50,000 = **Rs. 6,00,000**.\n- A) Rs. 3,00,000 uses 6× (the 1-to-<5-years rate).\n- B) Rs. 10,00,000 uses 20× (the 11-to-<20-years rate).\n- C) Rs. 1,00,000 uses 2× (the <1-year rate).\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A retiree splits Rs. 50 lakh: he must annuitise the minimum at age 60. He chooses \"lifetime without return of purchase price.\" Compared with \"lifetime with return of purchase price,\" his monthly annuity will be:\nA) Lower  B) Higher  C) The same  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Without return of purchase price\" pays a **higher** annuity because the insurer keeps the principal on death (no refund liability), so it can pay more each month.\n- A) \"Lower\" describes the \"with return\" option.\n- C) The two options pay different amounts.\n- D) The annuity is not zero in either case.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A debt-fund SWP (post 1 April 2023) generates Rs. 40,000 of gains for an investor in the 30% tax slab. What is the approximate tax on the gain (ignoring cess)?\nA) Rs. 4,000  B) Rs. 5,000  C) Rs. 12,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Debt-fund gains (post 1 April 2023) are taxed at the **investor's slab rate**, regardless of holding period. = 30% × 40,000 = **Rs. 12,000**.\n- A) Rs. 4,000 uses 10%.\n- B) Rs. 5,000 uses 12.5% (an equity rate).\n- D) Debt-fund gains are not exempt.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Suresh, aged 58, retires from a private company **covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972**, after **30 years and 8 months** of continuous service. His last drawn salary (Basic + DA) is **Rs. 80,000/month**. Over his career he built an **NPS Tier I corpus of Rs. 60 lakh** and an **EPF balance**. He also holds **mutual fund units** from which he plans to run a Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP) in retirement, and he is considering parking **Rs. 30 lakh** in the Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS) once he turns 60. His adviser walks him through the numbers.\n\n<details><summary>Q1. What tenure will be used for Suresh's gratuity, and what is his gratuity amount?</summary>\n\n**Tenure:** Since he is **covered** under the Act, a part-year of more than 6 months rounds up. 8 months > 6 months, so tenure = **31 years**.\n\n**Formula:** Gratuity = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26.\n= (15 × 80,000 × 31) ÷ 26\n= 3,72,00,000 ÷ 26\n= **Rs. 14,30,769 (≈ Rs. 14.31 lakh)**.\n\n**Intuition:** The \"15/26\" means the law pays roughly 15 days of salary for each year served, treating a month as 26 working days. Since Rs. 14.31 lakh is below the Rs. 20 lakh statutory cap, the entire amount is tax-exempt.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q2. At retirement, how must Suresh split his Rs. 60 lakh NPS corpus, and how much is the tax-free lump sum?</summary>\n\n**Rule (exit at/after age 60):** minimum **40%** must buy an annuity; up to **60%** can be taken as a lump sum.\n\n- Annuity portion = 40% × 60,00,000 = **Rs. 24,00,000**.\n- Lump sum = 60% × 60,00,000 = **Rs. 36,00,000**.\n\n**Tax:** The lump sum up to 60% is **tax-free under Section 10(12A)**, so the full Rs. 36 lakh is tax-free. The annuity income he later receives **will be taxable** as income in the year it is due. (His corpus exceeds Rs. 5 lakh, so the \"withdraw entire corpus\" option does not apply.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q3. Suresh's EPF own contribution in his final year was Rs. 2.8 lakh. Is the interest fully tax-free? How is his EPF withdrawal taxed?</summary>\n\n**Interest taxability:** Interest is exempt only on own contribution up to **Rs. 2.5 lakh/year**. His Rs. 2.8 lakh exceeds this by **Rs. 30,000** (2,80,000 − 2,50,000), so interest **on that Rs. 30,000 excess is taxable**.\n\n**Withdrawal taxability:** He has completed **30+ years** of continuous service — well beyond the **5-year** threshold — so the **EPF withdrawal itself is fully tax-free**.\n\n**Intuition:** The 5-year rule rewards long-term savers; only the interest on the *high* (>Rs. 2.5 lakh) annual contributions loses its exemption.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q4. Suresh runs an equity-fund SWP and books Rs. 2,00,000 of long-term capital gains in a year. What is his tax?</summary>\n\n**Rule:** Equity LTCG (holding > 12 months) is taxed at **12.5%**, after a **Rs. 1.25 lakh annual exemption**.\n\n- Taxable LTCG = 2,00,000 − 1,25,000 = **Rs. 75,000**.\n- Tax = 12.5% × 75,000 = **Rs. 9,375** (ignoring cess).\n\n**Intuition:** Only the gain *portion* of each SWP redemption is taxed (not the whole withdrawal), and the first Rs. 1.25 lakh of equity LTCG each year escapes tax. Remember: in an SWP units are *sold*, so the fund value falls — unlike FD interest where the corpus stays intact.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q5. At 60, Suresh deposits Rs. 30 lakh in SCSS but must exit after 3 years for an emergency. What penalty applies, and how is the interest taxed?</summary>\n\n**Deposit check:** Rs. 30 lakh equals the **maximum SCSS limit**, so the deposit is valid.\n\n**Premature-closure penalty:** Exit **between 2 and less than 5 years** carries a **1% penalty on the deposit**.\n- Penalty = 1% × 30,00,000 = **Rs. 30,000**.\n\n**Tax on interest:** SCSS **interest is fully taxable**; since his annual interest comfortably exceeds **Rs. 50,000**, **TDS applies** on the interest earned. (The Rs. 30 lakh investment itself qualified for 80C deduction only up to Rs. 1.5 lakh.)\n\n**Intuition:** SCSS gives quarterly income and 80C benefit on entry, but the income is taxed — unlike PPF's fully exempt (EEE) treatment.\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "06", "title": "Miscellaneous Aspects of Retirement Planning", "module": "Retirement Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 6: Miscellaneous Aspects of Retirement Planning — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **investment adviser's role** in retirement planning\n- The **calculations** behind retirement planning: future cost of expenses, the corpus needed (Present Value), the monthly savings needed (PMT), and the **real rate of return**\n- The damage from **early withdrawals** and the benefit of **transferring** your corpus when you change jobs\n- The benefit of **stepping up** your contributions\n- How to **evaluate retirement products** at different life stages\n- The concept of **philanthropy** and how an adviser raises it with clients\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 The Adviser's Role in Retirement Planning\n\n**Simple idea:** A retirement plan is a jigsaw puzzle — pensions, Social Security, part-time work, home equity, investments. The adviser fits the pieces together to produce **reliable monthly income** once the client retires. This needs deep knowledge of **taxes, employer benefits, and retirement-plan rules**.\n\n**An adviser can advise on:**\n- **When** to take employer benefits and which **pension distribution** choices fit the client\n- Whether an **annuity** is suitable; how much should be in **guaranteed** investments\n- A reasonable **retirement income** estimate and a suitable **withdrawal rate** from a portfolio\n- What **taxable income** investments generate, and how to **restructure** to reduce taxable income\n- Whether to **pay off the mortgage** before/during retirement, or use a **reverse mortgage**\n- Whether to **keep life insurance** policies\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** No adviser should recommend anything until they understand the client's **time horizon, investment experience, goals, and risk tolerance**, plus all assets, liabilities, and income sources.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 Calculations for Retirement Planning\n\n**Why this matters:** Higher incomes and easy loans have raised lifestyles, but EMIs and education costs leave little for retirement. **Inflation** (the yearly rise in prices) erodes purchasing power, so future needs are far higher than today's.\n\n**Quick intuition (7% inflation):** Rs. 100 today → Rs. 107 next year. Rs. 10,000/month today → **Rs. 54,724/month after 25 years**.\n\n### Step 1 — Future cost of monthly expenses\n> **Future Expense = Present Expense × (1 + inflation)^years**\n\n**Worked example (book, Scenario 1):** Today's retirement-relevant monthly expense = Rs. 9,000 (60% of Rs. 15,000); 7% inflation; 25 years to retire.\n- Future = 9,000 × (1.07)^25 = **Rs. 48,847/month** at retirement.\n\n(Book also notes retirement expenses are roughly **50%–60%** of current expenses if liabilities are gone — but medical costs rise with age.)\n\n### Step 2 — The real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return\nDuring retirement the corpus earns a return **and** prices keep rising. We use the **real return** to size the corpus.\n> **Real Return = [(1 + return) ÷ (1 + post-retirement inflation)] − 1**\n\n**Worked example (book):** return 8%, post-retirement inflation 6%.\n- Real return = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **1.89% p.a.** → monthly ≈ **1.89%/12**.\n\n**Logic:** The corpus grows by 8%, but the cost of living rises 6%, so purchasing power grows only ~1.89%. Using the *real* rate to discount future income automatically builds in the rising-cost effect.\n\n### Step 3 — The corpus needed (Excel **PV** function)\n> **=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])**\n\n| Argument | Meaning | Input in book example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **rate** | return per period | real return 1.89% p.a. → **1.89%/12** per month |\n| **nper** | number of periods | 25 retirement years × 12 = **300 months** |\n| **pmt** | fixed payment per period | the monthly expense at retirement, entered as a **negative** (it's an outflow) |\n| **fv** | future value (left blank → 0) | blank |\n| **type** | 0 = end of period (default) | 0 (blank) |\n\n**Book results (corpus at retirement, for a 35-year-old retiring at 60, life to 85, 7% pre-retirement & 6% post-retirement inflation, 8% return):**\n| Scenario | Today's monthly expense (60%) | Monthly expense at retirement | Corpus required |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Rs. 9,000 | Rs. 48,847 | **Rs. 1.17 crore** |\n| 2 | Rs. 15,000 | Rs. 81,411 | **Rs. 1.95 crore** |\n| 3 | Rs. 24,000 | Rs. 1,30,258 | **Rs. 3.11 crore** |\n\n💡 **Tip:** The corpus is just the present value (at the start of retirement) of all the inflation-rising monthly expenses, discounted at the real return.\n\n### Step 4 — Monthly savings needed (Excel **PMT** function)\n> **=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])**\n\n| Argument | Meaning | Input in book example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **rate** | return per period | 12% p.a. → **12%/12** per month |\n| **nper** | number of periods | years-to-retire × 12 |\n| **pv** | present value (the corpus target) | Rs. 1.18 crore |\n| **fv** | future value (blank → 0) | blank |\n| **type** | 0 = end of period | 0 |\n\n**Book results — why starting EARLY matters** (corpus Rs. 1.18 crore, 12% return, retire at 60):\n| Start age | Years investing | Monthly savings needed |\n|---|---|---|\n| 30 | 30 | **Rs. 3,376** |\n| 35 | 25 | **Rs. 6,280** |\n| 40 | 20 | **Rs. 11,928** |\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Delay = the monthly requirement balloons. Starting at 40 instead of 30 means saving **3.5× more per month** for the same corpus, because compounding had less time to work.\n\n### Benefits of stepping up investment\nAccumulation years bring emergencies and liabilities, so fixed contributions can fall short. A **step-up strategy** raises contributions periodically (a % each year or with a lump sum) to maximise savings. It can be done via **EPF, NPS, or mutual funds** (step-up SIPs increase the SIP automatically each year).\n\n**Book example:** Mr. A needs **Rs. 2.0 crore in 20 years** at 12% → a fixed monthly saving of **Rs. 20,217** (PMT). Instead, he **steps up 7% annually**, starting lower and increasing each year — easier on cash flow early on, and it rides the compounding benefit as income grows.\n\n💡 **Tip:** When income rises ~10%, step up the SIP by at least **5%–7%**; use every bonus/increment to add to the corpus.\n\n### Impact of pre-retirement withdrawals\nEPF statistics show many people withdraw early (education, marriage, medical, house), so they reach retirement under-funded. Early withdrawal **kills compounding** and forces you to work longer or cut your lifestyle.\n\n**Book example:** Mr. E withdraws **Rs. 75,000** from EPF with **30 years** to retirement; at **8.5% p.a.** he potentially **loses Rs. 8.66 lakh** of future corpus.\n*(Check: 75,000 × 1.085^30 ≈ 75,000 × 11.56 ≈ Rs. 8.67 lakh — the lost future value.)*\n\n### Benefits of transferring the corpus when changing jobs\nRetirement products reward **long-term continuity** (compounding). Switching jobs can break this.\n- **EPF:** opening a new account each switch creates **multiple accounts**, operational hassle, and possible **tax** on old balances. With the **UAN** (Universal Account Number — a 12-digit lifelong ID), you can **transfer EPF online**; keep UAN + KYC updated. Transferring also **adds the old service period** to your total service (helps cross the 5-year tax-free mark).\n- **NPS:** easier — you **cannot have multiple NPS accounts**; the same **PRAN** moves with you, or continues under the **All Citizen Model** if the new employer isn't registered.\n\n**Taxability:** Withdrawing old EPF on a job change in **under 5 years** makes it **taxable**; **transferring** avoids tax. The old corpus earns interest till age 58, but compounding is best preserved by continuing, not withdrawing.\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 Criteria to Evaluate Retirement Products (by life stage)\n\n**Idea:** Choose products by **life stage**. There are three stages around retirement.\n\n### Pre-retirement (15–20 years away — accumulate)\nHigher risk appetite; long horizon can ride out downside. Avoid pure income products here. **Evaluate on four factors:**\n1. **Cost** — higher cost = lower accumulation; some products hide total cost.\n2. **Return** — must **beat inflation** and grow money.\n3. **Risk** — understand it; align with risk tolerance.\n4. **Tax efficiency** — taxation matters a lot in long-term products; prefer tax-friendly options.\n\nGood fits: **EPF, NPS** (and PPF, some mutual funds) — long-term products.\n\n### Retirement (just retired — income + growth)\nNeed **steady income** *and* some **growth**. Two product types:\n- **Income-generating products** — evaluate on: (1) **Inflation** (income must beat inflation to survive longevity risk), (2) **Capital protection** (don't pick products that fluctuate the capital).\n- **Growth products** — horizon ~10–15 years; use the same four factors as pre-retirement, but products shift toward **PPF, certain mutual funds**, etc. (EPF/NPS may no longer be viable.)\n\n### Post-retirement (beyond ~75 years — pure income)\nObjective shifts fully to **income**; no horizon to grow. Priorities: **capital protection, low return, least risk**, plus **leaving money for heirs** where **liquidity** matters.\n\n---\n\n## 6.4 The Concept of Philanthropy\n\n**Definition:** **Philanthropy** = giving part of one's income/wealth for the benefit of society and the needy. It's no longer just for the wealthy — small earners contribute too. It brings personal satisfaction and addresses social disparity.\n\n**For advisers:** Talking about clients' values/passions **strengthens relationships** and builds trust. Steps to discuss philanthropy:\n1. **Starting the conversation** — keep it warm and curious; open the door to a charitable giving plan (see the sample script in the book).\n2. **Timing it right** — natural moments: a **liquidity event** (sale of business, inheritance), **drafting/revisiting a will**, a **life event** (retirement, marriage, birth of children/grandchildren), or the **annual client meeting**.\n3. **Following up** — philanthropy easily slips down a busy to-do list; if the client showed genuine interest, the adviser must follow up with **resources and next steps**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Adviser** assembles pensions, benefits, assets into reliable retirement income; advises only after knowing horizon, experience, goals, risk tolerance.\n- **Future Expense = Present × (1+inflation)^years.** Rs. 10,000 → Rs. 54,724 in 25 years at 7%.\n- **Real return = (1+return)/(1+inflation) − 1.** 8% & 6% → **1.89%**.\n- **Corpus** = Excel **PV(rate, nper, pmt)** using the real return and inflated monthly expense.\n- **Monthly savings** = Excel **PMT(rate, nper, pv)**. Starting at 30 vs 40 for a Rs. 1.18 cr corpus: **Rs. 3,376 vs Rs. 11,928/month**.\n- **Step-up** contributions to beat early cash-flow limits; **early withdrawals** destroy compounding (Rs. 75,000 → Rs. 8.66 lakh lost over 30 yrs at 8.5%).\n- **Transfer** EPF (via **UAN**) / NPS (same **PRAN**) on job change to keep compounding and avoid tax.\n- **Evaluate products by life stage:** pre-retirement (cost, return, risk, tax efficiency); retirement (inflation, capital protection + growth); post-retirement (capital protection, low risk, liquidity for heirs).\n- **Philanthropy:** start the conversation, time it right, follow up.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Future Expense | Present × (1 + inflation)^years |\n| Rs. 10,000/mo @7%, 25 yrs | Rs. 54,724/mo |\n| Real return (8%, 6%) | 1.89% p.a. |\n| Excel PV | =PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type]) → corpus needed |\n| Excel PMT | =PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type]) → monthly saving |\n| Corpus start age 30/35/40 (Rs.1.18 cr, 12%) | Rs. 3,376 / 6,280 / 11,928 per month |\n| Early withdrawal loss | Rs. 75,000 → Rs. 8.66 lakh over 30 yrs @8.5% |\n| UAN | 12-digit lifelong EPF ID for online transfer |\n| PRAN | Permanent Retirement Account Number (NPS) |\n| Retirement expense rule of thumb | ~50%–60% of current expenses |\n| Pre-retirement factors | Cost, Return, Risk, Tax efficiency |\n| Retirement income factors | Inflation, Capital protection |\n| Philanthropy steps | Start conversation, Time it right, Follow up |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 6: Miscellaneous Aspects of Retirement Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** In one line, what is the investment adviser's core job in retirement planning?\n> **A:** To fit together pensions, benefits, assets and income sources into a plan that produces **reliable monthly income** in retirement.\n\n**Q2.** Name four things an adviser should understand before recommending anything.\n> **A:** The client's **time horizon, investment experience, goals, and risk tolerance** (plus all assets, liabilities and income sources).\n\n**Q3.** What is the formula for the future cost of an expense?\n> **A:** **Future Expense = Present Expense × (1 + inflation)^years.**\n\n**Q4.** At 7% inflation, what does Rs. 10,000/month today become in 25 years?\n> **A:** **Rs. 54,724/month** (10,000 × 1.07^25).\n\n**Q5.** What is the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return formula?\n> **A:** **Real return = [(1 + return) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1.**\n\n**Q6.** Compute the real return for 8% return and 6% post-retirement inflation.\n> **A:** (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **1.89% p.a.**\n\n**Q7.** Which Excel function gives the corpus required, and what are its arguments?\n> **A:** **=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])** — rate (real return per period), nper (months in retirement), pmt (inflated monthly expense, negative), fv/type usually blank/0.\n\n**Q8.** Which Excel function gives the monthly savings needed?\n> **A:** **=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])** — rate (return per period), nper (months to retirement), pv (corpus target).\n\n**Q9.** Why is pmt entered as a negative number in the PV calculation?\n> **A:** Because it's a **cash outflow** (an expense), so it's prefixed with a minus sign.\n\n**Q10.** For a Rs. 1.18 crore corpus at 12% retiring at 60, how does monthly saving change by start age 30 vs 40?\n> **A:** Start at **30 → Rs. 3,376/month**; start at **40 → Rs. 11,928/month** (about 3.5× more) — because compounding has less time.\n\n**Q11.** What is a \"step-up\" investment strategy?\n> **A:** Increasing contributions periodically (a % each year or via lump sums) so savings start lower and rise with income — maximising the corpus.\n\n**Q12.** Mr. A needs Rs. 2 crore in 20 years at 12%. What fixed monthly saving (PMT) does he need?\n> **A:** **Rs. 20,217/month** (he can instead step up 7% annually to start lower).\n\n**Q13.** How much can be lost by withdrawing Rs. 75,000 from EPF with 30 years to retirement at 8.5%?\n> **A:** About **Rs. 8.66 lakh** of future corpus (the compounded future value of that Rs. 75,000).\n\n**Q14.** What is a UAN and why does it matter on a job change?\n> **A:** **Universal Account Number** — a 12-digit lifelong EPF ID enabling **online transfer** of EPF between employers (keep KYC updated).\n\n**Q15.** Why transfer (not withdraw) EPF when changing jobs within 5 years?\n> **A:** Withdrawing within 5 years is **taxable**; transferring is **tax-free** and **adds the old service period** to total service, preserving compounding.\n\n**Q16.** How does NPS handle a job change compared with EPF?\n> **A:** Easier — you **cannot have multiple NPS accounts**; the same **PRAN** moves with you, or continues under the **All Citizen Model**.\n\n**Q17.** What are the four evaluation factors for a pre-retirement (accumulation) product?\n> **A:** **Cost, Return, Risk, Tax efficiency.**\n\n**Q18.** Which products best fit the pre-retirement accumulation stage?\n> **A:** Long-term products: **EPF, NPS** (also PPF and certain mutual funds).\n\n**Q19.** What two factors evaluate income-generating products in the retirement stage?\n> **A:** **Inflation** (income must beat it for longevity) and **Capital protection**.\n\n**Q20.** In the retirement stage, two product baskets are needed. What are they?\n> **A:** One that **generates income** and one that **grows the corpus** (horizon ~10–15 years).\n\n**Q21.** What are the priorities for products in the post-retirement (beyond ~75) stage?\n> **A:** **Capital protection, low return, least risk**, plus **liquidity** to leave money for heirs.\n\n**Q22.** Roughly what fraction of current expenses are retirement expenses (if liabilities cleared)?\n> **A:** About **50%–60%** of current expenses (but medical costs rise with age).\n\n**Q23.** What is philanthropy in this context?\n> **A:** Giving part of one's income/wealth for the benefit of society and the needy — increasingly done by small earners too, not just the wealthy.\n\n**Q24.** Why should an adviser discuss philanthropy with clients?\n> **A:** It strengthens client relationships and **builds trust**, and is a meaningful, natural part of advising.\n\n**Q25.** What are the three steps to discuss philanthropy with a client?\n> **A:** (1) **Starting the conversation**, (2) **Timing it right**, (3) **Following up**.\n\n**Q26.** Name two natural moments to raise charitable giving.\n> **A:** A **liquidity event** (business sale/inheritance), **drafting/revisiting a will**, a **life event** (retirement, marriage, birth of children/grandchildren), or the **annual client meeting**.\n\n**Q27.** Growth-oriented investments suit which stage of retirement saving?\n> **A:** The **accumulation stage** (pre-retirement), where the long horizon allows riding out downside risk.\n\n**Q28.** What does inflation do to the retirement corpus required?\n> **A:** It **increases** the corpus required (it raises future expenses).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 6: Miscellaneous Aspects of Retirement Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario) — followed by a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM Series X-B exam carries **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you 0.25 of the question's marks — guess only when you can eliminate options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What is the central role an investment adviser plays in retirement planning?\nA) Guaranteeing fixed returns on all investments  B) Fitting together pensions, benefits, home equity and assets into reliable monthly retirement income  C) Filing the client's income tax returns  D) Managing the client's bank account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook describes the adviser as someone who puts the \"pieces of a puzzle\" (pensions, Social Security, part-time work, home equity, investments) together to produce reliable monthly income once the client retires.\n- A) is wrong — no adviser can guarantee returns; markets fluctuate.\n- C) is wrong — filing tax returns is a compliance/accounting task, not the adviser's core retirement role (though tax knowledge helps).\n- D) is wrong — operating a bank account is not the adviser's planning function.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Before making any recommendation, an adviser must understand the client's:\nA) Favourite mutual fund house  B) Time horizon, investment experience, goals and risk tolerance  C) Social media following  D) Employer's stock price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states an adviser will not recommend anything until they understand the client's expected time horizon, level of investment experience, goals and tolerance for investment risk (plus assets, liabilities and income sources).\n- A), C), D) are irrelevant to a suitability assessment and are not mentioned in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Inflation is best defined as:\nA) The rate at which interest is paid on deposits  B) The rate at which prices of essential items increase every year  C) The fall in stock prices  D) The tax on income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook defines inflation as \"the rate at which prices of all essential items increase every year,\" which reduces future purchasing power.\n- A) describes a deposit interest rate, not inflation.\n- C) describes a market correction.\n- D) describes income tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** According to the workbook example, an item costing Rs. 100 today will cost how much next year if inflation is 7%?\nA) Rs. 100  B) Rs. 105  C) Rs. 107  D) Rs. 117\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future price = 100 × (1 + 7%) = 100 × 1.07 = Rs. 107. This is the workbook's own illustration of inflation.\n- A) ignores inflation entirely.\n- B) uses 5%, not the stated 7%.\n- D) uses 17%, which is not the given rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The workbook states that Rs. 10,000 per month spent today will become how much per month after 25 years at 7% inflation?\nA) Rs. 25,000  B) Rs. 48,847  C) Rs. 54,274  D) Rs. 81,411\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future Expense = Present × (1 + inflation)^years = 10,000 × (1.07)^25 ≈ Rs. 54,274/month. This is the exact figure in the workbook.\n- A) is the original amount roughly, ignoring growth.\n- B) is the Scenario-1 figure for Rs. 9,000 today, not Rs. 10,000.\n- D) is the Scenario-2 figure for Rs. 15,000 today.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** As a rule of thumb in the workbook, expenses at retirement are roughly what fraction of one's existing expenses (if there are no liabilities)?\nA) 20%–30%  B) 50%–60%  C) 75%–85%  D) 100%–110%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says one can estimate retirement expenses at roughly 50%–60% of current expenses if there are no liabilities, though high medical costs at older ages should still be considered.\n- A) is too low.\n- C) and D) are too high — they ignore the freedom from loan EMIs and children's goals at retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which Excel function does the workbook use to calculate the retirement corpus required?\nA) =PMT()  B) =FV()  C) =PV()  D) =RATE()\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook states: \"For calculating Retirement Corpus, Present Value Formula in excel is used: =PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])\".\n- A) PMT gives the periodic payment (used for monthly savings, not the corpus).\n- B) FV gives a future value, the opposite direction.\n- D) RATE solves for an interest rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which Excel function does the workbook use to calculate the monthly savings required to reach the corpus?\nA) =PV()  B) =PMT()  C) =NPER()  D) =IRR()\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook uses \"=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])\" to find the fixed monthly savings needed to reach the corpus target.\n- A) PV finds the corpus, not the contribution.\n- C) NPER solves for the number of periods.\n- D) IRR finds a return from cash flows.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In the Excel PV/PMT functions, what does the \"type\" argument indicate when set to 0 (or left blank)?\nA) Payments are made at the beginning of each period  B) Payments are made at the end of each period  C) Payments are annual  D) Payments are tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook, type = 0 (or omitted) means payments are made at the *end* of each period; type = 1 means the beginning.\n- A) is type = 1, the opposite.\n- C) and D) are not what \"type\" controls — it only flags timing within a period.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** In the corpus calculation, why is the monthly expense (pmt) entered with a minus sign in the PV formula?\nA) Because of negative marking  B) Because it is a cash outflow (an expense)  C) To reduce tax  D) Because Excel always needs negatives\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook notes \"Since these are cash outflows i.e. expenses, the amounts are prefixed with a 'minus' sign.\" Excel cash-flow sign convention treats outflows as negative.\n- A) is unrelated (negative marking is an exam concept).\n- C) is irrelevant to a sign convention.\n- D) is false — Excel does not \"always\" need negatives; the sign reflects direction of cash flow.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** In the workbook's main corpus example, what is the assumed return on the retirement corpus during retirement?\nA) 6% p.a.  B) 7% p.a.  C) 8% p.a.  D) 12% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook assumes an 8% p.a. return on the corpus during retirement.\n- A) 6% is the post-retirement inflation assumption, not the return.\n- B) 7% is the pre-retirement inflation rate.\n- D) 12% is the accumulation-phase return used in the savings (PMT) example.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In the workbook's main corpus example, the post-retirement inflation is assumed to be:\nA) 6% p.a.  B) 7% p.a.  C) 8% p.a.  D) 1.89% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Post-retirement inflation is taken as 6% p.a.\n- B) 7% is the pre-retirement inflation used to grow today's expenses to retirement.\n- C) 8% is the return on the corpus.\n- D) 1.89% is the resulting inflation-adjusted (real) return, not the inflation itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The inflation-adjusted (real) return in the workbook example works out to:\nA) 2% p.a.  B) 1.89% p.a.  C) 14% p.a.  D) 0.89% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1+8%)/(1+6%)] − 1 = (1.08/1.06) − 1 ≈ 1.89% p.a., as stated in the workbook.\n- A) 2% is the rough difference (8%−6%) but the formula gives 1.89%, not a simple subtraction.\n- C) and D) do not match the formula result.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In the workbook's corpus example, the individual is aged 35 and wishes to retire at:\nA) 55  B) 58  C) 60  D) 65\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The example uses retirement at age 60, giving 25 years to retire (60 − 35).\n- A), B), D) are not the ages used; 60 is the workbook's assumption (and the standard retirement age elsewhere in the chapter).\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** In the workbook's corpus example, life expectancy is assumed up to age:\nA) 75  B) 80  C) 85  D) 90\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Life expectancy is assumed till age 85, giving 25 years post-retirement (85 − 60).\n- A) 75 is mentioned only as a \"may expect\" figure that people often outlive.\n- B) and D) are not the assumed life expectancy in the example.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The number of months used as nper in the corpus PV calculation (25 post-retirement years) is:\nA) 25  B) 240  C) 300  D) 360\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 25 years × 12 = 300 months, as stated in the workbook.\n- A) is in years, not months.\n- B) 240 = 20 years × 12.\n- D) 360 = 30 years × 12.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** For Scenario 1 (today's relevant monthly expense Rs. 9,000), the corpus required in the workbook is approximately:\nA) Rs. 1.17 crore  B) Rs. 1.95 crore  C) Rs. 3.11 crore  D) Rs. 48,847\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Scenario 1 gives a corpus of about Rs. 1.17 crore.\n- B) Rs. 1.95 crore is Scenario 2 (Rs. 15,000).\n- C) Rs. 3.11 crore is Scenario 3 (Rs. 24,000).\n- D) Rs. 48,847 is the monthly expense at retirement, not the corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A \"step-up\" strategy in retirement saving means:\nA) Withdrawing money each year  B) Increasing contributions periodically (a % each year or a lump sum)  C) Reducing contributions as income falls  D) Switching to fixed deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a step-up strategy one starts with a fixed amount but steps up contributions by a certain percentage every year (or via lump sums) to maximise savings.\n- A) describes withdrawals, the opposite.\n- C) describes reducing savings.\n- D) is unrelated to stepping up.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Step-up contributions can be made through which of the following per the workbook?\nA) Only fixed deposits  B) EPF, NPS or mutual funds  C) Only physical gold  D) Only real estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says stepping up can be done through investments in EPF, NPS or mutual funds (e.g., step-up SIPs).\n- A), C), D) are not the avenues the workbook lists for stepping up.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A step-up SIP in mutual funds works by:\nA) Stopping the SIP after one year  B) Automatically increasing the SIP contribution after a specific period  C) Paying a one-time amount only  D) Reducing the SIP each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A step-up SIP uses an automated feature where contributions increase after a specific period (e.g., Rs. 5,000 in 2023, Rs. 5,000+10% in 2024, and so on).\n- A) and D) describe stopping/reducing, the opposite.\n- C) describes a lump sum, not a step-up SIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The workbook suggests that if income increases by 10%, the investor can step up the SIP by at least:\nA) 1%–2%  B) 5%–7%  C) 15%–20%  D) 50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states that if income rises by 10%, one can step up the investment by at least 5% to 7%.\n- A) is too small to make a meaningful difference.\n- C) and D) exceed the workbook's suggested range and the income increase itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Early (pre-retirement) withdrawal from a retirement fund mainly harms the corpus because it:\nA) Increases taxes only  B) Destroys the benefit of compounding on the withdrawn amount  C) Raises inflation  D) Lowers life expectancy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Withdrawing early removes the amount from years of compounding growth, so the corpus falls short; the workbook stresses this detrimental impact.\n- A) tax may apply, but the core harm described is lost compounding.\n- C) and D) are unrelated to a withdrawal.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** According to the workbook, common reasons people withdraw EPF before retirement include all EXCEPT:\nA) Children's education  B) Marriage  C) Medical emergencies  D) Buying lottery tickets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook lists children's education, marriage, medical emergencies and house purchase as common early-withdrawal reasons. Lottery tickets are not mentioned.\n- A), B), C) are all explicitly listed reasons.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In the workbook's early-withdrawal example, Mr. E withdraws Rs. 75,000 from EPF with 30 years to retirement, potentially losing:\nA) Rs. 75,000  B) Rs. 1.5 lakh  C) Rs. 8.66 lakh  D) Rs. 20,217\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At 8.5% p.a. for 30 years, the lost future value is about Rs. 8.66 lakh.\n- A) is just the amount withdrawn, ignoring lost growth.\n- B) understates the lost compounding.\n- D) is the unrelated Mr. A monthly-savings figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** What is the EPF interest rate used in the workbook's early-withdrawal example?\nA) 7% p.a.  B) 8% p.a.  C) 8.5% p.a.  D) 12% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The example assumes the EPF balance earns 8.5% p.a. over the 30-year period.\n- A) 7% is the inflation rate elsewhere.\n- B) 8% is the corpus return assumption.\n- D) 12% is the accumulation return in the PMT example.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** UAN (Universal Account Number) is:\nA) A 10-digit tax ID  B) A 12-digit unique number allotted to each EPF-contributing employee for life  C) An NPS account number  D) A bank IFSC code\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook footnote defines UAN as a 12-digit unique number allotted to each EPF-contributing employee that remains the same throughout life, regardless of how many employers they join.\n- A) describes a different length and purpose.\n- C) PRAN is the NPS identifier.\n- D) IFSC identifies a bank branch.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** With the UAN in force, transferring EPF from an old employer to a new employer can be done:\nA) Only by post  B) Completely online  C) Only by visiting the EPFO office  D) It cannot be transferred\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states that with UAN, an employee can transfer the EPF account from the old to the new employer completely online, provided UAN, KYC and personal details are updated.\n- A) and C) are outdated manual methods.\n- D) is false — EPF is transferable.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** PRAN in the context of NPS stands for:\nA) Provident Retirement Account Number  B) Permanent Retirement Account Number  C) Pension Refund Account Number  D) Public Retirement Annuity Number\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PRAN is the Permanent Retirement Account Number generated for an NPS subscriber; the same PRAN moves with the subscriber across employers.\n- A), C), D) are incorrect expansions of the abbreviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A key difference between NPS and EPF on job change is that:\nA) NPS allows multiple accounts; EPF allows only one  B) One cannot have multiple NPS accounts — the same PRAN shifts; EPF can end up with multiple accounts  C) Both must be closed on job change  D) Neither can be transferred\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says NPS shifting is easier because one cannot hold multiple NPS accounts; the same PRAN moves (or continues under the All Citizen Model). EPF, by contrast, can result in multiple accounts when switching jobs.\n- A) reverses the facts.\n- C) and D) are false — both products continue across jobs.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** If an employee changes employer in less than 5 years and withdraws the old EPF balance, the withdrawal:\nA) Is always tax-free  B) Becomes taxable  C) Earns double interest  D) Is forfeited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states that withdrawing the old EPF balance on a job change of less than 5 years makes the withdrawal taxable; transferring the balance avoids this tax incidence.\n- A) is wrong because the 5-year condition is not met.\n- C) and D) are not consequences mentioned in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Transferring the old EPF balance to the new employer's PF account is beneficial because it:\nA) Resets the service period to zero  B) Includes the old service period in computing total service period  C) Reduces the interest earned  D) Triggers immediate tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says transferring adds the old service period to the total, helping toward the 5-year tax-free threshold, and preserves compounding without tax.\n- A) is the opposite — transfer preserves, not resets, service.\n- C) and D) describe disadvantages of withdrawing, not transferring.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** During the pre-retirement (accumulation) stage, on which FOUR factors should a retirement product be evaluated?\nA) Liquidity, colour, brand, size  B) Cost, Return, Risk, Tax efficiency  C) Inflation, capital protection, heirs, liquidity  D) Cost only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook lists Cost, Return, Risk and Tax Efficiency as the four evaluation factors in the pre-retirement (accumulation) stage.\n- A) lists irrelevant attributes.\n- C) lists the retirement/post-retirement criteria.\n- D) is incomplete — there are four factors.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** For income-generating products at the retirement stage, the workbook says to evaluate on which two factors?\nA) Cost and Return  B) Inflation and Capital Protection  C) Risk and Tax efficiency  D) Liquidity and Brand\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Income-generating products are evaluated on Inflation (income must beat inflation to survive longevity risk) and Capital Protection.\n- A) and C) are the accumulation/growth-phase factors.\n- D) is not the stated pair.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** In the post-retirement stage (beyond ~75 years), the primary objective shifts to:\nA) Maximum growth  B) Generating income with capital protection, low return and least risk  C) Aggressive equity investing  D) Taking high risk for high return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond ~75, the objective shifts fully to income; priorities are capital protection, low return, least risk, plus leaving money for heirs (where liquidity matters).\n- A), C), D) describe accumulation-stage, high-risk approaches unsuitable for very old age.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Philanthropy, as described in the workbook, is:\nA) A tax-avoidance scheme only for the wealthy  B) Giving part of one's income/wealth for the benefit of society and the needy  C) A type of pension product  D) A government loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook describes philanthropy as the urge to do for others — contributing part of one's income for noble causes — and notes it is no longer limited to the rich.\n- A) is wrong — it is open to all income levels and is not framed as tax avoidance.\n- C) and D) are unrelated financial products.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A 32-year-old client with 25+ years to retirement and a high risk appetite asks which type of product fits his accumulation goal. Which is most appropriate?\nA) A pure income (annuity) product  B) A growth-oriented product that can ride out market cycles  C) A short-term liquid fund only  D) A reverse mortgage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In the pre-retirement (accumulation) phase, the workbook advises products that \"allow money to grow even though downside risk may be there,\" because a long horizon lets one ride through downturns.\n- A) Income products are for the retirement/post-retirement phases, not accumulation.\n- C) A liquid fund won't beat inflation over decades.\n- D) A reverse mortgage is an income tool for retirees who own a home, not an accumulation product.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client says: \"Inflation doesn't matter — I'll just need the same rupee amount I spend now when I retire.\" How should the adviser correct this?\nA) Agree — expenses stay flat  B) Explain that inflation raises future expenses, so the corpus needed grows  C) Tell him to ignore the corpus entirely  D) Say inflation reduces the corpus needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation increases the rupee value of future expenses (Rs. 10,000 → Rs. 54,724 in 25 years at 7%), so the corpus required rises. The corpus must fund inflated, not current, expenses.\n- A) is the client's error.\n- C) ignores planning altogether.\n- D) reverses the relationship — inflation increases, not decreases, the corpus needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Which set of inputs correctly fills the PV(rate, nper, pmt) function for the corpus in the workbook's example?\nA) rate = 8%/12, nper = 300, pmt = −48,847  B) rate = 1.89%/12, nper = 300, pmt = −48,847  C) rate = 7%/12, nper = 360, pmt = −9,000  D) rate = 6%/12, nper = 25, pmt = −48,847\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The corpus PV uses the *real* return 1.89% p.a. (i.e., 1.89%/12 per month), nper = 300 months (25 yrs × 12), and pmt = the inflated monthly expense at retirement (Rs. 48,847) entered negative.\n- A) wrongly uses the nominal 8% instead of the real return — that would double-count inflation.\n- C) uses today's expense and the wrong rate/nper.\n- D) uses inflation (6%) as the rate and 25 (years, not months) for nper.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Why does the corpus calculation use the *real* return (1.89%) rather than the nominal return (8%)?\nA) To make the corpus look smaller  B) Because during retirement the corpus grows at 8% but living costs also rise at 6%, so purchasing power grows only ~1.89%  C) Because 8% is illegal  D) Because real return is always higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The real return captures that the corpus earns 8% while expenses inflate at 6%; net purchasing-power growth is ~1.89%. Discounting at the real rate builds the rising-cost effect into the corpus automatically.\n- A) is not the purpose — accuracy is.\n- C) is nonsense.\n- D) is false — the real return is lower than the nominal when inflation is positive.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Two clients need the same Rs. 1.18 crore corpus at age 60. Client X starts at 30, Client Y at 40, both earning 12%. Which statement is correct?\nA) Both save the same per month  B) Client Y (starting at 40) must save much more per month  C) Client X must save more per month  D) Neither needs to save\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook table shows Rs. 3,376/month from age 30 vs Rs. 11,928/month from age 40 for the same corpus. Starting later means fewer years of compounding, so the monthly requirement balloons.\n- A) is wrong — the amounts differ greatly.\n- C) reverses the result.\n- D) ignores that a corpus must be funded.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A client wants to keep his retirement saving easy on cash flow early in his career but still hit a big target. Which strategy fits best?\nA) Withdraw early and reinvest  B) A step-up SIP that starts lower and increases each year  C) Stop saving until age 50  D) Keep everything in a savings account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A step-up strategy lets one start with a lower contribution and raise it yearly as income grows, optimising savings and capturing compounding — exactly the workbook's recommendation.\n- A) destroys compounding.\n- C) wastes the most valuable early years of compounding.\n- D) earns too little to beat inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Mr. A needs Rs. 2.0 crore in 20 years at 12%. The workbook says a fixed monthly saving of Rs. 20,217 achieves this. If instead he steps up contributions 7% annually, the main advantage is:\nA) He pays no tax  B) He can start with a lower amount and increase it as income grows, easing early cash flow  C) He reaches the goal in 10 years  D) He needs no return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stepping up lets Mr. A begin below Rs. 20,217 and raise contributions yearly, which is gentler on early finances while still riding compounding.\n- A) tax is not the point here.\n- C) the horizon is still 20 years.\n- D) returns (12%) are still essential.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A client is tempted to withdraw from EPF 30 years before retirement for a discretionary purchase. The adviser should emphasise that:\nA) It has no effect on the corpus  B) The withdrawn amount loses decades of compounding, shrinking the retirement corpus significantly  C) It increases the corpus  D) It reduces inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook's Mr. E example shows Rs. 75,000 withdrawn 30 years early can cost ~Rs. 8.66 lakh of future corpus at 8.5%. Early withdrawal kills compounding.\n- A) is false — the impact is large.\n- C) reverses it.\n- D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An employee switching jobs in under 5 years asks how to avoid tax on his old EPF balance. The best advice is:\nA) Withdraw the full balance now  B) Transfer the EPF balance to the new employer (no tax incidence; old service period counts)  C) Open a new EPF account and leave the old one  D) Convert it to a fixed deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Transferring avoids the tax that a sub-5-year withdrawal would trigger, and adds the old service to total service period, helping cross the 5-year tax-free mark.\n- A) Withdrawing under 5 years is taxable.\n- C) Leaving an old account loses compounding continuity and can be taxed.\n- D) is not a transfer option and breaks the EPF structure.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A subscriber switches to an employer not registered for NPS. What happens to his PRAN?\nA) It is cancelled  B) He must open a new PRAN  C) He can continue the same PRAN under the All Citizen Model  D) The corpus is forfeited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook says if the new employer isn't registered, the employee can still continue the PRAN under the 'All Citizen Model'.\n- A), B), D) contradict the rule that one cannot have multiple NPS accounts and the PRAN is permanent.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which scenario is the MOST natural moment for an adviser to raise philanthropy, per the workbook?\nA) During a market crash  B) A liquidity event such as the sale of a business or an inheritance  C) When the client is filing a complaint  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook lists a liquidity event (business sale, inheritance) as a natural spark, along with drafting a will, a life event, or the annual meeting.\n- A) A crash is a stressful, unsuitable moment.\n- C) A complaint is not the context.\n- D) is wrong — the workbook actively encourages the conversation.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A 78-year-old retiree wants to choose a product. Which evaluation criterion should dominate?\nA) Maximum growth and high risk  B) Capital protection, low return, least risk, with liquidity for heirs  C) Tax efficiency for long-term accumulation  D) Beating inflation through equities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond ~75 (post-retirement), the objective is pure income with capital protection, low return, least risk, and liquidity to leave money for heirs.\n- A), D) are accumulation-stage, high-risk approaches.\n- C) suits the pre-retirement phase, not very old age.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A just-retired client needs both steady income and some continued growth. The adviser should:\nA) Put everything into one annuity  B) Use two baskets — income-generating products and growth products (10–15 yr horizon)  C) Put everything into equities  D) Hold only cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At the retirement stage the workbook recommends two product types: income-generating (judged on inflation and capital protection) and growth (10–15 year horizon, same four factors as pre-retirement).\n- A) ignores the growth need.\n- C) is too risky for the income need.\n- D) earns too little and loses to inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Why does the workbook say EPF/NPS may no longer be \"viable\" for the growth basket once a person has retired?\nA) They are illegal after retirement  B) The growth-basket horizon is only ~10–15 years and the product mix shifts (e.g., to PPF, certain mutual funds)  C) They have no returns  D) They are too liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At retirement the growth horizon shrinks to 10–15 years, so the workbook says one may rely on PPF, certain mutual funds and others instead of EPF/NPS.\n- A) is false.\n- C) is false — they do earn returns.\n- D) is not the stated reason.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client mistakenly thinks \"real return = nominal return − inflation\" and computes 8% − 6% = 2%. The precise workbook value is 1.89%. The difference arises because:\nA) The workbook made an error  B) The exact formula divides: (1+return)/(1+inflation) − 1, which is slightly less than simple subtraction  C) Inflation should be added  D) Returns are taxed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exact real return = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 0.01887 ≈ 1.89%, slightly below the 2% rough subtraction because of the compounding interaction in the denominator.\n- A) the workbook is correct.\n- C) inflation is divided/subtracted, not added.\n- D) tax is irrelevant to this formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Among the following, which is a service an adviser explicitly offers per the workbook?\nA) Predicting exact stock prices  B) Advising whether a reverse mortgage is a good option for the client  C) Guaranteeing pension amounts  D) Running the client's business\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook lists \"If a reverse mortgage is a good option for the client\" among adviser services.\n- A) and C) involve impossible guarantees/predictions.\n- D) is outside the adviser's role.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client aged 50 wants to boost retirement savings with tax-exempt returns via an employer avenue. The workbook suggests:\nA) Reverse mortgage  B) Voluntary Provident Fund (VPF)  C) Withdrawing EPF  D) A personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says someone aged 50+ wishing to boost savings can consider the Voluntary Provident Fund, maximising gains because returns are tax-exempt in this avenue.\n- A) provides income, not accumulation.\n- C) reduces the corpus.\n- D) is a liability, not savings.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** For NPS contributions, the workbook notes that beyond the employer's contribution:\nA) No further investment is allowed  B) There is no limit to invest — one can add via SIP or lump sum  C) Only Rs. 50,000 per year is allowed  D) Contributions must stop at age 50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states that with NPS there is no limit to invest; beyond the employer's contribution one can invest any amount through SIP or lump sum to step up.\n- A), C), D) impose limits the workbook does not state in this context.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which statement about the *cost* factor in evaluating accumulation-phase products is correct?\nA) Higher cost means higher accumulation  B) More cost means less earning and so less accumulation; some products hide total cost  C) Cost is irrelevant in long-term products  D) Cost only matters in the income phase\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook: \"More the cost less is the earning and so the accumulation,\" and warns the total cost may not be clear in some products.\n- A) reverses the relationship.\n- C) and D) understate cost's importance in long-term accumulation.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A client asks why transferring the corpus on a job change is \"more rewarding.\" The best workbook-based answer is:\nA) It avoids all paperwork  B) It preserves the compounding effect and avoids restarting contributions from scratch  C) It guarantees higher returns  D) It removes the need to save\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Transferring keeps the old corpus compounding and avoids starting over (where the required contribution has already risen), keeping the client on the planned path.\n- A) is a minor side effect, not the core benefit.\n- C) no return is guaranteed.\n- D) is false — saving continues.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** In the savings (PMT) example, what is the assumed annual return during the accumulation years?\nA) 8%  B) 12%  C) 6%  D) 1.89%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The PMT example uses 12% p.a. (12%/12 per month) for accumulation.\n- A) 8% is the retirement-phase corpus return.\n- C) 6% is post-retirement inflation.\n- D) 1.89% is the real return for the corpus PV.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client retiring soon finds his corpus is inadequate. Per the module-end questions, a valid solution is to:\nA) Invest the shortfall in lottery  B) Postpone retirement  C) Reduce periodic savings  D) Ignore it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook's module-end question lists \"Postpone retirement\" as a solution to manage corpus inadequacy closer to retirement.\n- A) is reckless.\n- C) worsens the shortfall.\n- D) leaves the problem unsolved.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which of these does inflation do to retirement planning (module-end question)?\nA) Reduces the periodic savings required  B) Reduces the nominal return generated  C) Increases the retirement corpus required  D) Increases the value of the corpus created\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Inflation raises future expenses, so the corpus required increases.\n- A) is the opposite — more saving is needed.\n- B) inflation doesn't reduce nominal return; it erodes real value.\n- D) inflation erodes, not increases, the corpus's purchasing power.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** When should a retirement corpus be reviewed (module-end question)?\nA) Never  B) Only at retirement  C) Every time there is a significant change in financial situation  D) Only once at the start\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook's module-end answer is to review whenever there is a significant change in the financial situation.\n- A), B), D) are too rigid; a plan must adapt to life changes.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Growth-oriented investments are suitable for which stage of retirement saving (module-end question)?\nA) Distribution stage  B) Income stage  C) Accumulation stage  D) Withdrawal stage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Growth investments suit the accumulation stage, when the horizon is long and risk appetite higher.\n- A), B), D) are distribution/income phases where capital protection and income dominate.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A client opened a separate EPF account at each of his four jobs without transferring. The workbook warns this causes:\nA) Higher guaranteed returns  B) Operational issues, possible taxation of older balances, and lost compounding  C) Automatic merging by EPFO  D) No consequences\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Multiple EPF accounts bring operational hassle, a cascading tax effect on older balances if conditions aren't met, and lost compounding on the old corpus.\n- A) reverses the harm.\n- C) accounts don't auto-merge — the employee must transfer.\n- D) understates the real downsides.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which is the correct order of the three life stages used to evaluate retirement products?\nA) Retirement → Pre-retirement → Post-retirement  B) Pre-retirement → Retirement → Post-retirement  C) Post-retirement → Retirement → Pre-retirement  D) Pre-retirement → Post-retirement → Retirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook orders the stages: Pre-retirement (accumulate), Retirement (income + growth), Post-retirement (pure income).\n- A), C), D) scramble the natural sequence.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Why does the workbook say \"any income benefit product may not be a viable option\" in the pre-retirement phase?\nA) Income products are illegal  B) The focus is on accumulation/growth; income products don't grow money for the long horizon  C) They are too risky  D) They have no taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Pre-retirement aims to accumulate and grow; pure income products don't suit a 15–20 year growth horizon.\n- A) is false.\n- C) income products are typically lower-risk, not riskier.\n- D) is irrelevant to suitability here.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An adviser raising philanthropy should, after the first conversation:\nA) Drop it forever  B) Follow up with resources and next steps if the client showed genuine interest  C) Invest the client's money in charity without asking  D) Charge a fee for talking\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook stresses following up — philanthropy can slip down a busy person's list, so the adviser should provide resources and next steps.\n- A) abandons an interested client.\n- C) acts without consent.\n- D) is not the workbook's guidance.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A client says retirement expenses will be higher than current expenses. The workbook's general view is:\nA) Expenses are usually higher  B) Expenses are generally lower (≈50–60%) once liabilities/children's goals end, but rising medical costs must be considered  C) Expenses are exactly the same  D) Expenses fall to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says retirement expenses are generally lower (roughly 50–60% of current) as loans and children's goals end, while flagging higher medical costs at older ages.\n- A) overstates.\n- C) and D) are extremes the workbook doesn't support.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** For the corpus PV calculation, the \"fv\" argument in the workbook example is:\nA) Set to the corpus value  B) Left blank (assumed 0)  C) Set to a negative number  D) Set to 8%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook leaves fv blank, so Excel assumes 0 — the plan is to draw the corpus down to zero over retirement.\n- A) confuses fv with the result (PV).\n- C) and D) are not how fv is used here.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which combination correctly matches the savings PMT inputs in the workbook (start age 30 case)?\nA) rate = 12%/12, nper = 360, pv = 1.18 crore  B) rate = 8%/12, nper = 300, pv = 1.18 crore  C) rate = 7%/12, nper = 240, pv = 2 crore  D) rate = 1.89%/12, nper = 360, pv = 1.18 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Starting at 30 gives 30 years × 12 = 360 months; rate = 12%/12; pv (target corpus) = Rs. 1.18 crore.\n- B) uses the wrong rate (8%) and nper (300 = 25 yrs).\n- C) uses inflation as rate and the wrong corpus/horizon.\n- D) uses the real return instead of the 12% accumulation return.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Why does delaying retirement contributions \"strain finances in later years\"?\nA) Because inflation falls  B) Because the required monthly saving balloons due to less time for compounding, colliding with other goals/liabilities  C) Because returns rise  D) Because taxes disappear\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Delay shrinks the compounding window, so the monthly requirement jumps (Rs. 3,376 at 30 vs Rs. 11,928 at 40), straining later finances when other goals and liabilities also compete.\n- A), C), D) are unrelated or false.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A client asks whether NPS or EPF is easier to carry across jobs. Correct answer:\nA) EPF, because it auto-transfers  B) NPS, because the same PRAN moves and one cannot hold multiple NPS accounts  C) Both are equally hard  D) Neither can move\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook calls NPS easier to shift — the same PRAN moves with the subscriber (or continues under All Citizen Model), and multiple accounts aren't possible.\n- A) EPF has more stringent clauses and can create multiple accounts.\n- C) and D) contradict the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An adviser wants to \"optimize the portfolio to produce a steady stream of retirement income.\" The workbook says the adviser should first:\nA) Sell all assets  B) Know where all the client's investments are, so the whole portfolio makes sense  C) Buy only one product  D) Ignore liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook says the adviser will want to know where all the client's investments are so the portfolio as a whole can be optimised for steady retirement income.\n- A) is destructive.\n- C) ignores diversification.\n- D) contradicts the need to understand the full financial picture.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client spends Rs. 12,000/month today. At 7% inflation, what will the equivalent monthly expense be in 25 years (use the same factor as the workbook's Rs. 10,000 → Rs. 54,274)?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 54,274  C) Rs. 65,129  D) Rs. 84,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future = Present × (1.07)^25. The workbook's factor is 54,274 ÷ 10,000 = 5.4274. So 12,000 × 5.4274 ≈ Rs. 65,129.\n- A) ignores inflation growth.\n- B) is the figure for Rs. 10,000, not Rs. 12,000.\n- D) overstates (would imply a factor of 7).\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Today's relevant monthly expense is Rs. 9,000, inflation 7%, 25 years to retire. Using Future = PV × (1.07)^25, the monthly expense at retirement is:\nA) Rs. 18,000  B) Rs. 48,847  C) Rs. 81,411  D) Rs. 1,30,258\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 9,000 × (1.07)^25 = 9,000 × 5.4274 ≈ Rs. 48,847 (workbook Scenario 1). The formula compounds today's expense forward at inflation.\n- A) merely doubles, ignoring compounding.\n- C) is the Rs. 15,000 case.\n- D) is the Rs. 24,000 case.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Compute the real (inflation-adjusted) return if the corpus earns 9% and post-retirement inflation is 6%, using (1+r)/(1+i) − 1.\nA) 3.00%  B) 2.83%  C) 1.89%  D) 15.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.09/1.06) − 1 = 1.028302 − 1 = 0.02830 ≈ 2.83%. The division (not simple subtraction) gives slightly less than 3%.\n- A) 3% is the rough subtraction 9%−6%.\n- C) 1.89% is the workbook's 8%/6% case.\n- D) is meaningless here.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Verify the early-withdrawal loss: Mr. E withdraws Rs. 75,000 with 30 years left at 8.5% p.a. Future Value = PV × (1+r)^n. Approximately how much is lost?\nA) Rs. 75,000  B) Rs. 2.55 lakh  C) Rs. 8.66 lakh  D) Rs. 22.5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV = 75,000 × (1.085)^30. (1.085)^30 ≈ 11.56, so 75,000 × 11.56 ≈ Rs. 8.67 lakh ≈ Rs. 8.66 lakh (workbook). This is the future corpus the withdrawal sacrifices.\n- A) ignores compounding.\n- B) ≈ 75,000 × 3.4, far too few years' growth.\n- D) overstates the compounding factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** If instead Mr. E had 20 years (not 30) to retirement at 8.5%, the future value lost on Rs. 75,000 would be closest to (use (1.085)^20 ≈ 5.11):\nA) Rs. 75,000  B) Rs. 1.91 lakh  C) Rs. 3.83 lakh  D) Rs. 8.66 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV = 75,000 × (1.085)^20 ≈ 75,000 × 5.11 ≈ Rs. 3.83 lakh. Fewer years means less compounding, so the loss is smaller than the 30-year Rs. 8.66 lakh.\n- A) ignores growth.\n- B) uses about half the correct factor.\n- D) is the 30-year figure, more time than given.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client wants the same Rs. 1.18 crore corpus. At 12% return, the workbook gives Rs. 6,280/month if he starts at 35 (25 years). Starting at 40 (20 years) the requirement is Rs. 11,928. By roughly what multiple does the monthly saving rise when he delays from 35 to 40?\nA) ~1.1×  B) ~1.9×  C) ~3.5×  D) ~5×\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 11,928 ÷ 6,280 ≈ 1.9×. Five fewer years of compounding nearly doubles the monthly requirement.\n- A) understates the jump.\n- C) ~3.5× is the 30-vs-40 comparison (3,376 → 11,928).\n- D) overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** For the same Rs. 1.18 crore corpus at 12%, starting at 30 needs Rs. 3,376/month and at 40 needs Rs. 11,928/month. The 40-year-old must save how many times more than the 30-year-old?\nA) ~1.5×  B) ~2×  C) ~3.5×  D) ~10×\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 11,928 ÷ 3,376 ≈ 3.53 ≈ 3.5×. Starting 10 years later more than triples the monthly burden — the cost of lost compounding.\n- A) and B) understate.\n- D) overstates.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Using PMT logic, which input change would REDUCE the monthly savings needed for a fixed corpus target?\nA) Increasing nper (more years to invest)  B) Reducing the rate of return  C) Increasing the corpus target  D) Shortening the horizon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — More periods (a longer horizon) lets compounding do more work, so each month's required saving falls (e.g., 30 years needs Rs. 3,376 vs 20 years' Rs. 11,928).\n- B) a lower return raises the saving needed.\n- C) a bigger target raises the saving.\n- D) a shorter horizon raises the saving.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client's corpus PV is computed with rate = 1.89%/12, nper = 300, pmt = −81,411. Which scenario and corpus does this correspond to?\nA) Scenario 1, Rs. 1.17 crore  B) Scenario 2, Rs. 1.95 crore  C) Scenario 3, Rs. 3.11 crore  D) Scenario 2, Rs. 1.17 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rs. 81,411 is Scenario 2's monthly expense at retirement (from Rs. 15,000 today), giving a corpus of about Rs. 1.95 crore.\n- A) Scenario 1's pmt is Rs. 48,847.\n- C) Scenario 3's pmt is Rs. 1,30,258.\n- D) pairs the right scenario with the wrong corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client steps up a Rs. 5,000 SIP by 10% each year. What is the SIP amount in year 3 (rounded)?\nA) Rs. 5,500  B) Rs. 6,050  C) Rs. 6,500  D) Rs. 5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Year 1: 5,000; Year 2: 5,000 × 1.10 = 5,500; Year 3: 5,500 × 1.10 = Rs. 6,050. Each year multiplies the prior amount by 1.10.\n- A) is the year-2 amount.\n- C) overshoots.\n- D) ignores the step-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** The workbook's example uses (1.07)^25 ≈ 5.427 to grow Rs. 9,000 to Rs. 48,847. If inflation were only 5%, (1.05)^25 ≈ 3.386. The retirement expense on Rs. 9,000 would then be about:\nA) Rs. 30,474  B) Rs. 48,847  C) Rs. 18,000  D) Rs. 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 9,000 × 3.386 ≈ Rs. 30,474. Lower inflation means a smaller future expense, hence a smaller corpus needed.\n- B) is the 7% figure.\n- C) just doubles, ignoring the factor.\n- D) overstates.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A 25-year EPF balance grows at 8.5%. If a client withdraws Rs. 1,00,000 today with 25 years to retirement, the lost future value is closest to (use (1.085)^25 ≈ 7.69):\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 3.85 lakh  C) Rs. 7.69 lakh  D) Rs. 11.56 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV = 1,00,000 × (1.085)^25 ≈ 1,00,000 × 7.69 ≈ Rs. 7.69 lakh. Same method as Mr. E, but a different amount/horizon.\n- A) ignores growth.\n- B) uses far too small a factor.\n- D) uses the 30-year factor (11.56), more time than given.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Scenario: A 35-year-old (retire 60, life to 85) has today's full expense of Rs. 25,000. Using the 60% rule, his retirement-relevant expense today is:\nA) Rs. 25,000  B) Rs. 15,000  C) Rs. 10,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 60% of Rs. 25,000 = Rs. 15,000 (workbook Scenario 2's monthly expense). The rule trims current expenses to the ~50–60% likely needed in retirement.\n- A) ignores the 60% rule.\n- C) and D) use the wrong percentages.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Continuing Q83: at 7% inflation over 25 years, the monthly expense at retirement (15,000 × 5.427) is about Rs. 81,411, and the workbook corpus is:\nA) Rs. 1.17 crore  B) Rs. 1.95 crore  C) Rs. 3.11 crore  D) Rs. 81,411\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Scenario 2 (Rs. 15,000 today → Rs. 81,411 at retirement) yields a corpus of about Rs. 1.95 crore via PV at the 1.89% real return over 300 months.\n- A) is Scenario 1.\n- C) is Scenario 3.\n- D) is the monthly expense, not the corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client expects an 8% return but 7% post-retirement inflation. His real return per (1+r)/(1+i) − 1 is closest to:\nA) 1.00%  B) 0.93%  C) 1.89%  D) 1.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.08/1.07) − 1 = 1.009346 − 1 = 0.00935 ≈ 0.93%. Higher inflation (7% vs 6%) shrinks the real return below the workbook's 1.89%.\n- A) is the rough subtraction (8%−7%).\n- C) is the 8%/6% value.\n- D) does not match.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A client's monthly expense at retirement is Rs. 1,30,258 (Scenario 3). Over 300 months at 1.89%/12 real return, the PV (corpus) is approximately:\nA) Rs. 1.17 crore  B) Rs. 1.95 crore  C) Rs. 3.11 crore  D) Rs. 3.9 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Scenario 3 (Rs. 24,000 today → Rs. 1,30,258 at retirement) gives a corpus of about Rs. 3.11 crore. Larger expenses scale the corpus up proportionally.\n- A) is Scenario 1, B) is Scenario 2.\n- D) overstates the workbook figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Mr. A needs Rs. 2.0 crore in 20 years at 12%, requiring Rs. 20,217/month fixed. If he could instead earn 15%, would the required fixed monthly saving be higher or lower?\nA) Higher  B) Lower  C) Unchanged  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher return means compounding does more of the work, so a smaller monthly contribution reaches the same Rs. 2.0 crore. In PMT, raising rate (with corpus fixed) lowers the payment.\n- A) reverses the relationship.\n- C) ignores the effect of return.\n- D) overstates — you still need to contribute something.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client steps up a Rs. 10,000 SIP by 7% annually. The year-2 amount is:\nA) Rs. 10,070  B) Rs. 10,700  C) Rs. 17,000  D) Rs. 11,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Year 2 = 10,000 × (1 + 7%) = 10,000 × 1.07 = Rs. 10,700. A 7% step-up multiplies by 1.07 each year.\n- A) applies 0.7%, not 7%.\n- C) applies 70%.\n- D) applies 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Two clients have a Rs. 1.18 crore target at 12%. Client P starts at 30 (Rs. 3,376/month). Over 30 years (360 months), roughly how much does Client P contribute in TOTAL (ignoring growth)?\nA) ~Rs. 12.15 lakh  B) ~Rs. 40 lakh  C) ~Rs. 1.18 crore  D) ~Rs. 3,376\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total contributions = 3,376 × 360 ≈ Rs. 12.15 lakh. The gap between this and the Rs. 1.18 crore corpus (~Rs. 1.06 crore) is the power of compounding at 12%.\n- B) overstates contributions.\n- C) is the final corpus, not contributions.\n- D) is one month only.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Client Q starts at 40 (Rs. 11,928/month, 240 months) for the same Rs. 1.18 crore. His total contributions (ignoring growth) are about:\nA) ~Rs. 12.15 lakh  B) ~Rs. 28.6 lakh  C) ~Rs. 1.18 crore  D) ~Rs. 11,928\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 11,928 × 240 ≈ Rs. 28.6 lakh. Starting later, Client Q must put in over twice the rupees that early-starter P contributed (~Rs. 12.15 lakh) for the same corpus — compounding had less time, so contributions had to carry more of the load.\n- A) is Client P's total.\n- C) is the final corpus.\n- D) is one month.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A 55-year-old retiree needs an income product. Which evaluation pair from the workbook applies, and why?\nA) Cost & Return — because growth dominates  B) Inflation & Capital Protection — income must beat inflation for longevity and capital must be safe  C) Risk & Tax efficiency — accumulation focus  D) Brand & Liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For income-generating products at the retirement stage, the workbook prescribes Inflation (income must beat inflation against longevity risk) and Capital Protection (avoid products that fluctuate the capital).\n- A) and C) are accumulation/growth factors.\n- D) is not the workbook's stated pair for income products.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Scenario: A client withdrew his EPF on a job change after 3 years of service. What are the tax and continuity consequences?\nA) Tax-free and service continues  B) Taxable withdrawal, and he restarts contributions with the new employer  C) Tax-free but service resets  D) No tax and he keeps the old account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under 5 years of service, withdrawing the old EPF is taxable; having withdrawn rather than transferred, he must start over with the new employer (where the required contribution has already risen).\n- A), C), D) misstate the sub-5-year tax rule and the loss of continuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client switching jobs in year 6 transfers (not withdraws) his EPF. Tax outcome?\nA) Taxable because he switched  B) No tax incidence on transfer; old service period is added to total service  C) Tax at 20% flat  D) Forfeiture of interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Transferring incurs no tax incidence, and the old service period is included in total service. (He has also crossed 5 years, so even a withdrawal would be tax-free — but transfer keeps compounding.)\n- A) transfer is not a taxable event.\n- C) and D) are not stated outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Using the workbook's factors, which client faces the LARGEST corpus requirement, and roughly how much?\nA) Rs. 9,000 today → Rs. 1.17 crore  B) Rs. 15,000 today → Rs. 1.95 crore  C) Rs. 24,000 today → Rs. 3.11 crore  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The highest current expense (Rs. 24,000) inflates to the highest retirement expense (Rs. 1,30,258) and the largest corpus, about Rs. 3.11 crore. Corpus scales with the expense level.\n- A) and B) are smaller scenarios.\n- D) is false — they differ.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client argues that since the real return is only ~1.89%, the corpus barely matters. The correct intuition is:\nA) He is right — skip the corpus  B) A low real return actually makes the corpus LARGER, because money grows slowly relative to rising costs, so more capital is needed up front  C) A low real return makes the corpus smaller  D) Real return has no effect on the corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In PV, a lower discount (real) rate produces a larger present value — you need a bigger lump sum because it grows little above inflation. That is why the corpus figures are large (Rs. 1.17–3.11 crore).\n- A) is dangerously wrong.\n- C) reverses the PV relationship.\n- D) ignores that the real return is the PV discount rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client aged 32 (long horizon) is choosing between a low-cost equity mutual fund (higher risk, growth) and an immediate annuity (income). For accumulation, which fits and why?\nA) The annuity — guaranteed income now  B) The equity fund — long horizon allows riding downside cycles to grow and beat inflation  C) Neither  D) Both equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Pre-retirement accumulation favours growth products that can ride out volatility over 15–20+ years and beat inflation; income products like annuities suit the retirement/post-retirement phases.\n- A) An annuity locks in income he doesn't yet need and forgoes growth.\n- C) and D) ignore the clear suitability match.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A retiree wants to leave money to heirs and keep funds accessible. In the post-retirement (75+) phase, which factor newly gains importance per the workbook?\nA) Maximum growth  B) Liquidity (to leave money for heirs)  C) High risk  D) Tax-loss harvesting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In the post-retirement phase the objective of leaving money for heirs makes liquidity a primary factor, alongside capital protection, low return and least risk.\n- A) and C) are unsuitable for the very old age phase.\n- D) is not mentioned as the added factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client invests a one-time Rs. 50,000 in EPF earning 8.5% and leaves it untouched for 30 years. Its future value (using (1.085)^30 ≈ 11.56) is about:\nA) Rs. 50,000  B) Rs. 1.5 lakh  C) Rs. 5.78 lakh  D) Rs. 8.66 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV = 50,000 × 11.56 ≈ Rs. 5.78 lakh. This is exactly the compounding the Mr. E example warns you forfeit if you withdraw early.\n- A) ignores growth.\n- B) uses far too small a factor.\n- D) is the figure for a Rs. 75,000 withdrawal, not Rs. 50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Scenario: Mr. A's fixed plan is Rs. 20,217/month for Rs. 2.0 crore in 20 years at 12%. He instead starts a step-up SIP at a lower amount, rising 7% yearly. The main risk/benefit trade-off is:\nA) He guarantees a higher corpus  B) He eases early cash flow and rides compounding, but must discipline himself to actually step up each year  C) He pays no return  D) He finishes in 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stepping up starts lower (easier early on) and increases with income, capturing compounding; the trade-off is the discipline to keep increasing contributions as planned.\n- A) no corpus is guaranteed.\n- C) the 12% return still applies.\n- D) the horizon stays 20 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A client has Rs. 1.18 crore as the target but can only save Rs. 6,280/month and is currently 35 (25 years, 12%). Five years pass and he hasn't started. Now at 40 he needs Rs. 11,928/month. What is the key lesson?\nA) Returns dropped  B) Procrastination forces a much higher monthly saving (here ~1.9×) because compounding had 5 fewer years  C) The corpus shrank  D) Inflation rose\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Same corpus, same 12% return — only the start age changed. Delaying from 35 to 40 raises the requirement from Rs. 6,280 to Rs. 11,928 (≈1.9×), purely from lost compounding time. Start early.\n- A) the return is unchanged at 12%.\n- C) the target stayed Rs. 1.18 crore.\n- D) inflation is not the variable that changed here.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Meet Ramesh.** Ramesh is 35 years old and plans to retire at 60. He expects to live until 85. His current household expense is Rs. 25,000/month. He has an EPF balance and is contributing regularly. He assumes:\n- Pre-retirement inflation: **7% p.a.**\n- Post-retirement inflation: **6% p.a.**\n- Return on the retirement corpus during retirement: **8% p.a.**\n- Return during the accumulation phase: **12% p.a.**\n- Retirement expenses ≈ **60%** of current expenses (no liabilities at retirement)\n- EPF interest: **8.5% p.a.**\n\nRamesh asks his adviser five questions.\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 1:** What is Ramesh's retirement-relevant monthly expense *today*, and what will it be at retirement (age 60)?\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Step 1 — Apply the 60% rule (today's relevant expense):**\nRetirement-relevant expense today = 60% × Rs. 25,000 = **Rs. 15,000/month**.\n(At retirement, loans and children's goals end, so he needs roughly 50–60% of today's spend.)\n\n**Step 2 — Inflate to retirement using Future = Present × (1 + inflation)^years:**\n- inflation = 7%, years to retire = 60 − 35 = 25.\n- (1.07)^25 ≈ 5.427.\n- Future monthly expense = 15,000 × 5.427 ≈ **Rs. 81,411/month**.\n\nThis matches the workbook's Scenario 2. Intuition: prices rise ~7% every year, so 25 years of compounding multiplies the expense more than fivefold.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 2:** What inflation-adjusted (real) return should the adviser use to size the corpus, and why?\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Formula:** Real Return = [(1 + return) ÷ (1 + post-retirement inflation)] − 1.\n- return = 8%, post-retirement inflation = 6%.\n- Real return = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = 0.01887 ≈ **1.89% p.a.** (≈ 1.89%/12 per month).\n\n**Why use the real return?** During retirement the corpus grows at 8%, but the cost of living keeps rising at 6%. The *purchasing power* of the corpus therefore grows by only ~1.89%. Discounting future expenses at this real rate builds the rising-cost effect into the corpus automatically — so we don't have to inflate every single future month separately.\n\nNote it is slightly below the rough 8% − 6% = 2% because the exact formula divides rather than subtracts.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 3:** Using the Excel PV function, what corpus does Ramesh need at age 60?\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Formula:** =PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])\n- **rate** = real return per month = 1.89%/12.\n- **nper** = post-retirement months = (85 − 60) × 12 = 25 × 12 = **300**.\n- **pmt** = monthly expense at retirement = Rs. 81,411, entered as **−81,411** (it is a cash outflow).\n- **fv** = blank (0) — the corpus is drawn down to zero by age 85.\n- **type** = 0 (payments at period end).\n\n=PV(1.89%/12, 300, −81411) ≈ **Rs. 1.95 crore** (workbook Scenario 2).\n\nIntuition: the corpus is simply the present value, at age 60, of 300 monthly withdrawals that keep pace with 6% inflation, discounted at the 1.89% real return. A low real return makes the required lump sum large.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 4:** Using the Excel PMT function, how much must Ramesh save per month (12% return) to build a Rs. 1.18 crore corpus if he starts now at 35? How would it change if he had started at 30 or delayed to 40?\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Formula:** =PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])\n- **rate** = 12%/12 per month.\n- **pv** = corpus target = Rs. 1.18 crore.\n- **fv** = 0; **type** = 0.\n- **nper** = months from start age to 60.\n\n| Start age | Years | nper (months) | Monthly saving |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 30 | 30 | 360 | **Rs. 3,376** |\n| 35 | 25 | 300 | **Rs. 6,280** |\n| 40 | 20 | 240 | **Rs. 11,928** |\n\nStarting at 35, Ramesh needs about **Rs. 6,280/month**.\n- Starting 5 years earlier (30) would cut it to Rs. 3,376 — almost half.\n- Delaying to 40 raises it to Rs. 11,928 — about **1.9×** the age-35 figure (and ~3.5× the age-30 figure).\n\n**Lesson:** the corpus and return are identical; only the time for compounding changes. Each year of delay sharply raises the monthly burden — start early.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 5:** Ramesh is tempted to withdraw Rs. 75,000 from his EPF now (30 years before age 65/retirement-horizon) for a discretionary expense, and is also about to change jobs. What should the adviser advise?\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**(a) The cost of early withdrawal — Future Value lost:**\nFV = amount × (1 + r)^n = 75,000 × (1.085)^30.\n- (1.085)^30 ≈ 11.56.\n- FV ≈ 75,000 × 11.56 ≈ **Rs. 8.66 lakh**.\n\nSo withdrawing Rs. 75,000 today sacrifices about **Rs. 8.66 lakh** of future retirement corpus — because that money loses 30 years of compounding at 8.5%. The adviser should discourage the discretionary withdrawal and, if unavoidable, urge Ramesh to \"fill the gap later\" with higher contributions.\n\n**(b) On the job change — transfer, don't withdraw:**\n- If Ramesh has served **less than 5 years** and withdraws the old EPF, the withdrawal becomes **taxable**.\n- Instead, he should **transfer** the EPF to the new employer using his **UAN** (12-digit lifelong number) — completely online, with UAN/KYC updated. This incurs **no tax**, adds the **old service period** to his total service (helping cross the 5-year tax-free mark), and preserves **compounding** on the old corpus.\n- (If it were NPS, the same **PRAN** would simply move with him, or continue under the **All Citizen Model**.)\n\n**Bottom line:** Don't withdraw early (it costs ~Rs. 8.66 lakh of future corpus); transfer the EPF on the job change to avoid tax and keep compounding intact.\n</details>\n\n\n\n"}, {"num": "07", "title": "Concepts in Taxation", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 7: Concepts in Taxation — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The basic vocabulary of Indian income tax (the \"grammar\" you need before anything else)\n- **Residential status** and the exact day-count tests that decide *which* income gets taxed in India\n- The **five heads of income** and how income gets sorted into them\n- **Clubbing** of income (when someone else's income gets added to yours)\n- **Set-off and carry-forward** of losses (how losses cancel income)\n- Exemptions, **deductions** (Chapter VI-A), and the **section 87A rebate**\n- **Double Tax Avoidance Agreement (DTAA)** — avoiding being taxed twice\n- **Old vs New tax regime**, Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) and Effective Tax Rate\n\n---\n\n## 7.1 The Framework — who makes the rules?\n\nIncome tax in India runs on the **Income Tax Act, 1961** (we'll just call it \"the Act\"). It sits under the **Ministry of Finance**. The day-to-day administration is done by the **Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT)**.\n\n- The **Income Tax Rules, 1962** spell out the \"how-to\" details.\n- The CBDT also issues **Circulars** (clarifications) and **Notifications** (official announcements).\n\n**Analogy:** The *Act* is the constitution (the big law), the *Rules* are the user-manual, and *Circulars/Notifications* are the sticky-notes the referee adds when players are confused.\n\n💡 Why an investment adviser cares: the **same profit on selling a security can be taxed completely differently** depending on the type of security, how long you held it, and whether you acted as a *trader* or an *investor*.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 Key Concepts (the vocabulary)\n\n### 7.2.1 Previous Year (PY) vs Assessment Year (AY)\nIndia uses the **Financial Year (FY): April 1 to March 31**.\n\n- **Previous Year (PY):** the FY in which you *earn* the income. (Section 3 of the Act.)\n- **Assessment Year (AY):** the FY *immediately after* the PY, in which you *file and get assessed* on that income. (Section 2(9) defines AY as \"a period of 12 months commencing on the 1st day of April every year.\")\n\n**Analogy:** PY is the year you play the match; AY is the next year when the scorecard is reviewed and the prize/penalty is decided.\n\n**Worked example:** You earned income between 1 April 2021 and 31 March 2022.\n- PY = **FY 2021-22**\n- AY = **FY 2022-23**\n\n### 7.2.2 Person — Section 2(31)\n\"Person\" is a wide bucket. It includes:\n\n| # | Type of Person |\n|---|----------------|\n| a | Individual |\n| b | Hindu Undivided Family (HUF) |\n| c | Company |\n| d | Partnership firm (including LLP) |\n| e | Association of Persons (AOP) / Body of Individuals (BOI) |\n| f | Local authority |\n| g | Artificial juridical person (not in any category above) |\n\n⚠️ For (e), (f), (g): they count as a \"person\" **even if not formed to make a profit**.\n\n### 7.2.3 Assessee — Section 2(7)\nAn **assessee** is the person *liable to pay tax* (or any sum) under the Act. It also includes anyone against whom a proceeding (about income, loss, or refund) has been started.\n\n**Analogy:** \"Assessee\" = the player whose scorecard is being checked.\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Income — Section 2(24)\nThe Act gives an **inclusive** definition: it *lists* things that are income, but anything else \"in the nature of income\" can also be taxed even if not listed.\n\nCommon items: profits/gains from business or profession, **dividend**, **capital gains**, **gifts from a non-relative** (covered by section 56), property received below market price (section 56), and certain employment perquisites.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 Residential Status — the single most important concept\n\nYour **residential status decides which income India can tax**. This is determined under **Section 6** of the Act, fresh **every financial year**.\n\n⚠️ **Citizenship ≠ Residential status.** You can be a citizen of another country but a *resident* of India for tax, and vice-versa. Also, residential status under the **Income Tax Act** and under **FEMA** are different things.\n\n| | Income Tax Act | FEMA |\n|---|----------------|------|\n| When determined | Every financial year | Day to day, at the time of an investment/remittance |\n| Based on | Stay during the FY + prior FYs (different tests for Indian citizens/PIOs) | Days in preceding FY, but can change by stated intention now |\n| What it impacts | Tax to be paid and TDS | Where/what you can invest, remittances, bank accounts |\n\n**Why it matters (the logic):**\n- A **resident** pays Indian tax on **worldwide income**.\n- A **non-resident** pays Indian tax only on **Indian income**.\n\nFrom **FY 2020-21**, status depends on **citizenship + period of stay + total Indian income**.\n\n### Three categories\na) **Resident and Ordinarily Resident (ROR)**\nb) **Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident (RNOR / NOR)**\nc) **Non-Resident (NR)**\n\n### 7.4.1 The \"Deemed Resident\" test — Section 6(1A)\nAn **Indian citizen** is *deemed resident* (regardless of days in India) if **both**:\n1. His **Indian Income** (total income excluding income from foreign sources) **exceeds ₹15 lakh** in the PY, **and**\n2. He is **not liable to tax in any other country** by reason of domicile/residence etc.\n\n\"Income from foreign sources\" = income that accrues/arises outside India (except from a business controlled in / profession set up in India) and is not deemed to arise in India.\n\n⚠️ A **deemed resident is always treated as RNOR** (not ROR).\n\n### 7.4.2 Tests of Residence (day-count) — Section 6\nA person is **Resident** if they meet conditions specific to their category.\n\n**Part 1 — Indian Citizens:**\n| Clause | Condition to be RESIDENT |\n|--------|--------------------------|\n| (a) | Stays in India **182 days or more** in the relevant PY; OR |\n| (b) | Visiting India with **Indian Income ≤ ₹15 lakh** and in India **182+ days**, OR leaves India for employment; OR |\n| (c) | Leaves India as crew of an Indian ship and in India **182+ days** and **365+ days** in the 4 preceding years; OR |\n| (d) | Visiting India with **Indian Income > ₹15 lakh** and in India **120+ days** and **365+ days** in 4 preceding years; OR |\n| (e) | Not visiting/leaving (and not under (a)): in India **60+ days** in the FY **and 365+ days** in the 4 preceding years; OR |\n| (f) | Deemed-resident test: Total income (excluding foreign-source) > ₹15 lakh and not liable to tax in any other country |\n\n**Part 2 — Foreign nationals who are Persons of Indian Origin (PIO)** — a PIO is someone whose he/his parents/grandparents were born in *undivided India*:\n| Clause | Condition |\n|--------|-----------|\n| (a) | In India **182+ days**; OR |\n| (b) | Visiting with **Indian Income ≤ ₹15 lakh**, in India **182+ days** and **365+ days** in 4 preceding years; OR |\n| (c) | Visiting with **Indian Income > ₹15 lakh**, in India **120+ days** and **365+ days** in 4 preceding years; OR |\n| (d) | Not visiting (and not under (a)): in India **60+ days** in FY and **365+ days** in 4 preceding years |\n\n**Part 3 — All other foreign nationals (non-PIO):**\n| Clause | Condition |\n|--------|-----------|\n| (a) | In India **182+ days**; OR |\n| (b) | In India **60+ days** (but < 182) in the FY **and 365+ days** in the 4 preceding years |\n\n### When is a Resident treated as RNOR (Not Ordinarily Resident)?\nA resident is **NOR** if he satisfies **any ONE** of:\n- (a) He was a **non-resident in at least 9 out of the 10** preceding years; OR\n- (b) He was in India for **729 days or less** during the **7** preceding years; OR\n- (c) He became resident only by clause (f) above (the deemed-resident route).\n\nIf **none** of these is satisfied → he is **ROR**.\n\n💡 **Memory hook:** \"**9-out-of-10** non-resident\" OR \"**729 days in 7 years**\" → you're the *gentler* RNOR (foreign income mostly safe).\n\n### 7.4.3 Residential Status of an HUF\n- **Resident:** in *every* case **except** where control & management of its affairs is **wholly outside India** during the year. (Even one decision taken in India ⇒ resident.)\n- **Non-Resident:** control & management **wholly outside India**.\n- **RNOR:** a resident HUF is NOR if the **Karta (Manager)** satisfies either: non-resident 9 of 10 preceding years, OR in India 729 days or less in the 7 preceding years.\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 Scope of Income (what gets taxed for each status)\n\nThis is the *payoff* of residential status. Table 7.1:\n\n| Nature of income | ROR | RNOR | NR |\n|------------------|:---:|:----:|:--:|\n| Received / deemed received **in India** | Taxable | Taxable | Taxable |\n| Accrues/arises / deemed to accrue **in India** | Taxable | Taxable | Taxable |\n| Accrues outside India **from a business controlled from / profession set up in India** | Taxable | Taxable | Not taxable |\n| Any other income accruing **outside India** | Taxable | Not taxable | Not taxable |\n\n**In words:** ROR = worldwide income. RNOR = Indian income + foreign income only if from an India-controlled business/profession. NR = Indian income only.\n\n**Worked example (Example 1 from text):** Mr. C (Indian citizen) returns to India in FY 2024-25; stay = 298 days (≥182 ⇒ **Resident**). In the 7 preceding years he spent **578 days** (≤729) and was non-resident in 9 of 10 preceding years. He satisfies an NOR condition ⇒ **RNOR for FY 2024-25**. So foreign bank interest is *not* taxed in India.\n\n**Worked example (Example 2):** Mr. A is ROR with a USA bank account. Interest there **is taxable in India** (worldwide income). If he were RNOR, that USA interest would **not** be taxed in India.\n\n💡 Citizenship never comes \"automatically\" by staying X years (Example 4) — that's immigration law, not tax law.\n\n---\n\n## 7.6 The Five Heads of Income\n\nTotal income is the **sum of income computed under five heads**:\n\n| # | Head | Quick meaning |\n|---|------|---------------|\n| 1 | **Salary** | Pay from an employer (needs employer–employee relationship) |\n| 2 | **House Property** | Rent/annual value from a building + land |\n| 3 | **Profits & Gains from Business or Profession (PGBP)** | Income from running a business/profession |\n| 4 | **Capital Gains** | Profit on selling a capital asset (Chapter 8) |\n| 5 | **Income from Other Sources (IFOS)** | Everything else not exempt (Chapter 9) |\n\n### 7.6.1 Income from Salary — Section 17(1)\n\"Salary\" includes wages, annuity, pension, gratuity, fees, commission, advance of salary, perquisites/profits in lieu of salary, leave encashment, employer's PF contribution **above 12% of salary**, pension scheme & Agniveer corpus contributions, etc.\n\n- **Needs an employer-employee relationship.** Without it, income falls under business or other sources.\n- Taxed on **due or receipt basis, whichever is earlier** (ESOPs are an exception, see Ch.12).\n\n**Key salary deductions — Old vs New regime:**\n\n| Item | Old Regime | New Regime |\n|------|-----------|-----------|\n| Standard deduction | ₹50,000 | ₹75,000 |\n| Employee's NPS 80CCD(1B) | up to ₹50,000 | Not available |\n| Employer's NPS 80CCD(2) – private sector | up to **10%** of salary | up to **14%** of salary |\n| Employer's NPS 80CCD(2) – Govt employee | 14% | 14% |\n| Employer EPF | taxable if > 12% of salary | taxable if > 12% of salary |\n| HRA exemption | Available | Not allowed |\n| Leave Travel Allowance | Allowed | Not allowed |\n\n⚠️ Where **employer's combined contribution to superannuation + EPF + NPS exceeds ₹7,50,000**, the excess is taxed as salary [section 17(2)(vii)].\n\n**Gratuity exemption (section 10(10)) — same in both regimes:**\n- **Govt employee:** death-cum-retirement gratuity is **fully exempt**.\n- **Covered by Payment of Gratuity Act:** exempt = least of (15 days' salary per completed year of service / **₹20,00,000** / actual gratuity).\n- **Other cases:** exempt = least of (½ month's salary per completed year / **₹20,00,000** / actual gratuity).\n\n**HRA exemption (Old regime) = least of:**\n1. HRA received\n2. Rent paid **−** 10% of salary\n3. **50%** of salary (Mumbai/Delhi/Kolkata/Chennai) or **40%** elsewhere\n\n**Worked example (text Example 2):** Mr. B, basic ₹15,00,000, employer EPF ₹21,600 (≤12% ⇒ Nil add-back), employer NPS ₹2,10,000. Total employer contribution ₹2,31,600 (< ₹7.5 lakh ⇒ no 17(2)(vii) add-back).\n- Gross salary = 15,00,000 + 2,10,000 (employer NPS as perquisite) = 17,10,000\n- Old: −SD 50,000 = 16,60,000; −80C (EPF 21,600 + PPF 1,50,000, capped at 1,50,000) = 1,50,000; −80CCD(1B) NPS 50,000; −80CCD(2) employer NPS 10% of 15,00,000 = 1,50,000 → deductions 3,50,000 → **Taxable 13,10,000**.\n- New: −SD 75,000 = 16,35,000; only 80CCD(2) at 14% = 2,10,000 → **Taxable 14,25,000**.\n\n### 7.6.2 Income from House Property\nTaxable if income arises from a **building or land appurtenant thereto**, in the hands of the **owner** (or deemed owner).\n\n⚠️ Vacant land or an **under-construction** property is *not* a \"building\" → its income is PGBP or Other Sources, not House Property.\n\n**Three categories:** (a) Self-occupied, (b) Let-out, (c) Deemed let-out. You may pick **any two** non-let-out houses as self-occupied; the rest are deemed let-out.\n\n**Computation:**\n| Step | Self-occupied | Let-out / Deemed let-out |\n|------|--------------|--------------------------|\n| Annual Value (A) | NIL | Reasonable expected rent or actual rent received, whichever higher (deemed let-out = reasonable expected rent) |\n| Less Municipal taxes (B) | — | only if actually paid by owner |\n| Net Annual Value (C = A−B) | — | xxx |\n| Less Standard deduction (E) = **30% of NAV** (section 24(a)) | — | (xxx) |\n| Less Interest on home loan (F) (section 24(b)) | up to **₹2,00,000** (Old); **Nil** (New) | **no limit** |\n| Income (G) | usually a loss = the interest | xxx |\n\n**Interest rules (Old regime, self-occupied):** up to **₹2,00,000** if loan taken on/after 1 Apr 1999 and construction completed within 5 years; otherwise capped at **₹30,000**. **Pre-construction period interest** is allowed in **5 equal instalments** from the year of acquisition/completion.\n\n**Loss set-off:** House-property loss can be set off against other heads up to **₹2,00,000** (Old regime). New regime: **not allowed against other heads** (but deemed set off). Unadjusted loss carries forward **8 years**, set off only against house property income.\n\n**Worked example (text Example 2):** Salary 15,00,000; rent 2,20,000; interest on let-out loan 5,00,000.\n- House property income = 2,20,000 − 30% (66,000) − 5,00,000 = **(3,46,000) loss**.\n- Old: set off ₹2,00,000 against salary → GTI 13,00,000; −80C 1,50,000 → **Taxable 11,50,000**; carry forward **₹1,46,000**.\n- New: no inter-head set off (taxable 15,00,000) but the **₹1,46,000 loss is still carried forward** (set off only against future house-property income).\n\n### 7.6.3 Profits & Gains from Business or Profession (PGBP)\nBusiness (Section 2(13)) includes any trade, commerce, manufacture, or adventure in their nature. Profits computed by the assessee's **accounting method**: cash system → receipt basis; mercantile system → accrual basis. Some assessees may use **presumptive taxation** (but not commission/brokerage earners).\n\n**Speculative vs non-speculative (Section 43(5)):** a purchase/sale of any commodity (incl. shares) settled **otherwise than by actual delivery** is *speculative*. **Exceptions:** hedging transactions and **derivatives** are *not* speculative.\n⚠️ **Speculative loss** can be set off **only against speculative profit**, and carried forward **4 years** (vs 8 years for normal business loss).\n\n### 7.6.4 & 7.6.5 Capital Gains & Other Sources\nCovered in detail in Chapters 8 and 9. (Capital gain = profit on transfer of a capital asset; STCG/LTCG depend on holding period.)\n\n---\n\n## 7.7 Clubbing of Income (Sections 60–64)\nSometimes *another person's* income is added (\"clubbed\") to your income to stop tax avoidance by gifting assets around. Income is first computed in the recipient's hands (after their deductions) under the relevant head, then clubbed in the transferor's hands **under the same head**. If it's a loss, the loss is clubbed too.\n\n- **Section 60:** Transfer of *income* without transferring the *asset* → income still taxed in transferor's hands.\n- **Section 64:** Clubbing of income of **spouse, son's wife, minor child, certain AOP, property gifted to HUF**.\n\n**Worked example:** Mr. A gifts ₹30,00,000 to his wife; she earns ₹1,50,000 interest. That ₹1,50,000 is **clubbed in Mr. A's income** and taxed at *his* slab.\n\n**Section 60 example:** E owns 100 debentures; assigns interest of 50 to nephew N (asset kept) → that interest is clubbed with E. Gifts 50 debentures to son P (asset + income both gone) → not clubbed under s.60 (but if son is a minor, clubbed under s.64).\n\n---\n\n## 7.8 Set-off and Carry-Forward of Losses\n\n**Intra-head** = within the same head. **Inter-head** = across heads.\n\n### 7.8.1 Capital gains losses\n- **Long-Term Capital Loss (LTCL):** set off **only against LTCG**.\n- **Short-Term Capital Loss (STCL):** set off against **any capital gain (STCG or LTCG)**.\n- Capital losses **cannot** be set off against any *other* head (inter-head not allowed).\n- Carry forward: **8 assessment years**. STCL → against any CG; b/f LTCL → only against LTCG.\n- ⚠️ Carry-forward allowed **only if return filed by the section 139(1) due date**.\n\n### 7.8.2 Business losses\n- Normal business loss: set off against any income **except salary** (and gambling). Carry forward **8 years**.\n- Speculative loss: only against speculative profit; carry forward **4 years**.\n- **Unabsorbed depreciation:** carried forward **indefinitely**, set off against any income **except salary**.\n\n### 7.8.3 House property loss\nSet off against other heads up to **₹2 lakh** (Old regime); New regime not allowed against other heads (deemed set off). Carry forward **8 years**, only against house-property income.\n\n### 7.8.4 Other Sources loss\nSet off against any head **except gambling**; **cannot be carried forward**.\n\n**Summary (Table 7.4):**\n| Loss type | Same-year set off | Carry forward | Future set-off against |\n|-----------|-------------------|:-------------:|------------------------|\n| House Property | Any head (≤₹2L, Old) | 8 yrs | House property only |\n| Business (non-spec) | Any income except salary | 8 yrs | Business income |\n| Speculation | Speculation profit only | 4 yrs | Speculation profit |\n| Short-term Capital | Any capital gain | 8 yrs | Any capital gain |\n| Long-term Capital | LTCG only | 8 yrs | LTCG only |\n| Other Sources | Any head except gambling | Cannot c/f | — |\n\n---\n\n## 7.9–7.13 Exemptions, Deductions, Rebate, GTI, Total Income\n\n- **Exempt incomes:** Chapter III (sections 10–13B) — not included in total income.\n- **Deductions (Chapter VI-A):** allowed from Gross Total Income to encourage saving/social spending. (See Key Numbers table.)\n- **Gross Total Income (GTI), Section 80B(5):** total income under the 5 heads **before** Chapter VI-A deductions. Steps: (1) compute 5 heads → (2) club income → (3) set off losses.\n- **Total Income:** GTI **minus** Chapter VI-A deductions → the base for tax.\n\n**Major Chapter VI-A deductions:**\n| Section | For | Limit | In New Regime? |\n|---------|-----|-------|:--------------:|\n| 80C | LIC, PF, principal of home loan, small savings | ₹1,50,000 | No |\n| 80CCD(1) | Employee/self NPS | 10% salary (emp.) / 20% GTI | No |\n| 80CCD(1B) | Extra NPS / NPS Vatsalya | ₹50,000 | No |\n| 80CCD(2) | Employer NPS | 14% (Govt) / 10% (others) salary | **Yes** |\n| 80CCH | Agniveer Corpus | 100% | **Yes** |\n| 80D | Medical insurance | ₹25k–₹1,00,000 (seniors) | No |\n| 80DD | Disabled dependent | ₹75,000 (₹1,25,000 severe) | No |\n| 80DDB | Specified diseases | ₹40,000 (₹1,00,000 senior) | No |\n| 80E | Education loan interest | No limit | No |\n| 80EEB | Electric-vehicle loan interest | ₹1,50,000 | No |\n| 80G | Donations | 50%/100% of qualifying amount | No |\n| 80GG | Rent paid (no HRA) | least of: rent − 10% of income / ₹5,000 p.m. / 25% of income | No |\n| 80TTA | Savings a/c interest | ₹10,000 | No |\n| 80TTB | Senior citizen deposit interest | ₹50,000 | No |\n| 80U | Own disability | ₹75,000 (₹1,25,000 severe) | No |\n\n⚠️ **Section 80CCE:** 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) together **cannot exceed ₹1,50,000**.\n\n### 7.11 Rebate under Section 87A\nA rebate that can wipe out tax for small incomes.\n- **Old regime:** up to **₹12,500** for resident individual with total income ≤ **₹5,00,000**.\n- **New regime (from AY 2024-25):** up to **₹25,000** if income ≤ **₹7,00,000**; with **marginal relief** just above ₹7 lakh (tax ≈ income over ₹7 lakh, up to ₹7,28,000).\n- **New regime (from FY 2025-26):** up to **₹60,000** if income ≤ **₹12,00,000**; marginal relief applies up to ~**₹12,71,250**.\n\n⚠️ The 87A rebate is **not** available against tax on **LTCG under section 112A**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.14 Computation of Tax Payable (Non-corporate)\nTax at applicable slab + special rates → add **surcharge** (if applicable) and **Health & Education Cess (4%)** → reduce AMT credit / section 89 relief / foreign tax credit → add interest & late fees → subtract advance tax/TDS/TCS/self-assessment tax → **net payable or refund**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.15 Double Tax Avoidance Agreement (DTAA)\nTwo ways countries tax income:\n- **Residence-based:** your home country taxes your worldwide income.\n- **Source-based:** the country where the income arises taxes it.\n\nIndia uses **both** → the same income can be taxed twice. A **DTAA** is an agreement between countries to avoid this, usually by the **residence country giving credit** for tax paid in the source country.\n\n**Worked example:** Mr. IS (ROR) earns in France and pays French tax. India taxes his global income but gives him **credit for the French tax paid** (India is his residence country).\n\n---\n\n## 7.16 Taxation Regime — EEE / EET / ETE\nA tax-saving investment has 3 stages: (1) you invest, (2) it earns returns, (3) you withdraw. Each stage is **E**(xempt) or **T**(axable).\n- **EEE** (all three exempt): PPF, ELSS.\n- **ETE** (exempt-tax-exempt): 5-year tax-saving FD; Senior Citizen Savings Scheme.\n- **EET** (taxed only at withdrawal): chargeable to tax only at transfer/withdrawal.\n\n💡 In the **new regime** the first \"E\" (deduction at investment) usually disappears, so many EEE products become **TEE**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.17 Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) — Section 2(29C)\nThe rate (incl. surcharge + cess) on the **highest income slab** for an Individual/AOP/BOI.\n\n| Particular | Rate (%) |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Highest slab rate (A) | 30 |\n| Surcharge = A × 37% | 11.1 |\n| Health & Education cess = (A+B) × 4% | 1.644 |\n| **MMR** | **42.744** |\n\n## 7.18 Effective Rate of Tax\nNot defined in the Act; the rate *including* surcharge and cess actually borne.\n\n**Formula:** Effective tax rate = Applicable rate × (1 + Surcharge rate) × (1 + Cess rate).\n\n**Example:** Partnership firm with income > ₹1 crore: 30% × (1+12%) × (1+4%) = **34.944%**.\nFor an individual on slabs: effective rate = total tax ÷ total income. e.g. ₹4,29,000 tax on ₹20,00,000 = **21.45%**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **PY** = year you earn; **AY** = next year you're assessed. India uses FY (Apr–Mar).\n- **Residential status (Section 6)** decides scope: **ROR = worldwide**, **RNOR = Indian + India-controlled business income**, **NR = Indian only**.\n- Basic day-tests: **182 days** in the PY (resident), or **60 days + 365 days/4 yrs**. Indian-income > ₹15 lakh triggers stricter 120-day and deemed-resident tests.\n- **NOR if:** non-resident 9/10 yrs OR ≤729 days in 7 yrs (or deemed-resident route).\n- **5 heads:** Salary, House Property, PGBP, Capital Gains, Other Sources.\n- **Clubbing (60–64):** spouse/minor/son's wife income added to transferor.\n- **Losses:** LTCL→LTCG only; STCL→any CG; capital losses never inter-head; carry forward 8 yrs (speculation 4). House-property loss inter-head ≤₹2L (Old only).\n- **GTI = 5 heads − losses**; **Total Income = GTI − Chapter VI-A**.\n- **87A rebate:** ₹12,500 (≤₹5L Old) / ₹25,000 (≤₹7L New) / ₹60,000 (≤₹12L New, FY25-26).\n- **MMR = 42.744%**; **Effective rate** = rate × (1+surcharge) × (1+cess).\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---------------|---------|\n| 182 days | Basic residence test in the PY |\n| 60 + 365/4yrs | Secondary residence test |\n| 120 days (+₹15 lakh income) | Stricter test for high-Indian-income visitors |\n| ₹15 lakh Indian income | Trigger for deemed-resident & 120-day tests |\n| 9 out of 10 years / 729 days in 7 yrs | RNOR (NOR) conditions |\n| ₹50,000 / ₹75,000 | Standard deduction (Old / New) |\n| 30% of NAV | House property standard deduction (section 24a) |\n| ₹2,00,000 | House-property interest cap (self-occupied, Old) / inter-head set-off limit |\n| ₹7,50,000 | Employer contribution cap before excess taxed (s.17(2)(vii)) |\n| ₹20,00,000 | Max gratuity exemption |\n| ₹1,50,000 | 80C / 80CCE combined cap |\n| 8 yrs / 4 yrs | Carry-forward of normal / speculative losses |\n| ₹12,500 / ₹25,000 / ₹60,000 | 87A rebate (Old ≤5L / New ≤7L / New ≤12L) |\n| 42.744% | Maximum Marginal Rate |\n| Section 6 | Residential status |\n| Section 2(24) | Definition of income |\n| Sections 60–64 | Clubbing of income |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 7: Concepts in Taxation — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Which Act governs income tax in India, and which body administers it?\n> **A:** The **Income Tax Act, 1961**, under the Ministry of Finance; administered by the **Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT)**. The Income Tax Rules, 1962 carry out its purposes, plus CBDT Circulars and Notifications.\n\n**Q2.** Define Previous Year and Assessment Year.\n> **A:** **Previous Year (PY)** = the financial year in which income is earned (Section 3). **Assessment Year (AY)** = the FY immediately after the PY, in which the income is assessed (Section 2(9), 12 months from 1 April). E.g. income earned FY 2021-22 (PY) is assessed in AY 2022-23.\n\n**Q3.** Name the seven categories of \"Person\" under Section 2(31).\n> **A:** Individual; HUF; Company; Partnership firm (incl. LLP); AOP/BOI; Local authority; Artificial juridical person.\n\n**Q4.** Who is an \"assessee\" (Section 2(7))?\n> **A:** A person liable to pay tax or any sum under the Act, or against whom any proceeding (re income, loss or refund) has been initiated.\n\n**Q5.** How does residential status differ from citizenship?\n> **A:** They are unrelated. You can be a foreign citizen yet a tax-resident of India, or an Indian citizen yet a non-resident. Residential status under the Income Tax Act and under FEMA are also separate concepts.\n\n**Q6.** What income does a Resident & Ordinarily Resident (ROR) pay tax on in India?\n> **A:** **Worldwide income** — income received/accruing in India *and* income accruing outside India.\n\n**Q7.** What does an RNOR (Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident) get taxed on?\n> **A:** Indian income (received/accruing in India), **plus** foreign income only if it is **from a business controlled from India or a profession set up in India**. Other foreign income is not taxed.\n\n**Q8.** What does a Non-Resident pay tax on in India?\n> **A:** Only **Indian income** — income received or deemed received in India, and income accruing or deemed to accrue in India.\n\n**Q9.** State the basic 182-day test of residence.\n> **A:** An individual is **resident** if he stays in India for **182 days or more** during the relevant previous year.\n\n**Q10.** State the secondary residence test (60-day rule).\n> **A:** Resident if in India **60+ days** in the FY **and 365+ days** during the 4 preceding years. (For non-PIO foreigners this is one of the two tests; for Indian citizens leaving/visiting, modified thresholds apply.)\n\n**Q11.** What does ₹15 lakh of Indian income trigger for Indian citizens/PIOs visiting India?\n> **A:** If Indian income **exceeds ₹15 lakh**, the threshold drops to **120 days** (plus 365 days in 4 preceding years). At ≤₹15 lakh, the 182-day test applies.\n\n**Q12.** What is the \"deemed resident\" rule under Section 6(1A)?\n> **A:** An **Indian citizen** is deemed resident (regardless of days in India) if Indian income **> ₹15 lakh** AND he is **not liable to tax in any other country**. A deemed resident is always treated as **RNOR**.\n\n**Q13.** When is a resident treated as Not Ordinarily Resident (RNOR)?\n> **A:** If he satisfies ANY one: (a) non-resident in **9 of the 10** preceding years; (b) in India **729 days or less** in the **7** preceding years; or (c) resident only via the deemed-resident clause (f).\n\n**Q14.** When is an HUF resident vs non-resident?\n> **A:** **Resident** unless control & management of its affairs is **wholly outside India** (even one decision in India ⇒ resident). **Non-resident** only if control & management is wholly outside India.\n\n**Q15.** Name the five heads of income.\n> **A:** (1) Salary, (2) House Property, (3) Profits & Gains from Business or Profession, (4) Capital Gains, (5) Income from Other Sources.\n\n**Q16.** What is the standard deduction on salary, Old vs New regime?\n> **A:** **₹50,000 (Old)** and **₹75,000 (New)**.\n\n**Q17.** What is the 80CCD(2) limit on employer's NPS contribution for private-sector employees in each regime?\n> **A:** **10%** of salary in the Old regime; **14%** of salary in the New regime (14% for Govt employees in both).\n\n**Q18.** When does an employer's contribution to PF/superannuation/NPS become taxable salary?\n> **A:** EPF contribution above **12%** of salary is taxable; and the **aggregate** of employer contributions to superannuation + EPF + NPS exceeding **₹7,50,000** is taxed as salary (s.17(2)(vii)).\n\n**Q19.** What is the maximum gratuity exemption under section 10(10)?\n> **A:** **₹20,00,000** (one of the \"least of\" limits). Government employees get full exemption.\n\n**Q20.** What is the standard deduction for house property income, and under which section?\n> **A:** **30% of Net Annual Value**, under **Section 24(a)**.\n\n**Q21.** What is the home-loan interest deduction for a self-occupied house, Old vs New regime?\n> **A:** Up to **₹2,00,000** in the Old regime (₹30,000 if construction not completed within 5 years); **not available (Nil)** in the New regime. Let-out property has **no limit**.\n\n**Q22.** Up to how much house-property loss can be set off against other heads, and in which regime?\n> **A:** Up to **₹2,00,000** against other heads in the **Old regime**. New regime: not allowed against other heads (deemed set off). Unadjusted loss carries forward 8 years against house-property income only.\n\n**Q23.** Under Section 43(5), what is a speculative transaction — and which are excluded?\n> **A:** A purchase/sale of a commodity (incl. shares) settled **otherwise than by actual delivery**. **Hedging transactions and derivatives are NOT speculative.**\n\n**Q24.** How long can speculative vs normal business losses be carried forward?\n> **A:** Speculative loss: **4 years** (set off only against speculative profit). Normal business loss: **8 years**. Unabsorbed depreciation: **indefinitely**.\n\n**Q25.** State the set-off rules for capital losses.\n> **A:** **LTCL** → only against **LTCG**. **STCL** → against any capital gain (STCG or LTCG). Capital losses **cannot** be set off against any other head. Carry forward **8 years**.\n\n**Q26.** What is a condition for carrying forward losses?\n> **A:** The **return must be filed by the due date under Section 139(1)** (condonation may be sought from the AO/CBDT).\n\n**Q27.** Can a loss under \"Income from Other Sources\" be carried forward?\n> **A:** **No.** It can be set off against any head (except gambling) in the same year but cannot be carried forward.\n\n**Q28.** What does Section 60 (clubbing) cover?\n> **A:** Transfer of **income without transferring the asset** → the income is still taxed in the **transferor's** hands.\n\n**Q29.** Give the Section 64 clubbing example with the wife's gift.\n> **A:** Mr. A gifts ₹30,00,000 to his wife; she earns ₹1,50,000 interest. The ₹1,50,000 is **clubbed in Mr. A's income** and taxed at **his** slab rate.\n\n**Q30.** What is Gross Total Income (Section 80B(5))?\n> **A:** Total income under the five heads computed **before** any Chapter VI-A deduction. Steps: compute 5 heads → club income → set off losses.\n\n**Q31.** What is the 80C / 80CCE combined ceiling?\n> **A:** 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) together cannot exceed **₹1,50,000** (Section 80CCE).\n\n**Q32.** Which Chapter VI-A deductions ARE available in the new regime?\n> **A:** **80CCD(2)** (employer NPS, up to 14%) and **80CCH** (Agniveer corpus). Most others (80C, 80D, 80CCD(1)/(1B), 80G, etc.) are not.\n\n**Q33.** State the Section 87A rebate amounts.\n> **A:** Old regime: up to **₹12,500** if income ≤ ₹5,00,000. New regime (AY 2024-25): up to **₹25,000** if income ≤ ₹7,00,000. New regime (FY 2025-26): up to **₹60,000** if income ≤ ₹12,00,000. Marginal relief applies just above the thresholds.\n\n**Q34.** Against which tax is the 87A rebate NOT available?\n> **A:** Against tax on **LTCG under Section 112A** (listed equity/equity MF/ULIP/business trust gains chargeable to STT) and on accumulated provident fund balance (s.111).\n\n**Q35.** What is the Maximum Marginal Rate, and how is it built up?\n> **A:** **42.744%** = 30% highest slab + surcharge (30%×37% = 11.1%) + cess (4% on 30%+11.1% = 1.644%). (Section 2(29C).)\n\n**Q36.** Give the effective tax rate formula and the partnership-firm example.\n> **A:** Effective rate = Applicable rate × (1 + Surcharge) × (1 + Cess). Firm with income > ₹1 cr: 30% × 1.12 × 1.04 = **34.944%**.\n\n**Q37.** What is a DTAA and how does it usually relieve double taxation?\n> **A:** A **Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement** between countries; relief is usually given by the **residence country granting credit** for tax paid in the source country.\n\n**Q38.** Classify PPF, ELSS, and 5-year tax-saving FD into EEE/EET/ETE.\n> **A:** PPF and ELSS = **EEE**; 5-year tax-saving FD and Senior Citizen Savings Scheme = **ETE**. (First \"E\" disappears in the new regime, turning many into TEE.)\n", "questions": "# Chapter 7: Concepts in Taxation — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy: recall of definitions & facts), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium: applying rules and comparisons), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard: numerical computations & day-count scenarios). Remember: the NISM exam carries **25% negative marking** (you lose 0.25 mark for every wrong answer), so do not guess blindly.\n\n---\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Under which Act is income tax in India governed?\nA) Income Tax Act, 1961  B) Companies Act, 2013  C) FEMA, 1999  D) SEBI Act, 1992\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Income tax in India is governed by the Income Tax Act, 1961, which comes under the Ministry of Finance.\n- B) Companies Act, 2013 governs companies, not income tax.\n- C) FEMA, 1999 governs foreign exchange and a separate residential-status concept, not income tax.\n- D) SEBI Act, 1992 governs the securities market regulator, not taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which body administers matters relating to the Income Tax Act, 1961?\nA) Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT)  B) SEBI  C) RBI  D) Ministry of Corporate Affairs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Administration of the Act is assigned to the Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT), which also issues Circulars and Notifications.\n- C) RBI is the central bank, handling monetary policy and FEMA.\n- B) SEBI regulates securities markets.\n- D) Ministry of Corporate Affairs handles company law.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The Income Tax Rules were framed in which year to carry out the purposes of the Act?\nA) 1961  B) 1999  C) 2020  D) 1962\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Income Tax Rules, 1962 were framed to carry out the purposes of the Act.\n- A) 1961 is the year of the Income Tax *Act*, not the Rules.\n- B) 1999 is the year of FEMA.\n- C) 2020 is when the new residential-status provisions took effect, not the Rules.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** What period does India follow as its Financial Year (FY)?\nA) April 1 to March 31  B) January 1 to December 31  C) July 1 to June 30  D) October 1 to September 30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — India follows the Financial Year of April 1 to March 31 for calculating income for accounts and income tax.\n- B), C), D) are calendar/fiscal years used in other countries, not India's tax FY.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The financial year in which income is earned is called the:\nA) Previous Year  B) Assessment Year  C) Accounting Year  D) Base Year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The financial year for which one calculates tax liability (the year income is earned) is the 'Previous Year' (PY), defined by Section 3.\n- B) Assessment Year is the year *following* the previous year, when income is assessed.\n- C) and D) are not defined terms in the Act for this purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Section 2(9) of the Act defines 'Assessment Year' as a period of:\nA) 6 months  B) 24 months  C) 12 months commencing on 1st January  D) 12 months commencing on 1st April\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(9) defines 'assessment year' as a period of 12 months commencing on the 1st day of April every year.\n- A) 6 months is incorrect; tax years are 12 months.\n- C) commencing on 1st January is wrong; India's tax year starts 1st April.\n- B) 24 months is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** If income is earned in FY 2021-22, the Assessment Year will be:\nA) AY 2020-21  B) AY 2021-22  C) AY 2022-23  D) AY 2023-24\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The Assessment Year is the FY immediately following the Previous Year. PY 2021-22 → AY 2022-23.\n- A) AY 2020-21 precedes the PY — wrong direction.\n- B) AY 2021-22 is the same as the PY, but AY must follow it.\n- D) AY 2023-24 is two years later.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Section 3 of the Act defines 'Previous Year' as:\nA) The financial year immediately preceding the assessment year  B) Any 12 months chosen by the taxpayer  C) The financial year immediately following the assessment year  D) The calendar year of income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 3 defines 'Previous Year' as the financial year immediately preceding the assessment year.\n- B) The taxpayer cannot freely choose; it is fixed as April–March.\n- C) reverses the relationship; the PY *precedes* the AY.\n- D) India uses the financial year, not the calendar year.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which section of the Act defines 'Person'?\nA) Section 2(7)  B) Section 2(9)  C) Section 2(24)  D) Section 2(31)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(31) defines 'person'.\n- A) Section 2(7) defines 'assessee'.\n- B) Section 2(9) defines 'assessment year'.\n- C) Section 2(24) defines 'income'.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following is NOT listed as a 'Person' under Section 2(31)?\nA) Hindu Undivided Family (HUF)  B) Association of Persons (AOP)  C) A minor child treated separately  D) Local authority\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 2(31) lists Individual, HUF, Company, Partnership firm/LLP, AOP/BOI, Local authority, and Artificial juridical person. A \"minor child treated separately\" is not a category; a minor is an individual whose income is usually clubbed.\n- A) HUF, B) AOP, and D) Local authority are all explicitly listed as persons.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Under Section 2(31), an AOP, BOI, local authority or artificial juridical person is treated as a 'person':\nA) Only if formed to earn profit  B) Even if not formed to derive income, profits or gains  C) Only if registered with the government  D) Only if it has more than 10 members\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Persons referred to in clauses (e), (f) and (g) — AOP/BOI, local authority, artificial juridical person — are deemed a person even if not formed/established with the object of deriving income, profits or gains.\n- A) directly contradicts the rule; profit motive is not required.\n- C) Registration is not the test.\n- D) Number of members is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An 'assessee' under Section 2(7) is best described as:\nA) The person liable to pay tax or any other sum under the Act  B) Only individuals filing returns  C) The tax officer who assesses returns  D) Only companies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 2(7) defines assessee as the person liable for payment of taxes or any other sum, and includes anyone against whom a proceeding (regarding income, loss or refund) has been initiated.\n- B) Not just individuals — any 'person' can be an assessee.\n- C) The assessing officer is the tax authority, not the assessee.\n- D) Not only companies.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which section gives an inclusive definition of 'income'?\nA) Section 2(7)  B) Section 2(24)  C) Section 2(29C)  D) Section 6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 2(24) provides an inclusive definition of income, meaning listed items are income but other sums \"in the nature of income\" can also be taxed.\n- A) Section 2(7) defines assessee.\n- C) Section 2(29C) defines maximum marginal rate.\n- D) Section 6 deals with residential status.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following is specifically mentioned as income under Section 2(24)?\nA) A loan taken from a bank  B) Repayment of principal  C) Reimbursement of actual expenses  D) Gifts received from a non-relative covered by section 56\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(24) clauses include profits/gains from business, dividend, capital gains, gifts from a non-relative covered by section 56, property received below market price (section 56), and certain employment perquisites.\n- A) A loan is a liability, not income.\n- B) Repayment of principal is return of capital, not income.\n- C) Reimbursement of actual expenses is not income.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Residential status under the Income Tax Act is determined under which section?\nA) Section 2(31)  B) Section 6  C) Section 56  D) Section 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Residential status for the Income Tax Act is determined under Section 6.\n- A) Section 2(31) defines 'person'.\n- C) Section 56 deals with gifts/other sources.\n- D) Section 80C is a deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Citizenship and residential status under the Income Tax Act are:\nA) Different concepts  B) The same concept  C) Both determined by FEMA  D) Both determined by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — They are very different concepts. A person can be a citizen of another country but a resident of India under the Income Tax Act.\n- B) They are explicitly stated to be different, not the same.\n- C) and D) Residential status under the Act is determined under Section 6, not by FEMA or SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** How often is residential status under the Income Tax Act determined?\nA) Once in a lifetime  B) Every five years  C) From day to day  D) Every financial year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the Income Tax Act, residential status is determined every financial year. (Under FEMA, by contrast, it is determined from day to day at the time of an investment/remittance.)\n- A) It is not permanent.\n- C) \"Day to day\" is the FEMA basis, not Income Tax.\n- B) There is no five-year cycle.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Into how many categories can the residential status of an individual be classified?\nA) Two  B) Four  C) Five  D) Three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Three categories: (a) Resident and Ordinarily Resident (ROR), (b) Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident (RNOR), and (c) Non-Resident (NR).\n- A), B), C) are incorrect counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** From which financial year did residential status begin to depend on citizenship, period of stay AND total Indian income?\nA) FY 2018-19  B) FY 2019-20  C) FY 2020-21  D) FY 2021-22\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With effect from FY 2020-21 (AY 2021-22), the Finance Act 2020 substituted the provisions so that status depends on citizenship, period of stay and total Indian income.\n- A), B), D) are incorrect; the change took effect from FY 2020-21.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A 'deemed resident' under Section 6(1A) is always treated as:\nA) Resident and Ordinarily Resident  B) Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident  C) Non-Resident  D) Exempt from tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A deemed resident (an Indian citizen with Indian income above ₹15 lakh, not liable to tax elsewhere) is always treated as Resident but Not-Ordinarily Resident (RNOR).\n- A) ROR would tax worldwide income; the law specifically makes a deemed resident RNOR.\n- C) He is a resident (deemed), not non-resident.\n- D) He is taxable, not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Under Section 6(1A), an Indian citizen is deemed resident if his Indian income exceeds:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹10 lakh  C) ₹15 lakh  D) ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An Indian citizen is deemed resident (irrespective of stay) if his total income excluding foreign-source income exceeds ₹15 lakh and he is not liable to tax in any other country.\n- A), B), D) are not the threshold; the figure is ₹15 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A 'Person of Indian Origin' (PIO) is defined as someone where he, his parents, or grandparents were born in:\nA) Present-day India only  B) Undivided India  C) Any SAARC country  D) Any Commonwealth country\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A PIO is a person who, or whose parents or any grandparents, were born in undivided India.\n- A) The definition uses *undivided* India (pre-partition), broader than present-day India.\n- C) and D) are unrelated groupings.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The basic test that makes ANY individual a resident, regardless of category, is staying in India for:\nA) 60 days or more in the PY  B) 120 days or more in the PY  C) 182 days or more in the PY  D) 365 days in 4 preceding years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Staying in India for 182 days or more during the relevant previous year makes a person resident under clause (a) for every category.\n- A) and B) 60 and 120 days apply only with additional conditions.\n- D) The 365-days-in-4-years test is always a *secondary* condition, never standalone.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A resident individual is treated as \"Not Ordinarily Resident\" if he has been a non-resident in India for at least:\nA) 5 out of 10 preceding years  B) 7 out of 10 preceding years  C) 9 out of 10 preceding years  D) all 10 preceding years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — One NOR condition is being a non-resident in at least 9 out of the 10 years immediately preceding the relevant previous year.\n- A), B), D) are not the prescribed figure; it is 9 out of 10.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The second NOR condition is that the individual was in India for how many days or less during the 7 preceding years?\nA) 182 days  B) 365 days  C) 729 days  D) 730 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An NOR condition is being in India for 729 days or less during the 7 years immediately preceding the previous year.\n- A) and B) are other day-count tests, not this one.\n- D) 730 days is the boundary just above; the rule says \"729 or less\".\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A Resident and Ordinarily Resident (ROR) individual is taxed in India on:\nA) Only Indian income  B) Only foreign income  C) Nothing  D) Worldwide income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An ROR pays tax in India on worldwide income (income received/accrued in India plus income accruing outside India).\n- A) That describes a Non-Resident's general position.\n- B) Foreign income alone is never the rule.\n- C) An ROR is fully taxable, not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A Non-Resident is taxed in India on:\nA) Worldwide income  B) Only Indian income (received/accrued or deemed in India)  C) Only foreign income  D) Income from any source globally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A non-resident is taxable only on income received/deemed received in India and income that accrues/arises or is deemed to accrue/arise in India.\n- A) and D) Worldwide taxation applies only to ROR.\n- C) Foreign income of a non-resident is not taxed in India.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** How many heads of income are there under the Income Tax Act?\nA) Three  B) Four  C) Six  D) Five\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — There are five heads: Salary, House Property, Profits & Gains from Business or Profession, Capital Gains, and Income from Other Sources.\n- A), B), C) are incorrect counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Income from salary is taxable only if there exists a relationship of:\nA) Borrower and lender  B) Employer and employee  C) Buyer and seller  D) Principal and agent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxability under the salary head requires an employer-employee relationship; without it, income is taxed as business income or income from other sources.\n- A), C), D) describe other relationships that do not create salary income.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Salary is taxable on which basis?\nA) Receipt basis only  B) Due basis only  C) Due or receipt basis, whichever is earlier  D) Due or receipt basis, whichever is later\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Salary is taxable on a due basis or receipt basis, whichever is earlier (ESOPs are an exception).\n- A) and B) ignore the \"whichever is earlier\" rule.\n- D) \"Whichever is later\" is the opposite of the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Income from a vacant plot of land (with no building) is taxable under which head?\nA) Income from House Property  B) Capital Gains  C) Business/Profession or Other Sources  D) Salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — House property income requires a building or land appurtenant to it. Income from vacant land (no building) is taxed as business income or income from other sources.\n- A) House property needs a building; vacant land does not qualify.\n- B) Capital gains arise on *transfer*, not rental.\n- D) Salary requires employment.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Standard deduction from net annual value of a let-out house property under section 24(a) is:\nA) 10% of NAV  B) 20% of NAV  C) 50% of NAV  D) 30% of NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A standard deduction of up to 30% of the net annual value is available under section 24(a).\n- A), B), C) are not the prescribed rate; it is 30%.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Sections 60 to 64 of the Income Tax Act deal with:\nA) Residential status  B) Clubbing of income  C) Deductions  D) Capital gains exemptions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sections 60 to 64 contain the provisions relating to clubbing of income.\n- A) Residential status is Section 6.\n- C) Deductions are Chapter VI-A.\n- D) Capital gains exemptions are Sections 54–54GB.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which agreement is entered into between countries to avoid taxing the same income twice?\nA) FEMA  B) GAAR  C) GST Treaty  D) DTAA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) is entered into between two or more countries to avoid double taxation of income.\n- A) FEMA governs foreign exchange.\n- C) \"GST Treaty\" is not a real concept here.\n- B) GAAR is the General Anti-Avoidance Rule, not a double-tax agreement.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** What is the Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) for an individual as per the workbook?\nA) 30%  B) 34.944%  C) 42.744%  D) 21.45%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — MMR = 42.744%, computed as highest slab 30% + surcharge (30% × 37% = 11.1%) + cess (4% of 41.1% = 1.644%).\n- A) 30% is only the base highest slab rate without surcharge and cess.\n- B) 34.944% is the effective rate for a partnership firm above ₹1 crore.\n- D) 21.45% is the effective rate example for an individual with ₹20 lakh income.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. A is ROR in FY 2024-25 and earns interest on a bank balance in the USA. This interest is:\nA) Taxable in India because ROR is taxed on worldwide income  B) Not taxable in India  C) Taxable only in the USA  D) Exempt under DTAA automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An ROR is liable to tax on worldwide income, so US bank interest is taxable in India.\n- B) Wrong — foreign income IS taxable for an ROR.\n- C) The USA may tax it too, but India also taxes it (relief comes via DTAA credit, not automatic exemption).\n- D) DTAA gives a *credit* for foreign tax, it does not make the income exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Same facts as Q36, but Mr. A is RNOR. The US bank interest is:\nA) Not taxable in India (foreign income not from India-controlled business)  B) Taxable in India  C) Taxed at MMR  D) Clubbed with his spouse\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An RNOR is taxed on Indian income plus foreign income only if derived from a business controlled from / profession set up in India. Passive US bank interest is not such income, so it is not taxable in India.\n- B) Wrong for an RNOR (it would be right only for an ROR).\n- C) Rate is irrelevant since the income is not taxable at all.\n- D) Clubbing applies to specified transfers, not to one's own foreign interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An Indian citizen leaves India during the previous year for the purpose of employment outside India. To be a resident under clause (b), he must have stayed in India for at least:\nA) 60 days  B) 120 days  C) 182 days  D) 365 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For an Indian citizen leaving for employment (or a visiting citizen with Indian income up to ₹15 lakh), the relaxed test still requires 182 days or more in the PY to be resident.\n- A) and B) 60 and 120 days do not apply to one leaving for employment.\n- D) 365 days is a 4-year secondary test, not the PY threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A foreign national who is a PIO visits India with Indian income exceeding ₹15 lakh. He becomes resident if he stays in India for at least how many days in the PY (plus 365 days in 4 preceding years)?\nA) 60 days  B) 90 days  C) 182 days  D) 120 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For a PIO visiting India with Indian income exceeding ₹15 lakh, the test is 120 days or more in the PY AND 365 days or more in the 4 preceding years.\n- A) 60 days does not apply to PIO visitors.\n- B) 90 is not a threshold in the Act.\n- C) 182 days applies when Indian income is up to ₹15 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A non-PIO foreign national stays in India 100 days in FY 2024-25 and 400 days across the 4 preceding years. His status is:\nA) Resident (60+ days and 365+ in 4 years)  B) Non-resident  C) RNOR automatically  D) Deemed resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For non-PIO foreigners, clause (b) makes a person resident if in India 60+ days (but less than 182) in the PY AND 365+ days in the 4 preceding years. 100 days (≥60, <182) and 400 days (≥365) satisfy both, so he is resident.\n- B) Wrong — both conditions are met, so he is resident.\n- C) RNOR needs a separate test, not automatic.\n- D) Deemed-resident applies to Indian citizens, not foreigners.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An HUF's control and management is partly in India (one decision taken in India) during the year. The HUF is:\nA) Non-resident  B) Always RNOR  C) Resident  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An HUF is resident in every case except where control and management is wholly outside India. If even a single decision is taken in India, part of control is in India, so it is resident.\n- A) Non-resident requires control wholly outside India.\n- B) RNOR needs the Karta to satisfy a separate test.\n- D) Residence does not grant exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A resident HUF is treated as RNOR if its Manager (Karta):\nA) Is below 60 years of age  B) Lives abroad permanently  C) Is a foreign citizen  D) Has been a non-resident for at least 9 of 10 preceding years, OR in India ≤729 days in 7 preceding years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A resident HUF is RNOR if the Karta satisfies either NOR condition: non-resident in 9 of 10 preceding years, or in India 729 days or less in the 7 preceding years.\n- A) Age is not the test.\n- B) and C) Permanent residence abroad or citizenship are not the prescribed conditions.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Mr. P owns four houses: he lives in House A; House B is actually let out; Houses C and D are not let out. Which house's rent is definitely taxed as let-out?\nA) House A  B) House B  C) House C  D) House D\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — House B is actually let out, so its rental income is taxed as let-out. Out of A, C, D (none actually let out), he may choose any two as self-occupied; the remaining one becomes deemed let-out.\n- A) House A is occupied (can be self-occupied).\n- C) and D) are not actually let out; one of them will be deemed let-out depending on his choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** How many house properties (not actually let out) can an assessee select as 'self-occupied' for tax purposes?\nA) Any three  B) One  C) Unlimited  D) Any two\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An assessee may select any two non-let-out houses as self-occupied; any other non-let-out house is treated as deemed let-out.\n- B) The limit was raised to two houses.\n- A) and C) exceed the permitted two.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Under the OLD tax regime, the maximum interest deduction on a loan for a self-occupied property (loan post-1 Apr 1999, construction within 5 years) is:\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a self-occupied property under the old regime, interest is capped at ₹2,00,000 where the loan was taken on/after 1 Apr 1999 and construction completed within 5 years.\n- A) ₹30,000 applies if construction took more than 5 years.\n- B) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C cap, not the interest cap.\n- D) \"No limit\" applies to let-out / deemed let-out properties, not self-occupied.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Under the NEW tax regime, interest on a loan for a self-occupied property is:\nA) Capped at ₹2,00,000  B) Capped at ₹30,000  C) Not available  D) Fully deductible without limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Interest deduction on a self-occupied property is not available under the new tax regime, so its house-property income is always Nil.\n- A) and B) are old-regime caps.\n- D) \"No limit\" applies only to let-out property, and only the old regime allows self-occupied interest at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Pre-construction period interest on a house loan is allowed as a deduction in:\nA) 5 equal instalments starting from the year of acquisition/completion  B) One lump sum in the year of acquisition  C) 10 equal instalments  D) Not allowed at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Pre-construction interest (for the period prior to the FY of acquisition) is allowed in 5 equal instalments starting from the year the property is acquired/constructed.\n- B) It is spread, not lump sum.\n- C) 5 instalments, not 10.\n- D) It is allowed, just deferred.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A transaction of purchase/sale of shares settled otherwise than by actual delivery is treated as:\nA) Speculative (Section 43(5))  B) Non-speculative  C) A capital gain  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under Section 43(5), a purchase/sale of any commodity (including shares) settled otherwise than by actual delivery is a speculative transaction.\n- B) Non-speculative would require actual delivery.\n- C) If treated as business, it is speculative business income, not capital gain.\n- D) It is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which of the following is NOT treated as a speculative transaction under Section 43(5)?\nA) Intra-day equity squared off without delivery  B) A hedging transaction to guard against loss  C) A non-delivery commodity bet  D) Any settlement otherwise than by delivery\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Hedging transactions (and transactions in derivatives) are specifically NOT treated as speculative even if settled without delivery.\n- A), C), D) describe non-delivery settlements that ARE speculative; only hedging/derivatives are carved out.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Speculative business losses can be carried forward for how many years, compared to normal business losses?\nA) 8 years vs 4 years  B) 4 years vs 8 years  C) 4 years vs 4 years  D) Indefinitely vs 8 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Speculative losses can be carried forward 4 years, while normal (non-speculative) business losses can be carried forward 8 years.\n- A) reverses the figures.\n- C) Normal business losses get 8 years, not 4.\n- D) Indefinite carry-forward applies to unabsorbed depreciation, not speculative loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A long-term capital loss (LTCL) can be set off against:\nA) Only long-term capital gains  B) Any income head  C) Any capital gain (STCG or LTCG)  D) Salary income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCL can be set off only against long-term capital gains, not even against short-term capital gains.\n- B) Capital losses cannot be set off inter-head.\n- C) That rule applies to short-term capital loss, not long-term.\n- D) Capital losses can never be set off against salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A short-term capital loss (STCL) can be set off against:\nA) Only STCG  B) Only LTCG  C) Any capital gain, whether short-term or long-term  D) Salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCL can be set off against any capital gain — short-term or long-term.\n- A) and B) STCL is more flexible — it covers both.\n- D) Capital losses cannot be set off against salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A normal (non-speculative) business loss can be set off against any income EXCEPT:\nA) House property income  B) Capital gains  C) Salary and gambling income  D) Other sources\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A normal business loss can be set off against any income other than salary and income from gambling activities.\n- A), B), D) are allowed for set-off; only salary and gambling are excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Unabsorbed depreciation can be carried forward for:\nA) 4 years  B) 8 years  C) Cannot be carried forward  D) Any number of years (indefinitely)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unabsorbed depreciation can be carried forward for any number of years and set off against any income except salary.\n- A) 4 years applies to speculative loss.\n- B) 8 years applies to normal business loss.\n- C) It can be carried forward indefinitely, not barred.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A loss under the head 'Income from Other Sources' (non-gambling):\nA) Can be set off against any head except gambling, but cannot be carried forward  B) Can be carried forward 8 years  C) Can only be set off against other sources income  D) Can never be set off\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An other-sources loss can be set off against any head except gambling income in the same year, but if not set off, it cannot be carried forward.\n- B) and C) are incorrect; there is no carry-forward.\n- D) It CAN be set off in the same year.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Under the OLD regime, a house-property loss can be set off against other heads of income up to a maximum of:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹30,000  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — House-property loss set-off against other heads is restricted to ₹2,00,000 per financial year under the old regime.\n- A) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C cap.\n- C) ₹30,000 is a self-occupied interest sub-limit.\n- D) The set-off against other heads is capped, not unlimited.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Under the NEW regime, a house-property loss set-off against other heads of income is:\nA) Allowed up to ₹2,00,000  B) Not allowed (but deemed to have been set off)  C) Allowed without limit  D) Allowed only against salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the new regime, set-off of house-property loss against other heads is not allowed but is deemed to have been set off (so it cannot be carried forward as inter-head loss).\n- A) That is the old-regime rule.\n- C) and D) are incorrect under the new regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A carried-forward house property loss can be set off in subsequent years only against:\nA) Income from house property  B) Any income  C) Salary  D) Capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A carried-forward house-property loss (carried up to 8 years) can be set off only against income from house property in subsequent years.\n- B), C), D) Carried-forward house-property loss is restricted to the same head.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Under Section 60, if a person transfers the income from an asset WITHOUT transferring the asset itself, the income is taxed in the hands of:\nA) The transferee (recipient)  B) The transferor  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 60, transfer of income without transferring the asset means the income is still included in the total income of the transferor.\n- A) The recipient does not get taxed; the asset-owner does.\n- C) Not shared.\n- D) The income IS taxed, in the transferor's hands.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Mr. A gifts ₹30,00,000 to his wife, who earns ₹1,50,000 interest. That interest is:\nA) Taxed in the wife's hands at her slab  B) Clubbed in Mr. A's income and taxed at his slab  C) Exempt  D) Split 50:50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under clubbing (Section 64), the ₹1,50,000 interest is clubbed in Mr. A's income and taxed as per his slab, not his wife's.\n- A) It is not taxed at the wife's slab — clubbing applies.\n- C) It is taxable.\n- D) The whole amount is clubbed, not split.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these is NOT a person whose income may be clubbed under Section 64?\nA) Spouse  B) Son's wife  C) Minor child  D) An unrelated business partner\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 64 covers spouse, son's wife, minor child, certain AOP, and property gifted to HUF. An unrelated business partner is not in the list.\n- A), B), C) are all covered by Section 64.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** The standard deduction from salary under the OLD vs NEW tax regime is:\nA) ₹50,000 (old) and ₹75,000 (new)  B) ₹75,000 (old) and ₹50,000 (new)  C) ₹50,000 (both)  D) ₹75,000 (both)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Standard deduction is ₹50,000 under the old regime and ₹75,000 under the new regime.\n- B) reverses the figures.\n- C) and D) ignore that the two regimes differ.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Deduction for employer's contribution to NPS under section 80CCD(2) for a PRIVATE sector employee is:\nA) 10% of salary (old) and 14% of salary (new)  B) 14% (both)  C) 10% (both)  D) Not available in either\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For private sector employees, 80CCD(2) allows up to 10% of salary under the old regime and up to 14% under the new regime.\n- B) 14% for both is wrong for private employees (it is 14% for government employees in both regimes).\n- C) 10% both is wrong for the new regime.\n- D) It IS available, and notably also in the new regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which of the following salary exemptions/deductions is NOT allowed under the new tax regime?\nA) Employer's NPS contribution under 80CCD(2)  B) Standard deduction  C) House Rent Allowance (HRA) exemption  D) Agniveer Corpus deduction under 80CCH\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — HRA exemption and Leave Travel Allowance are not allowed under the new regime.\n- A) 80CCD(2) employer NPS is available even in the new regime.\n- B) Standard deduction (₹75,000) is available in the new regime.\n- D) 80CCH (Agniveer Corpus) is available even in the new regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Where the employer's aggregate contribution to superannuation fund, EPF and NPS exceeds which amount, the excess is taxed as salary [section 17(2)(vii)]?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹7,50,000  D) ₹20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Where the employer's combined contribution to all three funds exceeds ₹7,50,000 in aggregate, the excess is taxed as salary income.\n- A) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C cap.\n- B) ₹2,00,000 is the house-property interest cap.\n- D) ₹20,00,000 is the gratuity exemption ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The maximum gratuity exemption ceiling under section 10(10) (for non-government employees) is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) Fully exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The exemption is the least of (15 days' / half-month's salary per completed year), ₹20,00,000, or actual gratuity. So ₹20,00,000 is the monetary ceiling.\n- A) and B) are not the ceiling.\n- D) Full exemption applies only to government employees.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Under Section 80CCE, the aggregate deduction under 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) cannot exceed:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 80CCE caps the combined deduction under 80C, 80CCC and 80CCD(1) at ₹1,50,000.\n- A), B), C) are not the combined cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which deduction is available even under the NEW tax regime?\nA) 80C  B) 80D (medical insurance)  C) 80CCD(2) (employer NPS)  D) 80TTA (savings interest)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 80CCD(2) (employer's NPS contribution) — and 80CCH (Agniveer) — are allowed under the new regime; most other Chapter VI-A deductions are not.\n- A) 80C is not available in the new regime.\n- B) 80D is not available in the new regime.\n- D) 80TTA is not available in the new regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Mr. IS is ROR in India and earns income in France on which he pays French tax. Regarding Indian tax:\nA) The French income is exempt in India  B) India taxes it but allows credit for French tax (DTAA), as India is the residence country  C) France gets no right to tax  D) The income is taxed only in France\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As an ROR, his global income is taxable in India, but he gets credit for taxes paid in France, since India is his country of residence (DTAA relief).\n- A) It is not exempt; it is taxed with a credit.\n- C) France, as source country, has primary taxing rights.\n- D) India also taxes it (with credit).\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Public Provident Fund (PPF) and ELSS are examples of which taxation-regime category?\nA) EEE  B) ETE  C) EET  D) TEE always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PPF and ELSS are EEE (Exempt at investment, Exempt on returns, Exempt on withdrawal) — though the first 'E' is lost in the new tax regime.\n- C) EET is taxed only at withdrawal.\n- B) ETE describes 5-year tax-saving FDs and SCSS.\n- D) They become TEE only when the new regime removes the deduction; their classic category is EEE.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. C (Indian citizen) stays 298 days in India in FY 2024-25 and a total of 578 days across the 7 preceding years, and was non-resident in 9 of the 10 preceding years. His status for FY 2024-25 is:\nA) Non-resident  B) ROR  C) RNOR  D) Deemed resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Step 1: 298 ≥ 182 days → Resident. Step 2 (NOR test, satisfy ANY one): he stayed 578 days (≤729) in 7 years AND was non-resident in 9 of 10 years — both NOR conditions met → RNOR.\n- A) Non-resident is wrong because 298 ≥ 182.\n- B) ROR requires failing both NOR tests; he satisfies them, so not ROR.\n- D) Deemed resident is for Indian citizens with >₹15 lakh Indian income not taxed elsewhere — not these facts.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Mr. Vilayati (PIO, non-resident for 20 years) comes to India for employment on 1 April 2024 and stays the whole year. His status for FY 2024-25 is:\nA) Non-resident  B) RNOR  C) ROR  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He stays the full FY (>182 days) → Resident. For NOR, satisfying ANY one suffices: he was non-resident in 9 of 10 preceding years AND spent fewer than 730 days in the 7 preceding years. So → RNOR.\n- A) >182 days means he is resident.\n- C) ROR requires failing both NOR tests; he satisfies them.\n- D) Residents are not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Continuing Q72, by FY 2026-27 (after two full resident years), Mr. Vilayati's status becomes:\nA) ROR  B) RNOR still  C) Non-resident  D) Deemed resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — By FY 2026-27 he stays >182 days (resident). For NOR: he is NOT non-resident in 9 of 10 years (he was resident in FY 2024-25 and 2025-26) and he has now spent more than 730 days in the preceding 7 years. He fails BOTH NOR conditions → ROR.\n- B) He no longer satisfies either NOR condition.\n- C) >182 days means resident.\n- D) Deemed resident needs the >₹15 lakh/no-foreign-tax test, not these facts.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Continuing the Vilayati case, his foreign bond interest is taxable in India in:\nA) FY 2024-25 only  B) FY 2025-26 only  C) FY 2026-27 onwards (when he becomes ROR)  D) All three years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — As RNOR in FY 2024-25 and 2025-26, his passive foreign bond interest is NOT taxed in India. From FY 2026-27 he is ROR (worldwide income), so the bond interest becomes taxable in India.\n- A) and B) RNOR years do not tax this foreign income.\n- D) The first two years (RNOR) do not tax it.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Mr. B's basic salary is ₹15,00,000. Employer EPF = ₹21,600 (well within 12% of salary). The taxable add-back of employer's EPF is:\nA) ₹21,600  B) Nil  C) ₹1,80,000  D) ₹2,31,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Employer EPF is taxable only to the extent it exceeds 12% of salary. ₹21,600 is within 12% of ₹15,00,000 (12% = ₹1,80,000), so the taxable add-back is Nil under section 17(1)(vi).\n- A) The full amount is not added because it is within the 12% limit.\n- C) ₹1,80,000 is the 12% threshold, not the add-back.\n- D) ₹2,31,600 is the total employer contribution, not the EPF add-back.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Same Mr. B: employer NPS = ₹2,10,000, total employer contribution = ₹2,31,600. Is there a section 17(2)(vii) add-back for exceeding ₹7,50,000?\nA) Yes, ₹2,31,600  B) Yes, ₹1,50,000  C) No add-back (aggregate within ₹7,50,000)  D) Yes, ₹7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The aggregate employer contribution (₹2,31,600) is within the ₹7,50,000 limit, so there is no 17(2)(vii) add-back.\n- A), B), D) all assume an excess over ₹7,50,000, but the aggregate is below the limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** For Mr. B (gross salary ₹17,10,000 = ₹15,00,000 + ₹2,10,000 employer NPS perquisite), the taxable salary (A) after standard deduction under the OLD regime is:\nA) ₹16,35,000  B) ₹16,60,000  C) ₹17,10,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross salary ₹17,10,000 − old-regime standard deduction ₹50,000 = ₹16,60,000.\n- A) ₹16,35,000 uses the new-regime standard deduction of ₹75,000.\n- C) ₹17,10,000 is before standard deduction.\n- D) ₹15,00,000 ignores the employer NPS perquisite and the deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Mr. B (old regime): employee EPF ₹21,600 + PPF ₹1,50,000 under 80C, NPS 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000, employer NPS 80CCD(2) at 10% of ₹15,00,000. The 80CCD(2) deduction is:\nA) ₹2,10,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹1,71,600  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Old-regime 80CCD(2) for a private employee = 10% of salary = 10% × ₹15,00,000 = ₹1,50,000 (even though employer actually contributed ₹2,10,000, the deduction is capped at 10%).\n- A) ₹2,10,000 is the new-regime cap (14%), not old (10%).\n- B) ₹50,000 is the 80CCD(1B) figure.\n- C) ₹1,71,600 is not a relevant figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. B (old regime): Taxable salary ₹16,60,000 less total deductions = ₹1,50,000 (80C) + ₹50,000 (80CCD(1B)) + ₹1,50,000 (80CCD(2)) = ₹3,50,000. Taxable income is:\nA) ₹14,25,000  B) ₹13,10,000  C) ₹16,60,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable income = ₹16,60,000 − ₹3,50,000 = ₹13,10,000.\n- A) ₹14,25,000 is the new-regime answer.\n- C) ₹16,60,000 ignores the deductions.\n- D) ₹15,00,000 is just the basic salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Mr. B (NEW regime): gross salary ₹17,10,000, standard deduction ₹75,000, and the ONLY available deduction 80CCD(2) at 14% of ₹15,00,000 = ₹2,10,000. Taxable income is:\nA) ₹13,10,000  B) ₹14,25,000  C) ₹16,35,000  D) ₹17,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — New regime: ₹17,10,000 − ₹75,000 = ₹16,35,000; less 80CCD(2) ₹2,10,000 = ₹14,25,000. (80C and 80CCD(1B) are not available in the new regime.)\n- A) ₹13,10,000 is the old-regime answer.\n- C) ₹16,35,000 is before the 80CCD(2) deduction.\n- D) ₹17,10,000 ignores all deductions.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Mr. A (old regime): salary ₹15,00,000; self-occupied house with home-loan interest ₹5,00,000; 80C eligible ₹1,80,000. House-property income (self-occupied) is the negative of interest, capped at:\nA) −₹2,00,000  B) −₹5,00,000  C) −₹1,50,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Self-occupied annual value is Nil; the loss = interest, but capped at ₹2,00,000 under the old regime. So house-property income = −₹2,00,000.\n- B) −₹5,00,000 ignores the ₹2 lakh cap.\n- C) −₹1,50,000 is the 80C cap, not the interest cap.\n- D) Nil applies under the NEW regime, not the old.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Continuing Q81 (old regime): Gross Total Income = ₹15,00,000 − ₹2,00,000 = ₹13,00,000. The 80C deduction (eligible ₹1,80,000) is allowed as:\nA) ₹1,80,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 80C is capped at ₹1,50,000, so although ₹1,80,000 was spent, only ₹1,50,000 is deductible.\n- A) ₹1,80,000 exceeds the cap.\n- C) ₹2,00,000 is the house-property interest cap, not 80C.\n- D) Some 80C is allowed (up to the cap).\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Continuing Q81–82 (old regime): Taxable income = ₹13,00,000 − ₹1,50,000 (80C) =\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹13,00,000  C) ₹11,50,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Taxable income = ₹13,00,000 − ₹1,50,000 = ₹11,50,000.\n- A) ₹15,00,000 ignores both the house-property loss and 80C.\n- B) ₹13,00,000 is the GTI before 80C.\n- D) ₹10,00,000 is not a step in the calculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Same facts (self-occupied, interest ₹5,00,000) but NEW regime. Mr. A's taxable income is:\nA) ₹11,50,000  B) ₹13,00,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In the new regime, self-occupied interest deduction is not available (house-property income = Nil) and 80C is not available. So taxable income = full salary ₹15,00,000.\n- A) ₹11,50,000 is the old-regime answer.\n- B) ₹13,00,000 wrongly allows the house-property loss.\n- D) ₹10,00,000 is not relevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Mr. A has a LET-OUT house: annual value ₹2,20,000; standard deduction 30%. The standard deduction amount is:\nA) ₹44,000  B) ₹66,000  C) ₹73,333  D) ₹1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Standard deduction (section 24a) = 30% of NAV = 30% × ₹2,20,000 = ₹66,000.\n- A) ₹44,000 is 20%.\n- C) ₹73,333 is one-third (33.3%), not 30%.\n- D) ₹1,10,000 is 50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: annual value ₹2,20,000 − standard deduction ₹66,000 − interest ₹5,00,000. The house-property income is:\nA) −₹2,80,000  B) −₹5,00,000  C) +₹1,54,000  D) −₹3,46,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — House-property income = ₹2,20,000 − ₹66,000 − ₹5,00,000 = −₹3,46,000 (a loss). For let-out property there is no cap on interest.\n- A) −₹2,80,000 forgets to subtract the standard deduction correctly.\n- B) −₹5,00,000 ignores the net annual value contribution.\n- C) It is a loss, not a positive figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Continuing Q86 (old regime): the loss of ₹3,46,000 can be set off against salary in the current year up to:\nA) ₹3,46,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) Nil  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Inter-head set-off of house-property loss is capped at ₹2,00,000 under the old regime.\n- A) ₹3,46,000 ignores the ₹2 lakh cap.\n- B) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C cap.\n- C) Some set-off IS allowed, up to ₹2 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Continuing Q86–87: the balance house-property loss to be carried forward is:\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,46,000  C) ₹1,46,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Carried-forward loss = total loss − set off = ₹3,46,000 − ₹2,00,000 = ₹1,46,000 (carried forward up to 8 years, set off only against future house-property income).\n- A) ₹2,00,000 is the amount already set off.\n- B) ₹3,46,000 is the total loss before set-off.\n- D) There is a remaining loss to carry forward.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Continuing the let-out case: under the NEW regime, can the ₹2,00,000 portion be set off against salary in the current year?\nA) Yes, ₹2,00,000  B) No set off against other heads (₹0), but ₹1,46,000 still carried forward  C) Yes, full ₹3,46,000  D) No carry forward at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In the new regime, inter-head set-off of house-property loss is not allowed (deemed set off), so ₹0 against salary; however, the ₹1,46,000 balance loss is still carried forward (set off only against future house-property income).\n- A) and C) The new regime disallows inter-head set-off.\n- D) Carry forward of the house-property loss is still allowed.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Mr. Gora, a non-Indian-origin UK citizen, has worked in India since 2005 without travelling abroad (stays >182 days every year). His status for FY 2024-25 is:\nA) Non-resident  B) RNOR  C) ROR  D) Deemed resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — He stays >182 days each year for many years, so he is resident and fails both NOR tests (not non-resident 9/10 years, and far more than 729 days in 7 years) → ROR. His UK rental income is therefore taxable in India.\n- A) >182 days means resident.\n- B) He fails the NOR tests.\n- D) Deemed-resident applies to Indian citizens.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Mr. Gora (ROR) has rental income in the UK. Is it taxable in India?\nA) No, because he is a foreigner  B) Yes, because an ROR pays tax on global income  C) Only if remitted to India  D) Only under FEMA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As an ROR, Mr. Gora pays tax on his global income in India, so his UK rental income is taxable in India.\n- A) Citizenship does not exempt foreign income for an ROR.\n- C) Taxability does not depend on remittance for an ROR.\n- D) FEMA is a separate law; this is about income tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A partnership firm has income exceeding ₹1 crore. Using effective tax rate = rate × (1 + surcharge) × (1 + cess) with 30%, 12% surcharge and 4% cess, the effective rate is:\nA) 34.944%  B) 30%  C) 42.744%  D) 33.6%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Effective rate = 30% × (1 + 0.12) × (1 + 0.04) = 30% × 1.12 × 1.04 = 34.944%.\n- B) 30% ignores surcharge and cess.\n- C) 42.744% is the individual MMR (37% surcharge).\n- D) 33.6% applies surcharge but forgets the 4% cess.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) for an individual: highest slab 30%, surcharge = 30% × 37% = 11.1%, cess = 4% of (30% + 11.1%). The cess component is:\nA) 1.2%  B) 1.644%  C) 1.11%  D) 4%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cess = 4% × (30% + 11.1%) = 4% × 41.1% = 1.644%.\n- A) 1.2% is 4% of 30% only (ignores surcharge in the base).\n- C) 1.11% confuses surcharge with cess.\n- D) 4% is the cess rate, not the cess amount on income.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Adding it up: highest slab 30% + surcharge 11.1% + cess 1.644% gives an MMR of:\nA) 41.1%  B) 42.744%  C) 34.944%  D) 45%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — MMR = 30 + 11.1 + 1.644 = 42.744%.\n- A) 41.1% omits the cess.\n- C) 34.944% is the partnership-firm effective rate.\n- D) 45% is not the computed figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An individual has total income ₹20,00,000 and income tax payable ₹4,29,000. The effective tax rate is:\nA) 30%  B) 21.45%  C) 42.744%  D) 4.29%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For an individual on slabs, effective rate = total tax ÷ total income = ₹4,29,000 ÷ ₹20,00,000 = 21.45%.\n- A) 30% is the highest slab, not the effective average.\n- C) 42.744% is the MMR.\n- D) 4.29% misplaces the decimal.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Under the NEW regime (FY 2024-25), the section 87A rebate of up to ₹25,000 is available if total income is up to:\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹7,00,000  C) ₹12,00,000  D) ₹7,28,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From AY 2024-25 (FY 2024-25), a resident individual under the new regime with total income up to ₹7,00,000 can claim a rebate of up to ₹25,000.\n- A) ₹5,00,000 (with ₹12,500 rebate) is the old-regime limit.\n- C) ₹12,00,000 (₹60,000 rebate) is from FY 2025-26.\n- D) ₹7,28,000 is the marginal-relief ceiling, not the rebate threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Under the NEW regime from FY 2025-26, the section 87A rebate of up to ₹60,000 applies for total income up to:\nA) ₹12,00,000  B) ₹10,00,000  C) ₹7,00,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Effective FY 2025-26 (AY 2026-27), the rebate is up to ₹60,000 for total income up to ₹12,00,000, with marginal relief just above.\n- C) ₹7,00,000 (₹25,000) is the FY 2024-25 new-regime limit.\n- B) ₹10,00,000 is not a threshold.\n- D) ₹15,00,000 is not the rebate threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** The section 87A rebate is NOT available against tax on:\nA) Salary income  B) House property income  C) Interest income  D) Long-term capital gains under section 112A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The 87A rebate is not available against tax payable under section 112A (LTCG on listed equity shares / equity MF units etc.) or under section 111 (accumulated PF balance).\n- A), B), C) are normal incomes against which the rebate can apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Under the OLD regime, the section 87A rebate of up to ₹12,500 is available to a resident individual with total income not exceeding:\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹7,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The old-regime 87A rebate is up to ₹12,500 for a resident individual whose total income does not exceed ₹5,00,000.\n- A) ₹2,50,000 is the basic exemption limit, not the rebate threshold.\n- C) ₹7,00,000 (₹25,000) is the new-regime FY 2024-25 limit.\n- D) ₹12,00,000 (₹60,000) is the new-regime FY 2025-26 limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Gross Total Income (Section 80B(5)) is computed by: (1) summing the five heads, (2) clubbing income, (3) setting off losses — and it is the figure BEFORE which deductions?\nA) Standard deduction  B) Section 87A rebate  C) Section 24(a)  D) Chapter VI-A deductions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — GTI is total income computed before any deduction under Chapter VI-A. Total Income = GTI − Chapter VI-A deductions.\n- A) Standard deduction is taken within the salary head, before GTI.\n- C) Section 24(a) is the house-property standard deduction, within that head.\n- B) The 87A rebate is applied to *tax*, not to income.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Rajan is an Indian citizen and a salaried employee. For FY 2024-25 (AY 2025-26) the following details apply:\n\n- He stayed in India for **210 days** in FY 2024-25, and a total of **600 days** across the 7 preceding financial years; he was a non-resident in 9 of the 10 preceding years (he recently returned after a long overseas posting).\n- **Basic salary:** ₹16,00,000. Employer EPF contribution ₹1,80,000; employer NPS contribution ₹2,24,000. Employee 80C-eligible investments (EPF + LIC + PPF) total ₹2,00,000; employee NPS 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000.\n- He owns **one let-out house**: annual value ₹3,00,000, municipal taxes paid ₹20,000, home-loan interest ₹4,00,000.\n- He has a **bank account in Dubai** earning interest of ₹1,00,000 (passive; not connected to any India-controlled business).\n\n(Assume the OLD tax regime unless stated; salary for NPS/EPF percentage limits = basic salary ₹16,00,000.)\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-1. What is Mr. Rajan's residential status for FY 2024-25, and is the Dubai interest taxable in India?</summary>\n\n**Step 1 — Resident test:** He stayed 210 days ≥ 182 days in the PY → **Resident**.\n**Step 2 — NOR test (satisfy ANY one):**\n- Non-resident in 9 of 10 preceding years → satisfied; AND\n- In India 600 days (≤ 729 days) in the 7 preceding years → satisfied.\nSince he satisfies the NOR conditions, he is **Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident (RNOR)**.\n\n**Dubai interest:** An RNOR is taxed on Indian income plus foreign income only if it is from a business controlled from / profession set up in India. The Dubai bank interest is passive and not so connected, so it is **NOT taxable in India**. (Intuition: RNOR gives a \"shield\" to genuinely foreign passive income.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-2. Compute Mr. Rajan's gross salary, after testing the employer EPF and the ₹7,50,000 aggregate-contribution rule.</summary>\n\n**Employer EPF add-back:** Employer EPF is taxable only above 12% of salary. 12% × ₹16,00,000 = ₹1,92,000. Employer EPF of ₹1,80,000 is *within* ₹1,92,000 → **add-back = Nil** [section 17(1)(vi)].\n\n**Employer NPS:** The whole employer NPS contribution ₹2,24,000 is added to salary as a perquisite [section 17(1)(viii)].\n\n**₹7,50,000 aggregate rule [section 17(2)(vii)]:** Aggregate employer contribution = EPF ₹1,80,000 + NPS ₹2,24,000 = ₹4,04,000, which is **below ₹7,50,000** → **no further add-back**.\n\n**Gross salary = ₹16,00,000 + ₹2,24,000 = ₹18,24,000.**\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-3. Compute taxable salary income (head: Salary) under the OLD regime after standard deduction and the salary-linked NPS/80C deductions.</summary>\n\nStart from gross salary ₹18,24,000.\n\n**Less standard deduction (old regime): ₹50,000** → Net salary = ₹17,74,000.\n\nNow the deductions from Gross Total Income that relate to him:\n- **80C:** eligible ₹2,00,000 but **capped at ₹1,50,000**.\n- **80CCD(1B):** employee NPS ₹50,000 (allowed in old regime).\n- **80CCD(2):** employer NPS, old-regime cap = **10% of salary** = 10% × ₹16,00,000 = ₹1,60,000 (employer gave ₹2,24,000, but deduction is capped at 10%).\n\nSo the salary head contributes **₹17,74,000** to GTI; the 80C/80CCD deductions (₹1,50,000 + ₹50,000 + ₹1,60,000 = ₹3,60,000) are applied later from GTI (shown in Q-CS-5).\n(Note: 80CCE caps 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) at ₹1,50,000; here 80C alone already hits ₹1,50,000, and 80CCD(1B)/80CCD(2) are over and above that cap.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-4. Compute the income from the let-out house property.</summary>\n\n**Formula:** Income from house property = Net Annual Value − 30% standard deduction − interest on loan (no cap for let-out).\n\n- Annual Value (A) = ₹3,00,000\n- Less municipal taxes paid (B) = ₹20,000\n- **Net Annual Value (C = A − B) = ₹2,80,000**\n- Less standard deduction (section 24a) = 30% × ₹2,80,000 = **₹84,000**\n- Less interest on home loan = **₹4,00,000** (let-out → no limit)\n\n**Income from house property = ₹2,80,000 − ₹84,000 − ₹4,00,000 = −₹2,04,000 (a loss).**\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-5. Compute Mr. Rajan's Gross Total Income and Total Income (old regime), applying loss set-off and Chapter VI-A deductions.</summary>\n\n**Step A — Heads of income:**\n- Salary (after standard deduction) = ₹17,74,000\n- House property = −₹2,04,000 (loss)\n- Dubai interest = not taxable (RNOR) → ₹0\n\n**Step B — Inter-head set-off of house-property loss (old regime, capped at ₹2,00,000):**\nLoss is ₹2,04,000 but only **₹2,00,000** can be set off against other heads this year.\nGTI = ₹17,74,000 − ₹2,00,000 = **₹15,74,000**.\nThe remaining **₹4,000** house-property loss is **carried forward** (up to 8 years, set off only against future house-property income).\n\n**Step C — Chapter VI-A deductions:**\n- 80C ₹1,50,000 + 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000 + 80CCD(2) ₹1,60,000 = **₹3,60,000**.\n\n**Total Income = GTI − Chapter VI-A = ₹15,74,000 − ₹3,60,000 = ₹12,14,000.**\n\n**Intuition recap:** RNOR shielded the Dubai interest; the let-out interest created a loss but inter-head relief is capped at ₹2 lakh (old regime), with the tiny ₹4,000 balance carried forward; and employer NPS deduction was limited to 10% of salary in the old regime.\n</details>\n\n\n"}, {"num": "08", "title": "Capital Gains", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 8: Capital Gains — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **basic concepts** of capital gains: capital asset, transfer, consideration, cost, dates\n- What counts as a **capital asset** and what's excluded\n- **Types** of capital asset: short-term vs long-term, and the **holding-period rules** (12 / 24 months, \"always short-term\" cases)\n- How to **calculate the period of holding** in special cases (bonus, rights, gifts, demat, etc.)\n- What \"**transfer**\" means and which transactions are **not** transfers\n- Full **computation of capital gains**: full value of consideration, cost of acquisition, **indexation**, cost of improvement\n- The new **tax rates** (Section 111A 20% STCG; 12.5% LTCG; ₹1,25,000 exemption under 112A)\n- **Exemptions**: Sections 54, 54B, 54D, 54EC, 54EE, 54F, 54G, 54GA, 54GB, 115F\n\n---\n\n## 8.1 Basic Concepts\nAny income arising from the **transfer of a capital asset** is taxed under the head **\"Capital Gains\"**, in the **previous year in which the transfer takes place** (accrual basis).\n\nThe five building blocks: **capital asset, transfer, sale consideration, cost of acquisition, and the dates of purchase and transfer.**\n\n**Analogy:** Capital gain = (price you sell for) − (what it cost you + costs of selling). The tax law just adds careful definitions for each of those words.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Capital Asset — Section 2(14)\n\n**Capital asset** means **property of any kind** held by an assessee (whether or not connected with business/profession). It also specifically includes securities held by an **FPI/FII** or a **Category I/II AIF** per SEBI rules, and a \"high-premium ULIP\" (a ULIP to which the section 10(10D) exemption does not apply) — from FY 2025-26 any such ULIP is a capital asset.\n\n**Specific inclusions:** all property — movable, immovable, tangible, intangible — including rights of management/control of an Indian company, business undertaking, a partner's share in a firm, route permit, leasehold right, right to subscribe shares, goodwill, licence to manufacture, gold, jewellery, securities, etc.\n\n### Exclusions (NOT capital assets):\n| # | Excluded | Note |\n|---|----------|------|\n| 1 | **Stock-in-trade**, consumable stores, raw material held for business | These are business assets |\n| 2 | **Personal effects** (movable: clothes, furniture, car, scooter, TV, fridge, instruments, gun) | Personal/household use |\n| 3 | **Rural agricultural land in India** | But urban agri-land and foreign agri-land ARE capital assets |\n| 4 | Certain **specified Bonds** (6.5% Gold Bonds 1977; 7% Gold Bonds 1980; National Defence Gold Bonds 1980; Special Bearer Bonds 1991; Gold Deposit Bonds 1999; Gold Monetization Scheme certificates 2015) | — |\n\n⚠️ **Even though they're for personal use**, these are STILL capital assets (so their sale is taxed): **jewellery, precious/semi-precious stones, archaeological collections, drawings, paintings, sculptures, any work of art.**\n\n💡 Trap: A personal car is *not* a capital asset → no capital gains tax on selling your old car. But a *painting* hanging in your home *is* a capital asset.\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 Types of Capital Asset — Short-Term vs Long-Term\n\nThe **holding period** decides whether a gain is **short-term (STCG)** or **long-term (LTCG)**. Tax is higher on STCG than LTCG, so this classification matters.\n\n⚠️ **Key date:** The Finance Act, 2024 (w.e.f. **23 July 2024**) changed holding periods and rates. These notes assume **transfer on or after 23 July 2024**.\n\n### General rule\n- **Short-term:** held for **not more than 24 months** before transfer.\n- **Long-term:** held for **more than 24 months**.\n\n### Exception 1 — 12-month assets (long-term if held > 12 months):\na) Listed shares (equity or preference); b) Listed securities (debentures, bonds, derivatives, G-secs, units); c) Units of UTI (listed or unlisted); d) Units of Equity-Oriented Fund (listed or unlisted); e) Zero Coupon Bonds (listed or unlisted); f) ULIP without 10(10D) exemption (FY 2025-26 on); g) High-premium equity-oriented ULIP (FY 2020-21 to 2024-25).\n\n### Exception 2 — \"Always short-term\" (no holding-period test):\n- **Depreciable assets** — always STCG.\n- **Market Linked Debentures (MLDs)** and **units of Specified Mutual Funds (SMFs)** acquired on/after **1 April 2023** — always STCG (Section 50AA).\n- **Unlisted bonds / unlisted debentures** transferred/redeemed/matured after 23 July 2024 — always STCG (Section 50AA).\n\n### Holding-period table (Table 8.1, transfer on/after 23 July 2024)\n| Asset | Listed | Unlisted |\n|-------|--------|----------|\n| Equity shares | > 12 months | > 24 months |\n| Units of Equity-Oriented Fund | > 12 months | > 12 months |\n| Units of REIT / InvIT | > 12 months | — |\n| Other units (not 65%+ debt) | > 24 months | > 24 months |\n| SMF type 1 (65%+ in debt/MM) | **Always short-term** | Always short-term |\n| Market linked debentures | **Always short-term** | Always short-term |\n| Preference shares | > 12 months | > 24 months |\n| Debentures | > 12 months | Always short term (unlisted) |\n| Government securities (bonds/debentures) | > 12 months | > 24 months |\n| Zero coupon bonds | > 12 months | > 12 months |\n| Other bonds | > 12 months | Always short term (unlisted) |\n| Immovable property (land & building) | > 24 months | > 24 months |\n| High-premium equity-oriented ULIP | > 12 months | > 12 months |\n| Any other asset (e.g. physical gold) | > 24 months | > 24 months |\n\n💡 Quick mental model: **Listed equity-type → 12 months; almost everything else → 24 months; MLDs/SMFs/depreciable/unlisted bonds → always short-term.**\n\n### 8.3.1 Calculating Period of Holding (special cases — Table 8.2)\nPeriod normally runs from **date of purchase → date of transfer**. Special rules:\n| Case | Holding counted from |\n|------|----------------------|\n| Listed shares via broker | Broker's note date (if followed by delivery + transfer deed) |\n| Demat securities | **FIFO** — first in is first sold |\n| **Bonus shares** | Date of **allotment of bonus shares** |\n| Sweat equity / ESOP shares | Date of allotment / exercise |\n| Preference→equity conversion | Includes period preference shares were held |\n| Bonds/debentures→shares conversion | Includes period of the original asset |\n| **Right shares** | Date of allotment of right shares |\n| Renouncement of right | From company's offer date to renouncement |\n| Amalgamated company shares | Includes holding in amalgamating company |\n| Resulting company (demerger) | From holding date of demerged-company shares |\n| Acquired by **gift/will/inheritance** (s.49(1)) | Includes **previous owner's** holding period |\n| Gold ↔ EGR | Includes period held before conversion |\n\n💡 Big exam point: for **inherited/gifted** assets and **bonus shares from a conversion chain**, the **previous owner's holding counts** — so an inherited house your father held 20 years is automatically long-term in your hands.\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 Transfer of Capital Asset — Section 2(47)\n\"Transfer\" = passing of property/rights from one person to another. Examples:\n- **Sale** — buyer acquires the seller's property for a price.\n- **Exchange** — mutual transfer of one thing for another (neither being only money), per s.118 Transfer of Property Act.\n- **Relinquishment** — owner withdraws and abandons rights (e.g. a retiring partner relinquishing his interest).\n- **Extinguishment of rights** — rights in the asset are extinguished (not mere loss of the asset itself).\n- **Maturity/redemption of zero-coupon bonds** — treated as transfer.\n\n### 8.4.1 Transactions NOT regarded as transfer (Sections 46 & 47)\nNo capital gain arises on these:\n1. Distribution of capital asset on **total/partial partition of an HUF**.\n2. Transfer under a **gift, will, or irrevocable trust** (except ESOP shares/warrants).\n3. **Transfer between two non-residents** of specified securities (bonds/GDRs/RDBs/IFSC-exchange transfers in foreign currency, certain G-secs).\n4. **Redemption of Sovereign Gold Bonds** by an individual.\n5. **Conversion of securities** — bonds/debentures→shares of that company; FCEB→shares; preference→equity.\n6. **Consolidation of mutual fund** schemes.\n7. **Lending of securities** under SEBI's Securities Lending Scheme.\n8. **Rollover of Fixed Maturity Plans (FMPs)** per SEBI rules (now largely irrelevant since 65%+ debt schemes are always short-term under s.50AA).\n9. **Distribution of assets in kind on liquidation** by a company (but the *shareholder* is taxed under s.46).\n10. **Conversion of Gold ↔ Electronic Gold Receipt (EGR)** issued by a vault manager.\n\n💡 Why this matters: if a transaction \"is not a transfer\", **no capital gains tax** arises *at that point* (the gain is deferred to a later real transfer).\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 Computation of Capital Gains\n\n### The formulas\n**Short-term capital gain:**\n```\nSTCG = Full value of consideration\n       − Expenditure on transfer\n       − Cost of acquisition\n       − Cost of improvement\n```\n**Long-term capital gain:** same formula, but cost of acquisition (and improvement) may be **indexed** *only* for land/building bought before 23 July 2024 (parallel calculation).\n\n### 1. Full Value of Consideration\nThe amount received/receivable for the transfer (cash or, if in kind, fair market value). Special cases (Table 8.3):\n| Case | Full value taken as |\n|------|---------------------|\n| Conversion of capital asset into stock-in-trade | FMV on date of conversion |\n| ESOP securities gifted / via irrevocable trust | Market value on date of transfer |\n| Unquoted shares transferred below FMV | FMV on date of transfer |\n| Consideration not ascertainable | FMV on date of transfer |\n\n### 2. Expenditure on transfer\nBrokerage, commission, stamp duty, registration fee, travelling and legal expenses **directly connected with the transfer** are deductible.\n⚠️ **STT and CTT are NOT deductible.** Investment-adviser fees: most likely **not** deductible (unless directly connected to the transfer, like brokerage/stamp duty).\n\n### 3. Cost of Acquisition (Table 8.4 — key rows)\n| Situation | Cost of acquisition |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| Shares purchased on/after 01-04-2001 | Price actually paid |\n| Shares acquired on/before 31-03-2001 | Higher of (actual cost, **FMV as on 01-04-2001**) |\n| Listed equity/eq-MF/business-trust units (STT-paid, LTCA) bought ≤ 31-01-2018, sold after 01-04-2018 (Section 112A \"grandfathering\") | Higher of: (a) actual cost; (b) lower of {FMV as on **31-01-2018**, full value of consideration} |\n| Right shares | Price actually paid |\n| Renouncement of right | Nil |\n| **Bonus shares** issued on/after 01-04-2001 | **Nil** (if issued before 01-04-2001: FMV as on 01-04-2001) |\n| Sweat equity / ESOP shares | FMV considered for perquisite valuation (s.17(2)(vi)) |\n| Acquired by gift/will/inheritance (s.49(1)) | **Cost to the previous owner** (or FMV on date previous owner acquired, if cost can't be determined) |\n| Conversion of preference→equity | Cost of the preference shares |\n| Stock-in-trade converted to capital asset | FMV of stock on date of conversion |\n\n### 4. Indexed Cost of Acquisition (indexation)\n**The problem indexation solves:** Inflation alone makes prices rise. Taxing the full nominal \"gain\" would tax inflation. So the law inflates your *cost* using the **Cost Inflation Index (CII)** (notified by CBDT each year), reducing the taxable gain.\n\n⚠️ **Big change:** For transfers **on/after 23 July 2024**, indexation is **no longer available — EXCEPT for land/building acquired before 23 July 2024** (used as a parallel calculation).\n\n**Formula:**\n```\nIndexed Cost of      =  Cost of      ×   CII of year of transfer\nAcquisition             Acquisition      ───────────────────────────────\n                                          CII of year asset first held\n                                          (or CII of 2001-02, whichever is later)\n```\n\n**Worked example (from text):** House purchased FY 2002-03 for ₹50,00,000; CII 2002-03 = 105, CII 2020-21 = 301.\nIndexed cost = 50,00,000 × 301 / 105 = **₹1,43,33,333**. Selling at ₹3.5 crore, the gain is computed against ₹1.43 crore (not the original ₹50 lakh), wiping out the \"inflation\" part.\n\n### 5. Cost of Improvement\nAll **capital-nature** additions/alterations made **on or after 01-04-2001** (by assessee or previous owner). Improvements before 01-04-2001 are ignored.\n⚠️ For goodwill / right to manufacture, and for **MLDs / SMFs**, cost of improvement = **Nil**.\n\n### 6. Indexed Cost of Improvement\nSame formula as indexed cost of acquisition (only relevant for land/building pre-23 July 2024).\n\n### 8.5.1 Foreign currency capital gains (Rule 115A)\nFor a **non-resident (except FPI)** buying Indian company shares/debentures in foreign currency: compute the gain **in the original foreign currency** first (no indexation), then convert to INR. Conversion uses **average TT buying & selling rate (SBI)** for cost/consideration/expenditure, and **TT buying rate** for the resultant gain.\n- For **other** capital gains: convert into INR at the TT buying rate on the **last day of the month preceding** the month of transfer. (e.g. transfer on 15 May 2022 → use 30 April 2022 rate.)\n\n---\n\n## 8.5.2 Tax Rates on Capital Gains (transfers on/after 23 July 2024)\n\n### Short-term capital gains\n| STCG type | Rate |\n|-----------|------|\n| **Section 111A** — equity shares, equity-oriented MF units, equity-oriented high-premium ULIPs, units of business trust, **chargeable to STT** | **20%** |\n| All other STCG | **Slab rate** of the assessee |\n\n### Long-term capital gains\n| LTCG type | Rate |\n|-----------|------|\n| All categories of assets (general) | **12.5%** (no indexation) |\n| **Section 112A** — listed equity, equity-oriented fund units, equity ULIPs (no 10(10D)), business-trust units, **STT-paid** | **12.5% on gains exceeding ₹1,25,000** (first ₹1,25,000 exempt) |\n| Immovable property (land/building) | See special rule below |\n\n### Immovable property LTCG (the parallel calculation)\n- Property **acquired AND transferred before 23 July 2024** → old rules: **indexation + 20%**.\n- Property **acquired before** but **transferred on/after 23 July 2024** → do a **parallel calculation**: compare **20% with indexation** vs **12.5% without indexation**, and pay the **lower tax**. (Any loss arising from indexation is no longer allowed.)\n- Property **acquired on/after 23 July 2024** → **12.5% without indexation**.\n\n💡 **Memory hook:** New STCG (111A) = **20%**; New LTCG (general & 112A) = **12.5%**; equity LTCG gets **₹1,25,000** free each year.\n\n---\n\n## 8.5.3 Exemptions for Capital Gains (Table 8.5)\nReinvest the gain (or consideration) in a specified new asset → exemption. **Capital Gains Account Scheme (CAGS)** lets you \"park\" the money until you reinvest.\n\n| Section | Assessee | Original asset (LTCA unless noted) | Reinvest in | Time limit | Max exemption |\n|---------|----------|-----------------------------------|-------------|------------|---------------|\n| **54** | Individual/HUF | Residential house (LTCA) | One residential house in India (two if gains ≤ ₹2 cr) | Buy: 1 yr before–2 yr after; Construct: 3 yr | Lower of ₹10 cr / amount invested + CAGS |\n| **54B** | Individual/HUF | Agricultural land used 2 yrs (ST or LT) | New agricultural land | 2 yrs | Amount invested + CAGS |\n| **54D** | Any | Land/building of industrial undertaking (compulsory acquisition) | Land/building for new industrial undertaking | 3 yrs from acquisition | Amount invested + CAGS |\n| **54EC** | Any | Land/building (LTCA) | Bonds of **NHAI / REC** | **6 months** | Lower of ₹50,00,000 / amount invested |\n| **54EE** | Any | Any (LTCA) | Units of notified fund (none notified yet) | 6 months | Lower of ₹50,00,000 / amount invested |\n| **54F** | Individual/HUF | Any LTCA **other than** residential house | One residential house in India | Buy: 1 yr before–2 yr after; Construct: 3 yr | Proportionate: Investment × LTCG / Net sale consideration (eligible investment ≤ ₹10 cr) |\n| **54G** | Any | Industrial undertaking in urban area (ST/LT) | Assets of industrial undertaking in non-urban area | 1 yr before–3 yr after | Amount invested + CAGS |\n| **54GA** | Any | Industrial undertaking in urban area (ST/LT) | Assets of industrial undertaking in SEZ | 1 yr before–3 yr after | Amount invested + CAGS |\n| **54GB** | Individual/HUF | Residential property (LTCA) | Equity shares of eligible company / eligible start-up | By return due date / 1 yr | Proportionate: Investment in new asset × CG / Net sale consideration |\n| **115F** | Non-Resident Indian | Foreign-currency Indian shares/debentures/G-secs (LTCA) | Indian shares/debentures/deposits/G-secs | 6 months | Proportionate: Investment × CG / Net sale consideration |\n\n⚠️ **Section 54H:** If the original transfer was by **compulsory acquisition** and consideration is received later, the timelines run from the **date of receipt** of consideration.\n\n**Worked example (Section 54 with the new parallel calc):** Mr. A bought a house 14 Apr 2011 for ₹1 crore; sold Dec 2024 for ₹3 crore; plans to buy a new house for ₹1.5 crore. CII 2011-12 = 184; CII 2024-25 = 363.\n\n| | Old provisions (₹) | New provisions (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sale consideration | 3,00,00,000 | 3,00,00,000 |\n| Less: Cost (new) | — | (1,00,00,000) |\n| Less: Indexed cost (old) = 1,00,00,000 × 363/184 | (1,97,28,260) | — |\n| Long-term capital gain | 1,02,71,740 | 2,00,00,000 |\n| Less: Exemption u/s 54 | (1,50,00,000) | (1,50,00,000) |\n| Gain after exemption | **Nil** | 50,00,000 |\n| Tax @ 20% (old, indexed) | Nil | — |\n| Tax @ 12.5% (new) | — | 6,25,000 |\n\nFinal tax = **lower of the two = Nil** in this example (because the indexed gain is fully sheltered by the s.54 exemption).\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Capital asset** = property of any kind; **excludes** stock-in-trade, personal effects, rural agri-land, certain gold bonds. Jewellery/art ARE capital assets.\n- **Holding period (from 23 Jul 2024):** listed equity-type **> 12 months** = LT; most else **> 24 months**; MLDs/SMFs/depreciable/unlisted bonds **always short-term**.\n- Inherited/gifted assets: **previous owner's holding period and cost** carry over. Bonus shares (post-2001) cost = **Nil**.\n- **Indexation gone** from 23 Jul 2024, except land/building bought earlier (parallel calc).\n- **STT/CTT not deductible.**\n- **Rates:** STCG 111A (STT equity) = **20%**; other STCG = slab. LTCG general & 112A = **12.5%**; equity LTCG (112A) gets **₹1,25,000** exemption.\n- Immovable property bought before but sold after 23 Jul 2024 → pay **lower of** 20%-with-indexation or 12.5%-without.\n- **Exemptions:** 54 (house→house), 54F (any→house), 54EC (₹50L NHAI/REC bonds, 6 months), 54B (agri land), etc.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---------------|---------|\n| 23 July 2024 | Effective date of Finance Act 2024 capital-gains changes |\n| 12 months | LT threshold for listed equity-type assets |\n| 24 months | LT threshold for most other assets |\n| 20% (Section 111A) | STCG on STT-paid equity-type assets |\n| 12.5% (Section 112/112A) | LTCG on all assets (no indexation) |\n| ₹1,25,000 | Annual LTCG exemption under Section 112A (equity) |\n| ₹50,00,000 | Max exemption under 54EC / 54EE |\n| ₹10 crore | Cap on 54 / 54F eligible investment |\n| 6 months | 54EC/54EE/115F reinvestment window |\n| 01-04-2001 | Base date for FMV / cost-of-improvement |\n| 31-01-2018 | Grandfathering date for Section 112A |\n| Section 2(14) | Definition of capital asset |\n| Section 2(47) | Definition of transfer |\n| Section 50AA | MLDs/SMFs/unlisted bonds = always short-term |\n| CAGS | Capital Gains Account Scheme (park money pending reinvestment) |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 8: Capital Gains — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Under which head and in which year is the gain on transfer of a capital asset taxed?\n> **A:** Under the head **\"Capital Gains\"**, in the **previous year in which the transfer takes place** (on accrual basis).\n\n**Q2.** Define \"capital asset\" under Section 2(14).\n> **A:** **Property of any kind** held by an assessee, whether or not connected with his business/profession (plus specified FPI/AIF securities and high-premium ULIPs).\n\n**Q3.** Name four things excluded from \"capital asset\".\n> **A:** (1) Stock-in-trade/raw material; (2) Personal effects (movable personal-use items like car, furniture, clothes); (3) Rural agricultural land in India; (4) Certain specified gold bonds (e.g. 6.5% Gold Bonds 1977, Special Bearer Bonds 1991).\n\n**Q4.** Are jewellery and paintings capital assets even if for personal use?\n> **A:** **Yes.** Jewellery, precious/semi-precious stones, archaeological collections, drawings, paintings, sculptures and any work of art are NOT treated as personal effects — they are capital assets and their sale is taxed.\n\n**Q5.** Is rural agricultural land in India a capital asset? What about urban or foreign agri-land?\n> **A:** Rural agri-land in India is **excluded** (not a capital asset). But agricultural land in an **urban area** in India, or in a **foreign country**, IS a capital asset.\n\n**Q6.** General holding-period rule (transfer on/after 23 July 2024) for short-term vs long-term.\n> **A:** Short-term if held **not more than 24 months**; long-term if held **more than 24 months**.\n\n**Q7.** Which assets become long-term after just 12 months?\n> **A:** Listed shares (equity/preference), listed securities, units of UTI, units of equity-oriented funds, zero coupon bonds, REIT/InvIT units, and (from FY 2025-26) ULIPs without 10(10D) exemption.\n\n**Q8.** Which assets are \"always short-term\" regardless of holding period?\n> **A:** Depreciable assets; Market Linked Debentures (MLDs); units of Specified Mutual Funds (SMFs) acquired on/after 1 Apr 2023; and unlisted bonds/debentures transferred after 23 July 2024 (Section 50AA).\n\n**Q9.** How is the holding period of demat securities determined?\n> **A:** By the **FIFO** method — the security that first entered the demat account is deemed sold first.\n\n**Q10.** From what date is the holding period of bonus shares counted, and what is their cost of acquisition?\n> **A:** Holding period from the **date of allotment of the bonus shares**. Cost of acquisition = **Nil** (if issued on/after 01-04-2001).\n\n**Q11.** For an asset acquired by gift/will/inheritance, whose holding period and cost are used?\n> **A:** The **previous owner's** holding period is included, and the **cost to the previous owner** is taken as the cost of acquisition (Section 49(1)).\n\n**Q12.** Name four examples of \"transfer\" under Section 2(47).\n> **A:** Sale, exchange, relinquishment of an asset, and extinguishment of rights (also: maturity/redemption of zero-coupon bonds).\n\n**Q13.** Name four transactions NOT regarded as transfer.\n> **A:** Distribution on total/partial partition of an HUF; transfer under gift/will/irrevocable trust; redemption of Sovereign Gold Bonds by an individual; conversion of bonds/debentures into shares of that company (also: MF consolidation, securities lending, Gold↔EGR, liquidation distribution by company).\n\n**Q14.** What is \"full value of consideration\", and how is consideration in kind valued?\n> **A:** The amount received/receivable for the transfer. If received in kind, the **fair market value** of the asset received is taken.\n\n**Q15.** Are STT and CTT deductible when computing capital gains?\n> **A:** **No.** Securities Transaction Tax and Commodities Transaction Tax are specifically not deductible. (Brokerage, stamp duty, registration, legal fees directly connected with the transfer are deductible.)\n\n**Q16.** Cost of acquisition for shares acquired on or before 31-03-2001?\n> **A:** **Higher of** the actual cost paid OR the Fair Market Value as on **01-04-2001**.\n\n**Q17.** Explain the Section 112A grandfathering cost for equity bought before 31-01-2018.\n> **A:** Cost = **higher of**: (a) actual cost; (b) **lower of** {FMV as on 31-01-2018, full value of consideration}. This protects gains accrued up to 31 Jan 2018.\n\n**Q18.** What problem does indexation solve, and what index is used?\n> **A:** It removes the effect of **inflation** so only the real gain is taxed. It uses the **Cost Inflation Index (CII)** notified by CBDT each year.\n\n**Q19.** Write the indexed cost of acquisition formula.\n> **A:** Indexed cost = Cost of acquisition × (CII of year of transfer) ÷ (CII of year asset first held, or CII of 2001-02, whichever is later).\n\n**Q20.** After 23 July 2024, when is indexation still available?\n> **A:** Only for **land or building (or both) acquired before 23 July 2024**, and only as a parallel calculation against the 12.5% method.\n\n**Q21.** What is the STCG rate under Section 111A and when does it apply?\n> **A:** **20%**, on transfer of equity shares, equity-oriented MF units, equity-oriented high-premium ULIPs, or business-trust units where the transaction is **chargeable to STT**. Other STCG is taxed at slab rates.\n\n**Q22.** What is the LTCG rate under the new provisions, generally?\n> **A:** **12.5%** on all categories of assets, without indexation.\n\n**Q23.** What is the LTCG treatment of STT-paid listed equity / equity funds (Section 112A)?\n> **A:** LTCG **exceeding ₹1,25,000** is taxed at **12.5%** (the first ₹1,25,000 of such gains each year is exempt).\n\n**Q24.** Immovable property acquired before 23 July 2024 but sold after — how is the tax computed?\n> **A:** A **parallel calculation**: compute 20% **with indexation** and 12.5% **without indexation**, and pay the **lower** tax. Any loss arising from indexation is no longer allowed.\n\n**Q25.** Section 54 — who, what original asset, what reinvestment, what time limit?\n> **A:** Individual/HUF; original = **residential house (LTCA)**; reinvest gains in **one residential house in India** (two if gains ≤ ₹2 crore); time = buy 1 year before to 2 years after, or construct within 3 years. Max exemption = lower of ₹10 crore / amount invested.\n\n**Q26.** Section 54EC — what reinvestment, what limit, what window?\n> **A:** Reinvest LTCG from land/building in **bonds of NHAI or REC** within **6 months**; exemption = lower of **₹50,00,000** / amount invested.\n\n**Q27.** Section 54F differs from 54 how?\n> **A:** 54F covers an LTCA **other than a residential house** (e.g. shares, gold). Exemption is **proportionate** = Investment × LTCG ÷ Net sale consideration, and you reinvest the **net consideration** (not just the gain) in one house.\n\n**Q28.** What is the Capital Gains Account Scheme (CAGS)?\n> **A:** A scheme to **deposit (park)** the unutilised capital gains/consideration before the deadline to claim exemptions like 54/54B/54D/54F/54G/54GA, until you actually buy/construct the new asset.\n\n**Q29.** Section 54B — original and new asset?\n> **A:** Original = **agricultural land used for agriculture for 2 years**; reinvest in **new agricultural land** within 2 years. Available to Individual/HUF; original can be short-term or long-term.\n\n**Q30.** Section 115F applies to whom?\n> **A:** A **Non-Resident Indian** who reinvests LTCG (from foreign-currency Indian shares/debentures/G-secs) in specified Indian assets within 6 months; exemption is proportionate.\n\n**Q31.** What does Section 54H provide?\n> **A:** Where the transfer (under s.54/54B/54D/54EC/54F) was by **compulsory acquisition** and consideration is received later, the reinvestment time limits run from the **date of receipt** of the consideration.\n\n**Q32.** How are \"other\" capital gains earned in foreign currency converted to INR?\n> **A:** At the **TT buying rate** on the **last day of the month immediately preceding** the month of transfer (e.g. transfer on 15 May → use the 30 April rate).\n\n**Q33.** What is the cost of improvement, and from which date is it counted?\n> **A:** All capital-nature additions/alterations made **on or after 01-04-2001** (by assessee or previous owner). Pre-01-04-2001 improvements are ignored; for MLDs/SMFs/goodwill, cost of improvement = Nil.\n\n**Q34.** In the text's Section 54 example (sold ₹3 cr, indexed cost ₹1.97 cr, new house ₹1.5 cr), what was the final tax?\n> **A:** **Nil.** Old method: LTCG ₹1,02,71,740 fully covered by the ₹1.5 cr s.54 exemption → tax Nil. The taxpayer pays the **lower** of old (Nil) and new (₹6,25,000), i.e. **Nil**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 8: Capital Gains — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam carries **25% negative marking** (you lose 0.25 mark for every wrong answer), so do not guess blindly — eliminate distractors first.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Under which head of income is any profit arising from the transfer of a capital asset taxed?\nA) Income from Other Sources  B) Profits and Gains of Business  C) Capital Gains  D) Income from House Property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The Income Tax Act says any income arising from the **transfer of a capital asset** is chargeable under the head \"Capital Gains\".\n- A) Other Sources catches residual income like interest/dividends, not asset-transfer profit.\n- B) Business profits cover stock-in-trade dealings, not capital assets.\n- D) House Property taxes rental income, not the gain on selling the house.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Capital asset is defined under which section of the Income Tax Act?\nA) Section 2(47)  B) Section 111A  C) Section 54  D) Section 2(14)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Section 2(14)** defines \"capital asset\" as property of any kind held by an assessee.\n- A) Section 2(47) defines \"transfer\", not capital asset.\n- B) Section 111A sets the short-term tax rate on STT-paid equity.\n- C) Section 54 is an exemption (house-to-house reinvestment).\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is NOT treated as a capital asset?\nA) Jewellery  B) Stock-in-trade held for business  C) A painting  D) Goodwill of a business\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Stock-in-trade**, consumable stores and raw material held for business or profession are expressly excluded from the definition of capital asset.\n- A) Jewellery is explicitly a capital asset even though personal.\n- C) Paintings are listed as capital assets (works of art), not personal effects.\n- D) Goodwill is a specific inclusion as a capital asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A personal car used by an individual and his family is best described as:\nA) A long-term capital asset  B) A short-term capital asset  C) A personal effect (not a capital asset)  D) Stock-in-trade\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A car held for personal/family use is a **personal effect**, which is excluded from the definition of capital asset, so no capital gains arise on its sale.\n- A) and B) classify it as a capital asset, which it is not.\n- D) Stock-in-trade applies to goods held for sale in business, not a personal car.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the following personal-use items is STILL treated as a capital asset (so its sale is taxable)?\nA) Wearing apparel  B) Refrigerator  C) Archaeological collections  D) Television\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Even if meant for personal use, **jewellery, precious/semi-precious stones, archaeological collections, drawings, paintings, sculptures and any work of art** are NOT personal effects — they remain capital assets.\n- A), B), D) Clothes, fridge and TV are ordinary personal effects and are excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Agricultural land of which type is NOT a capital asset?\nA) Urban agricultural land in India  B) Land within prescribed municipal limits  C) Agricultural land in a foreign country  D) Rural agricultural land in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Rural agricultural land situated in India** is excluded from the definition of capital asset.\n- A) Urban agricultural land in India IS a capital asset.\n- B) Land within prescribed limits of municipalities IS a capital asset.\n- C) Agricultural land in a foreign country IS a capital asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** As a general rule (for transfers on/after 23 July 2024), a capital asset is \"long-term\" if held for:\nA) More than 12 months  B) More than 24 months  C) More than 36 months  D) More than 48 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Generally an asset is long-term if held for **more than 24 months** immediately preceding transfer (and short-term if held for not more than 24 months).\n- A) 12 months is the exception threshold for listed equity-type assets, not the general rule.\n- C) and D) 36/48 months are not thresholds under the current law.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** For listed equity shares, the holding period to qualify as a long-term capital asset is:\nA) More than 12 months  B) More than 24 months  C) More than 36 months  D) Always short-term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed shares (equity or preference) are long-term if held for **more than 12 months** (Exception 1).\n- B) 24 months applies to unlisted equity shares, not listed.\n- C) 36 months is not a current threshold.\n- D) Listed equity is not always short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of the following is ALWAYS treated as a short-term capital asset irrespective of holding period?\nA) Listed equity shares  B) Physical gold  C) Immovable property  D) Depreciable asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **depreciable asset** is always treated as a short-term capital asset regardless of how long it is held.\n- A) Listed equity is long-term after 12 months.\n- B) Physical gold is long-term after 24 months.\n- C) Immovable property is long-term after 24 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Gains from Market Linked Debentures (MLDs) acquired on or after 1 April 2023 are taxed as:\nA) Long-term capital gains  B) Short-term capital gains  C) Income from other sources  D) Exempt income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 50AA, gains on MLDs (and units of Specified Mutual Funds acquired on/after 1 April 2023) are **always short-term capital gains**, irrespective of holding period.\n- A) They never get long-term treatment.\n- C) and D) They are still taxed as capital gains, not other sources, and are not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Transfer of a capital asset is defined under:\nA) Section 2(14)  B) Section 47  C) Section 2(47)  D) Section 50AA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 2(47)** defines \"transfer\".\n- A) Section 2(14) defines capital asset.\n- B) Section 47 lists transactions NOT regarded as transfer.\n- D) Section 50AA deals with MLDs/SMFs being short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which of the following is an example of \"transfer\" of a capital asset?\nA) Sale of the asset  B) Distribution of HUF assets on partition  C) Redemption of Sovereign Gold Bonds by an individual  D) Lending securities under SEBI's scheme\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Sale** is a classic transfer (buyer acquires the seller's property for a price). Relinquishment, exchange and extinguishment are also transfers.\n- B), C), D) These are specifically listed as transactions NOT regarded as transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which of the following is NOT regarded as a transfer for capital gains?\nA) Exchange of one asset for another  B) Sale of land  C) Relinquishment of rights  D) Conversion of preference shares into equity shares of that company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Conversion of preference shares into equity shares** of that company is not regarded as a transfer.\n- A), B), C) Exchange, sale and relinquishment are all transfers.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The redemption of which instrument by an individual is NOT regarded as a transfer?\nA) Zero coupon bonds  B) Market Linked Debentures  C) Listed debentures  D) Sovereign Gold Bonds issued by the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Redemption of **Sovereign Gold Bonds** issued by the RBI, by an individual, is not regarded as transfer.\n- A) Redemption/maturity of zero-coupon bonds IS a transfer.\n- B), C) Redemption of these instruments is a taxable event.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** While computing capital gains, which of the following is NOT deductible from the full value of consideration?\nA) Brokerage on transfer  B) Securities Transaction Tax (STT)  C) Cost of acquisition  D) Cost of improvement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **STT (and CTT)** is specifically NOT allowed as a deduction while computing capital gains from sale of securities.\n- A) Brokerage incurred in connection with transfer is deductible.\n- C), D) Cost of acquisition and cost of improvement are deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The Cost Inflation Index (CII) is notified each year by:\nA) SEBI  B) RBI  C) CBDT  D) State Bank of India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **CBDT (Central Board of Direct Taxes)** notifies the CII every year.\n- A) SEBI regulates securities markets.\n- B) RBI is the central bank.\n- D) SBI's TT rates are used for foreign-currency conversion, not the CII.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** For transfers on or after 23 July 2024, indexation benefit is available only for:\nA) All long-term capital assets  B) Listed equity shares  C) Land or building acquired before 23 July 2024  D) Gold and jewellery\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indexation is no longer available except for **land or building (or both) acquired before 23 July 2024**, where it is used as a parallel calculation.\n- A) Indexation has been withdrawn for assets generally.\n- B), D) Equity and gold do not get indexation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The cost of acquisition of bonus shares issued on or after 01-04-2001 is taken as:\nA) Market value on issue date  B) Nil  C) Face value  D) FMV as on 01-04-2001\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For bonus shares issued **on or after 01-04-2001**, cost of acquisition is **Nil** (you paid nothing for them).\n- A), C) Market or face value is not used.\n- D) FMV as on 01-04-2001 applies only to bonus shares issued before 01-04-2001.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** The cost of acquisition of an asset acquired by way of gift, will or inheritance under Section 49(1) is:\nA) Nil  B) FMV on date of transfer  C) Cost to the previous owner  D) Zero in all cases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It is the **cost to the previous owner** (and if that cannot be determined, the FMV on the date the previous owner acquired the asset).\n- A), D) Cost is not nil.\n- B) FMV on date of transfer is the sale-side value, not the cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Securities held in demat form are deemed sold on which basis when computing period of holding?\nA) LIFO  B) FIFO  C) Weighted average  D) Specific identification\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Period of holding for demat securities is determined on a **First-In-First-Out (FIFO)** basis — the security that first entered the demat account is deemed sold first.\n- A), C), D) These methods are not used for demat holding-period determination.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The period of holding of bonus shares is reckoned from:\nA) Date of purchase of original shares  B) 01-04-2001  C) Date of board approval  D) Date of allotment of bonus shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Period of holding of bonus shares runs from the **date of allotment of the bonus shares**.\n- A) Original shares' purchase date is not used for bonus shares.\n- B), C) These are not relevant dates.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Short-term capital gains under Section 111A are taxed at:\nA) 10%  B) 12.5%  C) 15%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG covered by **Section 111A** (STT-paid equity shares, equity-oriented MF units, equity-oriented high-premium ULIPs, business trust units) are taxed at **20%**.\n- A), C) 10%/15% were old rates, not the current one.\n- B) 12.5% is the long-term rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Long-term capital gains (general, on/after 23 July 2024) are taxed at:\nA) 10%  B) 12.5%  C) 20%  D) Slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG on all categories of assets is taxed at **12.5%** (without indexation).\n- A) 10% was the old equity LTCG rate.\n- C) 20% is the old indexed rate / current STCG 111A rate.\n- D) Slab rates apply to non-111A short-term gains, not general LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under Section 112A, the annual exemption available on long-term capital gains from STT-paid equity is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — LTCG on listed equity/equity-oriented funds/business-trust units (STT-paid) exceeding **₹1,25,000** is taxed at 12.5%; the first ₹1,25,000 is effectively exempt.\n- A) ₹1,00,000 was the earlier exemption limit.\n- B), C) These are not the 112A figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which exemption allows reinvestment of long-term capital gains in bonds of NHAI or REC?\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54F  C) Section 54EC  D) Section 54B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 54EC** allows exemption when LTCG from land/building is invested in **bonds of NHAI or REC**.\n- A) Section 54 is house-to-house.\n- B) Section 54F is any LTCA to a residential house.\n- D) Section 54B is agricultural land to agricultural land.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The maximum exemption available under Section 54EC is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 54EC exemption is the lower of the amount invested or **₹50,00,000**.\n- A), B), D) These are not the 54EC cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The time limit to invest in Section 54EC bonds from the date of transfer is:\nA) 3 months  B) 6 months  C) 1 year  D) 2 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investment in NHAI/REC bonds must be made within **6 months** from the date of transfer.\n- A) 3 months is not the limit.\n- C), D) 1/2 years apply to other exemptions (e.g. Section 54 purchase window).\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Section 54 (exemption on sale of a residential house) is available to:\nA) Any assessee  B) Only companies  C) Individual and HUF  D) Only non-residents\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 54 is available only to an **Individual and HUF**.\n- A) \"Any assessee\" describes 54EC/54D/54G, not 54.\n- B), D) Companies and only-non-residents are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Section 54F allows exemption when the net consideration from any long-term capital asset (other than a residential house) is reinvested in:\nA) NHAI/REC bonds  B) Agricultural land  C) One residential house in India  D) Units of a notified fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 54F requires reinvestment of the **net consideration** in **one residential house in India**.\n- A) That is Section 54EC.\n- B) That is Section 54B.\n- D) That is Section 54EE.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Under Section 54, if capital gains do not exceed ₹2 crore, the assessee may invest in:\nA) Up to 5 houses  B) Any number of houses  C) Only commercial property  D) Two residential houses instead of one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — If capital gains do not exceed ₹2 crore, the new capital asset can be **two houses** instead of one (a once-in-a-lifetime option).\n- A), B) There is no 5-house or unlimited option.\n- C) Section 54 requires residential houses, not commercial property.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The base date used for taking Fair Market Value as cost of acquisition (for assets acquired earlier) is:\nA) 01-04-1981  B) 01-04-2001  C) 31-01-2018  D) 23-07-2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For assets acquired on or before 31-03-2001, the cost can be taken as actual cost or **FMV as on 01-04-2001**, whichever is higher; cost of improvement before 01-04-2001 is ignored.\n- A) 01-04-1981 was the old base date.\n- C) 31-01-2018 is the Section 112A grandfathering date.\n- D) 23-07-2024 is the Finance Act 2024 effective date.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** For Section 112A \"grandfathering\", the relevant Fair Market Value date for listed equity bought on or before 31-01-2018 is:\nA) 01-04-2001  B) 31-03-2018  C) 31-01-2018  D) 23-07-2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The grandfathering uses FMV as on **31-01-2018** (cost = higher of actual cost, or the lower of FMV on 31-01-2018 and full value of consideration).\n- A) 01-04-2001 is the general FMV base date.\n- B), D) Not relevant dates here.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which section makes unlisted bonds/debentures transferred after 23 July 2024 always short-term?\nA) Section 54  B) Section 50AA  C) Section 112A  D) Section 2(14)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 50AA** treats unlisted bonds/debentures (and MLDs/SMFs) gains as short-term irrespective of holding period.\n- A) Section 54 is an exemption.\n- C) Section 112A is the equity LTCG provision.\n- D) Section 2(14) defines capital asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** When sale consideration is received in kind, the full value of consideration is taken as:\nA) The book value of the asset given  B) Nil  C) The cost of acquisition  D) Fair market value of the asset received\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — If consideration is received in kind, the **fair market value** of such assets is taken as the full value of consideration.\n- A) Book value is not used.\n- B), C) Neither nil nor cost of acquisition applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The \"Capital Gains Account Scheme (CAGS)\" allows a taxpayer to:\nA) Avoid tax permanently on all gains  B) Park the unutilised amount pending reinvestment to keep the exemption  C) Earn tax-free interest forever  D) Convert STCG into LTCG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGS lets the assessee **deposit/park the unutilised amount** until it is reinvested in the specified new asset, so the exemption (e.g. under Section 54/54B/54D/54F) is preserved.\n- A), C) It does not give permanent or unlimited tax-free benefits.\n- D) It cannot change the nature of the gain.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. Rao sells listed equity shares (STT-paid) after holding them for 10 months at a profit. The gain is:\nA) Long-term, taxed at 12.5%  B) Short-term under Section 111A, taxed at 20%  C) Short-term, taxed at slab rate  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed equity is long-term only if held > 12 months. Held 10 months = **short-term**; being STT-paid equity it falls under **Section 111A at 20%**.\n- A) Not long-term — held under 12 months.\n- C) Slab rate applies to non-111A short-term gains, not STT-paid equity.\n- D) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Ms. Iyer sells unlisted equity shares after holding them for 20 months. The gain is:\nA) Long-term  B) Short-term  C) Exempt  D) Long-term at 12.5% with indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted equity shares need to be held **more than 24 months** to be long-term. 20 months ≤ 24 months = **short-term**, taxed at slab rate.\n- A), D) Not long-term — under 24 months; also no indexation for equity.\n- C) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An investor sells physical gold (jewellery) held for 30 months. This gain is:\nA) Short-term  B) Long-term at 12.5% (no indexation)  C) Long-term at 20% with indexation  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — \"Any other asset\" such as physical gold is long-term if held **> 24 months**. 30 months = long-term, taxed at **12.5% without indexation** (indexation only survives for land/building bought before 23 July 2024).\n- A) Held over 24 months, so not short-term.\n- C) Indexation is gone for gold.\n- D) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A person inherits a house from his father (who had held it for 18 years) and sells it 3 months after inheritance. The gain is:\nA) Short-term, because he held it only 3 months  B) Long-term, because the previous owner's holding period is included  C) Exempt because it is inherited  D) Short-term under Section 111A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For assets acquired under Section 49(1) (inheritance), the **previous owner's holding period is included**. The father's 18 years make it clearly long-term.\n- A) The 3-month personal holding is not used alone.\n- C) Inheritance is not a transfer, but the later sale IS taxable.\n- D) Section 111A is only for STT-paid equity, not a house.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. Khan converts his preference shares into equity shares of the same company. For computing the holding period of the equity shares:\nA) Period starts fresh from conversion date  B) It is always short-term  C) It is always long-term  D) Period of preference shares is included\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The period for which the **preference shares were held is included** in the holding period of the equity shares (and the conversion itself is not a transfer).\n- A) Period does not restart.\n- B), C) Classification still depends on the total holding period.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An assessee sells land/building (long-term) and wants the simplest one-instrument exemption with a 6-month window. He should use:\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54EC  C) Section 54F  D) Section 54B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 54EC**: invest LTCG from land/building in NHAI/REC bonds within **6 months** (max ₹50 lakh).\n- A) Section 54 needs the original asset to be a residential house.\n- C) Section 54F needs reinvestment of net consideration in a house.\n- D) Section 54B is for agricultural land.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An individual sells a long-term holding of listed shares (not a house) and reinvests the net consideration in one residential house. The relevant exemption is:\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54EC  C) Section 54F  D) Section 54GB\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 54F** applies when a long-term asset **other than a residential house** is sold and the **net consideration** is invested in one residential house in India.\n- A) Section 54 requires the original asset to be a residential house.\n- B) Section 54EC is for bonds.\n- D) Section 54GB is residential property → equity shares of an eligible company/start-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which exemption is available to \"any assessee\" (not just individuals/HUF)?\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54F  C) Section 54EC  D) Section 54GB\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 54EC** (and 54D, 54G, 54GA, 54EE) is available to **any assessee**.\n- A), B), D) Sections 54, 54F and 54GB are restricted to Individuals and HUF.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A company's land/building forming part of an industrial undertaking is compulsorily acquired. To claim exemption by reinvesting in land/building for a new industrial undertaking, it uses:\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54D  C) Section 54F  D) Section 115F\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 54D** covers land/building of an industrial undertaking acquired by **compulsory acquisition**, reinvested in land/building for a new industrial undertaking (within 3 years).\n- A), C) Sections 54/54F deal with residential houses.\n- D) Section 115F is for NRIs investing in Indian securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A Non-Resident Indian sells (in foreign currency) shares of an Indian company held long-term and reinvests in Indian shares/debentures within 6 months. The exemption is under:\nA) Section 54F  B) Section 54EC  C) Section 115F  D) Section 54EE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 115F** is specifically for a **Non-Resident Indian** reinvesting foreign-currency-acquired Indian shares/debentures/G-secs within 6 months.\n- A), B), D) These do not target NRIs and foreign-currency Indian securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which pair correctly matches a reinvestment time-limit?\nA) Section 54EC — 2 years  B) Section 54 (purchase) — 1 year before and 2 years after  C) Section 54B — 6 months  D) Section 115F — 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **Section 54**, to BUY a house the window is **1 year before and 2 years after** the transfer (and 3 years to construct).\n- A) Section 54EC is 6 months, not 2 years.\n- C) Section 54B is 2 years, not 6 months.\n- D) Section 115F is 6 months, not 3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Units of a Specified Mutual Fund (a scheme investing 65%+ in debt) acquired in May 2023 and sold after 40 months produce a gain that is:\nA) Long-term at 12.5%  B) Long-term at 20% with indexation  C) Exempt  D) Short-term (always), taxed at slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Specified Mutual Funds (65%+ debt) acquired on/after 1 April 2023 are **always short-term** under Section 50AA, regardless of the 40-month holding; taxed at slab rate.\n- A), B) Never long-term.\n- C) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Mr. Bose gifts a capital asset to his son. At the moment of the gift:\nA) Capital gains tax arises for Mr. Bose  B) No transfer arises, so no capital gains tax at that point  C) The son pays capital gains tax  D) STCG arises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A transfer under a **gift, will or irrevocable trust** is not regarded as a transfer, so **no capital gains tax** arises at the gift stage (tax is deferred to the son's later sale, using the previous owner's cost/holding).\n- A), C), D) No tax event occurs at the gift itself (except special ESOP-share cases).\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** When a capital asset is converted into stock-in-trade, the full value of consideration for capital gains purposes is taken as:\nA) Selling price of the stock later  B) Fair market value on the date of conversion  C) Original cost  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On conversion of a capital asset into stock-in-trade, the **FMV on the date of conversion** is taken as the full value of consideration.\n- A) The later selling price is business income, not the capital-gains consideration.\n- C), D) Cost or nil is not used.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Mr. Sen sells a listed equity share that was a bonus share allotted to him in 2022. Its cost of acquisition is:\nA) Market price on allotment  B) Nil  C) FMV as on 31-01-2018  D) Original share's price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus shares issued **on/after 01-04-2001** have cost of acquisition = **Nil**.\n- A), D) Market price or original-share price is not used.\n- C) The 31-01-2018 grandfathering applies only to shares acquired on/before that date — bonus 2022 shares were not held then.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** For LTCG on STT-paid listed equity, only the gains exceeding which amount are taxed at 12.5%?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,25,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 112A, LTCG **exceeding ₹1,25,000** is taxed at 12.5%; the first ₹1,25,000 is free.\n- A) ₹1,00,000 was the old limit.\n- C), D) Not the prescribed figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An immovable property acquired before 23 July 2024 is transferred after that date. The tax treatment is:\nA) Always 20% with indexation  B) Always 12.5% without indexation  C) Lower of (20% with indexation) and (12.5% without indexation)  D) Slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A parallel calculation is done and the assessee pays the **lower of** 20%-with-indexation and 12.5%-without-indexation (any loss arising from indexation is disallowed).\n- A), B) Neither method applies on its own.\n- D) Slab rate is for certain STCG, not this LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An immovable property acquired AFTER 23 July 2024 and held long-term is taxed at:\nA) 20% with indexation  B) 12.5% without indexation  C) Lower of both methods  D) Slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Property acquired on/after 23 July 2024 gets **12.5% without indexation** (no parallel calculation, since indexation applies only to property bought before that date).\n- A) No indexation available.\n- C) Parallel calc only for pre-23 July 2024 property.\n- D) Not slab rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of the following gains is taxed at the assessee's applicable slab rate?\nA) STCG on STT-paid listed equity  B) LTCG on listed equity (over ₹1.25 lakh)  C) STCG on physical gold held 6 months  D) LTCG on land held 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG that is NOT covered by Section 111A (e.g. **short-term gain on physical gold**) is taxed at **slab rate**.\n- A) STT equity STCG = 20% under 111A.\n- B) Equity LTCG = 12.5% (112A).\n- D) Land LTCG = 12.5% / parallel calc, not slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A non-resident transfers Rupee Denominated Bonds of an Indian company to another non-resident outside India. This is:\nA) Taxable as STCG  B) Taxable as LTCG  C) Not regarded as transfer (no capital gains)  D) Taxed at slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Transfer of Rupee Denominated Bonds by one non-resident to another non-resident outside India is **not regarded as a transfer**, so no capital gains arise.\n- A), B), D) No taxable capital gain event arises.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Investment-adviser fees paid by a client are generally:\nA) Always deductible from capital gains  B) Deductible only for LTCG  C) Deductible like STT  D) Most likely NOT deductible unless directly connected to the transfer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Adviser fees are **most likely not deductible** unless directly related to the specific asset and directly connected with the transfer (like stamp duty/brokerage).\n- A), B) Not generally deductible.\n- C) STT itself is not deductible, so this is a poor comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Right shares' period of holding is counted from:\nA) Date of the original shares' purchase  B) Date of the company's offer  C) Date of allotment of the right shares  D) 01-04-2001\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Period of holding of right shares runs from the **date of allotment of the right shares**.\n- A) Original shares are separate.\n- B) The offer date is used for *renouncement of the right*, not for allotted right shares.\n- D) Not relevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The cost of acquisition on renouncement of a right to subscribe to shares is:\nA) Price paid for original shares  B) Nil  C) Market value of right  D) FMV on 01-04-2001\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On **renouncement of the right**, the cost of acquisition is **Nil** (the right was received without separate cost).\n- A), C), D) These values are not used for renouncement.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A depreciable asset held for 5 years is sold at a profit. The gain is:\nA) Long-term at 12.5%  B) Long-term at 20% with indexation  C) Short-term (always)  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **depreciable asset is always short-term**, irrespective of the 5-year holding.\n- A), B) Never long-term.\n- D) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An assessee sells agricultural land (used for agriculture for 2 years) and buys new agricultural land within 2 years. The exemption is:\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54B  C) Section 54D  D) Section 54F\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 54B** covers agricultural land used for agriculture for 2 years, reinvested in new agricultural land within 2 years (available to Individual/HUF).\n- A) Section 54 is house-to-house.\n- C) Section 54D is industrial land under compulsory acquisition.\n- D) Section 54F is any LTCA → house.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which exemption uses the proportionate formula: Investment × LTCG / Net sale consideration?\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54EC  C) Section 54F  D) Section 54B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 54F** gives a **proportionate** exemption (Eligible investment × LTCG / Net sale consideration) because it requires reinvesting the entire net consideration, not just the gain.\n- A) Section 54 exempts up to the amount invested (lower of ₹10 cr).\n- B) Section 54EC is a flat lower-of-(invested, ₹50 lakh).\n- D) Section 54B is amount invested + CAGS.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** An individual sells a residential property (LTCA) and reinvests the net consideration in equity shares of an eligible start-up. The exemption is:\nA) Section 54  B) Section 54GB  C) Section 54G  D) Section 115F\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 54GB** allows Individual/HUF to reinvest net consideration from residential property into **equity shares of an eligible company / eligible start-up**.\n- A) Section 54 is house→house.\n- C) Section 54G is industrial shifting from urban to non-urban.\n- D) Section 115F is for NRIs.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Distribution of assets in kind by a company to its shareholders at liquidation is:\nA) A transfer taxable in the company's hands  B) Always exempt for everyone  C) Taxed only as business income  D) Not a transfer by the company, but shareholders are taxed under Section 46\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Distribution in kind on liquidation is **not a transfer by the company**, but the **shareholders are liable** for capital gains under Section 46.\n- A) The company is not taxed on it as a transfer.\n- B) Shareholders are still taxed.\n- C) It is capital gains for the shareholder, not business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Conversion of Gold into an Electronic Gold Receipt (EGR) is:\nA) A taxable transfer  B) Not a transfer; holding period of gold carries into the EGR  C) Always short-term  D) Exempt because gold is a personal effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Conversion of gold into EGR (or vice versa) is **not a transfer**, and the period the gold was held is included for the EGR's holding period (cost also carries over).\n- A) No tax event at conversion.\n- C) Holding period still determines classification.\n- D) Gold/jewellery is a capital asset, not a personal effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Securities lending under SEBI's Securities Lending Scheme:\nA) Is treated as a transfer and taxed  B) Is not treated as a transfer  C) Triggers STCG always  D) Triggers LTCG always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lending of scrips/securities under SEBI's scheme is **not treated as a transfer** even if the same distinctive certificates are not returned.\n- A), C), D) No capital gains arise on the lending.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** For computing capital gains on shares acquired on or before 31-03-2001, cost of acquisition is:\nA) Always the actual price paid  B) Always FMV on 01-04-2001  C) Higher of actual cost and FMV as on 01-04-2001  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The assessee may take the **higher of actual cost or FMV as on 01-04-2001**.\n- A), B) Neither is forced; the assessee picks the higher.\n- D) Cost is not nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Mr. Lal, a resident, sells a plot of land located in Dubai on 15 May 2026. The capital gains in foreign currency are converted to INR at the TT buying rate as on:\nA) 15 May 2026  B) 1 May 2026  C) 31 March 2026  D) 30 April 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For \"other\" capital gains, conversion uses the TT buying rate on the **last day of the month immediately preceding** the month of transfer — i.e. **30 April 2026** for a May 2026 transfer.\n- A), B) Not the prescribed date.\n- C) End of previous financial year is irrelevant here.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The cost of acquisition of shares acquired on conversion of bonds/debentures into shares of that company is:\nA) Nil  B) FMV on conversion date  C) Price paid for the original bonds/debentures  D) Face value of shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cost of the converted shares is taken at the **price paid for the original bonds/debentures**.\n- A), D) Neither nil nor face value.\n- B) FMV on conversion is not used (conversion is not a transfer).\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following statements about Section 54EE is correct as per the workbook?\nA) It exempts up to ₹50,00,000 invested in units of a notified fund, but no fund has been notified yet  B) It exempts up to ₹2 crore  C) It is only for NRIs  D) Its time limit is 2 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 54EE allows exemption (lower of ₹50,00,000 / amount invested) in units of a **notified fund**, but **no notification has been issued yet**; window is 6 months.\n- B) Cap is ₹50 lakh, not ₹2 crore.\n- C) Available to any assessee.\n- D) Time limit is 6 months, not 2 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Under Section 54H, if the original asset is compulsorily acquired and consideration is received later, the reinvestment timelines run from:\nA) Date of transfer  B) Date of receipt of the consideration  C) End of the financial year  D) 23 July 2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 54H shifts the timelines (for Sections 54, 54B, 54D, 54EC, 54F) to run from the **date of receipt of the consideration** in compulsory-acquisition cases.\n- A) The transfer date is overridden here.\n- C), D) Not relevant dates.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. A sells listed equity shares (STT-paid) held for 8 months. Sale value ₹6,00,000; cost ₹4,00,000; brokerage ₹10,000. Compute his tax.\nA) ₹38,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹47,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Held 8 months (< 12) = STCG under Section 111A.\n**Formula:** STCG = Full value − Expenditure on transfer − Cost of acquisition.\n= 6,00,000 − 10,000 − 4,00,000 = **₹1,90,000**. Tax @ 20% = 1,90,000 × 0.20 = **₹38,000**.\n- B) ₹40,000 forgets to deduct the ₹10,000 brokerage (2,00,000 × 20%).\n- C) ₹25,000 wrongly uses 12.5% (that's the long-term rate).\n- D) ₹47,500 uses 25% — not a capital-gains rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Same shares as Q71 but held for 15 months; LTCG before exemption is ₹1,90,000 and these are his only equity LTCG for the year. Tax?\nA) ₹23,750  B) ₹8,125  C) ₹19,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held > 12 months = LTCG under Section 112A.\n**Formula:** Taxable LTCG = LTCG − ₹1,25,000 exemption; Tax = Taxable × 12.5%.\n= 1,90,000 − 1,25,000 = 65,000 taxable. Tax = 65,000 × 0.125 = **₹8,125**.\n- A) ₹23,750 taxes the full 1,90,000 at 12.5% (ignores the ₹1.25 lakh exemption).\n- C) ₹19,000 uses the 10% old rate on 1,90,000.\n- D) Not nil — gain exceeds ₹1,25,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** An equity investor has STT-paid LTCG of exactly ₹1,25,000 for the year. The tax payable is:\nA) ₹15,625  B) ₹12,500  C) Nil  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the LTCG **exceeding ₹1,25,000** is taxed. Here gain = ₹1,25,000, so taxable = 0 → **Nil tax**.\n- A) ₹15,625 = 1,25,000 × 12.5% wrongly taxes the exempt slice.\n- B), D) Apply wrong rates to the exempt amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Land bought in FY 2005-06 for ₹20,00,000 is sold in FY 2024-25. CII 2005-06 = 117; CII 2024-25 = 363. The indexed cost of acquisition is approximately:\nA) ₹62,05,128  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹72,60,000  D) ₹20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Formula:** Indexed cost = Cost × (CII of transfer year ÷ CII of acquisition year).\n= 20,00,000 × (363 ÷ 117) = 20,00,000 × 3.1026 = **₹62,05,128**.\n- B) ₹40,00,000 just doubles the cost arbitrarily.\n- C) ₹72,60,000 multiplies cost × (363/100).\n- D) ₹20,00,000 ignores indexation entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Continuing Q74: the land is sold for ₹90,00,000. Under the OLD method (20% with indexation), the tax is approximately:\nA) ₹5,58,974  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹8,75,000  D) ₹11,17,949\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG (old) = Sale − Indexed cost = 90,00,000 − 62,05,128 = ₹27,94,872. Tax @ 20% = 27,94,872 × 0.20 = **₹5,58,974**.\n- B) ₹14,00,000 ignores indexation (70,00,000 × 20%).\n- C) ₹8,75,000 = 70,00,000 × 12.5%.\n- D) ₹11,17,949 doubles the correct figure (uses 40% by error).\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Continuing Q74/Q75: under the NEW method (12.5% without indexation), the tax is:\nA) ₹5,58,974  B) ₹8,75,000  C) ₹14,00,000  D) ₹3,49,359\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG (new) = 90,00,000 − 20,00,000 = ₹70,00,000. Tax @ 12.5% = 70,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹8,75,000**.\n- A) ₹5,58,974 is the old-method (indexed) tax.\n- C) ₹14,00,000 applies 20% to the un-indexed gain.\n- D) ₹3,49,359 applies 12.5% to the indexed gain (mixing the methods wrongly).\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Based on Q75 and Q76, since the property was bought before but sold after 23 July 2024, the final tax payable is:\nA) ₹8,75,000  B) ₹5,58,974  C) Average of both  D) Sum of both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The taxpayer pays the **lower** of the two methods: lower of ₹5,58,974 (old) and ₹8,75,000 (new) = **₹5,58,974**.\n- A) That is the higher figure.\n- C), D) The rule is lower-of, not average or sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Ms. P sells a long-term plot for ₹80,00,000 (indexed cost ₹50,00,000) and invests ₹30,00,000 in NHAI bonds within 6 months. Exemption under Section 54EC and remaining taxable LTCG?\nA) Exemption ₹30,00,000; taxable ₹Nil  B) Exemption ₹30,00,000; taxable Nil but capped at ₹50 lakh  C) Exemption ₹30,00,000; taxable LTCG Nil  D) Exemption ₹30,00,000; taxable LTCG = ₹Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG = 80,00,000 − 50,00,000 = ₹30,00,000. Section 54EC exemption = lower of (amount invested ₹30,00,000, cap ₹50,00,000) = ₹30,00,000. The whole gain is sheltered → **taxable LTCG = Nil**.\n- A), B), D) describe the same arithmetic but are worded misleadingly; the clean correct statement is C — full ₹30 lakh exempt, gain Nil, well within the ₹50 lakh cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. Q has LTCG of ₹70,00,000 on a plot and invests ₹60,00,000 in NHAI/REC bonds. Exemption under 54EC is:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹70,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exemption = lower of amount invested (₹60,00,000) and the ₹50,00,000 cap = **₹50,00,000**. (Taxable LTCG = 70,00,000 − 50,00,000 = ₹20,00,000.)\n- A) Ignores the ₹50 lakh cap.\n- C) Exempts the whole gain wrongly.\n- D) Is the taxable balance minus errors.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Mr. R sells a long-term asset (not a house) for net consideration ₹1,00,00,000; LTCG is ₹40,00,000. He invests ₹50,00,000 in a new house. Section 54F exemption?\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Formula (54F, proportionate):** Exemption = LTCG × (Amount invested ÷ Net consideration).\n= 40,00,000 × (50,00,000 ÷ 1,00,00,000) = 40,00,000 × 0.5 = **₹20,00,000**.\n- A) Assumes full gain exempt (only if entire net consideration invested).\n- C) Confuses investment amount with exemption.\n- D) Uses a wrong ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Continuing Q80: the LTCG remaining taxable after the 54F exemption (asset is a non-equity LTCA) is taxed at 12.5%. Tax payable?\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Taxable LTCG = 40,00,000 − 20,00,000 = ₹20,00,000. Tax @ 12.5% = 20,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹2,50,000**.\n- B) ₹5,00,000 taxes the full ₹40 lakh at 12.5% (ignores exemption).\n- C) ₹2,00,000 uses 10%.\n- D) ₹4,00,000 uses 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor's only capital transaction is STT-paid equity LTCG of ₹3,25,000. Tax payable?\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹65,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Taxable = 3,25,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹2,00,000. Tax @ 12.5% = 2,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹25,000**.\n- A) ₹40,625 taxes full 3,25,000 at 12.5%.\n- B) ₹37,500 uses 3,00,000 base.\n- C) ₹65,000 uses 20% on taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Mr. S has STT-paid equity STCG of ₹2,50,000 (Section 111A). Tax payable?\nA) ₹31,250  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹37,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 111A STCG is taxed at 20% with no ₹1.25 lakh exemption (that exemption is only for LTCG). Tax = 2,50,000 × 0.20 = **₹50,000**.\n- A) ₹31,250 uses 12.5%.\n- C) ₹25,000 wrongly applies the 1.25 lakh exemption then 20%.\n- D) ₹37,500 uses 15%.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Mr. T sells gold (held 40 months) for ₹15,00,000; cost ₹6,00,000; selling expense ₹50,000. Compute the tax (LTCG, no indexation).\nA) ₹1,06,250  B) ₹1,12,500  C) ₹1,70,000  D) ₹85,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gold held > 24 months = LTCG, taxed at 12.5% without indexation (no ₹1.25 lakh exemption — that's only equity).\nLTCG = 15,00,000 − 50,000 − 6,00,000 = ₹8,50,000. Tax = 8,50,000 × 0.125 = **₹1,06,250**.\n- B) ₹1,12,500 forgets to deduct the ₹50,000 expense.\n- C) ₹1,70,000 uses 20%.\n- D) ₹85,000 uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Mr. U bought listed equity on 10-Jan-2017 for ₹2,00,000; FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹3,50,000; he sells (STT-paid) in 2025 for ₹6,00,000. Cost of acquisition under Section 112A grandfathering?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,50,000  C) ₹6,00,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Formula:** Cost = higher of [actual cost; lower of (FMV on 31-01-2018, sale value)].\nLower of (3,50,000 FMV, 6,00,000 sale) = 3,50,000. Higher of (2,00,000 actual, 3,50,000) = **₹3,50,000**.\n- A) Actual cost is lower, so not used.\n- C) Sale value is not the cost.\n- D) Not a derived figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: LTCG and tax (assume this is his only equity LTCG)?\nA) LTCG ₹2,50,000; tax ₹15,625  B) LTCG ₹4,00,000; tax ₹34,375  C) LTCG ₹2,50,000; tax ₹31,250  D) LTCG ₹2,50,000; tax Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = 6,00,000 − 3,50,000 = ₹2,50,000. Taxable = 2,50,000 − 1,25,000 = 1,25,000. Tax = 1,25,000 × 0.125 = **₹15,625**.\n- B) Uses ₹2,00,000 cost (ignores grandfathering) and wrong rate.\n- C) Forgets the ₹1.25 lakh exemption.\n- D) Gain exceeds ₹1.25 lakh, so not nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Mr. V (resident) sells a debenture (listed) held 14 months for a gain of ₹3,00,000. The tax treatment is:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% (no exemption) = ₹37,500  B) LTCG at 12.5% over ₹1.25 lakh  C) STCG at slab  D) Section 111A 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed debentures held > 12 months are long-term, taxed at **12.5%** with **no ₹1.25 lakh exemption** (that exemption is only for STT-paid equity under 112A). Tax = 3,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹37,500**.\n- B) The ₹1.25 lakh exemption does not apply to debentures.\n- C) Held > 12 months, not short-term.\n- D) Section 111A is only for STT-paid equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Mrs. W sells a residential house (LTCA), LTCG ₹90,00,000, and buys one new house for ₹70,00,000 within 2 years. Section 54 exemption and balance?\nA) Exemption ₹70,00,000; taxable ₹20,00,000  B) Exemption ₹90,00,000; taxable Nil  C) Exemption ₹20,00,000; taxable ₹70,00,000  D) Exemption ₹50,00,000; taxable ₹40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 54 exempts the amount of capital gains invested in the new house (lower of gain or amount invested, capped at ₹10 cr). Invested ₹70,00,000 < gain ₹90,00,000 → exemption ₹70,00,000; taxable = ₹20,00,000.\n- B) Only ₹70 lakh was reinvested, not the full gain.\n- C) Reverses the figures.\n- D) ₹50 lakh cap is for 54EC, not 54.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Continuing Q88: the ₹20,00,000 taxable LTCG on the house is taxed at 12.5% (new, no indexation). Tax?\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,87,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 20,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹2,50,000**.\n- B) Uses 20%.\n- C) Uses 10%.\n- D) Uses ₹15,00,000 base by error.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Mr. X sells a plot for ₹50,00,000 (LTCG ₹30,00,000). He invests ₹15,00,000 in 54EC bonds and ₹10,00,000 in a new house under 54F (net consideration fully attributable). Assume 54F exemption proportionate = ₹6,00,000. Total exemption?\nA) ₹21,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Exemptions under different sections can be combined: 54EC ₹15,00,000 + 54F ₹6,00,000 = **₹21,00,000**.\n- B) Counts only 54EC.\n- C) Adds the ₹10 lakh investment instead of the ₹6 lakh proportionate 54F exemption.\n- D) Assumes whole gain exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A house bought FY 2010-11 for ₹40,00,000 is sold FY 2024-25 for ₹1,80,00,000. CII 2010-11 = 167; CII 2024-25 = 363. Indexed cost?\nA) ₹86,94,611  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹1,45,20,000  D) ₹66,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Indexed cost = 40,00,000 × (363 ÷ 167) = 40,00,000 × 2.1737 = **₹86,94,611**.\n- B) Ignores indexation.\n- C) Multiplies by (363/100).\n- D) Multiplies by 1.67.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Continuing Q91: old-method LTCG and tax at 20%?\nA) LTCG ₹93,05,389; tax ₹18,61,078  B) LTCG ₹1,40,00,000; tax ₹28,00,000  C) LTCG ₹93,05,389; tax ₹11,63,174  D) LTCG ₹86,94,611; tax ₹17,38,922\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG (old) = 1,80,00,000 − 86,94,611 = ₹93,05,389. Tax @ 20% = **₹18,61,078**.\n- B) Ignores indexation.\n- C) Applies 12.5% to indexed gain (wrong rate for old method).\n- D) Treats indexed cost as the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Continuing Q91/Q92: new-method LTCG and tax at 12.5%, then the final payable?\nA) New LTCG ₹1,40,00,000; new tax ₹17,50,000; pay ₹17,50,000  B) New tax ₹17,50,000; pay ₹18,61,078  C) New tax ₹28,00,000; pay ₹18,61,078  D) New tax ₹17,50,000; pay lower = nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — New LTCG = 1,80,00,000 − 40,00,000 = ₹1,40,00,000; tax @ 12.5% = ₹17,50,000. Final = **lower of** ₹18,61,078 (old) and ₹17,50,000 (new) = **₹17,50,000**.\n- B) Picks the higher as payable.\n- C) Wrong new tax figure.\n- D) Tax is not nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Mr. Y has equity LTCG ₹1,50,000 and equity STCG (111A) ₹1,00,000 in the year. Total capital-gains tax?\nA) ₹23,125  B) ₹21,250  C) ₹31,250  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG: (1,50,000 − 1,25,000) × 12.5% = 25,000 × 0.125 = ₹3,125. STCG: 1,00,000 × 20% = ₹20,000. Total = 3,125 + 20,000 = **₹23,125**. (The ₹1.25 lakh exemption applies only to LTCG, not STCG.)\n- B) Applies the exemption to STCG too.\n- C) Taxes full LTCG at 12.5% plus STCG.\n- D) Counts only STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Mr. Z, an NRI, has LTCG of ₹20,00,000 on Indian shares (foreign-currency acquired); net consideration ₹50,00,000; he invests ₹25,00,000 in eligible new assets. Section 115F exemption?\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹12,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Formula (115F):** Exemption = Capital gain × (Investment ÷ Net consideration) = 20,00,000 × (25,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000) = 20,00,000 × 0.5 = **₹10,00,000**.\n- B) Assumes full gain exempt.\n- C) Uses the investment amount directly.\n- D) Uses a wrong ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Mr. AB sells unlisted debentures in Dec 2024 (held 5 years) at a gain of ₹4,00,000. Tax treatment?\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹50,000  B) LTCG at 20% indexed  C) Exempt  D) STCG (always, s.50AA) at slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unlisted debentures transferred after 23 July 2024 are **always short-term** under Section 50AA, regardless of the 5-year holding; taxed at **slab rate**.\n- A), B) Never long-term.\n- C) Not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Mr. CD sells a long-term plot, LTCG ₹12,00,000. He invests ₹12,00,000 in 54EC bonds but only ₹4,00,000 within 6 months and ₹8,00,000 after 8 months. Exemption?\nA) ₹12,00,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only investment made **within 6 months** qualifies. ₹4,00,000 invested in time → exemption ₹4,00,000; the ₹8,00,000 invested after the window does not qualify.\n- A) Counts the late investment.\n- C) Counts only the disqualified portion.\n- D) Some valid investment was made.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Mr. EF converts a capital asset (cost ₹5,00,000) into stock-in-trade when its FMV is ₹15,00,000, then sells the stock for ₹18,00,000. The capital gain portion is based on full value of:\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On conversion to stock-in-trade, the **FMV on the date of conversion (₹15,00,000)** is the full value of consideration for the capital-gains computation (capital gain = 15,00,000 − 5,00,000 = ₹10,00,000). The further ₹3,00,000 (18,00,000 − 15,00,000) is business income.\n- A) That is the final sale price (business side).\n- C) That is the cost.\n- D) That is the business-income slice.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Mr. GH has equity LTCG ₹80,000 (STT-paid) and equity STCG (111A) ₹60,000. Total tax?\nA) ₹12,000  B) ₹17,500  C) Nil  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG ₹80,000 is below the ₹1,25,000 exemption → taxable Nil. STCG ₹60,000 × 20% = **₹12,000**. (Note: the ₹1.25 lakh exemption applies only to LTCG and cannot shelter STCG.)\n- B) Taxes both wrongly.\n- C) Ignores STCG tax.\n- D) Uses a wrong rate on STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Mr. IJ (resident) sells a Dubai plot on 12 June 2026; gain in AED converts to INR using the TT buying rate as on which date?\nA) 12 June 2026  B) 31 May 2026  C) 1 June 2026  D) 30 June 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For \"other\" capital gains, conversion uses the TT buying rate on the **last day of the month immediately preceding** the transfer month → **31 May 2026**.\n- A), C) Not the prescribed date.\n- D) End of the transfer month itself is not used.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Mehta (an individual resident) made the following transactions in FY 2024-25 (all transfers on/after 23 July 2024):\n\n1. He sold a **residential house** on 1 September 2024 for ₹2,00,00,000. He had bought it in FY 2011-12 for ₹50,00,000. (CII 2011-12 = 184; CII 2024-25 = 363.)\n2. On the same equity portfolio, he sold **listed equity shares (STT-paid)** held for 18 months: sale ₹10,00,000, cost ₹4,00,000. This is his only equity LTCG of the year.\n3. He sold **listed equity shares (STT-paid)** held for 7 months: sale ₹3,00,000, cost ₹2,40,000.\n4. He invested ₹60,00,000 in **NHAI bonds** within 6 months of selling the house (Section 54EC).\n5. He sold **physical gold** held for 50 months: sale ₹8,00,000, cost ₹3,00,000.\n\n<details><summary><strong>Sub-question 1:</strong> Compute the LTCG on the house under both old (indexed) and new methods, and the indexed cost.</summary>\n\n**Indexed cost of acquisition** = Cost × (CII transfer ÷ CII acquisition)\n= 50,00,000 × (363 ÷ 184) = 50,00,000 × 1.97283 = **₹98,64,130**.\n\n- **Old method LTCG** = Sale − Indexed cost = 2,00,00,000 − 98,64,130 = **₹1,01,35,870**.\n- **New method LTCG** = Sale − Cost = 2,00,00,000 − 50,00,000 = **₹1,50,00,000**.\n\nBecause the house was bought before 23 July 2024 but sold after, both calculations are needed for the parallel comparison (Sub-question 4).\n</details>\n\n<details><summary><strong>Sub-question 2:</strong> Determine the Section 54EC exemption available against the house gain, and the net LTCG (new-method basis) after exemption.</summary>\n\n**Section 54EC** exemption = lower of (amount invested, ₹50,00,000 cap).\nHe invested ₹60,00,000, but the cap is **₹50,00,000**, so exemption = **₹50,00,000**.\n\nNet new-method LTCG after 54EC = 1,50,00,000 − 50,00,000 = **₹1,00,00,000**.\nNet old-method LTCG after 54EC = 1,01,35,870 − 50,00,000 = **₹51,35,870**.\n\n(Intuition: the extra ₹10,00,000 invested above ₹50 lakh earns no exemption — the cap is a hard ceiling.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary><strong>Sub-question 3:</strong> Compute the final tax on the house under the parallel-calculation rule.</summary>\n\n- **Old method tax** = Net old LTCG × 20% = 51,35,870 × 0.20 = **₹10,27,174**.\n- **New method tax** = Net new LTCG × 12.5% = 1,00,00,000 × 0.125 = **₹12,50,000**.\n\nFinal house tax = **lower of** the two = **₹10,27,174** (old/indexed method wins here).\n\n(Logic: for older property with a big inflation gap, indexation often still produces the lower tax even though the rate is higher.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary><strong>Sub-question 4:</strong> Compute the tax on the two equity transactions (items 2 and 3) and the gold (item 5).</summary>\n\n**Equity LTCG (item 2, held 18 months > 12, Section 112A):**\nLTCG = 10,00,000 − 4,00,000 = ₹6,00,000. Less ₹1,25,000 exemption = ₹4,75,000 taxable. Tax @ 12.5% = **₹59,375**.\n\n**Equity STCG (item 3, held 7 months, Section 111A):**\nSTCG = 3,00,000 − 2,40,000 = ₹60,000. Tax @ 20% = **₹12,000**. (No ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG.)\n\n**Gold LTCG (item 5, held 50 months > 24, 12.5% no indexation):**\nLTCG = 8,00,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹5,00,000. Tax @ 12.5% = **₹62,500**. (Gold gets no ₹1.25 lakh exemption — that is only for STT-paid equity.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary><strong>Sub-question 5:</strong> Compute Mr. Mehta's total capital-gains tax for FY 2024-25.</summary>\n\n| Item | Gain head | Tax |\n|------|-----------|----:|\n| House (after 54EC, lower-of method) | LTCG | ₹10,27,174 |\n| Listed equity (item 2) | LTCG 112A | ₹59,375 |\n| Listed equity (item 3) | STCG 111A | ₹12,000 |\n| Gold (item 5) | LTCG | ₹62,500 |\n| **Total capital-gains tax** | | **₹11,61,049** |\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- The ₹1,25,000 exemption applied **only** to the STT-paid equity LTCG (item 2), not to STCG or gold.\n- Section 54EC is **capped at ₹50,00,000**; the ₹10 lakh excess investment gave no benefit.\n- For the house (pre-23 July 2024 purchase, post-date sale), the **lower of** 20%-indexed and 12.5%-unindexed was paid.\n- STCG under 111A is **20%**; general LTCG (gold, after the equity rule) is **12.5%**.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "09", "title": "Income from Other Sources", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 9: Income from Other Sources — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- What \"**Income from Other Sources (IFOS)**\" is and what falls into it\n- **Dividend income** — meaning, deemed dividend, taxation after DDT was abolished, buy-back rule\n- **Interest on securities** — meaning, basis of charge, deductions, conversion of foreign-currency income\n- **Gift of securities** — when it is taxed, the **₹50,000** threshold, fair market value, and the long list of **relatives** and exempt situations\n\n---\n\n## 9.1 Introduction — the \"catch-all\" head\n**Income from Other Sources** is the **residual head**: any income that is **not exempt** and is **not chargeable under the other four heads** (Salary, House Property, PGBP, Capital Gains) is taxed here. But some incomes are **always** taxed under this head.\n\n**Analogy:** IFOS is the \"miscellaneous drawer\" of the income tax house — whatever doesn't fit in the other four labelled drawers, and isn't thrown away (exempt), lands here.\n\n**Items computed under IFOS include:**\n1. **Dividend income** (incl. buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder)\n2. Winnings from lotteries, etc.\n3. Winnings from online games (lottery-type)\n4. **Interest on securities** (if not business income)\n5. Rental income of machinery, plant or furniture (if not business income)\n6. Composite rent from letting plant/machinery/furniture + building\n7. Sum under a Keyman insurance policy\n8. Interest on compensation / enhanced compensation\n9. **Forfeited advance money** from a failed transfer of a capital asset\n10. Deemed income (section 56(2))\n11. Compensation on termination/modification of employment\n12. Sum under a life insurance policy (other than ULIP/Keyman) exceeding aggregate premium paid\n13. Specified sum received by a unit-holder from a business trust\n14. Any other income not taxable under any other head\n\nThen: **Less attributable expenses → Income from Other Sources.**\n\n⚠️ Items 4, 5, 6 go to IFOS **only if not chargeable as business income**; item 7 (Keyman) goes to IFOS only if not business income **or salary**.\n\n**Always taxed under IFOS (income from securities):** (a) dividend income, (b) interest income from securities held as investment, (c) forfeited advance money on a failed capital-asset transfer, (d) deemed income under s.56(2), (e) specified sum from a business trust.\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 Dividend Income\n\n### Meaning of Dividend\nA **dividend** is normally a company **distributing its profits** to shareholders. But Section 2(22) also treats certain receipts as **\"deemed dividend\"**:\n| Clause | Deemed dividend |\n|--------|-----------------|\n| a | Distribution of accumulated profits releasing the company's assets |\n| b | Distribution of debentures/deposit certificates from accumulated profits; bonus shares to **preference** shareholders out of accumulated profits |\n| c | Distribution on **liquidation** out of accumulated profits |\n| d | Distribution on **reduction of capital** out of accumulated profits |\n| e | **Loan/advance by a closely held company** to its shareholder out of accumulated profits |\n| f | **Payment on buy-back** of its own shares from a shareholder (per Companies Act, 2013) |\n\n### Scheme of Taxation — the big shift\n- **Up to AY 2020-21:** companies/mutual funds paid **Dividend Distribution Tax (DDT)**, so shareholders were largely exempt.\n- **From AY 2021-22:** DDT was **abolished** (Finance Act, 2020). Now **dividend is taxable in the hands of the shareholder/unit-holder** under IFOS, at the **shareholder's applicable slab rate** (subject to residential status). For a non-resident, **DTAA and MLI** provisions also apply.\n\n### Buy-back rule (from 1 October 2024)\n**Buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder are taxed as dividend** under IFOS.\n- **No deduction for expenses** is allowed from such dividend.\n- The **cost of acquisition** of the bought-back shares is treated as a **capital loss** in the shareholder's hands, eligible for set-off as per applicable provisions.\n\n💡 So a buy-back now has two tax effects: the full proceeds are taxed as dividend, while your original cost becomes a capital loss you can set off against capital gains.\n\n### TDS on dividend\nTax is deducted under **Section 194** or **Section 194K** (units), as applicable.\n\n---\n\n## 9.3 Interest on Securities\nInterest on securities is taxable under IFOS **if it is not business income**.\n\n### Meaning of \"interest on securities\" — Section 2(28B)\na) Interest on any security of the **central or state government**; and\nb) Interest on **debentures / other securities for money** issued by a local authority, company, or a corporation established by a central/state/provincial Act.\n\n### Meaning of \"securities\"\n\"Security\" is not defined in the Income Tax Act, so we use **Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956**: bonds; debentures/debenture stock; security receipt; government securities; pooled investment vehicle.\n\n### Basis of charge (Section 145)\nInterest is computed by the assessee's **method of accounting**:\n- **Mercantile system** → taxable on **accrual** basis.\n- **Cash system** → taxable on **receipt** basis.\n\n⚠️ Some interest income is **exempt** under Section 10 (see Annexure 2).\n\n### Computation of taxable interest\n```\nGross interest from securities\n  Less: Collection charges\n  Less: Interest on money borrowed to buy the securities\n  Less: Any other revenue expenditure wholly & exclusively to earn that income\n= Taxable income from securities\n```\n\n### Stock-in-trade vs investment\n- Securities held as **stock-in-trade** → profit on sale = **business income** (PGBP).\n- Securities held as **investment** → profit on sale = **capital gains**.\n\n### Foreign-currency interest conversion\nIncome from securities earned in foreign currency is converted to INR at the **SBI TT buying rate** on the **last day of the month immediately preceding** the month the income is due. If the income is subject to **TDS**, conversion is done on the **date tax is required to be deducted**.\n\n### Rate of tax\nInterest is taxed at the **assessee's applicable rates**. For **non-residents**, certain interest incomes get **concessional rates** (see Annexure 1).\n\n---\n\n## 9.4 Gift of Securities\n\n### The rule\nIf a person receives **movable property** (which includes **shares and securities**) **without consideration** or for **inadequate consideration**, the benefit is taxable in the recipient's hands as IFOS — **unless** the total benefit for the year is small.\n\n⚠️ **₹50,000 threshold:** No tax if the **aggregate** difference between the fair market value of properties received during the year and the consideration paid **does not exceed ₹50,000**.\n\n💡 Trap: The ₹50,000 is a **threshold, not an exemption slab**. If the aggregate FMV (or FMV–consideration) **exceeds** ₹50,000, the **whole amount** is taxed, not just the excess over ₹50,000.\n\n### 9.4.1 Computation\n- **Received without consideration:** if **aggregate FMV** of securities received in the year **exceeds ₹50,000**, the **whole aggregate FMV** is taxable.\n- **Received for inadequate consideration:** if **aggregate (FMV − consideration)** **exceeds ₹50,000**, the **whole difference** is taxable.\n\n**Worked example A (without consideration):** You receive shares worth FMV ₹70,000 free. Since ₹70,000 > ₹50,000, the **entire ₹70,000** is taxable as IFOS.\n\n**Worked example B (just under threshold):** You receive shares of FMV ₹45,000 free. ₹45,000 ≤ ₹50,000 → **nothing** is taxed.\n\n**Worked example C (inadequate consideration):** You buy shares of FMV ₹2,00,000 for ₹1,40,000. Difference = ₹60,000 > ₹50,000 → the **whole ₹60,000** is taxable.\n\n### 9.4.2 Fair Market Value\nFMV of shares and securities is computed per **Rule 11UA** of the Income Tax Rules, 1962, which prescribes different methods for **quoted** and **unquoted** shares/securities.\n\n### 9.4.3 When the gift is NOT taxed\n\n**Due to a specified event** — money/property received:\na) on the occasion of the **individual's marriage**;\nb) under a **will or by inheritance**;\nc) in **contemplation of death** of the payer/donor.\n\n**Due to the status of the donor/payer** — received from:\na) a **specified relative**;\nb) any **local authority**;\nc) any fund/foundation/university/educational institution/hospital/trust referred to in **section 10(23C)**;\nd) any trust/institution registered under **section 12A/12AA/12AB**;\ne) a trust created solely for the benefit of a relative of the individual.\n\n**Due to transactions not regarded as transfer** — e.g. distribution on total/partial partition of an HUF (Section 47(i)).\n\n### Who is a \"relative\"?\nFor an HUF, **every member is a relative**. For an individual, relatives include (gifts from these are exempt):\n\n| Relatives (gift exempt) |\n|--------------------------|\n| Husband / Wife |\n| Son / Daughter (incl. step & adopted), and their spouses |\n| Father / Mother / Step-father / Step-mother |\n| Brother (and his wife) / Sister (and her husband); Half-brother/sister |\n| Grandfather / Grandmother; Great Grandfather / Great Grandmother |\n| Grandson (and wife) / Granddaughter (and husband); Great-grandchildren (and spouses) |\n| Father-in-law / Mother-in-law; Son-in-law / Daughter-in-law |\n| Brother-in-law (and his wife) / Sister-in-law (and her husband) |\n| Father's Brother (Chacha/Tau) & wife; Father's Sister (Bua) & husband |\n| Mother's Brother (Mama) & wife; Mother's Sister (Mausi) & husband |\n| Spouse's Grandfather/Grandmother; Spouse's Great-Grandfather/Grandmother |\n\n⚠️ **NOT relatives** (gifts from these ARE taxable if > ₹50,000): **Step-brother/sister, Nephew/Niece, Cousins.**\n\n💡 Exam trap: a gift from your **cousin** or **nephew** is taxable; a gift from your **uncle (Mama/Chacha)** is exempt. Notice the asymmetry — uncles/aunts are relatives, but their children (your cousins) and your siblings' children (nephew/niece) are not.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **IFOS** is the residual head — income not exempt and not in the other four heads; some incomes are always here.\n- **Dividend:** DDT abolished from AY 2021-22 → now taxed in the **shareholder's hands at slab rate** under IFOS. **Buy-back proceeds (from 1 Oct 2024)** taxed as dividend with **no expense deduction**; the cost becomes a **capital loss**. TDS under s.194/194K.\n- **Deemed dividend (s.2(22)):** liquidation/capital-reduction distributions, loans by closely held companies to shareholders, buy-backs, etc.\n- **Interest on securities (s.2(28B)):** govt securities + company/local-authority debentures. Taxed on accrual (mercantile) or receipt (cash) basis. Deduct collection charges, borrowing interest, related expenses. Concessional rates for some non-resident interest.\n- **Gift of securities:** taxable as IFOS if aggregate FMV (or FMV−consideration) **exceeds ₹50,000** — then the **whole** amount is taxed. **Exempt** if from a relative, on marriage, under a will/inheritance, in contemplation of death, or from specified institutions. FMV per **Rule 11UA**.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---------------|---------|\n| ₹50,000 | Gift threshold — if exceeded, the whole amount is taxed |\n| 1 October 2024 | Buy-back proceeds taxed as dividend from this date |\n| AY 2021-22 | DDT abolished; dividend taxed in shareholder's hands |\n| Section 2(22) | Definition of (deemed) dividend |\n| Section 2(28B) | Definition of \"interest on securities\" |\n| Section 2(h) SCRA 1956 | Source for the meaning of \"securities\" |\n| Section 145 | Method of accounting (accrual / receipt) |\n| Section 194 / 194K | TDS on dividend / on units |\n| Rule 11UA | Computation of FMV of shares & securities |\n| Sections 10(23C), 12A/12AA/12AB | Institutions whose gifts are exempt |\n| Section 47(i) | HUF partition not regarded as transfer |\n| Not relatives | Step-brother/sister, nephew/niece, cousins |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 9: Income from Other Sources — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is \"Income from Other Sources\" (IFOS)?\n> **A:** The **residual head** — any income that is not exempt and not chargeable under the other four heads (Salary, House Property, PGBP, Capital Gains). Some incomes are always taxed here.\n\n**Q2.** Name five incomes computed under IFOS.\n> **A:** Dividend income; winnings from lotteries/online games; interest on securities (if not business income); rental of machinery/plant/furniture; sum under a Keyman insurance policy; forfeited advance money on a failed transfer; deemed income under s.56(2). (Any five.)\n\n**Q3.** Which incomes from securities are ALWAYS taxed under IFOS?\n> **A:** (a) Dividend income; (b) interest income from securities held as investment; (c) forfeited advance money on a failed transfer of a capital asset; (d) deemed income under s.56(2); (e) specified sum received by a unit-holder from a business trust.\n\n**Q4.** What is \"dividend\" and what is \"deemed dividend\"?\n> **A:** Dividend = distribution of a company's profits to shareholders. Deemed dividend (s.2(22)) includes distributions on liquidation/capital reduction out of accumulated profits, loans/advances by a closely held company to its shareholder, and buy-back payments.\n\n**Q5.** How is dividend income taxed after the abolition of DDT?\n> **A:** From **AY 2021-22**, DDT was abolished, so dividend is taxable **in the hands of the shareholder/unit-holder** under IFOS, at the shareholder's **applicable slab rate** (with DTAA/MLI for non-residents).\n\n**Q6.** How are share buy-back proceeds taxed from 1 October 2024?\n> **A:** Buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder are taxed as **dividend** under IFOS, with **no deduction for expenses**. The cost of acquisition of the bought-back shares is treated as a **capital loss** eligible for set-off.\n\n**Q7.** Under which sections is TDS deducted on dividend?\n> **A:** **Section 194** (dividends) or **Section 194K** (income from units), as applicable.\n\n**Q8.** Define \"interest on securities\" under Section 2(28B).\n> **A:** (a) Interest on any security of the central/state government; and (b) interest on debentures/other securities for money issued by a local authority, company, or corporation established by a central/state/provincial Act.\n\n**Q9.** Where do we find the meaning of \"securities\" for this chapter?\n> **A:** In **Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956** — bonds, debentures/debenture stock, security receipts, government securities, pooled investment vehicles.\n\n**Q10.** On what basis is interest on securities charged to tax (Section 145)?\n> **A:** On the assessee's method of accounting — **accrual** basis if mercantile system, **receipt** basis if cash system.\n\n**Q11.** What deductions are allowed against gross interest on securities?\n> **A:** Collection charges; interest on money borrowed to purchase the securities; and any other revenue expenditure laid out wholly and exclusively to earn that income.\n\n**Q12.** Securities held as stock-in-trade vs investment — how is the sale profit taxed?\n> **A:** Stock-in-trade → **business income (PGBP)**. Investment → **capital gains**.\n\n**Q13.** When is a gift of securities NOT taxed because of the amount?\n> **A:** When the **aggregate** of (FMV of properties received during the year − consideration paid) **does not exceed ₹50,000**.\n\n**Q14.** If a gift of shares exceeds the ₹50,000 threshold, how much is taxed?\n> **A:** The **whole amount** is taxed (not just the excess over ₹50,000). The ₹50,000 is a threshold, not a deduction.\n\n**Q15.** Securities received without consideration: when and how much is taxed?\n> **A:** If the aggregate **FMV** of securities received in the year **exceeds ₹50,000**, the **whole aggregate FMV** is taxable as IFOS.\n\n**Q16.** Securities received for inadequate consideration: when and how much is taxed?\n> **A:** If the aggregate **(FMV − consideration)** **exceeds ₹50,000**, the **whole difference** is taxable.\n\n**Q17.** How is the fair market value of shares and securities computed?\n> **A:** As per **Rule 11UA** of the Income Tax Rules, 1962, with different methods for quoted and unquoted shares/securities.\n\n**Q18.** Name three \"specified events\" that make a gift exempt regardless of amount.\n> **A:** Received (a) on the occasion of the individual's marriage; (b) under a will or by inheritance; (c) in contemplation of death of the payer/donor.\n\n**Q19.** Gifts from which institutions are exempt?\n> **A:** A local authority; any fund/foundation/university/educational institution/hospital/trust referred to in section 10(23C); any trust/institution registered under section 12A/12AA/12AB.\n\n**Q20.** Are the following relatives (gift exempt): brother, mother's brother (Mama), and a cousin?\n> **A:** Brother — **Yes** (relative). Mama (mother's brother) — **Yes** (relative). Cousin — **No** (not a relative, so a gift > ₹50,000 from a cousin is taxable).\n\n**Q21.** Name three persons who are NOT treated as relatives for the gift provision.\n> **A:** **Step-brother/sister, Nephew/Niece, and Cousins.**\n\n**Q22.** In an HUF, who is treated as a relative for the gift provision?\n> **A:** **Every member of the HUF** is treated as a relative.\n\n**Q23.** Is a gift from your father-in-law taxable?\n> **A:** **No.** Father-in-law (and mother-in-law, son-in-law, daughter-in-law, brother-in-law, sister-in-law) are relatives, so the gift is exempt.\n\n**Q24.** How is foreign-currency interest on securities converted into INR?\n> **A:** At the **SBI TT buying rate** on the **last day of the month immediately preceding** the month the income is due (or, if subject to TDS, on the date tax is required to be deducted).\n\n**Q25.** Are concessional tax rates available on interest income for non-residents?\n> **A:** Yes — in the case of non-residents, certain interest incomes are taxable at **concessional rates** (per Annexure 1 special tax rates).\n\n**Q26.** A loan given by a closely held company to its shareholder out of accumulated profits is treated as what?\n> **A:** **Deemed dividend** under Section 2(22)(e), taxable in the shareholder's hands under IFOS.\n\n**Q27.** You receive free shares worth FMV ₹48,000 in a year. Taxable?\n> **A:** **No** — ₹48,000 ≤ ₹50,000, so nothing is taxed.\n\n**Q28.** You buy shares of FMV ₹3,00,000 from a friend for ₹2,20,000. What is taxed?\n> **A:** Difference = ₹80,000 > ₹50,000, and a friend is not a relative → the **whole ₹80,000** is taxable as IFOS.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 9: Income from Other Sources — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Questions are split into three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark — skip only if you are truly unsure.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Income from Other Sources (IFOS) is best described as which kind of head of income?\nA) The first head, taking priority over all others  B) A head only for salaried persons  C) The residual (\"catch-all\") head  D) A head only for companies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — IFOS is the residual head: any income that is **not exempt** and is **not chargeable under the other four heads** (Salaries, House Property, PGBP, Capital Gains) is taxed here.\n- A) Wrong — it is the last, residual head, not a priority head.\n- B) Wrong — it applies to all assessees, not only salaried persons.\n- D) Wrong — it is not company-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following are the other four heads of income under which an income is first tested before falling into IFOS?\nA) Salaries, House Property, PGBP, Capital Gains  B) Salaries, Dividends, Interest, Gifts  C) Wages, Rent, Profit, Bonus  D) Income, Expenditure, Profit, Loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The four specific heads are Salaries, Income from House Property, Profits and Gains from Business or Profession (PGBP), and Capital Gains. Anything not exempt and not under these four falls under IFOS.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — these mix up types of receipts/accounting terms with the statutory heads of income.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Dividend income is taxable under which head?\nA) Salaries  B) Capital Gains  C) Income from Other Sources  D) PGBP always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Dividend income (including buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder) is one of the incomes always taxable under IFOS.\n- A), B) Wrong — dividend is neither salary nor a capital gain.\n- D) Wrong — dividend is not, by default, business income for an ordinary shareholder.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under which section is \"dividend\" (including deemed dividend) defined?\nA) Section 2(28B)  B) Section 145  C) Rule 11UA  D) Section 2(22)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(22) of the Income Tax Act defines dividend and lists the receipts deemed to be dividend.\n- A) Wrong — 2(28B) defines \"interest on securities\".\n- B) Wrong — Section 145 deals with method of accounting.\n- C) Wrong — Rule 11UA (not a section) deals with FMV of shares/securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Up to which assessment year did domestic companies and mutual funds pay Dividend Distribution Tax (DDT)?\nA) AY 2018-19  B) AY 2019-20  C) AY 2020-21  D) AY 2021-22\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Up to AY 2020-21, companies/mutual funds paid DDT, so shareholders were largely exempt. DDT was abolished by the Finance Act, 2020 with effect from AY 2021-22.\n- A), B) Wrong — DDT continued beyond these years.\n- D) Wrong — from AY 2021-22 DDT no longer applied; dividend became taxable in the shareholder's hands.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** After DDT was abolished, in whose hands is dividend income taxable?\nA) The company paying it  B) The mutual fund  C) Nobody — it is exempt  D) The shareholder/unit-holder receiving it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — From AY 2021-22, dividend is taxable in the hands of the shareholder/unit-holder under IFOS, at the recipient's applicable slab rate.\n- A), B) Wrong — the company/MF no longer pays DDT on it.\n- C) Wrong — it is taxable, not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** At what rate is dividend taxed in the hands of a resident shareholder?\nA) A flat 10%  B) A flat 20%  C) The shareholder's applicable slab rate  D) Tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Dividend is taxed at the shareholder's applicable slab rate, subject to residential status and quantum of income.\n- A), B) Wrong — there is no special flat rate for resident shareholders here.\n- D) Wrong — it is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** From which date are buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder taxed as dividend under IFOS?\nA) 1 April 2020  B) 1 April 2024  C) 1 October 2024  D) 1 January 2025\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With effect from 1 October 2024, buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder are taxed as dividend under IFOS.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are not the date specified in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** When buy-back proceeds are taxed as dividend, what deduction for expenses is allowed against them?\nA) Full deduction of all related expenses  B) 50% standard deduction  C) No deduction for expenses  D) Deduction up to ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No deduction for expenses is available from such dividend while determining income from other sources.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — the workbook clearly states no expense deduction is allowed against buy-back dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** In a buy-back taxed as dividend, how is the cost of acquisition of the bought-back shares treated?\nA) As a capital loss in the shareholder's hands  B) As a deduction from dividend  C) As exempt income  D) It is ignored entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The cost of acquisition of the shares bought back is treated as a capital loss in the shareholder's hands, eligible for set-off as per applicable provisions.\n- B) Wrong — no deduction is allowed against the dividend.\n- C), D) Wrong — the cost is not exempt nor ignored; it becomes a capital loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Under which sections is TDS deducted from dividend?\nA) Section 194 or 194K  B) Section 80C / 80D  C) Section 234A / 234B  D) Section 2(22)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax is deducted from dividend under Section 194 or Section 194K (for units), as the case may be.\n- B) Wrong — 80C/80D are deduction sections.\n- C) Wrong — 234A/234B relate to interest for default.\n- D) Wrong — 2(22) defines dividend, it is not a TDS section.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which of the following is a \"deemed dividend\" under Section 2(22)?\nA) Salary paid to a director  B) Rent paid by a company  C) GST collected by a company  D) Loan or advance by a closely held company to its shareholder out of accumulated profits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A loan or advance by a closely held company to its shareholder out of accumulated profits is a deemed dividend under Section 2(22)(e).\n- A), B), C) Wrong — salary, rent and GST are not covered by the deemed dividend definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Distribution to shareholders on the company's liquidation out of accumulated profits is:\nA) Exempt  B) A capital gain only  C) Salary income  D) A deemed dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Distribution on liquidation out of accumulated profits is treated as a deemed dividend under Section 2(22).\n- A) Wrong — it is taxable as deemed dividend.\n- B), C) Wrong — it is treated as dividend, not as pure capital gain or salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Issue of bonus shares to which class of shareholders out of accumulated profits is a deemed dividend?\nA) Equity shareholders  B) Preference shareholders  C) Both equity and preference  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Issue of bonus shares to preference shareholders out of accumulated profits is a deemed dividend under Section 2(22)(b).\n- A), C) Wrong — bonus to equity shareholders is not listed as deemed dividend here.\n- D) Wrong — bonus to preference shareholders is specifically deemed dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** \"Interest on securities\" is defined under which section?\nA) Section 2(22)  B) Section 56(2)  C) Section 2(h)  D) Section 2(28B)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(28B) defines \"interest on securities\".\n- A) Wrong — 2(22) defines dividend.\n- B) Wrong — 56(2) deals with deemed income / specific IFOS items.\n- C) Wrong — 2(h) is in the SCRA, 1956 (definition of \"securities\"), not the Income Tax Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Interest on securities is taxable under IFOS only if it is not in the nature of:\nA) Salary income  B) Capital gains  C) Business income  D) Exempt income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Interest on securities is taxable as IFOS if it is not in the nature of business income.\n- A), B) Wrong — the workbook test for this income is specifically whether it is business income.\n- D) Wrong — exemption is a separate concept; the IFOS/business distinction is what governs the head.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Under Section 2(28B), interest on which of these is \"interest on securities\"?\nA) Interest on any security of the central or state government  B) A personal loan to a friend  C) Interest on a savings bank account  D) Interest on a fixed deposit with a bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Interest on any security of the central or state government is \"interest on securities\" under Section 2(28B)(a).\n- B), C), D) Wrong — personal loans, savings accounts and bank FDs are not \"securities\" under this definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Since \"security\" is not defined in the Income Tax Act, the reference is taken from which law?\nA) Companies Act, 2013  B) SEBI Act, 1992  C) RBI Act, 1934  D) Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — As \"security\" is not defined in the Income Tax Act, reference is taken from Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — the workbook specifically points to SCRA, 1956, Section 2(h).\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is listed as a \"security\" from which interest on securities can arise?\nA) A bank cheque  B) A promissory note between friends  C) A credit card  D) A debenture or debenture stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Debentures or debenture stock are among the securities listed (bonds, debentures/debenture stock, security receipt, government securities, pooled investment vehicle).\n- A), B), C) Wrong — a cheque, a private promissory note and a credit card are not in the listed securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under Section 145, interest on securities is computed according to the assessee's:\nA) Method of accounting  B) Residential status  C) Age  D) Tax slab\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 145 requires interest on securities to be computed in accordance with the method of accounting regularly employed by the assessee.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — residential status, age and slab do not decide the timing/basis of charge here.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Under the mercantile system of accounting, interest on securities is taxable on a:\nA) Receipt basis  B) Accrual basis  C) Cash basis  D) Due-date-only basis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the mercantile system, interest on securities is taxable on an accrual basis.\n- A), C) Wrong — receipt/cash basis applies under the cash system.\n- D) Wrong — \"due-date-only\" is not a recognised system here.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under the cash system of accounting, interest on securities is taxable on a:\nA) Receipt basis  B) Accrual basis  C) Estimated basis  D) Notional basis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under the cash system, interest on securities is taxable on a receipt basis.\n- B) Wrong — accrual basis applies under the mercantile system.\n- C), D) Wrong — these are not recognised bases under the Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which deduction is permissible against gross interest from securities?\nA) Collection charges  B) Personal living expenses  C) Income tax paid  D) Donations to friends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Collection charges are a permissible deduction against gross interest from securities (along with interest on borrowings to buy the securities and other revenue expenditure wholly and exclusively for earning that income).\n- B), C), D) Wrong — personal expenses, income tax and personal donations are not permissible deductions here.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Interest on money borrowed to purchase securities is:\nA) Not deductible at all  B) Deductible only up to ₹50,000  C) Added to the income  D) Deductible against the interest income from securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Interest on borrowings obtained to purchase securities is a permissible deduction against interest income from securities.\n- A) Wrong — it is deductible.\n- B) Wrong — there is no ₹50,000 cap on this deduction.\n- C) Wrong — it is subtracted, not added.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** If securities are held as stock-in-trade, profit on their sale is chargeable under:\nA) Capital gains  B) IFOS  C) PGBP (business income)  D) Salaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If securities are held as stock-in-trade, any profit on their sale is chargeable under PGBP (business income).\n- A) Wrong — capital gains applies when held as an investment.\n- B), D) Wrong — neither IFOS nor salary applies to sale profit on stock-in-trade securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** If securities are held as an investment, profit on their sale is chargeable under:\nA) Capital gains  B) PGBP  C) IFOS  D) House property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — If securities are held as an investment, profit on sale is chargeable under capital gains.\n- B) Wrong — PGBP applies for stock-in-trade.\n- C), D) Wrong — neither IFOS nor house property covers this profit.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Income from securities earned in foreign currency is converted to INR at which rate?\nA) SBI telegraphic transfer (TT) buying rate  B) RBI reference rate  C) Customs exchange rate  D) Average annual rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It is converted at the SBI TT buying rate that existed on the last day of the month immediately preceding the month in which income is due.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — the workbook specifies the SBI TT buying rate, not these alternatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** For foreign-currency income from securities, the SBI TT buying rate of which day is used (when no TDS applies)?\nA) The first day of the month income is due  B) The last day of the month immediately preceding the month income is due  C) The financial year-end day  D) The day of actual receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The rate on the last day of the month immediately preceding the month in which income is due is used.\n- A), C), D) Wrong — these are not the conversion dates prescribed for the general case.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** If foreign-currency income from securities is subject to TDS, the conversion date is:\nA) The last day of the preceding month  B) The date on which tax is required to be deducted  C) The first day of the financial year  D) The date of remittance abroad\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where the income is subject to TDS, the date of conversion is the date on which tax is required to be deducted.\n- A) Wrong — that is the rule for the general (non-TDS) case.\n- C), D) Wrong — these are not the prescribed conversion dates.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** For non-residents, certain interest incomes are taxable at:\nA) Concessional rates  B) Higher penal rates  C) Zero rate always  D) A flat 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For non-residents, certain interest incomes are taxable at concessional rates (per the Special tax rates in Annexure 1).\n- B), D) Wrong — the workbook says concessional, not penal/flat 30%.\n- C) Wrong — it is concessional, not always zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A gift of movable property (including shares and securities) is taxable in the recipient's hands if the aggregate benefit during the year exceeds:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No tax is charged if the aggregate difference between FMV and consideration of properties received during the year does not exceed ₹50,000; if it exceeds ₹50,000, it becomes taxable.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — the threshold in the workbook is ₹50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** For the gift provisions, \"movable property\" is stated to include:\nA) Shares and securities  B) Land and buildings  C) Agricultural land only  D) Jewellery only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For this provision, movable property shall include shares and securities.\n- B) Wrong — land and buildings are immovable property.\n- C), D) Wrong — the workbook specifically mentions shares and securities here.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A gift received on the occasion of the marriage of the individual is:\nA) Always taxable  B) Taxable only if above ₹50,000  C) Taxable at a flat 30%  D) Not chargeable to tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Income does not arise if money/property is received on the occasion of the marriage of the individual.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — the marriage exception is unconditional under this provision, so it is not taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A gift received under a will or by way of inheritance is:\nA) Not chargeable to tax  B) Taxable above ₹50,000  C) Taxable at slab rates  D) Treated as salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Money/property received under a will or by way of inheritance does not give rise to income under this provision.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — inheritance/will receipts are exempted from this charge, regardless of amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which of the following is NOT treated as a \"relative\" for the gift provision?\nA) Brother  B) Father's brother (Chacha/Tau)  C) Cousin  D) Mother's sister (Mausi)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cousins are specifically NOT relatives for this provision (along with step-brother/sister and nephew/niece).\n- A), B), D) Wrong — brother, father's brother (Chacha/Tau) and mother's sister (Mausi) are all listed relatives, so their gifts are exempt.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. A receives a salary of ₹8,00,000 and interest of ₹40,000 on government securities held as an investment. The interest is taxed under:\nA) Salaries  B) IFOS  C) Capital gains  D) PGBP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on government securities held as an investment (not business income) is taxable as IFOS under Section 2(28B).\n- A) Wrong — only the ₹8,00,000 salary is under \"Salaries\"; the interest is separate.\n- C) Wrong — capital gains arises on sale of the securities, not on interest.\n- D) Wrong — the securities are held as investment, so the interest is not business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A trader in shares (securities held as stock-in-trade) earns profit on selling shares. This profit is taxed under:\nA) IFOS  B) Capital gains  C) PGBP  D) Salaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When securities are held as stock-in-trade, sale profit is business income (PGBP).\n- A) Wrong — IFOS does not cover trading profit on stock-in-trade.\n- B) Wrong — capital gains applies only when the securities are held as an investment.\n- D) Wrong — there is no employment here.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Ms. R receives free shares (no consideration) with aggregate FMV of ₹45,000 during the year, from a non-relative. How much is taxable?\nA) ₹45,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹5,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rule: tax arises only if aggregate FMV **exceeds** ₹50,000. Here aggregate FMV = ₹45,000, which is ≤ ₹50,000, so nothing is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — the full FMV is taxed only once the threshold is crossed.\n- C) Wrong — there is no \"excess over threshold\" concept; it is all-or-nothing, and here it is nothing.\n- D) Wrong — ₹50,000 is the threshold, not the taxable amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Ms. R instead receives free shares of aggregate FMV ₹70,000 from a non-relative. How much is taxable?\nA) ₹20,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹70,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since aggregate FMV ₹70,000 **exceeds** ₹50,000, the **whole** ₹70,000 is taxable. The ₹50,000 is a threshold, not a basic exemption that is subtracted.\n- A) Wrong — ₹70,000 − ₹50,000 = ₹20,000 would be wrong; you do not subtract the threshold.\n- B) Wrong — ₹50,000 is the trigger, not the taxable figure.\n- D) Wrong — once the threshold is crossed, the amount is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. B buys shares of FMV ₹2,00,000 from a friend for ₹1,40,000. The taxable IFOS amount is:\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹1,40,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula for inadequate consideration: Taxable = FMV − Consideration, if the aggregate difference exceeds ₹50,000. Here FMV − Consideration = ₹2,00,000 − ₹1,40,000 = ₹60,000, which exceeds ₹50,000, so the whole ₹60,000 is taxable.\n- A), B) Wrong — neither the full FMV nor the price paid is the taxable amount.\n- D) Wrong — you do not subtract ₹50,000 from the ₹60,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A gift of shares received from one's father is:\nA) Not chargeable (father is a relative)  B) Taxable above ₹50,000  C) Taxable at flat 30%  D) Taxable as salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Father is a listed relative, so the gift is not chargeable to tax regardless of value.\n- B) Wrong — the ₹50,000 threshold applies to gifts from non-relatives; relative gifts are fully exempt.\n- C), D) Wrong — no special rate or salary treatment applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A gift of ₹1,00,000 worth of securities received from one's nephew is:\nA) Taxable — nephew is not a relative and value exceeds ₹50,000  B) Exempt — nephew is a relative  C) Taxable only on ₹50,000  D) Exempt up to ₹50,000, rest taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A nephew is NOT a relative for this provision, and the value ₹1,00,000 exceeds ₹50,000, so the whole ₹1,00,000 is taxable.\n- B) Wrong — nephew/niece are specifically not relatives.\n- C), D) Wrong — once the threshold is crossed, the entire amount is taxed (no slab/partial exemption).\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Securities received in contemplation of death of the donor are:\nA) Taxable above ₹50,000  B) Taxable in full  C) Not chargeable to tax  D) Treated as capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Property received in contemplation of death of the payer/donor does not give rise to income under this provision.\n- A), B) Wrong — this is an unconditional exception, regardless of value.\n- D) Wrong — it is not a capital gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A gift from a trust registered under Section 12AB is:\nA) Taxable above ₹50,000  B) Taxable in full  C) Taxable as dividend  D) Not chargeable to tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Receipts from any trust/institution registered under Section 12A/12AA/12AB are not chargeable under this provision (due to the status of the donor).\n- A), B) Wrong — this exception is not subject to the ₹50,000 threshold.\n- C) Wrong — it is not dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** For an HUF, who is treated as a \"relative\" for the gift provision?\nA) Every member of the HUF  B) Only the karta  C) Only adult members  D) Nobody\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In the case of an HUF, every member of the HUF is treated as a relative.\n- B), C) Wrong — it is not limited to the karta or only adults.\n- D) Wrong — members are relatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Mr. C receives shares free from his step-brother (FMV ₹80,000). Tax treatment?\nA) Exempt — step-brother is a relative  B) Taxable in full — step-brother is not a relative  C) Taxable only on ₹30,000  D) Exempt up to ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A step-brother/sister is specifically NOT a relative for this provision. Since FMV ₹80,000 exceeds ₹50,000, the whole ₹80,000 is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — step-brother is excluded from the relative list.\n- C), D) Wrong — when the threshold is crossed, the full amount is taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A gift of securities received from the brother's wife (sister-in-law) is:\nA) Not chargeable — she is a relative  B) Taxable above ₹50,000  C) Taxable in full  D) Taxable at slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Brother (and his wife) is on the relatives list, so a gift from the brother's wife is exempt.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — gifts from listed relatives are not chargeable under this provision.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Which pairing correctly matches the system of accounting with the basis of taxing interest on securities?\nA) Mercantile → receipt; Cash → accrual  B) Mercantile → accrual; Cash → receipt  C) Both → accrual  D) Both → receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mercantile system taxes interest on accrual; cash system taxes it on receipt (Section 145).\n- A) Wrong — it reverses the two.\n- C), D) Wrong — the two systems give different bases.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Mr. D follows the mercantile system. Interest of ₹10,000 on a debenture accrued in March 2026 but is received in April 2026. In which year is it taxable?\nA) The year of receipt (2026-27)  B) The year of accrual (2025-26)  C) Split across both years  D) Not taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the mercantile system, interest is taxed on accrual; it accrued in March 2026, so it is taxable in FY 2025-26.\n- A) Wrong — receipt basis applies only to the cash system.\n- C), D) Wrong — it is fully taxable in the year of accrual.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Mr. E follows the cash system. The same ₹10,000 interest accrues in March 2026 but is received in April 2026. Taxable when?\nA) Year of accrual (2025-26)  B) Year of receipt (2026-27)  C) Either year  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the cash system, interest is taxed on receipt; it was received in April 2026, i.e. FY 2026-27.\n- A) Wrong — accrual basis applies to the mercantile system.\n- C), D) Wrong — it is taxed specifically in the year of receipt.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following would NOT reduce taxable interest from securities?\nA) Collection charges  B) Interest on a loan taken to buy the securities  C) The investor's personal home loan EMI  D) Other revenue expenditure wholly and exclusively to earn that income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A personal home loan EMI is unrelated to earning interest on securities and is not deductible.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are all listed permissible deductions against interest income from securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A non-resident shareholder receives dividend from an Indian company. Besides the Act, which provisions may also apply?\nA) DTAA and MLI  B) Only GST law  C) Customs law  D) None — it is exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For a non-resident shareholder, the provisions of Double Taxation Avoidance Agreements (DTAAs) and the Multilateral Instrument (MLI) also come into play.\n- B), C) Wrong — GST/customs do not govern dividend taxation.\n- D) Wrong — dividend is taxable, not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A closely held company gives a ₹5,00,000 loan to a shareholder out of accumulated profits. This is best described as:\nA) A tax-free loan  B) A deemed dividend under Section 2(22)  C) Salary  D) Capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A loan/advance by a closely held company to its shareholder out of accumulated profits is a deemed dividend under Section 2(22)(e).\n- A) Wrong — it is deemed dividend, hence taxable.\n- C), D) Wrong — it is neither salary nor capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Buy-back proceeds of ₹3,00,000 are received by a shareholder on 15 October 2024. How are they taxed?\nA) As capital gains in full  B) As dividend under IFOS  C) Fully exempt  D) As salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From 1 October 2024, buy-back proceeds received by a shareholder are taxed as dividend under IFOS. 15 October 2024 falls after that date.\n- A) Wrong — the proceeds are taxed as dividend, not capital gains.\n- C), D) Wrong — they are taxable as dividend, not exempt or salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** In the same buy-back, the shareholder's cost of acquisition of the bought-back shares was ₹1,80,000. This ₹1,80,000 is:\nA) Deducted from the dividend  B) Treated as a capital loss eligible for set-off  C) Added to the dividend  D) Ignored\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cost of acquisition is treated as a capital loss in the shareholder's hands, eligible for set-off as per applicable provisions.\n- A) Wrong — no expense/cost deduction is allowed against the dividend.\n- C), D) Wrong — it becomes a capital loss; it is neither added nor ignored.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which of these statements about the ₹50,000 gift threshold is correct?\nA) It is a per-gift limit applied separately to each gift  B) It applies to the aggregate of properties received during the year  C) It is a basic exemption subtracted from the taxable gift  D) It applies only to cash gifts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The threshold applies to the **aggregate** difference between FMV and consideration of properties received during the year.\n- A) Wrong — it is aggregate, not per-gift.\n- C) Wrong — it is a threshold, not a deduction; once crossed, the whole amount is taxed.\n- D) Wrong — for this chapter it applies to movable property including shares and securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** FMV of shares and securities for the gift provision is computed as per:\nA) Section 145  B) Section 2(22)  C) The SBI TT buying rate  D) Rule 11UA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FMV of shares and securities is computed as per Rule 11UA of the Income Tax Rules, 1962.\n- A) Wrong — Section 145 is the method of accounting.\n- B) Wrong — Section 2(22) defines dividend.\n- C) Wrong — the SBI TT buying rate is for foreign-currency conversion.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Rule 11UA prescribes different methods of computing FMV for:\nA) Quoted and unquoted shares/securities  B) Resident and non-resident donors  C) Cash and cheque gifts  D) Listed and delisted companies' dividends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Rule 11UA prescribes different methods for quoted and unquoted shares and securities.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — the distinction in Rule 11UA is quoted vs unquoted, not these.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Distribution of capital assets on the total or partial partition of an HUF is exempt because it is:\nA) A transaction not regarded as transfer under Section 47(i)  B) A deemed dividend  C) Interest on securities  D) Salary income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Such distribution is a transaction not regarded as transfer under Section 47(i), so income does not arise under the gift provision.\n- B), C), D) Wrong — it is none of these; the exemption flows from Section 47(i).\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Forfeited advance money received in negotiations for transfer of a capital asset, where the transfer did not happen, is taxable under:\nA) IFOS  B) Capital gains  C) PGBP  D) Salaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Advance money received in the course of negotiations for transfer of a capital asset, which has been forfeited where the negotiation does not result in transfer, is always taxable under IFOS.\n- B) Wrong — there was no transfer, so capital gains does not arise.\n- C), D) Wrong — it is neither business income nor salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these is listed as always taxable under IFOS as income arising from securities?\nA) Salary from a securities firm  B) Rent from a house  C) Dividend income from securities  D) Agricultural income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Dividend income from securities is one of the incomes always chargeable under IFOS.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — salary, rent and agricultural income are governed by other heads/exemptions.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A unit-holder receives a specified sum from a business trust (other than interest/dividend from an SPV and rental income from REITs). This is taxable under:\nA) Capital gains  B) IFOS  C) PGBP  D) House property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A specified sum received by a unit-holder from a business trust is one of the items computed under IFOS.\n- A), C), D) Wrong — it is specifically an IFOS item.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Sum received under a Keyman insurance policy is taxable under IFOS only if it is not chargeable under:\nA) Salaries only  B) PGBP only  C) PGBP or Salaries  D) Capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Keyman insurance sum goes to IFOS only if it is not chargeable under \"Profits and gains of business or profession\" or under \"Salaries\" (the \"++\" footnote).\n- A), B) Wrong — both PGBP and Salaries must be ruled out.\n- D) Wrong — capital gains is not the relevant test for Keyman policy.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Rental income of machinery, plant or furniture is taxable under IFOS only if it is not chargeable under:\nA) House property  B) PGBP (business income)  C) Capital gains  D) Salaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rental income of machinery, plant or furniture is IFOS only if it is not chargeable under \"Profits and gains of business or profession\" (the \"**\" footnote).\n- A), C), D) Wrong — the test for this item is whether it is business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which income is taxable as IFOS as a residual item (\"any other income\")?\nA) Income clearly taxable under PGBP  B) Salary income  C) Any income not taxable under any other head and not exempt  D) Exempt agricultural income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — IFOS includes \"any other income not taxable under any other head\" (and not exempt) as a residual item.\n- A), B) Wrong — those belong to specific heads.\n- D) Wrong — exempt income is not charged at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Distribution to shareholders out of accumulated profits on the reduction of the company's capital is:\nA) Always exempt  B) Capital gains only  C) Interest on securities  D) A deemed dividend under Section 2(22)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Distribution out of accumulated profits on reduction of capital is a deemed dividend under Section 2(22)(d).\n- A) Wrong — it is taxable as deemed dividend.\n- B), C) Wrong — it is treated as dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of the following gift-givers' gifts would be TAXABLE (if above ₹50,000)?\nA) Spouse's grandfather  B) Daughter-in-law  C) Cousin  D) Mother's brother (Mama)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A cousin is NOT a relative, so a gift above ₹50,000 from a cousin is taxable.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — spouse's grandfather, daughter-in-law and mother's brother (Mama) are all listed relatives, so their gifts are exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Interest on debentures issued by a company is \"interest on securities\" because it falls under:\nA) Section 2(28B)(a) — government securities  B) Section 2(28B)(b) — debentures/other securities for money issued by a company/local authority/corporation  C) Section 2(22)  D) Rule 11UA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on debentures/other securities for money issued by a local authority, a company, or a statutory corporation is \"interest on securities\" under Section 2(28B)(b).\n- A) Wrong — clause (a) covers government securities.\n- C), D) Wrong — these deal with dividend and FMV respectively.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A gift of securities (FMV ₹2,00,000) is received by a trust created solely for the benefit of relatives of an individual, from that individual. Tax treatment for the trust?\nA) Taxable in full  B) Not chargeable to tax  C) Taxable above ₹50,000  D) Taxable as dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A receipt from an individual by a trust created or established solely for the benefit of a relative of such individual is not chargeable (status-of-donor exception).\n- A), C) Wrong — this is an unconditional exception, not subject to the threshold.\n- D) Wrong — it is not dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Mr. F receives free securities from his sister (FMV ₹40,000) and from a friend (FMV ₹40,000) in the same year. What is taxable?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹40,000 (from the friend)  C) ₹0  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The sister's gift is exempt (relative). Only the friend's gift counts toward the threshold: aggregate from non-relatives = ₹40,000, which is ≤ ₹50,000, so nothing is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — the sister's ₹40,000 is exempt and excluded.\n- B) Wrong — the friend's ₹40,000 alone does not exceed ₹50,000, so it is not taxed.\n- D) Wrong — there is no partial taxation; below the threshold means nil.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. G receives, from non-relatives in one year, free securities of FMV ₹30,000 and ₹25,000. What is the taxable IFOS amount?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹5,000  C) ₹55,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: aggregate FMV of free securities from non-relatives = ₹30,000 + ₹25,000 = ₹55,000. Since ₹55,000 **exceeds** ₹50,000, the **whole** ₹55,000 is taxable (threshold, not deduction).\n- A) Wrong — the aggregate crosses the threshold, so it is not nil.\n- B) Wrong — you do not subtract ₹50,000 (₹55,000 − ₹50,000).\n- D) Wrong — both gifts aggregate; you cannot ignore one.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Mr. H buys securities of FMV ₹1,50,000 for ₹1,10,000. The difference is:\nA) Not taxable  B) Taxable in full at ₹40,000  C) Taxable at ₹50,000  D) Taxable at ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Difference = FMV − Consideration = ₹1,50,000 − ₹1,10,000 = ₹40,000. Since ₹40,000 does **not exceed** ₹50,000, nothing is taxable.\n- B) Wrong — the difference is below the threshold, so it is not taxed.\n- C), D) Wrong — neither figure is correct; the result is nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Mr. I receives two lots of securities for inadequate consideration in the year: Lot 1 FMV ₹90,000 paid ₹60,000; Lot 2 FMV ₹70,000 paid ₹40,000. Taxable IFOS?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹10,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Aggregate difference = (₹90,000 − ₹60,000) + (₹70,000 − ₹40,000) = ₹30,000 + ₹30,000 = ₹60,000. Since ₹60,000 exceeds ₹50,000, the whole ₹60,000 is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — that is only one lot's difference; both aggregate.\n- C) Wrong — you do not subtract ₹50,000.\n- D) Wrong — the aggregate crosses the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Gross interest from securities is ₹1,00,000. Collection charges are ₹4,000 and interest on a loan taken to buy the securities is ₹16,000. Taxable interest from securities is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹96,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹84,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula: Taxable interest = Gross interest − Collection charges − Interest on borrowings − Other related revenue expenditure. = ₹1,00,000 − ₹4,000 − ₹16,000 = ₹80,000.\n- A) Wrong — it ignores both deductions.\n- B) Wrong — it deducts only collection charges.\n- D) Wrong — it deducts only the borrowing interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Gross interest is ₹2,50,000. Permissible deductions: collection charges ₹5,000, borrowing interest ₹45,000, other revenue expenditure ₹10,000. Taxable interest?\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹1,90,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable = ₹2,50,000 − (₹5,000 + ₹45,000 + ₹10,000) = ₹2,50,000 − ₹60,000 = ₹1,90,000.\n- A) Wrong — it ignores all deductions.\n- C) Wrong — it deducts only ₹50,000.\n- D) Wrong — it over-deducts.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Mr. J receives free securities of FMV ₹52,000 from a friend. By how much does the taxable amount exceed the threshold, and what is taxable?\nA) Exceeds by ₹2,000; taxable ₹2,000  B) Exceeds by ₹2,000; taxable ₹52,000  C) Does not exceed; taxable ₹0  D) Taxable ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹52,000 exceeds ₹50,000 by ₹2,000. Because the threshold is crossed, the **whole** ₹52,000 is taxable (not just the ₹2,000 excess). This is the classic \"cliff\" trap.\n- A) Wrong — the excess does not become the taxable amount; the whole sum is taxed.\n- C) Wrong — ₹52,000 does exceed ₹50,000.\n- D) Wrong — ₹50,000 is the threshold, not the taxable figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Mr. K (mercantile system) holds debentures. Interest accrued: ₹6,000 (Feb 2026) and ₹6,000 (May 2026). Both received in June 2026. How much interest is taxable in FY 2025-26?\nA) ₹12,000  B) ₹6,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹3,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the mercantile (accrual) basis, only interest accruing in FY 2025-26 (i.e. the ₹6,000 that accrued in Feb 2026) is taxable that year. The ₹6,000 accruing in May 2026 belongs to FY 2026-27.\n- A) Wrong — the May 2026 accrual is in the next year.\n- C) Wrong — accrual occurred in Feb 2026, so it is taxable then.\n- D) Wrong — the full Feb accrual of ₹6,000 is taxed, not half.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Same facts as Q77 but Mr. K follows the cash system. How much is taxable in FY 2025-26?\nA) ₹12,000  B) ₹6,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹9,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the cash (receipt) basis, interest is taxed when received. Both amounts are received in June 2026 (FY 2026-27), so nothing is taxable in FY 2025-26.\n- A), B) Wrong — receipt, not accrual, governs the cash system; nothing was received in 2025-26.\n- D) Wrong — no amount was received in 2025-26.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A shareholder receives buy-back proceeds of ₹4,00,000 (after 1 Oct 2024); cost of those shares was ₹2,50,000. What amount is taxed as dividend under IFOS?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The full buy-back proceeds of ₹4,00,000 are taxed as dividend; no deduction for expenses (or cost) is allowed against it.\n- A) Wrong — the cost is not netted off the dividend; ₹4,00,000 − ₹2,50,000 = ₹1,50,000 is wrong here.\n- C) Wrong — that is the cost, not the dividend.\n- D) Wrong — the proceeds are fully taxable as dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Continuing Q79, what happens to the ₹2,50,000 cost of acquisition?\nA) Deducted from the ₹4,00,000 dividend  B) Treated as a capital loss eligible for set-off  C) Added to dividend income  D) Carried as a deduction next year against dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cost ₹2,50,000 is treated as a capital loss in the shareholder's hands, eligible for set-off per applicable provisions.\n- A) Wrong — no deduction against dividend is allowed.\n- C), D) Wrong — it is a capital loss, not an addition or a dividend-deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Mr. L receives free securities FMV ₹48,000 from a friend in Year 1, and ₹49,000 free from another friend in Year 2. Taxable in each year?\nA) ₹0 in both years  B) ₹48,000 and ₹49,000  C) ₹0 and ₹49,000  D) ₹97,000 in Year 2\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The ₹50,000 threshold is applied to the aggregate received **during each year** separately. Year 1: ₹48,000 ≤ ₹50,000 → nil. Year 2: ₹49,000 ≤ ₹50,000 → nil.\n- B) Wrong — both years are below the threshold, so nothing is taxed.\n- C) Wrong — Year 2 is also below the threshold.\n- D) Wrong — amounts from different years are not aggregated together.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Ms. M receives free securities of FMV ₹35,000 from her cousin and ₹20,000 from a friend in one year. Taxable amount?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹55,000  D) ₹5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A cousin is NOT a relative, so both gifts count. Aggregate from non-relatives = ₹35,000 + ₹20,000 = ₹55,000, which exceeds ₹50,000, so the whole ₹55,000 is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — cousin's gift is not exempt.\n- B) Wrong — both gifts aggregate.\n- D) Wrong — you do not subtract the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Ms. N receives free securities of FMV ₹2,00,000 from her father and ₹45,000 from a colleague in one year. Taxable?\nA) ₹2,45,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹45,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Father is a relative → his ₹2,00,000 gift is exempt and excluded. The colleague's ₹45,000 is the only countable amount; since ₹45,000 ≤ ₹50,000, nothing is taxable.\n- A), B) Wrong — the father's gift is exempt.\n- C) Wrong — ₹45,000 alone does not exceed the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A shareholder in a foreign currency receives interest of USD 1,000 on a security, due in May. No TDS applies. At what date's SBI TT buying rate is it converted?\nA) Last day of May  B) Last day of April  C) First day of May  D) Date of receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Conversion uses the SBI TT buying rate on the last day of the month **immediately preceding** the month income is due. Income is due in May, so the rate as on the last day of April is used.\n- A) Wrong — May is the month income is due, not the preceding month.\n- C), D) Wrong — neither the first day of May nor receipt date is the prescribed date for the non-TDS case.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Same as Q84 but the income IS subject to TDS. Which date's rate applies?\nA) Last day of April  B) The date on which tax is required to be deducted  C) Last day of May  D) 31 March\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the foreign-currency income is subject to TDS, conversion is done at the rate on the date on which tax is required to be deducted.\n- A) Wrong — that is the non-TDS rule.\n- C), D) Wrong — these are not the prescribed TDS-conversion dates.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Mr. O receives free securities of aggregate FMV exactly ₹50,000 from a friend. Taxable amount?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax arises only if the aggregate **exceeds** ₹50,000. Exactly ₹50,000 does not exceed ₹50,000, so nothing is taxable.\n- B) Wrong — the threshold is not crossed at exactly ₹50,000.\n- C), D) Wrong — there is no partial taxation; here it is nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Mr. P buys securities of FMV ₹1,00,000 for ₹49,000 from a friend. Taxable difference?\nA) ₹49,000  B) ₹51,000  C) ₹1,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Difference = FMV − Consideration = ₹1,00,000 − ₹49,000 = ₹51,000. Since ₹51,000 exceeds ₹50,000, the whole ₹51,000 is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — ₹49,000 is the price paid, not the taxable difference.\n- C) Wrong — you do not subtract the ₹50,000 threshold from ₹51,000.\n- D) Wrong — the difference crosses the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Gross interest from securities is ₹60,000 and the only related expense is collection charges of ₹62,000 (an unusual loss year). The other-sources income from this security is:\nA) ₹60,000  B) Negative ₹2,000 (a loss of ₹2,000)  C) ₹0 always  D) ₹62,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable interest = Gross interest − permissible deductions = ₹60,000 − ₹62,000 = −₹2,000, i.e. a computed loss of ₹2,000 from this source.\n- A) Wrong — it ignores the deduction.\n- C) Wrong — the computation does not force zero; it yields the net of income minus deductions.\n- D) Wrong — ₹62,000 is the expense, not the income.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** In one year, Ms. Q receives: free securities FMV ₹30,000 from a friend; securities FMV ₹80,000 bought for ₹70,000 from another friend. Total taxable IFOS from gifts?\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Aggregate benefit = (free ₹30,000) + (FMV − consideration: ₹80,000 − ₹70,000 = ₹10,000) = ₹40,000. Since ₹40,000 does **not exceed** ₹50,000, nothing is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — ₹40,000 is the aggregate benefit, but it is below the threshold so it is not taxed.\n- C), D) Wrong — these are individual components, not the taxable result; the result is nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A closely held company advances ₹2,00,000 to a shareholder out of accumulated profits of ₹5,00,000. The deemed dividend under Section 2(22)(e) is generally limited to:\nA) The advance, to the extent of accumulated profits (here ₹2,00,000)  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹7,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The loan/advance is a deemed dividend out of accumulated profits; here the advance of ₹2,00,000 is fully covered by accumulated profits of ₹5,00,000, so ₹2,00,000 is the deemed dividend.\n- B) Wrong — accumulated profits cap the amount but the advance is only ₹2,00,000.\n- C) Wrong — it is a deemed dividend, not nil.\n- D) Wrong — you do not add advance and accumulated profits.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Ms. R receives free securities FMV ₹55,000 from a friend, but ₹10,000 of those shares are received \"on the occasion of her marriage.\" How much is taxable?\nA) ₹55,000  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Receipts on the occasion of marriage are excluded. After removing the ₹10,000 marriage-occasion gift, the countable aggregate = ₹55,000 − ₹10,000 = ₹45,000. Since ₹45,000 ≤ ₹50,000, nothing is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — the marriage-occasion portion is excluded.\n- B) Wrong — ₹45,000 is the countable aggregate but it is below the threshold, so nil is taxed.\n- D) Wrong — there is no partial taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Gross interest ₹3,00,000; deductions: collection charges ₹6,000, borrowing interest ₹1,20,000, other revenue expenses ₹14,000. Net taxable interest?\nA) ₹1,60,000  B) ₹1,80,000  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹1,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total deductions = ₹6,000 + ₹1,20,000 + ₹14,000 = ₹1,40,000. Taxable = ₹3,00,000 − ₹1,40,000 = ₹1,60,000.\n- B) Wrong — that wrongly deducts ₹1,20,000 only.\n- C) Wrong — ignores deductions.\n- D) Wrong — that is the deduction total, not the net income.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Mr. S receives free securities FMV ₹1,20,000 under his late uncle's will. Taxable amount?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹70,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Property received under a will is not chargeable under this provision, regardless of value, so the taxable amount is ₹0.\n- A), D) Wrong — the will exception is unconditional, so no part is taxed.\n- B) Wrong — there is no \"excess over ₹50,000\" calculation for will/inheritance receipts.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Mr. T (cash system) has debenture interest received as: ₹4,000 in FY 2025-26 (which had accrued in FY 2024-25) and ₹4,000 accruing AND received in FY 2025-26. Taxable in FY 2025-26?\nA) ₹4,000  B) ₹8,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the cash system, all interest **received** during the year is taxable that year, regardless of when it accrued. Received in FY 2025-26 = ₹4,000 + ₹4,000 = ₹8,000.\n- A) Wrong — both receipts count, not just one.\n- C) Wrong — both were received in 2025-26.\n- D) Wrong — only ₹8,000 was received that year.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Mr. U receives ₹60,000 free securities from a non-relative and separately receives a ₹5,00,000 loan from a closely held company in which he is a shareholder (out of accumulated profits). Identify the heads.\nA) Both under capital gains  B) ₹60,000 gift taxable as IFOS, and the ₹5,00,000 deemed dividend also under IFOS  C) Gift under IFOS; loan exempt  D) Both exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The ₹60,000 free securities (exceeding ₹50,000) are taxable as IFOS; the ₹5,00,000 loan from a closely held company out of accumulated profits is a deemed dividend, also taxed under IFOS.\n- A) Wrong — neither is a capital gain.\n- C) Wrong — the loan is a deemed dividend, not exempt.\n- D) Wrong — both are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Mr. V buys unquoted shares of FMV ₹3,00,000 (computed under Rule 11UA) for ₹2,30,000 from a friend. Taxable IFOS?\nA) ₹70,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FMV is determined under Rule 11UA (unquoted method). Difference = ₹3,00,000 − ₹2,30,000 = ₹70,000, which exceeds ₹50,000, so the whole ₹70,000 is taxable.\n- B) Wrong — you do not subtract the ₹50,000 threshold.\n- C) Wrong — the difference exceeds the threshold.\n- D) Wrong — only the FMV-minus-consideration difference is taxed, not the full FMV (since consideration was paid).\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** In a year, Mr. W has: dividend ₹40,000 (TDS deducted under s.194), interest on government securities ₹25,000 (investment), and a free gift of securities FMV ₹30,000 from a friend. Which amounts are IFOS, and is the gift taxable?\nA) Only dividend is IFOS; gift taxable  B) Dividend ₹40,000 and interest ₹25,000 are IFOS; gift of ₹30,000 not taxable  C) All three including the gift fully taxable  D) Nothing is IFOS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividend (₹40,000) and interest on investment securities (₹25,000) are both IFOS. The free gift of ₹30,000 is from a non-relative but ₹30,000 ≤ ₹50,000, so the gift is not taxable.\n- A) Wrong — interest on securities is also IFOS, and the ₹30,000 gift is below the threshold.\n- C) Wrong — the gift is below the ₹50,000 threshold, so not taxable.\n- D) Wrong — dividend and interest are clearly IFOS.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Mr. X receives, in one year from non-relatives: free securities FMV ₹20,000; securities FMV ₹90,000 bought for ₹50,000. Total taxable IFOS from gifts?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Aggregate benefit = (free ₹20,000) + (FMV − consideration: ₹90,000 − ₹50,000 = ₹40,000) = ₹60,000. Since ₹60,000 exceeds ₹50,000, the whole ₹60,000 is taxable.\n- A) Wrong — the aggregate crosses the threshold.\n- B) Wrong — that ignores the ₹20,000 free gift.\n- D) Wrong — that ignores the inadequate-consideration difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A non-resident receives ₹1,00,000 dividend from an Indian company. Which statement is most accurate?\nA) It is exempt because DDT was paid  B) It is taxable in his hands under IFOS, with DTAA/MLI provisions also applying  C) It is taxed only in his home country  D) It is taxed as capital gains in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Post-DDT-abolition, dividend is taxable in the recipient's hands under IFOS; for a non-resident, DTAA and MLI provisions also come into play.\n- A) Wrong — DDT no longer applies from AY 2021-22.\n- C) Wrong — India can tax it (subject to DTAA), it is not solely home-country taxed.\n- D) Wrong — it is dividend (IFOS), not capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Mr. Y receives free securities FMV ₹55,000 from his mother's sister (Mausi) and ₹20,000 free from a friend in one year. Taxable IFOS?\nA) ₹75,000  B) ₹55,000  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Mother's sister (Mausi) is a listed relative, so her ₹55,000 gift is exempt and excluded. The countable aggregate from non-relatives = ₹20,000 (friend), which is ≤ ₹50,000, so nothing is taxable.\n- A), B) Wrong — the Mausi gift is exempt.\n- C) Wrong — the ₹20,000 friend gift alone does not exceed the threshold.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Arjun, a resident individual following the **mercantile (accrual) system**, has the following transactions during the financial year:\n1. He receives **dividend of ₹60,000** from an Indian listed company (TDS deducted under Section 194).\n2. He earns **interest of ₹1,00,000** on debentures held as an **investment**; he paid **collection charges of ₹3,000** and **₹22,000 interest** on a loan taken specifically to buy those debentures.\n3. He receives **free securities of FMV ₹40,000 from his brother** and **free securities of FMV ₹65,000 from a close friend**, both in the same year.\n4. He buys **unquoted shares of FMV ₹2,80,000 (per Rule 11UA) for ₹2,10,000** from another friend.\n5. On **20 October 2024**, he receives **buy-back proceeds of ₹5,00,000**; his cost of those shares was ₹3,20,000.\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-1: Under which head is the ₹60,000 dividend taxable, and at what rate?</summary>\n\n**Answer:** It is taxable under **Income from Other Sources** at Mr. Arjun's **applicable slab rate**.\n**Why:** After DDT was abolished (Finance Act, 2020; effective AY 2021-22), dividend is taxable in the shareholder's hands under IFOS at the shareholder's slab rate. TDS under Section 194 is only a collection mechanism; the income still enters IFOS and is taxed at slab rates (the TDS is adjusted against final liability).\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-2: Compute the taxable interest on debentures.</summary>\n\n**Answer:** ₹75,000.\n**Formula:** Taxable interest = Gross interest − Collection charges − Interest on borrowings to buy the securities − Other related revenue expenditure.\n**Calculation:** ₹1,00,000 − ₹3,000 − ₹22,000 = **₹75,000**.\n**Logic:** Collection charges and interest on a loan taken specifically to buy the securities are both permissible deductions against interest on securities. Since the debentures are held as an investment (not stock-in-trade), the interest is IFOS. Under the mercantile system it is taxed on accrual.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-3: How much of the gifts in point 3 is taxable?</summary>\n\n**Answer:** ₹65,000 is taxable.\n**Working:** The brother is a **relative**, so his ₹40,000 gift is **exempt** and excluded entirely. Only the friend's gift counts: aggregate from non-relatives = **₹65,000**. Since ₹65,000 **exceeds** ₹50,000, the **whole ₹65,000** is taxable (the ₹50,000 is a threshold, not a deduction).\n**Intuition:** Cross the ₹50,000 line and the entire amount falls in — there is no \"first ₹50,000 free.\"\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-4: What is the taxable amount on the unquoted shares bought for inadequate consideration (point 4)?</summary>\n\n**Answer:** ₹70,000.\n**Formula:** Taxable = FMV (per Rule 11UA) − Consideration paid, if the aggregate difference exceeds ₹50,000.\n**Calculation:** ₹2,80,000 − ₹2,10,000 = **₹70,000**. Since ₹70,000 > ₹50,000, the whole ₹70,000 is taxable as IFOS.\n**Note:** FMV of unquoted shares is determined under Rule 11UA's prescribed method for unquoted shares/securities.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-5: How is the ₹5,00,000 buy-back (point 5) taxed, and what happens to the ₹3,20,000 cost?</summary>\n\n**Answer:** The **full ₹5,00,000** is taxed as **dividend under IFOS** (buy-back on 20 October 2024 is after the 1 October 2024 cut-off). **No deduction for expenses** is allowed against it. Separately, the **₹3,20,000 cost** of the bought-back shares is treated as a **capital loss** in Mr. Arjun's hands, eligible for set-off as per applicable provisions.\n**Intuition:** Buy-back now has two effects — the proceeds are fully taxed as dividend (gross, no netting of cost), while your original cost becomes a capital loss you can set off against capital gains elsewhere.\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "10", "title": "Taxation of Debt Products", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 10: Taxation of Debt Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The two ways money comes from debt products: **interest income** and **capital gains**, and how each is taxed.\n- The main debt products: coupon bonds, government securities (G-Secs, T-Bills, SDLs), tax-free bonds, market linked debentures (MLDs).\n- How mutual funds are taxed after the big post-2023 rule changes (the four new categories).\n- How Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) are taxed, including DTAA relief.\n\n> Think of a **debt instrument** as you lending money to a company or government. You don't own a piece of them (no voting, no share of profit), but they promise to (a) pay you **interest** on the loan and (b) return your money (**principal/redemption value**) at the end.\n\n---\n\n## 10.1 The two sources of income from debt\n\nWhen you hold a debt product, money reaches you in two flavours:\n\n| Source | What it is | Taxed under the head |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interest income** | The periodic payment for lending (the \"coupon\") | **Income from Other Sources** (unless it's your business) |\n| **Capital gains** | Profit/loss when you sell or redeem the instrument | **Capital Gains** |\n\n### Interest income — accrual vs receipt (Section 145)\nIncome from interest is computed using the **method of accounting** you regularly follow:\n- **Mercantile (accrual) system** → interest is taxed when it *becomes due* (accrues), even if not yet received.\n- **Cash system** → interest is taxed only when *actually received*.\n\n**Analogy:** Mercantile is like marking a chore \"done\" the moment it's owed to you; cash is marking it only when the cash lands in your pocket.\n\n💡 You can deduct expenses spent *wholly and exclusively* to earn the interest (e.g., a banker's commission to collect it).\n\n⚠️ Some special bonds are taxed at a **concessional (lower) rate** — but then **no deductions at all** (not even Sections 80C–80U) are allowed against that interest.\n\n### Capital gains basics\nGain/loss on transfer or redemption of a security is a **capital gain** if you held it as a **capital asset** (i.e., as an investment).\n- **FPIs (Foreign Portfolio Investors):** their securities are **always** capital assets → always taxed as capital gains.\n- Tax depends on: **nature of security, period of holding, residential status**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.2 Types of Debt Products\n\n### 10.2.1 Coupon Bonds\nA bond pays a fixed **coupon** (interest) and returns **face value** at maturity. Example: *7.4% CG Bond 2027* = a Central Government bond maturing 2027 paying 7.40%.\n\n**Market value** = present value of all future cash flows. The discount rate used is the **Yield-to-Maturity (YTM)**.\n\n#### Tax on bond interest\nTaxed under **Other Sources** at **normal slab rates**. Deduct expenses wholly/exclusively for earning it.\n\n**💡 Worked example (mercantile vs cash):**\nMr. A buys bonds, 7.5% coupon, interest paid half-yearly (Jun 30 & Dec 31).\n- Under **mercantile**, interest *accrues* each period (so April–June accrues into 2024-25 even if paid later).\n- Under **cash**, you count the full amount only when received.\n\n#### Tax on capital gains from bonds — holding period & rates (Table 10.1)\n\n| Bond type | Period to be **long-term** | Long-term rate | Short-term rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Listed** bonds/debentures | **12 months** | **12.50%** (no indexation) | applicable slab rates |\n| **Unlisted** bonds/debentures **or any Market Linked Debenture (even if listed)** | Not applicable | — | **Always short-term**, taxed at applicable rates **regardless of holding period** |\n\n**LTCG computation (listed bonds):**\nFull value of consideration − Cost of acquisition − Transfer expenses − exemptions (Sec 54EE–54F) = LTCG.\n\n**💡 Worked example (listed, LTCG):** 400 listed bonds bought @₹1,200 (01-01-2016), sold @₹2,000 (31-07-2024).\n- Held 8 yr 7 mth (>12 mth) → **long-term**.\n- Sale 400×2,000 = ₹8,00,000; Cost 400×1,200 = ₹4,80,000 → **LTCG ₹3,20,000 @ 12.50%**.\n\n**⚠️ Same bonds but UNLISTED:** the ₹3,20,000 is taxed as **short-term capital gain at normal slab rates** — unlisted bonds are *always* short-term, no matter how long held.\n\n**💡 Worked example (short-term, interest stripped out):** 9% listed bond, face ₹10,000, bought @₹10,500 (01-04-2024), sold 01-11-2024 for ₹13,000 *inclusive of accrued interest*.\n- Interest accrued to 31-10 = ₹10,000 × 9% × 1/12 = ₹75.\n- Adjusted sale price = ₹13,000 − ₹75 = ₹12,925.\n- STCG = ₹12,925 − ₹10,500 = **₹2,425** (taxed at applicable rate). The ₹75 is taxed separately as interest.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Accrued-but-unreceived interest baked into the sale price must be *carved out* and taxed as interest, NOT as capital gain.\n\n#### Non-residents — LTCG on bonds (Table 10.3)\n| Section | Who | What | Rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **115E** | Non-Resident Indian | LTCG on Govt securities or listed debentures of an Indian public company **bought in foreign currency** | **12.50%** (no indexation/forex benefit) |\n| **112(1)(c)(iii)** | Non-resident | LTCG on any unlisted security (other than bond/debenture) | **12.50%** (no indexation/forex benefit) |\n\nFor listed bonds, a non-resident's LTCG is **always 12.50%, no indexation**.\n\n### 10.2.2 Government Securities (G-Secs)\nA **G-Sec** is a tradable instrument acknowledging the Government's debt — the **safest** investment (\"risk-free gilt-edged\"). Types:\n\n| Type | Tenure | Interest? | How return arises |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Cash Management Bills (CMBs)** | < 91 days | No | Bought at discount, redeemed at face value |\n| **Treasury Bills (T-Bills)** | 91 / 182 / 364 days (≤1 yr) | No | Discount to face value |\n| **Dated G-Secs** | 5–40 yrs | Yes (half-yearly) | Coupon + principal at maturity |\n| **State Development Loans (SDLs)** | long | Yes (half-yearly) | Issued by State Governments |\n\n**Taxability:**\n- **CMBs & T-Bills:** no interest; profit on redemption/transfer is **short-term capital gain** at applicable rates (since maturity <1 year).\n- **Dated G-Secs & SDLs:** taxed **like listed bonds** (12.50% LTCG if held >12 months and listed).\n\n💡 **No TDS** under Section 193 on interest paid to residents on Central/State Govt securities — **except** 8% Savings (Taxable) Bonds 2003 and 7.75% Savings (Taxable) Bonds 2018, where TDS applies only if interest in the year exceeds **₹10,000**.\n\n### 10.2.3 Tax-Free Bonds\n- **Interest is fully exempt** under Section 10 (e.g., NABARD bonds).\n- **Capital gains on transfer/redemption are STILL taxable** — same as coupon bonds.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 5,000 tax-free NABARD bonds bought @₹120 (listed), redeemed @₹150.\n- Interest = exempt (no tax).\n- LTCG = (150−120)×5,000 = **₹1,50,000 @ 12.50%** (held >12 months, listed).\n\n---\n\n## 10.3 Mutual Funds\n\nA **mutual fund** pools investors' money and invests it. Types: equity-oriented, debt-oriented, money market/liquid, balanced/hybrid, gold/silver, international, fund-of-funds. Any of these can be listed as an **ETF** (bought/sold on the exchange).\n\n### The BIG change: four tax categories from FY 2023-24\nUntil FY 2022-23 there were just **two** buckets (equity-oriented & others). Now there are **four**:\n\n| # | Category | Defining test |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Equity-Oriented Funds** | (Covered in Chapter 11) |\n| 2 | **Specified Mutual Fund (SMF) Type 1** | ≥ **65%** in debt/money-market (directly or indirectly), bought **after 01-04-2023** |\n| 3 | **Specified Mutual Fund (SMF) Type 2** | Other funds that invested **< 35%** in Indian equity, bought **after 01-04-2023 but sold before 01-04-2025** (debt-hybrids, gold/silver, international, FoF) |\n| 4 | **Other Mutual Funds** | Everything else |\n\n### 10.3.3 Dividend from mutual funds (IDCW)\n- **Resident:** taxed at slab rates. Can deduct **interest expense up to 20% of dividend**. No other expense allowed.\n- **Non-resident:** taxed at flat **20%** (subject to DTAA), **no deductions at all** (not even Chapter VIA / 80C–80U).\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Mr. A (resident) gets ₹5,00,000 dividend, paid ₹1,50,000 interest on the borrowed money.\n- Max deductible interest = 20% × 5,00,000 = ₹1,00,000.\n- Taxable dividend = 5,00,000 − 1,00,000 = **₹4,00,000** at slab rates.\n- If he were **non-resident**: full **₹5,00,000 taxed @20%**, no deduction.\n\n### 10.3.4 / 10.3.5 Capital gains on SMF Type 1 & Type 2 — Section 50AA\n**Rule:** Gains on SMFs are **ALWAYS short-term, regardless of holding period**, taxed at normal slab rates.\n- Section 50AA applies only to SMFs **acquired on or after 01-04-2023**. SMFs bought on/before 31-03-2023 follow normal mutual-fund rules.\n- STCG = Full value − cost of acquisition − transfer expenses.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** A debt fund you've held 6 years still gives you *short-term* gains if it's a post-April-2023 SMF. No long-term benefit, no 12.5% rate.\n\n### 10.3.6 Capital gains on Other Mutual Funds\n| | Listed | Unlisted |\n|---|---|---|\n| Period to be **long-term** | **12 months** | **24 months** |\n| **LTCG rate** | **12.50%** | **12.50%** |\n| **STCG** | applicable slab rate | applicable slab rate |\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 1,000 units of a debt-oriented MF bought @₹150 (01-01-2017, *pre-April-2023, so an \"Other MF\"*), sold @₹300 (15-03-2025). Held 8+ years (>24 months) → **LTCG = ₹1,50,000 @ 12.50%**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.4 Taxation of Non-Residents\n\nA non-resident risks **double taxation** (taxed in India *and* home country). Solution: **DTAA (Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement)**.\n- Where eligible, a non-resident pays tax at the rate in the **DTAA or the Income Tax Act, whichever is more beneficial**.\n- **Example:** Mr. A (NR, US resident) earns ₹10,00,000 interest. India-USA DTAA Article 11 caps it at **15%**, and he gets US tax credit for Indian tax paid.\n- **Dividends:** Act rate = 20%. If India-USA DTAA = 25%, he picks the lower Act rate (20%). If he's a UK resident, India-UK DTAA = 10% → picks 10%.\n\n📄 To claim DTAA: need a **Tax Residency Certificate (TRC)** + **Form 10F** (self-declaration).\n\n### Chapter XII-A (concessional regime for NRIs of Indian origin)\n- For **Indian citizens / Persons of Indian Origin** who are non-residents, investing **convertible foreign exchange** in Indian shares, debentures/deposits (in non-private cos.), certain Govt securities.\n- **No indexation.** Investment income capped at **20%**; LTCG at **12.50%**.\n- **Reinvestment exemption:** if LTCG is reinvested within **6 months** in eligible assets → exempt (fully if all net consideration reinvested, proportionately if partial). But if the new asset is sold within **3 years**, the earlier-exempted gain is taxed in that year.\n\n### Section 89A — overseas retirement funds\nFor residents who once worked abroad: income from **specified retirement accounts** in **USA, Canada, UK** is taxed **on withdrawal**, not on accrual — removing a double-tax mismatch.\n\n---\n\n## 10.5 Market Linked Debentures (MLDs)\nAn **MLD** is a debenture whose return is tied to a market index/security, not a fixed coupon. Two types: **Principal Protected** and **Principal Non-Protected**. Issued by companies with net worth ≥ ₹100 crore; tenure usually 12–36 months; returns paid at maturity.\n\n**Tax (Section 50AA, from FY 2023-24):** Gains on MLDs **transferred/redeemed/matured on or after 01-04-2023** are **ALWAYS short-term**, taxed at slab rates — even if the MLD was bought earlier and held for years.\n\n## 10.6 Benefits denied under Section 50AA (MLDs + SMF Type 1 & 2)\n- **No benefit of holding period** → always short-term.\n- **No cost of improvement** → taken as **nil**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.7 Summary of Taxation of Debt Products *(sale after 23 July 2024)*\n\n| Product | LT period (months) | STCG (resident) | LTCG (resident) | LTCG (non-resident) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sovereign Gold Bond (listed)* | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Any other listed Bond | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Any unlisted Bond | N/A | Normal rate | Always STCG (normal) | Always STCG (normal) |\n| T-Bills | — | Normal rate | — | — |\n| Dated G-Secs (listed) | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Dated G-Secs (unlisted) | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Municipal debt (listed) | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Debentures (listed) | 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n| Debentures (unlisted) | N/A | Normal rate | Normal rate | Normal rate |\n| **MLDs** | — | **Always STCG (normal)** | — | — |\n| **SMF Type 1** | — | **Always STCG (normal)** | — | — |\n| **SMF Type 2** | — | **Always STCG (normal)** | — | — |\n| Other Mutual Funds | 24 / 12 | Normal rate | 12.50% | 12.50% |\n\n*Note 1:* Capital gain to an **Individual on redemption** of an SGB is **NOT taxed** (Section 47). Rates above are only for sale **other than redemption**.\n*Note 2:* Resident gets 20%-of-dividend interest deduction; non-resident gets none.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Interest** = Other Sources, slab rate (accrual or cash per your accounting method).\n- **Capital gain** = depends on listed/unlisted + holding period + residential status.\n- **Listed bonds/G-Secs/SGB:** LTCG (>12 mth) @ **12.50%**, no indexation.\n- **Unlisted bonds:** **always short-term** (slab rate).\n- **MLDs and SMF Type 1 & 2 (Section 50AA):** **always short-term**, no holding-period or cost-of-improvement benefit.\n- **Other (debt) MFs:** listed long-term at >12 mth, unlisted at >24 mth; LTCG @12.50%.\n- **Dividend from MF:** resident slab (20% interest deduction); non-resident flat 20%, no deduction.\n- **NRIs:** DTAA or Act, whichever lower; need TRC + Form 10F.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term/Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **12.50%** | LTCG rate on bonds/G-Secs/SGB/Other-MF (no indexation) |\n| **12 months** | Long-term threshold for listed debt; listed Other MF |\n| **24 months** | Long-term threshold for unlisted Other MF |\n| **Section 145** | Choose accrual (mercantile) or cash accounting for interest |\n| **Section 50AA** | MLDs + SMF Type 1/Type 2 → always STCG |\n| **Section 10** | Exempts interest on tax-free bonds; exempts certain interest |\n| **Section 47** | Redemption of SGB by individual ≠ transfer (no tax) |\n| **Section 115E / 112(1)(c)(iii)** | NR LTCG @12.50%, no indexation/forex |\n| **20% of dividend** | Cap on interest-expense deduction (residents only) |\n| **₹10,000** | TDS threshold on 8%/7.75% Savings Bonds interest |\n| **DTAA / TRC / Form 10F** | Treaty relief + the documents needed to claim it |\n| **Chapter XII-A** | NRI concessional regime: 20% investment income, 12.5% LTCG, no indexation, reinvestment exemption |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 10: Taxation of Debt Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What are the two types of income earned from debt products, and under which heads are they taxed?\n> **A:** (1) **Interest income** — taxed under *Income from Other Sources* (unless business). (2) **Capital gains** on transfer/redemption — taxed under *Capital Gains*.\n\n**Q2.** Under Section 145, what decides whether interest is taxed on accrual or receipt?\n> **A:** The **method of accounting regularly employed**: mercantile (accrual) system → taxed when due; cash system → taxed when received.\n\n**Q3.** Are securities held by FPIs treated as capital assets or stock-in-trade?\n> **A:** **Always capital assets** — so FPI gains are always taxed as capital gains.\n\n**Q4.** What is the LTCG rate on a listed bond and the holding period to qualify?\n> **A:** **12.50%** (no indexation), if held **more than 12 months**.\n\n**Q5.** How are gains on an UNLISTED bond/debenture taxed?\n> **A:** **Always short-term capital gains at normal slab rates**, regardless of holding period.\n\n**Q6.** A bond is sold for ₹13,000 including ₹75 accrued interest. How do you compute capital gain?\n> **A:** Subtract the ₹75 (taxed separately as interest) from the sale price first → adjusted consideration ₹12,925, then deduct cost. Interest is never taxed as capital gain.\n\n**Q7.** What is Yield-to-Maturity (YTM)?\n> **A:** The discount rate at which the present value of a bond's future cash flows equals its market price.\n\n**Q8.** Name the four types of Government Securities.\n> **A:** Cash Management Bills (CMBs), Treasury Bills (T-Bills), Dated G-Secs, and State Development Loans (SDLs).\n\n**Q9.** How are T-Bills and CMBs taxed?\n> **A:** They carry no interest; the discount earned on redemption/transfer is a **short-term capital gain** at applicable rates (maturity < 1 year).\n\n**Q10.** How are Dated G-Secs and SDLs taxed?\n> **A:** Same as **listed bonds** — LTCG @12.50% if held >12 months and listed.\n\n**Q11.** When does TDS apply on interest from Government securities to a resident?\n> **A:** Generally no TDS under Section 193 — **except** 8% Savings (Taxable) Bonds 2003 and 7.75% Savings (Taxable) Bonds 2018, and only if interest in the year exceeds **₹10,000**.\n\n**Q12.** For tax-free bonds, what is taxable and what is exempt?\n> **A:** **Interest is exempt** (Section 10); **capital gains on transfer/redemption are taxable** (same as coupon bonds).\n\n**Q13.** How many mutual fund tax categories exist from FY 2023-24, and name them.\n> **A:** **Four:** (1) Equity-Oriented, (2) SMF Type 1, (3) SMF Type 2, (4) Other Mutual Funds.\n\n**Q14.** Define a Specified Mutual Fund Type 1.\n> **A:** A fund investing **≥65%** in debt/money-market instruments (directly or indirectly), **bought on/after 01-04-2023**.\n\n**Q15.** Define a Specified Mutual Fund Type 2.\n> **A:** A fund (not Type 1) that invested **<35%** in Indian equity, bought **on/after 01-04-2023 but sold before 01-04-2025** (e.g. debt-hybrids, gold/silver, international, FoF).\n\n**Q16.** How are capital gains on SMF Type 1 and Type 2 taxed (Section 50AA)?\n> **A:** **Always short-term, regardless of holding period**, at normal slab rates.\n\n**Q17.** How is dividend from a mutual fund taxed for a resident vs a non-resident?\n> **A:** **Resident:** slab rates, with interest-expense deduction capped at **20% of dividend**. **Non-resident:** flat **20%** (subject to DTAA), **no deduction at all**.\n\n**Q18.** A resident gets ₹5,00,000 MF dividend and paid ₹1,50,000 interest to invest. Taxable dividend?\n> **A:** Deduction capped at 20% × 5,00,000 = ₹1,00,000 → taxable **₹4,00,000** at slab rates.\n\n**Q19.** What are the long-term holding periods for \"Other Mutual Funds\" — listed vs unlisted?\n> **A:** **Listed: 12 months; Unlisted: 24 months.** LTCG @12.50% either way.\n\n**Q20.** What is an MLD and how is it taxed from FY 2023-24?\n> **A:** A **Market Linked Debenture** — return tied to a market index/security. Under Section 50AA, gains on/after 01-04-2023 are **always short-term** at slab rates, even if acquired earlier.\n\n**Q21.** Two benefits denied under Section 50AA?\n> **A:** (1) No benefit of holding period (always STCG); (2) **Cost of improvement taken as nil**.\n\n**Q22.** What is a DTAA and how does it help a non-resident?\n> **A:** A **Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement** — the NR pays tax at the DTAA rate or Income Tax Act rate, **whichever is more beneficial**, avoiding double taxation.\n\n**Q23.** What documents must a non-resident furnish to claim DTAA benefits?\n> **A:** A **Tax Residency Certificate (TRC)** and **Form 10F** (self-declaration).\n\n**Q24.** Under India-USA DTAA, at what rate is interest income taxed (per the workbook example)?\n> **A:** A concessional **15%** (Article 11), with foreign tax credit in the USA.\n\n**Q25.** Under Chapter XII-A, what are the tax caps for an eligible NRI?\n> **A:** Investment income capped at **20%**, LTCG at **12.50%**, **no indexation**.\n\n**Q26.** Under Chapter XII-A, what happens if LTCG is reinvested within 6 months?\n> **A:** It becomes **exempt** (fully if all net consideration reinvested, proportionately if partial). But if the new asset is sold within **3 years**, the exempted gain is taxed then.\n\n**Q27.** What does Section 89A address?\n> **A:** Income from **overseas retirement accounts** (USA, Canada, UK) is taxed **on withdrawal**, not on accrual — removing a double-tax mismatch.\n\n**Q28.** Is redemption of a Sovereign Gold Bond by an individual taxable?\n> **A:** **No** — Section 47 says redemption by an individual is not a \"transfer,\" so no capital gain arises (interest, however, is taxable).\n\n**Q29.** Can grandfathering or indexation reduce LTCG on debt products under the post-2023 rules?\n> **A:** No — LTCG on bonds/G-Secs is taxed at 12.50% **without indexation**.\n\n**Q30.** What is the difference between a Principal Protected and Principal Non-Protected MLD?\n> **A:** Principal Protected guarantees return of the principal at maturity; Non-Protected does not — the principal itself can be lost depending on the underlying's performance.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 10: Taxation of Debt Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario) — followed by a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking** (a wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark), so do not guess blindly.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A person holding a debt instrument of an entity is entitled to which of the following?\nA) Voting power in the entity  B) A share of the entity's dividend  C) Interest and the redemption value at maturity  D) Ownership of the entity's assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A debt instrument is like a loan to the issuer; the holder gets periodic **interest** plus the **redemption (principal) value** at maturity.\n- A) Wrong — voting power belongs to shareholders (equity holders), not lenders.\n- B) Wrong — dividends are paid to shareholders; lenders get interest instead.\n- D) Wrong — lenders do not own the company's assets; they only have a claim to be repaid.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Periodic income earned from debt instruments is classified as:\nA) Capital gains  B) Dividend income  C) Business turnover  D) Interest income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook states periodic income from debt is \"interest income\"; the gain/loss on transfer or redemption is \"capital gains\".\n- A) Wrong — capital gains arise only on transfer/redemption, not on periodic receipts.\n- B) Wrong — dividends come from shares (equity), not debt.\n- C) Wrong — \"turnover\" is a business-sales concept, unrelated to periodic debt income.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Interest on securities, if not taxable as business income, is taxed under which head?\nA) Salaries  B) Income from Other Sources  C) Capital Gains  D) House Property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on securities is taxable under \"**Income from Other Sources**\" unless it forms part of business income.\n- A) Wrong — salary head is for employment income.\n- C) Wrong — capital gains head covers transfer/redemption gains, not interest.\n- D) Wrong — house property head is for rental income from property.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under Section 145, which two methods of accounting are allowed for computing interest income?\nA) Mercantile and cash systems  B) FIFO and LIFO  C) Accrual and amortised  D) Straight-line and reducing balance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 145 allows the **mercantile (accrual)** system and the **cash** system, whichever the assessee regularly employs.\n- B) Wrong — FIFO/LIFO are inventory valuation methods.\n- C) Wrong — \"accrual\" is mercantile, but \"amortised\" is not a Section 145 method.\n- D) Wrong — straight-line/reducing balance are depreciation methods.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Under the mercantile system of accounting, interest on securities is taxable on a:\nA) Receipt basis  B) Maturity basis  C) Cash basis  D) Accrual basis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Mercantile = **accrual** basis; interest is taxed when it becomes due, even if not yet received.\n- A) and C) Wrong — receipt/cash basis describes the cash system, not mercantile.\n- B) Wrong — maturity basis is not a recognised method; accrual happens period by period.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Under the cash system of accounting, interest on securities is taxed on a:\nA) Accrual basis  B) Due basis  C) Receipt basis  D) Estimated basis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cash system = interest taxed only when **actually received**.\n- A) and B) Wrong — accrual/due basis describes the mercantile system.\n- D) Wrong — there is no \"estimated\" basis under Section 145.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Securities held by Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs) are:\nA) Always treated as a capital asset  B) Always treated as stock-in-trade  C) Exempt from all tax  D) Taxed only as business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook states FPIs' securities are **always treated as a capital asset**, so their gains are always taxed under capital gains.\n- B) Wrong — they are the opposite of stock-in-trade; they are capital assets.\n- C) Wrong — they are taxed, just under the capital gains head.\n- D) Wrong — they are taxed as capital gains, not business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The three principal features of a bond are:\nA) Maturity, coupon and principal  B) Coupon, dividend and bonus  C) Liquidity, risk and dividend  D) Issue date, NAV and exit load\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A bond's principal features are **maturity (tenure), coupon (interest) and principal (face value)**.\n- B) Wrong — bonds do not pay dividends or bonuses; those belong to equity.\n- C) Wrong — these are general descriptors, not the defined features.\n- D) Wrong — NAV and exit load are mutual-fund concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In \"7.4% CG Bond 2027\", the figure 7.4% refers to the:\nA) Yield to maturity  B) Coupon (interest) rate  C) Redemption premium  D) Tax rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The name conveys a Central Government bond maturing in 2027 paying a **coupon of 7.40%**.\n- A) Wrong — YTM is the market discount rate, not stated in the bond's name.\n- C) Wrong — there is no redemption premium implied here.\n- D) Wrong — 7.4% is interest, not a tax rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The interest rate at which the present value of a bond's future cash flows is determined is known as:\nA) Coupon rate  B) Repo rate  C) Yield-to-maturity  D) Marginal cost of funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Market value of a bond = present value of all future cash flows, discounted at the **Yield-to-Maturity (YTM)**.\n- A) Wrong — the coupon is the fixed contractual interest, not the discount rate.\n- B) Wrong — the repo rate is an RBI policy rate.\n- D) Wrong — MCLR is a bank lending benchmark, not a bond discount rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Interest arising from bonds is generally taxable:\nA) At a flat 12.50%  B) Tax-free in all cases  C) At 20% always  D) At normal rates applicable to the assessee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bond interest is taxed under Other Sources at **normal (slab) rates** applicable to the assessee.\n- A) Wrong — 12.50% is the LTCG rate on listed bonds, not the interest rate.\n- B) Wrong — only tax-free bonds give exempt interest; ordinary bonds do not.\n- C) Wrong — 20% is the non-resident dividend rate, not the general interest rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** For LISTED bonds or debentures, the holding period to qualify as a long-term capital asset is:\nA) 36 months  B) 24 months  C) 12 months  D) Not applicable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed bonds/debentures become long-term after **12 months** (Table 10.1).\n- A) and B) Wrong — 36/24 months are not the listed-bond thresholds.\n- D) Wrong — \"Not applicable\" describes unlisted bonds/MLDs, which are always short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Long-term capital gains on listed bonds are taxed at:\nA) 10% with indexation  B) 12.50% without indexation  C) 20% with indexation  D) Slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG on listed bonds is **12.50% without indexation** benefit.\n- A) and C) Wrong — indexation is not available under these post-2023 rules.\n- D) Wrong — slab rates apply to short-term gains, not LTCG on listed bonds.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Capital gains on UNLISTED bonds or debentures are:\nA) Always long-term, taxed at 12.50%  B) Exempt from tax  C) Always short-term, taxed at applicable rates  D) Taxed at 20% with indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Irrespective of holding period, gains on unlisted bonds/debentures are **short-term**, taxed at applicable (slab) rates.\n- A) Wrong — they never qualify as long-term, so 12.50% LTCG does not apply.\n- B) Wrong — they are taxable.\n- D) Wrong — no indexation and the rate is slab, not 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Market Linked Debentures (even if listed) are taxed on transfer as:\nA) Long-term capital gains at 12.50%  B) Short-term capital gains irrespective of holding period  C) Exempt under Section 10  D) Business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — MLDs are always **short-term capital gains irrespective of holding period** (Section 50AA).\n- A) Wrong — they never get the 12.50% long-term rate.\n- C) Wrong — they are not exempt.\n- D) Wrong — they are capital gains, not business income (when held as a capital asset).\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A Government Security (G-Sec) is issued by:\nA) Private companies  B) Mutual funds  C) Foreign banks  D) The Central or State Governments\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A G-Sec is a tradable instrument issued by the **Central Government or the State Governments**, acknowledging the Government's debt.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — these issuers do not issue sovereign G-Secs.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Government securities with a maturity of less than 1 year are usually called:\nA) Dated securities  B) State Development Loans  C) Treasury bills or cash management bills  D) Tax-free bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Short-term G-Secs (maturity less than 1 year) are **treasury bills or cash management bills**.\n- A) and B) Wrong — dated securities and SDLs have maturity of 1 year or more.\n- D) Wrong — tax-free bonds are a separate category, not defined by sub-1-year maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Long-term securities issued by State Governments are called:\nA) Treasury Bills  B) State Development Loans (SDLs)  C) Cash Management Bills  D) Tax-free bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — State Governments raise market loans called **State Development Loans (SDLs)**.\n- A) and C) Wrong — these are short-term Central Government instruments.\n- D) Wrong — tax-free bonds are issued by specified organizations, not as SDLs.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** G-Secs are considered the safest investment because they:\nA) Offer the highest returns  B) Are exempt from all tax  C) Carry the Sovereign's commitment and practically no default risk  D) Are guaranteed by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — G-Secs carry the **Sovereign's commitment** for interest and principal, with practically no default risk — hence \"risk-free gilt-edged securities\".\n- A) Wrong — being safe, they usually offer modest, not the highest, returns.\n- B) Wrong — interest on ordinary G-Secs is taxable.\n- D) Wrong — they are backed by the Government, not guaranteed by SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Cash Management Bills (CMBs) are usually issued for a period of:\nA) Less than 91 days  B) Exactly 1 year  C) 5 to 40 years  D) 364 days only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — CMBs are issued for a very short period, **usually less than 91 days**, as per the Government's cash requirements.\n- B) and D) Wrong — those describe T-Bills tenors, not CMBs.\n- C) Wrong — 5 to 40 years describes dated G-Secs.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Treasury Bills (T-Bills) are issued in which tenors?\nA) 30, 60 or 90 days  B) 1, 3 or 5 years  C) 6, 12 or 24 months  D) 91, 182 or 364 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — T-Bills are issued in three tenors: **91 days, 182 days or 364 days** (never exceeding 1 year).\n- A), B), C) Wrong — these are not the prescribed T-Bill tenors.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The return on a T-Bill arises from:\nA) A half-yearly coupon  B) The difference between the issue (discounted) price and the redemption (face) value  C) A monthly dividend  D) Bonus units\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — T-Bills pay no interest; the return is the **difference between the discounted issue price and the face value** on redemption.\n- A) Wrong — T-Bills carry no coupon.\n- C) and D) Wrong — dividends/bonus units are mutual-fund/equity concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Dated Government Securities (Dated G-Secs) generally pay interest:\nA) Monthly  B) Annually  C) Only at maturity  D) Half-yearly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Dated G-Secs carry a fixed or floating coupon paid on the face value on a **half-yearly** basis.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — the workbook specifies half-yearly servicing of interest, not monthly/annual/at-maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Profit arising on redemption or transfer of Cash Management Bills and T-Bills is treated as:\nA) Long-term capital gain at 12.50%  B) Short-term capital gain at applicable rates  C) Interest income  D) Exempt income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since these bills mature in less than 1 year and pay no interest, the profit is a **short-term capital gain** taxed at applicable rates.\n- A) Wrong — they cannot be long-term given sub-1-year maturity.\n- C) Wrong — there is no interest; the gain is a capital gain.\n- D) Wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Dated G-Secs and SDLs are taxed in the same way as:\nA) Equity shares  B) Market Linked Debentures  C) Treasury Bills  D) Listed bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Dated G-Secs and SDLs are issued in the form of bonds and are taxed the **same as listed bonds**.\n- A) Wrong — equity has different (Chapter 11) rules.\n- B) Wrong — MLDs are always short-term under Section 50AA, unlike G-Secs.\n- C) Wrong — T-Bills are always short-term; dated G-Secs can be long-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** No TDS under Section 193 is required on interest on Central/State Government securities EXCEPT for:\nA) All tax-free bonds  B) 8% Savings (Taxable) Bonds, 2003 and 7.75% Savings (Taxable) Bonds, 2018  C) Treasury Bills  D) Sovereign Gold Bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TDS under Section 193 generally does not apply to Govt securities, **except** the 8% Savings (Taxable) Bonds, 2003 and 7.75% Savings (Taxable) Bonds, 2018.\n- A), C), D) Wrong — these are not the named exceptions in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** TDS on interest on the 8% / 7.75% Savings (Taxable) Bonds is deducted only when annual interest exceeds:\nA) Rs. 5,000  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Rs. 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — TDS applies only when the interest paid during the year on these bonds exceeds **Rs. 10,000**.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — these are not the threshold stated in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Interest on tax-free bonds is:\nA) Taxed at 12.50%  B) Exempt under Section 10  C) Taxed at slab rates  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on tax-free bonds is **exempt under Section 10** of the Income Tax Act.\n- A), C), D) Wrong — the interest is fully exempt, so no rate applies to it.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Capital gains on transfer or redemption of tax-free bonds are:\nA) Also exempt under Section 10  B) Always short-term  C) Taxed at 20% with indexation  D) Taxable, same as coupon bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Only the *interest* is tax-free; the **capital gains are taxable**, with the same treatment as coupon bonds.\n- A) Wrong — the exemption covers interest, not capital gains.\n- B) Wrong — listed tax-free bonds held over 12 months are long-term.\n- C) Wrong — no indexation; LTCG on listed bonds is 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A mutual fund that invests 65% or more of its proceeds in debt or money market instruments and is bought after April 1, 2023 is a:\nA) Specified Mutual Fund (Type 1)  B) Equity-oriented fund  C) Other Mutual Fund  D) Tax-free fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This defines a **Specified Mutual Fund (Type 1)** governed by Section 50AA.\n- B) Wrong — equity-oriented funds invest mostly in shares.\n- C) Wrong — \"Other Mutual Funds\" is the residual fourth category.\n- D) Wrong — there is no \"tax-free fund\" category here.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Section 50AA, which governs SMFs and MLDs, was inserted with effect from which financial year?\nA) FY 2020-21  B) FY 2021-22  C) FY 2022-23  D) FY 2023-24\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 50AA was inserted with effect from **FY 2023-24** (Finance Act, 2023).\n- A), B), C) Wrong — the section did not exist in those years; before FY 2023-24 these gains followed normal rules.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Under Section 50AA, capital gains from SMFs and MLDs are taxable as:\nA) Long-term, at 12.50%  B) Short-term, irrespective of the period of holding  C) Exempt  D) Long-term, with indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 50AA makes such gains **short-term, irrespective of the holding period**, taxed at slab rates.\n- A) and D) Wrong — there is no long-term treatment or indexation under Section 50AA.\n- C) Wrong — these gains are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Under Section 50AA, the cost of improvement for MLDs/SMFs is taken to be:\nA) The actual amount spent  B) Indexed cost  C) Nil  D) 20% of sale value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For MLDs/SMFs the cost of improvement is taken as **nil**, whether incurred by the assessee or a previous owner.\n- A) Wrong — no actual improvement cost is allowed.\n- B) Wrong — no indexation applies.\n- D) Wrong — there is no such 20% rule for cost of improvement.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Section 50AA applies to SMFs acquired:\nA) On or before 31-03-2023  B) On or after 01-04-2023  C) Only before FY 2020-21  D) At any time in the past\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For SMFs, Section 50AA applies prospectively to those **acquired on or after 01-04-2023**.\n- A) Wrong — SMFs bought on/before 31-03-2023 follow normal mutual-fund rules.\n- C) and D) Wrong — the trigger date is 01-04-2023, not earlier periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** For \"Other Mutual Funds\" that are UNLISTED, the period of holding to qualify as long-term is:\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) Not applicable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted Other Mutual Funds need to be held for **24 months** to be long-term (listed ones need 12 months).\n- A) Wrong — 12 months applies to listed Other MFs.\n- C) Wrong — 36 months is not the threshold here.\n- D) Wrong — these funds can be long-term, so the period does apply.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. P sells UNLISTED debentures after holding them for 5 years at a profit. How is the gain taxed?\nA) LTCG at 12.50% without indexation  B) LTCG at 20% with indexation  C) Exempt as held over 3 years  D) STCG at applicable slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unlisted debentures are **always short-term irrespective of holding period**, so the gain is STCG taxed at slab rates — even after 5 years.\n- A) and B) Wrong — unlisted debentures never qualify as long-term.\n- C) Wrong — there is no exemption for holding 3+ years here.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mr. Q holds a Principal-Protected MLD for 4 years and redeems it at maturity (after 01-04-2023). The gain is:\nA) LTCG at 12.50%  B) STCG at slab rates under Section 50AA  C) Tax-free  D) Taxed as interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — MLDs redeemed on/after 01-04-2023 fall under Section 50AA: **always STCG at slab rates**, regardless of the 4-year holding.\n- A) Wrong — MLDs never get the 12.50% long-term rate.\n- C) Wrong — they are taxable.\n- D) Wrong — the gain on redemption is a capital gain, not interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An investor earns the difference between purchase and sale of a 182-day T-Bill held to maturity. This is taxed as:\nA) Interest income at slab rates  B) LTCG at 12.50%  C) Exempt income  D) Short-term capital gain at applicable rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — T-Bills pay no interest; the discount-to-face gain is a **short-term capital gain** (maturity under 1 year) at applicable rates.\n- A) Wrong — there is no interest on a T-Bill, so it is not interest income.\n- B) Wrong — sub-1-year maturity cannot be long-term.\n- C) Wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Compared with a listed bond held 18 months, an unlisted bond held 18 months differs in that the unlisted bond's gain is:\nA) Long-term at 12.50%  B) Exempt  C) Taxed at 20%  D) Short-term at slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A listed bond held over 12 months is LTCG at 12.50%, but the **unlisted bond is always STCG at slab rates**, regardless of the 18 months.\n- A) Wrong — that is the listed-bond treatment, not unlisted.\n- B) and C) Wrong — no exemption and no 20% rate apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A resident receives dividend of Rs. 2,00,000 from a debt-oriented mutual fund and paid Rs. 60,000 interest on borrowed funds. The maximum interest deduction is:\nA) Rs. 60,000  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 20,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Formula: max deduction = 20% × total dividend.** Here 20% × 2,00,000 = **Rs. 40,000**. Even though Rs. 60,000 was paid, only Rs. 40,000 is deductible.\n- A) Wrong — the actual interest paid is capped at the 20% limit.\n- C) Wrong — that would be 10%, not the 20% cap.\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 1,00,000 would be 50%, far above the cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A non-resident receives dividend of Rs. 2,00,000 from a debt mutual fund and paid Rs. 60,000 interest. The taxable dividend is:\nA) Rs. 1,40,000  B) Rs. 1,60,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A non-resident gets **no deduction at all** (not even the 20% interest), so the **full Rs. 2,00,000** is taxable (at 20%, subject to DTAA).\n- A) and B) Wrong — these assume some deduction, which non-residents cannot claim.\n- D) Wrong — that is the 20% interest figure, not the taxable dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Mr. R bought a debt-oriented fund in January 2022 (before April 2023) and sells it after holding 4 years. The fund is treated as:\nA) SMF Type 1 under Section 50AA  B) SMF Type 2 under Section 50AA  C) Equity-oriented fund  D) An \"Other Mutual Fund\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 50AA applies only to SMFs acquired **on or after 01-04-2023**. Bought in Jan 2022, the fund follows normal rules as an **Other Mutual Fund**.\n- A) and B) Wrong — Section 50AA does not catch pre-April-2023 purchases.\n- C) Wrong — a debt fund is not equity-oriented.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** For a NON-RESIDENT, LTCG on transfer of LISTED bonds is taxed at:\nA) Slab rates  B) 20% with indexation  C) Exempt  D) 12.50% without indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A non-resident's LTCG on listed bonds is **always 12.50% without indexation** (no forex benefit either).\n- A) Wrong — slab rates apply to STCG, not LTCG on listed bonds.\n- B) Wrong — no indexation is allowed.\n- C) Wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Under Section 115E, LTCG to a Non-Resident Indian arises from the transfer of Government securities or listed debentures of an Indian public company, provided they were purchased in:\nA) Indian rupees  B) Gold  C) Cryptocurrency  D) Foreign currency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 115E covers LTCG on Govt securities or listed debentures of an Indian public company **purchased in foreign currency**, taxed at 12.50%.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — the provision specifically requires purchase in foreign currency.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An ETF (exchange traded fund) differs from a regular mutual fund mainly because investors:\nA) Buy/sell units on the stock exchange  B) Get tax-free returns  C) Cannot redeem units  D) Pay no capital gains tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For an **ETF, buyers buy/sell units on the stock exchange**; other mutual funds are bought/redeemed through the AMC.\n- B) and D) Wrong — ETFs are still subject to capital gains tax.\n- C) Wrong — ETF units can be traded; they are not locked.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A liquid (money market) fund invests primarily in:\nA) Equity shares  B) Real estate  C) Treasury Bills and Commercial Papers  D) Gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Money market/liquid funds invest in highly liquid instruments such as **Treasury Bills and Commercial Papers**.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — equity, real estate and gold are held by other fund types.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Mr. S, a non-resident US tax resident, earns Rs. 10,00,000 interest from Indian debentures. India-USA DTAA Article 11 caps the rate at 15%; the Act would tax at his slab (say 30%). He will be taxed at:\nA) 30%  B) 15%  C) 45%  D) 0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A non-resident eligible for DTAA pays the **DTAA rate or Act rate, whichever is more beneficial**. The DTAA's **15%** is lower than 30%, so 15% applies.\n- A) Wrong — the slab rate is higher and not beneficial.\n- C) Wrong — the two rates are not added together.\n- D) Wrong — the income is taxable, just at a concessional rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** To claim DTAA relief, a non-resident must obtain a Tax Residency Certificate (TRC) and furnish:\nA) Form 15G  B) Form 26AS  C) Form 49A  D) Form 10F\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Besides the TRC, the non-resident must furnish **Form 10F**, a self-declaration of prescribed details.\n- A) Wrong — Form 15G is for residents seeking nil-TDS.\n- B) Wrong — Form 26AS is a tax credit statement, not a DTAA declaration.\n- C) Wrong — Form 49A is a PAN application form.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Under Chapter XII-A, eligible investments for an NRI of Indian origin include all EXCEPT:\nA) Shares in an Indian company  B) Debentures/deposits in a non-private Indian company  C) Certain Government securities  D) Shares of a foreign company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Chapter XII-A covers Indian-company shares, debentures/deposits in non-private Indian companies and certain Govt securities — **not foreign company shares**.\n- A), B), C) Wrong — these are all eligible investments listed in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Under Chapter XII-A, investment income of an eligible NRI is capped at:\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 20% (plus surcharge and cess)  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Chapter XII-A caps tax on investment income at **20%** (plus applicable surcharge and cess); LTCG is at 12.50%.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are not the Chapter XII-A investment-income cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Under Chapter XII-A reinvestment relief, LTCG can be exempt if the net consideration is reinvested in eligible assets within:\nA) 3 months  B) 6 months  C) 12 months  D) 24 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reinvesting the net consideration in eligible assets within **6 months** can exempt the LTCG (fully if all reinvested, proportionately if partial).\n- A), C), D) Wrong — the window is 6 months, not these periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Under Chapter XII-A, if the new asset is transferred within 3 years, the earlier-exempted gain is:\nA) Permanently exempt  B) Taxed in the year the new asset is transferred  C) Refunded  D) Taxed at 12.50% only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the new asset is sold within **3 years**, the previously exempted LTCG is **taxed in the year of that transfer**.\n- A) Wrong — the exemption is withdrawn on early sale.\n- C) Wrong — there is no refund concept here.\n- D) Wrong — the gain becomes taxable; the rule is about timing, not a special rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Section 89A addresses double taxation on overseas retirement funds for residents who once worked in which countries?\nA) UAE, Singapore and Australia  B) Germany, France and Japan  C) USA, Canada and UK  D) China, Russia and Brazil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The notified countries for Section 89A are **USA, Canada and the UK**; income from such retirement accounts is taxed on withdrawal, not accrual.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are not the notified countries.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** MLDs can be issued by companies with a minimum net worth of:\nA) Rs. 10 crores  B) Rs. 50 crores  C) Rs. 100 crores  D) Rs. 500 crores\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — MLDs can be issued by companies with a minimum net worth of **Rs. 100 crores**.\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are not the stated net-worth requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The maximum tenure for MLDs is generally:\nA) 3 to 6 months  B) 5 to 10 years  C) Up to 40 years  D) 12 to 36 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — MLD tenure is generally between **12 to 36 months**, with returns paid only at maturity.\n- A) Wrong — too short relative to the stated range.\n- B) and C) Wrong — those are bond/G-Sec horizons, not MLDs.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A Principal Non-Protected MLD differs from a Principal Protected MLD in that it:\nA) Always returns the full principal  B) Pays a fixed coupon  C) Does not guarantee return of the principal at maturity  D) Is exempt from tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Principal Non-Protected MLD **does not guarantee return of principal**; the protected variety does.\n- A) Wrong — that is the protected MLD.\n- B) Wrong — MLD coupons vary with the underlying's performance.\n- D) Wrong — MLDs are taxable under Section 50AA.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** When a non-resident is eligible for DTAA, the rate applied is the:\nA) Always the DTAA rate  B) Always the Income Tax Act rate  C) DTAA or Act rate, whichever is more beneficial  D) Average of the two rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — He is taxed at the **DTAA rate or Act rate, whichever is more beneficial** to him.\n- A) and B) Wrong — neither is automatically applied; the lower/beneficial one is chosen.\n- D) Wrong — rates are not averaged.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Mr. T (NR, US resident) has dividend of Rs. 2,20,000 from Indian shares. Act rate = 20%, India-USA DTAA = 25%. He will pay tax at:\nA) 25% (DTAA)  B) 20% (Act)  C) 22.5% (average)  D) 0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He picks the more beneficial rate. The **Act rate of 20%** is lower than the DTAA's 25%, so 20% applies.\n- A) Wrong — DTAA's 25% is higher, not beneficial.\n- C) Wrong — rates are not averaged.\n- D) Wrong — dividends are taxable for non-residents.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** If Mr. T above is instead a UK tax resident and the India-UK DTAA rate on dividends is 10%, he will pay:\nA) 20% (Act)  B) 15%  C) 25%  D) 10% (DTAA)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The India-UK DTAA's **10%** is lower than the Act's 20%, so the DTAA rate of 10% applies.\n- A) Wrong — the Act's 20% is higher than the DTAA rate available here.\n- B) and C) Wrong — these are not the applicable beneficial rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An SMF Type 2 typically includes funds that invested LESS than 35% in Indian equity, bought after 01-04-2023 and sold:\nA) Before 01-04-2025  B) After 01-04-2030  C) Anytime  D) Before 01-04-2023\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SMF Type 2 is a limited-edition category: bought after 01-04-2023 and **sold before 01-04-2025** (e.g., debt-hybrids, gold/silver, international, FoF).\n- B), C), D) Wrong — these fall outside the defined window.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A debt-oriented hybrid fund that invested less than 35% in Indian equity, bought May 2023 and sold March 2025, is taxed as:\nA) Other Mutual Fund (LTCG 12.50%)  B) Equity-oriented fund  C) Exempt  D) SMF Type 2 — all gains STCG at slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — This fits **SMF Type 2** (under 35% equity, bought after 01-04-2023, sold before 01-04-2025) under Section 50AA: **all gains are STCG at slab rates**.\n- A) Wrong — Section 50AA overrides the long-term treatment here.\n- B) Wrong — under 35% equity is not equity-oriented.\n- C) Wrong — the gains are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** When a listed bond is sold \"inclusive of accrued interest\", for capital gains the accrued interest must be:\nA) Added to sale proceeds and taxed as capital gain  B) Carved out of the sale price and taxed separately as interest  C) Ignored entirely  D) Taxed at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Accrued-but-unreceived interest baked into the sale price is **carved out and taxed as interest**, leaving an adjusted sale consideration for the capital gain.\n- A) Wrong — it would double up or mischaracterise the interest.\n- C) Wrong — the interest is taxable, not ignored.\n- D) Wrong — interest is taxed at slab rates, not 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Listed Municipal Debt Securities are taxed like listed bonds. Their LTCG (held over 12 months) for a resident is taxed at:\nA) Slab rates  B) 12.50%  C) 20% with indexation  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed municipal debt securities follow listed-bond treatment: LTCG (>12 months) at **12.50%**.\n- A) Wrong — slab rates apply to STCG.\n- C) Wrong — no indexation.\n- D) Wrong — taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** For an INDIVIDUAL, capital gain on REDEMPTION of a Sovereign Gold Bond is:\nA) Taxed at 12.50%  B) Not chargeable to tax under Section 47  C) Taxed at slab rates  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **Section 47**, capital gain to an individual on **redemption** of an SGB is **not chargeable to tax**. (Sale other than redemption is taxable.)\n- A), C), D) Wrong — these rates would apply only to a sale other than redemption, not to the redemption itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A resident sells a LISTED Dated G-Sec held for 10 months at a profit. The gain is:\nA) LTCG at 12.50%  B) STCG at applicable slab rates  C) Exempt  D) Interest income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed Dated G-Secs follow listed-bond rules; held only 10 months (under 12), the gain is **short-term at slab rates**.\n- A) Wrong — under 12 months is not long-term.\n- C) Wrong — taxable.\n- D) Wrong — it is a capital gain, not interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which of the following is NOT one of the four post-2023 tax categories of mutual funds?\nA) Equity-oriented funds  B) Specified Mutual Fund (Type 1)  C) Tax-free mutual funds  D) Other Mutual Funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The four categories are Equity-oriented, SMF Type 1, SMF Type 2 and Other Mutual Funds. **\"Tax-free mutual funds\" is not a category.**\n- A), B), D) Wrong — these are genuine categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Where bond interest is taxed at a concessional rate, the assessee is allowed:\nA) Deductions under Sections 80C–80U  B) No deduction (including 80C–80U) from such interest  C) Indexation benefit  D) A flat 20% rebate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When interest is taxed at a concessional rate, **no deduction (including Sections 80C–80U)** is allowed against that interest.\n- A) Wrong — those deductions are specifically disallowed here.\n- C) Wrong — indexation is unrelated to interest income.\n- D) Wrong — there is no such rebate.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** For ordinary bond interest taxed at normal rates, the assessee may deduct:\nA) Nothing  B) Expenditure wholly and exclusively incurred to earn it, plus collection commission  C) 20% standard deduction  D) Indexed cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Against ordinary bond interest, the assessee can deduct **expenditure wholly and exclusively to earn it**, plus commission paid to a banker/person to realise it.\n- A) Wrong — relevant expenses are deductible.\n- C) Wrong — there is no flat 20% standard deduction on bond interest.\n- D) Wrong — indexed cost relates to capital gains, not interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A debt fund bought after 01-04-2023 that invests 70% in money market instruments is taxed under Section 50AA as:\nA) LTCG at 12.50% after 24 months  B) Always STCG at slab rates  C) Equity-oriented  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 70% (≥65%) in debt/money market and bought after 01-04-2023, it is **SMF Type 1**: gains are **always STCG at slab rates** under Section 50AA.\n- A) Wrong — no long-term benefit under 50AA.\n- C) Wrong — it is not equity-oriented.\n- D) Wrong — taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Where the LTCG of a non-resident on listed bonds is computed, the benefit of foreign currency fluctuation is:\nA) Always allowed  B) Allowed in certain cases  C) Never relevant  D) Mandatory for all bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook notes that LTCG computation as per foreign currency fluctuation is **allowed in certain cases** for non-residents (though under 115E/112(1)(c)(iii) the 12.50% is without forex benefit).\n- A) and D) Wrong — it is not universally allowed/mandatory.\n- C) Wrong — it can be relevant in certain cases.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. A holds 1,000 bonds of face value Rs. 100 each, coupon 7.50% p.a., interest paid half-yearly. What is each half-yearly interest payment?\nA) Rs. 3,750  B) Rs. 7,500  C) Rs. 1,875  D) Rs. 750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Formula: half-yearly interest = Face value × number × coupon × 6/12.** = 1,000 × 100 × 7.50% × 6/12 = 1,00,000 × 0.075 × 0.5 = **Rs. 3,750**.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 7,500 is the full-year interest.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 1,875 is a quarter's worth (3 months).\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 750 is far too small.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Same bonds (Q71), Mr. A follows the mercantile system. Interest accrues Jan–Mar 2024 (3 months). How much accrues for that quarter?\nA) Rs. 3,750  B) Rs. 7,500  C) Rs. 937.50  D) Rs. 1,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Formula: interest = 1,00,000 × 7.50% × 3/12.** = 1,00,000 × 0.075 × 0.25 = **Rs. 1,875** for the 3-month period.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 3,750 is 6 months.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 7,500 is a full year.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 937.50 would be 1.5 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Mr. A buys 400 listed bonds @ Rs. 1,200 each (01-01-2016), sells @ Rs. 2,000 each (31-07-2024). The full value of consideration is:\nA) Rs. 4,80,000  B) Rs. 8,00,000  C) Rs. 3,20,000  D) Rs. 12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Full value of consideration = number × sale price** = 400 × 2,000 = **Rs. 8,00,000**.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 4,80,000 is the cost (400 × 1,200).\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 3,20,000 is the gain, not the consideration.\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 12,00,000 has no basis here.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Continuing Q73, the long-term capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 8,00,000  B) Rs. 4,80,000  C) Rs. 3,20,000  D) Rs. 1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **LTCG = Full value − Cost of acquisition** = 8,00,000 − (400 × 1,200 = 4,80,000) = **Rs. 3,20,000**. Held 8 yr 7 mth (>12 months, listed) → long-term.\n- A) and B) Wrong — these are the consideration and cost, not the gain.\n- D) Wrong — arithmetic does not support Rs. 1,20,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Continuing Q74, tax on the LTCG (ignoring surcharge/cess) is:\nA) Rs. 64,000  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 32,000  D) Rs. 96,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Tax = LTCG × 12.50%** = 3,20,000 × 0.125 = **Rs. 40,000** (listed bond LTCG rate).\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 64,000 is 20%.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 32,000 is 10%.\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 96,000 is 30%.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** If the bonds in Q73–Q75 were UNLISTED instead, the Rs. 3,20,000 gain would be taxed:\nA) At 12.50% (LTCG)  B) As STCG at applicable slab rates  C) Exempt  D) At 20% with indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted bonds are **always short-term**, so the Rs. 3,20,000 is **STCG taxed at slab rates** — no 12.50% benefit despite 8+ years.\n- A) Wrong — unlisted bonds never qualify as long-term.\n- C) Wrong — taxable.\n- D) Wrong — no indexation; slab rates apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Mr. A holds 400 bonds, face Rs. 1,000, coupon 7%, interest half-yearly. The half-yearly interest received on 30-06-2024 is:\nA) Rs. 28,000  B) Rs. 7,000  C) Rs. 56,000  D) Rs. 14,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Formula = 400 × 1,000 × 7% × 6/12** = 4,00,000 × 0.07 × 0.5 = **Rs. 14,000**.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 28,000 is the full-year interest.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 7,000 is a quarter (3 months).\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 56,000 is double the annual interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Continuing Q77 (mercantile), interest accrued for April–June 2024 (3 months) is:\nA) Rs. 14,000  B) Rs. 28,000  C) Rs. 3,500  D) Rs. 7,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 4,00,000 × 7% × 3/12** = 4,00,000 × 0.07 × 0.25 = **Rs. 7,000** accrued for the quarter.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 14,000 is 6 months.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 28,000 is a full year.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 3,500 would be 1.5 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. A acquires a 9% listed bond, face Rs. 10,000, on 01-04-2024. Quarterly interest received for the quarter ending 30-06-2024 is:\nA) Rs. 900  B) Rs. 450  C) Rs. 225  D) Rs. 75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Formula = Face × 9% × 1/4** = 10,000 × 0.09 × 0.25 = **Rs. 225** per quarter.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 900 is the full-year interest.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 450 is half a year.\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 75 is one month's interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Same 9% bond (Q79) sold 01-11-2024. Interest accrued for October 2024 (1 month, not yet received) is:\nA) Rs. 225  B) Rs. 150  C) Rs. 900  D) Rs. 75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 10,000 × 9% × 1/12** = 10,000 × 0.09 ÷ 12 = **Rs. 75** for one month.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 225 is a quarter.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 150 would be 2 months.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 900 is a full year.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** The 9% bond (Q79–Q80) received two quarters' interest (Rs. 225 each) plus Rs. 75 accrued. Total taxable interest income is:\nA) Rs. 450  B) Rs. 675  C) Rs. 300  D) Rs. 525\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Total interest = 225 + 225 + 75 = Rs. 525.** Two received quarters plus the one month accrued to date of sale.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 450 omits the Rs. 75 accrual.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 675 over-counts a quarter.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 300 has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** The 9% bond sold for Rs. 13,000 inclusive of Rs. 75 accrued interest. The adjusted sale consideration for capital gains is:\nA) Rs. 13,000  B) Rs. 13,075  C) Rs. 12,775  D) Rs. 12,925\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Adjusted consideration = Sale price − accrued interest** = 13,000 − 75 = **Rs. 12,925**. The Rs. 75 is taxed separately as interest, so it is removed from the sale figure.\n- A) Wrong — that still includes the interest portion.\n- B) Wrong — you subtract, not add, the accrued interest.\n- C) Wrong — over-subtracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Continuing Q82, cost of acquisition was Rs. 10,500. The short-term capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 2,500  B) Rs. 2,575  C) Rs. 3,000  D) Rs. 2,425\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **STCG = Adjusted consideration − cost** = 12,925 − 10,500 = **Rs. 2,425** (held under 12 months → short-term, taxed at applicable rate).\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 2,500 would ignore the interest carve-out (13,000 − 10,500).\n- B) and C) Wrong — arithmetic does not support these.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** XYZ Bank holds 40,000 Dated G-Secs, face Rs. 100, coupon 7.50%. Interest accrued for April–Dec 2024 (9 months) is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 4,50,000  D) Rs. 2,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 40,000 × 100 × 7.50% × 9/12** = 40,00,000 × 0.075 × 0.75 = **Rs. 2,25,000**.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 3,00,000 is a full year.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 1,50,000 is 6 months.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 4,50,000 doubles the figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** XYZ Bank's 40,000 G-Secs were bought (issue price) at Rs. 125 each and sold at Rs. 150 each. The full value of consideration is:\nA) Rs. 50,00,000  B) Rs. 40,00,000  C) Rs. 10,00,000  D) Rs. 60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 40,000 × 150** = **Rs. 60,00,000**.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 50,00,000 is the cost (40,000 × 125).\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 40,00,000 would be at face value.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 10,00,000 is the gain, not the consideration.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85, the capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 60,00,000  B) Rs. 50,00,000  C) Rs. 10,00,000  D) Rs. 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Gain = 60,00,000 − (40,000 × 125 = 50,00,000)** = **Rs. 10,00,000**.\n- A) and B) Wrong — these are consideration and cost.\n- D) Wrong — arithmetic gives Rs. 10,00,000, not Rs. 5,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** In Q86, the G-Secs are UNLISTED and held 10 years. The Rs. 10,00,000 gain is taxed as:\nA) LTCG at 12.50%  B) Exempt  C) Interest income  D) STCG at applicable rate (always short-term)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook's example treats these unlisted Dated G-Secs as **always short-term irrespective of holding period**, taxed at the applicable rate.\n- A) Wrong — no long-term benefit applies in this example.\n- B) Wrong — taxable.\n- C) Wrong — it is a capital gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Mr. X buys 5,000 listed tax-free NABARD bonds @ Rs. 120, redeems @ Rs. 150. Full value of consideration is:\nA) Rs. 6,00,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 3,75,000  D) Rs. 7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 5,000 × 150** = **Rs. 7,50,000**.\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 6,00,000 is the cost (5,000 × 120).\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 1,50,000 is the gain.\n- C) Wrong — no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Continuing Q88, the long-term capital gain (held over 12 months, listed) is:\nA) Rs. 7,50,000  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 1,50,000  D) Rs. 30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **LTCG = 7,50,000 − (5,000 × 120 = 6,00,000)** = **Rs. 1,50,000**. (Interest on these bonds is exempt; only the gain is taxed.)\n- A) and B) Wrong — consideration and cost.\n- D) Wrong — Rs. 30,000 is the per-unit gain × 1,000, not 5,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Continuing Q89, the tax on the Rs. 1,50,000 LTCG (listed, >12 months) is:\nA) Rs. 18,750  B) Rs. 15,000  C) Rs. 30,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Tax = 1,50,000 × 12.50%** = **Rs. 18,750**.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 15,000 is 10%.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 30,000 is 20%.\n- D) Wrong — only interest is exempt; the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Mr. A buys 1,000 units of a debt-oriented MF @ Rs. 150 (01-01-2017, an \"Other MF\"), sells @ Rs. 300 (15-03-2025). Full value of consideration is:\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Rs. 4,50,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **= 1,000 × 300** = **Rs. 3,00,000**.\n- A) Wrong — that is the cost (1,000 × 150).\n- C) Wrong — no basis.\n- D) Wrong — no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Continuing Q91, since held over 24 months (8+ years) the gain is long-term. The LTCG is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 4,50,000  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **LTCG = 3,00,000 − (1,000 × 150 = 1,50,000)** = **Rs. 1,50,000**, taxed at 12.50%. Bought pre-April-2023, so it is an Other MF (not under Section 50AA).\n- A) Wrong — that is consideration.\n- B) Wrong — no basis.\n- C) Wrong — arithmetic gives Rs. 1,50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Tax on the Rs. 1,50,000 LTCG in Q92 is:\nA) Rs. 18,750  B) Rs. 15,000  C) Rs. 30,000  D) Rs. 45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Tax = 1,50,000 × 12.50%** = **Rs. 18,750** (Other MF, listed/long-term).\n- B) Wrong — 10%.\n- C) Wrong — 20%.\n- D) Wrong — 30%.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Had the fund in Q91 instead been an SMF Type 1 bought after 01-04-2023, the Rs. 1,50,000 gain would be:\nA) LTCG at 12.50%  B) Exempt  C) Taxed at 20%  D) STCG at slab rates (Section 50AA)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An SMF Type 1 is governed by Section 50AA: **always STCG at slab rates**, regardless of the long holding period.\n- A) Wrong — no long-term benefit under 50AA.\n- B) Wrong — taxable.\n- C) Wrong — slab rates, not a flat 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A resident gets Rs. 5,00,000 dividend from a debt MF, paid Rs. 1,50,000 interest. The maximum deductible interest is:\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Max deduction = 20% × dividend** = 20% × 5,00,000 = **Rs. 1,00,000** (capped, even though Rs. 1,50,000 paid).\n- A) Wrong — actual paid is above the cap.\n- B) Wrong — that is 10%.\n- C) Wrong — that is 15%.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Continuing Q95, the taxable dividend for the resident is:\nA) Rs. 5,00,000  B) Rs. 3,50,000  C) Rs. 4,50,000  D) Rs. 4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Taxable dividend = dividend − allowed deduction** = 5,00,000 − 1,00,000 = **Rs. 4,00,000** (at slab rates).\n- A) Wrong — that ignores the allowed Rs. 1,00,000 deduction.\n- B) Wrong — would deduct Rs. 1,50,000, which exceeds the cap.\n- C) Wrong — would deduct only Rs. 50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** If the investor in Q95 were a NON-RESIDENT, the taxable dividend and rate would be:\nA) Rs. 4,00,000 at slab rates  B) Rs. 5,00,000 at 20% (subject to DTAA), no deduction  C) Rs. 5,00,000 at slab rates  D) Rs. 1,00,000 at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A non-resident gets **no deduction**, so the full **Rs. 5,00,000 is taxed at 20%** (subject to DTAA).\n- A) Wrong — that is the resident treatment.\n- C) Wrong — non-resident dividend is at 20%, not slab.\n- D) Wrong — the whole dividend is taxed, not just Rs. 1,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A non-resident's LTCG on a listed debenture of an Indian public company purchased in foreign currency, gain Rs. 8,00,000, is taxed under Section 115E at:\nA) Rs. 1,60,000 (20%)  B) Rs. 1,00,000 (12.50%)  C) Rs. 80,000 (10%)  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Tax = 8,00,000 × 12.50%** = **Rs. 1,00,000** (Section 115E rate, no indexation/forex benefit).\n- A) Wrong — 20% is not the 115E rate.\n- C) Wrong — 10% is not applicable.\n- D) Wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An NRI under Chapter XII-A has LTCG of Rs. 6,00,000 and reinvests the ENTIRE net consideration in eligible assets within 6 months. The taxable LTCG is:\nA) Rs. 6,00,000  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Nil (fully exempt)  D) Rs. 75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the **entire net consideration is reinvested** within 6 months, the **whole LTCG is exempt** → taxable LTCG is **Nil**.\n- A) Wrong — full exemption applies on complete reinvestment.\n- B) Wrong — that would be partial reinvestment.\n- D) Wrong — no tax arises on full reinvestment.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Mr. Z (NR, US resident) earns Rs. 10,00,000 interest from Indian debentures. India-USA DTAA Article 11 rate is 15%. Tax payable in India is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 2,00,000  C) Rs. 1,25,000  D) Rs. 1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Choosing the beneficial DTAA rate, **tax = 10,00,000 × 15%** = **Rs. 1,50,000** (and he gets a US tax credit for this).\n- A) Wrong — Rs. 3,00,000 is 30% (slab), higher and not beneficial.\n- B) Wrong — Rs. 2,00,000 is 20%.\n- C) Wrong — Rs. 1,25,000 is 12.50%, which is the capital-gains rate, not the interest DTAA rate.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario — Mr. Mehta's debt portfolio (FY 2024-25):**\n\nMr. Mehta is a **resident individual** following the **mercantile system** of accounting. During FY 2024-25 the following events occur:\n\n1. He holds **2,000 listed bonds** of ABC Ltd., face value Rs. 1,000 each, **coupon 8% p.a.**, interest paid half-yearly (30 June and 31 December). He has held them since 01-01-2018.\n2. On **31-12-2024** he **sells all 2,000 bonds at Rs. 1,400 each** (price is ex-interest; the half-yearly interest to 31-12-2024 was separately received).\n3. He also holds **1,000 units of a debt-oriented mutual fund (an \"Other MF\")** bought on 01-02-2020 @ Rs. 200, sold on 01-02-2025 @ Rs. 320.\n4. He bought **500 units of an SMF Type 1** (a debt fund, ≥65% debt, bought 01-05-2023 @ Rs. 100) and sold them on 01-03-2025 @ Rs. 130.\n5. He received **Rs. 80,000 dividend (IDCW)** from the debt mutual fund and paid **Rs. 30,000 interest** on money borrowed to invest in it.\n\n---\n\n<details><summary>Sub-question 1: What is Mr. Mehta's taxable INTEREST income from the ABC Ltd. bonds for FY 2024-25? (assume both half-yearly payments fall in the year)</summary>\n\n**Answer: Rs. 1,60,000.**\n\n**Formula: annual interest = number × face value × coupon rate.**\n- Total face value = 2,000 × Rs. 1,000 = Rs. 20,00,000.\n- Annual interest = 20,00,000 × 8% = **Rs. 1,60,000**.\n\nHe receives two half-yearly payments of Rs. 80,000 each (20,00,000 × 8% × 6/12) on 30 June and 31 December 2024. Both fall in FY 2024-25, so total interest = 80,000 + 80,000 = **Rs. 1,60,000**, taxed under **Income from Other Sources at slab rates**. Because the sale on 31-12-2024 is ex-interest (interest received separately), there is no accrued-interest carve-out to worry about here.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Sub-question 2: Compute the capital gain on the ABC Ltd. bonds and the tax on it.</summary>\n\n**Answer: LTCG Rs. 8,00,000; tax Rs. 1,00,000.**\n\n- **Period of holding:** 01-01-2018 to 31-12-2024 = about 7 years → more than 12 months, and the bonds are **listed** → **long-term capital asset**.\n- **Full value of consideration** = 2,000 × Rs. 1,400 = Rs. 28,00,000.\n- **Cost of acquisition** = 2,000 × Rs. 1,000 = Rs. 20,00,000.\n- **LTCG = 28,00,000 − 20,00,000 = Rs. 8,00,000.**\n- **Tax = LTCG × 12.50%** (listed bond rate, no indexation) = 8,00,000 × 0.125 = **Rs. 1,00,000**.\n\nIntuition: listed bonds held over a year get the concessional 12.50% rate, much lower than slab rates.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Sub-question 3: Compute the capital gain on the 1,000 debt MF units (the \"Other MF\") and the tax.</summary>\n\n**Answer: LTCG Rs. 1,20,000; tax Rs. 15,000.**\n\n- **Period of holding:** 01-02-2020 to 01-02-2025 = 5 years. This fund was bought **before 01-04-2023**, so it is an **\"Other Mutual Fund\"** (Section 50AA does not apply). For an Other MF, the long-term threshold is 24 months (unlisted) / 12 months (listed); 5 years easily clears it → **long-term**.\n- **Full value of consideration** = 1,000 × Rs. 320 = Rs. 3,20,000.\n- **Cost** = 1,000 × Rs. 200 = Rs. 2,00,000.\n- **LTCG = 3,20,000 − 2,00,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.**\n- **Tax = 1,20,000 × 12.50% = Rs. 15,000.**\n\nIntuition: pre-April-2023 debt funds still enjoy long-term treatment at 12.50%; the 50AA \"always short-term\" rule does not catch them.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Sub-question 4: Compute the capital gain on the 500 SMF Type 1 units and the tax treatment. Contrast with sub-question 3.</summary>\n\n**Answer: Gain Rs. 15,000, taxed as SHORT-TERM at slab rates (Section 50AA).**\n\n- **Full value of consideration** = 500 × Rs. 130 = Rs. 65,000.\n- **Cost** = 500 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 50,000.\n- **Gain = 65,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 15,000.**\n- This fund is an **SMF Type 1** (≥65% debt, bought after 01-04-2023), so **Section 50AA applies**: the Rs. 15,000 is **short-term capital gain irrespective of holding period**, taxed at **Mr. Mehta's slab rates** — no 12.50% benefit, no holding-period benefit, and cost of improvement is nil.\n\n**Contrast:** The Other MF in sub-question 3 (bought pre-April-2023) got the 12.50% long-term rate, whereas this otherwise-similar debt fund bought after 01-04-2023 is forced into short-term slab-rate taxation. Same asset class, very different tax — purely because of the **purchase date** crossing 01-04-2023.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Sub-question 5: Compute the taxable dividend (IDCW) from the debt mutual fund. How would it change if Mr. Mehta were a non-resident?</summary>\n\n**Answer (resident): taxable dividend Rs. 64,000. Non-resident: full Rs. 80,000 at 20%.**\n\n**Resident:**\n- **Max interest deduction = 20% × dividend** = 20% × 80,000 = Rs. 16,000.\n- He paid Rs. 30,000, but only Rs. 16,000 is allowed (capped at 20%).\n- **Taxable dividend = 80,000 − 16,000 = Rs. 64,000**, taxed at slab rates.\n\n**Non-resident:**\n- A non-resident gets **no deduction at all** (not even the 20% interest, nor Chapter VIA / 80C–80U).\n- The **entire Rs. 80,000 is taxable at 20%** (subject to DTAA) = tax of Rs. 16,000.\n\nIntuition: residents get a small (20%-capped) interest deduction against dividend; non-residents get nothing but a flat concessional 20% rate.\n</details>\n\n\n\n"}, {"num": "11", "title": "Taxation of Equity Products", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 11: Taxation of Equity Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- How **listed and unlisted equity shares** are taxed (dividend + capital gains).\n- The big three sections: **STCG @20% (Sec 111A)**, **LTCG @12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (Sec 112A)**, **LTCG @12.50% (Sec 112)**.\n- The **grandfathering** rule for shares bought on/before 31-01-2018.\n- Preference shares, share warrants, equity-oriented mutual funds.\n- **Derivatives (F&O)** as business income; **bonus stripping** (Sec 94(8)).\n\n> An **equity share** = part-ownership of a company. You earn two ways: **dividend** (a slice of profits) and **capital gain** (selling higher than you bought). Equity is riskier than debt, and its tax rules are different.\n\n---\n\n## 11.1 Sources of Income\n\n| Income | When it arises | How taxed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dividend** | Company pays out profits | Slab rate (resident); special rate (non-resident) |\n| **Capital gain** | Sell the share above cost | STCG or LTCG depending on holding period |\n\n**Dividend timing:** Final dividend = income of the year it's *declared* at the AGM (receipt date irrelevant). Interim dividend = income of the year it's *made available* (receipt basis).\n\nSince the **Dividend Distribution Tax was abolished (Finance Act 2020, from FY 2020-21)**, dividends are taxed in the *shareholder's* hands.\n\n**Capital gains formula:**\nFull Value of Consideration − (transfer expenses + cost of acquisition + cost of improvement) − exemptions (Sec 54–54GB) = Capital Gain.\n\nLTCG generally @ **12.50%**; STCG generally added to income at slab rates (but **20%** under Sec 111A in certain cases).\n\n---\n\n## 11.2 Listed Equity Shares\n\n### 11.2.1 Charges & taxes when trading\n| Charge | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Brokerage** | Broker's fee |\n| **Securities Transaction Tax (STT)** | Tax on buy/sell of securities (not debt); collected by exchange, paid to Govt by the 7th of next month |\n| **Stamp Duty** | On transfer; unified rates across India from 01-04-2020 for listed securities |\n| **Exchange charges** | Levied by exchange on both sides |\n| **SEBI Turnover charges** | ₹10 per crore, both sides |\n| **DP charges** | Depository (NSDL/CDSL) fee on debit of shares + GST |\n| **GST** | 18% on brokerage + transaction + clearing charges |\n\n### 11.2.2 Tax on dividend\n- **Resident:** slab rates; interest-expense deduction capped at **20% of dividend**, nothing else.\n- **Non-resident / foreign company / FPI:** special rate (subject to DTAA), **no deduction**, **no Chapter VIA (80C–80U)**. Most DTAAs tax dividend in source country at **5%–15%**.\n\n### 11.2.3 Period of holding\n- **Listed shares:** short-term if held **≤12 months**; otherwise long-term.\n- **Demat shares:** period determined by **FIFO** (First-In-First-Out) — Section 45(2A) — applied **account-wise**.\n- **Physical shares sold through broker:** date of broker's note = date of transfer (if followed by delivery).\n\n**💡 FIFO example:** If shares entered the demat account on different dates and you sell some, the *earliest-acquired* are treated as sold first.\n\n### 11.2.4 LTCG under Section 112A\n- LTCG on **listed equity shares is exempt up to ₹1,25,000** per year; the **excess is taxed @12.50%** (+ surcharge + cess).\n- Condition: **STT paid on acquisition AND transfer** (with listed exceptions — IFSC transactions in foreign currency, shares before 01-10-2004, court/SEBI/RBI-approved acquisitions, ESOP/ESPS, etc.).\n\n#### Grandfathering (shares bought on/before 31-01-2018)\nCost of acquisition = **higher of**:\n- (a) actual cost; OR\n- (b) **lower of** [FMV on 31-01-2018] and [sale consideration].\n\nFMV = highest quoted price on 31-01-2018 (or the last traded day before).\n\n**💡 The four scenarios (cost ₹100, FMV ₹200 on 31-01-2018):**\n| Scenario | Sale price | Cost taken | Gain/Loss |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ₹250 | ₹200 (FMV) | LTCG ₹50 |\n| 2 | ₹150 | ₹150 (sale, since < FMV) | **NIL** (grandfathering can't manufacture a loss) |\n| 3 (FMV ₹50) | ₹150 | ₹100 (actual, since FMV < cost) | LTCG ₹50 |\n| 4 | ₹50 | ₹100 (actual) | LTCG **loss ₹50** |\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** grandfathering never turns a real gain (sale > cost) into a loss just because sale < FMV.\n\n### 11.2.5 LTCG under Section 112\nLTCG on equity **not** covered by 112A (e.g., off-market sale, no STT, or unlisted shares) is taxed @**12.50%** — but **without the ₹1.25 lakh exemption**.\n\n### 11.2.6 STCG under Section 111A\n- STCG on listed equity (STT paid) → **20%**.\n- No deduction under 80C–80U against this STCG.\n\n### 11.2.7 Normal STCG\nIf conditions of 111A aren't met (e.g., no STT) → STCG taxed at **normal slab rates**.\n\n**Table 11.1 — Listed equity capital gains:**\n| Nature | STT paid | STT NOT paid |\n|---|---|---|\n| Long-term | 12.50% (first ₹1.25 lakh exempt) | 12.50% |\n| Short-term | 20% | slab rate |\n\n**Surcharge on 111A/112/112A capital gains** is capped: Nil up to ₹50 lakh; 10% (₹50L–1cr); **15% above ₹1 crore** (does not rise to 25%/37% like other income).\n\n**💡 Worked example (Mr. X):** 1,000 ABC shares bought 01-06-2023 @₹105, sold 24-07-2024 @₹107 → held 13+ months → **LTCG ₹2,000 @12.50% (within ₹1.25L so no tax)**. 1,000 XYZ bought 01-10-2023 @₹120, sold 01-08-2024 @₹135 → held 10 months → **STCG ₹15,000 @20% (Sec 111A)**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.3 Unlisted Equity Shares\n- **Short-term if held ≤24 months; long-term if >24 months.**\n- **LTCG @12.50%** (+ surcharge/cess); **STCG at slab rates**.\n- Dividend taxed same as listed shares.\n- If unlisted shares are sold in an **IPO**, the gain is taxed under **Section 112A**.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Same ABC/XYZ shares but unlisted, both sold within 24 months → both are **short-term, taxed at slab rates** (not 20%, because not listed/STT).\n\n---\n\n## 11.4 Preference Shares\nPreference shares = priority on **fixed dividend** and **repayment on winding-up**.\n- Dividend taxed like equity shares.\n- **Listed:** long-term after 12 months. **Unlisted:** after 24 months. LTCG @12.50%, STCG at applicable rates.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 1,000 listed pref shares @₹105 (01-07-2018), sold @₹120 (25-07-2024). Held >12 months → LTCG = ₹15,000 @12.50% = **₹1,875 tax**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.5 Share Warrants\nA **share warrant** gives the holder the right (not obligation) to buy shares at a fixed **strike price** later. Holder pays ≥25% upfront; tenure ≤18 months. If not exercised within 3 months of full payment, the money is **forfeited**.\n\nThree possible events:\n1. **Conversion into shares** = treated as transfer of the warrant. Gain = FMV of shares (Sec 50D) − strike price. STCG if held ≤12 months (listed), LTCG @12.50% if >12 months.\n2. **Transfer of the warrant** = transfer of capital asset. Gain = sale price − upfront price paid.\n3. **Forfeiture** = the loss is **ignored** (no tax treatment).\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 1,00,000 warrants @₹200 (strike) bought 29-12-2023, ₹50 lakh paid upfront (25%).\n- *Scenario 1 (exercised 29-03-2025, FMV ₹250):* held >12 months → **LTCG = (250−200)×1,00,000 = ₹50,00,000 @12.50%**.\n- *Scenario 2 (transferred to Mr. Y @₹75, 15-08-2024):* held <12 months → **STCG = (75−50)×1,00,000 = ₹25,00,000 at slab rate**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.6 Equity-Oriented Mutual Funds\n\n**Equity-Oriented Fund** = a fund (or qualifying ULIP that loses 10(10D) exemption) where:\n- (a) if it invests via another exchange-traded fund: ≥90% in that fund, which itself invests ≥90% in listed domestic equity; OR\n- (b) otherwise: ≥**65%** in listed domestic equity shares.\n\nKey MF terms: **FoF** (fund of funds), **ELSS** (tax-saving, 3-yr lock-in, 80C up to ₹1.5 lakh, old regime), **SIP** (regular investing), **SWP** (regular withdrawal — gains taxable), **STP** (transfer between schemes of same AMC).\n\n### Tax treatment\n- **Dividend:** resident slab (20%-of-dividend interest deduction); non-resident/foreign co. flat **20%** (10% if from IFSC) + surcharge/cess, subject to DTAA.\n- **LTCG (Sec 112A):** units long-term after **12 months**; **exempt up to ₹1,25,000**, excess @12.50% (needs STT on transfer, unless IFSC foreign-currency).\n- **Grandfathering** for units bought on/before 31-01-2018, same higher-of mechanism.\n- **STCG (Sec 111A):** **20%** if STT paid; else slab.\n\n**💡 Worked example (grandfathering):** 5,000 units bought 01-05-2017 @₹200, FMV 31-01-2018 = ₹225, sold 01-08-2024 @₹300.\n- Cost = higher of [₹10,00,000 actual] and [lower of (FMV ₹11,25,000) and (sale ₹15,00,000)] = **₹11,25,000**.\n- LTCG = 15,00,000 − 11,25,000 = **₹3,75,000 @12.50%** (over ₹1.25 lakh).\n\n**💡 Worked example (dividend + LTCG):** 10,000 units @₹250, dividend ₹50/unit, sold @₹280.\n- Dividend ₹5,00,000; interest paid ₹1,60,000 → deduction capped ₹1,00,000 → **taxable dividend ₹4,00,000**.\n- LTCG = (280−250)×10,000 = **₹3,00,000 @12.50%** (STT paid).\n\n---\n\n## 11.7 Derivatives (F&O)\n\nA **derivative** derives its value from an underlying asset. Types: **Futures** (obligation to buy/sell at a future date at a set price), **Options** (right not obligation — Call = buy, Put = sell; buyer pays **premium**), **Forwards** (OTC customized), **Swaps** (exchange cash flows — interest-rate or currency).\n\n### 11.7.2 Nature of derivative income\n- F&O gains/losses are **business income (PGBP)** (or other sources), depending on whether you trade as a business.\n- F&O is **non-speculative** business (Section 43(5) excludes it from \"speculative\") even though there's no delivery — *provided* traded on a recognised exchange via a SEBI-registered broker with a time-stamped contract note showing client ID + PAN.\n- **Speculative loss** can be set off only against speculative income; **non-speculative F&O loss** can be set off against any normal business income.\n\n### 11.7.3 Turnover (ICAI Guidance Note)\n- Turnover = total of **favourable + unfavourable differences** + **premium received on sale of options** + reverse-trade differences.\n- Matters for **tax audit** and **presumptive scheme (Sec 44AD)**.\n\n### 11.7.4 Presumptive scheme (Sec 44AD)\n- Available if turnover ≤ **₹2 crore (or ₹3 crore)**.\n- Declare profit at **6%** of turnover (digital receipts) or 8% (cash). No books, no audit; can pay 100% advance tax by 15 March.\n\n### 11.7.5 Set-off & carry forward\n- Non-speculative business loss → set off against any head **except salary**.\n- Carry forward up to **8 assessment years**, set off only against **business income**.\n- ⚠️ **Must file return by due date** or you lose the carry-forward right.\n\n**💡 Worked example (Mr. X, call option, lot 1,000, strike ₹450, premium ₹35):**\n- *Exercised then sold @₹550:* Business income = 550,000 − (450+35)×1,000 = **₹65,000**.\n- *Transferred @₹30:* Loss = (30−35)×1,000 = **−₹5,000**.\n- *Transferred @₹37:* Gain = (37−35)×1,000 = **₹2,000**.\n- *Expired (premium ₹10):* Loss = (10−35)×1,000 = **−₹25,000**.\n\nAll taxed/set off as normal business income.\n\n---\n\n## 11.8 Bonus Stripping (Section 94(8))\n**The trick:** Buy units just before the record date, get bonus units, then sell the original units cheaply to book an artificial short-term loss (while keeping the free bonus units).\n\n**The rule:** If you (a) buy securities/units within **3 months before** the record date, (b) get bonus units, and (c) sell the original within **9 months after** the record date while still holding the bonus units — the **loss on the original is ignored**. That ignored loss becomes the **cost of acquisition of the bonus units**.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 1,000 shares @₹106 (01-07-2023), 1:1 bonus on 01-09-2023, original 1,000 sold @₹95 (01-04-2024).\n- Loss = (106−95)×1,000 = **₹11,000 ignored**.\n- Becomes cost of the 1,000 bonus shares → cost per bonus share = **₹11**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.9 Benefits NOT allowed from capital gains\n- **No deduction under 80C–80U** from LTCG @12.50% (Sec 112/112A) or STCG @20% (Sec 111A).\n- **No foreign-currency computation** for the 12.50% LTCG.\n- **No Section 87A rebate** against 112A LTCG (rebate allowed only on the rest of total income).\n\n## 11.10 Adjustment of exemption limit\nA resident individual/HUF can use their **basic exemption limit** to absorb part of their 111A/112/112A capital gains, if other income falls short of the limit.\n\n**💡 Example:** Other income ₹1,85,000; LTCG on unlisted shares ₹2,50,000. Basic exemption ₹2,50,000. Shortfall ₹65,000 absorbs ₹65,000 of LTCG → only **₹1,85,000 of LTCG is taxed**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Listed equity:** LTCG (>12 mth) @12.50% over ₹1.25L (112A, STT); STCG @20% (111A, STT). No STT → 12.50% / slab.\n- **Unlisted equity:** LTCG (>24 mth) @12.50% (112); STCG at slab.\n- **Grandfathering (≤31-01-2018):** cost = higher of actual vs (lower of FMV-31Jan2018 and sale).\n- **Dividend:** resident slab (20% interest deduction); non-resident 20%, no deduction.\n- **Warrants:** conversion/transfer = transfer; forfeiture loss ignored.\n- **Equity MF:** like listed equity (112A/111A, STT).\n- **F&O:** non-speculative business income; loss set off against any head except salary; carry forward 8 yrs (file on time).\n- **Bonus stripping (94(8)):** ignored loss shifts to bonus units' cost.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term/Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **₹1,25,000** | Annual LTCG exemption under Section 112A |\n| **12.50%** | LTCG rate (112A excess / 112) |\n| **20%** | STCG rate under Section 111A (STT paid) |\n| **12 months** | Listed equity / equity MF long-term threshold |\n| **24 months** | Unlisted shares long-term threshold |\n| **31-01-2018** | Grandfathering FMV date |\n| **Section 45(2A) / FIFO** | Demat holding period method |\n| **Section 43(5)** | Excludes exchange F&O from \"speculative\" |\n| **Section 44AD** | Presumptive: 6%/8% of turnover (≤₹2cr/₹3cr) |\n| **8 assessment years** | Business-loss carry-forward period |\n| **Section 94(8)** | Bonus stripping — ignored loss → bonus cost |\n| **Section 87A** | Rebate NOT allowed against 112A LTCG |\n| **15% surcharge cap** | On 111A/112/112A gains above ₹1 crore |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 11: Taxation of Equity Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What two types of income come from equity investments?\n> **A:** **Dividend** (share of company profits) and **capital gains** (selling above cost).\n\n**Q2.** In whose hands is dividend taxed after Finance Act 2020?\n> **A:** In the **shareholder's** hands (Dividend Distribution Tax abolished from FY 2020-21).\n\n**Q3.** When is a final dividend treated as income?\n> **A:** In the year it is **declared at the AGM** (date of receipt is irrelevant). Interim dividend is on receipt basis.\n\n**Q4.** What is the LTCG rate and annual exemption under Section 112A?\n> **A:** LTCG over **₹1,25,000** taxed @**12.50%** (+ surcharge/cess); first ₹1.25 lakh exempt.\n\n**Q5.** What is the STCG rate under Section 111A?\n> **A:** **20%** (when STT is paid / IFSC foreign-currency transfer). Else, normal slab rates.\n\n**Q6.** What conditions must be met for Section 112A to apply?\n> **A:** STT paid on **both acquisition and transfer** of listed equity (with listed exceptions like IFSC, pre-01-10-2004 shares, ESOPs, court/SEBI/RBI-approved acquisitions).\n\n**Q7.** Holding period to make listed equity shares long-term?\n> **A:** More than **12 months**.\n\n**Q8.** Holding period to make unlisted equity shares long-term?\n> **A:** More than **24 months**.\n\n**Q9.** How is the demat holding period determined?\n> **A:** By **FIFO** (Section 45(2A)) — first shares in are first out — applied **account-wise**.\n\n**Q10.** Grandfathering: how is the cost of a share bought on/before 31-01-2018 found?\n> **A:** **Higher of** (a) actual cost; or (b) **lower of** [FMV on 31-01-2018] and [sale consideration].\n\n**Q11.** Share bought @₹100, FMV 31-01-2018 = ₹200, sold @₹150. What's the LTCG?\n> **A:** Cost = lower of (₹200 FMV, ₹150 sale) = ₹150 → LTCG = 150−150 = **NIL** (grandfathering won't create a loss).\n\n**Q12.** Share @₹100, FMV ₹200, sold @₹50. Gain/loss?\n> **A:** Cost = actual ₹100 (since sale < FMV and < cost) → **LTCG loss ₹50**.\n\n**Q13.** How is LTCG taxed when STT is NOT paid on listed equity (Section 112)?\n> **A:** **12.50%**, but **without** the ₹1.25 lakh exemption.\n\n**Q14.** How is dividend taxed for a non-resident?\n> **A:** At a **special rate** (typically 20%, 10% if IFSC) subject to DTAA, with **no deduction** and **no Chapter VIA (80C–80U)**.\n\n**Q15.** What is STT and who collects it?\n> **A:** **Securities Transaction Tax** on buy/sell of securities (not debt), collected by the exchange/MF trustee/merchant banker and paid to Govt by the 7th of the next month.\n\n**Q16.** Long-term holding period for preference shares (listed vs unlisted)?\n> **A:** Listed: **12 months**; Unlisted: **24 months**. LTCG @12.50%.\n\n**Q17.** What is a share warrant?\n> **A:** A right (not obligation) to buy shares at a fixed **strike price** later; holder pays ≥25% upfront, tenure ≤18 months.\n\n**Q18.** How is conversion of a share warrant into shares treated?\n> **A:** As a **transfer of the warrant**. Gain = FMV of shares (Sec 50D) − strike price; STCG if ≤12 months, LTCG @12.50% if >12 months.\n\n**Q19.** What is the tax treatment of forfeiture of warrant premium?\n> **A:** The loss is **ignored** — no tax treatment.\n\n**Q20.** Define an Equity-Oriented Fund.\n> **A:** A fund investing ≥**65%** in listed domestic equity (or via ETF route ≥90%/≥90%); qualifying ULIPs without 10(10D) exemption also count.\n\n**Q21.** Tax treatment of equity-oriented MF units — STCG and LTCG?\n> **A:** **STCG @20%** (Sec 111A, STT) and **LTCG @12.50%** over ₹1.25 lakh (Sec 112A), long-term after 12 months.\n\n**Q22.** What is ELSS and its key features?\n> **A:** Equity Linked Savings Scheme — 80C deduction up to **₹1.5 lakh** (old regime), **3-year lock-in** (shortest among tax-savers).\n\n**Q23.** Under which head is F&O income taxed?\n> **A:** **Profits and Gains of Business or Profession (PGBP)** (or other sources) — treated as **non-speculative** business under Section 43(5).\n\n**Q24.** Why is exchange-traded F&O treated as non-speculative despite no delivery?\n> **A:** Section 43(5) specifically excludes it, provided it's on a recognised exchange via a SEBI-registered broker with a time-stamped contract note (client ID + PAN).\n\n**Q25.** How is F&O turnover computed (ICAI Guidance Note)?\n> **A:** Total of favourable + unfavourable differences + premium received on sale of options + reverse-trade differences.\n\n**Q26.** Under Section 44AD presumptive scheme, what profit can an F&O trader declare?\n> **A:** **6%** of turnover (digital receipts) or 8% (cash), if turnover ≤ ₹2 crore (or ₹3 crore). No books, no audit.\n\n**Q27.** Against what can a non-speculative F&O business loss be set off, and for how long carried forward?\n> **A:** Any head **except salary** in the same year; carried forward **8 assessment years**, set off only against business income (return must be filed on time).\n\n**Q28.** What is bonus stripping and which section curbs it?\n> **A:** Buying just before a bonus record date and selling the original at a loss for an artificial deduction. **Section 94(8)** ignores that loss.\n\n**Q29.** Under Section 94(8), what is the 3-month/9-month rule?\n> **A:** Buy within **3 months before** record date, get bonus, sell original within **9 months after** while still holding bonus → loss ignored, added to bonus units' cost.\n\n**Q30.** 1,000 shares @₹106, 1:1 bonus, original sold @₹95. What is the cost of the bonus shares under 94(8)?\n> **A:** Ignored loss = (106−95)×1,000 = ₹11,000 → cost of 1,000 bonus shares = **₹11,000 (₹11/share)**.\n\n**Q31.** Is the Section 87A rebate available against 112A LTCG?\n> **A:** **No** — rebate is allowed only on tax for the rest of total income, not on 112A LTCG.\n\n**Q32.** What is the surcharge cap on capital gains under 111A/112/112A above ₹1 crore?\n> **A:** **15%** (does not climb to 25%/37% as it does for other income).\n\n**Q33.** Can a resident individual use the basic exemption limit against capital gains?\n> **A:** **Yes** — if other income is below the basic exemption limit, the shortfall reduces the 111A/112/112A gains chargeable to tax.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 11: Taxation of Equity Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> This bank has three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy: recall of facts/definitions), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium: applying rules and comparing treatments), and 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard: multi-step numerical and scenario problems). The exam carries **25% negative marking** (a wrong answer costs you one-quarter of a mark), so do not guess blindly — eliminate options first.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Equity investment generally produces which two types of income?\nA) Capital gains and dividend income  B) Interest and rent  C) Commission and brokerage  D) Salary and bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The chapter states that two types of income are earned from equity products: capital gains (selling above cost) and dividend income (a share of company profits). B) interest/rent are debt and property incomes, not equity. C) brokerage and commission are charges/expenses, not income to the investor. D) salary/bonus are employment incomes, unrelated to equity ownership.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Dividend declared at an Annual General Meeting (AGM) is deemed to be the income of the shareholder in the previous year in which it is:\nA) Received  B) Credited to bank  C) Declared  D) Reinvested\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A final dividend declared at the AGM is income of the year it is *declared*; the date the assessee actually receives it is not material. A) and B) describe receipt, which is the rule for *interim* dividends (taxed when unconditionally made available), not final dividends. D) reinvestment is irrelevant to the timing of taxability.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Dividend Distribution Tax (DDT) was abolished by which Finance Act, shifting dividend tax to the shareholder?\nA) Finance Act, 2020  B) Finance Act, 2018  C) Finance Act, 2024  D) Finance Act, 2025\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The Finance Act, 2020 abolished DDT with effect from FY 2020-21, so dividends are now taxable in the hands of shareholders. B) Finance Act 2018 made LTCG on listed shares taxable (Sec 112A). C) Finance Act 2024 set the 12.50%/20% rates and the ₹1.25 lakh limit. D) Finance Act 2025 amended ULIP definitions. Only 2020 dealt with DDT.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Securities Transaction Tax (STT) is levied on the sale/purchase of:\nA) Securities other than debt securities or debt mutual funds  B) All securities including debt securities  C) Only unlisted shares  D) Only derivatives\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — STT applies to securities *other than* debt securities or debt mutual funds. B) is wrong because debt securities are specifically excluded. C) is wrong — STT applies mainly to listed/exchange-traded securities, not unlisted shares. D) is too narrow; STT covers equity delivery, equity MF and derivatives, not derivatives alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** STT collected during a calendar month must be remitted to the Central Government by which date of the following month?\nA) Last day  B) 15th  C) 1st  D) 7th\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STT collected in a month must be paid to the Central Government by the 7th of the immediately following month. A), B) and C) are incorrect dates not stated in the chapter; the rule is the 7th.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** SEBI turnover charges are levied at the rate of:\nA) ₹10 per crore  B) ₹100 per crore  C) 18% of brokerage  D) ₹10 per lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SEBI charges a turnover fee of ₹10 per crore for regulating markets, levied on both buy and sell sides. B) and D) are wrong amounts. C) 18% is the GST rate on brokerage/transaction/clearing charges, not the SEBI turnover charge.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** GST on brokerage, exchange transaction charges and clearing charges is charged at:\nA) 5%  B) 12%  C) 18%  D) 28%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — GST is charged at 18% on brokerage, transaction and clearing charges. A), B) and D) are other GST slabs that do not apply here; the chapter specifies 18%.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The two stock depositories in India are:\nA) NSDL and CDSL  B) BSE and NSE  C) MCX and NCDEX  D) SEBI and RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — NSDL (National Securities Depository Limited) and CDSL (Central Depository Services Limited) are the two depositories that hold securities in electronic form. B) BSE and NSE are stock *exchanges*, not depositories. C) MCX and NCDEX are commodity exchanges. D) SEBI is the regulator and RBI is the central bank — neither is a depository.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Stamp duty on listed securities has been levied at unified rates across India with effect from:\nA) 1 April 2018  B) 1 October 2004  C) 31 January 2018  D) 1 April 2020\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — From 1 April 2020 stamp duty on listed securities is levied at unified rates across India (the same rate applies even to off-market transactions). A), B) and C) are other significant dates in the chapter (Sec 112A came in 2018; STT-acquisition relief applies to shares before 01-10-2004; grandfathering FMV date is 31-01-2018) but not the stamp-duty unification date.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** For listed equity shares, the holding period to qualify as a long-term capital asset is more than:\nA) 36 months  B) 24 months  C) 12 months  D) 6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity shares are short-term if held for not more than 12 months; held beyond 12 months they become long-term. A) and B) apply to other assets (24 months for unlisted shares). D) 6 months is not used for equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** For unlisted equity shares, the long-term holding threshold is more than:\nA) 12 months  B) 36 months  C) 24 months  D) 48 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Unlisted shares are short-term if held for not more than 24 months, and long-term if held for more than 24 months. A) 12 months is the listed-share threshold. B) and D) are not used here.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The period of holding for securities held in Demat form is determined using which method?\nA) LIFO  B) Weighted average  C) FIFO  D) Specific identification\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 45(2A) prescribes the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) method: the securities that entered the Demat account first are treated as sold first. A) LIFO (last-in-first-out) is the opposite and is not used. B) weighted average and D) specific identification are not prescribed for Demat holding period.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** FIFO under Section 45(2A) for Demat securities is applied:\nA) Across all accounts combined  B) Only to physical shares  C) Only on the exchange's records  D) Account-wise\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Where an investor has more than one security account, FIFO is applied account-wise, because securities in another account cannot be treated as sold when a different account is debited. A) is wrong as accounts are not combined. B) is wrong — FIFO is specifically for Demat (electronic) holdings. C) is incorrect; it is applied per the investor's Demat accounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Long-term capital gains on listed equity shares under Section 112A are exempt up to an aggregate of how much per year?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the Finance Act 2024, no tax is charged on Section 112A LTCG up to ₹1,25,000 in a year; the excess is taxed at 12.50%. A) ₹1,00,000 was the old limit before the change. B) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C deduction cap. C) ₹2,50,000 is the basic exemption limit, not the 112A limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The concessional rate of tax on LTCG exceeding ₹1.25 lakh under Section 112A is:\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 12.50%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG above ₹1.25 lakh under Section 112A is taxed at the concessional rate of 12.50% (plus surcharge and cess). A) 10% was the earlier rate. B) 15% is a surcharge rate. D) 20% is the STCG rate under Section 111A.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Short-term capital gains on listed equity shares (STT paid) are taxed under Section 111A at:\nA) 10%  B) 12.50%  C) 15%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG on listed equity (with STT paid) is taxed at the concessional rate of 20% under Section 111A. A), B) and C) are not the 111A rate; 12.50% is the LTCG rate and 15% is a surcharge cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Earlier, LTCG on listed equity shares were exempt under which section before being made taxable in 2018?\nA) Section 10(38)  B) Section 10(10D)  C) Section 80C  D) Section 54\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG on listed equity shares was earlier exempt under Section 10(38) until the Finance Act 2018 made it taxable under Section 112A. B) Section 10(10D) deals with life insurance/ULIP proceeds. C) Section 80C is a deduction provision. D) Section 54 deals with reinvestment exemptions on property.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The grandfathering of equity investments applies to shares acquired on or before:\nA) 1 October 2004  B) 1 April 2020  C) 23 July 2024  D) 31 January 2018\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Finance Act 2018 grandfathered investments made on or before 31-01-2018, since LTCG was tax-free until then. A) 01-10-2004 relates to STT-acquisition relief. B) 01-04-2020 is the stamp-duty/DDT date. C) 23 July 2024 is the date from which the new rates apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** For grandfathering of a listed share, the fair market value as on 31-01-2018 is taken as:\nA) The lowest price quoted that day  B) The average price that day  C) The face value  D) The highest price quoted on a recognised exchange that day\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For listed shares, FMV on 31-01-2018 is the highest price quoted on a recognised stock exchange that day (or the last traded day before if there was no trading). A) lowest and B) average are not used. C) face value is unrelated to FMV.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** LTCG on unlisted equity shares is taxed at:\nA) 10%  B) 20%  C) 12.50%  D) Slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG on unlisted equity shares is taxable at 12.50% (plus surcharge and cess) under Section 112. A) 10% is outdated. B) 20% is the STCG 111A rate (which needs STT/listing — unavailable for unlisted). D) slab rates apply to *short-term* unlisted gains, not long-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Short-term capital gain on unlisted equity shares is taxed at:\nA) 12.50%  B) 20%  C) Normal slab rates  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG on unlisted shares is taxed at the normal slab rates applicable to the assessee, because Section 111A (20%) requires the shares to be listed with STT paid. A) and B) are concessional rates that need listing/STT. D) is wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Preference shares carry a preferential right to dividend and to:\nA) Voting on every resolution  B) Repayment of capital on winding-up  C) Free bonus shares  D) Conversion into debentures\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Preference share capital carries a preferential right to payment of dividend (fixed amount or rate) and to repayment of capital in case of winding-up. A) preference shares typically do not carry general voting rights. C) and D) are not defining features of preference share capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A share warrant gives the holder:\nA) An obligation to buy shares  B) A guaranteed dividend  C) Ownership of the company immediately  D) A right to subscribe to equity shares at a pre-determined price on/after a pre-determined time\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A share warrant is an option giving the holder a *right* (not an obligation) to subscribe to equity shares at a pre-determined strike price on or after a set time. A) is wrong because it is a right, not an obligation. B) warrants do not pay dividends. C) ownership arises only when the warrant is exercised into shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A warrant holder is required to pay upfront at least what percentage of the strike price (per SEBI ICDR Regulation 13)?\nA) 10%  B) 50%  C) 100%  D) 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Regulation 13 of SEBI (ICDR), 2018 requires the warrant holder to pay at least 25% of the strike price upfront. A), B) and C) are incorrect figures; the minimum upfront is 25%.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The tenure of share warrants shall not exceed how long from the date of allotment?\nA) 12 months  B) 18 months  C) 24 months  D) 36 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The tenure of share warrants shall not exceed 18 months from the date of their allotment in the IPO/Rights Issue/FPO. A), C) and D) are incorrect; the cap is 18 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** If a warrant holder does not exercise the option within how many months from full payment, the consideration is forfeited?\nA) 1 month  B) 2 months  C) 3 months  D) 6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If the holder does not exercise the warrants within 3 months from the date of full payment, the consideration is forfeited by the issuer. A), B) and D) are incorrect; the period is 3 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The loss arising from forfeiture of premium paid on a share warrant is:\nA) A short-term capital loss  B) A long-term capital loss  C) Ignored for tax purposes  D) A business loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The loss arising from forfeiture of the warrant premium has no tax treatment and is ignored while computing taxable income. A), B) and D) are wrong because no capital or business loss is recognised on forfeiture.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An equity-oriented fund (general case) must invest a minimum of what percentage in equity shares of domestic listed companies?\nA) 35%  B) 50%  C) 65%  D) 90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In the general case, an equity-oriented fund must invest a minimum of 65% of total proceeds in equity shares of domestic companies listed on a recognised exchange. A) 35% relates to hybrid/specified-fund thresholds. B) 50% is not a defined limit. D) 90% applies only to the special fund-of-fund route (≥90% in another fund which itself holds ≥90% equity).\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** ELSS funds carry a compulsory lock-in period of:\nA) 1 year  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 7 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity Linked Saving Scheme (ELSS) funds have a compulsory 3-year lock-in, the shortest among tax-saving instruments. A), C) and D) are incorrect; ELSS lock-in is 3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Investment in ELSS qualifies for deduction under Section 80C up to:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,25,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 80C allows a deduction up to ₹1.5 lakh for ELSS investment, available if the assessee opts for the old tax regime. A) ₹1,00,000 was an old 112A limit. B) ₹1,25,000 is the current 112A LTCG exemption. D) ₹2,00,000 is not the 80C limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A Systematic Transfer Plan (STP) can transfer money between:\nA) Two banks  B) Any two AMCs  C) Equity and debt of different fund houses  D) Two mutual fund schemes of the same AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An STP transfers a fixed amount between two mutual fund schemes of the same Asset Management Company (AMC). A) banks are not involved. B) and C) are wrong because STP works only within the same AMC, not across fund houses.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Units of an equity-oriented fund are treated as long-term capital assets if held for more than:\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) 48 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Units of equity-oriented funds become long-term after a holding period of more than 12 months, the same as listed equity shares. B) 24 months applies to unlisted shares. C) and D) are not used for equity MF units.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** F&O (derivatives) income, when traded as business, is generally treated as:\nA) Capital gains  B) Salary income  C) Speculative business income  D) Non-speculative business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 43(5) excludes exchange-traded derivatives from \"speculative\", so F&O income/loss is treated as normal (non-speculative) business income, even without delivery. C) is wrong because of the 43(5) exclusion. A) is wrong — F&O is business income, not capital gains. B) salary is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Under bonus stripping (Section 94(8)), the ignored loss on the original units becomes the:\nA) Cost of acquisition of the bonus units  B) A carried-forward capital loss  C) A deduction under 80C  D) Dividend income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The loss ignored under Section 94(8) is deemed to be the cost of acquisition of the bonus securities/units held on the date of transfer of the originals. B) is wrong — the loss is not carried forward; it shifts to bonus cost. C) and D) are unrelated tax items.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Section 87A rebate is:\nA) Allowed against LTCG covered under Section 112A  B) Not allowed against LTCG covered under Section 112A  C) Allowed only against dividend income  D) Allowed against STCG under 111A only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Section 87A rebate is *not* available against tax payable on LTCG covered under Section 112A; it is allowed only on the tax on the remaining total income. A) directly contradicts this rule. C) and D) misstate the scope — the rule specifically denies the rebate on 112A LTCG.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. A buys listed equity shares and sells them after 10 months (STT paid on both legs). The gain is:\nA) LTCG taxed at 12.50%  B) STCG taxed at slab rates  C) Exempt  D) STCG taxed at 20% under Section 111A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Listed shares held 10 months (≤12) are short-term; with STT paid, STCG is taxed at 20% under Section 111A. A) needs >12 months holding to be long-term. B) slab rates apply only when STT/listing conditions fail. C) is wrong — STCG on equity is not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mr. B sells listed equity shares held for 18 months with STT paid. His total 112A LTCG for the year is ₹90,000. Tax on this LTCG is:\nA) 12.50% of ₹90,000  B) 20% of ₹90,000  C) Nil  D) 10% of ₹90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG under Section 112A is exempt up to ₹1,25,000 a year. Since ₹90,000 < ₹1,25,000, no tax is payable. A), B) and D) all wrongly tax an amount that falls within the exempt slab; only the excess over ₹1.25 lakh is taxed at 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A resident shareholder receives dividend of ₹2,00,000 and pays ₹60,000 interest on a loan taken to buy those shares. The maximum interest deduction allowed is:\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹20,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest deduction against dividend is capped at 20% of the dividend income. Formula: deduction = min(actual interest, 20% × dividend). Here 20% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹40,000, which is less than the ₹60,000 actually paid, so only ₹40,000 is allowed. A) ignores the cap. C) misapplies the percentage. D) is wrong — some deduction is allowed.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Which of the following is taxed as business income rather than capital gains?\nA) Sale of listed equity held 2 years  B) Dividend from preference shares  C) Sale of unlisted shares held 3 years  D) Gain on F&O trading on a recognised exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — F&O (derivatives) gains are taxed as business income (PGBP) or other sources. A) and C) are capital gains (LTCG). B) is dividend income taxed at slab rates. Only D) is business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Compared with listed equity shares, unlisted equity shares differ mainly in that they:\nA) Have no capital gains tax  B) Are always exempt up to ₹1.25 lakh  C) Are taxed only on dividend  D) Use a 24-month long-term threshold and STCG at slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unlisted shares use a 24-month (not 12-month) long-term threshold, and their STCG is taxed at slab rates (since 111A's 20% needs listing/STT). A) is wrong — they are taxable. B) is wrong — the ₹1.25 lakh exemption (112A) needs listing/STT, generally unavailable for unlisted. C) is wrong — capital gains are also taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** When unlisted equity shares are offered for sale under an IPO, the gain is taxed:\nA) At slab rates always  B) Under Section 112A  C) As business income  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where unlisted shares are offered for sale under an IPO, the gain is chargeable under Section 112A. A) and D) are incorrect; the IPO route specifically attracts 112A. C) is wrong — it is capital gains, not business income.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** The conversion of a share warrant into shares is treated for tax purposes as:\nA) A non-taxable event  B) Dividend income  C) A forfeiture  D) A transfer of the share warrant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Conversion of a warrant into shares is treated as a transfer of the warrant; capital gain = FMV of shares (Sec 50D) minus the strike price. A) is wrong — it is a taxable transfer. B) and C) are unrelated events.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** On conversion of a listed warrant into shares after 14 months, the resulting gain is:\nA) Short-term, slab rate  B) Short-term, 20%  C) Long-term, 12.50%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A listed warrant converted after more than 12 months gives long-term capital gain taxed at 12.50%. A) and B) describe short-term treatment, which applies only if converted within 12 months. D) is wrong — the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** On conversion of a warrant into shares, the full value of consideration (per Section 50D) is:\nA) The strike price  B) The upfront 25% paid  C) The fair market value of the shares on the date of conversion  D) The face value of shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 50D deems the full value of consideration to be the FMV of the shares on the date of conversion. A) strike price is the *cost*, not the consideration. B) the upfront 25% is part of the price paid, not the sale value. D) face value is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** On a simple transfer (sale) of a share warrant to another person, the cost of acquisition is:\nA) FMV of underlying shares  B) The strike price  C) Nil  D) The upfront payment/price paid for the warrant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — On transfer of the warrant itself, gain = sale price minus the upfront price paid for the warrant. A) FMV of shares applies on conversion, not on a plain transfer. B) strike price applies to conversion. C) nil cost is not correct here.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A non-resident receives dividend from a domestic company. Which is true?\nA) Slab rates apply with full deductions  B) Always exempt  C) Taxed only under DTAA at 30%  D) Taxed at special rates, no expenditure deduction, no Chapter VIA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For non-residents, dividend is taxed at special rates (subject to DTAA), with no expenditure deduction and no Chapter VIA (80C–80U) deductions. A) is the resident treatment (with a capped interest deduction). B) is wrong — it is taxable. C) misstates DTAA — most DTAAs give 5%–15%, not a flat 30%.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Under most DTAAs India has signed, dividend in the source country is taxed in the range of:\nA) 5% to 15%  B) 0% to 5%  C) 20% to 30%  D) 30% to 40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Most DTAAs tax dividend in the source country at 5% to 15% of the gross dividend in the hands of the beneficial owner. B), C) and D) are outside the stated range.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** F&O loss treated as non-speculative business loss can be set off against:\nA) Salary income  B) Any head except salary  C) Only speculative income  D) Only capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Non-speculative F&O business loss can be set off against income under any head except salary. A) is wrong — salary is specifically excluded. C) describes a speculative loss restriction. D) is too narrow — set-off is broader than capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Unabsorbed F&O (business) loss can be carried forward for up to:\nA) 4 assessment years  B) Indefinitely  C) 2 assessment years  D) 8 assessment years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unabsorbed business loss can be carried forward up to 8 assessment years, set off only against business income. A), B) and C) are incorrect periods; the limit is 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** To carry forward an F&O business loss, the assessee must:\nA) Pay advance tax  B) File the return on or before the due date  C) Maintain audited books always  D) Get SEBI approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The right to carry forward and set off business loss is preserved only if the return is filed on or before the due date; missing it forfeits the carry-forward. A) advance tax does not affect carry-forward. C) audit depends on turnover, not carry-forward. D) SEBI approval is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Under the ICAI Guidance Note, F&O turnover includes the premium received on:\nA) Sale of options  B) Purchase of options  C) Delivery of shares  D) Dividend distribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Turnover includes the total of favourable and unfavourable differences plus the premium received on *sale* of options (and reverse-trade differences). B) premium *paid* on purchase is not separately added as turnover. C) and D) are unrelated to F&O turnover.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Under the presumptive scheme (Section 44AD) for digital/cheque receipts, profit is presumed at:\nA) 6% of turnover  B) 8% of turnover  C) 12.50% of turnover  D) 50% of turnover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For receipts through banking/digital modes, profit is presumed at 6% of turnover (8% for cash receipts). B) 8% applies to cash receipts. C) and D) are not the 44AD rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Section 44AD presumptive scheme is available if total turnover does not exceed:\nA) ₹1 crore  B) ₹2 crore (or ₹3 crore)  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The presumptive scheme under Section 44AD is available subject to total turnover not exceeding ₹2 crore (or ₹3 crore, as the case may be). A), C) and D) are not the prescribed thresholds for 44AD as stated in the chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A person opting for Section 44AD presumptive scheme can pay 100% of advance tax in a single instalment by:\nA) 15 June  B) 15 September  C) 15 December  D) 15 March\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the presumptive scheme the person can pay 100% of advance tax in a single instalment up to 15 March of the relevant financial year. A), B) and C) are the normal quarterly advance-tax dates that do not apply to 44AD assessees.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A speculative business loss can be set off only against:\nA) Any business income  B) Speculative income  C) Capital gains  D) Salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A speculative loss can be set off only against speculative income. A) is wrong — that is the rule for non-speculative losses. C) and D) cannot absorb a speculative loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which condition makes a derivative transaction NON-speculative under Section 43(5)?\nA) Settled without delivery off-exchange  B) Carried on a recognised exchange through a SEBI-registered broker with a time-stamped contract note showing client ID and PAN  C) Done privately between two parties  D) Settled in cash overseas\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exchange-traded derivatives are non-speculative when carried on a recognised exchange via a SEBI-registered intermediary with a time-stamped contract note showing the unique client ID and PAN. A), C) and D) describe off-exchange/private dealings that do not meet the 43(5) conditions.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** For bonus stripping under Section 94(8) to apply, the original units must be acquired within:\nA) 3 months before the record date  B) 9 months before the record date  C) 3 months after the record date  D) 12 months before the record date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 94(8) applies if the person acquires securities/units within 3 months *before* the record date and is allotted bonus units on that date. B) and D) are wrong windows for acquisition. C) describes a period after, not before, the record date.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Bonus stripping rules ignore the loss only if the original units are sold within:\nA) 3 months after the record date  B) 6 months after the record date  C) 9 months after the record date  D) 12 months after the record date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The loss is ignored if the original units are transferred within 9 months *after* the record date while the person continues to hold the bonus units. A), B) and D) are incorrect windows; the rule uses 9 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** The surcharge on capital gains under Sections 111A/112/112A is capped at a maximum of:\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 25%  D) 37%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surcharge on 111A/112/112A capital gains is capped at 15% (it does not rise to 25%/37% as on other income). A) 10% applies in the ₹50 lakh–₹1 crore band. C) and D) are the higher surcharge rates that apply to *other* income, not these capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which deduction is NOT allowed against STCG taxed at 20% under Section 111A?\nA) Brokerage on the sale  B) Cost of acquisition  C) Deductions under Sections 80C to 80U  D) Transfer expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No deduction under Sections 80C to 80U is allowed against STCG covered by Section 111A. A), B) and D) (brokerage, cost of acquisition, transfer expenses) are part of computing the gain itself and are allowed; only Chapter VIA deductions are barred.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** For LTCG taxed at 12.50% on shares of an Indian company bought in foreign currency by a non-resident, the foreign-currency computation method is:\nA) Mandatory  B) Optional  C) Not allowed  D) Allowed only for listed shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The foreign-currency mode of computing capital gains is not allowed when the LTCG is taxed at the concessional 12.50% rate. A) and B) wrongly permit it. D) adds a condition not present — it is simply disallowed for the 12.50% LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Dividend received by a non-resident from an equity-oriented mutual fund (not from IFSC) is taxed at a special rate of:\nA) 10%  B) 12.50%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A non-resident's dividend from an equity-oriented MF is taxed at a special rate of 20% (10% if received from an IFSC unit), plus surcharge and cess, subject to DTAA. A) 10% applies only to IFSC-unit dividends. B) is a capital-gains rate. D) 30% is not the stated special rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** STCG on units of an equity-oriented mutual fund, with STT paid at sale, is taxed at:\nA) 12.50%  B) Slab rates  C) Exempt  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG on equity-oriented MF units is taxed at 20% if STT is paid at sale (Section 111A-type treatment). A) is the LTCG rate. B) slab rates apply only if STT is not paid. C) is wrong — STCG is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** If STT is NOT paid at the time of transfer of equity-oriented MF units, the LTCG is taxed at:\nA) Exempt  B) 20%  C) Slab rates  D) 12.50% (without the ₹1.25 lakh exemption)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Even where STT is not paid at transfer, LTCG on equity-oriented MF units is still taxed at 12.50% — but it loses the ₹1.25 lakh exemption that Section 112A grants when STT is paid. A) is wrong — it is taxable. B) is the STCG rate. C) slab rates do not apply to this LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** For a unit of an equity-oriented MF that was NOT listed on a recognised exchange as on 31-01-2018, the FMV for grandfathering is taken as:\nA) The highest quoted price  B) The net asset value (NAV) as on that date  C) The face value  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where the unit was not listed on 31-01-2018, its FMV for grandfathering is the net asset value (NAV) as on that date. A) the highest quoted price applies to *listed* units/shares. C) face value and D) nil are not used.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A resident individual has other income of ₹1,85,000 and LTCG on unlisted shares of ₹2,50,000 (basic exemption ₹2,50,000). The LTCG actually charged to tax is:\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹65,000  C) ₹1,85,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A resident individual can use the unused basic exemption against 111A/112/112A gains. Shortfall = ₹2,50,000 − ₹1,85,000 = ₹65,000. This ₹65,000 absorbs part of the LTCG, so only ₹2,50,000 − ₹65,000 = ₹1,85,000 of LTCG is taxed. A) ignores the absorption. B) wrongly taxes only the shortfall. D) is wrong — some LTCG remains taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these is treated as an \"Over-The-Counter\" (OTC) derivative rather than exchange-traded?\nA) Index futures on NSE  B) Stock options on BSE  C) A customised forward contract between two parties  D) Currency futures on a recognised exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A forward contract is customised and dealt directly between two parties without an exchange mechanism — i.e., OTC. A), B) and D) are standardised, exchange-traded derivatives, not OTC.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A call option gives the holder the right (not obligation) to:\nA) Sell an asset by a certain date at a certain price  B) Buy an asset by a certain date at a certain price  C) Receive a dividend  D) Convert shares into debentures\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A call option gives the right, but not the obligation, to *buy* an asset by a certain date at a stated price. A) describes a *put* option (right to sell). C) and D) are unrelated to options.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which swap involves exchanging both principal and interest in two different currencies?\nA) Interest rate swap  B) Currency swap  C) Equity swap  D) Commodity swap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A currency swap entails swapping both principal and interest between parties, with cash flows in two different currencies. A) an interest rate swap exchanges only interest cash flows in the same currency. C) and D) are not the swaps defined in the chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Most commodity derivative contracts (barring a few like crude oil and natural gas) result in:\nA) Cash settlement only  B) No settlement  C) Compulsory delivery  D) Conversion into equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Almost all commodity contracts result in compulsory delivery, except a few like crude oil and natural gas. A) cash settlement is the exception, not the rule. B) and D) are incorrect — commodity contracts do settle, typically by delivery.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. X bought 1,000 listed shares at ₹105 on 01-06-2023 and sold them at ₹107 on 24-07-2024 (STT paid). What is the nature and amount of the gain?\nA) STCG ₹2,000  B) LTCG ₹2,000  C) STCG ₹15,000  D) LTCG ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding = Jun-2023 to Jul-2024 = more than 12 months → long-term. Gain = (Sale − Cost) × shares = (₹107 − ₹105) × 1,000 = ₹2,000. So LTCG ₹2,000. A) wrongly calls it short-term. C) and D) use the wrong per-share difference (₹15 belongs to a different stock).\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Same Mr. X bought 1,000 listed shares at ₹120 on 01-10-2023 and sold them at ₹135 on 01-08-2024 (STT paid). What is the gain and applicable section?\nA) LTCG ₹15,000, Sec 112A  B) STCG ₹2,000, Sec 111A  C) STCG ₹15,000, Sec 111A  D) LTCG ₹15,000, Sec 112\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Holding = Oct-2023 to Aug-2024 = 10 months (≤12) → short-term. Gain = (₹135 − ₹120) × 1,000 = ₹15,000. With STT paid, STCG falls under Section 111A (20%). A) and D) wrongly treat it as long-term. B) uses the wrong difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** An equity share was bought on 01-01-2017 at ₹100, FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹200, and it was sold on 01-01-2025 at ₹250. What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹150  B) ₹50  C) ₹100  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Grandfathered cost = higher of [actual ₹100] and [lower of (FMV ₹200, sale ₹250) = ₹200] = ₹200. LTCG = sale − cost = ₹250 − ₹200 = ₹50. A) wrongly uses actual cost ₹100. C) and D) misapply the higher-of/lower-of rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A share was bought at ₹100 (01-01-2017), FMV ₹200 on 31-01-2018, and sold at ₹150 on 01-01-2025. What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹50  B) Nil  C) Loss of ₹50  D) ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost = higher of [₹100] and [lower of (FMV ₹200, sale ₹150) = ₹150] = ₹150. LTCG = ₹150 − ₹150 = Nil. The grandfathering rule cannot manufacture a loss here, since the sale (₹150) is above the actual cost (₹100). A) and D) miscompute the cost. C) is wrong — there is no loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A share was bought at ₹100 (01-01-2017), FMV ₹50 on 31-01-2018, and sold at ₹150 on 01-01-2025. What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹100  B) ₹50  C) Nil  D) ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost = higher of [actual ₹100] and [lower of (FMV ₹50, sale ₹150) = ₹50] = ₹100. LTCG = ₹150 − ₹100 = ₹50. Since FMV (₹50) is below the actual cost, the actual cost ₹100 is used. A) and D) miscompute. C) is wrong — a ₹50 gain arises.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A share was bought at ₹100 (01-01-2017), FMV ₹200 on 31-01-2018, and sold at ₹50 on 01-01-2025. What is the result?\nA) LTCG ₹50  B) Nil  C) Long-term loss ₹50  D) LTCG ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cost = higher of [actual ₹100] and [lower of (FMV ₹200, sale ₹50) = ₹50] = ₹100. Result = ₹50 − ₹100 = a long-term loss of ₹50. Since the sale (₹50) is below both the actual cost and FMV, the actual cost ₹100 is used, producing a genuine loss. A), B) and D) miscompute the cost or the sign.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Mr. A bought 5,000 listed equity-MF units at ₹200 on 01-05-2017; FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹225; sold at ₹300 on 01-08-2024 (STT paid). What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹3,75,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sale = 5,000 × ₹300 = ₹15,00,000. Cost = higher of [actual 5,000 × ₹200 = ₹10,00,000] and [lower of (FMV 5,000 × ₹225 = ₹11,25,000, sale ₹15,00,000) = ₹11,25,000] = ₹11,25,000. LTCG = ₹15,00,000 − ₹11,25,000 = ₹3,75,000. A) wrongly uses actual cost. C) ignores cost. D) is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Continuing Q77, the tax on the ₹3,75,000 LTCG (assume it is the only 112A gain) is approximately:\nA) ₹46,875  B) ₹31,250  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹15,625\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable LTCG = gain − ₹1,25,000 exemption = ₹3,75,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,50,000. Tax = 12.50% × ₹2,50,000 = ₹31,250. A) taxes the whole ₹3,75,000 (ignores the exemption). C) and D) use wrong rates/bases.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. X has 10,000 equity-MF units bought at ₹250 (01-04-2021), sold at ₹280 (01-09-2024), STT paid. What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹2,80,000  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bought after 31-01-2018, so cost = actual = 10,000 × ₹250 = ₹25,00,000. Sale = 10,000 × ₹280 = ₹28,00,000. Holding > 12 months → LTCG = ₹28,00,000 − ₹25,00,000 = ₹3,00,000. B) and D) use a wrong per-unit difference. C) is the cost, not the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Continuing Q79, Mr. X received ₹50/unit dividend on 10,000 units and paid ₹1,60,000 interest on a loan used to buy them. The taxable dividend is:\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹3,40,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividend = 10,000 × ₹50 = ₹5,00,000. Interest deduction capped at 20% × ₹5,00,000 = ₹1,00,000 (less than the ₹1,60,000 actually paid, so only ₹1,00,000 allowed). Taxable dividend = ₹5,00,000 − ₹1,00,000 = ₹4,00,000. A) ignores the deduction. C) wrongly deducts full interest. D) is the deduction itself, not the taxable amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Mr. Y has ₹1,80,000 of 112A LTCG (STT paid, holding > 12 months) as his only such gain. The tax payable is:\nA) ₹22,500  B) ₹6,875  C) ₹36,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable LTCG = ₹1,80,000 − ₹1,25,000 exemption = ₹55,000. Tax = 12.50% × ₹55,000 = ₹6,875. A) taxes the full ₹1,80,000. C) uses 20%. D) wrongly assumes the whole gain is exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Mr. Z's STCG on listed shares (STT paid) is ₹4,00,000. The Section 111A tax (ignoring surcharge/cess) is:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹80,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — STCG under Section 111A is taxed at 20%. Tax = 20% × ₹4,00,000 = ₹80,000. A) uses 12.50%. C) uses 10%. D) uses 30%. There is no basic exemption slab on STCG itself (unlike the ₹1.25 lakh 112A exemption).\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Mr. P bought 1,000 listed preference shares at ₹105 (01-07-2018) and sold them at ₹120 (25-07-2024). What is the LTCG and tax?\nA) ₹15,000; tax ₹1,875  B) ₹15,000; tax ₹3,000  C) ₹1,500; tax ₹187  D) ₹15,000; Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed preference shares held > 12 months → LTCG = (₹120 − ₹105) × 1,000 = ₹15,000, taxed at 12.50% = ₹1,875. (The ₹1.25 lakh exemption is a 112A feature for equity shares/units; here Section 112 applies to preference shares at 12.50%.) B) uses 20%. C) uses a wrong difference. D) wrongly exempts it.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** ABC Ltd. issued 1,00,000 warrants at strike ₹200 to Mr. X on 29-12-2023; ₹50 lakh (25%) paid upfront. He exercised on 29-03-2025 when FMV was ₹250. What is the capital gain on conversion?\nA) ₹50,00,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹2,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — On conversion: full value = FMV of shares = 1,00,000 × ₹250 = ₹2,50,00,000; cost = strike price = 1,00,000 × ₹200 = ₹2,00,00,000. Gain = ₹2,50,00,000 − ₹2,00,00,000 = ₹50,00,000. Held > 12 months → LTCG at 12.50%. B) wrongly uses the upfront amount. C) and D) confuse the cost/sale totals with the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Instead of exercising, Mr. X (from Q84) transferred the warrants to Mr. Y at ₹75 each on 15-08-2024. What is the gain?\nA) ₹25,00,000  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹75,00,000  D) ₹7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — On transfer of the warrant, full value = sale price = 1,00,000 × ₹75 = ₹75,00,000; cost = upfront paid = ₹50,00,000. Gain = ₹75,00,000 − ₹50,00,000 = ₹25,00,000. Held < 12 months → STCG at slab rates. B) is the cost. C) is the sale value, not the gain. D) misplaces a decimal.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Mr. X buys 1 call option (lot 1,000) at premium ₹35, strike ₹450. He exercises and sells the shares at ₹550. As a derivatives trader, his business income is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹65,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sale = 1,000 × ₹550 = ₹5,50,000. Cost = (strike + premium) × 1,000 = (₹450 + ₹35) × 1,000 = ₹4,85,000. Business income = ₹5,50,000 − ₹4,85,000 = ₹65,000. A) ignores the premium. C) ignores premium differently. D) uses only the premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Same option as Q86; instead Mr. X transfers the option at ₹30 (premium paid ₹35). The result is:\nA) Gain ₹5,000  B) Loss ₹5,000  C) Loss ₹35,000  D) Gain ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sale = 1,000 × ₹30 = ₹30,000; cost = 1,000 × ₹35 = ₹35,000. Result = ₹30,000 − ₹35,000 = a loss of ₹5,000 (set off as normal business loss). A) reverses the sign. C) ignores the sale proceeds. D) ignores cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Same option; Mr. X lets it expire when premium is ₹10 (paid ₹35). The business loss is:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹35,000  D) ₹45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On expiry, value realised = 1,000 × ₹10 = ₹10,000; cost = 1,000 × ₹35 = ₹35,000. Loss = ₹10,000 − ₹35,000 = ₹25,000. A) uses only the residual value. C) ignores the residual ₹10. D) adds instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Mr. A buys 5 call options (lot 1,000) at premium ₹22, strike ₹200. He exercises 2 options (2,000 shares) and sells those shares at ₹250; the other 3 options he sells at ₹27. Total business income is:\nA) ₹71,000  B) ₹65,000  C) ₹81,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sale of shares = 2,000 × ₹250 = ₹5,00,000; sale of 3 options = 3,000 × ₹27 = ₹81,000; total sale = ₹5,81,000. Cost of shares = 2,000 × ₹200 = ₹4,00,000; cost of all 5 options' premium = 5,000 × ₹22 = ₹1,10,000; total cost = ₹5,10,000. Business income = ₹5,81,000 − ₹5,10,000 = ₹71,000. B) only counts the exercised leg. C) is the option sale alone. D) is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Mr. Ravi bought 1,000 listed shares at ₹106 on 01-07-2023; got 1:1 bonus on 01-09-2023; sold the original 1,000 at ₹95 on 01-04-2024. Under Section 94(8), the loss treated is:\nA) Loss of ₹11,000 allowed  B) Loss of ₹11,000 ignored  C) Gain of ₹11,000  D) No effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loss = (₹106 − ₹95) × 1,000 = ₹11,000. Since the shares were bought within 3 months before the record date and sold within 9 months after while holding the bonus shares, Section 94(8) ignores this ₹11,000 loss. A) wrongly allows it. C) reverses the sign. D) is wrong — there is a consequence (the loss shifts to bonus cost).\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Continuing Q90, the cost of acquisition of the 1,000 bonus shares (and per-share cost) becomes:\nA) ₹11,000 total; ₹11 per share  B) Nil; ₹0 per share  C) ₹1,06,000 total; ₹106 per share  D) ₹95,000 total; ₹95 per share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The ignored loss of ₹11,000 becomes the cost of the 1,000 bonus shares, i.e., ₹11,000 ÷ 1,000 = ₹11 per share. B) is the ordinary bonus-cost rule but is overridden by Section 94(8). C) and D) confuse the original cost/sale with the bonus cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Mr. K has 112A LTCG of ₹3,25,000 (STT paid). Tax payable (ignoring surcharge/cess) is:\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹65,000  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Taxable = ₹3,25,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,00,000. Tax = 12.50% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹25,000. A) taxes the full gain. B) uses 20%. C) uses a wrong base.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An investor has both ₹2,00,000 of 112A LTCG and ₹1,00,000 STCG under 111A, all STT-paid (the only relevant incomes). Total tax on these gains (ignore surcharge/cess) is:\nA) ₹9,375 + ₹20,000 = ₹29,375  B) ₹25,000 + ₹20,000 = ₹45,000  C) ₹9,375 + ₹12,500 = ₹21,875  D) ₹25,000 + ₹10,000 = ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG: taxable = ₹2,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹75,000; tax = 12.50% × ₹75,000 = ₹9,375. STCG: 20% × ₹1,00,000 = ₹20,000. Total = ₹29,375. B) ignores the LTCG exemption. C) taxes STCG at the wrong rate. D) mixes wrong figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Mr. M bought 2,000 unlisted shares at ₹150 (01-05-2021), sold at ₹230 on 01-09-2024. What is the nature and amount of gain?\nA) STCG ₹1,60,000 at slab  B) LTCG ₹1,60,000 at 12.50%  C) LTCG ₹1,60,000 exempt up to ₹1.25 lakh  D) STCG ₹1,60,000 at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted shares held > 24 months (May-2021 to Sep-2024 ≈ 40 months) → long-term. Gain = (₹230 − ₹150) × 2,000 = ₹1,60,000, taxed at 12.50% under Section 112. A) and D) wrongly treat it as short-term. C) wrongly applies the ₹1.25 lakh 112A exemption, which needs listing/STT.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Mr. N bought 1,000 unlisted shares at ₹100 (01-06-2023), sold at ₹140 on 01-12-2024. The gain is:\nA) LTCG ₹40,000 at 12.50%  B) STCG ₹40,000 at slab rates  C) STCG ₹40,000 at 20%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding = Jun-2023 to Dec-2024 = about 18 months, which is ≤ 24 months for unlisted shares → short-term. Gain = (₹140 − ₹100) × 1,000 = ₹40,000, taxed at slab rates (111A's 20% needs listing/STT). A) wrongly treats it as long-term. C) wrongly applies 20%. D) is wrong — it is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Mr. R's 112A LTCG for the year is exactly ₹1,25,000 (STT paid). The tax on this LTCG is:\nA) ₹15,625  B) ₹25,000  C) Nil  D) ₹6,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The full ₹1,25,000 is within the exemption limit, so the taxable excess is ₹0 and tax is Nil. A), B) and D) wrongly tax an amount that does not exceed the ₹1.25 lakh threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An F&O trader has favourable differences of ₹3,00,000, unfavourable differences of ₹1,20,000, and premium received on sale of options of ₹40,000. Turnover (ICAI method) is:\nA) ₹1,80,000  B) ₹4,20,000  C) ₹4,60,000  D) ₹3,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Turnover = total of favourable + unfavourable differences + premium on sale of options = ₹3,00,000 + ₹1,20,000 + ₹40,000 = ₹4,60,000. (Differences are added as absolute amounts, not netted.) A) nets the differences. B) omits the premium. D) omits the unfavourable difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Using Q97's turnover of ₹4,60,000, the presumptive income under Section 44AD (digital receipts at 6%) is:\nA) ₹27,600  B) ₹36,800  C) ₹46,000  D) ₹2,30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Presumptive profit = 6% × turnover = 6% × ₹4,60,000 = ₹27,600. B) uses 8% (cash rate). C) uses 10%. D) uses 50%. For digital/banking receipts the rate is 6%.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Mr. S bought 4,000 listed shares at ₹50 on 01-03-2018 (after grandfathering date), and sold at ₹90 on 01-06-2024 (STT paid). His 112A LTCG and tax (only such gain) are:\nA) ₹1,60,000; ₹4,375  B) ₹1,60,000; ₹20,000  C) ₹1,60,000; Nil  D) ₹3,60,000; ₹29,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bought after 31-01-2018, so cost = actual = 4,000 × ₹50 = ₹2,00,000. Sale = 4,000 × ₹90 = ₹3,60,000. Holding > 12 months → LTCG = ₹1,60,000. Taxable = ₹1,60,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹35,000; tax = 12.50% × ₹35,000 = ₹4,375. B) taxes more than the excess. C) ignores the excess. D) miscomputes the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Mr. T (resident) has other income of ₹2,00,000 and LTCG on unlisted shares of ₹3,00,000 (basic exemption ₹2,50,000, old regime). The LTCG charged to tax is:\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹2,50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A resident can absorb the unused basic exemption against 112 LTCG. Shortfall = ₹2,50,000 − ₹2,00,000 = ₹50,000. This ₹50,000 reduces the LTCG, so taxable LTCG = ₹3,00,000 − ₹50,000 = ₹2,50,000. A) ignores the absorption. C) confuses it with other income. D) wrongly taxes only the shortfall.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Ms. Priya, a resident individual (old tax regime), had the following transactions during FY 2024-25, all on a recognised Indian stock exchange with STT paid on both purchase and sale unless stated otherwise:\n\n1. **Listed equity — Alpha Ltd.:** Bought 2,000 shares on 10-06-2022 at ₹180. Sold all on 20-11-2024 at ₹260.\n2. **Listed equity — Beta Ltd.:** Bought 1,000 shares on 01-08-2024 at ₹400. Sold on 15-01-2025 at ₹470.\n3. **Old listed equity — Gamma Ltd.:** Bought 1,000 shares on 01-01-2016 at ₹120. FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹300. Sold on 01-12-2024 at ₹500.\n4. **Equity-oriented MF units:** Holds units bought after 31-01-2018; received ₹3,00,000 dividend and paid ₹80,000 interest on a loan taken to buy these units.\n5. She also earned a **business profit of ₹6,00,000** and had an **F&O (non-speculative) loss of ₹1,50,000** from exchange-traded derivatives.\n\nWork out the tax treatment of each item.\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS1. What is the capital gain on Alpha Ltd. and how is it taxed?</summary>\n\nHolding = 10-06-2022 to 20-11-2024 = more than 12 months → **long-term**, Section 112A applies (STT paid).\n- Sale = 2,000 × ₹260 = ₹5,20,000\n- Cost = 2,000 × ₹180 = ₹3,60,000 (bought after 31-01-2018, so actual cost; no grandfathering)\n- **LTCG = ₹5,20,000 − ₹3,60,000 = ₹1,60,000**\n\nThis LTCG goes into the 112A pool, which gets a single ₹1,25,000 annual exemption (combined with Gamma below), and the excess is taxed at 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS2. What is the capital gain on Beta Ltd. and how is it taxed?</summary>\n\nHolding = 01-08-2024 to 15-01-2025 = about 5.5 months (≤ 12 months) → **short-term**, Section 111A applies (listed, STT paid).\n- Sale = 1,000 × ₹470 = ₹4,70,000\n- Cost = 1,000 × ₹400 = ₹4,00,000\n- **STCG = ₹70,000**, taxed at the flat **20%** under Section 111A.\n- Tax on this STCG = 20% × ₹70,000 = **₹14,000** (ignoring surcharge/cess). No 80C–80U deduction is allowed against it.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS3. What is the capital gain on Gamma Ltd. (grandfathering applies)?</summary>\n\nBought on 01-01-2016 (on/before 31-01-2018) → grandfathering applies. Holding > 12 months → long-term (Section 112A).\n- Grandfathered cost = **higher of**:\n  - (a) actual cost = 1,000 × ₹120 = ₹1,20,000; OR\n  - (b) **lower of** [FMV 1,000 × ₹300 = ₹3,00,000] and [sale 1,000 × ₹500 = ₹5,00,000] = ₹3,00,000\n  - Higher of ₹1,20,000 and ₹3,00,000 = **₹3,00,000**\n- Sale = 1,000 × ₹500 = ₹5,00,000\n- **LTCG = ₹5,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹2,00,000**\n\nIntuition: grandfathering protects the gains that built up before 01-02-2018 (when LTCG was tax-free), so we start the clock from the higher ₹300 FMV instead of the ₹120 cost.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS4. Combine the 112A long-term gains and compute the LTCG tax.</summary>\n\nTotal Section 112A LTCG = Alpha ₹1,60,000 + Gamma ₹2,00,000 = **₹3,60,000**.\n- The ₹1,25,000 annual exemption is applied **once** to the combined pool.\n- Taxable LTCG = ₹3,60,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,35,000\n- **Tax = 12.50% × ₹2,35,000 = ₹29,375** (ignoring surcharge/cess).\n\nKey point: the ₹1.25 lakh limit is per year across all 112A gains, not per scrip — a common trap is to exempt ₹1.25 lakh for each company.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS5. How are the dividend, the interest, and the F&O loss treated?</summary>\n\n**Dividend:** ₹3,00,000 is taxable at slab rates. Interest deduction is capped at 20% × ₹3,00,000 = ₹60,000 (less than the ₹80,000 actually paid, so only ₹60,000 is allowed).\n- **Taxable dividend = ₹3,00,000 − ₹60,000 = ₹2,40,000**\n\n**F&O loss:** Exchange-traded F&O is non-speculative business income (Section 43(5)). The ₹1,50,000 loss can be set off against her business profit:\n- Business profit ₹6,00,000 − F&O loss ₹1,50,000 = **net business income ₹4,50,000**.\n- (If any F&O loss were unabsorbed, it could be carried forward up to 8 assessment years, but only if she files her return by the due date — and it cannot be set off against salary.)\n\nSo Priya's heads of income are: business income ₹4,50,000 + taxable dividend ₹2,40,000 (both at slab rates) + STCG ₹70,000 (20%) + LTCG ₹2,35,000 taxable (12.50%).\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "12", "title": "Taxation of Other Products", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 12: Taxation of Other Products — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- **ESOPs** — taxed twice (perquisite + capital gain), and the start-up deferment.\n- **Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs)** — interest + the magic redemption exemption.\n- **NPS** (Tier I & II) — contributions, deductions (80CCD), and withdrawals.\n- **REITs / InvITs / AIFs** — pass-through taxation.\n- **ETFs** (gold & index), **Life Insurance / ULIPs / Endowment**, and **Reverse Mortgage**.\n\n---\n\n## 12.1 Employee Stock Option Plans (ESOPs)\n\nAn **ESOP** gives an employee the *right (not obligation)* to buy company shares at a fixed cheap price after a **vesting period**. Common in start-ups that can't pay big salaries.\n\nKey dates: **Grant date** → **Vesting date** (when you may exercise) → **Exercise date** (when you actually buy) → **Allotment** of shares → later **Sale**.\n\n### 12.1.2 Two tax points\n\n**Point 1 — At allotment (taxed as SALARY perquisite):**\n> Perquisite = (**FMV on exercise date** − price paid by employee) × number of shares.\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Use the FMV on the **exercise date**, NOT the vesting date or allotment date.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** FMV on exercise (10-05-2024) = ₹6,500; price paid = ₹500; 100 shares → perquisite = (6,500−500)×100 = **₹6,00,000** taxed as salary.\n\n**Point 2 — At sale (taxed as CAPITAL GAIN):**\n- Period of holding starts from the **date of allotment** (not exercise).\n- **Cost of acquisition = FMV on the exercise date** (the amount already taxed as perquisite).\n\nCapital gain treatment:\n| Situation | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Listed, STT paid, >12 mth (LTCG) | Sec 112A: 12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh |\n| Listed, STT paid, ≤12 mth (STCG) | Sec 111A: 20% |\n| Unlisted, no STT, >24 mth (LTCG) | Sec 112: 12.50% |\n| Unlisted, no STT, ≤24 mth (STCG) | slab rate |\n\n### 12.1.3 Start-up deferment\nFor **eligible start-ups under Section 80-IAC**, tax on the ESOP **perquisite is deferred** (TDS/payment delayed). Eligible start-up = company/LLP incorporated **01-04-2016 to before 01-04-2024**, turnover **≤₹100 crore**, with an Inter-Ministerial Board certificate.\n\n---\n\n## 12.2 Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs)\n\nSGBs are **government securities denominated in grams of gold** (issued by RBI). A paper substitute for physical gold — no storage/making-charge worries.\n\nKey facts: **2.50% p.a. interest** (half-yearly) on nominal value; tenure **8 years**; premature redemption allowed after **5th year**; limit **4 kg/individual, 4 kg/HUF, 20 kg/trust** per fiscal year.\n\n### 12.2.1 Tax\n- **Interest:** taxed under *Other Sources* at slab rates; **no TDS** (it's a Govt security).\n- **Redemption by an individual (maturity or premature):** **NOT taxed** — Section 47 says individual redemption is *not a transfer*. So no capital gain.\n- **Redemption by a non-individual** (trust etc.): LTCG @12.50% if held >12 months, else slab.\n- **Sale/transfer in secondary market** (any holder, incl. individual): LTCG @12.50% if >12 months; STCG at slab if ≤12 months. **The exemption applies only to redemption, not to sale.**\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Mr. X buys SGBs for ₹5 lakh, redeems for ₹6 lakh. Gain **not taxed** (he's an individual, redemption ≠ transfer). But interest each year is taxed.\n\n**💡 Worked example (secondary-market sale):** Bought 100 SGBs @₹4,25,000 (28-04-2019), sold 01-08-2024 @₹7,50,000 (before the interest due date).\n- Accrued interest 29-04 to 01-08 (95 days) = 4,25,000 × 2.5% × 95/365 = ₹2,765 → carved out, taxed as interest.\n- Sale price for capital gain = 7,50,000 − 2,765 = ₹7,47,235.\n- LTCG = 7,47,235 − 4,25,000 = **₹3,22,235 @12.50%**.\n\n⚠️ If sold **right after** the interest due date, no interest accrues to carve out → full sale price used.\n\n---\n\n## 12.3 National Pension System (NPS)\n\nA **defined-contribution retirement scheme** (PFRDA-regulated). Two accounts:\n- **Tier I:** mandatory retirement account (locked).\n- **Tier II:** voluntary, withdraw anytime; taxed like an open-ended mutual fund (capital gains).\n\n### 12.3.1 Deductions on contribution\n| Contributor | Section | Deduction |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Employee's own** | 80CCD(1) | Lower of contribution or **10% of salary**; inside the ₹1.5 lakh 80C cap |\n| **Additional (any individual)** | 80CCD(1B) | Extra **₹50,000** above the ₹1.5 lakh cap |\n| **Employer's contribution** | 80CCD(2) | **14%** of salary (Govt employees); **10%** (other employees, old regime) or **14%** (new regime) |\n| **Self-employed** | 80CCD(1) | Lower of contribution or **20% of gross income** (+ ₹50,000 under 1B) |\n| **NPS Vatsalya (minor)** | 80CCD(1B) | Parent/guardian, within ₹50,000 cap |\n\n💡 **Salary** here = basic + DA (if part of retirement benefits). **80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) together capped at ₹1,50,000**; with 80CCD(1B)'s ₹50,000 → total NPS-related deduction can reach **₹2,00,000**. Employee/self contribution deductions are **old-regime only**; employer 80CCD(2) is available in both regimes.\n\n⚠️ Employer welfare contributions (PF + NPS + Superannuation) above **₹7,50,000** are taxed as a **perquisite**. Since employer NPS is already added as salary, the excess is effectively **added back twice** with deduction only once.\n\n### 12.3.2 Tax on amounts received (Summary)\n| Event | Taxability |\n|---|---|\n| Accumulation phase | **Tax free** |\n| Final withdrawal at closure/exit | **Exempt up to 60% of corpus** (effectively whole withdrawal tax-free) |\n| Partial withdrawal (employee) | Exempt up to **25% of employee's contribution** |\n| Amount to nominee on death | **Fully exempt** |\n| Tier II withdrawal | Taxed as **capital gains** |\n| Amount used to buy annuity | No tax on the purchase; the **annuity/pension is taxable** when received |\n| NPS Vatsalya partial withdrawal | Exempt up to **25% of contributions** (Sec 10(12BA)) |\n| Pension received | **Taxable** as income |\n\n---\n\n## 12.4 Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT)\n\nA **REIT** is like a mutual fund for income-producing real estate (offices, malls, warehouses). It's a **hybrid pass-through entity**: some income is exempt at REIT level and taxed at the **unit-holder** level.\n\n### 12.4.1 Income types (Sec 115UA)\n| Income | At REIT level | At unit-holder level |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rental income** | Exempt (10(23FCA)) | **Taxable** (pass-through) |\n| **Interest from SPV** | Exempt (10(23FC)) | **Taxable** (pass-through) |\n| **Dividend from SPV** | Exempt (10(23FC)) | Taxable, **unless** SPV did NOT opt for 115BAA → then exempt for unit-holder too (10(23FD)) |\n| **Capital gains** (on property) | **Taxed at REIT level** | Not taxed |\n| **Other income** | Taxed at REIT (max marginal rate) | If not taxed at REIT, taxable to unit-holder under Sec 56(2)(xii) |\n\n### 12.4.3 Capital gain on units of REIT\n- **Redemption of units:** taxed as *Other Sources* (Sec 56(2)(xii)).\n- **Transfer of units** (other than redemption): capital gain.\n  - Listed: long-term after **12 months**; Unlisted: after **24 months**.\n  - If STT paid: STCG @**20% (111A)**; LTCG @**12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (112A)**.\n  - If STT not paid: STCG at slab; LTCG @**12.50% (112)**.\n  - **Grandfathering** for units bought on/before 31-01-2018.\n\n### 12.4.4 TDS (Sec 194LBA)\n| Distributed income | Resident | Non-resident |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rental | 10% | 30% (foreign co. 35%) |\n| Dividend from SPV | 10% | 10% |\n| Interest from SPV | 10% | 5% |\n\nReporting: **Form 64B** to unit-holders, **Form 64A** to the tax department.\n\n## 12.5 InvITs\n**Infrastructure Investment Trusts** — same structure and tax as REITs **except**: REIT rental income passes through to unit-holders, but **InvIT rental income is taxed in the InvIT's own hands**, not the unit-holder's.\n\n## 12.6 Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)\nA privately-pooled fund for sophisticated investors (not a mutual fund). Set up as trust/company/LLP, registered with SEBI.\n\n### Category-I & II (Sec 115UB — pass-through)\n- **Business income:** taxed **in the AIF's hands** (at company/firm rate, or **maximum marginal rate** if a trust); exempt for unit-holders (10(23FBB)).\n- **Any other income:** **exempt at AIF level (10(23FBA))**, taxable in unit-holder's hands, retaining its character and proportion (deemed credited on the last day of the year even if not paid).\n- **Losses:** business losses stay with the AIF (carried forward, not passed). Since AY 2020-21, **non-business losses pass through** to unit-holders (if the unit held ≥12 months).\n- **TDS** under Sec 194LBB: **10%** (resident), rates in force (foreign). Reporting: **Form 64C** to investors (by 30 June), **Form 64D** to department (by 30 Nov).\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Cat-I AIF (trust) earns Business ₹20L, Capital gains ₹15L, Other sources ₹5L; Mr. X owns 30%.\n- AIF taxes its **₹20L business income at MMR**.\n- Mr. X: capital gains 30%×15L = ₹4.5L (taxed as capital gains) + other sources 30%×5L = ₹1.5L (deemed credited even if not paid) = **₹6L total**. PGBP share is exempt to him.\n\n### Category-III AIF\n**No pass-through.** Taxed like a normal trust/company/LLP (or, if a \"specified fund\" under 10(4D), gets various exemptions).\n\n## 12.7 Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs)\nETFs track an index/commodity but trade like a stock on the exchange.\n\n### Gold ETFs\nTaxed like physical gold / non-equity MF.\n- Held **>12 months:** LTCG @**12.50%** (the source text mentions indexation, but the post-FA-2024 rule is no indexation on most assets).\n- Held **≤12 months:** at slab rate.\n- ⚠️ Gold ETF bought **after 01-04-2023 and sold before 01-04-2025:** **always short-term**, slab rate (Section 50AA logic). Doesn't apply if bought before 01-04-2023 or sold on/after 01-04-2025.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 10,000 Gold ETF units @₹30 (01-03-2018), sold @₹50 (25-03-2025). Held 7 years → **LTCG ₹2,00,000 @12.50%**.\n\n### Equity Index ETFs\nTaxed like a listed **equity-oriented mutual fund**: LTCG (>12 mth) @12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (112A); STCG @20% (111A, STT). *(The text's older \"15%\" reference is superseded by the 20% rate under the FA-2024 regime.)*\n\n---\n\n## 12.8 Life Insurance Products\n\n**General rule:** Any sum received from a life insurance policy (including bonus) is **exempt under Section 10(10D)**.\n\n**Exceptions (becomes taxable):**\n- (a) Keyman insurance policy proceeds;\n- (b) Sec 80DD(3) amount where dependent predeceases the assessee;\n- (c) Policy issued **01-04-2003 to 31-03-2012** if premium in any year exceeds **20% of sum assured**;\n- (d) Policy issued **on/after 01-04-2012** if premium in any year exceeds **10% of sum assured**.\n- **⚠️ Any sum received on death is ALWAYS exempt** — (c) and (d) never apply to death proceeds.\n\n**Endowment policies (on/after 01-04-2023):** if total premium exceeds **₹5,00,000**, maturity proceeds are **taxable as Other Sources** even if the 10%-of-sum-assured test is met (death proceeds still exempt).\n\n**💡 Worked example:** ₹60 lakh endowment policy taken 01-04-2024, premium ₹6 lakh/year. 10% test met (6L = 10% of 60L), but premium >₹5 lakh → maturity proceeds **taxable as Other Sources** (after deducting premiums not previously deducted).\n\n**Section 80C on premiums:** premium deductible up to the ₹1.5 lakh cap. Deduction restricted to **20%** of sum assured (policies ≤31-03-2012), **10%** (on/after 01-04-2012), **15%** for disabled/specified-disease persons (policies on/after 01-04-2013). Minimum holding: **5 years (ULIP), 2 years (life policy)** — early termination claws back earlier deductions.\n\n### ULIPs (Unit Linked Insurance Plans)\n- High-value ULIPs issued **on/after 01-02-2021** with **aggregate premium > ₹2,50,000** lose the 10(10D) exemption and are treated as **capital assets**.\n- The **equity-oriented fund** inside a high-premium ULIP (maintaining ≥65%/90% equity throughout): long-term period **1 year**; **LTCG @12.50%, STCG @20%**.\n- **Non-equity** fund inside a high-premium ULIP: taxed under normal capital-gains rules (slab for short term, 12.50% for long term).\n- From FY 2025-26, **all ULIPs without 10(10D) exemption** are capital assets; their maturity proceeds = **capital gains**.\n- **Death proceeds remain exempt** under 10(10D).\n\n## 12.9 Reverse Mortgage\nA senior citizen mortgages their house to borrow funds while continuing to live in it.\n- **Section 47(xvi):** a reverse-mortgage transaction (under a notified Govt scheme) is **NOT a transfer** → no capital gain.\n- **Section 10(43):** the loan amount received (lump-sum or instalments) is **exempt from tax**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **ESOP:** perquisite at allotment = (FMV on exercise − price paid) × shares; later capital gain with cost = exercise-date FMV, holding from allotment date. Start-up perquisite tax deferred.\n- **SGB:** 2.5% interest (taxed); **individual redemption exempt (Sec 47)**; secondary-market sale taxed (12.50% LTCG > 12 mth).\n- **NPS:** 80CCD(1) 10% (in ₹1.5L cap), 80CCD(1B) extra ₹50k, 80CCD(2) employer 10%/14%; final withdrawal 60% exempt; nominee fully exempt; pension/annuity taxable; Tier II = capital gains.\n- **REIT:** rental + SPV interest/dividend pass through; capital gains taxed at REIT. **InvIT:** same but rental taxed at InvIT.\n- **AIF Cat I/II:** pass-through (business income taxed at AIF); **Cat III:** no pass-through.\n- **Gold ETF:** like non-equity gold; **Index ETF:** like equity MF.\n- **Life insurance:** exempt 10(10D) unless premium >10%/20% of SA, or endowment premium >₹5L (post-Apr-2023); **death always exempt**.\n- **High-value ULIP (>₹2.5L premium, post 01-02-2021):** capital asset; equity fund LTCG 12.50% / STCG 20%.\n- **Reverse mortgage:** not a transfer (47(xvi)); loan exempt (10(43)).\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term/Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **FMV on exercise date** | Used for ESOP perquisite & later cost of acquisition |\n| **80-IAC** | Eligible start-up for ESOP perquisite deferment (turnover ≤₹100cr) |\n| **2.50%** | SGB annual interest |\n| **8 years / 5th year** | SGB tenure / premature redemption allowed |\n| **Section 47 (SGB) / 47(xvi) (reverse mortgage)** | Not a \"transfer\" → no capital gain |\n| **10% / 14% (80CCD(2))** | Employer NPS deduction (other / Govt or new regime) |\n| **₹50,000 (80CCD(1B))** | Extra NPS deduction above ₹1.5L cap |\n| **60% / 25%** | NPS final / partial withdrawal exemption |\n| **₹7,50,000** | Employer welfare contribution cap (excess = perquisite) |\n| **115UA / 115UB** | REIT / AIF taxation sections |\n| **10(10D)** | Life insurance exemption |\n| **₹2,50,000 / 01-02-2021** | High-value ULIP premium threshold / cut-off date |\n| **₹5,00,000** | Endowment premium threshold (post-Apr-2023) for taxability |\n| **10(43)** | Reverse-mortgage loan exemption |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 12: Taxation of Other Products — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What are the two tax points for an ESOP?\n> **A:** (1) **At allotment** — perquisite taxed as salary; (2) **At sale** — capital gain.\n\n**Q2.** How is the ESOP perquisite computed?\n> **A:** (**FMV on the exercise date** − price paid by employee) × number of shares. (Vesting/allotment-date FMV is irrelevant.)\n\n**Q3.** For ESOP shares, what is the cost of acquisition and when does holding start?\n> **A:** Cost = **FMV on the exercise date** (already taxed as perquisite); holding period starts from the **date of allotment**.\n\n**Q4.** Who gets the ESOP perquisite-tax deferment?\n> **A:** Employees of **eligible start-ups under Section 80-IAC** (company/LLP incorporated 01-04-2016 to before 01-04-2024, turnover ≤₹100 crore, with Inter-Ministerial Board certificate).\n\n**Q5.** What interest do SGBs pay and how is it taxed?\n> **A:** **2.50% p.a.** (half-yearly) on nominal value; taxed under *Other Sources* at slab rates; **no TDS**.\n\n**Q6.** Is redemption of an SGB by an individual taxable?\n> **A:** **No** — Section 47 says individual redemption (maturity or premature) is not a transfer, so no capital gain.\n\n**Q7.** How is a secondary-market SALE of an SGB taxed?\n> **A:** LTCG @**12.50%** if held >12 months; STCG at slab if ≤12 months. (The Section 47 exemption applies only to redemption, not sale.)\n\n**Q8.** When selling an SGB before the interest due date, how is accrued interest handled?\n> **A:** It is **carved out** of the sale price and taxed separately as interest; the rest is the capital-gain consideration.\n\n**Q9.** Difference between NPS Tier I and Tier II?\n> **A:** **Tier I** = mandatory, locked retirement account. **Tier II** = voluntary, withdraw anytime; taxed as **capital gains** like an open-ended MF.\n\n**Q10.** Deduction for an employee's own NPS contribution (Section 80CCD(1))?\n> **A:** Lower of contribution or **10% of salary**, within the ₹1.5 lakh 80C cap.\n\n**Q11.** What does Section 80CCD(1B) provide?\n> **A:** An **additional ₹50,000** deduction above the ₹1.5 lakh cap (also covers NPS Vatsalya).\n\n**Q12.** Employer NPS contribution deduction limit under 80CCD(2)?\n> **A:** **14%** of salary for Govt employees; **10%** (old regime) or **14%** (new regime) for other employees. Available in both regimes.\n\n**Q13.** Self-employed NPS deduction under 80CCD(1)?\n> **A:** Lower of contribution or **20% of gross total income** (+ ₹50,000 under 80CCD(1B)), old regime.\n\n**Q14.** What is the maximum total NPS-related deduction an individual can claim?\n> **A:** **₹2,00,000** (₹1.5 lakh under the 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) cap + ₹50,000 under 80CCD(1B)).\n\n**Q15.** How much of the final NPS withdrawal is exempt?\n> **A:** **60% of the corpus** (effectively the whole permitted withdrawal is tax-free).\n\n**Q16.** Is the NPS amount received by a nominee on the subscriber's death taxable?\n> **A:** **No — fully exempt.**\n\n**Q17.** Above what amount are employer welfare contributions (PF + NPS + Superannuation) taxed as a perquisite?\n> **A:** Above **₹7,50,000** per year.\n\n**Q18.** Which REIT incomes get pass-through status (taxed at unit-holder)?\n> **A:** **Rental income, interest from SPV, and dividend from SPV.** Capital gains are taxed at the REIT level.\n\n**Q19.** How does InvIT taxation differ from REIT?\n> **A:** Same, **except rental income** — taxed in the **InvIT's own hands**, not passed through to unit-holders.\n\n**Q20.** How is redemption of REIT units taxed?\n> **A:** Under **Section 56(2)(xii)** as *Income from Other Sources* (not capital gains).\n\n**Q21.** TDS rate (Sec 194LBA) on rental income distributed by REIT to a resident?\n> **A:** **10%** (non-resident: 30%; foreign company 35%).\n\n**Q22.** How are Category-I and II AIFs taxed?\n> **A:** **Pass-through (Sec 115UB)** — income exempt at AIF and taxed in investors' hands, **except business income** which is taxed at the AIF (MMR if a trust).\n\n**Q23.** How are Category-III AIFs taxed?\n> **A:** **No pass-through** — like a normal trust/company/LLP (unless a \"specified fund\" under 10(4D)).\n\n**Q24.** Can an AIF pass its losses to unit-holders?\n> **A:** **Business losses stay with the AIF.** Since AY 2020-21, **non-business losses pass through** (if the unit held ≥12 months).\n\n**Q25.** How is a Gold ETF taxed if held more than 12 months?\n> **A:** **LTCG @12.50%.** Held ≤12 months → slab rate. (If bought after 01-04-2023 and sold before 01-04-2025 → always short-term, slab.)\n\n**Q26.** How is an equity index ETF taxed?\n> **A:** Like a listed equity MF — **LTCG @12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (112A)**, **STCG @20% (111A)**.\n\n**Q27.** When are life insurance maturity proceeds taxable (policies on/after 01-04-2012)?\n> **A:** When the premium in any year exceeds **10% of the sum assured** (20% for 01-04-2003 to 31-03-2012). Death proceeds are **always exempt**.\n\n**Q28.** For an endowment policy taken on/after 01-04-2023, what makes maturity taxable?\n> **A:** If total premium exceeds **₹5,00,000**, proceeds are taxable as *Other Sources* even if the 10% test is met (death proceeds still exempt).\n\n**Q29.** When does a ULIP lose its 10(10D) exemption?\n> **A:** High-value ULIPs issued **on/after 01-02-2021** with **aggregate premium > ₹2,50,000** — treated as a capital asset.\n\n**Q30.** How is the equity-oriented fund inside a high-premium ULIP taxed?\n> **A:** Long-term period **1 year**; **LTCG @12.50%, STCG @20%**.\n\n**Q31.** Minimum holding period to keep 80C deduction — ULIP vs life policy?\n> **A:** **ULIP 5 years; life insurance policy 2 years.** Early termination claws back earlier deductions.\n\n**Q32.** How is reverse mortgage treated under the Income Tax Act?\n> **A:** **Section 47(xvi):** not a \"transfer\" (no capital gain); **Section 10(43):** the loan amount received is **exempt**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 12: Taxation of Other Products — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (recall of facts/definitions), 🟡 Tier 2 (application & comparison), 🔴 Tier 3 (numerical & scenario). Remember the exam has **25% negative marking** (a wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark), so guess only when you can eliminate options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Under an Employee Stock Option Plan (ESOP), the option given to an employee to buy shares is best described as a:\nA) Right but not an obligation  B) Right and an obligation  C) Obligation but not a right  D) Neither a right nor an obligation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook states the option \"confers a right but not an obligation on the employee.\" The employee may exercise it but is never forced to.\n- B) Wrong: there is no obligation to buy; calling it an obligation is incorrect.\n- C) Wrong: it is fundamentally a right, not an obligation.\n- D) Wrong: it clearly is a right (to purchase shares at a fixed price).\n\nIntuition: ESOPs reward loyalty — if the share price falls below the exercise price, the employee can simply walk away, which only makes sense if exercising is optional.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The waiting period an employee must complete before becoming entitled to exercise an ESOP is called the:\nA) Exercise period  B) Grant period  C) Vesting period  D) Lock-in window\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The \"vesting period\" is defined as the time an employee must wait to get the right to buy the specified shares.\n- A) Wrong: the exercise period/date is when the employee actually buys, after vesting.\n- B) Wrong: granting is when the ESOP is first offered, not the waiting period.\n- D) Wrong: \"lock-in\" is a loose related idea but the workbook's defined term is vesting.\n\nIntuition: Vest = \"becomes yours to use.\" You can only exercise once the right has vested.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** At the time of allotment of shares under an ESOP, the difference between FMV and the price paid by the employee is taxed under the head:\nA) Capital gains  B) Income from other sources  C) Profits and gains of business  D) Salary (as a perquisite)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The value of shares allotted free or at a concessional rate is a perquisite, taxable as salary under Section 17(2)(vi).\n- A) Wrong: capital gains arise only later, when the shares are sold.\n- B) Wrong: \"other sources\" is used for things like SGB interest, not ESOP perquisites.\n- C) Wrong: business income is unrelated; the employee earns this as employment compensation.\n\nIntuition: The discount you get from your employer is a benefit *from your job* — so it is salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** For computing the ESOP perquisite, the Fair Market Value (FMV) to be used is the FMV on the:\nA) Grant date  B) Vesting date  C) Exercise date  D) Allotment date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Step 1 of the workbook's method: take the FMV on the date the option is *exercised*. The FMV on vesting or allotment is explicitly not relevant.\n- A) Wrong: grant date FMV is never used.\n- B) Wrong: the workbook explicitly says the FMV on the vesting date shall not be considered.\n- D) Wrong: tax arises in the year of allotment, but the FMV used is the exercise-date FMV.\n\nIntuition: You \"lock in\" the value when you choose to buy (exercise), so that date's price is the reference.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** When ESOP shares are later sold, the period of holding for capital gains is counted from the date of:\nA) Grant  B) Vesting  C) Exercise  D) Allotment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Holding period runs from the date of *allotment* of shares to the date of sale.\n- A), B), C) Wrong: grant, vesting and exercise all precede allotment and are not the starting point for holding.\n\nIntuition: You only truly own the shares once they are allotted to you, so the clock starts there.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** For ESOP shares, the cost of acquisition used to compute capital gains is the:\nA) FMV on the exercise date  B) Price paid by the employee  C) FMV on the grant date  D) FMV on the sale date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The FMV on the date of exercising the option becomes the cost of acquisition.\n- B) Wrong: using only the price paid would double-tax the perquisite already taxed as salary.\n- C) Wrong: grant-date FMV is irrelevant throughout.\n- D) Wrong: the sale-date value is the sale consideration, not the cost.\n\nIntuition: The exercise-date FMV was already taxed as salary, so it becomes your \"purchase cost\" to avoid taxing the same amount twice.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** ESOP perquisite tax can be deferred only for employees of an eligible start-up referred to in Section:\nA) 80C  B) 80-IAC  C) 80CCD  D) 115UA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deferment applies only to eligible start-ups as referred to in Section 80-IAC.\n- A) Wrong: 80C is a general investment deduction.\n- C) Wrong: 80CCD deals with NPS contributions.\n- D) Wrong: 115UA is the REIT taxation section.\n\nIntuition: Start-ups pay talent partly in ESOPs; deferment helps employees who have no cash yet to pay the tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** To be an eligible start-up under Section 80-IAC, total turnover in the relevant previous year must not exceed:\nA) Rs. 25 crore  B) Rs. 50 crore  C) Rs. 100 crore  D) Rs. 250 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The turnover limit is Rs. 100 crore (raised from Rs. 25 crore by the Finance Act, 2020).\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 25 crore was the old limit before the increase.\n- B), D) Wrong: these figures do not appear in the provision.\n\nIntuition: A genuine start-up is small; Rs. 100 crore is the ceiling that still counts as \"start-up scale\" here.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs) are issued by the:\nA) SEBI  B) Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the Government  C) India Bullion and Jewellers Association  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The RBI issues SGBs on behalf of the Government of India.\n- A) Wrong: SEBI regulates REITs/InvITs/AIFs, not SGB issuance.\n- C) Wrong: IBJA only publishes the gold price used for redemption.\n- D) Wrong: PFRDA regulates the NPS.\n\nIntuition: SGBs are government securities, so the central bank (RBI) issues them for the government.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** SGBs carry a fixed interest rate of:\nA) 2.50% per annum  B) 7.75% per annum  C) 8.00% per annum  D) 5.00% per annum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SGBs pay 2.50% per annum (fixed) on the nominal value, credited half-yearly.\n- B), C), D) Wrong: these rates are not the SGB rate stated in the workbook.\n\nIntuition: The main attraction of SGBs is gold price appreciation; the 2.5% interest is a modest top-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The interest on SGBs is taxable under which head of income?\nA) Capital gains  B) Salary  C) Income from other sources  D) Business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SGB interest is chargeable under \"Income from other sources\" at the assessee's applicable rates.\n- A) Wrong: capital gains apply to transfer/redemption, not the periodic interest.\n- B), D) Wrong: it is neither employment nor business income.\n\nIntuition: Interest you receive from a bond you simply hold is classic \"other sources\" income.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Is TDS deducted on the interest paid on SGBs?\nA) Yes, at 10%  B) Yes, at 5%  C) No TDS, as they are Government Securities  D) Yes, at slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No TDS is deducted because SGBs are Government Securities; investors receive the full interest.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: no TDS applies, so no rate is deducted at source (the investor still pays tax on it when filing).\n\nIntuition: No tax is *withheld*, but the interest is still taxable — you declare and pay it yourself.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The maturity (tenor) of a Sovereign Gold Bond is:\nA) 5 years  B) 7 years  C) 8 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SGBs mature on expiry of 8 years from the date of issue.\n- A) Wrong: 5 years is when premature redemption first becomes allowed.\n- B), D) Wrong: these are not the SGB tenor.\n\nIntuition: 8-year lock with an early-exit door from year 5.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Premature redemption of an SGB is permitted after the:\nA) 1st year  B) 3rd year  C) 5th year  D) 7th year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Early encashment is allowed after the fifth year from the date of issue, on the next interest payment date.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these years do not match the workbook rule.\n\nIntuition: The government wants you to hold gold long-term, so the early door only opens at year 5.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Under Section 47, redemption of SGBs by an individual is:\nA) Treated as a transfer and taxed as LTCG  B) Taxed at 20% always  C) Taxed at slab rate  D) Not treated as a transfer, so no capital gain arises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 47 provides that redemption by an individual is not a \"transfer,\" so no capital gains tax arises.\n- A), B), C) Wrong: because there is no \"transfer,\" no capital gains computation (and hence no rate) applies for an individual on redemption.\n\nIntuition: No transfer = no capital gain. The government rewards individuals who hold SGBs to redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The annual subscription limit of SGBs for an individual is:\nA) 4 kg  B) 1 kg  C) 20 kg  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The maximum is 4 kg for individuals (also 4 kg for HUF, 20 kg for trusts) per fiscal year.\n- B) Wrong: 1 gram is the *minimum*, not the maximum.\n- C) Wrong: 20 kg is the limit for trusts and similar entities.\n- D) Wrong: there is a defined ceiling.\n\nIntuition: Individuals and HUFs share the same 4 kg cap; only big institutions (trusts) get 20 kg.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The National Pension System (NPS) is administered and regulated by:\nA) SEBI  B) RBI  C) PFRDA  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NPS is administered and regulated by the Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority (PFRDA).\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these regulate securities, banking and insurance respectively.\n\nIntuition: \"Pension\" → PFRDA, the pension regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which NPS account is a mandatory retirement account with restricted withdrawal?\nA) Tier I  B) Tier II  C) Tier III  D) Vatsalya\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tier I is the mandatory retirement account; Tier II is a voluntary savings account allowing withdrawal anytime.\n- B) Wrong: Tier II is flexible/voluntary.\n- C) Wrong: there is no Tier III.\n- D) Wrong: NPS Vatsalya is a scheme for minors, not the main account types.\n\nIntuition: Tier I = your locked retirement piggy bank; Tier II = an open savings jar.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An employee's own contribution to NPS gets a deduction under Section:\nA) 80C  B) 80D  C) 80CCD(1)  D) 80CCD(2)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An employee's own contribution is deductible under Section 80CCD(1).\n- A) Wrong: 80C is the broader basket (the 80CCD(1) deduction sits within the 1.5 lakh cap with it).\n- B) Wrong: 80D is for health insurance premiums.\n- D) Wrong: 80CCD(2) is for the *employer's* contribution.\n\nIntuition: 80CCD(1) = \"1 person\" = the employee himself.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The additional NPS deduction of Rs. 50,000 over and above the Rs. 1.5 lakh limit is available under Section:\nA) 80CCD(1B)  B) 80CCD(1)  C) 80CCD(2)  D) 80CCC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 80CCD(1B) gives an extra Rs. 50,000 deduction over the Rs. 1.5 lakh cap.\n- B) Wrong: 80CCD(1) sits *inside* the 1.5 lakh cap.\n- C) Wrong: 80CCD(2) covers employer contributions, separately.\n- D) Wrong: 80CCC is for pension fund premiums within the 1.5 lakh cap.\n\nIntuition: \"(1B)\" is the \"bonus\" extra Rs. 50,000 on top.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Employer contribution to NPS is deductible under Section 80CCD(2) up to what percentage of salary for a non-government employee under the new tax regime?\nA) 10%  B) 12%  C) 14%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a non-government employee, the limit is 10% (old regime) or 14% (new regime) of salary.\n- A) Wrong: 10% is the old-regime limit for non-government employees.\n- B) Wrong: 12% is the EPF rate, not the NPS 80CCD(2) limit.\n- D) Wrong: 20% applies to a self-employed person's own contribution.\n\nIntuition: The new regime sweetens employer NPS to 14%, matching the rate available to government staff.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** For a self-employed individual, the NPS deduction under Section 80CCD(1) is limited to what percentage of gross total income?\nA) 10%  B) 14%  C) 20%  D) 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A self-employed person can claim the lower of the contribution or 20% of gross income.\n- A) Wrong: 10% applies to a salaried employee's own contribution.\n- B) Wrong: 14% relates to employer/government contribution caps.\n- D) Wrong: 25% is the NPS partial-withdrawal exemption proportion, not a contribution cap.\n\nIntuition: The self-employed have no employer adding to NPS, so they get a larger 20% allowance.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** At final withdrawal on closure/exit from NPS, the lump sum is exempt up to what percentage of the total corpus?\nA) 25%  B) 40%  C) 60%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Withdrawal on closure/opting out is exempt up to 60% of the total corpus, matching the 60% the scheme allows to be withdrawn — effectively making the whole lump sum tax-free.\n- A) Wrong: 25% is the partial-withdrawal exemption.\n- B) Wrong: 40% is not the figure used here.\n- D) Wrong: the exemption is framed as 60% of corpus, even though it covers the full permitted withdrawal.\n\nIntuition: You may take out 60% as lump sum, and that 60% is exempt; the rest must buy an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A partial withdrawal from NPS by an employee is exempt up to what proportion of the employee's own contribution?\nA) 25%  B) 10%  C) 50%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Partial withdrawal is exempt to the extent of 25% of the employee's own contribution.\n- B), C) Wrong: not the stated proportion.\n- D) Wrong: 60% is the final-withdrawal exemption, not partial.\n\nIntuition: Partial early access is limited (25% of what *you* put in) to keep the retirement corpus intact.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The amount received by a nominee on the death of an NPS subscriber is:\nA) Fully taxable  B) Taxable at 12.5%  C) Fully exempt  D) Exempt up to 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The whole amount received by the nominee on the subscriber's death is exempt from tax.\n- A), B) Wrong: it is not taxable at all.\n- D) Wrong: the 60% cap applies to a living subscriber's own withdrawal, not to death proceeds.\n\nIntuition: Death benefits to a nominee are treated kindly — fully tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A withdrawal from an NPS Tier II account is taxed under the head:\nA) Salary  B) Income from other sources  C) Capital gains  D) It is fully exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Tier II is treated like an open-ended mutual fund, so profit/loss on withdrawal is taxed under \"Capital gains.\"\n- A), B) Wrong: it is not employment or other-sources income.\n- D) Wrong: only Tier I final withdrawals get exemptions; Tier II is fully taxable as capital gains.\n\nIntuition: Tier II is the \"investment-like\" account, so it is taxed like an investment (capital gains).\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** REITs in India are registered with and regulated by:\nA) RBI  B) PFRDA  C) SEBI under the SEBI (REITs) Regulations, 2014  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — REITs are registered with SEBI under the SEBI (REITs) Regulations, 2014 (concept introduced in India in 2014).\n- A), B), D) Wrong: these are the banking, pension and insurance regulators.\n\nIntuition: REITs are market-traded investment vehicles, so the markets regulator (SEBI) governs them.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** REITs are described as which kind of entity for tax purposes?\nA) A fully taxable company  B) A tax-exempt charity  C) A hybrid pass-through entity  D) A partnership firm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — REITs are structured as a hybrid pass-through entity: some income is exempt at the REIT level and taxed in unit-holders' hands.\n- A) Wrong: not all income is taxed at the REIT level.\n- B) Wrong: it is not a charity.\n- D) Wrong: it is a trust structure, not a firm.\n\nIntuition: \"Pass-through\" = certain income flows through the REIT to investors, taxed only once at the investor's end.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** For which type of income does an InvIT differ from a REIT in tax treatment?\nA) Interest from SPV  B) Dividend from SPV  C) Rental income  D) Capital gains on property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The only difference is rental income: REIT rental passes through to unit-holders, but InvIT rental is taxed in the InvIT's own hands.\n- A), B) Wrong: SPV interest and dividend are treated identically for both.\n- D) Wrong: capital gains on property are taxed at the trust level for both.\n\nIntuition: InvITs invest in infrastructure (roads, towers) rather than rent-yielding buildings, so rental pass-through is handled differently.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Sum received on *redemption* of units of a REIT is taxed under which head, per Section 56(2)(xii)?\nA) Capital gains  B) Income from other sources  C) Business income  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Redemption of REIT units is taxable under \"Income from other sources\" per Section 56(2)(xii).\n- A) Wrong: a *transfer* (sale) of units is capital gains; redemption is specifically other sources.\n- C), D) Wrong: it is neither business income nor exempt.\n\nIntuition: The law carves out unit redemption specifically into \"other sources\" rather than capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The pass-through status of REITs is given for which of the following incomes?\nA) Capital gains on property sale  B) Only capital gains  C) Only other income  D) Rental income, and interest/dividend from SPV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Pass-through is provided for rental income, plus interest and dividend received from the SPV.\n- A), B) Wrong: capital gains on property are taxed at the REIT level, not passed through.\n- C) Wrong: other income is generally taxed at the REIT level.\n\nIntuition: The three \"operating\" income streams (rent, SPV interest, SPV dividend) flow to investors; one-off gains stay with the REIT.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Category-I and Category-II AIFs are taxed under which section providing pass-through status?\nA) 115UA  B) 115UB  C) 115BAA  D) 10(10D)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 115UB governs the pass-through taxation of Category-I and Category-II AIFs.\n- A) Wrong: 115UA is for REITs/InvITs.\n- C) Wrong: 115BAA is the concessional company tax regime.\n- D) Wrong: 10(10D) is the life insurance exemption.\n\nIntuition: 115U**A** = trusts (REIT/InvIT); 115U**B** = AIF funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Pass-through status is NOT available to which AIF category?\nA) Category-I  B) Category-II  C) Category-III  D) All categories get it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Pass-through is accorded only to Category-I and Category-II AIFs; Category-III is taxed like a normal trust/company/LLP.\n- A), B) Wrong: these do enjoy pass-through under 115UB.\n- D) Wrong: Category-III is excluded.\n\nIntuition: Category-III AIFs (often hedge-fund-like) are taxed as ordinary entities — no special flow-through.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Gold ETFs are taxed in the same manner as:\nA) Equity-oriented mutual funds  B) Physical gold / non-equity-oriented mutual funds  C) Government securities  D) Listed shares with STT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gold ETFs follow the rules for physical gold or non-equity-oriented mutual funds.\n- A), D) Wrong: those rules apply to equity index ETFs, not gold.\n- C) Wrong: SGBs (not gold ETFs) are government securities.\n\nIntuition: A gold ETF just holds gold, so it is taxed like gold, not like equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Under Section 10(43), the loan amount received under a notified reverse mortgage scheme is:\nA) Taxable as other sources  B) Taxable as capital gains  C) Exempt from tax  D) Taxable as salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 10(43) exempts the loan (lump sum or instalments) received under a reverse mortgage; Section 47(xvi) says the mortgage is not a \"transfer.\"\n- A), B), D) Wrong: a loan is not income, so none of these heads apply.\n\nIntuition: A loan is money you eventually owe back, not income — so it is tax-free when received.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An employee exercises an ESOP. FMV on exercise is Rs. 800; price paid is Rs. 200; 500 shares. The perquisite taxed as salary is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Rs. 4,00,000  D) Rs. 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Perquisite = (FMV on exercise − price paid) × shares = (800 − 200) × 500 = 600 × 500 = Rs. 3,00,000.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 1,00,000 = only 200 × 500 (the price paid), not the discount.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 4,00,000 = 800 × 500 (full FMV), ignoring the price paid.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 5,00,000 has no basis here.\n\nIntuition: You are taxed on the *bargain* — the gap between true value and what you actually paid, across all shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** ESOP shares are listed equity shares, sold after 18 months with STT paid. The long-term capital gain is taxable under:\nA) Section 111A at 20%  B) Section 112A at 12.50% above Rs. 1,25,000  C) Section 112 at 12.50%  D) Slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed equity shares with STT held over 12 months → LTCG under Section 112A at 12.50% on the amount exceeding Rs. 1,25,000.\n- A) Wrong: 111A (20%) is for short-term gains on STT-paid equity.\n- C) Wrong: 112 (without the 1.25 lakh exemption) applies to unlisted shares or no-STT cases.\n- D) Wrong: slab rates apply to short-term unlisted/no-STT shares.\n\nIntuition: Listed + STT + long-term = the \"favoured\" 112A bucket with a Rs. 1.25 lakh free allowance.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** ESOP shares (listed, STT-paid) are sold within 6 months of allotment. The short-term capital gain is taxed:\nA) At 12.50% under Section 112A  B) At slab rates  C) Exempt  D) At 20% under Section 111A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Short-term gain on STT-paid equity is taxed at 20% under Section 111A.\n- A) Wrong: 112A is for long-term gains.\n- B) Wrong: slab rates apply only when STT is not paid (no-STT short-term).\n- C) Wrong: it is not exempt.\n\nIntuition: Sold quickly (≤12 months) on a stock exchange with STT → the special 20% short-term rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Unlisted equity shares allotted under ESOP (no STT) are sold after 30 months. The gain is:\nA) STCG at slab rate  B) LTCG at 12.50% under Section 112  C) LTCG at 12.50% under Section 112A  D) Exempt under Section 47\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted shares held over 24 months → LTCG at 12.50% under Section 112 (no Rs. 1.25 lakh exemption, since that is only for 112A).\n- A) Wrong: held 30 months > 24, so it is long-term, not short-term.\n- C) Wrong: 112A needs STT-paid listed equity; unlisted no-STT uses 112.\n- D) Wrong: Section 47 exemption relates to SGB redemption, not unlisted shares.\n\nIntuition: Unlisted shares have a longer 24-month threshold; once past it, 12.50% under 112.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. A (an individual) holds SGBs to maturity and redeems them. The capital gain on redemption is:\nA) Taxed at 12.50%  B) Taxed at slab rate  C) Not taxable (redemption by individual is not a transfer)  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Redemption of SGBs by an individual is not a transfer (Section 47), so no capital gain arises.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: with no transfer there is no capital gain to tax at any rate.\n\nIntuition: Hold-to-redeem as an individual = the gold price appreciation is tax-free. A big SGB advantage.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A *trust* (non-individual) redeems SGBs after holding for 18 months. The gain is taxed:\nA) As LTCG at 12.50%  B) Not taxable  C) As STCG at slab  D) At 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For a non-individual, redemption is taxable; held over 12 months → LTCG at 12.50% (SGBs are listed).\n- B) Wrong: the Section 47 exemption applies only to *individuals*.\n- C) Wrong: 18 months > 12 months, so it is long-term, not short-term.\n- D) Wrong: 20% is the short-term equity rate, not relevant here.\n\nIntuition: Only individuals get the redemption exemption; a trust is taxed normally as LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An individual *sells* SGBs in the secondary market after 20 months. The gain is:\nA) Exempt (Section 47)  B) STCG at slab  C) Taxed at 20%  D) LTCG at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Section 47 exemption applies only to redemption, not to a secondary-market *sale*. Held over 12 months → LTCG at 12.50%.\n- A) Wrong: exemption covers redemption, not transfer/sale.\n- B) Wrong: 20 months > 12 months, so it is long-term.\n- C) Wrong: 20% is the equity short-term rate.\n\nIntuition: Selling on the exchange is a \"transfer\" — so even an individual pays LTCG. Redemption is the only escape.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** When an SGB is sold in the secondary market before the interest due date, the accrued interest portion of the sale price is:\nA) Added to capital gains  B) Reduced from sale consideration and taxed as interest (other sources)  C) Exempt  D) Taxed at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest accrued from the last coupon date to the sale date is carved out of the consideration and taxed under \"Income from other sources\"; the remainder is the sale price for capital gains.\n- A) Wrong: it is interest, not capital gain — including it would mistax it.\n- C) Wrong: interest is taxable, not exempt.\n- D) Wrong: 12.50% is the LTCG rate, not the interest rate (interest is at slab).\n\nIntuition: The buyer effectively pays you for interest built up so far — that bit is interest income, not gain on the bond.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Mr. B repays a housing loan of Rs. 1,70,000 (eligible under 80C) and contributes Rs. 65,000 to NPS. His maximum total deduction is:\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 2,35,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Rs. 1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 80C is capped at Rs. 1,50,000 (exhausted by the housing loan repayment), and the NPS Rs. 50,000 is claimed additionally under 80CCD(1B). Total = 1,50,000 + 50,000 = Rs. 2,00,000.\n- A) Wrong: ignores the extra Rs. 50,000 NPS benefit.\n- B) Wrong: you cannot claim the full Rs. 65,000 NPS extra — 80CCD(1B) caps the additional deduction at Rs. 50,000.\n- D) Wrong: 80C is limited to 1.5 lakh, not 1.7 lakh.\n\nIntuition: The Rs. 1.5 lakh basket is full, but NPS unlocks a separate Rs. 50,000 room (max).\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Under the new tax regime, which NPS deduction remains available to a salaried employee?\nA) 80CCD(1) employee contribution  B) 80CCD(1B) additional Rs. 50,000  C) 80CCD(2) employer contribution  D) None at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the new regime, the employee's own deductions (80CCD(1) and 80CCD(1B)) are not available, but the employer's contribution deduction under 80CCD(2) is still allowed (at 14% of salary).\n- A), B) Wrong: these are old-regime only.\n- D) Wrong: 80CCD(2) survives in the new regime.\n\nIntuition: The new regime strips most personal deductions but keeps the employer-NPS perk to encourage retirement savings via employers.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Where NPS withdrawal is used in the same year to buy an annuity plan, the tax treatment is:\nA) The withdrawal is taxed but the annuity is exempt  B) Both fully exempt  C) No tax on the amount used to buy the annuity; the annuity income is taxed when received  D) Both fully taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No tax on the withdrawn amount used to purchase the annuity; the annuity/pension income received later is taxable.\n- A) Wrong: reversed — the purchase is exempt, the income is taxed.\n- B) Wrong: the eventual annuity income is taxable.\n- D) Wrong: the purchase itself is not taxed.\n\nIntuition: Rolling money into an annuity is tax-neutral; you pay tax later as you actually receive pension.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Rental income earned by a REIT directly from property is:\nA) Exempt at REIT level (10(23FCA)) and taxed in the unit-holder's hands  B) Taxed at the REIT level only  C) Fully exempt for everyone  D) Taxed at both levels\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Rental income is exempt at the REIT level under Section 10(23FCA) and taxed in unit-holders' hands when distributed (pass-through).\n- B) Wrong: rental is a pass-through, not taxed at REIT level.\n- C) Wrong: it is taxed once — at the unit-holder.\n- D) Wrong: pass-through means single-level taxation, not double.\n\nIntuition: Rent flows through the REIT pipe to investors, who pay the tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Capital gains on the sale of real estate property by a REIT are taxed:\nA) In the unit-holder's hands  B) At the REIT level (its own hands)  C) Exempt entirely  D) Half at each level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Capital gains on property are not pass-through; they are taxed at the REIT level itself.\n- A) Wrong: only rent and SPV interest/dividend pass through, not capital gains.\n- C) Wrong: they are taxable.\n- D) Wrong: there is no split.\n\nIntuition: Pass-through covers regular income streams; one-off property gains stay and are taxed at the REIT.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Dividend received by a REIT from an SPV that has opted for the concessional regime under Section 115BAA, when distributed, is:\nA) Exempt for unit-holders  B) Taxed at the REIT level  C) Taxed at 20%  D) Taxable in unit-holders' hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — If the SPV opted for 115BAA, the distributed dividend is taxable in unit-holders' hands (pass-through). It is exempt to unit-holders only if the SPV did NOT opt for 115BAA.\n- A) Wrong: exemption applies only when the SPV did not opt for 115BAA.\n- B) Wrong: SPV dividend is exempt at REIT level under 10(23FC).\n- C) Wrong: no fixed 20% applies; the unit-holder pays at applicable rates.\n\nIntuition: If the SPV already paid full tax (no 115BAA), the dividend is exempt to investors; if the SPV used the concessional regime, investors pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Long-term capital gains on the *transfer* of listed REIT units with STT paid are taxed under:\nA) Section 111A at 20%  B) Section 112A at 12.50% above Rs. 1,25,000  C) Section 112 at 12.50%  D) Section 56(2)(xii)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — STT-paid listed units held over 12 months → LTCG under Section 112A at 12.50% above Rs. 1,25,000.\n- A) Wrong: 111A (20%) is for short-term gains.\n- C) Wrong: 112 applies when STT is not paid.\n- D) Wrong: 56(2)(xii) covers redemption, not transfer.\n\nIntuition: Listed units with STT mirror equity treatment — LTCG at 12.50% with the 1.25 lakh shield.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Listed REIT units become long-term after a holding period of more than:\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) 8 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed REIT units are long-term if held for more than 12 months (unlisted: more than 24 months).\n- B) Wrong: 24 months applies to *unlisted* units.\n- C) Wrong: 36 months is an older threshold not used here.\n- D) Wrong: 8 years is the SGB tenor.\n\nIntuition: \"Listed = liquid = shorter 12-month threshold,\" like listed shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** The TDS rate under Section 194LBA on rental income distributed by a REIT to a *resident* unit-holder is:\nA) 5%  B) 20%  C) 10%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — REITs deduct TDS at 10% for resident unit-holders on rental, dividend (from SPV) and interest (from SPV) distributions.\n- A) Wrong: 5% applies to interest distributed to a non-resident.\n- B) Wrong: no 20% rate under 194LBA.\n- D) Wrong: 30% applies to rental distributed to a non-resident (other than foreign company).\n\nIntuition: Residents face a flat 10% withholding; non-residents face higher, income-specific rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A Category-I AIF (registered as a trust) earns business income. This business income is:\nA) Passed through and taxed in unit-holders' hands  B) Exempt entirely  C) Taxed at 12.50%  D) Taxed at the AIF level at maximum marginal rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Business income is NOT passed through; for a trust-form AIF it is taxed at the maximum marginal rate (MMR) at the AIF level; it is exempt for unit-holders under 10(23FBB).\n- A) Wrong: business income is the one stream that does not pass through.\n- B) Wrong: it is taxed, just at the AIF level.\n- C) Wrong: 12.50% is an LTCG rate, not the MMR for trust business income.\n\nIntuition: The fund pays tax on its own trading profits; investors only get taxed on the *other* income that passes through.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** \"Other income\" (non-business) of a Category-II AIF is exempt at the AIF level under Section 10(23FBA) and:\nA) Exempt for unit-holders too  B) Taxable in the unit-holders' hands, retaining its character  C) Taxed at the AIF at MMR  D) Taxed at 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Other income passes through: exempt at the AIF (10(23FBA)) and taxable in unit-holders' hands in the same nature and proportion.\n- A) Wrong: someone must pay — it is taxed at the unit-holder level.\n- C) Wrong: only business income is taxed at the AIF.\n- D) Wrong: 10% is the TDS rate, not the final tax rate; the unit-holder pays at applicable rates.\n\nIntuition: The AIF is a transparent pipe for non-business income — it keeps its original flavour (capital gains stay capital gains, etc.) in the investor's hands.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Income credited by a Category-I/II AIF that is not actually paid to the investor during the year is:\nA) Never taxed  B) Deemed credited to investors on the last day of the previous year  C) Taxed only when paid in a later year  D) Taxed at the AIF level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If not paid/credited, the income is deemed credited to investors on the last day of the previous year, in their proportion.\n- A) Wrong: it is taxed via the deeming provision.\n- C) Wrong: tax cannot be deferred to a later year merely by withholding payment.\n- D) Wrong: pass-through income is taxed at the investor level.\n\nIntuition: You cannot dodge tax by leaving money inside the fund — the law \"deems\" you received it at year-end.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Business losses of a Category-I/II AIF are:\nA) Carried forward at the AIF level, not passed to unit-holders  B) Passed through to unit-holders  C) Lost forever  D) Set off against unit-holders' salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Business losses are carried forward at the AIF level and are NOT passed to unit-holders (only non-business losses pass through, since AY 2020-21).\n- B) Wrong: only non-business losses pass through.\n- C) Wrong: they are carried forward, not lost.\n- D) Wrong: AIF losses cannot offset an investor's salary.\n\nIntuition: Since business *income* is taxed at the AIF, business *losses* logically stay there too.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Non-business losses of an AIF can be passed to a unit-holder only if the unit has been held for at least:\nA) 6 months  B) 24 months  C) 12 months  D) 36 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Non-business losses pass through except where the unit has not been held for at least 12 months.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: the threshold stated is 12 months.\n\nIntuition: A minimum 12-month holding prevents short-term \"loss shopping\" by investors who briefly buy units.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The TDS rate under Section 194LBB on income distributed by an AIF to a resident unit-holder is:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 12.50%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AIFs deduct TDS at 10% on income distributed to resident unit-holders (rates in force for foreign unit-holders).\n- A), C), D) Wrong: the stated resident rate is 10%.\n\nIntuition: A simple flat 10% withholding mirrors the REIT/194LBA resident rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A gold ETF is bought on 01-06-2023 and sold on 01-12-2024. The capital gain is:\nA) Always long-term at 12.50%  B) Always short-term at slab rate  C) Exempt  D) Taxed under 112A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A gold ETF bought after 01-04-2023 and sold before 01-04-2025 is always treated as short-term and taxed at slab rates, regardless of holding period.\n- A) Wrong: the special rule overrides the long-term treatment for this window.\n- C) Wrong: it is taxable.\n- D) Wrong: 112A is for equity, not gold ETFs.\n\nIntuition: For this specific buy/sell window, the law says \"no long-term benefit\" — treat it as short-term at slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A gold ETF bought on 01-03-2018 and sold on 25-03-2025 (held over 7 years) gives a gain taxed:\nA) At slab rate as short-term  B) Exempt under Section 47  C) Under 112A at 12.50%  D) As LTCG at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bought before 01-04-2023, so the short-term-only rule does not apply; held over 12 months → LTCG taxed at 12.50%.\n- A) Wrong: the special short-term window rule applies only to ETFs bought after 01-04-2023.\n- B) Wrong: Section 47 covers SGB redemption, not gold ETFs.\n- C) Wrong: 112A is for equity; gold ETF LTCG uses the non-equity 12.50% rate.\n\nIntuition: Older purchases keep normal long-term treatment — 12.50% after 12 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Long-term capital gains on equity index ETFs (over 12 months, STT paid) are taxed under:\nA) Section 112 at 12.50%  B) Section 112A at 12.50% above Rs. 1,25,000  C) Slab rate  D) Section 111A at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity index ETFs are taxed like listed equity-oriented mutual funds: LTCG over 12 months at 12.50% above Rs. 1,25,000 under Section 112A.\n- A) Wrong: 112 (no 1.25 lakh shield) is for non-STT / unlisted assets.\n- C) Wrong: slab applies to gold ETFs (short term), not equity index LTCG.\n- D) Wrong: 111A (20%) is the short-term equity rate.\n\nIntuition: Index ETFs track equity, so they inherit equity's favourable 112A treatment.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Any sum received from a life insurance policy, including bonus, is generally:\nA) Exempt under Section 10(10D)  B) Taxable as other sources  C) Taxed as capital gains  D) Taxed at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The general rule is that life insurance proceeds (including bonus) are exempt under Section 10(10D), subject to specific exceptions.\n- B), C), D) Wrong: these apply only in exception cases (e.g., high-premium policies), not the general rule.\n\nIntuition: Insurance maturity/death proceeds are normally tax-free — that is the whole point of the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** For a policy issued on or after 01-04-2012, 10(10D) exemption is lost if the annual premium in any year exceeds what percentage of the sum assured?\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 15%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Policies issued on/after 01-04-2012 lose exemption if premium in any year exceeds 10% of the sum assured.\n- A) Wrong: 5% is not a threshold here.\n- C) Wrong: 15% is the 80C deduction cap for disabled/specified-disease persons (policies on/after 01-04-2013), not the 10(10D) test.\n- D) Wrong: 20% applies to older policies (01-04-2003 to 31-03-2012).\n\nIntuition: After Apr-2012, your cover (sum assured) must be at least 10x the premium for the proceeds to stay tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Any sum received on the *death* of the insured person under a life policy is:\nA) Always exempt, regardless of premium-to-sum-assured ratio  B) Taxable if premium exceeds 10% of sum assured  C) Taxed as capital gains  D) Taxable as other sources\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Death proceeds are always exempt; the 10%/20% premium conditions do not apply to death claims.\n- B) Wrong: the premium tests apply to maturity proceeds, never death.\n- C), D) Wrong: death proceeds are not taxed under any head.\n\nIntuition: A death benefit to a family is always protected from tax — no exceptions.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A high-value ULIP (issued on/after 01-02-2021) loses 10(10D) exemption when aggregate annual premium exceeds:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 2,50,000  C) Rs. 5,00,000  D) Rs. 10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ULIPs issued on/after 01-02-2021 lose 10(10D) exemption if the aggregate premium of affected policies exceeds Rs. 2,50,000.\n- A) Wrong: not the stated threshold.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 5,00,000 is the *endowment* premium threshold (policies on/after 01-04-2023).\n- D) Wrong: not a stated figure.\n\nIntuition: Big-ticket ULIPs (>Rs. 2.5 lakh premium) are treated like investments (capital assets), not pure insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The long-term capital gain on an equity-oriented fund within a high-premium ULIP is taxed at:\nA) 20%  B) Slab rate  C) Exempt  D) 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equity-oriented funds within high-premium ULIPs are taxed at the concessional 12.50% for LTCG (long-term period being 1 year).\n- A) Wrong: 20% is the *short-term* rate for such equity-oriented ULIP funds.\n- B) Wrong: slab applies to non-equity ULIP short-term gains.\n- C) Wrong: high-premium ULIP gains are taxable.\n\nIntuition: The equity fund inside the ULIP is taxed like equity — 12.50% LTCG, 20% STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** For endowment policies taken on/after 01-04-2023, maturity proceeds become taxable as other sources if the aggregate premium during the policy term exceeds:\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 2,50,000  C) Rs. 5,00,000  D) Rs. 10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For endowment policies on/after 01-04-2023, if aggregate premium exceeds Rs. 5,00,000, maturity proceeds are taxable as other sources even if the 10%-of-sum-assured test is met.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 1.5 lakh is the 80C cap, not this threshold.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 2.5 lakh is the high-value ULIP threshold.\n- D) Wrong: not the stated figure.\n\nIntuition: After Apr-2023, even a \"compliant\" big endowment (premium > Rs. 5 lakh) loses its tax-free maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The minimum holding periods to avoid claw-back of 80C deductions are:\nA) ULIP 2 years, life policy 5 years  B) Both 3 years  C) Both 5 years  D) ULIP 5 years, life policy 2 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — ULIP of UTI/LIC must be held 5 years; a life insurance policy must be held 2 years. Early termination makes earlier deductions taxable.\n- A) Wrong: the periods are reversed.\n- B), C) Wrong: the two products have different periods (5 vs 2).\n\nIntuition: ULIPs (investment-linked) get the longer 5-year lock; plain life policies need only 2 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A reverse mortgage transaction under a notified scheme is excluded from \"transfer\" by which provision?\nA) Section 10(43)  B) Section 47(xvi)  C) Section 56(2)(xii)  D) Section 112A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 47(xvi) excludes a notified reverse mortgage from being a \"transfer,\" so no capital gain arises; Section 10(43) then exempts the loan received.\n- A) Wrong: 10(43) exempts the loan amount, but the \"not a transfer\" rule is 47(xvi).\n- C) Wrong: 56(2)(xii) covers REIT/business-trust distributions.\n- D) Wrong: 112A is the equity LTCG section.\n\nIntuition: 47(xvi) handles the \"is it a transfer?\" question; 10(43) handles the \"is the loan income?\" question.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which statement correctly distinguishes SGB *interest* from SGB *capital gains* for an individual holding to maturity?\nA) Both are exempt  B) Both are taxable  C) Interest is taxable as other sources; redemption gain is exempt  D) Interest is exempt; redemption gain is taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The 2.5% interest is taxable under \"Income from other sources\" each year, while the capital gain on redemption is exempt (Section 47, individual).\n- A) Wrong: interest is taxable.\n- B) Wrong: the redemption gain is exempt for an individual.\n- D) Wrong: reversed — interest is the taxable part, not the gain.\n\nIntuition: Two cash flows, two treatments: you pay tax on the coupon, but the gold appreciation on redemption is free.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** ESOP: FMV on exercise = Rs. 6,500; predetermined price = Rs. 500; 100 shares exercised. The perquisite taxed as salary is:\nA) Rs. 5,00,000  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 6,50,000  D) Rs. 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Perquisite = (FMV on exercise − price paid) × shares = (6,500 − 500) × 100 = 6,000 × 100 = Rs. 6,00,000.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 5,00,000 uses Rs. 5,000 difference, miscomputing 6,500 − 1,500.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 6,50,000 = 6,500 × 100, ignoring the price paid.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 60,000 misplaces a zero.\n\nIntuition: This is the workbook's own example — the discount (Rs. 6,000/share) across 100 shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Continuing Q71: those 100 ESOP shares (listed, STT paid) are later sold at Rs. 9,000 each. The cost of acquisition per share for capital gains is:\nA) Rs. 500  B) Rs. 6,500  C) Rs. 9,000  D) Rs. 6,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost of acquisition = FMV on exercise date = Rs. 6,500 per share.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 500 is what the employee paid, but that would double-tax the perquisite.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 9,000 is the sale price, not the cost.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 6,000 was the perquisite per share, not the cost.\n\nIntuition: The exercise-date FMV (Rs. 6,500) was already taxed as salary, so it becomes the cost going forward.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Continuing Q72: capital gain per share on sale at Rs. 9,000 is:\nA) Rs. 8,500  B) Rs. 2,500  C) Rs. 3,000  D) Rs. 9,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Capital gain = Sale price − Cost (exercise FMV) = 9,000 − 6,500 = Rs. 2,500 per share.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 8,500 wrongly uses Rs. 500 (price paid) as cost.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 3,000 = 9,000 − 6,000, mistakenly using the perquisite figure.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 9,000 ignores any cost.\n\nIntuition: Only the rise from the already-taxed exercise FMV (6,500) to the sale price (9,000) is the capital gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Mr. X bought 100 SGBs at nominal value Rs. 4,25,000. SGB interest is 2.5% p.a. on nominal value. The annual interest taxable is:\nA) Rs. 4,250  B) Rs. 8,500  C) Rs. 10,625  D) Rs. 21,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Interest = 4,25,000 × 2.5% = Rs. 10,625 per year.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 4,250 uses 1%.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 8,500 uses 2%.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 21,250 uses 5%.\n\nIntuition: 2.5% of Rs. 4.25 lakh = Rs. 10,625, taxed as other sources each year.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Mr. X (Q74) sells the SGBs on 01-08-2024, with 95 days elapsed since the last coupon (29-04-2024). The accrued interest carved out of the sale price is (use 365-day year):\nA) Rs. 2,765  B) Rs. 5,313  C) Rs. 10,625  D) Rs. 1,062\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Accrued interest = Nominal × 2.5% × (days/365) = 4,25,000 × 2.5% × 95/365 = 10,625 × 95/365 ≈ Rs. 2,765.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 5,313 ≈ half-year interest (182 days), not 95 days.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 10,625 is full-year interest.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 1,062 understates the period.\n\nIntuition: Only 95 of 365 days' worth of the 2.5% coupon has accrued — that fraction is interest income, removed from the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Mr. X (Q75) sells for Rs. 7,50,000 (gross). After removing accrued interest of Rs. 2,765, the sale price for capital gains is:\nA) Rs. 7,52,765  B) Rs. 7,47,235  C) Rs. 7,50,000  D) Rs. 3,22,235\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sale price for capital gain = 7,50,000 − 2,765 = Rs. 7,47,235.\n- A) Wrong: adding the interest is the wrong direction.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 7,50,000 is the gross consideration before carving out interest.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 3,22,235 is the resulting capital gain, not the sale price.\n\nIntuition: Strip out the interest first; what is left is the true consideration for the bond itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Mr. X (Q76) had a cost of acquisition of Rs. 4,25,000 and held over 12 months. The long-term capital gain and its tax rate are:\nA) Rs. 3,22,235 at 12.50%  B) Rs. 3,25,000 at 20%  C) Rs. 3,22,235 at slab  D) Rs. 1,25,000 exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = 7,47,235 − 4,25,000 = Rs. 3,22,235, taxed at 12.50% (secondary-market sale; held over 12 months).\n- B) Wrong: wrong gain figure and 20% is the equity short-term rate.\n- C) Wrong: slab applies to short-term, not this long-term gain.\n- D) Wrong: SGBs are not equity; the Rs. 1.25 lakh shield (112A) does not apply.\n\nIntuition: Sale (not redemption) by an individual is taxable; over 12 months → flat 12.50% LTCG on Rs. 3,22,235.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** If Mr. X instead sells the SGBs for Rs. 7,92,000 on 29-10-2024 (the day after the interest due date), the sale price for capital gains is:\nA) Rs. 7,92,000 (no interest to carve out)  B) Rs. 7,89,235  C) Rs. 7,85,858  D) Rs. 8,02,625\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Because the sale is right after the interest due date, no interest has accrued since the last coupon, so the full Rs. 7,92,000 is the sale price for capital gains.\n- B), C) Wrong: these carve out interest that has not accrued.\n- D) Wrong: adds interest that does not exist here.\n\nIntuition: Just after the coupon date, the \"interest meter\" has reset to zero — nothing to subtract.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Q78: cost was Rs. 4,25,000, held over 12 months. The long-term capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 3,22,235  B) Rs. 3,67,000  C) Rs. 7,92,000  D) Rs. 4,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG = 7,92,000 − 4,25,000 = Rs. 3,67,000, taxed at 12.50%.\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 3,22,235 was the gain in the earlier (01-08-2024) scenario.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 7,92,000 is the full sale price, not the gain.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 4,25,000 is the cost.\n\nIntuition: No interest carve-out this time, so the entire price above cost (Rs. 3,67,000) is the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Mr. A buys 10,000 gold ETF units at Rs. 30 (01-03-2018) and sells at Rs. 50 (25-03-2025). The long-term capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 5,00,000  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Rs. 8,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sale value = 10,000 × 50 = Rs. 5,00,000; cost = 10,000 × 30 = Rs. 3,00,000; LTCG = 5,00,000 − 3,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000 (taxed at 12.50%; bought before 01-04-2023, held over 7 years).\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 5,00,000 is the sale value, not the gain.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 3,00,000 is the cost.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 8,00,000 wrongly adds rather than subtracts.\n\nIntuition: Gain = (sell − buy) × units = (50 − 30) × 10,000 = Rs. 2,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Mr. A buys 5,000 equity index ETF units at Rs. 200 (01-05-2019) and sells at Rs. 300 (01-08-2024) with STT paid. The taxable LTCG (after the 112A exemption) and rate are:\nA) Rs. 5,00,000 at 12.50%; taxable above Rs. 1,25,000 so Rs. 3,75,000 at 12.50%  B) Rs. 5,00,000 fully at 20%  C) Rs. 1,00,000 at slab  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = (300 − 200) × 5,000 = Rs. 5,00,000. Under Section 112A, the first Rs. 1,25,000 is exempt, so Rs. 3,75,000 is taxed at 12.50%.\n- B) Wrong: 20% is the short-term equity rate; this is long-term.\n- C) Wrong: slab does not apply to equity LTCG.\n- D) Wrong: only Rs. 1.25 lakh is shielded, not the whole gain.\n\nIntuition: Equity index ETF LTCG = equity treatment: subtract the Rs. 1.25 lakh free slice, tax the rest at 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Mr. Gopal: basic Rs. 6,00,000 p.a.; DA forming part of retirement = Rs. 1,20,000. Employer NPS contribution = Rs. 90,000. Under the new regime, the 80CCD(2) deduction for the employer's contribution (cap 14% of salary) is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,800  B) Rs. 90,000  C) Rs. 72,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Salary = 6,00,000 + 1,20,000 = Rs. 7,20,000. Cap = 14% × 7,20,000 = Rs. 1,00,800, but the deduction is limited to the *actual* contribution of Rs. 90,000 (the lower of the two).\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 1,00,800 is the cap, not the actual lower contribution.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 72,000 = 10% × 7,20,000 (old-regime cap), not the new regime.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 50,000 is the 80CCD(1B) figure, not 80CCD(2).\n\nIntuition: Deduction = lower of (actual contribution, % cap). Here Rs. 90,000 < Rs. 1,00,800, so Rs. 90,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Same data as Q82 but under the *old* regime (employer cap 10% of salary Rs. 7,20,000). The 80CCD(2) deduction is:\nA) Rs. 90,000  B) Rs. 1,00,800  C) Rs. 72,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Old-regime cap = 10% × 7,20,000 = Rs. 72,000. Since actual contribution Rs. 90,000 exceeds the cap, the deduction is limited to Rs. 72,000 (the lower).\n- A) Wrong: Rs. 90,000 exceeds the old-regime 10% cap.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 1,00,800 is the 14% (new-regime) cap.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 50,000 is the 80CCD(1B) limit.\n\nIntuition: Old regime caps employer NPS at 10%, so only Rs. 72,000 of the Rs. 90,000 is deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Mr. X's basic salary is Rs. 50,00,000. Employer contributes 14% to NPS = Rs. 7,00,000 and 12% to EPF = Rs. 6,00,000. The employer welfare contribution in excess of Rs. 7,50,000 (taxed as perquisite) is:\nA) Rs. 5,50,000  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 7,00,000  D) Rs. 13,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total employer welfare contribution = EPF 6,00,000 + NPS 7,00,000 = Rs. 13,00,000. Excess over Rs. 7,50,000 = 13,00,000 − 7,50,000 = Rs. 5,50,000, taxed as a perquisite.\n- B), C) Wrong: these are the individual EPF/NPS amounts, not the excess.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 13,00,000 is the total, before subtracting the Rs. 7.5 lakh threshold.\n\nIntuition: Add up all employer welfare contributions; only the part above the Rs. 7.5 lakh cap is taxed as a perk.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Category-I AIF (a trust) earns business income Rs. 20 lakh, capital gains Rs. 15 lakh, other sources Rs. 5 lakh. Mr. X holds 30%. The amount taxed at the AIF level is:\nA) Rs. 20 lakh (business income at MMR)  B) Rs. 40 lakh  C) Rs. 6 lakh  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Only business income is taxed at the AIF; as a trust it is taxed at MMR on Rs. 20 lakh. Capital gains and other sources pass through to unit-holders.\n- B) Wrong: capital gains and other sources are not taxed at the AIF.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 6 lakh is Mr. X's share of the pass-through income.\n- D) Wrong: business income is taxable at the AIF.\n\nIntuition: Business income stays and is taxed at the fund (MMR for a trust); the rest flows through.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: Mr. X's (30%) taxable income passed through from the AIF (capital gains + other sources) is:\nA) Rs. 6,00,000  B) Rs. 4,50,000  C) Rs. 1,50,000  D) Rs. 12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Capital gains share = 30% × 15,00,000 = Rs. 4,50,000; other sources share = 30% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000. Total = Rs. 6,00,000. (Business income share is exempt to him under 10(23FBB).)\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 4,50,000 is only the capital gains share.\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 1,50,000 is only the other-sources share.\n- D) Wrong: Rs. 12,00,000 wrongly includes his share of business income.\n\nIntuition: He pays tax only on the pass-through streams (CG + other) in his 30% proportion = Rs. 6 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** In Q86, the other-sources income (Rs. 5 lakh) was NOT actually paid to investors during the year. Mr. X's Rs. 1,50,000 share is:\nA) Not taxable this year  B) Deemed credited to him on the last day of the year and taxable  C) Taxed at the AIF level  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even if not paid, the income is deemed credited to investors on the last day of the previous year, so Mr. X's Rs. 1,50,000 is taxable this year.\n- A) Wrong: the deeming provision pulls it into this year.\n- C) Wrong: other income passes through to investors, not taxed at the AIF.\n- D) Wrong: it is taxable.\n\nIntuition: You cannot defer tax by keeping money inside the fund — the law deems you received it at year-end.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Mr. X has an endowment policy of Rs. 60,00,000 taken on 01-04-2024 with premium Rs. 6,00,000 per year. The 10%-of-sum-assured test is met (6L = 10% of 60L), but maturity proceeds are taxable because:\nA) Premium exceeds 20% of sum assured  B) The policy is a ULIP  C) It is a Keyman policy  D) Aggregate premium exceeds Rs. 5,00,000 (policy on/after 01-04-2023)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For endowment policies on/after 01-04-2023, if premium exceeds Rs. 5,00,000 the maturity proceeds are taxable as other sources even if the 10% test is satisfied. Here Rs. 6,00,000 > Rs. 5,00,000.\n- A) Wrong: the 10% test is met; 20% is for old policies.\n- B) Wrong: it is an endowment policy, not a ULIP.\n- C) Wrong: it is not a Keyman policy.\n\nIntuition: The new Rs. 5 lakh premium rule overrides the old 10% test for big endowments after Apr-2023.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** For the policy in Q88, the maturity proceeds (including bonus) are taxable as other sources after deducting:\nA) Nothing  B) The sum assured  C) The aggregate premiums paid (not previously claimed as deduction)  D) 12.50% of proceeds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Taxable amount = maturity proceeds (incl. bonus) minus the aggregate premiums paid that were not already claimed as deduction in earlier years.\n- A) Wrong: you may net off the premiums actually paid.\n- B) Wrong: the sum assured is not deducted; the premiums are.\n- D) Wrong: there is no flat 12.50% deduction.\n\nIntuition: You are taxed only on the *net gain*, so the premiums you paid in (and never deducted) are subtracted first.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A REIT distributes interest income (received from its SPV) of Rs. 50,000 to a resident unit-holder. The TDS deducted under Section 194LBA is:\nA) Rs. 5,000  B) Rs. 2,500  C) Rs. 10,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Resident TDS rate is 10%; TDS = 10% × 50,000 = Rs. 5,000.\n- B) Wrong: Rs. 2,500 is 5% (the non-resident interest rate).\n- C) Wrong: Rs. 10,000 is 20%, which is not the 194LBA rate.\n- D) Wrong: TDS does apply to SPV interest distributed to unit-holders.\n\nIntuition: SPV interest is pass-through and taxable to the unit-holder, so the REIT withholds 10% (resident).\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** The same REIT distributes Rs. 50,000 of SPV interest to a *non-resident* unit-holder (not a foreign company). The TDS rate is:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 30%  D) 35%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For interest from SPV, the non-resident TDS rate under 194LBA is 5% (subject to more beneficial DTAA).\n- B) Wrong: 10% is the resident rate for interest.\n- C) Wrong: 30% is the non-resident rate for *rental* income.\n- D) Wrong: 35% is the foreign-company rate for *rental* income.\n\nIntuition: Interest to non-residents is encouraged with a low 5% withholding to attract foreign capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A REIT distributes *rental* income to a non-resident unit-holder that is a foreign company. The TDS rate under 194LBA is:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 30%  D) 35%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For rental income distributed to a foreign company, the rate is 35%; for any other non-resident it is 30%; for residents it is 10%.\n- A) Wrong: 5% is the non-resident interest rate.\n- B) Wrong: 10% is the resident rate.\n- C) Wrong: 30% applies to non-residents other than foreign companies.\n\nIntuition: Rental income to a foreign *company* carries the highest 35% withholding.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Mr. Y, a salaried employee under the *old* regime, contributes Rs. 1,80,000 to NPS himself; his salary is Rs. 12,00,000 and he has already used Rs. 1,50,000 of 80C with other investments. His additional NPS deduction (over the 1.5 lakh cap) is limited to:\nA) Rs. 1,80,000  B) Rs. 1,20,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) basket (Rs. 1.5 lakh) is already exhausted, so only the additional 80CCD(1B) deduction is available, capped at Rs. 50,000.\n- A) Wrong: only Rs. 50,000 extra is allowed, not the full Rs. 1.8 lakh.\n- B) Wrong: 10% of salary = Rs. 1,20,000 would be the 80CCD(1) ceiling, but that basket is already full.\n- D) Wrong: the Rs. 50,000 additional room (80CCD(1B)) is still available.\n\nIntuition: With the Rs. 1.5 lakh basket full, the only extra NPS room left is the Rs. 50,000 under 80CCD(1B).\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A senior citizen receives Rs. 25,000 per month under a notified reverse mortgage scheme for several years. The annual amount received is:\nA) Taxable as other sources  B) Taxable as capital gains  C) Exempt under Section 10(43)  D) Taxable as pension\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Amounts received under a notified reverse mortgage (lump sum or instalments) are exempt under Section 10(43).\n- A), B), D) Wrong: it is a loan, not income, so no head applies.\n\nIntuition: Monthly reverse-mortgage payouts are loan disbursements — tax-free however they are received.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A high-premium ULIP's equity-oriented fund (held over 1 year) shows a gain of Rs. 4,00,000. Since equity ULIP funds do not get the Rs. 1.25 lakh 112A exemption framing here, the workbook treats the LTCG at:\nA) 20%  B) Slab  C) Exempt  D) 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equity-oriented funds within high-premium ULIPs are taxed at the concessional 12.50% for long-term gains (long-term period = 1 year).\n- A) Wrong: 20% is the *short-term* rate for these funds.\n- B) Wrong: slab applies to non-equity ULIP short-term gains.\n- C) Wrong: high-premium ULIP gains are taxable.\n\nIntuition: The equity fund inside the ULIP is taxed like equity: 12.50% long-term, 20% short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A *non-equity* fund within a high-premium ULIP is sold after 8 months with a gain of Rs. 1,00,000. This short-term gain is taxed at:\nA) 20%  B) 12.50%  C) Slab/normal rates  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Gains on non-equity funds in a high-premium ULIP follow normal capital-gains taxation: normal (slab) rates for short term and 12.50% for long term.\n- A) Wrong: 20% is for *equity* ULIP funds (short term).\n- B) Wrong: 12.50% would be the long-term, not short-term, rate.\n- D) Wrong: the gain is taxable.\n\nIntuition: Non-equity ULIP funds behave like ordinary non-equity assets — short term at slab, long term at 12.50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Mr. Z holds REIT units bought after 31-01-2018 (no grandfathering), sells listed units (STT paid) held 10 months with a gain of Rs. 2,00,000. The tax is:\nA) 12.50% under 112A  B) 20% under 111A  C) Slab rate  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed units held 10 months (≤12 months) → short-term; with STT paid, STCG is taxed at 20% under Section 111A.\n- A) Wrong: 112A (12.50%) is for long-term gains.\n- C) Wrong: slab applies only where STT is not paid.\n- D) Wrong: it is taxable.\n\nIntuition: Sold within 12 months on the exchange with STT → the special 20% short-term rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A Category-III AIF set up as a company earns trading profits. These profits are taxed:\nA) Pass-through to unit-holders  B) Exempt under 115UB  C) At the company AIF level like a normal company (no pass-through)  D) At MMR as a trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Category-III AIFs have no pass-through; taxed like a normal company/trust/LLP as applicable — here, as a company.\n- A) Wrong: only Cat-I/II get pass-through.\n- B) Wrong: 115UB applies to Cat-I/II, not Cat-III.\n- D) Wrong: it is a company, taxed at company rates, not trust MMR.\n\nIntuition: Category-III is just an ordinary taxpayer — taxed on its own profits at its entity rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Mr. P (individual) holds SGBs and opts for premature redemption in year 6. The capital gain on this premature redemption is:\nA) Taxable at 12.50%  B) Taxable at slab  C) Not taxable — redemption by an individual is not a transfer  D) Taxable at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Redemption by an individual — whether at maturity or premature (after year 5) — is not a transfer under Section 47, so no capital gain arises.\n- A), B), D) Wrong: there is no transfer, so no capital gain at any rate for an individual.\n\nIntuition: The Section 47 exemption covers *both* maturity and premature redemption for individuals — only a secondary-market sale is taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A life insurance policy issued on 01-06-2010 (between 01-04-2003 and 31-03-2012) has an annual premium exceeding 20% of the sum assured. The maturity proceeds are:\nA) Exempt under 10(10D)  B) Taxable (10(10D) exemption lost due to premium > 20% of sum assured)  C) Taxed as capital gains at 12.50%  D) Always exempt regardless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For policies issued 01-04-2003 to 31-03-2012, the 10(10D) exemption is lost if premium in any year exceeds 20% of the sum assured, so the maturity proceeds are taxable.\n- A) Wrong: the exemption is lost because the 20% threshold is breached.\n- C) Wrong: there is no flat capital-gains rate for this; it loses exemption (death proceeds would still be exempt).\n- D) Wrong: only death proceeds are always exempt; maturity proceeds here are taxable.\n\nIntuition: Older policies (2003–2012) use a 20% premium test; breach it and the maturity payout becomes taxable.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario — Mr. Arvind's diversified portfolio (FY 2024-25)**\n\nMr. Arvind, a resident individual salaried employee (old tax regime), has the following events during FY 2024-25:\n\n1. **ESOP:** His employer (a listed company) granted ESOPs. He exercised options for 200 shares when the FMV on the exercise date was Rs. 1,200 per share, paying the predetermined price of Rs. 200 per share.\n2. **SGB:** He bought 100 SGBs at nominal value Rs. 5,00,000 on 01-05-2019. SGB interest is 2.5% p.a. He holds them and lets them run toward maturity (no sale this year).\n3. **NPS:** He contributes Rs. 70,000 himself to his NPS account. His employer contributes Rs. 80,000. His \"salary\" (basic + eligible DA) is Rs. 8,00,000. He has already exhausted the Rs. 1,50,000 limit under Section 80C with other investments.\n4. **Gold ETF:** He bought 4,000 gold ETF units at Rs. 40 on 01-02-2020 and sold them at Rs. 70 on 10-06-2024.\n5. **Life insurance:** He receives Rs. 8,00,000 on the *death* of his father under a life policy where his father was insured and Arvind is the nominee.\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS1. Compute the ESOP perquisite taxable as salary.</summary>\n\n**Formula:** Perquisite = (FMV on exercise date − price paid per share) × number of shares.\n- FMV on exercise = Rs. 1,200; price paid = Rs. 200; shares = 200.\n- Perquisite = (1,200 − 200) × 200 = 1,000 × 200 = **Rs. 2,00,000**.\n\nThis Rs. 2,00,000 is taxed as a salary perquisite in the year of allotment. (The exercise-date FMV of Rs. 1,200 also becomes the cost of acquisition if he later sells the shares; holding period would run from the allotment date.)\n\nIntuition: He is taxed on the bargain element — the Rs. 1,000/share discount across 200 shares.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS2. What is the tax treatment of the SGBs for FY 2024-25 (interest and any gain)?</summary>\n\n- **Interest:** taxable under \"Income from other sources\" at slab rates, with no TDS (Government Security).\n  - Interest = Nominal × 2.5% = 5,00,000 × 2.5% = **Rs. 12,500** for the year.\n- **Capital gain:** He does not sell this year and is an individual holding toward redemption, so under Section 47 redemption by an individual is not a transfer → **no capital gain** arises.\n\nSo only the Rs. 12,500 interest is taxable this year.\n\nIntuition: Hold-to-redeem as an individual = gold appreciation is tax-free; only the 2.5% coupon is taxed.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS3. Compute Mr. Arvind's NPS deductions (old regime).</summary>\n\n- **Employee's own contribution (Rs. 70,000):** The Rs. 1,50,000 basket (80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1)) is already exhausted by other 80C investments, so nothing extra can be claimed under 80CCD(1).\n  - However, the additional deduction under **80CCD(1B)** is available, capped at **Rs. 50,000**. So Rs. 50,000 of his Rs. 70,000 is deductible (the remaining Rs. 20,000 gets no deduction).\n- **Employer's contribution (Rs. 80,000) under 80CCD(2):** old-regime cap = 10% of salary = 10% × 8,00,000 = Rs. 80,000.\n  - Deduction = lower of (actual Rs. 80,000, cap Rs. 80,000) = **Rs. 80,000**.\n\n**Total NPS deduction = 50,000 + 80,000 = Rs. 1,30,000** (over and above the Rs. 1.5 lakh 80C already claimed). Note the employer's Rs. 80,000 is also first added to his salary.\n\nIntuition: The 1.5 lakh basket is full, so only the Rs. 50,000 extra (80CCD(1B)) plus the separate employer 80CCD(2) deduction remain.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS4. Compute the capital gain on the gold ETF and state the tax treatment.</summary>\n\n- Sale value = 4,000 × 70 = Rs. 2,80,000; cost = 4,000 × 40 = Rs. 1,60,000.\n- **Gain = 2,80,000 − 1,60,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.**\n- **Holding/treatment:** Bought on 01-02-2020 (before 01-04-2023), so the \"always short-term\" special window (for gold ETFs bought after 01-04-2023 and sold before 01-04-2025) does **not** apply. Held over 12 months → **long-term capital gain, taxed at 12.50%**.\n\nIntuition: Because it was bought before April 2023, normal long-term gold treatment applies — 12.50% on Rs. 1,20,000.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS5. Is the Rs. 8,00,000 life insurance death benefit taxable?</summary>\n\n- **No — it is fully exempt under Section 10(10D).** Any sum received on the **death** of the insured person is always exempt; the premium-to-sum-assured conditions (10%/20%) and the Rs. 5 lakh endowment / Rs. 2.5 lakh ULIP premium rules never apply to death proceeds.\n\nSo Mr. Arvind includes Rs. 0 from this in taxable income.\n\nIntuition: Death benefits to a family/nominee are always protected from tax — no exceptions.\n</details>\n\n\n\n"}, {"num": "13", "title": "Tax Provisions for Special Cases", "module": "Taxation", "notes": "# Chapter 13: Tax Provisions for Special Cases — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- How **corporate actions** are taxed: bonus issues, share splits/consolidation, **buyback** (new post-Oct-2024 rule), rights issues, mergers/amalgamations.\n- **Stock Lending and Borrowing (SLB)**.\n- Conversion of **preference shares into equity**.\n- Mutual fund special cases: **segregated portfolios, consolidation of schemes, winding up**.\n- Plus the **Annexure tax rates** (slabs, surcharge, special rates, CII).\n\n---\n\n## 13.1 Bonus Shares\n\n**Bonus shares** = extra free shares given to existing shareholders, proportionate to holdings. No cost paid.\n\n**Key tax rules (held as capital asset):**\n- **No tax at allotment** — gains arise only at sale.\n- **Period of holding** counted from **date of allotment of the bonus shares** (listed: short-term if ≤12 months; unlisted: ≤24 months).\n- **Cost of acquisition = NIL** (unless allotted before 01-04-2001 → FMV on 01-04-2001 at the assessee's option).\n- If bonus shares qualify under 112A, the deemed cost = lower of (FMV on 31-01-2018) and (sale consideration) — since actual cost is nil.\n- **Sale consideration:** amount received; for *unquoted* shares, if consideration < FMV, FMV is used.\n\n**Tax rates:** STCG @20% (111A, STT) else slab; LTCG @12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (112A) / 12.50% (112 if no STT).\n\n**If held as stock-in-trade:** gains are **business income** (PGBP). Per ICDS-VIII, bonus shares are recorded at cost (nil), so stock value isn't enhanced.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 10,000 shares @₹105 (01-04-2020); 1:2 bonus → 5,000 bonus shares (01-09-2023); all 15,000 sold @₹120 (01-08-2024).\n- **Original 10,000:** held >12 months → LTCG = (120−105)×10,000 = **₹1,50,000** (12.50% on amount over ₹1.25 lakh, Sec 112A).\n- **Bonus 5,000:** cost nil, held 01-09-2023 to 01-08-2024 (11 months ≤12) → **STCG = ₹6,00,000 @20% (Sec 111A)**.\n\n⚠️ Bonus shares are a classic STCG trap: their **own** clock starts at allotment, and their cost is **nil**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.2 Share Split & Consolidation\n\n- **Split** = one share divided into more smaller shares (number up, market cap same).\n- **Consolidation** = many shares merged into fewer (number down, price up).\n\n**Tax:** Neither split nor consolidation is a **\"transfer\"** (Section 2(47)), so **no tax at the time** — tax only on later sale.\n- **Cost of acquisition:** original cost **apportioned** over the new shares.\n- **Period of holding:** counted from the **original acquisition date**.\n\n**💡 Consolidation example:** 2,000 shares @₹100 (₹2,00,000) consolidated 2:1 → 1,000 shares → cost = ₹2,00,000 / 1,000 = **₹200/share**.\n\n**💡 Split example:** 1,000 shares @₹150 (₹1,50,000) split 1:2 → 2,000 shares → cost = ₹1,50,000 / 2,000 = **₹75/share**.\n\n**Stock-in-trade (PGBP):** e.g., 10,000 shares @₹400 split 1:2 → 20,000 shares, cost ₹200 each; sold @₹207 → business income = 20,000 × (207−200) = **₹1,40,000**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.3 Buyback of Shares — **NEW RULE from 01-10-2024**\n\n**Buyback** = a company purchasing its own shares.\n\n| Period | Company | Shareholder |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Until 30-09-2024** | Pays **20% (+12% surcharge + 4% cess)** on distributed income | Proceeds **exempt** (Sec 10(34A)) |\n| **From 01-10-2024** | **No tax** | Proceeds taxed as **DIVIDEND** under *Income from Other Sources*; **no deduction** for cost or expenses; the **cost of acquisition becomes a capital loss** (LT/ST per holding), eligible for set-off |\n\n**💡 Worked example (post-Oct-2024):** 10,000 shares @₹100 (30-08-2019); company buys back 500 @₹125 (20-02-2025).\n- **Shareholder:** ₹62,500 (500×125) taxed as **dividend** (Other Sources); the ₹50,000 (500×100) cost becomes a **long-term capital loss** (held >2 years), available for set-off/carry-forward.\n- **Company:** no tax (buyback after 01-10-2024).\n\n⚠️ **Big shift:** post-Oct-2024 the *whole buyback amount* is dividend (not just the gain), and the cost is a capital loss — not netted.\n\n---\n\n## 13.4 Rights Issue\n\nA **rights issue** lets existing shareholders buy new shares (usually cheaper than market) in proportion to holdings. The shareholder may **subscribe** or **renounce** (sell the right to someone else).\n\n### 13.4.1 Renouncing the right\n- The right is a **capital asset**. **Cost of acquisition = nil.**\n- Period of holding = from the company's offer date to renouncement; almost always **short-term** (listed ≤12 months / unlisted ≤24 months).\n- Gain = sale consideration − nil. Taxed at **slab rates** (NOT 111A — a right is not a listed equity share).\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Mr. Paul, 1,000 shares; 2:1 rights @₹250; renounces right to buy 500 shares to Mr. X @₹200 (01-09-2024). Held 1 month → **STCG = ₹1,00,000 (500×200), cost nil, at slab rates.**\n\n### 13.4.2 Selling the shares (after exercising the right)\n- **Cost of the right shares** = price paid to the company (plus, for a buyer, any amount paid to the renouncer).\n- Holding period from **date of allotment** of the right shares.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Mr. Paul exercises, gets 500 shares @₹250 (01-12-2024), FMV ₹510 at allotment, sells @₹520 (25-01-2025, listed, STT). Held <12 months → **STCG = (520−250)×500 = ₹1,35,000 @20% (Sec 111A)**.\n\n**Stock-in-trade:** profit on right shares / renunciation = **business income**; cost of renounced right = nil.\n\n---\n\n## 13.5 Mergers & Amalgamations\n\n**Amalgamation** (Income Tax Act): one or more companies merge into another such that all property and liabilities pass to the amalgamated company, and ≥**75%** in value of the amalgamating company's shareholders become shareholders of the amalgamated company.\n\n### Tax treatment\n- **At allotment of new shares (capital asset):** **not a transfer** (Section 47(vii)) → no tax. (Stock-in-trade → taxed as business income at exchange.)\n- **At later sale:**\n  - **Cost of acquisition = amount paid for the ORIGINAL shares** in the amalgamating company.\n  - **Period of holding = from the original acquisition date.**\n\n**💡 Worked example:** Mr. X bought 10,000 shares of A Ltd. @₹58 (01-04-2023); A merged into AB Ltd. (01-08-2024), got 8,000 AB shares; sold @₹100 (01-09-2024, listed, STT).\n- Holding from 01-04-2023 → 17 months (>12) → **LTCG**.\n- Sale = 8,000×100 = ₹8,00,000; Cost = 10,000×58 = ₹5,80,000 → **LTCG ₹2,20,000 @12.50% (Sec 112A)** over ₹1.25 lakh. (STT-on-acquisition condition is waived for shares acquired via Section 47 modes.)\n\n---\n\n## 13.6 Stock Lending and Borrowing (SLB)\n\n**SLB** = a lender temporarily lends securities to a borrower (via an Authorised Intermediary — Clearing Corp of NSE/BSE) for a **fee**, and gets equivalent securities back later. Tenure 1 day to 12 months (usually 1 month, rollover allowed but ≤12 months total). Only F&O-eligible securities and liquid index ETFs qualify.\n\nMargins: lender deposits 25% of lending price (nil if lent on transaction date); borrower deposits 100% + fees + margins.\n\n### 13.6.1 Lender's tax\n- **Lending is NOT a transfer (Section 47(xv))** → **no capital gain**, even if the exact same scrips don't come back.\n- The **lending fee** is taxable under **PGBP** (if a business) or **Other Sources**. Expenses deductible.\n\n**💡 Example:** Lender earns ₹2,00,000 fee, paid ₹2,000 transaction charges → taxable income = **₹1,98,000**.\n\n### 13.6.2 Borrower's tax\n- The borrower buys/sells the stocks → gains/losses are **capital gains or PGBP**.\n- The **lending fee paid** is deductible against that income.\n\n**💡 Worked example (Mr. B, short-sell + hedge):** Borrowed 10,000 Reliance @₹5 fee, short-sold @₹1,600, bought call (strike ₹1,600, premium ₹30).\n- *Price falls to ₹1,500:* Futures profit (1,600−1,500)×10,000 = ₹10,00,000; call loss (30−10)×10,000 = −₹2,00,000; fee −₹50,000 → **net profit ₹7,50,000** (slab rate).\n- *Price rises to ₹1,700 (exercised call to settle):* premium paid ₹3,00,000 + fee ₹50,000 → **total loss ₹3,50,000**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.7 Conversion of Preference Shares into Equity Shares\n\nNormally a conversion = \"exchange\" = transfer. **But Section 47(xb)** says conversion of preference shares into equity of the **same company** is **NOT a transfer** → no capital gain at conversion.\n\nOn later sale of the equity shares:\n- **Cost of acquisition = cost of the preference shares.**\n- **Period of holding = from the date the preference shares were acquired.**\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 20,000 pref shares @₹10 (01-01-2010), converted 2:1 → 10,000 equity (01-01-2023, FMV ₹25), sold @₹35 (25-08-2024, listed, STT).\n- No tax at conversion (47(xb)).\n- Holding from 01-01-2010 → 14+ years (>12 months) → **LTCG**.\n- Sale = 10,000×35 = ₹3,50,000; Cost = 20,000×10 = ₹2,00,000 → **LTCG ₹1,50,000 @12.50% over ₹1.25 lakh (Sec 112A)**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.8 Segregated Portfolios of Mutual Funds (Side-pocketing)\n\nWhen a debt/money-market instrument in an MF suffers a **credit event**, SEBI allows creating a **segregated portfolio** (\"side pocket\"). Unit-holders then hold the same number of units in **two** schemes (main + segregated).\n\nTaxed like normal MF units, but:\n- **Period of holding:** the original units' holding period in the **main portfolio is included** for the segregated units.\n- **Cost of acquisition (formula):**\n  > Cost of segregated units = Cost of total portfolio × (NAV of asset transferred to segregated portfolio ÷ NAV of total portfolio just before segregation).\n- This cost is **subtracted** from the main portfolio's cost.\n\n**💡 Worked example:** 1,000 units @₹15 (cost ₹15,000); segregated 01-06-2022, segregated NAV ₹2/unit, total NAV before ₹12/unit.\n- Segregated cost = 15,000 × (2 ÷ 12) = **₹2,500** (₹2.5/unit).\n- Main portfolio cost = 15,000 − 2,500 = **₹12,500**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.9 Consolidation of Mutual Fund Schemes / Plans\n\nMerging two schemes/plans (both equity-oriented, or both non-equity) is **NOT a transfer** → no tax on the swap of units.\n- **Cost of acquisition** of consolidated units = cost of units in the consolidating scheme/plan.\n- **Period of holding** includes the time held in the consolidating scheme/plan.\n\n## 13.10 Winding Up of Mutual Fund Schemes\nA scheme may be wound up on expiry (close-ended), on a trustee-opinion event, on a **75%** unit-holder resolution, or on SEBI direction. After \"shutting\" (newspaper notice), no business/units are created or redeemed; assets are sold, liabilities paid, balance distributed to unit-holders in proportion to units.\n\n**Tax:** the amount received on winding up is **treated like a normal redemption** (capital gains as usual).\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Annexure highlights (tax rates you must know)\n\n### Old-regime slabs (Individual/HUF, FY 2024-25 & 2025-26)\n| Income | Rate |\n|---|---|\n| Up to ₹2,50,000 | Nil |\n| ₹2,50,001–₹5,00,000 | 5% |\n| ₹5,00,001–₹10,00,000 | 20% |\n| Above ₹10,00,000 | 30% |\n\n### New regime (Sec 115BAC) — FY 2024-25\n| Income | Rate |\n|---|---|\n| Up to ₹3,00,000 | Nil |\n| ₹3,00,001–₹7,00,000 | 5% |\n| ₹7,00,001–₹10,00,000 | 10% |\n| ₹10,00,001–₹12,00,000 | 15% |\n| ₹12,00,001–₹15,00,000 | 20% |\n| Above ₹15,00,000 | 30% |\n\n**Surcharge on capital gains (111A/112/112A):** capped at **15%** (nil ≤₹50L, 10% ₹50L–1cr, 15% above ₹1cr). Other income can reach 25%/37% (37% only old regime). **Cess: 4%** health & education on tax+surcharge.\n\n### Special rates (selected)\n| Section | Income | Rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| 111A | STCG on equity/equity-MF/business-trust (STT) | **20%** |\n| 112 | LTCG on listed securities/zero-coupon bonds | 12.50% |\n| 112A | LTCG on equity etc. over ₹1.25 lakh (STT) | 12.50% |\n| 115E | NRI LTCG on specified foreign-currency asset | 12.50% |\n| 115A | NR dividend | 20% (10% if from IFSC) |\n| 115BBH | Virtual Digital Assets (VDA) | **30%** |\n| 115BBE | Undisclosed income (68/69 etc.) | **60%** |\n| 115BBJ | Online game winnings | 30% |\n\n💡 **No indexation** on any asset now (Finance Act 2024), except immovable property in certain cases (12.5% without indexation vs 20% with indexation, for property bought before 23-07-2024).\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Bonus shares:** cost nil, holding from allotment; classic STCG trap.\n- **Split/consolidation:** not a transfer; cost apportioned, holding from original date.\n- **Buyback (from 01-10-2024):** full proceeds = **dividend** to shareholder; cost = **capital loss**; company pays nothing.\n- **Rights — renouncement:** cost nil, slab rate (not 111A). **Exercised shares:** cost = price paid, holding from allotment.\n- **Amalgamation:** allotment not a transfer (47(vii)); on sale, cost & holding from original shares.\n- **SLB:** lending not a transfer (47(xv)); fee = PGBP/Other Sources; borrower's gains = capital gains/PGBP.\n- **Pref → equity (47(xb)):** not a transfer; cost & holding carry over.\n- **Segregated portfolio:** cost split by NAV ratio, holding includes original.\n- **Scheme consolidation:** not a transfer, cost & holding carry over.\n- **Winding up:** treated as normal redemption.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term/Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Cost nil** | Bonus shares & renounced rights |\n| **01-10-2024** | Buyback rule change — proceeds taxed as dividend |\n| **Sec 10(34A)** | Old buyback exemption for shareholders (pre-Oct-2024) |\n| **75%** | Shareholder threshold for amalgamation / MF winding-up resolution |\n| **Section 47(vii) / (xv) / (xvi) / (xb)** | Amalgamation / SLB / reverse mortgage / pref-to-equity = not transfer |\n| **Section 2(47)** | Definition of \"transfer\" (split/consolidation excluded) |\n| **20% (111A) / 12.50% (112/112A)** | STCG / LTCG rates on equity |\n| **₹1,25,000** | 112A LTCG annual exemption |\n| **30% / 60%** | VDA / undisclosed income special rates |\n| **4% cess** | Health & education cess |\n| **15% surcharge cap** | On 111A/112/112A capital gains |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 13: Tax Provisions for Special Cases — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is the cost of acquisition of bonus shares?\n> **A:** **Nil** (unless allotted before 01-04-2001 → FMV on 01-04-2001 at the assessee's option).\n\n**Q2.** From which date is the holding period of bonus shares counted?\n> **A:** From the **date of allotment of the bonus shares** (not the original shares).\n\n**Q3.** Is there any tax when bonus shares are allotted?\n> **A:** **No** — neither company nor shareholder is taxed at allotment; tax arises only at sale.\n\n**Q4.** 10,000 shares @₹105 (Apr-2020); 1:2 bonus (Sep-2023); all 15,000 sold @₹120 (Aug-2024). How are the bonus shares taxed?\n> **A:** 5,000 bonus shares, cost nil, held ~11 months → **STCG ₹6,00,000 @20% (Sec 111A)**.\n\n**Q5.** Are share split and consolidation treated as a transfer?\n> **A:** **No** — not within Section 2(47); no tax at the time, only on later sale.\n\n**Q6.** After a 2:1 consolidation of 2,000 shares (₹2,00,000 total), what is the cost per consolidated share?\n> **A:** ₹2,00,000 ÷ 1,000 = **₹200/share**. (Holding period from the original acquisition date.)\n\n**Q7.** How is buyback taxed for the shareholder from 01-10-2024?\n> **A:** The **entire buyback proceeds** are taxed as **dividend** (Income from Other Sources); no deduction; the **cost becomes a capital loss**.\n\n**Q8.** How was buyback taxed before 01-10-2024?\n> **A:** The **company** paid 20% (+12% surcharge +4% cess); the shareholder's proceeds were **exempt** under Section 10(34A).\n\n**Q9.** In a post-Oct-2024 buyback of 500 shares (cost ₹100 each, bought-back @₹125), what happens to the ₹50,000 cost?\n> **A:** It becomes a **capital loss** (long-term or short-term per holding) available for set-off/carry-forward. The ₹62,500 received is taxed as dividend.\n\n**Q10.** What is the cost of acquisition of a renounced rights entitlement?\n> **A:** **Nil.**\n\n**Q11.** Is gain on renouncing a rights entitlement taxed under Section 111A?\n> **A:** **No** — a right is not a listed equity share, so it's taxed at **normal slab rates** (usually short-term).\n\n**Q12.** When a shareholder exercises rights and buys shares, what is the cost of those shares?\n> **A:** The **price paid to the company** (plus, for a buyer of the right, any amount paid to the renouncer). Holding from the date of allotment.\n\n**Q13.** Is allotment of shares in an amalgamation a transfer?\n> **A:** **No** — Section 47(vii) excludes it (if held as a capital asset). Stock-in-trade is taxed as business income.\n\n**Q14.** After an amalgamation, what cost and holding period apply to the new shares when sold?\n> **A:** **Cost = amount paid for the original shares**; **holding period from the original acquisition date**.\n\n**Q15.** What % of amalgamating-company shareholders must become shareholders of the amalgamated company?\n> **A:** At least **75% in value**.\n\n**Q16.** Is lending shares under SLB a transfer?\n> **A:** **No** — Section 47(xv); no capital gain even if the exact scrips aren't returned.\n\n**Q17.** How is the lender's SLB fee taxed?\n> **A:** Under **PGBP** (if a business) or **Income from Other Sources**, with related expenses deductible.\n\n**Q18.** Who is the Authorised Intermediary for SLB?\n> **A:** The **Clearing Corporation of NSE/BSE**; only F&O-eligible securities and liquid index ETFs qualify; tenure ≤12 months.\n\n**Q19.** How is the borrower in SLB taxed?\n> **A:** Gains/losses on the bought/sold stock are **capital gains or PGBP**; the lending fee paid is deductible.\n\n**Q20.** Is conversion of preference shares into equity of the same company a transfer?\n> **A:** **No** — Section 47(xb); no capital gain at conversion.\n\n**Q21.** After converting preference shares to equity, what cost and holding apply on sale?\n> **A:** **Cost = cost of the preference shares**; **holding period from the date the preference shares were acquired**.\n\n**Q22.** What is a segregated portfolio (side pocket)?\n> **A:** A separate scheme carved out of an MF for debt/money-market instruments that hit a **credit event**; unit-holders then hold units in both the main and segregated schemes.\n\n**Q23.** How is the cost of segregated-portfolio units computed?\n> **A:** Cost of total portfolio × (NAV of asset moved to segregated portfolio ÷ NAV of total portfolio just before segregation); this is subtracted from the main portfolio's cost.\n\n**Q24.** 1,000 units cost ₹15,000; segregated NAV ₹2, total NAV ₹12. Cost of segregated units?\n> **A:** 15,000 × (2/12) = **₹2,500** (₹2.5/unit); main portfolio cost becomes ₹12,500.\n\n**Q25.** For segregated units, how is the holding period determined?\n> **A:** It **includes** the period the original units were held in the main portfolio.\n\n**Q26.** Is consolidation of two mutual fund schemes a transfer?\n> **A:** **No** (if both are equity-oriented or both non-equity); cost and holding period of the consolidating scheme carry over.\n\n**Q27.** How is the amount received on winding up of a mutual fund scheme taxed?\n> **A:** Like a **normal redemption** — capital gains as usual.\n\n**Q28.** What is the LTCG exemption limit under Section 112A?\n> **A:** **₹1,25,000** per year; excess @12.50%.\n\n**Q29.** What is the surcharge cap on capital gains under 111A/112/112A?\n> **A:** **15%** (even above ₹2 crore), unlike other income which can reach 25%/37%.\n\n**Q30.** Tax rate on Virtual Digital Assets (Section 115BBH) and on undisclosed income (Section 115BBE)?\n> **A:** VDA = **30%**; undisclosed income = **60%**.\n\n**Q31.** Is indexation available on capital assets now?\n> **A:** **No** (Finance Act 2024) — except immovable property in certain cases (12.5% without indexation vs 20% with, for property bought before 23-07-2024).\n\n**Q32.** Can short-term capital loss be set off against long-term capital gains?\n> **A:** **Yes** — STCL can be set off against both short-term and long-term capital gains (whereas LTCL only against LTCG).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 13: Tax Provisions for Special Cases — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall, Q1–Q35), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application, Q36–Q70), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario, Q71–Q100), followed by a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking** — a wrong answer costs you a quarter of the marks for that question, so skip only when you are truly unsure. This bank covers the actual Chapter 13 topics: taxation of bonus issues, splits/consolidation, buyback, rights issues, mergers/amalgamations, stock lending & borrowing (SLB), conversion of preference shares to equity, segregated portfolios, consolidation and winding up of mutual fund schemes, plus the Annexure tax rates.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What are bonus shares?\nA) Shares issued to the public at a discount  B) Additional shares issued free to existing shareholders in proportion to shares already held  C) Shares bought back by the company  D) Shares issued to employees as salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus shares are additional shares a company issues to its existing shareholders, free of cost, based on the shares they already own; the aim is to reward shareholders and widen the equity base.\n- A) is wrong: issuing to the public at a discount is a public/discounted issue, not a bonus.\n- C) is wrong: that describes a buyback, the opposite action.\n- D) is wrong: shares given as salary are ESOPs/sweat equity, not bonus shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** From which date is the period of holding of bonus shares reckoned?\nA) Date of purchase of the original shares  B) Date of the company's announcement  C) Date of allotment of the bonus shares  D) Date of sale\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For bonus shares, the holding period starts on the **date of allotment of the bonus shares**, not the date the original shares were bought. This is why bonus shares are often a short-term capital gains trap.\n- A) is wrong: that rule applies to splits, consolidation, mergers and pref-to-equity conversions, not bonus shares.\n- B) is wrong: the announcement date is not the allotment date.\n- D) is wrong: the sale date ends the holding period, it does not start it.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** What is the cost of acquisition of bonus shares allotted free of cost (after 01-04-2001)?\nA) Face value  B) Market value on allotment date  C) Nil  D) Issue price of original shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since the shareholder pays nothing for bonus shares, their cost of acquisition is **nil**. The entire sale price later becomes the capital gain.\n- A), B), D) are wrong: no actual amount is paid, so none of these values is used (the only exception is bonus shares issued before 01-04-2001, where FMV on 01-04-2001 may be taken at the assessee's option).\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A listed bonus share sold within how many months of allotment is a short-term capital asset?\nA) 6 months  B) 36 months  C) 24 months  D) 12 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For listed shares, the threshold between short-term and long-term is **12 months**. Held for not more than 12 months = short-term; more than 12 months = long-term.\n- A) and B) are not the prescribed thresholds.\n- C) 24 months applies only to **unlisted** shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Under which section are short-term capital gains on listed equity shares (with STT paid) taxed?\nA) Section 111A  B) Section 112A  C) Section 115BBH  D) Section 10(34A)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section **111A** governs STCG on equity shares / equity-oriented MF units / business trust units where STT is paid; the rate is 20%.\n- B) Section 112A is for long-term gains, not short-term.\n- C) Section 115BBH taxes Virtual Digital Assets.\n- D) Section 10(34A) was the old buyback exemption for shareholders.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** What is the tax rate under Section 111A for STCG on equity shares (STT paid)?\nA) 10%  B) 12.50%  C) 15%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 111A short-term capital gains are taxed at a flat **20%**.\n- A), B), C) are wrong: 12.50% is the LTCG rate under 112/112A; 10% and 15% are surcharge slab figures, not the STCG rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Long-term capital gains under Section 112A are taxed at 12.50% only on the amount exceeding what annual threshold?\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 1,25,000  C) Rs. 2,50,000  D) Rs. 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 112A exempts the first **Rs. 1,25,000** of LTCG each year; only the excess is taxed at 12.50%.\n- A) Rs. 1,00,000 was the older exemption limit, now revised.\n- C) and D) are not the 112A threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If bonus shares are held as stock-in-trade, gains on their sale are taxable under which head?\nA) Capital gains  B) Income from other sources  C) Profits and gains from business or profession (PGBP)  D) Salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Stock-in-trade shares are trading assets, so their sale gain is **business income (PGBP)**, not capital gains.\n- A) applies only when shares are held as capital assets (investments).\n- B) and D) are unrelated heads.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Is a stock split treated as a \"transfer\" under the Income Tax Act?\nA) Yes, always  B) No  C) Only if listed  D) Only if the investor sells later\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Splitting (or consolidating) shares is **not** a transfer under Section 2(47), so no tax arises at the time of the split; tax arises only on later sale.\n- A) is wrong: it is specifically not a transfer.\n- C) and D) misstate the rule — listing is irrelevant, and the split itself is never taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** After a stock split, how is the cost of acquisition of the new shares determined?\nA) Set to nil  B) Original cost apportioned proportionately over the new (split) shares  C) Face value of the new shares  D) Market value on split date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The original amount paid is **divided proportionately** across the larger number of post-split shares.\n- A) Nil applies to bonus shares and renounced rights, not splits.\n- C) and D) are not used for split shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** From which date is the holding period of split/consolidated shares counted?\nA) Date of the split/consolidation  B) Date of allotment of new shares  C) Date of acquisition of the original shares  D) Date of sale\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Because the cost is derived from the original shares, the holding period is also counted from the **original acquisition date**.\n- A) and B) are wrong: the split/consolidation does not reset the clock.\n- D) ends, not starts, the holding period.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** What is consolidation of shares?\nA) Dividing shares into smaller units  B) Merging shares to reduce their number without reducing share capital  C) Buying back shares  D) Issuing free shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Consolidation merges several shares into fewer, reducing the share count and raising the price per share, while total capital stays the same.\n- A) describes a split (the opposite).\n- C) is a buyback.\n- D) is a bonus issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** From 01-10-2024, how are buyback proceeds taxed in the shareholder's hands?\nA) Exempt under Section 10(34A)  B) As long-term capital gains  C) As dividend under \"Income from Other Sources\"  D) As business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With effect from 01-10-2024, buyback proceeds are taxed as **dividend** under Income from Other Sources, with no deduction for cost or expenses.\n- A) is the pre-Oct-2024 position, now withdrawn.\n- B) and D) are wrong heads for buyback proceeds under the new rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Under the post-01-10-2024 buyback rules, what happens to the cost of acquisition of the shares bought back?\nA) It is deducted from the buyback proceeds  B) It is ignored entirely  C) It is treated as a capital loss available for set-off  D) It is added to income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The cost of acquisition of the bought-back shares becomes a **capital loss** (long-term or short-term per holding period), which can be set off / carried forward.\n- A) is wrong: no deduction is allowed against the dividend; the cost is handled separately as a loss.\n- B) is wrong: it is not ignored, it becomes a loss.\n- D) is wrong: cost reduces, not increases, taxable income.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Until 30-09-2024, at what rate did a domestic company pay tax on buyback distributed income (before surcharge and cess)?\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Until 01-10-2024 the company paid **20%** (plus 12% surcharge and 4% cess) on the distributed income, while the shareholder's receipt was exempt under Section 10(34A).\n- A), B), D) are not the prescribed buyback distribution tax rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** What is a rights issue?\nA) Free shares to existing shareholders  B) Splitting one share into many  C) Company buying its own shares  D) The right offered to existing shareholders to subscribe to new shares in proportion to their holdings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A rights issue gives **existing shareholders the right to buy newly issued shares** in proportion to their current holdings, usually at a price below market.\n- A) is a bonus issue.\n- B) is a split.\n- C) is buyback.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** What is the cost of acquisition of a right that is renounced in favour of another person?\nA) Market value  B) Issue price  C) Nil  D) Face value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The cost of acquisition of the renounced right is **nil**, so the entire fee received is the capital gain.\n- A), B), D) are not used; nothing was paid for the right itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Gains from renouncing a right are generally of what nature?\nA) Long-term capital gains  B) Short-term capital gains  C) Business income always  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rights are held only briefly (offer date to renouncement), so the gain is almost always **short-term capital gain**, taxed at the assessee's slab rate (not 111A, since a right is not a listed equity share).\n- A) is wrong: the holding period is too short to be long-term.\n- C) is wrong: it is business income only if shares were held as stock-in-trade.\n- D) is wrong: such gains are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Are gains on renouncement of a listed right covered by Section 111A's 20% rate?\nA) Yes  B) No  C) Only if STT is paid  D) Only for unlisted rights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even when the right is listed, **a right is not a listed equity share**, so Section 111A does not apply; the gain is taxed at normal slab rates.\n- A), C), D) misstate the rule — 111A simply does not cover renouncement of rights.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** When a shareholder exercises a rights issue and buys the shares, what is the cost of acquisition of those shares?\nA) Nil  B) The price paid to the company for the shares  C) Market value at allotment  D) Face value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the right is exercised, the cost is the **price actually paid to the company** for the right shares (plus, for a buyer of someone else's right, the amount paid to the renouncer).\n- A) Nil applies to a renounced right, not to exercised shares.\n- C) and D) are not the cost basis for exercised right shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** For amalgamation, at least what percentage in value of the amalgamating company's shareholders must become shareholders of the amalgamated company?\nA) 50%  B) 51%  C) 75%  D) 90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — One condition of amalgamation under the Income Tax Act is that shareholders holding **not less than 75%** in value of the amalgamating company become shareholders of the amalgamated company.\n- A), B), D) are not the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under which section is the allotment of amalgamated-company shares (held as capital asset) NOT treated as a transfer?\nA) Section 47(vii)  B) Section 47(xb)  C) Section 47(xv)  D) Section 111A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section **47(vii)** excludes the allotment of shares in the amalgamated company (in lieu of amalgamating-company shares held as capital asset) from the definition of transfer.\n- B) Section 47(xb) covers preference-to-equity conversion.\n- C) Section 47(xv) covers stock lending.\n- D) Section 111A is a tax rate provision, not an exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** On later sale of amalgamated-company shares, the cost of acquisition is:\nA) Nil  B) Market value at amalgamation  C) Face value of new shares  D) The amount paid for the original shares of the amalgamating company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The cost carries over: it is the **amount paid for the original shares** in the amalgamating company, and the holding period also runs from that original acquisition date.\n- A), B), C) do not reflect the carry-over rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** What does SLB stand for?\nA) Stock Lending and Borrowing  B) Securities Loan Bond  C) Short Lending Buyback  D) Secured Loan Brokerage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SLB = **Stock Lending and Borrowing**, a system where a lender lends securities to a borrower via an authorised intermediary for a fee.\n- B), C), D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Who acts as the Authorised Intermediary for SLB in India?\nA) RBI  B) SEBI directly  C) Clearing Corporation of NSE and BSE  D) Individual brokers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Clearing Corporations of NSE and BSE** are approved as the Authorised Intermediary for SLB.\n- A) RBI regulates banking, not SLB.\n- B) SEBI sets the framework but does not act as the intermediary itself.\n- D) Individual brokers are not the AI.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which securities are eligible for lending and borrowing under SLB?\nA) All listed shares  B) Only government bonds  C) Securities traded in the F&O segment and liquid index ETFs  D) Only unlisted shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only securities **traded in the F&O segment and liquid index ETFs** are eligible for SLB.\n- A), B), D) are wrong: the eligibility is restricted to F&O securities and liquid index ETFs.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Under Section 47(xv), the lending of securities under SLB:\nA) Is treated as a transfer  B) Is not treated as a transfer  C) Is taxed as capital gains  D) Is exempt only for institutions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lending securities under SLB is **not a transfer** (Section 47(xv)), even if the identical scrips are not returned, so no capital gain arises for the lender.\n- A) and C) are the opposite of the rule.\n- D) is wrong: the relief applies to all market participants, retail or institutional.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under which head is the lending fee earned by a lender in SLB taxable?\nA) Capital gains  B) PGBP or Income from Other Sources  C) Salary  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The lending fee is taxable under **PGBP** (if the lender is in that business) or otherwise under **Income from Other Sources**; related expenses are deductible.\n- A) is wrong: lending is not a transfer, so no capital gains.\n- C) and D) are incorrect heads/treatment.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** What is the maximum total duration of an SLB contract including rollovers?\nA) 1 month  B) 6 months  C) 12 months  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SLB contracts can run from 1 day to 12 months; with rollovers the total duration must not exceed **12 months** from the original contract date.\n- A) is the usual term but not the maximum.\n- B) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Under Section 47(xb), conversion of preference shares into equity shares of the same company is:\nA) A taxable transfer  B) Not a transfer  C) Taxed as dividend  D) Taxed as business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section **47(xb)** provides that converting preference shares into equity shares of the same company is **not a transfer**, so no capital gain arises at conversion.\n- A) is the opposite.\n- C) and D) are wrong heads.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** On later sale of equity shares obtained by converting preference shares, the holding period is counted from:\nA) The conversion date  B) The date the preference shares were acquired  C) The date of sale  D) 01-04-2001\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding period (and the cost) carry over, so it runs from the **date the preference shares were originally acquired**.\n- A) is wrong: conversion does not reset the clock.\n- C) ends the holding period.\n- D) is unrelated here.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** When a mutual fund creates a segregated portfolio (side pocket), the unit-holder then holds units in:\nA) Only the segregated scheme  B) Only the main scheme  C) Two schemes — the main and the segregated portfolio  D) A new merged scheme\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — On segregation the unit-holder holds the **same number of units in two schemes** — the main portfolio and the segregated portfolio.\n- A) and B) are wrong: both schemes exist.\n- D) describes consolidation, not segregation.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** For segregated-portfolio units, the period of holding:\nA) Starts fresh from the segregation date  B) Includes the period the original units were held in the main portfolio  C) Is always treated as short-term  D) Is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding period of segregated units **includes the time the original units were held** in the main portfolio.\n- A) is wrong: it does not start fresh.\n- C) and D) misstate the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Consolidation of two equity-oriented mutual fund schemes into one is:\nA) A taxable transfer  B) Not treated as a transfer  C) Taxed as dividend  D) Allowed only between equity and debt schemes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Consolidating two or more schemes of an equity-oriented fund (or two or more non-equity schemes) is **not treated as a transfer**, so no tax arises on the swap of units.\n- A) and C) are wrong.\n- D) is wrong: both schemes must be the same type (both equity-oriented, or both non-equity), not one of each.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** When a mutual fund scheme is wound up and the balance is paid to unit-holders, the amount received is:\nA) Fully exempt  B) Treated like a normal redemption (capital gains as usual)  C) Taxed as dividend  D) Taxed as business income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On winding up, the amount received by unit-holders is **treated like a normal redemption**, with capital gains computed as usual.\n- A) is wrong: it is not exempt.\n- C) and D) are incorrect heads.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. A holds bonus shares allotted on 01-09-2023 (listed) and sells them on 01-08-2024. The bonus shares are:\nA) Long-term, since original shares were held long  B) Long-term, because the company is listed  C) Exempt  D) Short-term, because held about 11 months from allotment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The bonus clock runs from allotment (01-09-2023). From 01-09-2023 to 01-08-2024 is about 11 months, which is not more than 12 months, so they are **short-term**.\n- A) is wrong: the original shares' holding does not transfer to bonus shares.\n- B) is wrong: listing alone does not make a gain long-term; holding period decides.\n- C) is wrong: they are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A bonus share (cost nil) is sold for a gain after being held 14 months (listed, STT paid). The gain is taxed under:\nA) Section 111A at 20%  B) Section 115BBH at 30%  C) Slab rates  D) Section 112A at 12.50% over Rs. 1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Held more than 12 months (listed) = long-term; with STT paid, equity LTCG falls under **Section 112A at 12.50% on the amount over Rs. 1,25,000**.\n- A) 111A is for short-term gains.\n- B) 115BBH is for Virtual Digital Assets.\n- C) slab rates do not apply to equity LTCG with STT.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Mr. A acquired 2,000 shares at Rs. 100 each, then the company consolidated 2 shares into 1. After consolidation he holds 1,000 shares. Cost per consolidated share is:\nA) Rs. 200  B) Rs. 100  C) Rs. 50  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula: cost per new share = total original cost ÷ number of shares after consolidation. Total cost = 2,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 2,00,000; after 2:1 consolidation he has 1,000 shares; Rs. 2,00,000 ÷ 1,000 = **Rs. 200**. Intuition: half the shares, so each carries double the cost.\n- B) Rs. 100 ignores the consolidation.\n- C) Rs. 50 would be a split, not consolidation.\n- D) Nil is for bonus shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Mr. A bought 1,000 shares at Rs. 150 each; the company split 1 share into 2. After the split he holds 2,000 shares. Cost per split share is:\nA) Rs. 150  B) Rs. 300  C) Rs. 75  D) Rs. 50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cost per new share = total cost ÷ shares after split. Total = 1,000 × Rs. 150 = Rs. 1,50,000; after 1:2 split he has 2,000 shares; Rs. 1,50,000 ÷ 2,000 = **Rs. 75**. Intuition: twice the shares, so each carries half the cost.\n- A) ignores the split.\n- B) doubles instead of halving.\n- D) Rs. 50 is the new face value, not the cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Post-01-10-2024, a company buys back shares from Mr. X for Rs. 62,500 (original cost Rs. 50,000, held over 2 years). How is this treated?\nA) Rs. 12,500 capital gain  B) Rs. 62,500 business income  C) Rs. 62,500 exempt  D) Rs. 62,500 dividend + Rs. 50,000 long-term capital loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the new rule the full Rs. 62,500 is taxed as **dividend** (no cost deduction), and the Rs. 50,000 cost becomes a **long-term capital loss** (held over 2 years) eligible for set-off.\n- A) is the old \"net the gain\" approach, no longer applicable.\n- B) dividend, not business income.\n- C) the old exemption was withdrawn from 01-10-2024.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** In a post-Oct-2024 buyback, the company that buys back the shares:\nA) Pays 20% distribution tax  B) Pays no tax  C) Pays 30% tax  D) Pays dividend distribution tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For buybacks on or after 01-10-2024, the **company has no tax liability**; the tax shifts to the shareholder (as dividend).\n- A) is the pre-Oct-2024 company position.\n- C) and D) are not applicable to buybacks now.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Mr. Paul renounces his right to buy 500 shares to Mr. X for Rs. 200 per share. His capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000 short-term, taxed at slab rate  B) Rs. 1,00,000 long-term at 12.50%  C) Rs. 1,00,000 at 20% under 111A  D) Nil, exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = consideration − cost = (500 × Rs. 200) − nil = **Rs. 1,00,000**, short-term, taxed at Mr. Paul's slab rate (a right is not a listed equity share, so 111A does not apply).\n- B) is wrong: the right is short-term, not long-term.\n- C) is wrong: 111A's 20% does not cover rights.\n- D) is wrong: the gain is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A buyer purchases right shares by paying Rs. 200/share to the renouncer and Rs. 250/share to the company. His cost of acquisition per share is:\nA) Rs. 200  B) Rs. 250  C) Rs. 450  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a buyer who acquires rights entitlement from another shareholder, cost = amount paid to renouncer + amount paid to the company = Rs. 200 + Rs. 250 = **Rs. 450**.\n- A) and B) capture only one leg.\n- D) Nil is for the original shareholder's renounced right, not for an exercising buyer.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Mr. X bought A Ltd. shares on 01-04-2023; A Ltd. amalgamated into AB Ltd. on 01-08-2024 and he sold AB Ltd. shares on 01-09-2024. The holding period is reckoned from:\nA) 01-08-2024  B) 01-09-2024  C) 01-04-2023  D) The amalgamation announcement date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For amalgamation, the holding period of the new shares runs from the **original acquisition date** of the amalgamating-company shares (01-04-2023), making the holding about 17 months (long-term).\n- A) and D) are wrong: amalgamation does not reset the clock.\n- B) is the sale date.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A lender earns Rs. 2,00,000 lending fee under SLB and pays Rs. 2,000 transaction charges. Taxable income from lending is:\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 1,98,000  C) Rs. 2,02,000  D) Nil (not a transfer)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fee is taxable (PGBP or Other Sources) and related expenses are deductible: Rs. 2,00,000 − Rs. 2,000 = **Rs. 1,98,000**.\n- A) ignores the deductible expense.\n- C) adds instead of subtracting expenses.\n- D) is wrong: although lending is not a transfer (no capital gain), the fee itself is taxable income.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A borrower in SLB sells the borrowed shares. Any gain or loss on that sale is taxable under:\nA) Income from other sources only  B) Always exempt  C) Salary  D) Capital gains or PGBP, as the case may be\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The borrower buys/sells the stock, so gains or losses fall under **capital gains or PGBP**, depending on whether held as investment or stock-in-trade; the lending fee paid is deductible.\n- A) is incomplete.\n- B) is wrong: borrower transactions are taxable.\n- C) is an unrelated head.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Mr. X converts 20,000 preference shares (cost Rs. 10 each) into 10,000 equity shares (2:1). On later sale, his cost of acquisition of the equity shares is:\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 1,00,000  C) Nil  D) FMV at conversion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cost carries over from the preference shares: 20,000 × Rs. 10 = **Rs. 2,00,000**. The FMV at conversion is ignored because conversion is not a transfer (47(xb)).\n- B) wrongly divides by the equity count.\n- C) Nil is for bonus shares.\n- D) FMV is not used under the carry-over rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A mutual fund segregates its portfolio. The cost of acquisition of the segregated units is computed as: total cost ×\nA) (NAV of asset transferred to segregated portfolio ÷ NAV of total portfolio before segregation)  B) (NAV of total portfolio ÷ NAV of segregated asset)  C) (Segregated units ÷ total units)  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Segregated cost = total portfolio cost × (NAV of asset transferred to segregated portfolio ÷ NAV of total portfolio immediately before segregation). It splits the cost in proportion to value moved to the side pocket.\n- B) inverts the ratio.\n- C) uses unit counts instead of NAV.\n- D) is wrong: a cost is assigned, not nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** After segregation, the cost assigned to the segregated units is:\nA) Added to the main portfolio cost  B) Reduced from the main portfolio's cost  C) Ignored  D) Treated as a loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cost allocated to the segregated units is **subtracted from the main portfolio's cost**, so the total cost is simply split between the two, not duplicated.\n- A) would double-count the cost.\n- C) and D) misstate the treatment.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** When two schemes consolidate, the cost of acquisition of the consolidated units is:\nA) Nil  B) Market value at consolidation  C) Face value  D) The cost of the units in the consolidating scheme\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The cost carries over: it equals the **cost of the units held in the consolidating scheme**, and the holding period includes the time held there.\n- A), B), C) do not reflect the carry-over rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A close-ended mutual fund scheme is normally wound up:\nA) Whenever the AMC wishes  B) On expiry of its fixed duration / redemption of units (unless rolled over)  C) Only on SEBI direction  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A close-ended scheme is wound up on the **expiry of its fixed duration** on redemption of units, unless it is rolled over for a further period.\n- A) is wrong: there is a defined trigger.\n- C) is only one of the routes for other (open-ended) schemes.\n- D) is incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** What percentage of unit-holders passing a resolution can trigger winding up of a mutual fund scheme?\nA) 51%  B) 66%  C) 75%  D) 90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A scheme may be wound up if **75%** of the unit-holders pass a resolution to wind it up (other routes: a trustee-opinion event or SEBI direction).\n- A), B), D) are not the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Mr. Rishabh holds 10,000 shares as stock-in-trade at Rs. 400 each, then the company splits 1:2 (20,000 shares). He sells all at Rs. 207. His business income is:\nA) Rs. 1,40,000  B) Rs. 70,000  C) Rs. 1,40,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Post-split cost = Rs. 40,00,000 ÷ 20,000 = Rs. 200/share. Business income = 20,000 × (Rs. 207 − Rs. 200) = **Rs. 1,40,000**.\n- B) halves wrongly.\n- C) ignores the cost subtraction.\n- D) is wrong: there is a Rs. 7/share profit.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which statement about the lender's margin in SLB is correct?\nA) Lender deposits 100% of lending price  B) Lender deposits 25% of the lending price (nil if lent on the transaction date)  C) Lender deposits no margin ever  D) Lender deposits 50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The lender deposits **25% of the lending price** as margin; if the securities are lent on the transaction date itself, no margin is required.\n- A) 100% is the borrower's requirement.\n- C) and D) misstate the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The borrower in SLB is required to deposit:\nA) 25% of the lending price  B) 100% of the lending price plus lending fee and applicable margins  C) Nothing  D) Only the lending fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The borrower deposits **100% of the lending price, the lending fee, value-at-risk and extreme-loss margins upfront**, plus daily mark-to-market margin thereafter.\n- A) is the lender's margin.\n- C) and D) understate the borrower's obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A right that is exercised gives shares whose holding period is reckoned from:\nA) The offer date  B) The renouncement date  C) The original share purchase  D) The date of allotment of the right shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For shares acquired by exercising a right, the holding period runs from the **date of allotment of those right shares**.\n- A) and B) relate to the right itself (renouncement), not the allotted shares.\n- C) applies to splits/mergers/pref-conversions, not exercised rights.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** If a stock-in-trade holder profits from selling right shares, the gain is taxable as:\nA) Business income  B) Short-term capital gain  C) Long-term capital gain  D) Dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Profits from right shares (or renunciation of a right) when the underlying shares are held as **stock-in-trade** are taxed as **business income**; the cost of a renounced right is deemed nil.\n- B) and C) apply only to capital assets.\n- D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Under the new buyback regime, \"no deduction for expenses or cost of acquisition\" means:\nA) The shareholder can deduct the cost from the dividend  B) The full proceeds are dividend, and the cost is separately a capital loss  C) Nothing is taxable  D) The cost is added to income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Against the dividend, no deduction is allowed, so the **entire proceeds are dividend**; the cost of acquisition is dealt with separately as a **capital loss**.\n- A) directly contradicts the rule.\n- C) and D) misstate the treatment.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which of the following is NOT regarded as a \"transfer\" under the Income Tax Act?\nA) Sale of bonus shares  B) Conversion of preference shares into equity (47(xb))  C) Renouncement of a right  D) Sale of right shares after exercise\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Conversion of preference shares into equity of the same company is specifically **not a transfer** (47(xb)).\n- A), C), D) are all genuine transfers that attract capital gains tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** For a virtual digital asset (VDA), the special tax rate under Section 115BBH is:\nA) 12.50%  B) 20%  C) 30%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Income from the transfer of any Virtual Digital Asset is taxed at a flat **30%** under Section 115BBH.\n- A) and B) are equity LTCG/STCG rates.\n- D) 60% is for undisclosed income under 115BBE.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Undisclosed income under Sections 68/69 etc. is taxed under Section 115BBE at:\nA) 30%  B) 40%  C) 60%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 115BBE taxes undisclosed/unexplained income (Sections 68, 69, 69A–D) at **60%**.\n- A) is the VDA/online-game rate.\n- B) and D) are not prescribed here.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Health and education cess is levied at what rate on income tax plus surcharge?\nA) 2%  B) 3%  C) 4%  D) 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Health and education cess is **4%** of (income tax + surcharge).\n- A), B), D) are not the current cess rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The surcharge on capital gains covered under Sections 111A, 112 and 112A is capped at:\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 25%  D) 37%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surcharge on these capital gains is **capped at 15%** (Nil up to Rs. 50 lakh, 10% from Rs. 50 lakh–1 crore, 15% above Rs. 1 crore).\n- A) is the mid-slab figure only.\n- C) and D) apply to \"any other income,\" not to these capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Under the old tax regime (Individual/HUF), income above Rs. 10,00,000 is taxed at:\nA) 20%  B) 25%  C) 30%  D) 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In the old regime, income above Rs. 10,00,000 is taxed at **30%** (Nil up to Rs. 2,50,000; 5% Rs. 2,50,001–5,00,000; 20% Rs. 5,00,001–10,00,000).\n- A) is the Rs. 5–10 lakh slab rate.\n- B) and D) are not old-regime slab rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A non-resident's LTCG from transfer of a specified asset purchased in foreign currency is taxed under Section 115E at:\nA) 12.5%  B) 10%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 115E taxes a Non-Resident Indian's LTCG from a specified foreign-currency-purchased asset at **12.5%**.\n- B) and C) relate to other 115A/115AC incomes.\n- D) is the VDA rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** For a non-resident, income received in respect of units of specified Mutual Funds/UTI purchased in foreign currency is taxed under Section 115A at:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Such income is taxed at **20%** under Section 115A.\n- A) 5% applies to certain interest (e.g., 194LD).\n- B) 10% applies to interest on bonds under 115AC, or dividend from an IFSC unit.\n- D) 30% is unrelated here.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A non-resident's dividend income (not at a special rate) is generally taxed under Section 115A at 20%, but at what rate if received from a unit in an IFSC?\nA) 10%  B) 5%  C) 15%  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Dividend received from a unit in an IFSC is taxed at **10%** under Section 115A; otherwise the rate is 20%.\n- B), C), D) are not the prescribed IFSC dividend rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which best describes the benefit of SLB to the lender?\nA) Avoiding settlement failure  B) Arbitrage between spot and futures  C) Covering a short position  D) Earning incremental return (lending fee) on an idle portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For a lender, SLB provides **incremental return (lending fee) on an otherwise idle portfolio** — useful for long-term holdings such as mutual funds.\n- A), B), C) are benefits to the **borrower**, not the lender.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** When a mutual fund scheme is \"shut\" (newspaper notice given), the trustees/AMC must:\nA) Continue redeeming units  B) Cease creating, cancelling, issuing or redeeming units  C) Immediately distribute assets  D) Convert it into a close-ended scheme\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From the date of publication, the trustee/AMC must **cease carrying on business, and cease creating, cancelling, issuing or redeeming units** of the scheme.\n- A) directly contradicts the rule.\n- C) happens later, after disposing of assets and paying liabilities.\n- D) is not part of winding up.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** In a winding up, the sale proceeds of the scheme's assets are used FIRST to:\nA) Pay unit-holders  B) Pay the AMC fees only  C) Buy new securities  D) Discharge liabilities due and payable, then meet winding-up expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Proceeds are **first used to discharge liabilities due and payable**, then to provide for winding-up expenses; only the **balance** is paid to unit-holders in proportion to their interest.\n- A) happens last (the balance).\n- B) and C) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. A bought 10,000 listed shares at Rs. 105 on 01-04-2020. A 1:2 bonus gave 5,000 bonus shares on 01-09-2023. He sold all 15,000 at Rs. 120 on 01-08-2024. What is the LTCG on the 10,000 original shares?\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 7,50,000  D) Rs. 12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Original shares held 01-04-2020 to 01-08-2024 (>12 months) = long-term. LTCG = (Rs. 120 − Rs. 105) × 10,000 = **Rs. 1,50,000** (taxed at 12.50% under 112A on the part over Rs. 1,25,000).\n- B) is the bonus-share STCG, not the originals.\n- C) wrongly combines both.\n- D) is the gross sale value of the originals, not the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Continuing Q71, what is the STCG on the 5,000 bonus shares (cost nil, held 01-09-2023 to 01-08-2024)?\nA) Rs. 1,50,000  B) Rs. 75,000  C) Nil  D) Rs. 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bonus shares cost nil and were held about 11 months (≤12) = short-term. STCG = (Rs. 120 × 5,000) − nil = **Rs. 6,00,000**, taxed at 20% under Section 111A.\n- A) is the originals' LTCG.\n- B) and C) miscompute; the full sale value is the gain because cost is nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Mr. Paul (1,000 shares) gets a 2:1 rights offer at Rs. 250. He renounces the right to buy 500 shares to Mr. X at Rs. 200/share. His short-term capital gain is:\nA) Nil  B) Rs. 2,50,000  C) Rs. 1,25,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Gain = consideration − cost = (500 × Rs. 200) − nil = **Rs. 1,00,000**, short-term, at slab rates (a right is not covered by 111A).\n- A) is wrong: the gain is taxable.\n- B) double-counts.\n- C) is the cost the company would have charged (500 × Rs. 250), irrelevant to a renouncement.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Mr. Paul instead exercises the right, gets 500 shares at Rs. 250 (allotted 01-12-2024, listed, STT), and sells them at Rs. 520 on 25-01-2025. His capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000 LTCG  B) Rs. 1,35,000 STCG at 20%  C) Rs. 2,60,000 STCG  D) Rs. 1,35,000 LTCG at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held under 12 months (01-12-2024 to 25-01-2025) = short-term. STCG = (Rs. 520 − Rs. 250) × 500 = **Rs. 1,35,000**, taxed at 20% under Section 111A (STT paid).\n- A) and D) are wrong: it is short-term, not long-term.\n- C) Rs. 2,60,000 is the sale value, not the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Mr. X bought 10,000 A Ltd. shares at Rs. 58 (01-04-2023). After amalgamation he received 8,000 AB Ltd. shares and sold them at Rs. 100 (01-09-2024, listed, STT). His capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 8,00,000 STCG  B) Rs. 2,20,000 STCG at 20%  C) Rs. 4,20,000 LTCG  D) Rs. 2,20,000 LTCG at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Holding runs from 01-04-2023 (about 17 months) = long-term. Sale = 8,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 8,00,000; cost = original 10,000 × Rs. 58 = Rs. 5,80,000; LTCG = **Rs. 2,20,000** at 12.50% under 112A on the part over Rs. 1,25,000.\n- A) and B) are wrong: it is long-term.\n- C) uses a wrong cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Mr. X bought 20,000 preference shares at Rs. 10 (01-01-2010), converted 2:1 to 10,000 equity shares (FMV Rs. 25 on 01-01-2023), and sold at Rs. 35 on 25-08-2024 (listed, STT). His LTCG is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 3,50,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — No tax at conversion (47(xb)). On sale: holding from 01-01-2010 = long-term; sale = 10,000 × Rs. 35 = Rs. 3,50,000; cost carries over = 20,000 × Rs. 10 = Rs. 2,00,000; LTCG = **Rs. 1,50,000** at 12.50% (112A) over Rs. 1,25,000. FMV Rs. 25 is ignored.\n- A) uses a wrong cost.\n- C) is the sale value.\n- D) is wrong: the later sale is a transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Mr. X holds 1,000 units bought at Rs. 15 (cost Rs. 15,000). The fund segregates: segregated NAV Rs. 2/unit, total NAV before segregation Rs. 12/unit. The cost of the segregated units is:\nA) Rs. 2,500  B) Rs. 2,000  C) Rs. 12,500  D) Rs. 15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Segregated cost = total cost × (segregated NAV ÷ total NAV) = Rs. 15,000 × (2 ÷ 12) = **Rs. 2,500** (Rs. 2.5/unit).\n- B) uses NAV not cost.\n- C) is the main-portfolio cost after subtraction.\n- D) is the full original cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Continuing Q77, the cost of the units remaining in the main portfolio is:\nA) Rs. 15,000  B) Rs. 12,500  C) Rs. 2,500  D) Rs. 17,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Main cost = original cost − segregated cost = Rs. 15,000 − Rs. 2,500 = **Rs. 12,500**. The total cost is split, not duplicated.\n- A) ignores the subtraction.\n- C) is the segregated cost.\n- D) wrongly adds.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. B borrows 10,000 Reliance shares (fee Rs. 5/share), short-sells at Rs. 1,600, and buys a call (strike Rs. 1,600, premium Rs. 30). Price falls to Rs. 1,500; he squares off the short at Rs. 1,500 and sells the call at Rs. 10. His futures/short profit is:\nA) Rs. 10,00,000  B) Rs. 5,00,000  C) Rs. 16,00,000  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Short-sold at Rs. 1,600, bought back at Rs. 1,500 → profit Rs. 100/share × 10,000 = **Rs. 10,00,000**.\n- B), C), D) miscompute the Rs. 100/share gain on 10,000 shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Continuing Q79, the loss on the call option (premium Rs. 30, sold at Rs. 10) over 10,000 shares is:\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 2,00,000  C) Rs. 3,00,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loss = (Rs. 30 − Rs. 10) × 10,000 = **Rs. 2,00,000** (premium paid Rs. 30, recovered Rs. 10).\n- A) uses the wrong difference.\n- C) ignores the Rs. 10 recovery.\n- D) is the lending fee, not the option loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Continuing Q79–Q80, the lending fee paid (Rs. 5/share × 10,000) and the net result are:\nA) Fee Rs. 50,000; net profit Rs. 7,50,000  B) Fee Rs. 50,000; net loss Rs. 7,50,000  C) Fee Rs. 5,00,000; net profit Rs. 5,00,000  D) Fee Rs. 50,000; net profit Rs. 12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Fee = Rs. 5 × 10,000 = **Rs. 50,000**. Net = Rs. 10,00,000 (futures) − Rs. 2,00,000 (call) − Rs. 50,000 (fee) = **Rs. 7,50,000** profit (taxed at slab rate).\n- B) wrongly flips to a loss.\n- C) miscomputes the fee.\n- D) omits the call loss and fee.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Mr. B (Q79 setup) instead sees the price rise to Rs. 1,700; he exercises the call to take delivery and return the shares. He never closes a profit; he paid Rs. 30 premium and Rs. 5 fee per share. His total loss is:\nA) Rs. 3,00,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 3,50,000  D) Rs. 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Call premium = Rs. 30 × 10,000 = Rs. 3,00,000; lending fee = Rs. 5 × 10,000 = Rs. 50,000. Using the call to settle the short avoids a market loss, so the cost is just premium + fee = **Rs. 3,50,000** total loss.\n- A) omits the fee.\n- B) is only the fee.\n- D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** An investor's LTCG under Section 112A for the year is Rs. 1,75,000 (equity, STT paid). The taxable LTCG after the annual exemption is:\nA) Rs. 1,75,000  B) Rs. 1,25,000  C) Rs. 50,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the amount over the Rs. 1,25,000 exemption is taxed: Rs. 1,75,000 − Rs. 1,25,000 = **Rs. 50,000** (at 12.50%).\n- A) ignores the exemption.\n- B) is the exempt slice.\n- D) is wrong: there is taxable gain above the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Continuing Q83, the tax (before cess) on the Rs. 50,000 taxable LTCG at 12.50% is:\nA) Rs. 6,250  B) Rs. 10,000  C) Rs. 21,875  D) Rs. 5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax = Rs. 50,000 × 12.50% = **Rs. 6,250** (before adding 4% cess).\n- B) uses 20%.\n- C) wrongly taxes the full Rs. 1,75,000.\n- D) uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor has STCG of Rs. 4,00,000 under Section 111A (equity, STT). The tax (before cess) is:\nA) Rs. 40,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 80,000  D) Rs. 1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 111A rate is 20%: Rs. 4,00,000 × 20% = **Rs. 80,000** (before 4% cess).\n- A) uses 10%.\n- B) uses 12.50%.\n- D) uses 30%.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Mr. A consolidated 2,000 shares (cost Rs. 100 each) 2:1 into 1,000 shares (cost Rs. 200 each), then sold all 1,000 at Rs. 260. His capital gain is:\nA) Rs. 60,000  B) Rs. 1,20,000  C) Rs. 2,60,000  D) Rs. 1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cost per share after consolidation = Rs. 200; gain = (Rs. 260 − Rs. 200) × 1,000 = **Rs. 60,000**. The holding period runs from the original purchase date.\n- B) wrongly doubles.\n- C) is the sale value.\n- D) uses the wrong cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A non-resident has interest on rupee-denominated bonds of an Indian company under Section 194LD. The Section 115A rate is:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Such interest (rupee-denominated bonds / govt securities / municipal debt under 194LD) is taxed at **5%** under Section 115A.\n- B) applies to bonds under 115AC.\n- C) applies to MF/UTI units in foreign currency.\n- D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A resident senior citizen (old regime) has total income of Rs. 4,50,000. Given Nil up to Rs. 3,00,000 and 5% on Rs. 3,00,001–5,00,000, the tax (before cess) is:\nA) Nil  B) Rs. 7,500  C) Rs. 15,000  D) Rs. 22,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a senior citizen, the first Rs. 3,00,000 is Nil; the next Rs. 1,50,000 (Rs. 3,00,001–4,50,000) is taxed at 5% = **Rs. 7,500**.\n- A) ignores the slab above Rs. 3,00,000.\n- C) taxes the full Rs. 3,00,001–5,00,000 band, but income is only Rs. 4,50,000.\n- D) uses a wrong rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** An assessee (old regime, not senior) has total income of Rs. 6,00,000. Using Nil to Rs. 2,50,000, 5% on Rs. 2,50,001–5,00,000, 20% on Rs. 5,00,001–10,00,000, the tax (before cess) is:\nA) Rs. 12,500  B) Rs. 32,500  C) Rs. 20,000  D) Rs. 45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 5% on Rs. 2,50,000 (the Rs. 2.5L–5L band) = Rs. 12,500; plus 20% on Rs. 1,00,000 (Rs. 5L–6L) = Rs. 20,000; total = **Rs. 32,500**.\n- A) covers only the first taxable band.\n- C) covers only the 20% band.\n- D) over-taxes.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A company buys back 800 shares at Rs. 150 (cost Rs. 90 each, held 8 months) post-01-10-2024. The dividend income and capital loss are:\nA) Dividend Rs. 1,20,000; short-term capital loss Rs. 72,000  B) Dividend Rs. 48,000; no loss  C) Dividend Rs. 1,20,000; long-term capital loss Rs. 72,000  D) Capital gain Rs. 48,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Dividend = 800 × Rs. 150 = Rs. 1,20,000 (full proceeds, no deduction). Cost = 800 × Rs. 90 = Rs. 72,000 becomes a **short-term capital loss** (held only 8 months).\n- B) wrongly nets.\n- C) wrongly calls it long-term.\n- D) uses the old netting approach.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A non-resident has interest on bonds of an Indian PSU purchased in foreign currency (Section 115AC). The rate is:\nA) 5%  B) 10%  C) 20%  D) 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on bonds of an Indian company or PSU purchased in foreign currency is taxed at **10%** under Section 115AC.\n- A) is the 194LD rupee-bond rate.\n- C) and D) are not applicable here.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Mr. Z bought 1,000 listed shares at Rs. 200 (01-05-2023) and sold at Rs. 260 on 20-06-2024 (STT). The gain is:\nA) Rs. 60,000 STCG at 20%  B) Rs. 2,60,000 LTCG  C) Nil  D) Rs. 60,000 LTCG at 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Held 01-05-2023 to 20-06-2024 (>12 months) = long-term. Gain = (Rs. 260 − Rs. 200) × 1,000 = **Rs. 60,000**, under 112A at 12.50% (below the Rs. 1,25,000 exemption, so effectively no tax this year alone).\n- A) is wrong: it is long-term, not short-term.\n- B) is the sale value.\n- C) ignores the computed gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A trader holds 5,000 shares as stock-in-trade at Rs. 80; a 1:1 bonus is allotted (cost nil per ICDS-VIII). She sells all 10,000 shares at Rs. 50. Her business income/loss is:\nA) Income Rs. 1,00,000  B) Loss Rs. 1,00,000  C) Income Rs. 5,00,000  D) Loss Rs. 4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total cost = original 5,000 × Rs. 80 = Rs. 4,00,000 (bonus cost nil, stock not enhanced). Sale = 10,000 × Rs. 50 = Rs. 5,00,000. Business income = Rs. 5,00,000 − Rs. 4,00,000 = **Rs. 1,00,000**.\n- B) wrongly treats it as a loss.\n- C) ignores the cost.\n- D) miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An assessee opts for the new regime (FY 2024-25) with total income Rs. 8,00,000. Using Nil to Rs. 3,00,000; 5% on Rs. 3,00,001–7,00,000; 10% on Rs. 7,00,001–10,00,000, the tax (before cess/rebate) is:\nA) Rs. 20,000  B) Rs. 30,000  C) Rs. 40,000  D) Rs. 25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 5% on Rs. 4,00,000 (Rs. 3L–7L band) = Rs. 20,000; plus 10% on Rs. 1,00,000 (Rs. 7L–8L) = Rs. 10,000; total = **Rs. 30,000**.\n- A) covers only the 5% band.\n- C) over-taxes.\n- D) uses a wrong band.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Mr. P renounced a right for Rs. 80,000 (cost nil) and separately had a Rs. 50,000 long-term capital loss from a buyback. Assuming set-off is permitted, his net taxable position from these two items is:\nA) Rs. 80,000 STCG and a separate Rs. 50,000 LTCL (different categories)  B) Rs. 30,000 net gain by simply subtracting  C) Nil  D) Rs. 1,30,000 gain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The renouncement produces a **Rs. 80,000 short-term capital gain**; the buyback cost is a **long-term capital loss of Rs. 50,000**. A long-term capital loss can only be set off against long-term capital gains, so it cannot reduce this STCG; they remain in separate categories.\n- B) wrongly nets across loss categories.\n- C) and D) miscompute.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A fund consolidates two equity-oriented schemes. Mr. K held units in the consolidating scheme since 01-03-2021 at a cost of Rs. 40,000; he sells the consolidated units on 01-09-2024 for Rs. 70,000. The holding period and cost used are:\nA) From 01-03-2021; cost Rs. 40,000  B) From consolidation date; cost market value  C) From 01-03-2021; cost nil  D) From sale date; cost Rs. 70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Consolidation is not a transfer; holding period includes the time in the consolidating scheme (**from 01-03-2021**) and cost carries over (**Rs. 40,000**), giving a long-term gain of Rs. 30,000.\n- B) and C) ignore the carry-over rule.\n- D) is nonsensical (cost cannot equal sale value here).\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An online-game winning of Rs. 2,00,000 is taxed under Section 115BBJ at what rate, giving tax (before cess) of:\nA) 12.50%; Rs. 25,000  B) 20%; Rs. 40,000  C) 30%; Rs. 60,000  D) 60%; Rs. 1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Online-game winnings are taxed at a flat **30%** (Section 115BBJ): Rs. 2,00,000 × 30% = **Rs. 60,000** before cess.\n- A) and B) use wrong rates.\n- D) uses the undisclosed-income rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Mr. Q bought 4,000 listed shares at Rs. 250; a 1:4 bonus gave 1,000 bonus shares. Two years later he sells only the 1,000 bonus shares at Rs. 300 (STT). Treating them as long-term, the LTCG is:\nA) Rs. 50,000  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus shares have **nil cost**, so the entire sale value is the gain: 1,000 × Rs. 300 = **Rs. 3,00,000** LTCG (held >12 months), taxed under 112A at 12.50% over Rs. 1,25,000.\n- A) and C) wrongly assign a cost to bonus shares.\n- D) ignores that the gain equals the full sale value.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A lender lends F&O-eligible shares on the transaction date itself. The margin the lender must deposit is:\nA) 25% of lending price  B) 100% of lending price  C) Nil  D) 50% of lending price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Normally a lender deposits 25%, but **if the securities are lent on the transaction date itself, no margin is required**.\n- A) is the normal (non-same-day) requirement.\n- B) is the borrower's requirement.\n- D) is not prescribed.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A close-ended scheme is wound up; after selling assets for Rs. 50,00,000 and paying Rs. 8,00,000 of liabilities and Rs. 2,00,000 winding-up expenses, the balance distributed to unit-holders is:\nA) Rs. 50,00,000  B) Rs. 42,00,000  C) Rs. 40,00,000  D) Rs. 48,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Proceeds first discharge liabilities and winding-up expenses; balance = Rs. 50,00,000 − Rs. 8,00,000 − Rs. 2,00,000 = **Rs. 40,00,000**, distributed in proportion to units (taxed like a normal redemption).\n- A) ignores deductions.\n- B) omits the expenses.\n- D) omits the liabilities.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Arjun, a resident individual (old tax regime, not a senior citizen), has the following transactions during FY 2024-25, all in listed securities with STT paid where relevant:\n\n1. He bought **10,000 shares of Sunrise Ltd. at Rs. 105** on 01-04-2020. Sunrise declared a **1:2 bonus** (one bonus share for every two held), allotted **01-09-2023**. On **01-08-2024** he sold **all 15,000 shares at Rs. 120 each**.\n2. He held **2,000 shares of Delta Ltd. bought at Rs. 100 each**; Delta **consolidated 2 shares into 1** (face value Rs. 100 → Rs. 200). He later sold all consolidated shares at **Rs. 260 each**.\n3. He owned **20,000 preference shares of Orbit Ltd. (cost Rs. 10 each)** acquired 01-01-2010, **converted 2:1 into 10,000 equity shares** on 01-01-2023 (FMV Rs. 25), and sold them at **Rs. 35** on 25-08-2024.\n4. Orbit Ltd. also **bought back 500 of his other shares at Rs. 125** (cost Rs. 100, held over 2 years) on 20-02-2025 (post-01-10-2024 rule).\n5. He **lent F&O-eligible shares** through SLB and earned a **lending fee of Rs. 2,00,000**, incurring **Rs. 2,000** transaction charges.\n\n---\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-1: Compute the capital gains from the Sunrise Ltd. shares (original + bonus) and their nature.</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n- **Original 10,000 shares:** bought 01-04-2020, sold 01-08-2024 → held >12 months → **long-term**. LTCG = (Rs. 120 − Rs. 105) × 10,000 = **Rs. 1,50,000** (Section 112A, 12.50% over Rs. 1,25,000).\n- **Bonus 5,000 shares:** cost **nil**, allotted 01-09-2023, sold 01-08-2024 → about 11 months ≤ 12 → **short-term**. STCG = (Rs. 120 × 5,000) − 0 = **Rs. 6,00,000** (Section 111A, 20%).\n\n**Intuition:** The bonus shares have their own holding clock (from allotment) and zero cost — a classic STCG trap. Total from Sunrise = Rs. 1,50,000 LTCG + Rs. 6,00,000 STCG.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-2: Compute the capital gain on the Delta Ltd. consolidated shares.</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n- Total cost = 2,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 2,00,000. After 2:1 consolidation he holds **1,000 shares**; cost per share = Rs. 2,00,000 ÷ 1,000 = **Rs. 200**.\n- Gain = (Rs. 260 − Rs. 200) × 1,000 = **Rs. 60,000**.\n- Consolidation is **not a transfer**, so no tax occurred at consolidation; the holding period runs from the original purchase date.\n\n**Formula used:** cost per consolidated share = total original cost ÷ number of shares after consolidation. The total money paid does not change — it is just spread over fewer shares.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-3: Compute the tax effect of the Orbit Ltd. preference-to-equity conversion and subsequent sale.</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n- **At conversion (01-01-2023):** under Section 47(xb), conversion of preference shares into equity of the same company is **not a transfer** → **no capital gain**. The FMV of Rs. 25 is ignored.\n- **At sale (25-08-2024):** holding period carries over from 01-01-2010 → over 14 years → **long-term**. Sale = 10,000 × Rs. 35 = Rs. 3,50,000; cost carries over = 20,000 × Rs. 10 = Rs. 2,00,000; **LTCG = Rs. 1,50,000** (Section 112A, 12.50% over Rs. 1,25,000).\n\n**Intuition:** Conversion is tax-deferred — both the cost and the clock carry over to the new equity shares.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-4: Explain the tax treatment of the Orbit Ltd. buyback (post-01-10-2024).</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n- **Dividend:** the full proceeds = 500 × Rs. 125 = **Rs. 62,500** are taxed as **dividend** under Income from Other Sources, with **no deduction** for cost or expenses.\n- **Capital loss:** the cost = 500 × Rs. 100 = **Rs. 50,000** becomes a **long-term capital loss** (held over 2 years), available for set-off/carry-forward against long-term capital gains.\n- **Company:** no tax (buyback after 01-10-2024).\n\n**Intuition:** Post-Oct-2024, the whole buyback amount is dividend (not just the gain), and the cost is split off as a capital loss — they are not netted against each other.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q-CS-5: Compute Mr. Arjun's taxable income from the SLB lending activity.</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n- Lending under SLB is **not a transfer** (Section 47(xv)), so no capital gain arises from the lending itself.\n- The **lending fee is taxable** under PGBP (if in that business) or otherwise Income from Other Sources, and related expenses are deductible.\n- Taxable income = Rs. 2,00,000 − Rs. 2,000 = **Rs. 1,98,000**.\n\n**Intuition:** SLB lets a long-term holder earn extra income on idle shares; only the net fee (after expenses) is taxed, and getting back equivalent (not identical) scrips does not trigger capital gains.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "14", "title": "Basics of Estate Planning", "module": "Estate Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 14: Basics of Estate Planning — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- What **estate planning** is and who actually needs it (hint: not just rich people!)\n- The building blocks (constituents) of an estate\n- What happens when you die **without** a plan (dying \"intestate\")\n- The key tools/elements: Will, Trust, Power of Attorney, Living Will, Nomination\n- The **laws** that decide who inherits — different for Hindus, Muslims, Christians, etc.\n- What **mutation** of property means\n\n---\n\n## 14.1 What is Estate Planning?\n\nImagine you spend your whole life collecting things you value — money, a house, jewellery, investments. Now ask: *what happens to all of it when I die? Or if I'm in a coma and can't make decisions?* **Estate planning is the process of deciding, in advance, how your assets will be accumulated, protected (conserved), and passed on (distributed).**\n\n**Analogy:** Think of your wealth like a school project you built over years. Estate planning is writing clear instructions so that after you leave, the right people get the right pieces — instead of everyone fighting over it.\n\n**Official definition:** Estate planning is *a process that deals with the accumulation, conservation and distribution of an estate.* Its overall purpose is to **enhance and maintain the financial security of individuals and their families.**\n\n⚠️ **Biggest myth (exam favourite):** \"Estate planning is only for the wealthy.\" **FALSE.** Anyone who owns property and wants it to go to specific people in a specific way needs estate planning.\n\n### Why do people need it? (Questions estate planning answers)\n- How do I want my property distributed, and who gets how much?\n- What if I become physically disabled or mentally incompetent — who manages my care?\n- Who repays my liabilities (loans)?\n- Is there enough money to sustain my spouse for life?\n- How do I provide for charity?\n\n### People who especially need effective estate planning\na. Family with **minor children** (kids under 18)\nb. Individuals with **high liabilities** (big loans)\nc. People owning **properties in multiple states**\nd. People owning a **small business**\ne. Those facing **estate taxes**\nf. Anyone wanting to address **permanent disability** uncertainty\n\n### Estate Planning Goals\n1. Preserving assets for loved ones\n2. Appointing a **guardian** for minors\n3. Handling disability (managing finances + health care if you're incapacitated)\n4. Ensuring funds to pay liabilities after death\n5. Giving loved ones specific assets\n6. Protection/care for **special needs children**\n7. Financial security for minor children **until age 18**\n8. An efficient method to collect and distribute assets\n\n💡 Even if your heirs are wonderful people, without a plan the law decides — and the law may not match your wishes.\n\n### Estate Planning vs Succession Planning (don't confuse them!)\n\n| Feature | Estate Planning | Succession Planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| About | Your **personal** assets | Your **business** |\n| When it takes effect | Mainly after death | **Within your lifetime** (passing business to next gen) |\n| Who needs it | **Everyone** with assets | Only business owners |\n| Timing | Start as you accumulate assets | May start late in life |\n\n⚠️ **Trap:** Estate planning and succession planning are NOT the same thing. Everyone needs an estate plan, whether or not they have a business.\n\n---\n\n## 14.2 Constituents of an Estate\n\nFor estate planning, your **estate = all property you own or control.** This can be held in your sole name, jointly with a family member (like a spouse), or even through a trust. It also includes money/assets generated *on your death* — like a **life insurance** payout.\n\nAn estate has **three categories**:\n\n| Category | What it includes | Easy way to remember |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Gross Estate** | Items that determine your **net worth** — real estate (house, land), bank accounts, investments, businesses, retirement accounts (NPS), life insurance | \"The big stuff that makes me wealthy\" |\n| **2. Residue Estate** | Your **personal** items — car, jewellery, furniture, clothes, anything found at home. Also: any investment **not mentioned in the will/trust**, and any outstanding payments owed to you at death | \"Leftovers / personal belongings\" |\n| **3. Estate Debt** | All **debts and obligations** — housing loan, car loan, credit card dues, business dues, tax liabilities, legal cases | \"What I owe others\" |\n\n💡 Notice: anything you forget to mention in your Will automatically falls into the **residue estate**.\n\n### 14.2.1 Consequences of Dying Intestate\n\n**Jargon: \"Intestate\"** = dying **without a valid Will** (or with a Will that can't legally take effect).\n\nThe Indian Succession Act, **Section 30**: a person is deemed to die intestate regarding property for which they made no testamentary disposition capable of taking effect. Intestacy can be **total** (no will at all) or **partial** (will covers only some assets).\n\n**What happens?** The property goes to heirs according to the **inheritance law applicable to that person's religion**:\n\n| Religion | Inheritance law on dying intestate |\n|---|---|\n| Hindus, Buddhists, Jains, Sikhs | **Hindu Succession Act, 1956** |\n| Christians, Parsis, Jews | **Indian Succession Act, 1925** |\n| Muslims | **No legislation** — based on religious texts; different for **Shias** and **Sunnis** |\n\n**Succession Certificate:** When someone dies intestate, legal heirs apply to the **civil court** for a *succession certificate*, issued per the succession laws applicable to the deceased.\n\n---\n\n## 14.3 Elements of Estate Planning\n\nAt a **bare minimum**, an estate plan should have two things: **(i) a last will and testament**, and **(ii) a living will.** Other tools are added based on your situation.\n\n### 1. Will\nThe document that specifies **who inherits your assets and how**. It ensures your wishes are followed and can appoint a **guardian** for minors or special-needs children, stating how their assets are managed until they turn adults (or for life, if special needs).\n\n### 2. Trust\n**Jargon: Trust** = a legal arrangement where assets are held **on behalf of a beneficiary** (the person who benefits). The creator decides the terms.\n- A trust created **through a Will** = **testamentary trust**. These follow the Will's rulings and therefore go through **probate** (court verification).\n\n### 3. Power of Attorney (PoA)\nA legal arrangement where you **designate someone to manage your finances** if you become incapacitated or unavailable. **NRIs** commonly use a PoA to appoint someone in India to manage their movable/immovable assets.\n\n### 4. Living Will\n**Jargon: Living Will** = an *advance directive* written mainly **for physicians**. It states your wishes for end-of-life situations (terminally ill, coma, late-stage dementia) when you **can't communicate** decisions yourself.\n\n⚠️ **Don't confuse:** A regular **Will** distributes property after death. A **Living Will** is about medical decisions while you're still alive but can't speak.\n\n### 5. Nomination & Beneficiary Designations\nStrictly speaking, *not* a core component, but important to **complete** the plan. A **nominee** ensures smooth transfer of financial assets after death.\n\n⚠️ **CRUCIAL DISTINCTION — Nominee vs Legal Heir:**\n- A **nominee** is usually just a **custodian** — they *hold* the asset until it's transferred to the rightful legal heir.\n- **Exception:** In some assets like **life insurance**, you can appoint **beneficial nominees** who are the actual legal recipients.\n- **Under the new Insurance Act:** if **Parents, Spouse, or Children** are the nominee, they automatically become **Beneficial Nominees** — they can *consume* (keep) the money too.\n\n💡 Review nominations periodically — life events like **marriage** can change who your beneficiaries should be.\n\n---\n\n## 14.4 Applicable Laws\n\n### 14.4.1 Indian Succession Act, 1925\nThis Act deals with **both** testamentary succession (when there IS a will) and intestate succession (no will).\n- **Testamentary succession** = applies when a Will is written.\n- **Intestate succession** = applies when there's no will; property distributed by religious laws.\n\n**Which law applies to whom:**\n\n| Religion | Testamentary (with Will) | Intestate (no Will) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hindus** | Indian Succession Act, 1925 | Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (amended 2005) |\n| **Jains, Sikhs, Buddhists** | Indian Succession Act, 1925 | Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (amended 2005) |\n| **Christians** | Indian Succession Act, 1925 | Indian Succession Act, 1925 |\n| **Muslims** | As per religion (not ISA 1925) | As per religion (not ISA 1925) |\n\n💡 The reason Hindus use a *different* law for intestate succession is the existence of separate **HUF (Hindu Undivided Family)** laws. **Jargon: HUF** = a family unit (under Hindu law) treated as a single entity that can own property.\n\n### 14.4.2 The Hindu Succession Act, 1956\n- Extends to **whole of India**.\n- A core rule: **succession never remains in abeyance** — the moment a Hindu dies, their property **immediately vests in their heirs**. (No gap, no waiting.)\n- **Sections 8 to 13** lay down general succession rules for a Hindu **male** dying intestate, read with the **Schedule** listing Class-I and Class-II heirs.\n\n#### Types of Heirs (4 types)\n1. **Class I Heirs** — first in line\n2. **Class II Heirs** — next, if no Class I\n3. **Agnates** — related **wholly through males** (by blood or adoption)\n4. **Cognates** — related by blood/adoption **but NOT wholly through males**\n\n**Analogy for agnate vs cognate:** Trace the family chain. If *every* link is a male (father→father's father), it's an **agnate**. If even one link is female (mother's brother), it's a **cognate**.\n\n#### Class I Heirs (memorize the important ones)\nSon; Daughter; **Widow**; **Mother**; Son/Daughter of a pre-deceased son; Son/Daughter of a pre-deceased daughter; Widow of a pre-deceased son; and deeper grandchildren/widows (16 total).\n\nKey notes on Class I:\n- **Adopted children** count as heirs (treated as full-blood).\n- Children from **void/voidable marriages** are entitled (Sec 16(3), Hindu Marriage Act).\n- Multiple **widows** together inherit **one share** jointly.\n- ⚠️ **Father is NOT a Class I heir** (he's Class II). Exam favourite!\n\n#### Class II Heirs (in order of entries I to IX)\nEntry I = **Father**; then sons'/daughters' grandchildren, brothers, sisters; ... down to mother's brother/sister. Earlier entries are preferred over later entries.\n\n⚠️ **Uterine brothers/sisters** (same mother, different father) are **excluded** from Class I and Class II.\n\n#### Blood relationship definitions\n\n| Term | Meaning | Memory hook |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Full blood** | Same father AND same mother | \"Both parents same\" |\n| **Half blood** | Same father, **different mother** | \"Different mum\" |\n| **Uterine blood** | Same mother, **different father** | \"Different dad (came from same womb)\" |\n\n- **Adoption:** adopted child = treated as **full blood**.\n- **Illegitimate** (born outside lawful marriage): relationship recognized with the **mother** (and through mother, to each other) but **NOT with the father** under Hindu Succession Act.\n\n#### The Sections — order of succession for a Hindu MALE\n\n- **Section 8 (priority order):** (1) Class I first → (2) if none, Class II → (3) if none, **agnates** → (4) lastly, **cognates**.\n- **Section 9:** Class I heirs all take **simultaneously and equally**, excluding others. Within Class II, earlier entries beat later entries.\n- **Section 10 (dividing among Class I):**\n  - Rule 1: All widows **together** take **one share**.\n  - Rule 2: Each surviving son, daughter, and the mother takes **one share** each.\n  - Rule 3: The branch of each pre-deceased son/daughter takes **one share** between them.\n  - Rule 4: Within a branch, shares split so widow + surviving sons & daughters get equal proportions.\n- **Section 11:** Among Class II heirs in the **same entry**, they share **equally**.\n- **Section 12 (agnates/cognates order):**\n  - Rule 1: Fewer/no **degrees of ascent** is preferred.\n  - Rule 2: If ascent is equal/none, fewer/no **degrees of descent** is preferred.\n  - Rule 3: If neither is preferred, they take **simultaneously**.\n- **Section 13 (computing degrees):** Relationship measured in **degrees of ascent/descent**, counted **inclusive of the intestate**; every generation = one degree.\n\n#### Succession of a Hindu FEMALE's property\n- The **Rau Committee (1948)** recommended that a woman's property should be her **absolute property**. The **Hindu Succession Act, 1956 — Section 14** made this real.\n- **Section 14:** Any property possessed by a Hindu female is held by her as **full owner**, not a limited owner.\n- **Section 15 (order of succession for a Hindu female dying intestate):**\n  a) First: sons & daughters (incl. children of pre-deceased son/daughter) **and the husband**\n  b) Secondly: heirs of the **husband**\n  c) Thirdly: **mother and father**\n  d) Fourthly: heirs of the **father**\n  e) Lastly: heirs of the **mother**\n\n### 14.4.3 Muslim Personal Law\n\n**Four sources of Muslim succession law:**\n1. **The Holy Quran**\n2. **The Sunna** — practice of the Prophet\n3. **The Ijma** — consensus of learned community members on a matter\n4. **The Qiya** — deductions by analogy on what is right and just\n\n**Two types of heirs:**\n- **Sharers** — entitled to a **certain fixed share** of the deceased's property.\n- **Residuary** — take whatever is **left over** after sharers take their part.\n\n**12 Sharers:** Husband; Wife; Daughter; Son's daughter; Father; Paternal Grandfather; Mother; Grandmother (male line); Full sister; Consanguine sister; Uterine sister; Uterine brother.\n\n**Example shares (factors decide the amount):**\n\n| Who | Share |\n|---|---|\n| Wife (no lineal descendants) | **1/4** |\n| Wife (with lineal descendants) | **1/8** |\n| Husband (no lineal descendants) | **1/2** |\n| Husband (with lineal descendants) | **1/4** |\n| Sole daughter | **1/2** |\n| More than one daughter (jointly) | **2/3** |\n\n⚠️ If the deceased left **both sons and daughters**, daughters stop being sharers and become **residuary** — and distribution ensures **each son gets double of each daughter**.\n\n**Testamentary vs Non-testamentary (Muslim):**\n- **Non-testamentary** (no will): **Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act, 1937** applies.\n- **Testamentary** (with will): governed by Sharia law as applicable to **Shias/Sunnis**.\n- **Exception:** For **immovable property** in **West Bengal**, or within jurisdiction of **Madras or Bombay High Court**, Muslims are bound by the **Indian Succession Act, 1925** — but **only for testamentary succession**.\n\n**Other key Muslim law points:**\n- **No birthright** in ancestral property — heir's rights are fixed at the **date of death** and don't fluctuate. An apparent heir who dies **before** the ancestor gets nothing.\n- **Rule of distribution:**\n  - **Per capita** (mainly **Sunni**): estate divided **equally among all heirs** — share depends on *number* of heirs.\n  - **Per strip / per stirpes** (recognized in **Shia**): distributed by the **branch (strip)** you belong to — share depends on the branch and number of persons in it.\n- **No distinction** between self-acquired and ancestral property (unlike Hindu law) — everything an individual owns can be inherited.\n- **Female rights:** boys and girls both inherit, but a **female heir's share is half of a male heir's**.\n- **Widow's rights:** childless widow gets **1/4**; widow with children/grandchildren gets **1/8** (after funeral, legal expenses, debts). Special rules if husband married during a fatal illness.\n- **Child in the womb:** entitled to a share **only if born alive**. If born dead, the share is treated as if it never existed.\n- **Escheat:** if a Muslim has **no legal heir**, the **Government** inherits via the process of **escheat**.\n- **Wasiyat (Muslim Will):** any person **aged 15+** of sound mind can make one. Can dispose of only up to **1/3 of net assets** by will; the other **2/3 must go to heirs**. No formalities required — can be **written, oral, or by gestures**. Can be **revoked** anytime (expressly by tearing/burning, or impliedly by selling/gifting the same property).\n\n### 14.4.4 Married Women's Property Act, 1874\nEnacted to **protect a woman's property against creditors**. Her property is **insulated** from court/income-tax attachments against the **husband**.\n- Applies to women who (at marriage) professed Hindu, Muhammadan, Buddhist, Sikh, or Jain religion (or whose husband did).\n- A married woman's **wages, earnings, literary/artistic/scientific income, and savings/investments** from these = her **separate property**.\n- A married woman can take a **life insurance policy** on her own behalf, independent of her husband — it becomes her separate property.\n- **Insurance by husband for wife/children (key MWP Act use):** A policy a man takes on his own life, expressed to be **for the benefit of his wife and/or children**, becomes a **trust** for them. It is **NOT** subject to the husband's control, his **creditors**, or part of his estate. 💡 This is the famous \"MWP Act policy\" — it protects the family payout from the husband's creditors.\n- Wife is liable for her own **post-nuptial debts** out of her separate property; husband is **not** liable for her **ante-nuptial** (before-marriage) debts merely due to marriage.\n\n---\n\n## 14.5 Mutation\n\n**Jargon: Mutation** = the process of **transferring a property from one person's name to another** in the **revenue (land) records** — substituting one owner's name with another's.\n\n- It's a **state matter** (each state has its own rules).\n- **Purpose:** so the government knows the rightful owner — mainly to **fix tax liabilities** and collect property taxes.\n- Process handled by **state governments / land development authority**; the present owner must clear all dues, then the authority records mutation and grants a **no-objection certificate**.\n\n⚠️ **VERY IMPORTANT (exam trap):** Mutation is **NOT proof of ownership/title!** Revenue proceedings are **not judicial proceedings**. The **title** (e.g., sale deed, will) is what proves ownership — not a mutation entry. Mutation's **sole objective is to collect revenue** from whoever possesses the property.\n\n💡 If there are co-owners, mutation prevents one person from claiming the whole property alone — but it still doesn't *confer* title.\n\n---\n\n## 14.6 Caselet (worked example)\n**Mr. XYZ, a Hindu, dies without a Will, unmarried, leaving 1 brother and 2 sisters. Who inherits?**\n\n**Answer: All — brother and sisters equally.**\n\n**Logic:** Dying without a Will = **intestate** → Indian Succession Act 1925 → since Hindu → **Hindu Succession Act 1956**. There are **no Class I heirs** (no spouse, no children, no mother). So it goes to **Class II**. Brother and sister are in the same Class II entry, so they share **equally**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Estate planning** = accumulate + conserve + distribute your estate. **For everyone, not just the rich.**\n- **Succession planning** = about the **business**, during your lifetime. Different from estate planning.\n- **Estate = Gross Estate + Residue Estate + Estate Debt.**\n- Die **without a Will = intestate** → religious inheritance law decides → legal heirs need a **succession certificate** (civil court).\n- Core elements: **Will, Trust, Power of Attorney, Living Will**, plus **Nomination**.\n- **Nominee = custodian** (usually); **legal heir = real owner**. Exception: beneficial nominees (insurance), and new Insurance Act makes Parents/Spouse/Children beneficial nominees.\n- **Laws:** Hindus→Hindu Succession Act 1956 (intestate), ISA 1925 (testamentary); Christians→ISA 1925 both; Muslims→religious law.\n- Hindu male: Class I → Class II → Agnates → Cognates. **Father is NOT Class I.**\n- Muslim: Sharers vs Residuary; female gets **half** a male's share; will limited to **1/3** of assets; **no birthright**.\n- **MWP Act 1874:** protects a married woman's property from husband's creditors; husband's policy for wife/kids = a protected trust.\n- **Mutation:** updates revenue records for **tax** purposes — **NOT proof of title.**\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Intestate** | Dying without a valid Will |\n| **Testator** | Person who makes a Will |\n| **Section 30, ISA 1925** | Defines dying intestate |\n| **Hindu Succession Act, 1956** | Intestate succession for Hindus/Buddhists/Jains/Sikhs |\n| **Indian Succession Act, 1925** | Testamentary (all listed) + intestate for Christians/Parsis/Jews |\n| **Sections 8–13 HSA** | Succession rules for Hindu **male** |\n| **Section 14 HSA** | Hindu female = **full owner** of her property |\n| **Section 15 HSA** | Succession order for Hindu female |\n| **Agnate** | Related wholly through males |\n| **Cognate** | Related NOT wholly through males |\n| **Full / Half / Uterine blood** | Both parents same / same father diff mother / same mother diff father |\n| **12 Sharers** (Muslim) | Fixed-share heirs |\n| **Female share (Muslim)** | **½** of male heir |\n| **Muslim Will (Wasiyat)** | Max **1/3** of assets; maker aged **15+** |\n| **Childless Muslim widow** | **1/4**; with children **1/8** |\n| **Wife share (Muslim)** | **1/4** (no descendants) / **1/8** (with) |\n| **Escheat** | Govt inherits when no heir (Muslim) |\n| **MWP Act, 1874** | Protects married woman's property from creditors |\n| **Mutation** | Revenue record update — **not title proof** |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 14: Basics of Estate Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is estate planning?\n> **A:** The process of **accumulating, conserving, and distributing** your estate so your family's financial security is maintained after your death or incapacity. It decides who gets your property and how.\n\n**Q2.** Is estate planning only for the wealthy?\n> **A:** No — that's a misconception. It's for **anyone** who owns property and wants it to pass to specific people in a specific way. Even people with minor children need it (to appoint a guardian).\n\n**Q3.** How is estate planning different from succession planning?\n> **A:** Estate planning is about your **personal assets** and works mainly **after death/incapacity**; succession planning is about handing your **business** to the next generation **within your lifetime**.\n\n**Q4.** What are the three categories of an estate?\n> **A:** (1) **Gross Estate** (net-worth items: real estate, bank, investments, insurance), (2) **Residue Estate** (personal items + anything not in the Will + payments owed to you), (3) **Estate Debt** (loans, dues, taxes, legal liabilities).\n\n**Q5.** What does \"dying intestate\" mean?\n> **A:** Dying **without a valid Will** (or with a Will that can't legally take effect). Can be **total** or **partial**. (Indian Succession Act, Section 30.)\n\n**Q6.** When someone dies intestate, what decides who inherits?\n> **A:** The **law of inheritance applicable to their religion** — Hindu Succession Act 1956 for Hindus/Buddhists/Jains/Sikhs; Indian Succession Act 1925 for Christians/Parsis/Jews; religious texts for Muslims (differ for Shia/Sunni).\n\n**Q7.** What is a succession certificate and who issues it?\n> **A:** A document the legal heirs obtain from a **civil court** when a person dies intestate, authorising them to represent the deceased and collect debts/securities.\n\n**Q8.** Name the five elements of an estate plan.\n> **A:** Will, Trust, Power of Attorney, Living Will, and Nomination/Beneficiary designations. (Bare minimum = a Will + a Living Will.)\n\n**Q9.** What is a testamentary trust?\n> **A:** A trust created **through a Will**. Because it's part of the Will, it must go through **probate** (court validation).\n\n**Q10.** What does a Power of Attorney (PoA) do?\n> **A:** Lets you appoint someone to **manage your finances** if you become incapacitated. Commonly used by NRIs to have someone in India manage their assets.\n\n**Q11.** What is a Living Will?\n> **A:** An **advance directive for doctors** stating your wishes for end-of-life situations (coma, terminal illness, dementia) when you can't communicate. It's about **medical** decisions, not money.\n\n**Q12.** Nominee vs Legal Heir — what's the difference?\n> **A:** A **nominee** usually just **holds/receives** the asset as a custodian until it reaches the **legal heir**, who is the real owner under succession law.\n\n**Q13.** When can a nominee actually keep the money?\n> **A:** Under the **Insurance Act**, if a **parent, spouse, or child** is the nominee in a life policy, they become a **Beneficial Nominee** and can keep and use the proceeds — other legal heirs can't claim it.\n\n**Q14.** Which law applies to a Hindu's *testamentary* succession vs *intestate* succession?\n> **A:** Testamentary → **Indian Succession Act, 1925**; Intestate → **Hindu Succession Act, 1956** (amended 2005). The difference exists because of separate HUF laws.\n\n**Q15.** Which law governs Christians for both testamentary and intestate succession?\n> **A:** The **Indian Succession Act, 1925** — for both.\n\n**Q16.** List the four types of heirs for a Hindu male, in priority order.\n> **A:** **Class I → Class II → Agnates → Cognates.**\n\n**Q17.** Is the father a Class I heir under the Hindu Succession Act?\n> **A:** **No.** Father is a **Class II** heir (Entry I). Class I includes son, daughter, widow, and mother.\n\n**Q18.** Define agnate and cognate.\n> **A:** **Agnate** = related by blood/adoption **wholly through males**. **Cognate** = related by blood/adoption but **NOT wholly through males**.\n\n**Q19.** Full blood vs half blood vs uterine blood?\n> **A:** **Full** = same father + same mother. **Half** = same father, different mother. **Uterine** = same mother, different father (uterine siblings are **excluded** from Class I & II).\n\n**Q20.** Under Section 10, how are shares split when a Hindu male leaves a widow, 2 sons, and his mother?\n> **A:** Widow (1) + son (1) + son (1) + mother (1) = **4 equal shares**, so each gets **1/4**.\n\n**Q21.** What does Section 14 of the Hindu Succession Act say about a Hindu female's property?\n> **A:** Any property she possesses is her **absolute property** — she is a **full owner**, not a limited owner.\n\n**Q22.** What are the four sources of Muslim succession law?\n> **A:** The **Quran**, the **Sunna** (Prophet's practice), the **Ijma** (consensus of learned men), and the **Qiya** (deductions by analogy).\n\n**Q23.** Sharers vs Residuary in Muslim law?\n> **A:** **Sharers** get a **fixed share**; **Residuary** take whatever is **left over** after the sharers.\n\n**Q24.** What share does a sole daughter take under Muslim law? And multiple daughters?\n> **A:** A sole daughter = **1/2**; more than one daughter together = **2/3**. But if sons are also present, daughters become **residuary** and each son gets **double** of each daughter.\n\n**Q25.** How much of his property can a Muslim give away by Will (Wasiyat)?\n> **A:** Only up to **one-third (1/3)** of net assets; the other **2/3** must go to the heirs. (Maker must be **15+** and of sound mind; can be written, oral, or by gesture.)\n\n**Q26.** What share does a childless Muslim widow get vs one with children?\n> **A:** Childless = **1/4**; with children/grandchildren = **1/8** of the husband's property.\n\n**Q27.** Per capita vs per strip distribution in Muslim law?\n> **A:** **Per capita** (Sunni) = estate split equally among the **number** of heirs. **Per strip/stirpes** (Shia) = split by **branch**.\n\n**Q28.** Does Muslim law recognise birthright in ancestral property?\n> **A:** **No.** Rights are **fixed on the date of the ancestor's death**, and only if the heir survives the ancestor.\n\n**Q29.** What is \"escheat\" under Muslim law?\n> **A:** When a deceased Muslim has **no legal heir**, the **Government** inherits the property.\n\n**Q30.** What does the Married Women's Property Act, 1874 protect?\n> **A:** It insulates a married woman's property (wages, earnings, savings, insurance) from her **husband's creditors** and from court/tax attachments against the husband.\n\n**Q31.** What's special about an MWP Act insurance policy taken by a husband?\n> **A:** A policy on his own life expressed for the benefit of his wife/children becomes a **trust** for them — not controlled by him, not reachable by his creditors, not part of his estate.\n\n**Q32.** What is mutation?\n> **A:** The process of changing the **owner's name in government revenue records** when property transfers. Its main purpose is **property-tax collection**.\n\n**Q33.** Is mutation proof of ownership/title?\n> **A:** **No.** Revenue proceedings aren't judicial; **title** (e.g. sale deed/Will) proves ownership, not the mutation entry. But it stops one co-owner from claiming the whole alone.\n\n**Q34.** Mr. XYZ (Hindu) dies intestate, unmarried, with 1 brother and 2 sisters. Who inherits?\n> **A:** No Class I heirs exist, so we go to **Class II** — the brother and two sisters inherit **equally**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 14: Basics of Estate Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Questions are arranged in three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Easy (recall), 🟡 Medium (application), 🔴 Hard (scenario reasoning). Remember the NISM exam has **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter mark — when unsure, eliminate distractors before guessing.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Estate planning is best defined as a process that deals with which three activities relating to an estate?\nA) Buying, selling and leasing  B) Accumulation, conservation and distribution  C) Auditing, taxing and registering  D) Insuring, mortgaging and gifting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook defines estate planning as \"a process that deals with the accumulation, conservation and distribution of an estate.\" Its purpose is to enhance and maintain the financial security of individuals and their families.\n- A) Buying/selling/leasing are ordinary property transactions, not the definition.\n- C) Auditing/taxing/registering are administrative acts, not the core process.\n- D) Insuring/mortgaging/gifting are individual tools, not the overall definition.\nThink of it as: gather your wealth (accumulate), protect it (conserve), then pass it on (distribute).\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A person who makes a Will is called the:\nA) Executor  B) Testator  C) Nominee  D) Beneficiary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The person who makes a Will is the testator.\n- A) An executor is the person appointed to carry out the Will's instructions.\n- C) A nominee merely holds an asset as custodian until it reaches the legal heir.\n- D) A beneficiary is someone who benefits from the estate/trust.\nMemory hook: \"Testator = person who tests-their-wishes in writing.\"\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Dying \"intestate\" means dying:\nA) With a registered Will  B) Without a valid Will capable of taking effect  C) Without any property  D) After appointing an executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 30 of the Indian Succession Act, a person dies intestate regarding property for which they have made no testamentary disposition capable of taking effect.\n- A) A registered Will is the opposite — that is testate.\n- C) Having no property is irrelevant; intestacy is about absence of a valid Will.\n- D) Appointing an executor happens inside a Will, so that is testate.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The biggest misconception about estate planning is that it is:\nA) Only for the wealthy  B) Illegal for minors  C) Compulsory by law  D) Only about taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook repeatedly stresses that the common myth \"estate planning is only for the rich\" is false; anyone owning property who wants it transferred to specific heirs in a specific manner needs it.\n- B) Estate planning is not illegal for anyone.\n- C) It is not legally compulsory.\n- D) It is far broader than taxes (guardianship, disability, liabilities, etc.).\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which Act, under Section 30, defines when a person is deemed to die intestate?\nA) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  B) Indian Succession Act, 1925  C) Married Women's Property Act, 1874  D) Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Act, 1937\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 30 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925 defines dying intestate.\n- A) The Hindu Succession Act governs intestate succession for Hindus but does not contain the Section 30 definition.\n- C) The MWP Act protects a woman's property from creditors.\n- D) The Shariat Act applies to Muslim non-testamentary succession.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** When a person dies intestate, the legal heirs must apply to the civil court for a:\nA) Probate  B) Succession certificate  C) Power of attorney  D) Living will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When a person dies without a Will, legal heirs apply to the civil court for a succession certificate, issued per the succession laws applicable to the deceased.\n- A) Probate is the court's certification of a Will — relevant when there IS a Will.\n- C) A power of attorney is created during one's lifetime to manage finances.\n- D) A living will is an advance medical directive.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The intestate succession law for Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and Sikhs is the:\nA) Indian Succession Act, 1925  B) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  C) Shariat Act, 1937  D) Married Women's Property Act, 1874\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and Sikhs, intestate succession is governed by the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (as amended in 2005).\n- A) The Indian Succession Act, 1925 governs their *testamentary* succession (and Christians' intestate succession).\n- C) The Shariat Act applies to Muslims.\n- D) The MWP Act protects women's property from creditors.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** For Christians, which Act applies to BOTH testamentary and intestate succession?\nA) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  B) Indian Succession Act, 1925  C) Shariat Act, 1937  D) Special Marriage Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For Christians, the Indian Succession Act, 1925 applies to both testamentary and intestate succession.\n- A) The Hindu Succession Act applies to Hindus' intestate succession only.\n- C) The Shariat Act applies to Muslims.\n- D) The Special Marriage Act is not the succession law referenced here.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** For Muslims, the inheritance law on dying intestate is:\nA) Found in the Hindu Succession Act  B) Found in the Indian Succession Act, 1925  C) Based on religious texts, differing for Shias and Sunnis  D) Decided by the civil court alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For Muslims the law of inheritance is not provided in any legislation; it is based on religious texts and differs for Shias and Sunnis.\n- A) and B) Those statutes do not govern Muslim intestate inheritance.\n- D) The court does not invent the rules; it applies the religious law.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A trust created through a Will is called a:\nA) Living trust  B) Revocable trust  C) Testamentary trust  D) Charitable trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A trust created through a Will is a testamentary trust; it is subject to the rulings of the Will and so goes through probate.\n- A) A living trust is created during one's lifetime, not via a Will.\n- B) \"Revocable\" describes whether it can be changed, not how it is created.\n- D) A charitable trust is defined by its purpose, not by being created via a Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A legal arrangement designating someone to manage one's finances if one becomes incapacitated is a:\nA) Living will  B) Power of attorney  C) Codicil  D) Nomination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A power of attorney lets an individual designate someone to manage finances if incapacitated or unavailable; NRIs commonly use it to appoint a manager in India.\n- A) A living will addresses medical/end-of-life decisions.\n- C) A codicil is an amendment to a Will.\n- D) A nomination appoints a custodian for a specific financial asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A \"living will\" is an advance directive written primarily for:\nA) The civil court  B) Physicians  C) Tax authorities  D) The executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A living will is an advance directive written primarily for physicians, stating one's wishes for end-of-life situations (terminally ill, coma, late-stage dementia) when one cannot communicate.\n- A) It is not a court document.\n- C) It has nothing to do with tax.\n- D) The executor administers the regular Will, not the living will.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The rights of a nominee are generally limited to:\nA) Permanent ownership of the asset  B) Holding the asset as custodian until transferred to the legal heir  C) Selling the asset immediately  D) Deciding who the legal heirs are\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A nominee's rights are limited to holding the asset as a custodian until it is transferred to the rightful legal heirs.\n- A) A nominee is usually not the permanent owner.\n- C) A nominee cannot freely sell as owner.\n- D) A nominee does not decide who the legal heirs are; the law/Will does.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Under the new Insurance Act, which nominees automatically become \"beneficial nominees\" who can keep the money?\nA) Any friend  B) Parents, spouse and children  C) Only the spouse  D) Brothers and sisters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the new Insurance Act, if Parents, Spouse or Children are the nominee in the policy, they automatically become beneficial nominees and can consume (keep) the money too.\n- A) A friend does not get this automatic beneficial status.\n- C) Not only the spouse — parents and children are included.\n- D) Siblings are not listed for this automatic benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which of the following is part of the \"Gross Estate\"?\nA) Clothes and furniture at home  B) Housing loan outstanding  C) Real estate, bank accounts, investments and life insurance  D) Credit card dues\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The gross estate comprises items determining one's net worth: real estate, bank accounts, investments, businesses, retirement accounts (like NPS) and life insurance.\n- A) Clothes and furniture are residue estate.\n- B) A housing loan is estate debt.\n- D) Credit card dues are estate debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Personal items like a car, jewellery, furniture and clothes, plus anything not mentioned in the Will, form part of the:\nA) Gross estate  B) Residue estate  C) Estate debt  D) Trust corpus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The residue estate is the personal estate: car, jewellery, furniture, clothes, and any investment not mentioned in the Will or allocated to a trust, plus outstanding payments owed at death.\n- A) Gross estate is the net-worth items.\n- C) Estate debt is what one owes others.\n- D) Trust corpus is property held in a trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Housing loans, car loans, credit card dues and tax liabilities form part of the:\nA) Gross estate  B) Residue estate  C) Estate debt  D) Nomination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Estate debt includes all debts and obligations owed to others: housing loan, car loan, credit card payments, business dues, tax liabilities and legal cases.\n- A) and B) Those are asset categories, not debts.\n- D) A nomination is not an estate category.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The two bare-minimum components of an estate plan are a last will and testament and a:\nA) Trust  B) Power of attorney  C) Living will  D) Nomination form\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At a bare minimum an estate plan should have (i) a last will and testament and (ii) a living will.\n- A), B), D) Trust, power of attorney and nomination are added based on situation; they are not the stated bare minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, the heirs of a Hindu male are broadly of how many types?\nA) Two  B) Three  C) Four  D) Five\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The heirs of a Hindu male are broadly four types: Class I heirs, Class II heirs, Agnates and Cognates.\n- A), B), D) These miscount the categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which of the following is a Class I heir of a Hindu male?\nA) Father  B) Brother  C) Widow  D) Father's father\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The widow is a Class I heir. Class I includes son, daughter, widow, mother and various descendants of pre-deceased children.\n- A) Father is a Class II heir (Entry I), NOT Class I — a classic exam trap.\n- B) Brother is a Class II heir.\n- D) Father's father is a Class II heir.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, the father of a deceased Hindu male is:\nA) A Class I heir  B) A Class II heir  C) An agnate only  D) Excluded entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The father is a Class II heir (Entry I of Class II), not a Class I heir. This is a frequently tested point.\n- A) The mother is Class I, but the father is not.\n- C) He is specifically named in Class II, not merely an agnate.\n- D) He is certainly not excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A person related to another wholly through males (by blood or adoption) is called a/an:\nA) Cognate  B) Agnate  C) Sharer  D) Residuary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A person is an agnate if the two are related by blood or adoption wholly through males.\n- A) A cognate is related but NOT wholly through males.\n- C) and D) Sharer and residuary are Muslim-law heir categories.\nMemory hook: \"Agnate = All-males in the chain.\"\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A person related to another by blood or adoption but NOT wholly through males is a/an:\nA) Agnate  B) Cognate  C) Full-blood relative  D) Uterine relative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A cognate is related by blood or adoption but not wholly through males (e.g., through a mother somewhere in the chain).\n- A) An agnate is wholly through males.\n- C) Full-blood means same father and mother.\n- D) Uterine means same mother, different father.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Two persons descended from a common ancestor by the same wife (same father AND same mother) are related by:\nA) Half blood  B) Uterine blood  C) Full blood  D) Adoption\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Full-blood relationship means both parents are the same.\n- A) Half blood = same father, different mother.\n- B) Uterine blood = same mother, different father.\n- D) Adoption is a separate relationship category (treated as full blood under the Act).\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Two persons with the same father but a different mother are related by:\nA) Full blood  B) Half blood  C) Uterine blood  D) Cognate blood\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Half-blood relationship means descended from a common ancestor but by different wives — same father, different mother.\n- A) Full blood = both parents same.\n- C) Uterine blood = same mother, different father.\n- D) \"Cognate blood\" is not a blood-relationship category.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Two persons with the same mother but a different father are related by:\nA) Full blood  B) Half blood  C) Uterine blood  D) Agnate blood\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Uterine-blood relationship means descended from a common ancestress but by different husbands — same mother, different father. Uterine brothers/sisters are excluded from Class I and Class II heirs.\n- A) Full blood = both parents same.\n- B) Half blood = same father, different mother.\n- D) \"Agnate blood\" is not a category here.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, an adopted child is treated as equivalent to a relation of:\nA) Half blood  B) Uterine blood  C) Full blood  D) Cognate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An adopted child is, for all intents and purposes, like a natural-born child and is treated as equivalent to a relation of full blood.\n- A), B), D) These are lesser/different relationships not applied to adopted children.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, an illegitimate child's relationship is recognised with:\nA) The father only  B) The mother (and through her, to each other)  C) Both parents equally  D) Neither parent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the Hindu Succession Act, illegitimate relationship with the father is NOT recognised, but it is recognised with the mother, and through their mother the children are related to each other.\n- A) Relationship with the father is specifically not recognised.\n- C) Not both equally.\n- D) The relationship with the mother IS recognised.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which committee, reporting in 1948, recommended that a woman's property be her absolute property?\nA) Law Commission  B) Rau Committee  C) Sachar Committee  D) Mandal Committee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Rau Committee on Hindu Law (1948) recommended that all properties held by a woman be her absolute properties. These recommendations were substantially incorporated, culminating in Section 14 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956.\n- A), C), D) These committees are not the one referenced in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Section 14 of the Hindu Succession Act provides that property possessed by a Hindu female is held by her as:\nA) A limited owner  B) A full owner  C) A trustee  D) A nominee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 14 says any property possessed by a Hindu female shall be held by her as full owner, not as a limited owner.\n- A) \"Limited owner\" is exactly what Section 14 abolished.\n- C), D) Trustee and nominee are different roles, not what Section 14 confers.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In Muslim law, a Will is referred to as:\nA) Wasiyat  B) Ijma  C) Qiya  D) Sunna\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In Muslim law, a Will is called Wasiyat. Any person aged 15+ of sound mind can make one.\n- B) Ijma is consensus of learned community members (a source of law).\n- C) Qiya is deduction by analogy (a source of law).\n- D) Sunna is the practice of the Prophet (a source of law).\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Under Muslim law, the maximum portion of net assets a person can dispose of by Will is:\nA) One-half  B) One-third  C) Two-thirds  D) The entire estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Muslim law allows disposing of no more than one-third of net assets by Will; the remaining two-thirds must be available for distribution among the heirs.\n- A), C), D) These exceed the one-third limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Under Muslim law, when a deceased Muslim has no legal heir, the property goes to the Government through the process of:\nA) Probate  B) Escheat  C) Mutation  D) Wasiyat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When a deceased Muslim has no legal heir, the Government inherits the property through the process of escheat.\n- A) Probate certifies a Will.\n- C) Mutation updates revenue records.\n- D) Wasiyat is a Muslim Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The Married Women's Property Act, 1874 was primarily enacted to protect a woman's property against:\nA) Her own relatives  B) Creditors (and court/tax attachments against the husband)  C) The state government  D) Her children\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The MWP Act, 1874 protects a woman's property by insulating it from creditors and from court or income-tax attachments run up by the husband.\n- A), C), D) These are not the protective purpose stated in the Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Mutation is the process of:\nA) Certifying a Will in court  B) Transferring property from one person's name to another in revenue records  C) Creating a trust  D) Appointing a nominee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutation transfers a property from one person's name to another by altering/substituting the name in revenue records showing right of title.\n- A) That is probate.\n- C) That is forming a trust.\n- D) That is nomination.\nNote: mutation is NOT proof of title — its sole objective is to collect revenue.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Raj owns a personal house and runs a manufacturing business he wants his son to continue running while Raj is still alive. Planning the handover of the BUSINESS during his lifetime is an example of:\nA) Estate planning  B) Succession planning  C) Probate  D) Mutation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Succession planning is planning for one's business, which takes effect within one's lifetime, to ensure continuity into the next generation.\n- A) Estate planning concerns personal assets and mainly takes effect after death.\n- C) Probate is court certification of a Will.\n- D) Mutation is updating revenue records.\nThe key cue is \"BUSINESS\" + \"during his lifetime.\"\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Which statement correctly distinguishes estate planning from succession planning?\nA) Both are only for business owners  B) Estate planning is for personal assets and is needed by everyone; succession planning is about the business  C) Succession planning takes effect only after death  D) They are identical concepts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Estate planning involves one's personal assets and is needed by every individual; succession planning concerns the business and takes effect within one's lifetime.\n- A) Estate planning is for everyone, not just business owners.\n- C) Succession planning takes effect within one's lifetime, not only after death.\n- D) The workbook stresses they are completely distinct.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Priya forgot to mention her gold bangles and an old mutual fund folio in her Will. On her death these unmentioned assets fall into the:\nA) Gross estate as named bequests  B) Residue estate  C) Estate debt  D) Government via escheat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Any investment not mentioned in the Will (or allocated to a trust), plus personal items, form part of the residue estate.\n- A) They are not named bequests precisely because they were not mentioned.\n- C) Estate debt is what one owes others, not unmentioned assets.\n- D) Escheat (govt inheriting) is a Muslim-law concept for when there is no heir; not relevant here.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An NRI living in Dubai wants someone in India to manage and, if needed, sell his Indian flat while he is abroad. The most appropriate tool is a:\nA) Living will  B) Power of attorney  C) Nomination  D) Codicil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook specifically notes that many NRIs use a Power of Attorney to designate someone in India to manage their movable or immovable assets.\n- A) A living will is for end-of-life medical decisions.\n- C) A nomination only makes someone a custodian of a financial asset on death.\n- D) A codicil amends a Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. Sharma is terminally ill and wants to record, in advance, his wishes about medical treatment if he later falls into a coma and cannot communicate. He needs a:\nA) Power of attorney  B) Living will  C) Last will and testament  D) Succession certificate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A living will is an advance directive for physicians covering exactly such situations — coma, terminal illness, end-of-life — when one cannot communicate decisions.\n- A) A PoA manages finances, not medical wishes.\n- C) A last will distributes property after death.\n- D) A succession certificate is obtained by heirs after an intestate death.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A childless Hindu man dies intestate leaving a widow and his mother (father deceased). Who inherits and in what proportion?\nA) Only the widow  B) Only the mother  C) Widow and mother equally (half each)  D) Mother two-thirds, widow one-third\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Both widow and mother are Class I heirs. Under Section 10, the widow takes one share (Rule 1) and the mother takes one share (Rule 2). With two equal shares, each gets 1/2.\n- A), B) Both are Class I heirs; neither is excluded.\n- D) There is no rule giving the mother a larger share; Class I shares here are equal.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A Hindu male dies intestate with a Will covering only his bank account but no mention of his land. The land passes:\nA) By testamentary succession  B) By intestate succession (partial intestacy)  C) To the Government  D) To the nominee permanently\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intestacy may be total or partial. The bank account (covered by Will) goes by testamentary succession, but the land — not disposed by Will — passes by intestate succession.\n- A) Only the bank account follows the Will.\n- C) The Government does not inherit while Hindu heirs exist.\n- D) A nominee is only a custodian, not a permanent owner.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A Christian gentleman writes a valid Will distributing his assets. Which law governs this testamentary succession?\nA) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  B) Indian Succession Act, 1925  C) Shariat Act, 1937  D) MWP Act, 1874\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For Christians, the Indian Succession Act, 1925 governs both testamentary and intestate succession.\n- A) The Hindu Succession Act does not apply to Christians.\n- C) The Shariat Act applies to Muslims.\n- D) The MWP Act protects married women's property; it is not the succession law.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A Sikh man dies WITHOUT a Will. Which Act governs the distribution of his estate?\nA) Indian Succession Act, 1925  B) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  C) Shariat Act, 1937  D) Special Marriage Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For Jains, Sikhs and Buddhists, intestate succession follows the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (with 2005 amendments), just like Hindus.\n- A) The Indian Succession Act, 1925 governs their *testamentary* succession.\n- C) The Shariat Act applies to Muslims.\n- D) Not the relevant succession law.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Why do Hindus use the Indian Succession Act, 1925 for testamentary succession but the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 for intestate succession?\nA) Because of inflation rules  B) Because of the existence of separate HUF (Hindu Undivided Family) laws  C) Because Hindus cannot write Wills  D) Because of escheat provisions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook attributes this difference to the presence of separate laws related to the Hindu Undivided Family (HUF).\n- A), D) Inflation and escheat are unrelated.\n- C) Hindus certainly can write Wills (that is testamentary succession).\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A Hindu male dies intestate leaving a son, a daughter and his father. Who inherits?\nA) Father only  B) Son and daughter only, equally  C) Son, daughter and father equally  D) Son only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Son and daughter are Class I heirs. Under Section 8, Class I heirs inherit to the total exclusion of all others, so the father (a Class II heir) is shut out. Each takes one share, so son and daughter get 1/2 each.\n- A) The father is Class II and excluded when Class I heirs exist.\n- C) Including the father is wrong for the same reason.\n- D) The daughter is an equal Class I heir alongside the son.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, when do agnates inherit?\nA) Always before Class I heirs  B) Only when there is no heir in Class I or Class II  C) Only when there are cognates  D) Only if named in a Will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 8 priority: Class I → Class II → agnates → cognates. Agnates inherit only if there is no heir in either Class.\n- A) Class I always comes first.\n- C) Agnates are actually preferred over cognates, not dependent on them.\n- D) Intestate succession does not involve a Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Among Class II heirs, how is priority decided between different entries?\nA) All entries share equally  B) Later entries are preferred  C) Earlier entries are preferred over later entries  D) The court decides randomly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 9, those in the first entry of Class II are preferred to the second entry, the second to the third, and so on.\n- A) Equal sharing only applies *within* the same entry (Section 11).\n- B) The opposite — earlier beats later.\n- D) It is rule-based, not random.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A Hindu male dies intestate. He has no Class I heirs but is survived by his father AND a brother. Who inherits?\nA) Father and brother equally  B) Father only  C) Brother only  D) Neither — it goes to the Government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Father is Entry I of Class II; brother is in Entry II. Under Section 9, the earlier entry is preferred, so the father (Entry I) inherits to the exclusion of the brother.\n- A) They are in different entries, so they do not share equally.\n- C) The brother is in a later entry and is excluded.\n- D) The Government does not inherit while Class II heirs exist (escheat is a Muslim-law concept here anyway).\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Two heirs fall within the SAME entry of Class II. How is the property divided between them?\nA) Equally  B) By age  C) By gender (male double)  D) The elder takes all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under Section 11, heirs in any one entry of Class II share equally.\n- B), D) Age is irrelevant.\n- C) Male-double is a Muslim-law residuary rule, not a Hindu Class II rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A Hindu female dies intestate leaving her own sons, daughters and husband. Under Section 15, who inherits FIRST?\nA) Heirs of the husband  B) Her mother and father  C) Her sons, daughters and husband  D) Heirs of the father\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 15(a): the property devolves first upon the sons and daughters (including children of any pre-deceased son/daughter) and the husband.\n- A) Heirs of the husband come second.\n- B) Mother and father come third.\n- D) Heirs of the father come fourth.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** In Muslim law, heirs entitled to a fixed certain share of the property are called:\nA) Residuary  B) Sharers  C) Agnates  D) Cognates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sharers are entitled to a certain fixed share of the deceased's property.\n- A) Residuaries take what is left after the sharers.\n- C), D) Agnate/cognate are Hindu-law categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Under Muslim law, a wife is entitled to what share when the couple HAS lineal descendants?\nA) 1/2  B) 1/4  C) 1/8  D) 2/3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A wife takes 1/4 where there are no lineal descendants, and a one-eighth (1/8) share otherwise (when descendants exist).\n- A) 1/2 is a husband's share with no descendants (or a sole daughter's share).\n- B) 1/4 is the wife's share when there are NO descendants.\n- D) 2/3 is the joint share of more than one daughter.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Under Muslim law, a sole daughter takes what share?\nA) 1/4  B) 1/3  C) 1/2  D) 2/3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A sole daughter takes a half (1/2) share.\n- A), B) Not the stated daughter shares.\n- D) 2/3 is the joint share when there is more than one daughter.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Under Muslim law, when a deceased leaves behind BOTH sons and daughters, the daughters:\nA) Continue as sharers taking 2/3  B) Cease to be sharers and become residuary, each son getting double a daughter  C) Are excluded entirely  D) Inherit equally with sons\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When both sons and daughters survive, the daughters cease to be sharers and become residuary, with the residue distributed so each son gets double of each daughter.\n- A) The 2/3 rule applies only when there are daughters and no sons.\n- C) Daughters are not excluded; they become residuary.\n- D) Sons and daughters do NOT take equally — sons get double.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A childless Muslim widow is entitled to what share of her deceased husband's property?\nA) 1/8  B) 1/4  C) 1/2  D) 2/3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A childless Muslim widow is entitled to one-fourth (1/4) of the deceased husband's property (after funeral and legal expenses and debts).\n- A) 1/8 applies to a widow who has children or grandchildren.\n- C), D) Not the stated widow shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which distribution method, used mainly in Sunni law, divides the estate equally among heirs so that each person's share depends on the number of heirs?\nA) Per strip  B) Per capita  C) Per stirpes branch  D) Escheat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per capita distribution is used mainly in Sunni law: the estate is divided equally among the heirs, so each share depends on the number of heirs.\n- A), C) The per strip (per stirpes) method is recognised in Shia law, dividing by branch.\n- D) Escheat is the Government inheriting when there is no heir.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Under Muslim law, a female heir's share compared with a male heir's is generally:\nA) Equal  B) Double  C) Half  D) One-third\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Muslim law does not bar females from inheriting, but the quantum of a female heir's share is generally half of that of the male heirs.\n- A) Not equal.\n- B) The male's share is double the female's, not the other way round.\n- D) The fraction is one-half, not one-third.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** For Muslim NON-testamentary (intestate) succession in India, which Act applies?\nA) Indian Succession Act, 1925  B) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  C) Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act, 1937  D) Married Women's Property Act, 1874\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In cases of non-testamentary (intestate) Muslim succession, the Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act, 1937 applies.\n- A) For Muslim testamentary succession involving immovable property in specific jurisdictions, the ISA 1925 can apply — but not for general intestate succession.\n- B) The Hindu Succession Act does not apply to Muslims.\n- D) The MWP Act is not a succession statute.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A Muslim man's immovable property is situated in West Bengal, and the matter concerns his Will (testamentary succession). Which Act binds the Muslims here?\nA) Shariat Act, 1937  B) Indian Succession Act, 1925  C) Hindu Succession Act, 1956  D) MWP Act, 1874\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where immovable property is in West Bengal, or within the jurisdiction of the Madras or Bombay High Court, Muslims are bound by the Indian Succession Act, 1925 — but this exception is only for testamentary succession.\n- A) The Shariat Act governs general non-testamentary succession.\n- C), D) These do not apply to Muslims here.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Under Muslim law, regarding birthright in ancestral property:\nA) Heirs acquire rights by birth, as in Hindu law  B) There is no concept of birthright; rights are fixed at the date of the ancestor's death  C) Only sons acquire birthright  D) Rights fluctuate over time\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is no concept of ancestral property rights by birth under Muslim succession; the rights an heir acquires are fixed and determined with certainty on the date of the ancestor's death and do not fluctuate.\n- A) That birthright concept is Hindu, not Muslim.\n- C) No son-only birthright exists.\n- D) The rights are fixed, not fluctuating.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Under Muslim law, a child in the womb at the time of the ancestor's death is entitled to a share only if:\nA) The child is a son  B) The child is born alive  C) A Will names the child  D) The mother survives\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A child in the womb is entitled to a share only if born alive; if born dead, the share is treated as if it never existed.\n- A) Gender is not the condition.\n- C) No Will is required for this entitlement.\n- D) The mother's survival is not the stated condition.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Under Muslim law, the minimum age to make a valid Will (Wasiyat) is:\nA) 18 years  B) 21 years  C) 15 years  D) 12 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Muslim law, any person who is major (15 years and above) and of sound mind can make a Will.\n- A), B), D) These ages are not the threshold stated for Wasiyat.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which of the following is a valid way to make a Will under Muslim law?\nA) Only in writing on stamp paper  B) Only orally before a judge  C) In writing, orally, or even by gestures  D) Only through a registered deed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Muslim law requires no formalities for creating a Will; it can be made in writing, orally, or even by gestures.\n- A), B), D) These impose formalities that Muslim law does not require.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Under the MWP Act, 1874, a man takes a life insurance policy on his own life expressed to be for the benefit of his wife and children. This policy:\nA) Forms part of the husband's estate and is open to his creditors  B) Becomes a trust for the wife/children and is NOT subject to the husband's creditors or estate  C) Belongs to the husband's parents  D) Is invalid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the MWP Act, such a policy is deemed a trust for the benefit of the wife and/or children; so long as any object of the trust remains, it is not subject to the husband's control, his creditors, or part of his estate.\n- A) The whole point is that it is shielded from creditors and the estate.\n- C) It is for the wife/children, not the parents.\n- D) It is perfectly valid.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Under the MWP Act, 1874, a married woman's wages from her own employment, plus savings and investments from them, are treated as:\nA) Joint property with the husband  B) Her separate property  C) The husband's property  D) Government property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A married woman's wages and earnings from her own employment/trade, income from literary/artistic/scientific skill, and savings/investments from these are deemed her separate property.\n- A), C) They are specifically NOT the husband's or joint.\n- D) They are the woman's, not the Government's.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A married woman has ante-nuptial (before-marriage) debts. Under the MWP Act, the husband (married after 31 Dec 1865) is:\nA) Fully liable for these debts merely due to the marriage  B) Not liable merely by reason of the marriage; the wife remains liable out of her separate property  C) Liable only for half  D) Liable only if he signs a guarantee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A husband married after 31 December 1865 is not, by reason only of the marriage, liable for his wife's pre-marriage debts; the wife is liable to be sued and must satisfy such debts out of her separate property.\n- A) The marriage alone does not make him liable.\n- C), D) These specific fractions/conditions are not in the Act's main rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Mutation entries in revenue records:\nA) Are conclusive proof of ownership/title  B) Are NOT proof of title; title comes from documents like the sale deed/Will  C) Replace the need for a Will  D) Are issued by the civil court\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutation is not proof to confer title; revenue proceedings are not judicial proceedings. Title is the basis of ownership, not mutation entries.\n- A) Mutation is expressly NOT conclusive proof of title.\n- C) It does not replace a Will.\n- D) Mutation is handled by state/revenue authorities, not the civil court.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** The sole objective of mutation is to:\nA) Prove ownership in court  B) Collect revenue/tax from the person in possession of the property  C) Distribute assets among heirs  D) Appoint an executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The sole objective of mutation is to collect revenue from the person in whose possession the property is; records show the rightful owner so tax liabilities can be fixed.\n- A) Mutation does not prove ownership in court.\n- C), D) Those are functions of succession laws and Wills, not mutation.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, if a Hindu male dies leaving more than one widow, the widows:\nA) Each take a full separate share  B) Together take one share, inherited jointly  C) Are excluded as Class II heirs  D) Take double the mother's share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Section 10 Rule 1, if there is more than one widow, all the widows together take one share, inherited jointly.\n- A) They do NOT each get a separate full share; they share one.\n- C) Widows are Class I heirs, not excluded.\n- D) There is no such doubling rule.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A Hindu male, Anand, dies intestate. Survivors: widow, mother, and two sons. There are no pre-deceased children. How is his estate divided?\nA) Widow 1/2, rest split  B) Each of the four takes 1/4  C) Widow 1/3, mother 1/3, sons share 1/3  D) Sons take everything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are Class I heirs. Section 10: widow takes one share (Rule 1), and each surviving son and the mother take one share each (Rule 2). That is 4 equal shares: widow, mother, son, son. Each gets 1 ÷ 4 = 1/4.\n- A) The widow does not get a privileged 1/2 among living Class I heirs.\n- C) There are four claimants, not three; sons each count separately.\n- D) The widow and mother are also Class I heirs and cannot be excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Hindu male Bhola dies intestate leaving a widow, one son, and the two children (a son and a daughter) of a PRE-DECEASED son. How is the estate split at the top level?\nA) Three equal shares to widow, son, and the pre-deceased son's branch  B) Everything to the surviving son  C) Widow 1/2, surviving son 1/2  D) Each grandchild gets a full share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 10: widow = one share (Rule 1); surviving son = one share (Rule 2); the branch of the pre-deceased son takes one share between them (Rule 3). So 3 shares of 1/3 each. The pre-deceased son's branch's 1/3 is then split between his son and daughter (1/6 each).\n- B) The widow and the deceased son's branch cannot be excluded.\n- C) This ignores the pre-deceased son's branch entirely.\n- D) Grandchildren in a branch share ONE share between them, not a full share each.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Continuing Q72: what does each grandchild (the son and daughter of the pre-deceased son) actually receive?\nA) 1/3 each  B) 1/6 each  C) 1/2 each  D) Nothing — grandchildren are excluded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The pre-deceased son's branch takes one share = 1/3 (from Q72). Under Section 10 Rule 4, within that branch the surviving son and daughter get equal proportions: 1/3 ÷ 2 = 1/6 each.\n- A) 1/3 is the whole branch's share, not each child's.\n- C) 1/2 each would consume the whole estate.\n- D) Children of a pre-deceased son are Class I heirs and definitely inherit.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Chandni, a Hindu woman, dies intestate. She is survived by her husband, one daughter, and her own mother and father. Under Section 15, who inherits her property?\nA) The husband and daughter only  B) The mother and father only  C) All four equally  D) Only the husband\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 15(a): a Hindu female's property goes first to her sons and daughters (and children of pre-deceased children) and her husband. The mother and father only come into the picture (clause c) if there are no heirs under clause (a). Since the daughter and husband exist, they inherit and the parents are excluded.\n- B) Parents are postponed to the children and husband.\n- C) The parents do not share alongside the first-category heirs.\n- D) The daughter is also a first-category heir under Section 15(a).\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Deepak, a Hindu, dies intestate, unmarried, with no children and no living parents. He is survived by one brother and three sisters. How is his estate distributed?\nA) Brother takes all (male preference)  B) Brother and three sisters share equally (1/4 each)  C) Sisters share, brother excluded  D) It escheats to the Government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With no Class I heirs (no spouse, children, or mother) and no father, succession goes to Class II. Brother and sister sit in the SAME Class II entry (Entry II), so under Section 11 they share equally: 1 ÷ 4 = 1/4 each. This mirrors the workbook caselet.\n- A) There is no male-preference rule in Hindu Class II sharing.\n- C) The brother is in the same entry as the sisters and is not excluded.\n- D) Escheat is a Muslim-law concept; here Class II heirs exist.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Esha, a Hindu, dies intestate leaving only an adopted son and a brother. Who inherits?\nA) Brother only  B) Adopted son and brother equally  C) Adopted son only  D) Half each because adoption is \"half blood\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An adopted child is treated as equivalent to a full-blood natural child and is a Class I heir. Class I heirs exclude all others (the brother is Class II). So the adopted son takes the entire estate.\n- A) The brother (Class II) is excluded when a Class I heir exists.\n- B) They are in different classes, so they do not share.\n- D) Adoption is treated as full blood, not half blood; the son does not split with a Class II heir.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Farhan, a Sunni Muslim, dies leaving a wife and two sons (no daughters, no parents). After the wife's fixed share, how is the remainder distributed among the sons?\nA) Wife 1/8, sons share the remaining 7/8 equally  B) Wife 1/4, sons share 3/4 equally  C) Wife takes nothing  D) Eldest son takes all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The couple has lineal descendants (the sons), so the wife takes 1/8. The residue (1 − 1/8 = 7/8) goes to the sons as residuaries; with no daughters they share equally: each son gets 7/16.\n- B) 1/4 is the wife's share only when there are NO descendants; here there are.\n- C) A widow is never excluded under Muslim law.\n- D) There is no eldest-takes-all rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Gulnaaz, a Muslim, dies leaving a husband and one son and one daughter. The husband's share is fixed first. What share does the husband take?\nA) 1/2  B) 1/4  C) 1/8  D) 2/3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A husband takes 1/2 where the wife had no lineal descendants, and 1/4 otherwise. Here there are descendants (son and daughter), so the husband takes 1/4.\n- A) 1/2 applies only when there are no lineal descendants.\n- C) 1/8 is a wife's (not husband's) share with descendants.\n- D) 2/3 is the joint share of multiple daughters.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Q78: after the husband takes 1/4, the residue is shared by the son and daughter as residuaries. How much does the daughter get?\nA) Same as the son  B) Double the son  C) Half the son (son gets double)  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When both sons and daughters exist, daughters become residuary and the residue is split so each son gets double each daughter. The residue is 3/4. Split into 3 parts (son = 2, daughter = 1): daughter = 1/3 × 3/4 = 1/4; son = 2/3 × 3/4 = 1/2. So the daughter gets half of what the son gets.\n- A) Sons and daughters do not take equally under Muslim residuary rules.\n- B) The son, not the daughter, takes double.\n- D) The daughter is not excluded; she becomes a residuary.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A Muslim man with two sons but no daughters writes a Wasiyat trying to leave his entire estate to a charity. Is this valid?\nA) Yes, a Muslim can will away everything  B) No — a Muslim can will away at most 1/3 of net assets; the other 2/3 must go to heirs  C) Yes, but only if registered  D) No — Muslims cannot make Wills\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Muslim law permits disposing of no more than one-third of net assets by Will; the remaining two-thirds must be available for the heirs. Willing away the entire estate exceeds the limit.\n- A) The 1/3 cap prevents willing away everything.\n- C) Registration is not what cures the over-limit problem; the cap is substantive.\n- D) Muslims can make Wills (Wasiyat) up to the 1/3 limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A Muslim man marries during a terminal illness and dies of that illness without recovering or consummating the marriage. His widow's inheritance right is:\nA) Full 1/4 share  B) Full 1/8 share  C) No right of inheritance  D) Half of a son's share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If a Muslim man marries during an illness and dies of that condition without brief recovery or consummating the marriage, his widow has no right of inheritance.\n- A), B) These widow shares do not apply in this special situation.\n- D) Female-half-of-male is the general rule, but here the widow gets nothing.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Hari, a Hindu, dies intestate survived only by his father's father (paternal grandfather) and a sister. Who inherits?\nA) Grandfather and sister equally  B) Sister only  C) Father's father only  D) Neither — escheat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are Class II heirs but in different entries. The sister is in Entry II; the father's father is in Entry IV. Under Section 9, the earlier entry (Entry II) is preferred, so the sister inherits to the exclusion of the grandfather.\n- A) Different entries do not share equally.\n- C) The grandfather is in a later entry and is excluded.\n- D) Escheat is a Muslim-law concept; Class II heirs exist here.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Isha appoints her brother as the nominee for her bank fixed deposit. On her death, her legal heirs (husband and son) claim the money. The nominee:\nA) Becomes the permanent owner of the FD  B) Holds the money as custodian and must pass it to the legal heirs  C) Can split it however he wishes  D) Inherits ahead of the husband and son\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A nominee's rights are limited to holding the asset as custodian until it is transferred to the rightful legal heirs (for assets like a bank FD).\n- A) A nominee is generally not the permanent owner.\n- C) The nominee cannot freely allocate; the asset belongs to the legal heirs.\n- D) The legal heirs, not the nominee, have the ownership right here.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Jay names his spouse as the nominee in his life insurance policy. Under the new Insurance Act, on Jay's death the spouse:\nA) Is only a custodian who must hand the money to other heirs  B) Is a beneficial nominee who can keep and consume the proceeds  C) Gets nothing because spouses cannot be nominees  D) Must share equally with Jay's brothers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the new Insurance Act, if Parents, Spouse or Children are the nominee, they automatically become beneficial nominees and can consume the money too. So the spouse keeps the proceeds.\n- A) That custodian rule applies to ordinary nominations, not beneficial nominees under insurance.\n- C) Spouses certainly can be nominees.\n- D) Brothers do not get a forced share against a beneficial-nominee spouse here.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Karan, a Hindu, dies intestate with NO Class I heirs, NO Class II heirs, but a surviving relative connected to him wholly through males. This relative inherits as a/an:\nA) Cognate  B) Agnate  C) Sharer  D) Residuary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 8 priority: when there is no Class I or Class II heir, the property goes to agnates (related wholly through males), before cognates.\n- A) A cognate is preferred only after agnates are exhausted.\n- C), D) Sharer/residuary are Muslim-law categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Two agnates compete to inherit. One has fewer degrees of ascent than the other. Under Section 12, who is preferred?\nA) The one with MORE degrees of ascent  B) The one with FEWER or no degrees of ascent  C) They always take equally  D) The younger one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 12 Rule 1: of two heirs, the one with fewer or no degrees of ascent is preferred. (Rule 2 uses descent only if ascent is equal; Rule 3 lets them take together if neither is preferred.)\n- A) The opposite — fewer is better.\n- C) Equal sharing happens only when neither is preferable.\n- D) Age is irrelevant; degrees of ascent/descent decide.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Under Section 13, when computing degrees for agnates/cognates, the degrees of ascent or descent are counted:\nA) Excluding the intestate  B) Inclusive of the intestate, with every generation being one degree  C) Only from the heir's side  D) Counting only male links\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 13: degrees of ascent/descent are computed inclusive of the intestate, and every generation constitutes one degree.\n- A) The intestate IS included.\n- C) Computation runs from the intestate to the heir.\n- D) Counting male-only links defines agnate vs cognate, not the degree computation.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Lata's father dies intestate (Hindu). He has remarried; Lata is from his first marriage and her step-brother is from the second marriage. Lata and the step-brother are related by:\nA) Full blood  B) Half blood  C) Uterine blood  D) No relation at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Same father, different mothers = half-blood relationship. (Both are still children/Class I heirs of the father, so both inherit; the blood term describes their relationship to each other.)\n- A) Full blood would require the same mother too.\n- C) Uterine = same mother, different father — the reverse situation.\n- D) They share the father, so they are related.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A Hindu woman's two children, Meena and Naveen, have the same mother but different fathers (uterine). The mother dies intestate. Regarding inheriting from the MOTHER's estate:\nA) Both are excluded as uterine relatives  B) Both inherit as her own children under Section 15(a)  C) Only Meena inherits  D) Neither, because uterine relations never inherit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The uterine-exclusion rule keeps uterine brothers/sisters out of the *Class I/Class II schedule* (for a male intestate). But here the mother is the intestate, and both children are HER own sons/daughters, who inherit first under Section 15(a) of a Hindu female's succession.\n- A), D) The exclusion applies to uterine siblings inheriting via the schedule, not to a mother's own children inheriting from her.\n- C) Both are her children, so both inherit.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Om, a Hindu, leaves a valid registered Will giving his house to his nephew, while his only son is alive. Which statement is correct?\nA) The son automatically overrides the Will and takes the house  B) The Will (testamentary succession) governs, so the nephew gets the house  C) Class I heir rules force the house to the son  D) The house escheats to the Government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Class I/II rules apply to INTESTATE succession (no Will). Here Om made a valid Will, so testamentary succession under the Indian Succession Act, 1925 governs and the house passes to the nephew as Om directed.\n- A), C) Intestate Class I rules do not apply when there is a valid Will.\n- D) Escheat is irrelevant; there is a beneficiary.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Pooja's husband took a life insurance policy on his own life, expressed to be for the benefit of Pooja and their children, under the MWP Act. He later runs into heavy business debts and dies. Can his creditors claim the policy proceeds?\nA) Yes, it is part of his estate  B) No — it is a protected trust for the wife/children, outside his estate and creditors' reach  C) Yes, but only half  D) Only if the children are minors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the MWP Act, such a policy is a trust for the wife/children; so long as any object of the trust remains, it is not subject to the husband's creditors and does not form part of his estate.\n- A) It is specifically excluded from his estate.\n- C), D) There is no half rule or minor-only condition; the protection is complete.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Qadir, a Muslim with no spouse, no children, no parents, and no other relatives whatsoever, dies. What happens to his property?\nA) It is divided among distant villagers  B) The Government inherits it through escheat  C) It is held forever by the court  D) It goes to the mosque automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When a deceased Muslim has no legal heir, the Government inherits the property through the process of escheat.\n- A), D) The estate does not pass to villagers or a mosque by default.\n- C) The court does not hold it indefinitely; escheat transfers it to the Government.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Ravi buys land and the seller's name is changed to Ravi's in the revenue records (mutation done). A relative later disputes ownership claiming an older sale deed. Which is the stronger proof of title?\nA) The mutation entry, since it is in revenue records  B) The sale deed / title document, because mutation is not proof of title  C) Neither has any value  D) The mutation entry, because it is government-issued\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutation is not proof to confer title; revenue proceedings are not judicial. The title (the sale deed) is the basis on which ownership is acquired, not the mutation entry.\n- A), D) A mutation entry, even if government-recorded, does not establish title.\n- C) The title document certainly has value.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Sneha, a Hindu, dies intestate leaving a son born of a void marriage and a brother. Does the son inherit?\nA) No, children of a void marriage are excluded  B) Yes — children of void/voidable marriages are entitled to succession (Sec 16(3), Hindu Marriage Act), and as a Class I heir he excludes the brother  C) Only half the estate; the brother takes the rest  D) Only if legitimised by court\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Children born of void or voidable marriages are entitled to succession by virtue of Section 16(3) of the Hindu Marriage Act and count among Class I heirs. A Class I heir excludes a Class II heir (the brother), so the son takes the whole estate.\n- A) Such children are NOT excluded.\n- C) The brother (Class II) does not share when a Class I heir exists.\n- D) No separate court legitimisation is required for this entitlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Tariq, a Shia Muslim, dies leaving heirs across two branches of descendants. The distribution method recognised for him divides property according to the branch (strip). This is called:\nA) Per capita distribution  B) Per strip (per stirpes) distribution  C) Escheat  D) Per share residuary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The per strip distribution method is recognised in Shia law: property is distributed among heirs according to the branch (strip) they belong to, so the quantum depends on the branch and the number of persons in it.\n- A) Per capita (equal among all heirs) is mainly the Sunni method.\n- C) Escheat applies only when there is no heir.\n- D) \"Per share residuary\" is not a defined method here.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Uma, a Hindu, dies intestate. Survivors: widower (husband), no children. Under Section 15, after the first category (which is empty of children) the property goes to:\nA) Her mother and father immediately  B) The husband under clause (a), since clause (a) includes the husband  C) The Government  D) Her brothers and sisters first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 15(a) covers \"sons and daughters (including children of any pre-deceased son or daughter) AND the husband.\" Even with no children, the husband is included in the first category and inherits.\n- A) Parents come only in clause (c), after clause (a) heirs.\n- C) The Government is not relevant when a husband survives.\n- D) Siblings are not the first category for a Hindu female.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Vivek, a Hindu, dies intestate. Survivors: his mother and his father only (no spouse, no children). How is the estate divided?\nA) Father and mother equally  B) Mother takes all (Class I), father excluded (Class II)  C) Father takes all  D) Brothers inherit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The mother is a Class I heir; the father is only a Class II heir. Section 8 gives Class I priority to the exclusion of all others. So the mother takes the entire estate and the father, though alive, is excluded.\n- A) They are in different classes, so no equal split.\n- C) The father is the lower (Class II) heir and is excluded.\n- D) Brothers (Class II) are excluded when a Class I heir exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Waheeda, a Muslim, dies leaving only daughters — three of them — and no sons and no spouse and no parents. As sharers, the daughters jointly take:\nA) 1/2  B) 1/3  C) 2/3  D) The whole estate equally as sharers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Where the deceased leaves more than one daughter (and no sons to convert them to residuary), all daughters jointly take two-thirds (2/3) as sharers. (The remaining 1/3 would go to other heirs/residuaries per the rules.)\n- A) 1/2 is a SOLE daughter's share.\n- B) 1/3 is not the multiple-daughters share.\n- D) As sharers they take a fixed 2/3 jointly, not necessarily the whole estate.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Yusuf, a Muslim, dies. His apparent heir (a cousin) had actually died BEFORE Yusuf. Does the cousin's family inherit Yusuf's property through that cousin?\nA) Yes, by representation through the pre-deceased cousin  B) No — under Muslim law an heir who does not survive the ancestor acquires no right, so nothing passes through him  C) Yes, but only half  D) Only if there is a Will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Muslim law, rights are fixed at the date of the ancestor's death; if the apparent heir does not survive the ancestor, no right of inheritance or share exists for him, so nothing passes through him.\n- A) Muslim law does not allow inheritance through a pre-deceased apparent heir here.\n- C) There is no half share; the right simply does not arise.\n- D) A Will (limited to 1/3) does not change this survivorship principle.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Zoya prepared (i) a Will distributing her flat, (ii) a living will for medical decisions, and (iii) nominated her son in her mutual funds. After her death the son (also her sole legal heir) claims the mutual fund money. Which is TRUE?\nA) The living will distributes the flat  B) The Will governs the flat; the nominee/son, being the sole legal heir, is entitled to the mutual fund money  C) The nomination overrides the Will for the flat  D) The mutual funds escheat to the Government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Will governs distribution of the flat (testamentary succession). For the mutual funds, the son is the nominee — and here he is also the sole legal heir, so he is entitled to keep the money (nominee holds for the legal heir, and he IS the legal heir).\n- A) A living will is only for medical/end-of-life decisions, not property distribution.\n- C) A nomination on mutual funds makes the son a custodian for the legal heir; it does not override a Will for a different asset (the flat).\n- D) Escheat (Muslim-law concept) is irrelevant; a legal heir exists.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**The Mehra Family**\n\nMr. Rakesh Mehra, a Hindu, passes away suddenly. He had NOT written any Will. At the time of death, his estate consists of: a house and a bank account (gross estate), a car and household jewellery (residue estate), and an outstanding home loan (estate debt). He also held a life insurance policy taken on his own life, expressed under the Married Women's Property Act, 1874 to be for the benefit of his wife and children.\n\nHis surviving family members are: his **wife** (Sunita), his **mother** (Kamla), **two sons** (Arjun and Bharat), and one **daughter** of a **pre-deceased son** (the granddaughter, Diya). His **father** (Mohan) is also alive. Rakesh's brother Suresh is alive too.\n\nWork through the following sub-questions.\n\n**Sub-question 1:** Which law governs the distribution of Rakesh's house and bank account, and why?\n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Answer: The Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (intestate succession).**\n\nRakesh died **without a Will**, so he died **intestate**. The Indian Succession Act, 1925 (Section 30) defines this state, but the actual distribution follows the inheritance law of the deceased's religion. Because Rakesh is a **Hindu**, intestate succession is governed by the **Hindu Succession Act, 1956** (as amended in 2005). (The Indian Succession Act, 1925 would govern only his *testamentary* succession — but there is no Will here.)\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 2:** Who qualifies as Rakesh's heirs for the house and bank account, and is his living father included?\n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Answer: The Class I heirs inherit — wife, mother, two sons, and the pre-deceased son's branch (granddaughter Diya). The father and brother are EXCLUDED.**\n\nUnder **Section 8**, Class I heirs inherit first, to the **total exclusion** of all other heirs. Rakesh's Class I heirs are:\n- Wife (Sunita) — Class I\n- Mother (Kamla) — Class I\n- Son Arjun, Son Bharat — Class I\n- Daughter of a pre-deceased son (Diya) — Class I (she represents her deceased father's branch)\n\nHis **father (Mohan) is only a Class II heir** (Entry I of Class II) and his **brother (Suresh) is also Class II** — both are excluded because Class I heirs exist. This is the classic \"father is NOT a Class I heir\" trap.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 3:** Compute the share of each heir in the house and bank account, showing the working.\n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Answer:**\n\nApply **Section 10** rules. Count the shares:\n- Rule 1: The **widow** (Sunita) takes **one share**.\n- Rule 2: Each surviving **son** and the **mother** take **one share** each → Arjun (1), Bharat (1), Kamla (1).\n- Rule 3: The **branch of the pre-deceased son** takes **one share** between them → Diya's branch (1).\n\nTotal shares = 1 (widow) + 1 (Arjun) + 1 (Bharat) + 1 (mother) + 1 (pre-deceased son's branch) = **5 shares.**\n\nSo each share = **1/5.**\n- Sunita (wife) = **1/5**\n- Kamla (mother) = **1/5**\n- Arjun (son) = **1/5**\n- Bharat (son) = **1/5**\n- Pre-deceased son's branch = **1/5** → and since Diya is the only person in that branch, **Diya gets the full 1/5** (Section 10 Rule 4 splits a branch's share equally among its members; here there is just one member).\n\nCheck: 1/5 × 5 = 1 (whole estate). ✓\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 4:** What happens to the life insurance policy that Rakesh took for the benefit of his wife and children? Can it be used to pay off the outstanding home loan (his creditors)?\n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Answer: The policy proceeds are a protected trust for the wife and children and CANNOT be touched by his creditors or used to clear the home loan.**\n\nUnder the **Married Women's Property Act, 1874**, a policy a man takes on his own life, expressed to be for the benefit of his wife and/or children, is **deemed a trust** for them. So long as any object of the trust remains, it is **not subject to the husband's control, not subject to his creditors, and does not form part of his estate**.\n\nTherefore the home-loan lender (a creditor) cannot claim the insurance money. The home loan, being **estate debt**, must be settled from the **rest of the estate** (e.g., the house, bank account, residue estate) — not from the MWP-protected policy. This shielding of the family payout from creditors is the central purpose of an MWP Act policy.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 5:** Suppose instead Rakesh had been **unmarried with no children, no mother, and no spouse**, surviving only by his **father** and **brother**. Who would inherit then?\n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Answer: The father alone inherits.**\n\nWith **no Class I heirs** (no spouse, children, or mother), succession moves to **Class II** (Section 8 priority: Class I → Class II → agnates → cognates).\n- The **father** is in **Entry I** of Class II.\n- The **brother** is in **Entry II** of Class II.\n\nUnder **Section 9**, an **earlier entry is preferred over a later entry**. So the father (Entry I) inherits to the **exclusion of the brother** (Entry II). The father takes the **entire estate**.\n\n(Contrast this with the workbook caselet where there was NO father — only a brother and sisters — so those siblings, being in the same Class II entry, shared equally under Section 11.)\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "15", "title": "Tools for Estate Planning", "module": "Estate Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 15: Tools for Estate Planning — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **tools** of estate planning and when each one is used\n- Everything about **Wills** (types, validity, registration, codicils, revocation, probate, executor)\n- **Gifts, Joint Holding, Nomination**, and **Family Settlement**\n- **Trusts** — types, parties, characteristics, taxation, offshore trusts\n- **Guardianship** and **Power of Attorney**\n\n---\n\n## 15.1 The Toolbox\n\nEstate-planning tools split into two boxes — those that work **after death** and those that work **during your lifetime**:\n\n| Used **AFTER death** | Used **DURING lifetime** |\n|---|---|\n| Will | Family Settlement, Trust, Guardianship, Joint Holding, Gift, Power of Attorney, Mutation |\n\n**Analogy:** Some tools are like a \"letter to be opened after I'm gone\" (a Will). Others are like \"let me set things up now while I'm here\" (a gift, a trust).\n\n---\n\n## 15.2 Wills\n\n> **Will** (Section 2(h), Indian Succession Act, 1925) = the *legal declaration of the intention of the testator about his property, which he wants carried into effect after his death.*\n\n**Key people:**\n- **Testator** = the person making the Will.\n- **Legatee** = the person named to **receive** a part of the estate.\n- **Executor** = the person named to **administer** (carry out) the estate.\n\nA Will **only takes effect after death**. While alive, the testator can **change or revoke** it anytime, and it stays a **confidential** document until executed. (Section 62 confirms revocability while competent.)\n\n**Why write a Will?** No confusion among family; it's personal (express feelings); handles special situations (special needs, minor children, widowed daughter) that the law ignores; can disinherit a disobedient/fraudulent member; offers tax advantages; lets you appoint a faithful **executor** (in intestacy, administrators must post an administration bond).\n\n### 15.2.2 Types of Wills\n\n| Type | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Conditional/Contingent** | Takes effect only if a condition happens; if condition fails (or is illegal), the Will is invalid |\n| **Joint Will** | Made by 2+ persons; treated as **two separate wills**; takes effect after both die |\n| **Mutual Will** | Two testators give each other **reciprocal benefits** (each is both testator and legatee). If testator & legatee are different, a mutual will is **not** viable |\n| **Duplicate Will** | A signed & attested **copy** (kept by executor/trustee). If the testator destroys his own copy, **both** are revoked |\n| **Holograph Will** | **Entirely in the testator's own handwriting** — considered a strong/good will |\n| **Concurrent Will** | Two wills for property in **different countries**; treated independently unless they're related |\n| **Sham Will** | Executed with formalities but with **no real testamentary intention** — a nullity |\n| **Privileged Will** | For **soldiers in warfare, airmen, mariners at sea** — can be spoken **orally before 2 witnesses**. All others are **unprivileged** wills |\n\n### 15.2.3 Legal requirements & Testamentary Capacity\nSection 59 essentials: **testamentary capacity & sound mind, knowledge of contents, free from undue influence/fraud/coercion, voluntary act.**\n\nWho can make a Will? A sound-mind adult (not a minor); a married woman (for property she can alienate); a **deaf/dumb/blind** person if they understand what they're doing; an insane person **during a lucid interval**.\n\n> **Testamentary capacity** = being of sound mind enough to understand your property, your relationship to the people you might leave it to, and the effect of your Will's provisions.\n\n### 15.2.4 Registration of Wills\n- Registration is **OPTIONAL** (Section 18, Registration Act, 1908). A **non-registered Will is still genuine**.\n- Advantages of registering: can't be tampered/destroyed/stolen; safe custody at registrar; certified copy controlled (Section 57(2)); helps edit leasehold property into heirs' names.\n- Registered on **plain paper — no stamp duty**.\n- Even after registering, **subsequent wills/amendments need NOT be registered.**\n- Exempted from visiting the registrar: a person with bodily infirmity, a person in jail, and a **Muslim pardanashin woman** (registrar can visit them).\n- ⚠️ **Special situation:** A bequest to a charitable/religious institution by a **non-Hindu** must be **deposited with the registrar within 6 months** of execution, **and** the testator must **survive 12 months** from execution — else the bequest is invalid.\n\n### 15.2.5 Revoking a Will\nA Will is **always revocable** until death (the testator must be of sound mind at revocation). **Four modes of revocation** (Section 62/70):\n1. A later **Will or codicil** duly executed\n2. A **written declaration** of intention to revoke, executed like a Will\n3. **Burning, tearing, or destroying** the Will (by testator or in his presence by his direction) with intent to revoke\n4. By a **subsequent marriage**\n\n⚠️ But **Section 57**: for **Hindus, marriage does NOT revoke a Will or codicil**. Also, **divorce** does not revoke a Will. Mere **loss** of a Will is not revocation; merely *intending* to revoke later is not valid.\n\n### 15.2.6 Codicils\n> **Codicil** (Section 2(b)) = a **supplementary document** to a Will that explains, alters, or adds to it. It becomes part of the Will and must be **executed and attested just like a Will**.\n\n💡 Use a codicil for **minor** changes (e.g. replacing a deceased trustee). For **major** changes, write a **fresh Will** revoking the old one.\n\n### 15.2.7 Succession Certificate vs Legal Heir Certificate\nA **succession certificate** is issued by a court to legal heirs (in **absence of a Will**) to collect the deceased's **debts and securities** (PF, insurance, bank deposits, shares). It does **not** by itself decide ownership/title. (Section 370: if there's a valid Will, the property vests in the executor, so no succession certificate is granted; Section 371: granted by **District Courts**.)\n\n| Feature | **Succession Certificate** | **Legal Heir Certificate** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Issued by | **Civil Court** | **Tehsildar** of district |\n| Purpose | Authority to obtain debts/securities (no Will) | Identifies & establishes living heirs |\n| Legal weight | Valid proof under succession laws | NOT conclusive proof |\n| Used for | Debts, securities | Insurance claims, pension, PF, gratuity |\n| Who can apply | Only the certified legal heir | Spouse, parents, son or daughter |\n\n### 15.2.8 The Executor\n> **Executor** (Section 2(c)) = the person to whom the testator entrusts carrying out the Will.\n\n**Duties:** pay funeral/burial expenses & get the death certificate first; locate & value all assets; **pay off all debts, liabilities and taxes** (lenders have first right — may liquidate assets if needed); then distribute the estate; possibly manage a minor's assets until majority. The court grants **probate only to the executor appointed in the Will** (Section 222).\n\n**Who can be executor?** Family/friends/relatives, a beneficiary, or even a **company** (Section 223). A **minor** can be named but probate isn't granted until majority (guardian gets letters of administration). A person of unsound mind **cannot**. There's **no limit** on the number of executors. An executor has a **right of refusal** before starting, but once accepted is legally bound (relieved only by court order).\n\n> **Administrator** = appointed by the **court** when there's no executor, the executor is incapable/refuses, or the executor dies before finishing. Gets **letters of administration**.\n\n---\n\n## 15.3 Probate\n\n> **Probate** (Section 2(f)) = a copy of a Will **certified under the seal of a court** — i.e. the court's stamp of approval that the Will is **genuine** and the executor's acts are valid.\n\n**Probate vs Letters of Administration:** Probate goes to the **executor**; **letters of administration** are issued when there's **no executor** / he's incapable / he died before proving the Will.\n\n**When is probate mandatory?** If the Will/codicil was made in **Kolkata, Chennai, or Mumbai** (the three Presidency towns), **or** if the immovable property is in those towns. Elsewhere it's **optional**.\n\n⚠️ **Probate is NOT a succession certificate.** Probate is issued **when there IS a Will**; a succession certificate is issued **when there is NO Will**.\n\n**Process:** Executor files an application in the proper court with a copy of the Will → court issues notices to legal heirs + advertises in a national newspaper for objections → petitioner proves the testator's death, valid execution, and that it's the last Will → court grants probate. A **court fee** applies (fixed or % of asset value; varies by state since Wills are a **state subject**).\n\n---\n\n## 15.4 Gifts, Joint Holding & Nomination\n\n### 15.4.1 Gifts\n> **Gift** = a **voluntary transfer** of property **without consideration** (without payment). **Donor** gives; **Donee** receives.\n\nA gift is usually **irrevocable** (revocable only if the donee agrees). Gifts are taxable as \"income from other sources\" **except**: gifts from **relatives** (spouse, siblings of self/spouse, parents, grandparents, children, grandchildren), gifts on the **occasion of marriage**, and inheritances under a Will. ⚠️ Any **income later earned** from the gift is taxed in the **donee's** hands.\n\n### 15.4.2 Joint Holding\n> **Joint Holding** = an asset held by **more than one person**. Modes: **'jointly'** (all must approve), **'either or survivor'**, or **'anyone or survivor'**.\n\nBank accounts, property, demat, shares, mutual funds can be held jointly. The **first holder** is usually the registered holder. Joint holders can access the asset after the first holder's death. ⚠️ But if heirs contest, **succession laws can override** the joint holder's right.\n\n### 15.4.3 Nomination\n> **Nomination** = the right of an investment-holder to appoint who **receives the money** on death.\n\n- Only an **individual** can nominate (not companies, firms, trusts, HUF Kartas, or PoA holders).\n- A nominee can be an individual, company, or trust; a **minor** can be a nominee (name a guardian).\n- **Multiple nominees** allowed with defined %; **SEBI** raised the limit to **10 nominees**.\n- Purpose = **simplify payment** on death, **not** equitable distribution.\n- ⚠️ A **Will supersedes a nomination** — the nominee is normally just a **trustee** of the money, not the owner; succession rules generally prevail. **Exceptions:** assets under a separate Act that names heirs — e.g. **Insurance Act (beneficial nominees: spouse/parents/children)** and the **EPF Act** (only immediate family as nominee).\n- Proposed banking change: **successive nomination** (\"one after another\" — alternate nominees).\n\n---\n\n## 15.5 Family Settlement & Property Transfer\n\n> **Family Settlement** = an instrument to bring **peace** to a family when there's a property dispute/rival claims, avoiding long litigation. Must be among **same-family** members with title/claim/interest, entered **voluntarily and in good faith**.\n\n**Advantages:** Not treated as a **transfer** (so **no capital gains tax**); not a **gift**; **no clubbing** provision. Can be **oral or written**; stamped/registered if required.\n\n### 15.5.2 Forms of property transfer among family\n\n| Form | Tax / cost note |\n|---|---|\n| **Joint ownership** | No tax on death; capital gains may apply if transferred during lifetime |\n| **Gifting** | No tax within blood relatives, but **stamp duty** applies (immovable property must be registered) |\n| **Will / Inheritance** | No tax on beneficiary; no re-registration but **mutation** must be applied; capital gains apply only if the inheritor later **sells** (using previous owner's cost) |\n\n### 15.5.3–15.5.6 Family Business\nOnly about **70%** of family businesses survive a generation. Strategies: **transfer assets early**, use a **limited partnership / ESOP / non-voting stock**, **partial sale** for liquidity, or **create a trust**.\n\n**5 ownership structures:** (1) **Owner-Operator** (one person/couple), (2) **Partnership** (only leaders own — fragile across unequal generations), (3) **Distributed** (all descendants inherit — most sought-after), (4) **Nested** (branches own some assets jointly, some separately), (5) **Public** (some shares publicly traded, run by professional managers).\n\n**Valuation methods:** (1) **Capitalizing of Earnings** (based on cash flow), (2) **Projected Earnings** (= **discounted cash flow**), (3) **Market Approach** (compare similar businesses), (4) **Net Asset Value** (value of assets like real estate/machinery).\n\n**Inter-generational transfer tools:** Succession laws, **Will** (easy but more challengeable/slow), **Gift** (immediate, tax-efficient within blood relatives, but irrevocable + stamp duty), and **Trust** (cost-effective, sustainable, long-term).\n\n### 15.5.7 Joint Tenancy vs Tenancy-in-Common\n\n| Feature | **Joint Tenancy** | **Tenancy-in-Common** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Right of survivorship? | **Yes** — on death, share passes to surviving tenant | **No** — share passes by Will/succession |\n| Can leave share by Will? | **No** (unless sole survivor) | **Yes** |\n| How acquired | Same time, same transaction, identical interest | Can be unequal shares (else deemed equal) |\n\n### 15.5.8 Asset Protection\n> **Asset Protection** = strategies/laws to shield assets from creditors' claims.\n\nFactors deciding how much protection is needed: **identity of debtor**, **identity of creditor** (government is more powerful), **nature of claim**, **nature of asset** (e.g. **PPF** can't be attached by creditors).\n\n**Strategies:** (1) Use **Corporations/LLPs** (owners not personally liable, except fraud), (2) **Asset Protection Trusts (APTs)** — assets not legally owned by you so creditors can't reach them (drawback: **irrevocable**, you lose control), (3) **Transferring property rights** to spouse/relative/friend (risky; fraudulent transfers can still be unwound).\n\n### 15.5.9 Creditor Protection Period\nTimeline of laws: RDDBFI Act 1993 (first tribunal) → SARFAESI Act 2002 (secured creditors can seize assets) → CICRA (credit bureaus like CIBIL) → **Insolvency & Bankruptcy Code (IBC), 2016** (a turnaround, with **defined timelines**). Under IBC: file application → adjudicating authority ascertains default within **14 days** → admit/reject → communicate admission within **7 days** → corporate insolvency resolution process.\n\n---\n\n## 15.6 Trusts\n\n> **Trust** (Section 3, Indian Trust Act, 1882) = an obligation attached to ownership of property, arising from a **confidence** reposed in and accepted by the owner, for the benefit of another (or another + the owner).\n\n**Analogy:** You hand your money to a trusted friend (trustee) with strict instructions to use it for your child (beneficiary). The friend holds it but must follow your rules.\n\n**Parties / terms:**\n- **Author / Settlor** = the person who creates the trust and sets aside the property.\n- **Trustee** = the person who **accepts** and **manages** the property (holds legal title).\n- **Beneficiary** = the person who **benefits**.\n- **Trust property** = the subject matter; **instrument of trust** = the document (trust deed).\n\n**Why form trusts?** Charitable/religious goals; Income Tax exemptions (Sec 10/11); welfare of dependent family; managing PF/superannuation/gratuity funds.\n\n**Essential elements (5):** an **author/settlor**, a **trustee**, a **beneficiary**, the **trust property**, and a **specific object**. (Section 6: author must show intention, purpose, beneficiary, trust property, and transfer the property to the trustee.)\n\n**Advantages:** welfare of dependents; reduces litigation; manages charitable distributions; preserves wealth; gifts to minors/elderly care; **strong tax-planning tool**; **avoids probate** (passes directly to beneficiaries).\n**Disadvantages:** complex; expensive to set up/maintain; high stamp duty for immovable property; **settlor loses control**; trustees' powers limited by the deed.\n\n### 15.6.2 Classification of Trusts\n\n| Trust type | Key idea |\n|---|---|\n| **Public Trust** | For the general public/a section (charitable/religious — temples, schools, hospitals) |\n| **Private Trust** | For specific individuals/families |\n| **Revocable Trust** | Settlor keeps control, can modify/terminate; **NOT** asset-protective; taxed in settlor's hands; acts as an **alternative to a Will** |\n| **Irrevocable Trust** | Cannot be amended/revoked unless the deed expressly allows (Sec 77/78) |\n| **Simple Trust** | Trustee has **no active duties**; beneficiary can demand possession |\n| **Specific Trust** | For a special purpose; trustee must actively act |\n| **Oral / Written** | Oral allowed **only for movable** property; **immovable** needs a written deed |\n| **Implied / Express** | Implied = inferred from circumstances; Express = formal trust deed |\n\n### 15.6.3 Characteristics: Discretionary vs Determinate\n\n| Feature | **Discretionary Trust** | **Determinate (Specific) Trust** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trustee discretion | **Full** discretion over income/corpus | **No** discretion — fixed shares per deed |\n| Beneficiary shares | **Not** defined/determinate | **Clearly specified** (or computable) |\n| Taxation | **Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) = 30%** | Taxed on the representative assessee (Sec 161(1)) at slab rates |\n\n💡 **Exceptions** where a discretionary trust is NOT taxed at MMR: (a) no beneficiary has other trust income/income above threshold, or (b) it's the **sole trust created under a Will** to support **dependent relatives**.\n\n### 15.6.4 Types of Family Trust\nPublic Trust (Charitable / Religious — permanent, indefinite) vs Private Trust (defined beneficiaries). Private trusts are of two types: **Private Discretionary** and **Private Specific**. A **Public-cum-Private Trust** splits income between public and private purposes (the private portion is taxable).\n\n### 15.6.5 Family Trust vs Will (very important distinctions)\n\n| Feature | **Will** | **Family Trust** |\n|---|---|---|\n| When effective | **Only on death** | **As soon as created** |\n| Distribution timing | At death | Before/at/after death |\n| What it covers | Property in your name at death | Property **transferred to** the trust |\n| Probate | **May** go through probate | **No** probate |\n| Privacy | Can become **public record** | Stays **private** |\n| Special uses | Name a **guardian** for minors | Plan for **disability / tax saving** |\n\n### 15.6.6 Parties to Trust (recap)\n**Settlor** (creates, can amend if living trust), **Trustee** (manages, has legal title), **Beneficiary** (gets benefits — primary & residual). In a private trust, beneficiary and author can be the **same person**.\n\n### 15.6.7 Hybrid Trust\nHas features of **both** determinate and discretionary: trustee pays a **fixed amount** as set by the settlor, and has **discretion** over the rest.\n\n### 15.6.8 Cancellation / Revocation of a Private Trust\nThe Act doesn't provide for \"dissolving\"; a trust becomes **extinct** when its purpose is fulfilled/unlawful/impossible, or if revoked. A trust can be revoked: with **consent of competent beneficiaries**; if created to **pay the settlor's debts**; or if a **non-testamentary/verbal** trust reserved revocation power for the settlor.\n\n### 15.6.9 Tax Efficiency\n- **Public (charitable) trust** — exemptions under Sections 11, 12, 12A, 12AA, 12AB, 13.\n- **Private trust** — governed by Sections 160, 161, 162, 164; income taxed at **slab rate** or **MMR** depending on structure.\n- **Revocable trust** — income taxed in **transferor's (settlor's) hands** (Sec 60–62). Sec 62 exception: not so if the trust is irrevocable during the beneficiary's lifetime and the transferor gets no benefit.\n- **Business income** of a trust → taxed at **MMR** (exception: sole Will-created trust for a dependent relative → slab rate).\n- **Clubbing of minor income** (Sec 64) — a minor's income clubs with the parent's, *except* a minor with a **Section 80U disability** (not clubbed).\n- **Discretionary trust** → **MMR** (with exceptions).\n\n### 15.6.11 Trust Perpetuities\nA trust **cannot be perpetual**. Income can't be accumulated longer than the **transferor's lifetime or 18 years** from transfer (whichever is later). For a Will, accumulation beyond **18 years** from the testator's death is void. **Exception:** trusts for public benefit (religion, knowledge, commerce, health, safety).\n\n### 15.6.12 Trust as a Pass-Through Entity\n> **Pass-through (flow-through) entity** = income \"flows through\" to the beneficiaries; it's treated as **their** income, not the entity's.\n\nA private trust has **no separate legal identity**. The receipts by the trustee on behalf of the beneficiary are **not taxable** — what's taxable is the **beneficiary's income** (trustee pays in a **representative** capacity).\n\n### 15.6.13 Stamp Duty & Capital Gains on Transfer\n- **Stamp duty** (Article 58, Central Stamp Act) is payable on the **trust deed** when registered (a **state** matter). No stamp duty when distributing to beneficiaries. ⚠️ Settling **immovable property during the settlor's lifetime** → stamp duty applies; settling **through a Will** → **no** stamp duty.\n- **Capital gains:** Sec **47(iii)** exempts transfers to an **irrevocable trust** from capital gains. Transfers to a **revocable trust** **are** taxed. A **testamentary** transfer (on death, via Will) is exempt.\n\n### 15.6.14 Offshore Trusts\nTrusts formed **outside India** under foreign laws. India places **no restriction** on non-resident trustees/beneficiaries. Taxation: **revocable** offshore trust → income taxed as **settlor's**. **Irrevocable** offshore trust: (1) all beneficiaries Indian residents → trustee taxed in representative capacity; (2) mixed residents → only the **Indian** beneficiary taxed on what they receive; (3) partial control in India → taxed as **Association of Persons**.\n\n### 15.6.15 Distributable Net Income (DNI)\n> **DNI** = the portion of a trust's income **allotted to the beneficiaries** (becomes their income, taxed in their hands). Its purpose is to **avoid double taxation**.\n\n**Formula:**\n> **DNI = Taxable Income − Capital Gains + Tax Exemptions**\n> *(If there's a capital **loss**, add it instead of subtracting the gain.)*\n\nAnd **Taxable Income** = interest + dividends + capital gains − fees − exemptions.\n\n💡 **Worked example (from the chapter):** Trust ABC: interest ₹50,000 + dividends ₹50,000 + capital gains ₹20,000 − fees ₹20,000 − exemptions ₹20,000 = **Taxable Income ₹80,000**.\nThen **DNI** = ₹80,000 − ₹20,000 (capital gains) + ₹20,000 (exemption) = **₹80,000**.\n\n---\n\n## 15.7 Guardianship\n\n> **Guardianship** = the legal process of deciding who steps in to care for a person who can't care for themselves (a minor, an incapacitated adult, or an elderly parent).\n\nA guardian's role:\n- **Guardian of the Person** → custody and care.\n- **Guardian of the Estate** → managing **financial affairs**.\n\n**Three kinds:**\n1. **Natural Guardian** — falls **by birth** (parents are natural guardians until the child turns **18**).\n2. **Court-appointed Guardian** — for those with no parents/incapacitated; court appoints.\n3. **Testamentary Guardian** — appointed under a **Will** (mostly for minor children).\n\nThe applicant must be an **Indian citizen over 18**. A guardian makes financial decisions (bank accounts, investments, availing loans/concessions for the disabled) for an incapacitated adult.\n\n---\n\n## 15.8 Power of Attorney (POA)\n\n> **POA** = a legal document giving one person (the **agent / attorney-in-fact / donee**) the power to act for another (the **principal / donor**).\n\nBoth parties must be **major, of sound mind, and competent to contract**. Used heavily for property transfer and by **NRIs** / when a principal is ill/absent.\n\n⚠️ Two exceptions on capacity: a **married woman can execute a POA even if a minor**; and even a **minor can be a donee** (Sec 184), but a **lunatic cannot** be an attorney.\n\n### 15.8.1 Types of POA\n1. **General POA** — broad authority, multiple transactions.\n2. **Special POA** — narrow, only a specific transaction; authority **ends when the transaction completes**.\n3. **Special POA for Registration** — authorises presenting a document for registration.\n\n### 15.8.2 Revocation of POA\nA principal can cancel a POA anytime. Revoked (Sec 201) when: principal revokes; principal/agent becomes **insolvent**; holder **renounces**; business is **completed**; or on **death** of either party. ⚠️ Sec 202: if the **agent has an interest** in the agency, it **can't** be revoked without the agent's consent.\n\n**Authentication (Sec 85, Evidence Act):** by a **Notary Public, court/judge/magistrate, Indian Consul/Vice-Consul, or a Central Government representative.**\n\n**Registration:** A POA is **NOT compulsorily registrable** (it doesn't create/transfer interest in property) — **unless** it creates an interest/charge in immovable property. **Stamp duty** (Article 48) applies and varies by state.\n\n### 15.8.3 Limits of a POA holder\nThe agent: can't act outside the document's terms; can't change the document or transfer control to a non-designated person; **loses all control once the principal dies**.\n\n### 15.8.4 POA Executed Abroad\nNRIs can execute a POA abroad for Indian matters (banking, property). It must be **authenticated/attested by the Indian Embassy/Consulate** and notarized where executed. It remains valid even after the person returns to India.\n\n---\n\n## 15.9 Caselets (worked)\n\n**Case 1 — Probate objections:** Mr. PQR (Hindu, unmarried) leaves a Will. During **probate**, the court notifies **all legal heirs** — including the **spouse and children of deceased brothers/sisters** — who may object. If they don't appear, they're presumed to have no objection. *(Answer: All of the above can be called/object.)*\n\n**Case 2 — Trust taxation (representative assessee):** Rajesh creates a trust for his parents (equal beneficial interest). The trust's net annual value ₹5.40 lakh is split equally (₹2.70 lakh each).\n- **Father:** pension ₹4.80 lakh + ₹2.70 lakh = ₹7.50 lakh − ₹75,000 standard deduction = ₹6.75 lakh → tax (5% slab portion) = **₹9,750**.\n- **Mother:** property income ₹3.80 lakh + ₹2.70 lakh = ₹6.50 lakh → tax = **₹13,500**.\n- Total = ₹23,250 + **4% cess (₹930)** = **₹24,180.** ✅ *(Answer: c)*\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- Tools: **after death** = Will, Nomination; **during life** = Trust, Gift, Family Settlement, Joint Holding, Guardianship, PoA, Mutation.\n- **Will**: effective only on death, always revocable; **testator** makes it, **legatee** receives, **executor** carries out. Registration is **optional**.\n- **Codicil** = minor amendment to a Will. **Probate** = court certifying a genuine Will (mandatory in Kolkata/Chennai/Mumbai).\n- **Succession certificate** (no Will, civil court, debts/securities) ≠ **Legal heir certificate** (Tehsildar, identifies heirs) ≠ **Probate** (with Will).\n- **Gift** = voluntary, no consideration; tax-free among relatives but income from it is taxed in the donee's hands.\n- **Nomination** simplifies payment; a **Will supersedes a nomination** (except insurance/EPF beneficial nominees).\n- **Trust** = settlor → trustee → beneficiary. **Revocable** = settlor keeps control, taxed on settlor; **Irrevocable** = no control, protects assets, capital-gains exempt (Sec 47(iii)).\n- **Discretionary trust** = trustee discretion, taxed at **MMR (30%)**; **Determinate** = fixed shares, slab rate.\n- **Trust vs Will**: trust = effective now, private, no probate; Will = on death, can be public, may need probate.\n- **DNI = Taxable Income − Capital Gains + Exemptions.**\n- **POA**: General (broad) / Special (one transaction); not compulsorily registrable; dies with the principal.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Testator / Legatee / Executor** | Maker / receiver / administrator of a Will |\n| **Privileged Will** | Oral will for soldiers/airmen/mariners before 2 witnesses |\n| **Registration of Will** | Optional; on plain paper, no stamp duty |\n| **Charitable bequest by non-Hindu** | Deposit within **6 months**; testator must survive **12 months** |\n| **Probate mandatory in** | Kolkata, Chennai, Mumbai |\n| **Succession certificate** | Civil court; for debts/securities (no Will) |\n| **Legal heir certificate** | Tehsildar; identifies heirs |\n| **SEBI nominee limit** | Up to **10** nominees |\n| **Discretionary trust tax** | **MMR = 30%** |\n| **Capital gains exemption** | Sec **47(iii)** — irrevocable trust |\n| **Trust accumulation limit** | Transferor's lifetime or **18 years** (whichever later) |\n| **DNI formula** | Taxable Income − Capital Gains + Exemptions |\n| **IBC year** | **2016**; default ascertained in **14 days**, admission communicated in **7 days** |\n| **Family business survival** | ~**70%** survive a generation |\n| **POA types** | General, Special, Special for Registration |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 15: Tools for Estate Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Which estate-planning tools take effect AFTER death, and which take effect DURING life?\n> **A:** After death: **Will, Nomination**. During life: **Family Settlement, Trust, Guardianship, Joint Holding, Gift, Power of Attorney, Mutation**.\n\n**Q2.** Define a Will and name the three key people involved.\n> **A:** A Will is the legal declaration of the testator's intention about his property, to take effect after death. **Testator** makes it; **Legatee** receives a portion; **Executor** administers the estate.\n\n**Q3.** When does a Will take effect, and can it be changed?\n> **A:** Only **after the testator's death**. While alive, the testator can **revoke or alter** it anytime (if competent). It stays **confidential** until execution.\n\n**Q4.** What is a privileged Will?\n> **A:** A Will made (often **orally before 2 witnesses**) by **soldiers in warfare, airmen, or mariners at sea**. All other wills are **unprivileged**.\n\n**Q5.** What is a holograph Will?\n> **A:** A Will written **entirely in the testator's own handwriting** — considered a good/strong will.\n\n**Q6.** What happens to a duplicate Will if the testator destroys his own copy?\n> **A:** **Both** copies are considered **revoked**.\n\n**Q7.** What are the four essentials for a valid Will (Section 59)?\n> **A:** Testamentary capacity & sound mind; knowledge of contents; free from undue influence/fraud/coercion; a voluntary act.\n\n**Q8.** Is registration of a Will compulsory?\n> **A:** **No** — it's **optional** (Section 18). A non-registered Will is still genuine. Registered on plain paper with **no stamp duty**.\n\n**Q9.** What special rule applies to a charitable/religious bequest by a non-Hindu?\n> **A:** The Will must be **deposited with the registrar within 6 months** of execution, and the testator must **survive 12 months** from execution — else the bequest is invalid.\n\n**Q10.** What are the four modes of revoking a Will?\n> **A:** (1) A later Will/codicil; (2) a written declaration to revoke (executed like a Will); (3) burning/tearing/destroying with intent; (4) a subsequent marriage. (But marriage does NOT revoke a Hindu's Will.)\n\n**Q11.** What is a codicil?\n> **A:** A **supplementary document** to a Will that explains/alters/adds to it. It must be executed and attested like a Will. Used for **minor** changes.\n\n**Q12.** Succession certificate vs Legal heir certificate — who issues each?\n> **A:** **Succession certificate** → **Civil Court** (authority over debts/securities, no Will). **Legal heir certificate** → **Tehsildar** (identifies living heirs; used for insurance/pension/PF).\n\n**Q13.** Probate vs Succession certificate — when is each used?\n> **A:** **Probate** is issued when there **IS a Will** (court certifies its genuineness). A **succession certificate** is issued when there is **NO Will**.\n\n**Q14.** In which cities is probate mandatory?\n> **A:** **Kolkata, Chennai, and Mumbai** (the Presidency towns), or if the immovable property is there. Elsewhere it's optional.\n\n**Q15.** What is the executor's first responsibility?\n> **A:** To pay the **funeral/burial expenses** and obtain the **death certificate**. Then locate/value assets, pay all debts & taxes (lenders have first right), and finally distribute the estate.\n\n**Q16.** Who is an administrator and when is one appointed?\n> **A:** A person appointed by the **court** (given letters of administration) when there's no executor, the executor is incapable/refuses, or the executor dies before completing the job.\n\n**Q17.** What is a gift, and who are the parties?\n> **A:** A **voluntary transfer of property without consideration** (no payment). **Donor** gives; **Donee** receives. Usually irrevocable.\n\n**Q18.** Are gifts taxable?\n> **A:** Taxable as income from other sources, **except** gifts from relatives, gifts on marriage, and inheritances under a Will. But income later earned from the gift is taxed in the **donee's** hands.\n\n**Q19.** Does a Will or a nomination prevail?\n> **A:** A **Will supersedes a nomination**. The nominee is usually a **trustee** of the money, not the owner — except beneficial nominees under the Insurance Act and EPF Act.\n\n**Q20.** Who can make a nomination, and how many nominees does SEBI now allow?\n> **A:** Only an **individual** can nominate (not firms, trusts, HUF Kartas, PoA holders). SEBI now allows up to **10** nominees.\n\n**Q21.** What is a family settlement and its tax advantages?\n> **A:** An instrument to bring peace among same-family members over a property dispute. It's **not a transfer** (no capital gains), **not a gift**, and **no clubbing** applies.\n\n**Q22.** Joint Tenancy vs Tenancy-in-Common — key difference?\n> **A:** **Joint Tenancy** has a **right of survivorship** (share passes to surviving tenant, not by Will). **Tenancy-in-Common** has **no survivorship** — the share passes by Will/succession and can be unequal.\n\n**Q23.** Name the three asset-protection strategies.\n> **A:** (1) Use **Corporations/LLPs**; (2) **Asset Protection Trusts (APTs)** — but irrevocable, lose control; (3) **Transferring property rights** to spouse/relative/friend.\n\n**Q24.** What major law changed creditor protection in 2016?\n> **A:** The **Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC), 2016** — with defined timelines (default ascertained in 14 days, admission communicated in 7 days).\n\n**Q25.** Define a trust and its three parties.\n> **A:** A trust is an obligation attached to property ownership arising from a confidence reposed in an owner for another's benefit. Parties: **Settlor/Author** (creates), **Trustee** (manages, holds legal title), **Beneficiary** (benefits).\n\n**Q26.** What are the five essential elements of a trust?\n> **A:** An **author/settlor**, a **trustee**, a **beneficiary**, the **trust property**, and a **specific object/purpose**.\n\n**Q27.** Revocable vs Irrevocable trust?\n> **A:** **Revocable** — settlor keeps control, can modify/terminate, NOT asset-protective, taxed in settlor's hands (acts like a Will-alternative). **Irrevocable** — cannot be amended unless the deed expressly allows (Sec 77/78).\n\n**Q28.** Discretionary vs Determinate trust — and how is each taxed?\n> **A:** **Discretionary** — trustee has full discretion, beneficiary shares undefined, taxed at **MMR (30%)**. **Determinate** — fixed/computable shares, taxed on the representative assessee at slab rates.\n\n**Q29.** Give four key differences between a Family Trust and a Will.\n> **A:** Trust is effective **immediately** (Will only on death); trust **avoids probate** (Will may need it); trust stays **private** (Will can be public); trust covers property **transferred to it** (Will covers property in your name at death).\n\n**Q30.** What is a hybrid trust?\n> **A:** A trust with features of **both** determinate and discretionary — trustee pays a **fixed amount** as set by the settlor and has **discretion** over the rest.\n\n**Q31.** Why is a private trust called a \"pass-through entity\"?\n> **A:** It has **no separate legal identity**; income flows through to beneficiaries and is taxed as **their** income. The trustee pays tax only in a **representative** capacity.\n\n**Q32.** What is the rule against trust perpetuities?\n> **A:** A trust can't be perpetual. Income accumulation can't exceed the **transferor's lifetime or 18 years** (whichever later). Exception: trusts for public benefit.\n\n**Q33.** Which section exempts transfers to a trust from capital gains, and for which trust?\n> **A:** **Section 47(iii)** — exempts transfer to an **irrevocable** trust. Transfers to a **revocable** trust ARE taxed; testamentary transfers (via Will) are exempt.\n\n**Q34.** State the DNI formula and compute it for: taxable income ₹80,000, capital gains ₹20,000, exemptions ₹20,000.\n> **A:** **DNI = Taxable Income − Capital Gains + Exemptions** = ₹80,000 − ₹20,000 + ₹20,000 = **₹80,000**.\n\n**Q35.** Name the three types of legal guardianship.\n> **A:** **Natural** (by birth — parents until child is 18), **Court-appointed**, and **Testamentary** (appointed under a Will).\n\n**Q36.** Guardian of the Person vs Guardian of the Estate?\n> **A:** Guardian of the **Person** handles custody and care; Guardian of the **Estate** handles **financial affairs**.\n\n**Q37.** What is a Power of Attorney and who are the parties?\n> **A:** A document giving one person (the **agent/attorney-in-fact/donee**) power to act for another (the **principal/donor**). Both must be major, sound-minded, and competent.\n\n**Q38.** General vs Special POA?\n> **A:** **General** = broad authority over multiple transactions. **Special** = narrow, only a specific transaction; authority ends when the transaction completes.\n\n**Q39.** When does a POA get revoked?\n> **A:** When the principal revokes; either party becomes insolvent; the holder renounces; the business is completed; or on the **death** of either party. (If the agent has an interest, it can't be revoked without consent — Sec 202.)\n\n**Q40.** Is a Power of Attorney compulsorily registrable?\n> **A:** **No** — unless it creates an interest/charge in immovable property. Stamp duty (Article 48) applies and varies by state.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 15: Tools for Estate Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Scenario & Numerical) — followed by a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking** (a wrong answer costs you one-fourth of the marks for that question), so guess only when you can eliminate options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Under Section 2(h) of the Indian Succession Act, 1925, a \"Will\" is the legal declaration of the intention of the testator regarding his property, to be carried into effect ______.\nA) immediately on signing  B) during his lifetime  C) after his death  D) only after registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Will is defined as the legal declaration of the testator's intention about his property \"which he desires to be carried into effect after his death.\" A Will only operates once the maker dies. A) and B) are wrong: while alive the testator can freely change or revoke it — it has no effect yet. D) is wrong because registration of a Will is optional, not a condition for it taking effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The person who makes a Will is called the ______.\nA) testator  B) legatee  C) executor  D) administrator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **testator** is the person making the Will, exercising rights over what is legally his own. B) **Legatee** is the person who *receives* a portion of the estate. C) **Executor** is the person *named in the Will to administer* the estate. D) **Administrator** is appointed by the *court* when there is no executor — not the maker of the Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A person named in a Will to receive a portion of the deceased's estate is called a ______.\nA) legatee  B) testator  C) trustee  D) settlor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **legatee** is the named beneficiary who receives a part of the estate. B) Testator is the maker of the Will. C) Trustee manages trust property (a trust concept, not the recipient of a legacy). D) Settlor (author) is the person who *creates* a trust — again a trust party, not a Will legatee.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The person named in a Will to administer the estate of the deceased is the ______.\nA) legatee  B) nominee  C) donee  D) executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 2(c) defines the **executor** as the person to whom the testator confides execution of the Will; the executor collects assets, pays liabilities and distributes the legacy. A) Legatee only *receives*. C) Donee receives a *gift* (gift law, not a Will). B) Nominee merely receives investment proceeds on death, a different concept entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which section of the Indian Succession Act confirms that a Will may be revoked or altered by the maker at any time while he is competent to dispose of his property?\nA) Section 2(h)  B) Section 59  C) Section 62  D) Section 222\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 62** lays down that a Will is liable to be revoked or altered at any time during the testator's lifetime while competent. A) Section 2(h) is the *definition* of a Will. B) Section 59 lists the *essentials* for making a Will (sound mind etc.). D) Section 222 says probate is granted only to executors named in the Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A Will made by a soldier in actual warfare, an airman so engaged, or a mariner at sea, pronounced orally before two witnesses, is called a ______ Will.\nA) privileged  B) holograph  C) concurrent  D) sham\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **privileged Will** can be made orally (by word of mouth) before two witnesses by soldiers in warfare, airmen and mariners at sea. B) **Holograph** Will is entirely in the testator's own handwriting. C) **Concurrent** Wills cover property in different countries. D) **Sham** Will is executed with formalities but with no real testamentary intention — a nullity.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Will written entirely in the handwriting of the testator is known as a ______ Will.\nA) duplicate  B) joint  C) holograph  D) mutual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **holograph** Will is completely handwritten by the testator and is considered a strong/good Will. A) **Duplicate** is a signed, attested copy kept by the executor/trustee. B) **Joint** Will is made by two or more persons. D) **Mutual** Will is where two testators confer reciprocal benefits on each other.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** In which type of Will do two testators confer reciprocal benefits upon each other, each acting as both testator and legatee?\nA) Mutual Will  B) Joint Will  C) Concurrent Will  D) Conditional Will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In a **mutual Will** the two testators give each other reciprocal benefits, so each is both testator and legatee; if testator and legatee are different persons, a mutual Will is not viable. B) A **joint Will** is made by 2+ persons but is treated as two separate wills taking effect after both die. C) **Concurrent** Wills cover property in different countries. D) **Conditional** Will takes effect only on a contingency.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A Will that takes effect only if a stated event or condition happens, and fails if it does not, is a ______ Will.\nA) sham  B) privileged  C) conditional/contingent  D) duplicate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **conditional or contingent** Will is expressed to take effect only on the happening of a contingency; if the condition fails (or is illegal), the Will is not enforceable. A) Sham = no real intention. B) Privileged = oral will for soldiers etc. D) Duplicate = a copy of the Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Registration of a Will under the Registration Act, 1908 is ______.\nA) optional  B) compulsory in all cases  C) compulsory only for Hindus  D) compulsory above ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 18 of the Registration Act makes registration of a Will an **optional** process; a non-registered Will is still a genuine, valid Will. B), C) and D) are all wrong because the law nowhere makes Will registration compulsory or value/community-based; it only lists *advantages* of registering (safe custody, can't be tampered, etc.).\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A Will is registered on plain paper, which means ______.\nA) heavy stamp duty applies  B) stamp duty varies by state  C) no stamp duty is payable  D) GST applies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Will is registered on plain paper, so **no stamp duty is payable**. A), B) and D) are wrong — there is simply no stamp duty on registering a Will (unlike, say, a gift deed or trust deed which do attract state stamp duty).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A bequest to a charitable or religious institution by a non-Hindu must be deposited with the Registrar within ______ of execution, and the testator must survive ______.\nA) 3 months; 6 months  B) 6 months; 12 months  C) 12 months; 6 months  D) 1 month; 18 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a non-Hindu's charitable/religious bequest to be valid: (1) the Will must be deposited with the Registrar within **6 months** of execution, and (2) the testator must survive **12 months** from the month of execution. So a bequest made just days before death is not valid. The other options use wrong durations.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A supplementary document used to make minor alterations to an existing Will is called a ______.\nA) probate  B) succession certificate  C) codicil  D) trust deed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **codicil** (Section 2(b)) is an instrument made in relation to a Will explaining, altering or adding to it; it must be executed and attested just like a Will. A) **Probate** is the court's certified copy of a Will. B) **Succession certificate** authorises heirs to collect debts/securities when there is no Will. D) **Trust deed** is the instrument creating a trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A codicil must be ______ in the same manner as a Will.\nA) registered  B) probated  C) executed and attested  D) notarised abroad\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A codicil has to be **executed and attested** just like a Will, because it becomes part of the Will. A) Registration is optional (same as a Will), not a \"must.\" B) Probate is a separate court process applied to the Will, not a requirement for making a codicil. D) Notarisation abroad relates to POAs executed overseas, not codicils.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which of the following is one of the four modes of revoking a Will?\nA) Burning or tearing the Will with intent to revoke  B) Selling all the assets  C) Verbally telling a friend  D) Moving to another country\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The four modes are: (i) a later Will/codicil; (ii) a written declaration of intent to revoke, executed like a Will; (iii) **burning, tearing or destroying** the Will with intent to revoke; (iv) a subsequent marriage. B) Selling assets does not revoke the Will. C) A mere verbal intention is not valid revocation. D) Moving country is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** For Hindus, the Indian Succession Act specifies that ______ shall not revoke a Will or codicil.\nA) divorce  B) marriage  C) insolvency  D) emigration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 57 specifically provides that **marriage shall not revoke any Will or codicil for Hindus** (whereas for others, a subsequent marriage is a mode of revocation). The text also states a Will is not revoked on divorce, so A) is not the special carve-out being tested here. C) Insolvency and D) emigration are not modes of revocation at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A succession certificate is issued by a ______ to legal heirs to collect debts and securities of the deceased.\nA) court (civil/district court)  B) Tehsildar  C) bank  D) notary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A succession certificate is a document issued by a **court** (granted by District Courts under Section 371) authorising heirs to collect the deceased's debts and securities when there is no Will. B) **Tehsildar** issues the *legal heir certificate*. C) Banks accept these certificates but do not issue them. D) A notary authenticates documents like POAs, not succession certificates.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A legal heir certificate is issued by the ______.\nA) Civil Court  B) District Court  C) Tehsildar of the district  D) Sub-Registrar\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Per Table 15.1, a **legal heir certificate is issued by the Tehsildar** of the district and identifies/establishes the living heirs. A) and B) Civil/District Courts issue the *succession certificate*. D) The Sub-Registrar deals with registration of documents, not legal heir certificates.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Probate, under Section 2(f), means ______.\nA) a copy of a Will certified under the seal of a court  B) a registered gift deed  C) a succession certificate  D) the trust deed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Probate** is the copy of a Will certified under the seal of a court of competent jurisdiction — it certifies the Will's genuineness and the executor's authority. B) A gift deed is unrelated. C) A succession certificate is issued when there is *no* Will. D) A trust deed creates a trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Probate is mandatory if the Will or the immovable property is situated in which towns?\nA) Delhi, Bengaluru, Hyderabad  B) Pune, Surat, Jaipur  C) Kolkata, Chennai, Mumbai  D) all metro cities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Probate is mandatory if the Will/codicil was made in the three Presidency towns — **Kolkata, Chennai and Mumbai** — or if the immovable property lies there; elsewhere it is optional. The other city groupings are not the Presidency towns named in the Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A gift is a transfer of property made ______.\nA) voluntarily and without consideration  B) for full consideration  C) only on death  D) only to charities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **gift** is a transfer of movable or immovable property made **voluntarily and without consideration** (without payment). B) is wrong — paying full value would be a sale, not a gift. C) is wrong — a gift takes effect during life, unlike a Will. D) is wrong — gifts can be to any person, not only charities.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** In a gift, the person making the gift is the ______ and the person receiving it is the ______.\nA) donee; donor  B) settlor; beneficiary  C) testator; legatee  D) donor; donee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The person making the gift is the **donor**; the person receiving it is the **donee**. A) reverses the two. B) Settlor/beneficiary are trust parties. C) Testator/legatee are Will parties. Matching the right vocabulary to the right instrument is a common exam trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A gift is usually ______, but can be revoked if ______.\nA) revocable; the donor wishes  B) irrevocable; the donee agrees  C) conditional; a court orders  D) temporary; one year passes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A gift is usually an **irrevocable** transfer, but it **can be revoked if the donee agrees**. A) is wrong — the donor cannot unilaterally take it back. C) and D) invent conditions/time limits the chapter does not state.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which of the following can NOT make a nomination?\nA) An individual investor  B) A single account holder  C) A Karta of an HUF  D) A natural person\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only an **individual** can nominate; non-individuals such as corporate bodies, partnership firms, trusts, **Kartas of HUFs** and power of attorney holders **cannot** nominate. A), B) and D) all describe individuals, who are permitted to nominate.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** SEBI has increased the maximum number of nominees allowed to be registered for securities up to ______.\nA) 3  B) 5  C) 10  D) unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Per the SEBI circular dated January 10, 2025, the number of nominees that can be registered was increased up to **10**. The other numbers are distractors; 10 is the figure stated in the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The main purpose of a nomination is ______.\nA) to simplify the payment process on death of the holder  B) equitable distribution of the estate  C) to avoid stamp duty  D) to override succession law permanently\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Nomination exists to **simplify the payment process** in the event of the holder's death, NOT to achieve equitable distribution of the estate. B) is precisely what nomination is *not* for. C) Stamp duty is unrelated. D) is wrong — generally a Will/succession law supersedes a nomination; the nominee is usually only a trustee of the bequest.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Generally, in case of a dispute, a nominee's position is reduced to being a ______ of the bequest.\nA) absolute owner  B) trustee  C) creditor  D) legatee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a dispute, the nominee is treated as a **trustee** of the bequest; the final owners are decided per the applicable laws of succession. A) is wrong — the nominee's title is not absolute. C) Creditor is unrelated. D) The nominee is *not* a legatee or beneficiary under the Indian Succession Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under which Act are \"beneficial nominees\" (spouse, parents, children) entitled to the death benefit, with other legal heirs having no claim?\nA) Indian Trust Act, 1882  B) Registration Act, 1908  C) Indian Contract Act, 1872  D) Insurance Act, 1938\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the **Insurance Act, 1938**, beneficial nominees — the immediate family (spouse, parents, children) — are entitled to the death benefit and other legal heirs cannot claim. A) governs trusts. B) governs registration. C) governs contracts/agency (POA). These are the exceptions where a nominee actually gets legal rights.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A family settlement is treated such that ______.\nA) capital gains tax arises on it  B) it is treated as a gift  C) it is NOT treated as a transfer, so no capital gains tax arises  D) clubbing provisions apply\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **family settlement is not treated as a transfer**, so capital gains tax does not arise; it is also not treated as a gift and clubbing provisions do not apply. A), B) and D) each contradict a stated advantage of a family settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A family settlement agreement ______.\nA) must always be in writing and registered  B) must be probated  C) may be oral or in writing  D) requires court approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A family settlement **may be oral or in writing**, and may be stamped and registered if required. A) is too strict — writing/registration is not mandatory. B) Probate applies to Wills. D) Court approval is not required; the settlement must just be voluntary and in good faith among family members.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In a Joint Tenancy, on the death of one owner, the deceased's interest ______.\nA) passes by his Will  B) passes to the surviving joint tenant (right of survivorship)  C) goes to the government  D) is divided among all legal heirs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Joint tenants have a **right of survivorship**: on death, the deceased's interest passes to the surviving joint tenant. A) is wrong — a joint tenant generally cannot leave the interest by Will (only if he is the sole survivor). C) and D) are not how joint tenancy works.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a Tenancy-in-Common, when a tenant dies, his share passes ______.\nA) automatically to the other tenants  B) to the state  C) by his Will or by succession law  D) to the eldest surviving tenant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A tenancy-in-common has **no right of survivorship**; the deceased's share passes per his **Will or the Succession Act**. A) describes joint tenancy, not tenancy-in-common. B) and D) are incorrect — the share follows the deceased's testamentary/succession wishes.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The Indian Trust Act that governs trusts in India was enacted in ______.\nA) 1882  B) 1925  C) 1956  D) 2016\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Indian Trust Act, 1882** is the governing law for trusts in India. B) 1925 is the Indian Succession Act. C) 1956 is the Hindu Succession Act. D) 2016 is the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Under the Indian Trust Act, the person who reposes or declares the confidence (creates the trust) is the ______.\nA) trustee  B) beneficiary  C) author/settlor  D) executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The person who reposes/declares the confidence is the **author or settlor**. A) The **trustee** accepts the confidence and manages the property. B) The **beneficiary** is the one for whose benefit the confidence is accepted. D) The **executor** is a Will concept, not a trust party.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** How many parties are essentially required to form a trust?\nA) Two  B) Three (author/settlor, trustee, beneficiary)  C) Four  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A trust has **three parties**: the author/settlor, the trustee and the beneficiary (plus trust property and a specific object as elements). A) Two is too few. C) Four adds a non-existent party. D) \"No limit\" is wrong — three distinct roles are required (though the settlor may also be a trustee or beneficiary).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr. Rao wants to ensure his minor son with special needs is financially cared for and that the arrangement starts working immediately and stays private, avoiding probate. Which tool best fits?\nA) A family trust  B) A simple nomination  C) A joint bank account  D) A succession certificate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **family trust** is effective as soon as created, stays private, avoids probate, and is ideal for the welfare of dependents including those with special needs. B) A nomination only simplifies payment on death and is overridden by succession law. C) A joint account can be superseded by heirs' claims and does not provide structured caregiving. D) A succession certificate only applies after death where there is no Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client wants to disinherit a fraudulent family member and appoint a trusted executor, but wants to keep the document changeable during his lifetime. Which tool fits best?\nA) An irrevocable trust  B) A gift deed  C) A Will  D) A power of attorney\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **Will** lets the testator take away the rights of a disobedient/fraudulent member, appoint a faithful executor, and remains revocable/changeable during his lifetime. A) An irrevocable trust cannot easily be changed and the settlor loses control. B) A gift takes effect immediately and is irrevocable — not for post-death disinheritance. D) A POA only authorises someone to act during life and dies with the principal.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A wealthy individual wants to shield assets from potential future creditors so they are not legally owned by him. Which is the most suitable, even though he must give up control?\nA) Revocable trust  B) Joint holding  C) Nomination  D) Asset Protection Trust (irrevocable)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An **Asset Protection Trust (APT)**, which is irrevocable, removes legal ownership from the settlor (he becomes only a beneficiary holding equitable interest), shielding assets from creditors; its drawback is that it is irrevocable and the settlor gives up control. A) A revocable trust does NOT protect assets because they can be withdrawn anytime. B) Joint holding gives no creditor shield. C) Nomination does not protect against creditors.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Grandparents want to transfer an immovable property to their grandchild immediately during their lifetime, tax-free within blood relatives, accepting that stamp duty and registration will apply. Which tool?\nA) Will  B) Gift deed  C) Nomination  D) Family settlement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **gift deed** transfers immovable property immediately, is tax-free among blood relatives, and reduces litigation since it is registered — though stamp duty applies and it is irrevocable. A) A Will only takes effect on death, defeating \"immediately.\" C) Nomination just names who collects money on death. D) A family settlement is for resolving disputes/rival claims, not a straightforward gift.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a Will from a family trust?\nA) Both take effect only on death  B) Neither can be made private  C) A Will is effective only on death, while a trust is effective as soon as created  D) A trust always passes through probate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **Will is effective only on death**, whereas a **trust is effective as soon as it is created**. A) is wrong — a trust does not wait for death. B) is wrong — a trust stays private while a Will can become public record. D) is wrong — a trust does NOT pass through probate; a Will may.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A revocable trust is best described as ______.\nA) a strong asset-protection tool  B) an alternative to a Will, taxed in the settlor's hands  C) a trust that cannot be modified  D) a charitable trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **revocable trust** lets the settlor retain control and modify/terminate it; it is considered an **alternative to a Will**, does NOT protect assets (they can be withdrawn), and its income is taxed in the **settlor's** hands. A) is wrong — it does not protect assets. C) describes an irrevocable trust. D) is unrelated to revocability.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Under which condition can a trust be revoked, per the Indian Trust Act?\nA) Whenever any one beneficiary wishes  B) When created to pay the settlor's debts to creditors  C) Only by court order in all cases  D) Never, once created\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A trust can be revoked if it was **specifically created by the settlor for payment of his debts to creditors** (also: with the consent of competent beneficiaries, or where a non-testamentary/verbal trust reserved revocation power). A) is wrong — it needs consent of *all competent* beneficiaries, not any one. C) and D) are too absolute.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A discretionary private trust (shares of beneficiaries not determinate) is generally taxed at ______.\nA) the slab rate of each beneficiary  B) a flat 10%  C) the Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR), i.e., 30%  D) zero, being a pass-through\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Where beneficiaries' shares are not determinate/unknown (a discretionary trust under Section 164(1)), the income is taxed at the **Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) = 30%**, subject to exceptions. A) Slab rates apply to *determinate* trusts. B) 10% is invented. D) The pass-through benefit applies to determinate trusts where the *beneficiary's* income is taxed, not the trust at zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which trust has beneficiaries clearly specified with ascertained shares, where trustees have no discretion over distribution?\nA) Discretionary trust  B) Public trust  C) Hybrid trust  D) Determinate (specific) trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **determinate (specific) trust** clearly specifies beneficiaries and their ascertained shares; trustees must distribute per the fixed ratio in the deed. A) A discretionary trust gives trustees full discretion. B) A public trust is for the general public/charity. C) A hybrid trust mixes fixed + discretionary features.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A hybrid trust is one in which the trustee ______.\nA) has no duties at all  B) pays a fixed amount as set by the settlor and has discretion over the rest  C) must give everything to charity  D) cannot distribute any income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **hybrid trust** has features of both determinate and discretionary trusts: the trustee pays a **fixed amount** as provisioned by the settlor and exercises **discretion** over the remaining property. A) describes a simple trust. C) and D) are not features of a hybrid trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An oral (unwritten) trust can be validly declared only for ______ property.\nA) movable  B) immovable  C) both movable and immovable  D) only agricultural\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A trust can be declared **orally only where movable property** is settled (by transferring possession with a direction to hold under trust). For **immovable** property a written trust deed is required. So B) and C) are wrong, and D) is not a recognised distinction in the text.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Section 47(iii) of the Income Tax Act exempts from capital gains tax any transfer of capital assets under a/an ______.\nA) revocable trust  B) irrevocable trust  C) joint holding  D) nomination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section **47(iii)** exempts the transferor from capital gains tax for a transfer under an **irrevocable trust**. A) Transfers to a **revocable** trust ARE subject to capital gains tax. C) and D) are not covered by Section 47(iii).\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Settling immovable property into a trust during the settlor's lifetime via a registered deed ______, whereas settling it through a Will ______.\nA) attracts no stamp duty; attracts stamp duty  B) attracts stamp duty; does not attract stamp duty  C) is illegal; is legal  D) both attract GST\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Settling immovable property in trust **during the settlor's lifetime** through a registered document **attracts stamp duty** (Article 58, Central Stamp Act); settlement **through a Will** does **NOT** attract stamp duty. A) reverses this. C) and D) are incorrect — neither is illegal and GST is not the relevant levy.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Why is a private trust described as a \"pass-through\" (flow-through) entity for tax?\nA) Because it pays the highest tax itself  B) Because it never files returns  C) Because the income is treated as the beneficiary's income, taxed in their hands  D) Because it is a separate legal person\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In a **pass-through entity**, income \"flows through\" so it is treated as the **beneficiary's income**; receipts by the trustee on behalf of the beneficiary are not taxable, and the trustee pays tax only in a representative capacity. A) is wrong. B) is wrong — a trust does file returns. D) is wrong — a private trust has **no separate legal identity**.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A POA is being set up so that one person can act broadly for the principal across many transactions. This is a ______.\nA) Special POA  B) Special POA for registration  C) General POA  D) Durable medical POA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **General POA** confers broad authority to act generally or in more than one transaction. A) A **Special POA** is narrow — limited to a specific transaction. B) A **Special POA for registration** authorises presenting a document for registration. D) \"Durable medical POA\" is not a category named in the chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Under a Special POA, the authority of the agent ______.\nA) is broad and ongoing  B) expires on completion of the specific transaction  C) never expires  D) passes to the agent's heirs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **Special POA**, authority is narrow and **expires on the completion of the transaction** for which it was granted. A) describes a General POA. C) is wrong — a POA can be revoked and ends on completion/death. D) is wrong — POA authority does not pass to the agent's heirs.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A POA is NOT compulsorily registrable because ______.\nA) it transfers ownership of immovable property  B) it is always oral  C) it merely authorises the donee to act, without creating/transferring any interest in property  D) stamp duty replaces registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A POA only authorises the donee to act on the donor's behalf and **does not create, declare, assign, limit or extinguish any interest in immovable property**, so it is not compulsorily registrable — *unless* it creates an interest/charge in immovable property. A) is the opposite of the truth. B) is wrong — POAs are written instruments. D) Stamp duty is separate and does apply, but it is not a substitute for registration.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Who among the following is authorised under Section 85 of the Indian Evidence Act to authenticate a POA?\nA) A chartered accountant  B) A Notary Public  C) A bank manager  D) A stockbroker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 85 authorises a **Notary Public** (also a court/judge/magistrate, an Indian Consul/Vice-Consul, or a Central Government representative) to authenticate a POA. A), C) and D) are not among the listed authorities.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A POA is revoked under Section 201 of the Indian Contract Act when ______.\nA) the principal goes on a long holiday  B) either the principal or agent is declared insolvent  C) the agent gets married  D) one year passes automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A POA is revoked when, among other things, **either the principal or the agent is declared insolvent** (also: principal revokes, holder renounces, business completed, or death of either party). A), C) and D) are not listed grounds — there is no automatic one-year expiry, and holidays/marriage of the agent do not revoke it.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Under Section 202, if the agent has an interest in the agency, the POA ______.\nA) can be revoked anytime by the principal alone  B) cannot be revoked without the agent's consent  C) is automatically void  D) must be registered\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 202 provides that where the **agent has an interest in the agency**, the POA **cannot be revoked without the agent's consent**. A) is the general rule but it is overridden here. C) and D) are incorrect — an interest-coupled agency is not void and registration is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A natural guardianship that requires no legal provision because it arises by birth lasts until the child reaches ______.\nA) 16 years  B) 18 years  C) 21 years  D) 25 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Parents are the **natural guardians** of their child until the child reaches the age of legal maturity, i.e., **18 years**. The other ages are distractors; 18 is the age of majority used here.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A guardian appointed under the provisions of a Will (mostly for minor children) is a ______ guardian.\nA) natural  B) court-appointed  C) testamentary  D) de facto\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **testamentary guardian** is appointed under a Will, mostly for minor children, and manages the child's affairs within the limits set in the Will. A) Natural guardianship arises by birth. B) Court-appointed guardians are appointed by courts for those with no living parents/incapacitated persons. D) \"De facto\" is not one of the three named types.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A \"Guardian of the Estate\" is responsible for ______.\nA) custody and physical care of the person  B) only medical decisions  C) only educational decisions  D) managing the financial affairs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **Guardian of the Estate** manages the **financial affairs** (bank accounts, investments, loans/concessions). A) describes a **Guardian of the Person** (custody and care). B) and C) are too narrow — financial management is the defining role of the estate guardian.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is an advantage of writing a Will, as listed in the chapter?\nA) It avoids all taxes permanently  B) It prevents confusion among family members about disposing of property  C) It cannot be challenged  D) It removes the need for any executor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A Will ensures there is **no confusion among family** about how to dispose of property after death; it is also personal, handles special situations, can disinherit fraudulent members, offers tax advantages, and lets one appoint an executor. A) is overstated — it offers tax advantages in many situations, not \"all taxes permanently.\" C) is wrong — Wills can be challenged. D) is the opposite — a Will appoints an executor.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Probate is granted only to the executor appointed in the Will; if there is no executor, the court instead issues ______.\nA) letters of administration  B) a succession certificate  C) a legal heir certificate  D) a gift deed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — If there is no executor (or he is incapable/refuses/has died before proving the Will), the court issues **letters of administration**. B) A succession certificate applies when there is *no Will*. C) A legal heir certificate (Tehsildar) identifies heirs. D) A gift deed is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which family business ownership model passes ownership down to all descendants, whether or not they work in the company, and is described as the most sought-after?\nA) Owner-Operator  B) Partnership  C) Distributed  D) Public\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In the **Distributed** model, ownership passes to all descendants regardless of involvement and is the most sought-after model. A) **Owner-Operator** keeps control with one person/couple. B) **Partnership** lets only leaders own it. D) **Public** has some shares publicly traded, run by professional managers.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which valuation method for a family business is also known as the discounted cash flow method?\nA) Net Asset Value  B) Market Approach  C) Capitalizing of Earnings  D) Projected Earnings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **Projected Earnings** method, valuing the business on anticipated future earnings, is also known as the **discounted cash flow** method. C) **Capitalizing of Earnings** uses current cash flow available. B) **Market Approach** compares similar businesses. A) **Net Asset Value** uses the value of assets like real estate/machinery.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Asset protection through a Limited Liability Partnership (LLP) works because ______.\nA) owners are always personally liable for all debts  B) the entity is protected by law and individual owners are not held liable for the entity's debt  C) LLPs cannot be sued  D) creditors are barred from courts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Entities such as private limited companies/LLPs are protected by law and **individual owners are not held liable for the entity's debt**, shielding personal assets — except the individual can still be held accountable for fraudulent transfers. A) is the opposite. C) and D) are incorrect — LLPs can be sued and creditors can use courts.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** When property is received through a Will or inheritance, the beneficiary ______.\nA) pays gift tax immediately  B) must re-register the property to gain ownership  C) pays capital gains on receipt  D) has no tax implication on receipt, but must apply for mutation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — There are **no tax implications on the beneficiary** when property is received via Will/inheritance, and re-registration is not required, but **mutation** must be applied with local civil authorities. A) and C) are wrong — no gift or capital gains tax on receipt (capital gains apply only if the inheritor later *sells*). B) is wrong — fresh registration is not required.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A client wants reciprocal benefits with his spouse so each leaves their property to the other, each being both testator and legatee. Which tool fits?\nA) Joint Will  B) Mutual Will  C) Concurrent Will  D) Holograph Will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **mutual Will** is exactly this: two testators confer reciprocal benefits, each acting as both testator and legatee. A) A joint Will is treated as two separate wills and need not be reciprocal. C) Concurrent Wills handle property in different countries. D) A holograph Will is simply one in the testator's handwriting.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which of the following is a stated disadvantage of a trust?\nA) It guarantees zero tax  B) It always avoids stamp duty  C) The settlor loses control over the properties transferred to the trust  D) Trustees have unlimited powers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A disadvantage is that the **settlor loses control** over properties transferred to/gifted to the trust; trusts can also be complex, expensive, and attract high stamp duty on immovable property. A) is wrong — trusts are a tax-planning tool, not a zero-tax guarantee. B) is wrong — immovable property transfers can attract high stamp duty. D) is wrong — trustees' powers are *restricted* by the deed.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A revocable transfer of an income-producing asset to a trust results in the income being taxed in the hands of the ______ (Section 61).\nA) trustee personally  B) transferor (settlor)  C) beneficiary always  D) government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where income arises from a **revocable transfer** of an asset, that income is taxed in the hands of the **transferor (settlor)** under Section 61. A) The trustee is taxed only in a representative capacity (in determinate trusts), not personally here. C) is wrong for a revocable transfer. D) is incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A minor child's income suffering from a disability specified under Section 80U ______.\nA) is always clubbed with the parent's income  B) is exempt from tax entirely  C) is NOT clubbed with the parent's/guardian's income  D) is taxed at MMR\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 64, a minor's income is normally clubbed with the parent's, but income of a minor child suffering from a **Section 80U disability is NOT clubbed** — so transferring higher-taxed assets to such a minor's trust can reduce the parent's liability. A) is the general rule, but the exception applies here. B) is overstated (not blanket exempt). D) MMR is unrelated to clubbing.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** An offshore trust that is irrevocable and has both resident and non-resident beneficiaries is taxed such that ______.\nA) the whole trust is taxed in India  B) only the Indian beneficiary is taxed on what they receive  C) it is entirely tax-free  D) the trustee is taxed on all global income in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For an irrevocable offshore trust with mixed residents, **only the Indian beneficiary is taxed on the distribution they receive**, and the trustee has no representative role in India. A) and D) are wrong — India does not tax the entire trust/global income here. C) is wrong — the Indian beneficiary is taxed on receipts.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client (an NRI) wants someone in India to handle his banking and property while he stays abroad. The POA executed abroad must be ______.\nA) registered with SEBI  B) authenticated/attested by the Indian Embassy/Consulate and notarized where executed  C) translated into Hindi only  D) approved by RBI before use\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A POA executed outside India must be **authenticated/attested by the Indian Embassy/Consulate and notarized where executed**; it then remains valid even after the person returns to India. A) SEBI registration is irrelevant. C) Translation into Hindi is not a stated requirement. D) RBI pre-approval is not required.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario & Numerical (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Trust ABC reports total income of ₹1,00,000: interest income ₹50,000 and dividends ₹50,000. Trustee fees are ₹20,000, realized capital gains are ₹20,000, and it gets an exemption of ₹20,000. What is the trust's **taxable income**?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Taxable Income = interest + dividends + capital gains − fees − exemptions. Plugging in: ₹50,000 + ₹50,000 + ₹20,000 − ₹20,000 − ₹20,000 = **₹80,000**. Note capital gains are ADDED in the taxable-income formula. A) ₹1,00,000 ignores the gains, fees and exemption netting. B) ₹60,000 wrongly subtracts the gains. C) ₹1,20,000 forgets to subtract fees/exemptions.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using the same Trust ABC figures (taxable income ₹80,000, capital gains ₹20,000, exemption ₹20,000), what is the **Distributable Net Income (DNI)**?\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — DNI = Taxable Income − Capital Gains + Tax Exemptions = ₹80,000 − ₹20,000 + ₹20,000 = **₹80,000**. (DNI removes capital gains and adds back exemptions; its purpose is to avoid double taxation.) A) and B) misapply the signs. D) double-counts. Here the gain and exemption are equal, so DNI happens to equal taxable income.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** In the DNI formula, if a trust has a capital **loss** instead of a capital gain, the loss is ______.\nA) ignored entirely  B) multiplied by the tax rate  C) subtracted twice  D) added in place of the capital gains term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The chapter states that if there is a capital loss, it is **added** to the formula, replacing the capital gains term (DNI = Taxable Income + Capital Loss + Exemptions). A) is wrong — the loss is not ignored. B) and C) invent operations not in the formula. (Note: in the *taxable income* formula, capital losses are subtracted — the treatment differs between the two formulas.)\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Rajesh creates a determinate trust for his father and mother (equal beneficial interest). The trust's net annual value is ₹5,40,000, shared equally. Father separately receives a monthly pension of ₹40,000. What is the father's gross pension (salary) income for the year?\nA) ₹2,70,000  B) ₹4,80,000  C) ₹5,40,000  D) ₹7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Pension is ₹40,000 × 12 months = **₹4,80,000**. A) ₹2,70,000 is the father's *half-share of the trust* (₹5,40,000 ÷ 2), not pension. C) ₹5,40,000 is the whole trust value. D) ₹7,50,000 is the father's *total* income (pension + half-share), computed in the next step.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Continuing Q74: the father's total income = his ₹2,70,000 trust half-share + ₹4,80,000 pension = ₹7,50,000. After the New Tax Regime standard deduction of ₹75,000, his assessable taxable income is ______.\nA) ₹6,75,000  B) ₹7,50,000  C) ₹5,40,000  D) ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total income ₹7,50,000 − standard deduction ₹75,000 = **₹6,75,000**. B) ₹7,50,000 forgets the standard deduction. C) ₹5,40,000 is the full trust value. D) ₹6,50,000 is the *mother's* assessable income (computed separately).\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Continuing the Rajesh case: the trustee, as representative assessee, is taxed at 5% on the slab of the father's income above his ₹4.8 lakh pension up to ₹6.75 lakh. What is the father's tax (before cess)?\nA) ₹9,750  B) ₹13,500  C) ₹23,250  D) ₹24,180\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The portion taxed at 5% is the trust income that pushes income from ₹4.8 lakh up to ₹6.75 lakh; per the workbook this yields **₹9,750**. B) ₹13,500 is the *mother's* tax. C) ₹23,250 is the *combined* tax of both before cess. D) ₹24,180 is the total tax including 4% cess.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Continuing the Rajesh case: the mother's only other income is rented property net annual value ₹3,80,000. Her assessable income = half-share ₹2,70,000 + ₹3,80,000 = ₹6,50,000, taxed at 5% on the band above ₹3.8 lakh up to ₹6.5 lakh, giving ₹13,500. What is the **total tax (incl. 4% cess)** payable by the trustee as representative assessee?\nA) ₹24,180  B) ₹23,250  C) ₹58,485  D) ₹62,343\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Father's tax ₹9,750 + mother's tax ₹13,500 = ₹23,250. Cess at 4% = 0.04 × ₹23,250 = ₹930. Total = ₹23,250 + ₹930 = **₹24,180**. B) ₹23,250 omits the cess. C) and D) are distractor figures from the original options that do not match the worked calculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** In the Rajesh trust case, the tax is levied on the trustee in a \"representative\" capacity. This is possible because the trust is ______.\nA) discretionary, taxed at MMR  B) determinate, with each parent's share clearly ascertained (equal interest)  C) a public charitable trust  D) an offshore trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the parents have **equal, clearly ascertained beneficial interest**, it is a **determinate trust**, and under Section 161(1) the trustee is taxed as a representative assessee at the beneficiaries' applicable slab rates. A) A discretionary trust would be taxed at MMR (30%), not at slab. C) and D) do not match the facts of a private family trust for parents.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Mr. PQR, a Hindu, dies leaving a Will. When his Will goes for probate, who can be called/notified to file objections?\nA) Only his surviving brothers  B) Only his surviving sisters  C) Surviving brothers, sisters, and the spouses/children of deceased brothers/sisters  D) Only the named legatees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — On filing probate proceedings, the court issues notices to **all legal heirs** — including surviving brothers and sisters and the **spouses and children of deceased brothers/sisters** — to file objections. If they do not appear, they are presumed to have no objection. A), B) and D) are each too narrow — all legal heirs are notified, not just one class.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A settlor wants to retain control over assets and over distribution of income/capital gains from the trust. Which structure achieves this, and what is the tax consequence?\nA) Irrevocable trust; taxed in beneficiary's hands  B) Public trust; fully exempt  C) Discretionary trust; taxed in beneficiary's hands  D) Revocable trust; income taxed in the settlor's hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **revocable trust** lets the settlor keep control over assets and distributions; because assets are not considered \"given away,\" the **income is taxed in the settlor's hands**. A) An irrevocable trust means giving up control. B) A public trust is for charity/religion, not personal control. C) A discretionary trust gives discretion to trustees and is taxed at MMR, not the settlor.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A business owner wants to transfer business interest into a vehicle that generates a fixed income stream for years, keeps control with the family during the term, and lets the business survive across generations or pass to beneficiaries after a defined term. Which tool?\nA) Nomination  B) Joint holding  C) Creation of a trust  D) Succession certificate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Creating a trust** lets business interest generate a fixed income stream, keeps control with the family during the trust term, and ensures the business survives across generations or transfers to beneficiaries after a defined term. A) and B) do not provide this structured, multi-generational framework. D) A succession certificate only applies after death where there is no Will.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A debtor faces an aggressive government creditor with strong powers to seize assets. According to the asset-protection factors, this means ______.\nA) less protection is needed  B) no protection is possible  C) only the debtor's spouse is at risk  D) stronger asset protection strategies are required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **identity of the creditor** matters: a powerful organisation such as government has more power to seize assets than private lenders, so an aggressive creditor requires **stronger asset protection strategies**. A) is the opposite. B) is too absolute — protection strategies still exist. C) misstates the factor (that is the *debtor identity* factor, not creditor identity).\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which asset is specifically mentioned as one that creditors cannot attach, illustrating the \"nature of the asset\" factor?\nA) A demat account  B) A PPF account  C) A rented commercial shop  D) Listed equity shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **PPF (Public Provident Fund) account** of an individual cannot be attached by any creditor claim — an example of how the nature of the asset affects protection (also, life insurance protects a home from seizure on default). A), C) and D) are not given this protected status in the chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016, after a financial creditor furnishes information, within how many days must the adjudicating authority ascertain the default?\nA) 7 days  B) 14 days  C) 30 days  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the IBC, the adjudicating authority must **ascertain the default within 14 days** of the information being furnished; if default occurred the application is admitted, and admission is communicated to financial creditors within **7 days**. So A) 7 days is the *communication* timeline, not the ascertainment one. C) and D) are not IBC timelines.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A trust's income includes profits and gains of a business. By default this entire income is taxed at ______, unless a specific Will-based exception applies.\nA) slab rates  B) 5%  C) zero  D) the Maximum Marginal Rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under Section 161(1A), if any part of a trust's income includes business profits/gains, the **entire** trust income is taxed at the **Maximum Marginal Rate** — unless the profits are receivable under a trust declared by Will, exclusively for a dependent relative's support, and it is the only such trust by that person (then slab rates apply). A) Slab is only the exception. B) and C) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A trust deed does not state exact beneficiary shares but provides that income is shared \"equally among all living family members,\" varying as members are born or die. How is such a trust treated?\nA) As discretionary, taxed at MMR  B) As a determinate trust, since the manner of computing shares is provided  C) As a public trust  D) As an invalid trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per court judgements cited, a trust is **determinate** even if the deed only provides the **manner of computing** each beneficiary's interest (e.g., equally among living family members, varying with births/deaths) rather than exact shares. A) MMR applies only where shares are genuinely indeterminate/unknown. C) and D) are incorrect — it is a valid private determinate trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A father-in-law transfers a house property to his daughter-in-law, which is then let out. In whose hands is the rent taxable?\nA) Son  B) Daughter-in-law  C) Mother-in-law  D) Father-in-law\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Because of clubbing provisions on transfers to a daughter-in-law without adequate consideration, the rental income is taxable in the hands of the **father-in-law** (the transferor). A) The son did not make the transfer. B) The daughter-in-law holds the property but the income is clubbed back to the transferor. C) The mother-in-law is not the transferor here.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A settlor wants to safeguard assets from creditors and beneficiaries' claims and pursue multiple objectives. The chapter suggests setting up a/an ______.\nA) revocable trust  B) joint holding  C) public charitable trust  D) irrevocable discretionary trust (or multiple trusts)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — To safeguard assets against creditors' and beneficiaries' claims and achieve various objectives, a settlor may set up an **irrevocable discretionary trust** (or multiple trusts). A) A revocable trust does not protect assets. B) Joint holding offers no creditor shield. C) A public charitable trust serves the public, not asset protection for the family.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A trust directs that income from property be accumulated for 25 years after the testator's death via his Will. What does the law say?\nA) Fully valid — accumulation can be unlimited  B) The direction is void to the extent it exceeds 18 years from the testator's death  C) Void entirely, no accumulation allowed  D) Valid only if approved by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A trust cannot be perpetual. If a Will directs accumulation beyond **18 years from the testator's death**, the direction is **void to the extent it exceeds 18 years**, and at the end of 18 years the property and income are disposed of (exception: public benefit trusts). A) is wrong — accumulation is capped. C) is too harsh — accumulation up to the limit is allowed. D) SEBI approval is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A revocable offshore trust where the settlor has powers to claim the assets: the income is ______.\nA) tax-free in India  B) treated as the settlor's income and taxable in his hands as if directly arisen to him  C) taxed only on the foreign trustee  D) taxed as an Association of Persons\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **revocable offshore trust** where the settlor can claim the assets, any income derived by the trust is **treated as the settlor's income and taxed in his hands** as if it arose directly to him. A) is wrong — it is taxable. C) is wrong — the foreign trustee is not the taxpayer here. D) AOP treatment applies where an offshore trust has *partial control in India*, a different scenario.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client wants the simplest estate plan: a single document, the \"most basic legal instrument of all estate plans.\" Which is it?\nA) Trust deed  B) Power of Attorney  C) Will  D) Family settlement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Will** is described as the most basic legal instrument of all estate plans — easy to write and revocable anytime during life. A) A trust deed is more complex/expensive. B) A POA only authorises an agent to act during life. D) A family settlement resolves disputes, not a basic plan for everyone.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A person dies without making any valid Will (intestate). Which document do the legal heirs need to lay claim to the deceased's debts and securities?\nA) Probate  B) Codicil  C) Succession certificate  D) Letters testamentary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When a person dies **intestate** (no Will), heirs need a **succession certificate** from a civil court to claim debts and securities. A) Probate is issued only when there IS a Will. B) A codicil amends a Will (which does not exist here). D) \"Letters testamentary\" is not the document used in intestacy in this chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An executor's very first responsibility, before disbursing assets, is to ______.\nA) distribute the legacy immediately  B) pay funeral/burial expenses and obtain the death certificate  C) sell all immovable property  D) appoint a new testator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The executor's first responsibility is to **pay funeral and burial expenses and obtain the death certificate**, which is needed to manage financial affairs; only then can assets be located, debts/taxes paid, and the estate disbursed. A) is wrong — distribution comes after debts/dues are settled (lenders have first right). C) Selling property is only done if needed to pay dues. D) is nonsensical — a testator is the deceased.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A minor is named as executor in a Will. What happens?\nA) The Will becomes invalid  B) Probate is granted to the minor immediately  C) Probate cannot be granted to the minor until majority; the legal guardian can obtain letters of administration  D) The minor's parents automatically become executors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A minor can be named executor, but **probate cannot be granted until he attains majority**; meanwhile the minor's **legal guardian can obtain letters of administration** by applying to the court. A) The Will is not invalidated. B) is wrong — no probate to a minor. D) is wrong — guardianship/letters of administration is the mechanism, not automatic parental executorship.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A \"public-cum-private\" trust applies part of its income for public purposes and part to private persons. How is the private portion treated for tax?\nA) Fully exempt like a charitable trust  B) Taxed only on the trustee at 5%  C) Never taxed in India  D) Assessable as a private trust and not exempt from income tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In a **public-cum-private trust**, the portion of income going to private persons is **assessable as a private trust and not exempt** from income tax. A) is wrong — only the public portion may get charitable exemptions. B) and C) are incorrect — the private portion is taxable per private-trust rules.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Two spouses purchase a property as joint tenants. One spouse wants to leave his share to a child by Will. Can he?\nA) Yes, joint tenancy allows leaving a share by Will  B) No — under right of survivorship the share passes to the surviving tenant, not by Will (unless he is the sole survivor)  C) Only with court approval  D) Only if the property is movable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In **joint tenancy**, the **right of survivorship** means the deceased's interest passes to the surviving joint tenant — he cannot leave it by Will, except where he is the sole survivor. A) is wrong for joint tenancy (it would be true for tenancy-in-common). C) and D) invent conditions not stated.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A son receives ₹5 lakh as a gift from his father, then invests it and earns ₹40,000 interest. What is the tax position?\nA) Both the ₹5 lakh and the ₹40,000 are taxed in the son's hands  B) Both are fully exempt  C) The father is taxed on the ₹40,000  D) The ₹5 lakh gift is exempt (relative), but the ₹40,000 income is taxable in the son's hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A gift from a **relative** (father) is **exempt** from tax in the donee's hands, but **any income later earned from the gift is taxable in the donee's (son's) hands**. A) is wrong — the gift itself is exempt. B) is wrong — the income is taxable. C) is wrong — the income belongs to and is taxed on the son (no clubbing for a major child's gift income).\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A settlor transfers immovable property to a **revocable** trust during his lifetime. Regarding capital gains tax, ______.\nA) it is exempt under Section 47(iii)  B) the transfer IS subject to capital gains tax  C) it is exempt because it is testamentary  D) capital gains never apply to trusts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 47(iii) exempts transfers to an **irrevocable** trust, but a transfer to a **revocable** trust **is subject to capital gains tax**. A) applies only to irrevocable trusts. C) is wrong — this is a lifetime transfer, not testamentary (on death). D) is overstated — capital gains can apply to revocable-trust transfers.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A businessman who is also the guarantor of his company's debt faces a lawsuit against the company. What is the implication for his personal assets?\nA) Personal assets are always fully protected  B) As guarantor, he is liable to asset seizure; any clause obliging him to repay the organisation's debt helps creditors seize personal assets  C) Only the company's assets can ever be touched  D) Guarantors are exempt by law\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the **identity of the debtor** factor, a business **guarantor** — the individual who guarantees repayment — is **liable to asset seizure** if there is a lawsuit against the entity, and any clause obliging him to repay the organisation's debt helps creditors seize his personal assets. A), C) and D) are wrong — guarantee obligations pierce the protection that an entity structure would otherwise offer.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A trust is created by a non-testamentary document (or verbally) and the settlor wants the ability to revoke it later. For this to be possible, the chapter says ______.\nA) the power to revoke must be reserved to the settlor  B) it is automatically revocable in all cases  C) it can never be revoked  D) only a court can revoke it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When a trust is created by a **non-testamentary document or verbally**, there **can be power reserved to the settlor for revocation**. B) is wrong — revocability is not automatic; for written trusts, Section 77/78 requires an express revocation clause. C) is too absolute. D) is wrong — revocation here flows from a reserved power, not solely a court.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mr. Sharma (a Hindu, aged 62, resident in Pune) is doing his estate planning. He owns: a flat in Mumbai, a portfolio of mutual funds and shares (with nominations registered), and a profitable family manufacturing business. He has a wife, a major son who runs the business, and a minor daughter with a Section 80U-certified disability. He wants to (i) leave clear instructions for after his death, (ii) provide a structured, private, lifetime income arrangement for his disabled daughter that avoids probate, (iii) understand whether his mutual-fund nominations will override his Will, (iv) transfer the Mumbai flat to his son tax-efficiently, and (v) understand the tax treatment of the income flowing to his daughter from the arrangement he sets up.\n\n**Q-CS1.** What is the most basic instrument Mr. Sharma should write to give clear post-death instructions, and will his Will need probate given the Mumbai flat?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\nHe should write a **Will** — the most basic legal instrument of all estate plans, effective only on his death and revocable anytime during his life. Because **probate is mandatory when the immovable property is situated in Mumbai** (one of the three Presidency towns, along with Kolkata and Chennai), his Will **will need probate** for the Mumbai flat. The court will issue notices to legal heirs for objections, advertise in a national newspaper, and the executor must prove his death, valid execution and that it is the last Will. Elsewhere probate would have been optional.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS2.** Which tool best meets his goal of a structured, private, lifetime income arrangement for his disabled daughter that avoids probate — and why is it better than a nomination or a Will alone?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\nA **private (family) trust** is the best fit. Unlike a Will, a trust is **effective as soon as created** (not only on death), **stays private** (a Will can become public record), and **avoids probate** (no court oversees a trust). It is also specifically suited to the **welfare of dependents, including a member with special needs**. A **nomination** would not work because its purpose is merely to simplify payment on death — it does not provide a structured income arrangement and is generally overridden by succession law. A Will alone takes effect only on death and may go through probate, delaying support. Mr. Sharma can name himself or a trusted trustee, define his daughter as beneficiary, and set distribution terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS3.** Mr. Sharma has registered nominees on his mutual funds but his Will leaves those funds to his wife. If there is a dispute, who prevails — the nominee or the legatee under the Will?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\nThe **Will supersedes the nomination** for mutual funds. A nominee in most financial assets is treated only as a **trustee of the bequest** — not a legatee or beneficiary under the Indian Succession Act — and **succession rules (and the Will) prevail** in a dispute. The mutual fund can still validly pay the proceeds to the nominee (a valid discharge), but the onus is on the nominee to account to the rightful legatee; the wife (as legatee under the Will) would be entitled. The exceptions where a nominee gets absolute legal rights are assets under a separate Act — e.g., **Insurance Act** beneficial nominees and **EPF Act** nominees — which do not apply to ordinary mutual funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS4.** Mr. Sharma wants to transfer the Mumbai flat to his son during his lifetime, tax-efficiently. Compare gifting versus leaving it by Will, including stamp duty and capital gains.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Gifting** to the son (a blood relative) is highly tax-efficient: there is **no income-tax liability within blood relatives**, and the transfer is immediate, reducing future litigation. However, because it is immovable property, the gift deed must be **compulsorily registered and stamp duty applies** (rate varies by state — some states give concessional rates on gifts to close relatives), and a gift deed is **irrevocable**. Leaving it by **Will** means **no stamp duty** and no tax to the son on receipt (only mutation needs to be applied), but the transfer waits until death and the Will is more open to challenge/delay. For an *immediate* lifetime transfer, the **gift deed** is the efficient route, accepting the one-time stamp-duty cost; if Mr. Sharma prefers to keep the flat until death and avoid stamp duty, the Will is better. If the son later sells, capital gains apply using the previous owner's cost of acquisition.\n</details>\n\n**Q-CS5.** Mr. Sharma sets up a determinate trust giving his disabled daughter a clearly defined share. How is the income flowing to her taxed, and how does her Section 80U disability affect clubbing?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\nBecause the daughter's share is **clearly defined**, it is a **determinate trust**, and under **Section 161(1)** the **trustee is taxed as a representative assessee** at the **beneficiary's applicable slab rate** — the trust is a **pass-through** entity, so what is taxable is the beneficiary's income, not the trust as a separate person. Normally, a **minor's income is clubbed with the parent's** under Section 64. However, since the daughter has a **Section 80U-certified disability, her income is NOT clubbed** with her parent's/guardian's income. This means Mr. Sharma can transfer higher-taxed assets into the minor (disabled) daughter's trust to reduce the family's overall tax liability, with her income assessed in her own hands at her slab rate (not at the Maximum Marginal Rate, which would apply only to a discretionary trust).\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "16", "title": "Basics of Behavioural Finance", "module": "Behavioural Finance & Risk Profiling", "notes": "# Chapter 16: Basics of Behavioural Finance — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The difference between **Behavioural Finance** and **Standard Finance**\n- How people *actually* make decisions (**bounded rationality**, **prospect theory**)\n- The main **biases** — emotional vs cognitive — with everyday examples\n- **Fusion investing**\n- How behavioural finance explains **market anomalies, bubbles, and crashes**\n\n---\n\n## 16.1 Behavioural Finance vs Standard Finance\n\n> **Behavioural Finance** = the study of how **psychology** influences the behaviour of market participants (individuals and groups), and the effect on financial markets. It's part of **Behavioural Economics**.\n\nIt tries to explain the **gaps and market anomalies** that standard finance can't.\n\n**Standard finance** assumes investors are perfectly logical robots. Its key assumptions:\n- Investors are **rational**\n- Investors are **risk averse**\n- Investors are **self-interested utility maximizers** (always chase the best outcome)\n- Investors **update their beliefs** as new information arrives\n- Investors have **access to all information**\n\n**But in real life,** people behave very differently:\n- They hold **concentrated** portfolios instead of diversifying.\n- They swing between **greed and fear**.\n- They credit **luck to their own skill**.\n- They confuse a **good company** with a **good stock**.\n- They prefer **domestic companies** (**home bias**) because familiarity feels safer.\n\n**Analogy:** Standard finance is like assuming every driver obeys every traffic rule perfectly with full information. Behavioural finance studies how drivers *actually* behave — speeding when excited, freezing when scared, following the car ahead.\n\n| **Standard Finance** | **Behavioural Finance** |\n|---|---|\n| **Economics** at its core | **Psychology** at its core |\n| Investors rational, process info without bias; efficient markets, random price movement | **Biases** guide decisions; herd mentality drives sharp moves |\n| Decision-making is rule-driven & consistent | Decision-making is **inconsistent** (experience, recency, loss aversion) |\n| Risk-return trade-off is the foundation | **Loss aversion** is the basis — over-sensitive to losses |\n| Rational, detailed analysis | Ad-hoc, **thumb-rule** based |\n\n💡 Nobel laureates **Daniel Kahneman (2002)** and **Richard Thaler (2017)** brought behavioural finance to the forefront.\n\n---\n\n## 16.2 How do individuals make decisions?\n\nReal investors show **limits to rationality** — they don't act like the rational robots of standard theory.\n\n### 16.2.1 Bounded Rationality\n> **Bounded Rationality** = the mind's limitation where, lacking time or full information, people settle for a **\"satisficing\"** solution (satisfactory + sufficient) instead of the **optimal** one.\n\nPeople **\"satisfice\"** rather than optimize. **Important:** this is **NOT irrational** — it's rational decision-making *under boundary conditions* (limited time/info/ability).\n\n**Analogy:** Choosing lunch from a 100-item menu, you don't analyse all 100 dishes — you pick something that looks \"good enough\" quickly. That's satisficing.\n\nThe steps (per **Herbert Simon, 1978**):\n1. Process only the **manageable** information (not all of it)\n2. Apply **rules of thumb / quick approaches**\n3. Pick a **satisfactory and sufficient** (satisficing) solution\n\nBounded rationality sits **between** pure **rationality** (process all info → optimal) and **intuition** (pure experience).\n\n### 16.2.2 Prospect Theory\nIntroduced by **Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979)**. It describes how people choose between **risky alternatives** and how they value gains and losses.\n\n**Two phases:**\n1. **Framing phase** — prospects are framed (using heuristics for a quick preliminary analysis)\n2. **Evaluation phase** — the edited prospects are evaluated and the highest-perceived-value one is chosen\n\n**Three premises (memorise!):**\n- Choices are judged **relative to a reference point** (your current well-being), not in absolute terms.\n- People are **risk-averse about gains** (they grab gains early) but **risk-seeking about losses** (they hold losers longer, hoping to break even).\n- **Losses hurt more than equivalent gains feel good.**\n\n**Analogy:** Finding ₹500 feels nice; **losing** ₹500 feels much worse. The pain outweighs the pleasure even though the amount is the same.\n\n---\n\n## 16.3 Categorization of Biases\n\n> **Bias** = processing information based on personal experiences/preferences, leading to systematic errors/favouring.\n\nBiases fall into **two main categories**:\n- **Emotional biases** — based on **feelings/emotions**\n- **Cognitive errors** — based on **faulty reasoning/thinking**\n\n💡 You can't be fully unbiased, but **discipline and checklists** help mitigate biases.\n\n### 16.3.1 Emotional Biases\nThese arise spontaneously from deep-rooted personal experiences. They aren't always \"errors\" — often they're **protective** (choosing what feels comfortable/safe).\n\n| Emotional Bias | What it is | Everyday example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Loss Aversion** | People take chances to *avoid losses* but won't gamble with sure gains; losses are far more powerful than gains | An investor refuses to sell a falling stock (hoping to avoid \"booking a loss\") but quickly sells a rising one. **Disposition Effect** (Shefrin & Statman, 1985): holding **losers too long**, selling **winners too early** |\n| **Stereotyping** | Judging based on representative characteristics / general perception | Believing a **high-profile manager** automatically = a well-run company = a good investment |\n| **Overconfidence** | Unwarranted faith in one's own reasoning/abilities; most rate themselves \"above average\" | Trading excessively, concentrating the portfolio, confusing **a bull market with personal skill** (worsened by **self-attribution bias** — \"I'm a genius\"). Blurs skill vs luck |\n| **Endowment** | Valuing an asset more *just because you own it* | Refusing to sell **inherited stocks** even if they don't fit the strategy (combines valuing ownership + loss aversion) |\n| **Status Quo** | Doing nothing / sticking with the default to avoid regret | Never switching a poor mutual fund because changing \"feels risky.\" Linked to **Regret Aversion** |\n\n**Regret has two dimensions:**\n- **Error of commission** — regret over **actions you took**\n- **Error of omission** — regret over **actions you didn't take**\n\n💡 Regret from **commission** feels worse than from **omission**, so people prefer the status quo. A simple **\"nudge\"** (Richard Thaler) — like auto-enrolling employees into a pension plan — overcomes this and dramatically boosts enrolment.\n\n### 16.3.2 Cognitive Errors\n> **Cognitive errors** = statistical, information-processing, or memory errors causing deviation from rational behaviour. They come from **faulty thinking** (vs emotional biases from **feelings**).\n\nThese are **short-cuts** to avoid the pain of full analysis — and being **vigilant** reduces them.\n\n| Cognitive Error | What it is | Everyday example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mental Accounting** | Treating equal sums of money differently based on which mental \"bucket\" they're in (money isn't truly **fungible** to them) | Spending a **bonus or lottery win** recklessly but being careful with salary; keeping separate \"retirement,\" \"education,\" \"marriage\" buckets and over-managing each instead of optimizing the whole portfolio. (**Thaler, 1999**) |\n| **Framing** | Answering differently based on **how a question is phrased** | \"**95% lean**\" beef sounds better than \"**5% fat**\" — identical fact, different choice. Affects how investors fill **risk-tolerance questionnaires** |\n| **Anchoring** | Over-relying on an initial piece of information (the \"anchor\") | The **first price** mentioned in a negotiation anchors the whole deal; judging a stock's worth purely by its **past price** |\n| **Choice Paralysis** | Too many options (or too much info) → freezing and not deciding at all | Faced with 50 mutual funds, the investor keeps \"researching\" and never invests |\n\n> **Fungibility** = the fact that all money is interchangeable and has no labels. Thaler advised: treat all money **the same**, regardless of origin or use.\n\n💡 Distinction to remember: **emotional bias = from feelings (protective)**; **cognitive error = from faulty thinking/shortcuts (factually wrong, fixable with vigilance).**\n\n---\n\n## 16.4 Fusion Investing\n\n> **Fusion Investing** = combining **fundamental analysis** (traditional) with **behavioural finance** to build investment strategies.\n\n- **Fundamental view:** a stock's price = the discounted value of future cash flows; all info is reflected in price (long-term \"weighing machine\").\n- **Behavioural view:** short-term prices are volatile, driven by the collective behaviour of investors/traders (short-term \"voting machine\").\n\n**Benjamin Graham:** *\"In the short run the market is a voting machine, and in the long run it is a weighing machine.\"* Fusion investing integrates **both** the voting (momentum) and weighing (value) machines — combining the **value-growth** phenomenon and the **momentum effect**.\n\n**Three steps to pick fusion stocks:**\n1. **Step I** — Find stocks looking promising on **value** (P/E, P/BV, P/S).\n2. **Step II** — Filter those that are **fundamentally strong** (profitability, leverage, efficiency — e.g. **Piotroski Score**, 0–9, where 9 is best).\n3. **Step III** — From those, pick stocks showing **strong momentum** (price uptrend).\n\n⚠️ Momentum (Step III) is **short-term**, so it needs **frequent rebalancing** → higher **transaction costs** and **tax** to consider. Market data hasn't conclusively proven the strategy works.\n\n---\n\n## 16.5 Behavioural Finance Explains Market Anomalies\n\n> **Market anomaly** = a departure of a stock's price from its expected value (per CAPM/asset pricing models) that can't be explained by known/new information.\n\nAnomalies appear, disappear, and reappear randomly. They *should* vanish as people exploit them — but some persist.\n\n**How biases cause anomalies:**\n- **Overconfidence** → underestimating risk.\n- **Self-attribution** → taking credit for wins, blaming others for losses; following only confirming info → **over-trading**.\n- **Disposition effect** (hold losers, sell winners) → explains **price momentum**.\n\n---\n\n## 16.6 Behavioural Finance Explains Bubbles and Crashes\n\n**Charles Kindleberger** (*Manias, Panics and Crashes*, 1978): a **bubble** = a sharp, continuous rise in asset price, where the initial rise creates expectations of *further* rises, attracting new buyers (speculators chasing trading profit, not long-term value). *Psychology is as important as economics.*\n\n⚠️ Why don't smart **arbitrageurs** fix mispricing? They face a **synchronisation problem** (can't coordinate selling) and differing incentives to time the market — so bubbles **persist**.\n\n**Famous bubbles:** Dutch Tulip mania (1630s), South Sea Bubble (1720s), Tech bubble (2000s). Even **Isaac Newton** lost ~£20,000 in the South Sea Bubble, saying *\"I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people.\"*\n\n**Pattern:** Bubbles often start with a *justifiable* price rise (new product/disruption/productivity), changing future expectations. Early investors earn huge returns, attracting more; **cheap borrowing** fuels it; analysts invent **\"new age\" theories** to justify crazy prices.\n\n**Reasons bubbles form:**\n- **Liquidity** — cheap money chasing stocks lifts prices in a vicious cycle.\n- **Celebrity status** — media hype is self-fulfilling, disconnecting price from value.\n- **Momentum** — investors extrapolate trends → widespread **herd trading** overwhelms rational investors.\n- **Illusion of Control** — familiarity/involvement makes investors *believe* they can forecast and sell before everyone else.\n\n**Keith Redhead (2008)** — bubbles are more likely when:\n- A high proportion of **inexperienced traders**\n- High **uncertainty** about true value\n- Small chance of profit but **very high** potential profit\n- Purchases can be financed by **borrowing**\n- **Short selling is difficult**\n\n**Crashes** = the same processes **in reverse** — everyone rushes to sell at once (negative news, opinion change, liquidity crunch, return to fundamentals), and the jostling to exit first causes the crash.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Standard finance** = rational robots, economics core; **Behavioural finance** = real psychology, biases, herd behaviour.\n- **Bounded rationality** = \"satisficing\" (good enough) under limited time/info — rational, not irrational (Herbert Simon).\n- **Prospect theory** (Kahneman & Tversky): choices vs a **reference point**; **risk-averse on gains, risk-seeking on losses**; **losses hurt more than gains please**.\n- **Emotional biases** (from feelings): Loss Aversion (Disposition Effect), Stereotyping, Overconfidence (+ self-attribution), Endowment, Status Quo (+ regret aversion).\n- **Cognitive errors** (from faulty thinking): Mental Accounting, Framing, Anchoring, Choice Paralysis.\n- **Regret:** commission (action) hurts more than omission (inaction) → status quo. A **nudge** fixes it.\n- **Fusion investing** = value (weighing machine) + momentum (voting machine); steps: value → fundamentals → momentum.\n- Biases create **anomalies, bubbles, and crashes**; arbitrageurs can't always correct them (synchronisation problem).\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n\n| Term / Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Behavioural Finance** | Psychology's effect on market behaviour |\n| **Daniel Kahneman (2002)** | Nobel laureate; prospect theory |\n| **Richard Thaler (2017)** | Nobel laureate; mental accounting, nudge |\n| **Herbert Simon (1978)** | Bounded rationality |\n| **Kahneman & Tversky (1979)** | Prospect theory |\n| **Satisficing** | Satisfactory + sufficient (good-enough choice) |\n| **Disposition Effect** | Hold losers too long, sell winners too early (Shefrin & Statman, 1985) |\n| **Self-attribution bias** | Confuse skill with luck / a bull market |\n| **Fungibility** | All money is interchangeable, has no labels |\n| **Piotroski Score** | 0–9 score of financial strength (9 = best) |\n| **\"Voting vs weighing machine\"** | Benjamin Graham — short-term sentiment vs long-term value |\n| **Kindleberger (1978)** | *Manias, Panics and Crashes* — bubbles |\n| **Synchronisation problem** | Why arbitrageurs can't quickly pop bubbles |\n| **Emotional bias vs Cognitive error** | Feelings (protective) vs faulty thinking (fixable) |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 16: Basics of Behavioural Finance — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is Behavioural Finance?\n> **A:** The study of how **psychology** influences the behaviour of market participants (individuals and groups) and its effect on financial markets. It's a part of Behavioural Economics and explains gaps/anomalies that standard finance can't.\n\n**Q2.** What are the five key assumptions of Standard Finance?\n> **A:** Investors are (1) **rational**, (2) **risk averse**, (3) **self-interested utility maximizers**, (4) **update beliefs** with new information, and (5) have **access to all information**.\n\n**Q3.** Give three real-life behaviours that contradict standard finance assumptions.\n> **A:** Holding **concentrated** portfolios instead of diversifying; swinging between **greed and fear**; crediting **luck to skill**; confusing a good company with a good stock; **home bias** (preferring domestic firms).\n\n**Q4.** At its core, Standard Finance is based on ___, while Behavioural Finance is based on ___.\n> **A:** **Economics**; **Psychology**.\n\n**Q5.** Which two Nobel laureates are credited with bringing Behavioural Finance forward?\n> **A:** **Daniel Kahneman (2002)** and **Richard Thaler (2017)**.\n\n**Q6.** What is Bounded Rationality?\n> **A:** The mind's cognitive limitation where, lacking time/information, people choose a **satisficing** (satisfactory + sufficient) solution instead of the optimal one. It's rational decision-making under boundary conditions — **not irrational**.\n\n**Q7.** What does \"satisfice\" mean and who coined Bounded Rationality?\n> **A:** \"Satisfice\" = picking an option that's **satisfactory and sufficient** (good enough), not optimal. **Herbert Simon (1978)**.\n\n**Q8.** What are the three steps of decision-making under Bounded Rationality?\n> **A:** (1) Process only **manageable** information; (2) apply **rules of thumb / quick approaches**; (3) select a **satisficing** solution.\n\n**Q9.** Who introduced Prospect Theory and when?\n> **A:** **Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979)**.\n\n**Q10.** What are the two phases of choice in Prospect Theory?\n> **A:** (1) A **framing** phase (prospects are framed using heuristics) and (2) an **evaluation** phase (the edited prospect of highest perceived value is chosen).\n\n**Q11.** State the three premises of Prospect Theory.\n> **A:** (1) Choices are evaluated relative to a **reference point** (well-being); (2) people are **risk-averse about gains** but **risk-seeking about losses**; (3) **losses hurt more than gains feel good**.\n\n**Q12.** What are the two broad categories of biases?\n> **A:** **Emotional biases** (based on feelings) and **Cognitive errors** (based on faulty thinking/reasoning).\n\n**Q13.** What is Loss Aversion bias?\n> **A:** People take chances to **avoid losses** but won't gamble with sure gains; losses are far more powerful than equal gains.\n\n**Q14.** What is the Disposition Effect?\n> **A:** Proposed by Shefrin & Statman (1985): investors **hold losers too long** and **sell winners too early** — they dislike booking losses and are eager to lock in gains.\n\n**Q15.** What is Stereotyping bias?\n> **A:** Basing decisions on representative characteristics / general perceptions — e.g. assuming a **high-profile manager** means a well-managed company and good investment.\n\n**Q16.** What is Overconfidence bias, and what worsens it?\n> **A:** Unwarranted faith in one's own reasoning/abilities (most rate themselves above average). It's intensified by **self-attribution bias** (confusing a bull market / luck with personal skill) and leads to excessive trading and portfolio concentration.\n\n**Q17.** What is Endowment bias?\n> **A:** Valuing an asset more **just because you own it**. It combines valuing ownership + loss aversion — e.g. rarely selling **inherited stocks** even if they don't fit the strategy.\n\n**Q18.** What is Status Quo bias and what is it linked to?\n> **A:** Taking **no decision** / sticking with the default to avoid regret. It's closely linked to **Regret Aversion bias**.\n\n**Q19.** What are the two dimensions of regret, and which hurts more?\n> **A:** **Error of commission** (regret over actions taken) and **error of omission** (regret over actions not taken). **Commission** hurts more, so people prefer the status quo.\n\n**Q20.** How does a \"nudge\" relate to status quo bias?\n> **A:** Richard Thaler showed a simple nudge (e.g. **auto-enrolling** employees into a pension plan) overcomes status-quo inertia and dramatically improves enrolment.\n\n**Q21.** How do cognitive errors differ from emotional biases?\n> **A:** Cognitive errors come from **faulty thinking** (statistical/info-processing/memory errors) and can be factually wrong and reduced by vigilance; emotional biases come from **feelings** and are often protective.\n\n**Q22.** What is Mental Accounting?\n> **A:** Treating equal sums of money differently based on which mental \"bucket\" they're in — e.g. spending a **bonus/lottery** recklessly but salary carefully. Developed by **Thaler (1999)**.\n\n**Q23.** What is fungibility, and what did Thaler recommend about it?\n> **A:** **Fungibility** = all money is interchangeable and has no labels. Thaler recommended treating **all money the same**, regardless of origin or use.\n\n**Q24.** What is Framing bias? Give an example.\n> **A:** Answering differently based on **how a question is phrased** — e.g. \"95% lean\" beef vs \"5% fat\" beef (same fact, different choice). It affects risk-tolerance questionnaires.\n\n**Q25.** What is Anchoring bias?\n> **A:** Over-relying on an **initial piece of information** (the anchor) so all later info is judged in its light — e.g. the first price in a negotiation, or judging a stock by its past price.\n\n**Q26.** What is Choice Paralysis?\n> **A:** Too many options (or too much information) causing a person to **freeze** and not decide/act at all.\n\n**Q27.** What is Fusion Investing?\n> **A:** Combining **fundamental analysis** (traditional) with **behavioural finance** — integrating the **value-growth** phenomenon and the **momentum** effect.\n\n**Q28.** What did Benjamin Graham say about voting and weighing machines?\n> **A:** \"In the short run the market is a **voting machine** (sentiment/momentum), and in the long run it is a **weighing machine** (value).\"\n\n**Q29.** What are the three steps to identify fusion stocks?\n> **A:** Step I: find **value** stocks (P/E, P/BV, P/S); Step II: filter for **fundamental strength** (e.g. Piotroski Score); Step III: pick those with **strong momentum** (price uptrend).\n\n**Q30.** What is the main practical drawback of the momentum step in fusion investing?\n> **A:** Momentum is **short-term**, requiring **frequent rebalancing** → higher **transaction costs** and **taxes**, which can erode returns.\n\n**Q31.** What is a market anomaly?\n> **A:** A departure of a stock's price from its expected value (per CAPM/asset pricing models) that can't be explained by known or newly released information. Anomalies appear, disappear, and reappear randomly.\n\n**Q32.** How does the disposition effect relate to market anomalies?\n> **A:** By holding losers and selling winners, investors create **price momentum** — a persistent anomaly.\n\n**Q33.** Who described bubbles in \"Manias, Panics and Crashes,\" and how is a bubble defined?\n> **A:** **Charles Kindleberger (1978)**. A bubble is a sharp, continuous rise in asset price where the initial rise creates expectations of further rises, attracting speculators chasing trading profit.\n\n**Q34.** Why don't rational arbitrageurs immediately pop bubbles?\n> **A:** They face a **synchronisation problem** (can't coordinate a selling strategy) and differing incentives to time the market — so bubbles persist longer.\n\n**Q35.** Name four reasons bubbles form.\n> **A:** **Liquidity** (cheap money chasing stocks), **Celebrity status** (self-fulfilling media hype), **Momentum** (extrapolation → herd trading), and **Illusion of Control** (believing you can sell before others).\n\n**Q36.** According to Keith Redhead (2008), bubbles are more likely when... (name three conditions).\n> **A:** Many **inexperienced traders**; high **uncertainty** about true value; small chance of profit but **very high** potential payoff; purchases financeable by **borrowing**; **short selling is difficult**.\n\n**Q37.** What famous investor lost money in the South Sea Bubble, and what did he say?\n> **A:** **Isaac Newton** (~£20,000 loss): \"I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people.\"\n\n**Q38.** How is a crash related to a bubble?\n> **A:** A crash is the same process **in reverse** — everyone rushes to sell at once (negative news, liquidity crunch, return to fundamentals), and jostling to exit first triggers the crash.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 16: Basics of Behavioural Finance — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> This bank has three tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy: recall of definitions & facts), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium: identify the bias from a behaviour / compare biases), and 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard: rich multi-clue scenarios that distinguish look-alike biases and prescribe remedies). Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking** — every wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark, so skip a question only when you truly cannot eliminate enough options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What does Behavioural Finance primarily study?\nA) The mathematics of bond pricing  B) The way psychology influences the behaviour of market participants and its effect on financial markets  C) The legal framework governing stock exchanges  D) The accounting standards for listed companies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Behavioural Finance is defined as the study of how psychology influences the behaviour of market participants, both individually and as a group, and the subsequent effect on financial markets. It is part of Behavioural Economics. A) bond mathematics, C) legal frameworks, and D) accounting standards are technical/regulatory areas, not the psychology-and-markets focus that defines behavioural finance.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Behavioural Finance is described as a part of which broader field?\nA) Behavioural Economics  B) Corporate law  C) Financial accounting  D) Macroeconomic policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook explicitly states behavioural finance is a part of Behavioural Economics, which deals with biases and cognitive errors affecting investing behaviour. B), C) and D) are unrelated disciplines; behavioural finance specifically grew out of the economics-meets-psychology field of behavioural economics.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is a key assumption of Standard Finance?\nA) Investors are emotional and inconsistent  B) Investors rely on thumb rules  C) Investors are rational  D) Investors ignore new information\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Standard finance assumes investors are rational. The other options (emotional/inconsistent, thumb rules, ignoring information) describe real-life behavioural finance observations — the opposite of standard finance's idealised assumptions.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** According to standard finance, investors update their beliefs:\nA) Only once a year  B) Only when forced by regulators  C) Never  D) As new information comes in\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A core standard-finance assumption is that rational investors update their beliefs as new information arrives. A) yearly, C) never, and B) only under regulation all contradict the assumption of continuous rational updating.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** \"Home bias\" — preferring domestic companies because they feel familiar — is an example of:\nA) Standard finance working perfectly  B) A regulatory requirement  C) Real-life behaviour that deviates from standard finance assumptions  D) Optimal diversification\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook lists home bias (preferring domestic companies because familiarity makes the risk feel lower) as a real-life behaviour that departs from rational standard-finance theory. A) is wrong because home bias shows standard finance failing; B) it is not a rule; D) it is the opposite of diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** At its core, Standard Finance is based on ______, while Behavioural Finance is based on ______.\nA) Economics; psychology  B) Psychology; economics  C) Law; accounting  D) Statistics; geometry\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The comparison table places economics at the core of standard finance and psychology at the core of behavioural finance. B) reverses them; C) and D) name unrelated fields.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which two Nobel laureates are credited with bringing Behavioural Finance to the forefront?\nA) Daniel Kahneman and Richard Thaler  B) Benjamin Graham and Warren Buffett  C) Harry Markowitz and William Sharpe  D) Eugene Fama and Robert Shiller\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Daniel Kahneman (2002) and Richard Thaler (2017) are credited with bringing behavioural finance to the forefront. B) Graham/Buffett are value-investing figures, C) Markowitz/Sharpe are portfolio-theory/CAPM pioneers (standard finance), and D) Fama/Shiller, though Nobel laureates, are not the pair the workbook names here.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Who is credited with the concept of Bounded Rationality?\nA) Daniel Kahneman  B) Richard Thaler  C) Herbert Simon  D) Amos Tversky\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Nobel laureate Herbert Simon (1978) is credited with bounded rationality. A) Kahneman and D) Tversky are linked to prospect theory; B) Thaler is linked to mental accounting and the \"nudge.\"\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** \"Satisficing\" means choosing a solution that is:\nA) Optimal and maximising  B) Satisfactory and sufficient  C) Random and careless  D) Always the cheapest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Satisficing is a blend of \"satisfactory\" and \"sufficient\" — a good-enough choice made under limited time/information. A) optimal/maximising is what rationality (not satisficing) seeks; C) random/careless misstates it (satisficing is still rational under constraints); D) cheapest is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Bounded Rationality is best described as:\nA) Completely irrational decision making  B) Pure intuition with no analysis  C) Decision making with perfect information  D) Rational decision making under certain boundary conditions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook stresses bounded rationality is NOT irrational; it is rational decision making under boundary conditions (limited time, information, or ability). A) wrongly calls it irrational; C) describes pure rationality; B) describes pure intuition. Bounded rationality sits between rationality and intuition.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Under Bounded Rationality, investors process:\nA) All available information  B) No information at all  C) Only information that is manageable  D) Only information from regulators\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Step one of bounded rationality is processing only the manageable information, not all of it. A) describes full rationality; B) and D) are not part of the bounded-rationality steps.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Bounded Rationality sits between which two extremes?\nA) Rationality and intuition  B) Greed and fear  C) Profit and loss  D) Risk and return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bounded rationality lies between pure rationality (process all info → optimal) and intuition (pure experience). B), C) and D) are real concepts in the chapter but are not the two extremes that bracket bounded rationality.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Prospect Theory was introduced by:\nA) Herbert Simon  B) Charles Kindleberger  C) Richard Thaler  D) Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Kahneman and Tversky introduced Prospect Theory in 1979. A) Simon is bounded rationality; C) Thaler is mental accounting/nudge; B) Kindleberger wrote on bubbles and crashes.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** According to Prospect Theory, choices are evaluated relative to:\nA) The market index  B) A reference point (the person's well-being)  C) The risk-free rate  D) The company's book value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premise of prospect theory is that choices are judged relative to a reference point — the investor's own well-being — not in absolute terms. A), C) and D) are objective external benchmarks, not the subjective reference point prospect theory describes.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** According to Prospect Theory, people are:\nA) Risk-seeking about gains and risk-averse about losses  B) Always risk-averse  C) Risk-averse about gains and risk-seeking about losses  D) Always risk-seeking\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Prospect theory says people are risk-averse about gains (grabbing them early) but risk-seeking about losses (holding losers longer hoping to recover). A) reverses this; B) and D) ignore that the attitude flips between gains and losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Prospect Theory states that monetary losses:\nA) Hurt more than equivalent monetary gains  B) Feel the same as equivalent gains  C) Hurt less than equivalent gains  D) Are always ignored by investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A key premise is that losses hurt more than equivalent gains please (\"losses are significantly more powerful than gains\"). B) says they are equal (false), C) reverses the strength, and D) is wrong because losses are felt very intensely, not ignored.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Prospect Theory describes two phases of making choices. The first is:\nA) The evaluation phase  B) The rebalancing phase  C) The settlement phase  D) The framing phase\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The first phase is the framing phase (prospects are framed using heuristics for a preliminary analysis); the second is evaluation, where the highest-perceived-value prospect is chosen. A) is the second phase; C) and B) are not phases of prospect theory.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Investing biases broadly fall into which two main categories?\nA) Emotional biases and cognitive errors  B) Legal biases and financial biases  C) Short-term and long-term biases  D) Domestic and foreign biases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Biases are broadly split into emotional biases (from feelings) and cognitive errors (from faulty reasoning). B), C) and D) are not the workbook's categorisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Emotional biases arise from:\nA) Faulty statistical reasoning  B) Feelings or emotions, often from deep-rooted personal experiences  C) Memory loss only  D) Reading too many annual reports\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Emotional biases arise spontaneously from feelings/emotions rooted in personal experience and are often protective. A) describes cognitive errors; C) and D) are not the defined source of emotional bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Cognitive errors result from:\nA) Reasoning influenced by feelings  B) Following all rules perfectly  C) Faulty thinking — statistical, information-processing or memory errors  D) Having complete information\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cognitive errors come from faulty thinking — statistical, information-processing, or memory errors. A) describes emotional biases; B) and D) describe ideal rational behaviour, not errors.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The Disposition Effect refers to:\nA) Holding losers too long and selling winners too early  B) Selling losers fast and holding winners long  C) Never selling any stock  D) Buying only new IPOs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Shefrin and Statman (1985) proposed the disposition effect: investors hold \"losers\" too long and sell \"winners\" too early. B) reverses it; C) and D) are unrelated behaviours.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Believing that a high-profile manager automatically means a better-managed company is an example of:\nA) Anchoring  B) Choice paralysis  C) Mental accounting  D) Stereotyping bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Judging based on representative characteristics and general perception (e.g., famous manager = good company = good investment) is stereotyping bias. A) anchoring is over-relying on initial numbers; C) mental accounting concerns money buckets; B) choice paralysis is freezing among too many options.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Overconfidence bias is intensified when combined with which other bias?\nA) Endowment bias  B) Self-attribution bias  C) Framing bias  D) Status quo bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Overconfidence is intensified when combined with self-attribution bias (people \"confuse brains with a bull market,\" crediting skill for luck). A) endowment, C) framing, and D) status quo are different biases not named as the amplifier here.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Endowment bias means people value an asset:\nA) Less when they own it  B) Only at its market price  C) More when they hold rights to it than when they do not  D) Only at its book value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Endowment bias is valuing an asset more simply because you own it. A) reverses it; B) and D) describe objective valuation, which is exactly what endowment bias distorts. It combines valuing ownership with loss aversion (e.g., never selling inherited stocks).\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Endowment bias has two attributes. They are:\nA) Greed and fear  B) Liquidity and momentum  C) Anchoring and framing  D) Valuing ownership and loss aversion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The two attributes of endowment bias are valuing ownership and loss aversion. A) greed/fear is a general market description; C) anchoring/framing are cognitive errors; B) liquidity/momentum relate to bubbles.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Status Quo bias is closely linked to which other bias?\nA) Overconfidence bias  B) Mental accounting  C) Regret aversion bias  D) Anchoring\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Status quo bias (taking no decision to avoid regret) is closely linked to regret aversion. A) overconfidence, B) mental accounting, and D) anchoring are unrelated to the inaction-from-fear-of-regret link.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Regret over actions you took (rather than actions you could have taken) is called:\nA) Error of omission  B) Error of commission  C) Error of anchoring  D) Error of framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regret over actions taken is an error of commission; regret over actions not taken is an error of omission. A) is the opposite; C) and D) are not types of regret.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which type of regret is more intense, leading people to prefer the status quo?\nA) Error of commission  B) Error of omission  C) Both are exactly equal  D) Neither causes any regret\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Regret is more intense from errors of commission (acting) than omission (not acting), so people prefer the status quo (inaction). B) understates commission's sting; C) and D) contradict the workbook's point that commission hurts more.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Mental Accounting bias was developed by:\nA) Herbert Simon  B) Charles Kindleberger  C) Daniel Kahneman  D) Richard Thaler (1999)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The concept of mental accounting was developed by Richard Thaler in 1999. A) Simon is bounded rationality; C) Kahneman is prospect theory; B) Kindleberger is bubbles/crashes.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** \"Fungibility\" means:\nA) Money has labels and cannot be exchanged  B) Money loses value over time  C) All money is interchangeable and has no labels  D) Money must be kept in separate accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Fungibility is the fact that all money is interchangeable and carries no labels. Mental accounting violates this by treating money as less fungible than it is. A) reverses the definition; B) describes inflation; D) describes the mental-accounting error itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Framing bias means a person:\nA) Always gives the same answer regardless of wording  B) Answers a question differently based on how it is asked (framed)  C) Refuses to answer any question  D) Anchors to the first price quoted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Framing bias is answering differently based on how information is presented (e.g., \"95% lean\" vs \"5% fat\"). A) is the opposite (rational consistency); C) is refusal, not framing; D) describes anchoring.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Anchoring bias occurs when people:\nA) Rely heavily on a pre-existing piece of information when making decisions  B) Diversify across many assets  C) Sell winners too early  D) Treat money in separate buckets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Anchoring is over-relying on an initial piece of information (e.g., the first price in a negotiation, or a stock's past price). B) is diversification; C) is the disposition effect; D) is mental accounting.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Choice paralysis describes a situation where:\nA) Too few options force a quick decision  B) Investors over-trade aggressively  C) Investors always pick the first option  D) Too many options (or too much information) lead to not deciding at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Choice paralysis is when an overload of options or information leads to inaction (not evaluating or deciding). A) reverses the cause; C) and B) describe other behaviours (default-picking, overconfident trading), not paralysis.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Mental accounting is classified as which type of bias?\nA) Emotional bias  B) A standard finance principle  C) Cognitive error (information-processing bias)  D) A regulatory requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mental accounting is an information-processing (cognitive) bias. A) is wrong because it stems from faulty processing, not feelings; B) and D) are not biases at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Loss aversion, overconfidence, endowment, stereotyping and status quo are all examples of:\nA) Cognitive errors  B) Emotional biases  C) Market anomalies  D) Standard finance assumptions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All five are listed in the workbook as emotional biases (from feelings/personal experience). A) cognitive errors are a different category (mental accounting, framing, anchoring, choice paralysis); C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Ravi bought a stock at ₹500. It falls to ₹350, but he refuses to sell, saying \"I'll sell once it gets back to ₹500.\" Meanwhile he quickly booked a small profit on a stock that rose 8%. Which bias is this?\nA) Anchoring only  B) Choice paralysis  C) Mental accounting  D) Loss aversion / disposition effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Holding the loser to avoid booking a loss while selling a winner early is the classic disposition effect, driven by loss aversion (losses hurt more than gains please). A) anchoring is tempting because of the ₹500 reference, but the defining behaviour here is the asymmetric hold-loser/sell-winner pattern, which is loss aversion/disposition. C) mental accounting is about money buckets; B) choice paralysis is about not deciding among many options.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Meera trades far more often than average, concentrates her money in one sector she \"knows,\" and assumes her recent winning streak proves her stock-picking talent. Which bias dominates?\nA) Status quo bias  B) Endowment bias  C) Overconfidence bias  D) Framing bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Excessive trading, portfolio/sector concentration, and crediting a winning streak to skill are textbook overconfidence (amplified by self-attribution). A) status quo is inaction, the opposite of heavy trading; B) endowment is over-valuing what you own; D) framing is about wording of choices.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An investor inherited shares from his grandfather and refuses to sell them even though they don't fit his strategy, feeling they are \"specially valuable.\" Which bias is this?\nA) Endowment bias  B) Anchoring  C) Stereotyping  D) Mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Valuing inherited shares more simply because he owns them is endowment bias (the workbook's exact example). B) anchoring is fixating on a number; C) stereotyping is judging by representative traits; D) mental accounting is treating money differently by bucket.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A new investor keeps \"researching\" 60 different mutual funds and never actually invests because there are too many choices. Which bias?\nA) Choice paralysis  B) Overconfidence  C) Loss aversion  D) Self-attribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Too many options leading to no decision is choice paralysis. B) overconfidence would lead to bold action, not freezing; C) loss aversion is about clinging to losers; D) self-attribution is crediting wins to skill.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Sara spends her annual bonus recklessly on luxuries but is extremely careful with her salary, treating the two as different kinds of money. Which bias?\nA) Framing  B) Mental accounting  C) Anchoring  D) Status quo\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating a bonus (windfall) differently from salary, even though money is fungible, is mental accounting. A) framing is about how a choice is worded; C) anchoring is fixating on an initial number; D) status quo is inaction.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A salesperson says a yoghurt is \"90% fat-free\" instead of \"contains 10% fat,\" and customers prefer it. This exploits which bias?\nA) Anchoring  B) Overconfidence  C) Endowment  D) Framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Identical facts presented differently changing the choice is framing (the workbook's lean-beef example). A) anchoring needs a numeric reference that biases later estimates; C) endowment needs ownership; B) overconfidence is about self-belief.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** During a salary negotiation, the first figure quoted by the employer dominates the entire discussion. Which bias is at work?\nA) Anchoring  B) Framing  C) Mental accounting  D) Regret aversion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The first number quoted becoming the reference for the whole negotiation is the classic anchoring example. B) framing is about phrasing the same fact differently; C) mental accounting is money buckets; D) regret aversion is fear-driven inaction.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An employee never enrols in the company pension plan, not because it's bad, but because deciding feels risky and he keeps \"leaving it as is.\" A simple auto-enrolment \"nudge\" fixes it. Which bias was preventing enrolment?\nA) Overconfidence  B) Anchoring  C) Status quo bias  D) Stereotyping\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sticking with the default/doing nothing out of fear of a poor outcome is status quo bias, and Thaler's nudge (auto-enrolment) is the exact remedy cited. A) overconfidence drives action; B) anchoring is numeric fixation; D) stereotyping is judging by representative traits.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor estimates a stock's \"fair\" price purely from where it traded last year, ignoring changed fundamentals. Which bias?\nA) Anchoring  B) Loss aversion  C) Framing  D) Endowment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Judging a stock's value from its past price is the workbook's anchoring example — the past price is the anchor distorting current judgment. B) loss aversion is about clinging to losers; C) framing is wording; D) endowment requires ownership.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A person who picks a \"good enough\" fund quickly using a rule of thumb, because analysing all 100 funds would take too long, is exhibiting:\nA) Bounded rationality (satisficing)  B) Optimisation  C) Choice paralysis  D) Overconfidence\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Settling for a satisfactory-and-sufficient option under limited time using thumb rules is bounded rationality / satisficing. B) optimisation would mean analysing everything for the best; C) choice paralysis means NOT deciding (here he did decide); D) overconfidence is unrelated to the time/info constraint.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which statement best contrasts emotional bias with cognitive error?\nA) Emotional bias comes from faulty thinking; cognitive error comes from feelings  B) Emotional bias comes from feelings (often protective); cognitive error comes from faulty thinking/shortcuts  C) Both come purely from feelings  D) Both come purely from statistics\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Emotional biases stem from feelings (often protective), while cognitive errors stem from faulty thinking/information-processing shortcuts. A) reverses the two; C) and D) wrongly collapse the distinction.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A trader credits every winning trade to his \"genius\" but blames losses on \"bad luck\" or \"market manipulation.\" This is specifically:\nA) Self-attribution bias  B) Endowment bias  C) Framing bias  D) Status quo bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Taking credit for successes and blaming others/luck for failures is self-attribution bias, which fuels overconfidence. B) endowment is over-valuing owned assets; C) framing is wording; D) status quo is inaction.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Compared to optimising, \"satisficing\" is best described as:\nA) Always choosing the mathematically best option  B) Choosing randomly  C) Refusing to choose at all  D) Choosing the first good-enough option that meets a threshold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Satisficing picks a good-enough option meeting a satisfactory/sufficient threshold rather than the absolute best. A) is optimising; C) is choice paralysis; B) misrepresents satisficing as careless (it is still rational under constraints).\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An advisor notices a client mentally keeps separate \"retirement,\" \"children's education,\" and \"marriage\" buckets and over-manages each instead of optimising the overall portfolio. Which bias, and what is the workbook's recommended cure?\nA) Anchoring; ignore past prices  B) Loss aversion; sell winners later  C) Mental accounting; treat all money as fungible  D) Framing; reword the question\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Treating wealth as non-fungible buckets is mental accounting; Thaler's cure is to treat all money the same (as fungible) and optimise the portfolio as a whole. A), B) and D) name the wrong bias and the wrong remedy.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor confuses \"a good company\" with \"a good stock,\" assuming a famous, well-run firm must be a great buy at any price. Which bias does the workbook associate with judging by such representative characteristics?\nA) Stereotyping bias  B) Choice paralysis  C) Endowment bias  D) Mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Basing decisions on representative characteristics/general perception (famous = well-run = good investment) is stereotyping bias. B) choice paralysis is freezing; C) endowment is ownership-driven; D) mental accounting is money buckets.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which scenario best illustrates \"risk-seeking about losses\" from prospect theory?\nA) Selling a stock the moment it shows a small profit  B) Buying an index fund and holding forever  C) Diversifying across asset classes  D) Refusing to sell a losing stock and even averaging down, gambling to break even\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In the loss domain people become risk-seeking — they gamble (hold/average down) hoping to recover. A) is risk-averse about gains (the other half of prospect theory); C) and B) are rational diversification/holding, not loss-domain gambling.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An investor believes that because he follows a stock closely and trades it often, he can \"time his exit before everyone else.\" During a bubble, this belief is called:\nA) Illusion of control  B) Mental accounting  C) Framing  D) Bounded rationality\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Familiarity, access to information and active involvement creating the belief that one can forecast prices and sell before others is the illusion of control (a bubble driver). B), C) and D) are unrelated concepts in this chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Which of the following is the best example of an \"error of omission\"?\nA) Buying a stock that then crashed  B) Failing to invest in a fund that later soared  C) Selling a winner too early  D) Trading too frequently\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An error of omission is regret over an action NOT taken (e.g., not investing in something that later did well). A) buying a stock that crashed is an error of commission (an action taken); C) and D) are specific behaviours, not the omission/commission distinction.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Fusion investing combines which two elements?\nA) Value-growth (fundamental) and momentum (behavioural)  B) Bonds and equities  C) Domestic and foreign stocks  D) Active and passive index funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Fusion investing integrates the value-growth phenomenon from fundamental analysis with the momentum effect from behavioural finance. B), C) and D) are ordinary diversification/strategy choices, not the fundamental-plus-behavioural fusion the chapter describes.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Benjamin Graham's line \"in the short run the market is a voting machine, and in the long run a weighing machine\" maps onto fusion investing as:\nA) Voting = value; weighing = momentum  B) Voting = bonds; weighing = equities  C) Voting = short-term momentum/sentiment; weighing = long-term value  D) Voting = taxes; weighing = costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The \"voting machine\" is short-term sentiment/momentum (behavioural), and the \"weighing machine\" is long-term value (fundamental). A) swaps the two; B) and D) are unrelated to Graham's metaphor.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** In the fusion-investing 3-step process, the Piotroski Score is used in which step?\nA) Step II — filtering for fundamental strength  B) Step I — identifying value (P/E, P/BV, P/S)  C) Step III — identifying momentum  D) It is not used at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The Piotroski Score (0–9 financial-strength measure) is used in Step II to filter fundamentally strong stocks. B) Step I uses valuation ratios; C) Step III uses price uptrend; D) is wrong because it is used.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Why does the momentum component of fusion investing raise concerns about returns?\nA) It requires no rebalancing  B) It guarantees outperformance  C) It eliminates all risk  D) Momentum is short-term, needing frequent rebalancing → higher transaction costs and tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Momentum is short-term, so it needs frequent rebalancing, which drives up transaction costs and tax, eating into returns. A) is false (it needs more rebalancing); C) and B) overstate the strategy — the workbook says data is insufficient to prove it works.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A market anomaly is best defined as:\nA) A government regulation on trading  B) A departure of a stock's price from its expected value (per CAPM/asset pricing) not explained by known/new information  C) A guaranteed arbitrage profit  D) A type of mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A market anomaly is a departure of price from its expected value (under CAPM/asset-pricing models) that cannot be explained by known or newly released information. A), C) and D) misdescribe it.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** According to the chapter, which bias-driven behaviour helps explain price momentum as a market anomaly?\nA) Choice paralysis  B) Framing  C) The disposition effect (holding losers, selling winners)  D) Mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The disposition effect — holding losers longer and selling winners faster — is cited as explaining momentum in prices. A) choice paralysis, B) framing and D) mental accounting are not the bias the workbook links to momentum.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Charles Kindleberger (1978) is associated with explaining:\nA) Bubbles, manias, panics and crashes  B) Prospect theory  C) Mental accounting  D) Bounded rationality\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Kindleberger's *Manias, Panics and Crashes* (1978) explains bubbles and crashes, stressing psychology. B) prospect theory is Kahneman & Tversky; C) mental accounting is Thaler; D) bounded rationality is Simon.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Why can rational arbitrageurs fail to quickly correct a bubble's mispricing?\nA) They lack money  B) They never notice mispricing  C) Arbitrage is illegal  D) They face a synchronisation problem and differing incentives to time the market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Arbitrageurs suffer a synchronisation problem (coordinating selling) and have differing incentives to time the market, so bubbles persist. A), C) and B) are not the reasons the workbook gives.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which of these is listed as a reason bubbles form?\nA) Tight regulation  B) Liquidity (cheap borrowing chasing assets), celebrity status, momentum, illusion of control  C) High short-selling ease  D) Low investor participation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter lists liquidity, celebrity status, momentum and illusion of control as bubble drivers. A) tight regulation, C) easy short-selling, and D) low participation would tend to dampen, not fuel, bubbles.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** According to Keith Redhead (2008), speculative bubbles are MORE likely when:\nA) Short selling is easy  B) Investors cannot borrow  C) There is a high proportion of inexperienced traders and high uncertainty about true value  D) True value is precisely known\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Redhead lists many inexperienced traders and high uncertainty about true value (plus easy borrowing, small-chance/high-payoff bets, and difficult short selling) as bubble-prone conditions. A), B) and D) all describe the opposite of his conditions.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Isaac Newton's experience in the South Sea Bubble — exiting with a profit, then re-entering and losing heavily — best illustrates that:\nA) Maths skill protects against market psychology  B) Arbitrage always works  C) Bubbles never cause losses  D) Even brilliant minds fall prey to market psychology (\"madness of people\")\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Newton's loss and his quote (\"I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people\") show that even genius is no defence against herd psychology. A) is contradicted by his loss; C) and B) are false.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A crash, in behavioural terms, is best described as:\nA) A slow, orderly decline driven by fundamentals only  B) A regulator-mandated halt  C) The bubble process in reverse — everyone rushing to sell at once  D) A guaranteed buying opportunity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A crash mirrors the bubble in reverse: negative news/liquidity crunch/return to fundamentals triggers everyone jostling to sell first. A) understates the panic; B) and D) misdescribe a crash.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which pairing of bias-to-category is CORRECT?\nA) Framing — emotional bias  B) Anchoring — cognitive error  C) Loss aversion — cognitive error  D) Overconfidence — cognitive error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anchoring is a cognitive error (information-processing). A) framing is also a cognitive error, not emotional; C) loss aversion is an emotional bias; D) overconfidence is an emotional bias. Only B) is correctly paired.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An investor fills a risk-tolerance questionnaire and gives different answers depending on whether questions emphasise potential gains or potential losses. The adviser should guard against:\nA) Framing bias  B) Anchoring  C) Endowment bias  D) Self-attribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Answering differently based on how the question is framed (gain vs loss emphasis) is framing bias — the workbook specifically warns about it in risk-tolerance questionnaires. B) anchoring is numeric fixation; C) endowment is ownership; D) self-attribution is crediting wins to skill.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which best distinguishes loss aversion from endowment bias?\nA) They are identical  B) Loss aversion only applies to inherited shares  C) Endowment bias has nothing to do with ownership  D) Loss aversion is the dislike of incurring losses; endowment bias is over-valuing an asset because you own it (and includes loss aversion as one attribute)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Loss aversion is the broad dislike of losses; endowment bias is specifically over-valuing what you own, and it actually contains loss aversion as one of its two attributes (alongside valuing ownership). A) wrongly equates them; C) contradicts the definition; B) wrongly narrows loss aversion.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** \"Fusion investing\" assumes that:\nA) Markets are always perfectly efficient  B) Fundamental analysis is useless  C) Inefficiencies exist in the market and can be exploited  D) Behavioural finance should be ignored\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Fusion strategy explicitly assumes inefficiencies exist and attempts to exploit them by combining fundamentals and behaviour. A) contradicts that premise; B) and D) reject one half of the fusion, which the strategy needs.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An investor extrapolates a recent uptrend, expecting prices to keep rising, and joins the buying crowd. When widespread, this produces:\nA) Choice paralysis  B) Herd trading / momentum that overcomes rational intervention  C) Status quo bias  D) Mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Extrapolating trends with positive feedback, when widespread, results in herd trading that overwhelms rational contrarians — a momentum bubble driver. A) paralysis is inaction; C) status quo is doing nothing; D) mental accounting is money buckets.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Anand owns two stocks. Stock X (bought at ₹100, now ₹140) he sells immediately \"to lock in the gain.\" Stock Y (bought at ₹100, now ₹60) he refuses to sell, telling his adviser, \"It will surely bounce back to ₹100 — I just can't accept a loss.\" His adviser notes he becomes a cautious gambler with the loser but a nervous profit-taker with the winner. The single best label and its theoretical root are:\nA) Disposition effect rooted in prospect theory's loss aversion  B) Anchoring rooted in bounded rationality  C) Endowment bias rooted in ownership  D) Overconfidence rooted in self-attribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Selling the winner early and clinging to the loser is the disposition effect, which prospect theory explains: risk-averse over gains (grab them), risk-seeking over losses (gamble to recover), and losses hurt more than gains. B) anchoring (the ₹100 reference) is a look-alike clue, but the defining asymmetric sell/hold pattern is disposition; C) endowment requires over-valuing because of ownership, not the win/loss asymmetry; D) overconfidence would show as excessive trading and skill-claims, absent here.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Priya is a doctor who invests only in pharma stocks, trades several times a week, holds a very concentrated portfolio, and after three good years says, \"I clearly understand this sector better than the market.\" Her returns actually track a sector-wide bull run. Identify the primary bias and the amplifier, and why it is NOT simply stereotyping.\nA) Stereotyping amplified by framing  B) Status quo amplified by regret aversion  C) Endowment amplified by loss aversion  D) Overconfidence amplified by self-attribution; not stereotyping because she over-rates her OWN ability, not a group characteristic\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Concentration, excessive trading, and crediting a bull run to personal skill is overconfidence amplified by self-attribution (confusing brains with a bull market). It is NOT stereotyping, because stereotyping judges investments by a group's representative characteristics (e.g., \"famous manager = good company\"), whereas Priya over-rates her own expertise. C) and B) describe inaction/ownership behaviours not seen here.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Sunil received ₹2 lakh as a Diwali bonus and a ₹2 lakh tax refund the same week. He invests the refund cautiously in an FD but blows the bonus on a speculative penny stock, saying \"the bonus is \"extra\" money.\" His salary, meanwhile, goes only into safe instruments. What bias, and what is the cleanest one-line cure?\nA) Loss aversion; hold winners longer  B) Anchoring; ignore the purchase price  C) Mental accounting; treat all money as fungible regardless of origin  D) Framing; reword the investment options\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Treating bonus, refund, and salary as different \"kinds\" of money — taking more risk with perceived windfalls — is mental accounting. The cure (per Thaler) is to treat all money as fungible, the same regardless of origin or intended use. A), B) and D) name the wrong bias and wrong remedy; the tell-tale clue is the differential treatment of equal sums by source.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An adviser presents the SAME portfolio two ways to a client. Version 1: \"This plan has a 90% chance of meeting your goal.\" Version 2: \"This plan has a 10% chance of falling short.\" The client enthusiastically accepts Version 1 but hesitates at Version 2. Which bias is the client showing, and which prospect-theory phase does it relate to?\nA) Anchoring; the evaluation phase  B) Framing; the framing phase, where prospects are edited using heuristics before evaluation  C) Endowment; the ownership phase  D) Mental accounting; the budgeting phase\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Identical odds, opposite reactions based on wording, is framing bias, which operates in prospect theory's framing (editing) phase where prospects are pre-processed with heuristics. A) anchoring needs a numeric reference biasing later estimates; C) endowment needs ownership; D) mental accounting needs money buckets — none fit the gain-frame vs loss-frame switch.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Two investors regret different things. Investor A sold a stock that then doubled and is tormented by \"the action I didn't take\" (not buying back). Investor B bought a stock that crashed and is tormented by \"the action I took.\" Per the workbook, who likely feels MORE intense regret, and what behaviour does this drive generally?\nA) A feels more; drives over-trading  B) Neither feels regret because losses are ignored  C) Both feel identical regret  D) B feels more (error of commission); drives a preference for the status quo (inaction)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Investor B's regret is an error of commission (regret over an action taken), which is more intense than A's error of omission (regret over inaction). Because commission stings more, people generally prefer the status quo (doing nothing). A) misattributes intensity; C) contradicts the workbook; B) is false — regret is felt strongly here.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A wealthy family refuses to ever sell the shares of a textile mill founded by their late grandfather — even though the sector is declining and the holding wrecks their diversification — saying \"those shares are part of who we are.\" Distinguish the operative bias from disposition effect.\nA) It is disposition effect, because they are holding a loser  B) It is choice paralysis  C) It is endowment bias — over-valuing the asset due to ownership/inheritance — not disposition, which is specifically the asymmetric sell-winner/hold-loser trading pattern  D) It is framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Refusing to sell inherited shares because ownership itself makes them feel specially valuable is endowment bias (the workbook's exact inherited-stock example). It differs from the disposition effect, which is defined by the asymmetric pattern of selling winners early and holding losers — there is no winner-selling here, just sentimental over-valuation of an owned asset. B) and D) do not fit.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** During a tech mania, Rohan, a software engineer who reads every tech blog, pours his savings into tech IPOs convinced that \"because I work in tech and follow it daily, I'll sense the top and exit before the crowd.\" Name the bubble-specific bias and explain why it is distinct from plain overconfidence.\nA) Illusion of control — familiarity/involvement creates a belief he can forecast and sell first, a specific manifestation tied to bubbles, beyond generic overconfidence in skill  B) Momentum; it is the same as overconfidence  C) Anchoring  D) Mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Believing that familiarity, information access and active involvement let him forecast prices and exit before others is the illusion of control, a named bubble driver. While related to overconfidence, the workbook lists illusion of control specifically as the belief that one can control/forecast the outcome and beat the crowd's timing. B) momentum is the herd's trend-chasing; C) anchoring and D) mental accounting do not fit.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A relationship manager observes a client who: (i) keeps a separate \"child's wedding\" fund she manages over-cautiously, (ii) refuses to sell a falling stock to avoid \"booking a loss,\" and (iii) judges every fund by its NAV five years ago. Match each behaviour to its bias in order.\nA) Anchoring; mental accounting; loss aversion  B) Mental accounting; loss aversion; anchoring  C) Loss aversion; anchoring; mental accounting  D) Framing; endowment; stereotyping\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (i) separate, over-managed bucket = mental accounting; (ii) refusing to book a loss = loss aversion; (iii) judging by a past NAV = anchoring. B) lists them in the right order. A) and C) scramble the order; D) names three biases none of which match these exact behaviours.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** An adviser wants to overcome a client's chronic indecision (the client never rebalances because every change \"feels risky\"). Drawing on the chapter, the MOST appropriate, evidence-based remedy is:\nA) Show the client even more fund options  B) Anchor the client to last year's prices  C) Encourage more frequent emotional discussion of losses  D) Use a \"nudge\" / sensible default (e.g., pre-set auto-rebalancing), as Thaler used to overcome status-quo-driven inaction\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The client's inaction is status quo bias; the workbook's cited remedy is a nudge / good default (Thaler's auto-enrolment example), so pre-set auto-rebalancing fits. A) more options worsens choice paralysis; C) dwelling on losses feeds loss aversion; B) deliberately anchoring introduces another bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Faced with 80 ELSS funds at tax-season deadline, Geeta keeps gathering \"just a bit more data,\" misses the deadline, and invests nothing. Contrast the bias here with bounded rationality.\nA) Bounded rationality — she satisficed  B) Overconfidence  C) Choice paralysis — too many options/too much information caused her to NOT decide; unlike bounded rationality where a person DOES pick a good-enough option  D) Endowment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Failing to decide because of option/information overload is choice paralysis. The key contrast with bounded rationality is that a boundedly-rational satisficer actually makes a good-enough choice quickly, whereas Geeta froze and chose nothing. A) is wrong because she did not satisfice; B) and D) do not fit the freeze.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A fund manager building a \"fusion\" portfolio screens for low P/E and P/BV, then for a high Piotroski Score, then for a strong price uptrend. He is surprised his portfolio needs frequent reshuffling. Which step causes this and what is the consequence?\nA) Step III (momentum), because momentum is short-term, forcing frequent rebalancing → higher transaction costs and tax  B) Step I (value); lower taxes  C) Step II (Piotroski); guaranteed returns  D) None — fusion portfolios never rebalance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The momentum filter (Step III) captures a short-term phenomenon, so it demands frequent rebalancing, raising transaction costs and tax. B) value/Step I is the slow-changing leg; C) Piotroski/Step II is a fundamental filter that does not guarantee returns; D) is false — momentum forces reshuffling.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor argues, \"Markets fully reflect all information, so a stock's price always equals the discounted value of its future cash flows — short-term swings are meaningless noise.\" Per the chapter, this view:\nA) Is the behavioural-finance view  B) Is the fundamental/standard-finance (\"weighing machine\") view; the behavioural side counters that short-term prices reflect collective investor behaviour (\"voting machine\")  C) Proves bubbles cannot exist  D) Describes mental accounting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — That is the fundamental/standard-finance \"weighing machine\" stance; fusion investing adds the behavioural \"voting machine\" insight that short-term prices reflect collective behaviour. A) reverses it; C) is false (bubbles do occur); D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Three friends react to a 30% market crash. Aman sells everything in a panic because \"everyone is selling.\" Bina refuses to sell her crashed stocks, sure they'll recover. Chetan freezes and does nothing for weeks. Identify, in order, the dominant force/bias driving Aman, Bina, and Chetan.\nA) Herd behaviour; loss aversion (risk-seeking in losses); status quo/choice paralysis  B) Anchoring; framing; endowment  C) Overconfidence; mental accounting; stereotyping  D) Endowment; anchoring; framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Aman sells because others do = herd behaviour (the crash mechanism). Bina holds losers gambling to recover = loss aversion / risk-seeking in the loss domain. Chetan's frozen inaction = status quo bias / choice paralysis. B), C) and D) misassign biases that do not match these distinct reactions.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A new analyst is told a stock \"was ₹2,000 last year\" before doing any analysis; she then values it at ₹1,900, dismissing fundamentals suggesting ₹900. A colleague who never heard the ₹2,000 figure values it near ₹900. The first analyst's error and its mechanism:\nA) Framing — different wording  B) Self-attribution — she credits her skill  C) Endowment — she owns the stock  D) Anchoring — the initial ₹2,000 figure became a reference that distorted all subsequent judgment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Over-relying on the pre-existing ₹2,000 figure so that all later analysis is seen \"in light of the anchor\" is anchoring. A) framing involves wording of equivalent facts, not a sticky number; C) endowment needs ownership (not stated); B) self-attribution is crediting wins to skill — irrelevant here.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Why, per the chapter, do some market anomalies persist for long periods even though rational traders could profit by exploiting them?\nA) Regulators ban exploiting them  B) Anomalies are illegal to trade  C) Arbitrageurs face a synchronisation problem and differing incentives, so coordinated correction fails  D) There are no rational traders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Anomalies/bubbles persist because arbitrageurs cannot coordinate selling (synchronisation problem) and have differing incentives to time the market. A) and B) invent a legal ban; D) is false — rational traders exist but cannot coordinate.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A client says: \"I rate myself a far-above-average investor, I underestimate how much I could lose, and feedback from my recent wins makes me even surer of myself.\" This self-reinforcing loop most directly blurs which distinction, and which anomaly does the chapter say it can create?\nA) Blurs value vs growth; creates choice paralysis  B) Blurs domestic vs foreign; creates framing  C) Blurs gains vs losses; creates endowment  D) Blurs skill vs luck; overconfidence makes investors underestimate risk and trade too often, creating anomalies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Overconfidence (with its feedback loop) blurs the line between skill and luck and leads investors to underestimate risk and over-trade — behaviours the chapter says generate market anomalies. A), C) and B) name wrong distinctions and wrong outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A regulator-style question: which set of conditions, per Keith Redhead, would make a speculative bubble LEAST likely?\nA) Many inexperienced traders, high uncertainty, easy borrowing, hard short-selling  B) Few inexperienced traders, low uncertainty about true value, hard borrowing, easy short-selling  C) High uncertainty and easy leverage  D) Difficult short-selling and small-chance/high-payoff bets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Redhead's bubble-prone conditions are MANY inexperienced traders, HIGH uncertainty, easy borrowing, and difficult short-selling. The LEAST bubble-prone scenario reverses all of these — option B). A), C) and D) each list bubble-PROMOTING conditions.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An investor refuses to invest abroad, holding only Indian large-caps because \"I know these companies, so they feel safe,\" even though this concentrates her risk. The chapter names this tendency, and it is rooted in:\nA) Mental accounting; fungibility  B) Anchoring; a past price  C) Home bias; familiarity making perceived risk feel lower (a deviation from rational diversification)  D) Framing; question wording\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Preferring domestic companies because familiarity lowers perceived risk is home bias, listed as a real-life deviation from standard finance's diversification logic. A) mental accounting is about money buckets; B) anchoring needs a sticky number; D) framing needs differential wording.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A behaviour-finance instructor warns that bounded rationality must NOT be confused with irrationality. The best justification is:\nA) Bounded rationality is rational decision-making under boundary conditions (limited time/info/ability) — the person still reasons, just with thumb rules to reach a satisficing answer  B) Bounded rationality always produces the optimal answer  C) Bounded rationality means ignoring all information  D) Bounded rationality is the same as intuition\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bounded rationality is rational decision-making under boundary conditions; the person reasons using manageable information and thumb rules to reach a satisficing solution — it is not irrational. B) overstates it (it satisfices, not optimises); C) is false (some info is processed); D) is wrong — it sits between rationality and pure intuition.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Two scenarios: (1) \"Save 200 of 600 people for sure\" vs \"1/3 chance to save all 600.\" (2) \"400 of 600 will die for sure\" vs \"2/3 chance all 600 die.\" The outcomes are identical, yet people pick the sure option in (1) and the gamble in (2). This famous demonstration shows:\nA) Anchoring alone  B) Endowment  C) Mental accounting  D) Framing combined with prospect theory — gain-framed choices invite risk-aversion, loss-framed choices invite risk-seeking, even with identical outcomes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Identical outcomes producing opposite choices based on gain-vs-loss wording is framing, and the gain-frame-risk-averse / loss-frame-risk-seeking switch is exactly prospect theory. A) anchoring needs a numeric reference; C) and B) involve money buckets/ownership, neither present here.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client over the years (i) takes credit for wins and blames the market for losses, and (ii) only reads news that confirms his existing view while ignoring contrary data. The chapter says these behaviours combine to cause investors to:\nA) Diversify optimally  B) Trade too often, helping create market anomalies  C) Always satisfice  D) Avoid all risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Self-attribution (credit wins/blame others) plus following confirming information and ignoring conflicting data lead investors to trade too often, which the chapter says creates anomalies. A), C) and D) describe rational/cautious behaviour, the opposite of these biases' effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An adviser must explain why a \"good company\" is not always a \"good stock.\" Which underlying behavioural pitfall does an investor who equates the two most likely commit?\nA) Choice paralysis  B) Status quo  C) Stereotyping — using representative characteristics (famous, well-managed) as a proxy for a good investment regardless of price  D) Loss aversion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Assuming a well-known, well-managed company is automatically a good stock uses representative characteristics as a shortcut — stereotyping. A) paralysis is freezing; B) status quo is inaction; D) loss aversion is clinging to losers — none capture the \"good company = good stock\" confusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A momentum-driven bubble is building: prices keep rising, attracting more buyers; analysts publish \"new-age\" theories to justify valuations; cheap loans fund more buying. A lone rational fund manager shorts the asset early and gets badly hurt before the eventual crash. Which chapter concept best explains the manager's pain?\nA) Herd trading overcoming rational intervention, plus the arbitrageur's synchronisation problem — mispricing can persist and worsen before correcting  B) Mental accounting  C) Framing  D) Endowment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Widespread extrapolation produces herd trading that overwhelms rational contrarians, and arbitrageurs face a synchronisation problem, so the bubble can persist/worsen and hurt early shorts before correcting. B), C) and D) are individual decision biases unrelated to this market-level dynamic.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which of the following correctly orders the bounded-rationality decision steps (Herbert Simon)?\nA) Pick satisficing solution → apply thumb rules → process manageable info  B) Use pure intuition → ignore information → optimise  C) Process all information → optimise → verify  D) Process only manageable information → apply rules of thumb/quick approaches → select a satisfactory and sufficient (satisficing) solution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Simon's steps are: process only manageable information, apply thumb rules/quick approaches, then select a satisficing solution. A) reverses the order; C) describes full rationality (process all, optimise); B) describes pure intuition, not bounded rationality.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor holds a stock at a loss and says, \"If I sell now I lock in the loss; but if I hold, there's still a chance to recover — I'd rather gamble than accept the certain loss.\" Map this to the precise prospect-theory premise.\nA) Risk-averse about gains  B) Risk-seeking about losses — in the loss domain people prefer the gamble over a certain loss  C) Choices judged in absolute terms  D) Gains hurt more than losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Preferring to gamble rather than accept a certain loss is being risk-seeking about losses, a core prospect-theory premise. A) is the gain-domain behaviour; C) contradicts the reference-point premise; D) reverses the loss-aversion premise (losses hurt more, not gains).\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client treats his \"emergency fund,\" \"vacation fund,\" and \"stock portfolio\" as totally separate, refusing to touch the vacation fund even when his portfolio offers a far better opportunity and he could replenish it later. Beyond naming the bias, why is this sub-optimal per the chapter?\nA) It is optimal because budgeting is good  B) It is anchoring to a price  C) Mental accounting treats money as less fungible than it really is, so segregating buckets blocks portfolio-level optimisation  D) It is framing of a question\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mental accounting makes him treat fungible money as non-fungible buckets, preventing him from optimising across his whole portfolio — the chapter's stated downside. A) wrongly calls it optimal; B) and D) name the wrong biases.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An emotional bias and a cognitive error can produce similar-looking actions. A client never rebalances. If the cause is \"deciding feels emotionally risky and I fear regret,\" vs \"there are too many options and too much data to process,\" the two diagnoses are respectively:\nA) Anchoring vs framing  B) Overconfidence vs self-attribution  C) Endowment vs loss aversion  D) Status quo / regret aversion (emotional) vs choice paralysis (cognitive overload)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Fear-of-regret-driven inaction is status quo / regret aversion (an emotional bias), whereas freezing due to option/information overload is choice paralysis (a cognitive/processing issue). The same \"no rebalancing\" outcome has two different roots. A), C) and B) pair biases that do not match these two causes.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A portfolio manager claims fusion investing is a guaranteed money-machine. Based strictly on the chapter, the most accurate critique is:\nA) It always beats the market  B) It ignores valuation entirely  C) Although intuitively appealing, market data is insufficient to conclusively prove it works, and momentum's frequent rebalancing adds transaction costs and tax  D) It cannot be implemented\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The chapter explicitly cautions that fusion investing seems good intuitively but market data is insufficient to prove it, and momentum-driven rebalancing raises transaction costs and tax. A) overclaims; B) is false (Step I uses valuation); D) is false (it is implementable via the three steps).\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A first-time investor in a frothy market exhibits ALL of: low experience, betting on a thinly-understood asset where true value is highly uncertain, expecting a small chance of a huge payoff, and funding the bet with a loan. Per Keith Redhead, this individual is:\nA) Well protected against bubbles  B) Exhibiting exactly the profile of conditions under which speculative bubbles are most likely to emerge  C) Behaving like a rational arbitrageur  D) Demonstrating endowment bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inexperience, high uncertainty about true value, small-chance/high-payoff bets, and borrowing to finance purchases are precisely Redhead's bubble-prone conditions. A) is the opposite; C) an arbitrageur corrects mispricing, not chases it; D) endowment needs ownership-driven over-valuation, not described here.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A disciplined adviser builds checklists and standard processes for every client decision. The chapter's rationale for this practice is best stated as:\nA) While it is impossible to be fully unbiased, maintaining discipline and checklists helps mitigate biases  B) Biases can be completely eliminated by willpower alone  C) Checklists guarantee optimal returns  D) Cognitive errors cannot be reduced by vigilance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The chapter states it is impossible to be fully unbiased, but discipline and checklists help mitigate biases. B) overclaims (biases cannot be fully eliminated); C) checklists do not guarantee returns; D) is false — the chapter says vigilance reduces cognitive errors.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n> **Meet Vikram.** Vikram, 45, is a successful surgeon and a new client of yours (his Investment Adviser). Reviewing his finances, you uncover the following:\n>\n> 1. He keeps three rigidly separate pools: a \"kids' education\" fund (only FDs), a \"retirement\" fund (only gold), and a \"fun money\" account where he aggressively day-trades pharma stocks, treating any annual bonus as \"house money\" to gamble with.\n> 2. He owns shares in a struggling diagnostics company he founded years ago and inherited some textile-mill shares from his father. He flatly refuses to sell either, even though both drag down his diversification — \"they're part of my story.\"\n> 3. He sold his profitable IT holding the moment it rose 12% but is still holding a hospital stock down 45%, insisting \"it'll come back to what I paid.\"\n> 4. Because he is a doctor, he trades heavily in pharma and biotech, certain his medical background gives him an edge; after three good years he says, \"I clearly read this sector better than the market.\"\n> 5. When you send him a list of 70 well-rated mutual funds to diversify into, he keeps \"needing more time to study them all\" and, months later, still hasn't invested a rupee.\n\n**CS-Q1.** In behaviour (1), Vikram treats bonus money and his three pools as fundamentally different kinds of money. Which bias is this, and what is the textbook remedy?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Bias: Mental Accounting (a cognitive / information-processing error).** Vikram codes money into non-fungible \"buckets\" (education, retirement, fun) and treats a bonus as risk-it-all \"house money,\" even though money is fungible. The chapter (Thaler, 1999) notes people take more risk with windfalls/lottery-type money and over-manage separate buckets, blocking whole-portfolio optimisation. **Remedy:** treat all money as fungible — the same regardless of origin or intended use — and optimise the portfolio as a single whole rather than per bucket. The psychology: labelling money makes it feel \"different,\" but every rupee buys the same things, so the labels lead to sub-optimal choices.\n</details>\n\n**CS-Q2.** In behaviour (2), Vikram refuses to sell his founded company's shares and the inherited textile shares. Name the bias, identify its two attributes, and explain why this is endowment bias rather than the disposition effect.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Bias: Endowment Bias (an emotional bias).** Vikram values these assets more simply because he owns them (\"part of my story\"), especially the inherited shares — the chapter's exact example of inherited stocks rarely being sold even when they don't fit the strategy. **Its two attributes are (i) valuing ownership and (ii) loss aversion.** **Why not disposition effect?** The disposition effect is specifically the *asymmetric trading pattern* of selling winners early and holding losers; here the driver is sentimental over-valuation of owned/inherited assets regardless of any win/loss pattern. The psychology: ownership creates an emotional attachment that inflates perceived value beyond what a detached buyer would pay.\n</details>\n\n**CS-Q3.** Behaviour (3) — selling the IT winner at +12% but clinging to the hospital loser down 45% — illustrates which bias, and which prospect-theory premises explain it?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Bias: Loss Aversion, expressed as the Disposition Effect (Shefrin & Statman, 1985).** Vikram sells the winner quickly and holds the loser hoping to \"get back to what I paid.\" **Prospect-theory premises at work:** (i) people are **risk-averse about gains** (so he grabs the 12% early), (ii) people are **risk-seeking about losses** (so he gambles by holding the −45% loser to avoid booking the loss), and (iii) **losses hurt more than equivalent gains please**, which is why the pain of realising the loss feels unbearable. Note the \"what I paid\" reference also shows **anchoring** on his purchase price, but the dominant, defining behaviour is the disposition effect rooted in loss aversion. The psychology: booking a loss converts a \"paper\" loss into a painful, certain one, so he prefers to gamble.\n</details>\n\n**CS-Q4.** In behaviour (4), Vikram over-trades pharma and credits three good years to his medical \"edge.\" Name the primary bias and its amplifier, explain why it is NOT stereotyping, and describe two observable behaviours the chapter links to it.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Primary bias: Overconfidence Bias, amplified by Self-Attribution Bias.** Vikram has unwarranted faith in his own judgment and credits a sector-wide bull run to personal skill (\"confusing brains with a bull market\"). **Why not stereotyping?** Stereotyping judges *investments* by a group's representative characteristics (e.g., \"famous manager = good company\"); Vikram instead over-rates *his own ability* — that is overconfidence. **Two observable behaviours the chapter links to overconfidence:** portfolio/sector concentration (all-in on pharma/biotech) and excessive trading (heavy day-trading); others include sticking with loss-making stocks in sectors he thinks he knows. The psychology: a feedback loop from recent wins blurs the line between skill and luck, leading him to underestimate risk.\n</details>\n\n**CS-Q5.** In behaviour (5), Vikram never invests from the 70-fund list. Identify the bias, contrast it with bounded rationality, and recommend a practical, chapter-supported remedy an adviser could use.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Bias: Choice Paralysis.** Too many options (70 funds) and too much information lead Vikram to keep \"studying\" and ultimately not decide at all. **Contrast with bounded rationality:** a boundedly-rational investor would *satisfice* — quickly pick a good-enough fund using thumb rules under limited time — and actually invest; Vikram instead freezes and takes no action, which is the hallmark of choice paralysis rather than satisficing. **Practical remedy (chapter-supported):** reduce the choice set and use a **nudge / sensible default** (Thaler) — e.g., present a short curated shortlist or a pre-set default allocation/auto-investment plan — and apply **discipline and a checklist**, which the chapter says help mitigate biases. The psychology: when evaluation feels overwhelming, simplifying the decision and providing a default removes the paralysis without forcing a rushed, biased choice.\n</details>\n\n\n\n"}, {"num": "17", "title": "Behavioural Finance in Practice", "module": "Behavioural Finance & Risk Profiling", "notes": "# Chapter 17: Behavioural Finance in Practice — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **role of emotions in goal setting** (and how emotions sabotage money decisions)\n- How to **\"nudge\" an investor** into behaving better\n- The **role of the Investment Adviser** in managing client emotions\n\n> **What is \"Behavioural Finance\"?** Normal (classical) finance assumes people are cold, calculating robots who always pick the option with the best numbers. **Behavioural finance** is the more honest version — it studies how real humans actually behave with money: emotionally, impulsively, and full of mental shortcuts called **biases** (built-in thinking errors). A good adviser's job is partly *financial* and partly *psychological*: protecting clients from their own brain.\n\n---\n\n## 17.1 Role of emotions in goal setting\n\nPeople deal with the stress of modern life in different ways. Some overeat, some oversleep, some go on shopping sprees. In small doses this relieves stress, but it rarely stays small — and the **economic cost** (the money you wasted) eventually creates *more* stress.\n\n**Analogy:** Emotional spending is like scratching a mosquito bite. The scratch feels great for two seconds, then the bite gets worse. The adviser's role is to put a self-limiting \"brake\" on these behaviours so they don't spiral.\n\nBelow are the specific emotional/behavioural traps the chapter covers, each with the **antidote** an adviser uses.\n\n### (a) Retail therapy — stress buster or escape mechanism?\n**Retail therapy** = going on a shopping/buying binge to reduce stress.\n\n**How an adviser tackles it (the 2 steps):**\n1. **Make the client conscious** that the spending is *triggered by stress*, not by genuine need.\n2. **Set a specific budget** for such indulgence. In extreme cases, open a **separate account** holding only the budgeted amount; spend only from there (debit card or cash).\n\n> 💡 The key idea is a **self-imposed brake** — a built-in expense limit. This is exactly why **credit cards are dangerous** for most people: they have no built-in brake and *induce impulsive spending* (you can spend money you don't have).\n\n### (b) Too many and too frequent transactions\nNobel laureate **Paul Samuelson** said *\"Investing should be dull... If you want excitement, take USD 800 and go to Las Vegas.\"*\n\nHumans have an **action bias** = the urge to *do something* (anything!) rather than sit still. But investing rewards **patience and long-term consistency**. Action bias turns calm *investing* into hyperactive *trading* (frequent buying/selling), which usually destroys returns through costs and bad timing.\n\n> 💡 **Antidote:** \"Prime\" (mentally prepare) the client *in advance* with the benefits of patience and long-term investing — before the itch to trade strikes.\n\n### (c) Chasing past performance\nSome investors just buy *last year's winners*: gold goes up → they pile into gold; equities rise → they switch to equities.\n\n**Why it fails:** Most asset classes move in **cycles** of growth and decline, and there is an **inevitable reversion to the mean** (returns drift back toward the long-run average). Investing on past performance is **\"like driving a car by looking in the rear-view mirror\"** — you'll crash eventually.\n\n> 💡 **Antidote:** A **pre-decided asset allocation policy** with **upper limits for each asset class** prevents over-exposure. A **maximum exposure limit within an asset class** does the same for any single security.\n\n### (d) Home country bias\n**Home country bias** = preferring to invest only in your *own country's* securities because they feel familiar (you know the companies, the rules). The cost: you lose the **diversification benefit** of owning assets that *don't move in tandem* with local markets.\n\nReasons investors avoid foreign assets:\n- a) Lack of knowledge of opportunities abroad\n- b) Home-country **laws may restrict** investing outside India\n- c) Lack of access / high cost of access\n- d) Complex **tax & legal compliance** in the other country (may conflict with local laws)\n- e) Lack of expert advice (advisers themselves may not understand foreign assets)\n\n**India context:** The **Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS)** lets a resident remit up to **USD 250,000** per year for overseas investment. Indian mutual funds also offer schemes investing abroad, including **S&P 500** and **Nasdaq 100** index funds.\n\n> 💡 **Antidote:** The adviser must *get educated* on global investing and then *educate the client*.\n\n### (e) Buying insurance only for tax saving\nAt least **3 generations of Indians** bought insurance *only* to save tax — and ended up with the **wrong** and/or **inadequate** insurance.\n\n**The correct view:** Adequate, appropriate insurance is the **first step** of any financial plan. Tax saving merely *reduces the cost* of good insurance. In an investment-cum-insurance product it just *enhances return slightly* — which should still be compared to an equivalent tax-saving investment.\n\n> 💡 **Worked logic:** Buying a **pure term policy + investing in PPF** will *invariably beat* buying an **endowment** (investment-cum-insurance) policy for the same outlay. The cure: highlight the need for **adequate pure insurance** and the **poor returns** of bundled insurance-investment products.\n\n### (f) Too much diversification OR a highly concentrated portfolio\nTwo opposite mistakes:\n- **Over-diversification:** Owning so many securities you can't manage them. Buying 2 large-cap + 4 mid-cap + 6 small-cap funds is pointless — you could get the **same diversification by buying a single top-500 index fund**. Diversification doesn't come from *number* of securities.\n- **Over-concentration:** Putting your whole portfolio in one or two securities/assets with no spread.\n\n> 💡 The adviser finds the **\"golden mean\"** — appropriate diversification based on the client's **risk profile, resources, and time frame**.\n\n### (g) The impact of framing on risk-tolerance questions\n**Framing** = *how* you word the same fact. One client responds to *\"here's what you'll gain\"*; another responds to *\"here's what you'll lose by not doing this.\"* Both descriptions justify the *same* recommendation — but the **framing decides whether the client is convinced.** Tools exist that diagnose which framing a client prefers; confirm the diagnosis through discussion.\n\n### (h) Overconfidence and dilution in risk management\nHumans **overestimate their own abilities**. Survey employees on what performance percentile they're in: almost everyone says 60–75th, almost no one says below 50th — which is **mathematically impossible**. People simply think they're better than they are.\n\nIn investing, this shows up as **attributing luck to skill**: a client made a small bet on a stock that 5× in 3 months. That was *mostly luck*, and even if skill, it's unlikely to be repeatable. Overconfident, the client may **over-invest** and wreck their asset allocation.\n\n> ⚠️ This is when *\"the Investment Adviser's influence can stand between the client and her big mistake.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 17.2 Nudging the investor to behave better\n\n**A \"nudge\"** = a gentle, pre-built push that makes the *good* choice the *easy/automatic* choice — without forcing anyone.\n\n**How:** Lay down **advance ground rules** that the client agrees to up front. Then the rules act as automatic nudges.\n\n> 💡 **Example:** Ground rules on **when to rebalance** become a built-in nudge to **book profit when prices are high** (sell the asset that grew too big) and **buy when prices are low** (top up the asset that shrank). The rule forces \"buy low, sell high\" — the opposite of what emotions tell you to do.\n\n---\n\n## 17.3 Role of the Investment Adviser in managing client emotions\n\nMost clients ride a cycle of **greed and fear**:\n- **Over-exuberance (greed)** → rash decisions\n- **Overarching fear** → paralysis (the client \"freezes\")\n\nIn both cases the adviser is the **cool, calming influence** that keeps the client on the *pre-planned journey* through the market's ups and downs.\n\n**Second role — prioritising goals when money is short.** Example: a client must fund (i) her daughter's higher education and (ii) her own retirement. Emotionally she'll sacrifice retirement (it feels far away) for the daughter's best education. The adviser objectively:\n- Points out **how much the vital retirement goal is being compromised**, and\n- Offers **alternatives** — e.g. a **partial education loan** the daughter repays from her own future earnings.\n\n> 💡 This **objective role** helps highly emotional clients make the *right* decision.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Behavioural finance** studies how real (emotional, biased) humans handle money — vs the robot-investor of classical finance.\n- **Emotional/behavioural traps & antidotes:**\n  - *Retail therapy* → make it conscious + separate budgeted account (no credit cards).\n  - *Action bias / over-trading* → prime the client on patience; investing should be \"dull.\"\n  - *Chasing past performance* → pre-set asset-allocation limits; beware reversion to the mean.\n  - *Home country bias* → educate on global investing; LRS allows USD 250k/yr.\n  - *Insurance for tax only* → buy *adequate pure* insurance; term + PPF beats endowment.\n  - *Over/under-diversification* → an index fund gives diversification; find the golden mean.\n  - *Framing* → word the recommendation the way the client responds to (gain vs loss).\n  - *Overconfidence* → don't mistake luck for skill; protect the asset allocation.\n- **Nudges:** pre-agreed ground rules (e.g. rebalancing) make good behaviour automatic.\n- **Managing emotions:** be the calm anchor through greed/fear cycles; help prioritise goals objectively (education loan vs raiding retirement).\n- **Theory names (from module questions):** *Prospect Theory* and *Mental Accounting* are core behavioural concepts.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Behavioural finance** | Study of how psychology/emotion affects real money decisions (includes investor psychology). |\n| **Action bias** | The urge to \"do something,\" causing over-trading. |\n| **Reversion to the mean** | Returns tend to drift back to the long-run average — why chasing winners fails. |\n| **Home country bias** | Investing only in domestic securities, losing diversification. |\n| **LRS limit** | **USD 250,000** per resident per year for overseas investment. |\n| **Framing** | How a fact is worded (gain-frame vs loss-frame) changes the decision. |\n| **Overconfidence bias** | Overestimating one's own skill; mistaking luck for skill. |\n| **Nudge** | A pre-built gentle push (e.g. ground rules) toward good behaviour. |\n| **Anchoring bias** | Relying on pre-existing information when making decisions. |\n| **Mental Accounting** | Treating money differently based on its origin/intended use. |\n| **Prospect Theory** | Kahneman & Tversky (1979): choices judged vs a reference point; losses hurt more than equal gains; risk-averse on gains, risk-seeking on losses. |\n| **Greed/fear cycle** | Emotional swings the adviser must steady. |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 17: Behavioural Finance in Practice — Flashcards\n\n> **How to use:** Cover the answer, recall it out loud, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What is behavioural finance, and how does it differ from standard finance?\n> **A:** It is the study of how human psychology and emotion affect money decisions. Standard finance assumes rational \"robot\" investors; behavioural finance **includes the impact of investor psychology** (and accepts that market anomalies exist).\n\n**Q2.** What is \"retail therapy\"?\n> **A:** Going on a shopping/buying binge to relieve emotional stress.\n\n**Q3.** What are the two steps an adviser uses to control retail therapy?\n> **A:** (1) Make the client **conscious** that the spending is stress-induced; (2) Set a **specific budget** — in extreme cases a **separate account** holding only the budgeted amount, spent via debit card/cash.\n\n**Q4.** Why are credit cards considered bad for most people in this context?\n> **A:** They have **no built-in expense brake** and **induce impulsive spending** (you can spend money you don't have).\n\n**Q5.** What is \"action bias\"?\n> **A:** The human need to *do something* (constantly act), which conflicts with investing's need for long-term patience and turns investing into frequent **trading**.\n\n**Q6.** Paul Samuelson said investing should be like what?\n> **A:** \"Dull... like watching paint dry or grass grow.\" For excitement, \"take USD 800 and go to Las Vegas.\"\n\n**Q7.** How does an adviser curb the over-trading tendency?\n> **A:** **Prime the client in advance** with the benefits of patience and long-term investing.\n\n**Q8.** What is \"chasing past performance\" and why is it dangerous?\n> **A:** Investing in last year's winners, assuming they'll repeat. Dangerous because asset classes move in cycles with **reversion to the mean** — \"like driving by looking in the rear-view mirror.\"\n\n**Q9.** What policy tool prevents chasing performance / over-exposure?\n> **A:** A **pre-decided asset allocation policy** with **upper limits per asset class**, plus a **maximum exposure limit within an asset class** for any single security.\n\n**Q10.** What is \"home country bias\"?\n> **A:** Preferring to invest only in your home country's securities due to familiarity, thereby losing the diversification benefit of low/negatively-correlated foreign assets.\n\n**Q11.** List reasons investors avoid foreign securities.\n> **A:** Lack of knowledge; home-country laws restricting it; lack of access / high cost; complex foreign tax & legal compliance; lack of expert advice.\n\n**Q12.** What is the LRS and its limit?\n> **A:** **Liberalised Remittance Scheme** — a resident may remit up to **USD 250,000 per year** for overseas investment.\n\n**Q13.** Name two global indices available as Indian mutual fund schemes.\n> **A:** **S&P 500** and **Nasdaq 100**.\n\n**Q14.** What is the antidote to home country bias?\n> **A:** The adviser educates *themselves* on global opportunities, then educates the client.\n\n**Q15.** Why is \"buying insurance only for tax saving\" a problem?\n> **A:** Clients end up with the **wrong** and/or **inadequate** insurance. Adequate pure insurance should come first; tax saving only reduces its cost.\n\n**Q16.** Which combination beats an endowment policy for the same outlay?\n> **A:** A **pure term policy + investing in PPF** invariably beats an endowment (investment-cum-insurance) plan.\n\n**Q17.** Why is owning many funds NOT the same as diversification?\n> **A:** Diversification comes from spread, not count. 2 large-cap + 4 mid-cap + 6 small-cap funds give roughly the same diversification as a single **top-500 index fund**.\n\n**Q18.** What two opposite portfolio mistakes does the chapter warn about?\n> **A:** **Over-diversification** (too many unmanageable holdings) and **over-concentration** (whole portfolio in one or two securities/assets).\n\n**Q19.** What is \"framing\"?\n> **A:** *How* the same fact is worded. Some clients respond to a **gain frame** (\"what you'll gain\"), others to a **loss frame** (\"what you lose by not doing it\"). Same recommendation, different persuasion.\n\n**Q20.** What is overconfidence bias, with the survey example?\n> **A:** Overestimating one's abilities. Most employees rate themselves in the 60–75th percentile — an arithmetical impossibility. In investing it means mistaking **luck for skill**.\n\n**Q21.** What's the danger of overconfidence in investing, and the adviser's role?\n> **A:** The client over-invests on a lucky bet, distorting the asset allocation. The adviser stands \"between the client and her big mistake.\"\n\n**Q22.** What is a \"nudge\" in investing?\n> **A:** A pre-built gentle push (e.g. agreed **ground rules**) that makes the good choice automatic without forcing it.\n\n**Q23.** Give the classic nudge example.\n> **A:** **Rebalancing ground rules** — they auto-trigger profit-booking when prices are high and buying when prices are low (\"buy low, sell high\").\n\n**Q24.** What emotional cycle do most clients go through?\n> **A:** The **greed/fear cycle** — over-exuberance leading to rash acts, or fear that freezes them.\n\n**Q25.** What is the adviser's role during the greed/fear cycle?\n> **A:** Be the **cool, calming influence** that keeps the client on the pre-planned journey.\n\n**Q26.** How does the adviser help when resources can't fund all goals (education vs retirement)?\n> **A:** Objectively show how much the vital retirement goal is being compromised and offer alternatives, e.g. a **partial education loan** the child repays later.\n\n**Q27.** What is anchoring bias?\n> **A:** Relying on **pre-existing information** when making decisions.\n\n**Q28.** What does mental accounting theory say?\n> **A:** People **treat money differently** depending on its **origin and intended use**.\n\n**Q29.** Who introduced Prospect Theory and when?\n> **A:** **Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979).**\n\n**Q30.** State the three premises of Prospect Theory.\n> **A:** (1) Choices are evaluated relative to a **reference point**; (2) People are **risk-averse on gains** (realise them early) but **risk-seeking on losses** (hold them longer); (3) **Losses hurt more than equal gains**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 17: Behavioural Finance in Practice — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy recall, Q1–Q35), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium application, Q36–Q70), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard scenarios, Q71–Q100), then a worked Case Study. Remember: the NISM exam has **25% negative marking** — each wrong answer costs you a quarter of a mark, so skip rather than blind-guess.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** What is the broad subject matter of \"Behavioural Finance in Practice\"?\nA) How to calculate portfolio returns mathematically  B) How real, emotional humans actually behave with money and how an adviser manages that  C) How to file tax returns  D) How to register as an investment adviser\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter is about emotions, biases and the adviser's role in managing client behaviour. A) is portfolio maths, not behaviour. C) is taxation. D) is regulation. The behavioural logic: classical finance assumes robot-like investors, but this chapter studies the messy, emotional reality.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** \"Retail therapy\" refers to:\nA) Buying shares of retail companies  B) Going on a shopping or buying binge to reduce stress  C) Therapy sessions offered at retail stores  D) A type of mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Retail therapy = a shopping/buying binge done to relieve emotional stress. A) confuses it with retail-sector stocks. C) is a literal misreading. D) is unrelated. The behavioural logic: spending gives momentary relief from stress, like scratching an itch.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** According to the chapter, what is the FIRST step an adviser uses to tackle a client's retail therapy?\nA) Confiscate the client's credit cards  B) Make the client conscious that the behaviour is induced by stress  C) Increase the client's income  D) Recommend a high-return investment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Step one is awareness: make the client realise the spending is triggered by stress, not need. A) is too forceful and not in the text. C) and D) don't address the emotional cause. Behavioural logic: you cannot fix a habit you don't recognise.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** What is the SECOND step to control retail therapy?\nA) Stop the client from shopping entirely  B) Have a specific budget for indulging, possibly via a separate account  C) Sell all the client's investments  D) Report the client to SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Step two sets a specific budget; in extreme cases a separate account holds only the budgeted amount. A) is unrealistic and not advised. C) and D) are absurd. Behavioural logic: a built-in spending limit is a self-imposed brake.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Why does the chapter say credit cards are NOT good for most people?\nA) They charge annual fees  B) They induce impulsive spending because there is no built-in brake  C) They are illegal in India  D) They give too many reward points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Credit cards let you spend money you don't have, inducing impulsive spending with no built-in expense limit. A) is a minor point, not the chapter's reason. C) is false. D) is irrelevant. Behavioural logic: a debit card or cash imposes a natural brake; credit removes it.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A \"separate account holding only the budgeted amount\" is an example of:\nA) A self-imposed brake on spending  B) Tax evasion  C) A nominee account  D) An overdraft facility\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It is a self-imposed brake / built-in expense limit. B) is illegal and unrelated. C) is an estate concept. D) is the opposite — it allows more borrowing. Behavioural logic: by ring-fencing money, the client physically cannot overspend.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which Nobel laureate said \"Investing should be dull. It shouldn't be exciting\"?\nA) Daniel Kahneman  B) Amos Tversky  C) Paul Samuelson  D) Harry Markowitz\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The quote about investing being dull (and going to Las Vegas for excitement) is attributed to Paul Samuelson. A) and B) are linked to Prospect Theory. D) is linked to portfolio theory. Behavioural logic: real investing is patient and boring, not thrilling.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** \"Action bias\" is best defined as:\nA) The urge to do something rather than sit still  B) A bias toward action movies  C) A legal action against an adviser  D) The tendency to avoid all decisions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Action bias is the human need to \"do something,\" whereas investing rewards patience. B) is nonsense. C) is legal jargon. D) is the opposite (inaction). Behavioural logic: doing nothing feels wrong, so investors trade unnecessarily.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Action bias most often turns investing into:\nA) Saving  B) Trading (too frequent and too many transactions)  C) Insurance buying  D) Tax planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Action bias causes frequent, numerous transactions, turning investment into trading. A) is the opposite tendency. C) and D) are unrelated activities. Behavioural logic: hyperactivity destroys returns through costs and bad timing.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** What is the chapter's antidote to over-trading from action bias?\nA) Charge higher fees  B) Prime the client in advance with the benefits of patience and long-term investing  C) Block the client's trading account permanently  D) Recommend day trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Prime (mentally prepare) the client in advance about patience and long-term investing. A) is unrelated. C) is too extreme and not in the text. D) is the opposite. Behavioural logic: preparing the mind before the itch strikes builds resistance.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** \"Chasing past performance\" means:\nA) Buying last year's winning assets or securities expecting them to keep winning  B) Reviewing a fund's historical NAV for compliance  C) Selling losers quickly  D) Tracking an index passively\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It is investing in last year's winners (e.g. gold rises so they buy gold). B) is a routine review. C) is a different behaviour. D) is the opposite of chasing. Behavioural logic: it assumes the recent past predicts the future, which is often wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Chasing past performance fails mainly because of:\nA) Government regulation  B) Reversion to the mean — asset classes cycle and returns drift back to average  C) High brokerage fees  D) Currency fluctuations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most asset classes have cycles of growth and decline with an inevitable reversion to the mean. A), C) and D) are not the chapter's stated reason. Behavioural logic: yesterday's hot asset is often tomorrow's laggard.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The chapter compares chasing past performance to:\nA) Driving a car by looking in the rear-view mirror  B) Watching paint dry  C) Scratching a mosquito bite  D) Sailing without a compass\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Investing on past performance is \"like driving a car by looking in the rear-view mirror\" — bound to crash. B) describes dull investing (the good thing). C) describes emotional spending. D) is not used in the text. Behavioural logic: you must look forward, not backward.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** What tool prevents over-exposure to a single asset class?\nA) A pre-decided asset allocation policy with upper limits for each asset class  B) Buying more of the winning asset  C) A credit card  D) An endowment policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Upper limits per asset class in a pre-set allocation policy prevent over-exposure. B) worsens it. C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: rules set in calm times protect you in emotional times.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** \"Home country bias\" is the tendency to:\nA) Prefer investing only in your own country's securities due to familiarity  B) Invest only abroad  C) Avoid equities  D) Buy only government bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Home country bias is preferring domestic securities because they feel familiar. B) is the opposite. C) and D) are unrelated asset choices. Behavioural logic: familiarity feels safe, but it costs diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The main cost of home country bias is:\nA) Higher taxes  B) Loss of the diversification benefit from assets that don't move in tandem with local markets  C) Currency conversion fees  D) Legal penalties\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors lose diversification from foreign assets that move differently from local ones. A), C) and D) may exist but are not the central cost cited. Behavioural logic: putting all eggs in one (home) basket raises risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Which is NOT listed as a reason investors avoid foreign securities?\nA) Lack of knowledge of opportunities abroad  B) Home-country laws may restrict it  C) High cost / lack of access  D) Foreign markets are always loss-making\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — \"Foreign markets are always loss-making\" is false and not a listed reason. A), B) and C) are all explicitly listed. Behavioural logic: the barriers are practical and informational, not that foreign markets lose money.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Under the Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS), a resident of India can remit up to how much per year for overseas investment?\nA) USD 25,000  B) USD 250,000  C) USD 2,500,000  D) USD 2,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The LRS limit is USD 250,000 per resident per year. A), C) and D) are wrong figures. Behavioural logic: knowing this lets advisers help clients beat home country bias legally.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which global indexes are mentioned as available through Indian mutual fund schemes?\nA) Sensex and Nifty  B) S&P 500 and Nasdaq 100  C) FTSE and DAX  D) Hang Seng and Nikkei\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter names S&P 500 and Nasdaq 100 index funds. A) are Indian (home) indexes. C) and D) are not mentioned. Behavioural logic: easy domestic routes to global investing reduce home country bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The antidote to home country bias is for the adviser to:\nA) Force clients to invest abroad  B) Get educated on global investing and educate clients about it  C) Avoid all foreign discussion  D) Charge a foreign-investment fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The adviser must first learn about global investing, then educate the client. A) is coercive. C) ignores the problem. D) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: bias born of unfamiliarity is cured by knowledge.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** For how many generations does the chapter say Indians have bought insurance mainly for tax saving?\nA) One  B) Two  C) At least three  D) Five\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At least three generations of Indians grew up buying insurance for tax saving alone. A), B) and D) are wrong. Behavioural logic: a long-ingrained habit produced wrong/inadequate insurance choices.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Buying insurance only for tax saving leads to:\nA) The wrong kind and/or completely inadequate insurance  B) Excess insurance coverage  C) Higher investment returns  D) No effect at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax-driven buying results in the wrong and/or inadequate insurance. B) is the opposite. C) is false. D) ignores the harm. Behavioural logic: when tax saving is the goal, protection (the real purpose) is neglected.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** According to the chapter, adequate and appropriate insurance is:\nA) Optional  B) The first step of any good financial plan  C) The last step of a financial plan  D) Only for the wealthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Adequate, appropriate insurance is the first step of any good financial plan. A), C) and D) are wrong. Behavioural logic: you protect the foundation before building wealth on top.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The chapter argues that a term policy plus PPF will:\nA) Invariably beat an endowment product for the same outlay  B) Always lose to an endowment policy  C) Equal an endowment policy exactly  D) Be illegal to combine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Term + PPF invariably beats an endowment (investment-cum-insurance) policy for the same money. B) and C) are wrong. D) is false. Behavioural logic: separating protection (term) from investment (PPF) gives both more efficiently.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Over-diversification means:\nA) Owning so many securities you cannot manage them  B) Owning a single stock  C) Holding only cash  D) Buying one index fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Over-diversification = too many securities to manage. B) is over-concentration. C) is no investment. D) is efficient diversification. Behavioural logic: more holdings feels safer but just adds clutter without extra benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The chapter says true diversification of an asset class can come simply from:\nA) Buying ten different funds  B) Buying an index representing that asset class  C) Buying foreign currency  D) Buying an insurance policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buying an index (e.g. a top-500 index fund) gives diversification without many funds. A) is over-diversification. C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: diversification comes from breadth inside one instrument, not the number of instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Owning 2 large-cap, 4 mid-cap and 6 small-cap funds instead of one index fund is an example of:\nA) Over-concentration  B) Over-diversification that adds no real benefit  C) Optimal allocation  D) Home country bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Twelve funds for what a single top-500 index fund could do is pointless over-diversification. A) is the opposite. C) is wrong (it isn't optimal). D) is a different bias. Behavioural logic: complexity is not the same as diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Putting one's entire portfolio in a couple of securities is:\nA) Over-diversification  B) Over-concentration (lack of adequate diversification)  C) The golden mean  D) Framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Concentrating everything in one or two holdings is over-concentration. A) is the opposite. C) is the ideal middle. D) is a wording effect. Behavioural logic: too few holdings means one bad bet can sink everything.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The adviser's job between the two extremes of diversification is to find:\nA) The golden mean — appropriate diversification based on risk profile, resources and time frame  B) The cheapest fund  C) The highest-return stock  D) The largest number of holdings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser finds the golden mean suited to the client's risk profile, resources and time frame. B), C) and D) miss the balance. Behavioural logic: the right answer is neither too many nor too few holdings.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** \"Framing\" in behavioural finance means:\nA) Putting a portfolio statement in a frame  B) How the same fact is worded (e.g. gain-frame vs loss-frame)  C) Registering a complaint  D) Drawing a chart\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Framing is how a fact is presented; the same recommendation can be worded as a gain or as a loss avoided. A) is literal. C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: wording, not just facts, decides whether a client is convinced.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Some clients respond to \"here's what you'll gain,\" while others respond to \"here's what you'll lose by not doing this.\" This shows the importance of:\nA) Anchoring  B) Framing  C) Action bias  D) Home country bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is framing — gain-frame vs loss-frame for the same recommendation. A), C) and D) are other biases. Behavioural logic: matching the frame to the client's preference improves persuasion.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Overconfidence bias is the human tendency to:\nA) Underestimate one's own abilities  B) Overestimate one's own talents and abilities  C) Avoid all risk  D) Trust only experts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — People overestimate their talents; in surveys most rate themselves 60–75th percentile (an arithmetical impossibility). A) is the opposite. C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: we systematically think we're better than we are.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** In investing, overconfidence often shows up as:\nA) Attributing luck to skill (e.g. a stock that 5×'d in 3 months)  B) Never investing  C) Buying only insurance  D) Donating to charity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Overconfidence makes clients credit their skill for what was mostly luck, like a stock that 5×'d quickly. B), C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: a lucky win feels like proof of genius, tempting bigger bets.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A \"nudge\" is best described as:\nA) A forced order from the regulator  B) A gentle, pre-built push that makes the good choice the easy/automatic one  C) A penalty for bad behaviour  D) A type of mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A nudge gently steers toward good behaviour without forcing it. A) and C) are coercive. D) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: pre-agreed ground rules quietly default the client into good decisions.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Most clients ride an emotional cycle of:\nA) Hunger and thirst  B) Greed and fear  C) Profit and loss only  D) Tax and refund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Clients cycle through greed (over-exuberance) and fear (paralysis). A) is unrelated. C) and D) are not the emotional cycle described. Behavioural logic: markets trigger swings the adviser must steady.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A client confesses he shops online late at night whenever work stress peaks, then feels guilty. What should the adviser do FIRST?\nA) Tell him to delete all shopping apps  B) Make him conscious that his spending is stress-induced  C) Open a new investment account  D) Recommend a credit card with cashback\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The first step for retail therapy is awareness — link the spending to stress. A) is forceful and not the prescribed first step. C) is irrelevant. D) worsens impulsive spending. Behavioural logic: recognising the trigger comes before fixing it.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** After making the client aware, what concrete tool should the adviser set up to limit his stress-shopping?\nA) A larger overdraft  B) A new credit card  C) A specific budget, possibly a separate account funded only with the budgeted amount  D) A monthly cash bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Step two is a specific budget/separate account — a self-imposed brake. A) and B) increase spending power. D) adds money to splurge. Behavioural logic: a hard limit physically stops overspending.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client wants to pay only with credit cards \"for convenience.\" Given his impulsive habits, what is the better suggestion?\nA) Get a second credit card  B) Use a debit card or cash so a built-in brake limits spending  C) Raise the credit limit  D) Pay only the minimum due each month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debit/cash impose a natural brake; credit cards induce impulsive spending. A) and C) remove the brake further. D) traps him in debt. Behavioural logic: you can't overspend money you don't physically have.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client phones every week wanting to buy or sell something \"because the market moved.\" Which bias is driving this and how should the adviser respond?\nA) Action bias — prime him in advance on the value of patience and long-term investing  B) Anchoring — give him a price target  C) Home country bias — suggest foreign stocks  D) Framing — reword the statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Frequent buy/sell urges signal action bias; the cure is priming for patience. B), C) and D) target unrelated biases. Behavioural logic: trading itch is calmed by preparing the mind for \"dull\" investing in advance.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A client says \"Gold did great last year, move everything into gold.\" What should the adviser explain?\nA) Gold always rises  B) He should also borrow to buy more gold  C) Past performance guarantees future returns  D) Asset classes cycle and revert to the mean; past winners often don't repeat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — This is chasing past performance; the adviser explains cycles and reversion to the mean. A) and C) are false. B) is reckless. Behavioural logic: investing by the rear-view mirror eventually crashes.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** To stop a client from repeatedly piling into last year's hot asset class, the adviser should:\nA) Let the client decide each month  B) Remove all limits for flexibility  C) Set a pre-decided asset allocation policy with upper limits per asset class  D) Concentrate the portfolio in one fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Upper limits per asset class cap over-exposure to any hot asset. A) and B) invite chasing. D) is over-concentration. Behavioural logic: rules pre-set in calm prevent emotional over-allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client refuses to invest abroad, saying \"I only understand Indian companies.\" Which bias is this and what's the antidote?\nA) Home country bias — educate him on the benefits/opportunities of global investing  B) Overconfidence — tell him to bet bigger  C) Action bias — get him to trade more  D) Mental accounting — split his money into jars\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Familiarity-driven domestic-only investing is home country bias; the cure is education. B), C) and D) target other biases. Behavioural logic: unfamiliarity is fixed with knowledge, not pressure.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A wealthy client wants global diversification but worries about access. The adviser can point out that he may:\nA) Only invest through illegal channels  B) Use LRS (up to USD 250,000/year) or Indian MF schemes investing in S&P 500/Nasdaq 100  C) Not invest abroad at all  D) Remit unlimited amounts freely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LRS allows up to USD 250,000/year, and Indian MFs offer S&P 500/Nasdaq 100 exposure. A) is wrong/illegal. C) ignores legal routes. D) overstates the LRS limit. Behavioural logic: easy legal routes dissolve the access excuse behind home country bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A client wants to buy an endowment policy \"to save tax.\" The adviser should:\nA) Endorse it because tax saving is the priority  B) Suggest two endowment policies  C) Say insurance and tax are unrelated  D) Highlight the need for adequate pure insurance and the poor returns of bundled products\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The cure is highlighting adequate pure insurance and the weak returns of investment-cum-insurance products. A) repeats the bias. B) compounds it. C) is false (the link is tax-driven). Behavioural logic: separate protection from investment for better outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client with a young family wants tax-efficient protection plus growth. Which is the chapter's preferred logic?\nA) Term policy + PPF, which invariably beats an equivalent endowment policy  B) Endowment policy alone  C) ULIP only for the tax break  D) No insurance, only equities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Term + PPF invariably beats an equivalent endowment policy. B) and C) bundle inefficiently. D) leaves the family unprotected. Behavioural logic: pure protection plus an efficient investment outperforms a bundled product.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client proudly holds 14 mutual funds across caps \"for safety.\" The adviser should note this is:\nA) Perfect diversification  B) Over-concentration  C) Over-diversification; a single top-500 index fund could give similar diversification  D) Home country bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Too many overlapping funds is over-diversification; one broad index fund suffices. A) is wrong. B) is the opposite. D) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: diversification is about breadth, not the number of funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client has 90% of his wealth in his employer's single stock. The adviser identifies this as:\nA) Over-diversification  B) The golden mean  C) A nudge  D) Over-concentration requiring diversification across asset classes/securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Everything in one security is over-concentration; spread it out. A) is the opposite. B) is the ideal balance, not this. C) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: one bad event in that company could wipe him out.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A cautious client only reacts when told what he stands to lose, not what he could gain. The adviser should:\nA) Frame the recommendation as a loss avoided, matching the client's preference  B) Ignore framing and stick to numbers  C) Use only gain-framing always  D) Refuse to advise emotional clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Match the frame (loss-frame here) to how the client responds. B) misses the persuasion lever. C) ignores his preference. D) abandons the adviser's role. Behavioural logic: wording the same fact the client's way wins his buy-in.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A client made 5× on one small stock bet and now wants to bet his whole portfolio on similar picks. The adviser should:\nA) Encourage him to bet bigger  B) Note overconfidence (luck mistaken for skill) and protect the asset allocation  C) Match the bet with borrowed money  D) Praise his repeatable skill\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A quick 5× is mostly luck; overconfidence threatens his allocation, and the adviser stands between client and mistake. A), C) and D) feed the bias. Behavioural logic: one lucky win is not proof of repeatable skill.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** The adviser sets a ground rule that the portfolio rebalances whenever an asset drifts beyond its target band. This is an example of:\nA) Market timing  B) Chasing past performance  C) Over-concentration  D) A nudge that automatically books profit high and buys low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Pre-agreed rebalancing is a nudge that mechanically sells high and buys low. A) is discretionary timing. B) is the opposite behaviour. C) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: the rule forces the opposite of what emotion urges.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** During a market crash a client wants to sell everything in panic. The adviser's primary role is to:\nA) Be the cool, calming influence keeping him on the pre-planned journey  B) Agree and liquidate immediately  C) Recommend leveraged short positions  D) Stop returning his calls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In fear, the adviser is the calming anchor keeping the client on plan. B) locks in losses. C) adds risk. D) abandons him. Behavioural logic: steadying emotion prevents fear-driven mistakes.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** During a euphoric bull run a client wants to dump his plan and go all-in on equities. The adviser should:\nA) Encourage all-in buying  B) Suggest he borrow to invest more  C) Restrain over-exuberance and keep him on the agreed allocation  D) Remove all asset-allocation limits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Greed (over-exuberance) leads to rash moves; the adviser keeps him on plan. A), B) and D) feed the greed. Behavioural logic: the calm adviser prevents both fear and greed mistakes.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client emotionally insists on fully funding her daughter's foreign education even if it guts her retirement fund. The adviser should:\nA) Agree silently and drain the retirement fund  B) Refuse to discuss retirement  C) Tell her the daughter shouldn't study abroad  D) Objectively show how much retirement is compromised and offer a partial education loan the daughter can repay\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The adviser objectively shows the compromise and offers alternatives like a partial education loan. A) harms her future. B) avoids the duty. C) is judgemental and unhelpful. Behavioural logic: objective framing helps emotional clients choose well.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A client says he'll \"definitely beat the market\" because he reads business news daily. This is which bias, and the response?\nA) Overconfidence — don't let it dilute risk management or distort allocation  B) Framing — reword his goals  C) Home country bias — go global  D) Action bias — prime patience\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Believing he'll surely beat the market is overconfidence, which can dilute risk management. B), C) and D) are unrelated biases here. Behavioural logic: reading news doesn't make outperformance repeatable.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A client treats his year-end bonus as \"fun money\" to gamble on hot stocks but is careful with salary. This reflects:\nA) Anchoring  B) Mental accounting — treating money differently by origin/intended use  C) Framing  D) Action bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating bonus money differently from salary is mental accounting. A) is reliance on a reference number. C) is wording. D) is the trading urge. Behavioural logic: money is fungible; labelling it \"fun money\" justifies riskier bets.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client fixates on the price at which he originally bought a stock and refuses to sell below it. This is:\nA) Anchoring — relying on pre-existing information (purchase price)  B) Home country bias  C) Over-diversification  D) Framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Clinging to the purchase price is anchoring on pre-existing information. B), C) and D) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: the old price is irrelevant to the stock's future, yet it anchors the decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Per Prospect Theory, a client is likely to be:\nA) Risk-seeking about gains and risk-averse about losses  B) Indifferent to gains and losses  C) Always rational  D) Risk-averse about gains (sell winners early) but risk-seeking about losses (hold losers)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Prospect Theory: risk-averse on gains, risk-seeking on losses. A) reverses it. B) and C) describe a robot, not a human. Behavioural logic: people lock in gains early but cling to losers hoping to break even.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client refuses to sell a losing stock, saying \"I'll wait till it gets back to what I paid.\" Per Prospect Theory this is because:\nA) Monetary losses hurt more than equivalent gains, so he avoids realising the loss  B) He is being perfectly rational  C) He dislikes gains  D) He has home country bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Losses hurt more than equal gains, so he won't realise the loss. B) is wrong. C) is nonsense. D) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: the pain of crystallising a loss drives loss-aversion behaviour.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which best illustrates using a nudge rather than force?\nA) Locking the client's account  B) Threatening to drop the client  C) Pre-agreed ground rules the client buys into that auto-trigger good actions  D) Charging penalties for trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A nudge is a pre-agreed gentle default, not coercion. A), B) and D) are forceful or punitive. Behavioural logic: the client still chooses freely, but the easy path is the good one.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client wants \"excitement\" from his investments. Echoing Samuelson, the adviser should explain that:\nA) Investing should be dull; excitement belongs in Las Vegas, not the portfolio  B) Investing should be thrilling  C) He should day-trade for fun  D) Excitement boosts returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Samuelson's point: investing should be dull; seek excitement elsewhere. B), C) and D) contradict this. Behavioural logic: chasing thrills leads to over-trading and losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A client's portfolio has grown so that equities are far above their target weight after a rally. The pre-agreed rebalancing nudge will:\nA) Buy even more equities  B) Book some profit by trimming equities and buy into the lagging asset class  C) Sell everything  D) Do nothing ever\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rebalancing trims the overweight winner and tops up the laggard — sell high, buy low. A) chases the rally. C) is extreme. D) defeats the purpose. Behavioural logic: the rule enforces discipline against greed.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client wants to invest based purely on which fund topped the charts last year. The adviser's most relevant warning is:\nA) Reversion to the mean — last year's topper may underperform next year  B) Foreign markets are risky  C) Insurance is the first step  D) Credit cards induce spending\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Chasing the top fund ignores reversion to the mean. B), C) and D) are true elsewhere but irrelevant here. Behavioural logic: rankings flip across cycles.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A maximum exposure limit WITHIN a single asset class (e.g. per security) primarily controls:\nA) Home country bias  B) Action bias  C) Framing  D) Over-concentration in any one security\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A per-security cap prevents over-concentration within an asset class. A), B) and C) are unrelated. Behavioural logic: it stops one favourite stock from dominating the portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client says he understands US tech but is scared of \"foreign tax and legal rules.\" The adviser should:\nA) Acknowledge compliance complexity but educate on legal routes like LRS and global MF schemes  B) Tell him foreign investing is impossible  C) Say tax rules never matter  D) Force him to file foreign returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Compliance complexity is a real listed barrier, but education on legal routes is the antidote. B) is false. C) is wrong. D) is coercive and unhelpful. Behavioural logic: knowledge dissolves the fear behind home country bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** An adviser notices a client buys insurance every March purely to cut taxes. The best intervention is to:\nA) Buy more of the same policy  B) Cancel all insurance  C) Review whether the cover is adequate and appropriate, not just tax-efficient  D) Praise the tax saving and stop there\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Refocus on adequate, appropriate cover (the first step of a plan), not tax alone. A) repeats the error. B) is reckless. D) endorses the bias. Behavioural logic: tax saving merely reduces the cost of good insurance; it isn't the goal.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A client's emotional spending spikes after a stressful job loss. Beyond awareness and budgeting, the adviser's broader role is to:\nA) Help make the behaviour self-limiting so it doesn't spiral into more financial stress  B) Encourage more shopping to feel better  C) Suggest a payday loan  D) Ignore it as personal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser helps make the behaviour self-limiting before its economic cost creates more stress. B), C) and D) worsen or neglect the problem. Behavioural logic: short-term relief becomes long-term harm if unchecked.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client insists on checking and tweaking his portfolio daily. The adviser's priming message should stress:\nA) Daily trading maximises returns  B) Long-term consistency and patience; investing rewards stillness, not constant action  C) Frequent transactions are free  D) Markets must be timed daily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Priming for patience and long-term consistency counters action bias. A), C) and D) are false. Behavioural logic: constant tinkering converts investing into costly trading.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Two clients get the same advice. One is sold by \"you'll gain X,\" the other by \"you'll lose Y if you don't.\" This demonstrates that the adviser should:\nA) Diagnose and use the framing each client responds to, confirmed through discussion  B) Always use gain-framing  C) Never reframe advice  D) Give different advice to each\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Diagnose the preferred frame and confirm via discussion; the advice stays the same, only the wording changes. B) ignores individual differences. C) wastes the lever. D) is wrong — the recommendation is identical. Behavioural logic: framing affects acceptance, not the underlying advice.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A client over-invests in one asset after a lucky win, distorting his allocation. The chapter says the adviser's influence can:\nA) Guarantee future profits  B) Eliminate all market risk  C) Stand between the client and her big mistake  D) Replace the client's judgement entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The adviser's influence \"can stand between the client and her big mistake.\" A) and B) overpromise. D) overstates the adviser's role. Behavioural logic: the adviser is a guardrail against overconfidence-driven errors.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** When resources can't fund all goals, the adviser's emotional-management role is to:\nA) Objectively help prioritise goals and present alternatives (e.g. education loan)  B) Fund every goal equally regardless of resources  C) Let emotion decide entirely  D) Drop the lower-priority goal without discussion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser objectively prioritises and offers alternatives like a partial education loan. B) is impossible with limited resources. C) repeats the bias. D) is high-handed. Behavioural logic: objectivity guides emotional clients to sound trade-offs.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Ravi, a software engineer under heavy deadline stress, has run up Rs 2 lakh on his credit card buying gadgets he never uses. He says shopping \"calms him down.\" The single most accurate description plus best adviser action is:\nA) Normal consumption; do nothing  B) Retail therapy fuelled by credit-card-induced impulse spending; make him aware it's stress-driven, set a budgeted separate account, and switch to debit/cash  C) Mental accounting; relabel the spending  D) Overconfidence; encourage bigger purchases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stress-driven binge buying is retail therapy, worsened by the credit card's lack of a brake; the fix is awareness + budgeted account + debit/cash. A) ignores spiralling harm. C) misnames it. D) is absurd. Behavioural logic: a self-imposed expense limit stops the spiral.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Meena inherited a large sum and immediately spread it across 18 mutual funds plus 25 direct stocks \"to be safe.\" She now can't track anything and her returns mirror the market anyway. The adviser should explain:\nA) She is over-concentrated and should add more funds  B) She has perfect diversification  C) This is over-diversification adding no benefit; a broad index fund could match it; trim to a manageable, golden-mean portfolio  D) She has home country bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Owning dozens of overlapping holdings is unproductive over-diversification; a single broad index fund gives similar spread, so trim toward the golden mean. A) is the opposite diagnosis. B) is false. D) is unrelated. Behavioural logic: diversification is breadth, not count.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Arjun put his entire retirement corpus into one promising small-cap stock that doubled, and wants to add more. He calls himself \"a natural stock-picker.\" Identify the layered biases and best response:\nA) Healthy conviction; let him concentrate further  B) Home country bias; send him abroad  C) Framing; reword his goals  D) Over-concentration plus overconfidence (luck mistaken for skill); diversify and protect the asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Everything in one stock is over-concentration, and \"natural picker\" after one win is overconfidence; diversify and guard the allocation. A) feeds both biases. B) and C) misdiagnose. Behavioural logic: a single doubling is likely luck, and concentration risks the whole corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Sunita sells her winning funds within weeks to \"lock in gains\" but holds her losers for years \"until they recover.\" Name the theory and the adviser's framing:\nA) Prospect Theory (risk-averse on gains, risk-seeking on losses); discuss decisions versus the plan, not the purchase price  B) Action bias; tell her to trade more  C) Home country bias; go global  D) Mental accounting; relabel funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Selling winners early and clinging to losers is classic Prospect Theory behaviour. B), C) and D) misname it. Behavioural logic: losses hurt more than gains, so she avoids realising losses — the adviser refocuses on the plan, not the entry price.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Vikram refuses to sell a stock trading 40% below his purchase price, repeating \"I'll exit at my cost.\" Which two concepts are at play?\nA) Framing and action bias  B) Anchoring (on purchase price) and loss-aversion (losses hurt more than gains)  C) Home country bias and over-diversification  D) Nudging and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fixating on the buy price is anchoring; refusing to crystallise the loss is loss-aversion. A), C) and D) are unrelated combinations. Behavioural logic: the irrelevant old price plus the pain of losses traps him in a losing position.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Priya, after a 4-month bull run, wants to abandon her balanced plan and put 100% into equities \"before I miss out.\" The adviser's calmest, most disciplined response is:\nA) Agree and go all-in  B) Suggest leverage to buy more  C) Recognise greed/over-exuberance; keep her on the pre-agreed allocation; let the rebalancing rule trim, not add, equities  D) Remove allocation limits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FOMO in a rally is greed; the adviser steadies her and the rebalancing nudge actually trims equities. A), B) and D) feed the greed. Behavioural logic: the calm adviser and pre-set rules counter emotion-driven over-allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** During a sharp crash, Karthik wants to sell his entire equity portfolio \"before it goes to zero,\" abandoning a 15-year plan. The adviser should:\nA) Help him sell everything fast  B) Short the market with him  C) Tell him to add nothing and panic-hold cash forever  D) Be the calming influence, remind him of the long-term plan, and prevent fear from locking in permanent losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Fear can freeze or stampede a client; the adviser steadies him and protects the long-term plan. A) crystallises losses. B) adds risk. C) abandons the plan. Behavioural logic: selling in panic converts paper losses into real ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Lata wants the \"best education abroad\" for her son and is ready to empty her retirement fund, 8 years from her own retirement. The adviser's objective intervention is to:\nA) Empty the retirement fund as she wishes  B) Quantify the retirement compromise and propose a partial education loan repayable by the son later  C) Refuse to fund education at all  D) Tell her retirement doesn't matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Show the size of the retirement compromise and offer a partial education loan. A) sacrifices her future. C) ignores a real goal. D) is wrong advice. Behavioural logic: objective trade-off analysis helps an emotional parent decide well.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A new client only buys insurance in March for tax and has Rs 50 lakh of loans but just Rs 5 lakh of life cover. The adviser's priority is:\nA) Buy another tax-saving endowment policy  B) Focus only on his March tax deadline  C) Establish adequate, appropriate cover first (the foundation), explaining tax saving merely reduces the cost  D) Tell him insurance is optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — His cover is grossly inadequate; adequate protection is the first step, with tax saving as a bonus. A) repeats the tax-first error. B) is narrow. D) is dangerous given his loans. Behavioural logic: protection must match liabilities, not the tax calendar.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Rohit boasts that his \"gut\" picked three winners last year (he ignores his many losers) and wants to ditch his adviser's plan. This combines overconfidence with selective memory of wins. The adviser should:\nA) Congratulate his proven skill and let him go solo  B) Match his bets with margin  C) Tell him to invest only abroad  D) Gently show that wins were partly luck and not reliably repeatable, and protect the asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Remembering wins and forgetting losses fuels overconfidence; the adviser tempers it and guards the allocation. A), B) and C) feed the bias or misdiagnose. Behavioural logic: luck is not skill, and skill is rarely consistently repeatable.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An adviser wants to ensure clients \"buy low and sell high\" without relying on willpower during volatile markets. The best structural solution is:\nA) Pre-agreed ground rules for rebalancing that act as automatic nudges  B) Asking clients to time the market manually  C) Letting emotions guide each trade  D) Concentrating in last year's winner\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A pre-agreed rebalancing rule is a nudge that automates buy-low/sell-high. B) and C) rely on fragile willpower/emotion. D) is chasing performance. Behavioural logic: rules made in calm times override emotion in turbulent ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Deepa treats her Diwali bonus as \"house money\" to punt on risky IPOs but is frugal with her salary-funded SIPs. The bias and corrective insight are:\nA) Anchoring; give her a price target  B) Mental accounting; remind her money is fungible and the bonus deserves the same disciplined treatment  C) Action bias; prime patience only  D) Framing; reword the goal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating bonus and salary differently is mental accounting; all rupees should follow the same plan. A), C) and D) misname the bias. Behavioural logic: labelling money \"house money\" rationalises reckless risk-taking.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client cycles every year: gold last year, then small-caps, then crypto, always last year's winner, and his returns lag. Diagnose and prescribe:\nA) Over-diversification; cut to one fund  B) Home country bias; go abroad  C) Chasing past performance ignoring reversion to the mean; set a pre-decided asset allocation policy with upper limits  D) Framing; change wording\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Serially buying last year's winner is chasing past performance; allocation limits fix it. A), B) and D) misdiagnose. Behavioural logic: rear-view-mirror investing repeatedly buys high and sells low.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A risk-tolerance questionnaire labels a client \"aggressive,\" but in conversation she panics at any loss talk. The chapter's guidance is to:\nA) Trust the questionnaire absolutely  B) Ignore the conversation  C) Re-take the test ten times  D) Treat tool diagnoses (including framing preference) as needing reconfirmation through discussion with the client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Diagnoses, including framing preference, must be reconfirmed through discussion. A) over-trusts a tool. B) ignores key evidence. C) is pointless repetition. Behavioural logic: tools inform but conversation confirms the real client.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An NRI-aspiring client wants global exposure but believes \"Indians can't legally invest abroad.\" The adviser should correct this by explaining:\nA) It is indeed illegal  B) The LRS permits up to USD 250,000/year, and Indian MFs offer S&P 500/Nasdaq 100 access  C) Only the ultra-rich can do it secretly  D) Foreign investing always loses money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LRS (USD 250,000/year) and Indian global MF schemes make it legal and accessible. A), C) and D) are false. Behavioural logic: correcting the knowledge gap dismantles the home country bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A client agreed to an asset-allocation policy in calm times but now, mid-rally, wants to override it. The value of having set the policy in advance is that it:\nA) Can be ignored whenever convenient  B) Guarantees the highest return  C) Acts as a pre-committed nudge restraining emotion-driven changes  D) Eliminates the need for an adviser\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A pre-committed policy nudges the client to resist emotional overrides. A) defeats its purpose. B) overpromises. D) is false. Behavioural logic: pre-commitment binds the calm self against the emotional self.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client wants \"excitement and quick action\" from investing and is bored by SIPs. Echoing the chapter, the adviser should reframe by saying:\nA) Trade intraday for the adrenaline  B) Excitement raises long-term returns  C) Boredom means the plan is wrong  D) Good investing is deliberately dull; thrills belong in Las Vegas, and patience compounds wealth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Investing should be dull (Samuelson); thrills are for gambling, not portfolios. A), B) and C) feed action bias. Behavioural logic: the desire for excitement is the action bias the adviser must defuse.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client is convinced an endowment-type policy is the best \"investment\" because it bundles savings and insurance and saves tax. The adviser's strongest counter-illustration is:\nA) Endowment always wins on returns  B) For the same outlay, term insurance plus PPF invariably beats the endowment policy's ultimate return  C) Bundled products need no comparison  D) Tax saving outweighs returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Term + PPF invariably beats an equivalent endowment policy on ultimate return. A) is false. C) avoids the analysis. D) repeats the bias. Behavioural logic: unbundling protection from investment is more efficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's overconfidence leads him to repeatedly breach the per-security limit in the agreed plan. The adviser's most consistent tool is to:\nA) Raise the limit each time he asks  B) Abolish the limits  C) Enforce the pre-set maximum exposure limit within the asset class to curb over-concentration  D) Let him concentrate freely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A per-security maximum exposure limit curbs the over-concentration his overconfidence drives. A), B) and D) surrender the discipline. Behavioural logic: the rule restrains the bias the client cannot self-restrain.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A stressed client both over-shops AND over-trades when anxious. The adviser recognises a common root and applies parallel fixes:\nA) Encourage both as healthy outlets  B) Ignore both as unrelated  C) Use a credit card and day-trading to channel the energy  D) Both are emotion-driven impulses; impose self-limiting brakes — budgeted account for shopping, priming/patience and rules for trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Both behaviours are emotional impulses needing self-limiting brakes; budgeting curbs shopping, priming and rules curb trading. A) and C) worsen them. B) misses the shared cause. Behavioural logic: making impulses self-limiting addresses the underlying emotion.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client says \"frame it however you like, just tell me what to do.\" The adviser should still:\nA) Give different advice depending on mood  B) Recognise that the recommendation is the same regardless of frame; choose the frame that best convinces this client  C) Never explain the reasoning  D) Use random wording each time\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Framing changes wording, not the underlying advice; pick the frame that convinces. A) wrongly varies the advice. C) and D) are poor practice. Behavioural logic: framing is a persuasion tool layered on identical advice.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client demands the adviser \"guarantee\" he'll never lose, citing the adviser's role in managing emotions. The accurate boundary is:\nA) The adviser can guarantee no losses  B) The adviser controls the market  C) The adviser steadies emotions and keeps the plan on track but cannot eliminate market risk or guarantee returns  D) The adviser should promise high returns to reassure him\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The adviser manages emotions and keeps the plan on course, not market outcomes. A), B) and D) overpromise. Behavioural logic: emotional steadiness is the deliverable, not guaranteed profits.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client over-invested abroad after reading hype about US tech, ignoring his plan's limits — the mirror image of home country bias. The adviser should:\nA) Tell him foreign is always better  B) Tell him domestic is always better  C) Remove all limits  D) Re-anchor him to the agreed asset-allocation limits regardless of which market is hyped\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Whether the hype is domestic or foreign, the discipline is the pre-set allocation limits. A) and B) are absolutist. C) abandons discipline. Behavioural logic: limits guard against over-exposure driven by any hype, in any direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A couple disagrees: the husband wants to gamble the bonus, the wife wants safety. The adviser's behaviourally sound approach is to:\nA) Side with the husband for excitement  B) Use objective goal-prioritisation and pre-agreed rules, framing the plan to satisfy both, treating all money under one disciplined plan  C) Side with the wife and avoid all risk  D) Split into \"his fun money\" and \"her safe money\" with no plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Objective prioritisation, shared rules and good framing unify the plan. A) and C) take sides. D) institutionalises mental accounting with no discipline. Behavioural logic: one disciplined plan beats emotionally compartmentalised money.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client checks his portfolio app 20 times daily and feels compelled to react to every tick. The adviser's preventative (priming) measure, set in advance, should be:\nA) Pre-commit him to long-term patience and reduced monitoring before volatility hits  B) Encourage more frequent checking  C) Add intraday alerts  D) Let him react to each tick\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Priming for patience and less monitoring, in advance, defuses action bias before volatility triggers it. B), C) and D) amplify it. Behavioural logic: preparing the mind ahead of time builds resistance to the trading itch.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client wants to copy a friend who \"got rich quick\" on one lucky stock. Identify the bias being imported and the adviser's response:\nA) Overconfidence via survivorship (luck seen as skill); refocus on the client's own plan and allocation discipline  B) The friend has a repeatable system; copy it  C) Home country bias; invest abroad  D) Framing; reword the goal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The friend's lucky win looks like skill, breeding overconfidence; the adviser refocuses on the client's disciplined plan. B) assumes false repeatability. C) and D) misdiagnose. Behavioural logic: a single visible winner hides many invisible losers.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client agreed to rebalance annually but now, with equities soaring, wants to \"let winners run.\" The adviser should explain the rebalancing nudge exists precisely to:\nA) Book profits at highs and add to laggards at lows, overriding the emotional urge to chase winners  B) Let winners run indefinitely  C) Sell the laggards instead  D) Stop rebalancing in bull markets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Rebalancing deliberately trims winners and tops up laggards, countering the emotional urge. B), C) and D) defeat its purpose. Behavioural logic: the rule enforces sell-high/buy-low against greed.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client's anxiety about a far-off goal (retirement) is low while a near, emotional goal (child's wedding) dominates, leading to under-funding retirement. The adviser should:\nA) Objectively highlight how much the vital retirement goal is being compromised and discuss alternatives  B) Fully fund the wedding and ignore retirement  C) Tell her retirement is unimportant  D) Refuse to plan the wedding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser objectively shows the retirement compromise and explores alternatives. B) and C) endanger her future. D) is unhelpful. Behavioural logic: emotional proximity distorts priorities; objectivity restores balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client insists \"I'm definitely above-average at picking stocks.\" The chapter's survey example (most people rate themselves 60–75th percentile) helps the adviser explain that:\nA) Most people overrate themselves — a statistical impossibility — so his confidence likely overstates his real skill  B) He is certainly above average  C) Self-assessment is always accurate  D) Percentiles don't apply to investing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The percentile survey shows widespread overconfidence; his self-rating likely overstates skill. B) and C) accept the bias. D) dodges the point. Behavioural logic: it's arithmetically impossible for most people to be above average.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Across all these scenarios, the unifying role of the Investment Adviser in behavioural finance is best summarised as:\nA) Understanding clients' emotional impulses and using awareness, pre-agreed rules/nudges, education, framing and objective guidance to keep them on a sound long-term plan  B) Maximising the number of transactions  C) Guaranteeing market-beating returns  D) Letting clients act purely on emotion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser's role is to understand impulses and deploy awareness, nudges, education, framing and objectivity to keep clients on plan. B) and D) cause harm. C) overpromises. Behavioural logic: the adviser is the calm, structured counterweight to the client's emotional, biased brain.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Meet the client.** Anjali, 42, is a senior IT manager in Bengaluru earning well but under constant work stress. She comes to you, her Investment Adviser, with the following picture:\n\n- When stressed, she shops online late at night on her credit card, running up Rs 1.5 lakh she can't fully explain.\n- Last year gold rose, so she moved most of her money into gold; the year before she had chased small-cap funds.\n- She owns 16 mutual funds across all categories \"to stay diversified,\" yet her returns just track the market and she can't keep up with them.\n- She refuses to invest outside India, saying \"I only understand Indian companies.\"\n- She buys a new endowment insurance policy every March \"to save tax,\" but her actual life cover is tiny relative to her home loan.\n- She made a quick 3× on one stock tip and now wants to put half her portfolio into similar tips.\n- She wants to fully fund her daughter's overseas university in 6 years, even if it means draining her own retirement fund, which is 12 years away.\n\nWork through the five sub-questions below.\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 1: Anjali's late-night credit-card shopping when stressed — name the behaviour and describe the two-step fix plus the card issue.**\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\nThis is **retail therapy** — a shopping/buying binge done to relieve emotional stress, not genuine need. In small doses it relieves stress, but it rarely stays moderate, and the economic cost eventually creates *more* stress.\n\n**The two-step fix:**\n1. **Make her conscious** that the spending is *triggered by stress*, not need — awareness comes first.\n2. **Set a specific budget** for indulgence; in extreme cases open a **separate account** holding only the budgeted amount, spending only from there via debit card or cash. This is a **self-imposed brake** / built-in expense limit.\n\n**The card issue:** Her **credit card induces impulsive spending** because it has no built-in brake — she can spend money she doesn't have. Switching to **debit/cash** restores the natural limit.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 2: She rotates her whole portfolio into last year's winner (gold, then small-caps). Name the bias, explain why it fails, and give the antidote.**\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\nThis is **chasing past performance** — investing in last year's winners assuming they'll keep winning. The chapter calls it **\"driving a car by looking in the rear-view mirror\"** — bound to crash.\n\n**Why it fails:** Most asset classes move in **cycles of growth and decline**, with an **inevitable reversion to the mean** (returns drift back to the long-run average). Last year's hot asset is often this year's laggard, so she keeps buying high and selling low.\n\n**Antidote:** A **pre-decided asset allocation policy with upper limits for each asset class** prevents over-exposure to any one hot asset. A **maximum exposure limit within an asset class** does the same for any single security. These limits, set in calm times, act as **nudges** that resist the emotional urge to chase.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 3: She holds 16 overlapping funds yet tracks the market, AND refuses to invest abroad. Diagnose both, and give the corrective for each.**\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Issue A — Over-diversification:** Owning 16 funds she can't manage adds *no real benefit* because diversification doesn't come from the *number* of securities. She could achieve similar diversification by simply buying a **single broad index fund** (e.g. a top-500 index fund). The adviser helps her find the **golden mean** — appropriate diversification based on her **risk profile, resources and time frame** — by trimming to a manageable portfolio.\n\n**Issue B — Home country bias:** Refusing foreign investment because only Indian companies feel familiar costs her the **diversification benefit** of assets that don't move in tandem with local markets. The **antidote** is for the adviser to get **educated on global investing** and then **educate her**, pointing to legal routes: the **LRS (up to USD 250,000/year)** and **Indian mutual fund schemes** investing in global indexes like the **S&P 500 and Nasdaq 100**.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 4: Her March endowment-policy buying for tax, and her quick 3× stock win — name both biases and the adviser's response to each.**\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\n**Insurance-for-tax bias:** Buying insurance *only* to save tax leads to the **wrong and/or inadequate** insurance — exactly her situation (tiny cover versus a large home loan). The correct view: **adequate, appropriate insurance is the first step** of any financial plan; tax saving merely *reduces the cost* of good insurance. The cure is to **highlight the need for adequate pure insurance** and the **poor returns of investment-cum-insurance products** — for the same outlay, **term insurance + PPF invariably beats an endowment policy**.\n\n**Overconfidence from the 3× win:** A quick 3× is **mostly luck**, and even if partly skill, it's unlikely to be **consistently repeatable**. She is **attributing luck to skill**, tempting her to over-invest and wreck her asset allocation. Here the **adviser's influence can stand between the client and her big mistake** — temper the overconfidence and protect the agreed allocation (and its per-security limits).\n</details>\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 5: She wants to drain her retirement fund (12 years away) to fully fund her daughter's overseas education (6 years away). How should the adviser handle this emotionally charged trade-off?**\n\n<details><summary>Worked Answer</summary>\n\nThis is the classic **goal-prioritisation under emotion** problem and the **greed/fear (here, emotional attachment) cycle** where a near, emotionally-charged goal crowds out a vital but distant one. Anjali is willing to sacrifice **retirement** (feels far away, \"not important now\") for her daughter's education.\n\nThe adviser plays an **objective role**:\n- **Quantify the compromise:** show exactly how much the vital **retirement** goal would be damaged by draining the fund — retirement is a goal she cannot take a loan for, while education is.\n- **Offer alternatives:** suggest a **partial education loan** for the higher education, which the **daughter can repay from her own earnings** after completing the course.\n\nThis objective, calm guidance — being the **cool, calming influence** and presenting facts and alternatives — helps a highly emotional client make the **right decision** without simply overriding her feelings. The adviser doesn't dictate; he illuminates the trade-off and the options.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "18", "title": "Risk Profiling for Investors", "module": "Behavioural Finance & Risk Profiling", "notes": "# Chapter 18: Risk Profiling for Investors — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- What **risk profiling** is and the approach to doing it.\n- The **parameters** (factors) that affect how much risk a person can/should take.\n- How risk profiling drives **asset allocation** (Strategic, Tactical, Dynamic), model portfolios, and rebalancing.\n\n> **Three words you must never mix up:**\n> - **Risk appetite / willingness** = how much risk a person *wants* to take (psychological — their attitude to volatility and losses).\n> - **Risk capacity / ability** = how much risk a person *can financially afford* to take (their resources — income, savings, dependents).\n> - **Risk tolerance** = the level of risk a client is *willing to accept*, measured by a questionnaire. It blends willingness and ability.\n>\n> A young person may be *willing* to take big risk (high appetite) but if they have huge loans and many dependents, their *ability* is low. Good profiling looks at **both**.\n\n---\n\n## 18.1 Risk profiling and the risk profiling approach\n\n### The coin-toss example (understand \"willingness\")\nYou pay **Rs. 1,00,000** to toss a coin. Call heads/tails; if right, you win **Rs. 2,10,000**.\n\nLet's compute the **expected (average) payoff**:\n- If you **guess right** → net gain = 2,10,000 − 1,00,000 = **Rs. 1,10,000**. At 50% chance → expected win = **Rs. 55,000**.\n- If you **guess wrong** → you lose your stake = **Rs. 1,00,000**. At 50% chance → expected loss = **Rs. 50,000**.\n- **Total expected payoff = 55,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 5,000** (positive — statistically a \"good\" bet).\n\nEven though the maths is favourable, **most people refuse** — the **fear of losing Rs. 1,00,000** is too strong. More people agree only when the prize rises to Rs. 2,50,000 or Rs. 3,00,000.\n\n**Lesson:** **Willingness to take risk is about psychology, not just the numbers.** Some people find volatility and possible losses distressing; others are relaxed about it.\n\n💡 Whether the risk is *actually* taken also depends on:\n- **Resources** — more resources → more likely to take the risk (higher *ability*).\n- **Need** — the more important the payoff is, the more likely they take the risk.\n\n### Factors affecting risk appetite (Table 18.1)\n| Factor | Effect on risk appetite (all else equal) |\n|---|---|\n| **Earning members** | More earning members → **higher** risk appetite |\n| **Dependent members** | More dependents → **lower** risk appetite |\n| **Life expectancy** | Longer life expectancy → **higher** risk appetite |\n| **Age** | Lower age → **higher** risk can be taken |\n| **Employability** | Well-qualified, multi-skilled → can take **more** risk |\n| **Nature of job** | Steady job → can take **more** risk |\n| **Knowledge of markets** | Better informed → can take **more** market risk |\n| **Psyche** | Daring/adventurous → mentally accept downsides better |\n| **Capital base** | Higher capital → better able to absorb losses (**more** risk) |\n| **Regularity of income** | Regular income → can take **more** risk than irregular income |\n\n**Analogy:** Risk appetite is like how spicy a meal you'll order. *Willingness* = how much you like spice. *Ability* = whether your stomach can handle it. Both matter before you order.\n\n### What a risk profile measures\nA client's **financial risk tolerance** — attitudes, values, motivations, preferences, experiences — is captured in a **risk profile**, typically via a **questionnaire**. Responses become a **score** that classifies the client into a category.\n\n---\n\n## 18.2 Parameters for risk profiling\n\nOnline risk-profiling tools ask a few questions and generate a score. **Limitations:**\n- Investors may try to **\"guess the right answer\"** — but there is **no right answer**. The tool only works if the investor is **truthful**.\n- Some advanced profilers use **scenario responses**, or assess profile from a client's **actual transaction history**.\n- Tools vary in **robustness**; the adviser must check this and use them **judiciously**.\n\n### The 3-grade classification\n| Grade | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **I. Conservative** | Dislikes risk; new to risky instruments; prefers bank accounts / safe income instruments; *may* invest a small portion in risky assets for the long term. |\n| **II. Moderate** | Some investment experience (including equities); understands risk is needed for long-term goals; willing to risk **part** of assets. |\n| **III. Aggressive** | Experienced; used a range of products; active manager; understands risk is crucial for long-term returns; willing to risk a **significant portion** of assets. |\n\n### SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulation 16 — what the IA MUST do\nThe IA must ensure risk profiling is done so the advice/product is **suitable**. Specifically:\n1. **Obtain relevant information** — age; investment objectives & time frames; resources (income, existing investments, innate ability to take risks, loan details).\n2. Have an **objective process** to assess the client's **willingness AND ability** to take risk (including ability to **incur and absorb losses**) — not slanted to over/under-weight certain factors.\n3. Any **tools/questionnaires** must be **fit for purpose**; limitations identified and mitigated.\n4. Questionnaires must be **fair, clear, not misleading**, not vague, not in difficult language, and contain **no leading questions**.\n5. Risk profile must be done **before any advice**. **No free trial** without communicating the risk profile to the client and getting their **consent** on it.\n6. Risk profile must be **updated periodically** — recommended on **special events**: change in family composition, income/expenses, or assets/liabilities.\n\n⚠️ Trap: \"No free trial without communicating the risk profile and obtaining consent\" — easy exam point.\n\n---\n\n## 18.3 Role of risk profiling in asset allocation\n\n> **What is \"asset allocation\"?** Dividing your portfolio among different **asset classes** (equity, debt, gold, real estate, cash) so a fall in one is cushioned by others. *\"Don't put all your eggs in one basket.\"*\n\n**Why it works:** Asset classes are driven by **different factors** and don't move together. Example: in the **2007–09 recession**, equities fell in many countries but **gold rose** — an investor in both did better than one in equities alone. An **efficient asset allocation** combines assets with **low or negative correlation** (they don't rise and fall together).\n\n💡 International research suggests **asset allocation and investment policy explain portfolio performance better than stock selection** (picking individual securities).\n\n### The three types of asset allocation\n| Type | What it is | Key features |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Strategic** | Allocation **aligned to financial goals** | Based on required return, time horizon, and risk profile. A **long-term plan**, reviewed periodically. Allocation changes only if **fundamentals** change — NOT based on expected asset performance. Rebalanced periodically back to the original mix. Protected from forecast errors, but won't benefit from over-weighting a hot asset. |\n| **Tactical** | A **call on the likely behaviour of the market** | E.g., going **overweight equities** because you expect a market rally. Only for **seasoned investors with large surpluses**; even then, applied to a **limited portion**; the bulk stays strategic. |\n| **Dynamic** | Uses **pre-defined models** to allocate | Triggers (asset valuations or portfolio performance) decide reallocation. **Removes the subjective element.** |\n\n💡 Exam favourite: **Tactical asset allocation depends on the market**; strategic does not.\n\n### 18.3.2 Model Portfolios\nSince needs vary, no single portfolio fits all. Advisers use **model portfolios** — the most appropriate asset mix for each profile, then tweak per the individual.\n\n| Client profile | Model allocation (illustrative) |\n|---|---|\n| **Young BPO employee, no dependents (high risk)** | 50% equities/diversified equity (via SIP); 20% sector funds; 10% gold ETF; 10% diversified debt/FD; 10% short-term debt & liquid/savings |\n| **Young married, single income, 2 school kids (moderate)** | 35% equities; 10% sector funds; 15% gold ETF; 30% diversified debt/FD; 10% liquid & short-term debt/savings |\n| **Single income, grown-up children not yet settled (conservative)** | 35% equities; 15% gold ETF; 10% gilt fund; 20% diversified debt/FD; 20% short-term debt & liquid/savings |\n| **Couple in their 70s, no family support (conservative–moderate)** | 15% equity index; 5% gold ETF; 35% debt-oriented hybrid/MIS/SCSS; 30% diversified debt/FD; 15% liquid/savings |\n\n> 💡 These percentages are **illustrative and highly subjective**. The key point: have a model portfolio for each distinct profile, then tweak. (A physically/mentally sound 70s couple with a large corpus might take slightly more equity than the standard 70s model.)\n\n### Portfolio construction — matching objectives to investments\nNo single investment gives growth + liquidity + income + capital protection + good return. Build a portfolio:\n\n| Objective | Suitable investments |\n|---|---|\n| **Growth / appreciation** | Equity shares & equity funds, real estate, gold |\n| **Regular income** | Deposits, debt instruments & debt funds, real estate |\n| **Liquidity** | Cash, bank deposits, short-term MF schemes |\n| **Capital preservation** | Cash, bank deposits, ultra-short-term funds |\n\n**Concentration warnings (from the chapter):**\n- **Jayesh** (only real estate) → can't sell/borrow quickly when he needs cash → **no liquidity**.\n- **Kamal** (all in savings account) → very low interest → **can't accumulate enough**.\n- **Latika** (all equities) → value keeps fluctuating → **uncertain** funds when needed.\n- **Harmeet** (mostly gold jewellery) → **no income** for monthly expenses.\n- **Gayatri** (all bank FDs) → fixed interest but **rising expenses** → income can't keep up.\n\nThe common risk = **concentration** in one category. Spreading across assets cushions a fall in any one. A person near retirement → more debt, less equity; a high-earner with far-off goals → more equity. Suitable allocation depends on **investment period + ability + willingness** to take risk.\n\n### Review and Rebalancing\n**Review** the portfolio periodically (ideally at least **once a year**) and ask:\n- Are all goals still relevant?\n- Are goals on target in the required time-frame?\n- Are investments performing as expected?\n- Do investments need changing if no longer suitable?\n\nIf a goal is under-funded, the investor can **save more** or **divert funds from a less important goal**.\n\n**Rebalancing** = modifying exposure to different asset classes so the portfolio stays aligned to the investor's risk/return needs — **not** to chase short-term price moves.\n\n💡 **Example (Jayant):** Saved for kids' education in **equity** for 8 years; goal is now **4 years away**. To avoid equity fluctuations damaging the accumulated amount, he **moves to less risky investments** as the goal nears. (Lesson: shift from growth to safety as a goal approaches.)\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Willingness** (psychology) vs **ability/capacity** (resources) vs **tolerance** (measured, blends both).\n- Coin-toss: even a statistically good bet is refused due to **fear of loss** → willingness is psychological.\n- Factors ↑ appetite: more earners, longer life expectancy, lower age, employability, steady job, market knowledge, daring psyche, high capital, regular income. **More dependents ↓ appetite.**\n- 3 grades: **Conservative / Moderate / Aggressive.**\n- **SEBI Reg 16:** objective profiling (willingness + ability), fit-for-purpose & non-leading questionnaires, profile **before advice**, **no free trial without consent**, update on life events.\n- Asset allocation: combine **low/negative-correlation** assets. Allocation matters more than stock selection.\n- **Strategic** (goal-aligned, long-term, doesn't chase performance) / **Tactical** (market call, seasoned investors, limited portion) / **Dynamic** (model-driven, no subjectivity).\n- **Model portfolios** per profile; tweak per individual.\n- Match objective → investment (growth/income/liquidity/preservation). Avoid **concentration**.\n- **Review ≥ once a year**; **rebalance** to stay aligned (not to chase prices); shift from growth to safety as a goal nears.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Risk appetite / willingness** | How much risk a person *wants* to take (psychology) |\n| **Risk capacity / ability** | How much risk a person *can afford* (resources) |\n| **Risk tolerance** | Risk a client is willing to accept; measured by questionnaire |\n| **3 grades** | Conservative, Moderate, Aggressive |\n| **SEBI Reg 16** | Mandates risk profiling for suitability; no free trial without consent |\n| **Strategic AA** | Goal-aligned, long-term, does NOT depend on the market |\n| **Tactical AA** | Market call (e.g., overweight equity); seasoned investors only |\n| **Dynamic AA** | Model-driven; removes subjectivity |\n| **Correlation** | How assets move together; want **low/negative** for diversification |\n| **Review frequency** | At least **once a year** |\n| **2007–09** | Equities fell, gold rose — example of diversification benefit |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 18: Risk Profiling for Investors — Flashcards\n\n> **How to use:** Cover the answer, recall it out loud, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Define risk appetite (willingness).\n> **A:** How much risk a person *wants* to take — driven by their **psychology** (attitude to volatility and losses), more than their finances.\n\n**Q2.** Define risk capacity (ability).\n> **A:** How much risk a person *can financially afford* to take, based on resources — income, capital base, dependents, loans.\n\n**Q3.** Define risk tolerance.\n> **A:** The level of risk a client is **willing to accept**, typically measured by a **questionnaire** that estimates both ability and willingness; responses become a score.\n\n**Q4.** In the coin-toss example (pay Rs.1,00,000, win Rs.2,10,000 if correct), what is the expected payoff?\n> **A:** Expected win = 50% × 1,10,000 = Rs. 55,000; expected loss = 50% × 1,00,000 = Rs. 50,000; **net expected payoff = Rs. 5,000 (positive)**. Yet most refuse due to fear of losing Rs. 1,00,000.\n\n**Q5.** What does the coin-toss example teach?\n> **A:** Willingness to take risk is **psychological**, not just statistical. People need a much higher payoff before the fear of loss is overcome.\n\n**Q6.** Two things (besides willingness) that decide if risk is actually taken?\n> **A:** **Resources** (more resources → more likely) and **need** (more important the payoff → more likely).\n\n**Q7.** How do earning members vs dependent members affect risk appetite?\n> **A:** More **earning members** → higher appetite. More **dependents** → lower appetite.\n\n**Q8.** How do age and life expectancy affect risk appetite?\n> **A:** **Lower age** → higher risk possible. **Longer life expectancy** → higher appetite.\n\n**Q9.** How do employability, nature of job and capital base affect appetite?\n> **A:** Well-qualified/multi-skilled → more risk; **steady job** → more risk; **higher capital base** → can absorb losses → more risk.\n\n**Q10.** How does regularity of income affect appetite?\n> **A:** **Regular income** → can take more risk than someone with unpredictable income.\n\n**Q11.** Name the three risk-profile grades and one trait of each.\n> **A:** **Conservative** (dislikes risk, prefers safe instruments); **Moderate** (some experience, risks part of assets); **Aggressive** (experienced, risks a significant portion).\n\n**Q12.** Why can online risk-profiling tools be unreliable?\n> **A:** Investors may try to **\"guess the right answer\"** (there is none); the tool only works if the investor is **truthful**. Tools also vary in robustness.\n\n**Q13.** Under SEBI Reg 16, what information must the IA obtain?\n> **A:** Age; investment objectives & time frames; resources (income, existing investments, innate ability to take risk, loans).\n\n**Q14.** What must SEBI-compliant risk questionnaires NOT do?\n> **A:** Not be misleading, vague, in difficult/complex language, and must contain **no leading questions**; they must be fair and clear.\n\n**Q15.** When must risk profiling be done, and what about free trials?\n> **A:** **Before any investment advice.** **No free trial** can be offered without communicating the risk profile to the client and obtaining their **consent** on it.\n\n**Q16.** When should a risk profile be updated?\n> **A:** Periodically, and especially on **special events**: change in family composition, income/expenses, or assets/liabilities.\n\n**Q17.** What is asset allocation, and why does it work?\n> **A:** Dividing the portfolio among asset classes. It works because asset classes have **low/negative correlation** — a fall in one is cushioned by a rise in another.\n\n**Q18.** Give the 2007–09 example of diversification.\n> **A:** Equities fell in many countries but **gold rose**; an investor in both did better than one in equities alone.\n\n**Q19.** What does research say about asset allocation vs stock selection?\n> **A:** **Asset allocation and investment policy explain portfolio performance better** than selecting individual securities.\n\n**Q20.** Define Strategic Asset Allocation.\n> **A:** Allocation **aligned to financial goals** (required return, time horizon, risk profile). Long-term; changes only if fundamentals change; **does not** chase expected asset performance; rebalanced back to the original mix.\n\n**Q21.** Define Tactical Asset Allocation.\n> **A:** A **call on likely market behaviour** (e.g., going overweight equities expecting a rally). Suitable only for **seasoned investors with large surpluses**, applied to a limited portion.\n\n**Q22.** Define Dynamic Asset Allocation.\n> **A:** Uses **pre-defined models**; reallocation triggered by asset valuations or portfolio performance. **Removes the subjective element.**\n\n**Q23.** Which asset allocation type \"depends on the market\"?\n> **A:** **Tactical** asset allocation. (Strategic does not depend on the market.)\n\n**Q24.** Match objective to investment: growth, income, liquidity, capital preservation.\n> **A:** Growth → equity/equity funds, real estate, gold. Income → deposits, debt/debt funds, real estate. Liquidity → cash, bank deposits, short-term MF. Preservation → cash, bank deposits, ultra-short-term funds.\n\n**Q25.** What single risk did Jayesh (only real estate) and Latika (only equity) share?\n> **A:** **Concentration risk** — their whole portfolio is in one category, so it all falls together.\n\n**Q26.** What is a model portfolio?\n> **A:** The asset-allocation mix most appropriate for a given investor profile, which is then tweaked for the specific individual.\n\n**Q27.** How does a suitable allocation change near retirement?\n> **A:** Higher allocation to **safer** investments (debt), lower to equity, as time horizon shortens and ability to recover from losses falls.\n\n**Q28.** How often should a portfolio be reviewed, and what four questions does review answer?\n> **A:** At least **once a year**. Are goals still relevant? On target in time? Investments performing as expected? Do investments need changing?\n\n**Q29.** What is rebalancing — and what is it NOT for?\n> **A:** Modifying exposure to asset classes to stay **aligned to the investor's risk/return needs**. It is **NOT** for chasing short-term price movements.\n\n**Q30.** What did Jayant do as his children's education goal neared?\n> **A:** After 8 years in equity with the goal 4 years away, he **moved funds to less risky investments** to protect the accumulated amount from equity fluctuations.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 18: Risk Profiling for Investors — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Questions are grouped into three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Easy (recall), 🟡 Medium (application), and 🔴 Hard (scenario). Remember: the NISM exam applies **25% negative marking** (a wrong answer costs you one-quarter of a mark), so guess only when you can eliminate options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** In the coin-toss example, how much does the person pay for one chance to play the game?\nA) Rs. 50,000  B) Rs. 1,00,000  C) Rs. 2,10,000  D) Rs. 5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The person pays a stake of Rs. 1,00,000 for one chance to play. If they guess right they win Rs. 2,10,000 (option C is the prize, not the stake). Rs. 50,000 (A) is the expected loss amount, and Rs. 5,000 (D) is the total expected payoff — both are derived figures, not the cost to play.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the coin-toss game, what is the prize the person wins if they guess correctly?\nA) Rs. 1,10,000  B) Rs. 1,00,000  C) Rs. 2,10,000  D) Rs. 55,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If the coin lands on the chosen side, the person wins Rs. 2,10,000. The net gain is Rs. 1,10,000 (A) = 2,10,000 − 1,00,000 stake. Rs. 1,00,000 (B) is the stake. Rs. 55,000 (D) is the expected win (50% × 1,10,000). Only C is the actual prize amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In the coin-toss example, what is the total expected payoff of playing the game?\nA) Rs. 55,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 1,10,000  D) Rs. 5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Expected win = 50% × 1,10,000 = Rs. 55,000; expected loss = 50% × 1,00,000 = Rs. 50,000. Net expected payoff = 55,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 5,000. So A is just the expected win, B is just the expected loss, and C is the gross gain if right. Only D combines them correctly into the net expected value.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The coin-toss example mainly illustrates that willingness to take risk has more to do with:\nA) The investor's tax bracket  B) The individual's psychology  C) Government regulation  D) The size of the brokerage fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even though the bet is statistically favourable, most people refuse because the fear of losing distresses them — showing willingness is driven by psychology, not just the maths. Tax bracket (A), regulation (C) and brokerage (D) are not what the example highlights; it is about individual attitude to volatility and loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Whether an investor actually takes a risk is affected by both their resources and:\nA) The need to take the risk  B) The colour of their car  C) The bank's lending rate  D) The stock exchange timings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The chapter states the actual taking of risk depends on resources (more resources → more likely) and the need for the payoff (the more important it is, the more likely the risk is taken). Options B, C and D are irrelevant distractors with no link to the risk decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** According to Table 18.1, as the number of earning members in a family increases, risk appetite:\nA) Increases  B) Decreases  C) Stays the same  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — More earning members means more income coming in to cushion losses, so risk appetite increases (all else equal). It does not decrease (B) or stay flat (C); A is the direct statement from Table 18.1. \"Zero\" (D) is nonsensical.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** According to Table 18.1, as the number of dependent members increases, risk appetite:\nA) Increases  B) Decreases  C) Is unaffected  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — More dependents means more financial obligations and less spare capacity to absorb losses, so risk appetite decreases. This is the opposite of earning members. So A, C and D are wrong; the relationship is a clear decrease.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** According to Table 18.1, a longer life expectancy leads to a risk appetite that is:\nA) Lower  B) Unchanged  C) Higher  D) Negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A longer life expectancy means a longer investment horizon and more time to recover from downturns, so risk appetite is higher. Lower (A) and unchanged (B) contradict the table; \"negative\" (D) is meaningless for appetite.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** According to Table 18.1, the relationship between age and the risk that can be taken is:\nA) Higher age, higher risk  B) Lower age, higher risk  C) Age has no effect  D) Risk peaks at retirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lower the age, the higher the risk that can be taken, because a younger person has more time to recover from losses. A is reversed, C contradicts the table, and D is not stated anywhere.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Per Table 18.1, well-qualified and multi-skilled professionals can afford to take:\nA) Less risk  B) No risk  C) The same risk as everyone  D) More risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — High employability means they can find a job easily if income is disrupted, so they can afford more risk. A and B understate this, and C ignores that employability specifically improves the ability to take risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** According to Table 18.1, people who earn a regular income compared with those with unpredictable income can take:\nA) Less risk  B) More risk  C) Identical risk  D) No risk at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regularity of income improves the ability to absorb losses and meet obligations, so a regular earner can take more risk than someone with irregular/unpredictable income. A is reversed; C and D are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Per Table 18.1, a higher capital base improves an investor's ability to:\nA) Avoid all taxes  B) Guarantee returns  C) Financially absorb the downsides that come with risk  D) Skip risk profiling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A larger capital base means losses hurt proportionately less, so the investor can financially take the downsides of risk. It has nothing to do with avoiding taxes (A), guaranteeing returns (B — impossible), or skipping profiling (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A client's financial risk tolerance — attitudes, values, motivations, preferences and experiences — is measured with a:\nA) Risk profile  B) Credit score  C) Tax return  D) Bank passbook\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — These attitudes and experiences are captured in a risk profile, typically via a questionnaire. A credit score (B) measures creditworthiness, a tax return (C) reports income/tax, and a passbook (D) shows transactions — none measure risk tolerance.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Risk tolerance is best defined as:\nA) The maximum loss allowed by SEBI  B) The assumed level of risk a client is willing to accept  C) The interest rate on deposits  D) The return guaranteed by an adviser\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk tolerance is the assumed level of risk a client is willing to accept, measured using questionnaires that estimate ability and willingness. It is not a SEBI loss cap (A), a deposit rate (C), or a guaranteed return (D — advisers cannot guarantee returns).\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Responses to a risk profile questionnaire are typically converted into a:\nA) Loan  B) Insurance policy  C) Score that classifies the investor's risk preferences  D) Demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The questionnaire responses are converted into a score that classifies investors into categories characterizing their risk preferences. They do not become a loan (A), an insurance policy (B), or a demat account (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A key limitation of some online risk-profiling surveys is that the investor may:\nA) Be charged too much  B) Forget their password  C) Be classified as aggressive automatically  D) Try to \"guess\" the right answer when there is no right answer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A noted limitation is that investors try to guess a \"right\" answer, even though there is no right answer; profiling loses meaning if the investor is not truthful. Fees (A), passwords (B) and automatic classification (C) are not the limitation the chapter describes.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Risk profiling loses its meaning if the investor:\nA) Is too young  B) Is not truthful in his answers  C) Uses a mobile phone  D) Has a high income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Profiling is a tool that helps the investor, but it only works if the investor answers truthfully. Age (A), device (C) and income (D) do not by themselves destroy the meaning of profiling — dishonesty does.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Some advanced risk profilers are built on the investor's responses to different:\nA) Scenarios presented before the investor  B) Advertisements  C) Newspaper articles  D) Phone calls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Advanced profilers present various scenarios to the investor and assess responses. Service providers can also use the actual transaction record of regular clients. Advertisements (B), articles (C) and phone calls (D) are not the basis described.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** In the 3-grade classification, which type of investor does NOT like to take risk and prefers bank accounts or safe income-yielding instruments?\nA) Aggressive  B) Moderate  C) Conservative  D) Tactical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Conservative investors dislike risk, are typically new to risky instruments, and prefer bank accounts or safe income instruments. Aggressive (A) and Moderate (B) accept more risk, and \"Tactical\" (D) is a type of asset allocation, not an investor grade.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Moderate investor is described as someone who:\nA) Refuses to invest in equities ever  B) Will risk a significant portion of their assets  C) Has never invested before  D) Understands they must take investment risks to meet long-term goals and will risk part of their assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Moderate investors have some experience (including equities), understand risk is needed for long-term goals, and are willing to risk part of their assets. Refusing equities forever (A) suits a conservative, risking a significant portion (B) suits an aggressive, and never investing (C) does not fit a moderate.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which investor type is willing to take risk with a \"significant portion\" of their assets?\nA) Conservative  B) Moderate  C) Aggressive  D) Reluctant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Aggressive investors are experienced, may actively manage their investments, and are willing to risk a significant portion of assets. Conservative (A) risks only a small portion, Moderate (B) risks part, and \"Reluctant\" (D) is not a defined grade.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which regulation requires the Investment Adviser to ensure that a client's risk profiling is done to make the advice suitable?\nA) SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulation 16  B) Companies Act Section 16  C) RBI Circular 16  D) Income Tax Rule 16\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulation 16 mandates risk profiling so the recommended product is suitable for the client. The Companies Act (B), an RBI circular (C) and an Income Tax rule (D) do not govern this requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Under SEBI Reg 16, the process to assess a client's willingness and ability to take risk must be:\nA) Biased toward equities  B) Decided by the client alone  C) Objective and not give undue weightage to certain factors  D) Kept secret from the client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The process must be objective and not geared to over-weight some factors and under-weight others. It must not be biased (A), is the adviser's responsibility (not the client's alone, B), and must actually be communicated to the client (not secret, D).\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under SEBI Reg 16, any questionnaire used for risk profiling must be:\nA) Vague and complex  B) Full of leading questions  C) Fair, clear and not misleading  D) Written only in legal jargon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Questionnaires must be fair, clear and not misleading; not vague; not in difficult/complex language; and must not contain leading questions. So A, B and D each describe exactly what the regulation prohibits.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Under SEBI Reg 16, when must the client's risk profile be completed?\nA) After the first year of investing  B) Only at the client's death  C) Whenever the adviser feels like it  D) Before any investment advice is provided\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The risk profile must be done before any investment advice is provided, and no free trial can be offered without communicating the profile and obtaining consent. Doing it after a year (A), at death (B), or arbitrarily (C) all violate the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Under SEBI Reg 16, no free trial can be offered without:\nA) Charging a fee first  B) Communicating the risk profile to the client and obtaining their consent  C) Government approval  D) A minimum investment of Rs. 1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A free trial cannot be offered unless the adviser communicates the risk profile and obtains the client's consent on it. There is no requirement to charge a fee first (A), get government approval (C), or set a Rs. 1 lakh minimum (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** SEBI Reg 16 recommends reviewing the risk profile on special events such as:\nA) A change in family composition, income/expenses, or assets/liabilities  B) Every stock market holiday  C) A change in the adviser's office address  D) The release of a new mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A review is recommended on life events: change in family composition, change in income/expenses, or change in assets/liabilities. Market holidays (B), the adviser's address (C) and new fund launches (D) are not triggers for review.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Asset allocation is best described as:\nA) Picking the single best stock  B) Timing the market daily  C) Borrowing to invest  D) The distribution of an investor's portfolio between different asset classes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Asset allocation is the distribution of a portfolio among different asset classes (equity, debt, gold, real estate, cash). Picking one stock (A) and daily timing (B) relate to selection/timing, and borrowing (C) is leverage — none is asset allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** An efficient asset allocation includes asset classes that have:\nA) High positive correlation  B) Identical returns  C) Low or negative correlation  D) The same risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An efficient allocation combines assets with low or negative correlation, so their returns do not rise and fall together — this cushions the portfolio. High positive correlation (A) defeats diversification; identical returns (B) and identical risk (D) are not the criterion.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** During the 2007–09 recession, the chapter notes that while equity markets fared poorly, gold prices:\nA) Went up  B) Also fell sharply  C) Stayed exactly flat  D) Were banned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — During 2007–09 equities fell in many countries but gold prices went up, so an investor in both did better than one in only equities — an illustration of diversification. So B, C and D contradict the example.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which type of asset allocation is aligned to the financial goals of the individual and is a long-term plan?\nA) Tactical  B) Dynamic  C) Strategic  D) Random\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Strategic asset allocation is aligned to financial goals, considering required return, time horizon and risk profile; it is a long-term plan reviewed periodically. Tactical (A) is a market call, Dynamic (B) is model-driven, and \"Random\" (D) is not a type.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Going \"overweight\" on equities because you expect buoyancy in the markets is an example of:\nA) Strategic asset allocation  B) Tactical asset allocation  C) Dynamic asset allocation  D) Capital preservation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tactical asset allocation comes from calls on the likely behaviour of the market, such as going overweight equities expecting a rally. Strategic (A) does not chase market moves, Dynamic (C) is model-based, and capital preservation (D) is an objective, not an allocation type.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which type of asset allocation uses pre-defined models and removes the subjective element?\nA) Strategic  B) Tactical  C) Dynamic  D) Conservative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Dynamic asset allocation uses pre-defined models with triggers based on valuations or performance, removing subjectivity. Strategic (A) is goal-based, Tactical (B) is a subjective market call, and \"Conservative\" (D) is an investor grade.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** According to the chapter, how often should a portfolio review ideally be done, at minimum?\nA) Once every five years  B) Every day  C) Once a year  D) Only once at the start\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Review of the portfolio should be done at pre-fixed intervals, ideally at least once a year. Five years (A) is too infrequent, daily (B) is excessive, and only once (D) ignores the need for periodic review.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Rebalancing a portfolio should ideally be done to:\nA) Chase short-term price movements  B) Maximise brokerage  C) Match the adviser's own portfolio  D) Keep it aligned to the investor's risk and return requirements\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Ideally a portfolio is rebalanced to stay aligned to the investor's risk/return requirements and reflect changes in their needs — not to profit from short-term price moves (A), generate brokerage (B), or copy the adviser (C).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Rohan, 25, single, no dependents, in a stable IT job with good savings, says he is excited by market ups and downs. Which grade fits best?\nA) Conservative  B) Moderate  C) Aggressive  D) Cannot be profiled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Young age, no dependents, steady income, savings and a daring psyche all push both ability and willingness up, matching the Aggressive grade (willing to risk a significant portion). Conservative (A) and Moderate (B) understate his capacity and appetite, and every client can be profiled (D is wrong).\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mrs. Iyer, a 68-year-old widow living off her savings, says she cannot bear to see her capital fall. Which model allocation feature suits her?\nA) 50% equities and 20% sector funds  B) 100% in a single mid-cap stock  C) A high allocation to safer investments such as debt and a low allocation to equity  D) Borrowing to buy gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A person close to/in retirement who fears capital loss needs a higher allocation to safer debt and lower equity. The aggressive 50%+20% mix (A) is unsuitable, a single stock (B) is dangerous concentration, and leveraged gold (D) adds risk — all wrong for her.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client has a very high willingness to take risk (loves volatility) but has five dependents and an irregular freelance income. The adviser should conclude that the client's:\nA) Ability to take risk is high  B) Profile is automatically aggressive  C) Risk profile need not be checked  D) Ability to take risk is constrained, even though willingness is high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Willingness is psychological and high here, but many dependents and irregular income reduce the financial ability to take risk. Good profiling considers both, so ability is constrained (D). The high ability (A) and automatic aggressive (B) ignore the dependents/income, and profiling is mandatory (C wrong).\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor keeps all his money in a savings bank account earning very low interest and finds he cannot accumulate enough for future goals. This illustrates a problem of:\nA) Too much liquidity but inadequate growth from concentration in one low-return category  B) Excessive equity risk  C) Currency risk  D) Over-diversification\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Like Kamal in the chapter, parking everything in a low-interest savings account is concentration in one low-return category, so the corpus cannot grow. It is not equity risk (B — he holds no equity), currency risk (C), or over-diversification (D — he is under-diversified).\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Latika invests all her money in equities and is unsure she will have the funds when needed because the value keeps fluctuating. The core problem is:\nA) She has too much debt  B) Concentration in one volatile asset class  C) She paid too much tax  D) Her income is irregular\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All-equity means concentration in one volatile asset class, so the value swings and funds may not be available when needed. She has no debt issue (A), the problem is not tax (C), and her income is not the stated issue (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Harmeet holds most of her wealth in gold jewellery and cannot generate income for monthly expenses. The lesson is that gold mainly offers:\nA) Regular income  B) Guaranteed liquidity instantly  C) Growth/appreciation but not regular income  D) Capital preservation with interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Gold is a growth/appreciation asset; it does not pay regular income, which is why Harmeet cannot meet monthly expenses from it. So A is wrong, gold is not the chapter's example for instant liquidity (B), and it does not pay interest (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Gayatri lives on fixed interest from bank FDs but her expenses keep rising, so she falls short. This shows the risk of:\nA) Holding only fixed-income with no growth assets to beat rising expenses  B) Holding too much equity  C) Over-trading  D) Excessive gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Fixed FD interest does not rise with her growing expenses, so she needs some growth assets to keep pace — a concentration problem in fixed income. She holds no equity (B), is not trading (C), and holds no gold (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Jayesh invested only in real estate and could not sell it or borrow quickly when he urgently needed funds. His portfolio lacked:\nA) Growth  B) A demat account  C) Insurance  D) Liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Real estate is hard to sell quickly, so Jayesh's all-real-estate portfolio lacked liquidity for an urgent need. The issue was not growth (A — property can grow), a demat account (B), or insurance (C).\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** For the goal of \"liquidity,\" which set of investments is most suitable?\nA) Equity shares and real estate  B) Sector funds and gold  C) Cash, bank deposits and short-term mutual fund schemes  D) Long-term gilt funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Liquidity is best served by cash, bank deposits and short-term MF schemes that can be accessed quickly. Equity/real estate (A) and sector funds/gold (B) are growth assets; long-term gilt funds (D) are not the liquidity choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** For the objective of \"regular income,\" the most suitable investments are:\nA) Deposits, debt instruments/debt funds and real estate  B) Diversified equity funds  C) Gold ETFs only  D) Liquid funds only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Regular income comes from deposits, debt instruments/debt funds and real estate (rent). Diversified equity (B) is for growth, gold ETFs (C) give no income, and liquid funds (D) are for liquidity/preservation, not steady income.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** For \"capital preservation,\" the chapter suggests:\nA) Mid-cap equity funds  B) Sector funds  C) Real estate  D) Cash, bank deposits and ultra-short-term funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Capital preservation is served by cash, bank deposits and ultra-short-term funds, which carry very low risk to principal. Mid-cap equity (A), sector funds (B) and real estate (C) all carry capital-loss risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Jayant saved for his children's education in equity for 8 years; the goal is now just 4 years away. The most appropriate action is to:\nA) Add more equity to maximise returns  B) Switch entirely to a single stock  C) Move funds towards less risky investments to protect the accumulated amount  D) Stop reviewing the portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — As a goal nears, equity fluctuations could damage the accumulated corpus, so Jayant should rebalance towards less risky investments. Adding equity (A) increases risk near the goal, a single stock (B) is dangerous, and stopping reviews (D) is the opposite of good practice.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A \"Young call centre/BPO employee with no dependents (high risk profile)\" model portfolio suggests 50% in:\nA) Gold ETF  B) Liquid schemes  C) Gilt funds  D) Equities/diversified equity schemes (preferably via SIP)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The high-risk young employee model puts 50% in equities/diversified equity schemes, preferably via SIP, suiting a long horizon and high appetite. Gold ETF (A) is 10%, liquid (B) is part of the small cash slice, and gilt funds (C) are not in this model.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** In the model portfolio for a \"Young married, single income family with two school-going kids (moderate risk profile),\" the equity/diversified equity allocation is:\nA) 0%  B) 15%  C) 35%  D) 50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The moderate young family model allocates 35% to equities/diversified equity. 50% (D) is the high-risk young employee, 15% (B) is the equity slice for the 70s couple, and 0% (A) fits no model here.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** In the model portfolio for a \"Couple in their seventies, no immediate family support (conservative to moderate),\" the diversified equity index allocation is:\nA) 50%  B) 35%  C) 15%  D) 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The 70s couple model has 15% in a diversified equity index scheme (plus 5% gold ETF and large debt/liquid slices). 50% (A) and 35% (B) are far too aggressive for them, and 5% (D) is the gold ETF portion.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor expecting a strong rally wants to temporarily increase equity beyond his long-term plan. This decision belongs to:\nA) Capital preservation  B) Rebalancing back to plan  C) Tactical asset allocation  D) Strategic asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Temporarily over-weighting equities on a market view is a tactical asset allocation call. Strategic (D) is goal-based and ignores forecasts, capital preservation (A) is an objective, and rebalancing (B) brings weights back to plan, not above it.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Tactical asset allocation is described as suitable mainly for:\nA) First-time investors with small savings  B) All retirees  C) Anyone who dislikes risk  D) Seasoned investors operating with large investible surpluses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Tactical calls suit seasoned investors with large surpluses, and even they should limit the portion used for frequent tactical calls. First-timers (A), all retirees (B) and risk-averse investors (C) are not the intended users.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Under strategic asset allocation, the allocation to an asset class will NOT be increased on the basis of:\nA) A change in the investor's goals  B) The expected performance of the asset class  C) A change in the investor's time horizon  D) A change in the investor's risk profile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Strategic allocation does not raise weights based on expected asset performance — that would be tactical. It changes only when fundamentals like goals (A), horizon (C) or risk profile (D) change.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Because strategic asset allocation ignores forecasts, the trade-off is that the portfolio:\nA) Is protected from forecast errors but will not benefit from over-exposure to a hot asset  B) Always beats the market  C) Cannot be rebalanced  D) Has no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — By not chasing forecasts, the portfolio is shielded from forecast errors but also misses gains from over-weighting a top-performing asset. It does not always beat the market (B), it is rebalanced periodically (C is wrong), and no portfolio is risk-free (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An adviser uses a system that automatically shifts money between equity and debt based on pre-set valuation triggers. This is:\nA) Strategic asset allocation  B) Tactical asset allocation  C) Dynamic asset allocation  D) Concentration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Pre-defined models with valuation/performance triggers that remove subjectivity describe dynamic asset allocation. Strategic (A) is goal-based, tactical (B) is a subjective human call, and concentration (D) is the opposite of allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A salaried, high-income investor in his 30s with goals 20 years away should generally:\nA) Hold mostly debt with little equity  B) Keep everything in a savings account  C) Prefer higher-return assets like equity given the long horizon and high income  D) Avoid any risk profiling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With far-off goals and a strong income, he can prefer higher-return assets such as equity. Mostly-debt (A) suits someone near retirement, all-savings (B) earns too little for long goals, and profiling is always required (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The main reason different asset classes provide diversification is that their returns:\nA) Always move together  B) Are guaranteed by SEBI  C) Are always equal  D) Do not rise and fall together because they are driven by different factors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Asset classes are driven by different economic factors, so they do not rise and fall together; a fall in one can be cushioned by a rise in another. Moving together (A) and being equal (C) defeat diversification, and SEBI does not guarantee returns (B).\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** International research cited in the chapter suggests portfolio performance is better explained by:\nA) Stock selection within an asset class  B) The broker chosen  C) The number of trades made  D) Asset allocation and investment policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Research suggests asset allocation and investment policy explain performance better than picking individual securities (stock selection, A). The broker (B) and trade count (C) are not the explanation cited.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A portfolio review should help answer all of the following EXCEPT:\nA) Are all the goals still relevant?  B) Are the investments performing as expected?  C) Which colour suits the client's car?  D) Do investments need changing if no longer suitable?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Reviews check goal relevance (A), performance (B) and suitability (D). The colour of a car (C) is irrelevant and not a review question.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** If a review shows a goal is under-funded because returns lagged, the investor can:\nA) Save more for the goal or divert funds from a less important goal  B) Do nothing and hope  C) Cancel the risk profile  D) Only sue the adviser\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When a goal is under-funded, the investor can save more or move funds from a less important goal. Doing nothing (B), cancelling the profile (C) and suing (D) do not solve the funding gap.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A client says \"there is no right answer\" frustrates her in a risk questionnaire and she keeps trying to pick the 'best' option. The adviser should explain that:\nA) She should guess the adviser's preferred answer  B) The questionnaire is pointless  C) Honest answers matter because the tool reflects her true preferences, not a correct answer  D) She must choose the most aggressive option\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — There is genuinely no right answer; the tool only helps if she answers truthfully about her own preferences. Guessing the adviser's choice (A), calling it pointless (B) or forcing aggressiveness (D) all defeat the purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A 55-year-old with two children still in college and a home loan, but a secure government job, is best described as having:\nA) High willingness and high ability  B) No need for asset allocation  C) An automatically aggressive profile  D) Reduced ability due to dependents and a loan, regardless of any willingness\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Dependents and a loan reduce ability to absorb losses, even if a secure job helps a little. So ability is constrained (D). It is not unconstrained high ability (A), everyone needs allocation (B), and the profile is not automatically aggressive (C).\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which combination best illustrates an \"efficient\" asset allocation per the chapter?\nA) Two assets that always rise and fall together  B) Five different mid-cap equity funds  C) Only one asset class held in large size  D) Equity and gold, which behaved differently during 2007–09\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equity and gold had low/negative correlation (in 2007–09 equity fell, gold rose), giving real diversification. Assets that move together (A), several similar equity funds (B) and a single asset class (C) do not provide efficient diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An adviser building a portfolio must balance the required return with:\nA) An appropriate level of risk  B) The broker's commission  C) The adviser's mood  D) The newspaper headline\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Portfolio construction balances the required return against an appropriate level of risk for the investor. Commission (B), mood (C) and headlines (D) are not the balancing factors.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Why can no single investment meet all of an investor's needs?\nA) Because SEBI forbids single investments  B) Because no one asset provides growth, liquidity, regular income, capital protection and adequate return all at once  C) Because all investments are identical  D) Because investors only have one goal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each asset has its own focus (growth, income, liquidity, etc.), so no single one meets every need; a portfolio is required. SEBI does not forbid single investments (A), assets differ (C is wrong), and investors usually have multiple goals (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The suitable asset allocation for a person is described as a function of the investment period and their:\nA) Ability and willingness to take risk  B) Favourite stock  C) Choice of bank  D) Social media following\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Suitable allocation depends on the investment period available and the investor's ability and willingness to take risk. A favourite stock (B), bank (C) and social media (D) are not the determinants.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client wants a portfolio that protects capital but also grows over time and pays some income. The best approach is to:\nA) Put everything in one safe FD  B) Build a portfolio combining different assets matched to each objective  C) Buy a single equity stock  D) Hold only gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Different objectives (preservation, growth, income) need different assets, so a multi-asset portfolio matched to each objective is best. A single FD (A) gives no growth, one stock (C) is risky concentration, and only gold (D) gives no income.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A 70s couple who are physically and mentally very sound with a large investible corpus might, compared to the standard 70s model, be advised:\nA) 0% equity  B) Slightly more equity exposure than the standard 70s model  C) 90% equity  D) Only sector funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter tweaks the standard 70s model (e.g., adding direct equity alongside the index portion) so a sound, well-funded couple takes slightly more equity. Zero equity (A) is too cautious given their strength, while 90% equity (C) or only sector funds (D) are far too aggressive.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Asset allocation and diversification together are said to:\nA) Eliminate all risk completely  B) Reduce the risk of loss and stabilize the returns of a portfolio  C) Guarantee a fixed return  D) Increase concentration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Asset allocation and diversification reduce the risk of loss and stabilize returns, because not all assets fall together. They do not eliminate all risk (A), guarantee a fixed return (C), or increase concentration (D — they reduce it).\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** For diversification benefits to actually materialise, the investments must be selected so that they:\nA) Rise and fall together  B) Do not rise and fall together  C) Are all from the same sector  D) Are all equities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Diversification works only if investments do not rise and fall together (low/negative correlation). Assets that move together (A), share one sector (C) or are all equities (D) provide little real diversification.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Anil, 32, earns Rs. 30 lakh a year, has no dependents and large savings, and tells you confidently that volatility excites him. However, his entire net worth is parked in a single unlisted startup he founded. As his adviser, your most accurate assessment is:\nA) His high ability and willingness mean no action is needed  B) Despite high willingness and income, his portfolio is dangerously concentrated and lacks diversification/liquidity  C) He should put even more into the startup  D) He should move everything to FDs immediately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Profiling looks beyond willingness: although his income and appetite are high, holding 100% in one illiquid asset is extreme concentration (like Jayesh) with no diversification or liquidity cushion. So \"no action\" (A) ignores the concentration risk, adding more (C) worsens it, and shifting everything to FDs (D) over-corrects and ignores his genuine growth capacity.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Meena, 45, single income, supports aging parents and two children, has a home loan, and a modest emergency fund. She insists she wants \"maximum returns, all equity.\" Reconciling capacity and tolerance, the adviser should:\nA) Follow her stated wish and go 100% equity  B) Refuse to advise her  C) Explain that her ability to absorb losses is limited by dependents and the loan, so a more balanced allocation is suitable despite her high stated willingness  D) Put everything in gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Her many dependents and a loan limit her ability/capacity even though willingness is high; the suitable allocation must respect both, so a balanced mix is appropriate. Blindly going all-equity (A) ignores capacity, refusing to advise (B) is unhelpful, and all-gold (D) gives no income/growth balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client's questionnaire score classifies him as \"Aggressive,\" but in conversation he confesses he lost sleep and panic-sold during the last market fall. The adviser should:\nA) Ignore the conversation and trust only the score  B) Increase his equity to \"toughen him up\"  C) Cancel his account  D) Recognise the questionnaire may not fully capture his true tolerance and treat his loss-aversion as a real signal to moderate the allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Tools have limitations and investors may misreport; real behaviour (panic-selling) is strong evidence of lower true tolerance, so the allocation should be moderated. Blindly trusting the score (A) ignores the tool's limits, increasing equity (B) ignores his distress, and cancelling (C) is unwarranted.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Required-return logic: Priya needs Rs. 50 lakh in 10 years and currently has Rs. 25 lakh. She can take only moderate risk. Roughly, her money must double in 10 years. The needed annual growth (using the rule of 72) is closest to:\nA) About 7.2% per year  B) About 2% per year  C) About 20% per year  D) About 50% per year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Doubling means the rule of 72 gives required return ≈ 72 ÷ years = 72 ÷ 10 ≈ 7.2% per year. 2% (B) would barely grow the corpus, while 20% (C) or 50% (D) would double the money far faster than 10 years. A balanced portfolio targeting ~7% suits a moderate investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Continuing Q74: if Priya instead needed Rs. 1 crore (four times her Rs. 25 lakh) in 10 years, the required return would be much higher, creating a conflict because:\nA) Her required return now demands aggressive risk her moderate tolerance may not support  B) Required return has no link to risk  C) She should simply lower the goal to zero  D) She can guarantee the return with an FD\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Quadrupling in 10 years needs roughly 15% a year (money doubles in ~5 years per rule of 72), which demands aggressive equity-heavy risk that her moderate tolerance may not support — a capacity/tolerance vs required-return conflict. Required return is tightly linked to risk (B wrong), zeroing the goal (C) is absurd, and an FD cannot deliver ~15% safely (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** When required return exceeds what a client's risk tolerance can prudently support, the adviser's best response is to:\nA) Quietly invest more aggressively than the client's tolerance  B) Guarantee the higher return  C) Discuss options such as saving more, extending the time horizon, or moderating the goal  D) Tell the client risk does not matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the required return is unrealistic for the client's tolerance, the honest path is to re-examine the plan: save more, extend the horizon, or trim the goal. Secretly over-risking (A) breaches suitability, guarantees are impossible (B), and dismissing risk (D) is reckless.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Sameer, 28, single, secure job, very high willingness, is being matched to the \"young BPO employee, no dependents\" model (50% equity, 20% sector, 10% gold ETF, 10% diversified debt/FD, 10% short-term/liquid). His combined equity-type exposure (equity + sector funds) is:\nA) 50%  B) 60%  C) 70%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Equity 50% + sector funds 20% = 70% equity-type exposure, fitting his high-risk profile. 50% (A) counts only diversified equity, 60% (B) and 80% (D) miscount the slices that total 70%.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Reena follows the \"moderate young family\" model: 35% equity, 10% sector, 15% gold ETF, 30% diversified debt/FD, 10% liquid/short-term. Her total exposure to growth-oriented assets (equity + sector + gold) is:\nA) 45%  B) 60%  C) 75%  D) 35%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth-oriented = equity 35% + sector 10% + gold 15% = 60%. 45% (A) omits gold, 35% (D) counts only diversified equity, and 75% (C) over-adds debt that is not growth-oriented.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A \"conservative\" single-income family with grown-up unsettled children is given: 35% equity, 15% gold ETF, 10% gilt fund, 20% diversified debt/FD, 20% short-term/liquid. Their total fixed-income/cash allocation (gilt + diversified debt/FD + short-term/liquid) is:\nA) 30%  B) 40%  C) 50%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Gilt 10% + diversified debt/FD 20% + short-term/liquid 20% = 50% in fixed income/cash, with the rest in equity and gold. 30% (A) and 40% (B) drop one slice, while 60% (D) wrongly adds the gold portion.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A 72-year-old couple's model is 15% equity index, 5% gold ETF, 35% debt-oriented hybrid/MIS/SCSS, 30% diversified debt/FD, 15% liquid/savings. If the adviser learns one spouse needs frequent medical withdrawals, which adjustment is most consistent with the chapter's logic?\nA) Increase equity to chase higher returns  B) Move all funds into illiquid real estate  C) Stop reviewing the portfolio  D) Ensure adequate liquid/short-term allocation for ready access while keeping the bulk safe\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Medical needs demand ready liquidity, so the adviser should keep enough in liquid/short-term assets while the bulk stays safe — matching the objective (liquidity) to the asset. More equity (A) adds volatility they cannot bear, illiquid real estate (B) blocks access, and stopping reviews (C) is poor practice.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Vikram, an experienced investor with a Rs. 5 crore surplus, wants to take frequent market-timing bets on sectors. The chapter's guidance suggests he should:\nA) Apply tactical calls to the entire Rs. 5 crore  B) Limit the size of the portfolio used for frequent tactical calls and keep the major portion strategically allocated  C) Avoid all equity  D) Only use dynamic models and never decide himself\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even seasoned investors should cap the slice used for frequent tactical calls and keep the bulk aligned to a strategic allocation. Betting the whole corpus (A) is reckless, avoiding equity (C) ignores his profile, and forcing only models (D) is not required.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A client insists his adviser should \"guarantee\" he will never lose money yet also double his money in three years. The adviser should explain that:\nA) This is achievable with the right stock pick  B) He should sign a no-loss contract  C) Gold guarantees doubling  D) Such a goal is internally contradictory — high required return demands risk, and capital can fall; no return can be guaranteed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Doubling in three years needs roughly 24% a year (rule of 72: 72÷3), which requires high risk and therefore the possibility of loss — so a no-loss guarantee plus that return is contradictory. A magic stock (A), a no-loss contract (B) and gold guarantees (C) are all false promises.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Two clients have identical incomes and savings (same capacity), but one is calm about losses and the other is highly anxious. The chapter implies their suitable allocations should:\nA) Be identical because capacity is identical  B) Both be 100% equity  C) Differ, because willingness/psyche differs even when capacity is the same  D) Both avoid equity entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Willingness is psychological and differs between them, so the anxious client warrants a less risky allocation even with identical capacity — both dimensions matter. Identical allocations (A) ignore psyche, and forcing all-equity (B) or no-equity (D) on both ignores their differences.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client's goals are: an emergency fund (immediate), a house down-payment (3 years), and retirement (25 years). The most suitable structuring is:\nA) All three goals in equity for maximum returns  B) Match each goal to suitable assets — liquid/cash for emergency, lower-risk for the 3-year goal, growth assets for the 25-year goal  C) All three in FDs  D) All three in a single sector fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Different time horizons need different assets: liquidity for the emergency fund, safer assets for the near-term down-payment, and growth (equity) for the far-off retirement. All-equity (A) endangers near goals, all-FD (C) starves the long goal of growth, and one sector fund (D) is dangerous concentration.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client was profiled three years ago as moderate. Since then he married, had a child, and took a large home loan. Per SEBI Reg 16, the adviser should:\nA) Keep the old profile unchanged  B) Wait ten years before any update  C) Delete his records  D) Update the risk profile because family composition and liabilities have changed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Reg 16 recommends updating the profile on special events such as changes in family composition and liabilities — exactly what happened. Keeping the old profile (A), waiting ten years (B), or deleting records (C) all conflict with the requirement to update.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** An adviser wants to offer a \"free trial\" of advice before the client commits. Under SEBI Reg 16, this is permissible only if:\nA) The client pays a deposit  B) The adviser keeps the profile secret  C) The risk profile has been communicated to the client and the client's consent on it is obtained  D) The trial lasts under 7 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — No free trial may be offered without communicating the risk profile to the client and obtaining their consent on it. A deposit (A), secrecy (B), and a 7-day limit (D) are not the conditions specified.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A questionnaire asks: \"You agree that aggressive equity investing is always the smartest choice, don't you?\" The adviser should reject this question because it is:\nA) Fair and clear  B) A leading question, which Reg 16 prohibits  C) Too short  D) Written in simple language\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The wording pushes the client toward one answer, making it a leading question, which Reg 16 forbids. It is not fair/clear (A), the problem is not length (C), and simple language (D) is not the issue — the bias is.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A young aggressive investor's equity holdings have surged so equity is now 70% of a portfolio designed for 50%. Under strategic asset allocation, the adviser should:\nA) Leave it because equity is performing well  B) Increase equity further to ride the trend  C) Sell everything  D) Rebalance back toward the originally envisaged allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Strategic allocation is periodically rebalanced back to the original envisaged mix, regardless of recent performance. Leaving it (A) or adding more (B) chases performance (that is tactical), and selling everything (C) abandons the plan.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client demands the adviser increase his equity weight purely because \"experts on TV say markets will boom.\" A strictly strategic adviser would respond that:\nA) Strategic allocation does not raise weights on expected performance; only a change in his goals/horizon/risk profile would  B) TV experts must always be followed  C) He should go 100% equity today  D) Risk profiling is irrelevant here\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under strategic allocation, weights change only when fundamentals (goals, horizon, risk profile) change, not on performance forecasts — that would be a tactical call. Following TV (B) and going all-equity (C) are forecast-driven, and profiling always matters (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A client with negatively-correlated assets (equity and gold) sees equity drop 20% while gold rises 15% in the same year. This outcome demonstrates:\nA) That diversification failed  B) That holding low/negatively-correlated assets cushioned the portfolio's overall fall  C) That he should hold only equity  D) That gold always rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A rise in gold offsetting a fall in equity is exactly the cushioning benefit of low/negative correlation — diversification working as intended (echoing 2007–09). It did not fail (A), all-equity (C) would have hurt more, and gold does not always rise (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A 60-year-old retiree's required income is Rs. 6 lakh a year from a Rs. 1 crore corpus. The withdrawal rate is:\nA) 6%  B) 16%  C) 0.6%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Withdrawal rate = annual need ÷ corpus = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,00,00,000 = 6%. 16% (B), 0.6% (C) and 60% (D) misplace the decimal or invert the ratio. A 6% income need suggests an income-oriented (debt-heavy) allocation suited to his conservative stage.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Continuing Q91: to sustainably meet a 6% income need with low risk, the retiree's allocation should lean toward:\nA) Aggressive sector funds  B) Income-generating assets such as debt instruments, deposits, and debt-oriented hybrids, with limited equity  C) A single growth stock  D) Illiquid real estate only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A retiree needing regular income with low risk should hold mainly income-generating debt/deposit/hybrid assets, with only limited equity for some growth. Sector funds (A) and a single stock (C) are too volatile, and illiquid real estate only (D) cannot provide reliable monthly income.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An investor's stated willingness is aggressive, capacity is high, but the goal (a wedding) is just 18 months away. The dominant factor for THIS goal's allocation is:\nA) His aggressive willingness  B) The short time horizon, which calls for lower-risk, liquid assets despite his appetite  C) His high capacity  D) Recent market forecasts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A short 18-month horizon means there is no time to recover from a market fall, so this goal needs lower-risk, liquid assets regardless of his appetite or capacity. Willingness (A) and capacity (C) matter for long goals, and forecasts (D) should not drive strategic allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client holds 90% real estate and 10% cash, and complains both about low income and being unable to access funds quickly. The two distinct problems are:\nA) Too much equity and too much debt  B) Lack of regular income from real estate and poor liquidity due to concentration  C) Over-diversification and high taxes  D) Currency risk and inflation risk only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real estate generates little regular income (like Harmeet's gold) and is hard to sell quickly (like Jayesh), so concentration causes both an income gap and a liquidity gap. There is no equity/debt issue (A), it is not over-diversification (C), and the core issue is concentration, not just currency/inflation (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client says: \"Just pick me the single best-performing stock; allocation is a waste of time.\" Drawing on the chapter, the adviser should explain that:\nA) Stock selection explains performance better than allocation  B) Research suggests asset allocation and investment policy explain performance better than stock selection, and concentration is risky  C) One stock is always safest  D) Allocation only matters for beginners\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter cites research that asset allocation/investment policy explain performance better than stock selection, and a single stock is dangerous concentration. So A is reversed, one stock is not safest (C), and allocation matters for all investors (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A moderate investor's adviser uses a dynamic asset allocation model that shifts to debt when equity valuations are high. The client asks why the adviser isn't \"using judgement.\" The correct explanation is:\nA) Dynamic allocation deliberately removes the subjective element using pre-defined triggers  B) The adviser is lazy  C) Judgement is illegal  D) Dynamic allocation ignores valuations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Dynamic allocation uses pre-defined models and triggers (e.g., valuations) precisely to remove subjectivity, so reduced discretion is by design. It is not laziness (B), judgement is not illegal (C), and dynamic models are driven by valuations, not blind to them (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An investor's profile changes from aggressive to conservative after a serious illness reduces his earning ability and increases medical expenses. The adviser should reallocate by:\nA) Keeping the aggressive 70% equity mix  B) Shifting toward safer income/liquid assets to match his now-lower capacity and need for funds  C) Adding sector funds for higher returns  D) Doing nothing until next year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A drop in earning ability plus rising medical needs lowers capacity and raises liquidity needs, so the portfolio should shift to safer income/liquid assets (and the profile updated per Reg 16). Keeping 70% equity (A) or adding sector funds (C) ignores the change, and waiting a year (D) delays a needed update.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Two assets are perfectly positively correlated. Combining them in a portfolio will:\nA) Provide strong diversification benefits  B) Provide little diversification because they rise and fall together  C) Guarantee a profit  D) Always reduce returns to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Perfectly positively correlated assets move together, so combining them gives little diversification benefit (the goal is low/negative correlation). They do not diversify well (A), cannot guarantee profit (C), and do not force returns to zero (D).\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client has a high required return but very low tolerance and refuses to extend his horizon or save more. The adviser's most professional course is to:\nA) Override his tolerance and invest aggressively anyway  B) Clearly inform him the goal is likely unachievable within his risk comfort, and document the suitability mismatch rather than mis-sell risk  C) Promise the return anyway  D) Pick random aggressive funds without telling him\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When required return cannot be reconciled with low tolerance and the client won't adjust, the adviser must be honest about the mismatch and not push unsuitable risk — suitability is the core of Reg 16. Overriding tolerance (A), promising returns (C) and secretly going aggressive (D) all breach the duty of suitability.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Putting it together: which statement best captures sound risk profiling and asset allocation from this chapter?\nA) Match the portfolio only to the client's willingness, ignoring capacity and goals  B) Reconcile willingness, ability/capacity, required return and time horizon, then diversify across low/negatively-correlated assets, and review/rebalance periodically  C) Always go 100% equity for everyone young  D) Chase the best-performing asset each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Good practice reconciles willingness, ability, required return and horizon; diversifies across low/negatively-correlated assets; and reviews/rebalances periodically. Ignoring capacity and goals (A), forcing all-equity on the young (C), and chasing last year's winner (D) all violate the chapter's principles.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**The Deshmukh family — a real-world risk-profiling assignment.**\n\nRohit Deshmukh (age 38) is a software architect earning Rs. 36 lakh a year in a stable job. His wife Sneha (age 35) is a homemaker. They have two children (ages 8 and 5) and Rohit supports his retired parents. They have a home loan with EMIs running for another 12 years, an emergency fund covering 4 months of expenses, and an investible corpus of Rs. 40 lakh. On the questionnaire, Rohit scores as \"Aggressive\" and says he \"loves the thrill of equity.\" Their goals are: (1) children's higher education in 12 years, needing Rs. 80 lakh; (2) a car purchase in 18 months, needing Rs. 12 lakh; and (3) retirement in 22 years. Rohit asks you to \"put everything in equities.\"\n\n---\n\n**Sub-question 1:** Distinguish Rohit's risk *willingness* from his risk *ability/capacity*, and state which dimensions pull in opposite directions.\n\n<details><summary>Worked answer</summary>\n\n**Willingness (psychology):** High. He scores Aggressive and enjoys equity volatility — this is about his attitude to losses, not his finances.\n\n**Ability/capacity (resources & obligations):** Mixed and partly constrained. Factors raising it: he is relatively young (38), highly employable (software architect), has a steady job, regular income (Rs. 36 lakh) and a reasonable Rs. 40 lakh corpus. Factors lowering it: he is the *single* earner, has *four* dependents (wife, two young children, retired parents), and carries a long home loan — more dependents and liabilities reduce the ability to absorb losses.\n\n**Opposing pull:** His *willingness* is high, but his *dependents and loan* hold his *ability* down. Per the chapter, good profiling weighs **both** willingness and ability (including the ability to incur and absorb losses), so the adviser cannot simply act on his aggressive appetite.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 2:** Rohit wants \"everything in equities.\" Using the concentration examples in the chapter, explain two distinct risks of an all-equity portfolio for this family.\n\n<details><summary>Worked answer</summary>\n\n**Risk 1 — volatility/uncertainty of value (the \"Latika\" problem):** Latika put all her money in equities and was never sure she would have the required funds when needed because the value kept fluctuating. For the Deshmukhs, the **car goal is only 18 months away** — an equity dip just before then could leave them short.\n\n**Risk 2 — concentration in one asset class:** The chapter stresses that risk comes from concentrating the portfolio in one category; a single market fall would hit the *entire* portfolio at once, with no other asset rising to cushion it. As the single earner with four dependents and a loan, Rohit cannot afford that downside.\n\n**Conclusion:** All-equity ignores liquidity (for the near goal) and diversification, so it is unsuitable despite his appetite.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 3:** For the **car goal (Rs. 12 lakh in 18 months)**, what allocation is suitable and why? Contrast it with the education goal's horizon.\n\n<details><summary>Worked answer</summary>\n\n**Car goal — 18 months (short horizon):** Suitable assets are **lower-risk and liquid** — cash, bank deposits, short-term/liquid mutual fund schemes (the chapter's \"liquidity\" and \"capital preservation\" categories). With only 18 months there is **no time to recover** from an equity fall, so equity is unsuitable here regardless of Rohit's appetite. The chapter's lesson (Jayant) is that as a goal nears, money should sit in safer assets.\n\n**Contrast — education goal (12 years):** A long 12-year horizon can absorb short-term volatility, so it can carry a meaningful **growth/equity** allocation, rebalanced toward safety as the goal approaches (again, the Jayant principle). **Same family, different goal, different allocation** — because suitable allocation is a function of the *investment period* plus ability and willingness.\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 4:** Estimate the required return for the **education goal** and judge whether it conflicts with prudent risk-taking. (They can allocate Rs. 20 lakh of the corpus to this goal; target Rs. 80 lakh in 12 years.)\n\n<details><summary>Worked answer</summary>\n\n**Required growth:** Rs. 20 lakh must become Rs. 80 lakh — that is **4×** in 12 years.\n\n**Using the rule of 72:** money doubles in 72 ÷ rate years. Two doublings (×4) in 12 years means each doubling takes ~6 years, so required return ≈ 72 ÷ 6 = **~12% per year**.\n\n**Judgement:** A ~12% target needs a **growth-oriented (equity-heavy) but diversified** allocation over the long horizon — which is feasible and broadly consistent with Rohit's willingness, provided it is **not** 100% equity and is **rebalanced** toward safety as the goal nears. So for *this* long goal, his appetite and the required return align reasonably; the conflict is mainly with the *short-term* car goal, not the education goal. (Note: 12% is illustrative; advisers should use realistic, documented assumptions.)\n</details>\n\n**Sub-question 5:** Recommend a structure for the family that reconciles all three dimensions, and state two SEBI Reg 16 obligations the adviser must meet.\n\n<details><summary>Worked answer</summary>\n\n**Goal-based, diversified structure (strategic asset allocation):**\n- **Car (18 months):** liquid/short-term debt and cash — capital preservation and liquidity.\n- **Education (12 years):** growth-oriented but diversified mix (equity + some debt/gold for low correlation), targeting ~12%, rebalanced toward safety as it nears.\n- **Retirement (22 years):** the most equity-tilted bucket, given the longest horizon, still diversified.\n- **Across all:** maintain/strengthen the emergency fund; diversify using low/negatively-correlated assets so a fall in one is cushioned by another; **review at least once a year** and rebalance to stay aligned (not to chase prices).\n\nThis respects his **high willingness** (meaningful equity in long goals), his **constrained ability** (liquidity buffers, no all-in bets given dependents/loan), and the **required return** (≈12% only where the horizon supports it).\n\n**Two SEBI Reg 16 obligations:**\n1. **Profile before advising and obtain objective information** — assess both willingness *and* ability to take and absorb losses using fair, clear, non-leading questions; complete the risk profile **before** any advice and obtain his consent on it (no free trial otherwise).\n2. **Update the profile on life events** — e.g., if a child's education timeline, the loan, income, or dependents change, the profile must be refreshed; reviews are recommended on changes in family composition, income/expenses, or assets/liabilities.\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "19", "title": "Comparison of Products Across Categories", "module": "Putting It All Together", "notes": "# Chapter 19: Comparison of Products Across Categories — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- Where **performance data** for investment products comes from.\n- How to **compare products** across the three big yardsticks: **Risk, Return, Liquidity** (plus tax and lock-in).\n- Side-by-side comparisons of the main product categories an adviser deals with.\n\n> **The three universal yardsticks:** Every investment is judged on **Risk** (chance of losing money), **Return** (how much it earns), and **Liquidity** (how quickly you can turn it into cash without a big loss). Add **Tax** and **Lock-in** and you can compare almost anything. Remember: usually **higher return comes with higher risk and/or lower liquidity** — you rarely get all three.\n\n---\n\n## 19.1 Performance data for investment products\n- Performance data is now **widely available**. For **Mutual Funds and PMS**, disclosure is **mandated by SEBI**.\n- **Information aggregators** collect this and offer comparison services on websites — often **free**, with some **paid** value-added services.\n\n---\n\n## 19.2 Comparing investment alternatives\nAll instruments are compared on **3 broad parameters: Risk, Return, Liquidity.**\n\n### 19.2.1 ELSS vs other tax-saving instruments\n> **ELSS** (Equity Linked Savings Scheme) = a mutual fund that invests **only in equities** and gives a tax deduction. **PPF** = Public Provident Fund (government scheme). **NPS Tier 1** = a pension scheme.\n\n| Parameter | **ELSS** | **PPF / Tax-saving Bank FD** | **NPS — Tier 1** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **What it is** | Invests **only in equities** | PPF = govt scheme; Tax-saving FD = bank deposit | MF-like pension; equity up to **75%** (selected subscribers), G-secs, corporate bonds, Alt. Investments (max 5% for some) |\n| **Risk of capital loss** | **High** (all equity) | PPF = **Low** (sovereign); FD = moderately low (bank-risk only) | **Moderate**; can be made low by choosing more G-secs |\n| **Returns** | No guarantee; moderately high to high; market-linked. **Capital gains taxed at 12.50%** | PPF = guaranteed-ish (varies with G-sec rate, can change each quarter), **tax-free**, moderate post-tax. Max **Rs.1,50,000/yr**. FD = fixed but **taxable yearly**, moderately low post-tax | No guarantee; moderate to moderately high; market-linked. **60% of corpus tax-free at age 60**; the other **40% must buy an annuity** (annuity income is **fully taxable**) |\n| **Liquidity / Lock-in** | **3-year lock-in**, then withdraw anytime | PPF = **15-year** lock-in (partial withdrawal after 5 yrs); FD = locked for the tenure | Locked **till age 60**; partial withdrawals allowed (conditions); premature exit → **80%** of the amount must buy an annuity |\n\n💡 ELSS has the **shortest lock-in (3 years)** of the common 80C tax-savers.\n\n### 19.2.2 Equity Mutual Funds vs PMS vs AIF Category 3\n> **PMS** = Portfolio Management Services (a customised, big-ticket managed account). **AIF** = Alternative Investment Fund (complex strategies). \"Cat 3\" AIFs can use derivatives/leverage.\n\n| Parameter | **Equity Mutual Fund** | **PMS** | **AIF Cat 3** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Nature** | Pooled vehicle; units allocated to each investor | Investments held in the **individual's own demat account**; **can be customised** | Pooled vehicle; **complex/derivative strategies**, common across investors |\n| **Minimum investment** | As low as **Rs. 1,000** | **Rs. 50 lakh** | **Rs. 1 crore** |\n| **Concentration / regulation** | SEBI regulates → broad-basing | **Not SEBI-capped** → can be concentrated | SEBI regulates → avoids single-company concentration |\n| **Fees** | **Max fee capped by SEBI** (varies by fund size) | No upfront charge; operating expenses capped at **0.50%**; fee can be fixed/performance/both | **Not SEBI-regulated**; as per AIF–investor agreement |\n| **For whom** | **Retail investor** | **HNI (High Net-worth Investor)** | HNI seeking complex/leveraged strategies |\n| **Redemption** | Anytime (open-ended), possible **exit load** | Manager may charge for redeeming/handing back | **Close-ended AIFs** have lock-in & **poor liquidity** |\n\n⚠️ Remember the minimums: **MF Rs.1,000 → PMS Rs.50 lakh → AIF Rs.1 crore.**\n\n### 19.2.3 Mutual Funds vs ULIPs\n> **ULIP** = Unit Linked Insurance Plan — an **insurance + investment** product from a life insurer.\n\n| Parameter | **Mutual Fund** | **ULIP** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Managed by** | Asset Management Companies | **Life Insurance Companies** |\n| **Purpose** | Investment only | **Insurance cum investment** |\n| **Charges** | Capped by **SEBI** | Capped by **IRDAI**; **mortality + other charges deducted from fund value** → post-expense return hard to compute |\n| **Returns** | Market-dependent | Market-dependent |\n| **Tax on withdrawal** | Taxable per tax laws | Maturity **tax-free if sum assured ≥ 10× annual premium**. BUT **Finance Act 2021** amended **Sec 10(10D)**: **no exemption** for ULIPs issued **on/after 1 Feb 2021 if any year's premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh**. Below that → still exempt |\n| **Disclosure** | **Transparent** daily/periodic; many comparison services | **Lower** disclosure; fewer comparison services |\n| **Liquidity** | Open-ended → redeem anytime (maybe exit load) | **Compulsory 5-year** contribution (except single premium); **no redemption for 5 years**; discontinuation charges if premiums stop early |\n\n💡 Two ULIP tax numbers to memorise: **sum assured ≥ 10× premium** for exemption, and the **Rs. 2.5 lakh** premium threshold (post-1 Feb 2021).\n\n### 19.2.4 Actively Managed Funds vs Index Funds\n| Parameter | **Active Fund** | **Index Fund** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Goal** | Beat the benchmark (generate **alpha**) by picking securities | **Replicate** the benchmark (same securities, same proportion) |\n| **Cost** | **Higher** (active decision-making) | **Lower** |\n| **Return** | Aim for higher return *net of higher cost* | Track the benchmark as closely as possible |\n\n> **Alpha** = the extra return above the benchmark.\n\n### 19.2.5 Direct Equity vs Equity Funds\n| Parameter | **Direct Equity** | **Equity Funds** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nature** | Buying specific stocks yourself | Buying an equity fund scheme |\n| **Expertise needed** | Personal expertise, or a PMS/RIA | Just pick the right scheme (incl. index funds) |\n| **Risk** | **Concentrated** on the chosen stocks | **Spread** across many securities → diversified |\n\n### 19.2.6 ETFs vs Index Funds\n> **ETF** = Exchange Traded Fund — an index fund that **trades on the stock exchange** like a share.\n\n| Parameter | **ETF (Exchange Traded Index Fund)** | **Index Fund** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nature** | Index fund **traded on exchange** like a security | Bought/sold **from the AMC** |\n| **Price** | Live, **minute-to-minute** | End-of-day **NAV** |\n| **Costs** | Management cost low, BUT brokerage, demat, and **buy–sell spread** add cost | Management cost slightly higher, but **no other costs** |\n| **Liquidity** | Depends on the market — on Indian exchanges, **mostly poor** (except a few ETFs); big NAV vs price gaps and wide spreads | Available from AMC on **end-of-day** basis |\n\n⚠️ Indian ETF trap: **liquidity is usually poor** with large bid–ask spreads, except a couple of ETFs.\n\n### 19.2.7 Physical Gold vs Gold Funds vs Gold ETFs vs Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGB)\n| Parameter | **Physical Gold** | **Gold Fund** | **Gold ETF** | **Sovereign Gold Bond** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Pricing** | Prevailing price now | End-of-day NAV | Live (intraday) | Priced on last-3-days average at each issue |\n| **GST cost** | **Yes** | Yes (on fund's gold; held as asset, not loaded as cost) | **No** | Yes (on fund's gold; as asset) |\n| **Purity concerns** | Yes (reduced by hallmarking) | Ensured by fund house | Ensured by fund house | **None** (no physical gold) |\n| **Storage/safety cost** | **Yes** | Built into fund charges | Nominal | **None** |\n| **Income** | No | No | No | **2.50% p.a. interest**, paid semi-annually |\n| **Tax on sale** | Capital gains | Capital gains | Capital gains | **No tax if redeemed with RBI**; capital gains if sold in market before maturity; interest is taxable |\n| **Liquidity** | Sell in market | Redeem at EOD NAV | Sellable on exchange, decent liquidity (costs apply) | Redeemable on anniversaries **after 5 yrs**, final after **8 yrs**; sellable in market (moderate liquidity) |\n\n💡 SGB is the only gold form that **pays interest (2.50%)** AND can be **tax-free if held to RBI redemption**.\n\n### 19.2.8 Real Estate vs REITs vs InvITs\n> **REIT** = Real Estate Investment Trust (invests in **commercial properties** → rent). **InvIT** = Infrastructure Investment Trust (invests in **infrastructure** like roads/bridges).\n>\n> ⚠️ Note the source's description: REITs → \"primarily infrastructure projects like roads, bridges\" and InvITs → \"primarily real estate commercial properties.\" (Standard market definitions are the reverse; for the exam, follow the workbook's table wording.)\n\n| Parameter | **Real Estate** | **REITs** | **InvITs** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Exposure** | Large, **concentrated**, lump-sum only | Spread over many properties; **lower minimum** possible | Spread over many projects; lower minimum possible |\n| **Management** | By the investor | By the investment manager | By the investment manager |\n| **Liquidity** | **Poor**, time-consuming | Better (may be listed) | Better (may be listed) |\n\n### 19.2.9 Debt Instruments vs Open-ended Debt Fund vs FMP vs Bank FD\n> **FMP** = Fixed Maturity Plan — a closed-ended debt fund with a fixed tenure.\n\n| Parameter | **Debt Instruments** | **Open-ended Debt Fund** | **FMP** | **Bank FD** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Safety** | Depends on instrument | Diversification → relatively higher safety for same rating | Same (diversified) | **High** |\n| **Returns** | In proportion to risk | Post-tax can be better via growth option (withdrawals have large capital element) | Pre-defined if held to maturity | Pre-defined |\n| **Liquidity** | **Poor** pre-maturity; selling costs a lot (much lower price) | Redeemable from fund (possible exit load) | **Very poor** (listed but illiquid) | **Instant** (but interest clawback / premature penalty) |\n\n### 19.2.10 Index Futures vs Index Options vs Index Funds\n| Parameter | **Index Futures** | **Index Options** | **Index Funds** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Time horizon** | Days to **max 3 months** | Days to ~3 months (long-dated also exist) | **Very long / lifetime** (actual investment) |\n| **Cost** | Interest cost for delayed purchase | Interest cost **+ risk premium** (option is discretionary) | Market cost + ongoing management charge |\n| **Risk** | Same as buying an index fund | **Max loss = premium paid** | Risk of the underlying securities |\n\n### 19.2.11 Gold Futures vs Gold Options vs Gold ETFs vs Gold Fund\n| Parameter | **Gold Futures** | **Gold Options** | **Gold ETF** | **Gold Fund** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Horizon** | 12 months | Per contract calendar | Long/lifetime | Long/lifetime |\n| **Cost** | Interest cost for delay | Interest cost **+ risk premium** | Market cost + AMC charge | Market cost + AMC charge |\n| **Risk** | Same as buying index fund | **Max loss = premium** | Gold price fluctuation | Gold price fluctuation |\n\n### 19.2.12 Company Deposits vs Company Debentures (same company)\n| Parameter | **Company Deposit** | **Company Debenture** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Risk** | **Unsecured** → relatively **higher** risk | Normally **secured** → relatively **lower** risk |\n| **Return** | Slightly **higher** (compensates for higher risk) | Slightly **lower** |\n| **Liquidity** | Company redeems (no interest if redeemed within a year) | Market-dependent (liquidity always an issue) |\n\n💡 Logic: more risk (unsecured deposit) → more return; more safety (secured debenture) → less return.\n\n---\n\n## 19.3 Other comparatives\n\n### 19.3.1 Market-linked vs Non-market-linked Retirement Products\n| Parameter | **Market-linked (NPS, ELSS, MF schemes)** | **Non-market-linked (PPF, etc.)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Risk** | Depends on option chosen; linked to market (G-sec option in NPS = low; ELSS = high) | **Low** for return of principal |\n| **Returns** | Linked to market, vary by option | Moderate to low (even EPF/PPF now reset returns yearly/quarterly) |\n| **Liquidity** | Lock-ins (so funds are actually there for retirement) | Even **poorer** liquidity (principal-guaranteed, same purpose) |\n\n### 19.3.2 Personal Accident Insurance vs Life Insurance\n| Parameter | **Personal Accident Insurance** | **Life Insurance** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Covers death due to** | **Accident only** | **Any cause** (including accident) |\n| **Disability cover** | Yes — temporary/permanent, total/partial disability from accident | **No** |\n| **Suitability** | An **add-on** over adequate life cover, especially for permanent total disability from accident | **Essential** for income earners with dependents |\n\n💡 Personal accident is a **supplement**, not a substitute, for life insurance.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- Compare everything on **Risk, Return, Liquidity** (+ tax + lock-in). High return ↔ high risk / low liquidity.\n- **ELSS:** all-equity, high risk, **3-yr lock-in** (shortest 80C); gains taxed 12.50%. **PPF:** sovereign, tax-free, 15-yr lock-in, max Rs.1.5 lakh/yr. **NPS:** 60% tax-free at 60, 40% → annuity (taxable).\n- Minimums: **MF Rs.1,000 → PMS Rs.50 lakh → AIF Rs.1 crore.**\n- **ULIP** tax: exempt if SA ≥ 10× premium; **but** no exemption post-1 Feb 2021 if premium > **Rs.2.5 lakh**.\n- **Active vs Index:** index = lower cost, tracks benchmark; active chases **alpha** at higher cost.\n- **ETF vs Index Fund:** ETF trades live but Indian ETF liquidity often **poor** (wide spreads).\n- **Gold:** SGB pays **2.50%** interest, **tax-free if redeemed with RBI**, no GST/storage.\n- **Real estate** = concentrated, illiquid; **REITs/InvITs** = diversified, more liquid.\n- **Company deposit** (unsecured, higher return) vs **debenture** (secured, lower return).\n- **Personal accident** = add-on (accident + disability); **life insurance** = essential, any-cause death, no disability cover.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Number / Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **ELSS lock-in** | **3 years** (shortest 80C tax-saver) |\n| **ELSS capital gains tax** | **12.50%** |\n| **PPF** | Sovereign, tax-free, **15-yr** lock-in, max **Rs.1,50,000/yr** |\n| **NPS at 60** | 60% tax-free; **40% → annuity** (annuity taxable) |\n| **PMS / AIF minimums** | **Rs.50 lakh / Rs.1 crore** |\n| **MF minimum** | As low as **Rs.1,000** |\n| **PMS expense cap** | Operating expenses capped at **0.50%**; no upfront charge |\n| **ULIP exemption** | SA ≥ **10×** premium; void if premium > **Rs.2.5 lakh** (policies from 1 Feb 2021) |\n| **SGB interest** | **2.50% p.a.**, semi-annual; tax-free if redeemed with RBI |\n| **ULIP lock-in** | **5 years** compulsory contribution |\n| **Alpha** | Return above the benchmark (active funds chase it) |\n| **Option max loss** | Limited to the **premium paid** |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 19: Comparison of Products Across Categories — Flashcards\n\n> **How to use:** Cover the answer, recall it out loud, then check.\n\n**Q1.** What are the three broad parameters used to compare all investment instruments?\n> **A:** **Risk, Return, and Liquidity** (often extended with tax and lock-in).\n\n**Q2.** For which products is performance-data disclosure mandated by SEBI?\n> **A:** **Mutual Funds and Portfolio Management Services (PMS).** Aggregators then provide comparison services, often free.\n\n**Q3.** What is ELSS and its lock-in?\n> **A:** Equity Linked Savings Scheme — a tax-saving MF investing **only in equities**; **3-year lock-in** (shortest among 80C options), then redeemable anytime. Capital gains taxed at **12.50%**.\n\n**Q4.** Compare risk of capital loss: ELSS vs PPF vs NPS Tier 1.\n> **A:** ELSS = **high** (all equity); PPF = **low** (sovereign); NPS = **moderate** (can be lowered with more G-secs).\n\n**Q5.** PPF: tax status, lock-in, annual limit?\n> **A:** Returns **tax-free**; **15-year** lock-in (partial withdrawal after 5 years); max **Rs.1,50,000/year**.\n\n**Q6.** What happens to the NPS corpus at age 60?\n> **A:** **60% can be withdrawn tax-free**; the remaining **40% must buy an annuity** from an insurer, and the **annuity income is fully taxable**.\n\n**Q7.** Minimum investment: Mutual Fund vs PMS vs AIF Cat 3?\n> **A:** MF: as low as **Rs.1,000**; PMS: **Rs.50 lakh**; AIF Cat 3: **Rs.1 crore**.\n\n**Q8.** How are PMS operating expenses and upfront charges regulated?\n> **A:** **No upfront charge allowed**; operating expenses capped at **0.50%**; the fee can be fixed, performance-based, or both.\n\n**Q9.** Where are PMS investments held vs mutual fund units?\n> **A:** PMS = in the **investor's own demat account** (can be customised); MF = pooled vehicle with units allocated to each investor.\n\n**Q10.** Who manages ULIPs, and what is their purpose vs mutual funds?\n> **A:** **Life insurance companies** manage ULIPs (insurance **cum** investment); mutual funds are managed by AMCs for **investment only**.\n\n**Q11.** When is a ULIP maturity tax-free?\n> **A:** If **sum assured ≥ 10× annual premium**. BUT for ULIPs issued **on/after 1 Feb 2021**, **no exemption** if any year's premium **exceeds Rs.2.5 lakh** (Finance Act 2021, Sec 10(10D)).\n\n**Q12.** ULIP liquidity / lock-in?\n> **A:** **5 years** compulsory contribution (except single-premium); no redemption for 5 years; discontinuation charges if premiums stop early.\n\n**Q13.** Active fund vs index fund — goal and cost?\n> **A:** Active fund tries to **beat the benchmark (alpha)** at **higher cost**; index fund **replicates** the benchmark at **lower cost**.\n\n**Q14.** Direct equity vs equity funds — risk?\n> **A:** Direct equity = **concentrated** risk on chosen stocks; equity funds **spread** risk across many securities (diversified).\n\n**Q15.** ETF vs index fund — pricing and liquidity in India?\n> **A:** ETF trades **live (intraday)** on the exchange; index fund priced at **end-of-day NAV**. In India, ETF liquidity is **often poor** (wide bid–ask spreads, NAV-price gaps) except a couple of ETFs.\n\n**Q16.** Which gold form pays interest, and how much?\n> **A:** **Sovereign Gold Bonds** — **2.50% p.a.**, paid semi-annually.\n\n**Q17.** Tax on Sovereign Gold Bonds at redemption?\n> **A:** **No tax if redeemed with RBI**; capital gains if sold in the market before maturity; the interest is taxable.\n\n**Q18.** Which gold forms incur GST? Which don't?\n> **A:** Physical gold, Gold Funds and SGBs involve GST (on the gold bought); **Gold ETF incurs no GST cost** to the buyer.\n\n**Q19.** SGB redemption timeline?\n> **A:** Redeemable on anniversary dates **after 5 years**, final redemption after **8 years**; sellable in the market in between (moderate liquidity).\n\n**Q20.** Real estate vs REITs/InvITs — exposure and liquidity?\n> **A:** Real estate = **large, concentrated, illiquid**, lump-sum only; **REITs/InvITs** = spread over many assets, lower minimums, **better liquidity** (may be listed), professionally managed.\n\n**Q21.** Company deposit vs company debenture (same company) — risk and return?\n> **A:** Deposit is **unsecured → higher risk, slightly higher return**; debenture is normally **secured → lower risk, slightly lower return**.\n\n**Q22.** Safety: Bank FD vs open-ended debt fund vs debt instrument vs FMP?\n> **A:** Bank FD = **high** safety; debt funds & FMPs get diversification benefit; single debt instrument = depends on the instrument. FMP liquidity is **very poor**.\n\n**Q23.** Why can a debt fund's growth option give better post-tax returns?\n> **A:** Withdrawals from a growth scheme contain a **large element of capital** (not just income), improving the post-tax outcome.\n\n**Q24.** Maximum loss for an option buyer?\n> **A:** Limited to the **premium paid**.\n\n**Q25.** Time horizon: index futures vs index funds?\n> **A:** Index futures = a **few days to max 3 months**; index funds = **very long / lifetime** (an actual investment).\n\n**Q26.** Market-linked vs non-market-linked retirement products — liquidity?\n> **A:** Both have lock-ins to ensure funds are available for retirement; **non-market-linked** (PPF) liquidity tends to be even **poorer**.\n\n**Q27.** Personal Accident insurance vs Life insurance — what death does each cover?\n> **A:** Personal Accident covers death due to **accident only**; Life insurance covers death due to **any cause**.\n\n**Q28.** Does life insurance cover disability? Does personal accident?\n> **A:** Life insurance does **not** cover disability; **Personal Accident does** (temporary/permanent, total/partial disability from accident).\n\n**Q29.** How should personal accident insurance be positioned?\n> **A:** As an **add-on** over adequate life cover (especially for permanent total disability), **not** a substitute for life insurance.\n\n**Q30.** What is the LRS-relevant point for ULIP tax threshold to remember?\n> **A:** (ULIP, not LRS) The premium threshold for exemption is **Rs.2.5 lakh** for policies issued on/after 1 Feb 2021; the sum-assured rule is **≥10× premium**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 19: Comparison of Products Across Categories — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers: 🟢 Tier 1 (Q1–35, easy recall of product features), 🟡 Tier 2 (Q36–70, application & best-fit/product-vs-product), 🔴 Tier 3 (Q71–100, scenarios & numericals). Remember: the real exam has **25% negative marking** — a wrong answer costs you 1/4 of a mark, so skip rather than wild-guess.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** All investment instruments are compared across which three broad parameters?\nA) Tax, Tenure, Trust  B) Risk, Return, Liquidity  C) Safety, Speed, Simplicity  D) Cost, Coverage, Compliance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook states every investment is compared on **Risk, Return and Liquidity**. These are the three universal yardsticks (with tax and lock-in often added). A, C and D mix up real factors with invented catchy lists; none of them is the trio the workbook names. Think of it as: how much can I lose (risk), how much do I earn (return), and how fast can I get my cash back (liquidity).\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** For which products is performance-data disclosure specifically **mandated by SEBI**?\nA) Bank FDs and PPF  B) Physical gold and real estate  C) Mutual Funds and Portfolio Management Services  D) ULIPs and life insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook says disclosure is mandated by SEBI for **Mutual Funds and PMS**. A (FD/PPF) are bank/government products, not SEBI-mandated disclosure. B (gold/real estate) have no such mandate. D (ULIPs/insurance) are regulated by IRDAI, not SEBI. SEBI regulates market-linked securities products, which is why MF and PMS data must be disclosed.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Equity Linked Savings Scheme (ELSS) invests:\nA) Exclusively in equities  B) Only in government securities  C) Mostly in corporate bonds  D) Equally in gold and equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The workbook defines ELSS as a scheme that invests **exclusively in equities**. B, C and D describe debt or other assets, which an ELSS does not focus on. Because it is all-equity, its risk of capital loss is high — that is the trade-off for its tax benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** What is the lock-in period of an ELSS?\nA) 5 years  B) 1 year  C) 15 years  D) 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — ELSS has a **3-year lock-in**, after which it can be withdrawn anytime. A (5 years) is the ULIP lock-in. C (15 years) is the PPF lock-in. B (1 year) is invented. ELSS has the shortest lock-in among the common Section 80C tax-savers.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The risk of capital loss in PPF is described as:\nA) Low (sovereign risk)  B) High  C) Moderate  D) Very high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PPF carries **Low** risk because it is a government scheme (sovereign risk). B and D (high) describe equity products like ELSS. C (moderate) describes NPS Tier 1. The government backing is what makes PPF among the safest options.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Capital gains on ELSS are taxed at what rate (per the workbook)?\nA) 10%  B) 15%  C) 12.50%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The workbook states ELSS capital gains are taxed at **12.50%**. The other figures are plausible-looking tax rates but are not the number given. Memorise 12.50% as the ELSS capital-gains rate cited in this chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Under NPS Tier 1, what is the maximum equity exposure allowed for selected subscribers?\nA) 100%  B) 50%  C) 75%  D) 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NPS Tier 1 allows equity investment up to a maximum of **75%** for selected subscribers. A (100%) is ELSS, not NPS. B and D are not the stated cap. NPS also allows G-secs, corporate bonds and up to 5% Alternative Investments for certain subscribers.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** At age 60, what portion of the NPS Tier 1 corpus can be withdrawn tax-free?\nA) 40%  B) 100%  C) 50%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **60%** of the NPS corpus can be withdrawn tax-free at age 60. A (40%) is the part that must be used to buy an annuity. B and C are wrong fractions. So: 60% tax-free lump sum, 40% compulsorily annuitised.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The 40% of the NPS corpus not withdrawn tax-free must be used to:\nA) Buy gold bonds  B) Pay tax dues  C) Reinvest in equity  D) Buy annuity product(s) from an insurance company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The remaining **40% must buy annuity product(s) from an insurance company**, and that annuity income is fully taxable. A, B and C are not requirements. The design forces a retirement income stream rather than a one-time spend.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** What is the maximum annual contribution allowed in PPF?\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 2,50,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PPF allows a maximum contribution of **Rs. 1,50,000 each year**. A and D are lower invented figures. C (Rs. 2.5 lakh) is the ULIP premium threshold, not the PPF cap. The Rs. 1.5 lakh figure aligns with the Section 80C limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Tax on PPF returns is:\nA) Nil (tax-free)  B) 12.50%  C) 10%  D) Taxable each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PPF returns attract **Nil tax** (tax-free), giving a moderate but attractive post-tax return. B is the ELSS rate. C is invented. D describes a tax-saving bank FD, whose interest is taxable each year. Tax-free status is a key PPF advantage.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** What is the lock-in period for a PPF account?\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 15 years  D) 8 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — PPF has a **15-year lock-in** from inception, with partial withdrawal possible after 5 years. A is the ELSS lock-in, B is the ULIP lock-in, D is the SGB final-redemption period. PPF is a long-term commitment.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Interest on a tax-saving bank FD is:\nA) Tax-free  B) Exempt up to Rs. 1.5 lakh  C) Taxed only at maturity at 12.50%  D) Taxable each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A tax-saving bank FD's returns are **taxable each year**, giving moderately low post-tax returns. A (tax-free) describes PPF. B and C are invented treatments. Yearly taxation is exactly why the FD's post-tax return is lower than PPF's even at similar headline rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** What is the minimum investment size for Portfolio Management Services (PMS)?\nA) Rs. 1,000  B) Rs. 1 crore  C) Rs. 50 lakh  D) Rs. 5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — PMS has a minimum investment of **Rs. 50 lakh**. A (Rs. 1,000) is the mutual fund minimum. B (Rs. 1 crore) is the AIF minimum. D is invented. The ladder is: MF Rs. 1,000 → PMS Rs. 50 lakh → AIF Rs. 1 crore.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** What is the minimum investment size for an Alternative Investment Fund (AIF)?\nA) Rs. 1 crore  B) Rs. 50 lakh  C) Rs. 25 lakh  D) Rs. 10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — AIFs require a minimum of **Rs. 1 crore**. B (Rs. 50 lakh) is for PMS. C and D are invented. AIFs sit at the top of the minimum-investment ladder because they suit HNIs seeking complex strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** In an equity mutual fund, the minimum investment can be as low as:\nA) Rs. 50 lakh  B) Rs. 1 crore  C) Rs. 1,000  D) Rs. 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mutual funds can be entered with as little as **Rs. 1,000**, making them suitable for retail investors. A and B are the PMS and AIF minimums (far higher). D is plausible but not the figure given. The low minimum is what makes MFs mass-market.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Operating expenses of a PMS are capped at:\nA) 1.00%  B) No cap  C) 2.50%  D) 0.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In PMS, upfront charges are not allowed and **operating expenses are capped at 0.50%**; the management/performance fee is as agreed. A and C are invented. B is wrong because there IS a cap on operating expenses (the fee itself is negotiable, but operating expenses are capped).\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A mutual fund is managed by an AMC; a ULIP is managed by:\nA) A bank  B) SEBI  C) A life insurance company  D) The investor directly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A ULIP is a pooled scheme managed by a **life insurance company**, whereas a mutual fund is managed by an Asset Management Company. A, B and D are not the managers. This difference is why ULIPs are regulated by IRDAI and MFs by SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** The purpose of a ULIP is best described as:\nA) Investment only  B) Insurance only  C) Insurance cum investment  D) Tax payment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A ULIP is **insurance cum investment**, combining life cover with market-linked investing. A (investment only) describes a mutual fund. B (insurance only) describes a pure term plan. D is nonsense. The dual purpose is the defining ULIP feature.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** ULIP maturity proceeds are tax-free if the sum assured is at least how many times the annual premium?\nA) 10 times  B) 5 times  C) 20 times  D) 2 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Withdrawals/maturity are tax-free if the **sum assured is at least 10 times the annual premium**. B, C and D are wrong multiples. This \"10x\" rule is a classic exam number; pair it with the Rs. 2.5 lakh premium threshold (post-1 Feb 2021).\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Per the Finance Act 2021, ULIPs issued on/after 1 Feb 2021 lose tax exemption if any year's premium exceeds:\nA) Rs. 1.5 lakh  B) Rs. 1 lakh  C) Rs. 5 lakh  D) Rs. 2.5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 10(10D) (as amended) removes exemption for such ULIPs if premium in any year exceeds **Rs. 2.5 lakh**. A (Rs. 1.5 lakh) is the PPF/80C limit. B and C are invented. Below Rs. 2.5 lakh the maturity stays exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Compared to mutual funds, ULIP disclosure standards are:\nA) Higher and more transparent  B) Identical  C) Lower, with fewer comparison services  D) Not regulated at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — ULIPs have **lower disclosure standards and less availability of comparison services**, while mutual funds have transparent daily/periodic disclosures. A is the opposite. B is wrong as they differ. D is wrong as ULIPs are regulated by IRDAI. Transparency favours mutual funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A ULIP requires compulsory premium contribution for at least how many years (except single-premium plans)?\nA) 5 years  B) 3 years  C) 10 years  D) 1 year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — ULIPs need compulsory contribution for **5 years** with no redemption for at least 5 years; discontinuation charges apply if premiums stop earlier. B is the ELSS lock-in. C and D are wrong. The 5-year rule is the ULIP liquidity constraint.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An actively managed fund aims to:\nA) Generate additional return (alpha) over the benchmark  B) Replicate the benchmark exactly  C) Avoid all equities  D) Guarantee returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An active fund manager seeks to **generate alpha** (return above the benchmark) by selecting securities. B describes an index fund. C and D are not the objective. The trade-off is higher cost for the chance of higher net return.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Compared to an actively managed fund, an index fund's cost is:\nA) Higher  B) Zero  C) The same  D) Lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An index fund is **lower cost** because it simply replicates the benchmark instead of paying for active decision-making. A is the active fund. C is wrong as they differ. B is wrong — index funds still have (low) costs. Lower cost is the index fund's core appeal.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Direct equity investing carries what kind of risk relative to equity funds?\nA) Concentrated risk on the invested security  B) Diversified risk  C) No risk  D) Sovereign risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Direct equity carries **concentrated risk** on the specific stocks chosen, while equity funds spread risk across many securities (diversification). B describes the fund. C and D are wrong. Diversification is the fund's safety advantage over single-stock buying.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An Exchange Traded Fund (ETF) is priced:\nA) At end-of-day NAV only  B) Once a week  C) On the last-3-days average  D) On a minute-to-minute basis like any security\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An ETF trades on the exchange and is priced **minute-to-minute** like any security. A (end-of-day NAV) describes an index fund. C is SGB issue pricing. B is invented. Live pricing is what distinguishes an ETF from an ordinary index fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An index fund (non-ETF) is priced at:\nA) End-of-day NAV  B) Live intraday prices  C) Last-3-days average  D) Auction price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A regular index fund is bought/sold from the AMC at the **end-of-day NAV**. B (intraday) describes an ETF. C is SGB pricing. D is invented. The NAV-based, once-a-day pricing is standard for non-exchange-traded funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Per the workbook, liquidity of ETFs on Indian stock exchanges is generally:\nA) Excellent for all ETFs  B) Better than physical cash  C) Guaranteed by SEBI  D) Very poor except for a couple of ETFs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook warns that ETF liquidity on Indian exchanges is **very poor except for a couple of ETFs**, with large NAV-vs-price gaps and wide buy-sell spreads. A overstates it. B and C are invented. This is a key exam trap — ETFs sound liquid but often aren't here.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which gold form does NOT incur a GST cost on purchase by the investor?\nA) Physical gold  B) Gold fund  C) Gold ETF  D) Sovereign Gold Bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Gold ETF** does not incur GST cost (the workbook marks GST cost \"No\" for Gold ETF). Physical gold (A) incurs GST. Gold funds (B) and SGBs (D) involve physical gold purchase where GST is incurred but accounted as an asset. So among these, Gold ETF is the GST-free entry.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which gold investment pays interest of 2.50% p.a.?\nA) Physical gold  B) Gold fund  C) Sovereign Gold Bond  D) Gold ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the **Sovereign Gold Bond** pays **2.50% p.a. interest**, payable semi-annually. Physical gold (A), gold funds (B) and gold ETFs (D) produce no income — only capital gains. The interest is a unique SGB advantage.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** When is a Sovereign Gold Bond first redeemable on its anniversary date, and when is its final redemption?\nA) After 5 years; final after 8 years  B) After 3 years; final after 5 years  C) After 1 year; final after 5 years  D) After 8 years; final after 15 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SGBs are redeemable on anniversary dates **after 5 years**, with **final redemption after 8 years**. The other combinations are invented. If redeemed with RBI there is no capital-gains tax; selling in the market earlier gives only moderate liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** In the workbook's table, REITs are described as investing primarily in:\nA) Equity shares  B) Government securities  C) Infrastructure projects like roads and bridges  D) Gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Per the workbook's table wording, REITs are described as investing primarily in **infrastructure projects like roads, bridges, etc.** (Note: standard market definitions are the reverse, but follow the workbook for the exam.) A, B and D are not what the table lists. Always answer per the source table.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Personal Accident Insurance covers death due to:\nA) Any cause  B) Old age only  C) Illness only  D) Accident only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Personal Accident Insurance covers death due to **accident only**, while life insurance covers death due to any cause. A describes life insurance. B and C are not what PA covers. PA also uniquely covers disability arising from accident.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Between a company deposit and a company debenture from the same company, which is typically secured?\nA) Company debenture  B) Company deposit  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **company debenture** is normally secured, so it carries relatively lower risk; a company deposit is unsecured (higher risk). B is wrong (deposits are unsecured). C and D ignore the security difference. More security → lower risk → slightly lower return on the debenture.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A retail investor wants a tax-saving (80C) product with the **shortest lock-in** and is comfortable with high risk. Which fits best?\nA) PPF  B) Tax-saving bank FD  C) ELSS  D) NPS Tier 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **ELSS** has the shortest lock-in (3 years) and is all-equity (high risk), matching a risk-tolerant investor. PPF (A) locks in for 15 years. Tax-saving FD (B) is locked for its tenure (typically 5 years). NPS (D) locks in till age 60. Shortest lock-in + high-risk tolerance points squarely to ELSS.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor wants a tax-saving product with **sovereign safety and fully tax-free returns**. Which is best?\nA) PPF  B) ELSS  C) Tax-saving bank FD  D) AIF Cat 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PPF** is sovereign (government-backed, low risk) and its returns are entirely tax-free. ELSS (B) is high-risk equity with gains taxed at 12.50%. The FD (C) is taxable each year. AIF (D) is a high-risk HNI product, not a tax-saver. Safety + tax-free returns is PPF's signature combination.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A High Net-worth Investor wants a **customised, concentrated portfolio held in their own demat account**. Which product fits?\nA) Equity mutual fund  B) ULIP  C) Index fund  D) PMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **PMS** holds investments in the investor's own demat account and can be customised and concentrated (not SEBI broad-based). A mutual fund (A) is pooled, common to all and SEBI broad-based. An index fund (C) just tracks a benchmark. A ULIP (B) is insurance-cum-investment. Customisation + own demat = PMS.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor with Rs. 1,000 to start and wanting a SEBI-regulated, broad-based, retail product should choose:\nA) PMS  B) AIF Cat 3  C) Equity mutual fund  D) Real estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An **equity mutual fund** allows entry from Rs. 1,000, is SEBI-regulated for broad-basing and is meant for retail investors. PMS (A) needs Rs. 50 lakh. AIF (B) needs Rs. 1 crore. Real estate (D) needs a large lump sum. Small ticket + retail + broad-based = mutual fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An HNI wants exposure to **complex or leveraged trading strategies including derivatives**. Which is designed for that?\nA) Index fund  B) Tax-saving FD  C) AIF Category 3  D) PPF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **AIF Category 3** undertakes diverse/complex trading strategies, including listed or unlisted derivatives, and suits HNIs seeking leveraged strategies. An index fund (A) merely tracks a benchmark. FD (B) and PPF (D) are low-risk fixed products. Complex/leveraged = AIF Cat 3.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor wants market-linked investing but also **life insurance cover in the same product**. Which fits?\nA) Mutual fund  B) Index fund  C) ETF  D) ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **ULIP** is insurance-cum-investment, providing life cover plus market-linked returns in one product. A mutual fund (A), ETF (C) and index fund (B) are pure investments with no insurance. If the client wants both in one wrapper, it's a ULIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A cost-conscious investor simply wants to **match the benchmark return at the lowest cost** without trying to beat it. Best choice?\nA) Index fund  B) Actively managed fund  C) PMS  D) AIF Cat 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An **index fund** replicates the benchmark at low cost — exactly matching the goal. An active fund (B) costs more while trying to beat the benchmark. PMS (C) and AIF (D) are high-cost, high-ticket products. Low cost + track the benchmark = index fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An investor wants gold exposure with **no purity concerns, no storage cost, that also earns interest**. Which is ideal?\nA) Physical gold  B) Gold ETF  C) Sovereign Gold Bond  D) Gold fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Sovereign Gold Bonds** have no purity concerns (no physical gold), no storage cost, and pay 2.50% p.a. interest. Physical gold (A) has purity and storage issues and no income. Gold ETF (B) and gold fund (D) have no purity/storage worries but pay no interest. Interest + no storage = SGB.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor wants gold exposure with **intraday (live) pricing on the exchange**. Which fits?\nA) Gold fund  B) Physical gold  C) Gold ETF  D) Sovereign Gold Bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **Gold ETF** can be bought as per the prevailing price at the moment (intraday) on the exchange. A gold fund (A) is priced at end-of-day NAV. Physical gold (B) trades at prevailing dealer price but is not an exchange security. SGBs (D) are priced on a last-3-days basis at issue. Live exchange pricing = Gold ETF.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor wants property-type exposure that is **diversified across many properties, professionally managed, and relatively liquid**. Best fit?\nA) Direct real estate  B) Company deposit  C) Physical gold  D) REIT/InvIT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **REITs/InvITs** spread exposure over many properties/projects, are professionally managed, allow a lower minimum, and (being listed) have relatively better liquidity. Direct real estate (A) is concentrated, lumpy and illiquid. Gold (C) and a company deposit (B) are not property exposure. Diversified + liquid property = REIT/InvIT.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An investor wants **instant liquidity** for their parked savings even at the cost of some penalty. Which fits best?\nA) Bank fixed deposit  B) FMP  C) Closed-ended AIF  D) Sovereign Gold Bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **bank FD** offers instant liquidity from the bank (though with possible interest clawback or premature penalty). An FMP (B) is listed but effectively very illiquid. A closed-ended AIF (C) has lock-in and poor liquidity. An SGB (D) only redeems with RBI after 5 years. Instant access = bank FD.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A debt investor wants **better post-tax returns by withdrawing amounts that have a large capital element**. Which approach does the workbook suggest?\nA) Bank FD  B) Company deposit  C) FMP held to maturity  D) Open-ended debt fund growth option\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook says post-tax returns can be better by investing in an **open-ended debt fund growth scheme and withdrawing needed amounts**, since each withdrawal has a large element of capital (not just interest). FD (A) interest is fully taxed. FMP (C) is held to maturity. Company deposit (B) interest is taxed. Growth-option withdrawals are the tax-efficient route.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An income-earner with dependents needs cover against **death from any cause**. Which is essential?\nA) Life insurance  B) Personal accident insurance  C) Health insurance  D) ULIP only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Life insurance** covers death from any cause and is essential for income earners with dependents. Personal accident (B) covers only accidental death/disability — an add-on, not a substitute. Health insurance (C) covers medical bills, not death payout. A ULIP (D) is investment-heavy and not the core protection here. Any-cause death cover = life insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A client already has adequate life insurance and now wants cover specifically for **permanent total disability from an accident**. Recommend:\nA) More life insurance  B) A debt fund  C) PPF  D) Personal accident insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Personal accident insurance** covers temporary/permanent, total/partial disability arising from accident — the gap life insurance does not fill (life insurance does not cover disability). More life insurance (A) won't pay for disability. PPF (C) and a debt fund (B) are investments, not insurance. PA is the add-on for accident disability.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Between a company deposit and a company debenture of the same company, an investor who wants **slightly higher return and accepts higher risk** should pick:\nA) Company deposit  B) Company debenture  C) Neither  D) Both equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **company deposit** is unsecured, so it carries higher risk and tends to offer a slightly higher return than a debenture of the same company. The debenture (B) is secured, lower risk, lower return. C and D ignore the trade-off. Higher return for higher risk = the unsecured deposit.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor wants index exposure as an **actual long-term/lifetime investment** rather than a short derivative position. Which fits?\nA) Index futures  B) Index options  C) Index fund  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An **index fund** is an actual investment that can be held for a very long tenure or lifetime. Index futures (A) last days to a max of 3 months. Index options (B) are similarly short-dated (some long-dated exist) and are derivative positions. For a buy-and-hold long-term position, the index fund is right.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A trader wants index exposure where the **maximum loss is limited to the premium paid**. Which fits?\nA) Index options (buyer)  B) Index futures  C) Index fund  D) ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For an **index option buyer**, the maximum risk equals the option premium paid. Index futures (B) carry risk equivalent to buying the underlying index fund (no premium cap on loss). An index fund (C) and ETF (D) carry full market risk of the underlying. Capped loss = option premium = option buyer.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Which best describes how a Gold ETF and Gold Fund differ in pricing?\nA) Both price intraday  B) ETF prices intraday; gold fund at end-of-day NAV  C) Both at end-of-day NAV  D) Gold fund prices intraday; ETF at NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **Gold ETF prices intraday** (prevailing price at the moment) while a **gold fund prices at end-of-day NAV**. A and C wrongly make them identical. D reverses them. The exchange-traded nature of the ETF gives it live pricing, just like equity ETFs vs index funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A client wants a debt route where **safety is enhanced by diversification across many instruments** rather than holding a single bond. Best fit?\nA) A single debt instrument  B) Company deposit  C) Bank FD  D) Open-ended debt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An **open-ended debt fund** diversifies across many instruments, giving relatively higher safety for the same level of rated instruments. A single debt instrument (A) is concentrated. A bank FD (C) is safe but is a single deposit, not diversified market instruments. A company deposit (B) is a single unsecured exposure. Diversification = debt fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Which product is **not regulated by SEBI for concentration**, allowing a normally concentrated portfolio?\nA) Equity mutual fund  B) AIF Cat 3  C) PMS  D) Index fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PMS** is not regulated by SEBI for concentration and is normally a concentrated portfolio. Equity MFs (A) are SEBI-regulated to ensure broad-basing. AIF Cat 3 (B) is regulated to avoid single-company concentration. An index fund (D) holds the whole benchmark. The freedom to concentrate is a PMS hallmark.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A retirement saver wants a **market-linked** accumulation product. Which set fits per the workbook?\nA) NPS, ELSS, other mutual fund schemes  B) PPF only  C) Bank FD only  D) Physical gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Market-linked retirement accumulation products listed are **NPS, ELSS and other mutual fund schemes**. PPF (B) is the non-market-linked example. Bank FD (C) and physical gold (D) are not the workbook's retirement-accumulation examples. Market-linked = NPS/ELSS/MF.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** For a non-market-linked retirement product, the workbook notes that even \"fixed return\" products now reset returns. Which examples are given?\nA) ELSS quarterly, NPS yearly  B) ULIP weekly  C) FD monthly, gold daily  D) EPF yearly, PPF quarterly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The workbook notes **EPF declares returns every year and PPF declares returns every quarter** — so even principal-guaranteed products no longer offer long-term fixed returns. A misattributes the resets to market products. B and C are invented. The point: \"guaranteed\" no longer means a fixed lifelong rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** An investor seeks gold via futures and wants to know the time horizon. Per the workbook, gold futures have a horizon of:\nA) 3 months  B) 12 months  C) Lifetime  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook, **gold futures** have a time horizon of **12 months**. (Index futures, by contrast, are a max of 3 months — option A is that figure.) Lifetime (C) describes gold ETFs/funds (actual investments). D is invented. Gold futures = 12 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which statement correctly compares ETF vs index fund **costs**?\nA) ETFs have no costs at all  B) Index funds have brokerage and bid-ask spread costs  C) ETFs incur brokerage, demat and buy-sell spread; index funds have no such extra costs  D) Both are identical in cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **ETFs** carry brokerage, demat and buy-sell spread costs in addition to (low) management cost; **index funds** have slightly higher management cost but normally no such extra trading costs. A wrongly claims ETFs are free. B swaps the products. D ignores the difference. The spread/brokerage is the ETF's hidden cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An advisor compares a tax-saving FD and PPF at similar headline rates. Why does PPF give a better post-tax return?\nA) PPF returns are tax-free while FD interest is taxed yearly  B) PPF has higher headline rate always  C) FD has a lock-in penalty  D) PPF is market-linked\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PPF returns are tax-free** whereas **FD interest is taxable each year**, so even at the same headline rate PPF's post-tax return is higher. B is not claimed by the workbook. C is irrelevant to the comparison. D is false — PPF is not market-linked. Tax treatment is the decisive factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An investor wants to invest in a specific type of equity without picking individual stocks or needing personal stock expertise. Best fit?\nA) Direct equity  B) An equity (or index) fund scheme  C) Company debenture  D) Gold ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investing in an **equity/index fund scheme** of the desired type avoids the need for personal expertise (or a PMS/RIA) that direct equity (A) demands, and spreads risk. A company debenture (C) is debt. A gold ETF (D) is gold, not equity. For diversified equity without stock-picking, choose a fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which product's redemption may attract a charge from the fund manager for **handing back the investments to the investor**?\nA) Open-ended mutual fund  B) PPF  C) Bank FD  D) PMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In **PMS**, the fund manager may charge for redeeming the investments or for handing back the charge of the investments to the investor. An open-ended MF (A) may levy an exit load but per a pre-declared rule. Bank FD (C) and PPF (B) have their own penalty/lock-in rules, not a handover charge. The handover charge is PMS-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A close-ended AIF is being considered by an investor who may need money soon. What is the key warning?\nA) It has instant liquidity  B) It has a lock-in period and poor liquidity  C) It is SEBI broad-based  D) Its minimum is Rs. 1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **close-ended AIF has a lock-in period and poor liquidity** — unsuitable for someone needing money soon. A is the opposite. C is wrong (AIF is regulated to avoid concentration but is not a broad-based retail product). D is wrong (AIF minimum is Rs. 1 crore). Liquidity risk is the warning.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which best contrasts mutual fund vs ULIP **liquidity**?\nA) Both allow anytime redemption  B) Both lock in for 15 years  C) ULIP is more liquid than MF  D) MF (open-ended) redeemable anytime (maybe exit load); ULIP needs 5-year compulsory contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An **open-ended MF can be redeemed anytime** (with possible exit load), whereas a **ULIP requires 5 years of compulsory contribution** and no redemption for 5 years. A overstates ULIP liquidity. C reverses reality. B is the PPF figure. MFs win on liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** An advisor is asked why ULIP post-expense returns are hard to compute compared to mutual funds. The reason is:\nA) ULIPs don't disclose NAV  B) Mortality and other charges are deducted from fund value  C) ULIPs are not market-linked  D) SEBI hides the data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In ULIPs, **insurance (mortality) and other charges are deducted from the fund value**, making post-expense return calculation difficult. A is not the stated reason. C is false — ULIPs are market-dependent. D is wrong (ULIPs are IRDAI-regulated). The embedded insurance charges blur the investment return.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which gold form has the investor manage **safety and storage costs** themselves?\nA) Physical gold  B) Gold ETF  C) Sovereign Gold Bond  D) Gold fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Physical gold** carries safety and storage costs borne by the investor. A gold ETF (B) has only nominal cost. SGBs (C) have no such costs (no physical gold). Gold funds (D) build storage into fund charges. Self-managed storage/safety = physical gold.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An investor wants the gold form that can be **exchanged for physical gold**. Which qualifies?\nA) Physical gold  B) Gold fund  C) Sovereign Gold Bond  D) Gold ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Among the listed forms, **physical gold** is already physical; the workbook marks \"exchange with physical gold\" as Not available for gold funds, and No for Gold ETFs and SGBs. So B, C and D cannot be exchanged for physical gold. Only direct physical gold gives you the metal in hand.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A REIT/InvIT is preferred over direct real estate primarily because direct real estate is:\nA) Diversified and liquid  B) Concentrated, lump-sum, and illiquid; needs investor management  C) Professionally managed  D) Always cheaper\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct real estate involves **large concentrated, lump-sum exposure that is poor and time-consuming to liquidate, and must be managed by the investor**. A and C describe REITs/InvITs, not direct real estate. D is not claimed. The concentration, illiquidity and management burden push investors to REITs/InvITs.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A subscriber wants to keep NPS risk **low** despite it being a market-linked product. How can they do this?\nA) Choose 75% equity  B) Choose a higher proportion of Government securities  C) Add Alternative Investments  D) It cannot be made low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NPS risk can be **made low by choosing a higher proportion of Government securities**. Choosing 75% equity (A) raises risk. Adding Alternative Investments (C) raises risk. D is false — the workbook explicitly says risk can be made low. The asset-allocation choice controls NPS risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An investor wants infrastructure (roads/bridges) exposure with diversification and professional management. Per the workbook's table, choose:\nA) Direct real estate  B) REIT  C) Physical gold  D) Company debenture\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook's table, **REITs** are described as primarily investing in infrastructure projects like roads and bridges, spread over many projects and professionally managed. Direct real estate (A) is concentrated and self-managed. Gold (C) and a debenture (D) are different asset classes. (Answer per workbook table wording.)\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Scenario & Numerical (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr. A has Rs. 60 lakh and wants a customised, concentrated equity portfolio in his own demat with negotiable fees. Mr. B has Rs. 1.2 crore and wants leveraged derivative strategies. The best products for A and B respectively are:\nA) MF and MF  B) PMS and AIF Cat 3  C) AIF Cat 3 and PMS  D) ULIP and PMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mr. A (Rs. 60 lakh ≥ PMS minimum Rs. 50 lakh, wants customisation in own demat) fits **PMS**. Mr. B (Rs. 1.2 crore ≥ AIF minimum Rs. 1 crore, wants leveraged derivatives) fits **AIF Cat 3**. A ignores their needs. C swaps them (A's Rs. 60 lakh is below the Rs. 1 crore AIF minimum anyway). D's ULIP doesn't match A's demand. Match the corpus to the minimum and the need to the strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A tax-saving FD pays 7% p.a. interest. The investor is in the 30% tax slab; FD interest is taxed yearly. What is the approximate post-tax return? (Formula: post-tax return = pre-tax rate × (1 − tax rate))\nA) 7.0%  B) 4.9%  C) 5.6%  D) 2.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Using **post-tax return = 7% × (1 − 0.30) = 7% × 0.70 = 4.9%**. Because FD interest is taxed every year, the investor keeps only 70% of the 7%. A (7.0%) ignores tax. C and D use wrong arithmetic. This is exactly why a tax-free PPF at 7% beats a taxable FD at 7% for a high-slab investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** PPF yields 7.1% (tax-free) and a taxable FD yields 7.5% to a 30%-slab investor. Compare post-tax returns. (Formula: FD post-tax = 7.5% × (1 − 0.30))\nA) FD wins at 7.5%  B) PPF wins: 7.1% vs FD's 5.25%  C) Tie  D) FD wins: 5.25% vs 7.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **FD post-tax = 7.5% × 0.70 = 5.25%**, while PPF stays **7.1% tax-free**. So PPF (7.1%) beats the FD (5.25%) even though the FD's headline rate is higher. A ignores tax. C is wrong (they differ). D mislabels the winner. Tax-free status flips the apparent advantage — the core lesson of the ELSS/PPF/FD comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor buys an SGB. After holding to maturity, they redeem it with RBI. They also received the semi-annual interest. Which statement on tax is correct?\nA) Both the capital gain and interest are taxable  B) Capital gain on RBI redemption is tax-free; interest is taxable  C) Both are tax-free  D) Only the interest is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On **redemption with RBI there is no capital-gains tax**, but the **2.50% interest is taxable** as per the IT Act. A wrongly taxes the redemption gain. C wrongly exempts the interest. D reverses the treatment. The capital-gains exemption on RBI redemption is the standout SGB tax benefit; interest is always taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Two investors hold the same SGB. Investor X redeems with RBI at maturity; Investor Y sells in the secondary market before maturity. How does their capital-gains tax differ?\nA) Both pay capital gains  B) Neither pays  C) X pays nothing; Y is taxable as capital gains  D) Y pays nothing; X is taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **X (RBI redemption) pays no capital-gains tax; Y (sold in the market before maturity) is taxable as capital gains.** A and B are blanket and wrong. D reverses them. The exemption applies only on redemption with RBI, not on a market sale — an important distinction for advising SGB holders who need early exit.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client wants a tax-saving product but says: \"I want to be able to get my full money out in 3 years if needed.\" Among ELSS, PPF, NPS, tax-saving FD (5-yr), which uniquely allows full withdrawal at 3 years?\nA) PPF  B) NPS Tier 1  C) ELSS  D) Tax-saving FD\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **ELSS** has a 3-year lock-in, after which the full amount can be withdrawn. PPF (A) locks for 15 years. NPS (B) locks till age 60. The tax-saving FD (D) locks for its full 5-year tenure. Only ELSS matches the 3-year full-exit requirement — but the client must accept equity risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A 35-year-old wants a retirement product where 60% can be taken tax-free at 60 but accepts that 40% becomes a taxable annuity. Which product is this, and what is the liquidity caveat?\nA) PPF; 15-year lock-in  B) ELSS; 3-year lock-in  C) NPS Tier 1; locked till age 60 (premature exit needs 80% to annuity)  D) ULIP; 5-year lock-in\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — This is **NPS Tier 1**: 60% tax-free at 60, 40% to a taxable annuity, **locked till age 60**, and a premature withdrawal requires using **80% of the amount to buy an annuity**. PPF (A) and ELSS (B) don't have the 60/40 annuity structure. ULIP (D) is insurance-cum-investment, not this pension design. The annuity rule is the NPS signature.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor compares two routes for Rs. 10 lakh of debt: (i) an open-ended debt fund growth option with withdrawals, (ii) a bank FD. Both earn ~7% pre-tax. Why might the debt-fund route give a better post-tax outcome?\nA) Debt funds are guaranteed  B) Each withdrawal from the growth option has a large capital element, unlike fully-taxed FD interest  C) FDs have higher fees  D) Debt funds are tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook, **growth-option withdrawals carry a large element of capital** (return of your own principal), so less of each rupee is taxable income, unlike FD interest which is fully taxed. A is false (not guaranteed). C is not the stated reason. D is false (debt funds aren't tax-free). The capital-vs-income mix drives the tax efficiency.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client needs Rs. 5 lakh of debt exposure that he might have to liquidate in 6 months. Rank liquidity (best to worst) among: bank FD, open-ended debt fund, FMP.\nA) FMP > debt fund > FD  B) FD > open-ended debt fund > FMP  C) Debt fund > FD > FMP  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bank FD has instant liquidity** (with penalty), an **open-ended debt fund is redeemable from the fund house** (possible exit load), and an **FMP is effectively very illiquid** despite being listed. So FD > debt fund > FMP. A reverses it. C misplaces the FD. D ignores real differences. For a 6-month possible exit, avoid the FMP.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An options buyer pays a premium of Rs. 8,000 on an index option. The market moves against them and they let it lapse. What is their maximum loss, and how does this compare to an equivalent index futures position?\nA) Unlimited; same as futures  B) Rs. 8,000; futures risk is like buying the index fund (not premium-capped)  C) Zero; futures is safer  D) Rs. 8,000; futures also capped at Rs. 8,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For an option **buyer the maximum loss is the premium = Rs. 8,000**. An **index futures** position carries risk \"same as buying an index fund\" — i.e., full exposure to the index move, not capped at a premium. A wrongly calls option loss unlimited. C is wrong (loss isn't zero). D wrongly caps futures loss. The premium cap is the option buyer's protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A high-slab investor must choose a gold route for an 8-year horizon, wanting interest income and a tax-free exit. Best choice and why?\nA) Physical gold — easy to store  B) Gold ETF — live pricing  C) SGB — 2.50% interest and tax-free capital gain if redeemed with RBI  D) Gold fund — NAV pricing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Over 8 years (the SGB final-redemption point), **SGB** gives 2.50% p.a. interest and, if redeemed with RBI, **no capital-gains tax**. Physical gold (A) has storage cost and capital-gains tax. Gold ETF (B) and gold fund (D) pay no interest and gains are taxable. Interest + tax-free RBI redemption over 8 years = SGB.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor buys a Gold ETF on the exchange. Beyond the gold price move, which costs will reduce their net return that a SGB-with-RBI-redemption would avoid?\nA) Only GST  B) Brokerage, demat charges, and the buy-sell spread  C) Storage cost  D) Mortality charges\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exchange-traded gold (ETF) incurs **brokerage, demat and buy-sell spread costs**. An SGB held to RBI redemption avoids these market-trading costs. A (GST) is actually \"No\" for Gold ETF. C (storage) doesn't apply to an ETF. D (mortality) is a ULIP charge. The trading frictions are the ETF's drag versus an SGB held to redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** An advisor compares an actively managed fund (expense ~1.8%) versus an index fund (expense ~0.3%) tracking the same benchmark. For the active fund to be worthwhile, it must:\nA) Match the benchmark exactly  B) Generate enough alpha to beat the index fund net of its higher cost  C) Have lower risk than the index  D) Pay interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The active fund attempts higher returns **net of its higher management cost**, so it is only worthwhile if its **alpha exceeds the ~1.5% extra cost** over the index fund. A describes the index fund's goal, not a justification for paying more. C and D are not the comparison. If active can't out-earn its extra fee, the cheaper index fund wins.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has Rs. 800 to invest monthly and wants diversified equity. Why is a mutual fund the only viable choice among MF, PMS, and AIF?\nA) MF gives guaranteed returns  B) MF minimum can be as low as Rs. 1,000 while PMS (Rs. 50 lakh) and AIF (Rs. 1 crore) are far beyond reach  C) PMS is tax-free  D) AIF is for retail\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **mutual fund's minimum (as low as Rs. 1,000)** is within reach, while **PMS needs Rs. 50 lakh and AIF Rs. 1 crore** — impossible for a small monthly saver. A is false (no guarantee). C is invented. D is false (AIF is for HNIs). The minimum-ticket ladder excludes PMS/AIF for small investors.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A ULIP issued in March 2023 has an annual premium of Rs. 3 lakh and sum assured Rs. 30 lakh. Is the maturity tax-exempt under Section 10(10D)?\nA) Yes, because SA is 10× premium  B) No, because the premium exceeds Rs. 2.5 lakh and the policy is post-1 Feb 2021  C) Yes, ULIPs are always exempt  D) No, because SA is below 10× premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even though SA (Rs. 30 lakh) is 10× premium (Rs. 3 lakh), the policy was issued **after 1 Feb 2021 with premium > Rs. 2.5 lakh**, so the Finance Act 2021 amendment **removes the exemption**. A applies only the 10× rule and misses the Rs. 2.5 lakh trap. C is false. D is factually wrong (SA is exactly 10×). Both conditions must be checked.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A ULIP issued in March 2023 has an annual premium of Rs. 2 lakh and sum assured Rs. 25 lakh. Is the maturity tax-exempt?\nA) No, because post-2021 ULIPs are never exempt  B) Yes — premium is below Rs. 2.5 lakh and SA is ≥ 10× premium  C) No, SA below 10×  D) Yes, but only the interest portion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Premium Rs. 2 lakh is **below the Rs. 2.5 lakh threshold**, and SA Rs. 25 lakh is **≥ 10× the Rs. 2 lakh premium**, so the maturity **remains exempt**. A overstates the rule. C is wrong (25 lakh is well above 10×). D invents a partial exemption. Below Rs. 2.5 lakh and SA ≥ 10× → still exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An HNI is choosing between PMS and an equity mutual fund for the same equity strategy. Which difference about fee regulation is accurate?\nA) Both fees are SEBI-capped identically  B) MF max fee is SEBI-regulated by fund size; PMS has no upfront charge, operating expenses capped at 0.50%, the rest as agreed  C) PMS fee is fully SEBI-capped  D) MF charges no fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **MF maximum fees are SEBI-regulated and vary by fund size**, while **PMS allows no upfront charge, caps operating expenses at 0.50%, and the management/performance fee is negotiated**. A and C overstate PMS regulation. D is false. The negotiated PMS fee (fixed/performance/both) contrasts with the size-based MF cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client wants property exposure with Rs. 5 lakh only. Direct property needs a large lump sum. What does the workbook suggest REITs/InvITs make possible?\nA) Guaranteed rent  B) Lower minimum exposure with diversification and professional management  C) Tax-free returns  D) Instant same-day settlement always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — REITs/InvITs make a **lower minimum exposure possible**, with diversification across many properties/projects and professional management — fitting a Rs. 5 lakh budget. A (guaranteed rent), C (tax-free) and D (instant settlement) are not claimed by the workbook. The lower entry ticket plus diversification is the key REIT/InvIT benefit over direct property.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** An investor compares a company deposit (8.5%, unsecured) and a debenture (8.0%, secured) from the same company. Which is the rational pick for a risk-averse investor, and why?\nA) Deposit, for the higher rate  B) Debenture, because it is secured (lower risk) — the 0.5% extra on the deposit is compensation for taking unsecured risk  C) Neither is different  D) Deposit, because debentures are unsecured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A risk-averse investor should prefer the **secured debenture (8.0%)**; the deposit's extra 0.5% is precisely the **reward for bearing unsecured (higher) risk**. A chases yield while ignoring risk. C ignores the security difference. D has the facts backwards (deposits are unsecured, debentures secured). Risk-averse → secured, lower-yield debenture.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investor is told an ETF is \"liquid like a share.\" On Indian exchanges, what realistic caveat must the advisor add?\nA) ETFs settle T+0 always  B) Except for a couple of ETFs, liquidity is often very poor with large NAV-price gaps and wide buy-sell spreads  C) ETFs have no spread  D) ETFs are guaranteed by the AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The realistic caveat: on Indian exchanges, **except for a couple of ETFs, liquidity is very poor**, with big gaps between the NAV and quoted price and wide buy-sell spreads. A, C and D overstate ETF convenience. So a buy-and-hold investor might be better served by a regular index fund redeemable at NAV from the AMC.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A subscriber wants to keep NPS as a low-risk retirement vehicle, comparable to PPF on principal safety. What allocation choice achieves this, and what trade-off remains versus PPF?\nA) 75% equity; PPF is riskier  B) Higher G-sec proportion; but NPS still has the 40%-annuity rule and lock-in till 60, unlike PPF's tax-free withdrawal  C) Max Alternative Investments; no trade-off  D) Equal equity and gold; PPF has no lock-in\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choosing a **higher proportion of G-secs** lowers NPS risk toward PPF-like safety, but NPS still imposes the **40%-compulsory-annuity (taxable) rule and lock-in till age 60**, whereas PPF allows tax-free withdrawal at maturity. A raises risk. C and D contain false claims (no trade-off / PPF has no lock-in). Even safe NPS differs from PPF on exit and tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client wants index exposure for exactly 2 months expecting a short-term rise, with the smallest upfront outlay and capped downside. Which derivative fits best and why?\nA) Index fund — long term  B) Index option (buy) — short horizon and loss capped at premium  C) Index futures — but downside not capped  D) ETF — long-term holding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **bought index option** suits a short (days-to-~3-month) horizon and caps the maximum loss at the premium paid. An index fund (A) and ETF (D) are long-term actual investments, not short trades. Index futures (C) fit the horizon but the downside is not premium-capped (risk like the underlying). Short horizon + capped loss = buy an option.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Comparing index futures and an index fund on time horizon: a client says \"I'll hold this for 10 years.\" Which is appropriate and why?\nA) Index futures — they roll forever  B) Index fund — it is an actual investment that can be held for very long/lifetime, while futures max out around 3 months  C) Both equally  D) Index options — long-dated forever\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **index fund is an actual investment held for very long tenures or lifetime**, whereas **index futures only last up to ~3 months**. A is wrong — futures don't roll forever costlessly. C ignores the horizon mismatch. D overstates option tenor. For a 10-year hold, the fund is the right instrument.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A 40-year-old earning member with two dependent children and a home loan asks if a personal accident policy is enough protection. What should the advisor explain?\nA) Yes, PA covers all death  B) No — PA covers only accidental death/disability; life insurance (any-cause death) is essential, with PA as an add-on  C) Yes, PA is cheaper  D) No — only health insurance is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PA covers only accidental death and disability**; for a sole earner with dependents and a loan, **life insurance (covering death from any cause) is essential**, and PA is a useful add-on (especially for permanent total disability). A is false (PA is accident-only). C ignores the coverage gap. D wrongly substitutes health cover. Life cover first, PA as a supplement.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor compares Gold ETF vs Gold Fund redemption mechanics. Which statement is accurate?\nA) Both redeem at end-of-day NAV from the AMC  B) Gold ETF is sellable on the exchange (decent liquidity, costs apply); gold fund is redeemed by the fund house at end-of-day NAV  C) Gold fund trades intraday on exchange  D) Neither can be sold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **Gold ETF is sold on the exchange** (decent liquidity, but brokerage/spread costs apply), while a **gold fund is redeemed by the fund house at end-of-day NAV**. A wrongly makes both AMC-redeemed. C wrongly makes the fund exchange-traded. D is false. The exchange-vs-AMC route is the practical difference for the client.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An investor seeking maximum safety of principal among debt routes chooses between a single debt instrument, an open-ended debt fund, and a bank FD. Which two offer relatively higher safety, and through what mechanisms?\nA) Single instrument (rating) and FMP (lock-in)  B) Bank FD (high safety) and open-ended debt fund (diversification across instruments)  C) Only the single debt instrument  D) Company deposit and debenture\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **bank FD has pretty high safety**, and an **open-ended debt fund gains relatively higher safety through diversification** across many instruments (for the same rating). A single instrument (C) is concentrated and less safe. A and D introduce products not in this trio or with different risk profiles. Bank-backing and diversification are the two safety mechanisms.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client with Rs. 70 lakh wants concentrated, customised equity, but a friend with Rs. 9 lakh wants the same strategy. Why can only the first use PMS?\nA) PMS is tax-free for large investors  B) PMS minimum is Rs. 50 lakh; the Rs. 9 lakh friend falls short and should use a mutual fund  C) PMS minimum is Rs. 1 crore  D) The friend should use an AIF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PMS requires a Rs. 50 lakh minimum**; the Rs. 70 lakh client qualifies but the Rs. 9 lakh friend does not and should use a **mutual fund** (min as low as Rs. 1,000). A is invented. C is the AIF minimum, not PMS. D is wrong — AIF needs Rs. 1 crore, even further out of reach. The minimum-ticket rule gates PMS access.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An investor wants a single number to judge ULIP performance like a mutual fund's return, but struggles. The advisor explains the core reason and a related disclosure point. Which captures both?\nA) ULIPs don't invest in markets; and disclose more than MFs  B) Mortality and other charges are deducted from fund value (hard to compute post-expense return); and ULIPs have lower disclosure with fewer comparison services  C) ULIPs are SEBI-capped; and disclose daily NAV like MFs  D) ULIPs are tax products; and have no charges\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The post-expense return is hard to compute because **mortality and other charges are deducted from the fund value**, and separately, **ULIPs have lower disclosure standards with fewer independent comparison services** than MFs. A is false on both counts (ULIPs are market-linked; disclose less). C is wrong (IRDAI-regulated, weaker disclosure). D is false. Embedded charges plus weaker disclosure explain the difficulty.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A retiree compares a market-linked retirement product (NPS G-sec option) and a non-market-linked one (PPF) on principal risk and liquidity. Which comparison is correct?\nA) NPS G-sec is high risk; PPF is liquid  B) NPS risk depends on option (G-sec = low); both have lock-ins, with non-market-linked liquidity even poorer  C) PPF is market-linked; NPS is fixed  D) Both have instant liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NPS risk depends on the chosen option (G-sec = low)**, and **tax-advantaged retirement products carry lock-ins**; the workbook notes liquidity tends to be **even poorer in principal-guaranteed (non-market-linked) products**. A wrongly calls G-sec NPS high risk. C reverses the products. D is false (both have lock-ins). Lock-ins exist precisely to preserve funds for retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** An advisor must pick a gold route for a client who refuses any storage hassle, wants exchange-listed live pricing, and is fine paying brokerage. Which fits, and what is the main cost caveat?\nA) Physical gold; storage cost  B) Gold ETF; brokerage, demat and buy-sell spread costs apply  C) SGB; locked 8 years  D) Gold fund; only NAV, no costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **Gold ETF** gives exchange-listed, live (intraday) pricing with no storage hassle; the caveat is **brokerage, demat and buy-sell spread costs**. Physical gold (A) involves storage. SGB (C) suits a long horizon but is not about live intraday trading and has its own redemption schedule. D wrongly claims a gold fund has no costs (it has AMC charges and NAV-only pricing). Live pricing + brokerage = Gold ETF.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Case Study\n\n**Scenario:** Mrs. Iyer, aged 45, is in the 30% tax slab. She approaches you, her investment adviser, with these goals:\n1. Save tax under Section 80C while keeping her capital sovereign-safe and her returns tax-free.\n2. Invest a small monthly amount (Rs. 5,000) into diversified equity without picking stocks.\n3. Allocate Rs. 60 lakh to a customised, concentrated equity portfolio held in her own demat account.\n4. Add a gold allocation for 8 years that earns income and gives a tax-free exit.\n5. Ensure her family is protected if she dies of any cause, and additionally against disability from an accident.\n\nShe has compared a tax-saving FD (7.5% taxable) against PPF (7.1% tax-free) and is unsure which gives a better post-tax return.\n\n<details><summary>Q1. For goal 1 (sovereign-safe, tax-free 80C), which product do you recommend over a tax-saving FD, and prove the post-tax comparison?</summary>\n\n**Recommend PPF.** PPF is sovereign (government-backed, low risk) and its returns are entirely tax-free, matching \"sovereign-safe + tax-free.\"\n\n**Post-tax proof (Formula: post-tax return = pre-tax rate × (1 − tax rate)):**\n- Tax-saving FD: 7.5% × (1 − 0.30) = 7.5% × 0.70 = **5.25%** post-tax (interest taxed yearly).\n- PPF: **7.1% tax-free** = 7.1% post-tax.\n\nSince **7.1% > 5.25%**, PPF wins despite the FD's higher headline rate. The lesson: tax treatment can reverse an apparent rate advantage. (PPF also offers max Rs. 1,50,000/year contribution and a 15-year lock-in.)\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q2. For goal 2 (Rs. 5,000/month diversified equity, no stock-picking), what fits, and why not PMS or AIF?</summary>\n\n**Recommend an equity mutual fund (e.g., an index fund or diversified equity fund).** A mutual fund accepts amounts as low as Rs. 1,000, spreads risk across many securities (diversification), and needs no personal stock-picking expertise — ideal for a Rs. 5,000 monthly saver.\n\n**Why not PMS or AIF:** PMS requires a minimum of **Rs. 50 lakh** and AIF requires **Rs. 1 crore** — both far beyond a Rs. 5,000 monthly budget. They also suit HNIs seeking customised or complex strategies, not small-ticket diversified investing. The minimum-ticket ladder (MF Rs. 1,000 → PMS Rs. 50 lakh → AIF Rs. 1 crore) settles it.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q3. For goal 3 (Rs. 60 lakh, customised, concentrated, own demat), what fits, and how does it differ from a mutual fund?</summary>\n\n**Recommend PMS (Portfolio Management Services).** Her Rs. 60 lakh clears the **Rs. 50 lakh PMS minimum**. PMS holds investments in the **investor's own demat account**, can be **customised** to her needs, and is **not SEBI-capped on concentration** — so a concentrated portfolio is allowed.\n\n**Difference from a mutual fund:** A mutual fund is a **pooled** vehicle with units allocated to all investors in common, **SEBI-regulated for broad-basing** (so it can't be deliberately concentrated), with fees capped by SEBI by fund size. PMS, by contrast, allows customisation/concentration, no upfront charge, operating expenses capped at 0.50%, and a negotiated (fixed/performance/both) fee. Customisation + own demat + concentration = PMS.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q4. For goal 4 (8-year gold allocation with income and tax-free exit), which gold route, and what are the tax mechanics?</summary>\n\n**Recommend Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGB).** SGBs match an 8-year horizon (final redemption after 8 years; redeemable on anniversaries after 5 years).\n\n**Tax/income mechanics:**\n- SGBs pay **2.50% p.a. interest**, semi-annually — the only gold form with income. This **interest is taxable** under the IT Act.\n- If **redeemed with RBI** (e.g., at the 8-year maturity), there is **no capital-gains tax** — a tax-free exit on the capital gain.\n- (If she instead sold in the secondary market before maturity, the gain would be taxable as capital gains.)\n\nVersus alternatives: physical gold has GST, storage cost, purity worries and taxable gains; Gold ETFs/funds pay no interest and gains are taxable. So SGB uniquely delivers income + tax-free RBI redemption.\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Q5. For goal 5 (any-cause death protection plus accident disability cover), what combination do you advise, and why is one product alone insufficient?</summary>\n\n**Recommend Life Insurance as the core, with Personal Accident (PA) insurance as an add-on.**\n\n- **Life insurance** covers death due to **any cause** (including accident) — essential for an income earner with dependents. This is the foundation of her family's protection.\n- **Personal Accident insurance** covers **death due to accident only**, but crucially also covers **temporary/permanent, total/partial disability arising from an accident** — which life insurance does **not** cover.\n\n**Why one alone is insufficient:** Life insurance alone leaves a gap because it does not pay for disability. PA insurance alone is inadequate because it does not cover non-accidental (e.g., illness) death. Combining them gives any-cause death cover (life insurance) plus accident-disability cover (PA add-on). PA is a supplement, not a substitute, for life insurance.\n</details>\n\n"}, {"num": "20", "title": "Case Studies", "module": "Putting It All Together", "notes": "# Chapter 20: Case Studies — Short Notes\n\n## 🎯 What you'll learn\n- The **practical aspects** of giving financial advice through worked case studies.\n- How to build **comprehensive financial planning solutions** for a household.\n\n> ⚠️ **This is the most important chapter for the exam.** The NISM X-B exam has **60 marks of case studies**. The cases pull together everything: tax, retirement corpus, insurance need, asset allocation, and health-insurance mechanics. Get comfortable with the **MS Excel functions** used below — they are the whole game.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ How to attack a case study (the method)\n\n**Step 0 — Read twice and list the givens.** Write down every number: current cost, inflation, return, years, existing cover, etc. Note the **timing** (\"end of year\" vs \"beginning of year\") — it changes the formula.\n\n**Step 1 — Identify what's being asked.** Is it a **future value** (grow a number forward), a **present value / corpus** (how much you need today), an **insurance need**, a **return**, or an **allocation %**?\n\n**Step 2 — Pick the right Excel function.** This table is your toolkit:\n\n| Function | What it does | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **FV(rate, nper, pmt, pv)** | Future value — grows money **forward** | \"What will Rs.X cost / be worth after n years?\" Use `FV(infl, n,, -C0)` for future cost. |\n| **PV(rate, nper, pmt, fv, type)** | Present value — the **corpus needed today** to fund a stream | Retirement corpus, insurance corpus. `type=1` = payments at **start** of period. |\n| **PMT(rate, nper, pv)** | The level instalment | **EMI** of a loan. |\n| **RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv)** | The implied **return** | \"What return is this product giving?\" |\n| **NPER** | Number of periods | How long money lasts. |\n\n**Step 3 — Inflation-adjusted (real) return.** When an income stream **rises with inflation** every year (e.g., living expenses), you must discount with the **real return**, NOT the nominal return:\n\n$$\\text{Real return} = \\frac{1 + \\text{investment return}}{1 + \\text{inflation}} - 1$$\n\n💡 This single formula appears in almost every retirement/insurance case. Memorise it.\n\n**Step 4 — Solve and sanity-check.** Does the answer make sense? A corpus should be much bigger than one year's expense. A future cost should be bigger than today's cost.\n\n**Excel sign convention:** money you *pay out* is entered as **negative**; money you *receive* is **positive**. That's why you'll see `-600000` inside the functions.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Fully Worked Case Studies\n\n### CASE A — Mr. Z (Retirement corpus + asset allocation) [Source Case 1]\n\n**Scenario.** Mr. Z, **52**, retires at **60**. Net savings **Rs.50,000/month**, growing 20% p.a. (this excludes retirement contributions). Monthly retirement contributions **Rs.9,000** (own Rs.4,000 + employer Rs.5,000), growing 20% p.a. Existing **retirement corpus Rs.12 lakh** (all debt, 8% p.a.). Other savings/investments **Rs.50 lakh**, 30% in equity. Each year he invests disposable savings into **debt:equity = 80:20**. Long-run: **equity 15%, debt 8.5%**. At age 55, an **outflow of Rs.5 lakh** met from annual savings. Inflation **10%**, post-retirement return **11%**. Current expenses **Rs.40,000/month**. Zero date = end of age 52; annual calculations; ignore tax & interest on within-year savings.\n\n**Q1. Retirement corpus at 60?**\nThe Rs.9,000/month = **Rs.1,08,000/year**, growing 20%. So each year's contribution is grown to age 60 at 8%, and the existing Rs.12 lakh is grown 8 years at 8%.\n- Each annual contribution's future value uses `=FV(8%, years-to-retirement,, -contribution)`.\n- Existing corpus: `=FV(8%, 8,, -1200000)` = **Rs.22,21,116**.\n- Summing the future values of all contributions + the grown corpus gives **Rs. 48,65,914**. ✅ **Answer (d)**\n\n**Q2. At age 55, % of portfolio in debt (excluding retirement corpus)?**\nTrack two side-portfolios from the annual savings (split 80% debt : 20% equity each year), with the Rs.5 lakh outflow at age 55 reducing the savings that year:\n- Debt portfolio grows at **8.5%**, equity at **15%**.\n- At age 55 the debt allocation works out to **69.49%**. ✅ **Answer (a)**\n\n**Q3. If he re-invests the entire retirement corpus in debt, % in debt at retirement?**\nAt age 60: Total Corpus (A) = **48,65,914**; Total Debt Portfolio (B) = **1,80,37,404**; Total Equity Portfolio (C) = **79,60,886**.\n$$\\%\\text{ Debt} = \\frac{A+B}{A+B+C}\\times100 = \\frac{48,65,914 + 1,80,37,404}{48,65,914+1,80,37,404+79,60,886}\\times100 = \\mathbf{74.21\\%}$$\n✅ **Answer (c)** — (the retirement corpus is treated as debt, hence added to B in the numerator).\n\n**Q4. Corpus needed to sustain the same standard of living for 15 years after retirement?**\n1. Future living expense at retirement: `=FV(10%, 8,, -480000)` = **Rs.10,28,922.63** (today's Rs.4,80,000/yr grown 8 yrs at 10%).\n2. Real (inflation-adjusted) return: `=(1+11%)/(1+10%) - 1` = **0.91%**.\n3. Corpus: `=PV(0.91%, 15, -1028922.63,, 1)` (the **1** = payments at the **beginning** of each year) = **Rs. 1,44,96,632**.\n✅ **Answer (a)**\n\n💡 Notice the pattern: **FV to bring today's expense to retirement → real return → PV to find the corpus.**\n\n---\n\n### CASE B — Mr. Y (Goal funding) [Source Case 2]\n\n**Scenario.** Mr. Y, **40**. Goal 1: Son's PG education in **Year 5**, current cost **Rs.15,00,000/yr** for **2 years** (paid end of each year), inflation **15%**. Goal 2: Daughter's marriage at end of **Year 7**, current cost **Rs.1,00,00,000**, inflation **10%**. Corpus **Rs.2,00,00,000** invested at **8%**. Ignore tax.\n\n**Q1. Money to set apart at end of Year 5 for the son's education (the set-aside earns 6%)?**\n- Cost in Year 5: `=FV(15%, 5,, -1500000)` = **Rs.30,17,035.78** [A].\n- Cost in Year 6: `=FV(15%, 6,, -1500000)` = **Rs.34,69,591.15**.\n- Amount needed in Year 5 to fund the Year-6 cost (discount 1 year at 6%): `=PV(6%, 1,, -3469591.15)` = **Rs.32,73,199.20** [B].\n- Total at Year 5 = A + B = **Rs.62,90,234.98**. ✅ **Answer (b)**\n\n**Q2. Likely outflow on daughter's marriage in Year 7?**\n`=FV(10%, 7,, -10000000)` = **Rs.1,94,87,171**. ✅ **Answer (a)**\n\n**Q3. Balance left in corpus after both goals (if he does NOT use the separate 6% account)?**\nThe Rs.2 crore grows at 8% while the two education outflows (Year 5 and Year 6) and the marriage outflow (Year 7) are deducted at their respective times. Growing 2 crore and netting the outflows leaves **≈ Rs.69,65,820**. ✅ **Answer (b)**\n\n**Q4. How would you describe his investment policy for the corpus (8% return)?**\nAn 8% blended return on a debt-equity mix is **a little conservative** (heavy on debt). ✅ **Answer (a)**\n\n---\n\n### CASE C — Mr. & Mrs. Gupta (NRI returning to India — tax & insurance) [Source Case 3]\n\n**Scenario.** Both 45, returning to India after 20 years in the USA. They have: a US house **rented out**; **tax-deferred US retirement accounts**; **USD 1 million** life cover each (next 15 years); employer health policy valid till year-end.\n\n**Q1. After becoming Indian residents, is rental income from the US property taxable in India?**\n**(a) Yes — taxable in India, but they get credit for tax paid in the USA.** Indian residents are taxed on **global income**; double taxation is relieved by **foreign tax credit**.\n\n**Q2. Tax on income accrued in the US tax-deferred retirement account?**\n**(a)** If the account is **notified under Section 89A**, India taxes it **in the year of withdrawal** (matching the US treatment) and gives **credit for US tax**. (Section 89A removes the timing mismatch that would otherwise tax accruals each year in India.)\n\n**Q3. Do they need fresh life insurance in India?**\n**(c)** No — they **need not buy fresh** policies; they can **keep the existing US policies** and pay premiums from US rental income or remit from India. (Indian law permits continuing a foreign life policy.)\n\n**Q4. What about health insurance?**\n**(b)** Continue the existing (worldwide) policy **and also buy a fresh Indian health policy** to **build a no-claim history** in India, even at extra cost. (A local policy and claim record are valuable for the long term.)\n\n---\n\n### CASE D — Mr. Smart (Retired couple — inflation-adjustment corpus) [Source Case 4]\n\n**Scenario.** Mr. Smart (60) retires; pension **Rs.4,80,000/yr** received **yearly in advance**, adjusting only **50%** with inflation. Mrs. Smart (58) gets the pension for life if he dies first. Retirement dues **Rs.40 lakh (after tax)**. Living expenses = pension amount. Employer Mediclaim for life (adequate). Inflation **6%**. Life expectancy: Mr. 87, Mrs. 85 → plan for **~27 years**. They also want **Rs.1,00,000/yr** (inflation 6%) for social/leisure spend, plus to make up the **inflation-adjustment shortfall** in the pension. Expenses at **beginning of each year**.\n\n**Q1. Inflation adjustment required (discounted at 5%, at beginning of year)?**\nBecause the pension covers only **50%** of inflation, each year a growing shortfall must be funded, plus the Rs.1 lakh lifestyle amount. Discounting that growing stream over ~27 years at 5% gives **≈ Rs.80,18,710**. ✅ **Answer (a)**\n\n**Q2. The required amount exceeds the Rs.40 lakh corpus. This means:**\n**(c)** The couple **can** meet the requirement **only if the Rs.40 lakh earns about 9.22% p.a.** (the rate at which Rs.40 lakh suffices). At a mere 5%, Rs.40 lakh is not enough.\n\n**Q3. Highest-interest government fixed-income option for the couple?**\n**(a) Senior Citizen Savings Scheme (SCSS)** — the **government scheme** (PSU bank FDs are bank products, not government schemes).\n\n---\n\n### CASE E (extra practice, my own) — Ms. Rao (Pure retirement-corpus drill)\n\n**Scenario.** Ms. Rao, **45**, currently spends **Rs.60,000/month**. She retires at **60**, wants the **same lifestyle (inflation-adjusted)** for **20 years** in retirement. Inflation **7%**; investment return in retirement **9%**. Solve step by step.\n\n**Solution.**\n1. Current annual expense = 60,000 × 12 = **Rs.7,20,000**.\n2. Expense at retirement (15 years away): `=FV(7%, 15,, -720000)` = **Rs.7,20,000 × 1.07¹⁵ ≈ Rs.19,86,000**.\n3. Real return = `(1+9%)/(1+7%) − 1` = **1.8692%**.\n4. Corpus needed at 60: `=PV(1.8692%, 20, -1986000)` ≈ **Rs.3.27 crore**.\n\n💡 Same three-step pattern as Case A Q4: **FV the expense → real return → PV the corpus.**\n\n---\n\n### CASE F (extra practice, my own) — Mr. Iqbal (Life-insurance need / Human Life Value)\n\n**Scenario.** Mr. Iqbal, 40, earns **Rs.80,000/month**. He wants his family to keep receiving his income (rising with inflation) for the **20 years** he'd have worked. Inflation **6%**, investment return **10%**. He has an outstanding **home loan of Rs.40 lakh** and **existing cover of Rs.50 lakh**. How much **additional** life insurance does he need?\n\n**Solution (income-replacement / HLV method).**\n1. Current annual income = 80,000 × 12 = **Rs.9,60,000**.\n2. Real return = `(1+10%)/(1+6%) − 1` = **3.7736%**.\n3. Corpus to replace income for 20 years: `=PV(3.7736%, 20, -960000)` ≈ **Rs.1,32,90,000**.\n4. Add the **home loan** to be cleared: + **Rs.40,00,000** → total need ≈ **Rs.1,72,90,000**.\n5. Subtract **existing cover Rs.50,00,000** → **additional cover ≈ Rs.1,22,90,000 (≈ Rs.1.23 crore)**.\n\n💡 Insurance-need recipe: **PV of income stream (real return) + liabilities + lump-sum goals − existing cover − existing assets earmarked.**\n\n---\n\n## 🩺 Health-insurance mechanics you MUST know (from the source practice cases)\n\n> The exam loves **Top-up vs Super Top-up** and **deductible / co-pay** questions.\n\n- **Base policy:** pays first, up to its sum insured.\n- **Deductible:** the amount **you** pay before a top-up kicks in.\n- **Top-up policy:** activates only when a **single claim** exceeds the deductible.\n- **Super Top-up policy:** activates when the **total of all claims in the year** exceeds the deductible (aggregate). This is why Super Top-up is more useful.\n- **Co-pay:** a **% of the claim** the insured bears, applied **after** the deductible.\n\n**Worked examples:**\n- *Top-up (Rs.10L, Rs.5L deductible), single bill Rs.4L, base Rs.3L:* Base pays 3L, you pay **Rs.1L**; top-up doesn't trigger (single bill < 5L deductible).\n- *Top-up, three bills 2L/3L/4L (base 3L exhausted, deductible 5L per single claim):* No single bill ≥ 5L → top-up never triggers → out-of-pocket **Rs.6L**.\n- *Super Top-up (Rs.10L, Rs.3L deductible), three bills 2L/3L/4L:* Base 3L covers the first 3L; once **aggregate** crosses the 3L deductible, super top-up pays the rest → out-of-pocket **NIL**.\n- *Co-pay 5% + deductible Rs.10,000 on a Rs.50,000 claim:* Reimbursement = 50,000 − 10,000 − 5%×(50,000−10,000) = 50,000 − 10,000 − 2,000 = **Rs.38,000**.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 One-Page Recap\n- **Method:** list givens → identify ask → pick Excel function → use **real return** for inflating streams → sanity-check.\n- **Real return = (1+return)/(1+inflation) − 1.** Used in nearly every retirement/insurance corpus.\n- **Retirement corpus:** FV today's expense to retirement → PV with real return for the retirement years (use `type=1` if payments at start).\n- **Life-insurance need:** PV of income (real return) + liabilities + lump goals − existing cover.\n- **Goal funding:** FV the goal cost at its own inflation; discount back where money is set aside early.\n- **NRI returning:** global income taxable in India with **foreign tax credit**; **Sec 89A** defers retirement-account tax to withdrawal; can keep foreign life policy; build local health-insurance no-claim history.\n- **Health insurance:** Top-up = per single claim; **Super Top-up = aggregate**; co-pay applied after deductible.\n- **Excel sign rule:** outflows negative, inflows positive.\n\n## 🔑 Key Numbers & Terms\n| Term / Number | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **FV** | `FV(rate,nper,pmt,pv)` — grow money forward |\n| **PV** | `PV(rate,nper,pmt,fv,type)` — corpus needed today; `type=1` = start of period |\n| **PMT** | Loan **EMI** function |\n| **RATE** | Implied return of a product |\n| **Real return** | `(1+return)/(1+inflation) − 1` |\n| **Top-up** | Triggers when a **single** claim > deductible |\n| **Super Top-up** | Triggers when **aggregate** claims > deductible |\n| **Co-pay** | % of claim the insured pays (after deductible) |\n| **Section 89A** | Defers Indian tax on foreign retirement accounts to year of withdrawal |\n| **SCSS** | Senior Citizen Savings Scheme (a government scheme) |\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 20: Case Studies — Flashcards\n\n> **How to use:** Cover the answer, recall it, then check. These cards drill the **METHOD and formulas** the case studies use.\n\n**Q1.** Which Excel function grows a present amount into the future?\n> **A:** **FV(rate, nper, pmt, pv).** E.g., future cost of education = `FV(inflation, years,, -current_cost)`.\n\n**Q2.** Which Excel function gives the corpus needed today to fund a stream?\n> **A:** **PV(rate, nper, pmt, fv, type).** `type=1` means payments at the **beginning** of each period.\n\n**Q3.** Which Excel function gives a loan's EMI?\n> **A:** **PMT(rate, nper, pv).** (Not \"EMI\" — there is no EMI function.)\n\n**Q4.** Which Excel function finds the implied return of a product?\n> **A:** **RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv).** E.g., return on a ULIP investment portion = `RATE(20, -5850,, 200000)`.\n\n**Q5.** State the inflation-adjusted (real) return formula.\n> **A:** **Real return = (1 + investment return) / (1 + inflation) − 1.** Used to discount an income stream that rises with inflation.\n\n**Q6.** Why must you use the real return (not nominal) for retirement-corpus / insurance-need calculations?\n> **A:** Because the income/expense stream **rises with inflation each year**. Discounting at the real return correctly nets the growth against the earning rate.\n\n**Q7.** What is the Excel sign convention?\n> **A:** **Money paid out = negative; money received = positive.** That's why current cost is entered as `-C0`.\n\n**Q8.** Give the three-step recipe for a retirement corpus.\n> **A:** (1) **FV** today's annual expense to the retirement date at inflation; (2) compute **real return**; (3) **PV** that future expense over the retirement years at the real return (use `type=1` if expenses are at the start of the year).\n\n**Q9.** Give the recipe for life-insurance (income-replacement) need.\n> **A:** **PV of annual income** (using real return) over the working years + **outstanding liabilities** (loans) + **lump-sum goals (e.g., child's corpus)** − **existing life cover** = additional cover required.\n\n**Q10.** In Case A, what was Mr. Z's retirement corpus at 60?\n> **A:** **Rs.48,65,914** — sum of the future values of growing contributions plus the existing Rs.12 lakh grown 8 years at 8%.\n\n**Q11.** In Case A Q3, how is the % debt at retirement computed when the corpus is reinvested in debt?\n> **A:** `(A+B)/(A+B+C)×100` where A = retirement corpus, B = debt portfolio, C = equity portfolio → **74.21%**.\n\n**Q12.** In Case A Q4, what were the three steps to the Rs.1,44,96,632 corpus?\n> **A:** `FV(10%,8,,-480000)=10,28,922.63`; real return `(1+11%)/(1+10%)-1=0.91%`; `PV(0.91%,15,-1028922.63,,1)=1,44,96,632`.\n\n**Q13.** In Case B, how do you find money to set aside in Year 5 for an education cost incurred in Year 6?\n> **A:** Compute the Year-6 cost via FV, then **PV it back one year** at the set-aside rate (6%): `PV(6%,1,,-3469591.15)=32,73,199.20`.\n\n**Q14.** A goal cost of Rs.1 crore today, inflation 10%, due in 7 years — future cost?\n> **A:** `FV(10%,7,,-10000000) = Rs.1,94,87,171`.\n\n**Q15.** For an NRI returning to India, is global income (e.g., US rent) taxable in India?\n> **A:** **Yes**, once resident — but with **credit for foreign tax paid** (avoids double taxation).\n\n**Q16.** What does Section 89A do for a foreign tax-deferred retirement account?\n> **A:** If the account is **notified under Sec 89A**, India taxes the income **in the year of withdrawal** (like the US) and gives **credit for US tax**, removing the timing mismatch.\n\n**Q17.** Must a returning NRI buy fresh life insurance in India?\n> **A:** **No** — they can keep the existing foreign policy and pay premiums from foreign income or remit from India.\n\n**Q18.** Best health-insurance advice for a returning NRI with a worldwide policy?\n> **A:** Continue it **and** also buy a fresh Indian health policy to **build a no-claim history** locally, even at extra cost.\n\n**Q19.** Difference between a Top-up and a Super Top-up policy?\n> **A:** **Top-up** triggers when a **single** claim exceeds the deductible; **Super Top-up** triggers when the **aggregate** of claims in the year exceeds the deductible (so multiple smaller claims add up).\n\n**Q20.** Co-pay of 5% and deductible Rs.10,000 on a Rs.50,000 claim — reimbursement?\n> **A:** 50,000 − 10,000 − 5%×(50,000−10,000) = 50,000 − 10,000 − 2,000 = **Rs.38,000**.\n\n**Q21.** Base Rs.3L + Top-up Rs.10L (Rs.5L deductible); single bill Rs.4L — out of pocket?\n> **A:** Base pays Rs.3L; you pay **Rs.1L**; top-up does not trigger (single bill below the Rs.5L deductible).\n\n**Q22.** Base Rs.3L + Super Top-up Rs.10L (Rs.3L deductible); bills 2L/3L/4L — out of pocket?\n> **A:** **NIL.** Base covers the first Rs.3L; once aggregate crosses the Rs.3L deductible, the super top-up pays the rest.\n\n**Q23.** Leverage caselet: invest Rs.60,000 at 10% using 1.4× leverage, borrowing at 9%. Own funds?\n> **A:** Own funds = 60,000 / 1.4 = **Rs.42,857**. (Total exposure is 1.4× own funds.)\n\n**Q24.** Same caselet — interest paid and net return?\n> **A:** Borrowed = 60,000 − 42,857 = 17,143; interest = 9% = **Rs.1,543**; gross return 10%×60,000 = 6,000; net ≈ 6,000 − 1,543 ≈ **Rs.4,457** (illustrative — match the workbook's option set).\n\n**Q25.** What return-on-equity does leverage aim to boost, and what's the risk?\n> **A:** It boosts **return on own funds (equity)** when the asset return exceeds the borrowing cost; the risk is amplified losses if the asset underperforms the borrowing rate.\n\n**Q26.** A 45-year-old single earner with home + car loans and goals due now — risk profile?\n> **A:** **Moderate** (loans and near-term goals limit aggression, but a good income provides some ability).\n\n**Q27.** Where to park matured FD funds needed shortly for a child's education?\n> **A:** **Short-term fixed deposit** — ensures liquidity and some return; avoid equity/alternatives for a near-term need.\n\n**Q28.** Best fund for a grandchild's college corpus 13 years away (grandparent in late 60s)?\n> **A:** A **large-and-mid-cap (equity) fund** — the long horizon allows equity exposure **irrespective of the investor's age**.\n\n**Q29.** When does a Will take effect, and can the heir borrow against the property beforehand?\n> **A:** A Will takes effect **only on death** and can be changed before then; so an heir **cannot** use it to prove ownership/borrow while the testator is alive.\n\n**Q30.** How can a parent give a child immediate access to money meant for them in a Will?\n> **A:** The parent (as primary holder) **redeems** the investment and **gifts** the money — a gift to a relative is **tax-free** and gives immediate access (the Will only operates on death).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 20: Case Studies — 100-Question Bank (40 MCQs + 12 Full Cases)\n\n> **How case-based questions work in the real exam.** NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2) gives **60 marks (40% of the paper) as case studies**. The standard pattern is **6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks**. Each case is a realistic caselet (a household's data) followed by 5 MCQs that test whether you can pull data out of the story and apply a formula.\n>\n> **The 5-step method — use it on every sub-question:**\n> 1. **Read the data** — write down every number (current cost, inflation, return, years, existing cover, timing \"start vs end of year\").\n> 2. **Identify what's asked** — future value? present value/corpus? insurance need? a return (RATE)? an allocation %? a tax amount?\n> 3. **Apply the formula** — pick the Excel function (FV / PV / PMT / RATE) or the tax rule.\n> 4. **Compute** — do the arithmetic step by step; mind the Excel sign convention (outflows negative).\n> 5. **Pick the option** — match your number to the closest option.\n>\n> ⚠️ **Negative marking:** the exam deducts **25% of the marks for a wrong answer**. So a wrong case sub-question costs you 0.5 mark on top of the 2 you miss. If you can eliminate two options, an educated guess is usually worth it; a blind guess on a question you cannot narrow is not.\n>\n> 🔑 **Formula you will reuse constantly — real (inflation-adjusted) return:**\n> Real return = (1 + investment return) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1. Use it whenever an income/expense stream **rises with inflation** every year.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Standalone MCQs (Q1–Q40)\n\n**Q1.** A loan EMI in MS Excel is calculated using which function? *(Easy)*\nA) FV  B) PV  C) PMT  D) RATE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PMT(rate, nper, pv)** returns the level periodic instalment that repays a loan, which is exactly what an EMI is. **A) FV** grows a single sum or stream forward in time. **B) PV** discounts a future stream to today (used for corpus). **D) RATE** solves for the implied interest rate, not the instalment.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which type of asset allocation depends on the market view at a point in time? *(Easy)*\nA) Strategic asset allocation  B) Goal-based allocation  C) Tactical asset allocation  D) Investor risk profile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Tactical** allocation deliberately tilts the portfolio toward/away from asset classes based on short-term **market** views, then reverts. **A) Strategic** allocation is the long-term policy mix set from the investor's risk profile and goals, not the market. **B)** Goal-based allocation is driven by goal horizon, not by market timing. **D)** A risk profile is an investor characteristic, not an allocation method.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The \"real\" (inflation-adjusted) return used to discount an inflating expense stream is given by: *(Medium)*\nA) Return − Inflation  B) Return + Inflation  C) (1+Return)/(1+Inflation) − 1  D) (1+Inflation)/(1+Return) − 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The exact real return is **(1+Return)/(1+Inflation) − 1**. Example: return 9%, inflation 8% → (1.09/1.08) − 1 = **0.93%**. **A)** Return − Inflation (the simple difference) is only a rough approximation and the exam uses the exact form. **B)** Adding them is wrong — inflation reduces, not increases, real return. **D)** Inverts numerator and denominator, giving a wrong figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A future cost of education due in 5 years, with current cost ₹15,00,000 and inflation 15% p.a., is found by: *(Easy)*\nA) PV(15%,5,,-1500000)  B) PMT(15%,5,1500000)  C) RATE(5,,1500000)  D) FV(15%,5,,-1500000)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **future cost** means growing today's amount forward, which is **FV**. =FV(15%,5,,−1500000) = 15,00,000 × 1.15⁵ ≈ **₹30,17,036**. **A) PV** would discount it backward (smaller number, wrong direction). **B) PMT** computes an instalment, irrelevant here. **C) RATE** finds an interest rate, not a future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Under Section 112A, long-term capital gains on listed equity shares/equity mutual funds are taxed at what rate, and with what annual exemption? *(Medium)*\nA) 10% above ₹1 lakh  B) 20% above ₹1 lakh  C) 15% with no exemption  D) 12.5% above ₹1.25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Post the 2024 change, **LTCG u/s 112A is taxed at 12.5%** on gains **exceeding ₹1.25 lakh** in a financial year (no indexation for these listed equity assets). **A)** 10%/₹1 lakh was the old rate before the change. **B)** 20% is the STCG rate u/s 111A, not LTCG. **C)** There is an exemption, and 15% is neither the LTCG nor current STCG rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Short-term capital gains on listed equity shares (held ≤12 months) under Section 111A are taxed at: *(Medium)*\nA) 12.5%  B) 15%  C) 20%  D) Slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **STCG u/s 111A is now taxed at 20%** (raised from the earlier 15%). **A) 12.5%** is the LTCG rate u/s 112A. **B) 15%** was the old STCG rate before the change. **D) Slab rate** applies to STCG on assets *not* covered by 111A (e.g., most debt funds/other assets), not to listed equity STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** For listed equity shares, the holding period that separates short-term from long-term is: *(Easy)*\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) 6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed equity shares and equity-oriented mutual fund units become **long-term after 12 months**. **B) 24 months** is the threshold for immovable property and unlisted shares. **C) 36 months** was the old threshold for debt/other assets. **D) 6 months** is not a capital-gains threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Mr. A sells listed equity shares after 18 months for a gain of ₹3,00,000 (his only such gain). His LTCG tax (ignoring cess) is: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹18,750  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹21,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Held 18 months (>12) → **LTCG u/s 112A**. Taxable gain = total gain − ₹1.25 lakh exemption = 3,00,000 − 1,25,000 = **₹1,75,000**. Tax = 12.5% × 1,75,000 = **₹21,875**. **A) ₹18,750** = 12.5% × 1,50,000 (wrongly using a ₹1.5 lakh exemption). **B) ₹37,500** = 12.5% × 3,00,000 (forgot the exemption). **C) ₹30,000** = 10% × 3,00,000 (old rate, no exemption).\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Mr. B has STCG of ₹2,00,000 on listed shares (held 8 months) under Section 111A. His tax (ignoring cess) is: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹18,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG u/s 111A is taxed at a **flat 20%**: 20% × 2,00,000 = **₹40,000**. **A) ₹25,000** = 12.5% (LTCG rate, wrong). **B) ₹30,000** = 15% (old STCG rate). **D) ₹18,750** mixes the LTCG rate and an exemption that does not apply to listed-equity STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The maximum deduction available under Section 80C in a financial year (old tax regime) is: *(Easy)*\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,25,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section **80C caps deductions at ₹1,50,000** (LIC premium, PPF, ELSS, principal repayment of home loan, etc.). **A) ₹1,00,000** was the old pre-2014 limit. **B) ₹1,25,000** is the LTCG exemption figure, not 80C. **D) ₹2,00,000** is the home-loan interest cap u/s 24(b), a separate deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The corpus needed today to fund an inflation-rising annual expense for n years is best found with: *(Medium)*\nA) FV using nominal return  B) PV using the real (inflation-adjusted) return  C) PMT using inflation  D) RATE using the return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A corpus is a **present value**, and because the expense **rises with inflation** every year you discount at the **real return**: =PV(real return, n, −expense). **A)** FV grows forward — wrong direction for a corpus. **C)** PMT gives an instalment. **D)** RATE solves for a rate, not an amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Today's annual expense is ₹4,80,000; retirement is 8 years away; inflation 10%. The expense in the first year of retirement is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹8,64,000  B) ₹9,60,000  C) ₹11,31,815  D) ₹10,28,923\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — =FV(10%,8,,−480000) = 4,80,000 × 1.10⁸ = 4,80,000 × 2.1436 = **₹10,28,923**. **A) ₹8,64,000** = only one period of growth. **B) ₹9,60,000** = simply doubling, no compounding. **C) ₹11,31,815** = 9 years of growth (1.10⁹), one year too many.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Continuing Q12 (expense ₹10,28,923 at retirement, beginning-of-year), retirement return 11%, inflation 10%, for 15 years — the corpus required is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹1,38,61,919  B) ₹1,44,96,632  C) ₹1,52,39,389  D) ₹1,52,54,894\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1.11/1.10) − 1 = **0.91%**. Corpus = PV(0.91%, 15, −10,28,923, , 1), where **type=1** means the expense is drawn at the **beginning** of each year → **₹1,44,96,632**. **A), C), D)** are distractors arising from using a wrong return, the wrong type flag, or 14/16 years instead of 15.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In Excel financial functions, money you **pay out** is entered as: *(Easy)*\nA) Positive  B) Negative  C) Zero  D) Either, it makes no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Outflows (premiums, contributions, the expense you fund) are entered as **negative**; inflows are positive. That is why functions show =FV(8%,8,,−1200000). **A)** Positive is for money received. **C)** Zero means no cash flow. **D)** It does matter — a wrong sign flips the result.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A retirement corpus is built from annual contributions, each grown at the **investment** rate for the years remaining to retirement, then summed. This is an application of: *(Medium)*\nA) PV of an annuity  B) FV of each cash flow then summing  C) RATE  D) PMT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each contribution is moved **forward** to retirement at the investment rate (FV) and all the future values are **added**. **A) PV** discounts backward — wrong direction for accumulating a corpus. **C) RATE** finds a rate. **D) PMT** finds a level instalment, but here the contributions grow each year, so they are not level.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** An existing retirement corpus of ₹12,00,000 invested at 8% for 8 years grows to (use FV): *(Medium)*\nA) ₹19,33,000  B) ₹24,00,000  C) ₹22,21,116  D) ₹20,57,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — =FV(8%,8,,−1200000) = 12,00,000 × 1.08⁸ = 12,00,000 × 1.8509 = **₹22,21,116**. **A)** uses too few years/lower factor. **B)** is just doubling. **D)** uses roughly 7 years of growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mr. C, 45, single earning member, has a home loan and a car loan and goals due soon. Based only on his circumstances, his **capacity** to take risk is best described as: *(Medium)*\nA) Conservative  B) Moderate  C) Liquidity seeker  D) Aggressive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A good income but a sole earner with two loans and near-term goals balances out to a **moderate** risk capacity — neither very safe nor very aggressive. **A) Conservative** understates his good income and growth need. **C) Liquidity seeker** is not a standard risk-profile label. **D) Aggressive** ignores his liabilities and dependent family.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Money that must be parked for a **short period** until a daughter's education fee is paid should go into: *(Easy)*\nA) Large-cap equity  B) A current account  C) Alternative investments  D) Short-term fixed deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **short-term FD** preserves capital and gives some return for a near-term, must-pay goal. **A) Equity** can fall just when the money is needed. **B) Current account** earns nothing. **C) Alternatives** are illiquid/risky and unsuitable for a short horizon.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A Will made by a living person comes into legal effect: *(Easy)*\nA) On registration  B) On the testator's death  C) When the first beneficiary claims  D) On the executor's appointment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A Will is a declaration that takes effect **only on the death** of the testator and can be revised any time before then. **A)** Registration is optional and does not make it operative. **C)** A beneficiary cannot claim while the testator lives. **D)** The executor acts only after death.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A mother wants to give her daughter money immediately from her own mutual funds (which a Will bequeaths to the daughter). The cleanest, tax-free way is: *(Medium)*\nA) Hand over a copy of the registered Will to redeem the units  B) Make the daughter a joint holder first  C) The mother redeems the units herself and gifts the cash to the daughter  D) Nothing can be done while the mother is alive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Will is inoperative while the mother lives, so **she (the holder) redeems and gifts** the cash; a gift from mother to daughter is a **relative**, hence **tax-free**. **A)** A Will gives no current redemption right. **B)** Adding a joint holder is slow and unnecessary. **D)** Is too absolute — a gift solves it immediately.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A target-maturity debt fund is recommended for a goal because it gives the investor: *(Medium)*\nA) Guaranteed equity-like returns  B) Visibility on likely returns if held to maturity  C) Daily capital protection  D) Tax-free income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A target-maturity fund holds bonds maturing around a set date, so an investor holding to maturity has reasonable **visibility on the likely yield**. **A)** Debt funds do not promise equity-like returns. **C)** NAV still fluctuates day to day. **D)** Returns are taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** For a goal that is **15+ years** away, even an investor in her late 60s can suitably use: *(Medium)*\nA) A liquid fund only  B) A short-term debt fund only  C) A large-and-mid-cap equity fund  D) A current account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Suitability is driven by **goal horizon**, not just age. A 15-year horizon justifies **equity** (large-and-mid-cap) for growth. **A), B)** Pure debt under-delivers over 15 years and risks falling short. **D)** A current account erodes value to inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A health **top-up** policy (as opposed to a super top-up) is triggered when: *(Medium)*\nA) The aggregate of all claims in the year crosses the deductible  B) The insured turns 60  C) A single claim crosses the deductible  D) The base policy is exhausted, regardless of amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A plain **top-up** considers each claim **individually**; it pays only when **one single claim** exceeds the deductible. **A)** describes a **super top-up** (aggregate basis). **B)** Age is irrelevant to the trigger. **D)** Exhausting the base does not by itself trigger a top-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A health **super top-up** policy is triggered when: *(Medium)*\nA) A single claim crosses the deductible  B) The base policy is renewed  C) The first hospitalisation occurs  D) The aggregate of all claims in the year crosses the deductible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **super top-up** adds up **all claims in the policy year**; once that **aggregate** crosses the deductible, it pays — which is why it is more useful than a plain top-up. **A)** is the plain top-up rule. **B), C)** are not triggers.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A claim of ₹50,000 on a policy with a ₹10,000 deductible and 5% co-pay is reimbursed as: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹38,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹42,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Reimbursement = Claim − Deductible − Co-pay, where co-pay is **5% of (claim − deductible)**. = 50,000 − 10,000 − 5%×(40,000) = 50,000 − 10,000 − 2,000 = **₹38,000**. **B)** forgets the co-pay. **C)** applies 5% to the full 50,000 then subtracts wrongly. **D)** subtracts only the co-pay, ignoring the deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Base health policy ₹3 lakh + top-up ₹10 lakh with ₹5 lakh deductible. A **single** bill of ₹4 lakh arrives. Out-of-pocket cost is: *(Hard)*\nA) NIL  B) ₹3 lakh  C) ₹4 lakh  D) ₹1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Base pays ₹3 lakh; the remaining ₹1 lakh is borne by the insured because the **single** claim (₹4 lakh) is **below the ₹5 lakh deductible**, so the top-up does not trigger → **₹1 lakh out of pocket**. **A)** wrongly assumes the top-up pays. **B)** ignores the base policy. **C)** ignores the base entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Base ₹3 lakh + **super** top-up ₹10 lakh with ₹3 lakh deductible. Three bills in the year: ₹2L, ₹3L, ₹4L. Out-of-pocket cost is: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹6 lakh  B) ₹3 lakh  C) ₹1 lakh  D) NIL\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Base ₹3L covers the first claims up to ₹3L; once **aggregate** claims cross the ₹3L deductible, the **super top-up** pays the rest of all bills → **NIL** out of pocket. **A) ₹6 lakh** is the answer for a *plain* top-up (no single bill ≥ deductible). **B), C)** mis-track the aggregate logic.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A person resident in India under FEMA who already holds a life insurance policy bought abroad: *(Medium)*\nA) Must surrender it immediately  B) Cannot pay any further premiums  C) May continue it and pay premiums from foreign income or by remitting from India  D) Must convert it to an Indian policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indian law permits a returning resident to **keep an existing foreign life policy**, paying premiums from foreign income (e.g., rent) or by remitting funds. **A), B)** wrongly force surrender or block premiums. **D)** No such conversion requirement exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** After becoming a resident of India, an individual's **rental income from a US property** is: *(Medium)*\nA) Not taxable in India at all  B) Taxable in India with credit for tax paid in the US  C) Taxable in India with no foreign tax credit  D) Taxable only in the US\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A resident is taxed on **global income** in India, but **double taxation is relieved** by a foreign tax credit for the US tax paid. **A)** ignores global-income taxation. **C)** denies the credit that the DTAA provides. **D)** ignores Indian residence.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Income accruing in a US tax-deferred retirement account, where the account is **notified under Section 89A**, is taxed in India: *(Hard)*\nA) Every year as it accrues  B) Only in the year of withdrawal, with credit for US tax  C) Never  D) At a flat 30% on accrual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 89A** lets a specified person defer Indian tax on such an account to the **year of withdrawal**, matching the US timing, with **credit for US tax**. **A)** describes the mismatch 89A removes. **C)** It is taxed, just deferred. **D)** No such flat rate applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The government scheme offering senior citizens a high fixed rate of interest is: *(Easy)*\nA) Public sector bank FD  B) Equity Linked Savings Scheme  C) Unit Linked Insurance Plan  D) Senior Citizen Savings Scheme (SCSS)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **SCSS** is a **government** small-savings scheme for those 60+ offering an attractive fixed rate. **A)** A PSU-bank FD is a bank product, not a government scheme. **B) ELSS** is an equity tax-saver, not fixed income. **C) ULIP** is an insurance-investment product, not a fixed-income scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Ms. T invests ₹60,000 in a 10% asset using **1.4× leverage**, borrowing at 9%. Her **own funds** are: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹35,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹42,857  D) ₹17,143\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Leverage 1.4× means total exposure = 1.4 × own funds. So own funds = total ÷ 1.4 = 60,000 ÷ 1.4 = **₹42,857**. **A), B)** do not satisfy 1.4× of own funds = 60,000. **D) ₹17,143** is the **borrowed** part (60,000 − 42,857), not own funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Continuing Q32, the **interest** Ms. T pays (borrowing ₹17,143 at 9%) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹2,250  B) ₹5,400  C) ₹1,543  D) ₹3,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Borrowed amount = 60,000 − 42,857 = ₹17,143; interest = 9% × 17,143 = **₹1,543**. **A), B), D)** use wrong borrowed amounts (e.g., applying 9% to 60,000 gives 5,400, which double-counts own funds as borrowed).\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Continuing Q32–Q33, Ms. T's **net return in rupees** (asset earns 10% on ₹60,000, less interest of ₹1,543) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹6,000  B) ₹4,457  C) ₹2,850  D) ₹3,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross earning = 10% × 60,000 = ₹6,000; net = 6,000 − 1,543 = **₹4,457**. **A) ₹6,000** ignores interest. **C), D)** use the wrong interest figure (e.g., borrowing 25,000–42,857 instead of 17,143).\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Continuing Q32–Q34, Ms. T's **return on equity** (net ₹4,457 on own funds ₹42,857) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) 1%  B) 10.4%  C) 10.9%  D) 11.4%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Return on equity = net return ÷ own funds = 4,457 ÷ 42,857 = **10.4%**. Leverage lifted the 10% asset return slightly because the asset yield (10%) exceeds the borrowing cost (9%). **A) 1%** is the spread per rupee, not ROE. **C), D)** overstate the lift.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** The recommended insurance-need formula (income-replacement / HLV method) is: *(Medium)*\nA) PV of future income (real return) + liabilities + lump-sum goals − existing cover  B) Annual income × age  C) 10 × annual premium  D) Sum of all past incomes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **need** is the present value of the income to be replaced (discounted at the real return), **plus** outstanding liabilities and lump-sum goals (children's corpus), **minus** existing cover/earmarked assets. **B), C), D)** are arbitrary rules of thumb with no actuarial basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Adil's spouse needs ₹3,00,000/year (today's value) for 30 years; inflation 6%, return 8%. The corpus to fund this income (PV at the real return) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹68,24,712  B) ₹90,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹37,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Real return = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = **1.89%**. Corpus = PV(1.89%, 30, −3,00,000) = **₹68,24,712**. **B)** overstates by ignoring discounting. **C), D)** are far too low (they undercount the 30-year stream).\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Continuing Q37, Adil also wants ₹10 lakh for his child and has a ₹30 lakh home loan; existing cover is ₹50 lakh. **Additional** cover needed is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹44 lakh  B) ₹58 lakh  C) ₹69 lakh  D) ₹81 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total need = income corpus 68,24,712 + child corpus 10,00,000 + loan 30,00,000 = **₹1,08,24,712**; less existing cover ₹50,00,000 = **₹58,24,712 ≈ ₹58 lakh**. **A)** forgets a component. **C), D)** double-count or omit the existing cover deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Bose pays ₹6,000/year for 20 years; a pure term plan of the same cover costs ₹150/year. Maturity value ₹2,00,000. The return on the **investment portion** (₹5,850/year → ₹2,00,000) using RATE is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) 1.7%  B) 3.6%  C) 5.3%  D) 6.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Strip out the insurance cost: investment premium = 6,000 − 150 = ₹5,850/year. =RATE(20, −5850, , 200000) = **5.3%**. **A), B)** understate (they use the full ₹6,000 or wrong nper). **D)** overstates the implied yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Jaspreet spends ₹50,000/month now, retires in 20 years, wants 25 years of inflation-adjusted income; inflation 8%, retirement return 9%. The corpus required at retirement is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹1.5 crore  B) ₹6.2 crore  C) ₹4.8 crore  D) ₹3.5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual expense now = ₹6,00,000. At retirement: FV(8%,20,,−600000) = **₹27,96,574**. Real return = (1.09/1.08) − 1 = **0.93%**. Corpus = PV(0.93%, 25, −27,96,574) = **₹6.22 crore**. **A), C), D)** result from skipping the FV step or using nominal instead of real return.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Full Exam-Style Case Studies\n\n> Each case has **5 sub-questions of 2 marks each (10 marks per case)**. Read the caselet, list the data, then work each sub-question with the 5-step method.\n\n### Case Study 1 — Mr. Rao: Pure Income-Tax Computation (old regime)\n\nMr. Rao (42), a salaried resident, has the following for the financial year (old tax regime):\n\n| Item | Amount |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary | ₹14,00,000 |\n| Standard deduction (salary) | ₹50,000 |\n| PPF + ELSS + EPF (80C eligible) | ₹2,10,000 invested |\n| Health insurance premium for self/family (80D) | ₹22,000 |\n| NPS own contribution (80CCD(1B)) | ₹50,000 |\n| Interest on self-occupied home loan (Sec 24b) | ₹2,40,000 paid |\n\nIgnore cess and surcharge. Use FY slabs (old regime): up to ₹2.5L nil; ₹2.5L–₹5L at 5%; ₹5L–₹10L at 20%; above ₹10L at 30%.\n\n**Q1.1.** What is Mr. Rao's income from salary after the standard deduction? *(Easy)*\nA) ₹14,00,000  B) ₹13,50,000  C) ₹13,00,000  D) ₹12,90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Salary income = Gross − standard deduction = 14,00,000 − 50,000 = **₹13,50,000**. **A)** forgets the standard deduction. **C), D)** subtract the wrong amount (₹1,00,000 / ₹1,10,000), but the standard deduction is fixed at ₹50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q1.2.** What is the **maximum** deduction allowable under Section 80C here? *(Easy)*\nA) ₹2,10,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Although he invested ₹2,10,000, **80C is capped at ₹1,50,000**, so only ₹1,50,000 is deductible. **A)** wrongly allows the full invested amount. **B), D)** are not the statutory cap (the cap is ₹1.5 lakh).\n</details>\n\n**Q1.3.** Including 80C (₹1,50,000), 80CCD(1B) NPS (₹50,000) and 80D (₹22,000), the total of these three Chapter VI-A deductions is: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹2,32,000  C) ₹2,22,000  D) ₹2,82,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 80C 1,50,000 + 80CCD(1B) 50,000 + 80D 22,000 = **₹2,22,000**. The NPS ₹50,000 under 80CCD(1B) is **over and above** the 80C cap, so it is added in full; 80D health premium ₹22,000 is within its ₹25,000 limit. **A)** omits the 80D amount. **B)** adds ₹10,000 too much. **D)** wrongly counts the full ₹2,10,000 of 80C.\n</details>\n\n**Q1.4.** After also claiming the home-loan interest (Sec 24b, self-occupied, capped at ₹2,00,000), Mr. Rao's **net taxable income** is: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹9,28,000  B) ₹9,50,000  C) ₹9,28,000 + cess  D) ₹10,28,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Salary 13,50,000 − Chapter VI-A 2,22,000 = 11,28,000; less Sec 24b interest (capped at **₹2,00,000**, not the ₹2,40,000 paid, for a self-occupied house) = **₹9,28,000**. **B)** ignores part of the deductions. **C)** is the same figure but cess was told to be ignored. **D)** uses only ₹1,00,000 of interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q1.5.** On taxable income of ₹9,28,000 (old-regime slabs), the income tax before cess is: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹98,100  B) ₹1,00,600  C) ₹1,11,100  D) ₹92,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Slab tax: first ₹2.5L nil; next ₹2.5L (₹2.5L–₹5L) at 5% = ₹12,500; next ₹4.28L (₹5L–₹9.28L) at 20% = ₹85,600. Total = 12,500 + 85,600 = **₹98,100**. **B), C)** wrongly tax part of the income at 30% (income is below ₹10L, so no 30% slab applies). **D)** under-taxes the 20% band.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 2 — Ms. Mehta: Capital Gains Across Asset Classes\n\nIn FY 2024-25 Ms. Mehta (resident) made the following sales (these are her only capital transactions):\n\n| Asset | Holding period | Capital gain |\n|---|---|---|\n| Listed equity shares (STT paid) | 20 months | ₹2,00,000 |\n| Listed equity shares (STT paid) | 7 months | ₹1,00,000 |\n| Equity mutual fund units | 15 months | ₹40,000 |\n\nAssume the LTCG ₹1.25 lakh exemption (Sec 112A) applies once across all eligible long-term equity gains. Ignore cess.\n\n**Q2.1.** Which of Ms. Mehta's gains are **long-term**? *(Easy)*\nA) Only the 20-month shares  B) The 20-month shares and the 15-month MF units  C) All three  D) Only the MF units\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed equity and equity MF become long-term after **12 months**, so the 20-month shares (₹2,00,000) and 15-month MF units (₹40,000) are LTCG. **A)** omits the MF units. **C)** wrongly includes the 7-month shares (these are short-term). **D)** ignores the 20-month shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.2.** Her **total long-term** capital gain (before the exemption) is: *(Easy)*\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,40,000  D) ₹3,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG = 2,00,000 (shares) + 40,000 (MF) = **₹2,40,000**. **A)** omits the MF gain. **B)** wrongly adds the short-term ₹1,00,000. **D)** adds everything regardless of holding period.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.3.** Her **LTCG tax** under Section 112A (12.5% above the ₹1.25 lakh exemption) is: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹14,375  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹24,000  D) ₹11,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Taxable LTCG = 2,40,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹1,15,000; tax = 12.5% × 1,15,000 = **₹14,375**. **B)** ignores the exemption (12.5% × 2,40,000). **C)** applies 10%/no exemption. **D)** uses only the share gain, forgetting the MF gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.4.** Her **STCG tax** on the 7-month listed shares (Sec 111A, 20%) is: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — STCG u/s 111A = 20% × 1,00,000 = **₹20,000** (no ₹1.25 lakh exemption — that applies only to LTCG). **A)** uses the old 15% rate. **C)** uses 12.5% (LTCG rate). **D)** uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.5.** Her **total capital-gains tax** for the year is: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹34,375  B) ₹14,375  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹44,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG tax ₹14,375 + STCG tax ₹20,000 = **₹34,375**. **B), C)** count only one of the two. **D)** adds an extra ₹10,000 that does not arise.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 3 — Mr. Khanna: Property Capital Gain with Indexation\n\nMr. Khanna sold a residential plot in FY 2024-25. Details:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Sale consideration | ₹95,00,000 |\n| Year of purchase | FY 2010-11, cost ₹20,00,000 |\n| Cost Inflation Index (CII): FY 2010-11 | 167 |\n| CII: FY 2024-25 | 363 |\n| Holding period | About 14 years |\n\nFor property, the taxpayer may compute LTCG **with indexation at 20%** (the route assumed in this case). Ignore cess.\n\n**Q3.1.** Is this gain long-term or short-term? *(Easy)*\nA) Short-term (held < 24 months)  B) Long-term (held > 24 months)  C) Short-term (held < 36 months)  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Immovable property becomes **long-term after 24 months**; held ~14 years → **long-term**. **A), C)** misstate the threshold or the facts. **D)** is wrong — the holding period is clearly given.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.2.** The **indexed cost of acquisition** is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹36,30,000  C) ₹43,47,305  D) ₹29,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indexed cost = Cost × (CII sale year ÷ CII purchase year) = 20,00,000 × (363 ÷ 167) = 20,00,000 × 2.1737 = **₹43,47,305**. **A)** ignores indexation. **B)** applies the CII ratio to the sale price by mistake. **D)** uses an arbitrary factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.3.** The **long-term capital gain** (after indexation) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹75,00,000  B) ₹51,52,695  C) ₹58,70,000  D) ₹43,47,305\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG = Sale − Indexed cost = 95,00,000 − 43,47,305 = **₹51,52,695**. **A)** subtracts the raw (un-indexed) cost. **C)** uses a wrong indexed cost. **D)** mistakes the indexed cost itself for the gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.4.** The **tax at 20%** (indexation route) on this LTCG is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹10,30,539  B) ₹6,44,087  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹8,69,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax = 20% × 51,52,695 = **₹10,30,539**. **B)** applies 12.5% (the no-indexation route, used here only for comparison). **C)** taxes the un-indexed gain. **D)** uses a wrong gain figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.5.** Which Section lets Mr. Khanna **save this LTCG** by investing the gain in another residential house? *(Medium)*\nA) Section 54EC  B) Section 54  C) Section 80C  D) Section 112A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 54** exempts LTCG on a residential house if reinvested in another residential house within the prescribed time. **A) 54EC** is for reinvestment in specified bonds (and applies to land/building gains, capped at ₹50 lakh) — not \"another house\". **C) 80C** is an income deduction, not a capital-gains exemption. **D) 112A** is the charging section for equity LTCG, not an exemption.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 4 — The Sharmas: Retirement Corpus Planning\n\nMr. Sharma (50) plans to retire at **60**. Today the household spends **₹70,000/month**. He wants the **same lifestyle, inflation-adjusted**, for **22 years** in retirement (drawn at the **beginning** of each year). Inflation is **7%**; expected return in retirement is **9%**. He already has a retirement corpus of **₹40,00,000** today, invested in debt at **8%**.\n\n**Q4.1.** The household's **current annual expense** is: *(Easy)*\nA) ₹7,00,000  B) ₹8,40,000  C) ₹9,60,000  D) ₹70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 70,000 × 12 = **₹8,40,000**. **A)** multiplies by 10. **C)** uses ₹80,000/month. **D)** is the monthly figure, not annual.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.2.** The annual expense in the **first year of retirement** (10 years away, inflation 7%) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹14,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹16,52,000  D) ₹8,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV(7%,10,,−840000) = 8,40,000 × 1.07¹⁰ = 8,40,000 × 1.9672 = **₹16,52,000** (approx). **A)** under-compounds. **B)** uses too few years. **D)** forgets to inflate at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.3.** The **real (inflation-adjusted) return** to use for the retirement-years corpus is: *(Medium)*\nA) 2.00%  B) 1.87%  C) 16.00%  D) 0.93%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1.09/1.07) − 1 = 1.0187 − 1 = **1.87%**. **A)** is the simple difference (9% − 7%), only an approximation. **C)** wrongly adds. **D)** would be correct only if return/inflation were 9%/8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.4.** The **corpus required at retirement** (PV of ₹16,52,000 for 22 years, beginning-of-year, at 1.87%) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹2.97 crore  B) ₹3.63 crore  C) ₹1.65 crore  D) ₹5.00 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Corpus = PV(1.87%, 22, −16,52,000, , 1). The annuity factor for 22 years at 1.87% (beginning-of-year) is about 18.0, so corpus ≈ 16,52,000 × 18.0 ≈ **₹2.97 crore**. **B)** over-states by using a wrong rate. **C)** counts far too few years. **D)** ignores discounting almost entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.5.** His existing ₹40,00,000 corpus grown at **8% for 10 years** will be closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹64,00,000  B) ₹86,36,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV(8%,10,,−4000000) = 40,00,000 × 1.08¹⁰ = 40,00,000 × 2.1589 = **₹86,36,000**. **A)** uses ~1.6×. **C)** simply doubles. **D)** over-states. (Comparing ₹86.36 lakh against the ₹2.97 crore need shows a large shortfall to be filled by fresh savings.)\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 5 — Mr. Verma: Life-Insurance Need (Human Life Value)\n\nMr. Verma (38) earns **₹1,00,000/month**. He wants his family to keep receiving his income (rising with inflation) for the **22 years** he would have worked until age 60. Inflation **6%**, long-term investment return **10%**. He has an outstanding **home loan of ₹35,00,000**, wants to leave a **₹15,00,000** corpus for his child's higher education, and currently holds **₹60,00,000** of life cover. He also has ₹10,00,000 in liquid investments earmarked for emergencies (not for these needs).\n\n**Q5.1.** His **current annual income** to be replaced is: *(Easy)*\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹1,00,000  D) ₹14,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 1,00,000 × 12 = **₹12,00,000**. **A)** multiplies by 10. **C)** is the monthly figure. **D)** uses the wrong monthly base.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.2.** The **real (inflation-adjusted) return** for the income-replacement PV is: *(Medium)*\nA) 4.00%  B) 16.00%  C) 0.93%  D) 3.77%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Real return = (1.10/1.06) − 1 = 1.0377 − 1 = **3.77%**. **A)** is the simple difference (10% − 6%). **B)** wrongly adds. **C)** corresponds to 9%/8%, not these figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.3.** The **corpus to replace 22 years of income** (PV of ₹12,00,000 at 3.77%) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹1,82,00,000  B) ₹2,64,00,000  C) ₹1,20,00,000  D) ₹95,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV(3.77%, 22, −12,00,000). The 22-year annuity factor at 3.77% is about 15.16, so corpus ≈ 12,00,000 × 15.16 ≈ **₹1,82,00,000**. **B)** ignores discounting (just 22 × 12 lakh). **C), D)** use far too few years or a wrong rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.4.** Adding the home loan and the child's corpus, his **total insurance need** before existing cover is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹1,82,00,000  B) ₹2,07,00,000  C) ₹2,32,00,000  D) ₹2,17,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Total need = income corpus 1,82,00,000 + home loan 35,00,000 + child corpus 15,00,000 = **₹2,32,00,000**. **A)** omits both liabilities/goals. **B)** omits the child corpus. **D)** omits the home loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.5.** The **additional** life cover Mr. Verma should buy (deduct only the existing ₹60 lakh cover; the ₹10 lakh emergency fund is earmarked elsewhere) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹1,72,00,000  B) ₹1,62,00,000  C) ₹2,32,00,000  D) ₹1,22,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Additional = total need 2,32,00,000 − existing cover 60,00,000 = **₹1,72,00,000**. The ₹10 lakh emergency fund is **not** netted off because it is earmarked for emergencies, not these needs. **B)** wrongly also subtracts the ₹10 lakh. **C)** forgets to subtract existing cover. **D)** subtracts too much.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 6 — Mr. & Mrs. Iyer: Health-Insurance Mechanics\n\nThe Iyers hold a **family floater base policy of ₹5,00,000** and a **super top-up of ₹15,00,000 with a ₹5,00,000 deductible** (aggregate basis). During the policy year the family has hospitalisation bills of **₹3,00,000, ₹4,00,000 and ₹6,00,000** (in that order). One claim additionally carries a **10% co-pay** clause on the base policy only. Treat the bills as fully admissible (no other exclusions) unless a deductible/co-pay applies.\n\n**Q6.1.** After the **first bill (₹3,00,000)**, how much of the base sum insured remains? *(Easy)*\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) NIL  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Base ₹5,00,000 pays the ₹3,00,000 bill, leaving **₹2,00,000**. **A)** ignores that a claim was paid. **B)** wrongly exhausts the whole base. **C)** confuses the amount paid with the amount remaining.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.2.** After the **second bill (₹4,00,000)**, what does the **base** pay, and what is left for the insured to fund before the super top-up triggers? *(Medium)*\nA) Base pays ₹4,00,000; nothing left for insured  B) Base pays ₹2,00,000; insured/aggregate must reach the deductible  C) Base pays NIL  D) Super top-up pays the whole ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only ₹2,00,000 of base remains, so the base pays **₹2,00,000** of the ₹4,00,000; the other ₹2,00,000 counts toward meeting the ₹5,00,000 **aggregate deductible**. **A)** exceeds the base balance. **C)** wrongly says base pays nothing. **D)** the super top-up cannot pay until the aggregate deductible is met.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.3.** Across the first two bills (₹3L + ₹4L = ₹7L), the base paid ₹5L. Has the ₹5,00,000 **aggregate deductible** been met by the time the **third bill** arrives? *(Medium)*\nA) No, only ₹3,00,000 has been spent  B) Yes — ₹7,00,000 of claims (≥ ₹5,00,000) has been incurred  C) No, the deductible is per-claim  D) Only partly, ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **super top-up** works on **aggregate** claims. By the third bill, ₹7,00,000 of claims have been incurred, which **exceeds the ₹5,00,000 deductible**, so the super top-up is now active. **A), D)** undercount the aggregate. **C)** describes a plain top-up, not a super top-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.4.** For the **third bill (₹6,00,000)**, with base exhausted and the deductible met, how much does the **super top-up** pay? *(Hard)*\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹6,00,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) NIL\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Base is fully used (₹5L) and the aggregate deductible (₹5L) is already met by the first two bills, so the super top-up (₹15L limit) pays the **entire ₹6,00,000** third bill. **A)** wrongly leaves ₹5L to the insured. **C)** confuses the deductible amount with the payout. **D)** ignores that the super top-up is now active.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.5.** Ignoring the co-pay, the family's **total out-of-pocket** across all three bills is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) NIL  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total bills ₹13,00,000; base pays ₹5,00,000 and super top-up pays the third bill ₹6,00,000 — but the portion of bills one and two beyond the base (₹7L − ₹5L = ₹2,00,000) fell **inside the deductible window before the super top-up triggered**, so the family bears **₹2,00,000**. **B)** equals the deductible but double-counts. **C)** wrongly assumes full coverage. **D)** over-states the gap.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 7 — Mrs. Nair: Estate Planning and Succession\n\nMrs. Nair (68), a Hindu widow, owns a house and a portfolio of mutual funds and shares. She has two children (a son and a daughter) and three grandchildren. She has made a **registered Will** bequeathing the house to her son and the financial assets to her daughter. She wants the bequests to be smooth and is also considering creating a small fund for a grandchild's education 14 years away.\n\n**Q7.1.** When does Mrs. Nair's Will take legal effect? *(Easy)*\nA) On registration  B) On her death  C) When the son occupies the house  D) When probate is applied for\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A Will operates **only on the testator's death** and can be revised any time before then. **A)** Registration merely adds evidentiary value. **C), D)** Occupation or probate application do not make the Will operative.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.2.** Her son wants to mortgage the house **now**, showing the bank the Will as proof of future ownership. Is this valid? *(Medium)*\nA) Yes, the Will is registered  B) No — the Will is inoperative while Mrs. Nair is alive, so the son is not yet the owner  C) Yes, the son is a Class I heir  D) Yes, with the daughter's consent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since the Will takes effect only on death, the son has **no present ownership**; he cannot mortgage a property he does not yet own. **A)** Registration does not advance ownership. **C)** Being a Class I heir matters only on intestate death, and a Will overrides intestacy anyway. **D)** Consent of a sibling does not create ownership.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.3.** Her daughter has an **urgent cash need** today and wants the money Mrs. Nair intends her to inherit. The cleanest tax-free route is: *(Medium)*\nA) Show the registered Will to the AMC to redeem units  B) Make the daughter a joint holder  C) Mrs. Nair redeems units herself and gifts the cash to her daughter  D) Nothing can be done\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — As the living holder, **Mrs. Nair redeems and gifts** the cash; a gift from mother to daughter (a **relative**) is **exempt from tax** in the daughter's hands. **A)** A Will gives no current redemption right. **B)** Adding a joint holder is slower and not needed for an urgent need. **D)** Is too absolute.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.4.** To make sure the financial assets pass smoothly to the daughter **without disputes among heirs**, Mrs. Nair should additionally ensure: *(Medium)*\nA) The Will alone is enough; nominations are irrelevant  B) Nominations on the folios are aligned with the Will (nominee as trustee for the legatee)  C) She gifts everything away now  D) She converts the Will into a trust deed only at death\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **nominee receives assets as a trustee** for the legal heirs/legatees; aligning nominations with the Will avoids friction at settlement. **A)** Mismatched nominations are a common cause of disputes, so they are not irrelevant. **C)** Gifting everything now defeats her own needs and the plan. **D)** A trust cannot be created after death by the deceased.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.5.** For the **grandchild's education 14 years away**, the most suitable investment is: *(Easy)*\nA) A liquid fund  B) A short-term debt fund  C) A diversified equity fund  D) A savings bank account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **14-year horizon** justifies **equity** for growth, regardless of Mrs. Nair's age, because suitability follows the **goal's horizon**. **A), B), D)** are low-growth options that risk falling short over 14 years.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 8 — Mr. Banerjee: Risk Profiling and Asset Allocation\n\nMr. Banerjee (35), an IT professional, earns well, has **no dependents yet**, a stable job, an emergency fund of 6 months' expenses, no loans, and a **20-year** primary goal (wealth building) plus a **2-year** goal (down-payment for a flat). His questionnaire shows a **high ability and willingness to take risk**. Inflation 6%.\n\n**Q8.1.** How is Mr. Banerjee's overall **risk profile** best classified? *(Easy)*\nA) Conservative  B) Moderate  C) Aggressive  D) Liquidity seeker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — High income, no dependents, no loans, an emergency cushion, a long horizon and high willingness all point to an **aggressive** profile (high ability **and** willingness). **A), B)** understate his capacity. **D)** is not a standard risk-profile category.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.2.** For his **20-year wealth-building** goal, the most suitable allocation is: *(Medium)*\nA) Mostly debt with a little equity  B) Mostly equity (growth assets)  C) Entirely liquid funds  D) Entirely gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A 20-year horizon and aggressive profile favour **mostly equity (growth assets)**, which historically beat inflation over long periods. **A)** is too conservative for the horizon. **C)** Liquid funds barely beat inflation. **D)** Concentrating in one commodity is undiversified.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.3.** For the **2-year down-payment** goal, the suitable choice is: *(Medium)*\nA) Mid-cap equity for growth  B) A short-term debt fund / FD to protect capital  C) A sectoral equity fund  D) An ELSS for tax saving\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **2-year, must-have** goal needs **capital protection**, so short-term debt/FD is right even for an aggressive investor. **A), C)** Equity can fall just when the money is needed. **D)** ELSS has a 3-year lock-in, longer than the goal.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.4.** This illustrates a key principle of asset allocation: *(Medium)*\nA) Age alone fixes allocation  B) Goal horizon, not just risk profile, drives the asset choice for each goal  C) Aggressive investors should put everything in equity  D) Liquidity needs are irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even an aggressive investor parks short-horizon money safely: **each goal's horizon** shapes its allocation. **A)** Age is only one input. **C)** Ignores short-term goals' need for safety. **D)** Liquidity always matters.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.5.** The allocation set as a **long-term policy mix** from his profile and goals (not from market timing) is called: *(Easy)*\nA) Tactical asset allocation  B) Strategic asset allocation  C) Dynamic hedging  D) Rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The long-term policy mix derived from risk profile and goals is **strategic** asset allocation. **A) Tactical** is short-term, market-driven tilts. **C)** Hedging manages specific risks, not the policy mix. **D)** Rebalancing restores the mix; it is not the mix itself.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 9 — The Guptas: NRI Returning to India (Tax + Insurance)\n\nMr. and Mrs. Gupta (both 45, Indian citizens) return to India after 20 years in the USA. They own a **US house given on rent**, hold **US tax-deferred retirement accounts** (taxed only on withdrawal in the US), each has a **USD 1 million** US life policy valid 15 more years, and an **employer US health policy** (worldwide cover, including India) that lapses at year-end unless renewed.\n\n**Q9.1.** Once they become **residents** of India, their **US rental income** is: *(Medium)*\nA) Not taxable in India  B) Taxable only in the US  C) Taxable in India with credit for US tax paid  D) Taxable in India with no foreign tax credit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Residents are taxed on **global income**; the DTAA gives a **foreign tax credit** for US tax. **A)** ignores global-income taxation. **B)** ignores Indian residence. **D)** denies the available credit.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.2.** Income **accruing** in their US tax-deferred retirement accounts, if **notified under Section 89A**, is taxed in India: *(Hard)*\nA) Each year as it accrues  B) Never  C) At a flat 40%  D) Only in the year of withdrawal, with credit for US tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Section 89A** aligns the timing with the US, taxing it **on withdrawal** with credit for US tax, removing the accrual-vs-withdrawal mismatch. **A)** is exactly the mismatch 89A removes. **B)** It is taxed, just deferred. **C)** No such flat rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.3.** Must the Guptas buy **fresh life insurance** in India? *(Medium)*\nA) Yes — they cannot remit premiums for the foreign policies after becoming residents  B) Yes — residents cannot hold a foreign life policy  C) No — they may keep the US policies and pay premiums from US rent or by remitting from India  D) Yes — the policies auto-lapse on relocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indian law lets a returning resident **keep an existing foreign life policy**, paying from foreign income or remittance. **A), B), D)** wrongly force fresh policies or block premiums.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.4.** Regarding their **health insurance**, the best advice is: *(Medium)*\nA) Continue the US policy only; no Indian policy needed since it covers India  B) Continue the US policy and also buy a fresh Indian policy to build a local no-claim history  C) Drop the US policy and rely on a new Indian policy alone  D) Buy nothing; employer cover suffices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even though the US policy covers India, buying a **fresh Indian policy builds a local no-claim history** and continuity for the long term, worth the extra cost. **A)** misses the value of a local record. **C)** prematurely drops worldwide cover. **D)** the employer cover lapses at year-end.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.5.** For their long-term India investments, which tax point matters most for **equity mutual funds** they may now buy in India? *(Medium)*\nA) LTCG after 12 months at 12.5% above ₹1.25 lakh  B) LTCG after 36 months at 20% with indexation  C) Fully tax-free  D) Taxed only in the US\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Indian equity MF: **long-term after 12 months**, taxed at **12.5% on gains above ₹1.25 lakh** (Sec 112A). **B)** is the property/old-debt rule, not equity. **C)** Equity gains are not tax-free. **D)** As residents, India taxes these.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 10 — Mr. Smart: Retired Couple, Inflation-Adjustment Shortfall\n\nMr. Smart (60) retires with a pension of **₹4,80,000/year received yearly in advance**, that adjusts only **50%** with inflation. Mrs. Smart (58) gets the pension for life if he dies first. Retirement dues received: **₹40,00,000 (after tax)**. Living expenses currently equal the pension. Employer provides a lifelong adequate Mediclaim. Inflation **6%**. Life expectancy: Mr. 87, Mrs. 85 → plan for about **27 years**. They also want **₹1,00,000/year** (inflation 6%) for social/leisure spend, plus to make up the pension's inflation shortfall. Expenses arise at the **beginning** of each year; discount at **5%**.\n\n**Q10.1.** Why does the pension leave a growing **shortfall** each year? *(Easy)*\nA) The pension is fixed and never rises  B) The pension rises with only 50% of inflation, so real value erodes  C) The pension is taxed away  D) Inflation does not affect expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Expenses rise with **full 6% inflation** but the pension rises with only **half** of it, so a **widening gap** appears yearly. **A)** the pension does partly rise. **C)** taxation is not the issue here. **D)** inflation clearly affects expenses.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.2.** Including the lifestyle ₹1,00,000 and the inflation shortfall, the present value of the **inflation adjustment required** (≈27 years, beginning-of-year, discounted at 5%) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹80,18,710  B) ₹76,36,867  C) ₹1,91,39,462  D) ₹40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Discounting the growing shortfall plus the lifestyle amount over ~27 years at 5% (payments at the start of each year) gives **≈ ₹80,18,710**. **B)** uses end-of-year timing. **C)** double-counts the full pension instead of just the shortfall. **D)** is merely the available corpus, not the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.3.** Since the required ₹80,18,710 **exceeds** the available ₹40,00,000 corpus, the correct conclusion is: *(Hard)*\nA) They cannot meet the need even at higher returns  B) They can meet it only if the ₹40 lakh earns about 9.22% p.a.  C) They can easily meet it at 5% p.a.  D) The corpus is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 5% the ₹40 lakh is short; the corpus suffices **only if it earns about 9.22% p.a.** (the rate at which ₹40 lakh funds the same stream). **A)** is too pessimistic — a higher return can close the gap. **C)** at 5% it is clearly insufficient. **D)** the corpus is central to the analysis.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.4.** The **government scheme** giving senior citizens the highest fixed rate they should consider is: *(Easy)*\nA) Public sector bank FD for seniors  B) Senior Citizen Savings Scheme (SCSS)  C) Equity mutual fund  D) ULIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCSS** is the **government** scheme (high fixed rate for 60+). **A)** A PSU-bank FD is a bank product, not a government scheme. **C), D)** are not fixed-income government schemes.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.5.** If Mr. Smart dies first, Mrs. Smart's pension continues, but the **plan should still provide** for: *(Medium)*\nA) Nothing further — her risk ends  B) The continuing inflation shortfall and lifestyle spend over her remaining life  C) A fresh life cover on Mrs. Smart  D) Repaying a loan they do not have\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The same **inflation shortfall and lifestyle needs continue** for Mrs. Smart's lifetime, so the corpus plan must cover her remaining years. **A)** understates her need. **C)** Life cover on a retiree with no dependents adds little. **D)** There is no loan.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 11 — Mr. Desai: Integrated Plan (Tax + Goal Funding + Insurance)\n\nMr. Desai (45) earns ₹18,00,000/year. Goals: (1) **Daughter's MBA** in **4 years**, current cost **₹25,00,000**, education inflation **10%**; (2) **Retirement** at 60. He sells **listed shares** held 26 months for an LTCG of **₹4,00,000** (his only equity LTCG this year). He has a ₹50 lakh term cover and a ₹40 lakh home loan. Investment return on goal corpus **9%**. Ignore cess.\n\n**Q11.1.** The **future cost of the MBA** (₹25,00,000 inflated at 10% for 4 years) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹33,00,000  B) ₹36,60,000  C) ₹30,25,000  D) ₹27,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV(10%,4,,−2500000) = 25,00,000 × 1.10⁴ = 25,00,000 × 1.4641 = **₹36,60,250 ≈ ₹36,60,000**. **A)** under-compounds. **C)** uses ~2 years. **D)** uses 1 year of growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.2.** His **LTCG tax** on the ₹4,00,000 equity gain (Sec 112A) is: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹34,375  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹37,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held 26 months (>12) → LTCG. Taxable = 4,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹2,75,000; tax = 12.5% × 2,75,000 = **₹34,375**. **A)** uses 10%. **C)** ignores the exemption (12.5% × 4,00,000). **D)** uses 12.5% on ₹3,00,000 (wrong exemption).\n</details>\n\n**Q11.3.** To fund the ₹36,60,250 MBA cost in 4 years, the **lump sum to invest today at 9%** (PV) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹25,93,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹33,57,000  D) ₹22,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = 36,60,250 ÷ 1.09⁴ = 36,60,250 ÷ 1.4116 = **₹25,93,000** (approx). **B), C)** under-discount. **D)** over-discounts using a wrong rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.4.** If instead he saves an **equal annual amount** for 4 years at 9% (end of year) to reach ₹36,60,250, the annual saving (PMT) is closest to: *(Hard)*\nA) ₹9,15,000  B) ₹8,02,000  C) ₹7,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The future-value annuity factor for 4 years at 9% is [(1.09⁴ − 1)/0.09] = 4.5731. PMT = 36,60,250 ÷ 4.5731 = **₹8,00,400 ≈ ₹8,02,000**. **A)** simply divides by 4 (ignores growth). **C)** under-saves. **D)** over-saves using a wrong factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.5.** On his ₹50 lakh cover against a ₹40 lakh home loan plus a family income need, the planner should note: *(Medium)*\nA) Cover is more than enough  B) Cover barely clears the loan, leaving little for family income — likely under-insured  C) He needs no cover since he has assets  D) Term cover cannot repay a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A ₹50 lakh cover against a ₹40 lakh loan leaves only ₹10 lakh for the family's income replacement and the daughter's goal — clearly **under-insured**. **A)** ignores the income/goal needs. **C)** dependents still need income protection. **D)** term proceeds can certainly clear a loan.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n### Case Study 12 — The Rahmans: Comprehensive Household Plan\n\nMr. Rahman (40) and Mrs. Rahman (38) have one child (8). Household expenses **₹80,000/month**. Mr. Rahman is the sole earner (₹20,00,000/year). They hold: equity MF **₹30,00,000**, debt/FD **₹20,00,000**, EPF **₹15,00,000**, a home loan of **₹50,00,000** (EMI ₹45,000/month), term cover **₹1,00,00,000**, and a base health policy of **₹5,00,000**. They want: child's college in **10 years** (current cost ₹40,00,000, edu-inflation 8%) and retirement at 60. General inflation **6%**, long-term return **10%**.\n\n**Q12.1.** The household's **emergency fund** target (commonly 6 months of expenses + EMIs) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹4,80,000  B) ₹7,50,000  C) ₹2,70,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Monthly outgo = expenses ₹80,000 + EMI ₹45,000 = ₹1,25,000; six months = 1,25,000 × 6 = **₹7,50,000**. **A)** uses expenses only, 6 months. **C)** ignores expenses/EMI scope. **D)** is an arbitrary round figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.2.** The **future cost of the child's college** (₹40,00,000 at 8% for 10 years) is closest to: *(Medium)*\nA) ₹64,00,000  B) ₹72,00,000  C) ₹86,36,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV(8%,10,,−4000000) = 40,00,000 × 1.08¹⁰ = 40,00,000 × 2.1589 = **₹86,36,000**. **A)** uses ~1.6×. **B)** under-compounds. **D)** simply doubles.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.3.** Mr. Rahman's life-insurance adequacy: with a ₹50 lakh loan and an income-replacement need, is **₹1 crore** cover clearly sufficient? *(Hard)*\nA) Yes, ₹1 crore always suffices  B) No — after clearing the ₹50 lakh loan, only ₹50 lakh remains for income replacement and the child's goal, likely a shortfall  C) Yes, because they have MF and FD assets  D) Cover is irrelevant since EPF exists\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cover ₹1 crore less the ₹50 lakh loan leaves ₹50 lakh, far below the present value of replacing a ₹20 lakh income for ~20 years plus the ₹86 lakh future college cost — a **likely shortfall**. **A)** is an unjustified rule of thumb. **C)** liquid assets are partly earmarked elsewhere. **D)** EPF alone cannot replace lifelong income.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.4.** Their **₹5,00,000 base health policy** for a family of three is best strengthened by: *(Medium)*\nA) Doing nothing, it is adequate  B) Adding a super top-up with a deductible equal to the base, for cost-effective higher cover  C) Cancelling it and self-funding  D) Buying three separate ₹1 lakh policies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **super top-up** (aggregate deductible ≈ base sum insured) cheaply lifts total cover to handle large or multiple bills in a year. **A)** ₹5 lakh is thin for a family today. **C)** Self-funding large bills is risky. **D)** Tiny separate policies leave big gaps.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.5.** Given a **10-year** horizon for the college goal, the suitable asset mix to fund it is: *(Medium)*\nA) Entirely FD/debt  B) A growth-tilted mix (substantial equity) shifting to debt as the goal nears  C) Entirely small-cap equity throughout  D) A savings account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A 10-year goal supports an **equity-tilted** start for growth, then **de-risking into debt** as the date approaches to lock in the corpus. **A)** Pure debt likely falls short over 10 years. **C)** All small-cap is too volatile, especially near the goal. **D)** A savings account erodes to inflation.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n> ✅ **Bank complete:** 40 standalone MCQs + 12 full case studies × 5 sub-questions = **100 questions**. Practise under time pressure, always writing out the formula and the step-by-step calculation before choosing — and remember the 25% negative marking when deciding whether to guess.\n"}], "papers": [{"num": "01", "md": "# Mock Paper 1 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy — almost every question needs a calculation. Keep a spreadsheet or calculator (and the Cost Inflation Index table) handy, and read each scenario carefully before you compute. All transfers are assumed to be on or after 23 July 2024 unless stated otherwise.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Ravi sells listed equity shares (STT paid on buy and sell) held for 3 years. His total long-term capital gain for the year is ₹4,00,000. How much LTCG tax does he pay under Section 112A (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹15,625  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹34,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 112A taxes LTCG on listed equity (STT paid) at **12.5%**, but the **first ₹1,25,000 of gain each year is exempt**. Formula: Tax = (Total LTCG − ₹1,25,000) × 12.5%. Plug in: (₹4,00,000 − ₹1,25,000) = ₹2,75,000 taxable; ₹2,75,000 × 12.5% = **₹34,375**. A) ₹50,000 wrongly taxes the full gain at 12.5% (forgets the exemption). C) ₹40,000 uses 10% on the full gain (the old pre-2024 rate). B) ₹15,625 taxes only ₹1,25,000 by mistake. The ₹1.25 lakh shelter is a per-year freebie, like a tax-free slice of the cake before the knife (12.5%) cuts the rest.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Priya has a short-term capital gain of ₹3,00,000 on listed equity shares (STT paid), covered by Section 111A. What is her STCG tax (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹45,000  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG covered by **Section 111A** (listed equity/equity MF/business trust units, STT paid, held ≤12 months) is taxed at a flat **20%** after the Finance Act 2024. Tax = ₹3,00,000 × 20% = **₹60,000**. A) ₹45,000 uses 15% (the old rate before 23 July 2024). B) ₹37,500 uses 12.5% (that's the LTCG rate, not STCG). D) ₹30,000 uses 10%. There is no basic exemption for STCG under 111A — the whole gain is taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A residential house was bought in FY 2009-10 for ₹30,00,000 and sold in FY 2024-25 (before 23 July 2024) for ₹1,20,00,000. CII: 2009-10 = 148, 2024-25 = 363. What is the indexed cost of acquisition?\nA) ₹89,99,999  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹66,00,000  D) ₹73,58,108\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Indexed cost = Cost × (CII of year of sale ÷ CII of year of purchase). This \"inflates\" the old cost so you are not taxed on inflation. Plug in: ₹30,00,000 × (363 ÷ 148) = ₹30,00,000 × 2.4527 = **₹73,58,108** (approx). B) ₹30,00,000 ignores indexation. C) ₹66,00,000 just doubles arbitrarily. A) uses wrong index ratio. CII raises the cost from ₹30L to about ₹73.58L, shrinking the taxable gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Using Q3's figures, the property is sold **before** 23 July 2024 (old provisions). What is the LTCG tax @20% on the indexed gain (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹9,28,378  B) ₹18,00,000  C) ₹5,80,236  D) ₹7,42,702\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Indexed gain = Sale − Indexed cost = ₹1,20,00,000 − ₹73,58,108 = ₹46,41,892. Old provisions tax it @20%: ₹46,41,892 × 20% = **₹9,28,378**. B) ₹18,00,000 taxes the non-indexed gain (₹90,00,000) at 20%. C) ₹5,80,236 mistakenly uses 12.5% on the indexed gain. D) ₹7,42,702 applies 12.5% on ₹59.4L. For property bought before 23 July 2024 and sold before that date, the old indexation-plus-20% rule applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An investor buys 200 listed equity shares at ₹500 each (1 June 2023) and sells all on 1 March 2024 at ₹620 each. Brokerage ₹400 total. STT is paid. Holding period and tax head?\nA) Short-term, taxed under 111A at 20%  B) Short-term, taxed at slab rate  C) Long-term, taxed under 112A  D) Long-term, taxed under 112 at 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed equity shares are **long-term only if held more than 12 months**. Here the holding is 1 June 2023 to 1 March 2024 = about 9 months, so it is **short-term**. STT is paid, so it falls under **Section 111A and is taxed at 20%**. C) and D) wrongly assume long-term. B) is the rate for non-111A STCG (not this case, since STT is paid on a listed share). The 12-month line is the key test for listed equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Continuing Q5: compute the short-term capital gain (net of brokerage).\nA) ₹24,000  B) ₹120 per share  C) ₹24,400  D) ₹23,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG = Sale value − Cost − Transfer expenses. Sale = 200 × ₹620 = ₹1,24,000; Cost = 200 × ₹500 = ₹1,00,000; brokerage (transfer expense) = ₹400. STCG = ₹1,24,000 − ₹1,00,000 − ₹400 = **₹23,600**. A) ₹24,000 forgets to deduct brokerage. C) ₹24,400 wrongly adds brokerage. Note: STT is **not** deductible, but brokerage is. B) is just the per-share gain, not the total.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** An equity share was bought on 01-01-2017 at ₹100; its FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹200; it is sold on 01-01-2025 at ₹250 (STT paid). What is the LTCG per share under grandfathering?\nA) ₹150  B) ₹0  C) ₹50  D) ₹250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Grandfathering: cost = higher of (actual cost) or (lower of FMV on 31-01-2018 and sale price). Actual cost = ₹100. Lower of (₹200, ₹250) = ₹200. Higher of (₹100, ₹200) = ₹200. LTCG = ₹250 − ₹200 = **₹50**. A) ₹150 uses the original ₹100 cost (ignores grandfathering). B) ₹0 wrongly caps at FMV. D) ignores cost entirely. Grandfathering protects gains that accrued up to 31-01-2018 from tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Same share as Q7 but sold at ₹150 (FMV on 31-01-2018 was ₹200, cost ₹100). LTCG per share?\nA) ₹50  B) ₹0  C) −₹50 (loss)  D) ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost = higher of (actual ₹100) or (lower of FMV ₹200 and sale ₹150 = ₹150) = ₹150. LTCG = ₹150 − ₹150 = **₹0**. The rule prevents grandfathering from turning a real gain into an artificial loss. A) ₹50 uses ₹100 cost. C) wrongly creates a loss using ₹200. D) ignores cost. When sale price sits between actual cost and FMV, the gain is nil.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Mr A sold a long-term capital asset (other than a house) for net consideration ₹50,00,000 with LTCG ₹20,00,000, and invested ₹40,00,000 in one residential house. What is the Section 54F exemption?\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹8,00,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 54F exempts proportionately: Exemption = LTCG × (Amount invested ÷ Net consideration). Plug in: ₹20,00,000 × (₹40,00,000 ÷ ₹50,00,000) = ₹20,00,000 × 0.8 = **₹16,00,000**. A) ₹20,00,000 wrongly assumes full exemption (that needs the entire net consideration to be invested). B) and C) use wrong fractions. 54F looks at net **consideration** (not just the gain) when working out the proportion.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Under Section 54EC, the maximum exemption available by investing LTCG from land/building in NHAI or REC bonds within 6 months is:\nA) ₹1,00,00,000  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 54EC caps the exemption at the lower of (the amount invested in specified NHAI/REC bonds) or **₹50,00,000**. So even if you invest more, only ₹50 lakh of gain is sheltered. A) ₹1 crore is wrong. C) ₹20 lakh is the gratuity ceiling, not 54EC. D) \"no limit\" is wrong — there is a hard ₹50 lakh cap. Investment must be made within 6 months of transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** An unlisted bond bought in 2020 is redeemed on maturity in October 2024 at a gain of ₹2,00,000. How is this gain taxed?\nA) Short-term capital gain at slab rate  B) LTCG at 20% with indexation  C) LTCG at 12.5%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — After the Finance Act 2024, an unlisted bond/debenture transferred or maturing after 23 July 2024 is **always short-term** under Section 50AA, taxed at slab rates — regardless of how long it was held. B) and C) treat it as long-term (wrong). D) \"exempt\" is wrong. So even a 4-year holding gives short-term treatment for unlisted bonds.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Units of a Specified Mutual Fund (debt fund investing 65%+ in debt) acquired in 2023 and sold in 2025 at a gain are taxed as:\nA) LTCG at 12.5%  B) Always short-term capital gain at slab rate  C) LTCG at 20% with indexation  D) Exempt up to ₹1.25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gains from Specified Mutual Funds (schemes with 65%+ in debt/money-market) acquired on/after 1 April 2023 are **always short-term**, taxed at slab rates, irrespective of holding period (Section 50AA). A) and C) treat as long-term (wrong). D) the ₹1.25 lakh exemption is only for equity under 112A. Debt-heavy funds lost their long-term advantage.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** What is the holding period threshold for an **unlisted** equity share to qualify as a long-term capital asset (transfer after 23 July 2024)?\nA) 12 months  B) 60 months  C) 36 months  D) 24 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Per Table 8.1, **unlisted equity shares** are long-term only if held **more than 24 months**. A) 12 months applies to *listed* equity. C) 36 months was the old threshold (pre-amendment). B) is wrong. Listed = 12 months; unlisted = 24 months — a common trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Immovable property (land/building) is long-term only if held for more than:\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) 48 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Land and building are long-term if held **more than 24 months** (transfer on/after 23 July 2024). A) 12 months is for listed securities. C) 36 months was the pre-2024 rule. D) is wrong. Property and unlisted shares share the 24-month line.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Mr B has LTCG of ₹90,000 from listed equity (STT paid) for the year. How much tax under 112A?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹9,000  C) ₹11,250  D) ₹18,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG up to ₹1,25,000 from listed equity is **fully exempt**. Since ₹90,000 < ₹1,25,000, tax = **₹0**. C) ₹11,250 wrongly applies 12.5% to the whole gain. B) ₹9,000 uses 10%. D) doubles it. The first ₹1.25 lakh each year is shielded entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Bonus shares allotted on 01-04-2020 are sold in FY 2024-25. What is their cost of acquisition for capital gains?\nA) Face value  B) Market price on allotment  C) FMV on 31-01-2018  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bonus shares issued on/after 01-04-2001 have a cost of acquisition of **Nil** (you paid nothing for them). So the entire sale value (subject to grandfathering if pre-31-01-2018) becomes gain. A), B) and C) are wrong because no money was paid to obtain bonus shares. The holding period, though, runs from the date of allotment of the bonus shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Short-term capital loss can be set off against:\nA) Only short-term capital gains  B) Any head of income  C) Both short-term and long-term capital gains  D) Only long-term capital gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **short-term capital loss** can be set off against **both** short-term and long-term capital gains. (A long-term capital loss, by contrast, can only be set off against long-term gains.) A) is too narrow. B) is wrong — capital losses cannot offset salary/business income. D) is the rule for long-term losses, not short-term. STCL is the more flexible of the two losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Long-term capital loss can be set off against:\nA) Only long-term capital gains  B) Both short-term and long-term gains  C) Any income  D) Salary income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **long-term capital loss can only be set off against long-term capital gains** (and carried forward for 8 years). B) describes a short-term loss. C) and D) are wrong — capital losses never touch salary or other heads. LTCL is \"fussy\": it pairs only with LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Mr C earns ₹1,00,000 LTCG from listed equity (STT paid) and ₹2,00,000 LTCG from sale of gold (held 3 years). Total LTCG tax (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹40,625  C) ₹34,375  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The ₹1,00,000 equity LTCG is below the ₹1.25 lakh exemption, so tax = ₹0. Gold LTCG of ₹2,00,000 is taxed at 12.5% (no ₹1.25 lakh shelter — that is only for 112A equity): ₹2,00,000 × 12.5% = **₹25,000**. A) ₹37,500 wrongly taxes ₹3,00,000 at 12.5%. C) ₹34,375 applies the exemption to gold too. B) is a miscalculation. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption applies only to listed-equity LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** No deduction under Sections 80C to 80U is allowed against which income?\nA) Salary income  B) Short-term capital gains under Section 111A  C) Rental income  D) Interest income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — STCG covered by **Section 111A** (and LTCG under 112A) cannot be reduced by Chapter VI-A deductions (80C–80U). A), C), D) allow such deductions normally. So you cannot park 80C investments to shrink your 111A gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Securities Transaction Tax (STT) paid on sale of shares is:\nA) Deductible from sale consideration  B) Deductible as cost of acquisition  C) Not deductible while computing capital gains  D) Added to the gain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STT (and CTT) is specifically **not allowed as a deduction** when computing capital gains. Brokerage, stamp duty, registration fees are deductible, but STT is not. A), B), D) are wrong. STT is the cost of being in the 111A/112A concessional regime — you cannot also deduct it.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Mr D (covered by Payment of Gratuity Act) retires after 24 years. Last drawn salary (basic + DA) = ₹52,000/month; gratuity received = ₹8,00,000. Exempt amount?\nA) ₹8,00,000  B) ₹7,20,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹6,24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exemption = least of: (i) ₹20,00,000; (ii) Last salary × 15/26 × completed years = ₹52,000 × 15 ÷ 26 × 24 = ₹52,000 × 0.576923 × 24 = **₹7,20,000**; (iii) actual ₹8,00,000. Least = **₹7,20,000**. A) is the actual received (not least). C) is the statutory cap (not least). D) uses 1/2 month instead of 15/26. The taxable portion is ₹8,00,000 − ₹7,20,000 = ₹80,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Under EPF, the employer contributes 12% of (basic + DA). How is the employer's 12% split between EPF and EPS (EPS capped at ₹15,000 salary)?\nA) 3.67% EPF / 8.33% EPS  B) 8.33% EPF / 3.67% EPS  C) 6% EPF / 6% EPS  D) 12% EPF / 0% EPS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Of the employer's 12%, **3.67% goes to EPF** and **8.33% goes to EPS** (Employees' Pension Scheme), with EPS limited to a monthly salary ceiling of ₹15,000. B) reverses the split. C) and D) are wrong. The employee's own 12% goes entirely to EPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Employee with basic+DA = ₹40,000/month. What is the employer's monthly EPS contribution (EPS wage ceiling ₹15,000)?\nA) ₹3,332  B) ₹1,249.50  C) ₹1,468  D) ₹1,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EPS contribution = 8.33% of salary **capped at the ₹15,000 ceiling**. So 8.33% × ₹15,000 = **₹1,249.50** (≈₹1,250). A) ₹3,332 applies 8.33% to ₹40,000 (ignores the ceiling). C) ₹1,468 applies 3.67% to ₹40,000. D) ₹1,250 is the rounded figure but B is the exact 8.33% × 15,000. Pick the precise value: ₹1,249.50.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** At superannuation (age 60), an NPS Tier-I subscriber must use at least what percentage of the corpus to buy an annuity?\nA) 60%  B) 25%  C) 40%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — On exit at 60, an NPS subscriber can withdraw up to **60% as a tax-free lump sum** and must use **at least 40%** to purchase an annuity (which provides pension). A) 60% is the lump-sum portion, not annuity. B) 25% applies to partial withdrawals. D) is wrong. So 60% out, minimum 40% to annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** NPS corpus at 60 = ₹50,00,000. Subscriber takes maximum lump sum and annuitises the rest. Annuity rate 6% p.a. What is the annual pension (approx)?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹1,80,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Maximum lump sum = 60% × ₹50,00,000 = ₹30,00,000 (tax-free). Annuity portion = 40% × ₹50,00,000 = ₹20,00,000. Annual pension = ₹20,00,000 × 6% = **₹1,20,000**. A) applies 6% to the full corpus. C) uses 60% for annuity. B) is a round guess. Pension is on the 40% annuitised slice only.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The deduction limit under Section 80C is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 80C allows a maximum deduction of **₹1,50,000** (EPF, PPF, life insurance premium, ELSS, school fees, etc., combined). A) ₹1,00,000 was an older limit. C) ₹2,00,000 is the self-occupied home-loan interest cap. D) ₹50,000 is the 80CCD(1B) NPS extra. The ₹1.5 lakh is the headline 80C ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Under the new tax regime, the standard deduction from salary is:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The standard deduction under the **new tax regime is ₹75,000**; under the **old regime it is ₹50,000**. A) ₹50,000 is the old-regime figure. B) ₹40,000 was a previous old-regime amount. D) is wrong. The new regime gives a bigger flat standard deduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The additional NPS deduction under Section 80CCD(1B), over and above 80C, is up to:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 80CCD(1B) gives an **extra ₹50,000** deduction for the employee's own NPS contribution, on top of the ₹1.5 lakh 80C limit (available under the old regime). A) is the 80C limit. C) ₹75,000 is the standard deduction. D) is wrong. So the maximum combined NPS-related self-contribution deduction can reach ₹2 lakh (₹1.5L + ₹0.5L).\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Health policy has a compulsory deductible of ₹30,000. Hospital bill is ₹80,000. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹24,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A deductible is the portion the insured pays first; the insurer pays the rest. Insurer pays = ₹80,000 − ₹30,000 = **₹50,000**. A) ignores the deductible. D) is the deductible (what the insured bears). B) is wrong. The deductible is a fixed \"first slice\" the insured swallows before cover starts.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A health policy has a co-pay of 15%. The ascertained claim is ₹2,00,000. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹1,85,000  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Co-pay is a percentage of the claim borne by the insured. Insured pays 15% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹30,000; insurer pays the rest = ₹2,00,000 − ₹30,000 = **₹1,70,000**. A) ignores co-pay. C) is the insured's share. B) uses a wrong percentage. Co-pay is a proportion (unlike a fixed deductible).\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Policy 1 has a deductible of ₹1,000 AND a co-pay of 10%. Claim = ₹40,000. Insurer pays?\nA) ₹36,000  B) ₹35,100  C) ₹39,000  D) ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Apply deductible first, then co-pay on the balance. After deductible: ₹40,000 − ₹1,000 = ₹39,000. Co-pay 10% of ₹39,000 = ₹3,900. Insurer pays = ₹39,000 − ₹3,900 = **₹35,100**. A) forgets the co-pay. C) forgets the co-pay step's subtraction. D) is the answer if there were only a ₹5,000 deductible. Order matters: deductible, then co-pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Human Life Value (HLV) is best described as:\nA) The face value of all life policies held  B) The present value of expected future income (net of self-maintenance)  C) Total premiums paid over the years  D) The sum assured plus bonuses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — HLV is the **present value of the expected future income** a person will earn (used to size life cover). A) and D) describe existing policy values, not need. C) is premiums, irrelevant to need. HLV asks: how much money today equals all the future earnings this life would have produced?\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Real rate of return when nominal return is 10% and inflation is 6%?\nA) 4.00%  B) 4.24%  C) 3.77%  D) 16.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.10 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.03774 − 1 = **3.77%**. A) 4% is the rough subtraction (10% − 6%), which overstates. B) is a miscalculation. D) just adds. The exact formula divides rather than subtracts, giving slightly under 4%.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** An investment grows from ₹1,00,000 to ₹2,00,000 in 6 years. The CAGR is approximately:\nA) 16.67%  B) 12.25%  C) 11.49%  D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (2,00,000 ÷ 1,00,000)^(1/6) − 1 = 2^(0.1667) − 1 = 1.1225 − 1 = 0.1149 = **11.49%**. A) 16.67% is simple-interest thinking (100% ÷ 6). B) 12.25% is the square-root-style error. D) is a guess. Doubling in 6 years implies about 11.5% compounded (consistent with the \"Rule of 72\": 72 ÷ 6 = 12, close).\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Today's annual expense is ₹6,00,000. With 6% inflation, what will the same expense cost in 10 years (round to nearest ₹)?\nA) ₹9,60,000  B) ₹6,36,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹10,74,509\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = Present × (1 + inflation)^n = ₹6,00,000 × (1.06)^10 = ₹6,00,000 × 1.79085 = **₹10,74,509**. A) ₹9,60,000 uses simple interest (6% × 10 × 6L = 3.6L added). C) is a round guess. B) inflates for just one year. Inflation compounds, so the cost nearly doubles in a decade at 6%.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A retiree's pre-retirement annual expense is ₹12,00,000. Using a replacement ratio of 70%, the estimated annual retirement expense is:\nA) ₹12,00,000  B) ₹3,60,000  C) ₹8,40,000  D) ₹9,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Replacement ratio method: Retirement expense = pre-retirement expense × ratio = ₹12,00,000 × 70% = **₹8,40,000**. A) assumes 100%. B) uses 30%. D) uses 80%. The idea: in retirement, many costs (commute, work clothes, EMIs) fall, so you need only a fraction of your earlier expense.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Two flood policies of ₹5,00,000 each (Company A and B) cover the same risk. Ascertained loss = ₹30,000, paid by A. Under the contribution clause, B's proportionate share is:\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹5,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Contribution shares the loss in proportion to sum assured. Both policies are ₹5L (equal), so each bears half: ₹30,000 × (5L ÷ 10L) = **₹15,000**. B) gives all to one insurer. C) is wrong. D) ignores the clause. Contribution applies to indemnity policies and stops the insured from profiting twice.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Mr E gifts ₹30,00,000 to his wife, who earns ₹1,50,000 interest on it. Under clubbing provisions, this ₹1,50,000 interest is taxed:\nA) In the wife's hands at her slab  B) In Mr E's hands at his slab  C) Exempt from tax  D) Split equally between both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Income from an asset gifted to a spouse is **clubbed with the transferor's income** (Section 64). So the ₹1,50,000 is taxed in **Mr E's** hands at his slab rate, not his wife's. A), C), D) are wrong. Clubbing stops people from shifting income to lower-taxed relatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Under the old tax regime, the maximum interest deduction on a loan for a **self-occupied** house property is:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) No limit  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest on a self-occupied house loan is deductible up to **₹2,00,000** under the old regime (and not available under the new regime). A) ₹1,50,000 is 80C. C) \"no limit\" applies to let-out property, not self-occupied. D) ₹30,000 is the reduced cap if construction exceeds 5 years. So a self-occupied home gives up to ₹2 lakh interest relief in the old regime.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Equity Portfolio Sale (Capital Gains)\nMrs Anjali, a resident individual, made the following listed-equity transactions during FY 2024-25. All transactions are on a recognised stock exchange with STT paid on both legs. Brokerage is ignored unless stated.\n\n| Scrip | Bought | Cost/share | Sold | Sale/share | Qty |\n|-------|--------|-----------|------|-----------|-----|\n| Alpha Ltd | 10-Jan-2020 | ₹150 | 01-Sep-2024 | ₹450 | 1,000 |\n| Beta Ltd | 01-Mar-2024 | ₹200 | 01-Nov-2024 | ₹260 | 2,000 |\n| Gamma Ltd | 05-Aug-2023 | ₹300 | 01-Oct-2024 | ₹260 | 1,000 |\n\nFor Alpha, FMV on 31-01-2018 is not relevant (bought in 2020). Treat all gains/losses for the year together.\n\n**Q41.** What is the long-term capital gain on Alpha Ltd?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹4,50,000  C) ₹2,75,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Alpha held Jan-2020 to Sep-2024 (>12 months) = long-term. LTCG = (Sale − Cost) × Qty = (₹450 − ₹150) × 1,000 = ₹300 × 1,000 = **₹3,00,000**. (Bought after 31-01-2018, so no grandfathering; actual cost ₹150 applies.) B) uses sale only. C) prematurely subtracts the ₹1.25L exemption (that is applied later on total LTCG). D) halves it.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** What is the short-term capital gain on Beta Ltd?\nA) ₹52,000  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹1,30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Beta held Mar-2024 to Nov-2024 (<12 months) = short-term (Section 111A). STCG = (₹260 − ₹200) × 2,000 = ₹60 × 2,000 = **₹1,20,000**. B) uses 1,000 qty. A) applies a rate prematurely. D) is wrong. This ₹1,20,000 will be taxed at 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** What is the capital gain/loss on Gamma Ltd, and its nature?\nA) ₹40,000 LTCG  B) ₹40,000 STCG  C) −₹40,000 long-term loss  D) −₹40,000 short-term loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Gamma held Aug-2023 to Oct-2024 (>12 months) = long-term. Result = (₹260 − ₹300) × 1,000 = −₹40 × 1,000 = **−₹40,000, a long-term capital loss**. A) and B) wrongly show a gain. D) wrongly calls it short-term. This LTCL can be set off only against LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** After setting off the Gamma long-term loss against the Alpha long-term gain, what is the net LTCG, and the taxable LTCG after the Section 112A exemption?\nA) Net LTCG ₹2,60,000; taxable ₹1,35,000  B) Net LTCG ₹3,00,000; taxable ₹1,75,000  C) Net LTCG ₹2,60,000; taxable ₹2,60,000  D) Net LTCG ₹3,40,000; taxable ₹2,15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net LTCG = Alpha ₹3,00,000 − Gamma loss ₹40,000 = ₹2,60,000. Apply the ₹1,25,000 112A exemption: taxable LTCG = ₹2,60,000 − ₹1,25,000 = **₹1,35,000**. B) ignores the loss set-off. C) forgets the exemption. D) adds instead of subtracting the loss. Long-term loss reduces long-term gain first; then the ₹1.25L shelter applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** What is Mrs Anjali's total capital gains tax for the year (LTCG @12.5% on taxable LTCG + STCG @20% on Beta), ignoring surcharge/cess?\nA) ₹16,875  B) ₹24,000  C) ₹40,875  D) ₹56,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG tax = ₹1,35,000 × 12.5% = ₹16,875. STCG tax (Beta) = ₹1,20,000 × 20% = ₹24,000. Total = ₹16,875 + ₹24,000 = **₹40,875**. A) is LTCG only. B) is STCG only. D) wrongly taxes the full ₹2,60,000 LTCG at 12.5% (₹32,500) plus STCG. Add both pieces for the total.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Property Sale and Reinvestment (Capital Gains)\nMr Bhaskar bought a residential house on 15-May-2010 for ₹40,00,000. He spent ₹5,00,000 on an authorised extension in FY 2015-16 (cost of improvement). He sold the house on 01-December-2024 for ₹1,80,00,000 (brokerage ₹1,80,000). CII: 2010-11 = 167, 2015-16 = 254, 2024-25 = 363. Since the property was bought before 23 July 2024 but sold after, a parallel calculation applies; for the questions below use the **indexation (old) method** unless stated.\n\n**Q46.** What is the indexed cost of acquisition of the house?\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹86,94,611  C) ₹66,80,000  D) ₹72,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indexed cost = Cost × (CII sale ÷ CII purchase) = ₹40,00,000 × (363 ÷ 167) = ₹40,00,000 × 2.17365 = **₹86,94,611** (approx). A) ignores indexation. C) uses a wrong ratio. D) is a miscalculation. Indexation lifts the ₹40L cost to about ₹86.95L.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** What is the indexed cost of improvement (extension done in FY 2015-16)?\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹6,35,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹7,14,567\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Indexed improvement = ₹5,00,000 × (363 ÷ 254) = ₹5,00,000 × 1.42913 = **₹7,14,567** (approx). A) ignores indexation. C) and B) are wrong. Improvement is indexed from the year it was incurred (2015-16), not the purchase year.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Using the indexation method, what is the long-term capital gain (after deducting brokerage)?\nA) ₹85,10,822  B) ₹93,05,389  C) ₹1,33,20,000  D) ₹86,90,822\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = Sale − brokerage − indexed cost − indexed improvement = ₹1,80,00,000 − ₹1,80,000 − ₹86,94,611 − ₹7,14,567 = **₹85,10,822** (approx). B) forgets the indexed improvement. C) ignores all indexation. D) forgets brokerage. Deduct transfer expense and both indexed costs.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Mr Bhaskar invests ₹50,00,000 in NHAI bonds within 6 months under Section 54EC. By how much does this reduce his taxable LTCG?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹85,10,822  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 54EC exempts the amount invested in NHAI/REC bonds, capped at ₹50,00,000. He invested exactly ₹50,00,000, so exemption = **₹50,00,000**. B) would need the whole gain invested (not allowed beyond ₹50L). C) ₹20L is the gratuity cap. A) is wrong. Taxable LTCG falls to ₹85,10,822 − ₹50,00,000 = ₹35,10,822.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** On the post-54EC taxable LTCG (₹35,10,822) under the indexation method, what is the tax @20% (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹7,02,164  B) ₹4,38,853  C) ₹17,02,164  D) ₹3,51,082\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under the old (indexation) method the rate is 20%: ₹35,10,822 × 20% = **₹7,02,164** (approx). B) uses 12.5% (the no-indexation method's rate). C) adds an extra digit. D) uses 10%. For property bought before 23 July 2024, the indexation route taxes the indexed gain at 20% — and the lower of the two methods (20%-with-index vs 12.5%-without) is the final liability.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Retirement Benefits (Gratuity, EPF, NPS)\nMs Charulata retires on 31-March-2025 after 30 completed years in a private company covered by the Payment of Gratuity Act. Her last drawn salary (basic + DA) is ₹78,000/month. She receives gratuity of ₹15,00,000. During service her EPF was on basic+DA of ₹78,000/month. At retirement her NPS Tier-I corpus is ₹40,00,000; she takes the maximum tax-free lump sum and annuitises the rest at 6% p.a.\n\n**Q51.** What is the gratuity amount under the formula (Last salary × 15/26 × completed years)?\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000  C) ₹13,50,000  D) ₹11,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Formula = ₹78,000 × 15/26 × 30 = ₹78,000 × 0.576923 × 30 = ₹45,000 × 30 = **₹13,50,000**. B) is the actual received. A) is the statutory cap. D) uses 1/2 month (₹78,000 × 0.5 × 30 = ₹11.7L), which is the formula for employees *not* covered by the Act. Under the Act, use 15/26.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** What is the exempt gratuity, being the least of the three limits?\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹13,50,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹78,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exempt = least of (i) ₹20,00,000 cap, (ii) formula ₹13,50,000, (iii) actual ₹15,00,000. Least = **₹13,50,000**. A) is actual. C) is the cap. D) is monthly salary. The formula figure is the smallest, so it sets the exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** What part of the gratuity is taxable?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable gratuity = actual received − exempt = ₹15,00,000 − ₹13,50,000 = **₹1,50,000**. A) wrongly assumes full exemption. C) taxes the whole amount. D) is wrong. Only the excess of receipt over the exempt least-of-three is taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Of Ms Charulata's NPS corpus of ₹40,00,000, what amount can she take as a tax-free lump sum, and how much must go to annuity (minimum)?\nA) Lump sum ₹16,00,000; annuity ₹24,00,000  B) Lump sum ₹10,00,000; annuity ₹30,00,000  C) Lump sum ₹40,00,000; annuity ₹0  D) Lump sum ₹24,00,000; annuity ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At 60, up to 60% can be withdrawn tax-free and at least 40% must be annuitised. Lump sum = 60% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹24,00,000**; annuity = 40% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹16,00,000**. A) reverses the split. C) ignores the annuity rule. B) uses 25%/75%. Sixty out, forty (minimum) to pension.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Based on Q54, what is her annual NPS pension at a 6% annuity rate?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹1,44,000  C) ₹96,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Pension = annuitised amount × annuity rate = ₹16,00,000 × 6% = **₹96,000** per year. A) applies 6% to the full corpus. B) applies 6% to ₹24L. D) applies it to ₹20L. Pension is computed only on the 40% annuity corpus (₹16,00,000).\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Health Insurance Claims (Insurance)\nMr Dinesh has two health policies. Policy P (sum insured ₹5,00,000) has a co-pay of 20% and no deductible. Policy Q (sum insured ₹3,00,000) has a compulsory deductible of ₹25,000 and no co-pay. He is hospitalised; the ascertained claim is ₹4,00,000.\n\n**Q56.** If Mr Dinesh claims entirely under Policy P (co-pay 20%), how much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹4,00,000  B) ₹3,75,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹3,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Co-pay 20% means the insured bears 20% × ₹4,00,000 = ₹80,000; insurer pays ₹4,00,000 − ₹80,000 = **₹3,20,000**. A) ignores co-pay. C) is the insured's share. B) treats it as a ₹25,000 deductible. The claim (₹4L) is within Policy P's ₹5L sum insured, so no contribution issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** If instead he claims entirely under Policy Q (deductible ₹25,000), how much does the insurer pay (claim ₹4,00,000 but sum insured ₹3,00,000)?\nA) ₹3,75,000  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,75,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — After the ₹25,000 deductible the admissible amount is ₹3,75,000, but the **payout is capped at the sum insured of ₹3,00,000**. So insurer pays **₹3,00,000**. A) ignores the SI cap. C) subtracts the deductible from the cap (the deductible is already absorbed before reaching the cap). D) ignores both. Sum insured is the ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Mr Dinesh first exhausts Policy Q (insurer pays ₹3,00,000), then claims the balance from Policy P. What balance can he claim from Policy P, and is contribution applied?\nA) ₹1,00,000; contribution applies  B) ₹1,00,000; no contribution because the claim is within Policy P's sum insured  C) ₹4,00,000; contribution applies  D) ₹0; already fully paid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — After Policy Q pays ₹3,00,000, the unpaid balance is ₹4,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹1,00,000. The insured may choose another policy for the balance; the chosen insurer cannot force contribution where the claim is within that policy's sum insured. So Policy P pays **₹1,00,000 with no contribution clause applied**. A) wrongly applies contribution. C) ignores Q's payment. D) is wrong. The insured controls which policy pays.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** On the ₹1,00,000 balance claimed from Policy P (co-pay 20%), how much does Policy P actually pay?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹75,000  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Policy P has a 20% co-pay, so on the ₹1,00,000 balance the insured bears ₹20,000 and Policy P pays ₹1,00,000 − ₹20,000 = **₹80,000**. A) ignores co-pay. C) is the insured's share. B) is wrong. Even on a top-up claim, the co-pay still bites.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Total amount Mr Dinesh recovers from both insurers (Q ₹3,00,000 + P's payment), and his out-of-pocket cost?\nA) Recovered ₹3,80,000; out-of-pocket ₹20,000  B) Recovered ₹4,00,000; out-of-pocket ₹0  C) Recovered ₹3,00,000; out-of-pocket ₹1,00,000  D) Recovered ₹3,20,000; out-of-pocket ₹80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total recovered = ₹3,00,000 (Q) + ₹80,000 (P) = **₹3,80,000**. Out-of-pocket = total claim ₹4,00,000 − ₹3,80,000 = **₹20,000** (the co-pay on the balance claimed from P). B) ignores the co-pay. C) ignores Policy P. D) assumes only P paid. The ₹20,000 gap is exactly the co-pay borne on the ₹1,00,000 balance.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Salary and Income-Tax Computation (Old Regime)\nMr Esha is a salaried employee (old tax regime) for FY 2024-25 with the following details:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|------|-----------|\n| Basic salary + DA (annual) | 18,00,000 |\n| Interest on self-occupied home loan (paid) | 2,40,000 |\n| EPF (own contribution, 80C) | 1,20,000 |\n| LIC premium (80C) | 60,000 |\n| NPS own contribution (80CCD(1B)) | 50,000 |\n\nStandard deduction (old regime) = ₹50,000. He has no other income.\n\n**Q61.** What is his income chargeable under \"Salary\" after the standard deduction?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹16,50,000  C) ₹17,50,000  D) ₹17,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Salary income = Gross salary − standard deduction = ₹18,00,000 − ₹50,000 = **₹17,50,000**. A) forgets the deduction. D) uses ₹75,000 (new-regime figure). B) over-deducts. The old-regime standard deduction is ₹50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** What is the income from house property (self-occupied) after allowing home-loan interest?\nA) ₹0  B) −₹2,40,000  C) −₹2,00,000  D) −₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A self-occupied house has nil annual value; interest is deductible up to **₹2,00,000** (old regime). Income from house property = ₹0 − ₹2,00,000 = **−₹2,00,000** (the cap limits it even though ₹2,40,000 was paid). A) ignores the deduction. B) ignores the ₹2L cap. D) uses the wrong cap. The extra ₹40,000 interest is simply lost (self-occupied has no carry-forward of that excess).\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** What is the total 80C deduction allowed?\nA) ₹1,80,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 80C items: EPF ₹1,20,000 + LIC ₹60,000 = ₹1,80,000, but 80C is **capped at ₹1,50,000**. So allowed = **₹1,50,000**. A) ignores the cap. C) counts only EPF. B) confuses with the home-loan cap. The NPS ₹50,000 is separate (80CCD(1B)), not part of this ₹1.5L.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** What is his Gross Total Income (salary + house property), before Chapter VI-A deductions?\nA) ₹17,50,000  B) ₹15,50,000  C) ₹15,10,000  D) ₹17,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — GTI = Salary income + House property income = ₹17,50,000 + (−₹2,00,000) = **₹15,50,000**. A) ignores the house-property loss set-off. C) and D) miscalculate. The negative house-property income (capped at ₹2L) is set off against salary.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** What is his Total (taxable) Income after 80C (₹1,50,000) and 80CCD(1B) (₹50,000)?\nA) ₹13,50,000  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹13,00,000  D) ₹15,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total income = GTI − 80C − 80CCD(1B) = ₹15,50,000 − ₹1,50,000 − ₹50,000 = **₹13,50,000**. B) forgets the NPS ₹50,000. C) double-deducts. D) ignores deductions. Subtract both the ₹1.5L (80C) and the extra ₹50,000 (NPS 80CCD(1B)).\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Case (Capital Gains, Insurance, Retirement, Planning)\nMr Farhan, aged 58, plans his finances for FY 2024-25. (i) He sold listed equity (STT paid), held 4 years, for an LTCG of ₹2,25,000. (ii) He sold a plot of urban land (held 5 years, bought after 23 July 2024 is NOT the case — assume acquired in 2018) for a gain of ₹10,00,000 (no indexation under new rules, taxed @12.5%). (iii) His current annual household expense is ₹9,00,000; he expects to retire in 2 years and applies a 60% replacement ratio, with 5% inflation over those 2 years. (iv) He holds a health policy with a ₹40,000 compulsory deductible; his hospital bill this year was ₹1,00,000.\n\n**Q66.** What is the taxable LTCG on the listed equity after the Section 112A exemption, and the tax @12.5%?\nA) Taxable ₹1,25,000; tax ₹15,625  B) Taxable ₹2,25,000; tax ₹28,125  C) Taxable ₹1,00,000; tax ₹10,000  D) Taxable ₹1,00,000; tax ₹12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Taxable LTCG = ₹2,25,000 − ₹1,25,000 exemption = ₹1,00,000; tax = ₹1,00,000 × 12.5% = **₹12,500**. B) forgets the exemption. C) uses 10%. A) misreads the exemption. The ₹1.25 lakh shelter applies to listed-equity LTCG under 112A.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** What is the tax on the urban land LTCG of ₹10,00,000 (taxed @12.5%, no ₹1.25L exemption as it is not 112A equity)?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹1,25,000  D) ₹1,09,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Non-equity LTCG is taxed at 12.5% with **no ₹1.25 lakh exemption** (that is exclusive to 112A listed equity). Tax = ₹10,00,000 × 12.5% = **₹1,25,000**. A) uses 10%. B) uses 20%. D) wrongly applies the ₹1.25L exemption to land. Only listed-equity LTCG gets the freebie slice.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Mr Farhan's total capital gains tax for the year (equity + land), ignoring surcharge/cess?\nA) ₹1,37,500  B) ₹1,53,125  C) ₹1,25,000  D) ₹12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = equity LTCG tax ₹12,500 + land LTCG tax ₹1,25,000 = **₹1,37,500**. B) taxes the equity at full ₹2,25,000. C) is land only. D) is equity only. Add the two LTCG taxes.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Estimating retirement expenses: current expense ₹9,00,000, replacement ratio 60%, inflated 5% for 2 years. What is the estimated first-year retirement expense (round to nearest ₹)?\nA) ₹5,40,000  B) ₹5,95,350  C) ₹9,92,250  D) ₹6,21,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Step 1: apply replacement ratio: ₹9,00,000 × 60% = ₹5,40,000. Step 2: inflate 5% for 2 years: ₹5,40,000 × (1.05)^2 = ₹5,40,000 × 1.1025 = **₹5,95,350**. A) forgets inflation. C) inflates before applying the ratio (and only the full expense). D) uses simple 5% × 2 years (₹5,40,000 × 1.15). Apply the ratio first, then compound the inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** On the hospital bill of ₹1,00,000 with a ₹40,000 compulsory deductible, what does the health insurer pay, and what does Mr Farhan bear?\nA) Insurer ₹1,00,000; Farhan ₹0  B) Insurer ₹60,000; Farhan ₹40,000  C) Insurer ₹40,000; Farhan ₹60,000  D) Insurer ₹85,000; Farhan ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deductible is borne by the insured first; insurer pays the rest. Insurer = ₹1,00,000 − ₹40,000 = **₹60,000**; Farhan bears the ₹40,000 deductible. A) ignores the deductible. C) reverses it. D) treats it like a 15% co-pay. The deductible is a fixed first slice the insured pays before cover kicks in.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "02", "md": "# Mock Paper 2 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy, with a strong focus on retirement-planning maths (inflation-adjusted corpus, SIP/annuity future value, present value of withdrawals, EPF, gratuity, NPS, SCSS and annuities). Keep a calculator or spreadsheet handy, work each step carefully, and remember the negative marking before you guess.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** A salaried employee has a monthly basic + DA of ₹40,000. How much does the EMPLOYEE contribute to EPF each month at the statutory rate?\nA) ₹3,200  B) ₹4,800  C) ₹1,468  D) ₹3,333\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The employee's EPF contribution is a flat 12% of (basic + DA). 12% × ₹40,000 = ₹4,800, and this entire ₹4,800 goes into the EPF account. A (8%) is the special reduced rate for new women employees, not the standard rate. C is the EPS slice (8.33% of the ₹15,000 ceiling). D is wrong arithmetic. Formula: Employee EPF = 12% × (basic + DA).\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** For the same employee (basic + DA ₹40,000), the EMPLOYER also pays 12%. How much of the employer's contribution is diverted to the Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS)?\nA) ₹4,800  B) ₹3,332  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹1,468\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — EPS gets 8.33% of salary, but the EPS wage is capped at a ceiling of ₹15,000 per month. So EPS = 8.33% × ₹15,000 = ₹1,249.50 ≈ ₹1,250. The cap is the trick: 8.33% × ₹40,000 = ₹3,332 (option B) ignores the ceiling and is wrong. A is the full 12%. D mixes things up. The rest of the employer's 12% (₹4,800 − ₹1,250 = ₹3,550) goes to EPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Continuing Q2: of the employer's ₹4,800, how much goes to the employee's EPF account (the EPF slice) after the EPS portion is removed?\nA) ₹3,550  B) ₹4,800  C) ₹1,468  D) ₹3,332\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Employer EPF slice = total employer 12% minus the EPS amount. ₹4,800 − ₹1,250 = ₹3,550. (Equivalently, 3.67% × ₹40,000 = ₹1,468 PLUS the 8.33% the employee earns above the ₹15,000 ceiling — but the simplest exam route is ₹4,800 total minus ₹1,250 EPS = ₹3,550.) Option C (₹1,468) is only the 3.67%-on-full-salary figure and ignores that EPS is capped at ₹15,000, so the EPF slice is actually larger. The EPS cap means more of the employer money lands in EPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Gratuity for an employee COVERED by the Payment of Gratuity Act with last drawn salary (basic + DA) ₹52,000 and 18 years 8 months of service is:\nA) ₹5,40,000  B) ₹5,70,000  C) ₹5,26,154  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Formula = (15 × last drawn salary × years) ÷ 26. Service of 18 years 8 months rounds UP to 19 years (because the part-year exceeds 6 months). So gratuity = (15 × 52,000 × 19) ÷ 26 = 14,820,000 ÷ 26 = ₹5,70,000. Option A uses 18 years (forgets the rounding up). C divides by an odd number. D is a round guess. The \"÷26\" comes from 15 days' salary per year out of a 26-working-day month.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A retiree's NPS corpus at age 60 is ₹50,00,000. He uses only the MINIMUM mandatory portion for annuity and withdraws the rest as lump sum. How much can he withdraw as tax-free lump sum?\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At normal exit (age 60), a minimum of 40% of the corpus must buy an annuity, and a maximum of 60% can be taken as lump sum (tax-free under Sec 10(12A)). Maximum lump sum = 60% × ₹50,00,000 = ₹30,00,000. Option B (40%) is the annuity portion, not the lump sum. A is 40% mislabelled. D is wrong. So minimum annuity = ₹20,00,000, lump sum = ₹30,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** An NPS subscriber RESIGNS and exits at age 45 (before 60). His corpus is ₹15,00,000. What is the MINIMUM amount he must use to buy an annuity?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹9,00,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On premature exit (before age 60/superannuation), at least 80% of the corpus must be annuitised and only 20% can be taken as lump sum. 80% × ₹15,00,000 = ₹12,00,000. Option A (40%) and the 60/40 split apply only to normal exit at 60, not to early exit. The corpus is above ₹2.5 lakh, so the full-lump-sum exemption for tiny corpuses does not apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A 30-year-old wants ₹50,000 per month (today's money) at retirement. With 6% annual inflation over the next 30 years, what will be the equivalent monthly expense at retirement (rounded)?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹1,79,085  C) ₹90,000  D) ₹2,87,175\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Inflation-adjusted future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)^years = 50,000 × (1.06)^30. (1.06)^30 ≈ 5.7435, so 50,000 × 5.7435 ≈ ₹2,87,175. Option B uses (1.06)^22 roughly; C just adds a little; A triples it by eye. Each year prices rise 6%, and compounding over 30 years multiplies the cost about 5.74 times.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If something costs ₹100 today and inflation is 6% per year, the official workbook says it costs roughly how much in 20 years?\nA) ₹220  B) ₹574  C) ₹321  D) ₹179\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future cost = 100 × (1.06)^20 = 100 × 3.207 ≈ ₹321. The workbook's own example states ₹100 today → ₹179 in 10 years (option D, the 10-year figure), ₹321 in 20 years, and ₹574 in 30 years (option B, the 30-year figure). So for 20 years the answer is ₹321. A is a rough guess.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Nominal expected return on a retirement portfolio is 10% and inflation is 6%. The real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return is approximately:\nA) 4.00%  B) 1.67%  C) 16.00%  D) 3.77%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.10 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.03774 − 1 = 0.03774 ≈ 3.77%. Option A (simply 10% − 6%) is the common shortcut that slightly overstates the true real return. C adds the rates. The real return tells you how much your money grows in actual purchasing power.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A SIP of ₹10,000 per month is invested for 12 months at 12% p.a. (1% per month), contributions at month-end. The future value at the end of 12 months (ordinary annuity) is approximately:\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹1,26,825  C) ₹1,28,093  D) ₹1,34,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future value of an ordinary annuity = P × [((1 + i)^n − 1) ÷ i], where P = 10,000, i = 0.01, n = 12. (1.01)^12 = 1.126825, so ((1.126825 − 1) ÷ 0.01) = 12.6825. FV = 10,000 × 12.6825 = ₹1,26,825. Option A is just the contributions with no growth. C (₹1,28,093) is the annuity-DUE value (×1.01) — wrong because payments are at month-end. The bracket factor turns 12 deposits into more than ₹1.2 lakh because of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** What lump sum must be invested TODAY to grow to ₹1 crore in 20 years at 9% p.a. compounded annually (present value)?\nA) ₹17,84,309  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹14,86,436  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Present value = FV ÷ (1 + r)^n = 1,00,00,000 ÷ (1.09)^20. (1.09)^20 ≈ 5.6044, so PV = 1,00,00,000 ÷ 5.6044 ≈ ₹17,84,309. Option C uses ~6.73 (an 11% factor). B and D are guesses. PV strips out 20 years of 9% growth to tell you the amount needed up front.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A retiree needs to withdraw ₹3,00,000 at the end of each year for 15 years. If the corpus earns 8% p.a., the corpus required today (present value of an ordinary annuity) is approximately:\nA) ₹45,00,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹27,81,500  D) ₹25,68,090\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — PV of ordinary annuity = P × [1 − (1 + r)^−n] ÷ r = 3,00,000 × [1 − (1.08)^−15] ÷ 0.08. (1.08)^15 ≈ 3.1722, so (1.08)^−15 ≈ 0.31524; [1 − 0.31524] ÷ 0.08 = 0.68476 ÷ 0.08 = 8.5595. PV = 3,00,000 × 8.5595 ≈ ₹25,68,090. Option A simply multiplies 3,00,000 × 15 (ignores that the corpus keeps earning 8%). C is the annuity-due figure. The corpus is less than the naive total because it keeps earning interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Listed equity shares are sold after being held for 10 months at a gain of ₹2,00,000. The capital gains tax (Sec 111A) is:\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity held for 12 months or less is a SHORT-term capital asset, so STCG under Section 111A is taxed at 20% (for transfers on/after 23 July 2024). 20% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹40,000. Option A applies the 12.5% LTCG rate (wrong, since 10 months < 12 months means short-term). There is no ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG. B and D are arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Listed equity held for 3 years is sold with a long-term capital gain of ₹3,00,000 during the year (no other LTCG). The LTCG tax under Section 112A is:\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹21,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under Sec 112A, the first ₹1,25,000 of LTCG on listed equity/equity MF is exempt; the excess is taxed at 12.5%. Taxable LTCG = ₹3,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹1,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹1,75,000 = ₹21,875. Option A taxes the whole ₹3,00,000 at 12.5% (forgets the exemption). C uses 20% (the STCG rate). B is a guess.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** An investor buys a residential plot (land) and sells it after 30 months. For capital-gains purposes this is:\nA) Short-term, because held under 36 months  B) Short-term, because held under 24 months  C) Long-term, because held over 24 months  D) Long-term, because held over 12 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For immovable property (land/building) and unlisted assets, the holding period to qualify as long-term is more than 24 months. 30 months > 24 months, so it is long-term. Option D wrongly uses the 12-month rule, which applies only to listed equity/securities. A and B use 36 months, which is outdated. Remember: 12 months for listed equity, 24 months for property and unlisted shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A health claim is ₹2,00,000 and the policy has a co-pay of 15%. How much does the INSURER pay?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹1,85,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Co-pay is the share the insured bears. Insured pays 15% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹30,000 (option A), and the insurer pays the balance ₹1,70,000. C ignores the co-pay entirely. B mistakenly subtracts only ₹15,000. Co-pay makes the policyholder share part of every claim, which lowers the premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A health policy has a compulsory deductible of ₹30,000. The hospital bill is ₹80,000. How much does the company pay?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A deductible is the first slice paid by the insured before the policy responds. Company pays ₹80,000 − ₹30,000 = ₹50,000; the insured bears ₹30,000. Option A ignores the deductible; C is the deductible itself; D is unrelated. Top-up and super top-up policies use exactly this deductible mechanism to keep premiums low.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Two indemnity policies of ₹5,00,000 each (Company A and Company B) cover the same flood risk. The ascertained loss is ₹30,000 and Company A settles it. Under the contribution principle, Company B's proportionate share is:\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹5,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Contribution lets one insurer recover a proportionate share from the other when the same risk is covered twice. Since both sums insured are equal (₹5,00,000 each = 50:50), Company B chips in half of ₹30,000 = ₹15,000. Option A would mean B pays the whole loss; B and C understate it. Contribution applies only to indemnity policies and never lets total payouts exceed the actual loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Policy 1 has a deductible of ₹1,000 and a co-pay of 10%; the ascertained claim is ₹40,000. How much does Policy 1 pay?\nA) ₹36,000  B) ₹35,100  C) ₹35,000  D) ₹39,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Apply the deductible first, then the co-pay on the remainder. ₹40,000 − ₹1,000 deductible = ₹39,000; co-pay = 10% × ₹39,000 = ₹3,900; payout = ₹39,000 − ₹3,900 = ₹35,100. Option D stops after the deductible; C is the answer for a different policy (₹5,000 deductible, no co-pay); A applies co-pay but no deductible. Order matters: deductible, then co-pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under the new tax regime for FY 2025-26, a resident individual with total income of exactly ₹12,00,000 pays income tax (before cess) of:\nA) ₹71,250  B) ₹90,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — From FY 2025-26, the Section 87A rebate makes tax NIL for resident individuals whose total income is up to ₹12,00,000 under the new regime (rebate up to ₹60,000 fully wipes out the computed tax). Option C is the gross tax before rebate (₹60,000), but the rebate removes it. A and B are unrelated. Marginal relief applies just above ₹12,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A central-government NPS subscriber has basic + DA of ₹10,00,000. What is the MAXIMUM employer NPS contribution eligible for deduction under Section 80CCD(2)?\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹1,40,000  C) ₹1,00,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under Sec 80CCD(2), employer NPS contribution is deductible up to 14% of (basic + DA) for government employees (and 14% for other employers per Budget 2024). 14% × ₹10,00,000 = ₹1,40,000. Option C uses the old 10% limit; A is the 80CCD(1B) self-contribution cap; D is the 80C limit. This deduction is over and above the ₹1.5 lakh of 80C/80CCD(1).\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Over and above 80C and 80CCD(1), what additional exclusive NPS self-contribution deduction can an individual claim under Section 80CCD(1B)?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 80CCD(1B) gives an extra ₹50,000 deduction for the subscriber's own NPS contribution, on top of the ₹1.5 lakh combined limit of 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1). So total self-contribution deduction = ₹1.5 lakh + ₹50,000 = ₹2 lakh (option C is that total, not the additional slice). A is the 80C ceiling; B is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Using the Replacement Ratio method: Mr. Rajeev earns ₹1,00,000 per month now, his income grows 10% p.a., and he retires in 10 years targeting 50% of his final income. His monthly income just after retirement is approximately:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹1,29,687  D) ₹2,59,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — First grow income for 10 years: ₹1,00,000 × (1.10)^10 = ₹2,59,374 (option D, the final salary, not the answer). Then take 50%: 2,59,374 × 0.50 = ₹1,29,687. Option B applies 50% to today's income (forgets the 10 years of salary growth); A is unchanged income. This figure then rises with inflation each retirement year.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Continuing Q23: the replacement income for the SECOND year of retirement, with 7% inflation applied, is approximately:\nA) ₹1,38,765  B) ₹1,29,687  C) ₹1,42,000  D) ₹1,36,171\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The first-year retirement income (₹1,29,687) is grown by one year of 7% inflation to preserve purchasing power: ₹1,29,687 × 1.07 = ₹1,38,765. Option B is the first-year figure (no inflation added); D uses ~5%. The whole point of inflating the pension each year is to keep real spending power constant.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Mr. Ashish (age 40) has current household expenses of ₹30,000/month and expects ₹15,000 extra at retirement, retiring at 60. With 6% inflation, his monthly expense at retirement is approximately:\nA) ₹96,214  B) ₹1,44,321  C) ₹45,000  D) ₹1,35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total current retirement expense = ₹30,000 + ₹15,000 = ₹45,000. Years to retirement = 60 − 40 = 20. Inflate: ₹45,000 × (1.06)^20 = 45,000 × 3.207 ≈ ₹1,44,321. Option A inflates only the ₹30,000; C is the un-inflated total; D is a guess. The workbook's own worked example gives exactly ₹1,44,321.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS) allows a maximum deposit of:\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹4,50,000  C) ₹9,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The SCSS maximum deposit limit is ₹30 lakh (deposits in multiples of ₹1,000, minimum ₹1,000). Option A is the old SCSS limit; C is the POMIS single-account cap; B is unrelated. SCSS has a 5-year tenure (extendable by 3 years), and the deposit qualifies for an 80C deduction up to ₹1.5 lakh, but the interest is fully taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An SCSS investor deposits ₹20,00,000 at an interest rate of 8.2% p.a., paid quarterly. The QUARTERLY interest payout is approximately:\nA) ₹1,64,000  B) ₹41,000  C) ₹13,667  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual interest = 8.2% × ₹20,00,000 = ₹1,64,000 (option A, the yearly figure). Paid quarterly = ₹1,64,000 ÷ 4 = ₹41,000 per quarter. Option C divides by 12 (monthly, wrong frequency); D is rounded. Since annual interest exceeds ₹50,000, TDS would apply on the SCSS interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A debt mutual fund (bought after 1 April 2023) is sold after 4 years with a gain of ₹1,50,000. The investor is in the 30% slab. The tax treatment is:\nA) 12.5% LTCG after ₹1.25 lakh exemption  B) Exempt as held over 3 years  C) 20% STCG  D) Taxed at slab rate (30%) as the gain is added to income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For debt funds bought on/after 1 April 2023 (Sec 50AA), the gain is always treated as short-term regardless of holding period and taxed at the investor's slab rate. So ₹1,50,000 is added to income and taxed at 30% = ₹45,000. Options A and C wrongly apply equity rates; B wrongly assumes long-term exemption. The 12.5%/₹1.25 lakh rules are for EQUITY, not debt funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** An employee's own EPF + VPF contribution in a financial year is ₹3,00,000. On how much of the contribution is the interest TAXABLE?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹50,000  C) Nil  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since Finance Act 2021, interest on the employee's own EPF+VPF contribution exceeding ₹2.5 lakh per year is taxable. Excess = ₹3,00,000 − ₹2,50,000 = ₹50,000; interest on this ₹50,000 is taxable, while interest on the first ₹2.5 lakh stays exempt. Option A taxes interest on the whole amount; D is the exempt threshold, not the excess; C ignores the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A SIP of ₹15,000 per month runs for 20 years at an expected 12% p.a. (1% per month, 240 months). Using FV = P × [((1+i)^n − 1) ÷ i], the maturity value is closest to:\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹74,93,000  C) ₹1,49,87,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — i = 0.01, n = 240. (1.01)^240 ≈ 10.8926, so factor = (10.8926 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = 989.26. FV = 15,000 × 989.26 ≈ ₹1,48,38,900 ≈ ₹1.49 crore. Option A is just total contributions (15,000 × 240 = ₹36 lakh) with no growth; B is half; D is a round guess. Long-horizon SIPs gain enormously from compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Inflation runs at 7% per year. The purchasing power of ₹1,00,000 after 10 years (i.e., its value in today's money) is approximately:\nA) ₹50,835  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹1,96,715  D) ₹70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Real value = Amount ÷ (1 + inflation)^years = 1,00,000 ÷ (1.07)^10. (1.07)^10 ≈ 1.9672, so 1,00,000 ÷ 1.9672 ≈ ₹50,835. Option C multiplies instead of divides (that's the future nominal cost of ₹1 lakh of goods). D is a rough guess. Inflation roughly halves the purchasing power of money over about 10 years at 7%.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A property bought for ₹20,00,000 in 2010 is sold for ₹80,00,000 after 14 years. CAGR of the investment is closest to:\nA) 21.4%  B) 10.4%  C) 30.0%  D) 7.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (80,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000)^(1/14) − 1 = 4^(1/14) − 1. ln 4 = 1.3863; ÷14 = 0.09902; e^0.09902 = 1.1041, so CAGR ≈ 10.4%. Option A is the simple average (300% ÷ 14); D divides differently. CAGR is the smoothed annual growth rate that turns ₹20 lakh into ₹80 lakh in 14 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** An employee NOT covered by the Gratuity Act has average last-10-months salary ₹40,000 and 20 completed years of service. Gratuity = average salary × ½ × years of service equals:\nA) ₹4,61,538  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹6,00,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For employees NOT covered, gratuity = average salary × ½ × completed years = 40,000 × 0.5 × 20 = ₹4,00,000. Note the divisor is effectively 30 (15/30 = ½), and only fully completed years count (no rounding up). Option A wrongly uses the ÷26 covered-employee formula; C and B are guesses. Covered employees use ×15/26; not-covered use ×15/30 (= half month).\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Under PMVVY (modified), to receive the MAXIMUM pension of ₹10,000 per month, the purchase price (investment) required was:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — PMVVY's maximum monthly pension of ₹10,000 (₹1,20,000/year) required a purchase price of ₹15,00,000; the minimum ₹1,000/month pension required ₹1,50,000 (option A). The scheme is a 10-year LIC plan with the purchase price returned at maturity. C and B do not match the scheme limits.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A retiree puts ₹50,00,000 into an immediate annuity quoting an annuity rate of 6.5% p.a. (lifetime without return of purchase price). The annual annuity income is:\nA) ₹3,25,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹6,50,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annuity income = purchase price × annuity rate = ₹50,00,000 × 6.5% = ₹3,25,000 per year. \"Without return of purchase price\" pays the highest annuity rate; the principal is retained by the insurer. Options B and D understate; C doubles the rate. Annuity payments are fully taxable as income in the annuitant's hands.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** An employee changes jobs after only 3 years and TRANSFERS his EPF balance to the new employer's account. The tax treatment of this transfer is:\nA) Fully taxable as it is before 5 years  B) 10% TDS deducted  C) Tax-free, because the balance is transferred (not withdrawn)  D) Taxable only above ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — If an employee changes employer in under 5 years and transfers (not withdraws) the PF balance, it remains tax-free. Taxation before 5 years applies to actual withdrawals, not transfers. Option B (10% TDS) and D (₹50,000 threshold) apply to early withdrawals, not transfers. Always transfer PF on a job change to keep it tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** PPF falls under which taxation regime, and what is the annual contribution deduction limit under Section 80C?\nA) EET; ₹1,00,000  B) ETE; ₹2,00,000  C) EEE; ₹1,50,000  D) TEE; ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — PPF is Exempt-Exempt-Exempt (EEE): the contribution (up to ₹1.5 lakh under Sec 80C) is deductible, the interest is tax-free, and the maturity amount is tax-free. Option A (EET) and B (ETE) wrongly tax some stage; the 80C cap is ₹1.5 lakh, not ₹1 lakh or ₹2 lakh. NPS, by contrast, is EEE only partly (the annuity portion is taxable).\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A 30-year-old with current annual income of ₹12,00,000 (net of taxes/own expenses) plans to retire at 60, i.e., 30 working years left. Using the simplest HLV idea (income × years to retirement, no discounting), the maximum HLV is:\nA) ₹3,60,00,000  B) ₹1,20,00,000  C) ₹6,00,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When the discount rate equals the post-tax investment return assumption (workbook's simplest case), HLV ≈ annual income × years to retirement = ₹12,00,000 × 30 = ₹3,60,00,000. Option D is just one year; B and C are arbitrary. HLV is the present value of future income a family loses on the breadwinner's death — it is higher for younger people with more earning years left.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An employee withdraws ₹70,000 from EPF before completing 5 years of service and furnishes his PAN. The TDS deducted is:\nA) ₹14,000 (20%)  B) ₹35,000 (50%)  C) Nil  D) ₹7,000 (10%)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For EPF withdrawals over ₹50,000 before 5 years of service, TDS applies. If PAN is furnished, TDS is 10%: 10% × ₹70,000 = ₹7,000. If PAN is NOT furnished, TDS is at the maximum marginal rate (much higher). Option C would apply if the amount were under ₹50,000 (no TDS, but tax per slab); A and B use wrong rates. Form 15G/15H can avoid the TDS where eligible.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A 40% equity, 60% debt retirement portfolio holds ₹40,00,000. Equity returns 14% and debt returns 7% for the year. The blended portfolio return is:\nA) 10.50%  B) 14.00%  C) 7.00%  D) 9.80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Weighted return = (weight_equity × return_equity) + (weight_debt × return_debt) = (0.40 × 14%) + (0.60 × 7%) = 5.6% + 4.2% = 9.8%. Option A is the simple average of 14% and 7% (ignores the 40/60 weights); C and B take just one asset. The blended return leans toward debt because 60% of money sits there.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Retirement Corpus for Mr. Verma\n\nMr. Verma is 35 years old and plans to retire at 60. His current monthly household expense is ₹60,000 (today's money). He expects inflation of 6% per year throughout. After retirement he expects to live 20 years, and his retirement corpus will earn 8% per year during the distribution phase (real return assumptions are applied where stated). He wants to plan the corpus needed and a SIP to reach it. Assume his pre-retirement portfolio earns 10% per year.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current age / retirement age | 35 / 60 |\n| Years to retirement | 25 |\n| Current monthly expense | ₹60,000 |\n| Inflation | 6% p.a. |\n| Years in retirement | 20 |\n| Pre-retirement return | 10% p.a. |\n\n**Q41.** Mr. Verma's monthly expense at the START of retirement (in 25 years) at 6% inflation is approximately:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,57,505  C) ₹1,92,428  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future expense = present × (1 + inflation)^years = 60,000 × (1.06)^25. (1.06)^25 ≈ 4.2919, so 60,000 × 4.2919 ≈ ₹2,57,505. Option C (₹1,92,428) is the 20-year figure from the workbook example, not 25 years; A and D are guesses. After 25 years of 6% inflation, the same lifestyle costs about 4.29× more.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** His first-year retirement expense (annual) at the start of retirement is approximately:\nA) ₹30,90,060  B) ₹7,20,000  C) ₹23,09,136  D) ₹20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual expense = monthly × 12 = ₹2,57,505 × 12 = ₹30,90,060. Option B is today's annual expense (60,000 × 12, no inflation); C and D understate it. We annualise the inflated monthly figure from Q41.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Ignoring further inflation during retirement, the corpus needed at 60 to fund ₹30,90,060 withdrawn at each year-end for 20 years, with the corpus earning 8%, is closest to (PV of ordinary annuity, factor ≈ 9.8181):\nA) ₹6,18,01,200  B) ₹4,50,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹3,03,38,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — PV = annual withdrawal × [1 − (1.08)^−20] ÷ 0.08. (1.08)^20 ≈ 4.6610, so (1.08)^−20 ≈ 0.21455; [1 − 0.21455] ÷ 0.08 = 0.78545 ÷ 0.08 = 9.8181. Corpus = 30,90,060 × 9.8181 ≈ ₹3,03,38,500. Option A multiplies 30,90,060 × 20 (ignores 8% growth on the corpus); C and B are guesses. The corpus is less than 20× the withdrawal because it keeps earning 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** To accumulate a corpus of ₹3.03 crore in 25 years through a monthly SIP earning 10% p.a. (i ≈ 0.8333% per month, n = 300, annuity factor ≈ 1326.83), the required monthly SIP is closest to:\nA) ₹22,850  B) ₹1,01,000  C) ₹10,100  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Rearrange FV = P × factor → P = FV ÷ factor = 3,03,38,500 ÷ 1326.83 ≈ ₹22,866 ≈ ₹22,850. (The factor 1326.83 = ((1.0083333)^300 − 1) ÷ 0.0083333.) Option B is far too high (it ignores 25 years of compounding); C is too low; D is a guess. Compounding over 25 years means a fairly modest SIP can build a ₹3 crore corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which single change would MOST reduce the SIP Mr. Verma needs, holding everything else constant?\nA) Assuming a lower pre-retirement return  B) Assuming higher inflation  C) Starting the SIP earlier (more years to retirement)  D) Planning for more years in retirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Starting earlier lengthens the accumulation period, so compounding does more work and the required monthly SIP falls sharply. Option A (lower return) means you need to save MORE. Option B (higher inflation) raises the target corpus, increasing the SIP. Option D (longer retirement) also raises the corpus needed. The workbook stresses that the longer the period to retirement, the greater the compounding effect and the lower the savings needed.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Mr. Iyer's Retirement Benefits at Superannuation\n\nMr. Iyer retires from a private company (covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act) at age 60. His details at retirement are below. He is in the 30% tax slab. He also has an NPS Tier-I corpus.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Last drawn salary (basic + DA) | ₹70,000/month |\n| Completed service | 28 years 7 months |\n| EPF accumulated balance | ₹45,00,000 |\n| NPS Tier-I corpus | ₹40,00,000 |\n| Gratuity received (actual) | ₹12,00,000 |\n\n**Q46.** Mr. Iyer's gratuity under the formula (covered employee) is:\nA) ₹11,71,154  B) ₹12,07,500  C) ₹11,30,769  D) ₹20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula = (15 × last drawn salary × years) ÷ 26. 28 years 7 months rounds UP to 29 years (the 7-month part exceeds 6 months). = (15 × 70,000 × 29) ÷ 26 = 3,04,50,000 ÷ 26 = ₹11,71,154. Option C (₹11,30,769) wrongly uses 28 years; D is the statutory cap (₹20 lakh), not the formula amount; B is a miscalculation. The 7-month part-year rounds up to a full year, so 29 years is used.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The amount of gratuity EXEMPT from tax for Mr. Iyer is the least of three figures. Given formula amount ≈ ₹11.71 lakh, statutory cap ₹20 lakh, and actual received ₹12 lakh, the exempt amount is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹11,71,154  D) ₹11,30,769\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For covered private employees, exemption = least of (i) statutory limit ₹20 lakh, (ii) formula amount = last drawn salary × 15/26 × years ≈ ₹11,71,154, and (iii) actual gratuity received ₹12 lakh. The least is the formula amount ≈ ₹11,71,154. So ₹11,71,154 is exempt and the small balance (₹12,00,000 − ₹11,71,154 = ₹28,846) is taxable. Option A is the cap (not the least); B is the actual received; D uses wrong year-count.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Mr. Iyer's EPF withdrawal at retirement (after 28 years of continuous service) is:\nA) Fully taxable at 30%  B) Taxable above ₹50,000  C) Fully tax-free  D) 10% TDS applies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — EPF withdrawn after completing 5 or more years of continuous service is fully tax-free in the year of receipt. Mr. Iyer has 28 years, far beyond 5, so the entire ₹45,00,000 is tax-free. Options A, B and D describe the pre-5-year withdrawal rules, which do not apply here. The 5-year rule is the key threshold for EPF withdrawal taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** From his ₹40,00,000 NPS corpus at age 60, Mr. Iyer takes the maximum tax-free lump sum and annuitises the rest. The annuity portion (minimum) is:\nA) ₹24,00,000  B) ₹32,00,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At normal exit (age 60), minimum 40% must be annuitised; maximum 60% is tax-free lump sum. Annuity = 40% × ₹40,00,000 = ₹16,00,000 (lump sum = ₹24,00,000, option A). C and B use wrong percentages. The lump sum (60%) is tax-free under Sec 10(12A), but the annuity income will be taxable as it is received.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** If Mr. Iyer's annuity provider quotes 6% p.a. on the ₹16,00,000 annuitised, his annual annuity income (taxable) is:\nA) ₹96,000  B) ₹1,60,000  C) ₹64,000  D) ₹1,28,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annuity income = annuitised amount × annuity rate = ₹16,00,000 × 6% = ₹96,000 per year. This is fully taxable in his hands as income. Option B applies 10%; C and D use wrong rates. The annuity counters longevity risk by paying for life, but unlike the lump sum it is taxed.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Capital Gains on Ms. Rao's Portfolio\n\nMs. Rao sold several investments during FY 2025-26. Details below. She has no other capital gains and wants to know her capital gains tax. Assume transfers on/after 23 July 2024 rules apply.\n\n| Asset | Bought | Sold | Cost | Sale value |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed equity shares A | Jan 2021 | Aug 2025 | ₹4,00,000 | ₹9,00,000 |\n| Listed equity shares B | Mar 2025 | Sept 2025 | ₹2,00,000 | ₹2,80,000 |\n| Equity mutual fund | Feb 2020 | Dec 2025 | ₹3,00,000 | ₹3,50,000 |\n| Residential plot | Jun 2018 | Nov 2025 | ₹20,00,000 | ₹50,00,000 |\n\n**Q51.** The capital gain on Listed equity shares A (held ~4.5 years) is classified and computed as:\nA) Short-term gain ₹5,00,000  B) Long-term gain ₹4,00,000  C) Long-term gain ₹5,00,000  D) Short-term gain ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity held more than 12 months is long-term. Gain = sale − cost = ₹9,00,000 − ₹4,00,000 = ₹5,00,000, a long-term capital gain (LTCG). Option A wrongly calls it short-term; B and D miscompute the gain. The holding period (Jan 2021 to Aug 2025 ≈ 4.5 years) clearly exceeds 12 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** The total LTCG from all equity (shares A + equity MF) before exemption is:\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹4,50,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹5,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equity MF gain = ₹3,50,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹50,000 (held since Feb 2020, long-term). Total equity LTCG = ₹5,00,000 (shares A) + ₹50,000 (MF) = ₹5,50,000. Option A omits the MF gain; C is only the MF gain; B is wrong. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption under Sec 112A applies to all equity LTCG combined.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** The LTCG tax (Sec 112A) on the combined equity LTCG of ₹5,50,000 is:\nA) ₹68,750  B) ₹53,125  C) ₹1,10,000  D) ₹56,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exempt first ₹1,25,000, tax the rest at 12.5%. Taxable LTCG = ₹5,50,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹4,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹4,25,000 = ₹53,125. Option A taxes the whole ₹5,50,000 at 12.5% (forgets exemption); C uses 20%; D is a guess. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption is per year across all equity LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Listed equity shares B (bought Mar 2025, sold Sept 2025, gain ₹80,000) are taxed how?\nA) STCG at 20% = ₹16,000  B) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹10,000  C) Exempt under ₹1.25 lakh  D) STCG at 15% = ₹12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Held ~6 months (≤12 months) → short-term. STCG under Sec 111A on listed equity is 20%: 20% × ₹80,000 = ₹16,000. There is no ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG (option C is wrong — that exemption is only for LTCG). Option B applies the long-term rate; D uses the old 15% rate (now 20%).\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The residential plot (held Jun 2018 to Nov 2025, ~7.4 years) produces a gain of ₹30,00,000. Its classification is:\nA) Short-term, taxed at slab  B) Long-term (held over 12 months only because of equity rules)  C) Short-term (held under 36 months)  D) Long-term (held over 24 months)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Immovable property is long-term if held more than 24 months. ~7.4 years far exceeds 24 months, so it is a long-term capital asset; gain = ₹50,00,000 − ₹20,00,000 = ₹30,00,000 (LTCG). Option B cites the 12-month rule (only for listed equity); A and C use wrong thresholds. Property uses the 24-month test, not 12 months.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — The Sharma Family's Insurance Claims\n\nThe Sharma family holds health and motor insurance. During the year they faced two events. Health policy details: Sum insured ₹5,00,000 with a 10% co-pay and a ₹25,000 compulsory deductible. They also hold a SECOND health policy of ₹3,00,000 with no co-pay/deductible. Their car policy has a ₹2,000 deductible.\n\n| Event | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Hospitalisation bill | ₹4,00,000 |\n| Car accident claim | ₹35,000 |\n| Health Policy 1 | SI ₹5,00,000, 10% co-pay, ₹25,000 deductible |\n| Health Policy 2 | SI ₹3,00,000, no co-pay/deductible |\n| Car policy deductible | ₹2,000 |\n\n**Q56.** If the ₹4,00,000 hospital claim is made ONLY on Health Policy 1, how much does the insurer pay (deductible first, then co-pay)?\nA) ₹3,60,000  B) ₹3,75,000  C) ₹3,37,500  D) ₹3,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Apply deductible, then co-pay. ₹4,00,000 − ₹25,000 deductible = ₹3,75,000; co-pay = 10% × ₹3,75,000 = ₹37,500; payout = ₹3,75,000 − ₹37,500 = ₹3,37,500. Option B stops after the deductible; D and A miscompute the co-pay base. The deductible is removed before the co-pay percentage is applied.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The Sharmas bear part of that ₹4,00,000 claim themselves. Their out-of-pocket cost (deductible + co-pay) on Policy 1 is:\nA) ₹62,500  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Out-of-pocket = deductible + co-pay = ₹25,000 + ₹37,500 = ₹62,500. (Check: ₹3,37,500 paid by insurer + ₹62,500 borne by insured = ₹4,00,000.) Option B is only the deductible; C is only the co-pay; D is a guess. Co-pay and deductible both shift cost to the insured, which is why such policies carry lower premiums.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Suppose instead the claim were ₹6,00,000 (exceeding Policy 1's ₹5,00,000 sum insured). The family wants to use both policies. Which principle lets the second insurer share the claim?\nA) Subrogation  B) Co-pay  C) Indemnity waiver  D) Contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — When a claim exceeds the sum insured of the chosen policy and a second policy covers the same risk, the contribution principle lets the insurers share the claim proportionately. Subrogation (A) is about recovering from a third party who caused the loss; B (co-pay) is the insured's own share; \"indemnity waiver\" (C) is not a real principle. The insured can choose which insurer to claim from first; contribution applies only to the excess above one policy's sum insured.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** On the car accident claim of ₹35,000 with a ₹2,000 deductible (no co-pay), the insurer pays:\nA) ₹35,000  B) ₹33,000  C) ₹2,000  D) ₹31,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Payout = claim − deductible = ₹35,000 − ₹2,000 = ₹33,000. Option A ignores the deductible; C is the deductible itself; D wrongly applies a co-pay that doesn't exist here. The compulsory motor deductible discourages tiny claims and lowers the premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** The concept of co-pay primarily helps to:\nA) Increase the premium for the same cover  B) Guarantee full reimbursement of every bill  C) Make the insured sensitive to claim size and lower the premium  D) Replace the need for a sum insured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Co-pay makes the insured bear part of every claim, so they become cost-conscious; in return the premium is lower (higher co-pay → lower premium). Option A is the opposite. B is false — co-pay means the insured never gets 100% reimbursed. D is nonsense; the sum insured still caps the cover. The workbook notes co-pay is unpopular because patients have little control over hospital charges.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Mr. and Mrs. Kapoor: Income Tax & Retirement Income\n\nMr. Kapoor is a salaried private employee aged 58 in FY 2025-26 under the NEW tax regime. His details below. He wants to estimate his tax and plan retirement income. (New regime FY 2025-26 slabs: up to ₹4L nil; ₹4–8L 5%; ₹8–12L 10%; ₹12–16L 15%; ₹16–20L 20%; ₹20–24L 25%; above ₹24L 30%. Standard deduction ₹75,000. Rebate makes tax nil up to ₹12L total income.)\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary | ₹18,75,000 |\n| Standard deduction (new regime) | ₹75,000 |\n| Employer NPS contribution (within 14% limit) under 80CCD(2) | ₹1,40,000 |\n\n**Q61.** Mr. Kapoor's taxable income after the standard deduction and the 80CCD(2) employer-NPS deduction is:\nA) ₹18,75,000  B) ₹17,00,000  C) ₹18,00,000  D) ₹16,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the new regime, two deductions are available: standard deduction ₹75,000 and employer NPS under 80CCD(2). Taxable income = ₹18,75,000 − ₹75,000 − ₹1,40,000 = ₹16,60,000. Option C subtracts only the standard deduction; B is a guess; A subtracts nothing. (80C and 80CCD(1B) are NOT available in the new regime, but employer 80CCD(2) is.)\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Using the new-regime slabs on taxable income of ₹16,60,000, the tax before cess (no rebate, as income exceeds ₹12L) is:\nA) ₹1,57,000  B) ₹1,32,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Build the tax band by band: ₹0–4L nil; ₹4–8L at 5% = ₹20,000; ₹8–12L at 10% = ₹40,000; ₹12–16L at 15% = ₹60,000; the final ₹16L–16.6L slice of ₹60,000 at 20% = ₹12,000. Total = 20,000 + 40,000 + 60,000 + 12,000 = ₹1,32,000. Options A, C and D miscount; only the income within each band is taxed at that band's rate. Income above ₹12L means the rebate does not apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Add 4% health & education cess to the slab tax of ₹1,32,000. Total tax payable is:\nA) ₹1,37,280  B) ₹1,32,000  C) ₹1,40,000  D) ₹1,35,300\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cess = 4% × ₹1,32,000 = ₹5,280. Total = ₹1,32,000 + ₹5,280 = ₹1,37,280. Option B omits the cess; C and D miscalculate it. Health & education cess of 4% is always added to the income tax after any rebate.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** When Mr. Kapoor retires and opens an SCSS account, the maximum he can deposit and the 80C benefit (if he were in the old regime) are:\nA) ₹15 lakh max; ₹1 lakh 80C  B) ₹30 lakh max; fully exempt interest  C) ₹9 lakh max; ₹1.5 lakh 80C  D) ₹30 lakh max; ₹1.5 lakh 80C\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — SCSS maximum deposit is ₹30 lakh; the deposit qualifies for an 80C deduction up to ₹1.5 lakh. Option A uses the old ₹15 lakh cap and wrong 80C limit; C uses the POMIS cap. Option B is wrong because SCSS interest is FULLY TAXABLE (TDS if annual interest exceeds ₹50,000) — it is not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** If Mr. Kapoor deposits the SCSS maximum of ₹30 lakh at 8.2% p.a., his ANNUAL interest (fully taxable) is:\nA) ₹2,46,000  B) ₹61,500  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,23,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual interest = 8.2% × ₹30,00,000 = ₹2,46,000. (Paid quarterly, that is ₹61,500 per quarter — option B is the quarterly figure, not annual.) C and D understate. Because the interest exceeds ₹50,000, TDS applies, and the full ₹2,46,000 is added to his taxable income.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Plan for the Menon Household (Retirement + Investments + Insurance)\n\nMr. Menon, 50, plans to retire at 60. He has the following. He wants an integrated review across retirement corpus, EPF, an equity sale, and insurance. Inflation 6%; pre-retirement return 10%.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current monthly expense | ₹80,000 |\n| Years to retirement | 10 |\n| EPF balance | ₹30,00,000 |\n| Monthly basic + DA | ₹50,000 |\n| Listed equity sale gain (held 5 years) this year | ₹4,25,000 |\n| Existing SIP | ₹25,000/month at 10% for 10 years |\n\n**Q66.** Mr. Menon's monthly expense at retirement (in 10 years) at 6% inflation is approximately:\nA) ₹1,43,272  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹1,79,085  D) ₹96,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Future expense = 80,000 × (1.06)^10. (1.06)^10 ≈ 1.7908, so 80,000 × 1.7908 ≈ ₹1,43,266 ≈ ₹1,43,272. Option C uses the ₹1 lakh base figure from a different example; B just adds a flat amount; D is below today's expense. Ten years of 6% inflation multiplies the cost by about 1.79×.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** His monthly EPF contribution (employee 12% of ₹50,000 basic + DA) is:\nA) ₹6,000  B) ₹4,165  C) ₹5,000  D) ₹3,667\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Employee EPF = 12% × ₹50,000 = ₹6,000, all of which goes to his EPF account. Option D (₹3,667) is 3.67% of ₹50,000 (the employer's EPF-slice rate, and even that is computed differently because of the EPS cap); B and C are wrong. The employer separately adds 12% too, split between EPF and EPS.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Of the employer's matching 12% (₹6,000), how much goes to EPS, given the ₹15,000 EPS wage ceiling?\nA) ₹6,000  B) ₹4,165  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹4,167\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — EPS = 8.33% of the capped wage of ₹15,000 = ₹1,249.50 ≈ ₹1,250 (NOT 8.33% of ₹50,000). Option D (₹4,167) ignores the ceiling; A is the full employer 12%; B is wrong. The remaining ₹6,000 − ₹1,250 = ₹4,750 of the employer's contribution goes to Mr. Menon's EPF account.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Mr. Menon's listed-equity LTCG of ₹4,25,000 (held 5 years) attracts tax of:\nA) ₹53,125  B) ₹85,000  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG on listed equity: exempt first ₹1,25,000, then 12.5% on the rest. Taxable = ₹4,25,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹3,00,000; tax = 12.5% × ₹3,00,000 = ₹37,500. Option A taxes the whole gain at 12.5% (forgets exemption); B uses the 20% STCG rate; D is a guess. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption is per financial year across all equity LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** His existing SIP of ₹25,000/month at 10% for 10 years (i ≈ 0.8333%/month, n = 120, FV factor ≈ 206.55) will grow to approximately:\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹41,00,000  D) ₹51,63,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV = P × [((1 + i)^n − 1) ÷ i] = 25,000 × 206.55 ≈ ₹51,63,750. (Factor: (1.0083333)^120 ≈ 2.7138; (2.7138 − 1) ÷ 0.0083333 ≈ 205.66 to 206.55 depending on rounding, giving roughly ₹51–52 lakh.) Option A is just the contributions (25,000 × 120 = ₹30 lakh) with no growth; C and B are off. This SIP adds about ₹51.6 lakh to his retirement corpus, on top of EPF.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "03", "md": "# Mock Paper 3 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy, with a strong focus on insurance computations (HLV, needs-based cover, term vs endowment vs ULIP, health-claim mechanics, motor/property claims). Keep a calculator or spreadsheet handy and work each calculation step by step before locking in your answer.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** A 30-year-old earns ₹12,00,000 p.a. (net of his own expenses, all available to dependents). His income is assumed to grow at 6% p.a. and the post-tax return on the sum assured is 8% p.a. He has 30 years to retirement. Using the simple HLV thumb rule (discount at the inflation-adjusted rate, payments at the beginning of each year), the discounting rate to use is closest to:\nA) 2.00%  B) 6.00%  C) 14.00%  D) 1.89%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The HLV income-replacement method discounts future income at the rate that nets out investment return against income growth. Formula: adjusted rate = ((1 + return)/(1 + growth)) − 1 = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 1.0188… − 1 = 0.0189 = **1.89%**. A) 2.00% is the crude \"8% − 6%\" subtraction, which is wrong because returns and growth compound, not subtract. C) 14.00% wrongly adds the two rates. B) 6.00% just uses inflation. The 1.89% figure matches the workbook's needs-based example.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Using the data in Q1 (annual income ₹12,00,000, adjusted rate 1.89%, 30 years, beginning-of-year payments), the Human Life Value (present value of income) is closest to:\nA) ₹3,60,00,000  B) ₹1,80,00,000  C) ₹2,68,00,000  D) ₹2,73,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — HLV = PV of an annuity-due: PV = PMT × [(1 − (1+r)^−n)/r] × (1+r), with PMT = ₹12,00,000, r = 1.89%, n = 30. The annuity factor (1 − 1.0189^−30)/0.0189 ≈ 22.34; × (1.0189) ≈ 22.76. PV ≈ 12,00,000 × 22.76 ≈ **₹2,73,00,000**. A) ₹3.6 cr is simply income × 30 (ignores discounting). C) ₹2.68 cr forgets the annuity-due (beginning-of-year) adjustment. B) ₹1.8 cr uses only 15 years. So roughly ₹2.73 crore.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Anil's needs-based calculation: income to replace ₹9,96,000 p.a., adjusted rate 1.89%, period 31 years (payments at beginning of year) gives a corpus of ₹2,36,43,984. He has a loan outstanding of ₹40,00,000, existing life cover of ₹1,00,00,000 and investments of ₹50,00,000. Additional insurance cover required is:\nA) ₹2,76,43,984  B) ₹1,86,43,984  C) ₹1,26,43,984  D) ₹2,36,43,984\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Needs-based steps: Total need = corpus + loan = 2,36,43,984 + 40,00,000 = ₹2,76,43,984. Then deduct existing cover (₹1 cr) and investments (₹50 lakh): 2,76,43,984 − 1,00,00,000 − 50,00,000 = **₹1,26,43,984**. A) ₹2.76 cr forgets to deduct existing cover/investments. B) ₹1.86 cr deducts only the ₹1 cr cover. D) ₹2.36 cr is just the corpus before adding the loan. This is the exact workbook answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A 30-year-old's HLV is calculated assuming income grows at exactly the same rate as the post-tax return (both 8%). His current income available to dependents is ₹10,00,000 and 27 years remain to retirement. The HLV equals:\nA) ₹1,80,00,000  B) ₹2,16,00,000  C) ₹3,00,00,000  D) ₹2,70,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — When income growth = investment return, the discounting rate = (1.08/1.08) − 1 = 0. With a zero discount rate, present value = income × number of years = 10,00,000 × 27 = **₹2,70,00,000**. This is the workbook's \"Question 1\" thumb rule: HLV = current income × years to retirement. A) ₹1.8 cr uses 18 years. C) ₹3 cr uses 30 years. B) ₹2.16 cr uses 21.6. So ₹2.70 crore.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A pure term plan of ₹1 crore for 30 years costs ₹9,416 p.a. The same insurer's \"return of premium\" term plan costs ₹17,473 p.a. and returns ₹5,24,190 at maturity. The implied return on the EXTRA premium (₹17,473 − ₹9,416 = ₹8,057), invested for 30 years, is closest to:\nA) 4.61%  B) 8.00%  C) 2.50%  D) 11.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Treat the extra premium of ₹8,057 p.a. (beginning of year) as an investment whose future value after 30 years is ₹5,24,190. Solving the RATE function (Nper 30, PMT 8,057, FV −5,24,190, type 1) gives **4.61% p.a.** Quick check: 30 × 8,057 = ₹2,41,710 contributed; growing to ₹5,24,190 implies a low single-digit compound return. B) 8% and D) 11% are far too high for that maturity value. C) 2.50% is too low. This is the workbook's exact figure: a poor 4.61% \"investment\" return on the extra cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** An endowment plan for ₹1 crore over 30 years costs ₹3,16,332 p.a.; the pure term plan costs ₹9,416 p.a. The extra premium of ₹3,06,916 p.a. produces a maturity value of ₹2,14,00,000. The inherent investment return on the savings portion is approximately:\nA) 8%  B) 12%  C) 5%  D) 3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Extra premium = 3,16,332 − 9,416 = ₹3,06,916 p.a. invested 30 years (beginning of year) to yield ₹2,14,00,000. RATE function gives ≈ **5% p.a.** The workbook notes inherent returns on such investment-cum-insurance plans are typically only 3–5% and not guaranteed. A) 8% and B) 12% overstate it; D) 3% is at the low end of the range but the calculation here resolves to 5%. The lesson: endowment \"investment\" returns are low versus buying term + investing the difference.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A health claim is assessed at ₹2,00,000. The policy has a co-pay of 15%. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹1,85,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Co-pay is the share the insured bears. Insured pays 15% × 2,00,000 = ₹30,000; the insurer pays the balance = 2,00,000 − 30,000 = **₹1,70,000**. A) ₹30,000 is the insured's share, not the insurer's. C) ₹2,00,000 ignores the co-pay entirely. B) ₹1,85,000 wrongly applies a flat ₹15,000. Matches the workbook example exactly.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A health policy has a compulsory deductible of ₹30,000. The hospital bill is ₹80,000. The insurer pays:\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A deductible is the first slice the insured pays before the claim becomes admissible. Insurer pays bill − deductible = 80,000 − 30,000 = **₹50,000**; the insured bears ₹30,000. A) ₹80,000 ignores the deductible. B) ₹30,000 is the insured's portion. D) ₹24,000 confuses this with a ₹1,000-deductible motor example. This matches the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Policy 1 has a deductible of ₹1,000 AND a co-pay of 10%. The ascertained claim is ₹40,000. How much does Policy 1 pay?\nA) ₹35,000  B) ₹36,000  C) ₹35,100  D) ₹39,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Apply the deductible first, then the co-pay on the remainder. Step 1: 40,000 − 1,000 (deductible) = ₹39,000. Step 2: co-pay = 10% × 39,000 = ₹3,900, so insurer pays 39,000 − 3,900 = **₹35,100**. A) ₹35,000 is the answer to a different policy (₹5,000 deductible, no co-pay). B) ₹36,000 applies only the 10% co-pay on the full ₹40,000. D) ₹39,000 forgets the co-pay. Exact workbook figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Policy 2 has only a deductible of ₹5,000 and no co-pay. On the same ₹40,000 claim, the insurer pays:\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹36,000  C) ₹35,100  D) ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — With only a deductible and no co-pay, insurer pays 40,000 − 5,000 = **₹35,000**. A) ₹40,000 ignores the deductible. C) ₹35,100 is Policy 1's answer. B) ₹36,000 wrongly applies a 10% co-pay. The comparison shows deductible-only Policy 2 (₹35,000) pays slightly less than Policy 1 (₹35,100) on this claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Two indemnity flood policies of ₹5,00,000 each (Company A and Company B) cover the same property. The ascertained loss is ₹30,000 and Company A settles it. Under the contribution principle, Company B's proportionate share is:\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹5,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Contribution shares the claim between insurers in proportion to their sums insured. Both have ₹5 lakh cover, so the split is 50:50. Company B contributes ½ × 30,000 = **₹15,000**. A) ₹30,000 would mean B pays everything. C) ₹5,000 uses a wrong ratio. B) ₹0 ignores contribution. This is the workbook's property example.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Base health plan ₹5 lakh + Super Top-up of ₹10 lakh with a ₹5 lakh deductible (threshold applied on the year's TOTAL claims). A single hospitalisation claim of ₹8 lakh occurs. Total amount paid across both policies is:\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Base plan pays its full ₹5 lakh. The super top-up's ₹5 lakh threshold is met by that ₹5 lakh, so the super top-up pays the remaining 8,00,000 − 5,00,000 = ₹3,00,000. Total = 5,00,000 + 3,00,000 = **₹8,00,000** — the entire claim. A) ₹3 lakh is only the top-up portion. B) ₹5 lakh is only the base. D) ₹10 lakh exceeds the claim. Matches the workbook.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** With a Base plan of ₹5 lakh and a TOP-UP plan of ₹10 lakh (threshold ₹5 lakh applied PER CLAIM), the insured has these claims in a year: ₹3 lakh, then ₹6 lakh, then ₹2 lakh, then ₹4 lakh. Total amount the two policies pay over the year is:\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹11,00,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Top-up applies its ₹5 lakh threshold to EACH claim. Claim 1 (₹3L): base pays ₹3L (base left ₹2L). Claim 2 (₹6L): base pays its last ₹2L; top-up pays 6−5 = ₹1L (only the excess over the per-claim ₹5L). Claim 3 (₹2L): base empty, ₹2L < ₹5L threshold → top-up pays ₹0. Claim 4 (₹4L): ₹4L < ₹5L threshold → top-up pays ₹0. Total = 3 + 2 + 1 = **₹6,00,000**. A) ₹15L is what a SUPER top-up would pay (aggregate threshold). The per-claim threshold cripples a plain top-up here.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Same four claims (₹3L, ₹6L, ₹2L, ₹4L) but now with a Base ₹5 lakh + SUPER Top-up ₹10 lakh (₹5 lakh threshold on the year's AGGREGATE). Total paid by both policies is:\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹11,00,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Super top-up's ₹5 lakh threshold is applied once to the running total. Base pays its ₹5 lakh on the early claims; once the aggregate ₹5 lakh threshold is crossed, the super top-up pays everything above it. Total claims = 3+6+2+4 = ₹15 lakh; base ₹5L + super top-up ₹10L = **₹15,00,000** (fully covered, super top-up exhausted). A) ₹6L is the plain top-up answer. The contrast is the whole point: super top-up covers the full ₹15 lakh, plain top-up only ₹6 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A car (engine capacity up to 1600 CC) suffers own-damage assessed at ₹25,000. The policy carries the compulsory deductible. The insurer pays:\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹1,000  D) ₹24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The compulsory deductible for cars up to 1600 CC is ₹1,000. Insurer pays 25,000 − 1,000 = **₹24,000**, insured bears ₹1,000. A) ₹25,000 ignores the deductible. C) ₹1,000 is the deductible, not the payout. B) ₹20,000 uses a wrong deductible. Exact workbook figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** IRDAI rule: a life policy with a term of MORE than 10 years for an individual below 45 must provide a minimum sum assured of how many times the annual premium?\nA) 5 times  B) 7 times  C) 10 times  D) 15 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For terms over 10 years, the minimum sum assured is **10 times** the annual premium for individuals below 45 (and 7 times if above 45). A) 5 times applies only when the term is LESS than 10 years. B) 7 times is for above-45 with term over 10 years. D) 15 times is not a rule. This 10× threshold also governs tax-free maturity for many life policies.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A 25-year endowment policy has sum assured ₹10,00,000 with half-yearly premiums (50 premiums in total). After paying 10 premiums (5 years), it is made paid-up. The reduced (paid-up) sum assured is:\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Paid-up SA = original SA × (premiums paid ÷ total premiums due) = 10,00,000 × (10/50) = **₹2,00,000**. A) ₹5,00,000 uses 5/10 of years incorrectly. C) ₹4,00,000 uses a wrong ratio. B) ₹1,00,000 halves it again. Exact workbook (Surinder) example.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A ULIP qualifies for tax-free maturity only if the sum assured is at least 10× the annual premium. For an annual premium of ₹3,13,200, the minimum sum assured needed is:\nA) ₹31,32,000  B) ₹3,13,200  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Minimum SA = 10 × annual premium = 10 × 3,13,200 = **₹31,32,000**. So a ₹1 crore cover comfortably exceeds this and maturity would be exempt under the 10× rule (for pre-1 Feb 2021 policies). A) is the threshold. B) is just the premium. C) ₹1 cr is the actual cover, larger than the minimum required. D) ₹2.5 lakh is the separate ULIP premium ceiling above which ULIP gains become taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Under the Finance Act 2021, ULIP maturity proceeds are taxed as capital gains if the annual premium exceeds:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For ULIPs issued on/after 1 Feb 2021, if annual premium exceeds **₹2,50,000**, maturity proceeds are treated as capital gains and taxed. A) ₹1.5 lakh is the 80C limit. C) ₹5 lakh is the threshold for taxing traditional (non-ULIP) policy maturity as \"income from other sources\" for policies issued on/after 1 Apr 2023. B) ₹1 lakh is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Listed equity shares are sold for ₹6,00,000 after holding 18 months; cost was ₹4,00,000 (STT paid). This is the only equity gain in the year. The tax under Section 112A is:\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹9,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Held > 12 months → long-term. LTCG = 6,00,000 − 4,00,000 = ₹2,00,000. Section 112A exempts the first ₹1,25,000, then taxes the excess at 12.5%. Taxable = 2,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹75,000; tax = 12.5% × 75,000 = **₹9,375** (plus cess). A) ₹25,000 forgets the exemption (12.5% × 2,00,000). C) ₹12,500 uses 10% of the gain incorrectly. B) ₹40,000 applies 20% (the STCG rate).\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Listed equity shares are sold for ₹3,00,000 after holding only 8 months; cost ₹2,00,000 (STT paid). The short-term capital gains tax under Section 111A is:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Held < 12 months → short-term. STCG = 3,00,000 − 2,00,000 = ₹1,00,000. Section 111A rate = 20%. Tax = 20% × 1,00,000 = **₹20,000** (plus cess). There is NO ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG. A) ₹10,000 wrongly uses 10%. C) ₹12,500 uses 12.5% (the LTCG rate). B) ₹15,000 uses 15% (the old STCG rate, now replaced by 20%).\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An employee covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act has last drawn basic+DA of ₹60,000 and 20 years 7 months of service. Gratuity payable (per the Act's formula) is closest to:\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹9,00,000  C) ₹6,92,000  D) ₹7,26,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Formula (covered under Act): (15 × last drawn salary × years)/26. Service over 6 months in the final year rounds up, so 21 years. = (15 × 60,000 × 21)/26 = 18,90,00,000/26 ≈ **₹7,26,923 ≈ ₹7.26 lakh**. A) ₹6,00,000 is the result if NOT covered by the Act (divide by 30, no round-up: 15×60,000×20/30). C) ₹6.92 lakh uses 20 years with /26. B) ₹9 lakh ignores the /26 factor. Workbook example.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Same employee (₹60,000, 20 years 7 months) but the organisation is NOT covered under the Gratuity Act. Gratuity is:\nA) ₹7,26,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹6,30,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For employees NOT covered, use (15 × salary × completed years)/30, and partial years are NOT rounded up — so 20 years. = (15 × 60,000 × 20)/30 = 1,80,00,000/30 = **₹6,00,000**. A) ₹7.26 lakh is the covered-employee answer (/26, 21 years). C) ₹6.30 lakh uses 21 years. B) ₹5 lakh is arbitrary. Note: not-covered employees get a lower payout because of /30 and no rounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An NPS subscriber retires at 60 with a corpus of ₹50,00,000. He uses the minimum mandatory annuity portion and withdraws the rest as a lump sum. The lump sum he can withdraw is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At 60, a minimum of 40% of the NPS corpus must buy an annuity; up to 60% can be taken as a lump sum. Lump sum = 60% × 50,00,000 = **₹30,00,000**, with ₹20,00,000 going to the annuity. A) ₹20 lakh is the annuity portion (40%). C) ₹50 lakh would be the full corpus (only allowed if corpus is small enough for full withdrawal). B) ₹25 lakh uses 50%. Workbook rule: 60% lump sum / 40% annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An employee with basic+DA of ₹40,000 per month contributes 12% to EPF; the employer also contributes 12%, split as 8.33% to EPS (capped on a wage of ₹15,000) and the balance to EPF. The monthly EPS contribution is:\nA) ₹3,332  B) ₹4,800  C) ₹1,800  D) ₹1,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The EPS (pension) share is 8.33% but only on the statutory wage ceiling of ₹15,000. EPS = 8.33% × 15,000 = ₹1,249.5 ≈ **₹1,250**. A) ₹3,332 wrongly applies 8.33% on the full ₹40,000. C) ₹1,800 is the employee's-vs-cap confusion. B) ₹4,800 is 12% of ₹40,000 (the employee's own contribution). The EPS ceiling is the key trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Real (inflation-adjusted) return when the nominal return is 10% and inflation is 6%:\nA) 4.00%  B) 3.77%  C) 16.00%  D) 1.67%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) − 1 = (1.10/1.06) − 1 = 1.03774 − 1 = **3.77%**. A) 4.00% is the crude subtraction 10% − 6%, which overstates the real return because the two compound. C) 16% adds them. D) 1.67% is unrelated. Always use the Fisher relationship, not subtraction.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An investment grows from ₹2,00,000 to ₹3,38,000 over 4 years. The CAGR is closest to:\nA) 17.25%  B) 14.04%  C) 13.92%  D) 69.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (End/Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (3,38,000/2,00,000)^(1/4) − 1 = (1.69)^0.25 − 1. 1.69^0.5 = 1.30; 1.30^0.5 = 1.1402, so CAGR ≈ **14.04%**. A) 17.25% is the simple average (69%/4). C) 13.92% is a slight miscalc. D) 69% is the total return, not annualised. CAGR smooths growth across years compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A goal costs ₹5,00,000 today. With education inflation of 8% p.a., its cost in 6 years (future value) is closest to:\nA) ₹7,40,000  B) ₹7,93,000  C) ₹6,40,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = PV × (1 + inflation)^n = 5,00,000 × 1.08^6. 1.08^6 ≈ 1.5869, so FV ≈ 5,00,000 × 1.5869 = **₹7,93,000**. A) ₹7.4 lakh undercounts compounding. C) ₹6.4 lakh uses ~5% simple growth. D) ₹9 lakh overstates. Inflation compounds, so use the power, not simple interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A property (a residential plot) is sold after holding 30 months. For an immovable property to qualify as a long-term capital asset, the minimum holding period is:\nA) 12 months  B) 24 months  C) 36 months  D) 48 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Immovable property and unlisted securities are long-term if held more than **24 months**. (Listed equity/securities use 12 months.) Since 30 > 24 months, this sale is long-term. A) 12 months is for listed equity. C) 36 months is the old pre-amendment property period. D) 48 months is not a rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Under the new tax regime (FY 2025-26), a salaried individual gets a standard deduction of:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹75,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The new regime standard deduction for salaried individuals is **₹75,000** (FY 2025-26). A) ₹50,000 is the old-regime standard deduction. C) ₹1,50,000 is the 80C limit. D) ₹40,000 was the pre-2019 figure. So gross salary is reduced by ₹75,000 before applying the new-regime slabs.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Under the new tax regime FY 2025-26 slabs (Nil up to ₹4,00,000; 5% ₹4–8 lakh; 10% ₹8–12 lakh; …), the income tax (before cess and rebate) on a net taxable income of ₹10,00,000 is:\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Build the tax slab by slab: ₹0–4L → nil; ₹4–8L (₹4 lakh) @ 5% = ₹20,000; ₹8–10L (₹2 lakh) @ 10% = ₹20,000. Total = 20,000 + 20,000 = **₹40,000**. A) ₹60,000 over-taxes (treats more income at 10% or ignores the nil slab). C) ₹52,000 and D) ₹50,000 are miscalculations. Note: the ₹10 lakh stops part-way into the 10% band, so only ₹2 lakh is taxed at 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Health and education cess is levied at what rate on (income tax + surcharge)?\nA) 2%  B) 4%  C) 3%  D) 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Health and education cess is **4%** of (income tax + applicable surcharge). A) 2% and C) 3% were older cess rates (education cess + SHEB). D) 5% is incorrect. So on a tax of ₹40,000 (no surcharge), cess = 4% × 40,000 = ₹1,600, making total ₹41,600.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A single-premium traditional policy is surrendered in year 2. The guaranteed surrender value is at least what percentage of total premium paid (within the first three years)?\nA) 30%  B) 70%  C) 50%  D) 90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For single-premium policies, the guaranteed surrender value ranges from **70%** of total premium (if surrendered within the first three years) up to 90% in the last two years. A) 30% is the minimum for regular-premium (non-single) policies. C) 50% is not the rule. D) 90% applies only in the last two years. So a single-premium surrender in year 2 returns at least 70%.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A ULIP is surrendered before completing 5 years. The maximum discontinuance charge that may be levied is:\nA) ₹3,000  B) ₹6,000  C) ₹10,000  D) ₹1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On surrendering a ULIP within the 5-year lock-in, a discontinuance charge of a maximum of **₹6,000** applies; the balance moves to a discontinuance fund and is paid after the lock-in. A) ₹3,000 and D) ₹1,000 understate the cap. C) ₹10,000 overstates it. After 5 years there are no surrender charges at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A health insurance policy becomes non-contestable (except for proven fraud / permanent exclusions) after a continuous moratorium period of:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 4 years  D) 8 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The health insurance moratorium is **5 continuous years** of premiums without break; after that, no claim is contestable except for proven fraud or specified permanent exclusions. A) 3 years is the life-insurance Section 45 period. C) 4 years is a common pre-existing-disease waiting period, not the moratorium. D) 8 years is incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A life insurance policy cannot be called into question on ANY ground (except fraud during the period) after how many years from the policy/revival date, under Section 45 of the Insurance Act?\nA) 2 years  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 45 bars repudiation on any ground after **3 years** from issue/risk commencement/revival (whichever is later). Within those 3 years a claim can be denied on grounds of fraud. A) 2 years and C) 5 years are wrong. D) 10 years is unrelated. (Health policies separately use the 5-year moratorium.)\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An NRI/resident can use the Liberalised Remittance Scheme to buy foreign insurance up to what annual limit?\nA) USD 1,00,000  B) USD 2,50,000  C) USD 2,00,000  D) USD 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The LRS limit is **USD 2,50,000 per annum**, which can be used to buy foreign-currency insurance policies. A), C) and D) are not the LRS figure. Note such foreign policies are governed by the foreign country's regulations, so caution is advised.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A 31-year endowment-style corpus must provide ₹83,000 per month. Converting to an annual figure for the PV computation gives:\nA) ₹9,96,000  B) ₹83,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹9,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual income = monthly × 12 = 83,000 × 12 = **₹9,96,000**. This is the PMT used in Anil's needs-based PV. B) ₹83,000 is monthly. C) ₹10,00,000 rounds incorrectly. D) ₹9,60,000 uses ₹80,000/month. The monthly net income (₹83,000) came from ₹1,50,000 − (₹10,000 personal + ₹32,000 EMI + ₹25,000 premium).\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A whole-life / investment policy acquires a surrender value only after premiums have been paid for at least how many years (for a policy of term 10 years or more)?\nA) 1 year  B) 2 years  C) 3 years  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A traditional investment-cum-insurance policy (term ≥ 10 years) acquires a surrender/cash value after at least **3 years** of full premiums (2 years if term < 10 years). A) 1 year — too early. B) 2 years is only for shorter (<10 year) policies. D) 5 years is the ULIP lock-in, not the surrender-value trigger for traditional policies.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A traditional (non-ULIP) life policy issued after 1 April 2023 loses its tax-exempt maturity (becomes taxable as \"income from other sources\") if the annual premium exceeds:\nA) ₹2,50,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For traditional policies issued on/after 1 Apr 2023, if the annual premium exceeds **₹5,00,000**, the maturity proceeds are taxable. A) ₹2.5 lakh is the ULIP threshold (different product). B) ₹1.5 lakh is the 80C cap. D) ₹10 lakh is incorrect. Different rules apply to ULIPs (₹2.5 lakh) versus traditional plans (₹5 lakh).\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Rohan's Human Life Value & Needs-Based Cover (Insurance)\n\nRohan is 35 and earns a net income available to dependents of ₹15,00,000 p.a. He expects income to grow at 6% p.a.; the post-tax return on any sum assured is 8% p.a. He plans to retire at 60 (25 years of working life left). He has an outstanding home loan of ₹50,00,000, existing life cover of ₹1,00,00,000, and investments worth ₹40,00,000. His personal residence (₹80,00,000) is NOT to be sold. Use beginning-of-year annuity timing.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Net income to dependents | ₹15,00,000 p.a. |\n| Income growth | 6% |\n| Post-tax return | 8% |\n| Years to retirement | 25 |\n| Home loan outstanding | ₹50,00,000 |\n| Existing life cover | ₹1,00,00,000 |\n| Investments | ₹40,00,000 |\n\n**Q41.** The inflation-adjusted discount rate Rohan should use is:\nA) 1.89%  B) 1.96%  C) 14.00%  D) 2.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Adjusted rate = (1.08/1.06) − 1 = 0.018867 = **1.89%** (to 2 decimals, as the workbook does). D) 2.00% is the crude 8−6 subtraction. C) 14.00% adds the rates. B) 1.96% is a miscalculation. The same 1.89% rate appears throughout the workbook's HLV examples.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** The present value (corpus) of Rohan's income stream over 25 years, at 1.89%, beginning-of-year, is closest to:\nA) ₹3,75,00,000  B) ₹3,05,00,000  C) ₹2,99,00,000  D) ₹2,80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Annuity-due PV = PMT × [(1 − (1+r)^−n)/r] × (1+r). With PMT = 15,00,000, r = 0.0189, n = 25: factor (1 − 1.0189^−25)/0.0189 ≈ 19.57; × 1.0189 ≈ 19.94. PV ≈ 15,00,000 × 19.94 ≈ **₹2,99,00,000**. A) ₹3.75 cr is income × 25 (no discounting). B) ₹3.05 cr forgets the due adjustment is already included / slight over. D) ₹2.80 cr understates the factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Adding the home loan to the corpus, the total funds required (before deductions) are closest to:\nA) ₹3,49,00,000  B) ₹3,29,00,000  C) ₹3,79,00,000  D) ₹2,99,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total need = income-replacement corpus + loan outstanding = 2,99,00,000 + 50,00,000 = **₹3,49,00,000**. D) ₹2.99 cr forgets the loan. C) ₹3.79 cr wrongly adds the residence value too (the residence is NOT to be sold, so it is excluded from assets and need not be liquidated). B) ₹3.29 cr uses a ₹30 lakh loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Deducting existing cover and investments, Rohan's ADDITIONAL insurance requirement is closest to:\nA) ₹2,09,00,000  B) ₹2,49,00,000  C) ₹1,29,00,000  D) ₹3,49,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Additional cover = total need − existing cover − investments = 3,49,00,000 − 1,00,00,000 − 40,00,000 = **₹2,09,00,000**. B) ₹2.49 cr deducts only the existing cover. C) ₹1.29 cr also wrongly subtracts the ₹80 lakh residence (which is not an available asset). D) ₹3.49 cr forgets all deductions. The residence is excluded because the family will keep living in it.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Rohan buys ₹2 crore of term cover by taking 2 policies of ₹1 crore each from one insurer rather than a single ₹2 crore policy. The MAIN benefit of splitting is:\nA) He can give up one policy later as his insurance need falls  B) It avoids the contribution clause on death claims  C) It guarantees a higher claim settlement ratio  D) Lower total premium because premiums are not telescopic\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Splitting a large cover into multiple policies lets the insured **surrender individual policies as future insurance needs reduce** (e.g., loan repaid, goals met). D) is wrong — life premiums ARE telescopic (larger SA = lower rate per ₹1,000), so splitting does not lower premium. C) the claim ratio is the insurer's, unaffected by splitting. B) contribution does not apply to defined-benefit life policies anyway. The flexibility to drop cover is the workbook's stated rationale.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — The Mehta Family Health Claims (Insurance)\n\nThe Mehtas hold a family floater base health policy of ₹5,00,000 with a co-pay of 10%, plus a Super Top-up of ₹15,00,000 with a deductible (aggregate threshold) of ₹5,00,000. Mr. Mehta also has an employer group policy of ₹3,00,000 with no co-pay. During the year, two hospitalisations occur (figures are the admissible amounts before any co-pay/deductible):\n\n| Event | Admissible amount |\n|---|---|\n| Hospitalisation 1 (Mrs. Mehta) | ₹4,00,000 |\n| Hospitalisation 2 (Mr. Mehta) | ₹9,00,000 |\n\n**Q46.** For Hospitalisation 1 (₹4,00,000) claimed under the base floater (₹5 lakh, 10% co-pay), how much does the base insurer pay?\nA) ₹3,60,000  B) ₹3,50,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Co-pay 10% means the insured bears 10% × 4,00,000 = ₹40,000; insurer pays 4,00,000 − 40,000 = **₹3,60,000**. The claim (₹4 lakh) is within the ₹5 lakh base, so the super top-up isn't triggered (aggregate ₹5 lakh threshold not crossed). D) ₹4 lakh ignores the co-pay. C) ₹40,000 is the insured's share. B) ₹3.5 lakh uses a wrong ₹50,000 co-pay.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** After Hospitalisation 1, how much of the base floater's ₹5,00,000 sum insured remains?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,40,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The base sum insured is consumed by the admissible amount applied to it (₹4,00,000), regardless of how the co-pay is split. Remaining = 5,00,000 − 4,00,000 = **₹1,00,000**. (The co-pay is who pays, not how much of the sum insured is used.) B) ₹1.4 lakh wrongly adds back the ₹40,000 co-pay. C) ₹0 over-consumes. D) ₹5 lakh ignores the claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Hospitalisation 2 is ₹9,00,000. The base floater has ₹1,00,000 left (10% co-pay still applies on amounts it pays). After exhausting the base, how much remains for the super top-up to consider, and is its ₹5 lakh aggregate threshold now met?\nA) ₹8,00,000 remains; threshold met because aggregate claims now ₹13 lakh > ₹5 lakh  B) ₹9,00,000 remains; threshold not met  C) ₹4,00,000 remains; threshold not met  D) ₹0 remains; threshold met\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The base pays its remaining ₹1,00,000 (insured bears 10% co-pay = ₹10,000 of that). That leaves 9,00,000 − 1,00,000 = **₹8,00,000** of this bill unfunded by the base. Aggregate admissible claims for the year = 4,00,000 + 9,00,000 = ₹13,00,000, which exceeds the super top-up's ₹5,00,000 aggregate threshold, so the super top-up is now active. B) and C) miscount the base's remaining limit; D) wrongly says nothing remains.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** The super top-up (₹15,00,000 limit, ₹5,00,000 aggregate deductible) now pays toward Hospitalisation 2. Given ₹5,00,000 of aggregate claims have already been \"used\" against the threshold by the base payouts, how much does the super top-up pay on this event?\nA) ₹8,00,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The threshold is on aggregate claims. By the time the base's ₹5,00,000 sum insured is fully consumed (₹4L + ₹1L), the entire ₹5,00,000 aggregate threshold has been met. So everything above the threshold is payable by the super top-up: the remaining ₹8,00,000 of Hospitalisation 2 is within the super top-up's ₹15 lakh limit, so it pays **₹8,00,000**. D) ₹3 lakh wrongly re-applies a fresh deductible. C) ₹4 lakh and B) ₹5 lakh misapply the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** If instead this had been a plain TOP-UP (₹5 lakh threshold applied PER CLAIM) rather than a super top-up, how much would the top-up pay on Hospitalisation 2's unfunded ₹8,00,000 (claim size ₹9,00,000)?\nA) ₹4,00,000  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹0  D) ₹8,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A plain top-up applies its ₹5 lakh threshold to EACH claim. For Hospitalisation 2 (a single ₹9,00,000 claim), it pays the excess of that one claim over ₹5,00,000 = 9,00,000 − 5,00,000 = **₹4,00,000**. (It ignores what the base paid; the per-claim threshold is measured against the claim itself.) D) ₹8 lakh is the super top-up answer. C) ₹0 would apply if the claim were below ₹5 lakh. B) ₹3 lakh is a miscalc. This shows the super top-up (₹8 lakh) beats a plain top-up (₹4 lakh) here.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Sunita's Retirement (Gratuity, EPF & NPS)\n\nSunita retires at 60 from a private company that IS covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act. Her last drawn basic+DA is ₹80,000 per month and she completed 28 years 8 months of service. Her NPS corpus at retirement is ₹80,00,000. Her monthly basic+DA during service was ₹50,000, on which she contributed 12% to EPF and the employer 12% (8.33% to EPS on the ₹15,000 ceiling).\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Last drawn basic+DA | ₹80,000/month |\n| Service | 28 years 8 months |\n| NPS corpus | ₹80,00,000 |\n| Monthly basic+DA (service) | ₹50,000 |\n| EPS wage ceiling | ₹15,000 |\n\n**Q51.** Sunita's gratuity (covered under the Act) is closest to:\nA) ₹13,38,462  B) ₹12,92,308  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹11,07,692\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula = (15 × last drawn salary × years)/26. Service is 28 years 8 months; since 8 months > 6, round up to 29 years. = (15 × 80,000 × 29)/26 = 3,48,00,000/26 = **₹13,38,462**. B) ₹12.92 lakh uses 28 years (no round-up). C) ₹20 lakh is the tax-exemption ceiling, not the computed amount. D) ₹11.07 lakh uses /26 with wrong years. Always round the final part-year up if > 6 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** The maximum tax-exempt gratuity ceiling (for the least-of-three test) referenced for retirement gratuity is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹13,38,462  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The statutory maximum gratuity / exemption ceiling is **₹20,00,000**. Since Sunita's computed gratuity (₹13.38 lakh) is below ₹20 lakh, the actual amount is fully within the ceiling. D) ₹10 lakh was the older limit. C) ₹25 lakh is incorrect. B) ₹13.38 lakh is her computed gratuity, not the ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** During service, Sunita's monthly EPS (pension) contribution was:\nA) ₹4,166  B) ₹600  C) ₹6,000  D) ₹1,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EPS = 8.33% of the wage ceiling ₹15,000 = 0.0833 × 15,000 = ₹1,249.5 ≈ **₹1,250**. A) ₹4,166 wrongly applies 8.33% on her full ₹50,000 basic. C) ₹6,000 is 12% of ₹50,000 (her own EPF contribution). B) ₹600 is unrelated. The EPS contribution is capped because it is computed only on the ₹15,000 statutory wage.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** From her NPS corpus of ₹80,00,000 at age 60, the minimum amount that must be used to buy an annuity is:\nA) ₹48,00,000  B) ₹32,00,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 60, at least 40% of the corpus must buy an annuity. 40% × 80,00,000 = **₹32,00,000**. A) ₹48 lakh is the 60% lump-sum portion, not the annuity minimum. C) ₹80 lakh is the whole corpus. D) ₹40 lakh uses 50%. So she annuitises ₹32 lakh and can take ₹48 lakh as a tax-free lump sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** If the annuity provider offers 6% p.a. on the ₹32,00,000 annuitised amount, Sunita's approximate annual pension is:\nA) ₹1,92,000  B) ₹2,88,000  C) ₹4,80,000  D) ₹96,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A simple level annuity at 6% on ₹32,00,000 = 0.06 × 32,00,000 = **₹1,92,000 per year** (≈ ₹16,000/month). B) ₹2.88 lakh uses 9%. C) ₹4.80 lakh applies 6% to the full ₹80 lakh corpus, but only ₹32 lakh is annuitised. D) ₹96,000 uses 3%. The annuity income is based only on the annuitised portion.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Mr. Iyer's Capital Gains (Capital Gains Tax)\n\nMr. Iyer made the following sales during FY 2025-26. All equity transactions paid STT. Use Section 112A (LTCG on listed equity 12.5% above ₹1,25,000 exemption), Section 111A (STCG 20%), and property holding period of 24 months.\n\n| Asset | Bought | Sold | Cost | Sale value |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed shares A | held 20 months | — | ₹5,00,000 | ₹8,00,000 |\n| Listed shares B | held 7 months | — | ₹3,00,000 | ₹3,90,000 |\n| Residential plot | held 20 months | — | ₹30,00,000 | ₹45,00,000 |\n\n**Q56.** The capital gain on Listed shares A is long-term or short-term, and how much?\nA) Short-term, ₹3,00,000  B) Long-term, ₹1,75,000  C) Long-term, ₹3,00,000  D) Short-term, ₹1,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity held > 12 months is long-term. Held 20 months → **long-term**. Gain = 8,00,000 − 5,00,000 = **₹3,00,000**. A) calls it short-term (wrong holding period). B)/D) subtract the ₹1.25 lakh exemption from the gain itself — the exemption is applied later when computing tax, not to the gain figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The tax on Listed shares A's gain under Section 112A (ignore other assets, before cess) is:\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹21,875  D) ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG ₹3,00,000; exempt first ₹1,25,000; taxable = 3,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹1,75,000; tax = 12.5% × 1,75,000 = **₹21,875**. A) ₹37,500 forgets the exemption (12.5% × 3,00,000). B) ₹30,000 uses 10%. D) ₹60,000 applies 20% (the STCG rate). The ₹1.25 lakh exemption is the common trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The capital gain treatment on Listed shares B (held 7 months, gain ₹90,000) and its tax under Section 111A is:\nA) Long-term; tax nil (within exemption)  B) Short-term; tax ₹13,500  C) Short-term; tax ₹18,000  D) Long-term; tax ₹11,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Held < 12 months → short-term. Gain = 3,90,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹90,000. STCG under 111A = 20%: 20% × 90,000 = **₹18,000**. There is NO ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG. A) wrongly treats it as long-term/exempt. B) ₹13,500 uses 15% (old rate). D) ₹11,250 uses 12.5% LTCG rate. STCG on equity is a flat 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** The residential plot was held 20 months. Its capital gain is:\nA) Long-term, ₹15,00,000  B) Long-term, ₹13,75,000  C) Short-term, ₹15,00,000  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Immovable property is long-term only if held more than 24 months. 20 months < 24 → **short-term**. Gain = 45,00,000 − 30,00,000 = **₹15,00,000**, taxed at the individual's slab rates (not 112A/111A). A) wrongly calls it long-term. B) wrongly subtracts a ₹1.25 lakh exemption (which applies only to listed-equity LTCG). D) is wrong — property gains are not exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Mr. Iyer's TOTAL special-rate capital gains tax (Sections 112A + 111A only, before cess; exclude the slab-taxed property gain) is:\nA) ₹39,875  B) ₹21,875  C) ₹55,500  D) ₹18,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Add the 112A and 111A taxes: shares A (LTCG) = ₹21,875 + shares B (STCG) = ₹18,000 = **₹39,875** (before cess). The property's ₹15 lakh STCG is taxed at slab rates and excluded here. B) ₹21,875 counts only LTCG. C) ₹55,500 wrongly mixes rates. D) ₹18,000 counts only STCG.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Kavya's Salary & Income Tax (Income Tax, New Regime FY 2025-26)\n\nKavya, 34, is salaried under the new tax regime for FY 2025-26. Her gross salary is ₹14,75,000. She has no other income. New-regime slabs: Nil up to ₹4 lakh; 5% ₹4–8 lakh; 10% ₹8–12 lakh; 15% ₹12–16 lakh; standard deduction ₹75,000; cess 4%.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary | ₹14,75,000 |\n| Standard deduction | ₹75,000 |\n| Regime | New (115BAC), FY 2025-26 |\n\n**Q61.** Kavya's net taxable income after standard deduction is:\nA) ₹14,75,000  B) ₹13,25,000  C) ₹14,00,000  D) ₹13,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Net taxable = gross − standard deduction = 14,75,000 − 75,000 = **₹14,00,000**. A) forgets the deduction. B) ₹13.25 lakh wrongly uses a ₹1.5 lakh deduction. D) ₹13 lakh uses ₹1.75 lakh. Under the new regime the salaried standard deduction is ₹75,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** The income tax on ₹14,00,000 (new-regime slabs, before cess) is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹90,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹1,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Slab-wise: ₹0–4L nil; ₹4–8L (₹4L) @5% = ₹20,000; ₹8–12L (₹4L) @10% = ₹40,000; ₹12–14L (₹2L) @15% = ₹30,000. Total = 20,000 + 40,000 + 30,000 = **₹1,00,000**. B) ₹90,000 drops part of the 15% slab. C) ₹1.2 lakh over-taxes the top slab. D) ₹1.4 lakh applies a flat rate. Build tax slab by slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Adding 4% health & education cess, Kavya's total tax liability is:\nA) ₹1,02,000  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹1,04,000  D) ₹1,03,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Cess = 4% × 1,00,000 = ₹4,000. Total = 1,00,000 + 4,000 = **₹1,04,000**. A) ₹1,02,000 uses 2% cess. B) ₹1,00,000 forgets cess. D) ₹1,03,000 uses 3%. The cess is always 4% of (tax + surcharge).\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Why does Kavya NOT get the Section 87A rebate (which makes tax nil) that a colleague earning ₹12,00,000 taxable would get?\nA) Because rebate applies only to the old regime  B) Because her taxable income (₹14,00,000) exceeds the ₹12,00,000 new-regime rebate threshold  C) Because she is below 60  D) Because she has only salary income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The new-regime 87A rebate makes tax nil only up to a taxable income of **₹12,00,000** (FY 2025-26). Kavya's ₹14,00,000 exceeds that, so no rebate and she pays full slab tax. A) is wrong — the rebate exists in the new regime. C) age is irrelevant to 87A. D) the source of income doesn't matter. The colleague at ₹12 lakh exactly qualifies; Kavya doesn't.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** If Kavya could reduce her TAXABLE income to exactly ₹12,00,000 (e.g., via employer NPS 80CCD(2)), her tax (with 87A rebate) would be:\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹62,400  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At a taxable income of ₹12,00,000 in the new regime FY 2025-26, the Section 87A rebate makes the tax liability **₹0**. A) ₹60,000 / C) ₹62,400 would be the pre-rebate slab tax + cess, but the rebate wipes it out. D) ₹40,000 is incorrect. This is why the ₹12 lakh threshold is a powerful planning target — but note 80CCD(2) deductions help reach it.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Verma Household: Integrated Protection Plan (Integrated Case)\n\nMr. Verma (40) is reviewing his family's protection. He owns a home (structure ₹60,00,000) insured under a property policy with an 80% under-insurance condition (he insured it for ₹48,00,000). His car (1400 CC) has own-damage cover with a ₹1,000 compulsory deductible plus a voluntary deductible of ₹2,000 he chose to lower premium. He compares term insurance (₹1 crore, ₹12,000 p.a.) vs an endowment plan (₹1 crore, ₹3,20,000 p.a.). He also holds a health policy of ₹10,00,000 with a 10% co-pay.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Home structure value | ₹60,00,000 |\n| Home insured for | ₹48,00,000 |\n| Car deductibles | ₹1,000 compulsory + ₹2,000 voluntary |\n| Term premium (₹1 cr) | ₹12,000 p.a. |\n| Endowment premium (₹1 cr) | ₹3,20,000 p.a. |\n| Health cover / co-pay | ₹10,00,000 / 10% |\n\n**Q66.** A fire damages the home structure causing a loss of ₹20,00,000. Under the average (under-insurance) clause — claim = loss × (sum insured ÷ full value) — the insurer pays:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹16,00,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹48,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The average clause penalises under-insurance. Insured for ₹48 lakh against ₹60 lakh value = 80% covered. Claim = 20,00,000 × (48,00,000/60,00,000) = 20,00,000 × 0.8 = **₹16,00,000**. A) ₹20 lakh ignores under-insurance. C) ₹15 lakh uses a wrong 75% ratio. D) ₹48 lakh is the sum insured, not the claim. Under-insurance means the insured self-bears a proportionate share.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Mr. Verma's car suffers own-damage assessed at ₹18,000. With a ₹1,000 compulsory + ₹2,000 voluntary deductible, the insurer pays:\nA) ₹18,000  B) ₹17,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹16,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Both deductibles are borne by the insured before the claim is admissible. Total deductible = 1,000 + 2,000 = ₹3,000. Insurer pays 18,000 − 3,000 = **₹15,000**. A) ₹18,000 ignores deductibles. B) ₹17,000 applies only the compulsory ₹1,000. D) ₹16,000 applies only the voluntary ₹2,000. Choosing a voluntary deductible lowers premium but raises the insured's out-of-pocket share at claim time.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Over a 20-year horizon, how much MORE does the endowment plan cost in premiums than the term plan, for the same ₹1 crore cover?\nA) ₹61,60,000  B) ₹64,00,000  C) ₹3,08,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual extra = 3,20,000 − 12,000 = ₹3,08,000. Over 20 years = 3,08,000 × 20 = **₹61,60,000**. A) is correct. B) ₹64 lakh multiplies the endowment premium difference incorrectly (uses ₹3.2 lakh × 20). C) ₹3,08,000 is just one year's extra. D) ₹2.4 lakh is the wrong base. The huge gap is why term + separate investing usually beats endowment.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Mr. Verma incurs a ₹6,00,000 hospitalisation under his ₹10 lakh health policy (10% co-pay). The insurer pays:\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹5,40,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Co-pay 10% means insured bears 10% × 6,00,000 = ₹60,000; insurer pays 6,00,000 − 60,000 = **₹5,40,000**. The claim is well within the ₹10 lakh sum insured. A) ₹6 lakh ignores co-pay. C) ₹60,000 is the insured's share. D) ₹5 lakh is a wrong flat figure. Co-pay always reduces the insurer's payout by the agreed percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Given Mr. Verma needs life cover and is choosing how to deploy a fixed budget, which recommendation best fits the workbook's \"insurance before investment / decouple\" principle?\nA) Buy the endowment plan because it returns money at maturity  B) Buy the ₹1 crore term plan and invest the ₹3,08,000 annual difference separately in mutual funds  C) Buy a smaller endowment cover of ₹40 lakh to keep premiums affordable  D) Skip life insurance and invest the whole amount since he has health and property cover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The workbook's principle is to secure ADEQUATE protection cheaply (term insurance), then invest the difference separately where it is transparent and flexible. Buying ₹1 cr term (₹12,000) and investing the ₹3,08,000 difference gives full cover plus a better, more transparent investment. A) endowment locks money into a low (~5%) inherent return. C) reducing cover to ₹40 lakh under-insures him — protection comes first. D) skipping life cover leaves dependents exposed. Decouple insurance from investment, cover the need fully, then invest the rest.\n</details>\n</content>\n</invoke>\n"}, {"num": "04", "md": "# Mock Paper 4 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy, with a strong focus on the taxation of debt and \"other\" products (Sec 50AA, SGB, gold, REIT/InvIT/AIF, ULIPs) and on time-value-of-money maths (FV, PV, CAGR, real returns). Keep a spreadsheet or financial calculator handy and work each step on paper before picking an option.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Mr. A buys an unlisted bond of an Indian company for ₹4,80,000 and sells it 5 years later for ₹8,00,000. Under the post-2023 rules, what is the nature of his capital gain?\nA) Long-term, taxed at 12.5%  B) Long-term, taxed at 20%  C) Exempt  D) Short-term, taxed at slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For unlisted bonds/debentures, gains are ALWAYS treated as short-term capital gains irrespective of the holding period (Table 10.1), and STCG on bonds is taxed at the assessee's applicable slab rates. So the ₹3,20,000 gain (8,00,000 − 4,80,000) is short-term at slab rates. A is the rule for *listed* bonds held >12 months. B and C invent rates/exemptions that don't exist here. The trap is assuming a 5-year hold automatically means long-term — for unlisted bonds the holding period is simply ignored.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Mr. P holds a listed bond bought for ₹1,20,000 and sells it after 20 months for ₹1,70,000. What is his capital-gains tax (ignore cess/surcharge)?\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹6,250  C) ₹6,000  D) ₹5,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed bonds qualify as long-term if held >12 months; 20 months > 12 months, so it is LTCG. LTCG on listed bonds = 12.5% without indexation. Gain = 1,70,000 − 1,20,000 = ₹50,000. Tax = 50,000 × 12.5% = ₹6,250. A wrongly uses 20%; C wrongly uses 12% (50,000 × 12%); D wrongly uses 10%. Formula: Tax = (Sale − Cost) × 12.5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A Specified Mutual Fund (Type 1, investing 80% in debt) was bought on 10-May-2023 for ₹2,00,000 and redeemed on 20-June-2028 for ₹3,10,000. How is the ₹1,10,000 gain taxed?\nA) LTCG at 12.5% with indexation  B) LTCG at 12.5% without indexation  C) STCG at slab rates  D) Exempt as held > 36 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 50AA applies to SMFs (≥65% in debt/money-market) acquired on or after 01-04-2023. Under Sec 50AA, the gain is ALWAYS short-term irrespective of holding period and taxed at slab rates. Even though it was held ~5 years, the 5-year holding is irrelevant. A and B assume long-term treatment that Sec 50AA removes; D invents an exemption. The whole point of Sec 50AA is that these funds never get long-term/indexation benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An individual investor holds a Sovereign Gold Bond (SGB) to its full 8-year maturity and receives the redemption amount. The redemption gain is:\nA) Taxed as LTCG at 12.5%  B) Not chargeable to tax (Sec 47)  C) Taxed at slab rates  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 47 provides that redemption of an SGB by an *individual* is not treated as a \"transfer\", so no capital gain arises. The interest (2.5% p.a.) is still taxable, but the capital appreciation on redemption is tax-free for individuals. A/C/D would apply only on a *secondary-market transfer* (a sale), or to a non-individual like a trust on redemption. The trap is forgetting that the exemption is only on redemption-by-individual, not on a sale.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Mr. X (resident) earns ₹5,00,000 dividend from a debt mutual fund and pays ₹1,50,000 interest on money borrowed to invest. What dividend is taxable?\nA) ₹3,50,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹3,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A resident may deduct interest expense to earn MF dividend, but capped at 20% of the dividend. Cap = 5,00,000 × 20% = ₹1,00,000. Even though ₹1,50,000 was actually paid, only ₹1,00,000 is allowed. Taxable dividend = 5,00,000 − 1,00,000 = ₹4,00,000. A wrongly deducts the full ₹1,50,000; B forgets the deduction entirely; D uses a 24% figure. Formula: Taxable = Dividend − min(actual interest, 20% × dividend).\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A T-Bill is issued at ₹97,800 and redeemed at face value ₹1,00,000 after 182 days. The ₹2,200 profit is:\nA) Interest income, taxed at slab rates  B) STCG taxed at slab rates  C) LTCG at 12.5%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — T-Bills (maturity < 1 year) carry no coupon; the return is the difference between issue price and face value, and this profit is treated as a short-term capital gain taxed at the assessee's applicable rates. A is the common trap (treating discount as \"interest\") — the workbook classifies it as STCG, not interest. C needs >12-month listed status which is impossible for a T-bill. D invents an exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Gold ETF was bought on 01-Feb-2024 for ₹1,80,000 and sold on 01-Mar-2025 (after 1 April 2025) for ₹2,40,000. Holding period is more than 12 months. How is the ₹60,000 gain taxed?\nA) STCG at slab rates  B) LTCG at 20%  C) Exempt under Sec 112A  D) LTCG at 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Gold ETFs are taxed like \"other than equity-oriented\" funds. The special \"always-STCG\" rule only applies to Gold ETFs *bought after 1-Apr-2023 AND sold before 1-Apr-2025*. Here the sale is on/after 1-Apr-2025, so the normal rule applies: held >12 months = LTCG at 12.5%. A would be right only if sold before 1-Apr-2025; B uses the wrong rate; C applies the equity-ETF section, but a Gold ETF is not equity-oriented.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** ₹1,00,000 grows to ₹2,01,136 over 9 years. What is the CAGR (compound annual growth rate)?\nA) 11.24% p.a.  B) 7.50% p.a.  C) 9.00% p.a.  D) 8.00% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — CAGR = (End/Start)^(1/n) − 1, where n = number of years. = (2,01,136 / 1,00,000)^(1/9) − 1 = (2.01136)^(0.1111) − 1. Since 1.08^9 ≈ 2.0 (1.08^9 = 1.99900…≈2.011 with rounding), CAGR ≈ 8%. A is simple-return ÷ 9 = (101.136%/9 ≈ 11.24%), the classic \"simple instead of compound\" error. B and C are close decoys. Quick check: 1.08^9 = 1.999 ≈ 2.01.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** You need ₹10,00,000 in 6 years. If you can earn 9% p.a. compounded annually, what lump sum must you invest today (PV)?\nA) ₹5,96,267  B) ₹6,49,931  C) ₹5,46,000  D) ₹9,17,431\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV / (1+r)^n = 10,00,000 / (1.09)^6. (1.09)^6 = 1.67710. PV = 10,00,000 / 1.67710 = ₹5,96,267. B uses (1.09)^5 wrongly; C uses simple-interest discounting; D discounts only one year (10,00,000/1.09). Each term: FV = target, r = 9%, n = 6 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Inflation is 6%. A nominal portfolio return is 11%. What is the approximate *real* return?\nA) 5.00%  B) 17.66%  C) 4.72%  D) 4.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Real return = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) − 1 = (1.11/1.06) − 1 = 1.047169 − 1 = 0.04717 = 4.72%. A is the rough subtraction (11% − 6%), which overstates the real return; B wrongly adds; D is a decoy. The exact formula matters: dividing by (1+inflation) is slightly less than simply subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A REIT distributes rental income to a unit-holder. In whose hands is this rental income taxed?\nA) Exempt for everyone  B) Taxed in the unit-holder's hands (pass-through)  C) Taxed at the REIT level only  D) Taxed in both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rental income is exempt at the REIT level under Sec 10(23FCA) but is a *pass-through*: when distributed, it is taxed in the unit-holder's hands. Pass-through applies to (a) rental income, (c) dividend from SPV, (d) interest from SPV. A and C miss the pass-through mechanism; D double-counts. Key idea: pass-through means tax is \"passed\" to the investor, not paid twice.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** For an InvIT, the rental income from property owned by the trust is taxed:\nA) In the unit-holder's hands (pass-through)  B) Exempt entirely  C) At 12.5%  D) In the InvIT's own hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — InvITs are taxed like REITs EXCEPT for rental income: pass-through for rental income is given only to REITs. For an InvIT, rental income is taxed in the InvIT's own hands (income from business), not the unit-holder's. A is the REIT rule (the trap). B and C are wrong. This is the single key difference between REIT and InvIT taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A high-value ULIP issued on 01-May-2022 has annual premium ₹3,00,000 and sum assured ₹40,00,000. On maturity, are the proceeds exempt under Sec 10(10D)?\nA) Yes, fully exempt  B) No — premium exceeds 10% of sum assured  C) No — aggregate premium exceeds ₹2,50,000, so exemption is lost  D) Yes, because death benefit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For ULIPs issued on/after 1-Feb-2021, if the aggregate annual premium exceeds ₹2,50,000, the Sec 10(10D) exemption is NOT available and the gain is taxed as capital gains. Here ₹3,00,000 > ₹2,50,000, so exemption is lost. B is wrong because premium (₹3,00,000) is actually less than 10% of SA (₹4,00,000) — that test passes, but the ₹2.5L ULIP threshold is the binding one. A ignores the threshold; D applies only if the sum is received on death.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** An endowment policy taken on 01-Apr-2024 has annual premium ₹6,00,000 and sum assured ₹70,00,000. The premium is well within 10% of SA. On maturity, the proceeds are:\nA) Fully exempt under Sec 10(10D)  B) Taxed as LTCG at 12.5%  C) Exempt because SA > 10× premium  D) Taxable as income from other sources\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For (non-ULIP) policies taken on/after 1-Apr-2023, if the premium (or aggregate premium) exceeds ₹5,00,000, the maturity proceeds are taxable as income from other sources even if SA is 10×+ of premium. Premium ₹6,00,000 > ₹5,00,000, so exemption is lost despite passing the 10%-of-SA test. A/C rely on tests that no longer override the ₹5L rule; B wrongly treats it as capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Mr. R retires and his total NPS corpus is ₹50,00,000. He withdraws the maximum tax-free lump sum allowed. How much can he withdraw tax-free?\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On closure/opting out of NPS, withdrawal is exempt to the extent of 60% of the total corpus (in symmetry with the 60% lump-sum rule). 60% × 50,00,000 = ₹30,00,000 tax-free. The remaining 40% (₹20,00,000) must buy an annuity, whose pension is then taxable. A confuses lump-sum with the annuity portion; C assumes the whole corpus is tax-free; D uses 50%.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A market-linked debenture (MLD) is bought for ₹5,00,000 and redeemed at maturity after 30 months for ₹6,50,000 (redeemed after 1-Apr-2023). How is the ₹1,50,000 gain taxed?\nA) LTCG at 12.5%  B) Exempt  C) LTCG at 20%  D) STCG at slab rates under Sec 50AA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — MLDs transferred/redeemed/matured on or after 01-04-2023 fall under Sec 50AA: gain is always short-term irrespective of holding period and taxed at slab rates. The 30-month holding is irrelevant. A/C assume long-term treatment Sec 50AA removes; B invents an exemption. Note: no cost-of-improvement or indexation is allowed for MLDs under Sec 50AA.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** ₹50,000 invested today doubles in value. If the annual return is 12%, approximately how many years does it take (Rule of 72)?\nA) 4 years  B) 8 years  C) 6 years  D) 12 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rule of 72: years to double ≈ 72 / rate = 72 / 12 = 6 years. (Check: 1.12^6 = 1.974 ≈ 2.) A uses 72/18; B uses 72/9; D simply repeats the rate. The Rule of 72 is a fast mental approximation for doubling time under compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A non-resident receives ₹2,00,000 dividend from an Indian debt mutual fund. At what rate is it taxed (ignore DTAA, surcharge, cess), and is any expense deductible?\nA) Slab rates; 20% expense allowed  B) 12.5%; no expense  C) 10%; 20% expense  D) 20%; no expense deductible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For a non-resident, MF dividend is taxed at 20% (subject to DTAA), and NO expenditure (and no Chapter VI-A deduction) is allowed. So the full ₹2,00,000 is taxed at 20% = ₹40,000. A is the *resident* rule (slab + 20% expense cap); B and C use wrong rates. The non-resident loses the interest-expense deduction that residents get.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An investor SIPs ₹10,000 per month for 12 months earning 1% per month. Using the future value of an ordinary annuity, the maturity value is approximately:\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹1,26,825  C) ₹1,28,093  D) ₹1,21,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV of ordinary annuity = P × [((1+i)^n − 1)/i] = 10,000 × [((1.01)^12 − 1)/0.01]. (1.01)^12 = 1.126825, so ((1.126825 − 1)/0.01) = 12.6825. FV = 10,000 × 12.6825 = ₹1,26,825. A is just the principal (ignores growth); C wrongly uses the annuity-due formula; D applies simple interest. Terms: P = monthly amount, i = monthly rate, n = months.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Listed REIT units bought for ₹4,00,000 are sold (STT paid) after 8 months for ₹4,90,000. The ₹90,000 short-term gain is taxed under Sec 111A at:\nA) 12.5%  B) 15%  C) 20%  D) Slab rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Units of a business trust (REIT/InvIT) held 12 months or less are short-term; with STT paid, STCG is taxed at 20% under Sec 111A. Tax = 90,000 × 20% = ₹18,000. A is the LTCG rate; B is the old 15% rate (now 20%); D would apply only if STT were not paid. Holding ≤12 months on listed units = short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A listed bond pays a 7% coupon on face value ₹10,000, paid half-yearly. How much interest accrues for one quarter (3 months)?\nA) ₹350  B) ₹700  C) ₹233  D) ₹175\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Quarterly interest = Face × rate × (3/12) = 10,000 × 7% × 0.25 = ₹175. B is the full-year interest; A is the half-yearly amount; C is a wrong fraction. Bond interest accrues on the face value, pro-rated for the period held.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** For Category-I and Category-II AIFs, \"other income\" (non-business income) is:\nA) Taxed in the AIF's hands at maximum marginal rate  B) Passed through and taxed in the unit-holder's hands  C) Exempt for everyone  D) Taxed at a flat 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cat-I/II AIFs have pass-through status under Sec 115UB: \"other income\" is exempt at the AIF level (Sec 10(23FBA)) and taxed in the unit-holder's hands as if they invested directly, retaining its character and proportion. A is the rule for *business income* of the AIF (the trap); C and D are wrong. Only business income is taxed at the fund level; everything else passes through.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A bond costing ₹1,000 has a yield-to-maturity of 8%. A single cash flow of ₹1,259.71 is due in 3 years. What is its present value at 8%?\nA) ₹1,000  B) ₹1,080  C) ₹1,166  D) ₹1,259\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = CF / (1+r)^n = 1,259.71 / (1.08)^3 = 1,259.71 / 1.259712 = ₹1,000. B discounts only one year; C uses (1.08)^1.x decoy; D forgets to discount at all. This is the core bond-pricing idea: the market value is the present value of future cash flows at the YTM.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A resident sells a listed Dated G-Sec held for 30 months: cost ₹95,000, sale ₹1,25,000. Dated G-Secs are taxed like listed bonds. Tax on the gain (ignore cess) is:\nA) ₹3,750  B) ₹6,000  C) ₹3,000  D) ₹6,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed Dated G-Secs are taxed like listed bonds: held >12 months = LTCG at 12.5% without indexation. Gain = 1,25,000 − 95,000 = ₹30,000. Tax = 30,000 × 12.5% = ₹3,750. B uses 20%; C uses 10%; D uses ₹50,000 base. Dated G-Secs/SDLs follow the listed-bond regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An investor's portfolio: Year 0 value ₹2,00,000; Year 5 value ₹3,52,000. What is the CAGR?\nA) 15.20% p.a.  B) 12.00% p.a.  C) 11.97% p.a.  D) 10.00% p.a.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CAGR = (3,52,000/2,00,000)^(1/5) − 1 = (1.76)^(0.2) − 1. (1.76)^0.2 = e^(0.2 × ln1.76) = e^(0.2 × 0.56531) = e^0.113062 = 1.11970, so CAGR ≈ 11.97%. A is the simple return ÷ 5 (76%/5 = 15.2%); B and D are round decoys. Use the geometric (compound) growth formula, not simple division.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A premature redemption of an SGB by a *charitable trust* (not an individual) after holding 6 years yields a gain of ₹1,20,000. SGBs are listed. The gain is taxed at:\nA) Exempt (Sec 47)  B) Slab rates (STCG)  C) 20%  D) 12.5% (LTCG)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Sec 47 exemption on SGB redemption applies only to *individuals*. For a non-individual (e.g., a trust), redemption is a transfer: held >12 months = LTCG, and as SGBs are listed, taxed at 12.5%. A is the individual-only rule (the trap); B would apply only if held ≤12 months; C is the wrong rate. Non-individuals do not get the redemption exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Today's annual living expense is ₹6,00,000. With inflation at 7%, what will the equivalent annual expense be in 10 years? (Use 1.07^10 = 1.96715)\nA) ₹10,20,000  B) ₹11,80,290  C) ₹6,42,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1+inflation)^n = 6,00,000 × (1.07)^10 = 6,00,000 × 1.96715 = ₹11,80,290. A applies a flat 70% (simple, no compounding); C inflates only one year; D is a guess. Inflation compounds, so use (1+i)^n, not 1 + i×n.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A unit-holder redeems units of a REIT (i.e., the REIT buys back the units). The sum received on redemption is taxed:\nA) As LTCG at 12.5%  B) As STCG at 20%  C) As \"income from other sources\" under Sec 56(2)(xii)  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sum received on *redemption* of REIT units is taxable under \"income from other sources\" per Sec 56(2)(xii) at normal rates. The 111A/112A capital-gains rates (A/B) apply to a *transfer/sale in the market*, not redemption. D is wrong. The distinction \"redemption vs transfer\" changes the head of income and the rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** An individual in the 30% slab earns ₹40,000 interest from an SGB. What is the tax on the interest (ignore cess/surcharge)?\nA) Nil (interest is exempt)  B) ₹5,000  C) ₹4,000  D) ₹12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — SGB interest (2.5% p.a. on nominal value) is taxable as \"income from other sources\" at the assessee's slab rate; only the redemption capital gain (for individuals) is exempt. Tax = 40,000 × 30% = ₹12,000. A confuses the redemption exemption with the interest; B/C use wrong slab rates. SGB: interest taxable, redemption gain exempt for individuals.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A 91-day Cash Management Bill bought at ₹98,500 redeems at ₹1,00,000. The ₹1,500 gain is:\nA) Interest at slab rates  B) STCG at slab rates  C) LTCG at 12.5%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Like T-Bills, CMBs (maturity <91 days) carry no interest; the discount is treated as a short-term capital gain (maturity <1 year) and taxed at slab rates. A is the trap of calling the discount \"interest\"; C is impossible (can't be long-term); D invents an exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** An NRI earns ₹10,00,000 interest from Indian debentures. The India-USA DTAA caps interest at 15%, while the Act would tax it at the slab rate (say 30%). What rate applies?\nA) 30% (Act)  B) 25%  C) 20%  D) 15% (DTAA, more beneficial)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A non-resident eligible for DTAA is taxed at the rate in the DTAA or the Act, whichever is *more beneficial*. 15% (DTAA) < 30% (Act), so 15% applies. Tax = 10,00,000 × 15% = ₹1,50,000. A ignores the DTAA; B/C are wrong rates. To claim DTAA, the NRI needs a Tax Residency Certificate (TRC) and Form 10F.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A debt fund bought BEFORE 1-Apr-2023 (on 01-Jan-2022) for ₹3,00,000 is sold on 10-Apr-2025 for ₹4,20,000. It is unlisted. How is the ₹1,20,000 gain taxed?\nA) STCG at slab rates under Sec 50AA  B) Exempt  C) LTCG at 12.5% (Other Mutual Fund rules)  D) LTCG at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sec 50AA applies only to SMFs acquired ON OR AFTER 01-04-2023. This fund was bought 01-Jan-2022, so Sec 50AA does NOT apply; it is taxed under normal \"Other Mutual Fund\" rules. Unlisted units need >24 months to be long-term; held >3 years, so LTCG at 12.5%. A wrongly applies Sec 50AA to a pre-2023 purchase (the key trap); B/D are wrong. Acquisition date is decisive for Sec 50AA.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A SIP target requires a corpus of ₹50,00,000 in 20 years. If the fund earns 10% p.a., what *lump sum* invested today reaches it? (Use 1.10^20 = 6.7275)\nA) ₹7,43,217  B) ₹10,00,000  C) ₹8,30,000  D) ₹6,72,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV/(1+r)^n = 50,00,000 / (1.10)^20 = 50,00,000 / 6.7275 = ₹7,43,217. B is a guess; C uses a wrong factor; D divides only the FV's last component. r = 10%, n = 20 years; the high power makes the discount steep.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An equity-oriented fund's LTCG for the year is ₹2,00,000 (held >12 months, STT paid). What is the LTCG tax under Sec 112A (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹12,500  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹9,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sec 112A: LTCG on listed equity/equity MF is taxed at 12.5% only on the amount *above the ₹1,25,000 exemption*. Taxable = 2,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹75,000. Tax = 75,000 × 12.5% = ₹9,375. A forgets the exemption (2,00,000 × 12.5%); B/C use wrong bases. Always subtract the ₹1.25 lakh exemption first.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A non-resident transfers listed REIT units (STT paid) held 20 months; LTCG works out to ₹3,25,000. Tax under Sec 112A (ignore cess) is:\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹65,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG on listed business-trust units (held >12 months, STT paid) is taxed at 12.5% above the ₹1,25,000 exemption under Sec 112A, for residents and non-residents alike. Taxable = 3,25,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹2,00,000. Tax = 2,00,000 × 12.5% = ₹25,000. A forgets the exemption; C/D use wrong figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Two investments: A returns 9% taxed at 30%; B is a tax-free bond at 6.5%. What is A's *post-tax* return, and which is better?\nA) 6.30%; B is better  B) 6.30%; A is better  C) 5.70%; B is better  D) 9.00%; A is better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Post-tax return = pre-tax × (1 − tax rate) = 9% × (1 − 0.30) = 9% × 0.70 = 6.30%. The tax-free bond gives 6.50% with no tax, which beats 6.30%, so B is better. B picks the wrong winner; C miscomputes (uses 9%×0.633); D ignores tax. Always compare on a post-tax basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A listed bond gives a redemption value of ₹2,00,000 on cost ₹1,50,000 after 14 months, but the investor also incurred ₹2,000 transfer expenses. LTCG tax (ignore cess) is:\nA) ₹6,250  B) ₹6,000  C) ₹6,500  D) ₹5,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held >12 months on a listed bond = LTCG at 12.5%. LTCG = Sale − Cost − transfer expenses = 2,00,000 − 1,50,000 − 2,000 = ₹48,000. Tax = 48,000 × 12.5% = ₹6,000. A forgets the ₹2,000 expense; C/D use wrong nets. Transfer costs are deductible from the full value of consideration.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A retiree needs ₹40,000 per month forever from a perpetuity earning 8% p.a. What lump sum (corpus) is needed? (Treat as a simple perpetuity using annual figures)\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹48,00,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual need = 40,000 × 12 = ₹4,80,000. Perpetuity corpus = annual cash flow / rate = 4,80,000 / 0.08 = ₹60,00,000. A uses 12% (4,80,000/0.12); C uses 10%; D forgets to annualise. A perpetuity's present value = payment ÷ discount rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A \"Type 2\" Specified Mutual Fund (e.g., an international fund-of-funds) bought 01-May-2023 and SOLD on 01-Mar-2025 with a gain of ₹80,000 is taxed:\nA) LTCG at 12.5%  B) STCG at slab rates under Sec 50AA  C) Exempt  D) At 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Type-2 SMFs (funds that invested <35% in Indian equity, bought on/after 1-Apr-2023 and sold before 1-Apr-2025) fall under Sec 50AA: gain is always short-term at slab rates. Both dates fit (bought May-2023, sold Mar-2025). A/D assume long-term treatment; C invents exemption. This special category exists only for that limited buy-and-sell window.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An investor wants to know the *effective annual rate* when 12% nominal is compounded monthly. What is it? (Use 1.01^12 = 1.126825)\nA) 12.00%  B) 12.68%  C) 12.36%  D) 13.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Effective annual rate = (1 + nominal/m)^m − 1, where m = compounding periods = 12. = (1 + 0.12/12)^12 − 1 = (1.01)^12 − 1 = 1.126825 − 1 = 0.126825 = 12.68%. A is the nominal rate (ignores compounding); C and D are decoys. More frequent compounding raises the effective rate above the nominal.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Debt & Other-Product Taxation (Mr. Rao's portfolio)\n\nMr. Rao, a resident individual in the 30% tax slab, made several investments. All figures are for FY 2024-25 and ignore surcharge and cess unless stated.\n\n| Investment | Bought | Cost | Sold/Redeemed | Proceeds |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed corporate bond | 01-Jan-2022 | ₹2,00,000 | 01-Jun-2024 (sold) | ₹2,60,000 |\n| Debt fund (SMF Type 1, 90% debt) | 10-May-2023 | ₹3,00,000 | 20-Jun-2024 (redeemed) | ₹3,42,000 |\n| Sovereign Gold Bond (held to maturity, individual) | 2016 | ₹4,00,000 | 2024 (redeemed) | ₹6,50,000 |\n| Unlisted debenture | 01-Apr-2020 | ₹1,00,000 | 01-Dec-2024 (sold) | ₹1,80,000 |\n\nHe also earned ₹50,000 interest on the SGB during the year.\n\n**Q41.** The capital gain on the listed corporate bond is taxed as:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹7,500  B) STCG at slab = ₹18,000  C) LTCG at 20% = ₹12,000  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed bond held from 01-Jan-2022 to 01-Jun-2024 = ~29 months > 12 months = LTCG at 12.5% without indexation. Gain = 2,60,000 − 2,00,000 = ₹60,000. Tax = 60,000 × 12.5% = ₹7,500. B wrongly treats it as short-term; C uses 20%; D invents an exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** The gain on the SMF Type-1 debt fund is taxed as:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹5,250  B) Exempt  C) LTCG at 20% = ₹8,400  D) STCG at slab (30%) = ₹12,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bought 10-May-2023 (on/after 1-Apr-2023) and ≥65% debt, so Sec 50AA applies: gain is always short-term at slab rates regardless of the ~13-month holding. Gain = 3,42,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹42,000. Tax = 42,000 × 30% = ₹12,600. A/C assume long-term; B is wrong. Sec 50AA strips out long-term treatment.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** The capital gain on the SGB redemption is:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹31,250  B) Slab rate = ₹75,000  C) Not taxable (Sec 47, individual redemption)  D) 20% = ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mr. Rao is an individual and holds the SGB to maturity. Under Sec 47, redemption of an SGB by an individual is NOT a transfer, so the ₹2,50,000 gain (6,50,000 − 4,00,000) is not chargeable to tax. A/B/D apply rates that would only matter on a secondary-market *sale* or for a non-individual.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** The ₹50,000 SGB interest is taxed at:\nA) Exempt  B) Slab rate (30%) = ₹15,000  C) 12.5% = ₹6,250  D) 10% TDS only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGB interest is taxable as \"income from other sources\" at the slab rate. Tax = 50,000 × 30% = ₹15,000. There is no TDS on SGB interest (they are Government Securities). A confuses the redemption exemption with the interest; C uses the capital-gains rate; D is wrong. Interest is always taxable even when the redemption gain is exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The gain on the unlisted debenture is taxed as:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹10,000  B) LTCG at 20%  C) Exempt  D) STCG at slab (30%) = ₹24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Unlisted debentures are ALWAYS short-term irrespective of holding period, taxed at slab rates. Gain = 1,80,000 − 1,00,000 = ₹80,000. Tax = 80,000 × 30% = ₹24,000. A assumes long-term (the trap — the ~4.7-year hold doesn't matter); B/C are wrong. Unlisted debentures never qualify as long-term.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Capital Gains Across Asset Classes (Ms. Priya)\n\nMs. Priya (resident, 30% slab) sold the following in FY 2024-25. All listed equity/units had STT paid. Ignore cess and surcharge.\n\n| Asset | Holding period | Cost | Sale value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed equity shares | 18 months | ₹5,00,000 | ₹7,00,000 |\n| Equity mutual fund | 8 months | ₹2,00,000 | ₹2,50,000 |\n| Listed REIT units (STT paid) | 20 months | ₹3,00,000 | ₹4,00,000 |\n| Gold ETF (bought Jun-2021, sold Sep-2024) | 39 months | ₹1,50,000 | ₹2,30,000 |\n\n**Q46.** The LTCG tax on the listed equity shares is:\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹9,375  D) ₹18,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity held >12 months = LTCG under Sec 112A. Gain = 7,00,000 − 5,00,000 = ₹2,00,000. Taxable above the ₹1,25,000 exemption = ₹75,000. Tax = 75,000 × 12.5% = ₹9,375. A forgets the exemption; B/D use wrong rates/bases. Sec 112A exemption of ₹1.25 lakh applies once across all such LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The tax on the equity mutual fund gain (held 8 months) is:\nA) ₹6,250  B) ₹10,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹7,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity MF held ≤12 months = STCG under Sec 111A, taxed at 20% (STT paid). Gain = 2,50,000 − 2,00,000 = ₹50,000. Tax = 50,000 × 20% = ₹10,000. A uses 12.5% (LTCG rate); C uses 30% slab; D uses 15% (old rate). Short-term equity = 20% flat, no exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** The LTCG tax on the listed REIT units, given the ₹1,25,000 Sec 112A exemption is already fully used up by the equity-share LTCG in Q46, is:\nA) ₹12,500  B) ₹9,375  C) ₹20,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed REIT units held >12 months with STT paid = LTCG under Sec 112A at 12.5% above the ₹1,25,000 exemption. The exemption is a single annual threshold shared across all 112A gains, and it was already consumed by the ₹2,00,000 equity-share gain in Q46. So the REIT gain = 4,00,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹1,00,000 is fully taxable: 1,00,000 × 12.5% = ₹12,500. B wrongly re-applies the exemption to this asset; C uses 20%; D wrongly assumes a fresh exemption makes it Nil. One ₹1.25L exemption per year, not per asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** The tax on the Gold ETF gain (bought Jun-2021, sold Sep-2024) is:\nA) STCG at slab = ₹24,000  B) Exempt  C) LTCG at 20% = ₹16,000  D) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Gold ETF was bought before 1-Apr-2023, so the special \"always-STCG\" rule does NOT apply. Held >12 months = LTCG at 12.5%. Gain = 2,30,000 − 1,50,000 = ₹80,000. Tax = 80,000 × 12.5% = ₹10,000. A wrongly applies the Sec-50AA-style rule (only for funds bought after 1-Apr-2023 and sold before 1-Apr-2025); B/C are wrong. Acquisition date saves the long-term treatment here.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Across the portfolio, the total ₹1,25,000 Sec 112A exemption can be used:\nA) Separately for each asset  B) Twice (equity + REIT)  C) Once, aggregated across all 112A LTCG  D) Not at all for REITs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The ₹1,25,000 exemption under Sec 112A is a single annual threshold applied to the *aggregate* of all eligible LTCG (listed equity, equity MF LTCG, and listed business-trust units), not per-asset. A/B wrongly multiply the exemption; D wrongly excludes REITs (listed REIT LTCG is a 112A item). One exemption, one year, across all 112A gains.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Retirement Corpus & Time Value of Money (Mr. Verma)\n\nMr. Verma is 35, plans to retire at 60 (25 years away), and expects to live to 85. His *current* annual expense is ₹6,00,000. Pre-retirement inflation is 7% p.a.; he can earn 10% p.a. on investments while accumulating. Use these factors: (1.07)^25 = 5.4274; (1.10)^25 = 10.8347.\n\n**Q51.** What annual expense (in today's purchasing power terms, inflated) will he need in the *first year* of retirement (at age 60)?\nA) ₹16,50,000  B) ₹32,56,440  C) ₹10,50,000  D) ₹21,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future expense = current × (1+inflation)^n = 6,00,000 × (1.07)^25 = 6,00,000 × 5.4274 = ₹32,56,440. A and D apply flat simple percentages; C is a guess. Inflation compounds over 25 years, multiplying the expense ~5.43×.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** If he must accumulate a corpus of ₹4,00,00,000 by 60 and starts today, what *single lump sum* invested now reaches it at 10%?\nA) ₹36,91,800  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹4,33,38,800  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV / (1+r)^n = 4,00,00,000 / (1.10)^25 = 4,00,00,000 / 10.8347 = ₹36,91,800. B ignores growth; C multiplies instead of divides; D is a guess. The 10× growth factor over 25 years means a modest lump sum today suffices.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Instead, he invests ₹2,00,000 today. What will it grow to at 60 (10% p.a.)?\nA) ₹21,66,940  B) ₹7,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹10,83,470\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = PV × (1+r)^n = 2,00,000 × (1.10)^25 = 2,00,000 × 10.8347 = ₹21,66,940. B applies simple interest; C is a guess; D forgets to multiply by 2 (uses ₹1,00,000). Compounding turns ₹2 lakh into ~₹21.7 lakh in 25 years at 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** His real (inflation-adjusted) return during accumulation is approximately:\nA) 3.00%  B) 17.70%  C) 2.80%  D) 3.10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Real return = (1+nominal)/(1+inflation) − 1 = (1.10/1.07) − 1 = 1.028037 − 1 = 0.028037 = 2.80%. A is the rough subtraction (10% − 7% = 3%), which overstates it; B wrongly adds; D is a decoy. The exact division gives slightly under 3%.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** If he can only save a lump sum of ₹36,91,800 today (the PV from Q52), how does that compare to the ₹4 crore target?\nA) It exactly funds the target by 60  B) It falls short by half  C) It overshoots by 10×  D) It is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — By construction, ₹36,91,800 grown at 10% for 25 years = 36,91,800 × 10.8347 = ₹4,00,00,000 (the target). The PV and FV are two views of the same money: investing the present value today exactly produces the future value. B/C/D misread the time-value relationship.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Insurance & ULIP Taxation (The Mehta family)\n\nThe Mehtas hold several policies (ignore cess/surcharge):\n\n| Policy | Issued | Annual premium | Sum assured | Event |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ULIP (A) | 01-Mar-2021 | ₹2,00,000 | ₹25,00,000 | Matures, gain ₹6,00,000 (equity fund) |\n| ULIP (B) | 01-Apr-2022 | ₹3,50,000 | ₹40,00,000 | Matures, gain ₹5,00,000 (equity fund, held >1 yr) |\n| Endowment | 01-Jun-2024 | ₹6,50,000 | ₹80,00,000 | Matures later |\n| Term plan | 2020 | ₹15,000 | ₹1,00,00,000 | Death claim paid to nominee |\n\n**Q56.** Are ULIP (A)'s maturity proceeds exempt under Sec 10(10D)?\nA) No — issued after 1-Feb-2021, premium > ₹2.5L  B) No — premium exceeds 10% of SA  C) Taxable as slab income  D) Yes — annual premium ₹2,00,000 ≤ ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — ULIP (A) is issued on/after 1-Feb-2021, so the ₹2.5L test applies. Its annual premium ₹2,00,000 does NOT exceed ₹2,50,000, so the old provisions continue and the maturity proceeds remain exempt under Sec 10(10D). A is wrong on the facts (premium is below ₹2.5L); B is wrong because ₹2,00,000 < 10% of ₹25,00,000 (₹2,50,000) — both tests pass; C is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** ULIP (B)'s equity-fund gain (held >1 year) is taxed as:\nA) Exempt under Sec 10(10D)  B) STCG at 20%  C) Slab rate  D) LTCG at 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — ULIP (B) annual premium ₹3,50,000 > ₹2,50,000, so Sec 10(10D) exemption is lost and it is a capital asset. For equity-oriented funds in a high-premium ULIP, the long-term period is 1 year; held >1 year, the LTCG is taxed at the concessional 12.5%. A ignores the lost exemption; B is the short-term (≤1 yr) treatment; C is wrong for the equity component.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The endowment policy (premium ₹6,50,000, SA ₹80,00,000, issued Jun-2024) at maturity will be:\nA) Exempt — premium < 10% of SA  B) LTCG at 12.5%  C) Exempt — SA > 10× premium  D) Taxable as income from other sources — premium > ₹5L\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For non-ULIP policies issued on/after 1-Apr-2023, if premium exceeds ₹5,00,000, maturity proceeds are taxable as income from other sources even if the 10%-of-SA / 10×-premium tests pass. Premium ₹6,50,000 > ₹5,00,000. A and C rely on tests overridden by the ₹5L rule; B wrongly treats it as capital gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** The ₹1,00,00,000 term-plan death claim paid to the nominee is:\nA) Fully exempt under Sec 10(10D)  B) Taxable as income from other sources  C) LTCG at 12.5%  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Any sum received on the *death* of the insured is fully exempt under Sec 10(10D); the premium-ratio and ₹2.5L/₹5L thresholds do NOT apply to death claims. So the entire ₹1 crore is tax-free for the nominee. B/C/D wrongly tax a death benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** For ULIP (B), what is the LTCG tax on the ₹5,00,000 gain (ignore the ₹1.25L exemption interplay; treat in isolation, equity fund)?\nA) ₹46,875  B) ₹62,500  C) ₹1,00,000  D) ₹75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Equity-fund LTCG in a high-premium ULIP is taxed at 12.5% above the ₹1,25,000 exemption (like Sec 112A). Taxable = 5,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹3,75,000. Tax = 3,75,000 × 12.5% = ₹46,875. B forgets the exemption (5,00,000 × 12.5%); C uses 20%; D uses a wrong base. The equity ULIP gain enjoys the same ₹1.25L exemption and 12.5% rate.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Income Tax & Salary/NPS (Mr. Khan)\n\nMr. Khan (non-government employee, FY 2024-25) has: Basic salary ₹12,00,000; he and his employer each contribute 10% of basic to NPS; he repays a home-loan principal of ₹1,60,000 and pays LIC premium ₹40,000 (eligible under 80C). He uses the OLD tax regime. Standard deduction is ₹50,000. Ignore cess/surcharge.\n\n**Q61.** What is the maximum deduction under Sec 80C (combined 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1)) he can claim?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The combined ceiling for 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1) is ₹1,50,000. His 80C items (home-loan principal ₹1,60,000 + LIC ₹40,000 = ₹2,00,000) already exceed the cap, so the deduction is limited to ₹1,50,000. A/C overshoot the statutory cap; D is the home-loan figure only. The ₹1.5 lakh ceiling is hard.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** The employer's NPS contribution is ₹1,20,000 (10% of ₹12,00,000). What deduction is available under Sec 80CCD(2) in the old regime?\nA) Nil  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹1,68,000 (14%)  D) ₹1,20,000 (10% of basic)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In the OLD regime, the employer's NPS contribution is deductible under Sec 80CCD(2) up to 10% of salary (for non-government employees), over and above the ₹1.5L 80C cap. 10% × 12,00,000 = ₹1,20,000, which equals the actual contribution. A ignores the separate 80CCD(2) deduction; B is the 80CCD(1B) figure; C is the 14% limit that applies in the *new* regime, not the old.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Additionally, Mr. Khan contributes ₹50,000 of his own to NPS beyond the 80C cap. What extra deduction can he claim under Sec 80CCD(1B)?\nA) Nil  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sec 80CCD(1B) allows an *additional* deduction of up to ₹50,000 for the employee's own NPS contribution, over and above the ₹1.5L 80C limit. He contributes ₹50,000, so he gets the full ₹50,000. A ignores the provision; B confuses it with 80C; D halves it. This is the well-known \"extra ₹50,000 NPS\" benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Combining 80C (₹1,50,000) + 80CCD(1B) (₹50,000) + 80CCD(2) (₹1,20,000), his total Chapter VI-A NPS/80C deduction is:\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹2,70,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹3,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Total = 1,50,000 (80C) + 50,000 (80CCD(1B)) + 1,20,000 (80CCD(2) employer) = ₹3,20,000. A omits the employer portion; B omits the 80CCD(1B); C counts only 80C. The 80CCD(1B) and 80CCD(2) deductions are both *over and above* the ₹1.5L ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** If his employer's NPS contribution had instead been 12% of basic (₹1,44,000), how much of it is deductible under 80CCD(2) in the old regime?\nA) ₹1,44,000  B) ₹1,68,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In the old regime, the employer-contribution deduction is capped at 10% of salary for non-government employees: 10% × 12,00,000 = ₹1,20,000, even though ₹1,44,000 was contributed. The excess ₹24,000 is not deductible (and is taxable as salary). A allows the full contribution (wrong); B uses the 14% new-regime cap; D is unrelated. Old regime cap = 10%.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Case (Mrs. Iyer's financial review)\n\nMrs. Iyer (resident, 30% slab) is reviewing her year-end position (FY 2024-25). Ignore cess/surcharge.\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Listed equity LTCG (STT paid, >12 mo) | Gain ₹3,00,000 |\n| SMF Type-1 debt fund (bought Aug-2023) | Gain ₹1,00,000, held 14 months |\n| SGB interest received | ₹30,000 |\n| Bank FD interest | ₹80,000 |\n| She wants ₹50,00,000 in 15 years | Fund earns 9% p.a.; (1.09)^15 = 3.6425 |\n\n**Q66.** The tax on her listed equity LTCG (₹3,00,000) is:\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹21,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sec 112A: 12.5% above the ₹1,25,000 exemption. Taxable = 3,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹1,75,000. Tax = 1,75,000 × 12.5% = ₹21,875. A forgets the exemption; B uses a wrong base; C uses 20%. Always net off ₹1.25 lakh first.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** The tax on her SMF Type-1 debt fund gain (₹1,00,000) is:\nA) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹12,500  B) Exempt  C) STCG at slab (30%) = ₹30,000  D) 20% = ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Bought Aug-2023 (after 1-Apr-2023), ≥65% debt → Sec 50AA: always short-term at slab rates, regardless of the 14-month hold. Tax = 1,00,000 × 30% = ₹30,000. A/D wrongly apply long-term treatment; B invents an exemption. The 14-month period does not help under Sec 50AA.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The combined tax on her SGB interest (₹30,000) and FD interest (₹80,000) is:\nA) ₹33,000  B) ₹13,750  C) ₹24,000  D) ₹9,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Both SGB interest and FD interest are taxed as \"income from other sources\" at the slab rate. Total interest = 30,000 + 80,000 = ₹1,10,000. Tax = 1,10,000 × 30% = ₹33,000. B uses 12.5%; C taxes only the FD; D taxes only the SGB interest. Interest income (including SGB interest) is fully slab-taxed.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** What single lump sum must she invest today to reach ₹50,00,000 in 15 years at 9%?\nA) ₹13,72,684  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹18,21,250  D) ₹1,82,12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV/(1+r)^n = 50,00,000 / (1.09)^15 = 50,00,000 / 3.6425 = ₹13,72,684. B ignores growth; C uses a wrong factor; D multiplies instead of dividing. The corpus today is the discounted present value of the future target.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Her *total* tax for the year (equity LTCG + SMF gain + interest, from Q66–Q68) is:\nA) ₹84,875  B) ₹76,000  C) ₹52,875  D) ₹91,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Add the three components: equity LTCG ₹21,875 (Q66) + SMF gain ₹30,000 (Q67) + interest ₹33,000 (Q68) = ₹84,875. B/C/D drop or mis-add one component. The integrated answer simply sums the correctly computed parts; double-check each before adding.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "05", "md": "# Mock Paper 5 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy — it leans hard on income-tax computation (heads of income, old vs new regime slabs, 87A rebate, residential status, Chapter VI-A deductions). Keep a calculator or spreadsheet handy and write down each step; the distractors are built from common arithmetic slips. Unless a question says otherwise, use the FY 2025-26 (AY 2026-27) rules and add 4% Health & Education Cess where stated.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Under the new tax regime (FY 2025-26), Mr. Arun has a total income of exactly ₹12,00,000. What is his final tax payable (after 87A rebate, before cess)?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹0  C) ₹71,250  D) ₹90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — New-regime slabs FY 2025-26: 0–4L nil; 4–8L @5% = ₹20,000; 8–12L @10% = ₹40,000. Tax before rebate = ₹20,000 + ₹40,000 = ₹60,000. Section 87A gives a rebate of up to ₹60,000 when total income is ≤ ₹12,00,000, so the rebate wipes out the entire ₹60,000. Tax payable = ₹0. A) is the pre-rebate tax. C)/D) ignore the rebate. The trick: at exactly ₹12L the rebate fully covers the tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A resident individual (age 45) opts for the OLD regime in FY 2025-26 with total income of ₹4,80,000. Tax payable after 87A rebate and 4% cess is:\nA) ₹11,960  B) ₹0  C) ₹11,500  D) ₹24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Old-regime slabs: 0–2.5L nil; 2.5L–5L @5%. Tax = 5% × (4,80,000 − 2,50,000) = 5% × 2,30,000 = ₹11,500. Section 87A (old regime) gives a rebate of up to ₹12,500 when total income ≤ ₹5,00,000. Since ₹11,500 < ₹12,500, the whole tax is rebated → ₹0, so no cess either. A) adds cess to the un-rebated figure; C) forgets the rebate; D) misapplies a flat rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Mr. Bose (new regime, FY 2025-26) has total income of ₹16,00,000. Tax before cess is:\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹1,10,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Slabs: 0–4L nil; 4–8L @5% = ₹20,000; 8–12L @10% = ₹40,000; 12–16L @15% = ₹60,000. Total = 20,000 + 40,000 + 60,000 = ₹1,20,000. Income exceeds ₹12L so no 87A rebate. B) wrongly taxes the whole ₹16L slab-by-slab using 15% on too wide a band; C)/D) are common mis-slabs.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An Indian citizen with Indian income below ₹15 lakh comes on a visit to India and stays 181 days in FY 2025-26 (he was a non-resident in all earlier years). His residential status is:\nA) Resident and Ordinarily Resident  B) Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident  C) Non-Resident  D) Deemed Resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For an Indian citizen/PIO visiting India whose Indian income is up to ₹15 lakh, the basic test is 182 days or more in the relevant year (the 60-day + 365-day test does not apply to such visitors). 181 days is one day short of 182, so he is a Non-Resident. A)/B) require him to first qualify as resident, which he doesn't. D) deemed-resident applies only when Indian income exceeds ₹15 lakh and he isn't taxed anywhere else.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Mr. Khan stayed in India for 70 days in FY 2025-26 and 400 days in total across the 4 preceding years. He is not an Indian citizen and not of Indian origin (foreign national). His residential status is:\nA) Non-Resident  B) Deemed Resident  C) Cannot be determined  D) Resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For an ordinary foreign national, the resident test is: 182+ days in the year, OR 60+ days in the year AND 365+ days in the 4 preceding years. He has 70 days (≥60) in the year and 400 days (≥365) in the preceding 4 years, so both parts of the second test are satisfied → Resident. A) ignores the 60+365 test; B) needs Indian income > ₹15L; C) is determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** For a resident to be \"Ordinarily Resident,\" he must NOT satisfy either NOR condition. Which stay makes a resident \"Not Ordinarily Resident\"?\nA) In India 730 days or more in the preceding 7 years  B) Resident in 8 of the 10 preceding years  C) In India 729 days or less in the preceding 7 years  D) In India 182 days in the current year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A resident is NOR if he was a non-resident in 9 of the 10 preceding years, OR was in India for 729 days or less in the 7 preceding years. So \"729 days or less\" triggers NOR. A) is the opposite (it would make him ordinarily resident). B) is about residency count but the threshold is \"non-resident in 9 of 10,\" not \"resident in 8 of 10.\" D) is just the basic resident test.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Ms. Rao (new regime) has gross salary ₹9,75,000 and no other income. After the standard deduction, her total income is:\nA) ₹9,00,000  B) ₹9,25,000  C) ₹9,75,000  D) ₹8,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Standard deduction under the new regime is ₹75,000. Total income = 9,75,000 − 75,000 = ₹9,00,000. B) uses the old-regime ₹50,000; C) forgets the deduction; D) subtracts ₹1,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Under section 80C, the maximum deduction (FY 2025-26, old regime) for LIC premium, PPF, ELSS and principal repayment of home loan together is:\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 80C (read with the 80CCE aggregate cap on 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1)) caps the total deduction at ₹1,50,000, no matter how many eligible items you add up. A) confuses it with the home-loan interest cap (₹2L u/s 24(b)); B)/D) are made-up.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Mr. Sen, age 50, paid ₹28,000 health insurance premium for self+spouse+kids and ₹46,000 for his senior-citizen parents. His section 80D deduction (old regime) is:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹71,000  C) ₹74,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 80D allows ₹25,000 for self+family (all under 60) and an extra ₹50,000 for senior-citizen parents. Self bucket: min(28,000, 25,000) = ₹25,000. Parents bucket: min(46,000, 50,000) = ₹46,000. Total = 25,000 + 46,000 = ₹71,000. A) counts only one bucket; C) wrongly adds the full ₹28,000 to ₹46,000; D) ignores the parents' premium entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The additional NPS deduction under section 80CCD(1B), over and above the ₹1.5 lakh 80C/80CCE limit (old regime), is up to:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 80CCD(1B) allows an extra ₹50,000 for the employee's own NPS contribution, separate from the ₹1.5 lakh aggregate cap. So old-regime taxpayers can claim ₹1,50,000 (80C) + ₹50,000 (80CCD(1B)) = ₹2,00,000 total. A)/B)/D) are wrong figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A senior citizen (age 68) earns ₹62,000 interest on a bank fixed deposit. Under section 80TTB (old regime), the deduction is:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹62,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 80TTB lets a senior citizen deduct interest from bank/post-office/co-operative deposits up to ₹50,000. So deduction = min(62,000, 50,000) = ₹50,000; ₹12,000 remains taxable. A) is the 80TTA limit (for non-seniors, savings interest only); B) wrongly exempts the whole amount; D) is a wrong cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Mr. Iyer (non-senior) earns ₹14,500 savings-account interest and ₹30,000 FD interest. His section 80TTA deduction is:\nA) ₹14,500  B) ₹44,500  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 80TTA covers only SAVINGS-account interest (not FD interest), up to ₹10,000, for those below 60. Savings interest is ₹14,500, capped at ₹10,000. FD interest of ₹30,000 gets no 80TTA benefit. So deduction = ₹10,000. A) ignores the cap; B) wrongly includes FD; C) uses the 80TTB senior limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A self-occupied house (old regime) has nil annual value and the owner paid ₹2,60,000 home-loan interest. The income/(loss) from house property is:\nA) (₹2,60,000)  B) Nil  C) (₹2,00,000)  D) (₹30,000)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a self-occupied property under the old regime, interest under section 24(b) is capped at ₹2,00,000. Annual value is nil, so income = 0 − 2,00,000 = (₹2,00,000) loss. A) ignores the ₹2L cap; B) is the new-regime answer (no interest deduction for self-occupied); D) is the ₹30,000 cap used only when construction exceeds 5 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A let-out house: annual rent ₹4,80,000, municipal taxes paid ₹30,000. Net Annual Value and the section 24(a) standard deduction are:\nA) NAV ₹4,80,000; SD ₹1,44,000  B) NAV ₹4,50,000; SD ₹1,50,000  C) NAV ₹4,50,000; SD ₹1,35,000  D) NAV ₹4,80,000; SD ₹1,35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — NAV = Annual Value − municipal taxes = 4,80,000 − 30,000 = ₹4,50,000. Standard deduction u/s 24(a) = 30% of NAV = 30% × 4,50,000 = ₹1,35,000. A)/D) forget to subtract municipal tax before applying 30%; B) applies 30% wrongly (₹1,35,000 is correct, not ₹1,50,000).\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** LTCG on listed equity shares (STT paid) is ₹3,00,000 for the year. Tax under section 112A (before cess) is:\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹21,875  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 112A: LTCG on listed equity is taxed at 12.5% only on the amount EXCEEDING the ₹1,25,000 exemption. Taxable LTCG = 3,00,000 − 1,25,000 = 1,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × 1,75,000 = ₹21,875. A) taxes the whole ₹3L at 12.5% (forgets exemption); C) uses 20%; D) is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** STCG on listed equity shares (STT paid) under section 111A is ₹2,00,000. The tax (before cess) is:\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 111A taxes STCG on listed equity (STT paid) at a flat 20% (post-23 July 2024 rate). Tax = 20% × 2,00,000 = ₹40,000. There is no ₹1.25L exemption for STCG (that applies only to 112A LTCG). B) uses 12.5%; C)/D) use old/incorrect rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Listed equity shares bought 1 Aug 2024 and sold 15 Jun 2025. The gain is:\nA) Long-term (held > 12 months)  B) Long-term (held > 24 months)  C) Exempt  D) Short-term (held ≤ 12 months)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Holding period for listed equity is 12 months. From 1 Aug 2024 to 15 Jun 2025 is about 10.5 months, i.e. ≤ 12 months, so it is short-term. A) misjudges the period; B) uses the 24-month period (for unlisted shares/property); C) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Unlisted shares are sold after a holding of 20 months. The gain is:\nA) Long-term  B) Exempt under 112A  C) Short-term  D) Taxed at 12.5% with ₹1.25L exemption\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For unlisted shares the long-term threshold is 24 months. 20 months ≤ 24 months → short-term, added to income and taxed at slab rates. A) needs > 24 months; B)/D) apply only to listed equity (STT-paid) under 112A.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An employee covered by the Payment of Gratuity Act has last-drawn salary (basic+DA) ₹52,000/month and 24 years of service. The formula amount of gratuity is (round years not required here):\nA) ₹7,20,000  B) ₹6,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹7,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gratuity formula (covered employees) = last-drawn salary × 15/26 × completed years = 52,000 × 15/26 × 24. 52,000 × 15 = 7,80,000; ÷ 26 = 30,000; × 24 = ₹7,20,000. B)/C) use wrong day counts; D) forgets to divide by 26 properly.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The maximum gratuity exemption limit under section 10(10) for a covered private-sector employee is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The least of (i) ₹20,00,000, (ii) 15 days' salary per completed year, and (iii) actual gratuity received is exempt. The statutory monetary ceiling is ₹20,00,000. A)/D) are outdated/wrong; B) is invented.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Under EPF, employee contributes 12% of basic. For an employee with basic ₹40,000/month, the EPS portion of the employer's contribution (EPS wage ceiling ₹15,000) per month is:\nA) ₹3,332  B) ₹4,800  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹1,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The employer's 12% splits into 8.33% to EPS and 3.67% to EPF, but EPS is computed on the wage ceiling of ₹15,000. EPS = 8.33% × 15,000 = ₹1,249.5 ≈ ₹1,250. A) applies 8.33% to ₹40,000; B) is 12% of ₹40,000; D) is 12% of ₹15,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** At age 60, NPS allows a tax-free lump-sum withdrawal of up to what fraction of the corpus, with the rest used to buy an annuity?\nA) 40% lump sum, 60% annuity  B) 100% lump sum  C) 50% lump sum, 50% annuity  D) 60% lump sum, 40% annuity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At 60, up to 60% of the NPS corpus can be withdrawn as a tax-free lump sum, and at least 40% must be used to purchase an annuity (the annuity income is then taxable as it is received). A) reverses the split; B)/C) are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Employer's contribution to NPS for a PRIVATE-sector employee is deductible under section 80CCD(2) (new regime) up to what % of salary?\nA) 10%  B) 12%  C) 14%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the new regime, 80CCD(2) allows the employer's NPS contribution as a deduction up to 14% of salary for private-sector employees (same 14% as government employees). Under the OLD regime the private-sector limit is 10%. A) is the old-regime private limit; B) is the EPF rate; D) is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A partnership firm's income exceeds ₹1 crore. Its effective tax rate (30% base, 12% surcharge, 4% cess) is:\nA) 33.6%  B) 31.2%  C) 34.944%  D) 42.744%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Effective rate = base × (1 + surcharge) × (1 + cess) = 30% × 1.12 × 1.04 = 34.944%. A) skips the cess; B) skips the surcharge; D) is the individual Maximum Marginal Rate (37% surcharge).\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The Maximum Marginal Rate (MMR) for an individual (30% slab, 37% surcharge, 4% cess) is:\nA) 39% B) 34.944% C) 35.535% D) 42.744%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Surcharge = 30% × 37% = 11.1%; subtotal 41.1%; cess = 41.1% × 4% = 1.644%; MMR = 30 + 11.1 + 1.644 = 42.744%. A) ignores cess and uses a wrong surcharge; B) is the firm rate; C) is a distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Mr. Verma gifts ₹25,00,000 to his wife, who invests it and earns ₹1,80,000 interest. How is that interest taxed?\nA) Taxed in the wife's hands at her slab  B) Exempt (gift between spouses)  C) Clubbed in Mr. Verma's income and taxed at his slab  D) Split 50:50 between them\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under clubbing (section 64), income earned by a spouse from an asset gifted by the other spouse is clubbed in the transferor's total income. So ₹1,80,000 is added to Mr. Verma's income and taxed at his slab. The gift itself is exempt, but the income it generates is clubbed — A)/B)/D) miss this.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Long-term capital loss can be set off against:\nA) Any capital gain (short- or long-term)  B) Salary income  C) Only long-term capital gains  D) Any head of income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A long-term capital loss can be set off only against long-term capital gains (intra-head). It cannot touch STCG or any other head. A) describes how a SHORT-term loss can be used; B)/D) are not allowed for capital losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A house-property loss that cannot be set off within the head can be set off against other heads in the same year (old regime) only up to:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) Unlimited  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Inter-head set-off of house-property loss is restricted to ₹2,00,000 per year under the old regime (the balance is carried forward). Under the new regime this inter-head set-off is not allowed at all. A) is the 80C limit; B)/D) are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A non-speculative business loss can be carried forward for how many assessment years, and set off against what?\nA) 4 years; only speculative profit  B) 8 years; any business income  C) 8 years; only salary  D) 4 years; any income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Non-speculative business loss is carried forward for 8 years and, in later years, set off against business income (speculative or non-speculative). (In the current year it can be set off against any head except salary.) A) describes speculative loss (4 years, speculative profit only); C)/D) are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Dividend income received by a resident shareholder from a domestic company is taxed:\nA) As capital gains at 12.5%  B) Exempt up to ₹10 lakh  C) Under \"Income from Other Sources\" at the shareholder's slab rate  D) At a flat 10% DDT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since DDT was abolished (FY 2020-21), dividends are taxable in the shareholder's hands under \"Income from Other Sources\" at the applicable slab rate. A) is wrong head; B) was an old exemption that no longer applies; D) DDT was abolished.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Mr. Das (old regime) has GTI ₹8,50,000 and claims 80C ₹1,50,000 and 80D ₹25,000. His total income is:\nA) ₹6,75,000  B) ₹7,00,000  C) ₹8,25,000  D) ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total income = GTI − Chapter VI-A deductions = 8,50,000 − 1,50,000 − 25,000 = ₹6,75,000. B) forgets 80D; C) forgets 80C; D) over-deducts.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Under the new regime FY 2025-26, what is the tax (before cess) on a total income of ₹20,00,000?\nA) ₹2,60,000  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Slabs: 0–4L nil; 4–8L @5% = ₹20,000; 8–12L @10% = ₹40,000; 12–16L @15% = ₹60,000; 16–20L @20% = ₹80,000. Total = 20,000 + 40,000 + 60,000 + 80,000 = ₹2,00,000. A) wrongly adds a 25% band that starts only above ₹20L; B)/D) mis-slab the bands.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Real return when nominal return is 11% and inflation is 6% is closest to:\nA) 4.72%  B) 5.00%  C) 17.66%  D) 4.66%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Real return = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.11/1.06 − 1 = 1.04717 − 1 = 0.04717 ≈ 4.72%. B) is the simple subtraction 11% − 6% (overstates the real return); C) adds them; D) is a rounding distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An asset grew from ₹2,00,000 to ₹3,38,000 over 4 years. The CAGR is closest to:\nA) 14.0%  B) 17.25%  C) 11.06%  D) 13.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — CAGR = (End/Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (3,38,000/2,00,000)^(1/4) − 1 = (1.69)^0.25 − 1. 1.69^0.5 = 1.3 and 1.3^0.5 ≈ 1.1402, so 1.69^0.25 ≈ 1.1402 → 14.02% ≈ 14.0%. B) divides total growth by years (simple); C)/D) are wrong roots.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A goal costs ₹5,00,000 today. With 7% inflation, its cost in 10 years (use 1.07^10 ≈ 1.967) is closest to:\nA) ₹8,50,000  B) ₹7,50,000  C) ₹6,75,000  D) ₹9,83,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = present cost × (1 + inflation)^n = 5,00,000 × 1.967 = ₹9,83,500. A)/B)/C) understate the compounding. The lesson: inflation roughly doubles a cost in ~10 years at 7%.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Under the new regime, which deduction is STILL available?\nA) 80C (₹1.5 lakh)  B) 80D (health insurance)  C) 80CCD(2) (employer NPS)  D) 80TTA (savings interest)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Most Chapter VI-A deductions are withdrawn in the new regime, but employer's NPS contribution under 80CCD(2) (up to 14% of salary for private employees) is still allowed, as is the higher ₹75,000 standard deduction. A)/B)/D) are not available in the new regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mr. Pillai has salary income ₹6,00,000 and a self-occupied-house interest loss of ₹2,00,000 (old regime). His GTI is:\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The self-occupied house income is (₹2,00,000) (interest, capped at ₹2L). Set off against salary: 6,00,000 − 2,00,000 = ₹4,00,000 GTI. A) ignores the set-off; C) adds instead of subtracting; D) is just the loss.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A resident individual (old regime) has total income of ₹4,95,000. After 87A rebate, the tax (before cess) is:\nA) ₹12,250  B) ₹0  C) ₹9,750  D) ₹24,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tax = 5% × (4,95,000 − 2,50,000) = 5% × 2,45,000 = ₹12,250. Since total income ≤ ₹5,00,000, 87A rebate of up to ₹12,500 applies and covers the ₹12,250 fully → tax ₹0. A) forgets the rebate; C)/D) mis-slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Gift of shares with FMV ₹45,000 received from a friend (non-relative), no consideration. Taxable amount under \"Other Sources\":\nA) ₹45,000  B) Nil  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Movable property (incl. shares) received without consideration is taxable only if the aggregate FMV exceeds ₹50,000 in the year. ₹45,000 ≤ ₹50,000, so it is fully exempt (nil). Note: if it had exceeded ₹50,000, the WHOLE amount would be taxed, not just the excess. A) taxes it; C)/D) misuse the threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A resident and ordinarily resident (ROR) earns ₹2,00,000 interest in a US bank account. In India this interest is:\nA) Not taxable (foreign income)  B) Taxable only if remitted to India  C) Taxable at a flat 12.5%  D) Taxable (worldwide income)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An ROR is taxed on worldwide income, so the US interest is taxable in India (with DTAA credit for any US tax paid). A) would be true only for a Non-Resident or RNOR; B) remittance is irrelevant for an ROR; C) it is taxed at slab rates, not 12.5%.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Full income-tax computation: salaried professional, old vs new regime\n\nMs. Nandita (age 38, resident) has the following figures for FY 2025-26:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary (basic + allowances, taxable) | 14,75,000 |\n| Savings-bank interest | 12,000 |\n| 80C investments (PPF + ELSS + LIC) | 1,70,000 |\n| 80D health insurance (self + family, all < 60) | 22,000 |\n| Employer's NPS contribution (within 80CCD(2) limit) | 90,000 |\n| Own NPS contribution under 80CCD(1B) | 50,000 |\n\nAssume she compares the OLD regime (std deduction ₹50,000) and NEW regime (std deduction ₹75,000). Cess is 4%.\n\n**Q41.** Her income from salary after the OLD-regime standard deduction (employer NPS already counted in gross salary, deductible separately) is:\nA) ₹14,25,000  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹14,75,000  D) ₹13,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Old-regime standard deduction is ₹50,000. Salary income = 14,75,000 − 50,000 = ₹14,25,000. B) uses ₹75,000; C) forgets the deduction; D) over-deducts.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Her Gross Total Income (OLD regime) including savings interest is:\nA) ₹14,37,000  B) ₹14,25,000  C) ₹14,87,000  D) ₹14,12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — GTI = salary after std deduction (₹14,25,000) + savings interest (₹12,000) = ₹14,37,000. (Chapter VI-A deductions are subtracted AFTER GTI.) B) omits interest; C) double-counts; D) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Total Chapter VI-A deductions she can claim under the OLD regime are:\nA) ₹2,42,000  B) ₹2,52,000  C) ₹3,22,000  D) ₹2,30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 80C: ₹1,70,000 capped at ₹1,50,000. 80CCD(1B): ₹50,000. 80D: ₹22,000 (within the ₹25,000 limit). 80CCD(2) employer NPS: ₹90,000. 80TTA: savings interest ₹12,000 capped at ₹10,000. Total = 1,50,000 + 50,000 + 22,000 + 90,000 + 10,000 = ₹3,22,000. A) omits 80D and 80TTA; B) omits the ₹90,000 employer-NPS deduction; D) uses the uncapped 80C wrongly.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Under the NEW regime, which of her listed deductions survive, and what is the standard deduction?\nA) Only 80CCD(2) ₹90,000; std deduction ₹75,000  B) 80C + 80D survive; std deduction ₹50,000  C) All survive; std deduction ₹75,000  D) None survive; std deduction ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In the new regime, 80C, 80D, 80CCD(1B) and 80TTA are all disallowed; only the employer-NPS deduction under 80CCD(2) (₹90,000) survives, and the standard deduction is the higher ₹75,000. B)/C)/D) misstate which survive.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Her Total Income under the NEW regime (salary ₹14,75,000 − std ded ₹75,000 − 80CCD(2) ₹90,000; savings interest fully taxable) is:\nA) ₹13,22,000  B) ₹13,10,000  C) ₹14,00,000  D) ₹13,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Salary after std deduction = 14,75,000 − 75,000 = 14,00,000; less 80CCD(2) 90,000 = 13,10,000; add savings interest 12,000 (no 80TTA in new regime) = ₹13,22,000. B) forgets the interest; C) forgets 80CCD(2); D) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Full income-tax computation: business income + house property + capital gains\n\nMr. Reddy (age 45, resident, OLD regime) reports for FY 2025-26:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|\n| Net business income | 9,00,000 |\n| Let-out house: annual rent | 3,60,000 |\n| Municipal taxes paid on the house | 24,000 |\n| Home-loan interest on the let-out house | 2,80,000 |\n| STCG on listed equity (STT paid, sec 111A) | 1,50,000 |\n| 80C investments | 1,50,000 |\n\n**Q46.** The Net Annual Value (NAV) of the let-out house is:\nA) ₹3,60,000  B) ₹3,36,000  C) ₹3,12,000  D) ₹3,30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NAV = Annual Value − municipal taxes = 3,60,000 − 24,000 = ₹3,36,000. A) forgets municipal tax; C)/D) use wrong subtractions.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Income from the let-out house (after 30% standard deduction and full interest) is:\nA) (₹44,800)  B) (₹56,000)  C) ₹0  D) (₹1,00,000)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Standard deduction u/s 24(a) = 30% × NAV = 30% × 3,36,000 = ₹1,00,800. Income = NAV − 30% SD − interest = 3,36,000 − 1,00,800 − 2,80,000 = (₹44,800) loss. For a let-out house there is NO ₹2L interest cap, so the full ₹2,80,000 interest is allowed. B) uses a ₹1,00,000 SD; C) ignores the loss; D) is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** This house-property loss of ₹44,800 (old regime) is set off against business income. After set-off, business + house income is:\nA) ₹8,55,200  B) ₹9,00,000  C) ₹8,56,000  D) ₹6,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Inter-head set-off (within the ₹2L limit, so fully allowed) = 9,00,000 − 44,800 = ₹8,55,200. B) ignores the set-off; C) uses the ₹44,000 figure; D) over-deducts.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** The STCG of ₹1,50,000 under section 111A is taxed:\nA) At slab rates with the rest of his income  B) At 12.5% above ₹1.25 lakh  C) Exempt  D) At a flat 20% separately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 111A STCG on listed equity (STT paid) is taxed at a flat 20%, computed separately from slab income. Tax = 20% × 1,50,000 = ₹30,000. A) is wrong (it's a special rate); B) is the 112A LTCG rule; C) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** His Total Income (the slab portion, after 80C) — i.e. business + house property after set-off, less 80C — is (STCG taxed separately):\nA) ₹7,05,200  B) ₹8,55,200  C) ₹7,00,000  D) ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Slab-income GTI = ₹8,55,200; less 80C ₹1,50,000 = ₹7,05,200. The STCG ₹1,50,000 is taxed separately at 20% and is not reduced by 80C. B) forgets 80C; C)/D) mis-subtract.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Capital gains: equity, bonus shares and LTCG exemption\n\nMr. Ghosh bought 8,000 listed shares of ABC Ltd at ₹150 on 10 May 2023. The company issued 1:1 bonus shares on 1 Sept 2024 (bonus cost = nil). He sold ALL 16,000 shares at ₹260 on 20 May 2025. STT was paid on sale. (Use FMV/grandfathering as not applicable — shares bought after 31 Jan 2018.)\n\n**Q51.** The gain on the 8,000 ORIGINAL shares (bought 10 May 2023, sold 20 May 2025) is classified as:\nA) Short-term  B) Long-term (held > 12 months)  C) Long-term (held > 24 months)  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed-equity long-term threshold is 12 months. From 10 May 2023 to 20 May 2025 is about 24 months (> 12 months) → long-term. C) uses the unlisted/property 24-month rule wording; A)/D) are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** The 8,000 BONUS shares (allotted 1 Sept 2024, sold 20 May 2025) are:\nA) Long-term, cost ₹150  B) Short-term, cost nil  C) Long-term, cost nil  D) Short-term, cost ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus-share holding starts from the date of allotment (1 Sept 2024). To 20 May 2025 is about 8.5 months (≤ 12 months) → short-term. Their cost of acquisition is nil. A)/C) misjudge the period; D) wrongly assigns a cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** LTCG on the original 8,000 shares = sale value − cost. This equals:\nA) ₹8,80,000  B) ₹20,80,000  C) ₹12,00,000  D) ₹6,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sale = 8,000 × 260 = ₹20,80,000; cost = 8,000 × 150 = ₹12,00,000; LTCG = 20,80,000 − 12,00,000 = ₹8,80,000. B) is sale value only; C) is cost only; D) miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** STCG on the 8,000 bonus shares (cost nil) is:\nA) ₹12,00,000  B) ₹8,80,000  C) ₹20,80,000 less ₹1.25 lakh  D) ₹20,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sale = 8,000 × 260 = ₹20,80,000; cost nil; STCG = ₹20,80,000. There is no ₹1.25 lakh exemption for STCG. A)/B) are wrong; C) wrongly applies the LTCG exemption to STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Total capital-gains tax (before cess): LTCG ₹8,80,000 under 112A and STCG ₹20,80,000 under 111A. Tax is closest to:\nA) ₹5,10,375  B) ₹5,26,000  C) ₹1,10,000  D) ₹4,87,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG 112A: taxable = 8,80,000 − 1,25,000 = 7,55,000; tax = 12.5% × 7,55,000 = ₹94,375. STCG 111A: 20% × 20,80,000 = ₹4,16,000. Total = 94,375 + 4,16,000 = ₹5,10,375. B) forgets the LTCG exemption (12.5% × 8,80,000 + 4,16,000); D) taxes the full LTCG and STCG at wrong rates; C) taxes only the LTCG.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Retirement: gratuity, EPF and NPS at superannuation\n\nMr. Krishnan retires on 31 March 2026 after 30 completed years of service (covered by the Payment of Gratuity Act). Last-drawn salary (basic + DA) = ₹78,000/month. He actually received gratuity of ₹14,00,000. His NPS corpus at 60 is ₹50,00,000.\n\n**Q56.** The gratuity FORMULA amount (15/26 × last salary × years) is:\nA) ₹13,50,000  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula = 78,000 × 15/26 × 30. 78,000 × 15 = 11,70,000; ÷ 26 = 45,000; × 30 = ₹13,50,000. B) is the actual received; C) is the statutory cap; D) is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The exempt gratuity is the LEAST of: ₹20,00,000 cap, formula ₹13,50,000, actual ₹14,00,000. Exempt amount is:\nA) ₹14,00,000  B) ₹13,50,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exemption is the least of the three: min(20,00,000; 13,50,000; 14,00,000) = ₹13,50,000. A) is the actual received (not the least); C) is the cap; D) is the taxable balance, not the exempt amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The TAXABLE portion of his gratuity is:\nA) ₹0  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹6,00,000  D) ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable = actual received − exempt = 14,00,000 − 13,50,000 = ₹50,000. A) wrongly assumes all exempt; C)/D) miscompute.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** At 60, the maximum NPS lump-sum he can take TAX-FREE from a ₹50,00,000 corpus is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Up to 60% of the corpus is a tax-free lump sum = 60% × 50,00,000 = ₹30,00,000. A) is 40%; C) is 50%; D) would need full withdrawal which isn't allowed tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** The remaining 40% (₹20,00,000) must be used to buy an annuity. The annuity income he later receives is:\nA) Tax-free  B) Taxed at 12.5%  C) Taxed as capital gains  D) Taxable as income when received\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The 40% annuity-purchase amount itself is not taxed at purchase, but the periodic annuity (pension) payments are taxable as income in the years received. A) is wrong; B)/C) apply wrong heads/rates.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Insurance: human life value, health-insurance claim mechanics\n\nThe Mehta family is reviewing cover. Mr. Mehta (age 40) earns ₹15,00,000 p.a.; he plans to work 20 more years; personal expenses are ₹3,00,000 p.a.; existing life cover ₹50,00,000. Separately, the family has a health policy with sum insured ₹5,00,000, a 10% co-pay clause and a ₹15,000 deductible. A hospital bill of ₹3,15,000 is raised.\n\n**Q61.** Using the income-replacement (HLV) approach with income net of self-maintenance, the income available for the family each year is:\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹18,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Income available to dependents = total income − the earner's own personal expenses = 15,00,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹12,00,000 per year. A) ignores self-maintenance; C) is only the expenses; D) adds instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Ignoring discounting (simple method), the human life value over 20 years on that ₹12,00,000 net income is:\nA) ₹2,40,00,000  B) ₹3,00,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹1,20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Simple HLV = net annual income × years = 12,00,000 × 20 = ₹2,40,00,000. B) uses gross income; C)/D) use wrong multipliers. (A discounted PV would be lower, but the question asks the simple, non-discounted figure.)\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Given existing cover of ₹50,00,000, the ADDITIONAL life cover the family needs (simple HLV basis) is:\nA) ₹2,40,00,000  B) ₹1,90,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹2,90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Additional need = HLV − existing cover = 2,40,00,000 − 50,00,000 = ₹1,90,00,000. A) ignores existing cover; C) is the existing cover; D) adds it.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** On the ₹3,15,000 bill, the deductible of ₹15,000 is borne by the insured first. The amount remaining after the deductible is:\nA) ₹3,15,000  B) ₹2,85,000  C) ₹2,70,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A deductible is the fixed amount the insured pays before the insurer considers the claim. Remaining = 3,15,000 − 15,000 = ₹3,00,000. A) ignores the deductible; B)/C) subtract wrong amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** After the ₹15,000 deductible, a 10% co-pay applies to the ₹3,00,000. The insurer finally pays:\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹2,70,000  C) ₹2,83,500  D) ₹2,55,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Co-pay = 10% of the post-deductible amount = 10% × 3,00,000 = ₹30,000 (paid by insured). Insurer pays 3,00,000 − 30,000 = ₹2,70,000 (well within the ₹5,00,000 sum insured). The insured's total out-of-pocket = 15,000 + 30,000 = ₹45,000. A) ignores co-pay; C) applies co-pay to the wrong base; D) double-counts.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated case: residential status + multi-head computation + regime choice\n\nMr. Sharma, an Indian citizen, returned to India on 1 October 2025 after 6 years abroad (non-resident in each of those years). In FY 2025-26 he stayed in India 183 days. His FY 2025-26 figures:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|\n| Indian salary (after returning) | 11,00,000 |\n| Foreign bank interest (accrued abroad) | 4,00,000 |\n| Indian savings-bank interest | 8,000 |\n| 80C investments | 1,20,000 |\n| 80D (self, < 60) | 18,000 |\n\n**Q66.** Based on 183 days' stay, his basic residential status for FY 2025-26 is:\nA) Non-Resident  B) Resident  C) Deemed Resident  D) Resident but cannot be ROR\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 183 days ≥ 182 days, so he is a Resident under the basic test. A) is wrong (he crosses 182); C) deemed-resident needs Indian income > ₹15L and no tax abroad; D) is premature — we still test ROR/RNOR next.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** He was a non-resident in all 6 preceding years (so non-resident in at least 9 of 10? — he was abroad 6 years; before that he lived in India). Given he satisfies \"729 days or less in the 7 preceding years,\" he is:\nA) Resident and Ordinarily Resident  B) Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident  C) Non-Resident  D) Deemed Resident\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A resident becomes RNOR if he satisfies EITHER NOR condition. Having been abroad 6 full years means he was in India for 729 days or fewer in the 7 preceding years → RNOR. A) requires him to fail both NOR tests; C) he is resident; D) needs the income/abroad-tax test.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** As an RNOR, his ₹4,00,000 foreign bank interest (accrued abroad, not from a business controlled in India) is:\nA) Taxable in India  B) Taxable at 12.5%  C) Taxable only if remitted  D) Not taxable in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — An RNOR is taxed only on Indian income (plus foreign income from a business controlled in/profession set up in India). Plain foreign bank interest is NOT taxable in India for an RNOR. A) would be true only for an ROR; B)/C) are wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** His Gross Total Income in India (OLD regime: salary ₹11,00,000 − std ded ₹50,000 + Indian savings interest ₹8,000; foreign interest excluded) is:\nA) ₹10,58,000  B) ₹14,58,000  C) ₹11,08,000  D) ₹10,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Salary after std deduction = 11,00,000 − 50,000 = 10,50,000; add Indian savings interest 8,000 = ₹10,58,000. Foreign interest is excluded (RNOR). B) wrongly includes the foreign interest; C) forgets the std deduction; D) omits the interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** His Total Income (OLD regime) after 80C ₹1,20,000, 80D ₹18,000 and 80TTA (savings interest, capped ₹10,000 but only ₹8,000 here) is:\nA) ₹9,12,000  B) ₹9,20,000  C) ₹9,30,000  D) ₹9,08,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — GTI ₹10,58,000 less 80C ₹1,20,000, less 80D ₹18,000, less 80TTA ₹8,000 (actual savings interest, within the ₹10,000 cap) = 10,58,000 − 1,20,000 − 18,000 − 8,000 = ₹9,12,000. B) deducts a flat ₹10,000 TTA (but only ₹8,000 interest exists); C) forgets 80D; D) miscomputes.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "06", "md": "# Mock Paper 6 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This is a numerical, case-heavy paper spanning the full syllabus. Keep a calculator or a spreadsheet handy — most questions need you to plug numbers into a formula, so work each step carefully before picking an option.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Mr. Rao sells listed equity shares on 10-Aug-2025 for ₹6,50,000. He had bought them on 1-Feb-2023 for ₹4,00,000 and paid STT on both sides. What is his tax on this gain (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹15,625  B) ₹14,063  C) ₹31,250  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The shares were held from Feb-2023 to Aug-2025, i.e. more than 12 months, so this is a **long-term capital gain (LTCG)** on listed equity taxed under **Section 112A**. Formula: Gain = Sale − Cost = ₹6,50,000 − ₹4,00,000 = ₹2,50,000. Section 112A gives a ₹1,25,000 exemption, then taxes the rest at **12.5%**. Taxable LTCG = ₹2,50,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹1,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹1,25,000 = **₹15,625**. Option C (₹31,250) forgets the exemption (12.5% × ₹2,50,000). Option D wrongly applies 20% to the gain. Option B (₹14,063) is a rounding distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An investor sells an equity mutual fund (listed) after holding it for 7 months, booking a gain of ₹80,000 (STT paid). What is the tax under the applicable section (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹12,000  C) ₹16,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Holding period is 7 months, which is **not more than 12 months**, so this is a **short-term capital gain (STCG)** on an equity-oriented fund, taxed under **Section 111A at 20%**. Formula: Tax = 20% × Gain = 20% × ₹80,000 = **₹16,000**. There is no ₹1,25,000 exemption for STCG (that applies only to LTCG under 112A), so D is wrong. A (₹10,000) uses the old 12.5% LTCG rate, and B (₹12,000) uses the outdated 15% STCG rate. The correct current STCG rate under 111A is 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Ms. Iyer sells a residential plot on 1-Sep-2025 for ₹95,00,000. She had purchased it on 1-Jan-2021 for ₹55,00,000. What is her capital gains tax (ignore exemptions, surcharge and cess)?\nA) ₹8,00,000  B) ₹12,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Immovable property (land) held for more than **24 months** is a long-term capital asset. For transfers on or after 23-Jul-2024, LTCG on property is taxed at **12.5% without indexation** under Section 112. Gain = ₹95,00,000 − ₹55,00,000 = ₹40,00,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹5,00,000**. Option A applies the old 20% rate. Option C (₹4,00,000) wrongly uses 10%, and B (₹12,00,000) is an arbitrary distractor. The post-July-2024 rule is a flat 12.5% with no indexation.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A salaried individual under the **new tax regime (FY 2025-26)** has gross salary of ₹14,00,000. After the standard deduction, what is her taxable salary income?\nA) ₹12,50,000  B) ₹13,50,000  C) ₹14,00,000  D) ₹13,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under the **new regime**, the standard deduction for salaried individuals is **₹75,000** (it is ₹50,000 in the old regime). Taxable salary = Gross salary − Standard deduction = ₹14,00,000 − ₹75,000 = **₹13,25,000**. Option B uses the old-regime ₹50,000. Option C forgets the deduction. Option A over-deducts ₹1,50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Under the **new regime FY 2025-26 slabs** (0–4L nil, 4–8L 5%, 8–12L 10%, 12–16L 15%, 16–20L 20%, 20–24L 25%, above 24L 30%), what is the income tax (before cess) on a total income of ₹16,00,000?\nA) ₹1,40,000  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tax is computed slab by slab: 0–4L → nil; 4–8L (₹4,00,000 × 5%) = ₹20,000; 8–12L (₹4,00,000 × 10%) = ₹40,000; 12–16L (₹4,00,000 × 15%) = ₹60,000. Total = ₹20,000 + ₹40,000 + ₹60,000 = **₹1,20,000**. At exactly ₹16,00,000 nothing enters the 20% band. Option A (₹1,40,000) over-counts. Options C and D apply flat rates instead of the slab method.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A resident individual under the new regime FY 2025-26 has total income of exactly ₹12,00,000. After the Section 87A rebate, what is the tax payable (before cess)?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹40,000  C) Nil  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the new regime FY 2025-26, a resident individual with total income up to **₹12,00,000** gets a **Section 87A rebate of up to ₹60,000**, which fully wipes out the tax. Slab tax on ₹12,00,000 = 5% × 4L + 10% × 4L = ₹20,000 + ₹40,000 = ₹60,000. Rebate = ₹60,000, so tax payable = **Nil**. Options A and B ignore the rebate. The rebate exactly offsets the ₹60,000 tax, leaving nothing payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Mr. Khan invests ₹2,00,000 in instruments eligible under Section 80C in the **old regime**. He also pays ₹40,000 LIC premium and ₹60,000 PPF. What deduction can he claim under 80C?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 80C (read with 80CCE) caps the total deduction at **₹1,50,000** regardless of how much is invested. His eligible investments total ₹2,00,000 + ₹40,000 + ₹60,000 = ₹3,00,000, but the deduction is restricted to the **₹1,50,000 ceiling**. Option A and B ignore the cap. Option D uses a wrong ₹1,00,000 limit. The maximum 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) deduction combined is ₹1.5 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** An employee covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act has a last drawn salary (basic + DA) of ₹52,000 per month and 24 years of service. What is the gratuity payable under the Act's formula?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹7,20,000  C) ₹7,49,538  D) ₹8,32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gratuity for a covered employee = (15 × last drawn salary × years of service) ÷ 26. Plug in: (15 × ₹52,000 × 24) ÷ 26 = ₹1,87,20,000 ÷ 26 = **₹7,20,000**. Option C uses ÷26 with 25 years. Option A uses the not-covered (÷30) formula. Option D forgets to divide by 26 properly. The \"15/26\" reflects 15 days' wages per completed year.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** An employee's monthly EPF wage (basic + DA) is ₹40,000. Of the employer's 12% contribution, how much goes to the Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS) each month?\nA) ₹4,800  B) ₹3,332  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹1,468\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The employer's EPS share is **8.33%**, but it is capped at a wage ceiling of **₹15,000** per month. EPS = 8.33% × ₹15,000 = **₹1,250** (rounded). Option B applies 8.33% to the full ₹40,000 (₹3,332) ignoring the ceiling. Option A is the total 12% on ₹40,000. Option D mixes rates. The EPS contribution is always ₹1,250 once wages exceed ₹15,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A retiree's NPS corpus at age 60 is ₹50,00,000. Per the NPS exit rule, what is the **minimum amount** that must be used to buy an annuity?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹25,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At age 60, an NPS subscriber can withdraw up to **60% as a lump sum** and must use at least **40% to purchase an annuity**. Minimum annuity portion = 40% × ₹50,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**. Option A (₹30,00,000) is the 60% lump-sum portion. Option C uses 50%, option D uses 20%. The mandatory annuitisation is 40% of the corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** What is the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return if the nominal return is 11% and inflation is 6%?\nA) 5.00%  B) 4.72%  C) 5.66%  D) 4.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1 = (1.11 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.047169 − 1 = **0.04717 ≈ 4.72%**. Option A (5.00%) is the crude subtraction (11% − 6%), which overstates the real return because it ignores compounding of inflation on the base. Options C and D are arithmetic distractors. Always use the division formula, not simple subtraction.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An investment grew from ₹2,00,000 to ₹3,38,000 over 4 years. What is the CAGR (rounded)?\nA) 17.25%  B) 14.05%  C) 13.30%  D) 12.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (₹3,38,000 ÷ ₹2,00,000)^(1/4) − 1 = (1.69)^(0.25) − 1. The 4th root of 1.69 ≈ 1.1405, so CAGR ≈ **14.05%**. Option A (17.25%) is the simple average (69% ÷ 4). Options C and D are close distractors. CAGR uses the geometric (compounding) root, not a simple annual average.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A goal costs ₹5,00,000 today. With inflation of 7% per year, what will it cost in 5 years (rounded)?\nA) ₹7,15,000  B) ₹6,75,000  C) ₹6,55,000  D) ₹7,01,276\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)^n = ₹5,00,000 × (1.07)^5. (1.07)^5 = 1.402552, so future cost = ₹5,00,000 × 1.402552 = **₹7,01,276**. Option B uses simple interest (₹5,00,000 × (1 + 0.07×5) = ₹6,75,000). Options A and C are distractors. Inflation compounds, so use the power formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A person invests ₹10,000 per month via SIP for 3 years at 12% p.a. (1% per month). Using the future value of an ordinary annuity, what is the approximate corpus?\nA) ₹3,60,000  B) ₹3,90,000  C) ₹4,03,200  D) ₹4,30,769\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV of an ordinary annuity = P × [((1+i)^n − 1) ÷ i], where P = ₹10,000, i = 0.01 monthly, n = 36 months. (1.01)^36 = 1.430769, so [(1.430769 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 43.0769. FV = ₹10,000 × 43.0769 = **₹4,30,769**. Option A is just the sum invested (₹3,60,000) ignoring growth. Options B and C undershoot the compounding. The annuity factor captures monthly compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A health insurance policy of ₹5,00,000 sum insured has a **10% co-pay** clause. The insured incurs a hospital bill of ₹2,00,000. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹1,80,000  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **co-pay** means the insured bears a fixed percentage of every claim. With 10% co-pay, the insured pays 10% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹20,000, and the insurer pays the remaining 90% = **₹1,80,000**. Option A ignores the co-pay. Option C is the insured's share, not the insurer's. Option D is unrelated. The sum insured (₹5,00,000) is large enough, so it does not cap the claim here.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A super top-up health policy has a **deductible of ₹5,00,000** and a cover of ₹15,00,000. In a year the insured's total hospital claims are ₹8,00,000. How much does the super top-up pay?\nA) ₹8,00,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) ₹3,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A super top-up pays only the amount of **aggregate claims that exceed the deductible** in a policy year. Claims = ₹8,00,000; deductible = ₹5,00,000. Super top-up pays ₹8,00,000 − ₹5,00,000 = **₹3,00,000** (well within its ₹15,00,000 cover). Option A ignores the deductible. Option B is the deductible itself. Option D wrongly assumes nothing is payable. The deductible is the threshold the base policy/insured covers first.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Using the income (need) approach, a family needs ₹6,00,000 per year and assumes funds earn 5% real return. What life cover replaces this income in perpetuity?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹1,20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — To replace an annual income permanently from a corpus earning a real return, Cover = Annual need ÷ real return = ₹6,00,000 ÷ 0.05 = **₹1,20,00,000**. The logic: a ₹1.2 crore corpus earning 5% real return throws off ₹6,00,000 each year without eroding capital. Option A divides by 0.20, option C divides by 0.10, option B is an arbitrary round figure. Lower assumed returns require larger corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A Hindu male dies intestate leaving a widow, two sons, one daughter and his mother. His estate is ₹1,80,00,000. As Class I heirs each take one equal share, what does the widow receive?\nA) ₹45,00,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹36,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 10 of the Hindu Succession Act, the **widow, each surviving son, each surviving daughter and the mother** each take one share. Heirs here: widow (1) + 2 sons (2) + 1 daughter (1) + mother (1) = **5 shares**. Each share = ₹1,80,00,000 ÷ 5 = **₹36,00,000**. The widow gets one share = ₹36,00,000. Option A divides by 4 (forgetting the mother). Option B divides by 6. Option D divides by 3. The father is NOT a Class I heir; the mother is.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An investor sells a debt-oriented Specified Mutual Fund (acquired in June 2023) after 30 months, with a gain of ₹1,50,000. Her income otherwise falls in the 30% slab. What is the tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹18,750  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹45,000  D) ₹3,125\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A Specified Mutual Fund (65%+ in debt) acquired **on or after 1-Apr-2023** is governed by Section 50AA: any gain is treated as **short-term capital gain irrespective of holding period** and taxed at the investor's **slab rate**. Slab = 30%, so tax = 30% × ₹1,50,000 = **₹45,000**. Option A applies 12.5% (LTCG), option D applies the equity 12.5% on the post-exemption amount, option B applies 20%. Post-April-2023 debt funds get no LTCG benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bank fixed deposit pays ₹80,000 interest in a year. The bank deducts TDS at the normal rate where PAN is provided. How much TDS is deducted?\nA) ₹16,000  B) ₹8,000  C) ₹4,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Banks deduct **TDS at 10%** on FD interest exceeding the threshold when PAN is furnished. TDS = 10% × ₹80,000 = **₹8,000**. The interest itself is taxable at the depositor's slab rate; TDS is only an upfront deduction adjusted against final liability. Option A uses 20% (the no-PAN rate). Options C and D are distractors. With PAN, the standard FD TDS rate is 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Mr. Bose's only income is LTCG of ₹3,00,000 on listed shares (Sec 112A) and he has no other income. Ignoring the basic exemption adjustment, what is his LTCG tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹21,875  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹15,625  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under Section 112A, the first ₹1,25,000 of LTCG is exempt; the balance is taxed at 12.5%. Taxable = ₹3,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹1,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹1,75,000 = **₹21,875**. Option B taxes the full ₹3,00,000 at 12.5% (forgets exemption). Option C uses ₹1,25,000 taxable. Option D applies a flat rate. The ₹1,25,000 exemption is per year, per taxpayer.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An employee contributes ₹2,80,000 to EPF (own contribution) in a year. Interest accrues at 8%. On how much of the interest is tax exempt under the ₹2.5 lakh rule?\nA) Interest on full ₹2,80,000  B) Interest on ₹2,50,000  C) Interest on ₹30,000  D) No interest is exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From 1-Apr-2021, interest on an employee's **own EPF contribution above ₹2.5 lakh** in a year is taxable; interest on contributions up to ₹2.5 lakh remains exempt. So interest on **₹2,50,000 is exempt** and interest on the excess ₹30,000 is taxable. Option A ignores the cap. Option C describes the taxable (not exempt) portion. Option D is wrong — the first ₹2.5 lakh keeps its exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A 30-year-old buys a 30-year term plan for ₹13,800/year and could instead buy a 40-year term for ₹16,200/year. What extra annual premium buys the option to extend cover by 10 years?\nA) ₹2,400  B) ₹3,000  C) ₹1,800  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Extra premium = ₹16,200 − ₹13,800 = **₹2,400 per year**. This buys the option to keep the cover running 10 years beyond the assumed retirement at 60. Option D multiplies wrongly. Options B and C are arithmetic distractors. The workbook uses exactly this example to show how a small extra premium extends coverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A REIT unit (listed) is sold after 18 months with an LTCG of ₹2,00,000 (it is the only capital gain). What is the tax under Section 112A (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹40,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹15,625  D) ₹9,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Listed REIT units held for **more than 12 months** are long-term; LTCG above ₹1,25,000 is taxed at **12.5% under Section 112A**. Taxable = ₹2,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹75,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹75,000 = **₹9,375**. Option B taxes the full ₹2,00,000 at 12.5%. Option C uses ₹1,25,000 taxable. Option A uses 20%. The ₹1,25,000 exemption applies because it is a 112A asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An investor's portfolio shows: equity LTCG ₹1,00,000 and equity STCG ₹50,000 (both listed, STT paid, only gains this year). What is the total tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹18,750  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹6,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG of ₹1,00,000 is **below the ₹1,25,000 exemption**, so LTCG tax = Nil. STCG of ₹50,000 under Section 111A is taxed at **20%** = ₹10,000. Total = 0 + ₹10,000 = **₹10,000**. Option B taxes both wrongly. Option C uses 25% on STCG; option D uses 12.5% on STCG. The ₹1,25,000 exemption is only for LTCG, not STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A person needs a retirement corpus that provides ₹50,000/month for 20 years. Assuming the corpus earns 0% real return (just keeps pace with inflation), what corpus is needed?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹2,40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — At 0% real return, the corpus is simply the total real withdrawals: ₹50,000 × 12 months × 20 years = ₹50,000 × 240 = **₹1,20,00,000**. With zero real growth, present value equals the undiscounted sum of withdrawals. Option B halves it, option C is arbitrary, option D doubles it. Use total = monthly × 12 × years when real return is zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A government employee retires with 33+ years of service. As per the workbook, retirement gratuity is capped at a maximum of:\nA) ₹10 lakh  B) ₹20 lakh  C) ₹25 lakh  D) No cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the pensioners' portal rule cited in the workbook, retirement gratuity for 33+ years of qualifying service is 16½ times basic pay plus DA, subject to a **maximum of ₹20 lakh**. Option A is too low, option C is wrong, and option D ignores the cap. The ceiling on tax-exempt gratuity is also ₹20 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Mr. Verma (private employee, NOT covered under the Gratuity Act) has an average monthly salary of ₹40,000 and 15 completed years. What gratuity does the \"not covered\" formula give?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹3,46,154  C) ₹2,76,923  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For employees **not covered** under the Act, gratuity = half month's salary for each completed year = (½ × ₹40,000) × 15 = ₹20,000 × 15 = **₹3,00,000**. Equivalently (15 × salary × years) ÷ 30 = (15 × 40,000 × 15) ÷ 30 = ₹3,00,000. Option B uses ÷26 (the covered formula). Option C uses 13 years. Option D rounds up. Not-covered uses ÷30 (half-month), covered uses ÷26.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Anchoring bias most directly leads an investor to:\nA) follow the crowd into a trending stock  B) refuse to sell a stock until it returns to the price he paid for it  C) treat a bonus differently from salary  D) overestimate his stock-picking skill\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Anchoring** is relying on a pre-existing reference point (here, the purchase price) when making decisions. Insisting a stock \"must\" return to the buy price before selling is classic anchoring to a number that the market does not care about. Option A is herding, option C is mental accounting, option D is overconfidence. Anchoring fixes on an arbitrary prior figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A debenture pays ₹9,000 interest. The investor follows the **accrual** method and the interest accrues evenly over the year but only ₹6,000 has been received by year-end. How much is taxable this year under the accrual method?\nA) ₹6,000  B) ₹3,000  C) ₹9,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the **accrual (mercantile) method**, interest on securities is taxed when it **accrues**, not when it is received. The full ₹9,000 has accrued during the year, so **₹9,000 is taxable** even though only ₹6,000 was received. Option A uses the cash basis. Option B and D are distractors. Accrual taxes income as it is earned.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** An employee's gross salary is ₹9,00,000 (new regime). Employer's NPS contribution under 80CCD(2) is 14% of salary and is fully deductible. What is the 80CCD(2) deduction (assume \"salary\" = ₹9,00,000)?\nA) ₹90,000  B) ₹1,26,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the **new regime**, the employer's NPS contribution deduction under **Section 80CCD(2)** is allowed up to **14% of salary**. Deduction = 14% × ₹9,00,000 = **₹1,26,000**. Option A uses 10% (the old-regime private-sector limit). Option C is the 80CCD(1B) limit. Option D is the 80C cap. The new regime raised the private-sector 80CCD(2) limit to 14%.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A person buys SGBs (Sovereign Gold Bonds, listed) and sells them on the exchange after 15 months with a gain of ₹1,00,000. As an individual, what tax applies (ignore exemptions/cess)?\nA) Slab rate  B) 12.5%  C) 20%  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGBs sold on the exchange after **more than 12 months** give long-term capital gains taxed at **12.5%** (per the workbook). Tax = 12.5% × ₹1,00,000 = ₹12,500. If held 12 months or less, it would be slab rate (option A). Option C uses the wrong 20% rate; option D (exempt) applies only on **redemption at maturity** to the original individual subscriber, not on a secondary-market sale. Here it is sold on the exchange, so 12.5% applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A household has assets of ₹80,00,000 and liabilities of ₹50,00,000 (home loan). For estate-planning purposes, the net estate value is:\nA) ₹1,30,00,000  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Net estate = Assets − Liabilities = ₹80,00,000 − ₹50,00,000 = **₹30,00,000**. The liabilities (the outstanding home loan) must be settled from the estate before distribution to heirs. Option A adds them, option B is the liability, option D ignores the loan. Estate planning works with net worth, not gross assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Two indemnity health policies (₹5,00,000 and ₹4,00,000) cover the same person. A claim of ₹3,00,000 arises and the insured claims from the ₹5,00,000 policy. How much does that insurer pay (claim < its sum insured)?\nA) ₹1,66,667  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹1,33,333  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — When the **claim is less than the sum insured** of the chosen policy, the chosen insurer **cannot invoke the contribution clause** and must pay the **full ₹3,00,000**. Contribution (proportionate sharing) applies only when the claim exceeds the chosen policy's sum insured. Options A and C apply proportionate sharing incorrectly. Option B halves it. The insured chooses one insurer; that insurer pays in full if within its limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** An investor earns dividend of ₹50,000 on equity shares. She is in the 30% slab. What tax does she pay on this dividend (ignore cess)?\nA) Nil (exempt)  B) ₹5,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹6,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since the abolition of Dividend Distribution Tax, **dividends are taxed in the investor's hands at the applicable slab rate**. At 30%, tax = 30% × ₹50,000 = **₹15,000** (the company also deducts 10% TDS = ₹5,000, adjusted later). Option A wrongly assumes the old exemption. Option B is just the TDS. Option D applies 12.5%. Dividend is fully taxable at slab rate now.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A goal of ₹10,00,000 is due in 6 years. Money can be invested at 9% p.a. compounded annually. What lump sum must be invested today (present value, rounded)?\nA) ₹5,96,267  B) ₹6,49,931  C) ₹4,80,000  D) ₹5,46,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Present value = FV ÷ (1 + r)^n = ₹10,00,000 ÷ (1.09)^6. (1.09)^6 = 1.677100, so PV = ₹10,00,000 ÷ 1.677100 = **₹5,96,267**. Option B uses 7%, options C and D are distractors. Discounting brings a future amount back to today's value using compound growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A young single individual with no dependents but a home loan EMI and elderly parents is best advised that his **most relevant** insurance need is:\nA) a large endowment life policy  B) personal accident/critical illness cover (income protection of his earning ability)  C) no insurance at all  D) a child education plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook, a young person without dependents primarily needs to protect **his own earning ability** — a personal accident disability or critical illness policy that provides income if he is incapacitated — more than a death-benefit life policy (since no dependents rely on his death payout, though the loan/parents add some need). Option A is a costly investment-cum-insurance mismatch. Option C ignores real risks. Option D is irrelevant (no child). Match the product to the actual risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Unlisted equity shares bought for ₹4,00,000 are sold after 30 months for ₹10,00,000. What is the LTCG tax under the post-July-2024 rules (ignore exemptions/cess)?\nA) ₹75,000  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Unlisted** equity shares need to be held more than **24 months** to be long-term. Here 30 months qualifies. LTCG on unlisted shares is taxed at **12.5% under Section 112** (no ₹1,25,000 exemption — that is only for STT-paid 112A assets). Gain = ₹10,00,000 − ₹4,00,000 = ₹6,00,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹6,00,000 = **₹75,000**. Option B uses 20%, option C uses 10%, option D is the gain itself. Unlisted shares: 24-month rule, 12.5%, no 1.25-lakh exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A super top-up plan covers ₹20,00,000 with a ₹3,00,000 deductible. The insured has two hospitalisations in a year: ₹2,00,000 and ₹4,00,000. How much does the super top-up pay (aggregate deductible)?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹6,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A super top-up applies the deductible on an **aggregate (annual) basis**, not per claim. Total claims = ₹2,00,000 + ₹4,00,000 = ₹6,00,000. The plan pays the amount above the ₹3,00,000 deductible = ₹6,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = **₹3,00,000**. Option B ignores the deductible. Option C applies the deductible per claim (a top-up, not super top-up). Option D is wrong. Super top-up aggregates all claims before applying the deductible once.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An NPS subscriber's corpus at 60 is ₹40,00,000. She withdraws the maximum permitted lump sum and annuitises the rest. If the annuity pays 6% p.a., what is her annual annuity income?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹1,44,000  C) ₹2,40,000  D) ₹96,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Maximum lump sum = 60%, so annuity portion = 40% × ₹40,00,000 = ₹16,00,000. Annuity income = 6% × ₹16,00,000 = **₹96,000 per year**. Option B applies 6% to 60% (₹24,00,000). Option C applies 6% to the full corpus. Option A uses a wrong rate. Only the mandatory 40% buys the annuity.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Salary & Income-Tax Computation (New Regime, FY 2025-26)\n\nMs. Priya is a private-sector employee. Her figures for FY 2025-26 are below. She opts for the **new tax regime**. Slabs: 0–4L nil, 4–8L 5%, 8–12L 10%, 12–16L 15%, 16–20L 20%, 20–24L 25%, above 24L 30%. Cess is 4%. Standard deduction (new regime) is ₹75,000; 80CCD(2) employer NPS limit is 14% of salary.\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary (basic + allowances) | 18,75,000 |\n| Employer NPS contribution (within 14% limit, deductible u/s 80CCD(2)) | 1,75,000 |\n| Bank FD interest (income from other sources) | 1,00,000 |\n| LTCG on listed equity (Sec 112A), STT paid | 2,25,000 |\n\n**Q41.** What is Ms. Priya's taxable salary income after the standard deduction?\nA) ₹18,75,000  B) ₹18,00,000  C) ₹16,25,000  D) ₹18,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable salary = Gross salary − Standard deduction = ₹18,75,000 − ₹75,000 = **₹18,00,000**. The employer NPS contribution is first added to salary and then claimed as a deduction under 80CCD(2); since the problem already states it is \"within the limit and deductible,\" we treat the net effect as nil on salary for simplicity here — the standard deduction is the only reduction shown. Option A forgets the standard deduction. Option C wrongly subtracts the NPS amount twice. Option D uses ₹50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Treating the FD interest as ordinary income and excluding the LTCG (taxed separately), what is her **total income taxed at slab rates**?\nA) ₹19,75,000  B) ₹18,00,000  C) ₹21,25,000  D) ₹19,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Slab-rate income = taxable salary + other-sources income = ₹18,00,000 + ₹1,00,000 (FD interest) = **₹19,00,000**. The LTCG of ₹2,25,000 is taxed separately at the special 112A rate and is NOT added to slab income. Option B forgets the FD interest. Option C wrongly adds the LTCG. Option A miscounts. Special-rate income is kept out of the slab computation.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** What is the slab-rate income tax (before cess) on ₹19,00,000?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹1,80,000  C) ₹2,10,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute slab by slab on ₹19,00,000: 4–8L (5% × ₹4L) = ₹20,000; 8–12L (10% × ₹4L) = ₹40,000; 12–16L (15% × ₹4L) = ₹60,000; 16–19L (20% × ₹3L) = ₹60,000. Total = ₹20,000 + ₹40,000 + ₹60,000 + ₹60,000 = **₹1,80,000**. Option C (₹2,10,000) over-counts the 20% band. Option A and D apply flat rates. The 16–19L slice is ₹3,00,000 (not ₹4,00,000) because income stops at ₹19,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** What is the tax on her LTCG under Section 112A (before cess)?\nA) ₹28,125  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹15,625\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — LTCG = ₹2,25,000. Exempt first ₹1,25,000; taxable = ₹1,00,000 at 12.5% = **₹12,500**. Option A taxes the full ₹2,25,000 at 12.5%. Option B uses 20%. Option D uses ₹1,25,000 as taxable. The ₹1,25,000 exemption applies once per year to 112A gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Adding the slab tax (₹1,80,000) and LTCG tax (₹12,500), then 4% health & education cess, what is her total tax liability (rounded)?\nA) ₹2,00,200  B) ₹1,92,500  C) ₹2,00,500  D) ₹1,99,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Tax before cess = ₹1,80,000 + ₹12,500 = ₹1,92,500. Cess = 4% × ₹1,92,500 = ₹7,700. Total = ₹1,92,500 + ₹7,700 = **₹2,00,200**. Option B omits cess. Options C and D miscompute the cess. Cess of 4% applies to total tax including the special-rate LTCG tax.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Capital Gains Across Asset Classes\n\nMr. Sharma made the following transactions in FY 2025-26 (all transfers after 23-Jul-2024). He has no other capital gains and no carried-forward losses.\n\n| Asset | Bought | Sold | Cost (₹) | Sale (₹) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed equity shares (STT paid) | Mar-2022 | Oct-2025 | 6,00,000 | 9,50,000 |\n| Listed equity MF (STT paid) | May-2025 | Dec-2025 | 3,00,000 | 3,40,000 |\n| Residential flat | Apr-2019 | Nov-2025 | 40,00,000 | 70,00,000 |\n\n**Q46.** Classify the listed equity shares transaction and state the gain.\nA) STCG of ₹3,50,000  B) LTCG of ₹3,50,000  C) LTCG of ₹2,25,000  D) STCG of ₹2,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held from Mar-2022 to Oct-2025 = more than 12 months, so **long-term**. Gain = ₹9,50,000 − ₹6,00,000 = **₹3,50,000 LTCG**. Option A misclassifies as short-term. Options C and D wrongly net the exemption into the gain figure (the exemption is applied at the tax step, not when stating the gain).\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The listed equity MF was held ~7 months. Classify it and compute its tax (Sec 111A, ignore cess).\nA) STCG ₹40,000, tax ₹8,000  B) LTCG ₹40,000, tax ₹5,000  C) STCG ₹40,000, tax ₹6,000  D) STCG ₹40,000, tax Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Held ~7 months (≤12), so **short-term** under Section 111A, taxed at **20%**. Gain = ₹3,40,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹40,000. Tax = 20% × ₹40,000 = **₹8,000**. Option B uses LTCG 12.5%, option C uses 15%, option D wrongly applies the LTCG exemption (which is not available for STCG).\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Classify the flat and compute the LTCG (post-July-2024 rule).\nA) STCG ₹30,00,000  B) LTCG ₹15,00,000  C) LTCG ₹30,00,000  D) LTCG ₹24,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The flat was held from Apr-2019 to Nov-2025 = more than 24 months, so **long-term**. For transfers after 23-Jul-2024, LTCG = Sale − Cost with **no indexation**. Gain = ₹70,00,000 − ₹40,00,000 = **₹30,00,000 LTCG**. Option A misclassifies. Options B and D apply some indexation/adjustment, which is no longer allowed.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** What is the tax on the listed equity shares LTCG of ₹3,50,000 (only 112A gain; ignore cess)?\nA) ₹28,125  B) ₹43,750  C) ₹70,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Apply the single ₹1,25,000 exemption to the 112A gain: taxable = ₹3,50,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹2,25,000 = **₹28,125**. Option B taxes the full ₹3,50,000 at 12.5%. Option C uses 20%. Option D applies a flat number. The ₹1,25,000 exemption is for 112A (listed equity/equity MF) gains only — the equity MF here was short-term, so the entire exemption goes against the share LTCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** What is the tax on the flat's LTCG of ₹30,00,000 (ignore exemptions, surcharge and cess)?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹3,75,000  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Property LTCG after July-2024 is taxed at a flat **12.5% without indexation** under Section 112. Tax = 12.5% × ₹30,00,000 = **₹3,75,000**. There is no ₹1,25,000 exemption for property (that is only for 112A equity assets). Option A applies the old 20% rate, option C uses 10%, option D uses 8%. The post-July-2024 property rate is 12.5%.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Retirement Corpus Planning\n\nMr. Anil is 35 and plans to retire at 60 (25 years away). He wants a retirement corpus and uses these assumptions:\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current monthly expenses (today's value) | ₹60,000 |\n| Inflation | 6% p.a. |\n| Expected SIP return till retirement | 12% p.a. |\n| Monthly SIP he can start now | ₹15,000 |\n| EPF balance accumulated so far | ₹8,00,000 |\n| Years of SIP (monthly compounding) | 25 years = 300 months |\n\n**Q51.** What will be Mr. Anil's monthly expense (in future value) at retirement, 25 years from now?\nA) ₹2,57,500  B) ₹2,57,434  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹1,90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future expense = Current × (1 + inflation)^n = ₹60,000 × (1.06)^25. (1.06)^25 = 4.291871, so ₹60,000 × 4.291871 = **₹2,57,512 ≈ ₹2,57,434** (using a slightly rounded factor 4.2906 gives ₹2,57,434). Either way the answer is closest to **₹2,57,434 (B)**. Option C uses simple ×2.5, option D undershoots. Expenses compound with inflation — that is why retirement targets look large.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Using FV of an ordinary annuity, what is the future value of his ₹15,000 monthly SIP at 1% per month for 300 months? (Annuity factor for i=1%, n=300 is ≈ 1897.4)\nA) ₹2,84,610  B) ₹45,00,000  C) ₹1,50,00,000  D) ₹2,84,61,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV = P × annuity factor = ₹15,000 × 1897.4 = **₹2,84,61,000** (about ₹2.85 crore). Option B (₹45,00,000) is just the money invested (₹15,000 × 300), ignoring growth. Option C undershoots. Option A drops two zeros. Long-horizon monthly SIPs compound into very large corpuses — this is the power of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** His EPF balance of ₹8,00,000 grows at 8% p.a. for 25 years (annual compounding). What is its value at retirement (rounded)?\nA) ₹54,79,000  B) ₹24,00,000  C) ₹68,48,000  D) ₹40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = PV × (1 + r)^n = ₹8,00,000 × (1.08)^25. (1.08)^25 = 6.848475, so ₹8,00,000 × 6.848475 = **₹54,78,780 ≈ ₹54,79,000**. Option B uses simple interest, option D is arbitrary, option C uses a wrong factor. Even an existing lump sum grows substantially over 25 years of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Combining the SIP corpus (₹2,84,61,000) and the grown EPF (₹54,79,000), what is his total retirement corpus (rounded)?\nA) ₹3,39,40,000  B) ₹2,84,61,000  C) ₹3,00,00,000  D) ₹3,39,40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = ₹2,84,61,000 + ₹54,79,000 = **₹3,39,40,000** (about ₹3.39 crore). Option B counts only the SIP. Option C rounds away the EPF. Option D has an extra digit. Always sum all retirement pools — SIP plus EPF plus any other — to get the true corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** If his target monthly income at retirement is ₹2,57,434 and his corpus earns a 5% real return in retirement, roughly what annual income (in today's purchasing power terms) can a ₹3.39 crore corpus sustainably support at 5%?\nA) ₹16,97,000  B) ₹30,89,208  C) ₹6,78,800  D) ₹40,72,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A sustainable annual draw (so the corpus is not depleted) ≈ real return × corpus = 5% × ₹3,39,40,000 = **₹16,97,000 per year** (about ₹1,41,400 per month). His target was ₹2,57,434 per month (≈ ₹30,89,208 per year), so the corpus alone, drawn sustainably at 5%, falls short — he would need to draw down principal or save more. Option B is the annual target need (not sustainable income). Option C uses 2%, option D uses 12% (unsustainably high). Sustainable income ≈ real return × corpus.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Insurance Need & Health Cover\n\nThe Mehta family: Mr. Mehta (earning ₹15,00,000/year), spouse (homemaker), two young children. They want to size life cover and review health insurance.\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Annual income to replace | ₹12,00,000 |\n| Outstanding home loan | ₹40,00,000 |\n| Children's future education fund needed | ₹50,00,000 |\n| Existing life cover | ₹50,00,000 |\n| Existing liquid savings/investments | ₹20,00,000 |\n| Real return assumption for income replacement | 6% |\n| Employer health cover (base) | ₹3,00,000 |\n| Super top-up cover with ₹3,00,000 deductible | ₹15,00,000 |\n\n**Q56.** Using the income-replacement (capitalised need) approach, what corpus replaces ₹12,00,000/year at a 6% real return?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹72,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Capitalised income need = Annual income ÷ real return = ₹12,00,000 ÷ 0.06 = **₹2,00,00,000**. The idea: a ₹2 crore corpus earning 6% real return yields ₹12,00,000 each year indefinitely. Option B multiplies (₹12L × 6). Option A divides by 0.10, option D by 0.12. Lower assumed returns push the required cover higher.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** What is the **total insurance need** (income replacement + loan + education fund)?\nA) ₹2,00,00,000  B) ₹2,50,00,000  C) ₹3,40,00,000  D) ₹2,90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Total need = income-replacement corpus + outstanding loan + education fund = ₹2,00,00,000 + ₹40,00,000 + ₹50,00,000 = **₹2,90,00,000**. Option A counts only income replacement. Option B omits the loan. Option C double-counts. The needs approach adds all the lump-sum and capitalised requirements.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Netting off existing life cover and liquid assets, what **additional life cover** should Mr. Mehta buy?\nA) ₹2,90,00,000  B) ₹2,20,00,000  C) ₹2,70,00,000  D) ₹1,90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Additional cover = Total need − existing cover − liquid assets = ₹2,90,00,000 − ₹50,00,000 − ₹20,00,000 = **₹2,20,00,000**. Option A ignores existing resources. Option C only subtracts the existing cover. Option D over-subtracts. Existing cover and investible assets reduce the fresh cover required.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A hospitalisation bill of ₹2,50,000 arises. The employer base cover is ₹3,00,000 (no co-pay). How is it settled, and does the super top-up pay anything?\nA) Base pays ₹2,50,000; super top-up pays nothing  B) Super top-up pays ₹2,50,000  C) Base pays ₹3,00,000  D) Both share equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The ₹2,50,000 bill is within the ₹3,00,000 base cover, so the **base policy pays ₹2,50,000** in full. The super top-up has a ₹3,00,000 deductible, and since total claims (₹2,50,000) are below that threshold, the **super top-up pays nothing**. Option B ignores the deductible. Option C pays more than the bill. Option D misapplies sharing. The top-up only kicks in above its deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Next year a major bill of ₹10,00,000 arises. The base cover (₹3,00,000) is used first; the super top-up has a ₹3,00,000 deductible. How much does the super top-up pay?\nA) ₹7,00,000  B) ₹10,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The super top-up pays claims **above its ₹3,00,000 deductible**. Total claim = ₹10,00,000; above the ₹3,00,000 deductible = ₹10,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = **₹7,00,000** (within its ₹15,00,000 cover). The ₹3,00,000 deductible is met by the employer base cover. So base pays ₹3,00,000 and super top-up pays ₹7,00,000, fully settling the bill. Option B ignores the deductible. Option C and D miscompute. The deductible is the gap the base/insured fills first.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Estate & Intestate Succession\n\nMr. Gopal, a Hindu, dies **intestate** (no will). He is survived by: his widow, three children (two sons and one daughter), and his mother. His father predeceased him. One son (let's call him the \"pre-deceased branch\") had already died before Mr. Gopal, leaving behind a widow and one child — so that branch is represented. Re-state the survivors clearly:\n\n| Survivor | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Widow of Mr. Gopal | alive |\n| Son A | alive |\n| Daughter B | alive |\n| Mother | alive |\n| Son C | pre-deceased, leaving a widow + 1 child (one branch) |\n\nNet estate to distribute: **₹2,40,00,000**.\n\n**Q61.** How many \"shares\" does the estate divide into under Section 10 of the Hindu Succession Act?\nA) 4  B) 6  C) 5  D) 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Each of the following takes one share: the widow (1), Son A (1), Daughter B (1), the mother (1), and the **branch of the pre-deceased Son C** takes one share between them (1). Total = **5 shares**. Option A forgets the pre-deceased branch. Option B double-counts the branch members individually. Option D omits heirs. A pre-deceased child's branch collectively takes the one share that child would have taken.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** What is the value of one share?\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹48,00,000  C) ₹40,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — One share = Estate ÷ number of shares = ₹2,40,00,000 ÷ 5 = **₹48,00,000**. Option A divides by 4, option C by 6, option D by 3. Five equal shares of ₹48,00,000 each.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** How much does the **mother** receive?\nA) Nil  B) ₹24,00,000  C) ₹48,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The mother is a **Class I heir** and takes **one full share = ₹48,00,000**. (The father, by contrast, is NOT a Class I heir and would get nothing here.) Option A wrongly excludes the mother. Option B halves the share. Option D uses the wrong divisor. Remember: mother is Class I, father is not.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Son C's branch (his widow + 1 child) shares one share of ₹48,00,000 equally between them. How much does Son C's widow get?\nA) ₹48,00,000  B) ₹24,00,000  C) ₹16,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Within the pre-deceased son's branch, his widow and his surviving children share that one share in **equal proportions** (Rule 4). The branch share is ₹48,00,000, split between 2 people (widow + 1 child) = ₹48,00,000 ÷ 2 = **₹24,00,000** each. Option A gives her the whole branch share. Option C divides by 3, option D by 4. The branch's single share is split equally among its members.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Combining everyone, verify Mr. Gopal's own widow's share equals:\nA) ₹24,00,000  B) ₹48,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹96,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mr. Gopal's widow takes **one full share = ₹48,00,000** (Rule 1: the widow takes one share). Check the total: widow ₹48L + Son A ₹48L + Daughter B ₹48L + Mother ₹48L + Son C's branch ₹48L (split ₹24L + ₹24L) = ₹2,40,00,000. It all reconciles. Option A is a half-share, option C and D are wrong divisors. The surviving widow always takes one full share.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Household Plan\n\nMr. and Mrs. Roy (both 40) want an integrated review. Combined data for FY 2025-26:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Mr. Roy gross salary (new regime) | ₹16,75,000 |\n| Standard deduction (new regime) | ₹75,000 |\n| Listed equity LTCG (STT paid), Sec 112A | ₹3,25,000 |\n| Goal: child's college in 8 years, cost today | ₹25,00,000 |\n| Education inflation | 8% p.a. |\n| Lump sum investable today for the goal, return | 10% p.a. |\n| New-regime slabs | 0–4L nil, 4–8L 5%, 8–12L 10%, 12–16L 15%, 16–20L 20% |\n\n**Q66.** What is Mr. Roy's taxable salary after the standard deduction, and is it within the 16–20L slab?\nA) ₹16,00,000; no  B) ₹16,75,000; yes  C) ₹15,25,000; no  D) ₹16,00,000; yes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Taxable salary = ₹16,75,000 − ₹75,000 = **₹16,00,000**, which falls right at the boundary into the 16–20L band (the portion above ₹16,00,000 would be taxed at 20%, but here it equals ₹16,00,000 exactly). Option B forgets the deduction. Option C uses ₹1,50,000. Option A states the right figure but the wrong band conclusion. The standard deduction (new regime) is ₹75,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** What is the slab-rate income tax on ₹16,00,000 (before cess)?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹1,40,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Slab by slab on ₹16,00,000: 4–8L (5% × 4L) = ₹20,000; 8–12L (10% × 4L) = ₹40,000; 12–16L (15% × 4L) = ₹60,000. Total = ₹20,000 + ₹40,000 + ₹60,000 = **₹1,20,000**. Option B adds a non-existent top band, options C and D apply flat rates. At exactly ₹16,00,000, no income enters the 20% band.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** What is the tax on his LTCG of ₹3,25,000 under Section 112A (before cess)?\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹40,625  C) ₹65,000  D) ₹12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = ₹3,25,000. Exempt ₹1,25,000; taxable = ₹2,00,000 at 12.5% = **₹25,000**. Option B taxes the full ₹3,25,000 at 12.5%. Option C uses 20%. Option D uses ₹1,00,000 taxable. The ₹1,25,000 exemption applies once to the 112A gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** What will the child's college actually cost in 8 years at 8% education inflation (rounded)?\nA) ₹50,00,000  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹37,50,000  D) ₹46,29,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = ₹25,00,000 × (1.08)^8. (1.08)^8 = 1.850930, so ₹25,00,000 × 1.850930 = **₹46,27,325 ≈ ₹46,29,000** (small rounding). Option B uses simple interest, option C is too low, option A is arbitrary. Education inflation (often higher than general inflation) compounds the target up sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** What single lump sum must the Roys invest today at 10% p.a. to meet the ₹46,29,000 future cost in 8 years (present value, rounded)?\nA) ₹21,59,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹18,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)^n = ₹46,29,000 ÷ (1.10)^8. (1.10)^8 = 2.143589, so PV = ₹46,29,000 ÷ 2.143589 = **₹21,59,000** (approximately). Option B is today's cost (not discounted to fund the future figure). Options C and D are distractors. Because the investment return (10%) exceeds education inflation (8%), the lump sum needed today (₹21.59L) is less than today's cost (₹25L).\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "07", "md": "# Mock Paper 7 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy — it leans on intestate-succession share splits, corporate-action cost-basis maths, wills/trusts/HUF taxation and mutual-fund segregated portfolios. Keep a calculator or spreadsheet handy and write out each fraction and formula before picking an option.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** A Hindu male dies intestate leaving a widow, two sons and one daughter, all surviving. Under Section 10 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, what fraction of the estate does the widow get?\nA) 1/3  B) 1/2  C) 1/5  D) 1/4\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 10 Rule 1 gives the widow one share; Rule 2 gives each surviving son, each daughter and the mother one share each. Heirs here = widow (1) + son (1) + son (1) + daughter (1) = 4 equal shares (no surviving mother is mentioned). Each share = 1/4. So the widow gets 1/4. A) 1/3 wrongly counts only 3 heirs; C) 1/5 wrongly adds a 5th heir; B) 1/2 is the spouse share under some other religions, not the Hindu Class I rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Mr. A buys 12,000 listed shares at ₹80. The company issues bonus 1:3 (one bonus for every three held). What is the cost of acquisition of the 4,000 bonus shares for capital-gains purposes?\nA) ₹3,20,000  B) ₹1,06,667  C) ₹0  D) ₹80 each\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When bonus shares are allotted without payment on the basis of existing holdings, their cost of acquisition is NIL (Section 55). Bonus = 12,000 ÷ 3 = 4,000 shares, all at nil cost. A) and B) wrongly spread the original cost onto the bonus; D) uses the original purchase price, which applies only to the original shares, not the free bonus shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Mr. B holds 2,000 shares bought at ₹150 each (face value ₹100). The company splits each ₹100 share into two ₹50 shares. After the split, what is the cost of acquisition per share?\nA) ₹150  B) ₹100  C) ₹75  D) ₹50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A split is not a transfer; the original cost is spread proportionately over the larger number of shares. Total cost = 2,000 × ₹150 = ₹3,00,000; after split he holds 4,000 shares; cost per share = ₹3,00,000 ÷ 4,000 = ₹75. A) ignores the split; B) and D) confuse face value with cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A Hindu male dies intestate. He is survived by his mother, his widow and one son. There are no other heirs. What share does the son receive?\nA) 1/3  B) 1/2  C) 1/4  D) Whole estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Class I heirs sharing one share each: mother (1) + widow (1) + son (1) = 3 equal shares. Each = 1/3, so the son gets 1/3. B) wrongly drops the mother; C) wrongly adds a 4th heir; D) ignores that mother and widow are also Class I heirs.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A company buys back 600 listed shares from Mr. C at ₹400 each on 1-Feb-2025. He had bought them at ₹250 each. Under the rules effective 1-Oct-2024, how much is taxed as dividend (income from other sources) in his hands?\nA) ₹90,000  B) Nil  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹2,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — From 1-Oct-2024 the entire buyback proceeds are taxed as dividend under \"income from other sources\" with NO deduction for cost. Dividend = 600 × ₹400 = ₹2,40,000. The cost (600 × ₹250 = ₹1,50,000) is separately treated as a capital loss, not netted here. A) wrongly subtracts cost; C) is the cost itself; B) is the pre-Oct-2024 position when proceeds were exempt under Section 10(34A).\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Following Q5, what is the capital loss available to Mr. C from the buyback?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹90,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the post-Oct-2024 rule, the cost of acquisition of the bought-back shares becomes a capital loss in the shareholder's hands (long- or short-term by holding period). Cost = 600 × ₹250 = ₹1,50,000 = the capital loss. A) is the dividend, not the loss; C) wrongly nets dividend minus cost; D) ignores the loss provision.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Mr. D renounces (sells) his rights entitlement on 300 listed shares to Mr. E for ₹40 per right. He had paid nothing for the rights. What is his taxable capital gain on renunciation?\nA) ₹0  B) ₹4,000  C) ₹6,000  D) ₹12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Cost of acquisition of a right renounced is NIL. Gain = sale consideration − nil = 300 × ₹40 = ₹12,000. This is short-term and taxed at normal slab rates (it is a right, not a listed equity share, so Section 111A does not apply). A) wrongly treats the right as free of gain; B) and C) use wrong prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A Hindu male dies intestate survived by his widow, one surviving daughter, and the two children (a son and a daughter) of a pre-deceased son. Total heirs at the top level take how many shares between them?\nA) 4 shares  B) 3 shares  C) 2 shares  D) 5 shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At the top level: widow takes one share, surviving daughter takes one share, and the branch of the pre-deceased son takes ONE share between its members (Rule 3). So 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 shares. The pre-deceased son's two children then split that one share. A) wrongly counts the two grandchildren as separate top-level shares; C) drops a share; D) over-counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Continuing Q8, the pre-deceased son's branch (his son + daughter) takes 1/3 of the estate between them. What fraction does each grandchild get?\nA) 1/3 each  B) 1/12 each  C) 1/9 each  D) 1/6 each\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The branch's 1/3 is split equally between the surviving son and daughter of the pre-deceased son (Rule 4). 1/3 ÷ 2 = 1/6 each. A) ignores the split; B) and C) divide incorrectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Mr. F bought 8,000 shares at ₹105 on 1-Apr-2020. The company issued bonus 1:2 (one bonus for two) on 1-Sep-2023. He sold ALL shares (12,000) at ₹140 on 1-Aug-2024. What is the LTCG on the 8,000 original shares (no exemption applied yet)?\nA) ₹2,80,000  B) ₹4,20,000  C) ₹1,68,000  D) ₹5,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Original shares: held > 12 months, so long-term. Gain = 8,000 × (₹140 − ₹105) = 8,000 × ₹35 = ₹2,80,000. B) wrongly uses all 12,000; C) uses a wrong rate; D) uses a wrong cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Continuing Q10, the 4,000 bonus shares (cost nil, allotted 1-Sep-2023, sold 1-Aug-2024) generate what gain, and of what type?\nA) ₹5,60,000 LTCG  B) ₹1,40,000 STCG  C) ₹4,20,000 STCG  D) ₹5,60,000 STCG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bonus shares' holding starts from allotment (1-Sep-2023) to sale (1-Aug-2024) = 11 months < 12 months, so SHORT-term. Cost is nil. Gain = 4,000 × ₹140 = ₹5,60,000, all STCG (Section 111A, 20%). A) wrongly calls it long-term; B) and C) use wrong share counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A Hindu female dies intestate leaving no children and no husband, but her mother and father survive. Under Section 15, who inherits first?\nA) Mother and father  B) Heirs of the husband  C) Heirs of the father  D) Sons and daughters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 15 order: (a) sons/daughters and husband; (b) heirs of husband; (c) mother and father; ... Since there are no children and no husband, the next applicable entry that exists here is (c) mother and father. B) and C) come later; D) does not exist here.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Mr. G acquired 20,000 preference shares at ₹10 each on 1-Jan-2010. They convert 2:1 into 10,000 equity shares (FMV ₹25). He sells the equity at ₹35 on 25-Aug-2024 (STT paid). What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹3,50,000  C) ₹1,00,000  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Conversion of preference into equity is NOT a transfer (Section 47(xb)); cost and holding carry over from the preference shares. Sale = 10,000 × ₹35 = ₹3,50,000. Cost = 20,000 × ₹10 = ₹2,00,000. LTCG = ₹3,50,000 − ₹2,00,000 = ₹1,50,000. B) ignores cost; C) and D) use FMV wrongly.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Mr. H buys 10,000 units of a mutual fund at ₹15. The fund segregates a portfolio: NAV of asset moved to segregated portfolio is ₹3, total NAV before segregation is ₹15. What is the cost of acquisition of the segregated units?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹1,50,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost of segregated units = total cost × (NAV transferred ÷ total NAV) = ₹1,50,000 × (3 ÷ 15) = ₹30,000. A) is per-unit confusion; C) is total cost; D) is the main-portfolio cost (₹1,50,000 − ₹30,000).\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Continuing Q14, what is the cost of acquisition that remains attached to the main-portfolio units?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹1,35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Main-portfolio cost = original cost − segregated cost = ₹1,50,000 − ₹30,000 = ₹1,20,000. A) ignores the reduction; B) is the segregated cost; D) miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Under the Hindu Succession Act, which of the following is NOT a Class I heir of a Hindu male?\nA) Mother  B) Widow of a pre-deceased son  C) Father  D) Daughter of a pre-deceased daughter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Father is a Class II heir, not Class I. The mother, the widow of a pre-deceased son and the daughter of a pre-deceased daughter are all in the Class I list. So C) is the odd one out.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mr. I held 10,000 shares of A Ltd bought at ₹58 on 1-Apr-2023. A Ltd amalgamates into AB Ltd and he gets 8,000 shares, which he sells at ₹100 on 1-Sep-2024 (listed, STT paid). What is the LTCG?\nA) ₹2,20,000  B) ₹3,42,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹4,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — On amalgamation, cost and holding carry from the original (amalgamating) shares. Sale = 8,000 × ₹100 = ₹8,00,000. Cost = 10,000 × ₹58 = ₹5,80,000. LTCG = ₹8,00,000 − ₹5,80,000 = ₹2,20,000 (held > 12 months). B) uses 8,000 × ₹58 for cost; C) ignores cost; D) uses wrong figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Continuing Q17, what tax rate applies to that ₹2,20,000 LTCG, and what is the tax (ignore cess/surcharge)?\nA) 20% → ₹44,000  B) 12.5% on full → ₹27,500  C) 12.5% on amount above ₹1,25,000 → ₹11,875  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 112A: listed equity LTCG is taxed at 12.5% only on the amount exceeding the ₹1,25,000 exemption. Taxable = ₹2,20,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹95,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹95,000 = ₹11,875. A) uses STCG rate; B) forgets the exemption; D) ignores that the excess is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Mr. J holds 1,000 shares costing ₹500 each, bought 1-Apr-2024. He exercises a rights issue and is allotted 500 shares at ₹250 on 1-Dec-2024, then sells them at ₹520 on 25-Jan-2025 (listed, STT paid). What is the capital gain and type?\nA) ₹1,35,000 STCG  B) ₹1,35,000 LTCG  C) ₹2,60,000 STCG  D) ₹1,25,000 STCG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cost of right shares = price paid = 500 × ₹250 = ₹1,25,000. Sale = 500 × ₹520 = ₹2,60,000. Gain = ₹1,35,000. Holding from allotment (1-Dec-2024) to sale (25-Jan-2025) < 12 months = SHORT-term, taxed 20% under 111A. B) wrong type; C) ignores cost; D) is the cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Hindu male dies intestate with a widow, mother, and three sons. What fraction does the mother receive?\nA) 1/4  B) 1/3  C) 1/6  D) 1/5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Heirs each take one share: widow (1) + mother (1) + son (1) + son (1) + son (1) = 5 shares. Each = 1/5, so the mother gets 1/5. A) counts only 4; C) counts 6; B) counts 3.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Mr. K acquired 2,000 shares at face value ₹100. The company consolidates two ₹100 shares into one ₹200 share. What is the cost of acquisition per consolidated share?\nA) ₹200  B) ₹100  C) ₹400  D) ₹50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total cost = 2,000 × ₹100 = ₹2,00,000. After consolidation he holds 1,000 shares. Cost per share = ₹2,00,000 ÷ 1,000 = ₹200. B) ignores consolidation; C) doubles wrongly; D) is a split figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under Muslim law (Sunni), the per-capita method is used. If an estate is shared equally among 5 heirs of the same class, each heir's fraction is:\nA) 1/4  B) Depends on branch  C) 1/6  D) 1/5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In per-capita distribution (Sunni), the estate is divided equally among the number of heirs. 5 equal heirs → each = 1/5. A) and C) miscount; B) describes per-strip (Shia) distribution, not per-capita.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A childless Muslim widow is entitled to what fraction of her deceased husband's property (after expenses/debts)?\nA) 1/8  B) 1/2  C) 1/4  D) 1/3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A childless Muslim widow gets 1/4; a widow with children/grandchildren gets 1/8. A) is the with-children case; B) is a husband's share where the wife dies childless; D) is the maximum a Muslim may will away.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Mr. L lends 5,000 shares for a month and earns a lending fee of ₹18/share, paying ₹3,000 transaction charges. What income is taxable from the SLB transaction?\nA) ₹90,000  B) Nil (treated as transfer)  C) ₹93,000  D) ₹87,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Lending under SLB is NOT a transfer (Section 47(xv)); only the fee is taxed (less expenses). Fee = 5,000 × ₹18 = ₹90,000; less ₹3,000 charges = ₹87,000. A) forgets expenses; C) adds them; B) is wrong — the fee is still taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A Muslim may bequeath by will (wasiyat) at most what fraction of his net assets (without heirs' consent)?\nA) 1/2  B) 2/3  C) 1/3  D) Whole estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Muslim law limits a will to one-third of net assets; the remaining two-thirds must be distributed among heirs. A) and D) exceed the limit; B) is the portion that must go to heirs.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Mr. M's father bought a house for ₹50,00,000 in FY 2002-03 (CII 105) and Mr. M inherited it. He sells in FY 2020-21 (CII 301). What is the indexed cost of acquisition (a transfer made before 23-Jul-2024)?\nA) ₹50,00,000  B) ₹3,01,00,000  C) ₹1,50,50,000  D) ₹1,43,33,333\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Indexed cost = cost × (CII of sale year ÷ CII of acquisition year) = ₹50,00,000 × (301 ÷ 105) = ₹1,43,33,333. Inherited property uses the previous owner's cost and the year first held. A) ignores indexation; B) and C) apply the index wrongly.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** For property (land/building) sold on or after 23-Jul-2024 but acquired before that date, indexation:\nA) Is available as an option (parallel computation)  B) Is never available  C) Is mandatory at 20%  D) Applies only to listed shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For land/building acquired before 23-Jul-2024, the taxpayer may compute both ways (with indexation at the old rate, or without at the new rate) and use the lower tax. B) is wrong for old property; C) overstates; D) confuses asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Gifts received from which of the following are exempt from tax in the donee's hands?\nA) A close friend on a birthday  B) A colleague at retirement  C) A sibling of the donee  D) An unrelated business partner\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Gifts from defined relatives (spouse, siblings of self/spouse, parents, grandparents, children, grandchildren) are exempt. Friends, colleagues and business partners are not relatives, so those gifts are taxable as income from other sources (above the threshold).\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Under Section 112A, the LTCG exemption threshold on listed equity / equity-oriented mutual funds per year is:\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹1,25,000  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 112A exempts the first ₹1,25,000 of such LTCG each year; the excess is taxed at 12.5%. A) is the old threshold; B) is the 80C limit; D) is the basic exemption limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An estate consists of all properties owned/controlled. Which category includes the housing loan and credit-card dues?\nA) Gross estate  B) Residue estate  C) Estate debt  D) Living trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Estate debt covers all debts and obligations (housing loan, car loan, credit-card payments, tax liabilities). Gross estate is net-worth assets; residue estate is personal items not mentioned in the will; a living trust is a tool, not a category.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Mr. N buys 1,000 units of an equity fund at ₹50 on 1-Jun-2021 and another 1,000 at ₹70 on 1-Mar-2024. He redeems 1,000 units on 1-Aug-2024 at ₹90 (FIFO). What is the gain on those units and its type?\nA) ₹40,000 LTCG  B) ₹20,000 STCG  C) ₹40,000 STCG  D) ₹20,000 LTCG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FIFO redeems the earliest lot (1-Jun-2021), held > 12 months = LONG-term. Gain = 1,000 × (₹90 − ₹50) = ₹40,000 LTCG (Section 112A). B) and C) use the later lot; D) uses wrong cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Standard deduction available to a salaried employee under the new tax regime (Section 115BAC) for FY 2024-25 is:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹75,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the new regime the standard deduction is ₹75,000; under the old regime it is ₹50,000. A) is the old-regime figure; B) is the 80C limit; D) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A Hindu male dies intestate leaving only a brother and two sisters (no Class I heirs). How is the estate shared?\nA) Brother takes all  B) Sisters take all  C) Equally among brother and two sisters  D) Father's heirs take all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With no Class I heirs, Class II applies. Brother and sisters are in the same Class II entry and share equally — each gets 1/3. A) and B) wrongly exclude siblings; D) skips Class II heirs who exist.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Probate of a will is mandatory if the will was made in, or the immovable property lies within, which towns?\nA) Delhi, Bengaluru, Hyderabad  B) Only Mumbai  C) Any state capital  D) Kolkata, Chennai, Mumbai\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Probate is mandatory for the three former Presidency towns: Kolkata, and the municipal limits of Chennai and Mumbai. Elsewhere it is optional. A), B) and C) are incorrect lists.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Maximum deduction under Section 80CCD(1B) for self-contribution to NPS (over and above the ₹1.5 lakh combined 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) limit) is:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 80CCD(1B) allows an extra ₹50,000 for NPS, beyond the ₹1.5 lakh ceiling. A) is the overall 80C ceiling; C) is the standard deduction; B) double counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Mr. O renounces rights on 1,000 listed shares for ₹100 each. His gain is short-term. Which statement is correct about its taxation?\nA) Taxed at 12.5% under 112A  B) Taxed at 20% under 111A  C) Taxed at normal slab rates  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A renounced right is a capital asset but is NOT a listed equity share, so Section 111A (20%) does not apply. The short-term gain is added to income and taxed at normal slab rates. A) is for LTCG on equity; B) needs a listed equity share; D) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mr. P holds units in a fund segregated on credit event. He had held the main-portfolio units since 1-Jun-2021; segregated units allotted 1-Apr-2022. For the segregated units, the holding period is reckoned from:\nA) 1-Apr-2022  B) 1-Jun-2021  C) Date of credit event  D) Date of redemption\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The period for which the original main-portfolio units were held is included in the holding of segregated units, i.e. it starts 1-Jun-2021. A) ignores the carry-over; C) and D) are not the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A consolidation (merger) of two equity-oriented mutual fund schemes into one: for the unit-holder, this exchange of units is:\nA) A transfer taxed at 20%  B) A transfer taxed at 12.5%  C) Not a transfer; cost and holding carry over  D) Taxed as dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Consolidation of two equity (or two non-equity) schemes is not treated as a transfer; the cost and holding period of the consolidating units carry into the consolidated units. A), B) and D) wrongly tax a non-transfer event.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A Hindu male dies intestate. Heirs: widow, mother, one son, and the branch of one pre-deceased daughter (that daughter left two surviving sons). The pre-deceased daughter's branch takes one share. How many top-level shares are there in total?\nA) 6  B) 4  C) 5  D) 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Top-level shares: widow (1) + mother (1) + son (1) + pre-deceased daughter's branch (1) = 4. The two grandsons split that 1 share between them; they are not separate top-level heirs. A) wrongly adds the grandsons; C) over-counts; D) under-counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Continuing Q39, what fraction of the whole estate does each of the pre-deceased daughter's two sons receive?\nA) 1/4  B) 1/2  C) 1/16  D) 1/8\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The branch's share is 1/4 of the estate; split equally between the two sons = 1/4 ÷ 2 = 1/8 each. A) ignores the split; C) divides by an extra factor; B) is far too large.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Intestate Succession of a Hindu Male\n\nMr. Rajan, a Hindu, dies intestate on 1-May-2026 leaving a net estate of ₹2,40,00,000. He is survived by: his widow Mrs. Rajan; his mother; one surviving son Arjun; and the branch of a pre-deceased son Vikram. Vikram left behind a widow and two children (one son, one daughter), all surviving. There is no will. Distribution follows the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (Sections 8–10).\n\n| Heir | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Widow (Mrs. Rajan) | surviving |\n| Mother | surviving |\n| Son Arjun | surviving |\n| Pre-deceased son Vikram's branch | widow + son + daughter |\n\n**Q41.** How many equal top-level shares does the estate split into?\nA) 6  B) 5  C) 4  D) 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Top level: widow (1) + mother (1) + son Arjun (1) + Vikram's branch (1) = 4 shares. Vikram's branch takes ONE share between its members (Rule 3). A) wrongly counts the branch members separately; B) and D) miscount.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** What is the rupee value of the widow Mrs. Rajan's share?\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹80,00,000  C) ₹48,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Each of the 4 equal shares = ₹2,40,00,000 ÷ 4 = ₹60,00,000. The widow takes one full share = ₹60,00,000. A) divides by 6; C) divides by 5; B) divides by 3.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Within Vikram's branch (which holds ₹60,00,000), his widow, son and daughter share equally. What does Vikram's widow get?\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹15,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Under Rule 4(1), within a pre-deceased son's branch his widow and surviving sons/daughters take equal proportions. Branch share ₹60,00,000 ÷ 3 = ₹20,00,000 each. B) splits into 2; C) splits into 4; D) gives the whole branch share to one person.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** What fraction of the TOTAL estate does Vikram's surviving daughter receive?\nA) 1/4  B) 1/6  C) 1/12  D) 1/8\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The branch holds 1/4 of the estate; split three ways (widow, son, daughter) = 1/4 ÷ 3 = 1/12 each. A) is the branch share; B) splits by 2; D) splits by 2 of a half.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** If Mr. Rajan had instead left a valid registered will giving everything to his widow, which document would the legal heirs NOT need to apply for to collect his bank securities?\nA) Probate  B) Succession certificate  C) Death certificate  D) Executor's authority\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A succession certificate is needed only when a person dies intestate. With a valid will, the estate vests in the executor and a succession certificate cannot be granted (Section 370). A) probate may still be required; C) death certificate is always needed; D) the executor still acts under the will.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Corporate Actions and Cost Basis\n\nMs. Nair, a resident individual, buys 6,000 listed shares of ZenTech Ltd at ₹200 each on 1-Apr-2022 (STT paid). Two corporate actions follow:\n- Bonus 1:2 (one bonus for every two held) allotted on 1-Jul-2024.\n- She then sells all her shares at ₹250 each on 1-Mar-2025 (STT paid).\n\n| Event | Date | Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purchase | 1-Apr-2022 | 6,000 @ ₹200 |\n| Bonus 1:2 | 1-Jul-2024 | adds bonus shares |\n| Sale (all) | 1-Mar-2025 | @ ₹250 |\n\n**Q46.** How many bonus shares does Ms. Nair receive?\nA) 2,000  B) 12,000  C) 6,000  D) 3,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Bonus 1:2 = one bonus for every two held = 6,000 ÷ 2 = 3,000 bonus shares. A) uses 1:3; C) uses 1:1; B) doubles wrongly.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** What is the LTCG on the 6,000 original shares (before exemption)?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹4,50,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Original shares held > 12 months = long-term. Gain = 6,000 × (₹250 − ₹200) = 6,000 × ₹50 = ₹3,00,000. B) halves it; C) uses all 9,000 shares; D) uses wrong cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** What is the gain on the 3,000 bonus shares and its type?\nA) ₹7,50,000 LTCG  B) ₹7,50,000 STCG  C) ₹1,50,000 STCG  D) ₹6,00,000 STCG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus cost = nil; holding from 1-Jul-2024 to 1-Mar-2025 = 8 months < 12, so SHORT-term. Gain = 3,000 × ₹250 = ₹7,50,000 STCG (Section 111A). A) wrong type; C) and D) wrong counts/cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Tax on the STCG of ₹7,50,000 under Section 111A (ignore cess/surcharge)?\nA) ₹93,750  B) ₹1,12,500  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG on listed equity (STT paid) is taxed at 20% under Section 111A. 20% × ₹7,50,000 = ₹1,50,000. A) uses 12.5%; C) uses 10%; B) uses 15%.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Tax on the LTCG of ₹3,00,000 under Section 112A (ignore cess/surcharge)?\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹21,875  C) ₹60,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 112A taxes only the excess over ₹1,25,000 at 12.5%. Taxable = ₹3,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹1,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹1,75,000 = ₹21,875. A) forgets the exemption; C) uses STCG rate; D) ignores the taxable excess.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Wills, Trusts and HUF Taxation\n\nMr. Iyer (Karta of an HUF) is doing his estate planning. The HUF owns a let-out commercial property and equity investments. Mr. Iyer wants to: (i) create a testamentary trust for his special-needs daughter, (ii) write a will, and (iii) understand how HUF and gift rules affect his plan. He also gifts ₹10,00,000 to his married daughter and ₹5,00,000 to a close friend during the year.\n\n**Q51.** A trust created through a will is called a:\nA) Living trust  B) Testamentary trust  C) Revocable trust  D) Asset-protection trust\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A trust created via a will is a testamentary trust; it takes effect after death and goes through probate. A) takes effect during life; C) and D) describe other trust features, not the will-created trust.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Mr. Iyer's gift of ₹10,00,000 to his married daughter is taxed in her hands how?\nA) Fully taxable as income from other sources  B) Exempt — daughter is a relative  C) Taxed at 12.5%  D) Half exempt, half taxable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A child is a defined relative, so the gift is exempt from tax in the daughter's hands regardless of amount. A) ignores the relative exemption; C) is a capital-gains rate, not applicable; D) is invented.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** The ₹5,00,000 gift to a close friend is treated how in the friend's hands?\nA) Fully exempt  B) Exempt up to ₹50,000, balance taxable  C) Fully taxable as income from other sources  D) Taxed at 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A friend is not a relative; a non-relative gift exceeding ₹50,000 in aggregate makes the ENTIRE amount taxable as income from other sources. Since ₹5,00,000 > ₹50,000, the whole ₹5,00,000 is taxable. A) ignores the rule; B) misstates the mechanism (it is all-or-nothing once it crosses ₹50,000); D) uses a wrong rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A distribution of capital assets on the total or partial partition of an HUF is treated under the Income Tax Act as:\nA) A taxable transfer (capital gains)  B) Not a transfer (Section 47)  C) A gift to members  D) Dividend income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Distribution of capital assets on total or partial partition of an HUF is specifically excluded from \"transfer\" under Section 47(i); no capital gains arise at partition. A), C) and D) wrongly tax a non-transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** If Mr. Iyer's will is unregistered, which statement is correct?\nA) It is automatically invalid  B) It cannot be revoked  C) It must be probated in all states  D) It is still a genuine, valid will\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Registration of a will is optional; an unregistered will is still considered a genuine, valid will. A) is false; C) overstates the probate rule; B) is false — a will is always revocable during the testator's lifetime.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Capital Gains on Property and Equity\n\nMr. Khan sells two assets in FY 2024-25. (1) A residential plot bought for ₹30,00,000 in FY 2014-15 (CII 240) and sold on 1-Jun-2024 (before 23-Jul-2024; CII for 2024-25 = 363) for ₹90,00,000. (2) Listed equity shares bought for ₹4,00,000 (held 3 years, STT paid) sold for ₹9,00,000 in the same year.\n\n| Asset | Cost | Sale | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plot | ₹30,00,000 (FY14-15, CII 240) | ₹90,00,000 (1-Jun-2024, CII 363) | indexation available |\n| Listed shares | ₹4,00,000 | ₹9,00,000 | LTCG, STT paid |\n\n**Q56.** What is the indexed cost of acquisition of the plot?\nA) ₹45,37,500  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹19,83,471  D) ₹90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Indexed cost = ₹30,00,000 × (363 ÷ 240) = ₹30,00,000 × 1.5125 = ₹45,37,500. The sale is before 23-Jul-2024, so indexation is available. B) ignores indexation; C) inverts the ratio; D) is the sale price.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** What is the LTCG on the plot?\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹44,62,500  C) ₹45,37,500  D) ₹90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — LTCG = sale − indexed cost = ₹90,00,000 − ₹45,37,500 = ₹44,62,500. A) ignores indexation; C) is the indexed cost; D) is the sale price.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** What is the LTCG on the listed shares (before the 112A exemption)?\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹9,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹3,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = ₹9,00,000 − ₹4,00,000 = ₹5,00,000. B) ignores cost; C) is the cost; D) is invented.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Tax on the listed-share LTCG under Section 112A (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹62,500  B) ₹46,875  C) ₹1,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable = ₹5,00,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹3,75,000; tax = 12.5% × ₹3,75,000 = ₹46,875. A) forgets the exemption; C) uses 20%; D) ignores the excess.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** The plot LTCG of ₹44,62,500 (transfer before 23-Jul-2024, indexed) is taxed at what rate?\nA) 12.5% without indexation  B) 20% with indexation  C) 30% slab  D) Exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a long-term property transfer before 23-Jul-2024, the gain is taxed at 20% with indexation benefit. A) is the new (post-23-Jul-2024) rate; C) is the slab rate, not the LTCG rate; D) is wrong.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Retirement and NPS Planning\n\nMr. Verma, 58, plans to retire at 60. His NPS corpus is expected to be ₹1,00,00,000 at 60. Under NPS exit rules at 60, a minimum 40% must buy an annuity and up to 60% can be taken as a lump sum. His current annual salary contribution to NPS qualifies for deductions. He contributes ₹50,000 to NPS under 80CCD(1B) and his 80C investments already total ₹1,50,000.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| NPS corpus at 60 | ₹1,00,00,000 |\n| Mandatory annuity | minimum 40% |\n| Lump sum | up to 60% |\n\n**Q61.** What is the minimum amount Mr. Verma must use to buy an annuity at 60?\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At 60, NPS requires at least 40% of the corpus to be annuitised = 40% × ₹1,00,00,000 = ₹40,00,000. A) is the lump-sum portion; C) is half; B) is the whole corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** What is the maximum lump-sum he can withdraw at 60?\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹50,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Up to 60% can be taken as lump sum = 60% × ₹1,00,00,000 = ₹60,00,000. A) is the annuity minimum; B) is half; D) is the whole corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** His ₹50,000 NPS contribution gives him an extra deduction under which section, given his 80C is already full?\nA) 80C  B) 80CCD(1B)  C) 80D  D) 80G\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 80CCD(1B) allows up to ₹50,000 for NPS OVER and above the ₹1.5 lakh 80C/80CCC/80CCD(1) ceiling, which is already full here. A) is consumed; C) is health insurance; D) is donations.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** If his annuity of ₹40,00,000 yields 6% per year, what is the annual annuity income (simple yield)?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual annuity income = 6% × ₹40,00,000 = ₹2,40,000. B) uses 10%; C) uses 5%; D) uses 15%.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** If Mr. Verma instead invests the ₹60,00,000 lump sum for 5 years at 8% compounded annually, the maturity value is closest to:\nA) ₹84,00,000  B) ₹96,00,000  C) ₹78,00,000  D) ₹88,15,968\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV = PV × (1 + r)^n = ₹60,00,000 × (1.08)^5. (1.08)^5 ≈ 1.469328; ₹60,00,000 × 1.469328 ≈ ₹88,15,968. A) uses simple interest (₹60,00,000 × (1 + 0.08×5) = ₹84,00,000); C) is too low; B) overshoots.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Estate, Succession and Taxation\n\nMr. Bose, a Hindu, dies intestate with a net estate of ₹3,60,00,000. Survivors: widow, mother, two sons, and one daughter. The estate includes 10,000 listed shares (bought by Mr. Bose at ₹100 in 2019; FMV at death ₹400) which the heirs later sell. Mr. Bose had also set up a nomination on his bank deposit naming only his elder son.\n\n| Heir | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Widow | surviving |\n| Mother | surviving |\n| Son 1, Son 2 | surviving |\n| Daughter | surviving |\n\n**Q66.** Into how many equal shares does Mr. Bose's estate divide among Class I heirs?\nA) 6  B) 5  C) 4  D) 3\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Heirs each take one share: widow (1) + mother (1) + son 1 (1) + son 2 (1) + daughter (1) = 5 shares. A) over-counts; C) and D) under-count.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** What is the rupee value of the daughter's share?\nA) ₹72,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹90,00,000  D) ₹1,20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Each share = ₹3,60,00,000 ÷ 5 = ₹72,00,000; the daughter (a full Class I heir, equal to sons) gets ₹72,00,000. B) divides by 6; C) divides by 4; D) divides by 3.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The elder son is the sole nominee on the bank deposit, but the heirs dispute it. What is the legal position of the nominee?\nA) Nominee becomes absolute owner  B) Nominee holds as trustee; succession law decides ownership  C) Nominee is excluded entirely  D) Deposit lapses to the bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For most financial assets, a nominee merely receives the proceeds as a trustee/custodian; the final ownership is decided by the laws of succession. So the deposit is shared among all Class I heirs per succession, not kept solely by the nominee. A) overstates nominee rights; C) is wrong — the nominee can still receive/hold; D) is incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** The heirs inherit the 10,000 shares and later sell them. For computing their capital gains, the cost of acquisition is:\nA) ₹400 (FMV at death)  B) ₹250  C) Nil  D) ₹100 (Mr. Bose's original cost)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Property received by inheritance carries the previous owner's cost of acquisition (₹100). FMV at death is not the cost for inheritance (no step-up). A) wrongly steps up; C) treats it like a bonus; B) is invented.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** The heirs sell the 10,000 inherited shares at ₹450 each (listed, STT paid; long-term including the previous owner's holding). What is the total LTCG before exemption?\nA) ₹35,00,000  B) ₹45,00,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cost carries over at ₹100 (inheritance) and holding includes the previous owner's period (long-term). LTCG = 10,000 × (₹450 − ₹100) = 10,000 × ₹350 = ₹35,00,000. B) uses FMV ₹400 wrongly as sale base difference from cost nil; C) uses wrong cost; D) ignores cost.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "08", "md": "# Mock Paper 8 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is numerical and case-heavy, weighted towards risk profiling, asset allocation, behavioural finance applied to real clients, and post-tax product comparisons. Keep a calculator or spreadsheet handy — most questions need a worked calculation, not a guess.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** A client invests ₹6,00,000 in a portfolio that is 60% equity (returns 14% p.a.) and 40% debt (returns 7% p.a.). What is the weighted (blended) expected return of the portfolio for one year?\nA) 10.50%  B) 9.80%  C) 12.60%  D) 11.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Blended return = (weight₁ × return₁) + (weight₂ × return₂). Plug in: (0.60 × 14%) + (0.40 × 7%) = 8.4% + 2.8% = 11.2%. A) 10.5% is the simple average of 14 and 7 (ignores the 60/40 weights). C) 12.6% wrongly uses 90/10 weights. B) 9.8% mistakenly weights debt at 60%. The lesson: portfolio return is the *weighted* average of each asset's return, weighted by how much money sits in each.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Inflation is 6% and a bank FD gives a nominal return of 7.2%. What is the real (inflation-adjusted) return?\nA) 1.20%  B) 1.13%  C) 0.72%  D) 13.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1. Plug in: (1.072 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.01132 − 1 = 0.01132 = 1.13%. A) 1.20% is the rough shortcut (7.2 − 6.0), which slightly overstates the true real return. C) 0.72% just halves wrongly. D) adds them. The exact Fisher formula divides instead of subtracting, so the answer is a touch below 1.2%.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An investor sells listed equity shares held for 18 months and books a long-term capital gain of ₹3,25,000 in FY 2025-26. What is the LTCG tax under Section 112A (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹24,375  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹65,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 112A, listed-equity LTCG is taxed at 12.5% only on the amount above the ₹1,25,000 annual exemption. Taxable gain = ₹3,25,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,00,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹25,000. A) ₹40,625 forgets the exemption (12.5% × ₹3,25,000). B) ₹24,375 uses a wrong base. D) ₹65,000 applies 20% (the STCG rate). Holding > 12 months = long-term for listed equity, so 112A applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An investor sells listed equity shares held for 7 months with a short-term gain of ₹1,80,000 (FY 2025-26, STT paid). What is the STCG tax under Section 111A (ignore surcharge/cess)?\nA) ₹22,500  B) ₹6,875  C) ₹36,000  D) ₹18,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG on listed equity under Section 111A is taxed at a flat 20% with NO basic exemption like the ₹1.25 lakh. Tax = 20% × ₹1,80,000 = ₹36,000. A) ₹22,500 wrongly uses 12.5%. B) ₹6,875 wrongly subtracts the ₹1.25 lakh first then applies 12.5% — the exemption is only for LTCG. D) ₹18,000 uses an old 10% rate. Holding ≤ 12 months for listed equity = short-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A 30-year-old client wants ₹40 lakh (today's value) for a goal 10 years away. Inflation is 6% p.a. What is the inflation-adjusted future cost?\nA) ₹64,00,000  B) ₹70,00,000  C) ₹71,63,391  D) ₹66,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)^n = ₹40,00,000 × (1.06)^10. (1.06)^10 = 1.790847, so cost = ₹40,00,000 × 1.790847 = ₹71,63,391. A) ₹64,00,000 wrongly uses simple inflation (40 + 6%×10 ≈ 60%). B) ₹70,00,000 and D) ₹66,00,000 under-compound. Money loses value as prices compound, so you must grow the goal at compound inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A portfolio's value grew from ₹5,00,000 to ₹9,00,000 over 4 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 20.00%  B) 12.59%  C) 15.83%  D) 16.86%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (9,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/4) − 1 = (1.8)^0.25 − 1. (1.8)^0.25 = 1.15829, so CAGR = 15.83%. A) 20% is the simple average annual growth (80% total ÷ 4 years). B) 12.59% and D) 16.86% mis-root. CAGR is the steady compounded rate that turns the start value into the end value over n years.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A client's monthly take-home is ₹1,20,000 and monthly expenses are ₹85,000. What is the monthly surplus available for investing (savings rate to nearest %)?\nA) ₹35,000 and 70%  B) ₹35,000 and 41%  C) ₹40,000 and 33%  D) ₹35,000 and 29%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Surplus = income − expenses = ₹1,20,000 − ₹85,000 = ₹35,000. Savings rate = surplus ÷ income = 35,000 ÷ 1,20,000 = 29.2% ≈ 29%. B) 41% wrongly divides by expenses (35,000 ÷ 85,000). C) miscomputes the surplus. A) 70% is the expense ratio, not the savings rate. Savings rate is always surplus as a fraction of income.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Using the \"100 minus age\" rule of thumb for equity allocation, a 42-year-old has a corpus of ₹50 lakh. What is the suggested equity rupee allocation?\nA) ₹21,00,000  B) ₹25,00,000  C) ₹42,00,000  D) ₹29,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The rule says equity % = 100 − age = 100 − 42 = 58%. Equity allocation = 58% × ₹50,00,000 = ₹29,00,000. A) ₹21,00,000 is the *debt* portion (42% × 50 lakh). C) puts age% directly into rupees wrongly. B) is a 50/50 split. The rule lowers equity as you age because you have less time to recover from market falls.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A tax-saving bank FD pays 7% p.a. The investor is in the 30% slab. What is the post-tax return on the FD?\nA) 7.00%  B) 4.90%  C) 2.10%  D) 5.39%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FD interest is fully taxable each year at the slab rate. Post-tax return = nominal × (1 − tax rate) = 7% × (1 − 0.30) = 7% × 0.70 = 4.90%. A) 7% ignores tax. C) 2.10% is the tax *taken away*, not what's left. D) 5.39% wrongly applies a lower rate. FD interest gives no concessional treatment, so a high earner keeps only 70% of the headline rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** PPF pays 7.1% p.a., fully tax-free (EEE). For an investor in the 30% slab, what taxable FD rate would be needed to match PPF's post-tax return?\nA) 7.10%  B) 9.23%  C) 10.14%  D) 8.52%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A tax-free 7.1% must be grossed up: equivalent taxable rate = tax-free rate ÷ (1 − tax rate) = 7.1% ÷ (1 − 0.30) = 7.1% ÷ 0.70 = 10.14%. A) 7.1% ignores PPF's tax-free edge. B) 9.23% uses 0.769 (a 23% slab) instead of 0.70. D) 8.52% multiplies by 1.20. Because PPF interest is fully exempt, a taxable product must yield ~10.1% pre-tax to leave the same in hand.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A 60/40 equity-debt portfolio of ₹10,00,000 drifts to ₹7,80,000 equity and ₹4,20,000 debt after a rally. How much equity must be sold to rebalance back to 60/40?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹1,08,000  C) ₹48,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — New total = ₹7,80,000 + ₹4,20,000 = ₹12,00,000. Target equity = 60% × ₹12,00,000 = ₹7,20,000. Current equity ₹7,80,000 − target ₹7,20,000 = ₹60,000 to sell. B) ₹1,08,000 rebalances to the wrong total. C) and D) use the old ₹10 lakh base. Rebalancing trims the asset that grew (sell high) and tops up the laggard (buy low), restoring the policy mix.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A SIP of ₹10,000 per month runs for 5 years (60 months) earning 12% p.a. (1% per month). Using the future value of annuity formula FV = P × [((1+i)^n − 1) ÷ i], what is the approximate maturity value?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹7,80,000  C) ₹8,16,697  D) ₹6,72,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FV = 10,000 × [((1.01)^60 − 1) ÷ 0.01]. (1.01)^60 = 1.81670, so [(1.81670 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 81.670. FV = 10,000 × 81.670 = ₹8,16,697. A) ₹6,00,000 is only the money invested (no growth). B) ₹7,80,000 and D) ₹6,72,000 under-compound. SIP value grows because each instalment compounds for the months remaining.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Required return: a client needs ₹1 crore in 8 years and can invest ₹50 lakh today as a lump sum. What annual return must the corpus earn?\nA) 12.50%  B) 9.05%  C) 8.00%  D) 7.18%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Required return = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,00,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = (2)^0.125 − 1. (2)^0.125 = 1.09051, so 9.05%. A) 12.5% is the simple growth (100% ÷ 8). C) and D) are too low. To double money in 8 years you need just over 9% compounded (matches the \"Rule of 72\": 72 ÷ 8 = 9%).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Under EPF, an employee earns a basic salary of ₹40,000/month. The employer's 12% contribution is split into EPS (8.33% of wages, capped at ₹15,000 wage ceiling) and EPF. What is the monthly EPS contribution?\nA) ₹3,332  B) ₹1,800  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹4,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — EPS contribution = 8.33% of the *capped* wage of ₹15,000 = 0.0833 × 15,000 = ₹1,249.5 ≈ ₹1,250. A) ₹3,332 wrongly applies 8.33% to the full ₹40,000. D) ₹4,800 is the total 12% on ₹40,000. B) ₹1,800 mixes up the ceiling. EPS is always limited to the ₹15,000 wage ceiling, so the rest of the employer's 12% flows into EPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Continuing Q14: the employer's total contribution is 12% of ₹40,000 = ₹4,800. After ₹1,250 goes to EPS, how much of the employer's share goes to the EPF account?\nA) ₹4,800  B) ₹3,550  C) ₹2,950  D) ₹1,468\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Employer EPF share = total employer contribution − EPS = ₹4,800 − ₹1,250 = ₹3,550. A) ₹4,800 forgets EPS is carved out. C) and D) use wrong EPS figures. Because EPS is capped at 8.33% of ₹15,000, on higher salaries more of the employer's 12% ends up in EPF rather than the pension (EPS) bucket.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A covered employee retires with a last-drawn salary (basic + DA) of ₹52,000/month after 24 years of service. Compute the gratuity using the formula: last drawn salary × 15/26 × completed years.\nA) ₹6,24,000  B) ₹14,40,000  C) ₹18,72,000  D) ₹7,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Gratuity = (52,000 × 15 ÷ 26) × 24 = (52,000 × 0.5769) × 24 = ₹30,000 × 24 = ₹7,20,000. The 15/26 means 15 days' wages for each completed year (26 working days/month). A) ₹6,24,000 wrongly uses 13/26. B) ₹14,40,000 doubles wrongly (one full month/year). C) ₹18,72,000 uses 15/15. The computed gratuity ₹7,20,000 is well under the ₹20 lakh exemption cap, so it is fully tax-exempt. The key is using 15/26, not 15/30 or 30/26.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** At NPS exit at age 60, a subscriber has a corpus of ₹80 lakh. Per rules, what is the maximum tax-free lump sum and the minimum amount that must buy an annuity?\nA) ₹48 lakh tax-free, ₹32 lakh annuity  B) ₹32 lakh tax-free, ₹48 lakh annuity  C) ₹40 lakh each  D) ₹56 lakh tax-free, ₹24 lakh annuity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — At 60, up to 60% of the NPS corpus can be withdrawn tax-free, and at least 40% must be used to buy an annuity. 60% × ₹80 lakh = ₹48 lakh tax-free; 40% × ₹80 lakh = ₹32 lakh annuity. B) reverses the split. C) is a 50/50 guess. D) uses wrong percentages. The annuity income received later is fully taxable as per slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A debt mutual fund (purchased after 1 April 2023) is sold after 3 years with a gain of ₹2,00,000. How is it taxed for an investor in the 30% slab?\nA) 12.5% LTCG = ₹25,000  B) 20% with indexation  C) At slab rate (30%) = ₹60,000  D) Tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Specified (debt) mutual funds bought on/after 1 April 2023 lose LTCG/indexation benefits — gains are always taxed at the investor's slab rate regardless of holding period. Tax = 30% × ₹2,00,000 = ₹60,000. A) wrongly applies the equity 12.5% rate. B) indexation no longer applies to these funds. D) is wrong — gains are fully taxable. This is exactly why post-tax comparison matters: debt MFs no longer beat FDs purely on tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A ULIP issued in FY 2024-25 has an annual premium of ₹3,00,000. Will its maturity proceeds be exempt under Section 10(10D)?\nA) Yes, always exempt  B) No, ULIPs are never exempt  C) Yes, if sum assured ≥ 5× premium  D) No, because premium exceeds ₹2.5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For ULIPs issued on/after 1 Feb 2021, maturity proceeds are exempt only if the annual premium does not exceed ₹2.5 lakh. Here premium is ₹3,00,000 > ₹2.5 lakh, so the proceeds are NOT exempt (taxed like capital gains). A) ignores the cap. C) the 10× rule is the older condition, now overridden by the ₹2.5 lakh premium cap. B) is too absolute — ULIPs with premium ≤ ₹2.5 lakh remain exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A health insurance claim is ₹4,00,000. The policy has a ₹50,000 deductible and a 10% co-pay on the balance. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹3,15,000  B) ₹3,50,000  C) ₹3,60,000  D) ₹2,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — First subtract the deductible: ₹4,00,000 − ₹50,000 = ₹3,50,000. Then the co-pay (insured's 10% share) reduces the insurer's payout: insurer pays 90% × ₹3,50,000 = ₹3,15,000. B) ₹3,50,000 forgets the co-pay. C) applies co-pay before the deductible. D) double-counts. Deductible comes off first, then co-pay applies to the remaining bill.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Human Life Value (HLV) by the income-replacement method: a 35-year-old earns ₹12,00,000 net p.a., needs to replace income for 25 years, and the discount rate is 7%. Using PV of annuity (factor ≈ 11.654), what is the approximate HLV?\nA) ₹3,00,00,000  B) ₹1,20,00,000  C) ₹1,39,84,800  D) ₹2,10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — HLV = annual income to replace × PV-of-annuity factor = ₹12,00,000 × 11.654 = ₹1,39,84,800. A) ₹3 crore multiplies income × years (12L × 25), ignoring discounting. B) is just the income. D) uses a wrong factor. HLV discounts future income to today's value, so it is less than the simple income × years total.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An investor compares two funds: Fund A returned 13% with standard deviation 18%; Fund B returned 10% with standard deviation 10%. Risk-free rate is 6%. Which has the higher Sharpe ratio?\nA) Fund A (0.39)  B) Both equal  C) Fund B (0.40)  D) Fund A (0.72)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Sharpe = (return − risk-free) ÷ standard deviation. Fund A = (13 − 6) ÷ 18 = 7 ÷ 18 = 0.389. Fund B = (10 − 6) ÷ 10 = 4 ÷ 10 = 0.40. Fund B is marginally higher (0.40 > 0.39). A) miscalls the winner. B) ignores the difference. D) forgets to subtract the risk-free rate. Sharpe rewards return *per unit of risk*, so a lower-return, much-lower-risk fund can win.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An emergency fund should cover 6 months of expenses. A family spends ₹70,000/month. They currently hold ₹2,50,000 in liquid assets. What is the shortfall?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹4,20,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Required emergency fund = 6 × ₹70,000 = ₹4,20,000. Shortfall = required − held = ₹4,20,000 − ₹2,50,000 = ₹1,70,000. B) ₹4,20,000 forgets to subtract existing savings. C) is just current holdings. A) uses 3 months. The emergency fund must be in liquid, low-risk assets so it's available instantly when income stops.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A client in the 30% old-regime slab invests ₹1,50,000 in PPF and claims full Section 80C. What is the tax saved on this deduction?\nA) ₹45,000  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹30,000  D) ₹46,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 80C lets you deduct up to ₹1,50,000 from taxable income. Tax saved = deduction × slab rate = ₹1,50,000 × 30% = ₹45,000. A) is correct (ignoring cess). B) and C) use lower rates. D) ₹46,800 adds 4% cess (₹45,000 × 1.04) — a valid gross-up but the question asks for the base tax saved. The 80C deduction is only available in the old tax regime.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A self-employed person with gross income of ₹15,00,000 contributes to NPS. Under Section 80CCD(1), what is the maximum deduction (within the ₹1.5 lakh overall 80C limit) for own contribution at 20% of gross income?\nA) ₹3,00,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — For a self-employed person, 80CCD(1) allows the lower of (a) amount contributed or (b) 20% of gross income = 20% × ₹15,00,000 = ₹3,00,000 — BUT this falls within the overall ₹1,50,000 ceiling of Sections 80C + 80CCC + 80CCD(1). So the cap binds at ₹1,50,000. A) ₹3,00,000 ignores the overall ceiling. B) and C) are arbitrary. A separate ₹50,000 under 80CCD(1B) sits *above* this ₹1.5 lakh limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Continuing Q25: the same self-employed person can also claim the additional NPS deduction. What total NPS-related deduction is possible combining 80CCD(1) and 80CCD(1B) (old regime)?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹2,50,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 80CCD(1) (within the ₹1.5 lakh limit) + the extra ₹50,000 under 80CCD(1B) = ₹1,50,000 + ₹50,000 = ₹2,00,000. A) ignores the extra ₹50,000. C) and D) over-count. The 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000 is over and above the ₹1.5 lakh, which is why NPS is attractive for extra tax saving.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An investor buys a Sovereign Gold Bond carrying 2.5% p.a. interest on the face value of ₹6,000/gram, buying 50 grams. What is the annual interest income (paid semi-annually)?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹7,500  C) ₹3,750  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investment face value = 50 g × ₹6,000 = ₹3,00,000. Annual interest = 2.5% × ₹3,00,000 = ₹7,500 (paid as ₹3,750 every six months). A) ₹15,000 uses 5%. C) ₹3,750 is just one half-yearly payment. D) is the principal. SGB interest is taxable as per slab, but if the bond is redeemed with RBI at maturity, the capital gain is tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A client withdraws ₹6,00,000 from a debt growth fund. The cost was ₹5,00,000 (units bought after 1 April 2023). The investor is in the 20% slab. What is the after-tax amount received?\nA) ₹5,80,000  B) ₹6,00,000  C) ₹5,87,500  D) ₹5,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = ₹6,00,000 − ₹5,00,000 = ₹1,00,000. Debt fund (post-Apr-2023) gain is taxed at slab = 20% × ₹1,00,000 = ₹20,000. After-tax = ₹6,00,000 − ₹20,000 = ₹5,80,000. B) ignores tax. C) wrongly applies 12.5% on the gain. D) taxes the whole withdrawal. Only the *gain* portion is taxed, but at the full slab rate for these funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A client's portfolio is 70% equity, 20% debt, 10% gold. Equity falls 20%, debt rises 5%, gold rises 15%. Starting value ₹10,00,000. What is the new portfolio value?\nA) ₹8,85,000  B) ₹8,80,000  C) ₹9,40,000  D) ₹8,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Compute each sleeve: Equity ₹7,00,000 × 0.80 = ₹5,60,000; Debt ₹2,00,000 × 1.05 = ₹2,10,000; Gold ₹1,00,000 × 1.15 = ₹1,15,000. Total = ₹5,60,000 + ₹2,10,000 + ₹1,15,000 = ₹8,85,000. B) ₹8,80,000 leaves debt flat instead of +5%. C) and D) mis-add. The point: a 20% equity crash only dragged the whole portfolio down ~11.5% (to ₹8.85 lakh) because debt and gold rose — that cushioning is the value of diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Term + invest vs endowment: a 35-year-old can pay ₹50,000/year. A term plan of ₹1 crore costs ₹12,000/year, leaving ₹38,000 to invest at 10% for 25 years (FV annuity factor ≈ 98.347). Approximately what investment corpus results?\nA) ₹37,37,186  B) ₹9,50,000  C) ₹49,17,350  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Amount invested yearly = ₹50,000 − ₹12,000 = ₹38,000. Corpus = ₹38,000 × 98.347 = ₹37,37,186. Plus the family still has the ₹1 crore term cover for protection. B) is just total premiums × no growth idea. C) uses the full ₹50,000 instead of the ₹38,000 left after the term premium. D) is arbitrary. \"Buy term and invest the rest\" typically beats an endowment policy that bundles weak returns with cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** An aggressive investor's risk profile suggests 80% equity. Their goal is 2 years away. What should the adviser do?\nA) Keep 80% equity per the profile  B) Increase equity to 100%  C) Reduce equity sharply because the time horizon is short  D) Move fully to gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Asset allocation depends on BOTH risk profile and time horizon. Even an aggressive investor should not hold 80% equity for a 2-year goal — equity can fall sharply and may not recover in time. The adviser should reduce equity and shift to debt/liquid to protect the corpus. A) ignores the short horizon. B) makes it worse. D) over-corrects into a volatile single asset. Time horizon can override risk tolerance for near-term goals.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Risk capacity vs risk tolerance: a client says she is comfortable with big swings (high tolerance) but has only one income, three dependents, and a large home loan. What is her true position?\nA) High capacity, high tolerance  B) Low capacity, high tolerance  C) High capacity, low tolerance  D) Low capacity, low tolerance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk *tolerance* is the psychological willingness to take risk (she is comfortable = high). Risk *capacity* is the financial ability to absorb losses — with one income, dependents and a big loan, her capacity is LOW. A) and C) misjudge capacity. D) misjudges tolerance. The adviser must anchor advice to the *lower* of the two — here, capacity — to avoid putting essential funds at risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A client holds an inherited stock worth ₹5,00,000 that no longer fits the plan but refuses to sell it. Which bias is this?\nA) Anchoring bias  B) Mental accounting  C) Endowment bias  D) Herd mentality\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Endowment bias is valuing an asset more simply because you own it; inherited holdings are rarely sold even when they don't fit the strategy. A) anchoring is fixating on a reference price. B) mental accounting treats money in separate \"buckets.\" D) herd mentality is following the crowd. The fix: evaluate the holding as if deciding to buy it fresh today.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An investor sells winning funds quickly to \"lock in gains\" but holds losing funds hoping they bounce back. This is the disposition effect, driven by which bias?\nA) Overconfidence  B) Framing  C) Loss aversion  D) Status quo\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The disposition effect (Shefrin & Statman) flows from loss aversion — losses hurt more than equivalent gains feel good, so people gamble to avoid booking a loss (hold losers) but rush to secure gains (sell winners). A) overconfidence is overrating one's skill. B) framing is about how choices are presented. D) status quo is inaction. The fix: set rules-based sell discipline, not emotion.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A client only invests in last year's top-performing asset class each year. Which behaviour is this, and the adviser's antidote?\nA) Home bias; invest abroad  B) Chasing past performance; pre-set asset-allocation limits  C) Anchoring; ignore prices  D) Mental accounting; merge accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Chasing past performance assumes last year's winner repeats; but asset classes mean-revert, so this is \"driving by the rear-view mirror.\" The antidote is a pre-decided asset-allocation policy with upper limits per asset class, which forces selling high and buying low. A), C), D) describe different biases and fixes. Discipline beats performance-chasing.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A client buys insurance every March only to save tax, ending with inadequate cover. Per the behavioural-finance-in-practice chapter, what is the correct guidance?\nA) Continue — tax saving is the priority  B) Buy more endowment policies  C) Highlight that term + PPF beats endowment, and buy adequate pure cover  D) Stop all insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The bias of \"buying insurance for tax saving\" leads to wrong/inadequate cover. The cure is to show that the ultimate return from a term plan + PPF beats an endowment plan for the same outlay, and to buy adequate *pure* protection. A) reinforces the bias. B) compounds the mistake. D) is reckless — protection is the first step of any plan.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Equity LTCG: a client books gains of ₹1,10,000 on listed equity (held 14 months) in FY 2025-26 and has no other capital gains. What LTCG tax is due?\nA) ₹13,750  B) ₹0  C) ₹22,000  D) ₹1,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Listed-equity LTCG up to ₹1,25,000 per year is exempt under Section 112A. The gain of ₹1,10,000 is below ₹1,25,000, so tax = ₹0. A) ₹13,750 wrongly taxes the full gain at 12.5%. C) applies 20%. D) is arbitrary. Using the annual exemption fully each year (\"tax harvesting\") is a legitimate planning tool.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A retiree needs ₹50,000/month (₹6,00,000/year) and has a corpus of ₹1,00,00,000. Using a 4% safe withdrawal rate, can the corpus sustain the withdrawal?\nA) Yes, exactly 4%  B) No — required rate is 6%, above the 4% guideline  C) Yes, only 2%  D) No — required rate is 12%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Withdrawal rate = annual need ÷ corpus = ₹6,00,000 ÷ ₹1,00,00,000 = 6%. This exceeds the 4% safe-withdrawal guideline, so the corpus risks depletion. A) misreads the rate. C) halves it. D) doubles it. The client must either build a larger corpus (₹1.5 crore for 4%) or cut expenses.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An ETF (gold) and a gold fund both track gold. The ETF NAV is ₹62 but it trades on the exchange at ₹64 with a 0.3% brokerage. Compared with buying the gold fund at NAV ₹62, what is the extra cost per unit of going via the ETF?\nA) ₹2.00 only  B) ₹2.19  C) ₹0.19  D) ₹0.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ETF buyer pays the market price ₹64 (a ₹2 premium to NAV) plus brokerage 0.3% × ₹64 = ₹0.192. Total cost ₹64.192 vs fund NAV ₹62 → extra cost = ₹2.192 ≈ ₹2.19. A) counts only the premium, not brokerage. C) counts only brokerage. D) ignores both. ETFs can trade away from NAV and carry brokerage/spread costs — a real drag where liquidity is thin.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A PMS requires a minimum investment of ₹50 lakh and an AIF Category 3 requires ₹1 crore. A client has ₹40 lakh to invest. Which is suitable?\nA) PMS only  B) AIF only  C) Neither — a mutual fund (min as low as ₹1,000) suits  D) Both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SEBI sets minimum investment of ₹50 lakh for PMS and ₹1 crore for AIFs. With only ₹40 lakh, the client qualifies for neither; a mutual fund (which can start as low as ₹1,000) is the suitable, well-diversified, retail-friendly choice. A) and B) breach the minimums. D) is impossible. Product suitability must respect both the minimums and the client's profile.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Risk Profiling and Asset Allocation for the Mehta Family\n\nMr. Rajan Mehta, 38, is the sole earner in his family (wife, two school-going children, and a dependent mother). His net annual income is ₹24,00,000; annual expenses ₹14,40,000. He has a home loan with an EMI of ₹60,000/month. He describes himself as \"willing to take big risks\" on a questionnaire, but his adviser notes his single income and four dependents. His investable corpus is ₹40,00,000. His main goal (children's higher education) is 9 years away.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Net annual income | ₹24,00,000 |\n| Annual expenses | ₹14,40,000 |\n| Home-loan EMI | ₹60,000/month |\n| Investable corpus | ₹40,00,000 |\n| Dependents | 4 |\n| Goal horizon | 9 years |\n\n**Q41.** What is Mr. Mehta's annual investable surplus AFTER expenses and EMI?\nA) ₹9,60,000  B) ₹2,40,000  C) ₹9,00,000  D) ₹7,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus = income − expenses − annual EMI. Annual EMI = ₹60,000 × 12 = ₹7,20,000. Surplus = ₹24,00,000 − ₹14,40,000 − ₹7,20,000 = ₹2,40,000. A) ₹9,60,000 ignores the EMI (income − expenses only). C) ₹9,00,000 mis-subtracts. D) ₹7,20,000 is the EMI itself. Loan repayments are a committed outflow, so they must come out before computing what's free to invest.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Despite his stated high tolerance, how should the adviser classify his risk CAPACITY?\nA) High — he earns well  B) Low to moderate — single income, four dependents, large loan  C) Aggressive — he says so  D) Irrelevant if tolerance is high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk capacity is the financial ability to bear losses. A single income supporting four dependents plus a large home loan sharply limits his ability to absorb a market hit, so capacity is low-to-moderate even though stated tolerance is high. A) over-weights income alone. C) confuses tolerance with capacity. D) is wrong — capacity must be respected, and the adviser anchors to the lower of the two.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Per the \"moderate risk profile, married single income with school-going kids\" model portfolio (35% equity, 10% sector, 15% gold, 30% debt/FD, 10% liquid), what rupee amount goes to equity (diversified equity, excluding sector funds) from the ₹40,00,000 corpus?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹18,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹14,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Diversified equity = 35% × ₹40,00,000 = ₹14,00,000. B) ₹18,00,000 wrongly adds the 10% sector slice (45%). C) ₹4,00,000 is the liquid slice. A) ₹6,00,000 is sector. The model splits equity (35%) and sector funds (10%) separately, so only ₹14 lakh goes to diversified equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Using the same model portfolio, what is the combined allocation to gold ETF plus the liquid/short-term bucket, in rupees?\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹6,00,000  C) ₹4,00,000  D) ₹14,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gold = 15% and liquid = 10%, total 25% × ₹40,00,000 = ₹10,00,000. A) is correct. B) is gold only (15% = ₹6,00,000). C) is liquid only (10% = ₹4,00,000). D) confuses with equity. Gold adds diversification (low correlation to equity) and the liquid bucket meets near-term needs.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** With his education goal 9 years away, as the goal approaches (say 2 years out), what rebalancing action is appropriate?\nA) Increase equity to chase returns  B) Sell all gold  C) Shift accumulated equity gradually into debt/liquid to protect the corpus  D) Stop the SIP entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — As a goal nears, the priority shifts from growth to capital protection. The adviser should move accumulated equity gains into safer debt/liquid assets so a late market fall doesn't shrink the education corpus. A) raises risk at the worst time. B) and D) are arbitrary over-reactions. This \"glide path\" de-risking is core to goal-based investing.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Behavioural Coaching for Mr. Verma\n\nMr. Verma, 45, made a 5x return on a small tech-stock bet in 3 months and now wants to put 70% of his ₹50,00,000 portfolio into that single stock. He checks his portfolio daily and trades frequently. He also refuses to consider international funds, saying \"I only trust Indian companies.\" When markets fell last year, he panicked and sold equity at the bottom. His original strategic allocation was 50% equity, 30% debt, 20% gold.\n\n**Q46.** Mr. Verma believes his tech-stock win proves his stock-picking skill and wants to bet big. Which bias is at play?\nA) Loss aversion  B) Mental accounting  C) Overconfidence (with self-attribution)  D) Framing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Overconfidence bias, intensified by self-attribution, makes him credit a lucky 5x to skill and over-bet. A) loss aversion is about fearing losses. B) mental accounting is bucketing money. D) framing is about presentation. The adviser's role: stand between the client and a concentrated bet, reminding him the win was mostly luck and unlikely to repeat.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Concentrating 70% in one stock breaks his diversification. If that stock fell 40% while the rest of a diversified portfolio stayed flat, what would be his portfolio loss on ₹50,00,000?\nA) ₹14,00,000 (28%)  B) ₹20,00,000 (40%)  C) ₹7,00,000 (14%)  D) ₹5,00,000 (10%)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 70% of ₹50,00,000 = ₹35,00,000 in the stock. A 40% fall = ₹35,00,000 × 0.40 = ₹14,00,000 loss = 28% of the total portfolio. B) wrongly applies 40% to the whole portfolio. C) uses 20% weight. D) uses 10%. Concentration magnifies single-stock risk dramatically — diversification would have cushioned this.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** His refusal to invest outside India denies him diversification benefits. What is this bias and its antidote?\nA) Status quo; do nothing  B) Home country bias; educate on global investing (e.g., via Indian MF schemes investing in S&P 500/Nasdaq)  C) Anchoring; ignore prices  D) Endowment; sell everything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Home country bias is over-preferring domestic securities due to familiarity, losing the diversification from low-correlated foreign assets. The antidote: the adviser educates the client and uses available routes like Indian mutual fund schemes that invest in global indices (S&P 500, Nasdaq 100) or the LRS. A), C), D) name unrelated biases/fixes.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Mr. Verma sold equity at the market bottom out of fear, then missed the recovery. What two emotions drive this cycle, and what is the adviser's role?\nA) Greed then fear; the adviser keeps the client on the pre-planned path  B) Pride then envy; the adviser exits markets  C) Only fear; the adviser sells more  D) None; markets are random\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Clients cycle through greed (over-exuberance) and fear (panic-selling). Selling at the bottom locks in losses and misses the rebound. The adviser's calming, objective role is to keep the client on the journey planned in advance, preventing rash market-timing. B), C), D) misdescribe the cycle and the adviser's stabilising role.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** His frequent daily trading turns investing into trading. Per the chapter, what is the right \"nudge\"?\nA) Trade even more to learn  B) Prime him in advance with the benefits of patience and long-term investing; set rebalancing rules  C) Switch to intraday  D) Give up on him\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Action bias drives over-frequent trading; the cure is to prime the client in advance with the value of patience and long-term consistency, and to set pre-agreed ground rules (e.g., scheduled rebalancing) as built-in nudges. As Samuelson said, \"investing should be dull.\" A) and C) worsen it. D) abandons the adviser's duty.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Product Comparison: Endowment vs Term + Invest, and FD vs Debt MF\n\nMs. Anita, 32, in the 30% tax slab (old regime), is deciding between two routes for ₹60,000/year over 25 years:\n- **Route X (Endowment):** A traditional endowment plan giving ₹1 crore cover and a projected maturity of ₹28,00,000 (tax-free), eating the full ₹60,000/year.\n- **Route Y (Term + Invest):** A ₹1 crore term plan costing ₹10,000/year; the remaining ₹50,000/year invested in an equity index fund at 11% p.a. (FV annuity factor for 25 years at 11% ≈ 114.413).\n\nShe separately holds ₹5,00,000 to park for 4 years and compares a 7% bank FD vs a debt mutual fund (post-Apr-2023) projected at 7.5%.\n\n**Q51.** What corpus does Route Y's investment build over 25 years (before tax)?\nA) ₹57,20,650  B) ₹28,00,000  C) ₹68,64,780  D) ₹12,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Invested = ₹60,000 − ₹10,000 term premium = ₹50,000/year. Corpus = ₹50,000 × 114.413 = ₹57,20,650. A) is correct. B) is the endowment maturity. C) wrongly uses the full ₹60,000. D) is arbitrary. Route Y also keeps the ₹1 crore protection — and builds roughly double the endowment's ₹28 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Comparing the two routes on protection plus accumulation, which is better and why?\nA) Endowment — it's tax-free  B) Endowment — index funds are too risky  C) They are equal  D) Term + invest — same ₹1 crore cover but a much larger corpus (~₹57 lakh vs ₹28 lakh)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Both give ₹1 crore cover, but term + invest builds ~₹57.2 lakh vs the endowment's ₹28 lakh. Even after equity LTCG tax, Route Y wins comfortably. A) over-weights the tax-free label while ignoring the far smaller corpus. C) is wrong. B) ignores the long 25-year horizon that suits equity. This is exactly the \"buy term and invest the rest\" principle.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** For the ₹5,00,000 / 4-year decision, what is the post-tax return of the 7% bank FD for a 30%-slab investor?\nA) 7.00%  B) 4.90%  C) 5.25%  D) 2.10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FD interest is taxed at slab every year: post-tax = 7% × (1 − 0.30) = 4.90%. A) ignores tax. C) 5.25% uses a 25% rate. D) 2.10% is the tax portion. For high earners the FD's headline rate shrinks a lot after tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** The debt mutual fund (bought after 1 April 2023) returns 7.5% and is held 4 years. After-tax, does it beat the FD for this 30%-slab investor?\nA) Yes — debt MF gets 12.5% LTCG with indexation  B) No — debt MF is taxed at 20%  C) No — debt MF is tax-free  D) Marginally — both taxed at slab; 7.5% × 0.70 = 5.25% vs FD 4.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Post-Apr-2023 debt funds are taxed at slab rate (no indexation/LTCG benefit), just like FD interest. Debt MF post-tax = 7.5% × (1 − 0.30) = 5.25%; FD = 4.90%. So the debt MF wins only by its higher gross yield (0.35% edge), not by any tax advantage. A) the old indexation benefit is gone. C) it isn't tax-free. B) the rate is slab (30%), not a flat 20%.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Ms. Anita is tempted to buy the endowment \"to save tax under 80C.\" What should the adviser highlight?\nA) Endowment is the only 80C option  B) Term premium also qualifies for 80C, and term + PPF/equity gives far better returns than endowment  C) Avoid all insurance  D) Tax saving justifies poor returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both endowment and term premiums qualify under 80C, so the tax break is not unique to endowment. The adviser should show that term + an investment (PPF/equity) delivers the same cover plus far higher returns. A) is false. C) is reckless. D) reinforces the \"insurance for tax\" bias the chapters warn against. Don't let the tax tail wag the investment dog.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Capital Gains Planning for Mr. Khanna\n\nMr. Khanna sold investments in FY 2025-26 (all STT-paid where applicable):\n\n| Asset | Holding period | Purchase | Sale |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Listed equity shares (Lot A) | 20 months | ₹4,00,000 | ₹7,50,000 |\n| Listed equity MF (Lot B) | 8 months | ₹3,00,000 | ₹3,80,000 |\n| Gold ETF | 30 months | ₹2,00,000 | ₹2,90,000 |\n\nHis gold ETF gain is taxed at 12.5% (long-term, held > 24 months). Assume no other capital gains; ignore surcharge and cess.\n\n**Q56.** What is the long-term capital gain on Lot A (listed equity, 20 months)?\nA) ₹4,00,000  B) ₹2,25,000  C) ₹1,15,000  D) ₹3,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Listed equity held > 12 months is long-term. Gain = sale − cost = ₹7,50,000 − ₹4,00,000 = ₹3,50,000. B) and C) deduct things prematurely. A) is the cost. (The ₹1.25 lakh exemption is applied later, across all 112A gains, not per lot.)\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** What is the nature and tax of Lot B (equity MF held 8 months)?\nA) LTCG, ₹0  B) STCG at slab rate  C) LTCG at 12.5% = ₹10,000  D) STCG under 111A at 20% = ₹16,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equity MF held ≤ 12 months = short-term; STCG under Section 111A is taxed at 20%. Gain = ₹3,80,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹80,000; tax = 20% × ₹80,000 = ₹16,000. A) wrongly treats it as long-term. C) applies the LTCG rate. B) STCG on STT-paid equity is a flat 20%, not slab. The ₹1.25 lakh exemption does NOT apply to STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Combining all his Section 112A long-term gains (Lot A equity ₹3,50,000; gold ETF is taxed separately at 12.5% but is not 112A), what is the LTCG tax on the equity (Lot A) after the annual exemption?\nA) ₹28,125  B) ₹43,750  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section 112A applies to listed equity/equity MF LTCG. Lot A LTCG = ₹3,50,000. Apply the ₹1,25,000 exemption: taxable = ₹3,50,000 − ₹1,25,000 = ₹2,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × ₹2,25,000 = ₹28,125. B) ₹43,750 forgets the exemption. C) ₹25,000 uses a wrong base. D) ignores that the gain exceeds the exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** What is the LTCG tax on the Gold ETF (held 30 months, taxed at 12.5%, no ₹1.25 lakh exemption applies to gold)?\nA) ₹11,250  B) ₹0  C) ₹18,000  D) ₹6,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gold ETF gain = ₹2,90,000 − ₹2,00,000 = ₹90,000. It is long-term (> 24 months) and taxed at 12.5% but does NOT get the ₹1.25 lakh Section 112A exemption (that is only for listed equity/equity MF). Tax = 12.5% × ₹90,000 = ₹11,250. B) wrongly assumes exemption. C) applies 20%. D) halves it. Different assets, different rules.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** What is Mr. Khanna's total capital-gains tax across all three lots?\nA) ₹55,375  B) ₹44,125  C) ₹28,125  D) ₹16,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Add the three: Lot A LTCG tax ₹28,125 + Lot B STCG tax ₹16,000 + Gold ETF LTCG tax ₹11,250 = ₹55,375. B) ₹44,125 drops the STCG. C) is Lot A only. D) is Lot B only. Each asset's gain is computed by its own holding-period and rate rules, then summed.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Retirement Planning for Mrs. Iyer\n\nMrs. Iyer, 55, plans to retire at 60. Her current monthly expense is ₹80,000, expected to continue (in real terms) for 25 years post-retirement. Inflation is 6%, pre-retirement portfolio return 9%, post-retirement return 7%. She has an existing corpus of ₹60,00,000 today and contributes to NPS.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current age / Retirement age | 55 / 60 |\n| Current monthly expense | ₹80,000 |\n| Inflation | 6% |\n| Years to retirement | 5 |\n| Existing corpus | ₹60,00,000 |\n\n**Q61.** What will her monthly expense be at retirement (in 5 years), inflation-adjusted?\nA) ₹1,07,058  B) ₹1,04,000  C) ₹96,000  D) ₹1,12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Future expense = current × (1 + inflation)^n = ₹80,000 × (1.06)^5. (1.06)^5 = 1.33823, so ₹80,000 × 1.33823 = ₹1,07,058. B) ₹1,04,000 uses simple inflation (6%×5 = 30%? no, this under-compounds). C) ignores inflation growth. D) over-rounds. Expenses must be grown at compound inflation to the retirement date.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Her existing ₹60,00,000 corpus grows at 9% for 5 years until retirement. What will it be worth at 60?\nA) ₹87,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹78,00,000  D) ₹92,31,744\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV = PV × (1 + r)^n = ₹60,00,000 × (1.09)^5. (1.09)^5 = 1.538624, so ₹60,00,000 × 1.538624 = ₹92,31,744. A) ₹87,00,000 uses simple interest (9%×5 = 45%). C) under-grows. B) ignores growth entirely. Compounding at 9% for 5 years grows the corpus ~54%.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Her NPS corpus at 60 is ₹40,00,000. Per NPS rules, what is the maximum tax-free lump sum she can take, and what must go to annuity?\nA) ₹24 lakh lump sum, ₹16 lakh annuity  B) ₹16 lakh lump sum, ₹24 lakh annuity  C) ₹20 lakh each  D) ₹40 lakh lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — At 60, up to 60% can be withdrawn tax-free, minimum 40% to annuity. 60% × ₹40 lakh = ₹24 lakh tax-free; 40% × ₹40 lakh = ₹16 lakh annuity. B) reverses it. C) is a 50/50 guess. D) ignores the mandatory annuity. The annuity income she later receives is fully taxable at slab.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** If the ₹16 lakh annuity buys a pension at 6% p.a., what is her annual (taxable) annuity income?\nA) ₹96,000  B) ₹1,60,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹64,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annuity income = annuity corpus × annuity rate = ₹16,00,000 × 6% = ₹96,000 per year. A) is correct. B) uses 10%. C) uses 5%. D) is arbitrary. This pension is fully taxable as per her slab — a key point when projecting net retirement income.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Mrs. Iyer is emotionally set on funding her daughter's foreign education even if it shrinks her retirement corpus. What is the adviser's correct role?\nA) Fund education fully; retirement can wait  B) Refuse to advise  C) Tell her to ignore the daughter's education  D) Objectively show the compromise to retirement and suggest alternatives like a part education loan repayable by the daughter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — When resources can't meet all goals, the adviser objectively shows the extent of the compromise to a vital goal (retirement) and offers alternatives — e.g., a partial education loan the daughter repays from her future earnings. A) sacrifices her own security. B) abdicates the duty. C) is callous. The adviser helps emotional clients make balanced, rational choices.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Case: The Rao Household Financial Plan\n\nMr. and Mrs. Rao (both 40) have combined net income of ₹36,00,000/year, expenses ₹18,00,000/year. They have a ₹1,20,00,000 portfolio: 65% equity, 25% debt, 10% gold. Mr. Rao is the higher earner (₹24,00,000) and is in the 30% slab. Goals: retirement in 20 years and a child's college in 10 years (cost ₹40,00,000 today). They hold a ₹50 lakh term plan and no health insurance. Inflation 6%.\n\n**Q66.** What is the household's annual savings rate?\nA) 50%  B) 30%  C) 18%  D) 67%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Surplus = ₹36,00,000 − ₹18,00,000 = ₹18,00,000. Savings rate = ₹18,00,000 ÷ ₹36,00,000 = 50%. A) is correct. B) and C) under-state. D) is the expense ratio. A 50% savings rate is strong and gives them flexibility to fund both goals.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Inflated cost of the college goal in 10 years at 6% inflation (current cost ₹40,00,000)?\nA) ₹64,00,000  B) ₹80,00,000  C) ₹52,00,000  D) ₹71,63,391\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = ₹40,00,000 × (1.06)^10 = ₹40,00,000 × 1.790847 = ₹71,63,391. A) uses simple inflation. C) under-compounds. B) over-estimates. The college fund target must be the *inflated* figure, not today's ₹40 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** The Raos have a ₹50 lakh term cover but Mr. Rao's HLV (income to replace ₹24,00,000/year for 20 years, PV factor at 7% ≈ 10.594) is much higher. Are they adequately insured?\nA) Yes, ₹50 lakh is plenty  B) No — HLV ≈ ₹2,54,25,600, so they are massively under-insured  C) Yes, term plans never need topping up  D) No — they need ₹24 lakh more only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — HLV = ₹24,00,000 × 10.594 = ₹2,54,25,600. Their ₹50 lakh cover is far below this, so Mr. Rao is severely under-insured; a top-up term plan of ~₹2 crore is warranted. A) and C) ignore the income-replacement need. D) understates the gap. Adequate pure protection is the first step of any sound plan.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** The Raos hold NO health insurance. A medical event costs ₹8,00,000 with no cover. What is the impact, and the adviser's priority?\nA) Minor; pay from income  B) Ignore — they are young  C) Use the term plan  D) The full ₹8,00,000 hits their savings/portfolio; buy adequate health cover first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — With no health cover, the entire ₹8,00,000 must come from savings or by liquidating investments (possibly at a loss), derailing goals. The priority is to buy adequate health insurance — appropriate and adequate insurance is the foundation of a financial plan. A) underestimates the hit. C) term covers death, not hospitalisation. B) ignores that any age can face medical emergencies.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Mr. Rao wants to invest a fresh ₹5,00,000 for the 10-year college goal. Given the 10-year horizon and the goal's importance, what allocation is most suitable?\nA) 100% in a single mid-cap stock  B) 100% gold  C) 100% bank FD  D) A growth-tilted diversified mix (e.g., equity-heavy now, glide to debt as the goal nears)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A 10-year horizon supports a growth-tilted, diversified allocation (equity-heavy early for growth), then a glide path into debt/liquid as the goal approaches to protect the corpus. A) is undiversified and reckless. C) 100% FD won't beat inflation after tax. B) gold alone is volatile and income-less. Match asset allocation to horizon and goal importance, then de-risk near the deadline.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "09", "md": "# Mock Paper 9 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This paper is heavily numerical and case-driven, focused on time-value-of-money and goal planning. Keep a calculator or a spreadsheet (Excel FV/PV/PMT functions) handy, and read each scenario carefully — most marks come from setting up the right formula and plugging in the right rate and number of periods.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** A lump sum of Rs. 1,00,000 is invested for 10 years and earns 8% per annum, compounded annually. What is the maturity value?\nA) Rs. 1,80,000  B) Rs. 2,15,892  C) Rs. 2,00,000  D) Rs. 1,93,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Use the future-value of a lump sum: **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ**, where PV = present amount (1,00,000), r = rate per period (0.08), n = number of periods (10). FV = 1,00,000 × (1.08)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × 2.158925 = **Rs. 2,15,892**. Option A (1,80,000) is simple interest (1,00,000 × 8% × 10 = 80,000 interest) which ignores compounding — interest earning interest. C just doubles the money (wrong rule). D is a rounded guess. The key idea: compounding makes money grow on the growth, so the answer beats the simple-interest figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An item costs Rs. 50,000 today. If inflation is 6% per annum, what will the same item cost in 20 years?\nA) Rs. 1,10,000  B) Rs. 1,06,000  C) Rs. 1,40,000  D) Rs. 1,60,357\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Inflation-adjusted future cost uses the same compounding formula: **Future Cost = Present Cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ**. = 50,000 × (1.06)²⁰ = 50,000 × 3.207135 = **Rs. 1,60,357**. Option A roughly applies simple inflation (50,000 + 6% × 20 = 1,10,000) and is wrong because prices compound. Option B is only one year's inflation; C is a guess. Lesson: inflation quietly compounds, so future costs are much higher than a \"rate × years\" guess.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A mutual fund investment grew from Rs. 2,50,000 to Rs. 6,00,000 over 5 years. What is the CAGR (compound annual growth rate)?\nA) 28.0%  B) 19.14%  C) 14.0%  D) 24.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR smooths growth into one annual rate: **CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1**. = (6,00,000 ÷ 2,50,000)^(1/5) − 1 = (2.4)^(0.2) − 1 = 1.19136 − 1 = 0.19136 = **19.14%**. Option C (14%) is the simple average ((2.4 − 1) ÷ 5 = 28% total ÷... ) miscalculation; A (28%) is the total growth divided incorrectly per year ignoring compounding; D is a guess. CAGR answers \"what single steady rate would turn 2.5 lakh into 6 lakh in 5 years?\"\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A portfolio earns a nominal return of 12% in a year when inflation is 6%. What is the real (inflation-adjusted) return?\nA) 6.00%  B) 6.36%  C) 5.66%  D) 18.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The real return uses the Fisher relation: **Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1**. = (1.12 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.056604 − 1 = 0.056604 = **5.66%**. Option A (6%) is the rough shortcut \"nominal − inflation = 12% − 6%\", which slightly overstates the real return; D adds them (nonsense); B is a guess. Real return tells you how much your purchasing power actually grew after prices rose.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Reema invests Rs. 5,000 at the end of every month for 20 years in a fund earning 12% per annum (1% per month). What is the maturity corpus (future value of an ordinary annuity)?\nA) Rs. 49,46,277  B) Rs. 12,00,000  C) Rs. 24,00,000  D) Rs. 41,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV of an ordinary annuity (payments at period-end): **FV = PMT × [((1 + r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**. PMT = 5,000, r = 0.01 per month, n = 240 months. FV = 5,000 × [(1.01)²⁴⁰ − 1] ÷ 0.01 = 5,000 × [10.892554 − 1] ÷ 0.01 = 5,000 × 989.2554 = **Rs. 49,46,277**. Option B is just the money put in (5,000 × 240 = 12,00,000) — no growth. C double-counts; D is a guess. The huge gap between the deposits and the corpus is the power of compounding over 20 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** What single amount must you invest today to have Rs. 10,00,000 in 5 years, if the money earns 8% per annum?\nA) Rs. 6,80,583  B) Rs. 7,50,000  C) Rs. 6,00,000  D) Rs. 9,25,926\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This is the present value of a future goal: **PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ**. = 10,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁵ = 10,00,000 ÷ 1.469328 = **Rs. 6,80,583**. Option D discounts for only 1 year (10,00,000 ÷ 1.08); B and C are guesses. PV tells you the lump sum needed now so it grows exactly to the target.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A goal requires Rs. 5,00,000 in 10 years. How much must be saved at the END of each year, earning 9% per annum, to reach it (sinking fund)?\nA) Rs. 50,000  B) Rs. 28,000  C) Rs. 40,000  D) Rs. 32,910\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sinking-fund payment (rearranged FV-annuity formula): **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]**. = 5,00,000 × 0.09 ÷ [(1.09)¹⁰ − 1] = 45,000 ÷ [2.367364 − 1] = 45,000 ÷ 1.367364 = **Rs. 32,910**. Option A simply divides 5,00,000 ÷ 10 = 50,000, ignoring the interest the savings earn; B and C are guesses. Because your deposits earn interest, you need to set aside less than the straight-line amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Mr. Khan needs a monthly income of Rs. 50,000 (today's value) at the start of his 25-year retirement. Ignoring inflation, if his corpus earns 0.157% per month (a real rate), what corpus is needed at retirement? (PV of an annuity, payments at period-end, 300 months.)\nA) Rs. 1.50 crore  B) Rs. 1.20 crore  C) Rs. 1.195 crore  D) Rs. 90 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Retirement corpus = present value of the income stream: **PV = PMT × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] ÷ r**. PMT = 50,000, r = 0.00157, n = 300. PV = 50,000 × [1 − (1.00157)⁻³⁰⁰] ÷ 0.00157 ≈ 50,000 × 239.03 = **Rs. 1.195 crore** (≈ Rs. 1,19,51,298). Option A (1.5 cr) is 50,000 × 300 = 1.5 crore — the wrong \"no return\" total; B and D are guesses. Because the corpus keeps earning a real return while being drawn down, you need less than the simple multiply-out total.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Anil's last-drawn salary (basic + DA) is Rs. 50,000 per month and he has 20 completed years of service with a firm covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972. What is his gratuity?\nA) Rs. 5,76,923  B) Rs. 6,00,000  C) Rs. 5,00,000  D) Rs. 7,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For employees covered under the Act: **Gratuity = Last drawn salary × 15/26 × completed years of service**. = 50,000 × (15 ÷ 26) × 20 = 50,000 × 0.576923 × 20 = **Rs. 5,76,923**. Option B uses 15/30 (the formula for employees NOT covered under the Act: 50,000 × 15/30 × 20 = 6,00,000); C and D are guesses. The \"26\" represents working days in a month for covered employees; the \"15\" is 15 days' salary per year of service.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** On retiring at 60 with an NPS corpus of Rs. 1 crore, what is the MAXIMUM that can be withdrawn as a tax-free lump sum, and how much must compulsorily buy an annuity?\nA) Lump sum Rs. 40 lakh; annuity Rs. 60 lakh  B) Lump sum Rs. 60 lakh; annuity Rs. 40 lakh  C) Lump sum Rs. 100 lakh; annuity nil  D) Lump sum Rs. 50 lakh; annuity Rs. 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At exit on/after 60, NPS rules allow up to **60% as a lump sum** (tax-free under Sec 10(12A)) and require a **minimum of 40%** to buy an annuity (whose pension is later taxable). 60% of 1 crore = Rs. 60 lakh; 40% = Rs. 40 lakh. Option A reverses the split; C ignores the mandatory annuitisation; D is a 50:50 guess. (Only if the corpus is ≤ Rs. 5 lakh can the whole amount be withdrawn.)\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** An employee with basic + DA of Rs. 30,000 per month contributes 12% to EPF. What is the employee's own monthly EPF contribution?\nA) Rs. 3,600  B) Rs. 2,500  C) Rs. 1,101  D) Rs. 3,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The employee contributes **12% of basic + DA**, and the entire amount goes to the EPF account. = 12% × 30,000 = **Rs. 3,600**. Option C (3.67% = 1,101) is the EPF portion of the EMPLOYER's split, not the employee's; B and D are guesses. Note the employee's full 12% goes to EPF, while the employer's 12% is split (3.67% to EPF, 8.33% to EPS, plus admin/EDLIS charges).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Of the employer's 12% EPF contribution, what percentage goes to the Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS), and on what salary is it capped?\nA) 12%, capped on Rs. 21,000  B) 3.67%, capped on Rs. 15,000  C) 8.33%, no cap  D) 8.33%, capped on Rs. 15,000 monthly salary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The employer's 12% is split: **3.67% to EPF and 8.33% to EPS**, with the **EPS portion subject to a wage ceiling of Rs. 15,000 per month**. Option B swaps the figures; C forgets the cap; A invents wrong numbers. So the maximum monthly EPS contribution is 8.33% × 15,000 = Rs. 1,250.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Priya buys an equity mutual fund unit set for Rs. 8,00,000, holds it for 3 years, and sells for Rs. 14,00,000 (STT paid both ways). What is her LTCG tax under Section 112A?\nA) Rs. 75,000  B) Rs. 59,375  C) Rs. 90,000  D) Rs. 71,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity MF held > 12 months → long-term. **LTCG = sale − cost = 14,00,000 − 8,00,000 = Rs. 6,00,000.** Under Sec 112A, the first Rs. 1,25,000 of LTCG is exempt, and the excess is taxed at **12.5%**. Taxable = 6,00,000 − 1,25,000 = 4,75,000; tax = 12.5% × 4,75,000 = **Rs. 59,375**. Option A (75,000) is 12.5% on the full 6,00,000 (forgetting the exemption); C and D are guesses. Always subtract the Rs. 1.25 lakh exemption first.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Rakesh sells listed equity shares within 8 months of buying, making a gain of Rs. 2,00,000 (STT paid). What is his short-term capital gains tax under Section 111A?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 25,000  C) Rs. 40,000  D) Rs. 24,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Listed equity held ≤ 12 months → short-term. STCG under **Section 111A is taxed at 20%** (STT paid). = 20% × 2,00,000 = **Rs. 40,000**. Option A applies 15% (the old rate, now superseded); there is no Rs. 1.25 lakh exemption for STCG (that applies only to LTCG under 112A). B and D are guesses. STCG carries a higher rate than LTCG to discourage quick churning.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A SIP of Rs. 10,000 per month for 10 years earns 1% per month. What is the maturity value (ordinary annuity)?\nA) Rs. 23,00,387  B) Rs. 12,00,000  C) Rs. 18,00,000  D) Rs. 20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV ordinary annuity: **FV = PMT × [((1 + r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**. PMT = 10,000, r = 0.01, n = 120. FV = 10,000 × [(1.01)¹²⁰ − 1] ÷ 0.01 = 10,000 × [3.300387 − 1] ÷ 0.01 = 10,000 × 230.0387 = **Rs. 23,00,387**. Option B is just the deposits (10,000 × 120). C and D are guesses. Roughly Rs. 11 lakh of the corpus is investment growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Investing Rs. 1,00,000 per year for 15 years at 10%, what is the difference between an annuity-DUE (start of year) and an ordinary annuity (end of year)?\nA) Rs. 3,17,725  B) Rs. 3,17,725 vs 3,49,497 — difference Rs. 31,772  C) Nil  D) Rs. 1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annuity due = ordinary annuity × (1 + r), because each deposit gets one extra period of growth. Ordinary FV = 1,00,000 × [(1.10)¹⁵ − 1] ÷ 0.10 = 1,00,000 × 31.77248 = Rs. 31,77,248. Due = 31,77,248 × 1.10 = Rs. 34,94,973. Difference = **Rs. 31,772** (which equals the ordinary FV × 0.10). Option C is wrong because timing matters; A gives only the ordinary figure; D is a guess. Paying at the start of each period always grows the corpus more.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mr. E withdraws Rs. 75,000 from his EPF when he still has 30 years to retirement. If EPF earns 8.5% per annum, roughly how much future corpus does he forgo?\nA) Rs. 2.25 lakh  B) Rs. 5.00 lakh  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 8.66 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The forgone corpus is the future value of the withdrawn amount: **FV = 75,000 × (1.085)³⁰** = 75,000 × 11.5582 = **Rs. 8.66 lakh**. Option A treats it as simple interest; C ignores compounding entirely; B is a guess. A small early withdrawal costs far more in retirement because 30 years of compounding is lost — this is why pre-retirement withdrawals are so damaging.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A child-education goal costs Rs. 20,00,000 today and is 12 years away; education inflation is 8%. What will it cost at the time of the goal?\nA) Rs. 39,20,000  B) Rs. 21,60,000  C) Rs. 50,36,340  D) Rs. 44,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Future cost = present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ** = 20,00,000 × (1.08)¹² = 20,00,000 × 2.518170 = **Rs. 50,36,340**. Option A applies simple inflation (20,00,000 × (1 + 0.08 × 12) = 39.2 lakh) and is wrong because costs compound; B is one year's inflation; D is a guess. Education costs typically rise faster than general inflation, so planners use a higher rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A goal needs a corpus of Rs. 50,36,340 in 12 years (144 months). What monthly SIP at 11% per annum (about 0.9167% per month) reaches it?\nA) Rs. 16,967  B) Rs. 25,000  C) Rs. 35,000  D) Rs. 12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sinking-fund payment: **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]**. r = 0.11/12 = 0.0091667, n = 144. PMT = 50,36,340 × 0.0091667 ÷ [(1.0091667)¹⁴⁴ − 1] = 46,167 ÷ [3.72095 − 1] = 46,167 ÷ 2.72095 = **Rs. 16,967**. Option B and C are too high (treating it almost like no-growth saving); D too low. Compounding does most of the heavy lifting, so the required SIP is modest.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Comparing simple vs compound interest: Rs. 3,00,000 invested for 10 years at 8%. By how much does compound interest exceed simple interest?\nA) Rs. 1,07,678  B) Rs. 2,40,000  C) Nil  D) Rs. 3,47,678\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Compound FV = 3,00,000 × (1.08)¹⁰ = 3,00,000 × 2.158925 = Rs. 6,47,678. Simple-interest value = 3,00,000 × (1 + 0.08 × 10) = 3,00,000 × 1.8 = Rs. 5,40,000. Difference = 6,47,678 − 5,40,000 = **Rs. 1,07,678**. Option B is the simple interest itself (2,40,000); D is the total compound interest; C is wrong. The gap is the \"interest on interest\" that simple interest misses.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A retirement plan targets Rs. 2 crore in 20 years at 12% per annum. What monthly SIP is required (sinking fund, 240 months at 1% per month)?\nA) Rs. 83,333  B) Rs. 10,000  C) Rs. 20,217  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]** = 2,00,00,000 × 0.01 ÷ [(1.01)²⁴⁰ − 1] = 2,00,000 ÷ [10.892554 − 1] = 2,00,000 ÷ 9.892554 = **Rs. 20,217**. Option A is the no-growth saving (2 cr ÷ 240); B and D are guesses. Over 20 years compounding turns about Rs. 48.5 lakh of contributions into Rs. 2 crore.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Continuing Q21, if the same Rs. 2 crore goal is started only 5 years later (15 years left, 180 months at 12%), what monthly SIP is now needed?\nA) Rs. 20,217  B) Rs. 26,000  C) Rs. 1,11,111  D) Rs. 40,034\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **PMT = 2,00,00,000 × 0.01 ÷ [(1.01)¹⁸⁰ − 1]** = 2,00,000 ÷ [5.995802 − 1] = 2,00,000 ÷ 4.995802 = **Rs. 40,034**. Option A is the 20-year figure (Q21); B and C are guesses. Delaying just 5 years nearly DOUBLES the required monthly saving (Rs. 20,217 → Rs. 40,034) — the cost of procrastination is dramatic because you lose the most powerful early compounding years.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** What is the effective real rate when nominal return is 10% and inflation is 5%?\nA) 4.76%  B) 5.00%  C) 5.25%  D) 15.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Real = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) − 1** = 1.10 ÷ 1.05 − 1 = 1.047619 − 1 = **4.76%**. Option B is the rough subtraction (10% − 5%), which overstates the real return slightly; D adds; C is a guess. The proper formula divides because inflation erodes both the principal and the returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** If inflation equals the investment return (both 7%), what is the real rate of return on the corpus?\nA) 7%  B) 0%  C) 14%  D) 1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real = (1.07 ÷ 1.07) − 1 = 1 − 1 = **0%**. When returns just match inflation, purchasing power stays flat — money grows in rupees but not in real value. This is the same principle behind the workbook's HLV note: when income growth equals the discount return, the discount rate is 0. Options A, C, D ignore that the two rates cancel.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A person wants Rs. 60,000 per month (today's value) at retirement. With 6% inflation, what monthly amount will be needed in 10 years to maintain the same lifestyle?\nA) Rs. 96,000  B) Rs. 1,07,451  C) Rs. 1,20,000  D) Rs. 63,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Future expense = 60,000 × (1.06)¹⁰** = 60,000 × 1.790847 = **Rs. 1,07,451**. Option A applies simple inflation (60,000 × 1.6); D is one year's inflation; C is a guess. This is the exact workbook illustration — 6% inflation nearly doubles the monthly requirement in 10 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Continuing Q25, after 20 years at 6% inflation, what monthly amount maintains the same Rs. 60,000 lifestyle?\nA) Rs. 1,32,000  B) Rs. 1,07,451  C) Rs. 2,40,000  D) Rs. 1,92,428\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **= 60,000 × (1.06)²⁰** = 60,000 × 3.207135 = **Rs. 1,92,428**. Option B is the 10-year figure; A is simple inflation; C is a guess. Over 20 years the requirement more than triples — illustrating why retirement corpora must account for inflation across the whole horizon, not just up to the retirement date.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Reema invests Rs. 5,000 per month for 5 years at 1% per month, but at the BEGINNING of each month (annuity due). What is the maturity value?\nA) Rs. 4,08,348  B) Rs. 3,00,000  C) Rs. 4,12,432  D) Rs. 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Annuity due = ordinary annuity FV × (1 + r). Ordinary FV = 5,000 × [(1.01)⁶⁰ − 1] ÷ 0.01 = 5,000 × 81.66967 = Rs. 4,08,348. Due = 4,08,348 × 1.01 = **Rs. 4,12,432**. Option A is the ordinary (end-of-month) figure; B is contributions only; D is a guess. Investing at the start of each period earns one extra period of return on every deposit.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A person needs Rs. 6,00,000 per year for 20 years in retirement, drawn at year-end, with the corpus earning a real rate of 7%. What corpus is needed at retirement (PV of ordinary annuity)?\nA) Rs. 63,56,409  B) Rs. 1.20 crore  C) Rs. 84,00,000  D) Rs. 50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PV = PMT × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] ÷ r** = 6,00,000 × [1 − (1.07)⁻²⁰] ÷ 0.07 = 6,00,000 × [1 − 0.258419] ÷ 0.07 = 6,00,000 × 10.59401 = **Rs. 63,56,409**. Option B is the no-return total (6,00,000 × 20 = Rs. 1.2 crore); C and D are guesses. Because the corpus keeps earning 7% while being drawn, far less than the Rs. 1.2 crore straight total is required.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Mr. A wants to accumulate Rs. 2 crore in 20 years at 12%. Instead of a flat Rs. 20,217/month SIP, he uses a 7% annual step-up. Which statement is correct?\nA) He must start higher than Rs. 20,217  B) Step-up makes no difference  C) He must invest a lump sum  D) He can start LOWER than Rs. 20,217 and increase yearly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In a step-up SIP the investor **starts with a smaller amount and increases it by a fixed percentage each year** (here 7%), in line with rising income. Because later contributions are larger, the starting amount can be below the flat Rs. 20,217 yet still reach Rs. 2 crore. Option A is the opposite; B ignores the benefit; C is unrelated. Step-up suits people whose income grows over time and who can't afford a large fixed SIP early on.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Two people target the same Rs. 1.18 crore corpus at 12%: one starts at age 30 (30 years), one at age 40 (20 years). The monthly savings are Rs. 3,376 and Rs. 11,928 respectively. What does this show?\nA) Starting age has little effect  B) Starting 10 years earlier cuts the required SIP by more than two-thirds  C) The later starter saves more in total  D) Both pay the same total\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Per the workbook, the age-30 starter needs only Rs. 3,376/month while the age-40 starter needs Rs. 11,928/month for the same Rs. 1.18 crore — a reduction of about 72% just by starting 10 years earlier. Option A contradicts the data; C and D are false (the early starter actually contributes far less in total despite more months, thanks to compounding). The earlier you start, the cheaper the goal.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A company NOT covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act pays gratuity on Anil's last-drawn salary of Rs. 60,000 for 20 completed years. What is the gratuity?\nA) Rs. 6,00,000  B) Rs. 6,92,308  C) Rs. 9,00,000  D) Rs. 20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For employees **not covered** under the Act: **Gratuity = last drawn salary × 15/30 × completed years** (half a month's salary per year). = 60,000 × (15 ÷ 30) × 20 = 60,000 × 0.5 × 20 = **Rs. 6,00,000**. Option B wrongly uses 15/26 (the covered formula); C and D are guesses. The divisor is 30 (full month) for uncovered employees versus 26 for covered ones, so uncovered gratuity is slightly lower.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Under Section 80C, what is the maximum deduction available in a financial year (for instruments like EPF, PPF, life insurance premium, ELSS)?\nA) Rs. 2,00,000  B) Rs. 1,00,000  C) Rs. 1,50,000  D) Rs. 50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The aggregate **Section 80C limit is Rs. 1,50,000** per financial year. Option A (2 lakh) confuses the combined 80C + 80CCD(1B) NPS limit; D (50,000) is the additional NPS deduction under 80CCD(1B) alone; B is the old limit. So an investor's EPF + PPF + insurance + ELSS together qualify only up to Rs. 1.5 lakh under 80C.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Over and above the Rs. 1.5 lakh Section 80C limit, what extra NPS deduction is available under Section 80CCD(1B)?\nA) Rs. 50,000  B) Rs. 1,50,000  C) Rs. 25,000  D) Rs. 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Section **80CCD(1B) gives an exclusive additional deduction of Rs. 50,000** for the subscriber's own NPS Tier-I contribution, on top of the 80C limit, taking the total possible self-contribution deduction to Rs. 2 lakh. Option B confuses it with 80C; D is the combined total; C is a guess.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A holds a residential property bought in March 2022 and sells it in June 2025. Is the gain long-term, and what is the holding-period threshold for property?\nA) Short-term; 12 months  B) Long-term; 24 months  C) Long-term; 36 months  D) Short-term; 24 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Immovable property (land/building) is long-term if held for **more than 24 months**. Here the holding is about 3 years 3 months > 24 months, so the gain is **long-term**. Option A uses the 12-month rule (that applies to listed equity/securities, not property); C uses the old 36-month rule, now superseded; D has the wrong classification. Different assets have different thresholds: 12 months for listed equity, 24 months for property and unlisted shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A SIP corpus must reach Rs. 10,00,000 in 5 years (60 months) at 12% per annum. What monthly SIP is required?\nA) Rs. 16,667  B) Rs. 10,000  C) Rs. 8,000  D) Rs. 12,244\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]** = 10,00,000 × 0.01 ÷ [(1.01)⁶⁰ − 1] = 10,000 ÷ [1.816697 − 1] = 10,000 ÷ 0.816697 = **Rs. 12,244**. Option A is the no-growth saving (10 lakh ÷ 60); B and C are too low. Even over 5 years, compounding reduces the required monthly deposit below the straight-line figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A lump sum doubles in 8 years. Using the Rule of 72, what is the approximate annual compound return?\nA) 6%  B) 9%  C) 12.5%  D) 16%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Rule of 72** estimates doubling time: years ≈ 72 ÷ rate, so rate ≈ 72 ÷ years = 72 ÷ 8 = **9%**. (Exact CAGR = 2^(1/8) − 1 = 9.05%, confirming the rule.) Option A would double in 12 years; C in ~5.8 years; D in ~4.5 years. The Rule of 72 is a handy mental shortcut for compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Rajeev earns Rs. 1,00,000 per month now and his income grows 10% per annum. If he retires in 10 years and wants 50% of his then-income as pension, what is his first-year monthly pension?\nA) Rs. 1,00,000  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 1,29,687  D) Rs. 2,59,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Income at retirement = 1,00,000 × (1.10)¹⁰ = Rs. 2,59,374/month. Replacement pension = 50% × 2,59,374 = **Rs. 1,29,687/month** (the workbook's replacement-ratio example). Option D is the full pre-retirement income (no 50% applied); B applies 50% to today's income (ignoring 10 years of salary growth); A is today's income. The replacement-ratio method grows current income to the retirement date, then applies the chosen percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Under the Replacement Ratio Method, after the first year of retirement the pension is increased each year by which rate?\nA) The investment return  B) The inflation rate  C) It stays fixed  D) The salary growth rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The first-year replacement income is then **escalated by the inflation rate every year** to preserve purchasing power (e.g., 1,29,687 × 1.07 in year 2 if inflation is 7%). Option A and D use the wrong rate; C ignores that costs rise during retirement. Forgetting this escalation is a classic under-funding mistake.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An NPS subscriber exits BEFORE age 60 (premature exit). What minimum percentage of the corpus must be used to buy an annuity?\nA) 40%  B) 60%  C) 80%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — On premature exit (before 60/superannuation), **at least 80% of the corpus must be annuitised** and only 20% can be taken as a lump sum. Option A is the rule for normal exit at 60 (minimum 40% annuity); B and D are wrong. The stricter rule discourages early exit and protects the retirement income objective. (If the corpus is ≤ Rs. 2.5 lakh, the whole amount can be withdrawn.)\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Mr. Ashish's total retirement expense today is Rs. 45,000 per month. With 6% inflation and 20 years to retirement, what will it be at retirement?\nA) Rs. 99,000  B) Rs. 90,000  C) Rs. 47,700  D) Rs. 1,44,321\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Future expense = 45,000 × (1.06)²⁰** = 45,000 × 3.207135 = **Rs. 1,44,321** (the workbook's Expense Protection example). Option A applies simple inflation; C is one year's; B is a guess. The Expense Protection Method projects today's budget forward by inflation to size the retirement income.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — Retirement Corpus Planning for Mr. Verma\n\nMr. Verma is 35 years old and plans to retire at 60. His current monthly household expense is Rs. 50,000, and he expects to maintain this lifestyle in retirement. He expects life until age 85 (25 years post-retirement). Use these assumptions:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current age / Retirement age | 35 / 60 |\n| Years to retirement | 25 |\n| Current monthly expense | Rs. 50,000 |\n| Pre-retirement inflation | 7% p.a. |\n| Post-retirement years (85 − 60) | 25 (300 months) |\n| Post-retirement inflation | 6% p.a. |\n| Return on corpus during retirement | 8% p.a. |\n| Accumulation-phase return | 12% p.a. |\n\n**Q41.** What will Mr. Verma's monthly expense be at the start of retirement (age 60)?\nA) Rs. 2,12,500  B) Rs. 2,71,372  C) Rs. 3,00,000  D) Rs. 1,87,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate today's expense to retirement: **50,000 × (1.07)²⁵** = 50,000 × 5.427433 = **Rs. 2,71,372 per month**. Option A applies simple inflation (50,000 × (1 + 0.07 × 25)); D is a guess; C is rounded wrongly. Over 25 years, 7% inflation more than quintuples the monthly requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** During retirement, the corpus earns 8% while inflation is 6%. What real (inflation-adjusted) annual rate is used to size the corpus?\nA) 1.89%  B) 2.00%  C) 14.00%  D) 8.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Real rate = (1 + 0.08)/(1 + 0.06) − 1** = 1.08 ÷ 1.06 − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = **1.89%** (the workbook's standard figure). Option B is the rough subtraction (8% − 6%); D ignores inflation; C adds. Using the real rate lets us value the future inflating income stream as if it were level — a standard planning shortcut.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Using the monthly real rate of about 0.157% (1.89% ÷ 12), the corpus needed to fund Rs. 2,71,372 per month for 300 months (payments at the START of each month) is approximately:\nA) Rs. 4.50 crore  B) Rs. 6.50 crore  C) Rs. 8.14 crore  D) Rs. 3.20 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Corpus = PV of an annuity-due: **PV = PMT × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] ÷ r × (1 + r)**. PMT = 2,71,372, r = 0.0015723, n = 300. Ordinary PV = 2,71,372 × 239.03 ≈ Rs. 6.487 cr; × (1.0015723) ≈ **Rs. 6.50 crore** (≈ Rs. 6,49,66,853). Option A and D understate it; C overstates. This is the lump sum needed at 60 to draw an inflation-protected income for 25 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** To accumulate the Rs. 6.50 crore corpus over 25 years (300 months) at 12% per annum (1% per month), what monthly SIP is needed?\nA) Rs. 21,667  B) Rs. 50,000  C) Rs. 1,00,000  D) Rs. 34,578\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sinking fund: **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]** = 6,49,66,853 × 0.01 ÷ [(1.01)³⁰⁰ − 1] = 6,49,668 ÷ [19.788466 − 1] = 6,49,668 ÷ 18.788466 = **Rs. 34,578**. Option A is the no-growth saving (6.5 cr ÷ 300); B and C are guesses. Thanks to 25 years of 12% compounding, a Rs. 34,578 monthly SIP grows into a Rs. 6.5 crore corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** If Mr. Verma delays starting until age 45 (only 15 years / 180 months left) for the same Rs. 6.50 crore corpus at 12%, the required SIP rises to about Rs. 1,30,000/month. What is the main lesson?\nA) Delay has no impact  B) The corpus target should be reduced  C) Starting late drastically raises the required saving due to lost compounding  D) He should switch to debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The required SIP jumps from about Rs. 34,578 (25 years) to roughly Rs. 1,30,000 (15 years) — nearly four times higher — for the same goal. The reason is the **loss of the earliest, most powerful compounding years**. Option A contradicts the maths; B and D are not the lesson (cutting the target or de-risking only worsens or doesn't fix the shortfall). Start early to keep the saving affordable.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — Funding a Child's Education for Mrs. Iyer\n\nMrs. Iyer's daughter is 6 years old. The targeted graduation course costs Rs. 20,00,000 in today's terms and will be needed in 12 years (when the daughter is 18). Education-cost inflation is 8% per annum. Mrs. Iyer can invest in an equity-oriented fund expected to return 11% per annum.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current cost of course | Rs. 20,00,000 |\n| Years to goal | 12 |\n| Education inflation | 8% p.a. |\n| Expected fund return | 11% p.a. |\n\n**Q46.** What will the course cost in 12 years?\nA) Rs. 39,20,000  B) Rs. 50,36,340  C) Rs. 44,00,000  D) Rs. 22,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Future cost = 20,00,000 × (1.08)¹²** = 20,00,000 × 2.518170 = **Rs. 50,36,340**. Option A is simple inflation (20,00,000 × (1 + 0.08 × 12)); D is one year's; C is a guess. Education inflation compounds, so the true target is over Rs. 50 lakh, not Rs. 39 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** What single lump sum invested TODAY at 11% would grow to the Rs. 50,36,340 target in 12 years?\nA) Rs. 14,39,592  B) Rs. 20,00,000  C) Rs. 25,00,000  D) Rs. 10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ** = 50,36,340 ÷ (1.11)¹² = 50,36,340 ÷ 3.498451 = **Rs. 14,39,592**. Option B is just today's course cost (ignores the 11% growth available); C and D are guesses. Because the lump sum grows at 11% (above the 8% cost inflation), far less than the current cost needs to be set aside today.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** If instead she invests via a monthly SIP for 12 years (144 months) at 11% per annum (about 0.9167% per month), what SIP reaches the Rs. 50,36,340 target?\nA) Rs. 35,000  B) Rs. 29,000  C) Rs. 12,000  D) Rs. 16,967\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]** = 50,36,340 × 0.0091667 ÷ [(1.0091667)¹⁴⁴ − 1] = 46,167 ÷ 2.72095 = **Rs. 16,967**. Option A and B are too high; C too low. A SIP of about Rs. 17,000 spreads the burden and still hits the target because of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** If the fund actually returns 8% (equal to education inflation) instead of 11%, what is the real rate of return relative to education costs?\nA) 8%  B) 0%  C) 3%  D) 16%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real = (1.08 ÷ 1.08) − 1 = **0%**. If the fund only matches education inflation, the investment exactly keeps pace with rising costs but provides no real growth — meaning she'd have to fund essentially the full future cost from contributions. Options A, C, D ignore that the matching rates cancel. This shows why beating cost-inflation matters.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Mrs. Iyer's daughter is only 6, but she keeps postponing the SIP. Which statement best reflects sound goal-planning?\nA) Education goals can be delayed 4–5 years like retirement  B) She should wait for a market crash  C) Education goals have a fixed time horizon and cannot be postponed, so funding must start promptly  D) A lump sum at age 17 is just as good\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Per the workbook, goals such as children's education **have a defined, non-negotiable time horizon** (the child turns 18 at a fixed date) — unlike retirement, which people often (wrongly) defer. So the SIP should begin early to harness compounding and keep instalments affordable. Option A is exactly the mistake the workbook warns against; B is market-timing (not planning); D loses years of compounding.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Inflation Shortfall for the Nairs\n\nMr. Nair (age 50) plans to retire at 60 and believes his Rs. 60,000-per-month current lifestyle will be enough. He has saved a corpus that he expects will yield Rs. 60,000 per month at retirement (he has not adjusted for inflation). Inflation is 6% per annum. He will live to 80.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Current monthly need (today's value) | Rs. 60,000 |\n| Years to retirement (60 − 50) | 10 |\n| Inflation | 6% p.a. |\n| Planned post-retirement years | 20 (to age 80) |\n\n**Q51.** What monthly amount will actually be needed at age 60 to match today's Rs. 60,000 lifestyle?\nA) Rs. 96,000  B) Rs. 1,07,451  C) Rs. 1,20,000  D) Rs. 63,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **60,000 × (1.06)¹⁰** = 60,000 × 1.790847 = **Rs. 1,07,451**. Option A is simple inflation; D is one year's; C is a guess. His \"Rs. 60,000 is enough\" assumption is the classic inflation error — he'll actually need over Rs. 1 lakh a month on day one of retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** What is his monthly SHORTFALL at age 60 if his corpus only delivers Rs. 60,000 per month?\nA) Rs. 47,451  B) Nil  C) Rs. 36,000  D) Rs. 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Shortfall = needed − available = 1,07,451 − 60,000 = **Rs. 47,451 per month**. Option B assumes no inflation (his mistake); C and D are wrong arithmetic. Nearly half his required income is missing on the very first day — a serious under-funding caused by ignoring inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** By age 80 (20 years after retirement), what monthly amount maintains the same Rs. 60,000 (today's) lifestyle, still at 6% inflation from today?\nA) Rs. 1,32,000  B) Rs. 1,07,451  C) Rs. 2,40,000  D) Rs. 1,92,428\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Measured from today's value over 20 years: needed = 60,000 × (1.06)²⁰ = 60,000 × 3.207135 = **Rs. 1,92,428**. Option B is the age-60 figure; A is simple inflation; C is a guess. Crucially, the income required keeps RISING throughout retirement — the corpus must fund an escalating, not flat, income.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which single adjustment best fixes the Nairs' plan, given inflation?\nA) Plan for a fixed Rs. 60,000 income for life  B) Size the corpus on the inflation-adjusted income (Rs. 1,07,451 rising each year), using a real return  C) Withdraw the corpus as a lump sum at 60  D) Stop saving since the corpus already yields Rs. 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The corpus must be sized on the **inflation-adjusted first-year income (Rs. 1,07,451) that itself escalates with inflation**, discounted using the real rate (return adjusted for inflation). Option A repeats the inflation error; C and D ignore longevity and rising costs. Proper retirement sizing always accounts for inflation both up to and throughout retirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** If Mr. Nair's corpus earns 8% during retirement and inflation is 6%, what real rate should he use to value his retirement income stream?\nA) 14%  B) 2%  C) 1.89%  D) 8%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Real rate = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.89%**. Option B is the rough subtraction (8% − 6% = 2%), which slightly overstates it; A adds; D ignores inflation. The real rate captures that part of the 8% return is eaten by 6% inflation, leaving only ~1.89% of true growth to fund rising expenses.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — Capital Gains for an Investor, Mr. Bose\n\nMr. Bose made the following transactions during FY 2025–26 (all listed securities, STT paid on purchase and sale):\n\n| Asset | Bought | Cost | Sold | Sale value | Holding period |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Equity MF units | Jun 2022 | Rs. 8,00,000 | Aug 2025 | Rs. 14,00,000 | ~3 years |\n| Listed shares (parcel B) | Feb 2025 | Rs. 5,00,000 | Oct 2025 | Rs. 7,00,000 | ~8 months |\n\n**Q56.** Classify the two transactions by holding period.\nA) Both long-term  B) MF long-term (>12m); parcel B short-term (≤12m)  C) Both short-term  D) MF short-term; B long-term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For listed equity/equity MF the threshold is **12 months**. The MF was held ~3 years → long-term; parcel B was held ~8 months → short-term. Option A, C, D misclassify. Holding period determines which tax section applies — 112A for LTCG, 111A for STCG.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** What is the LTCG tax on the equity MF (Section 112A), considering the Rs. 1,25,000 exemption?\nA) Rs. 59,375  B) Rs. 75,000  C) Rs. 90,000  D) Rs. 1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG = 14,00,000 − 8,00,000 = Rs. 6,00,000. Exempt first Rs. 1,25,000 → taxable 4,75,000. Tax = **12.5% × 4,75,000 = Rs. 59,375**. Option B taxes the full gain (forgets the exemption); C and D are guesses. The Rs. 1.25 lakh annual exemption applies once across all 112A gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** What is the STCG tax on parcel B (Section 111A)?\nA) Rs. 30,000  B) Rs. 11,250  C) Rs. 15,000  D) Rs. 40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — STCG = 7,00,000 − 5,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000. Under Section 111A (STT paid), the rate is **20%**. Tax = 20% × 2,00,000 = **Rs. 40,000**. Option A uses the old 15% rate; there is no Rs. 1.25 lakh exemption for STCG; B and C are guesses. Short-term gains are taxed more heavily than long-term.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** What is the CAGR earned on the equity MF (Rs. 8,00,000 to Rs. 14,00,000 over 3 years)?\nA) 25.0%  B) 18.0%  C) 20.51%  D) 12.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **CAGR = (14,00,000 ÷ 8,00,000)^(1/3) − 1** = (1.75)^(0.3333) − 1 = 1.205071 − 1 = **20.51%**. Option A overstates; B and D understate. CAGR converts the 75% total gain over 3 years into a single annualised rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Mr. Bose's total capital-gains tax for the two transactions is:\nA) Rs. 99,375  B) Rs. 1,05,000  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 59,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = LTCG tax (Rs. 59,375) + STCG tax (Rs. 40,000) = **Rs. 99,375**. Option B forgets the LTCG exemption (would be 75,000 + 30,000); C is only STCG-related; D is only LTCG. Each gain is taxed under its own section and then summed.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Life Insurance Need for Mr. Sharma (HLV / Income Replacement)\n\nMr. Sharma earns Rs. 1,00,000 per month. Of this, Rs. 40,000 goes to his own personal needs, EMI, and insurance premium, leaving Rs. 60,000 per month to support his family. He wants to protect his wife (currently 45, expected to live to 80 — i.e., 35 years). Inflation is 6% and the corpus will be invested at 8%. He has an outstanding home loan of Rs. 30,00,000, existing life cover of Rs. 50,00,000, and investments of Rs. 20,00,000.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Monthly income | Rs. 1,00,000 |\n| Self/EMI/premium portion | Rs. 40,000 |\n| Income to replace (monthly) | Rs. 60,000 |\n| Wife's age / expected to | 45 / 80 (35 years) |\n| Inflation / Return | 6% / 8% |\n| Home loan outstanding | Rs. 30,00,000 |\n| Existing cover / investments | Rs. 50,00,000 / Rs. 20,00,000 |\n\n**Q61.** What annual income needs to be replaced for the family?\nA) Rs. 12,00,000  B) Rs. 7,20,000  C) Rs. 4,80,000  D) Rs. 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Income to replace = total − (self + EMI + premium) = 1,00,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 60,000/month → **× 12 = Rs. 7,20,000 per year**. Option A is the full income (doesn't deduct the portion the insured spent on himself); C and D are guesses. Only the income that actually supported dependents needs replacing.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** What real (inflation-adjusted) rate is used to value the income stream?\nA) 2%  B) 14%  C) 8%  D) 1.89%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Real rate = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.89%** (the workbook's standard HLV discount rate). Option A is the rough subtraction; B adds; C ignores inflation. The income to be provided rises with inflation, so it is discounted at the inflation-adjusted return.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Using PV of an annuity-due at 1.89% for 35 years on Rs. 7,20,000 per year, the corpus required to replace the income is approximately:\nA) Rs. 1.87 crore  B) Rs. 2.52 crore  C) Rs. 1.20 crore  D) Rs. 90 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Corpus = PV annuity-due = 7,20,000 × [1 − (1.0189)⁻³⁵] ÷ 0.0189 × (1.0189) ≈ 7,20,000 × 25.93 ≈ **Rs. 1.87 crore** (≈ Rs. 1,86,68,428). Option B overstates; C and D understate. This corpus, invested at 8%, would pay an inflation-rising income to the wife for 35 years and be fully drawn down by the end.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Adding the Rs. 30 lakh loan and deducting existing cover (Rs. 50 lakh) and investments (Rs. 20 lakh), what ADDITIONAL life cover does Mr. Sharma need?\nA) Rs. 2.17 crore  B) Rs. 1.87 crore  C) Rs. 1.47 crore  D) Rs. 67 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Total need = corpus (Rs. 1.87 cr) + loan (Rs. 30 lakh) = Rs. 2.17 crore. Deduct existing cover (Rs. 50 lakh) + investments (Rs. 20 lakh) = Rs. 70 lakh. Additional cover = 2,16,68,428 − 70,00,000 = **Rs. 1.47 crore** (≈ Rs. 1,46,68,428). Option A forgets to subtract existing resources; B omits both loan and offsets; D is a guess. Existing assets and cover reduce the new insurance required.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** If instead Mr. Sharma had Rs. 70,00,000 of investments (rather than Rs. 20 lakh), how would his additional insurance requirement change?\nA) Increase  B) Decrease (higher existing assets reduce the need)  C) Stay the same  D) Become zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the needs-based approach, **higher existing investments are deducted from the total need, lowering the additional cover required** (it would fall by an extra Rs. 50 lakh, to about Rs. 96.7 lakh). Option A is the opposite; C ignores the deduction; D overstates (the need wouldn't vanish). More owned assets means less insurance is needed to fill the gap.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — Integrated Plan for Mr. and Mrs. Kapoor\n\nMr. Kapoor (40) is building a Rs. 2 crore retirement corpus over 20 years and earns 12% per annum on his SIP. He also wants to evaluate a step-up SIP, examine the impact of an early EPF withdrawal, and check how his NPS corpus at 60 will be split. Assumptions: accumulation return 12% p.a. (1% per month), EPF interest 8.5% p.a.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Retirement corpus target | Rs. 2,00,00,000 |\n| Years to retirement | 20 (240 months) |\n| Accumulation return | 12% p.a. |\n| Considered EPF withdrawal now | Rs. 75,000 (30 years to retirement) |\n| Projected NPS corpus at 60 | Rs. 1,00,00,000 |\n\n**Q66.** What flat monthly SIP at 12% for 240 months reaches the Rs. 2 crore target?\nA) Rs. 83,333  B) Rs. 40,000  C) Rs. 10,000  D) Rs. 20,217\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]** = 2,00,00,000 × 0.01 ÷ [(1.01)²⁴⁰ − 1] = 2,00,000 ÷ 9.892554 = **Rs. 20,217** (the workbook's figure). Option A is the no-growth saving (2 cr ÷ 240); B and C are guesses. Compounding over 20 years does most of the work.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Mr. Kapoor prefers a step-up SIP rising 7% per year. Compared with the flat Rs. 20,217, his starting SIP will be:\nA) Higher than Rs. 20,217  B) Exactly Rs. 20,217  C) Lower than Rs. 20,217, then rising each year  D) A one-time lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A step-up SIP **starts below the flat amount and increases by 7% annually**, matching rising income, while still reaching Rs. 2 crore. Option A is the opposite; B ignores the step-up's effect; D misreads the strategy. Step-up suits investors who expect their income to grow and want an easier start.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** If Mr. Kapoor withdraws Rs. 75,000 from EPF now (30 years to retirement, 8.5% p.a.), what future corpus does he lose?\nA) Rs. 2.25 lakh  B) Rs. 8.66 lakh  C) Rs. 75,000  D) Rs. 5.00 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Forgone FV = **75,000 × (1.085)³⁰** = 75,000 × 11.5582 = **Rs. 8.66 lakh** (the workbook example). Option A uses simple interest; C ignores compounding; D is a guess. A Rs. 75,000 early withdrawal quietly costs over Rs. 8.6 lakh of retirement money.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** At 60, Mr. Kapoor's NPS corpus is Rs. 1 crore. What is the maximum tax-free lump sum and the minimum annuity portion?\nA) Lump Rs. 60 lakh; annuity Rs. 40 lakh  B) Lump Rs. 40 lakh; annuity Rs. 60 lakh  C) Lump Rs. 100 lakh; annuity nil  D) Lump Rs. 50 lakh; annuity Rs. 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — On normal exit at 60, NPS allows up to **60% (Rs. 60 lakh) as a tax-free lump sum** and requires a **minimum 40% (Rs. 40 lakh) to buy an annuity**. Option B reverses it; C ignores mandatory annuitisation; D is a 50:50 guess. The annuity income is later taxable in the year received.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** If Mr. Kapoor had instead delayed his Rs. 2 crore SIP plan by 5 years (15 years / 180 months left at 12%), the required monthly SIP becomes about Rs. 40,034. The integrated takeaway is:\nA) Delaying is harmless  B) Early start, avoiding withdrawals, and step-ups all protect the retirement goal at lower cost  C) He should reduce the target to Rs. 1 crore  D) NPS alone will fund retirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Across the case, the same Rs. 2 crore goal costs Rs. 20,217/month over 20 years but nearly double (Rs. 40,034) over 15 years; an early EPF withdrawal silently drains Rs. 8.66 lakh; and step-ups ease the start. So **starting early, not withdrawing prematurely, and stepping up contributions** keep the goal affordable. Option A contradicts the maths; C abandons the goal; D over-relies on one product. Disciplined, early, uninterrupted saving is the core of sound retirement planning.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "10", "md": "# Mock Paper 10 — NISM Series X-B (Investment Adviser Level 2)\n**Total: 100 marks · 70 questions · Suggested time: 2 hours · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n> This is the toughest, \"final\" paper of the set — it is numerical and case-heavy and spans the entire syllabus. Keep a spreadsheet or financial calculator handy, and write down each formula before you plug in the numbers.\n\n## Section A — Numerical MCQs (40 questions × 1 mark = 40 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Ravi buys 1,000 listed equity shares at ₹150 (STT paid) on 10-May-2023 and sells all on 20-Jun-2025 at ₹360. This is his only capital gain in FY 2025-26. What is his capital gains tax liability (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹26,250  B) ₹10,625  C) ₹42,000  D) ₹21,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These are listed shares with STT, held more than 12 months (May-2023 to Jun-2025 ≈ 25 months), so it is a Long-Term Capital Gain taxed under Section 112A at 12.5% on the gain above the ₹1,25,000 exemption. Gain = (360−150) × 1,000 = ₹2,10,000. Taxable = 2,10,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹85,000. Tax = 12.5% × 85,000 = **₹10,625**. Option A (₹26,250) forgets the ₹1.25 lakh exemption (12.5% × 2,10,000). Option C uses 20% STCG rate, wrong because the holding period is over 12 months. Option D (₹21,000) is 10% of the gross gain — the old pre-23-Jul-2024 rate, no longer applicable.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Sunita sells an unlisted equity share lot for a gain of ₹4,00,000 after holding it for 30 months (acquired and sold after 23-Jul-2024). What is the LTCG tax (ignore cess and surcharge)?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹34,375\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Unlisted shares become long-term after 24 months. Held 30 months, so it is LTCG. For assets other than STT-paid listed equity, LTCG is taxed at 12.5% without indexation and there is NO ₹1.25 lakh exemption (that exemption is only for Section 112A listed equity/equity MF). Tax = 12.5% × 4,00,000 = **₹50,000**. Option A uses 20% (old indexed rate). Option B uses 10%. Option D wrongly subtracts the ₹1.25 lakh exemption (12.5% × 2,75,000) which does not apply to unlisted shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A debt mutual fund (a \"Specified Mutual Fund\" bought after 1-Apr-2023) is sold after 4 years with a gain of ₹1,80,000. How is this gain taxed?\nA) LTCG at 12.5% without indexation  B) LTCG at 20% with indexation  C) Always at applicable slab rates as deemed short-term  D) Exempt up to ₹1,25,000 then 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 50AA, gains on Specified Mutual Funds (those investing 65%+ in debt/money-market and bought on/after 1-Apr-2023) are deemed short-term regardless of holding period and taxed at the investor's **applicable slab rates**. Option A is the rule for listed bonds, not these debt MFs. Option B (20% with indexation) is the old pre-Apr-2023 debt-fund rule, now removed. Option D mixes in the Section 112A equity exemption, which never applies to debt funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An employee's monthly basic salary is ₹40,000. Under EPF, the employer contributes 12% of basic. How much of the employer's monthly contribution goes to the Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS)?\nA) ₹4,800  B) ₹3,333  C) ₹1,250  D) ₹1,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The employer's 8.33% goes to EPS, but EPS contribution is capped on a wage ceiling of ₹15,000. So EPS = 8.33% × 15,000 = **₹1,250** (the balance of the employer's 12% goes to EPF). Option A is the total employer contribution (12% × 40,000 = ₹4,800). Option B wrongly applies 8.33% to the full ₹40,000. Option D (3.67% × 40,000 ≈ ₹1,468... actually ₹1,468) is not even that — ₹1,800 looks like a random EPF-share figure; the wage-ceiling cap is the key trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An employee covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act retires after 24 years and 8 months of service with a last drawn salary (basic + DA) of ₹52,000 p.m. What is the gratuity payable per the Act's formula?\nA) ₹7,50,000  B) ₹7,20,000  C) ₹6,24,000  D) ₹3,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula (covered employees) = (15 × last drawn salary × tenure) ÷ 26. A period beyond 6 months counts as a full year, so 24 years 8 months rounds up to 25 years. Gratuity = (15 × 52,000 × 25) ÷ 26 = 1,95,00,000 ÷ 26 = **₹7,50,000**. Option B uses 24 years instead of rounding up to 25 (15 × 52,000 × 24 ÷ 26 = ₹7,20,000). Option C uses the ÷30 (non-covered) formula with 24 years (15 × 52,000 × 24 ÷ 30 = ₹6,24,000). Option D halves the correct figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A retiree's NPS Tier-I corpus at age 60 is ₹80,00,000. Per NPS exit rules, what is the maximum lump sum she can withdraw, and how much must be annuitised?\nA) Withdraw ₹40,00,000; annuitise ₹40,00,000  B) Withdraw ₹48,00,000; annuitise ₹32,00,000  C) Withdraw ₹64,00,000; annuitise ₹16,00,000  D) Withdraw ₹80,00,000; annuitise nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On normal exit at 60, a minimum of 40% of the corpus must buy an annuity and a maximum of 60% can be taken as lump sum. Lump sum = 60% × 80,00,000 = **₹48,00,000**; annuity = 40% × 80,00,000 = ₹32,00,000. Option A is a 50:50 split (wrong proportion). Option C reverses to 80:20. Option D (full withdrawal) is allowed only if the corpus is ≤ ₹5 lakh — not the case here.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Inflation is 6% p.a. A goal that costs ₹10,00,000 today will cost how much in 12 years?\nA) ₹17,20,000  B) ₹18,98,299  C) ₹16,00,000  D) ₹20,12,196\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)^n = 10,00,000 × (1.06)^12. (1.06)^12 = 2.012196. So = **₹20,12,196**. Option A uses simple inflation (10,00,000 × (1 + 0.06×12) = ₹17,20,000) — wrong, inflation compounds. Option B uses 11 years instead of 12. Option C is plain 60% addition.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If the nominal return on an investment is 11% and inflation is 6%, what is the real (inflation-adjusted) rate of return?\nA) 5.00%  B) 4.72%  C) 17.66%  D) 5.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.11 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.04717 − 1 = 0.04717 = **4.72%**. Option A (5%) is the crude subtraction 11% − 6%, which slightly overstates the real return. Option C adds them. Option D is a rounding of the subtraction method.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A SIP of ₹10,000 per month earns 1% per month (effective monthly). What is the future value at the end of 24 months (ordinary annuity, contribution at month-end)?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹2,72,432  C) ₹2,66,074  D) ₹2,69,735\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV of an ordinary annuity = P × [((1+i)^n − 1) ÷ i], with P = 10,000, i = 0.01, n = 24. (1.01)^24 = 1.269735; minus 1 = 0.269735; ÷ 0.01 = 26.9735; × 10,000 = **₹2,69,735**. Option A is just 24 × 10,000 (ignores returns). Option B uses the annuity-due formula (×(1+i)). Option C uses 23 periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A health policy has a 20% co-pay clause. The admissible claim is ₹3,50,000. How much does the insurer pay?\nA) ₹3,50,000  B) ₹70,000  C) ₹2,80,000  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Co-pay is the share of the claim borne by the insured. Insured pays 20% × 3,50,000 = ₹70,000; the insurer pays the rest = 80% × 3,50,000 = **₹2,80,000**. Option A ignores the co-pay. Option B is the insured's share, not the insurer's. Option D is a random round figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A super top-up health policy has a deductible of ₹5,00,000 and a cover of ₹15,00,000. A hospital bill is ₹8,00,000 (this is the only claim in the year). How much does the top-up policy pay?\nA) ₹8,00,000  B) ₹5,00,000  C) Nil  D) ₹3,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A deductible is the amount the insured must bear (from own pocket or a base policy) before the top-up becomes payable. Payable = Claim − Deductible = 8,00,000 − 5,00,000 = **₹3,00,000** (within the ₹15 lakh cover). Option A ignores the deductible. Option B is the deductible itself. Option C wrongly assumes nothing is payable because the bill exceeds the deductible.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Under the contribution clause, an insured holds two health policies: Policy X (sum insured ₹6,00,000) and Policy Y (sum insured ₹4,00,000), totalling ₹10,00,000. A claim of ₹2,00,000 is settled by both proportionately. How much does Policy X bear?\nA) ₹1,00,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Under contribution (rateable proportion), each insurer pays in the ratio of its sum insured. X's share = 2,00,000 × (6,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000) = 2,00,000 × 0.6 = **₹1,20,000**. Option A is a 50:50 split (ignores the differing sums insured). Option B is the whole claim. Option C is Policy Y's share.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A person's annual expenses are ₹6,00,000, expected to be needed every year through retirement. Using the simple income-replacement (capitalisation) approach at a net real return of 4%, what corpus is needed to fund these expenses in perpetuity?\nA) ₹1,50,00,000  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹2,40,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A perpetuity corpus = Annual cash flow ÷ net real return = 6,00,000 ÷ 0.04 = **₹1,50,00,000**. The idea: if ₹1.5 crore earns a 4% real return, that produces ₹6 lakh a year of purchasing power forever. Option B uses 6% instead of 4%. Option C uses 2.5% (40× rule). Option D multiplies expenses by 10 (an arbitrary rule of thumb).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** An investor's portfolio grows from ₹4,00,000 to ₹9,00,000 over 6 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 20.83%  B) 14.47%  C) 12.50%  D) 17.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 = (9,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/6) − 1 = (2.25)^(0.16667) − 1. ln(2.25) = 0.81093; ÷ 6 = 0.135155; e^0.135155 = 1.14471; − 1 = **14.47%**. Option A is the simple average return (125% total ÷ 6). Option C and D are distractors not derived from the compounding formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A resident individual aged 45 has total taxable income of ₹11,80,000 (all normal income) for FY 2025-26 and opts for the new tax regime. After the Section 87A rebate, what is the income-tax payable (before cess)?\nA) ₹71,500  B) Nil  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹11,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the new regime for FY 2025-26, a resident individual with total income up to ₹12,00,000 gets a Section 87A rebate of up to ₹60,000, which wipes out the tax. Since ₹11,80,000 ≤ ₹12,00,000, the rebate covers the entire computed tax, so tax payable = **Nil**. Option A is the tax before rebate. Option C is the maximum rebate amount, not the tax. Option D is an arbitrary residual.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A domestic-company dividend of ₹50,000 is received by a resident in the 30% slab. He paid ₹6,000 interest on a loan taken to buy those shares. What dividend income is taxable and what is the maximum interest deduction?\nA) Taxable ₹50,000; deduction ₹6,000  B) Taxable ₹50,000; deduction ₹10,000  C) Taxable ₹40,000; deduction ₹10,000  D) Taxable ₹50,000; deduction ₹3,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Dividend from a domestic company is fully taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates, so ₹50,000 is taxable. Against it, only interest expense is deductible, capped at 20% of the dividend income = 20% × 50,000 = ₹10,000. Actual interest paid is ₹6,000, which is below the cap, so the full **₹6,000** is allowed. Option B wrongly allows the full ₹10,000 cap rather than the lower actual. Option C wrongly reduces taxable dividend. Option D halves the interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mr. A's basic salary is ₹20,00,000. His employer contributes 14% of basic to NPS Tier-I under Section 80CCD(2) (he is in the new regime). What employer-NPS deduction can he claim?\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,80,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the new tax regime, the employer's NPS contribution is deductible under Section 80CCD(2) up to 14% of salary. 14% × 20,00,000 = **₹2,80,000**, fully deductible. Option A is the 80C cap (not relevant to employer NPS). Option C is the 80CCD(1B) self-contribution limit. Option D uses a 10% rate (the old-regime private-employer limit).\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A property is bought on 1-Apr-2020 for ₹40,00,000 and sold on 1-Aug-2025 for ₹75,00,000 (no indexation benefit, acquired before but transferred after 23-Jul-2024 — assume the non-indexed 12.5% route applies). What is the LTCG tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹4,37,500  B) ₹7,00,000  C) ₹3,50,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Immovable property held more than 24 months is a long-term asset. Gain (without indexation) = 75,00,000 − 40,00,000 = ₹35,00,000. Tax at 12.5% = **₹4,37,500**. There is no ₹1.25 lakh exemption for property. Option B uses 20%. Option C uses 10%. Option D is an arbitrary round figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An investor has LTCG (Section 112A, listed equity) of ₹95,000 in FY 2025-26. What is the LTCG tax?\nA) ₹11,875  B) ₹9,500  C) ₹19,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Section 112A grants a ₹1,25,000 exemption on listed-equity LTCG. Since ₹95,000 ≤ ₹1,25,000, the entire gain is exempt and tax = **Nil**. Option A applies 12.5% to the whole gain (ignores exemption). Option B uses 10%. Option C uses 20% (the STCG rate).\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** STCG on listed equity (STT paid) under Section 111A is ₹2,00,000 in FY 2025-26. What is the tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — STCG covered by Section 111A is taxed at 20% (post-23-Jul-2024). Tax = 20% × 2,00,000 = **₹40,000**. There is NO ₹1.25 lakh exemption for short-term gains. Option A uses 15% (the old rate). Option B subtracts a non-existent exemption. Option D uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** PPF follows the EEE tax treatment. An investor deposits ₹1,50,000 a year. Which statement is correct?\nA) Contribution deductible under 80C, interest taxable, maturity exempt  B) Contribution not deductible, interest exempt, maturity taxable  C) Contribution deductible under 80CCD, interest exempt, maturity exempt  D) Contribution deductible under 80C, interest exempt, maturity exempt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EEE = Exempt-Exempt-Exempt: the contribution is deductible (under Section 80C up to ₹1.5 lakh), the interest earned is exempt, and the maturity proceeds are exempt. So option **D**. Option A wrongly taxes interest. Option B wrongly taxes maturity. Option C cites the wrong section (80CCD is for NPS, not PPF).\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An employee's EPF withdrawal before completing 5 years of continuous service is ₹2,00,000. How is it generally treated?\nA) Fully tax-free  B) Taxed at a flat 12.5%  C) Exempt up to ₹50,000 then taxable  D) Taxable in the year of receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EPF is tax-free only if withdrawn after 5 years of continuous service (or under specified exempt situations). A premature withdrawal of more than ₹50,000 before 5 years is **taxable in the year of receipt** (with TDS), and earlier deductions can be withdrawn. Option A applies only after 5 years. Option B invents a flat rate. Option C mixes up the ₹50,000 TDS threshold with an exemption — the ₹50,000 line is the TDS trigger, not a tax-free slab on a ₹2 lakh withdrawal.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The Human Life Value (HLV) approach values a person's economic worth as the present value of future income they will provide to dependants. If an earner provides ₹8,00,000 a year to dependants for the next 20 years and the discount rate is 7%, the HLV is approximately (PV of annuity factor for 20 yrs at 7% = 10.594):\nA) ₹1,60,00,000  B) ₹84,75,200  C) ₹56,00,000  D) ₹1,06,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — HLV = annual support × PV-annuity factor = 8,00,000 × 10.594 = **₹84,75,200**. This discounts 20 years of ₹8 lakh income to today's value. Option A simply multiplies 8,00,000 × 20 (ignores discounting). Option C uses 7 years. Option D uses an arbitrary factor of 13.25.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under the new tax regime FY 2025-26, the standard deduction available to a salaried employee is:\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) Nil  D) ₹75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — In the new tax regime the salary standard deduction is **₹75,000** (it is ₹50,000 in the old regime). Option A is the old-regime figure. Option B is the 80C limit. Option C is wrong — the standard deduction is available in both regimes.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A car insurance policy carries a 50% No Claim Bonus on the own-damage premium, and the own-damage premium before NCB is ₹18,000. After applying NCB, the own-damage premium is:\nA) ₹18,000  B) ₹9,000  C) ₹12,000  D) ₹27,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NCB is a discount on the own-damage premium for claim-free years, up to 50% after 5 years. 50% × 18,000 = ₹9,000 discount, so premium = 18,000 − 9,000 = **₹9,000**. Option A ignores the NCB. Option C applies a 33% discount. Option D adds rather than subtracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** An investor holds a listed bond, sold after 18 months for a long-term capital gain of ₹1,00,000. How is it taxed?\nA) 12.5% without indexation = ₹12,500  B) 20% with indexation  C) Slab rate  D) Exempt up to ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed bonds/debentures become long-term after 12 months. LTCG on listed bonds is taxed at **12.5% without indexation**, so 12.5% × 1,00,000 = ₹12,500. Option B (20% with indexation) is not allowed on listed bonds. Option C is for short-term. Option D applies the equity-only exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A salaried person in the old regime claims: 80C ₹1,50,000 (full), 80CCD(1B) NPS ₹50,000, and 80D health insurance ₹25,000. Total Chapter VI-A deduction from these is:\nA) ₹1,50,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹2,25,000  D) ₹1,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 80C (₹1,50,000) + 80CCD(1B) (₹50,000, an additional NPS limit over and above 80C) + 80D (₹25,000) = **₹2,25,000**. Option A counts only 80C. Option B forgets the 80D ₹25,000. Option D forgets the 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An investor sells listed equity (STT paid) for a ₹3,00,000 LTCG and separately has a brought-forward long-term capital loss of ₹50,000. After set-off and the Section 112A exemption, the LTCG tax is (ignore cess):\nA) ₹15,625  B) ₹18,750  C) ₹37,500  D) ₹21,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — First set off the ₹50,000 LTCL against the ₹3,00,000 LTCG → net LTCG ₹2,50,000. Then apply the ₹1,25,000 exemption → taxable ₹1,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × 1,25,000 = **₹15,625**. Option B skips the loss set-off (12.5% × (3,00,000 − 1,25,000) ÷ ... ). Option C ignores both the loss and exemption (12.5% × 3,00,000). Option D ignores the loss set-off but keeps exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Loss aversion most directly leads investors to:\nA) Sell winners too early and hold losers too long (disposition effect)  B) Buy more of falling stocks regardless of fundamentals  C) Diversify excessively across uncorrelated assets  D) Always prefer fixed deposits over equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Loss aversion (losses hurt more than equivalent gains please) underlies the **disposition effect**: investors hold losing positions hoping to \"break even\" and sell winners quickly to lock in gains. Option B describes averaging-down driven by other biases, not the defining behaviour. Option C is the opposite of a bias. Option D is overly broad and not specific to loss aversion.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A person aged 30 wants ₹2,00,00,000 at age 60 (in 30 years). If investments earn 10% p.a. compounded annually, the lump sum needed today is approximately:\nA) ₹11,46,000  B) ₹66,66,667  C) ₹6,66,667  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Present Value = FV ÷ (1+r)^n = 2,00,00,000 ÷ (1.10)^30. (1.10)^30 = 17.449. PV = 2,00,00,000 ÷ 17.449 = **₹11,46,197 ≈ ₹11,46,000**. Option B divides by 3 (no compounding). Option C divides by 30. Option D is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A debt fund that is NOT a Specified Mutual Fund (e.g., units bought before 1-Apr-2023) is sold after 40 months for a gain of ₹2,00,000. Under the post-23-Jul-2024 rules, what is the LTCG tax (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹20,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Long-term gains on such non-specified debt funds are taxed at 12.5% without indexation. 12.5% × 2,00,000 = **₹25,000**. Option B uses 20%. Option C uses 10%. Option D wrongly treats it as exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A person dies intestate (without a Will). What is the immediate legal consequence for distribution of his assets?\nA) Assets pass to the government automatically  B) Assets are frozen permanently  C) The nominee becomes the absolute owner  D) Assets devolve on legal heirs as per the applicable succession law\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Dying intestate means without a Will; the estate **devolves upon the legal heirs as per the applicable law of succession** (heirs typically apply for a succession certificate/letters of administration). Option A (escheat to government) happens only if there are no heirs at all. Option B is false. Option C is wrong — a nominee is only a custodian/trustee for the legal heirs, not the owner.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A 35-year-old invests ₹1,50,000 every year (year-end) for 25 years at 9% p.a. The future value (FV annuity factor for 25 yrs at 9% = 84.701) is:\nA) ₹37,50,000  B) ₹84,70,100  C) ₹1,16,56,560  D) ₹1,27,05,150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — FV = annual investment × FV-annuity factor = 1,50,000 × 84.701 = **₹1,27,05,150**. Option A is just 25 × 1,50,000 (ignores compounding). Option B multiplies the factor by ₹1,00,000 instead of ₹1,50,000. Option C uses 24 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Anchoring bias is best described as:\nA) Following the crowd into popular stocks  B) Over-trusting one's own forecasting ability  C) Treating money differently based on its source  D) Relying too heavily on an initial reference figure (e.g., purchase price) when making decisions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Anchoring is fixating on an initial number — like the price you paid — and judging later decisions relative to that anchor even when it is irrelevant. Option A is herd behaviour. Option B is overconfidence. Option C is mental accounting.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A retiree needs ₹50,000 per month (today's value) at the start of retirement. With 6% inflation, what monthly income will be required to maintain the same purchasing power 10 years from now?\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹80,000  C) ₹89,542  D) ₹95,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Future monthly need = 50,000 × (1.06)^10 = 50,000 × 1.790847 = **₹89,542**. (Consistent with the workbook example where ₹60,000 grows to ₹1,07,451 in 10 years at 6%.) Option A ignores inflation. Option B undercounts. Option D rounds up arbitrarily.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Under Section 80C, which of the following combination of investments, if each is fully made, can be claimed (maximum) in a year?\nA) PPF ₹1,00,000 + ELSS ₹80,000 → ₹1,80,000  B) PPF ₹1,00,000 + ELSS ₹80,000 → ₹1,50,000  C) PPF ₹1,00,000 + ELSS ₹80,000 → ₹2,00,000  D) Only ₹1,00,000 (PPF only)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both PPF and ELSS qualify under 80C, but the aggregate deduction under 80C is capped at **₹1,50,000** per year. So ₹1,00,000 + ₹80,000 = ₹1,80,000 of eligible investment, but the claim is limited to ₹1,50,000. Option A ignores the cap. Option C double-counts. Option D wrongly excludes ELSS.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Mental accounting causes an investor to:\nA) Treat a tax refund or bonus as \"free money\" and spend/invest it differently from salary  B) Always seek the lowest-cost fund  C) Avoid all risky assets  D) Make decisions purely on expected value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Mental accounting (Thaler) means money is treated as less fungible than it is — a windfall like a bonus or refund is mentally \"earmarked\" and spent more freely than salary, even though a rupee is a rupee. Option B is cost-consciousness. Option C is risk aversion. Option D is rational behaviour, the opposite of a bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A person buys a ₹50,00,000 term insurance policy. The annual premium is ₹15,000. Under the old regime, this premium qualifies for deduction under which section, and up to what overall limit (assuming no other 80C claims)?\nA) 80D up to ₹25,000  B) 80C within the ₹1,50,000 limit  C) 80CCD(1B) up to ₹50,000  D) 24(b) up to ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Life-insurance premiums are deductible under **Section 80C** within the overall ₹1,50,000 limit. The ₹15,000 premium fits within that. Option A (80D) is for health insurance. Option C (80CCD(1B)) is for additional NPS. Option D (24(b)) is for home-loan interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor in the 30% slab earns ₹3,00,000 interest from a bank fixed deposit. What is the tax on this interest (ignore cess, old regime, ignore other income)?\nA) ₹37,500  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹90,000  D) ₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Bank FD interest is \"Income from Other Sources\" taxed at the investor's slab rate. At 30%, tax = 30% × 3,00,000 = **₹90,000**. Option A applies 12.5% (a capital-gains rate, wrong for interest). Option B uses 20%. Option D uses 10% (the TDS rate, which is not the final tax).\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A goal needs ₹40,00,000 in 8 years. An investor already has ₹10,00,000 invested at 8% p.a. How much more lump sum must be invested today (also at 8%) to meet the goal? (1.08^8 = 1.85093)\nA) ₹11,61,290  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹21,61,290  D) ₹16,21,613\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The existing ₹10,00,000 grows to 10,00,000 × 1.85093 = ₹18,50,930 in 8 years. Shortfall at maturity = 40,00,000 − 18,50,930 = ₹21,49,070. Present value of that shortfall = 21,49,070 ÷ 1.85093 = **₹11,61,290** (additional lump sum needed today). Equivalently: total PV of goal = 40,00,000 ÷ 1.85093 = ₹21,61,290; minus existing ₹10,00,000 = **₹11,61,290**. Option C is the total PV of the goal (forgets to subtract the existing ₹10 lakh). Option B is the nominal shortfall ignoring growth. Option D miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Case Studies (6 cases × 5 sub-questions × 2 marks = 60 marks)\n\n### Case 1 — The Mehta Household: Salary, Tax Regime & Goal Funding\n\nRohan Mehta (age 38) is a salaried IT professional. For FY 2025-26 his figures are:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|\n| Gross salary | 18,00,000 |\n| Basic (40% of gross) | 7,20,000 |\n| Employer NPS contribution (Sec 80CCD(2)) | 14% of basic |\n| 80C investments (EPF + ELSS + PPF) | 2,10,000 actual |\n| 80CCD(1B) self NPS | 50,000 |\n| 80D health insurance premium | 28,000 |\n\nHe wants to fund his daughter's higher education: cost today ₹15,00,000, needed in 10 years; education inflation 8% p.a.; he expects investments to earn 11% p.a.\n\n**Q41.** What is Rohan's employer-NPS contribution under Section 80CCD(2)?\nA) ₹2,52,000  B) ₹72,000  C) ₹1,00,800  D) ₹2,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Employer NPS under 80CCD(2) = 14% of basic = 14% × 7,20,000 = **₹1,00,800**. Option A applies 14% to gross (wrong base — the limit is on basic/salary defined as basic+DA). Option B uses 10%. Option D is 14% of ₹20,00,000, not his basic.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Under the OLD regime, what is Rohan's total deduction from 80C, 80CCD(1B) and 80D combined? (Assume his ₹28,000 health premium is fully allowable under 80D.)\nA) ₹2,88,000  B) ₹2,28,000  C) ₹2,25,000  D) ₹2,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 80C is capped at ₹1,50,000 (his ₹2,10,000 actual investment is limited to the ₹1.5 lakh cap) + 80CCD(1B) ₹50,000 (an additional NPS limit over and above 80C) + 80D ₹28,000 = 1,50,000 + 50,000 + 28,000 = **₹2,28,000**. Option A wrongly uses the full ₹2,10,000 for 80C (no cap). Option C uses ₹25,000 for 80D instead of the ₹28,000 given. Option D adds the uncapped 80C with rounding errors. Key learning: 80C is capped at ₹1.5 lakh regardless of actual investment; 80CCD(1B) and 80D are over and above.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** What will be the inflation-adjusted future cost of the daughter's education in 10 years (cost ₹15,00,000 today, 8% education inflation)?\nA) ₹27,00,000  B) ₹32,38,393  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 15,00,000 × (1.08)^10. (1.08)^10 = 2.158925. = 15,00,000 × 2.158925 = **₹32,38,388 ≈ ₹32,38,393**. Option A uses simple interest (15,00,000 × (1 + 0.08×10) = ₹27,00,000). Option C doubles short. Option D is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** To accumulate ₹32,38,388 in 10 years at 11% p.a., what one-time lump sum must Rohan invest today? (1.11^10 = 2.83942)\nA) ₹11,40,505  B) ₹16,19,194  C) ₹14,72,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV ÷ (1.11)^10 = 32,38,388 ÷ 2.83942 = **₹11,40,505**. Option B halves the FV. Option C uses 9% discounting. Option D is arbitrary. The compounding at 11% over 10 years nearly triples the money, so far less than the future cost is needed today.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** If instead Rohan invests via an annual year-end SIP for 10 years at 11% to reach ₹32,38,388 (FV-annuity factor for 10 yrs at 11% = 16.722), the required annual investment is approximately:\nA) ₹1,93,659  B) ₹3,23,839  C) ₹1,61,919  D) ₹2,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual investment = FV ÷ FV-annuity factor = 32,38,388 ÷ 16.722 = **₹1,93,660 ≈ ₹1,93,659**. Option B just divides FV by 10 (ignores returns). Option C divides FV by ~20. Option D is arbitrary. Note this annual amount (₹1.94 lakh × 10 = ₹19.4 lakh invested) is far less than the ₹32.4 lakh goal because returns do the heavy lifting.\n</details>\n\n### Case 2 — The Iyer Family: Insurance Claims & Capital Gains\n\nSuresh Iyer (age 45) suffers a hospitalisation costing ₹6,00,000. He holds:\n- A base health policy with sum insured ₹4,00,000 and a 10% co-pay clause.\n- A super top-up policy with sum insured ₹15,00,000 and a deductible of ₹4,00,000.\n\nSeparately, in FY 2025-26 Suresh also realises capital gains:\n- Listed equity (STT paid), held 14 months: gain ₹2,50,000.\n- Listed equity (STT paid), held 5 months: gain ₹80,000.\n\n**Q46.** From the base policy (10% co-pay, SI ₹4,00,000), how much does the insurer pay on the ₹6,00,000 bill?\nA) ₹6,00,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹3,60,000  D) ₹5,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The base policy can pay at most its sum insured ₹4,00,000, but a 10% co-pay applies to the admissible claim. The base policy's admissible portion is capped at its SI ₹4,00,000; after 10% co-pay the insurer pays 90% × 4,00,000 = **₹3,60,000** (the insured bears ₹40,000 co-pay). Option A exceeds the SI. Option B ignores the co-pay. Option D applies the co-pay percentage to ₹6,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The super top-up has a deductible of ₹4,00,000. After the base policy pays ₹3,60,000 and Suresh has borne ₹40,000 co-pay, the remaining bill is ₹6,00,000 − ₹3,60,000 = ₹2,40,000. How much does the top-up pay?\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) Nil  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A super top-up pays only after total eligible expenses cross the deductible of ₹4,00,000 in the policy year. Here the aggregate bill (₹6,00,000) does cross ₹4,00,000, but the deductible is meant to be met by the insured/base cover first; the amount already covered by the base policy (₹3,60,000) plus the insured's bearing means the residual ₹2,40,000 is still within/around the ₹4,00,000 deductible threshold the top-up requires to be absorbed before it triggers. Since the unreimbursed amount (₹2,40,000) has not exceeded the ₹4,00,000 deductible, the top-up pays **Nil**. (Learning: a deductible-based top-up only kicks in for the portion of a claim that exceeds the deductible; a single ₹6 lakh claim largely absorbed by a ₹4 lakh base policy leaves a residual below the ₹4 lakh deductible.) Option A ignores the deductible. Option C and D assume partial payment that the deductible does not permit.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** What is the tax on Suresh's ₹2,50,000 listed-equity LTCG (held 14 months, STT paid), in FY 2025-26 (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹31,250  B) ₹15,625  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Held >12 months, STT paid → Section 112A LTCG. Taxable = 2,50,000 − 1,25,000 exemption = ₹1,25,000. Tax = 12.5% × 1,25,000 = **₹15,625**. Option A ignores the exemption (12.5% × 2,50,000). Option C uses 10% on the gross. Option D uses 20% on the gross.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** What is the tax on the ₹80,000 listed-equity gain held only 5 months (STT paid)?\nA) ₹16,000  B) ₹10,000  C) Nil  D) ₹8,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Held <12 months → STCG under Section 111A, taxed at 20%, with NO exemption. Tax = 20% × 80,000 = **₹16,000**. Option B uses 12.5%. Option C wrongly applies the ₹1.25 lakh exemption (that is LTCG-only). Option D uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** What is Suresh's total capital-gains tax for FY 2025-26 (LTCG + STCG, ignore cess)?\nA) ₹31,625  B) ₹47,250  C) ₹26,000  D) ₹15,625\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — LTCG tax ₹15,625 (from Q48) + STCG tax ₹16,000 (from Q49) = **₹31,625**. Option B over-taxes by ignoring the LTCG exemption. Option C uses wrong rates. Option D counts only the LTCG. The two gains are taxed under different sections (112A and 111A) and added together.\n</details>\n\n### Case 3 — Mr. Khanna's Retirement: EPF, Gratuity & NPS\n\nMr. Khanna (a private-sector employee covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act) retires at 60 after 30 years of service. At retirement:\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Last drawn salary (basic + DA) | ₹70,000 p.m. |\n| Monthly basic for EPF | ₹70,000 |\n| NPS Tier-I corpus | ₹1,00,00,000 |\n| Actual gratuity received | ₹14,00,000 |\n\n**Q51.** What is the gratuity payable under the Act's formula (15 × last drawn × tenure ÷ 26)?\nA) ₹12,11,538  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹10,50,000  D) ₹13,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Formula = (15 × 70,000 × 30) ÷ 26 = 3,15,00,000 ÷ 26 = **₹12,11,538**. Option B is the actual received (not the formula figure). Option C uses the ÷30 (non-covered) formula. Option D is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** What amount of gratuity is exempt from tax? (Exemption = least of: ₹20,00,000 cap, actual received, formula amount)\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹14,00,000  C) ₹12,11,538  D) ₹6,11,538\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Exemption is the least of (i) ₹20,00,000 statutory cap, (ii) actual gratuity ₹14,00,000, (iii) formula amount ₹12,11,538. The least is **₹12,11,538**, which is exempt. Option A is just the cap. Option B is the actual received. Option D is the taxable portion (₹14,00,000 − ₹12,11,538), not the exempt amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Therefore, how much of the gratuity is taxable?\nA) Nil  B) ₹1,88,462  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable gratuity = Actual received − Exempt = 14,00,000 − 12,11,538 = **₹1,88,462**. Option A would be true only if actual ≤ formula. Option C and D are arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** From his ₹1,00,00,000 NPS corpus at age 60, what is the maximum tax-free lump sum he can withdraw?\nA) ₹40,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At normal NPS exit at 60, up to 60% of the corpus can be withdrawn as a lump sum (tax-free) and at least 40% must buy an annuity. 60% × 1,00,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Option A is the annuity portion. Option C (full) is allowed only if corpus ≤ ₹5 lakh. Option D is a 50% split.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The remaining ₹40,00,000 buys an annuity. If the annuity provider offers 6.5% p.a. (paid monthly, simple yield), what is the approximate monthly pension?\nA) ₹21,667  B) ₹2,60,000  C) ₹26,000  D) ₹43,333\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual annuity income = 6.5% × 40,00,000 = ₹2,60,000. Monthly = 2,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹21,667**. Option B is the annual figure (not monthly). Option C divides by 100 wrongly. Option D doubles the monthly. The annuity income from the mandatory 40% is fully taxable as pension income at slab rates.\n</details>\n\n### Case 4 — The Sharma Estate: Will, Nomination & Asset Transfer\n\nMr. & Mrs. Sharma (both 62) own: a self-occupied house (₹2,00,00,000), a portfolio of listed shares (₹80,00,000, all held >12 months, STT paid, with embedded gains), bank deposits (₹40,00,000), and an insurance policy (₹50,00,000 sum assured, nominee: their son). Mr. Sharma has made a registered Will leaving the house to his wife and shares equally to his two children. He has nominated his wife in the bank deposits.\n\n**Q56.** Mr. Sharma dies. Who has the rightful claim to the bank deposits where the wife is the nominee, if the Will leaves those deposits to the children?\nA) The wife, as absolute owner because she is the nominee  B) The bank keeps the deposits  C) Split equally between nominee and children automatically  D) The children/legal heirs as per the Will; the nominee only receives and holds the money in trust for them\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A nominee is a custodian/trustee, not the owner. The asset ultimately belongs to the beneficiaries named in the Will (or legal heirs). So the nominee wife receives the deposits from the bank but holds them in trust for the rightful heirs per the Will. Option A confuses nomination with ownership. Option B is false. Option C has no legal basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The son (nominee and beneficiary) receives the ₹50,00,000 life-insurance death benefit. What is the tax on this receipt?\nA) Taxable at 30% slab  B) Taxable as capital gains at 12.5%  C) Taxable as income from other sources  D) Exempt under Section 10(10D) (death proceeds)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Death benefits from a life-insurance policy are exempt under Section 10(10D). So the ₹50,00,000 is **fully exempt** in the son's hands. Options A, B and C all wrongly tax a death claim — sum received on death is exempt (the taxability conditions of 10(10D) apply to maturity proceeds of high-premium policies, not death claims).\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The wife inherits the listed shares (cost to Mr. Sharma ₹30,00,000, value ₹80,00,000) and later sells them. For computing her capital gain, what cost of acquisition does she use, and is inheritance itself taxed?\nA) Cost ₹80,00,000; inheritance taxed at 12.5%  B) Cost ₹30,00,000 (previous owner's cost); inheritance itself not taxed  C) Cost nil; inheritance taxed as income  D) Cost ₹80,00,000; inheritance not taxed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Transfer of a capital asset by inheritance/will is NOT a \"transfer\" for capital-gains tax, so the inheritance itself is not taxed. When the heir later sells, she steps into the previous owner's shoes: cost of acquisition = the original owner's cost (₹30,00,000), and the holding period includes the previous owner's. Option A and D wrongly use market value as cost and/or tax the inheritance. Option C wrongly taxes the inheritance as income.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** When the wife sells those listed shares for ₹80,00,000 (cost ₹30,00,000, STT paid, long-term), what is her LTCG tax (assume this is her only capital gain; ignore cess)?\nA) ₹6,09,375  B) ₹6,25,000  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = 80,00,000 − 30,00,000 = ₹50,00,000. Section 112A: taxable = 50,00,000 − 1,25,000 exemption = ₹48,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × 48,75,000 = **₹6,09,375**. Option B ignores the exemption (12.5% × 50,00,000). Option C uses 20%. Option D uses 10%.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which document ensures the Sharmas' wishes on asset distribution are followed and lets them appoint a guardian for any dependent with special needs?\nA) A nomination form  B) A power of attorney  C) A bank account opening form  D) A Will (last will and testament)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A Will specifies who inherits which assets and in what manner, and is also the instrument used to appoint a guardian for minors/children with special needs. Option A (nomination) only designates who receives an asset operationally, not ownership or guardianship. Option B (POA) is for acting on one's behalf while alive. Option C is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n### Case 5 — Anita's Comprehensive Plan: Risk, Returns & Tax\n\nAnita (age 40) has a moderate risk profile. Her current investments and a planned redemption for FY 2025-26:\n\n| Asset | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Equity MF (STT, equity-oriented) | Bought ₹6,00,000; current ₹11,00,000; held 3 years |\n| Specified debt MF (bought after 1-Apr-2023) | Gain on sale ₹1,50,000; held 4 years |\n| Bank FD interest (this year) | ₹2,00,000 |\n| Slab rate (old regime, marginal) | 30% |\n\nShe wants a ₹50,00,000 retirement-supplement corpus in 18 years; expects 12% nominal return, 6% inflation.\n\n**Q61.** What is the tax on Anita's equity-MF gain when she redeems (₹11,00,000 − ₹6,00,000 = ₹5,00,000 LTCG, STT, held 3 years; ignore cess)?\nA) ₹62,500  B) ₹46,875  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity-oriented MF held >12 months → Section 112A LTCG. Gain ₹5,00,000; taxable = 5,00,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹3,75,000. Tax = 12.5% × 3,75,000 = **₹46,875**. Option A ignores the exemption (12.5% × 5,00,000). Option C uses 10% on the gross. Option D uses 20% on the gross.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** How is the ₹1,50,000 gain on the Specified debt MF (bought after 1-Apr-2023) taxed for Anita (30% slab)?\nA) 12.5% = ₹18,750  B) 20% with indexation  C) Slab rate 30% = ₹45,000  D) Exempt up to ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under Section 50AA, gains on Specified Mutual Funds (debt MFs bought after 1-Apr-2023) are deemed short-term and taxed at the investor's slab rate, regardless of holding period. 30% × 1,50,000 = **₹45,000**. Option A applies the LTCG equity rate. Option B is the abolished old debt rule. Option D applies the equity-only exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** What is the tax on Anita's ₹2,00,000 FD interest (old regime, 30% slab; ignore cess)?\nA) ₹20,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — FD interest is taxed at slab rate as income from other sources. 30% × 2,00,000 = **₹60,000**. Option A is the 10% TDS (not the final tax). Option B uses 20%. Option D uses 12.5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** What is Anita's required real (inflation-adjusted) return given 12% nominal and 6% inflation?\nA) 6.00%  B) 5.66%  C) 18.72%  D) 6.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = (1.12 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.056604 − 1 = 0.056604 = **5.66%**. Option A is the crude subtraction (12 − 6). Option C adds them. Option D is a rounding of the subtraction.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** To reach ₹50,00,000 in 18 years at 12% nominal, what annual year-end investment is needed? (FV-annuity factor for 18 yrs at 12% = 55.7497)\nA) ₹89,687  B) ₹2,77,778  C) ₹1,79,372  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual investment = FV ÷ FV-annuity factor = 50,00,000 ÷ 55.7497 = **₹89,687**. Option B divides FV by 18 (ignores returns). Option C is double the correct value. Option D is arbitrary. Note: investing ~₹89,687 × 18 = ₹16.1 lakh grows to ₹50 lakh because of 12% compounding.\n</details>\n\n### Case 6 — The Verma Household: Integrated Final Scenario\n\nMr. Verma (age 58, retiring at 60) and his family. Data:\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Current monthly household expense | ₹80,000 (today's value) |\n| Inflation | 6% p.a. |\n| Years to retirement | 2 |\n| Expected post-retirement net real return | 4% |\n| EPF corpus expected at 60 | ₹50,00,000 (>5 yrs service, withdrawn at retirement) |\n| Listed-equity LTCG planned in FY 2025-26 | ₹3,25,000 (STT, long-term) |\n| Term insurance sum assured | ₹1,00,00,000 (nominee: spouse) |\n| Health policy | SI ₹10,00,000, 15% co-pay |\n\n**Q66.** What will the Vermas' monthly expense be at retirement (in 2 years) after 6% inflation?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹89,888  C) ₹84,800  D) ₹95,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future monthly expense = 80,000 × (1.06)^2 = 80,000 × 1.1236 = **₹89,888**. Option A ignores inflation. Option C uses simple 6% once. Option D is arbitrary.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Using the monthly expense at retirement (₹89,888 → ₹10,78,656 per year) and a perpetuity at 4% net real return, what corpus is needed at age 60 to fund expenses?\nA) ₹2,69,66,400  B) ₹1,07,86,560  C) ₹2,16,00,000  D) ₹1,80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Annual expense at retirement = 89,888 × 12 = ₹10,78,656. Perpetuity corpus = annual ÷ real return = 10,78,656 ÷ 0.04 = **₹2,69,66,400**. Option B divides by 10% (uses the annual figure × 10). Option C and D use wrong rates/rounding. (At 4% real return, the corpus must be 25× the annual expense.)\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Mr. Verma withdraws his ₹50,00,000 EPF at retirement (>5 years of continuous service). What is the tax on this withdrawal?\nA) Taxable at 30%  B) Taxable at 12.5%  C) Taxable above ₹50,000  D) Fully exempt (tax-free)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — EPF withdrawn after 5 years of continuous service is fully exempt (tax-free). So the ₹50,00,000 is **tax-free**. Options A, B and C wrongly tax it; taxability arises only on premature withdrawal (before 5 years), which is not the case here.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** What is the tax on Mr. Verma's ₹3,25,000 listed-equity LTCG (STT, long-term) in FY 2025-26 (ignore cess)?\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹65,000  D) ₹32,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 112A: taxable = 3,25,000 − 1,25,000 exemption = ₹2,00,000. Tax = 12.5% × 2,00,000 = **₹25,000**. Option A ignores the exemption (12.5% × 3,25,000). Option C uses 20% on the gross. Option D uses 10% on the gross.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** During the year, Mrs. Verma is hospitalised; the admissible claim is ₹4,00,000 under the health policy (SI ₹10,00,000, 15% co-pay). How much does the insurer pay, and how much does the family bear?\nA) Insurer ₹4,00,000; family nil  B) Insurer ₹60,000; family ₹3,40,000  C) Insurer ₹3,40,000; family ₹60,000  D) Insurer ₹3,40,000; family nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A 15% co-pay means the family bears 15% of the admissible claim and the insurer pays the rest. Family co-pay = 15% × 4,00,000 = ₹60,000; insurer pays 85% × 4,00,000 = **₹3,40,000**. The claim (₹4,00,000) is within the ₹10,00,000 sum insured, so no SI cap applies. Option A ignores the co-pay. Option B reverses the shares. Option D forgets the family's ₹60,000 co-pay.\n</details>\n"}], "primer": "# 📐 Financial Maths Primer — PV, FV, PMT & Friends (one-page glance)\n\n> The exam makes you compute retirement corpus, SIP maturity, loan/EMI, capital-gains growth and inflation. **Almost all of it is the same five-variable \"money time-machine.\"** Learn these once and the numericals become easy. Keep the 🧮 calculator open while you read.\n\n---\n\n## 🎰 The 5 variables (the money time-machine)\n\nMoney today and money tomorrow are **not** the same — money grows over time (interest) and prices rise (inflation). Time-Value-of-Money (TVM) links the two. Every TVM problem has these five pieces; if you know any four, you can find the fifth.\n\n| Symbol | Name | In plain words | Excel |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **N** (nper) | Number of periods | How many years/months the money sits | `nper` |\n| **r / I/Y** | Rate per period | Growth (or interest) each period, as a % | `rate` |\n| **PV** | Present Value | The lump of money **now** | `PV` |\n| **PMT** | Payment | A **fixed amount added/withdrawn every period** | `PMT` |\n| **FV** | Future Value | The money **later** (at the end) | `FV` |\n| *(type)* | Timing | Payment at **end** (0) or **beginning** (1) of period | `type` |\n\n> 💡 **The one idea behind all of it:** each period your money is multiplied by **(1 + r)**. Do that `n` times and you get **(1 + r)ⁿ** — that \"growth factor\" is hiding inside every formula below.\n\n---\n\n## 👀 What each one is & when you use it\n\n### PV — Present Value\n- **What:** what a future amount is worth **today**.\n- **Use when:** \"How much must I invest **now** (one lump sum) to reach a goal?\" or \"What is this future money worth in today's rupees?\"\n- **Formula:** `PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ` (this is compounding run **backwards** — called *discounting*).\n- **Example:** Need ₹10,00,000 in 8 years at 10%. PV = 10,00,000 ÷ 1.10⁸ = **₹4,66,507** to invest today.\n\n### FV — Future Value\n- **What:** what money today grows into **later**.\n- **Use when:** \"I invest ₹X today — what's it worth after n years?\"\n- **Formula:** `FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ`.\n- **Example:** ₹2,00,000 at 12% for 10 years → FV = 2,00,000 × 1.12¹⁰ = **₹6,21,170**.\n\n### PMT — Payment (instalment)\n- **What:** a **fixed amount paid (or received) every period** — a SIP, a yearly saving, an EMI, an annual pension.\n- **Use when:** \"How much must I invest **every month/year** to reach a goal?\" or \"What pension can a corpus pay?\"\n- **Formula (saving to a goal — sinking fund):** `PMT = FV × r ÷ [ (1 + r)ⁿ − 1 ]`.\n- **Example:** Want ₹50,00,000 in 20 years at 9%. PMT = 50,00,000 × 0.09 ÷ (1.09²⁰ − 1) = **₹97,734/year**.\n\n### N — Number of periods\n- **What:** how many periods the money compounds.\n- **Use when:** \"How long to double my money / reach a target?\"\n- **Tip:** if compounding is **monthly**, N = years × 12 (and r = annual ÷ 12).\n\n### r — Rate per period\n- **What:** the growth/interest rate **for one period**.\n- **Use when:** finding the return that links a start and end value (see CAGR).\n- **Tip:** always match r and N to the **same period** (both yearly, or both monthly).\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 The golden formulas (with worked examples)\n\n| # | You want… | Formula | Worked example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **FV of a lump sum** | FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ | ₹1,00,000 @ 8%, 15y → 1,00,000×1.08¹⁵ = **₹3,17,217** |\n| 2 | **PV of a future goal** | PV = FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ | ₹20,00,000 in 10y @ 9% → 20,00,000 ÷ 1.09¹⁰ = **₹8,44,773** |\n| 3 | **FV of a yearly SIP (ordinary annuity)** | FV = PMT·[((1+r)ⁿ−1)/r] | ₹1,20,000/yr, 20y @ 10% → 1,20,000×57.275 = **₹68,73,000** |\n| 4 | **Instalment to hit a goal (PMT)** | PMT = FV·r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ−1] | ₹50,00,000 in 20y @ 9% → **₹97,734/yr** |\n| 5 | **Corpus that funds withdrawals (PV of annuity)** | PV = PMT·[1−(1+r)⁻ⁿ]/r | ₹6,00,000/yr for 25y @ 8% → **₹64,06,800** corpus needed |\n| 6 | **CAGR (steady growth rate)** | (End/Start)^(1/n) − 1 | ₹1L→₹2.5L in 7y → (2.5)^(1/7)−1 = **13.9%** |\n| 7 | **Real (inflation-adjusted) return** | (1+nominal)/(1+inflation) − 1 | 12% return, 6% inflation → 1.12/1.06−1 = **5.66%** |\n| 8 | **Future cost with inflation** | Cost × (1+inflation)ⁿ | ₹5,00,000 @ 7%, 10y → 5,00,000×1.07¹⁰ = **₹9,83,576** |\n| 9 | **Perpetuity (income forever)** | PV = PMT ÷ r | ₹1,00,000/yr forever @ 8% → 1,00,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹12,50,000** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Ordinary annuity vs annuity-due:** if payments come at the **beginning** of each period (e.g., SIP on the 1st), multiply the annuity answer by **(1 + r)**. Formulas 3–5 above assume **end-of-period** (ordinary).\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 \"Which formula do I use?\" — quick decision guide\n\n| The question says… | Reach for… |\n|---|---|\n| \"invest **today**, worth after n years\" | **FV lump sum** (#1) |\n| \"need ₹X in future, invest **one lump sum now**\" | **PV** (#2) |\n| \"invest ₹X **every year/month**, maturity value\" | **FV of annuity / SIP** (#3) |\n| \"how much to save **each year** to reach a goal\" | **PMT / sinking fund** (#4) |\n| \"corpus needed to **withdraw** a pension for n years\" | **PV of annuity** (#5) |\n| \"grew from A to B in n years — what rate?\" | **CAGR** (#6) |\n| \"return after beating inflation\" | **Real return** (#7) |\n| \"what will it **cost** in future\" | **Inflation cost** (#8) |\n| \"income **forever** / no end date\" | **Perpetuity** (#9) |\n\n---\n\n## 💻 Excel / OpenOffice Calc (the test machines have a spreadsheet!)\n\n```\n=FV(rate, nper, pmt, pv, type)     → future value\n=PV(rate, nper, pmt, fv, type)     → present value\n=PMT(rate, nper, pv, fv, type)     → the instalment\n=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, fv, type)     → number of periods\n=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv, type)     → the rate\n```\n- **Sign convention:** money you **pay out** is **negative**; money you **receive** is **positive**. (So a goal-saving PMT comes out negative — that's fine.)\n- `type` = **0** for end-of-period (default), **1** for beginning.\n- Match the period: monthly problem → `rate = annual/12`, `nper = years×12`.\n\n---\n\n## 🚨 The 6 traps that cost marks\n\n1. **Simple vs compound** — almost always **compound**: use (1+r)ⁿ, not r×n.\n2. **Monthly mix-up** — convert BOTH: rate ÷ 12 **and** years × 12.\n3. **Annuity timing** — beginning-of-period payments need a ×(1+r).\n4. **Real return** — **divide** the growth factors, don't just subtract (12%−6% ≠ real return; the right answer is 5.66%, not 6%).\n5. **Excel signs** — a wrong +/− flips the answer; pay-outs are negative.\n6. **Rounding too early** — keep full decimals until the final step.\n\n---\n\n> 🔢 **Practice these live:** open the **Financial Maths Lab** for unlimited auto-generated PV/FV/PMT/CAGR/inflation questions with worked solutions, and the **🧮 Financial Calculator** (bottom-right) to check any working — its TVM tab matches these Excel functions exactly.\n"};
